window.AIGT_PREVIEW_FILES = {"data/arxiv_abstract.jsonl":"{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Gamma-ray line emission from radioactive decay of 60Fe provides constraints on nucleosynthesis in massive stars and supernovae. The spectrometer SPI on board INTEGRAL has accumulated nearly three years of data on gamma-ray emission from the Galactic plane. We have analyzed these data with suitable instrumental-background models and sky distributions to produce high-resolution spectra of Galactic emission. We detect the gamma-ray lines from 60Fe decay at 1173 and 1333 keV, obtaining an improvement over our earlier measurement of both lines with now 4.9 sigma significance for the combination of the two lines. The average flux per line is (4.4 \\\\pm 0.9) \\\\times 10^{-5} ph cm^{-2} s^{-1} rad^{-1} for the inner Galaxy region. Deriving the Galactic 26Al gamma-ray line flux with using the same set of observations and analysis method, we determine the flux ratio of 60Fe/26Al gamma-rays as 0.148 \\\\pm 0.06. The current theoretical predictions are still consistent with our result.\", \"sampled\": \"Near and mid-infrared photometry of the Pleiades cluster was obtained to identify proposed cluster members. The new photometry was used to define the single-star main sequence locus. Candidate members were selected systematically. This survey extends effectively two magnitudes deeper than the 2MASS All-Sky Point Source catalog. The central square degree of the Pleiades was the spatial domain examined. Candidates fainter than Ks=14 were targeted within this region. A Ks magnitude of 14 corresponds to approximately 0.1 solar masses. 42 new candidates were identified. These candidates are expected to improve estimates of the cluster mass function down to approximately 0.04 solar masses. The candidate substellar members were drawn from the central square degree of the Pleiades cluster. New IRAC data, particularly images at 8 micrometers, were used to examine interstellar dust in and near the Pleiades. Dust results are notable.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We study Casimir forces on the partition in a closed box (piston) with perfect metallic boundary conditions. Related closed geometries have generated interest as candidates for a repulsive force. By using an optical path expansion we solve exactly the case of a piston with a rectangular cross section, and find that the force always attracts the partition to the nearest base. For arbitrary cross sections, we can use an expansion for the density of states to compute the force in the limit of small height to width ratios. The corrections to the force between parallel plates are found to have interesting dependence on the shape of the cross section. Finally, for temperatures in the range of experimental interest we compute finite temperature corrections to the force (again assuming perfect boundaries).\", \"sampled\": \"Relaxational critical dynamics of three-dimensional random anisotropy magnets are examined using field-theoretical methods. The system features an n-component order parameter. It is non-conserved and coupled to a conserved scalar density. Structural disorder enters through local quenched anisotropy axes of random orientation. Critical slowing down is a central focus. When anisotropy axes are distributed along the edges of the n-dimensional hypercube, asymptotic dynamical critical properties coincide with those of the random-site Ising model. Despite this asymptotic equivalence, structural disorder produces considerable effects on non-asymptotic critical dynamics. Field-theoretical renormalization group analysis is carried out to two-loop order. This approach yields quantitative estimates for both effective and asymptotic critical exponents. Exponents are obtained for both the order parameter and the conserved scalar density, characterizing their distinct dynamical behavior near criticality. Results follow.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The discovery of the string theory landscape has recently brought attention to the eternal nature of inflation. In contrast to the common belief that eternal inflation may be a generic feature of most inflationary models, in this note we argue that the suppressed amplitude of perturbations due to adiabatic regularization, together with a fine-tuning constraint on the equation of state of the rare inflating pockets with large fluctuations, render eternal inflation expensive in energy and may make it unlikely to occur. The energy scales of the eternally inflating pockets have to be very close to the transplanckian regime in order to compensate for the suppression of regularized perturbations.\", \"sampled\": \"Two-dimensional Rayleigh-Bénard convection was investigated using direct numerical simulation in Boussinesq fluids. The fluid has a Prandtl number of 6.8. It is confined between thermally conducting plates in a two-dimensional geometry. Chaotic movement of two-dimensional rolls normal to the roll axis occurs within a narrow range of the reduced Rayleigh number. This range of the reduced Rayleigh number extends between 770 and 890, marking the regime of chaotic traveling rolls. The lateral shift of the rolls can lead to global flow reversal of the convective motion. Chaotic traveling rolls were observed under both free-slip and no-slip boundary conditions on the velocity field.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We consider the ferromagnetic large-$q$ state Potts model in complex evolving networks, which is equivalent to an optimal cooperation problem, in which the agents try to optimize the total sum of pair cooperation benefits and the supports of independent projects. The agents are found to be typically of two kinds: a fraction of $m$ (being the magnetization of the Potts model) belongs to a large cooperating cluster, whereas the others are isolated one man's projects. It is shown rigorously that the homogeneous model has a strongly first-order phase transition, which turns to second-order for random interactions (benefits), the properties of which are studied numerically on the Barab\\\\'asi-Albert network. The distribution of finite-size transition points is characterized by a shift exponent, $1/\\\\tilde{\\\\nu}'=.26(1)$, and by a different width exponent, $1/\\\\nu'=.18(1)$, whereas the magnetization at the transition point scales with the size of the network, $N$, as: $m\\\\sim N^{-x}$, with $x=.66(1)$.\", \"sampled\": \"A five-dimensional effective description of dynamical electroweak symmetry breaking with walking behavior is constructed. The AdS/CFT dictionary serves as the theoretical framework for building this effective description. The minimal model is characterized by only two new free parameters introduced into the construction. These parameters are the confinement scale and the effective coupling of the new strong sector. Parameter space is restricted by precision electroweak constraints and the requirement that the five-dimensional coupling remain perturbative. The perturbative requirement on the five-dimensional coupling corresponds to the large-N regime in four-dimensional language. Precision electroweak constraints jointly bound the allowed region of the two-parameter space alongside the perturbativity condition. Both restrictions apply simultaneously. Together they define the viable region from which physical predictions about new states are derived. The lightest new observable states are spin-1 particles carrying standard-model electroweak quantum numbers.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"UBVRI photoelectric observations have been made of 109 stars around the sky, centered more or less at -50 degrees declination. The majority of the stars fall in the magnitude range 10.4 < V < 15.5 and in the color index range -0.33 < (B - V) < +1.66. These new broadband photometric standard stars average 16.4 measurements each from data taken on 116 different nights over a period of 4 yr. Similar data are tabulated for 19 stars of interest that were not observed often enough to make them well-defined standard stars.\", \"sampled\": \"A direct method is presented to classify all stable forms on R^n across relevant dimensions. The method also determines the automorphism groups of stable forms on R^n. Both classification and group determination are treated together. In dimensions 6, 7, and 8, stable forms coincide with non-degenerate forms, establishing a precise structural correspondence in those cases. Necessary conditions follow. Sufficient conditions are also presented for a manifold to admit a stable form. The geometric properties of manifolds admitting stable forms are examined in relation to these admissibility conditions. Results apply broadly.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We report on the electric measurements of an individual Au nanoparticle with an ultra-high contact resistance of about $10^{19} \\\\Omega$. The high-impedance measurements have been carried out by counting the electrons that are transferred onto the particle. In order to do this, a carbon nanotube is used as the electrode for the supply of electrons but also as the detector of the charge transfer. Measurements using single-electron detection allow us to determine the separation between the electron states in the Au nanoparticle, which is about 4 meV, consistent with the charging energy of the particle. Single-electron detection with nanotubes offers great promise for the study of electron transfer in highly resistive molecular systems.\", \"sampled\": \"PArthENoPE is a publicly available program for computing light element abundances produced during Big Bang Nucleosynthesis. The paper describing PArthENoPE was accepted by Comp. Phys. Com. It is 18 pages long and contains 2 figures. The program starts from nuclear statistical equilibrium conditions and solves coupled ordinary differential equations. The code follows the departure from chemical equilibrium of nuclear species. It then determines their asymptotic primordial abundances across a range of cosmological conditions. Input cosmological parameters include baryon density, number of effective neutrinos, cosmological constant value, and neutrino chemical potential. NAG library routines are required. The program depends on these commercial library routines for its numerical integration procedures.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Three-body decays of resonant states are studied using integral formulae for decay widths. Theoretical approach with a simplified Hamiltonian allows semianalytical treatment of the problem. The model is applied to decays of the first excited $3/2^{-}$ state of $^{17}$Ne and the $3/2^{-}$ ground state of $^{45}$Fe. The convergence of three-body hyperspherical model calculations to the exact result for widths and energy distributions are studied. The theoretical results for $^{17}$Ne and $^{45}$Fe decays are updated and uncertainties of the derived values are discussed in detail. Correlations for the decay of $^{17}$Ne $3/2^-$ state are also studied.\", \"sampled\": \"String theory on a compact negatively curved manifold can grow at least b_1 new effective dimensions as the space shrinks. U(1)^{b_1} winding symmetry is preserved. This symmetry enables the dimensional growth central to the analysis. Winding currents yield a D-dual description. A Riemann surface of genus h is described in terms of its 2h dimensional Jacobian torus. This D-dual description is perturbed by a closed string tachyon arising as a potential energy term in the worldsheet sigma model. D-branes follow. D-branes on negatively curved manifolds have a classical moduli space consisting of a b_1-torus.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We find sharp bounds for the norm inequality on a Pseudo-hermitian manifold, where the L^2 norm of all second derivatives of the function involving horizontal derivatives is controlled by the L^2 norm of the sub-Laplacian. Perturbation allows us to get a-priori bounds for solutions to sub-elliptic PDE in non-divergence form with bounded measurable coefficients. The method of proof is through a Bochner technique. The Heisenberg group is seen to be en extremal manifold for our inequality in the class of manifolds whose Ricci curvature is non-negative.\", \"sampled\": \"A new symplectic variational approach is developed for modeling dissipation in kinetic equations. The paper is 7 pages long and contains no figures, published in C. R. Math. Acad. Sci. Paris. The approach yields a double bracket structure operating in phase space across the framework. This double bracket structure generates kinetic equations representing coadjoint motion under canonical transformations. The Vlasov equation serves as an example within this framework. The Vlasov case admits measure-valued single-particle solutions. These solutions are reversible.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Magnetic properties of the spin doughnut Mo_{75}V_{20}, which is a frustrated magnetic cluster with twenty spins, are studied by using the finite-temperature Lanczos method. Our model Hamiltonian consists of one ten-spin ring and two five-spin rings with nearest-neighbor antiferromagnetic couplings J and J\\\". The five-spin rings are coupled to the ten-spin ring by an antiferromagnetic exchange interaction J' so as to form isosceles triangles of one J-bond and two J'-bonds. Fitting the theoretical magnetic susceptibility with the experimental one measured by Muller et al., the exchange parameters are estimated as J=388 K, J'=163 K and J\\\"=81 K. The most interesting feature of this system is that the two five-spin rings behave like two free-spins at low temperatures in despite of J'>J\\\". We also calculate temperature dependence of the specific heat, and find three peaks at T sim J, J\\\" and J'/100.\", \"sampled\": \"This study investigates whether mutual interactions between Jupiter and Saturn prevented Type II migration from driving both planets toward the Sun. The work extends prior results by Masset and Snellgrove (2001) using a wider set of initial conditions and disk parameters. A new hydrodynamical code was employed. It properly describes the global viscous evolution of the protoplanetary disk. Initially, both planets migrate inward toward the Sun, with Saturn's migration tending to be faster. Differential rates matter. The planets eventually become locked in a mean motion resonance due to their differential migration rates. When locked in the 2:3 resonance, the resonant configuration is particularly stable and the gaps opened by each planet in the disk may overlap. Gap overlap occurs. This overlap causes a drastic change in the torque balance, substantially slowing inward migration of both planets.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"First principle calculations were performed to study the ground state electronic properties of Barium titanate within the density functional theory (DFT). In our DFT computations, we used Vosko-Wilk-Nusair correlation energy functional and generalized gradient approximation (GGA) exchange and correlation energy functional as suggested by Perdew and Wang (PWGGA). The band structure, total density of states (DOS) and partial DOS have been systematically conducted to investigate the electronic configuration of this prototype ferroelectric perovskits compound. The band gap was 1.92 eV within our approach, and the quasi-flat band at -17 eV and -10 eV were attributed to the O 2s and Ba 5p states respectively, which was in good agreement with the corresponding total DOS and partial DOS. From the DOS investigation, it can be seen that the Ti eg state intended to interact with the oxygen octahedral orbitals to form the p-d hybridization. Moreover the strong p-d overlap and bonding can be observed in the electronic density redistribution along the different crystalline planes with respect to the corresponding space group, and the electronic isodense have been shown along the (001), (100), (110) and (111) crystal planes. From these electronic density maps, the strong bonding between Ti and O atoms can even be observed in the (111) crystalline plane.\", \"sampled\": \"A multi-wavelength photometric study of the globular cluster NGC 1904 is presented here. NGC 1904, also designated M79, was observed across far UV to near infrared wavelengths. Three instruments were used. HST-WFPC2, the 2.2m ESO-WFI, and GALEX each contributed distinct observational coverage. The photometric sample spans the full cluster extension from central regions to the tidal radius. A catalog of blue straggler stars was constructed from this combined dataset. Detection reached a magnitude limit of m_218 <= 19.5. A total of 39 bright blue straggler stars were identified within NGC 1904 under this limit. The spatial distribution of these blue straggler stars shows pronounced central concentration. The BSS population is highly segregated toward the cluster core. No significant frequency upturn was detected in the cluster outskirts. This absence of an outer upturn distinguishes NGC 1904 from comparison clusters including M 3, 47 Tuc, NGC 6752, and M 5. The radius of avoidance estimated for NGC 1904 reaches approximately 30 core radii, a notably large value. Such a large radius of avoidance implies that heavy stars, particularly binary systems, have largely sunk to the core. This dynamical state constrains the likely formation channels for the observed BSS.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The BL Lac object 3C 66A was observed in an extensive multiwavelength monitoring campaign from July 2003 till April 2004. The spectral energy distribution (SED) was measured over the entire electromagnetic spectrum, with flux measurements from radio to X-ray frequencies and upper limits in the very high energy (VHE) gamma-ray regime. Here, we use a time-dependent leptonic jet model to reproduce the SED and optical spectral variability observed during our multiwavelength campaign. Our model simulations could successfully reproduce the observed SED and optical light curves and predict an intrinsic cutoff value for the VHE gamma-ray emission at ~ 4 GeV. The effect of the optical depth due to the intergalactic infrared background radiation (IIBR) on the peak of the high-energy component of 3C 66A was found to be negligible. Also, the presence of a broad line region (BLR) in the case of 3C 66A may play an important role in the emission of gamma-ray photons when the emission region is very close to the central engine, but further out, the production mechanism of hard X-ray and gamma-ray photons becomes rapidly dominated by synchrotron self-Compton emission. We further discuss the possibility of an observable X-ray spectral variability pattern. The simulated results do not predict observable hysteresis patterns in the optical or soft X-ray regimes for major flares on multi-day time scales.\", \"sampled\": \"A model for charge transfer mechanism in Y0.8(Ca)0.2Ba2Cu3O6+x is proposed. The model counts hole doping of CuO2 planes and x dependence of T_c. It uses two channels. Total doped holes in CuO2 planes are sum of holes from two separate channels. One doping channel is substitution of Y3+ by Ca2+. The second doping channel comes from CuO chains longer than a minimal length l_min. Charge transfer from CuO chains requires chain length exceeding l_min. T_c(x) dependence is obtained by combining calculated p(x) and a universal T_c versus p relation. Calculated T_c(x) for l_min=3 and l_min=4 both correlate with experimental T_c(x). The value l_min=4 gives a reasonable overall agreement with experiment. The model accounts for the measured critical temperature variation with oxygen content. Hole doping of the CuO2 planes arises from two independent contributions. The first contribution stems from the partial replacement of Y3+ by Ca2+. The second contribution originates from charge transfer out of CuO chains that exceed a threshold length. This transfer is only active when the chain length surpasses l_min. The resulting hole concentration p(x) is then mapped onto a universal T_c versus p curve. For l_min equal to 3, the calculated T_c(x) reproduces the experimental trend. For l_min equal to 4, the agreement with the measured T_c(x) becomes quantitatively satisfactory.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We present the observations of a weak MgII absorption system detected at z~0.452 in the UVES high resolution spectrum of the QSO HE0001-2340. The weaker of the two MgII components forming the system shows associated absorptions due to SiI, CaI and FeI observed for the first time in a QSO spectrum. We investigate the nature of this absorber by comparing its properties with those of different classes of absorbers (weak MgII, Damped Ly-alpha systems and local interstellar clouds) and reproducing its ionization conditions with photoionization models. The observed absorber belongs to the class of weak MgII systems on the basis of its equivalent width, however the relative strength of commonly observed transitions deviates significantly from those of the above mentioned absorbers. A rough estimate of the probability to cross such a system with a QSO line of sight is P~0.03. The presence of rare neutral transitions suggests that the cloud is shielded by a large amount of neutral hydrogen. A detailed comparison of the observed column densities with the average properties of damped Ly-alpha systems and local interstellar cold clouds shows, in particular, deficient MgII/MgI and CaII/CaI ratios in our cloud. The results of photoionization models indicate that the cloud could be ionized by the UV background. However, a simple model of a single cloud with uniform density cannot reproduce the observed ionic abundance ratios, suggesting a more complex density structure for the absorber. Supposing that ionization corrections are negligible, the most puzzling result is the underabundance of magnesium with respect to iron which is hard to explain both with nucleosynthesis and with differential dust depletion.\", \"sampled\": \"Entanglement swapping projects two particles onto an entangled state without prior interaction. This work presents the first realization of entanglement swapping using continuous wave sources. Narrow-band filters increase photon coherence time beyond detector temporal resolution. CW sources eliminate the need for synchronization between sources. The experiment uses time-bin entangled photons at telecom wavelengths. The setup exploits improved time precision of photon detectors. The setup exploits efficient SPDC photon pair production with waveguides in nonlinear crystals. The setup exploits stability of narrow-band filters. The approach is independent of the form of entanglement. The setup is robust against thermal or mechanical fluctuations in optical fibres. Cm-long coherence lengths contribute to robustness. The experiment uses time-bin entangled photons at telecom wavelengths. The setup exploits improved time precision of photon detectors. The setup exploits efficient SPDC photon pair production with waveguides in nonlinear crystals. The setup exploits stability of narrow-band filters. The approach is independent of the form of entanglement. The setup is robust against thermal or mechanical fluctuations in optical fibres. Cm-long coherence lengths contribute to robustness. The approach is independent of the form of entanglement. The setup is robust against thermal or mechanical fluctuations in optical fibres. Narrow-band filters increase photon coherence time. The setup exploits improved time precision of photon detectors. The setup exploits efficient SPDC photon pair production with waveguides in nonlinear crystals. The setup exploits stability of narrow-band filters. The approach is independent of the form of entanglement. The setup is robust against thermal or mechanical fluctuations in optical fibres. Cm-long coherence lengths contribute to robustness. The approach is independent of the form of entanglement.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Internet-based personal digital belongings present different vulnerabilities than locally stored materials. We use responses to a survey of people who have recovered lost websites, in combination with supplementary interviews, to paint a fuller picture of current curatorial strategies and practices. We examine the types of personal, topical, and commercial websites that respondents have lost and the reasons they have lost this potentially valuable material. We further explore what they have tried to recover and how the loss influences their subsequent practices. We found that curation of personal digital materials in online stores bears some striking similarities to the curation of similar materials stored locally in that study participants continue to archive personal assets by relying on a combination of benign neglect, sporadic backups, and unsystematic file replication. However, we have also identified issues specific to Internet-based material: how risk is spread by distributing the files among multiple servers and services; the circular reasoning participants use when they discuss the safety of their digital assets; and the types of online material that are particularly vulnerable to loss. The study reveals ways in which expectations of permanence and notification are violated and situations in which benign neglect has far greater consequences for the long-term fate of important digital assets.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper addresses log-likelihood ratio calculation on fading channels without receiver channel state information. Existing methods assume known noise power. They use the expected fading gain in a linear function of the channel output. The authors find the optimum linear LLR approximation maximizing achievable transmission rate, assuming known noise power. The maximum achievable rate under the proposed linear LLR is almost equal to that under true LLR calculation. The method also appears optimum in terms of bit error rate performance. Results are extended to the case where noise power is unknown at the receiver. Performance with unknown noise power is almost identical to the case with perfectly known noise power. The work was presented at the IEEE International Symposium on Information Theory. The venue was located in Nice, France. The fading channel includes additive noise as a channel impairment. Channel state information remains unavailable at the receiver throughout the analysis. Log-likelihood ratios serve as the decoding input for the iterative decoding framework. The proposed linear LLR calculation forms the core approximation method. The maximum achievable transmission rate functions as the primary optimization criterion. Bit error rate provides a secondary performance criterion for evaluating the method.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In 2nd order causal dissipative theory, space-time evolution of QGP fluid is studied in 2+1 dimensions. Relaxation equations for shear stress tensors are solved simultaneously with the energy-momentum conservation equations. Comparison of evolution of ideal and viscous QGP fluid, initialized under the same conditions, e.g. same equilibration time, energy density and velocity profile, indicate that in a viscous dynamics, energy density or temperature of the fluid evolve slowly, than in an ideal fluid. Cooling gets slower as viscosity increases. Transverse expansion also increases in a viscous dynamics. For the first time we have also studied elliptic flow of 'quarks' in causal viscous dynamics. It is shown that elliptic flow of quarks saturates due to non-equilibrium correction to equilibrium distribution function, and can not be mimicked by an ideal hydrodynamics.\", \"sampled\": \"Quantum mechanical ensemble control underlies experiments in coherent nuclear and electron magnetic resonance as well as optical spectroscopy. These experiments employ pulse sequences as control inputs. The sequences implement precise unitary transformations on the spin system. Pulse sequences must minimize duration to reduce relaxation and decoherence. Shorter sequences also optimize sensitivity by limiting the time available for signal loss during the control operation. This work provides efficient syntheses of various unitary transformations on Ising spin chains of arbitrary length. The efficient realization is obtained by computing geodesics on a sphere under a special metric. The geodesic approach yields compact pulse sequences that respect the system geometry. It avoids unnecessary excursions in the control space.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper concludes our efforts in describing SU(3)-Yang-Mills theories at different couplings/temperatures in terms of effective Polyakov-loop models. The associated effective couplings are determined through an inverse Monte Carlo procedure based on novel Schwinger-Dyson equations that employ the symmetries of the Haar measure. Due to the first-order nature of the phase transition we encounter a fine-tuning problem in reproducing the correct behavior of the Polyakov-loop from the effective models. The problem remains under control as long as the number of effective couplings is sufficiently small.\", \"sampled\": \"We analyze a nonfrustrated magnetoelectric model where strong magnetoelectric coupling induces incommensurate magnetic order. That incommensurate magnetic order directly yields ferroelectricity. The magnetic field is applied perpendicular to the wave vector of the spin helix. Ferroelectric onset occurs at a critical field H_{c1} lower than the saturation field H_{c2}. When magnetoelectric coupling becomes sufficiently strong, H_{c1} coincides with H_{c2}. At zero magnetic field, ferroelectricity emerges at a temperature T_{FE} below the magnetic ordering temperature T_N.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A closed Riemannian manifold is said to have cross blocking if whenever distinct points p and q are at distance less than the diameter, all light rays from p can be shaded away from q with at most two point shades. Similarly, a closed Riemannian manifold is said to have sphere blocking if for each point p, all the light rays from p are shaded away from p by a single point shade. We prove that Riemannian manifolds with cross and sphere blocking are isometric to round spheres.\", \"sampled\": \"Fermionic polar molecules or atoms confined in a bilayer optical lattice can undergo a transition into a circulating current state. This circulating current state spontaneously breaks time reversal symmetry, representing a distinct quantum phase. Estimates of the relevant temperature scales for the transition are provided. Experimental signatures are identified. Related phenomena in bosonic systems with ring exchange are discussed. Related phenomena in spin systems with ring exchange are also discussed. These findings outline a route to realizing and detecting a time reversal symmetry broken phase. Keywords: circulating currents.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We present a protocol for growing graph states, the resource for one-way quantum computing, when the available entanglement mechanism is highly imperfect. The distillation protocol is frugal in its use of ancilla qubits, requiring only a single ancilla qubit when the noise is dominated by one Pauli error, and two for a general noise model. The protocol works with such scarce local resources by never post-selecting on the measurement outcomes of purification rounds. We find that such a strategy causes fidelity to follow a biased random walk, and that a target fidelity is likely to be reached more rapidly than for a comparable post-selecting protocol. An analysis is presented of how imperfect local operations limit the attainable fidelity. For example, a single Pauli error rate of 20% can be distilled down to $\\\\sim 10$ times the imperfection in local operations.\", \"sampled\": \"A review examines whether metallicity can indicate a galaxy's age. The most metal-poor star-forming galaxies in the local universe may be genuinely young. Observational evidence for downsizing shows average stellar age decreases with decreasing mass and metallicity. From both observational and theoretical viewpoints, metallicity is not an arrow of time. The most metal-poor galaxies in the local universe are not necessarily young. Current observations suggest an old stellar population is present in all metal-poor galaxies. A couple of cases remain under debate. One debated case is the galaxy IZw18. Further observations with more sensitive equipment should settle this question in the coming years. The review was published by the International Astronomical Union. The review examines whether metallicity can indicate a galaxy's age. The most metal-poor star-forming galaxies in the local universe may be genuinely young.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We consider a nearest-neighbor, one dimensional random walk $\\\\{X_n\\\\}_{n\\\\geq0}$ in a random i.i.d. environment, in the regime where the walk is transient but with zero speed, so that $X_n$ is of order $n^s$ for some $s 0$ and $\\\\to0$ for $x\\\\leq0$ (a spread out regime).\", \"sampled\": \"The hadronic form factor f+(q2) in D0 → K- e+ νe decay was measured. The measurement employed a model independent analysis of 75 fb^{-1} of data recorded with the BABAR detector at the PEPII electron-positron collider. The result was compared with theoretical calculations to assess consistency with Standard Model predictions.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Consider the family S of irreducible plane curves of degree n with d nodes and k cusps as singularities. Let W be an irreducible component of S. We consider the natural rational map from W to the moduli space of curves of genus g=(n-1)(n-2)/2-d-k. We define the \\\"number of moduli of W\\\" as the dimension of the image of W with respect to this map. If W has the expected dimension equal to 3n+g-1-k, then the number of moduli of W is at most equal to the min(3g-3, 3g-3+\\\\rho-k), dove \\\\rho is the Brill-Neother number of the linear series of degree n and dimension 2 on a smooth curve of genus g. We say that W has the expected number of moduli if the equality holds. In this paper we construct examples of families of irreducible plane curves with nodes and cusps as singularities having expected number of moduli and with non-positive Brill-Noether number.\", \"sampled\": \"The Gemini Planet Imager is an extreme adaptive optics system developed for the Gemini Observatory. GPI combines wavefront control with diffraction suppression. It also includes a speckle-suppressing science camera. The camera provides integral field and polarimetry capabilities. Its primary science goal is the direct detection and characterization of young, Jovian-mass exoplanets. For systems younger than 2 Gyr, detection completeness exceeds 50 percent. This applies to exoplanets more massive than 6 Jupiter masses with semimajor axes beyond 10 AU. GPI will also discover faint debris disks and explore icy moons and minor planets in the solar system. The instrument will reveal high dynamic range main-sequence binaries and study mass loss from evolved stars. A white paper explains GPI's role in exoplanet discovery and characterization. It summarizes recommendations to the NSF-NASA-DOE Astronomy and Astrophysics Advisory Committee ExoPlanet Task Force. The white paper outlines the instrument's expected contributions. The task force received these recommendations. End of abstract.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"To simulate the transient enhanced diffusion near the surface or interface, a set of equations describing the impurity diffusion and quasichemical reactions of dopant atoms and point defects in ion-implanted layers is proposed and analyzed. The diffusion equations obtained take into account different charge states of mobile or immobile species and drift the mobile species in the built-in electric field and field of elastic stresses. The absorption of self-interstitials on the surface and drift of the defects due to elastic stresses result in the nonuniform distributions of point defects. It was shown analytically and by means of numerical calculations that consideration of the nonuniform defect distributions enables one to explain the phenomenon of \\\"uphill\\\" impurity diffusion near the surface during annealing of ion-implanted layers. The performed calculations of the boron concentration profile after annealing of a shallow implanted layer agree well with the experimental data confirming the efficiency of the proposed equations.\", \"sampled\": \"John Hubbard's conjecture on the topological complexity of the hyperbolic horseshoe locus of the complex Hénon map is proven. The proof establishes the existence of several non-trivial loops in this locus. These loops generate infinitely many mutually different monodromies within the hyperbolic horseshoe locus. This result is obtained. A rigorous computational algorithm for verifying uniform hyperbolicity of chain recurrent sets serves as the main tool. The conjecture addresses fundamental questions about the topological structure of parameter space for the complex Hénon map. The hyperbolic horseshoe locus contains non-trivial loops whose monodromies exhibit infinite diversity. This diversity is now confirmed. The computational algorithm enables verification of uniform hyperbolicity for chain recurrent sets arising in the complex Hénon map dynamics. The algorithm operates on chain recurrent sets to confirm uniform hyperbolicity. This verification underpins the entire proof.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In SU(3) simplest little Higgs model, a characteristic particle is the light pseudoscalar boson eta, it leads interesting signals in the LHC/ILC and is studied in literature with different parameterizations. In this work, we show that these different descriptions for eta particle are equivalent up to some SU(3) rotations as long as we suitably redefine the pseudo Goldstone boson fields. We evaluate the necessary SU(3) rotations and built up explicit expressions for redefined fields.\", \"sampled\": \"R-band observations of comet C2004B1 were conducted over the period between June 21 and August 20, 2006. The observational data was processed into surface brightness maps, mean radial profiles of the coma, and light curves. A decrease in the comet's brightness was recorded. This occurred on two separate occasions. Each of the recorded brightness decrease events was observed to last for a duration of several days.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We compare waveforms obtained by numerically evolving nonspinning binary black holes to post-Newtonian (PN) template families currently used in the search for gravitational waves by ground-based detectors. We find that the time-domain 3.5PN template family, which includes the inspiral phase, has fitting factors (FFs) >= 0.96 for binary systems with total mass M = 10 ~ 20 Msun. The time-domain 3.5PN effective-one-body template family, which includes the inspiral, merger and ring-down phases, gives satisfactory signal-matching performance with FFs >= 0.96 for binary systems with total mass M = 10 ~ 120 Msun. If we introduce a cutoff frequency properly adjusted to the final black-hole ring-down frequency, we find that the frequency-domain stationary-phase-approximated template family at 3.5PN order has FFs >= 0.96 for binary systems with total mass M = 10 ~ 20 Msun. However, to obtain high matching performances for larger binary masses, we need to either extend this family to unphysical regions of the parameter space or introduce a 4PN order coefficient in the frequency-domain GW phase. Finally, we find that the phenomenological Buonanno-Chen-Vallisneri family has FFs >= 0.97 with total mass M=10 ~ 120Msun. The main analyses use the noise spectral-density of LIGO, but several tests are extended to VIRGO and advanced LIGO noise-spectral densities.\", \"sampled\": \"An autoregressive model was used to predict future solar cycle amplitude and period. The model utilized physical parameters from 22 solar cycles observed between 1755 and 1996. The prediction for current cycle 23 showed good agreement. It matched observations. The period for solar cycle 23 was determined to be 11.73 years based on this model. Solar cycle 24 is predicted to have a period of 9.34 years and an amplitude of 110 (± 11). Cycle 25. Its predicted period is 12.49 years with an amplitude of 110 (± 11). The study predicts fifteen future solar cycles. Very low sunspot activity, around 60, is predicted for solar cycle 31 from 2089 to 2100 AD. Cycle 33. The minimum predicted cycle length is 8.65 years for this cycle. The length of future solar cycles is predicted to vary. The maximum predicted length is 13.07 years for solar cycle 35. This study used an autoregressive model to forecast these cycles. It used physical parameters. The predictions extend through many future cycles, including cycle 38. Very high sunspot activity is predicted for cycles 26, 27, 34, 37, and 38. This study was performed by Hiremath 2006a. It predicted future activity. The model was based on 22 observed cycles. The study.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Advanced electromagnetic potentials are indigenous to the classical Maxwell theory. Generally however they are deemed undesirable and are forcibly excluded, destroying the theory's inherent time-symmetry. We investigate the reason for this, pointing out that it is not necessary and in some cases is counter-productive. We then focus on the direct-action theory in which the advanced and retarded contributions are present symmetrically, with no opportunity to supplement the particular integral solution of the wave equation with an arbitrary complementary function. One then requires a plausible explanation for the observed broken symmetry that, commonly, is understood cannot be met by the Wheeler-Feynman mechanism because the necessary boundary condition cannot be satisfied in acceptable cosmologies. We take this opportunity to argue that the boundary condition is already met by all expanding cosmologies simply as a result of cosmological red-shift. A consequence is that the cosmological and thermodynamic arrows of time can be equated, the direct action version of EM is preferred, and that advanced potentials are ubiquitous.\", \"sampled\": \"This study calculates the J/psi number distribution in central Au-Au collisions at RHIC. A ccbar pair is created. It expands to the size of a J/psi particle. These ccbar pairs are produced in the initial collision. They are also produced later. Pairs can also dissociate in the prethermal and thermal stages of the system, counteracting initial J/psi yield suppression. Dissociation occurs. A color singlet ccbar and a gluon become a color octet ccbar. A color octet ccbar and a gluon can also remain a color octet. Production from the partonic system counteracts the suppression of the initial J/psi yield. A bulge was found. It was in the J/psi number distribution. This bulge was found within the rapidity range of -1.5 < y < 1.5. This finding is attributed to the partonic system and serves as an indicator. It indicates the presence of a deconfined partonic medium. This is a key finding.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We present the construction and properties of a self-dual perverse sheaf S_0 whose cohomology fulfills some of the requirements of String theory as outlined by T. Hubsch in hep-th/9612075. The construction of this S_0 utilizes techniques that follow from MacPherson-Vilonen (Inv. Math. vol. 84, pp. 403-435, 1986). Finally, we will discuss its properties as they relate to String theory.\", \"sampled\": \"A quantum operation's nonlocal content is indicated by its entangling and disentangling capacities, which are a primary indicator. Significant research has been dedicated to the (dis)entangling capacities of unitary operations for quantum operations. Knowledge is limited. It concerns non-unitary operations. This work examines the capacities of unital CPTP maps. These capacities are for non-unitary operations. This is research.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Suppose that $h$ and $g$ belong to the algebra $\\\\B$ generated by the rational functions and an entire function $f$ of finite order on ${\\\\Bbb C}^n$ and that $h/g$ has algebraic polar variety. We show that either $h/g\\\\in\\\\B$ or $f=q_1e^p+q_2$, where $p$ is a polynomial and $q_1,q_2$ are rational functions.\", \"sampled\": \"This research provides explicit formulas for maps within a long exact sequence linking bialgebra cohomology and Hochschild cohomology. A sufficient condition is presented for the surjectivity of the connecting homomorphism in this long exact sequence. These results are used to compute cocycles. The study computes all bialgebra two-cocycles for specific Radford biproducts, also known as bosonizations.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"As a step towards formulating projective superspace techniques for supergravity theories with eight supercharges, this work is devoted to field theory in five-dimensional N = 1 anti-de Sitter superspace AdS^{5|8} = SU(2,2|1)/SO(4,1) x U(1) which is a maximally symmetric curved background. We develop the differential geometry of AdS^{5|8} and describe its isometries in terms of Killing supervectors. Various off-shell supermultiplets in AdS^{5|8} x S^2 are defined, and supersymmetric actions are constructed both in harmonic and projective superspace approaches. Several families of supersymmetric theories are presented including nonlinear sigma-models, Chern-Simons theories and vector-tensor dynamical systems. Using a suitable coset representative, we make use of the coset construction to develop an explicit realization for one half of the superspace AdS^{5|8} as a trivial fiber bundle with fibers isomorophic to four-dimensional Minkowski superspace.\", \"sampled\": \"This research concerns a complete quaternionic Kähler manifold, denoted as M^{4n}. The manifold’s scalar curvature is assumed to be bounded below by -16n(n+2). A sharp estimate was found for the first eigenvalue of the Laplacian, λ₁(M). The first eigenvalue is less than or equal to (2n+1)². This is the estimate. If the eigenvalue equals (2n+1)², the manifold M has a single end. It could also be diffeomorphic to R x N. N is a compact manifold. If M also has bounded curvature and the equality holds, it is covered. Its covering space is the quaternionic hyperbolic space. N is a compact quotient. It is a quotient of the generalized Heisenberg group. If the first eigenvalue is at least 8(n+2)/3, M has only one end.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The existence of certain TeV suppressed higher-dimension operators may open up new decay channels for the Higgs boson to decay into lighter right-handed neutrinos. These channels may dominate over all other channels if the Higgs boson is light. For a Higgs boson mass larger than $2 m_W$ the new decays are subdominant yet still of interest. The right-handed neutrinos have macroscopic decay lengths and decay mostly into final states containing leptons and quarks. A distinguishing collider signature of this scenario is a pair of displaced vertices violating lepton number. A general operator analysis is performed using the minimal flavor violation hypothesis to illustrate that these novel decay processes can occur while remaining consistent with experimental constraints on lepton number violating processes. In this context the question of whether these new decay modes dominate is found to depend crucially on the approximate flavor symmetries of the right-handed neutrinos.\", \"sampled\": \"A Cauchy-Riemann type operator can be used to factorize the angular part of the Laplacian. This factorization is possible if the unit sphere in R³ has at least one point removed. We establish this. A complex algebra of potential functions is established by solutions to the operator. The paper is 4 pages. A specific family of these solutions is normalizable on the sphere. This allows for construction. Associate solutions can be constructed. These solutions are for any radial solution to the time-independent Schrödinger equation with a radial potential. The resulting family of solutions is demonstrably square integrable within the space of R³. These solutions are singular. They are singular on at least one half-plane. Despite this singularity, the solutions are found to be square-integrable in almost all of R³. The solutions establish a complex algebra of potential functions. A specific family of these solutions is normalizable.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We present a multiwavelength study of the poor cluster RX J1117.4+0743 ([VMF 98] 097) at z=0.485, based on GMOS/Gemini South g', r' photometry and spectroscopy, and XMM-Newton observations. We examine its nature and surroundings by analyzing the projected galaxy distribution, the galaxy velocity distribution, the weak-lensing mass reconstruction, and the X-ray spectroscopy and imaging. The cluster shows a complex morphology. It is composed by at least two structures along the line-of-sight, with velocity dispersions of 592+-82 km s^-1 and 391+-85 km s^-1 respectively. Other structures are also detected in X-ray, in the galaxy projected number density map, and by weak-lensing. One of these clumps, could be gravitationally bound and associated to the main cluster. The derived temperature and bolometric X-ray luminosity reveal that [VMF 98] 097 behave like a normal cluster, in agreement with Lx-Tx correlation found for both local (z=0) and moderate redshift (z~0.4) clusters. We find that the mass determination inferred from weak-lensing is in average 3 to 4.8 times higher (depending on the model assumed) than the X-ray mass. We have two possible explanations for this discrepancy: i) the cluster is in non-equilibrium, then the deviation of the X-ray estimated mass from the true value can be as high as a factor of two; ii) the intervening mass along the line-of-sight of the cluster is producing an over-estimation of the weak-lensing mass. Based on the analysis presented, we conclude that [VMF 98] 097 is a perturbed cluster with at least two substructures in velocity space and with other nearby structures at projected distances of about 1 h^-1 Mpc. This cluster is an example of a poor cluster caught in the process of accreting sub-structures to become a rich cluster.\", \"sampled\": \"This work presents the first measurement of the soft X-ray cluster-AGN cross-correlation function conducted in 3D redshift space. The analysis utilized data from the ROSAT-NEP survey, which detected 442 X-ray sources that were almost fully identified spectroscopically. The survey covered a large area. This area was 9x9 square degrees. It was centered around the location of the North Ecliptic Pole. This study examined the spatial relationship between active galactic nuclei and clusters of galaxies. A clustering signal was detected. It had a significance over 3-sigma. This was found. The signal was detected on scales of less than 50 h70^-1 Mpc. A power-law fit was applied to the collected data. The fit yielded results. The fit produced a specific correlation length (s_0) for the sample of AGNs. This correlation length was 8.7+1.2-0.3. The value is given in h_70-1 Mpc. The same power-law fit was used. It also produced a slope value. The resulting slope, or gamma, was measured to be 1.7+0.2-0.7 for the data. This measurement of the slope included its associated 1-sigma statistical errors. The analysis provides strong evidence. This evidence suggests AGNs trace the Universe's large-scale structure. These results indicate that active galactic nuclei are effective tracers of this large-scale structure. The AGN bias factor was observed. It was found to be similar to that of galaxies. However, this observation of the AGN bias factor came with significant uncertainty. Previous deep Chandra observations had shown that AGN source surface density increases in proximity to known galaxy clusters. This investigation builds upon earlier work showing increased AGN source density near clusters. These earlier findings were noted. They came from deep Chandra observations.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We construct analytic solutions for marginal deformations satisfying the reality condition in open superstring field theory formulated by Berkovits when operator products made of the marginal operator and the associated superconformal primary field are regular. Our strategy is based on the recent observation by Erler that the problem of finding solutions for marginal deformations in open superstring field theory can be reduced to a problem in the bosonic theory of finding a finite gauge parameter for a certain pure-gauge configuration labeled by the parameter of the marginal deformation. We find a gauge transformation generated by a real gauge parameter which infinitesimally changes the deformation parameter and construct a finite gauge parameter by its path-ordered exponential. The resulting solution satisfies the reality condition by construction.\", \"sampled\": \"A proof is presented demonstrating that a specific q-difference equation can be successfully factorized. The equation under examination is the Sturm-Liouville form for the continuous q-ultraspherical polynomials of Rogers. This factorization is achieved through the use of an explicitly defined q-difference operator, D_x^{β, q}. This process reveals a new equation. This equation governs the polynomials denoted as C_n(x;β| q). The factorization shows that these polynomials are governed by a different second-order q-difference equation. This newly derived governing equation is expressed as D_x^{β, q} C_n(x;β| q) = (q^{-n/2} + βq^{n/2}) C_n(x;β| q). The relationship between the new equation and the original can be considered a \\\"square root\\\" form. The original second-order q-difference equation is the Sturm-Liouville form for these particular polynomials. This form was studied.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"As networks and their structure have become a major field of research, a strong demand for network visualization has emerged. We address this challenge by formalizing the well established spring layout in terms of dynamic equations. We thus open up the design space for new algorithms. Drawing from the knowledge of systems design, we derive a layout algorithm that remedies several drawbacks of the original spring layout. This new algorithm relies on the balancing of two antagonistic forces. We thus call it {\\\\em arf} for \\\"attractive and repulsive forces\\\". It is, as we claim, particularly suited for a dynamic layout of smaller networks ($n < 10^3$). We back this claim with several application examples from on going complex systems research.\", \"sampled\": \"A new interatomic potential for water was developed within a charge-transfer embedded atom method (EAM) framework. The potential models explicit charge transfer. This is based on local chemical environments. A simplified version of the potential was created for initial testing purposes. This was examined. The potential's ability to model the energetics of small water clusters was tested. Results were excellent. The outcomes showed strong agreement with existing experimental and high-level quantum computational data. This research is considered a first step. It moves toward a unified charge-transfer potential. The manuscript contains 19 pages. It also includes a total of 10 figures. The ultimate goal is a potential. It should accurately describe water's polymorphs and dynamics.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"We consider the bosonic sector of a N=2 supersymmetric Chern-Simons-Higgs\\ntheory in 2+1 dimensions. The gauge group is U(1)xU(N) and has N_f flavors of\\nfundamental matter fields. The model supports non-Abelian (axially symmetric)\\nvortices when N_f\\\\geq N, which have internal (orientational) moduli. When N_f >\\nN, the solutions acquire additional collective coordinates parameterizing their\\ntransverse size.\", \"sampled\": \"In a study, the conductors of p-adic differential modules were investigated within the global context. The research delved into variational properties and the definition of overconvergent isocrystals, showcasing some of their key aspects. Notably, the study highlighted 34 as a relevant numerical reference, along with the values 3, 3.4, 4.3, and 5, which played essential roles in the analytical framework.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In the Randall-Sundrum model, setting the ratio of up and down quark masses\\n$m_u/m_d << 1$, relevant to the strong CP problem, does not require chiral\\nsymmetry or fine-tuning, due to exponential bulk fermion profiles. We point out\\nthat such geometric suppression of the mass of a fermion magnifies the masses\\nof its corresponding Kaluza-Klein (KK) states. In this sense, these KK states\\nact as \\\"microscopes\\\" for probing light quark and lepton masses. In simple\\nrealizations, this hypothesis can be testable at future colliders, like the\\nLHC, by measuring the spectrum of level-1 KK fermions.\", \"sampled\": \"Self-organization processes in nonlinear systems with external forces and absorbers were studied. The analysis considered a model under specific assumptions, investigating the influence of external forces and absorbers separately. It was found that self-organization occurs through Hopf bifurcation, leading to stable pulse radiation. The study revealed that the external force can suppress self-organization processes, while the combined influence of external forces and absorbers results in a more complicated system. The nonlinear absorber's influence extends the control parameters' domain, allowing for the management of stable processes. Additionally, the analysis performed according to Floquet exponent techniques highlighted the intricate dynamics of the system.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"There is a huge amount of historical documents in libraries and in various\\nNational Archives that have not been exploited electronically. Although\\nautomatic reading of complete pages remains, in most cases, a long-term\\nobjective, tasks such as word spotting, text/image alignment, authentication\\nand extraction of specific fields are in use today. For all these tasks, a\\nmajor step is document segmentation into text lines. Because of the low quality\\nand the complexity of these documents (background noise, artifacts due to\\naging, interfering lines),automatic text line segmentation remains an open\\nresearch field. The objective of this paper is to present a survey of existing\\nmethods, developed during the last decade, and dedicated to documents of\\nhistorical interest.\", \"sampled\": \"The effects of mesonic correlations on QCD phase transition categories were explored in this study. FIZ texts extended the discussion with references to finite temperature phases, highlighting the importance of contrasting these findings with previous mean-field approximation investigations that included mesonic correlations. The study revealed that physical pion masses play a dominant role in pressure dynamics below the phase transition point, with pions being the primary contributors. Furthermore, lattice simulations demonstrated that large pion masses led to the suppression of meson effects, showcasing the intricate interplay of various factors in the transition process. This research adds valuable insights to the understanding of mesonic correlations in QCD phase transitions, with 11 instances of such correlations analyzed across 4 distinct categories.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Elastic light scattering by mature red blood cells (RBCs) was theoretically\\nand experimentally analyzed with the discrete dipole approximation (DDA) and\\nthe scanning flow cytometry (SFC), respectively. SFC permits measurement of\\nangular dependence of light-scattering intensity (indicatrix) of single\\nparticles. A mature RBC is modeled as a biconcave disk in DDA simulations of\\nlight scattering. We have studied the effect of RBC orientation related to the\\ndirection of the incident light upon the indicatrix. Numerical calculations of\\nindicatrices for several aspect ratios and volumes of RBC have been carried\\nout. Comparison of the simulated indicatrices and indicatrices measured by SFC\\nshowed good agreement, validating the biconcave disk model for a mature RBC.\", \"sampled\": \"The Eulerian Conservative High Order scheme is designed to work at a general level, providing a shock-capturing conservative method capable of operating at arbitrary levels. It aims to present a general framework based on a 3+1 Eulerian formalism. The method implements various high-order reconstruction techniques, utilizing a two-wave approximate Riemann solver. By adopting Upwind Constrained Transport (UCT) procedures, the induction equation is effectively managed. This approach is also adaptable to magnetodynamics, known as force-free degenerate electrodynamics, and can be implemented simply. The scheme has been tested against several astrophysical applications, demonstrating its accuracy, efficiency, versatility, and robustness. Particularly, the reconstruction based on a monotonicity-preserving filter has shown promising results when applied in fixed scenarios.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This article belongs to a series on geometric complexity theory (GCT), an\\napproach to the P vs. NP and related problems through algebraic geometry and\\nrepresentation theory. The basic principle behind this approach is called the\\nflip. In essence, it reduces the negative hypothesis in complexity theory (the\\nlower bound problems), such as the P vs. NP problem in characteristic zero, to\\nthe positive hypothesis in complexity theory (the upper bound problems):\\nspecifically, to showing that the problems of deciding nonvanishing of the\\nfundamental structural constants in representation theory and algebraic\\ngeometry, such as the well known plethysm constants--or rather certain relaxed\\nforms of these decision probelms--belong to the complexity class P.\", \"sampled\": \"Succinct Indexable Dictionaries and Prefix Sums and Multisets are discussed in the context of data structures. Leicester Tech presents work on \\\"Succinct Indexable Dictionaries,\\\" which efficiently encode k-ary applications. This data structure supports operations in O(1) time, a notable improvement. Unlike previous dictionaries, it handles membership queries without taking excessive space. Additionally, Fich and Miltersen propose a cell probe model that achieves O(lg m) additive removals. They extend the applications of indexable dictionary data structures, including information-theoretically secure encoding.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Future detection/non-detection of tensor modes from inflation in CMB\\nobservations presents a unique way to test certain features of string theory.\\nCurrent limit on the ratio of tensor to scalar perturbations, r=T/S, is r 10^{-2}-10^{-3}. At present all\\nknown string theory inflation models predict tensor modes well below the level\\nof detection. Therefore a possible experimental discovery of tensor modes may\\npresent a challenge to string cosmology.\\n The strongest bound on r in string inflation follows from the observation\\nthat in most of the models based on the KKLT construction, the value of the\\nHubble constant H during inflation must be smaller than the gravitino mass. For\\nthe gravitino mass in the usual range, m_{3/2} 10^{-3} would imply that the gravitinos in this class of models are\\nsuperheavy, m_{3/2} > 10^{13} GeV. This would have important implications for\\nparticle phenomenology based on string theory.\", \"sampled\": \"Recent research has delved into the phenomenon of Ongoing Galactic Accretion through simulations and observations of condensed gas, focusing on Hot Halos and Velocity Clouds within the context of Galaxy and Magellanic Stream dynamics. The study highlights changes in techniques and references ongoing accretion onto galactic disks, identifying high-velocity clouds (HVCs) analogous to those observed in the HI galaxy. By comparing the distribution of HVCs observed around our galaxy with extra-planar clouds, the findings reveal a strong correspondence between observations and simulations. Moreover, the research demonstrates that condensed cloud accretion can account for up to 0.2 million solar masses, with simulated halo clouds accelerating and becoming more massive. The parameter space of simulated clouds aligns well with all observed HVC complexes at varying distances, indicating a consistent relationship. Additionally, nearly half of these simulated halo clouds are projected to evolve into infalling condensed clouds, supporting the majority of HVCs as remnants of such accretion processes.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Epitaxial self-assembled quantum dots (SAQDs) are of interest for\\nnanostructured optoelectronic and electronic devices such as lasers,\\nphotodetectors and nanoscale logic. Spatial order and size order of SAQDs are\\nimportant to the development of usable devices. It is likely that these two\\ntypes of order are strongly linked; thus, a study of spatial order will also\\nhave strong implications for size order. Here a study of spatial order is\\nundertaken using a linear analysis of a commonly used model of SAQD formation\\nbased on surface diffusion. Analytic formulas for film-height correlation\\nfunctions are found that characterize quantum dot spatial order and\\ncorresponding correlation lengths that quantify order.\", \"sampled\": \"The IEEE Information Theory Workshop discussed task learning in compressed sensing, focusing on observations and categories in computer science, linguistics, and mathematics. The allowable predictors were from a specified class, aiming for asymptotically optimal performance. The setting considered involved perfect observations of a part of the sample, where encoding values of y depended on x. Admissible predictors were analyzed under suitable regularity conditions within an underlying family of probability distributions. The concepts were exemplified through nonparametric regression in the presence of Gaussian noise. The workshop highlighted the importance of these ideas in 2007, showcasing the practical applications of such approaches in the field.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In this paper I describe a specialized algorithm for anisotropic diffusion\\ndetermined by a field of transition rates. The algorithm can be used to\\ndescribe some interesting forms of diffusion that occur in the study of proton\\nmotion in a network of hydrogen bonds. The algorithm produces data that require\\na nonstandard method of spectral analysis which is also developed here.\\nFinally, I apply the algorithm to a simple specific example.\", \"sampled\": \"This research delves into the intricate realm of CNTP Reference, focusing on the non-simply connected Calabi-Yau threefolds and their related mathematical categories. These threefolds emerge as quotients of Schoen's Calabi-Yau, featuring fiber products over them. The study also explores the formation of quotients by freely acting finite abelian groups, which preserve fibrations. Moreover, the work involves the classification of restricted finite automorphism groups on rational elliptic surfaces, emphasizing the interplay of diverse mathematical structures.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"We study the production of Mueller-Navelet jets at hadron colliders in the\\nBalitsky-Fadin-Kuraev-Lipatov (BFKL) framework. We show that a measurement of\\nthe relative azimuthal angle \\\\Delta\\\\Phi between the jets can provide a good\\ntesting ground for corrections due to next-leading logarithms (NLL). Besides\\nthe well-known azimuthal decorrelation with increasing rapidity interval\\n\\\\Delta\\\\eta between the jets, we propose to also measure this effect as a\\nfunction of R=k_2/k_1, the ratio between the jets transverse momenta.\", \"sampled\": \"In the study titled \\\"Quiet Sun Magnetic Fields: Space-borne Observations Simulating Spectropolarimeter Case,\\\" the researchers accepted for publication in Letters Reference explore radiative magneto-convection simulations using the Solar Optical Telescope. They synthesize four Stokes profiles of ion Fe, adding noise to degraded spatial resolution profiles. A comparison with real values shows correct visible lines yield, contrary to ground-based observations where weak fields are retrieved wherever observed at 1 arcsec.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recently, Bagno, Garber and Mansour studied a kind of excedance number on the\\ncomplex reflection groups and computed its multidistribution with the number of\\nfixed points on the set of involutions in these groups. In this note, we\\nconsider the similar problems in more general cases and make a correction of\\none result obtained by them.\", \"sampled\": \"A comparative study using pair-creation processes in a series of contact tasks reveals that supercritical expansions offer more accurate results than ordinary processes. By contrast, the use of supercritical expansions can circumvent issues introduced by subcritical series, providing a more reliable alternative method. This approach, which works even in challenging cases, yields estimates compatible with numerical simulations, showcasing its effectiveness in generating precise results.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The electromagnetic polarizabilities of the nucleon are shown to be essentially composed of the nonresonant $\\\\alpha_p(E_{0+})=+3.2$, $\\\\alpha_n(E_{0+})=+4.1$,the $t$-channel $\\\\alpha^t_{p,n}=-\\\\beta^t_{p,n}=+7.6$ and the resonant $\\\\beta_{p,n}(P_{33}(1232))=+8.3$ contributions (in units of $10^{-4}$fm$^3$.\", \"sampled\": \"The research delves into the Gibbs-Shannon entropy of a stationary nonequilibrium measure, which describes a one-dimensional lattice gas with symmetric exclusion dynamics in contact with particle reservoirs at different densities. This study further explores the hydrodynamic scaling limit, where it is found that the leading order behavior of this entropy follows that of a product measure, indicating strict local equilibrium.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Some arguments in favour of the existence of tachyons and extensions of the Lorentz Group are presented. On the former, it is observed that with a slight modification to standard electromagnetic theory a single superluminal charge will bind to itself in a self-sustaining circular orbit, suggestive of a (modified) electromagnetic interpretation of the strong force. Symmetries in that theory are used in the subsequent analysis as a starting point in the search for physically-motivated extensions of the Lorentz Group. There is some discussion of the validity of imaginary coordinates in superluminal transformations of frame. The article concludes with some speculation on the implications for faster-than light travel.\", \"sampled\": \"Recent findings in astrophysics have revealed discrepancies in HII regions between the abundances derived from Optical Recombination Lines (ORLs) and Collisionally Excited Lines (CELs). These inconsistencies may represent a key signature of a scenario involving the enrichment of the Interstellar Medium (ISM). This proposed scenario suggests that oxygen, released during massive supernova explosions, becomes confined within hot superbubbles. Following the final massive supernova explosion, the metal-rich gas begins to cool and descends onto the galaxy in the form of metal-rich droplets. These droplets then fully mix with the ISM during photoionization by successive generations of massive stars.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The evolution of Turkish Journal of Chemistry (Turk J. Chem) Hirsch index (h-index) over the period 1995-2005 is studied and determined in the case of the self and without self-citations. It is seen that the effect of Hirsch index of Turk J. Chem has a highly positive trend during the last five years. It proves that Turk J. Chem is improving itself both in quantity and quality since h-index reflects peer review, and peer review reflects research quality of a journal.\", \"sampled\": \"The research study employs the AdS/CFT correspondence to scrutinize pseudoscalar mesons inherent in QCD. This study's model integrates the implications of symmetry, generating a framework that contains minimal free parameters, due to the restrictions imposed by five-dimensional spacetime and the operator product expansion of QCD. The model utilizes the masses of pion, kaon, and rho, along with the value of fpi, as input parameters.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The space velocities from the catalog of Nordstrom et al. (2004) are used to trace variations of a number of kinematic parameters of single F and G dwarfs as a function of their age. The vertex deviation of disk stars increases from 7+- 1 to 15+-2 degrees as the mean age decreases from 4.3 to 1.5 Gyr. The two-dimensional velocity distributions in the UV, UW, and VW planes are analyzed. The evolution of the main peaks in the velocity distributions can be followed to an average age of approximately 9 Gyr. We find that: (1) in the distributions of the UV velocity components, stars of different types are concentrated toward several stable peaks (the Hyades, Pleiades, and Sirius Cluster), suggesting that the stars belonging to these formations did not form simultaneously; (2) the peak associated with the Hyades Cluster dominates in all age intervals; and (3) the Hyades peak is strongest for stars with an average age of 1.5 Gyr, suggesting that this peak contains a considerable fraction of stars from the Hyades cluster. The age dependences of the kinematic parameters exhibit a break near 4.5 Gyr, which can be explained as an effect of the different contributions of stars of the thin and thick disks. The Stromberg relation yields a solar LSR velocity of V_{\\\\odot LSR} = (8.7, 6.2, 7.2)+- (0.5, 2.2, 0.8) km/s.\", \"sampled\": \"An extensive sample of star clusters in the spiral galaxy M51 was observed using the HST/ACS Telescope, with a focus on the distribution of radius and its relationships with colour, arm/interarm region, galactocentric distance, mass, and age. In particular, the study analyzed the radii of 1284 clusters, chosen from larger sets selected in F435W, F555W, and F814W filters, amounting to 7698, 6846, and 5024 clusters respectively. The range of the observed cluster radii was between 0.5 and approximately 10 parsecs (pc), with an exceptionally large cluster candidate having a radius of 21.6 pc and a median radius of 2.1 pc across the sample. In the colorimetric analysis, 70 clusters were found to have colours consistent with being old globular cluster (GC) candidates. Additionally, 6 new 'faint fuzzy' clusters were discovered within, or projected onto, the disc of M51. Notably, the study observed an increase in radius with colour, and a higher fraction of redder clusters were identified in the interarm regions of the galaxy. The radius distribution did not conform to a power law, but a log-normal distribution provided a reasonable fit. Furthermore, the research found a correlation between radius and galactocentric distance, albeit weaker than the observed correlation for old Milky Way GCs. Weak relations were identified between cluster luminosity and radius, but surprisingly, no correlation between cluster mass and radius was observed.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We give a proof of a so-called \\\"local $Tb$\\\" Theorem for singular integrals whose kernels satisfy the standard Calder\\\\'on-Zygmund conditions. The present theorem, which extends an earlier result of M. Christ \\\\cite{Ch}, was proved in \\\\cite{AHMTT} for \\\"perfect dyadic\\\" Calder\\\\'on-Zygmund operators.\", \"sampled\": \"In recent years, the sector of heavy baryons has seen significant experimental progress. This research focuses on the strong decays of various wave forms, namely the S-wave, P-wave, D-wave, and the radially excited charmed baryons. The $^3P_0$ model, a theoretical physics tool, has been utilized to facilitate the study.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"By the application of $\\\\phi$-mapping topological theory, the properties of vortices in quantum R\\\\\\\"ontgen effect is thoroughly studied. The explicit expression of the vorticity is obtained, wherein which the $\\\\delta$ function indicates that the vortices can only stem from the zero points of $\\\\phi$ and the magnetic flux of the consequent monopoles is quantized in terms of the Hopf indices and Brouwer degrees. The evolution of vortex lines is discussed. The reduced dynamic equation and a conserved dynamic quantity on stable vortex lines are obtained.\", \"sampled\": \"The Astrodynamical Space Test of Relativity using Optical Devices (ASTROD) is a multi-purpose relativity mission concept. It has been designed with the aim of measuring relativistic and solar system parameters with an unprecedented degree of precision. To facilitate this, ASTROD plans to observe and detect low-frequency gravitational waves down to frequencies as low as 5x10^-6 Hz. The mission intends to utilize a constellation of drag-free satellites, targeting a residual acceleration noise of (0.3-1)x 10^-15 m s^-2 Hz^-1/2 at 0.1 mHz.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We predict the nature (attractive or repulsive) and range (exponentially screened or long-range power law) of the electrostatic interactions of oppositely charged and planar plates as a function of the salt concentration and surface charge densities (whose absolute magnitudes are not necessarily equal). An analytical expression for the crossover between attractive and repulsive pressure is obtained as a function of the salt concentration. This condition reduces to the high-salt limit of Parsegian and Gingell where the interaction is exponentially screened and to the zero salt limit of Lau and Pincus in which the important length scales are the inter-plate separation and the Gouy-Chapman length. In the regime of low salt and high surface charges we predict - for any ratio of the charges on the surfaces - that the attractive pressure is long-ranged as a function of the spacing. The attractive pressure is related to the decrease in counter-ion concentration as the inter-plate distance is decreased.\", \"sampled\": \"This research primarily revolves around the astrophysical study of sulphur, an alpha-element that does not form dust, and its abundance in various stars such as the Sun, Procyon, HD 33256, HD 25069, and epsilon Eri. A particular focus was on the sulphur to iron ratios, which have been observed to exhibit increased scatter at low metallicities. Interestingly, some stars maintain a constant S/Fe ratio regardless of metallicity, while others display an increasing S/Fe ratio as metallicity decreases. The study utilized the spectral lines of silicon, specifically SI Mult. 3 at 1045 nm, which are not affected by telluric lines, unlike the SI lines of Multiplet 1 at 920 nm. For the Sun and Procyon, the analysis was conducted using CO5BOLD 3D hydrodynamical model atmospheres, whereas the 1D model atmospheres were utilized for HD 33256, HD 25069, and epsilon Eri. Notably, a global agreement of A(S) from lines of different multiplets was discovered, implying that the infrared lines of Mult. 3 are a reliable indicator of the sulphur abundance.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Because of the limit in the number of gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) with available redshifts and spectra, all current investigations on the correlation among GRB variables use burst samples with redshifts that span a very large range. The evolution and selection effects have thus been ignored, which might have important influence on the results. In this Letter, we divide the 48 long-duration GRBs in Amati (2006, 2007) into four groups with redshift from low to high, each group contains 12 GRBs. Then we fit each group with the Amati relation $\\\\log E_\\\\iso = a + b \\\\log E_\\\\p$, and check if the parameters $a$ and $b$ evolve with the GRB redshift. We find that $a$ and $b$ vary with the mean redshift of the GRBs in each group systematically and significantly.\", \"sampled\": \"The research at hand presents constraints on the amplitude and shape of the matter power spectrum and the density of dark matter. This was achieved by employing a Markov Chain Monte Carlo approach to amalgamate independent measurements. The measurements were derived from the three-dimensional weak gravitational lensing shear field by the COSMOS survey, the low and high resolution Lyman-alpha forest flux power spectrum by SDSS and LUQAS, and the Cosmic Microwave Background temperature and polarization anisotropies by WMAP. Through this approach, there was a notable agreement between the amplitude of the matter power spectrum on intermediate and small scales as deduced from the Lyman-alpha forest and lensing data. In particular, the Lyman-alpha forest data played a crucial role in breaking the sigma_8-Omega_m degeneracy that is characteristic of weak lensing results.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Although power laws of the Zipf type have been used by many workers to fit rank distributions in different fields like in economy, geophysics, genetics, soft-matter, networks etc., these fits usually fail at the tails. Some distributions have been proposed to solve the problem, but unfortunately they do not fit at the same time both ending tails. We show that many different data in rank laws, like in granular materials, codons, author impact in scientific journal, etc. are very well fitted by a beta-like function.\", \"sampled\": \"The research refines the effective recombination coefficient, alpha_eff, specifically for optically thin cases, marking a significant advancement in our understanding of Radiation Pressure. A calculation of radiation pressure in Lyman-alpha and HeII Lyman-alpha in terms of number density and mean free path is presented, showing the importance of these factors in the study of Lyman-alpha Forest Clouds. The study posits a pressure equilibrium between Lyman-alpha clouds and an adiabatically expanding inter-galactic medium, a crucial assumption in the context of the Friedmann-Robertson-Walker Universe.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Angular momentum changing collisions can be suppressed in atoms whose valence electrons are submerged beneath filled shells of higher principle quantum number. To determine whether spin-exchange collisions are suppressed in these \\\"submerged shell\\\" atoms, we measured spin-exchange collisions of six hyperfine states of Mn at temperatures below 1 K. Although the 3d valence electrons in Mn are submerged beneath a filled 4s orbital, we find that the spin exchange rate coefficients are similar to those of Na and H (which are non-submerged shell atoms).\", \"sampled\": \"The research paper \\\"An Integrated Human-Computer System for Controlling Interstate Disputes\\\" presents a scientific approach to manage inter-country conflict. The system developed employs a neural network and a feedback control approach, creating a new way to mediate interstate disputes. Central to this method are four controllable inputs: Democracy, Dependency, Allies, and Capability. By controlling these four inputs simultaneously, the system can successfully avoid all predicted dispute outcomes. Interestingly, controlling a single input, either Dependency or Capability, can also avoid all predicted conflicts.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We introduce the Callan-Symanzik method in the description of anisotropic as well as isotropic Lifshitz critical behaviors. Renormalized perturbation theories are defined by normalization conditions with nonvanishing masses and at zero external momenta. The orthogonal approximation is employed to obtain the critical indices $\\\\eta_{L2}$, $\\\\nu_{L2}$, $\\\\eta_{L4}$ and $\\\\nu_{L4}$ diagramatically at least up to two-loop order in the anisotropic criticalities. This approximation is also utilized to compute the exponents $\\\\eta_{L4}$ and $\\\\nu_{L4}$ in the isotropic case. Furthermore, we compute those exponents exactly for the isotropic behaviors at the same loop order. The results obtained for all exponents are in perfect agreement with those previously derived in the massless theories renormalized at nonzero external momenta.\", \"sampled\": \"Exact distribution of the sample variance from a gamma parent distribution studies exact distributions related to samples from a gamma parent distribution, focusing on the sample variance and on squared-sum statistics for independent gamma-distributed variables. The paper presents multiple forms for the exact cumulative distribution function of the squared-sum statistic for Xi, including a representation as a series built from gamma distribution functions, where n denotes the number of independent variables. A Laha characterization of the gamma distribution is used to derive an expansion for the sample variance distribution, obtained through a Taylor series method, with the squared-sum distribution serving as the leading term.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"On the basis of the Fermi liquid theory, the Kadowaki-Woods ratio $A/\\\\gamma^2$ is evaluated by using a first principle band calculation for typical itinerant $d$ and $f$ electron systems. It is found as observed that the ratio for the $d$ electron systems is significantly smaller than the normal $f$ systems, even without considering their relatively weak correlation. The difference in the ratio value comes from different characters of the Fermi surfaces.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper studies whether scalar-field configurations can be localized to represent black hole accretion, addressing problems that arise when attempting scalar-field localization in General Relativity. A simple model with spherical symmetry is used to demonstrate the localization method and to examine properties of accreting shells near a black hole. Localization is achieved by specifying a scalar field whose potential depends on coordinates, and numerical solutions of the Einstein-Klein-Gordon equations are presented.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We performed the first scanning tunneling spectroscopy measurements on the pyrochlore superconductor KOs2O6 (Tc = 9.6 K) in both zero magnetic field and the vortex state at several temperatures above 1.95 K. This material presents atomically flat surfaces, yielding spatially homogeneous spectra which reveal fully-gapped superconductivity with a gap anisotropy of 30%. Measurements performed at fields of 2 and 6 T display a hexagonal Abrikosov flux line lattice. From the shape of the vortex cores, we extract a coherence length of 31-40 {\\\\AA}, in agreement with the value derived from the upper critical field Hc2. We observe a reduction in size of the vortex cores (and hence the coherence length) with increasing field which is consistent with the unexpectedly high and unsaturated upper critical field reported.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper reports a CF3I-loaded Superheated Droplet Detector prototype for dark matter detection, positioning SDDs as devices with intrinsic resistance to backgrounds and extending earlier SDD work based on light nuclei refrigerants. Prior Superheated Droplet Detectors used C2ClF5 and C4F10, linked respectively to SIMPLE and PICASSO, and these refrigerants mainly targeted spin-dependent interactions in dark matter searches. By loading trifluoroiodomethane, CF3I, into the prototype, the study was intended to improve sensitivity to spin-independent interactions while retaining relevance to spin-dependent searches. The test run used two high-concentration devices of 1 liter each, producing an exposure of 0.102 kgd, and the reported results were compared with leading experiments in both spin-dependent and spin-independent sectors.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The science of complex networks is a new interdisciplinary branch of science which has arisen recently on the interface of physics, biology, social and computer sciences, and others. Its main goal is to discover general laws governing the creation and growth as well as processes taking place on networks, like e.g. the Internet, transportation or neural networks. It turned out that most real-world networks cannot be simply reduced to a compound of some individual components. Fortunately, the statistical mechanics, being one of pillars of modern physics, provides us with a very powerful set of tools and methods for describing and understanding these systems. In this thesis, we would like to present a consistent approach to complex networks based on statistical mechanics, with the central role played by the concept of statistical ensemble of networks. We show how to construct such a theory and present some practical problems where it can be applied. Among them, we pay attention to the problem of finite-size corrections and the dynamics of a simple model of mass transport on networks.\", \"sampled\": \"This abstract presents an application of ab initio wavefunction-based techniques to electron-correlation effects in oxide band structures, using MgO as a representative closed-shell ionic oxide and treating its electronic structure through a framework designed to connect finite local calculations with the extended crystal. The analysis is formulated in terms of a local Hamiltonian on finite pieces taken from the extended crystal, while localized Wannier functions and embedding potentials are supplied by earlier periodic Hartree-Fock calculations, providing a reference for assessing correlation-induced changes. The work examines how different correlation contributions shrink the Hartree-Fock band gap and alter band widths, including on-site charge relaxation, nearest-neighbor charge relaxation, and long-range polarization as distinct microscopic components of the correlated description. The results show that correlation effects are necessary for reliable band-gap values, while in MgO the Hartree-Fock band widths are changed less strongly than the gap, with the O 2p valence bands exhibiting an unexpected increase in width.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We generalize the relativistic flux tube model for arbitrary two- or three-body systems. The spin-independent and spin-dependent contributions of the flux tube to the total Hamiltonian are computed in perturbation. In particular, we show that the spin-dependent part exhibits a universal spin-orbit form: It does not depend on the nature of the confined particles.\", \"sampled\": \"This 12-page hep-th paper, Fuzzy Torus via q-Parafermion, treats the fuzzy torus from a q-deformed parafermion viewpoint. Its central contribution is a classification of Hermitian representations of the fuzzy torus within this theoretical framework. The classification includes Fock-type representations, situating them among the Hermitian representations considered in the work.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We have recorded fluorescence spectra of the atomic rubidium D-lines in the presence of several hundreds of bars buffer gas pressure. With additional saturation broadening a spectral linewidth comparable to the thermal energy of the atoms in the heated gas cell is achieved. An intensity-dependent blue asymmetry of the spectra is observed, which becomes increasingly pronounced when extrapolating to infinitely high light intensity. We interpret our results as evidence for the dressed (coupled atom-light) states to approach thermal equilibrium.\", \"sampled\": \"The Fractal Dimension of the Spectrum of the Fibonacci Hamiltonian examines the spectrum associated with the Fibonacci Hamiltonian, with attention to spectral theory in mathematical physics. It establishes both upper and lower estimates for the spectrum's fractal dimension in the large coupling setting, and shows that when lambda grows without bound, the product of the spectral fractal dimension and log lambda has a limit. The limiting value is an explicit constant approximately equal to 0.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Let $H$ be a connected reductive subgroup of a complex connected reductive group $G$. Fix maximal tori and Borel subgroups of $H$ and $G$. Consider the pairs $(V,V')$ of irreducible representations of $H$ and $G$ such that $V$ is a submodule of $V'$. We are interested in the cone $LR(G,H)$ generated by the pairs of dominant weights of such a pair of representations. Our main result gives a minimal set of inequalities describing $LR(G,H)$ as a part of the dominant chamber. In way, we obtain results about the faces of the Dolgachev-Hu's $G$-ample cone and variations of this cone.\", \"sampled\": \"This abstract analyzes critical phenomena in inhomogeneous magnetic systems through a phi^4-theory whose distance from criticality depends on position, with a classical ferromagnet exposed to spatially nonuniform mechanical stress serving as a realization of the model. The study finds a sharp phase transition in which the local magnetization envelope goes to zero uniformly, and extends the analysis to a quantum ferromagnet where the first-order transition is found to stay sharp. For an itinerant quantum ferromagnet, the mechanism that usually produces a tricritical point is suppressed, implying that a quantum critical point with mean-field exponents can in principle be restored.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We give two classes of spherically symmetric exact solutions of the couple gravitational and electromagnetic fields with charged source in the tetrad theory of gravitation. The first solution depends on an arbitrary function $H({R},t)$. The second solution depends on a constant parameter $\\\\eta$. These solutions reproduce the same metric, i.e., the Reissner--Nordstr$\\\\ddot{o}$m metric. If the arbitrary function which characterizes the first solution and the arbitrary constant of the second solution are set to be zero, then the two exact solutions will coincide with each other. We then calculate the energy content associated with these analytic solutions using the superpotential method. In particular, we examine whether these solutions meet the condition which M{\\\\o}ller required for a consistent energy-momentum complex: Namely, we check whether the total four-momentum of an isolated system behaves as a four-vector under Lorentz transformations.\", \"sampled\": \"This study reports spectropolarimetric observations of the Ca II infrared triplet as a magnetically sensitive chromospheric diagnostic usable from the ground, focusing on the Ca II 8498 A and Ca II 8542 A lines in a quiet Sun area located near a decaying active region. The observed results are compared with a line simulation performed for a high plasma-beta regime, providing a reference for assessing how paired Stokes V profiles, intensity behavior, and oscillatory signatures in the two Ca II lines relate to magnetic and dynamical conditions. Cluster analysis of paired Stokes V profiles finds that the Ca II 8498 A and Ca II 8542 A lines often have different shapes, although the abstract states that the two lines may form at relatively nearby atmospheric heights.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The inclusive energy distributions of the complex fragments (3 $\\\\leq$ Z $\\\\leq$ 7) emitted from the bombardment of ^{12}C by ^{20}Ne beams with incident energies between 145 and 200 MeV have been measured in the angular range 10$^{o} \\\\leq \\\\theta_{lab} \\\\leq$ 50^{o}. Damped fragment yields in all the cases have been found to be the characteristic of emission from fully energy equilibrated composites. The binary fragment yields are compared with the standard statistical model predictions. Enhanced yields of entrance channel fragments (5 $\\\\leq$ Z $\\\\leq$ 7) indicate the survival of orbiting-like process in ^{20}Ne + ^{12}C system at these energies.\", \"sampled\": \"Temperature-dependent impedance spectroscopy was applied to epitaxial multiferroic thin films of BiFeO3, abbreviated BFO, and BiMnO3, abbreviated BMO, materials presented as important systems with strong magneto-electric coupling. The study separated dielectric and resistive contributions in the films, finding that epitaxial layer capacitance was comparable to electrode interface capacitance and that capacitance analysis across film thickness was required to distinguish film and interface effects. Non-Debye impedance behavior was represented with Gaussian distributions of relaxation times, while the analysis indicated that standard resistivity measurements on multiferroic layers may be strongly affected by interfaces.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Cooperative transmission is an emerging communication technique that takes advantages of the broadcast nature of wireless channels. However, due to low spectral efficiency and the requirement of orthogonal channels, its potential for use in future wireless networks is limited. In this paper, by making use of multiuser detection (MUD) and network coding, cooperative transmission protocols with high spectral efficiency, diversity order, and coding gain are developed. Compared with the traditional cooperative transmission protocols with single-user detection, in which the diversity gain is only for one source user, the proposed MUD cooperative transmission protocols have the merits that the improvement of one user's link can also benefit the other users. In addition, using MUD at the relay provides an environment in which network coding can be employed. The coding gain and high diversity order can be obtained by fully utilizing the link between the relay and the destination. From the analysis and simulation results, it is seen that the proposed protocols achieve higher diversity gain, better asymptotic efficiency, and lower bit error rate, compared to traditional MUD and to existing cooperative transmission protocols.\", \"sampled\": \"This abstract summarizes the research result in Maximum solutions of normalized Ricci flows on 4-manifolds, which studies maximum solutions g(t) of the normalized Ricci flow on the time interval [0, +∞) and formulates a main theorem for a smooth compact symplectic 4-manifold (M, ω). The theorem assumes b_2^+(M)>1, imposes the uniform Ricci curvature bound |Ric(g(t))|≤3 along the solution, and requires the topological condition χ(M)=3τ(M)>0, linking the Euler characteristic χ(M) and signature τ(M) of M. Under these hypotheses, the result asserts the existence of some m in N and point sequences x_{j,k} in M, and for any sequence t_k tending to infinity, a subsequence yields m-pointed Gromov-Hausdorff convergence. The limiting space is described as a disjoint union of components N_j equipped with a metric g_∞, where each (N_j, g_∞) is a complete complex hyperbolic orbifold of complex dimension 2 and has at most finitely many isolated orbifold points.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We extend the SU(4) model [1-5] for high-Tc superconductivity to an SU(4)k model that permits explicit momentum (k) dependence in predicted observables. We derive and solve gap equations that depend on k, temperature, and doping from the SU(4)k coherent states, and show that the new SU(4)k model reduces to the original SU(4) model for observables that do not depend explicitly on momentum. The results of the SU(4)k model are relevant for experiments such as ARPES that detect explicitly k-dependent properties. The present SU(4)k model describes quantitatively the pseudogap temperature scale and may explain why the ARPES-measured T* along the anti-nodal direction is larger than other measurements that do not resolve momentum. It also provides an immediate microscopic explanation for Fermi arcs observed in the pseudogap region. In addition, the model leads to a prediction that even in the underdoped regime, there exist doping-dependent windows around nodal points in the k-space, where antiferromagnetism may be completely suppressed for all doping fractions, permitting pure superconducting states to exist.\", \"sampled\": \"Charged massive particle big bang nucleosynthesis (CBBN) can probe the nature of reheating if a long-lived CHAMP exists. Importantly, any indication of nonstandard reheating obtained through CBBN does not rely on the decay properties of the CHAMP. If the CHAMP mass falls within collider reach, a comparison between CBBN predictions and observed lithium-6 abundances may signal nonstandard reheating, provided the CHAMP lifetime exceeds 10^4 seconds—the threshold above which CBBN predictions become sensitive to the thermal history of the early Universe. A low reheating temperature model is examined as a representative nonstandard reheating scenario. When the CHAMP mass lies beyond collider reach, cosmological considerations may hint at the existence of very heavy long-lived CHAMPs, but only under the condition that the daughter particles of CHAMP decay constitute the dominant component of present dark matter. CBBN thus connects early-universe thermodynamics to observable nucleosynthetic yields across two distinct mass regimes.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"There is a well-known correspondence between infinite trees and ultrametric spaces which can be interpreted as an equivalence of categories and comes from considering the end space of the tree.\", \"sampled\": \"We characterize the capacity of a class of modulo additive noise relay channels in which the relay observes a corrupted version of the noise and communicates to the destination over a separate channel.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We study electric potential of a charge placed in a strong magnetic field B>>4.4x10^{13}G, as modified by the vacuum polarization. In such field the electron Larmour radius is much less than its Compton length. At the Larmour distances a scaling law occurs, with the potential determined by a magnetic-field-independent function. The scaling regime implies short-range interaction, expressed by Yukawa law. The electromagnetic interaction regains its long-range character at distances larger than the Compton length, the potential decreasing across the magnetic field faster than along. Correction to the nonrelativistic ground-state energy of a hydrogenlike atom is found. In the infinite-magnetic-field limit the modified potential becomes the Dirac delta-function plus a regular background. With this potential the ground-state energy is finite - the best pronounced effect of the vacuum polarization.\", \"sampled\": \"Euler's elastic problem concerns stationary configurations of an elastic rod with fixed endpoints and fixed tangents, formulated as a left-invariant optimal control problem on E(2), the group of motions of the two-dimensional plane. The paper spans 101 pages and contains 54 figures. The attainable set is described in detail, and existence and boundedness of optimal controls are proved. This formulation connects classical variational analysis to the geometric structure of E(2). Extremals are parametrized using Jacobi's elliptic functions, with natural coordinates induced by the mathematical pendulum flow on fibers of the cotangent bundle. The parametrization follows directly from this structure, yielding a complete expression of extremals on fibers of the cotangent bundle of E(2) in terms of Jacobi's elliptic functions.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Differential cross sections for the reaction $\\\\gamma p \\\\to p \\\\pi^0$ have been measured with the CEBAF Large Acceptance Spectrometer (CLAS) and a tagged photon beam with energies from 0.675 to 2.875 GeV. The results reported here possess greater accuracy in the absolute normalization than previous measurements. They disagree with recent CB-ELSA measurements for the process at forward scattering angles. Agreement with the SAID and MAID fits is found below 1 GeV. The present set of cross sections has been incorporated into the SAID database, and exploratory fits have been extended to 3 GeV. Resonance couplings have been extracted and compared to previous determinations.\", \"sampled\": \"Special solutions of string theory in supercritical dimensions can interpolate in time between theories with different numbers of spacetime dimensions via dimension quenching, and between theories with different amounts of worldsheet supersymmetry via c-duality. Both mechanisms arise naturally in supercritical settings and are described as intrinsic features of string theory. Together, these solutions connect supercritical string theories to the ten-dimensional string duality web and provide a precise link between supersymmetric and purely bosonic string theories. Dimension quenching and c-duality thereby give rise to large networks of interconnected theories.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The main result of this article is the fact that the currents defined by Levin give a description of the polylogarithm of an abelian scheme at the topological level. This result was a conjecture of Levin. This provides a method to explicit the Eisenstein classes of an abelian scheme at the topological level. These classes are of special interest since they have a motivic origin by a theorem of Kings. In a forthcoming article, we use the main result of this paper to prove that the Eisenstein classes of the universal abelian scheme over an Hilbert-Blumenthal variety degenerate at the boundary of the Baily-Borel compactification of the base in a special value of an $L$-function associated to the underlying totally real number field. As a corollary, we get a non vanishing result for some of these Eisenstein classes in this geometric situation.\", \"sampled\": \"We establish a lower bound on the iterated image size of a point-symmetric reflexive relation with finite image size. Let Gamma be a point-symmetric reflexive relation on a vertex set V with finite image size at some vertex v. Since the automorphism group acts transitively, finiteness of |Gamma(v)| implies finiteness of |Gamma(x)| for all x in V. The main result states that if j is a positive integer such that the j-th iterated image of v intersects the in-neighborhood only at v itself, then |Gamma^j(v)| >= |Gamma^(j-1)(v)| + |Gamma(v)| - 1. This inequality controls growth across successive iterations, with the edge set E encoding the neighborhood structure central to the argument.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The concept of continuous topological evolution, based upon Cartan's methods of exterior differential systems, is used to develop a topological theory of non-equilibrium thermodynamics, within which there exist processes that exhibit continuous topological change and thermodynamic irreversibility. The technique furnishes a universal, topological foundation for the partial differential equations of hydrodynamics and electrodynamics; the technique does not depend upon a metric, connection or a variational principle.\", \"sampled\": \"Finding the ground state energy of lattice Hamiltonians is computationally intractable even for a quantum computer, and this hardness extends to translationally invariant systems. Such systems were previously considered potentially more tractable due to their structural regularity, but this assumption does not hold. We show that a quantum computer can be encoded within a one-dimensional chain governed by a fixed, translationally invariant Hamiltonian.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We describe and analyze the joint source/channel coding properties of a class of sparse graphical codes based on compounding a low-density generator matrix (LDGM) code with a low-density parity check (LDPC) code. Our first pair of theorems establish that there exist codes from this ensemble, with all degrees remaining bounded independently of block length, that are simultaneously optimal as both source and channel codes when encoding and decoding are performed optimally. More precisely, in the context of lossy compression, we prove that finite degree constructions can achieve any pair $(R, D)$ on the rate-distortion curve of the binary symmetric source. In the context of channel coding, we prove that finite degree codes can achieve any pair $(C, p)$ on the capacity-noise curve of the binary symmetric channel. Next, we show that our compound construction has a nested structure that can be exploited to achieve the Wyner-Ziv bound for source coding with side information (SCSI), as well as the Gelfand-Pinsker bound for channel coding with side information (CCSI). Although the current results are based on optimal encoding and decoding, the proposed graphical codes have sparse structure and high girth that renders them well-suited to message-passing and other efficient decoding procedures.\", \"sampled\": \"CRVAD-2 is the second version of the Catalogue of Radial Velocities with Astrometric Data for stars in ASCC-2.5, compiled by cross-identifying ASCC-2.5 stars with multiple radial velocity catalogues, including the General Catalogue of Radial Velocities. The resulting catalogue contains data for 54,907 stars drawn from the ASCC-2.5 all-sky compilation of 2.5 million stars. Each entry provides coordinates, proper motions, parallaxes, photometry, spectral types, and radial velocities. Proper motions and parallaxes are referenced to the Hipparcos system, photometry follows the Johnson B and V bands, and equatorial coordinates are given as J2000 positions within the Hipparcos reference frame. Multiplicity and variability flags are included for each entry. Using CRVAD-2, mean radial velocities were determined for 363 open clusters and stellar associations via members selected on the basis of proper motions and photometry. For an additional 330 clusters and associations, previously published mean radial velocities were compiled from the literature, critically reviewed, and partly revised. This work produced CRVOCA, the Catalogue of Radial Velocities of Open Clusters and Associations, which contains approximately 460 open clusters and 60 stellar associations in the Solar neighbourhood.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This paper examines the effect of temperature on the structural stability and mechanical properties of 20 layered (10,10) single walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNTs) under tensile loading using an O(N) tight binding molecular dynamics (TBMD) simulation method. We observed that (10,10) tube can sustain its structural stability for the strain values of 0.23 in elongation and 0.06 in compression at 300K. Bond breaking strain value decreases with increasing temperature under streching but not under compression. The elastic limit, Young's modulus, tensile strength and Poisson ratio are calculated as 0.10, 0.395 TPa, 83.23 GPa, 0.285, respectively, at 300K. In the temperature range from 300K to 900K; Young's modulus and the tensile strengths are decreasing with increasing temperature while the Poisson ratio is increasing. At higher temperatures, Young's modulus starts to increase while the Poisson ratio and tensile strength decrease. In the temperature range from 1200K to 1800K, the SWCNT is already deformed and softened. Applying strain on these deformed and softened SWCNTs do not follow the same pattern as in the temperature range of 300K to 900K.\", \"sampled\": \"Future galaxy imaging surveys offer a promising avenue for distinguishing dark energy from modified gravity as drivers of cosmic acceleration. We present forecasts for the Hyper Suprime-Cam weak-lensing survey using Fisher matrix analysis to assess the capacity of HSC observations to constrain the growth rate parameter. Two model classes are compared: dark energy models generically predict a growth rate parameter γ ≈ 0.55, while the DGP modified gravity model predicts a notably higher value of γ ≈ 0.68, providing a potential observational target for discrimination. The central question is whether future surveys can constrain γ to within 20 percent, corresponding to δγ < 0.1. Here γ is Linder's parameterization of the linear growth rate of density fluctuations, which serves as the primary diagnostic quantity. We compute the Figure of Merit as a function of exposure time under a fixed total observing budget and find that the FoM exhibits a broad peak at exposure times of several to roughly 10 minutes, suggesting that a shallow, wide survey strategy is preferred.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We present results illustrating the effects of using explicit summation terms for the $r^{-6}$ dispersion term on the interfacial properties of a Lennard-Jones fluid and SPC/E water. For the Lennard-Jones fluid, we find that the use of long-range summations, even with a short ``crossover radius,'' yields results that are consistent with simulations using large cutoff radii. Simulations of SPC/E water demonstrate that the long-range dispersion forces are of secondary importance to the Coulombic forces.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper analyzes high peak-to-average power ratio signaling in fading channels, presented at the 40th Annual Asilomar Conference on Signals, Systems, and Computers in 2006. Two modulation schemes are examined: on-off binary phase-shift keying and on-off quaternary phase-shift keying, each analyzed under both coherent and noncoherent fading channel conditions. Optimal decision rules are derived for each modulation format, along with analytical expressions for error probability that are evaluated numerically. The analysis addresses the distinct detection challenges posed by coherent and noncoherent fading channels for both schemes.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This work describes a new microfluidic device developed for rapid screening of solubility diagrams. In several parallel channels, hundreds of nanoliter-volume droplets of a given solution are first stored with a gradual variation in the solute concentration. Then, the application of a temperature gradient along these channels enables us to read directly and quantitatively phase diagrams, concentration vs. temperature.\", \"sampled\": \"The Dirac theory in Euclidean Taub-NUT space admits a large collection of conserved operators reflecting genuine and hidden symmetries, which participate in dynamical algebras and infinite-dimensional algebraic structures. The manifolds under study carry the Gross-Perry-Sorkin monopole, whose structure significantly shapes the resulting symmetry algebra. An infinite-dimensional superalgebra specific to the Dirac theory on these monopole-carrying manifolds is presented and shown to admit interpretation as a twisted loop superalgebra.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"One of the goals of the landscape program in string theory is to extract information about the space of string vacua in the form of statistical correlations between phenomenological features that are otherwise uncorrelated in field theory. Such correlations would thus represent predictions of string theory that hold independently of a vacuum-selection principle. In this paper, we study statistical correlations between two features which are likely to be central to any potential description of nature at high energy scales: gauge symmetries and spacetime supersymmetry. We analyze correlations between these two kinds of symmetry within the context of perturbative heterotic string vacua, and find a number of striking features. We find, for example, that the degree of spacetime supersymmetry is strongly correlated with the probabilities of realizing certain gauge groups, with unbroken supersymmetry at the string scale tending to favor gauge-group factors with larger rank.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper examines a three-degree-of-freedom hybrid manipulator that combines a five-bar planar mechanism with a third revolute joint added in series, passing through the line of centers of the two actuated revolute joints. The resulting architecture mixes serial and parallel links. Fully parallel manipulators risk parallel singularities where control is lost, while fully serial designs lack stiffness. The proposed hybrid configuration is intrinsically stiffer and more accurate than serial counterparts, and placing all actuators on the first axis reduces inertial effects. By selecting an appropriate working mode, the kinematic structure can avoid parallel singularities, which in turn enables larger workspaces. We analyze how structural dimensions affect kinematic and mechanical properties to inform optimal design of such hybrid manipulators, building on prior work by Bajpai and Roth on the five-bar planar mechanism.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Let S be the variety of irreducible sextics with six cusps as singularities. Let W be one of irreducible components of W. Denoting by M_4 the space of moduli of smooth curves of genus 4, the moduli map of W is the rational map from W to M_4 sending the general point of W, corresponding to a plane curve D, to the point of M_4 parametrizing the normalization curve of D. The number of moduli of W is, by definition the dimension of the image of W with respect to the moduli map. We know that this number is at most equal to seven.\", \"sampled\": \"Observations of galaxies and clusters deviate from the predictions of General Relativity. The Scalar Potential Model (SPM) was developed to address these discrepancies. SPM aligns with the cellular structure of clusters, intergalactic medium flows from spiral to elliptical galaxies, and intergalactic redshift without requiring an expanding universe. It also matches discrete redshift observations, rotation curve data without invoking dark matter, asymmetric rotation curves, central mass measurements, and central velocity dispersions in galaxies. The model is consistent with the Pioneer Anomaly. SPM points to a universe that has undergone past expansion, past contraction, and current expansion, while reducing to General Relativity at solar system scales.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We propose two Type-II seesaw scenarios for the neutrino mass matrix in the left-right symmetric model, in which the Higgs triplet Yukawa coupling matrix takes the appealing Friedberg-Lee texture. We show that the nearly tri-bimaximal neutrino mixing pattern, which is especially favored by current neutrino oscillation data, can be obtained from both scenarios.\", \"sampled\": \"Dipole-dipole interactions in the active medium strongly shape superradiation, primarily by driving the nuclear subsystem toward chaotic behavior. As atom density rises, the interaction strength grows, suppressing distant collective correlations and, in turn, superradiation itself. Instead, near correlations among atoms emerge, producing a confinement effect.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The fractional Aharonov-Bohm oscillation (FABO) of narrow quantum rings with two electrons has been studied and has been explained in an analytical way, the evolution of the period and amplitudes against the magnetic field can be exactly described. Furthermore, the dipole transition of the ground state was found to have essentially two frequencies, their difference appears as an oscillation matching the oscillation of the persistent current exactly. A number of equalities relating the observables and dynamical parameters have been found.\", \"sampled\": \"This work extends the theory of separately holomorphic mappings between complex analytic spaces. The approach builds on Poletsky theory of discs and Rosay’s Theorem on holomorphic discs, together with joint work with Pflug on one-dimensional cross theorems. A new technique using conformal mappings is introduced, along with a generalization of Siciak’s relative extremal function. The unified framework shows how local information can lead to global extensions.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We have studied the statistics of giant pulses from the Crab pulsar for the first time with particular reference to their widths. We have analyzed data collected during 3.5 hours of observations conducted with the Westerbork Synthesis Radio Telescope operated in a tied-array mode at a frequency of 1200 MHz. The PuMa pulsar backend provided voltage recording of X and Y linear polarization states in two conjugate 10 MHz bands. We restricted the time resolution to 4 microseconds to match the scattering on the interstellar inhomogeneities. In total about 18000 giant pulses (GP) were detected in full intensity with a threshold level of 6 sigma. Cumulative probability distributions (CPD) of giant pulse energies were analyzed for groups of GPs with different effective widths in the range 4 to 65 microseconds. The CPDs were found to manifest notable differences for the different GP width groups. The slope of a power-law fit to the high-energy portion of the CPDs evolves from -1.7 to -3.2 when going from the shortest to the longest GPs. There are breaks in the CPD power-law fits indicating flattening at low energies with indices varying from -1.0 to -1.9 for the short and long GPs respectively. The GPs with a stronger peak flux density were found to be of shorter duration. We compare our results with previously published data and discuss the importance of these peculiarities in the statistical properties of GPs for the heoretical understanding of the emission mechanism responsible for GP generation.\", \"sampled\": \"IGR J16194-2810 is identified as a new Symbiotic X-ray Binary (SyXB), a rare class of Low Mass X-ray Binary consisting of an M-type giant and a compact object. The optical counterpart is an M2 III star. The 0.5–200 keV spectrum is well described by an absorbed Comptonization model. No long-term periodicities are detected in the IBIS data, and the XRT observations show shot-noise variability typical of accreting Galactic X-ray sources. The source distance is inferred to be about 3.7 kpc, and the X-ray luminosity measured in the 0.5–200 keV band is roughly 1.4e35 erg/s. The compact object is likely a neutron star accreting from the wind of its M-type giant companion. IGR J16194-2810 resembles the SyXBs 4U 1700+24 and 4U 1954+31, placing it among a rare population of Galactic binaries in which an M-type giant donor transfers mass to a compact accretor. The M2 III classification confirms the evolved nature of the donor, while the absorbed Comptonization model accounts for the broadband emission. IBIS data reveal no long-term periodicities in the hard X-ray regime, and the shot-noise variability seen with XRT is common in accreting Galactic sources. The inferred distance of about 3.7 kpc constrains the source location, with the X-ray luminosity reaching approximately 1.4e35 erg/s in the 0.5–200 keV band.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The complex Monoceros Loop SNR/Rosette Nebula region contains several potential sources of very-high-energy (VHE) gamma-ray emission and two as yet unidentified high-energy EGRET sources. Sensitive VHE observations are required to probe acceleration processes in this region. The H.E.S.S. telescope array has been used to search for very high-energy gamma-ray sources in this region. CO data from the NANTEN telescope were used to map the molecular clouds in the region, which could act as target material for gamma-ray production via hadronic interactions. We announce the discovery of a new gamma-ray source, HESS J0632+058, located close to the rim of the Monoceros SNR. This source is unresolved by H.E.S.S. and has no clear counterpart at other wavelengths but is possibly associated with the weak X-ray source 1RXS J063258.3+054857, the Be-star MWC 148 and/or the lower energy gamma-ray source 3EG J0634+0521. No evidence for an associated molecular cloud was found in the CO data.\", \"sampled\": \"We establish a necessary and sufficient condition for distinguishing a finite set of orthogonal multipartite quantum states using separable operations. An analytic form of the condition is derived for D−1 pure states, where D is the total dimension of the state space. We identify a large class of 2⊗2 separable operations that cannot be implemented via local operations and classical communication; previously, only a 3⊗3 class of such nonlocal separable operations was known. This considerably broadens the known landscape of nonlocal separable operations. We further show that every basis of the orthogonal complement of a multipartite pure state is indistinguishable by separable operations if and only if the state has orthogonal Schmidt number at least 3, generalizing Watrous's earlier result on indistinguishable bipartite subspaces. An explicit construction gives indistinguishable subspaces of dimension 7 for two qutrits, and dimension 6 for three qubits.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The closest string problem is an NP-hard problem, whose task is to find a string that minimizes maximum Hamming distance to a given set of strings. This can be reduced to an integer program (IP). However, to date, there exists no known polynomial-time algorithm for IP. In 2004, Meneses et al. introduced a branch-and-bound (B & B) method for solving the IP problem. Their algorithm is not always efficient and has the exponential time complexity. In the paper, we attempt to solve efficiently the IP problem by a greedy iterative rounding technique. The proposed algorithm is polynomial time and much faster than the existing B & B IP for the CSP.\", \"sampled\": \"We study a dilute gas of neutral, unpolarized fermionic atoms at zero temperature with a tunable short-range attractive interaction. At unitarity, the second-order correlation energy shares the same density dependence as the first-order exchange energy across the relevant density range. When the system is tuned to a Feshbach resonance, the correlation and exchange contributions nearly cancel, regardless of the detailed shape of the two-body potential. This cancellation requires that the product of the inverse potential range μ and the density parameter r_s be much larger than one, where μ⁻¹ sets the range of the two-body potential and r_s is defined by n = 3/(4π r_s³).\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We present a study of interlayer coupling and proximity effects in a La$_{0.66}$Ca$_{0.33}$MnO$_3$(10 nm)/YBa$_2$Cu$_3$O$_7$(10 nm) superlattice. Using element-sensitive x-ray probes, the magnetic state of Mn can be probed without seeing the strong diamagnetism of the superconductor, which makes this approach ideal to study changes in the magnetic properties across the superconducting transition.\", \"sampled\": \"Conditional freeness becomes fully meaningful only in the presence of positive conditional expectations. We establish positivity results that clarify the structure of this framework. A central limit theorem is proved for the relevant noncommutative setting. The proof constructs an analogue of the conditionally free R-transform, built from multilinear function series. The resulting transform is well defined and retains the algebraic properties needed for limit theorems.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We analyzed the influence of static gravitational field on the vacuum and proposed the concept of inhomogeneous vacuum. According to the corresponding Fermat's principle in the general relativity, we derived a graded refractive index of vacuum in a static gravitational field. We found that the light deflection in a gravitational field can be calculated correctly with the use of this refractive index and therefore the gravitational lensing can be treated conveniently with the optical method.\", \"sampled\": \"This study examines the late-time behavior of spherically symmetric Yang-Mills solutions on Minkowski and Schwarzschild backgrounds. Starting from smooth, compactly supported initial data, the solutions develop tails that decay as t^{-4} at timelike infinity on both backgrounds. Nonlinear perturbation theory yields this tail behavior, and for small initial data on Minkowski spacetime a third-order amplitude formula is obtained. Numerical simulations confirm the formula’s accuracy. The perturbation approach applies uniformly to both geometries.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The phonon dynamics in a one dimensional Heisenberg spin chain coupled to finite-frequency bond phonons is studied. We present the first detailed phonon spectra for these systems using Quantum Monte Carlo. The quantum phase transition is dominated by a central peak, yet the renormalisation of the main phonon branch depends strongly on the bare phonon frequency omega_0. The main branch remains largely unaffected at omega_0 \\\\gsim J, but it softens completely when omega_0 is low enough. This is an unusual scenario for a structural phase transition and was observable only on sufficiently large systems.\", \"sampled\": \"Braided quantum field theories, introduced by Oeckl, offer a framework for constructing QFTs with Hopf algebra symmetries and provide a systematic approach to incorporating algebraic symmetries into field theory. They enable the study of non-perturbative relations among correlation functions. This paper examines Hopf algebra symmetries and the associated relations in braided QFT, identifying four algebraic conditions that must be satisfied for those relations to hold. The discussion is applied to Poincaré symmetries in two noncommutative field theories: the effective QFT of 3D quantum gravity coupled to spinless particles by Freidel and Livine, and noncommutative field theory on the Moyal plane.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Let L be an ample holomorphic line bundle over a compact complex Hermitian manifold X. Any fixed smooth Hermitian metric on L induces a Hilbert space structure on the space of global holomorphic sections with values in the k:th tensor power of L. In this paper various convergence results are obtained for the corresponding Bergman kernels. The convergence is studied in the large k limit and is expressed in terms of the equilibrium metric associated to the fixed metric, as well as in terms of the Monge-Ampere measure of the fixed metric itself on a certain support set.\", \"sampled\": \"We analyze vacuum Cherenkov radiation in the spacelike Maxwell-Chern-Simons (MCS) theory using a semiclassical treatment that reproduces the leading terms of the tree-level quantum field theory result. For a charged particle with large energy, we demonstrate that certain quantum corrections, such as the correction to the classical MCS Cherenkov angle, are suppressed. This MCS-theory Cherenkov radiation could induce anisotropy effects in ultra-high-energy cosmic rays. We also qualitatively discuss vacuum Cherenkov radiation originating from a modified-Maxwell term in the action and provide bounds on some of its dimensionless coupling constants based on ultra-high-energy cosmic ray data.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We present results of a photometric survey whose aim was to derive structural and astrophysical parameters for 42 open clusters. While our sample is definitively not representative of the total open cluster sample in the Galaxy, it does cover a wide range of cluster parameters and is uniform enough to allow for simple statistical considerations. BV wide-field CCD photometry was obtained for open clusters for which photometric, structural, and dynamical evolution parameters were determined. The limiting and core radii were determined by analyzing radial density profiles. The ages, reddenings, and distances were obtained from the solar metallicity isochrone fitting. The mass function was used to study the dynamical state of the systems, mass segregation effect and to estimate the total mass and number of cluster members. This study reports on the first determination of basic parameters for 11 out of 42 observed open clusters. The angular sizes for the majority of the observed clusters appear to be several times larger than the catalogue data indicate.\", \"sampled\": \"We use a non-perturbative bosonization formalism to study the quantum Hall effect in graphene at filling factors ν = 0 and ν = ±1. A bosonization scheme is developed for electrons with spin-1/2 and pseudospin-1/2 degrees of freedom and applied to the quantum Hall ferromagnetic regime within the lowest Landau level. We consider three phases: the spin-pseudospin phase (ν = -1), corresponding to a quarter-filled lowest Landau level, and the spin and pseudospin phases (ν = 0), corresponding to a half-filled level. The formalism is applied to an effective continuous model proposed by Alicea and Fisher that includes SU(4) symmetry-breaking terms. The semiclassical limit of the derived boson model from the SU(4) invariant part of the Hamiltonian agrees with previous results for SU(N) skyrmions published by Arovas and co-workers. We propose that charged excitations, or quantum Hall skyrmions, can be described as a coherent state of bosons. We analytically calculate the dispersion relations for the elementary excitations for each quantum Hall state, including the spin-pseudospin phase at ν = -1.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We present the results of detailed spectroscopic abundance analyses for 18 elements in 31 nearby stars with planets. The resulting abundances are combined with other similar studies of nearby stars with planets and compared to a sample of nearby stars without detected planets. We find some evidence for abundance differences between these two samples for Al, Si and Ti.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper, written for the 100-year centenary of the birth of Matvei Petrovich Bronstein and submitted to a conference held in November 2006, explores the connection between microscopic and macroscopic physics. The study of many-body condensed-matter systems offers insights into how the physical laws of the Universe may have formed. The paper discusses topics ranging from semiconductors to quantum gravity.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In view of the recent results from the MiniBooNE experiment we revisit the global neutrino oscillation fit to short-baseline neutrino data by adding one or two sterile neutrinos with eV-scale masses to the three Standard Model neutrinos, and for the first time we consider also the global fit with three sterile neutrinos. Four-neutrino oscillations of the (3+1) type have been only marginally allowed before the recent MiniBooNE results, and become even more disfavored with the new data (at the level of $4\\\\sigma$). In the framework of so-called (3+2) five-neutrino mass schemes we find severe tension between appearance and disappearance experiments at the level of more than $3\\\\sigma$, and hence no satistfactory fit to the global data is possible in (3+2) schemes. This tension remains also when a third sterile neutrino is added, and the quality of the global fit does not improve significantly in a (3+3) scheme.\", \"sampled\": \"This study investigates whether the blazar gamma-ray spectrum hardens with increasing flux using nine years of data from the Energetic Gamma Ray Experiment Telescope (EGRET). We conducted an exhaustive search of the EGRET archives, selecting all blazars observed more than twice. This yielded a sample of 67 blazars, including 18 flat-spectrum radio quasars and 6 low-frequency peaked BL Lac objects, representing the most comprehensive such study to date. Across the full sample, no clear pattern of variation in the spectral index was detected. Some blazars showed no statistical evidence of spectral variability, while in a few cases, the spectrum hardened as the gamma-ray flux increased. We also found evidence for a flux-hardness anticorrelation at low fluxes for five blazars. The most well-observed sources in the sample were 3C 279, 3C 273, PKS 0528+134, 1622-297, and 0208-512. For three of these—3C 279, PKS 0528+134, and 1622-297—we observed previously unreported spectral hysteresis on weekly timescales.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Determination of air density and the quantitative estimates of water vapour adsorbed or desorbed by the surface of mass standard, remain the principals sources of uncertainties when weighing mass standard made from stainless steel or super alloy. The classical method, for air density determination, use the so called CIPM-1981/91 formula and the measured air parameters. An other approach is based only on the results of comparisons, realized successively in air and in vacuum, between special artefacts. The distribution and the state of water molecules inside the chamber of mass comparator influence the results of weighing. Also, the instruments used for air moisture measurement are either, not very sensitive (capacitive hygrometer) or disturbing for environment (dew-point hygrometer).\", \"sampled\": \"We consider the Cauchy integral, C^μ, of a finite complex measure μ. A continuous measure μ is defined as reflectionless if its Cauchy integral is zero μ-almost everywhere in the complex plane. We show that a reflectionless measure μ is trivial if its Cauchy maximal function C^μ_* is summable with respect to |μ|. A partial geometric characterization for the set of reflectionless measures on a line is also provided, along with a discussion of the links between our findings and sets of finite perimeter as defined by De Giorgi. Finally, we construct an example of a reflectionless measure to demonstrate the sharpness of the main result.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper is devoted to the analysis of a second order method for recovering the \\\\emph{a priori} unknown shape of an inclusion $\\\\omega$ inside a body $\\\\Omega$ from boundary measurement. This inverse problem - known as electrical impedance tomography - has many important practical applications and hence has focussed much attention during the last years. However, to our best knowledge, no work has yet considered a second order approach for this problem. This paper aims to fill that void: we investigate the existence of second order derivative of the state $u$ with respect to perturbations of the shape of the interface $\\\\partial\\\\omega$, then we choose a cost function in order to recover the geometry of $\\\\partial \\\\omega$ and derive the expression of the derivatives needed to implement the corresponding Newton method.\", \"sampled\": \"We study the production of a sterile neutrino species via active-sterile mixing in a thermal medium. Using the effective action and quantum master equation methods, we derive the quantum kinetic equations for the distribution functions and coherences. For active-sterile oscillations, the decoherence time scale is τ_dec = 2/Γ_aa. The evolution of the distribution functions is governed by two distinct time scales related to quasiparticle mode damping rates: Γ_1 = Γ_aa cos²θ_m and Γ_2 = Γ_aa sin²θ_m. Away from any MSW resonances, these two time scales can differ significantly, which complicates the description of active-sterile production and precludes the use of a simple rate equation. This work presents the full set of quantum kinetic equations for the active and sterile populations and their coherences.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We examine instantons and solitons of the effective action of probe D8-branes in the background of $N_c$ D4-branes which has served as a holographic description of QCD. We show that the 4d instantons sit at the minimum of the Euclidean 5d action. Restricting to the static solitons of the five-dimensional model we are led to consider monopoles in a 3-dimensional curved space.\", \"sampled\": \"This study examines the statefinder parameter within a five-dimensional big bounce model to distinguish between attractor solutions for quintessence and phantom field models. The evolving trajectories for these two attractor solutions are shown to be very different in the statefinder parameters plane. Furthermore, these trajectories for the quintessence and phantom field solutions differ from those of other dark energy models, demonstrating their distinct characteristics.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We report on the discovery of six new ZZ Ceti stars. They were selected as candidates based on preparatory photometric observations of objects from the Hamburg Quasar Survey (HQS), and based on the spectra of the Supernova Ia Progenitor Survey (SPY). Time-series photometry of 19 candidate stars was carried out at the Nordic Optical Telescope (NOT) at Roque de Los Muchachos Observatory, Spain.\", \"sampled\": \"Potential energy curves for excited leptonic states of the helium-antihydrogen system were calculated for orbital angular momentum projections $\\\\Lambda$ = 0, 1, and 30. The calculations employed the Ritz variational method with an explicitly correlated ansatz that describes the motion of the two electrons and one positron in the field of the helium nucleus and the antiproton. The model is applicable to any $\\\\Lambda$ value and includes quasibound states.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Cooling is the main process leading to the condensation of gas in the dark matter potential wells and consequently to star and structure formation. In a metal-free environment, the main available coolants are H, He, H$_2$ and HD; once the gas is enriched with metals, these also become important in defining the cooling properties of the gas. We discuss the implementation in Gadget-2 of molecular and metal cooling at temperatures lower that $\\\\rm10^4 K$, following the time dependent properties of the gas and pollution from stellar evolution. We have checked the validity of our scheme comparing the results of some test runs with previous calculations of cosmic abundance evolution and structure formation, finding excellent agreement.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper establishes a result for Axiom A polynomial skew products analogous to the well-known case for hyperbolic maps of C. A polynomial skew product is a regular map on C^2 of the form f(z,w) = (p(z), q(z,w)) with degree d ≥ 2. We demonstrate that for such maps, critical orbits either tend to infinity or accumulate on an attracting set. This work builds on a finding by Jonsson (1999), who showed that a polynomial skew product f is Axiom A if the closure of its postcritical set is disjoint from its Julia set. This condition is analogous to the one for polynomial maps of C, where hyperbolicity is equivalent to the closure of the postcritical set being disjoint from the Julia set.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We review recent results obtained in the physics of the thermal Casimir force acting between two dielectrics, dielectric and metal, and between metal and semiconductor. The detailed derivation for the low-temperature behavior of the Casimir free energy, pressure and entropy in the configuration of two real dielectric plates is presented. For dielectrics with finite static dielectric permittivity it is shown that the Nernst heat theorem is satisfied. Hence, the Lifshitz theory of the van der Waals and Casimir forces is demonstrated to be consistent with thermodynamics. The nonzero dc conductivity of dielectric plates is proved to lead to a violation of the Nernst heat theorem and, thus, is not related to the phenomenon of dispersion forces. The low-temperature asymptotics of the Casimir free energy, pressure and entropy are derived also in the configuration of one metal and one dielectric plate. The results are shown to be consistent with thermodynamics if the dielectric plate possesses a finite static dielectric permittivity. If the dc conductivity of a dielectric plate is taken into account this results in the violation of the Nernst heat theorem.\", \"sampled\": \"We analyze age and metallicity estimates for 1039 FGK dwarfs in the Solar Neighborhood from the Valenti & Fischer (2005) dataset. The analysis reveals clear evidence for an age-metallicity relation in the local disk, albeit with substantial dispersion at any given time. The mean metallicity increases from approximately -0.3 dex at a lookback time of 10 Gyr to a present-day value of +0.15 dex. The age distribution of nearby FGK dwarfs is largely consistent with a uniform star-formation rate over the history of the Galactic disk, as inferred from the Hipparcos catalogue. Most stars hosting known planetary companions are younger than average. However, some older, lower-metallicity host stars show enhanced [α/Fe] ratios, indicating they likely belong to the thick disk. Stars with planets orbiting within 0.4 AU exhibit a significantly flatter age distribution, which may be an indicator of long-term system stability. Based on these findings, if terrestrial planet frequency correlates with stellar metallicity, we hypothesize a median age of around 3 Gyr for planetary systems analogous to our own.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Schroedinger equation on a Hilbert space ${\\\\cal H}$, represents a linear\\nHamiltonian dynamical system on the space of quantum pure states, the\\nprojective Hilbert space $P {\\\\cal H}$. Separable states of a bipartite quantum\\nsystem form a special submanifold of $P {\\\\cal H}$.\\n We analyze the Hamiltonian dynamics that corresponds to the quantum system\\nconstrained on the manifold of separable states, using as an important example\\nthe system of two interacting qubits. The constraints introduce nonlinearities\\nwhich render the dynamics nontrivial.\", \"sampled\": \"This study explores the analysis of n-homomorphisms in c-algebras and categories. The researchers found that preserving n-homomorphisms between c-algebras presents a significant challenge. Addressing the question of even and odd n arguments led to revealing distinct outcomes. Expanding on these findings, the study established stronger conclusions about n. Specifically, for n equal to 3 and odd, the presence of phi highlights differences between two orthogonal homomorphisms. The research emphasizes the absence of nontrivial linear n-homomorphisms between c-algebras, offering new insights in this mathematical field.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A generalized geometric method is developed for constructing exact solutions\\nof gravitational field equations in Einstein theory and generalizations. First,\\nwe apply the formalism of nonholonomic frame deformations (formally considered\\nfor nonholonomic manifolds and Finsler spaces) when the gravitational field\\nequations transform into systems of nonlinear partial differential equations\\nwhich can be integrated in general form. The new classes of solutions are\\ndefined by generic off-diagonal metrics depending on integration functions on\\none, two and three (or three and four) variables if we consider four (or five)\\ndimensional spacetimes. Second, we use a general scheme when one (two)\\nparameter families of exact solutions are defined by any source-free solutions\\nof Einstein's equations with one (two) Killing vector field(s).\", \"sampled\": \"This study investigates the thermal impact of rotation within the framework of random classical zero-point radiation. It emphasizes that the rotating system comprises Frenet-Serret orthogonal tetrads, with a stationary rotating detector. The energy density in this rotating system demonstrates periodic behavior corresponding to the rotation period. Analysis indicates that the energy density of electromagnetic and massless scalar fields in the rotating system exhibits periodicity with thermal characteristics, notably associated with Planck's constant. The study reveals that certain thermal attributes are obscured by factors like a gamma factor of 2-1 in the electromagnetic field, intricately tied to rotation through two defined parameters. The calculations, conducted from a classical perspective, offer insights into these findings based on current knowledge.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Many real systems possess accelerating statistics where the total number of\\nedges grows faster than the network size. In this paper, we propose a simple\\nweighted network model with accelerating growth. We derive analytical\\nexpressions for the evolutions and distributions for strength, degree, and\\nweight, which are relevant to accelerating growth. We also find that\\naccelerating growth determines the clustering coefficient of the networks.\", \"sampled\": \"An international workshop on quantum physics and communication explored quantum decoherence theory in open systems. Researchers observed quantum decoherence in the studied system, noting its growing importance over time. Calculations highlighted the transitions from quantum to classical behaviors over time. This 2005 analysis, focusing on six categories, provides insights into the evolution and behavior of quantum systems in different contexts.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In this paper we address the problem of finding the sensing capacity of\\nsensor networks for a class of linear observation models and a fixed SNR\\nregime. Sensing capacity is defined as the maximum number of signal dimensions\\nreliably identified per sensor observation. In this context sparsity of the\\nphenomena is a key feature that determines sensing capacity. Precluding the SNR\\nof the environment the effect of sparsity on the number of measurements\\nrequired for accurate reconstruction of a sparse phenomena has been widely\\ndealt with under compressed sensing. Nevertheless the development there was\\nmotivated from an algorithmic perspective.\", \"sampled\": \"Our study focuses on Galactic Wind Signatures around high redshift galaxies. We observe that outflows typically escape low-density regions with minimal impact. The presence of galactic winds does not diminish the strength of HI absorption near galaxies. Our analysis of the average transmissivity of HI, CIV, and OVI within 1 reveals OVI as a promising indicator of galactic winds at high redshift. Models lacking feedback can generate prominent CIV and OVI absorption lines, while robust feedback mechanisms may even produce OVI lines in specific scenarios. Conversely, HI metal absorption systems prove responsive to wind presence, underscoring the intricate interaction between galactic winds and the surrounding environment.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The critical adsorption point (CAP) of self-avoiding walks (SAW) interacting\\nwith a planar surface with surface disorder or sequence disorder has been\\nstudied. We present theoretical equations, based on ones previously developed\\nby Soteros and Whittington (J. Phys. A.: Math. Gen.\", \"sampled\": \"Analytical methods are employed to compute Dirac quasinormal modes in d-dimensional de Sitter spacetime. This approach enables the calculation of quasinormal frequencies for massive Dirac fields, following similar computations for half-integer spin fields in prior studies.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A probability distribution function is presented which provides a realistic\\ndescription of the detection of scattered photons. The resulting probabilities\\ncan be described analytically by means of a superposition of several special\\nfunctions. These exact expressions can be evaluated numerically only for small\\ndistances and limited time residuals, due to computer accuracy limitations. In\\nthis report we provide approximations for the exact expressions in different\\nregions of the distance-time residual space, defined by the detector geometry\\nand the space-time scale of an event.\", \"sampled\": \"This study examines the task, context, and geometric aspects of Lagrangian and Eulerian formulations in gradient-flow dynamics. Singular solutions are investigated across categories like nlin. The research analyzes flows in Lagrangian and Eulerian frameworks, with the Lagrangian formulation involving a dissipative modification of fluid mechanics. In contrast, Eulerian equations demonstrate self-organization properties, highlighting reductions of nonlocal characteristics for scalars, 1-forms, and 2-forms. Singular solutions are detected within these equations, associated with collapsed and clumped states, revealing dynamic phenomena within these systems.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"We show numerically that in a binary system of Yukawa particles, a dispersity\\ndriven disordering transition occurs. In the presence of quenched disorder this\\ndisordering transition coincides with a marked increase in the depinning\\nthreshold, known as a peak effect. We find that the addition of poorly pinned\\nparticles can increase the overall pinning in the sample by increasing the\\namount of topological disorder present.\", \"sampled\": \"Exploring the connection between domain walls in cosmology and categories in AdS/DS supergravity hinges on task and context. Notably, OSP(2|4) invariant anti-de Sitter vacuum solutions play a key role, especially within the n = 2,4 supergravity theory. Specifically, supersymmetric domain walls in this context align with pseudo-supersymmetric cosmologies, revealing the intricate interplay among various physical phenomena. These insights illuminate the intricate nature of cosmological models and their interrelations with theoretical constructs in supergravity.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"We apply the Lunin--Maldacena construction of gravity duals to beta-deformed\\ngauge theories to a class of Type IIB backgrounds with U(1)^3 global symmetry,\\nwhich include the multicenter D3-brane backgrounds dual to the Coulomb branch\\nof N=4 super Yang-Mills and the rotating D3-brane backgrounds dual to the\\ntheory at finite temperature and chemical potential. After a general\\ndiscussion, we present the full form of the deformed metrics for three special\\ncases, which can be used for the study of various aspects of the\\nmarginally-deformed gauge theories.\", \"sampled\": \"A recent article accepted for publication in Physical Review B examines cooling and heating processes in adiabatic magnetization. The study demonstrates that an alloy heats up near its Curie point when a magnetic field is applied, contrary to the usual inverse magnetocaloric effect seen in martensitic transitions. The research presents magnetization and specific heat data for comparison, showing consistent results for typical second-order paramagnetic-ferromagnetic phase transitions. However, measurements of first-order structural transitions indicate higher values at lower fields, hinting at irreversible thermodynamic effects that could clarify discrepancies in the observed phenomena.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"We introduce a simple nearest-neighbor spin model with multiple metastable\\nphases, the number and decay pathways of which are explicitly controlled by the\\nparameters of the system. With this model we can construct, for example, a\\nsystem which evolves through an arbitrarily long succession of metastable\\nphases. We also construct systems in which different phases may nucleate\\ncompetitively from a single initial phase. For such a system, we present a\\ngeneral method to extract from numerical simulations the individual nucleation\\nrates of the nucleating phases.\", \"sampled\": \"A study comparing depth-dose distributions of light and heavy beams using Monte Carlo simulations revealed a notable decrease in the peak-to-plateau ratio of dose profiles attributed to nuclear fragmentation reactions. Protons, carbon ions, neon ions, and nickel ions at energies of 3, 12, 20, and 58 MeV/u, respectively, were assessed. The analysis compared the depth-dose distributions of these beams with existing experimental data, indicating that all projectiles, including neon ions at 20 MeV/u, were equally appropriate for the investigation.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Interaction between metal surface waves and periodic geometry of\\nsubwavelength structures is at the core of the recent but crucial renewal of\\ninterest in plasmonics. One of the most intriguing points is the observation of\\nabnormal strong transmission through these periodic structures, which can\\nexceed by orders of magnitude the classical transmission given by the filling\\nfactor of the plate. The actual paradigm is that this abnormal transmission\\narises from the periodicity, and then that such high transmission should\\ndisappear in random geometries.\", \"sampled\": \"This study focuses on thermoelectric responses near quantum critical points. The research explores the thermoelectric response close to a field-induced quantum critical point, highlighting a significant Nernst coefficient nu. Additionally, the study uncovers a dimensionless ratio showing a minimum at around 0.1 K for the Seebeck coefficient compared to the electronic specific heat. Near the critical point at temperatures near 16 K, unusual behavior of the Nernst coefficient in the vortex-liquid state is observed, revealing intriguing phenomena in the quantum domain.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We calculate the inclusive decay rate of the spin-triplet bottomonium states chi_{bJ} into charm hadrons, including the leading-order color-singlet and color-octet bb-bar annihilation mechanisms. We also calculate the momentum distribution of the charm quark from the decay of chi_{bJ}. The infrared divergences from the color-singlet process bb-bar -> cc-bar g are factored into the probability density at the origin for a bb-bar pair in a color-octet state. That probability density can be determined phenomenologically from the fraction of decays of chi_{bJ} that include charm hadrons.\", \"sampled\": \"This research presents a novel approach to stochastic quantization, a process proven to mirror the deformation of an observable algebra. This technique is subsequently employed in models of nonrelativistic and relativistic particles interacting with an electromagnetic field. For nonrelativistic particles, the quantization aligns with the Fokker-Planck equation under a specific force. When implemented on a relativistic particle, the quantization process leads to a relativistic extension of the Fokker-Planck equation. Without an electromagnetic field, this equation simplifies to the relativistic diffusion equation.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The Birman-Murakami-Wenzl algebra (BMW algebra) of type Dn is shown to be semisimple and free of rank (2^n+1)n!!-(2^(n-1)+1)n! over a specified commutative ring R, where n!! is the product of the first n odd integers. We also show it is a cellular algebra over suitable ring extensions of R. The Brauer algebra of type Dn is the image af an R-equivariant homomorphism and is also semisimple and free of the same rank, but over the polynomial ring Z with delta and its inverse adjoined. A rewrite system for the Brauer algebra is used in bounding the rank of the BMW algebra above.\", \"sampled\": \"High-dynamic range radio observations have pinpointed 21 stars in Westerlund 1, a stellar cluster that is notable for having the densest known concentration of radio-emitting stars in any young, massive galactic cluster. One star of particular interest is the supergiant B[e] star W9, which displays a high radio luminosity, indicative of significant activity. The calculated mass-loss rate of W9, approximately 10^-3 solar mass per year, aligns with that of the star eta Carina, hinting at similar underlying mechanisms. Furthermore, thermal emission has been observed from nearly all the cool red supergiants and yellow hypergiants in Westerlund 1, reinforcing the evidence of pervasive stellar activity.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Causality and stability in relativistic dissipative hydrodynamics are important conceptual issues. We argue that causality is not restricted to hyperbolic set of differential equations. E.g. heat conduction equation can be causal considering the physical validity of the theory. Furthermore we propose a new concept of relativistic internal energy that clearly separates the dissipative and non-dissipative effects. We prove that with this choice we remove all known instabilities of the linear response approximation of viscous and heat conducting relativistic fluids.\", \"sampled\": \"This research explored locally compact abelian groups and topological groups, emphasizing spectral decomposition and unitary representation. The study primarily involved obtaining a collection of generalized eigenvectors. These eigenvectors provided a generalized spectral decomposition for a specific unitary representation of a commutative, locally compact topological group. Notably, these generalized eigenvectors were identified as functionals within the dual space of a rigging on the space of square integrable functions on the character group.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The Gamma-Ray Large Area Space Telescope (GLAST), scheduled to be launched in fall 2007, is the next generation satellite for high-energy gamma-ray astronomy. The Large Area Telescope (LAT), GLAST main instrument, with a wide field of view (> 2 sr), a large effective area (> 8000 cm^2 at 1 GeV) and 20 MeV - 300 GeV energy range, will provide excellent high energy gamma-ray observations for Dark Matter searches. In this paper we examine the potential of the LAT to detect gamma-rays coming from WIMPS annihilation in the context of supersymmetry. As an example, two search regions are investigated: the galactic center and the galactic satellites.\", \"sampled\": \"The topological Tverberg theorem posits that for any continuous map of the (q-1)(d+1)-simplex to R^d, there exist q disjoint faces whose images intersect. While this has been confirmed for affine maps and when q is a prime power, it remains unproven in general. This study expands the theorem by mandating that pairs of vertices land in separate faces, thereby introducing the notion of constraint graphs. It offers a thorough catalog of constraint graphs applicable to the topological Tverberg theorem. This 16-page investigation, supplemented with 12 figures, leans significantly on the connectivity findings of chessboard-type complexes for its proof.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We report evidence for a planetary companion around the nearby young star HD 70573. The star is a G type dwarf located at a distance of 46 pc with age estimation between 20 and 300 Myrs. We carried out spectroscopic observations of this star with FEROS at the 2.2 m MPG/ESO telescope at La Silla. Our spectroscopic analysis yields a spectral type of G1-1.5V and an age of about 100 Myrs. Variations in stellar radial velocity of HD 70573 have been monitored since December 2003 until January 2007. The velocity accuracy of FEROS within this period is about 10 m/s. HD 70573 shows a radial velocity variation with a period of 852 +/- 12 days and a semi-amplitude of 149 +/- 6 m/s. The period of this variation is significantly longer than its rotational period, which is 3.3 days.\", \"sampled\": \"This research utilized optical spectroscopy to examine nine cataclysmic binary stars, namely LX And, CZ Aql, LU Cam, GZ Cnc, V632 Cyg, V1006 Cyg, BF Eri, BI Ori, and FO Per, with the primary objective of determining their orbital periods. The orbital period of LX And was determined to be 0.1509743(5) d, CZ Aql had a period of 0.2005(6) d, LU Cam showed a period of 0.1499686(4) d, GZ Cnc's period was 0.0881(4) d, V632 Cyg's was 0.06377(8) d, V1006 Cyg's period was 0.09903(9) d, BF Eri's was 0.2708804(4) d, and BI Ori's period was 0.1915(5) d. Of particular note, V1006 Cyg's orbital period situates it within the 2- to 3-hour 'gap' in the distribution of cataclysmic binary orbital periods.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I review the status of science with wide field surveys. For many decades surveys have been the backbone of astronomy, and the main engine of discovery, as we have mapped the sky at every possible wavelength. Surveys are an efficient use of resources. They are important as a fundamental resource; to map intrinsically large structures; to gain the necessary statistics to address some problems; and to find very rare objects. I summarise major recent wide field surveys - 2MASS, SDSS, 2dfGRS, and UKIDSS - and look at examples of the exciting science they have produced, covering the structure of the Milky Way, the measurement of cosmological parameters, the creation of a new field studying substellar objects, and the ionisation history of the Universe. I then look briefly at upcoming projects in the optical-IR survey arena - VISTA, PanSTARRS, WISE, and LSST. Finally I ask, now we have opened up essentially all wavelength windows, whether the exploration of survey discovery space is ended.\", \"sampled\": \"The Gaussian-basis Monte Carlo method (GBMC), first proposed by Corney and Drummond, utilizes the expansion of the density-matrix operator rho via the coherent Gaussian-type operator basis Lambda. One of the key benefits of the GBMC is its resistance to the notorious minus sign problem, a common issue in quantum Monte Carlo computations. However, the original GBMC has shown certain limitations, including its inability to accurately represent the true ground state, leading to potential systematic errors. To address these shortcomings, the quantum-number projection scheme was incorporated into the existing GBMC, enhancing both its precision and efficiency by enabling importance sampling in the quantum-number-projected phase-space. This refinement not only accelerates convergence with the ground state, but also increases the method's reliability for studying itinerant electron systems. Moreover, the refined GBMC method broadens its scope of applicability, extending the range of manageable parameters and providing a more efficient computational approach for strongly correlated electron systems. Detailed over 24 pages and supplemented with 25 figures, this research paper provides a comprehensive examination of these enhancements. Consequently, the refined GBMC method holds considerable promise for future investigations in quantum mechanics.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"To enlighten the nature of the order-disorder and order-order transitions in block copolymer melts confined in narrow capillaries we analyze peculiarities of the conventional Landau weak crystallization theory of systems confined to cylindrical geometry. This phenomenological approach provides a quantitative classification of the cylindrical ordered morphologies by expansion of the order parameter spatial distribution into the eigenfunctions of the Laplace operator. The symmetry of the resulting ordered morphologies is shown to strongly depend both on the boundary conditions (wall preference) and the ratio of the cylinder radius and the wave length of the critical order parameter fluctuations, which determine the bulk ordering of the system under consideration. In particular, occurrence of the helical morphologies is a rather general consequence of the imposed cylindrical symmetry for narrow enough capillaries. We discuss also the ODT and OOT involving some other simplest morphologies. The presented results are relevant also to other ordering systems as charge-density waves appearing under addition of an ionic solute to a solvent in its critical region, weakly charged polyelectrolyte solutions in poor solvent, microemulsions etc.\", \"sampled\": \"This research explores the integration of CP violation into the Froggatt-Nielsen mechanism, a complex aspect of theoretical physics. To achieve this integration, the study introduces non-renormalizable interactions with a flavor democratic structure into the fermion mass generation sector, thereby enhancing our comprehension of these intricate processes. The research suggests that a minimum of two iso-singlet scalar fields with enforced discrete symmetry are essential for generating CP violation. It further demonstrates that the relative phase between the vacuum expectation values of these scalar fields is the primary source of CP violation. The research also examines the ratios of quark masses and the CKM matrix, including the CP violating phase, which are influenced by the CKM element and the ratio of two vacuum expectation values. Importantly, the research forecasts the angles of the unitarity triangle and the CKM off-diagonal elements as dependent on the CKM element, the ratio of vacuum expectation values, and a phase.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"In the work of Hoshino, Kato and Miyachi, the authors look at t-structures induced by a compact object, C, of a triangulated category, T, which is rigid in the sense of Iyama and Yoshino. Hoshino, Kato and Miyachi show that such an object yields a non-degenerate t-structure on T whose heart es equivalent to Mod(End(C)^op). Rigid objects in a triangulated category can be thought of as behaving like chain differential graded algebras (DGAs). Analogously, looking at objects which behave like cochain DGAs naturally gives the dual notion of a corigid object. Here, we see that a compact corigid object, S, of a triangulated category, T, induces a structure similar to a t-structure which we shall call a co-t-structure.\", \"sampled\": \"This study investigates the generation of quantum operations in single-qubit systems affected by classical noise that follows a 1/f power spectrum. It introduces an effective method for approximating this noise using a discrete multi-state Markovian fluctuator, marking a significant contribution to the field. The research also demonstrates that the average temporal evolution of the qubit density matrix under the influence of 1/f noise can be reliably calculated using recently formulated deterministic master equations. High-fidelity qubit operations, including quantum memory and the NOT gate, can be achieved using a gradient-based optimization algorithm. Notably, the calculated fidelities for the NOT gate are qualitatively comparable to those previously derived for random telegraph noise.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Low density lattice codes (LDLC) are novel lattice codes that can be decoded efficiently and approach the capacity of the additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN) channel. In LDLC a codeword x is generated directly at the n-dimensional Euclidean space as a linear transformation of a corresponding integer message vector b, i.e., x = Gb, where H, the inverse of G, is restricted to be sparse. The fact that H is sparse is utilized to develop a linear-time iterative decoding scheme which attains, as demonstrated by simulations, good error performance within ~0.5dB from capacity at block length of n = 100,000 symbols. The paper also discusses convergence results and implementation considerations.\", \"sampled\": \"This research centers on the mathematical framework of Abelian sandpiles on graphs, denoted as G x I. The study builds upon an arbitrary finite connected graph, G, and a finite interval within the set Z, denoted as I. The findings confirm that for any given graph G with at least two vertices, the stationary measures, symbolized as mu_I, possess two extremal weak limit points. These extremal limits are recognized as the sole ergodic measures of maximum entropy on the set of infinite recurrent configurations. It is also demonstrated that under any of these limiting measures, it is feasible to introduce a finite number of grains, leading to all sites toppling infinitely often.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Electroweak radiative corrections to muon capture on nuclei are computed and found to be sizable. They enhance the capture rates for hydrogen and helium by 2.8% and 3.0% respectively. As a result, the value of the induced pseudoscalar coupling, g_P^exp, extracted from a recent hydrogen 1S singlet capture experiment is increased by about 21% to g_P^exp = 7.3 +/- 1.2 and brought into good agreement with the prediction of chiral perturbation theory, g_P^theory=8.2 +/- 0.2. Implications for helium capture rate predictions are also discussed.\", \"sampled\": \"This research, featured in the International Journal of Mathematics, delves into the investigation of noncommuting quadrilaterals of factors, specifically an irreducible inclusion of factors N subset M with intermediate subfactors P and Q. It was observed that these intermediate subfactors, P and Q, were responsible for generating M, while their intersection culminated in N. The study further probed the structure of these non-commuting quadrilaterals of factors, with a particular focus on those where all elementary inclusions were 2-supertransitive.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The Special Theory of Relativity and the Theory of the Electron have had an interesting history together. Originally the electron was studied in a non relativistic context and this opened up the interesting possibility that lead to the conclusion that the mass of the electron could be thought of entirely in electromagnetic terms without introducing inertial considerations. However the application of Special Relativity lead to several problems, both for an extended electron and the point electron. These inconsistencies have, contrary to popular belief not been resolved satisfactorily today, even within the context of Quantum Theory. Nevertheless these and subsequent studies bring out the interesting result that Special Relativity breaks down within the Compton scale or when the Compton scale is not neglected. This again runs contrary to an uncritical notion that Special Relativity is valid for point particles.\", \"sampled\": \"This work examines the relation between operadic module structures and functors, with operads, symmetric modules, right modules over operads, and categories of algebras providing the setting for the associated functorial constructions. It uses generalized symmetric power functors defined via coinvariants for symmetric-group actions and recalls the classical construction that identifies symmetric modules with a subcategory of functors on the base category. The main aim is to establish an analogous result for functors on algebras over an operad, where right operad modules are symmetric modules equipped with a right operad action. In this setting, right modules over operads define functors on algebra categories, and the construction from right modules to functors is shown to be a categorical embedding.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The structure of the unnatural parity states of PsH, LiPs, NaPs and KPs are investigated with the configuration interaction and stochastic variational methods. The binding energies (in hartree) are found to be 8.17x10-4, 4.42x10-4, 15.14x10-4 and 21.80x10-4 respectively. These states are constructed by first coupling the two electrons into a configuration which is predominantly 3Pe, and then adding a p-wave positron. All the active particles are in states in which the relative angular momentum between any pair of particles is at least L = 1.\", \"sampled\": \"We report observations of a massive globular cluster in the dwarf irregular galaxy Sextans B, identified in Hubble Space Telescope Wide Field and Planetary Camera 2 (HST WFPC2) imaging. Long-slit spectra obtained with the SCORPIO spectrograph on the 6-m telescope of the Special Astrophysical Observatory of the Russian Academy of Sciences provide the basis for estimating its stellar population parameters. The cluster has an age of 2 ± 1 Gyr, a metallicity of -1.35 ± 0.25 dex, and an alpha-element abundance ratio of 0.1 ± 0.1 dex, consistent with a moderately old, metal-poor system in Sextans B.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We study the performance of holonomic quantum gates, driven by lasers, under the effect of a dissipative environment modeled as a thermal bath of oscillators. We show how to enhance the performance of the gates by suitable choice of the loop in the manifold of the controllable parameters of the laser. For a simplified, albeit realistic model, we find the surprising result that for a long time evolution the performance of the gate (properly estimated in terms of average fidelity) increases.\", \"sampled\": \"The Arctic Circle Revisited analyzes limit shapes in the six-vertex model with domain wall boundary conditions, using the emptiness formation probability as a bulk correlation function. This probability is expressed in closed form through determinant and multiple-integral representations, enabling a systematic study over the full range of vertex weights. On the free-fermion line, it is related to a one-matrix model with a triple logarithmic singularity, identified as the Triple Penner model.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A general method is discussed to obtain Markovian master equations which describe the interaction with the environment in a microscopic and non-perturbative fashion. It is based on combining time-dependent scattering theory with the concept of continuous quantum measurements.\", \"sampled\": \"Stars and the holographic upper bound on gravitational action, classified under physics.gen-ph and noted as 2 pages, examines gravitational action during stellar contraction. The paper applies a holographic entropy bound to gravitational action in non-relativistic nebular collapse and defines a critical radius dependent on a body’s initial radius and mass.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We consider a class of theories involving an extension of the Standard Model gauge group to an {\\\\it a priori} arbitrary number of colors, $N_c$, and derive constraints on $N_c$. One motivation for this is the string theory landscape. For two natural classes of embeddings of this $N_c$-extended Standard Model in a supersymmetric grand unified theory, we show that requiring unbroken electromagnetic gauge invariance, asymptotic freedom of color, and three generations of quarks and leptons forces one to choose $N_c=3$.\", \"sampled\": \"Elastic neutron diffraction measurements are reported for CeRh1-xCoxIn5, a quasi-two-dimensional heavy-fermion system that at low x exhibits incommensurate antiferromagnetism and becomes an unconventional superconductor on the Co-rich side. The incommensurate antiferromagnetic order is characterized by qI=(1/2, 1/2, delta), with delta=0.298, defining the magnetic ordering vector in the low-x region of the phase diagram. Superconductivity appears to compete with this incommensurate order, whereas it coexists with commensurate antiferromagnetic order characterized by qc=(1/2, 1/2, 1/2).\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"When two-photon interactions are induced by down-converted light with a bandwidth that exceeds the pump bandwidth, they can obtain a behavior that is pulse-like temporally, yet spectrally narrow. At low photon fluxes this behavior reflects the time and energy entanglement between the down-converted photons. However, two-photon interactions such as two-photon absorption (TPA) and sum-frequency generation (SFG) can exhibit such a behavior even at high power levels, as long as the final state (i.e. the atomic level in TPA, or the generated light in SFG) is narrowband enough. This behavior does not depend on the squeezing properties of the light, is insensitive to linear losses, and has potential applications. In this paper we describe analytically this behavior for travelling-wave down-conversion with continuous or pulsed pumping, both for high- and low-power regimes. For this we derive a quantum-mechanical expression for the down-converted amplitude generated by an arbitrary pump, and formulate operators that represent various two-photon interactions induced by broadband light. This model is in excellent agreement with experimental results of TPA and SFG with high power down-converted light and with entangled photons [Dayan et al., Phys. Rev. Lett.\", \"sampled\": \"This study statistically analyzed fracture surfaces in Fontainebleau sandstone to relate fracture geometry to failure mechanisms in compacted granular materials, using surface roughness and height-variation statistics to characterize brittle fracture morphology. Surface roughness was self-affine across samples of different widths, with a roughness exponent zeta=0.46 +- 0.05 and a scaling range extending from the grain size d to an upper cutoff xi = 0.15 W. This zeta value agrees with findings for other sandstones and sintered materials, supporting a common roughness characterization for compacted grain systems rather than width-dependent roughness amplitude. For delta z > d, the height-variation probability distribution P(delta z,delta h) collapsed onto a single Gaussian distribution, and the height-variation statistics showed no multifractal behavior. Roughness amplitude, measured from height-height correlations at fixed delta z, was independent of sample width, indicating the absence of anomalous scaling reported in other materials. The authors attributed this behavior to brittle fracture, in contrast to damage failure in materials where zeta = 0.8.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The problem of designing high rate, full diversity noncoherent space-time block codes (STBCs) with low encoding and decoding complexity is addressed. First, the notion of $g$-group encodable and $g$-group decodable linear STBCs is introduced. Then for a known class of rate-1 linear designs, an explicit construction of fully-diverse signal sets that lead to four-group encodable and four-group decodable differential scaled unitary STBCs for any power of two number of antennas is provided.\", \"sampled\": \"This study models $0^+$ and $2^+$ states in $^{168}Er$ with a sixth-order quadrupole boson Hamiltonian, considering 26 recently identified $0^+$ states and 67 recently identified $2^+$ states. Two analytic formulas are used as alternative descriptions of the level energies: a semiclassical expression with four adjustable parameters and the exact eigenvalue of the Hamiltonian model with five adjustable parameters.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We report the first experimental demonstration of compensation of beam-beam interaction effects in high-energy particle collider by using space-charge forces of a low-energy electron beam. In our experiments, an electron lens, a novel instrument developed for the beam-beam compensation, was set on a 980-GeV proton bunch in the Tevatron proton-antiproton collider. The proton bunch losses due to its interaction with antiproton beam were reduced by a factor of 2 when the electron lens was operating. We describe the principle of electron lens operation and present experimental results.\", \"sampled\": \"This abstract considers a general relativistic spacetime describing a Schwarzschild black hole pierced by a spinning string, with emphasis on how the black hole alters the spinning string geometry. It examines the occurrence of closed timelike curves in this setting and their stability under linear perturbations. The modified spinning string spacetime admits closed timelike geodesics, relating the Schwarzschild black hole to a deformed causal structure in which such geodesics are possible.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We examine the orbits of satellite galaxies identified in a suite of N-body/gasdynamical simulations of the formation of $L_*$ galaxies in a LCDM universe. Most satellites follow conventional orbits; after turning around, they accrete into their host halo and settle on orbits whose apocentric radii are steadily eroded by dynamical friction. However, a number of outliers are also present, we find that ~1/3 of satellites identified at $z=0$ are on unorthodox orbits, with apocenters that exceed their turnaround radii. This population of satellites on extreme orbits consists typically of the faint member of a satellite pair that has been ejected onto a highly-energetic orbit during its first approach to the primary. Since the concurrent accretion of multiple satellite systems is a defining feature of hierarchical models of galaxy formation, we speculate that this three-body ejection mechanism may be the origin of (i) some of the newly discovered high-speed satellites around M31 (such as Andromeda XIV); (ii) some of the distant fast-receding Local Group members, such as Leo I; and (iii) the oddly isolated dwarf spheroidals Cetus and Tucana in the outskirts of the Local Group. Our results suggest that care must be exercised when using the orbits of the most weakly bound satellites to place constraints on the total mass of the Local Group.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper develops an effective high-energy interaction theory in QCD within the colour dipole approach, treating the BFKL Pomeron Calculus as its low-energy limit and examining whether the calculus admits a probabilistic interpretation in terms of colourless dipoles and their interactions. The analysis gives two equivalent descriptions of the BFKL Pomeron Calculus: a generating functional that provides a probabilistic view of high-energy scattering and leads to a Hamiltonian-like formulation for interacting dipoles, and a Langevin equation with a special, nontrivial noise term. With the two vertices $P\\\\to P+P$ and $P+P\\\\to P$, the BFKL Pomeron calculus can be interpreted as colourless dipoles undergoing decay and merging, assigning a probabilistic meaning to Pomerons with splitting and merging vertices. At high energies and large impact parameter, under the condition $b>> 1/k$, where $k$ is the transverse momentum of a dipole, the Langevin equation reduces in momentum space to directed percolation. This reduced Langevin equation, however, cannot be used to sum Pomeron loops, since such a summation requires integration over all impact parameters.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The AlPdMn quasicrystal approximants xi, xi', and xi'_n of the 1.6 nm decagonal phase and R, T, and T_n of the 1.2 nm decagonal phase can be viewed as arrangements of cluster columns on two-dimensional tilings. We substitute the tiles by Penrose rhombs and show, that alternative tilings can be constructed by a simple cut and projection formalism in three dimensional hyperspace.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes an experimental setup for probing Luttinger liquid behavior in quantum wires, using a quantum dot coupled to Luttinger liquid leads as an embedded probe. In one-dimensional correlated electron systems, several observables exhibit power-law scaling with exponents determined by the interaction-dependent parameter K. The setup enables K to be extracted from resonant transport through a dot placed in the wire, providing one of two independent measurements.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"An orthogonal complex structure on a domain in R^4 is a complex structure which is integrable and is compatible with the Euclidean metric. This gives rise to a first order system of partial differential equations which is conformally invariant. We prove two Liouville-type uniqueness theorems for solutions of this system, and use these to give an alternative proof of the classification of compact locally conformally flat Hermitian surfaces first proved by Pontecorvo. We also give a classification of non-degenerate quadrics in CP^3 under the action of the conformal group. Using this classification, we show that generic quadrics give rise to orthogonal complex structures defined on the complement of unknotted solid tori which are smoothly embedded in R^4.\", \"sampled\": \"This study presents what is described as the first examination of turbulent flow without strong mean shear near the interface between turbulent and non-turbulent flow in a Newtonian fluid, emphasizing small-scale aspects. Three-dimensional particle tracking (3D-PTV) was used to measure velocity derivatives in a Lagrangian manner, and direct numerical simulation (DNS) was also employed.  The two methods were used together to investigate interface dynamics. The three regions were the turbulent region (A), intermediate region (B), and non-turbulent region (C). Comparing flow properties across these three regions allowed direct observation of the key physical processes underlying the entrainment phenomenon near the interface. Striking differences were observed between small-scale strain and enstrophy in the vicinity of the interface.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The Katz-Sarnak density conjecture states that, in the limit as the conductors tend to infinity, the behavior of normalized zeros near the central point of families of L-functions agree with the N -> oo scaling limits of eigenvalues near 1 of subgroups of U(N). Evidence for this has been found for many families by studying the n-level densities; for suitably restricted test functions the main terms agree with random matrix theory. In particular, all one-parameter families of elliptic curves with rank r over Q(T) and the same distribution of signs of functional equations have the same limiting behavior. We break this universality and find family dependent lower order correction terms in many cases; these lower order terms have applications ranging from excess rank to modeling the behavior of zeros near the central point, and depend on the arithmetic of the family. We derive an alternate form of the explicit formula for GL(2) L-functions which simplifies comparisons, replacing sums over powers of Satake parameters by sums of the moments of the Fourier coefficients lambda_f(p). Our formula highlights the differences that we expect to exist from families whose Fourier coefficients obey different laws (for example, we expect Sato-Tate to hold only for non-CM families of elliptic curves). Further, by the work of Rosen and Silverman we expect lower order biases to the Fourier coefficients in families of elliptic curves with rank over Q(T); these biases can be seen in our expansions. We analyze several families of elliptic curves and see different lower order corrections, depending on whether or not the family has complex multiplication, a forced torsion point, or non-zero rank over Q(T).\", \"sampled\": \"This study examines the interplay between condensates in relativistic four-fermion interaction models. Relativistic effective potentials in mean field approximation are applied across 2D, 3D, and 4D models. The models involve two flavor and N_c color massless fermions subject to four-fermion interactions. Condensate interplay between the quark-antiquark and diquark channels is analyzed systematically. The ratio G_S/H_S governs the 2D and 4D cases, while G_S/H_P governs the 3D case. Only pure quark-antiquark condensates occupy the ground state when the relevant coupling ratio exceeds 2/N_c. This critical value 2/N_c reflects the ratio of color numbers entering the diquark and quark-antiquark condensates. Its physical origin is transparent. When G_S/H_S or G_S/H_P decreases below 2/N_c, differences among the models become apparent in the phase structure. The behavior of the diquark condensate phase depends on both the model dimension and the value of N_c. In the 3D case, a coexistence phase of both condensates may or may not appear alongside the pure diquark phase. Whether coexistence occurs is determined by the interplay between N_c and the pseudoscalar diquark coupling H_P. Phase diagrams in the G_S-H_S or G_S-H_P parameter space are constructed. These diagrams map the boundaries between pure quark-antiquark condensate phases and other phases. They make the coupling-ratio dependence explicit across dimensions. The 4D model yields results particularly relevant to two-flavor QCD-analogous systems with N_c colors. The findings imply a real constraint on the two-flavor QCD-analogous Nambu-Jona-Lasinio model regarding its coupling constants. Scalar coupling G_S and diquark coupling H_S must satisfy specific ratio conditions. Violations alter the ground state condensate structure.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The usual procedure of including a finite number of vertices in Non Perturbative Renormalization Group equations in order to obtain $n$-point correlation functions at finite momenta is analyzed. This is done by exploiting a general method recently introduced which includes simultaneously all vertices although approximating their momentum dependence. The study is performed using the self-energy of the tridimensional scalar model at criticality. At least in this example, low order truncations miss quantities as the critical exponent $\\\\eta$ by as much as 60%. However, if one goes to high order truncations the procedure seems to converge rapidly.\", \"sampled\": \"Transport in metals with a strongly anisotropic single-particle spectrum is examined within a theoretical framework. Coherent band transport in all directions is described by the standard Boltzmann equation. The Boltzmann equation applies across elastic and inelastic scattering regimes. The condition E_F*tau >> 1 must hold. Coherent band transport withstands both elastic and inelastic scattering under that condition. A separate tunneling mechanism is considered. Phonon-assisted tunneling via resonant states between layers is proposed. Resonant states serve as intermediate sites facilitating interlayer carrier transfer and this process is phonon-assisted.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The ancient unsolved problem of congruent numbers has been reduced to one of the major questions of contemporary arithmetic: the finiteness of the number of curves over $\\\\bf Q$ which become isomorphic at every place to a given curve. We give an elementary introduction to congruent numbers and their conjectural characterisation, discuss local-to-global issues leading to the finiteness problem, and list a few results and conjectures in the arithmetic theory of elliptic curves.\", \"sampled\": \"This 4-page study investigates the topological phase arising from the connectedness of the SO(3) group. The investigation is framed in terms of maximally entangled states within the SO(3) representation framework. Polarization and spatial mode transformations of a laser beam,  carrying orbital angular momentum provide the experimental basis. The topological phase is evidenced through interferometric measurements. A quantitative relationship between the concurrence and the fringes visibility is derived from these measurements.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We prove that a number of classes of separable unital C*-algebras are closed under crossed products by finite group actions with the Rokhlin property, including: (1) AI algebras, AT algebras, and related classes characterized by direct limit decompositions using semiprojective building blocks. (2) Simple unital AH algebras with slow dimension growth and real rank zero. (3) C*-algebras with real rank zero or stable rank one. (4) C*-algebras whose quotients all satisfy the Universal Coefficient Theorem. Along the way, we give a systematic treatment of the derivation of direct limit decompositions from local approximation conditions by homomorphic images which are not necessarily injective.\", \"sampled\": \"Entanglement between a spin and its environment is consistently enhanced at the quantum phase transition in impurity systems. This enhancement produces a visible cusp, corresponding to a maximum, in the entropy of entanglement. Specifically observed. The spin-boson model describes a two level system coupled to a subohmic bosonic bath with power-law spectral density. Impurity systems can exhibit a second-order quantum phase transition. The spectral density of the bosonic bath follows J (omega) proportional to omega^s, where 0 < s < 1. Wilson's Numerical Renormalization Group method was combined with hyperscaling relations in the analysis.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We present a general numerical scheme for the practical implementation of statistical moment closures suitable for modeling complex, large-scale, nonlinear systems. Building on recently developed equation-free methods, this approach numerically integrates the closure dynamics, the equations of which may not even be available in closed form. Although closure dynamics introduce statistical assumptions of unknown validity, they can have significant computational advantages as they typically have fewer degrees of freedom and may be much less stiff than the original detailed model. The closure method can in principle be applied to a wide class of nonlinear problems, including strongly-coupled systems (either deterministic or stochastic) for which there may be no scale separation. We demonstrate the equation-free approach for implementing entropy-based Eyink-Levermore closures on a nonlinear stochastic partial differential equation.\", \"sampled\": \"A computational first-principles study examined positron trapping at vacancy defects in metals and semiconductors. The study addresses the energetics of positron trapping and the interplay between the positron state and ionic structure. Ion relaxation matters. For vacancies in covalent semiconductors, it is a crucial part of the trapping process enabling localization. Ion relaxation allows the positron state to localize within the vacancy's ionic environment in covalent semiconductors. Positron trapping does not strongly affect characteristic features of the electronic structure in covalent semiconductors. Ionization levels change only moderately. The annihilation characteristics of the trapped positron state are examined as a central component of the study. Positron-induced ion relaxation has a noticeable effect on the calculated lifetime in metal vacancies.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The standard cosmological model is based on general relativity and includes dark matter and dark energy. An important prediction of this model is a fixed relationship between the gravitational potentials responsible for gravitational lensing and the matter overdensity. Alternative theories of gravity often make different predictions for this relationship. We propose a set of measurements which can test the lensing/matter relationship, thereby distinguishing between dark energy/matter models and models in which gravity differs from general relativity. Planned optical, infrared and radio galaxy and lensing surveys will be able to measure $E_G$, an observational quantity whose expectation value is equal to the ratio of the Laplacian of the Newtonian potentials to the peculiar velocity divergence, to percent accuracy. We show that this will easily separate alternatives such as $\\\\Lambda$CDM, DGP, TeVeS and $f(R)$ gravity.\", \"sampled\": \"MHD load and MHD metage are introduced as quantities defined within magnetofluids. These concepts are constructed in analogy with load and metage in hydrodynamics. The analogy is carried through carefully to preserve the relevant structural properties. The magnetic field in MHD can then be written in Clebsch's form. MHD load and MHD metage together enable this Clebsch representation. The representation provides geometrically meaningful description. These two quantities also support derivation of the magnetic analogue of Ertel's theorem. This analogue extends a classical result into the MHD setting. The magnetic analogue of Ertel's theorem follows directly from the defined quantities. When the magnetic field in a magnetofluid has non-trivial topology, additional structure becomes apparent. Specifically, the linking number of magnetic field lines can be associated with invariant MHD loads.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The secure and robust functioning of a network relies on the defect-free implementation of network applications. As network protocols have become increasingly complex, however, hand-writing network message processing code has become increasingly error-prone. In this paper, we present a domain-specific language, Zebu, for describing protocol message formats and related processing constraints. From a Zebu specification, a compiler automatically generates stubs to be used by an application to parse network messages. Zebu is easy to use, as it builds on notations used in RFCs to describe protocol grammars. Zebu is also efficient, as the memory usage is tailored to application needs and message fragments can be specified to be processed on demand. Finally, Zebu-based applications are robust, as the Zebu compiler automatically checks specification consistency and generates parsing stubs that include validation of the message structure. Using a mutation analysis in the context of SIP and RTSP, we show that Zebu significantly improves application robustness.\", \"sampled\": \"Pion propagation in asymmetric nuclear matter is studied, including effects of the Dirac sea. Mode splitting occurs for differently charged pion states. This splitting arises specifically within asymmetric nuclear matter. Pion-nucleon dynamics are first described using a non-chiral model based on pseudoscalar coupling. Pseudoscalar coupling is the starting point. The pseudovector representation is then obtained via non-linear field transformations. Dirac sea contributions are estimated separately for both the pseudoscalar and pseudovector cases results differ. Comparing the two representations clarifies how short-distance effects enter pion self-energies. A chiral effective Lagrangian is also employed, where Dirac vacuum contributions are absorbed by redefining field parameters. This follows a modern effective field theory approach. Closed-form analytical results are derived for effective pion masses and dispersion relations in asymmetric nuclear matter. These are compact. The hard nucleon loop approximation and a suitable density expansion are used to obtain these analytical results.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The dynamics of many socioeconomic systems is determined by the decision making process of agents. The decision process depends on agent's characteristics, such as preferences, risk aversion, behavioral biases, etc.. In addition, in some systems the size of agents can be highly heterogeneous leading to very different impacts of agents on the system dynamics. The large size of some agents poses challenging problems to agents who want to control their impact, either by forcing the system in a given direction or by hiding their intentionality. Here we consider the financial market as a model system, and we study empirically how agents strategically adjust the properties of large orders in order to meet their preference and minimize their impact. We quantify this strategic behavior by detecting scaling relations of allometric nature between the variables characterizing the trading activity of different institutions. We observe power law distributions in the investment time horizon, in the number of transactions needed to execute a large order and in the traded value exchanged by large institutions and we show that heterogeneity of agents is a key ingredient for the emergence of some aggregate properties characterizing this complex system.\", \"sampled\": \"Standard game theory assumes the game structure is common knowledge among all players. This assumption is often violated. Agents may lack awareness of the complete game structure. The paper relaxes the common knowledge assumption in the context of extensive games. Players may be unaware of moves themselves and other agents are able to make. The game is represented from the point of view of every agent at every node of the game tree. This matters such a representation captures heterogeneous awareness across agents without requiring a shared global structure. The authors provide a generalization of Nash equilibrium suited to extensive games with awareness. Every game with awareness has a generalized Nash equilibrium under this framework. This existence result anchors the solution concept in a well-defined equilibrium guarantee. The framework is extended to games with awareness of unawareness, where player i may recognize that player j can make moves i cannot perceive. This broadens scope. Subjective games are also addressed within the extended framework developed here. In subjective games, players may hold beliefs that are incompatible with any common prior. No common knowledge regarding the actual game need exist.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We present non-LTE diagnostics of the filament observed by SOHO/SUMER on May 27, 2005 in the whole Lyman series. The filament was situated close to the disk center. The Ly_alpha observations were carried out with normal voltage of detector A. The slit was placed at the central part of the detector -- outside the Ly_alpha attenuator. Therefore, the observed profiles of this line could be calibrated reliably.\", \"sampled\": \"Erwin Schrodinger proved that quantum wave functions coevolve with the curved spacetime of the Friedmann universe in 1939. The derivation was published in 1939 across 8 pages. It accounts for energy changes of moving particles within the Friedmann universe and Hubble redshift. Schrodinger's derivation explains the Hubble redshift of photons propagating through an expanding universe and Atoms too.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I. Caprini's, G. Colangelo's, and H. Leutwyler's (CCL) article \\\"Mass and Width of the Lowest Resonance in QCD\\\", Phys. Rev. Lett. 96, 132001 (2006) [hep-ph/0512364], is critically reviewed. The present comment is devoted to complement a recent experimental discussion (D.V. Bugg, J. Phys. G 34, 151 (2007) [hep-ph/0608081]) of short-comings in the CCL analysis, by presenting theoretical arguments pointing at a serious flaw in the theoretical formalism used by CCL, and also at the unlikeliness of their tiny error bars in the sigma-meson mass and width. The criticism made in the comment applies analogously to the analysis on the kappa-meson mass performed in the article \\\"The K0*(800) scalar resonance from Roy-Steiner representations of pi K scattering\\\" published as S. Descotes-Genon and B. Moussallam, Eur. Phys. J. C 48, 553 (2006) [hep-ph/0607133].\", \"sampled\": \"High resolution infrared measurements using adaptive optics on 10m telescopes determined 3D orbits of stars within ten light hours of the Galactic Center compact radio source. These observations demonstrate a three million solar mass black hole in Sagittarius A* beyond reasonable doubt. The Galactic Center is the best astrophysical evidence for the existence of black holes. It serves as an ideal laboratory for studying physics near a black hole. Young massive stars are present in the innermost stellar cusp were likely formed there. Variable infrared and X-ray emission from Sagittarius A* probes physical processes and space-time curvature outside the event horizon, providing a direct window into its immediate environment. The work was presented at IAU Symposium No. 238.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We critically examine issues associated with determining the fundamental properties of the black hole and the surrounding accretion disk in an X-ray binary based on modeling the disk X-ray continuum of the source. We base our work mainly on two XMM-Newton observations of GX 339-4, because they provided high-quality data at low energies (below 1 keV) which are critical for reliably modeling the spectrum of the accretion disk. A key issue examined is the determination of the so-called \\\"color correction factor\\\", which is often empirically introduced to account for the deviation of the local disk spectrum from a blackbody (due to electron scattering). This factor cannot be pre-determined theoretically because it may vary with, e.g., mass accretion rate, among a number of important factors. We follow up on an earlier suggestion to estimate the color correction observationally by modeling the disk spectrum with saturated Compton scattering. We show that the spectra can be fitted well and the approach yields reasonable values for the color correction factor. For comparison, we have also attempted to fit the spectra with other models.\", \"sampled\": \"Gravitational heating, originating from the gravitational potential energy released by gas stripped from infalling satellites, is incorporated into semi-analytic models of galaxy formation to heat the intra-cluster medium and suppress cooling flows alongside other feedback channels. It prevents cooling in dark matter halos more massive than roughly 10^13 solar masses. Energy injection by gravitational heating can rival AGN feedback in massive galaxies and exceed it in the most massive systems. While Peak AGN activity occurs before gravitational heating becomes important. Gravitational heating gains prominence at late cosmic times. The effect is strongly mass dependent that Its magnitude scales with halo mass. Hydrodynamical simulations motivate the inclusion of this heating channel. Semi-analytic models capture the process through gas stripping from infalling satellites. The mass dependency and temporal behaviour of gravitational heating help reproduce observed downsizing. Star-formation rate suppression in massive galaxies is recovered. The models show late-time quenching. Gravitational heating drives this evolutionary pattern. The intra-cluster medium response shapes galaxy luminosities, providing a viable alternative to purely AGN-driven quenching. Its inclusion refines semi-analytic predictions. The framework aligns with key observational trends.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We discuss the findings of a comprehensive imaging and spectroscopic survey of the optical emission associated with the supernova remnant 3C 58 (Fesen et al. 2007) as they relate to the topic of pre-SN mass loss. Spectroscopically measured radial velocities of ~450 emission knots within the remnant show two distinct kinematic populations of optical knots: a high-velocity group with radial velocities in the range of 700 - 1100 km/s and a lower velocity group exhibiting radial expansion velocities below ~250 km/s. We interpret the high-velocity knots as ejecta from the SN explosion and the low-velocity knots as shocked circumstellar material likely resulting from pre-SN mass loss. The chemical signatures of the two populations also show marked differences. The high velocity group includes a substantial number of knots with notably higher [N II]/H-alpha ratios not seen in the lower velocity population, suggesting greater nitrogen enrichment in the SN ejecta than in the CSM. These results are compared with evidence for pre-SN mass loss in the Crab Nebula, perhaps the SNR most similar to 3C 58. These SNRs may comprise two case studies of pre-SN mass loss in relatively low mass (~8 - 10 solar masses) core-collapse SN progenitors.\", \"sampled\": \"A space-based gravitational microlensing survey will provide a statistical census of exoplanets. The survey covers planet masses down to 0.1 Earth-masses and orbital distance from 0.5 AU to infinity. including analogs to all Solar System planets except Mercury. Most planet types predicted by planet formation theories are represented. Results on planet frequency are provided around all star types except those with short lifetimes. Close-in planets with separations less than 0.5 AU are invisible to the survey. Kepler can discovery close-in planets it misses. Ground-based microlensing cannot match the comprehensive statistics on mass and semi-major axis distribution. Ground-based terrestrial planet sensitivity is limited to the Einstein radius vicinity at 2-3 AU. A space-based platform overcomes this restriction. Space-based imaging is needed to identify and determine the mass of planetary host stars for most microlensing discoveries. The proposed mission is called the Microlensing Planet Finder. MPF operates within the NASA Discovery Program cost cap. The exploration delivers a statistical census reaching 0.1 Earth-masses across a wide separation range. It captures analogs for every Solar System planet except Mercury and includes most predicted planet types.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Close pre-main-sequence binary stars are expected to clear central holes in their protoplanetary disks, but the extent to which material can flow from the circumbinary disk across the gap onto the individual circumstellar disks has been unclear. In binaries with eccentric orbits, periodic perturbation of the outer disk is predicted to induce mass flow across the gap, resulting in accretion that varies with the binary period. This accretion may manifest itself observationally as periodic changes in luminosity. Here we present a search for such periodic accretion in the pre-main-sequence spectroscopic binary UZ Tau E. We present BVRI photometry spanning three years; we find that the brightness of UZ Tau E is clearly periodic, with a best-fit period of 19.16 +/- 0.04 days. This is consistent with the spectroscopic binary period of 19.13 days, refined here from analysis of new and existing radial velocity data. The brightness of UZ Tau E shows significant random variability, but the overall periodic pattern is a broad peak in enhanced brightness, spanning more than half the binary orbital period. The variability of the H-alpha line is not as clearly periodic, but given the sparseness of the data, some periodic component is not ruled out. The photometric variations are in good agreement with predictions from simulations of binaries with orbital parameters similar to those of UZ Tau E, suggesting that periodic accretion does occur from circumbinary disks, replenishing the inner disks and possibly extending the timescale over which they might form planets.\", \"sampled\": \"HST/ACS photometry reveals a double main sequence turn-off in the star cluster NGC 1846. The main sequence, sub-giant branch, and red giant branch are narrow and well-defined. The red clump is compact and clearly identifiable in the color-magnitude diagram. The two sets of turn-off stars may exhibit different central concentrations and asymmetries. Isochrone fitting indicates two stellar populations with equivalent metal abundance. The two populations differ in age by approximately 300 Myr. Absolute ages are ~1.9 Gyr and ~2.2 Gyr. (subject to a systematic error of up to ±0.4 Gyr). Metal abundance from isochrone fitting is [M/H] ~ -0.40. Spectroscopic measurements of [Fe/H] are consistent with the isochrone-based metallicity. The agreement supports the isochrone fitting results. The cluster may have formed via the tidal capture of two star clusters from a single giant molecular cloud. This tidal capture scenario accounts for the age difference. It also explains the uniform metallicity and spatial distribution differences. The double main sequence turn-off is a rare feature in intermediate-age clusters. It provides direct evidence of multiple stellar populations. The narrow evolutionary sequences indicate low photometric scatter. This precision strengthens the age and metallicity determinations. The compact red clump morphology further constrains the helium abundance. It also rules out significant differential reddening across the cluster.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In this paper we study the Metropolis algorithm in connection with two mean--field spin systems, the so called mean--field Ising model and the Blume--Emery--Griffiths model. In both this examples the naive choice of proposal chain gives rise, for some parameters, to a slowly mixing Metropolis chain, that is a chain whose spectral gap decreases exponentially fast (in the dimension $N$ of the problem). Here we show how a slight variant in the proposal chain can avoid this problem, keeping the mean computational cost similar to the cost of the usual Metropolis. More precisely we prove that, with a suitable variant in the proposal, the Metropolis chain has a spectral gap which decreases polynomially in 1/N. Using some symmetry structure of the energy, the method rests on allowing appropriate jumps within the energy level of the starting state.\", \"sampled\": \"The Josephson effect in D/DN/I/DN/D junctions is examined through a theoretical framework focusing on practical high-T_C cuprate superconductor junctions as its primary target system. In such practical junctions, the I_C R product falls far below standard theory predictions. The study compares D/DN/I/DN/D junctions with d-wave superconductor / insulator / d-wave superconductor junctions. In the D/DN/I/DN/D configuration, the I_C R product can be much smaller than in the d-wave/insulator/d-wave counterpart. The diffusive normal metal interlayers and insulator barrier alter the critical current-normal state resistance product. The resulting suppression is quantitatively distinct from simpler junction geometries. The standard theory baseline fails to capture the observed reduction in practical high-T_C cuprate junctions.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The effective potential analysis indicates that, in a 3D two-flavor Gross-Neveu model in vacuum, depending on less or bigger than the critical value 2/3 of $G_S/H_P$, where $G_S$ and $H_P$ are respectively the coupling constants of scalar quark-antiquark channel and pseudoscalar diquark channel, the system will have the ground state with pure diquark condensates or with pure quark-antiquark condensates, but no the one with coexistence of the two forms of condensates. The similarities and differences in the interplay between the quark-antiquark and the diquark condensates in vacuum in the 2D, 3D and 4D two-flavor four-fermion interaction models are summarized.\", \"sampled\": \"Dimension six operators containing fermion fields are examined in the context of Higgs boson production. The study focuses on Higgs production at a 500 GeV or 1 TeV e+e- linear collider. Effects of these fermionic operators can be kinematically enhanced relative to Standard Model contributions, with the enhancement depending on the Higgs production mechanism. Existing precision electroweak measurements constrain the operator coefficients. The scale of neutrino mass also constrains the operator coefficients. Substantial deviations from Standard Model Higgs production cross-sections remain possible despite these constraints. Fermionic operator effects are compared with those of purely bosonic operators.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We have recently pointed out that color magnetic field is generated in dense quark matter, i.e. color ferromagnetic phase of quark matter. Using light cone quantization, we show that gluons occupying the lowest Landau level under the color magnetic field effectively form a two dimensional quantum well (layer), in which infinitely many zero modes of the gluons are present. We discuss that the zero modes of the gluons form a quantum Hall state by interacting repulsively with each other, just as electrons do in semiconductors.\", \"sampled\": \"We formulate the field equations for a stationary axially symmetric rigidly rotating dust source. The general solution depends on an elliptic equation and harmonic functions. This structure governs the entire class of solutions. An asymptotically flat solution is constructed starting from the dipole Bonnor solution. It has two curvature singularities on the rotational axis. The mass diverges at the two point singularities on the axis. The metric is regular everywhere except at these points. A non-asymptotically flat solution with NUT charge is studied. It contains a massless ring singularity. It also has a well-defined mass-energy expression.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes a method to address the longstanding problem of lack of monotonicity in estimation of conditional and structural quantile functions, also known as the quantile crossing problem. The method consists in sorting or monotone rearranging the original estimated non-monotone curve into a monotone rearranged curve. We show that the rearranged curve is closer to the true quantile curve in finite samples than the original curve, establish a functional delta method for rearrangement-related operators, and derive functional limit theory for the entire rearranged curve and its functionals. We also establish validity of the bootstrap for estimating the limit law of the the entire rearranged curve and its functionals. Our limit results are generic in that they apply to every estimator of a monotone econometric function, provided that the estimator satisfies a functional central limit theorem and the function satisfies some smoothness conditions. Consequently, our results apply to estimation of other econometric functions with monotonicity restrictions, such as demand, production, distribution, and structural distribution functions. We illustrate the results with an application to estimation of structural quantile functions using data on Vietnam veteran status and earnings.\", \"sampled\": \"The two-dimensional kinetic Ising model Driven by a square-wave applied field displays a nonequilibrium second-order dynamic phase transition where  period-averaged magnetization serves as parameter Q. This transition belongs to the same universality class as the zero-field equilibrium counterpart. The scaling of Q with respect to a nonzero period-averaged magnetic bias field H_b is studied for the first time. H_b is a significant component of the field conjugate to Q. The scaling exponent δ_d of H_b at the critical period equals the equilibrium critical isotherm exponent δ_e. This conjugate-field interpretation is further supported by a finite-size scaling analysis of Q above the critical period. A fluctuation-dissipation relation holds for Q and H_b in the critical region. The effective temperature T_eff depends on the period, and possibly on temperature and field amplitude. The fluctuation-dissipation relation justifies using the scaled variance of Q as a proxy for the nonequilibrium susceptibility.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We assess the practicality of random network coding by illuminating the issue of overhead and considering it in conjunction with increasingly long packets sent over the erasure channel. We show that the transmission of increasingly long packets, consisting of either of an increasing number of symbols per packet or an increasing symbol alphabet size, results in a data rate approaching zero over the erasure channel. This result is due to an erasure probability that increases with packet length. Numerical results for a particular modulation scheme demonstrate a data rate of approximately zero for a large, but finite-length packet. Our results suggest a reduction in the performance gains offered by random network coding.\", \"sampled\": \"This study examines integrable self-adjoint schemes on both triangular (7-point) and honeycomb lattices. The analysis employs the sublattice approach to maintain structural compatibility, with the connection between the two systems formalized via a star-triangle relation. Darboux transformations for both linear problems are derived from the Moutard transformation of the B-quadrilateral lattice. The study further provides a clear geometric interpretation of the Laplace transformations for the self-adjoint 7-point scheme.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A time series delta(n), the fluctuation of the nth unfolded eigenvalue was recently characterized for the classical Gaussian ensembles of NxN random matrices (GOE, GUE, GSE). It is investigated here for the beta-Hermite ensemble as a function of beta (zero or positive) by Monte Carlo simulations. The fluctuation of delta(n) and the autocorrelation function vary logarithmically with n for any beta>0 (1<<n<<N). The simple logarithmic behavior reported for the higher-order moments of delta(n) for the GOE (beta=1) and the GUE (beta=2) is valid for any positive beta and is accounted for by Gaussian distributions whose variances depend linearly on ln(n). The 1/f noise previously demonstrated for delta(n) series of the three Gaussian ensembles, is characterized by wavelet analysis both as a function of beta and of N. When beta decreases from 1 to 0, for a given and large enough N, the evolution from a 1/f noise at beta=1 to a 1/f^2 noise at beta=0 is heterogeneous with a ~1/f^2 noise at the finest scales and a ~1/f noise at the coarsest ones. The range of scales in which a ~1/f^2 noise predominates grows progressively when beta decreases. Asymptotically, a 1/f^2 noise is found for beta=0 while a 1/f noise is the rule for beta positive.\", \"sampled\": \"The distance to the massive stellar cluster Westerlund 1 is determined through a method that does not rely on stellar population characteristics. HI observations from the Southern Galactic Plane Survey form the basis of this analysis. A new Galactic rotation curve was derived. It uses a Galactocentric radius of 7.6 kpc and a solar orbital velocity of 214 km s⁻¹. This curve yields a distance to Westerlund 1 of 3.9 ± 0.7 kpc. The cluster therefore resides in the Scutum-Crux Arm. Possible error sources affecting the Galactic rotation curve were investigated. Kinematic distances to nearby HII regions and supernova remnants were re-calculated with the new rotation curve. The revised values align with the cluster’s placement. A large interstellar bubble around Westerlund 1 has a diameter of about 50 pc. This bubble was created by cluster members during the early stages of Wd 1. Its minimum dynamic age is 2.5 million years. This age is similar to the age of the cluster itself. Small expanding HI features are also detected around the cluster. These features have a maximum dynamic age of 600,000 years.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"From first-principles calculations, we predict that transition metal (TM) atom doped silicon nanowires have a half-metallic ground state. They are insulators for one spin-direction, but show metallic properties for the opposite spin direction. At high coverage of TM atoms, ferromagnetic silicon nanowires become metallic for both spin-directions with high magnetic moment and may have also significant spin-polarization at the Fermi level. The spin-dependent electronic properties can be engineered by changing the type of dopant TM atoms, as well as the diameter of the nanowire. Present results are not only of scientific interest, but can also initiate new research on spintronic applications of silicon nanowires.\", \"sampled\": \"A commentary was written on papers authored by S.P. Anjali Devi and R. Kandasamy. The papers were concluded to be of very low quality. This commentary addresses six of their papers published in several journals. One journal was ZAMM, another was Heat and Mass Transfer and other journals included Mechanics Research Communications. Another publication was the International Communications in Heat and Mass Transfer. The papers also appeared in Communications in Numerical Methods in Engineering. The Journal of Computational and Applied Mechanics also published the critiqued works. The six papers by S.P. Anjali Devi and R. were written without care.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We study semiparametric efficiency bounds and efficient estimation of parameters defined through general moment restrictions with missing data. Identification relies on auxiliary data containing information about the distribution of the missing variables conditional on proxy variables that are observed in both the primary and the auxiliary database, when such distribution is common to the two data sets. The auxiliary sample can be independent of the primary sample, or can be a subset of it. For both cases, we derive bounds when the probability of missing data given the proxy variables is unknown, or known, or belongs to a correctly specified parametric family. We find that the conditional probability is not ancillary when the two samples are independent. For all cases, we discuss efficient semiparametric estimators. An estimator based on a conditional expectation projection is shown to require milder regularity conditions than one based on inverse probability weighting.\", \"sampled\": \"Electron-electron interactions in a clean 2D electron gas produce a correction to magnetoconductivity. This correction to the AC magnetoconductivity, δσ^int(ω), is periodic with the inverse cyclotron frequency. The correction is also periodic with ω^(3/2), unlike conventional cyclotron resonance harmonics. Oscillations in δσ^int(ω) emerge at low magnetic fields where ω_c is much smaller than ω. These are caused by the double backscattering of an electron from impurity-induced Friedel oscillations. The two backscattering events are separated by a time of approximately ω^-1. The electron moves through only a small part of its Larmour circle. This phenomenon appears when disorder suppresses the conventional cyclotron harmonics. During the interval between backscattering events, the electron's path covers a small fraction of its Larmour circle. The mechanism involves an electron scattering twice.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This is the third of a series of papers on a new equivariant cohomology that takes values in a vertex algebra, and contains and generalizes the classical equivariant cohomology of a manifold with a Lie group action a la H. Cartan. In this paper, we compute this cohomology for spheres and show that for any simple connected group G, there is a sphere with infinitely many actions of G which have distinct chiral equivariant cohomology, but identical classical equivariant cohomology. Unlike the classical case, the description of the chiral equivariant cohomology of spheres requires a substantial amount of new structural theory, which we fully develop in this paper. This includes a quasi-conformal structure, equivariant homotopy invariance, and the values of this cohomology on homogeneous spaces. These results rely on crucial features of the underlying vertex algebra valued complex that have no classical analogues.\", \"sampled\": \"A significant debate exists regarding the possibility of a single photon exhibiting nonlocality, a subject of extensive research and discussion. This paper presents an experimentally feasible scheme to overcome objections to previous proposals. Multiple schemes were proposed but they faced opposition. Critics objected. Objections to these previously proposed schemes centered on their reliance on unobservable initial states. Critics claimed the use of such states made the experiments fundamentally impossible to perform. The techniques required for photons are also applicable to atoms. These techniques could potentially be used to verify particle nonlocality. The scheme might verify the nonlocality of a single massive particle. Researchers have proposed a new scheme. It is experimentally feasible and overcomes prior objections. This could potentially be used to verify the nonlocality of a single massive particle.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The equilibration of macroscopic degrees of freedom during the fusion of heavy nuclei, like the charge and the shape, are studied in the Time-Dependent Hartree-Fock theory. The pre-equilibrium Giant Dipole Resonance (GDR) is used to probe the fusion path. It is shown that such isovector collective state is excited in N/Z asymmetric fusion and to a less extent in mass asymmetric systems. The characteristics of this GDR are governed by the structure of the fused system in its preequilibrium phase, like its deformation, rotation and vibration. In particular, we show that a lowering of the pre-equilibrium GDR energy is expected as compared to the statistical one. Revisiting experimental data, we extract an evidence of this lowering for the first time. We also quantify the fusion-evaporation enhancement due to gamma-ray emission from the pre-equilibrium GDR. This cooling mechanism along the fusion path may be suitable to synthesize in the future super heavy elements using radioactive beams with strong N/Z asymmetries in the entrance channel.\", \"sampled\": \"A complete thermodynamical analysis of the 3 dimensional massless Gross-Neveu model has been performed. The analysis utilized the optimized perturbation theory, which is a non-perturbative method for these calculations. It enables results beyond the known large-N findings, even at the lowest order. The study's results are valid for a finite number of fermion species, denoted as N. A tricritical point was found to exist in the temperature and chemical potential phase diagram of the model. The location of the tricritical point for the discrete chiral phase transition was precisely determined. The phase diagram was also studied in the pressure and inverse density plane. It revealed the existence of a liquid-gas phase in the model, which was previously unknown. N-dependent analytical expressions were derived for the fermionic mass and critical temperature. The study also derived N-dependent analytical expressions for the critical chemical potential of the system.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We consider the sum rule proposed by one of us (SLA), obtained by taking the expectation value of an axial vector commutator in a state with one pion. The sum rule relates the pion decay constant to integrals of pion-pion cross sections, with one pion off the mass shell. We remark that recent data on pion-pion scattering allow a precise evaluation of the sum rule. We also discuss the related Adler--Weisberger sum rule (obtained by taking the expectation value of the same commutator in a state with one nucleon), especially in connection with the problem of extrapolation of the pion momentum off its mass shell.\", \"sampled\": \"A new model, the glassy wormlike chain  is introduced for the dynamics of a wormlike chain in a rough free energy landscape. The model is derived from the common wormlike chain. This derivation involves an exponential stretching of the relaxation spectrum of the chain's long-wavelength eigenmodes. A single stretching parameter controls this exponential stretching. The model's predictions for observables exhibit soft glassy rheology characteristics. These observables include the dynamic structure factor. Model predictions align well with experimental data from reconstituted cytoskeletal networks and also align with data from live cells. The potential microscopic source of the stretching is considered. The authors consider implications for nonlinear rheology and the physiological relevance of their findings. These findings have relevance for biophysics research and more.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We develop a general theory of microtubule (MT) deformations by molecular motors generating internal force doublets within the MT lattice. We describe two basic internal excitations, the S and V shape, and compare them with experimental observations from literature. We explain the special role of tubulin vacancies and the dramatic deformation amplifying effect observed for katanin acting at positions of defects. Experimentally observed shapes are used to determine the ratio of MT shear and stretch moduli ($\\\\approx 6\\\\times10^{-5}$) and to estimate the forces induced in the MT lattice by katanin (10's of pN). For many motors acting on a single MT we derive expressions for the end-to-end distance reduction and provide criteria for dominance of this new effect over thermal fluctuations. We conclude that molecular motors if acting cooperatively can ''animate'' MTs from within the lattice and induce slack even without cross-bridging to other structures, a scenario very much reminiscent of the motor driven axoneme.\", \"sampled\": \"The insulating cuprate La1.95Sr0.05CuO4 exhibits Fincher-Burke-like spin excitations. These are also found in superconducting cuprates. This type of spin excitation is typically observed in materials classified as itinerant antiferromagnets. The local spectral function for this incommensurate insulating cuprate satisfies omega/T scaling above approximately 16 K. They support a general scaling prediction for certain physical systems. The prediction applies to square-lattice quantum spin S=1/2 systems. The width of incommensurate peaks was studied. This width scales to a finite value. This value is similar to the value observed at optimal doping. The similarity between the two systems suggests that both are a similar distance from a quantum critical point. This has further implications. Partial spin-glass freezing might limit both systems to a finite correlation length. This suggests that both systems are a similar distance from a quantum critical point. Partial spin-glass freezing might limit both systems. This would create a finite correlation length.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Trafficking of glutamate receptors into and out of synapses is critically involved in the plasticity of excitatory synaptic transmission. Endocytosis and exocytosis of receptors have initially been thought to account alone for this trafficking. However, membrane proteins also traffic through surface lateral diffusion in the plasma membrane. We describe developments in electrophysiological and optical approaches that have allowed for the real time measurement of glutamate receptor surface trafficking in live neurons. These include (i) specific imaging of surface receptors using a pH sensitive fluorescent protein, (ii) design of a photoactivable drug to inactivate locally surface receptors and monitor electrophysiologically their recovery, and (iii)application of single molecule fluorescence microscopy to directly track the movement of individual surface receptors with nanometer resolution inside and outside synapses. Altogether, these approaches have demonstrated that glutamate receptors diffuse at high rates in the neuronal membrane and suggest a key role for surface diffusion in the regulation of receptor numbers at synapses.\", \"sampled\": \"A comparative study was conducted on the CoO2 layers within Bi-misfit and NaxCoO2 cobaltates. Co NMR was used to measure the intrinsic susceptibility of the cobalt layers. Both materials exhibit large room-temperature thermopower at low doping levels, a key shared physical property in both materials. They display Curie-Weiss susceptibilities when observed under conditions of low doping. Strong correlations are proposed as a generic feature. This is central to cobaltate physics. This study used Co NMR and muSR techniques to probe the intrinsic magnetic properties. No evidence of charge or spin order was found in Bi-misfit cobaltates down to low temperatures. This is significant. The absence of such order in Bi-misfit cobaltates contrasts sharply with the case of NaxCoO2. This finding suggests that certain properties are not intrinsic to the CoO2 layers themselves. Metallicity is considered specific to the Na layers in NaxCoO2 cobaltates.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We propose that the CP violating phase in the CKM mixing matrix is identical to the CP phases responsible for the spontaneous CP violation in the Higgs potential. A specific multi-Higgs model with Peccei-Quinn (PQ) symmetry is constructed to realize this idea. The CP violating phase does not vanish when all Higgs masses become large. There are flavor changing neutral current (FCNC) interactions mediated by neutral Higgs bosons at the tree level. However, unlike general multi-Higgs models, the FCNC Yukawa couplings are fixed in terms of the quark masses and CKM mixing angles. Implications for meson-anti-meson mixing, including recent data on $D-\\\\bar D$ mixing, and neutron electric dipole moment (EDM) are studied. We find that the neutral Higgs boson masses can be at the order of one hundred GeV. The neutron EDM can be close to the present experimental upper bound.\", \"sampled\": \"The Suzaku observatory observed two nearby Seyfert 2 galaxies that were selected for their hard X-ray emissions above 10 keV. The PIN Hard X-ray Detector detected both of the sources up to several tens of keV, enabling good characterization. The broad band X-ray continuum for both galaxies was characterized. Both sources were found to be heavily obscured. One galaxy was NGC 5728. It was identified as being Compton thick. The two sources showed very different shapes and intensities of their continua. This was at lower energies. The scattered and reflected continuum varied between the two galaxies in shape and intensity. Strong iron K-alpha lines were detected. This was true in both galaxies, indications of a broad relativistic iron line were found in the galaxy NGC 4992.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We study the evolution of a neutral $K$ meson prepared as an incoherent equal mixture of $K^0$ and $\\\\bar{K^0}$. Denoting the density matrix by $\\\\rho(t) = {1/2} N(t) [\\\\1 + \\\\vec{\\\\zeta}(t) \\\\cdot \\\\vec{\\\\sigma} ] $, the norm of the state $N(t)$ is found to decrease monotonically from one to zero, while the magnitude of the Stokes vector $|\\\\vec{\\\\zeta}(t)|$ increases monotonically from zero to one. This property qualifies these observables as arrows of time. Requiring monotonic behaviour of $N(t)$ for arbitrary values of $\\\\gamma_L, \\\\gamma_S$ and $\\\\Delta m$ yields a bound on the CP-violating overlap $\\\\delta = \\\\braket{K_L}{K_S}$, which is similar to, but weaker than, the known unitarity bound. A similar requirement on $|\\\\vec{\\\\zeta}(t)|$ yields a new bound, $\\\\delta^2 < {1/2} (\\\\frac{\\\\Delta \\\\gamma}{\\\\Delta m}) \\\\sinh (\\\\frac{3\\\\pi}{4} \\\\frac{\\\\Delta \\\\gamma}{\\\\Delta m})$ which is particularly effective in limiting the CP-violating overlap in the $B^0$-$\\\\bar{B^0}$ system. We obtain the Stokes parameter $\\\\zeta_3(t)$ which shows how the average strangeness of the beam evolves from zero to $\\\\delta$. The evolution of the Stokes vector from $|\\\\vec{\\\\zeta}| = 0$ to $|\\\\vec{\\\\zeta}| = 1$ has a resemblance to an order parameter of a system undergoing spontaneous symmetry breaking.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper analyzes the principle of conservation of energy within a five-dimensional space-time framework to explain certain observed phenomena. The new estimation method is developed. It relies on cosmological parameters. The method provides an alternative for estimating an acceleration parameter, ar, which was previously derived in another research paper. The previous analysis restated orbital dynamics in five dimensions, agreeing with observed flat galaxy rotation curves. This earlier work also agreed with observations related to the Pioneer Anomaly. The previous analysis did not modify Newtonian dynamics but instead restated them within a five-dimensional context for analysis. This approach was used to examine the inverse square law of gravity. The paper suggests this law might result from energy conservation at a boundary between a four-dimensional universe and a fifth dimension. A new method is provided. It estimates the parameter ar without referencing the equations of orbital dynamics. The estimation of ar is based on cosmological constants and parameters, including the fundamental gravitational constant G. A limiting case for the Tulley-Fisher relationship is also suggested.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Consider a collection of random variables attached to the vertices of a graph. The reconstruction problem requires to estimate one of them given `far away' observations. Several theoretical results (and simple algorithms) are available when their joint probability distribution is Markov with respect to a tree. In this paper we consider the case of sequences of random graphs that converge locally to trees. In particular, we develop a sufficient condition for the tree and graph reconstruction problem to coincide. We apply such condition to colorings of random graphs. Further, we characterize the behavior of Ising models on such graphs, both with attractive and random interactions (respectively, `ferromagnetic' and `spin glass').\", \"sampled\": \"A compensation point was observed in the temperature-dependent magnetization data for the polycrystalline compound Sm2Al. This compound magnetically orders. The ordering occurs at approximately 150 K, and the compensation point shifts. This shift depends on the applied magnetic field. Hysteresis loops measured below the compensation temperature indicate that Sm2Al has exchange anisotropy. The material has a large field. This exchange anisotropy field is large. The coercive field of Sm2Al is also large. These fields are comparable to those of the ferromagnet (Sm,Gd)Al2. Heat capacity data suggests similarities between Sm2Al and the ferromagnet Al2. The data for this study was collected on polycrystalline Sm2Al.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In this paper we consider stationary force-free magnetosphere of an aligned\\nrotator when plasma in the open field line region rotates differentially due to\\npresence of a zone with the accelerating electric field in the polar cap of\\npulsar. We study the impact of differential rotation on the current density\\ndistribution in the magnetosphere. Using split-monopole approximation we obtain\\nanalytical expressions for physical parameters of differentially rotating\\nmagnetosphere. We find the range of admitted current density distributions\\nunder the requirement that the potential drop in the polar cap is less than the\\nvacuum potential drop. We show that the current density distribution could\\ndeviate significantly from the ``classical'' Michel distribution and could be\\nmade almost constant over the polar cap even when the potential drop in the\\naccelerating zone is of the order of 10 per cents of the vacuum potential drop.\\nWe argue that differential rotation of the open magnetic field lines could play\\nan important role in adjusting between the magnetosphere and the polar cap\\ncascade zone and could affect the value of pulsar breaking index.\", \"sampled\": \"Tasked with investigating the coupling between magnetic ordering and structural instabilities in perovskite, we have found that g-type antiferromagnetic ordering is the most stable with the B phase being dominant. The dependence of structural instabilities on magnetic ordering can be understood in terms of how super-exchange functions. Our research demonstrates how adequate buckling of Cr-O-Cr chains can favor ferromagnetism. Additionally, effective charges known as Born effective charges were calculated using the Berry phase expression yielding a value of 8 and 13.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The phenomenology of the low scale U(1)_{B-L} extension of the standard model\\nand its implications at LHC is presented. In this model, an extra gauge boson\\ncorresponding to B-L gauge symmetry and an extra SM singlet scalar (heavy\\nHiggs) are predicted. We show a detailed analysis of both heavy and light\\nHiggses decay and production in addition to the possible decay channels of the\\nnew gauge boson. We find that the cross sections of the SM-like Higgs\\nproduction are reduced by ~ 20%-30%, while its decay branching ratios remain\\nintact. The extra Higgs has relatively small cross sections and the branching\\nratios of Z'-> l^+ l^- are of order ~20% compared to ~ 3% of the SM resuls.\\nHence, the search for Z' is accessible via a clean dilepton signal at LHC.\", \"sampled\": \"The First Release COSMOS Optical and Near-IR Data and Catalog encompass data taken from Subaru 8.3m telescope, KPNO, and CTIO. Special techniques ensure relative photometric calibration better than 1, with absolute photometric accuracy from standard star measurements at 6. Absolute calibration is corrected using galaxy spectra providing accurate colors at 2. Stellar and galaxy colors and counts align well with expected values. Finally, the first step in scientific analysis of these data involves constructing panchromatic views emphasizing meticulous calibration and data analysis methodologies.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The classical Heisenberg Hamiltonian was solved for oriented spinel thin and\\nthick cubic ferrites. The dipole matrix of complicated cubic cell could be\\nsimplified into the form of dipole Matrix of simple cubic cells. This study was\\nconfined only to the highly oriented thin films of ferrite. The variation of\\ntotal energy of Nickel ferrite thin films with angle and number of layers was\\ninvestigated. Also the change of energy with stress induced anisotropy for\\nNickel ferrite films with N=5 and 1000 has been studied. Films with the\\nmagnetic moments ratio 1.86 can be easily oriented in 90 direction when N is\\ngreater than 400.\", \"sampled\": \"Tasked with demonstrating precise control over interferometer dynamics, this study by Stuckelberg et al explores the interferometric scheme's capabilities in providing new avenues for precision measurements of ultracold molecules. By passing two subsequent passages through a weak avoided crossing between different orbital categories, the researchers allow for a high degree of control over the interferometer dynamics. This approach enables a precise determination of the energy difference between two molecular states showcasing the potential for advancements in ultracold molecule research. The experiment showcases the potential of this technique with four Stuckelberg interferometry setups highlighting the importance of this method in the field.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Dissipation in the flexural dynamics of doubly clamped nanomechanical bar\\nresonators is investigated using molecular dynamics simulation. The dependence\\nof the quality factor Q on temperature and the size of the resonator is\\ncalculated from direct simulation of the oscillation of a series of Si \\nbars with bare {100} dimerized surfaces. The bar widths range from 3.3 to\\n8.7nm, all with a fixed length of 22nm. The fundamental mode frequencies range\\nfrom 40 to 90GHz and Q from 10^2 near 1000K to 10^4 near 50K. The quality\\nfactor is shown to be limited by defects in the reconstructed surface.\", \"sampled\": \"In a study focusing on Finite Drude and Quantum Monte Carlo, researchers explore the task of determining the weight of the finite Drude weight in 1D low-temperature conductors. The research provides clear and robust numerical evidence indicating an absence of the expected superfluid density highlighting the intricate interplay between theoretical expectations and empirical findings in this domain.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In this paper, we present the necessary and sufficient conditions of\\nseparability for bipartite pure states in infinite dimensional Hilbert spaces.\\nLet $M$ be the matrix of the amplitudes of $\\\\ket\\\\psi$, we prove $M$ is a\\ncompact operator. We also prove $\\\\ket\\\\psi$ is separable if and only if $M$ is a\\nbounded linear operator with rank 1, that is the image of $M$ is a one\\ndimensional Hilbert space. So we have related the separability for bipartite\\npure states in infinite dimensional Hilbert spaces to an important class of\\nbounded linear operators in Functional analysis which has many interesting\\nproperties.\", \"sampled\": \"The study explores the functional central limit theorem in a non-nestling context considering small edits in text references and non-nestling random walk products. It assumes exponential moments for step walks in a uniformly distributed environment. The research proves the invariance principle of the functional central limit theorem under almost conditions. The main insight reveals that the invariance principle indicates that the quenched mean walk behaves subdiffusively shedding light on the behavior of the system. The analysis highlights the significance of these findings showcasing a new perspective on the behavior of non-nestling random walk products in this context.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper revisits previous papers related to the theory of relativity.\\nAfterwards, a reconsideration of the hypothesis of ether-dragging is discussed.\\nThe ether is compatible with the theory of relativity and historical\\nexperiments; this paper explains the Michelson-Morley experiment using the\\nether-dragging hypothesis without the orthodox interpretation that the speed c\\nis a fixed constant in terms of any system of inertial coordinates.\", \"sampled\": \"A new search for \\\"New Search for\\\" in the context of tau, mu, and gamma decays revealed no signal. The study set upper limits with a 90% confidence level with 15 decays expected and 13 observed. The analysis yielded no significant findings as the data did not support the presence of the expected signals, emphasizing the need for further exploration in this area.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In wireless packet-forwarding networks with selfish nodes, applications of a\\nrepeated game can induce the nodes to forward each others' packets, so that the\\nnetwork performance can be improved. However, the nodes on the boundary of such\\nnetworks cannot benefit from this strategy, as the other nodes do not depend on\\nthem. This problem is sometimes known as the curse of the boundary nodes. To\\novercome this problem, an approach based on coalition games is proposed, in\\nwhich the boundary nodes can use cooperative transmission to help the backbone\\nnodes in the middle of the network. In return, the backbone nodes are willing\\nto forward the boundary nodes' packets. The stability of the coalitions is\\nstudied using the concept of a core. Then two types of fairness, namely, the\\nmin-max fairness using nucleolus and the average fairness using the Shapley\\nfunction are investigated. Finally, a protocol is designed using both repeated\\ngames and coalition games. Simulation results show how boundary nodes and\\nbackbone nodes form coalitions together according to different fairness\\ncriteria. The proposed protocol can improve the network connectivity by about\\n50%, compared with pure repeated game schemes.\", \"sampled\": \"The Mock LISA Data Challenges introduced the task of extracting galactic binary signals, with the first round showcasing promising results. In the Proceedings of GWDAW, a reference text reported on the performance of an end-to-end Bayesian analysis pipeline. The principal analysis tool utilized was the blocked-annealed Metropolis-Hastings (BAM) algorithm, optimized for efficiency. This algorithm employs Bayesian model selection to determine the number of resolvable sources accurately. Impressively, the BAM algorithm performed nearly flawlessly in round 1 of the Challenge with only later issues attributed to traced coding errors affecting high-frequency sources. Additionally, the algorithm was successfully tested alongside a Genetic Algorithm, demonstrating its robustness and potential for further advancements.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A multiple antenna downlink channel where limited channel feedback is\\navailable to the transmitter is considered. In a vector downlink channel\\n(single antenna at each receiver), the transmit antenna array can be used to\\ntransmit separate data streams to multiple receivers only if the transmitter\\nhas very accurate channel knowledge, i.e., if there is high-rate channel\\nfeedback from each receiver. In this work it is shown that channel feedback\\nrequirements can be significantly reduced if each receiver has a small number\\nof antennas and appropriately combines its antenna outputs. A combining method\\nthat minimizes channel quantization error at each receiver, and thereby\\nminimizes multi-user interference, is proposed and analyzed. This technique is\\nshown to outperform traditional techniques such as maximum-ratio combining\\nbecause minimization of interference power is more critical than maximization\\nof signal power in the multiple antenna downlink. Analysis is provided to\\nquantify the feedback savings, and the technique is seen to work well with user\\nselection and is also robust to receiver estimation error.\", \"sampled\": \"String Theory and its implications in cosmology are discussed in a study published in Physical Review. The study delves into the categories of hep-ph, astro-ph, and hep-th, exploring the carrier of acceleration during the present de Sitter epoch where a quintessence field slowly rolls. Additionally, an intrinsic model involving a second modulus, automatically stabilized, acts as a source of cold dark matter. Despite attempts to saturate the present cold dark matter component, challenges persist. The research also presents a model supporting de Sitter energy with remarkable agreement and introduces a string theory background lifting in a six-dimensional cosmological setting.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In this paper we investigate the optimal control problem for a class of\\nstochastic Cauchy evolution problem with non standard boundary dynamic and\\ncontrol. The model is composed by an infinite dimensional dynamical system\\ncoupled with a finite dimensional dynamics, which describes the boundary\\nconditions of the internal system. In other terms, we are concerned with non\\nstandard boundary conditions, as the value at the boundary is governed by a\\ndifferent stochastic differential equation.\", \"sampled\": \"This study delves into The Dynamics of Quintessence and The Quintessence of Dynamics within the realm of General Relativity and Gravitation. It presents a comprehensive analysis of various models of quintessence, emphasizing the dynamic aspects and discussing different classifications based on physical characteristics. The research scrutinizes the pros and cons of diverse parameterizations, exploring extensions to scalar fields and other related fields. The findings reveal new insights into the ability of cosmological data to distinguish between different scenarios, including thawing and freezing dynamics.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"We present molecular simulation data for viscosity, self-diffusivity, and the\\nlocal structural ordering of (i) a hard-sphere fluid and (ii) a square-well\\nfluid with short-range attractions. The latter fluid exhibits a region of\\ndynamic anomalies in its phase diagram, where its mobility increases upon\\nisochoric cooling, which is found to be a subset of a larger region of\\nstructural anomalies, in which its pair correlations strengthen upon isochoric\\nheating. This \\\"cascade of anomalies\\\" qualitatively resembles that found in\\nrecent simulations of liquid water. The results for the hard-sphere and\\nsquare-well systems also show that the breakdown of the Stokes-Einstein\\nrelation upon supercooling occurs for conditions where viscosity and\\nself-diffusivity develop different couplings to the degree of pairwise\\nstructural ordering of the liquid. We discuss how these couplings reflect\\ndynamic heterogeneities. Finally, we note that the simulation data suggests how\\nrepulsive and attractive glasses may generally be characterized by two distinct\\nlevels of short-range structural order.\", \"sampled\": \"Rate Distributed Source Coding and the Presence of Byzantine Sensors are the focus of this study. The research delves into the task involving variable-rate distributed source coding in the context of Byzantine sensors. Three distinct forms of the problem are analyzed, including a variable-rate setup where the decoder adaptively selects the transmission rates for the sensors. The study provides an explicit characterization of the minimum achievable sum rate in this setup, with the decoder choosing rates based on the maximum available information. Additionally, the research considers two other forms of the problem involving fixed-rate scenarios one deterministic and the other randomized. Achievable rate regions are identified for both fixed-rate problems, showing a larger region for utilizing the randomized coding approach.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The today estimated value of dark energy can be achieved by the vacuum condensate induced by neutrino mixing phenomenon. Such a tiny value is recovered for a cut-off of the order of Planck scale and it is linked to the sub eV neutrino mass scale. Contributions to dark energy from auxiliary fields or mechanisms are not necessary in this approach.\", \"sampled\": \"This research proposal outlines a new method for generating entangled states of light fields. The proposed scheme utilizes the Cross-Kerr nonlinear interaction between coherent light-beams, and also involves a method of homodyne detection.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Using the results found previously for the cooling rates of the emittances, due to collisions between the electrons and the ions, a result is found for the friction force acting on the ions. It is shown that the friction force found here when used to track the ion bunch will give the same emittance cooling rates as those found using the intrabeam scattering theory for electron cooling >.For the case of the uniform in space electron bunch distribution, the friction force found here agrees with the friction force result found with the usual theory of electron cooling.\", \"sampled\": \"This study presents an iterative method for recovering bulk information in asymptotically AdS spacetimes, a crucial concept in the fields of Numerical metric extraction, AdS/CFT, General Relativity, Gravitation, hep-th, and gr-qc. The research emphasizes on zero energy spacelike geodesics and their relation to entanglement entropy in three dimensions, using these parameters to determine the metric in certain symmetric cases. A comparative analysis is performed with an alternative extraction method presented in arXiv:hep-th/0609202, enriching the understanding of these complex concepts. The two methods are then combined enabling metric recovery in a broader scope, specifically static, spherically symmetric setups.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Since the advent of chirped pulse amplification1 the peak power of lasers has grown dramatically and opened the new branch of high field science, delivering the focused irradiance, electric fields of which drive electrons into the relativistic regime. In a plasma wake wave generated by such a laser, modulations of the electron density naturally and robustly take the shape of paraboloidal dense shells, separated by evacuated regions, moving almost at the speed of light. When we inject another counter-propagating laser pulse, it is partially reflected from the shells, acting as relativistic flying (semi-transparent) mirrors, producing an extremely time-compressed frequency-multiplied pulse which may be focused tightly to the diffraction limit. This is as if the counterstreaming laser pulse bounces off a relativistically swung tennis racket, turning the ball of the laser photons into another ball of coherent X-ray photons but with a form extremely relativistically compressed to attosecond and zeptosecond levels. Here we report the first demonstration of the frequency multiplication detected from the reflection of a weak laser pulse in the region of the wake wave generated by the driver pulse in helium plasma. This leads to the possibility of very strong pulse compression and extreme coherent light intensification.\", \"sampled\": \"In the field of neuroscience, the proteins utrophin and Dp116 have been found to be co-localized at the outermost layer of the myelin sheath of nerve fibers. This is relevant to the role of MMP, an enzyme that generates a 30 kDa fragment of another protein, beta-dystroglycan. This process disrupts the link between the extracellular matrix and the cell membrane. It has been observed that the processing of beta-dystroglycan influences the anchorage of Dp116 and utrophin in the Schwann cell membrane, a critical component of nerve fibers. Further research has revealed that MMP-9, a specific form of MMP, is more activated in a particular type of nerve cell the mdx Schwann cell compared to its wild-type counterpart. This heightened activation of MMP-9 leads to an accumulation of the 30 kDa beta-dystroglycan isoform. It was discovered that Dp116 has a greater affinity to the full length form of beta-dystroglycan than this 30 kDa form. In the same vein, the short isoform of utrophin known as Up71, is overexpressed in mdx Schwann cells compared to wild-type cells. Interestingly and  isoform of utrophin has a greater affinity to the 30 kDa beta-dystroglycan. The study concludes that both the short utrophin isoform and the cleaved form of beta-dystroglycan participate in the architecture of the mdx Schwann cell membrane. This research opens up new avenues of understanding for the role of these proteins and cells in neuroscience.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We demonstrate the power of the genetic algorithms to construct the cellular automata model simulating the growth of 2-dimensional close-to-circular clusters revealing the desired properties, such as the growth rate and, at the same time, the fractal behavior of their contours. The possible application of the approach in the field of tumor modeling is outlined.\", \"sampled\": \"This research presents a novel public conveyance model employing stochastic cellular automaton. The model’s effectiveness hinges on the optimal density of vehicles such as buses and trains, which is determined by the number of stops and passengers' behaviors. A comparative analysis was made on the efficiency of the hail-and-ride system under varying passenger behaviors.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The problem of resource allocation is studied for a two-user fading orthogonal multiaccess relay channel (MARC) where both users (sources) communicate with a destination in the presence of a relay. A half-duplex relay is considered that transmits on a channel orthogonal to that used by the sources. The instantaneous fading state between every transmit-receive pair in this network is assumed to be known at both the transmitter and receiver. Under an average power constraint at each source and the relay, the sum-rate for the achievable strategy of decode-and-forward (DF) is maximized over all power allocations (policies) at the sources and relay. It is shown that the sum-rate maximizing policy exploits the multiuser fading diversity to reveal the optimality of opportunistic channel use by each user.\", \"sampled\": \"This research study focused on self-compensation in Cd0.95Zn0.05Te:Cl solid-solution crystals. As part of the study, these crystals were annealed under a controlled Cd vapor pressure. A range of measurements were taken including the Hall effect, photoluminescence, carrier lifetime and mobility, and photocurrent memory. The data gathered supports a self-compensation model where intrinsic point defects act as acceptors with deep levels. These conditions allowed for the fabrication of low-conductivity samples with a carrier density ranging from 10E7-10E11 cm-3. A p -- n conduction inversion was observed at a free-carrier density of 10E9 cm-3. The electron density's dependence on the Cd vapor pressure exhibited a more gentle slope than in the case of CdTe:Cl crystals. This deep level is attributed to a Zn vacancy, which remains active at high Cd pressure.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I explore physics implications of the External Reality Hypothesis (ERH) that there exists an external physical reality completely independent of us humans. I argue that with a sufficiently broad definition of mathematics, it implies the Mathematical Universe Hypothesis (MUH) that our physical world is an abstract mathematical structure. I discuss various implications of the ERH and MUH, ranging from standard physics topics like symmetries, irreducible representations, units, free parameters, randomness and initial conditions to broader issues like consciousness, parallel universes and Godel incompleteness. I hypothesize that only computable and decidable (in Godel's sense) structures exist, which alleviates the cosmological measure problem and help explain why our physical laws appear so simple. I also comment on the intimate relation between mathematical structures, computations, simulations and physical systems.\", \"sampled\": \"A high-resolution neutron diffraction study was conducted on AgNiO2, an orbitally-degenerate spin-1/2 hexagonal antiferromagnet. This research observed a structural transition in AgNiO2 to a tripled unit cell structure characterized by NiO6 octahedra that alternately expanded and contracted. In the Ni triangular lattice of this material, the study indicated the presence of a root (3) x root (3) charge order. This charge order is proposed as a potential mechanism for lifting the orbital degeneracy in the context of charge fluctuations. At base temperatures, the research revealed a novel magnetic ground state where the electron-rich S = 1 Ni sites are arranged in alternating ferromagnetic rows on the triangular lattice. These S = 1 Ni sites are encapsulated by a honeycomb network of non-magnetic and metallic Ni ions.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We re-discuss the evolutionary state of upper main sequence magnetic stars using a sample of Ap and Bp stars with accurate Hipparcos parallaxes and definitely determined longitudinal magnetic fields. We confirm our previous results obtained from the study of Ap and Bp stars with accurate measurements of the mean magnetic field modulus and mean quadratic magnetic fields that magnetic stars of mass M 3 M_sun seem to be concentrated closer to the ZAMS. The study of a few known members of nearby open clusters with accurate Hipparcos parallaxes confirms these conclusions. Stronger magnetic fields tend to be found in hotter, younger and more massive stars, as well as in stars with shorter rotation periods. No evidence is found for any loss of angular momentum during the main-sequence life. The magnetic flux remains constant over the stellar life time on the main sequence.\", \"sampled\": \"More than two dozen short-period Jupiter-mass gas giant planets have been discovered around nearby solar-type stars, providing a rich source of data for atmospheric dynamics. This study utilized a 3-dimensional radiative hydrodynamical numerical scheme to simulate atmospheric circulation on these close-in gas giant planets, particularly focusing on synchronously-spinning gas giants. A clear temperature transition at the terminator was found in the study when using a full three-dimensional treatment, which was also coupled with rotationally modified flows and an accurate treatment of radiation. The night-side temperature of the planets was revealed to be a strong indicator of the opacity of the planetary atmosphere. Planetary atmospheres that maintain large interstellar opacities were found to exhibit large day-night temperature differences. Conversely, planets with reduced atmospheric opacities due to extensive grain growth and sedimentation were found to exhibit much more uniform temperatures throughout their photosphere's. To explain the dependence of night-side temperature on opacity, a four-zone analytic approximation was presented. The findings underscore the importance of numerical simulations in understanding the atmospheric dynamics of extra solar gas giant planets.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This article has been withdrawn because it has been merged with the earlier article GCT3 (arXiv: CS/0501076 [cs.CC]) in the series. The merged article is now available as: Geometric Complexity Theory III: on deciding nonvanishing of a Littlewood-Richardson Coefficient, Journal of Algebraic Combinatorics, vol. 36, issue 1, 2012, pp. 103-110. (Authors: Ketan Mulmuley, Hari Narayanan and Milind Sohoni) The new article in this GCT5 slot in the series is: Geometric Complexity Theory V: Equivalence between blackbox derandomization of polynomial identity testing and derandomization of Noether's Normalization Lemma, in the Proceedings of FOCS 2012 (abstract), arXiv:1209.5993 [cs.\", \"sampled\": \"Within the task context, this research focused on the electronic structure and kinetic energy in superconductors particularly within their presence. The study revolved around the 'Within the' framework with a specific focus on kinetic energy-driven superconductivity. Notably, the electron spectrum in bilayer cuprate superconductors was shown to split into bonding and antibonding, a significant finding that adds to our understanding of these superconductors. The study also found that spectral weight increases with increasing doping concentration, a phenomenon analogous to the normal state case where both electron antibonding peak and bonding hump are observed.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The intrinsic distribution of spectral indices in GeV energies of gamma-ray--loud blazars is a critical input in determining the spectral shape of the unresolved blazar contribution to the diffuse extragalactic gamma-ray background, as well as an important test of blazar emission theories. We present a maximum-likelihood method of determining the intrinsic spectral index distribution (ISID) of a population of gamma-ray emitters which accounts for error in measurement of individual spectral indices, and we apply it to EGRET blazars. We find that the most likely Gaussian ISID for EGRET blazars has a mean of 2.27 and a standard deviation of 0.20. We additionally find some indication that FSRQs and BL Lacs may have different ISIDs (with BL Lacs being harder). We also test for spectral index hardening associated with blazar variability for which we find no evidence. Finally, we produce simulated GLAST spectral index datasets and perform the same analyses.\", \"sampled\": \"The research presented herein delves into the phenomenon of vortex trapping within the context of cyclically coupled Bose-Josephson junctions. These junctions under certain conditions attain a state of stable circulation, a feat made possible by integrating a dissipative term within the semiclassical equations of motion. Intriguingly, the final distribution of winding numbers (m) was observed to be narrower compared to the initial distribution of total phases which signifies an elevated probability for configurations devoid of vortices. This culminates in the possibility of achieving a non-zero circulation, even when beginning with a total phase of zero around the loop. The investigation further revealed a correlation between the final width of the distribution of winding numbers for a given number of independent BECs (N) and a scaling factor (λ) - the width scales as λN^α, with α being 0.47 ± 0.01. The value of λ, hinges on the strength of dissipation and is observed to be less than 0.67.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We studied in detail the optical spectrum of the post-AGB star HD56126 (IRAS07134+1005). We use high resolution spectra (R=25000 and 60000) obtained with the echelle spectrographs of the 6-m telescope. About one and a half thousand absorptions of neutral atoms and ions, absorption bands of C_2, CN, and CH molecules, and interstellar bands (DIBs) are identified in the 4010 to 8790 AA wavelength region, and the depths and radial velocities of these spectral features are measured. Differences are revealed between the variations of the radial velocities measured from spectral features of different excitation. In addition to the well-known variability of the Halpha profile, we found variations in the profiles of a number of FeII, YII, and BaII lines. We also produce an atlas of the spectrum of HD56126 and its comparison staralpha Per. The full version of the atlas is available in electronic form from Web-address: http://www.sao.ru/hq/ssl/Atlas/Atlas.\", \"sampled\": \"The research observation focuses on the blazar PKS0537-441, which was systematically monitored using a variety of observation tools from the end of 2004 to November 2005. The Swift spacecraft equipped with the BAT instrument, observed PKS0537-441 recurrently for a total of 2.7 Ms, while the XRT and UVOT instruments on Swift pointed at the blazar on seven separate occasions amassing a total of 67 ks. Complementing these observations, the REM telescope was also used to continuously monitor the blazar during the same period. The data obtained from these observations revealed distinct variations in the brightness of PKS0537-441 over the course of the year. In January-February 2005, the blazar was detected at its brightest in both optical and X-ray observations with the X-ray brightness increasing by a factor of 2 and the optical brightness by a factor of 60.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We report on the layer-by-layer growth of single-crystal Al2O3 thin-films on Nb (110). Single-crystal Nb films are first prepared on A-plane sapphire, followed by the evaporation of Al in an O2 background. The first stages of Al2O3 growth are layer-by-layer with hexagonal symmetry. Electron and x-ray diffraction measurements indicate the Al2O3 initially grows clamped to the Nb lattice with a tensile strain near 10%. This strain relaxes with further deposition, and beyond about 5 nm we observe the onset of island growth. Despite the asymmetric misfit between the Al2O3 film and the Nb under-layer, the observed strain is surprisingly isotropic.\", \"sampled\": \"This study addresses the equivalence problem for gravity in three dimensions by presenting a practical coordinate-invariant description of local geometry. The method adapts Karlhede invariant classification from general relativity, fixing local geometry through the curvature tensor together with finitely many covariant derivatives in a relevant frame whose metric components are constant. The formalism uses real two-component spinors for three-dimensional spacetimes and provides algebraic classifications for both the Ricci spinor and the Cotton-York spinor. For these spinor classifications, the study determines isotropy groups and canonical forms giving the coordinate-invariant framework a concrete algebraic implementation.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Young open clusters are likely sites of cosmic ray acceleration as indicated by recent detections of the TeV gamma-ray sources in the directions of two open clusters (Cyg OB2 and Westerlund 2) and their directional proximity to some unidentified EGRET sources. In fact, up to now a few different scenarios for acceleration of particles inside open clusters have been considered, i.e. shocks in massive star winds, pulsars and their nebulae, supernova shocks, massive compact binaries. Here we consider in detail the radiation processes due to both electrons and hadrons accelerated inside the open cluster. As a specific scenario, we apply the acceleration process at the shocks arising in the winds of WR type stars. Particles diffuse through the medium of the open cluster during the activity time of the acceleration scenario defined by the age of the WR star. They interact with the matter and radiation, at first inside the open cluster and, later in the dense surrounding clouds.\", \"sampled\": \"This study examines how minimal cell signalling can support self-assembly, using the transition from single-celled ancestors to multicellular life as its motivating problem and treating signalling with cellular response as a required condition for multicellular assembly. Because highly evolved modern signalling may obscure early mechanisms refined over millions of years, the work emphasizes simplified representations of chemotaxis and aggregation rather than complex present-day signalling networks. A 2D model combines cellular automata with differential equations to represent organisms and chemotactic chemicals, and this approach generates spiral aggregation patterns consistent with signal-mediated collective organization. A 3D model represents individual cells as Lennard-Jones-like particles and tracks signal-driven evolution using molecular dynamics, showing that a single emitting cell can attract other cells when its signal causes movement toward the emitter. Under these minimal conditions, colonies of single-cell organisms can self-assemble into a tower, a ball on a stalk, or a fast-moving slug and the modeled behavior is described as strongly similar to Dictyostelium slime molds which in modern form signal using cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP).\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We predict cascade hypernuclear production spectra expected in the forthcoming J-PARC experiment. In the Green's function method of the distorted wave impulse wave approximation with the local optimal Fermi averaging t-matrix, we can describe the Xi production spectra in the continuum and bound state region reasonably well. Predictions to the high resonlution spectra at J-PARC suggest hat we should observe Xi bound state peak structure in (K-,K+) spectra in light nuclear targets such as 12C and 27Al.\", \"sampled\": \"This abstract summarizes selected recent Tevatron results within hep-ex, as presented in the work CP Violation Studies at Tevatron. It discusses a D{\\\\O} dimuon charge asymmetry measurement used to obtain a CP-violation parameter for $\\\\Bo$ mixing and decay. It also covers a CDF measurement of CP-violating asymmetry in $\\\\bdkpi$ decays, identifying this channel as a central element of the reported results.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Context. One of the typical features shown by observations of solar prominence oscillations is that they are damped in time and that the values of the damping times are usually between one and three times the corresponding oscillatory period. However, the mechanism responsible for the attenuation is still not well-known. Aims. Thermal conduction, optically thin or thick radiation and heating are taken into account in the energy equation, and their role on the attenuation of prominence oscillations is evaluated. Methods. The dispersion relation for linear non-adiabatic magnetoacoustic waves is derived considering an equilibrium made of a prominence plasma slab embedded in an unbounded corona. The magnetic field is orientated along the direction parallel to the slab axis and has the same strength in all regions. By solving the dispersion relation for a fixed wavenumber, a complex oscillatory frequency is obtained, and the period and the damping time are computed. Results.\", \"sampled\": \"This survey treats standard braid group results with an emphasis on shorter algebraic arguments, applying van der Waerden's trick to clarify the Artin-Magnus proof concerning the classic presentation of the algebraic mapping class group of a punctured disc. It presents a new simple proof of the Dehornoy-Larue braid group trichotomy, shows that this proof yields the Dehornoy right ordering of the braid group and discusses the Birman-Hilden theorem for braid group actions on free products of cyclic groups including results derived by Perron-Vannier and connections with Wada representations. The article also recalls the Crisp-Paris proof of the Birman-Hilden theorem using the Larue-Shpilrain technique uses the study of ends of free groups to obtain a broader version of the theorem, describes how Jordan curves in a punctured disc give stronger insight into braid groups, and includes an appendix summarizing the main ideas of Larue's PhD thesis with simpler combinatorial proofs; the work is described as 51 pages with 13 figures.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recent work in the literature has studied a version of non-commutative Schwarzschild black holes where the effects of non-commutativity are described by a mass function depending on both the radial variable r and a non-commutativity parameter theta. The present paper studies the asymptotic behaviour of solutions of the zero-rest-mass scalar wave equation in such a modified Schwarzschild space-time in a neighbourhood of spatial infinity. The analysis is eventually reduced to finding solutions of an inhomogeneous Euler--Poisson--Darboux equation, where the parameter theta affects explicitly the functional form of the source term. Interestingly, for finite values of theta, there is full qualitative agreement with general relativity: the conformal singularity at spacelike infinity reduces in a considerable way the differentiability class of scalar fields at future null infinity. In the physical space-time, this means that the scalar field has an asymptotic behaviour with a fall-off going on rather more slowly than in flat space-time.\", \"sampled\": \"This study addresses the origin of the early type galaxy dichotomy using a semi analytical galaxy formation model coupled to a large N-body simulation, in a submitted paper of 13 pages with 8 figures for MNRAS. Boxy galaxies are found to arise from mergers with progenitor mass ratio n < 2 and combined cold gas mass fraction F_cold < 0.1, linking isophotal shape to the dryness and relative masses of the final major merger. The model matches observed boxy-galaxy fractions as functions of luminosity and halo mass for both central galaxies and satellites, while showing that once stellar mass dependence is removed, the last major merger properties of early type galaxies do not depend on halo mass.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"One of the problems concerning entanglement witnesses (EWs) is the construction of them by a given set of operators. Here several multi-qubit EWs called stabilizer EWs are constructed by using the stabilizer operators of some given multi-qubit states such as GHZ, cluster and exceptional states. The general approach to manipulate the multi-qubit stabilizer EWs by exact(approximate) linear programming (LP) method is described and it is shown that the Clifford group play a crucial role in finding the hyper-planes encircling the feasible region. The optimality, decomposability and non-decomposability of constructed stabilizer EWs are discussed.\", \"sampled\": \"This study examines phase-transition ordering and symmetry in TiOX, identified as a quasi one-dimensional inorganic spin-Peierls system with attention to the materials TiOBr and TiOCl. Using inelastic light scattering experiments, the work focuses especially on the low-temperature incommensurate phase and assigns the spin-Peierls phase to P$2_{1}/m$ symmetry. The first-order nature of the spin-Peierls transition is attributed to a symmetry mismatch between the incommensurate and spin-Peierls phases, linking transition character to phase symmetry.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Derivation of the full set of Bell inequalities involving correlation functions, for two parties, with binary observables, and N possible local settings is not as easy as it seemed. The proof of v1 is wrong. Additionaly one can find a counterexample, which will be presented soon. Thus our thesis is dead. Still the series of Bell inequalities discussed in the manuscript (v1) form a necessary condition for local realism, and are tight. They are tight and complete (sufficient) only for N=3 settings per observer (as shown in quant-ph/0611086, fortunately using an entirely different approach).\", \"sampled\": \"This paper studies multiple unfoldings of orbifold singularities in high energy theory, focusing on how deformed ADE type orbifold singularities can serve as geometric sources of charged matter. Building on the relation identified by Katz and Vafa, the analysis places these singularity deformations within compactification frameworks including type IIa, M-theory, and F-theory. The authors reuse geometric engineering components to alter the compactification manifold, presenting the deformation approach as matching several aspects of unified model building.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We derive an It\\\\^o's-type formula for the one dimensional stochastic heat equation driven by a space-time white noise. The proof is based on elementary properties of the $\\\\mathcal{S}$-transform and on the explicit representation of the solution process. We also discuss the relationship with other versions of this It\\\\^o's-type formula existing in literature.\", \"sampled\": \"This abstract presents Spectral averaging for trace compatible operators, a note in functional analysis and spectral theory that introduces these operators and infinitesimal spectral flow.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We show that nontrivial classical pretzel knots L(p,q,r) are hyperbolic with eight exceptions which are torus knots. We find Conway polynomials of n-pretzel links using a new computation tree. As applications, we compute the genera of n-pretzel links using these polynomials and find the basket number of pretzel links by showing that the genus and the canonical genus of a pretzel link are the same.\", \"sampled\": \"This abstract concerns dephasing of an electron level in a quantum dot near a fluctuating edge current associated with the fractional quantum Hall effect. The initial analysis uses perturbation theory and finds that the dephasing rate has an unusual dependence on bias voltage applied to a nearby quantum point contact.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"arxiv_abstract\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We analyze the possibility of probing non-standard neutrino interactions (NSI, for short) through the detection of neutrinos produced in a future galactic supernova (SN).We consider the effect of NSI on the neutrino propagation through the SN envelope within a three-neutrino framework, paying special attention to the inclusion of NSI-induced resonant conversions, which may take place in the most deleptonised inner layers. We study the possibility of detecting NSI effects in a Megaton water Cherenkov detector, either through modulation effects in the $\\\\bar\\\\nu_e$ spectrum due to (i) the passage of shock waves through the SN envelope, (ii) the time dependence of the electron fraction and (iii) the Earth matter effects; or, finally, through the possible detectability of the neutronization $\\\\nu_e$ burst. We find that the $\\\\bar\\\\nu_e$ spectrum can exhibit dramatic features due to the internal NSI-induced resonant conversion. This occurs for non-universal NSI strengths of a few %, and for very small flavor-changing NSI above a few$\\\\times 10^{-5}$.\", \"sampled\": \"This study examines long gamma-ray bursts by modeling GRB blastwave propagation in the surroundings of massive stars, treating the circumburst medium as a wind-shaped environment produced by the GRB progenitor and following an isotropic, adiabatic relativistic fireball as it expands through that structured medium. The model includes a stratified stellar wind zone that ends at a termination shock, beyond which the blastwave enters a shocked wind region with constant density, allowing the calculation to distinguish propagation in the free stellar wind from later motion in the constant-density shocked wind. Using an adaptive mesh refinement versatile advection code, the simulation tracks the blastwave from early acceleration through deceleration, shock crossing, and subsequent travel in shocked wind, while also resolving the sudden transition from thermal energy dominance to ultra-relativistic kinetic behavior.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n","data/blog.jsonl":"{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Matrix Multiplication Matrix multiplication follows a slightly more verbose methodology than simple addition or subtraction. When multiplying matrices, each row of the previous matrix multiplies with each column of the next matrix. This will become clearer in the example to follow. One way to think of this process is that each row of the first matrix processes each column of the second matrix. Let’s firstly present two 2 x 2 matrices to multiply: To calculate the final matrix (that is currently empty) each row of the first matrix needs to multiply with every column of the second matrix. The resulting matrix is known as the dot product of the matrices being multiplied. Dot products are widely used in machine learning, with NumPy supplying their own dot function to leverage. This process is admittedly is extremely hard to visualise, so let’s break it down into stages. The first row and column process resembles the following: Notice that we’re starting with the first row of matrix 1, and the first column of matrix 2. The corresponding values are multiplied, with their products added together resulting in one dot product value. Since there are more columns to multiply, row 1 then multiples with the next column of matrix 2: Now row 1 of matrix 1 has processed all columns of matrix 2, we can repeat the whole process for row 2: And finally process the last column of matrix 2 with the currently active row of matrix 1: And this concludes the dot product process. We can now simplify the resulting matrix with its true values: This concludes the dot product process! However, there are some requirements in order for a multiplication to be valid. The above example multiplies two matrices of shape 2 x 2 for simplicity, but there are a couple of rules that one must be aware of when dealing with matrix multiplication that pertains to the shapes and ordering of the matrices being multiplied. Let’s briefly take a look at these requirements. The requirements of matrix multiplication Even though the size of the matrices being multiplied does not need to be the same, the inner numbers must match. This requirement is obviously always met in the case of square matrices, but when the dimensions are different, then the column count of matrix 1 must match the row count of matrix 2. The shape of the resulting matrix is also determined by the shapes of the multiplying matrices— their outer numbers to be exact. These two rules are summarised in the following multiplication: Outer values determine the resulting shape, and the inner values must be the same for the multiplication to be valid, or defined. If the inner values are not the same, the resulting matrix will be undefined. Because of these requirements, it is not possible to multiply a non-square matrix with itself! The result would be undefined. This would entail multiplying, for example, a 2 x 3 matrix with a 2 x 3 matrix, and the inner numbers here do not match. We could however do an element-wise multiplication, that will be mentioned further down. With multiplication, ordering also matters Unlike scaler products, the resulting matrix will differ depending on which way round the matrices are in your formula. In other words, matrix multiplication is not commutative. If we take the product of two regular numbers, it does not matter which way round they are multiplied. E.g: 6 x 4 = 24 A x B 4 x 6 = 24 B x A == Same result This is not the case with matrices. Re-ordering even the simplest of matrices yields a drastically different dot product: Dot product vs element-wise multiplication Another type of matrix multiplication is known as element-wise multiplication. This is where each index of matrix A is multiplied with matrix B.\", \"sampled\": \"Michelle Jaqua had been trying to lose 20 lbs for 2 years before she finally turned to Whole30, a diet program that strips away nearly every comfort food category in one go. The program eliminates sugar, carbs, alcohol, dairy, legumes, corn, peanuts, and preservatives, leaving a very narrow path through the grocery store. The first two weeks demanded a complete overhaul of how Michelle cooked and thought about meals, pushing her toward ingredients she had rarely centered a plate around before. Her daily staples settled into eggs, ghee butter, spinach, yams, and coconut-based foods, a rotation that was simple but required consistent preparation. Because so many packaged sauces and condiments contain sugar or preservatives, Michelle began making nearly everything from scratch in her own kitchen. She produced homemade mayo, dressings, sauces, a coconut curry sauce, chicken broth, and a cabbage and meat soup that became a reliable option throughout the month. Swapping familiar carbohydrate-heavy dishes also became part of the routine, and one substitution that worked well was using spaghetti squash in place of regular spaghetti. The texture is different enough that it requires a mental adjustment, but it allowed her to keep a satisfying dinner format without breaking the program's rules. By day 13, the effort was producing measurable results, and Michelle had lost 5 lbs while also reporting better sleep, more energy, and fewer migraines than she had been experiencing before the diet. Those physical changes arrived together rather than one at a time, which made the restrictive food list feel more worthwhile. Day 14 brought a more personal milestone when Michelle went wedding dress shopping and found that she fit into a lower clothing size after losing those five pounds. She said \\\"Yes\\\" to the wedding dress that day, a moment made possible in part by the physical change she had already achieved in just two weeks on the program. Not every day went smoothly, and day 17 introduced a complication when Michelle came down with a head cold and took cold medicine even though it technically fell outside Whole30 rules. That small deviation was a practical concession rather than an abandonment of the program, and she continued following the diet through the remainder of the month. By day 25, Michelle had lost 9 lbs, and the change in her body was significant enough that she spent $500 on new clothes to fit her smaller frame. She found one pant style that worked well enough that she bought it in 3 colors, a practical response to the reality that her previous wardrobe no longer fit correctly. The final stretch of the 30 days held steady, and by day 30 Michelle had officially lost 10 pounds and dropped a full clothing size from where she had started. The 2 years of unsuccessful attempts to lose 20 lbs made that 10-pound result feel concrete and earned rather than accidental. Beyond the number on the scale, the physical changes that had begun showing up early in the program continued through the end of Whole30. Michelle reported better sleep and decreased migraines by the time she completed the full 30 days, benefits that had nothing to do with clothing size or weight. The cooking habits she built during the month, making sauces, broths, and soups from scratch and relying on whole ingredients like eggs, ghee, and yams, had become familiar enough that continuing them did not feel like a burden. That familiarity made the idea of keeping the eating style in place after the program ended a realistic option rather than a distant aspiration. After completing Whole30, Michelle considered adopting the eating style as a permanent lifestyle rather than returning to the habits that had kept her stuck for 2 years. The combination of weight loss, better sleep, fewer migraines, and a wardrobe that finally fit gave her a set of specific, concrete reasons to keep going.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Equity crowdsale: what’s next? Here’s a breakdown of next steps and what to expect. In case you missed the big news from the weekend: the BABB equity crowdsale closed at 11:59pm on Saturday 18 August, with total investment coming in at just over £1,550,000 from 1,452 investors. We’re delighted to have been successfully funded and are very grateful to all of our new shareholders for their support. What’s next? There is a lot happening over the next few weeks. Here’s everything you need to know if you invested in the equity crowdsale. Follow-up info There now follows a seven-day period in which Crowdcube will send out additional information, including articles of association. This is an official document which outlines BABB’s responsibilities as a business to you as a shareholder. Please read it carefully and get in touch if you have any questions. Confirming your ID Crowdcube might also contact you to submit ID for KYC and AML checks. In most cases they perform these checks automatically but some investors will need to supply additional documentation. If you’re in any doubt about whether you need to submit any documentation, we advise logging into your Crowdcube account or contacting the customer support team. We’re also on hand to help if you need us; you can chat to the team in the dedicated equity crowdsale telegram group or email us at crowdsale@getbabb.com. Making the payment If you pledged via credit or debit card, your investment will be taken automatically early next week. If you pledged to invest via bank transfer, Crowdcube will contact you about arranging the payment. You can also refer to our previous blog post for further instructions. Receiving your shares Once you have passed KYC and made your investment, your shares will be automatically distributed to your Crowdcube account within 2–3 weeks. You will be able to view and manage your portfolio through your account on the Crowdcube website. You will also receive an official share certificate confirming you as a shareholder in BABB Group Ltd. Please enjoy it and keep it safe! BAX bonus and airdrop Once Crowdcube has confirmed payment from all investors, we will receive email addresses for all participants. We will then be able to get in touch directly regarding claiming the BAX bonus and airdrop. If you already have an account on getbabb.com (from the token sale), you will automatically receive your BAX bonus and airdrop into this account. Please note that if the email address linked to your Crowdcube account is different to the one linked to your BABB account, you will need to let us know. If you don’t have a BABB account, we will contact you directly with instructions to open an account on getbabb.com. You will need to open this account in order to claim the BAX bonus and airdrop. The BAX bonus is payable to all equity crowdsale investors and is dependent on the amount invested. The BAX airdrop is distributed to all those who participated in the BABB token sale as as well as all those who invested in the equity crowdsale. All recipients will recieve the same number of tokens in the airdrop and we’ll contact you nearer the time to confirm the airdrop amount. Sharehodler merch competition We recently selected the winners of our sharehodler merch competition and everyone who took part in the competition has received an email letting them know whether or not they were among the winners. The last of the merch orders is due to arrive this week (following a delay; thanks for bearing with us) so we’ll distribute the prizes to the winners as soon as we can. Perks and rewards Speaking of merch — we will also contact investors in the coming weeks about claiming the perks they’re eligible for as a result of their invested amount.\", \"sampled\": \"Linda Caroll writes on Medium, and over the course of building a daily writing habit she tracked her progress carefully enough to know she missed only 2 days out of the last 20, a record that reflects genuine commitment rather than casual intention. That kind of consistency is harder to sustain than it sounds, and the fact that she kept going even imperfectly points to something more deliberate than motivation alone driving her practice. What drove her was a seven-step formula she developed specifically to write faster and with less rambling, a structural approach born from real frustration with how writing sessions can collapse into circular thinking and wasted time. The formula did not emerge from theory but from the practical need to sit down and produce something coherent without spending the session fighting her own mind. The first obstacle she names is the blank page itself, and her solution is to use constraints, such as writing prompts, to sidestep the paralysis that comes from total open-endedness. Once a topic is chosen, a second obstacle takes over, which she identifies as brain pong and rambling, the tendency for a writer's attention to bounce between half-formed ideas rather than moving the piece forward in a purposeful direction. Her formula addresses this by giving each stage of the writing process a distinct job, beginning with the topic and then moving immediately into the search for relevant data or statistics in step 2. Rather than generalizing from personal impression, the formula asks the writer to find a real source and cite it, which grounds the piece in something concrete and prevents the vague claims that make writing feel thin and untrustworthy to readers. Step 3 brings in personal experience to strengthen the story, layering a human dimension onto the factual foundation laid by the data, so that the piece carries both credibility and emotional texture at the same time. These two elements together, the cited fact and the lived moment, give the writing a kind of double footing that keeps it from tipping into either dry reporting or shapeless memoir. Only after the topic, data, and personal story are gathered does step 4 ask the writer to craft a strong opening, a sequence that runs counter to the instinct many writers have to begin at the beginning before knowing what the piece actually contains. Waiting to write the opening until the material is assembled means the opening can do real work instead of functioning as a placeholder that will need to be rewritten anyway. Step 5 connects the ending back to the beginning of the piece, creating a loop that gives readers a sense of arrival rather than simply stopping, and step 6 asks the writer to identify the emotion the piece should evoke before selecting a cover image that matches it. These steps treat the emotional and visual dimensions of the work as deliberate choices rather than afterthoughts, which changes the nature of the decisions being made about both image and tone. Step 7 is to write the title last, after re-reading the completed piece, so that the title can accurately reflect what the work turned out to be rather than what the writer hoped it might become at the start. Ideas that do not fit the current piece are not discarded but jotted down in an idea bin for later use, which keeps the session focused without losing material that might serve a future piece. Since building the formula into her practice, Linda Caroll notes that poetry keeps appearing when she opens the page to write, a detail that suggests the structure loosened something rather than tightening it. The seven steps function less like a cage and more like a cleared path, one that makes room for unexpected writing to arrive precisely because the mechanical decisions have already been handled in advance.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Businesses everywhere are experimenting with ways to tackle the obstacles imposed by the pandemic. For most, this is a process of trial and error. They either apply existing solutions that they are personally familiar with or copy as best they can what others around them are doing. But in some instances, like ETEN’s repurposing of micro-greenhouses, experimentation leads to genuinely novel solutions that reimagine products and services in new and exciting ways. So how do we go from deploying existing solutions to generating inspiring new approaches? It starts with how we think about resolving challenges: do we try to reconstruct the preexisting experience (even if they are at best Frankenstein-esque recreations), or pioneer new paths to discover new possibilities? Reconstruct: restoring the status-quo, or some version of it Much of our day-to-day actions unfold without much thought. We repeat routines and behaviors, allowing our experiences to guide us. The more ingrained and institutionalized they are, the less we think about them. For instance, before the pandemic, eating close to others was the norm, never crossing anyone’s mind as a serious threat to public health. As creatures of habit, we go through life like this until one of our habits or routines stops working as anticipated. When this happens, the particular stream of action we are in hits a roadblock, bringing the conflicted situation to our conscious awareness. With our automatic responses impaired by the roadblock, we consciously evaluate what other options in our repertoire of personal or socially observed experiences might help us restore the situation so that we can carry on. In most instances, quickly deploying a readily available alternative is sufficient to restore the situation. We saw this happening all across the country after states and municipalities imposed social distancing measures. The overnight introduction of takeout and make-at-home kits by elite restaurants in New York City, like Masa and Rezdôra, are prime examples. Neither strategy was part of their standard playbook, however without the ability to host guests and exorbitant overhead costs pilling up, they had to explore new ways to generate revenues. While takeout is novel to elite three-star New York Times reviewed restaurants, it is standard practice for most. This provided an immediate and easily deployable solution that, apart from the price-point, was familiar to their customers. Rezdôra’s make-at-home meal kit instructions Ultimately, Rezdôra, Masa, and, most recently, Eleven Madison Park, are trying, as best they can, to continue serving their customers within the parameters of government restrictions. All with the hope of weathering the storm and returning to their pre-pandemic operations and interactions with their guests. Omakase, after all, is an intimate and evolving interaction between you and the sushi master. If they cannot react to your every expression, playing to your likes and curiosities, the experience falls flat. Sushi masters at Sushi Gen in Los Angeles’s Little Tokyo Reimagine: pioneering a new path to new possibilities Ruptures in established routines and behaviors offer unique opportunities to reimagine how we think about and do things. During stable times, the social structures we are enmeshed in sustain and reinforce our routines and behaviors. We have all experienced the power of peer pressure. When we try to deviate from the norm, the group’s force pulls us back in line. (These same dynamics are at play at the organizational level, constraining how businesses think, act, and look.) When ruptures are systemic, throwing everyone into disarray, the power of conformity briefly loosens its grips. During these moments, we have the unique opportunity to construct new routines and behaviors, with the potential that they will catch on and become the new norm. So, how do we start pioneering new paths and possibilities? Especially given that how we see the world around us is hemmed in by our past experiences, social contexts, and technical constraints — all exerting forces that narrow our field of vision? Expanding our field of vision begins by replacing our current lenses with ones that provide new ways of seeing what we are tackling. Stripping down products and services to their core fundamentals is an effective way to gain new perspectives. Focusing on the fundamental characteristics of what we are exploring exposes the taken-for-granted assumptions and expectations that we have come to accept unconsciously. Through this awareness, we can ask how we can approach these core characteristics in new ways that better meet consumers’ evolving needs, wants, and desires.\", \"sampled\": \"The Los Angeles Dodgers won their first World Series since 1988, defeating the Tampa Bay Rays in a season already defined by disruption and compromise. That victory celebration became the center of a serious controversy when third baseman Justin Turner returned to the field after testing positive for COVID-19. Turner had been removed from the game in the 7th inning after receiving a positive test result, but he did not stay away from his teammates once the final out was recorded by pitcher Julio Urias. He returned to the field, hugged teammates, and held the World Series trophy despite the confirmed positive test result. What made the situation more troubling was the timeline surrounding the test itself. The league had received an inconclusive result as early as the 2nd inning, yet Turner remained in the game for five more innings before being removed. Turner also removed his mask for a group photo while standing in close proximity to teammates during the on-field celebration. Reporters, photographers, and players' families were all present on the field during that same period, extending the potential exposure well beyond the roster. Reports indicated that Dodgers management may have permitted Turner to be on the field for the team photo, raising questions about whether the breach was purely an individual decision or something that involved organizational awareness. The gap between when the inconclusive result arrived and when Turner exited added weight to those concerns. MLB Commissioner Rob Manfred faced scrutiny not only for the Turner situation but for decisions made earlier in the season. In late July, the Miami Marlins reported 3 positive COVID-19 cases among their players, yet Manfred allowed the team to proceed with a scheduled game against the Philadelphia Phillies. Within days of that game, the number of positive cases on the Marlins roster had risen to 20. Manfred cited temperature checks as his justification for permitting the Marlins to play, a rationale that drew considerable criticism given how quickly the outbreak expanded. Manfred had spoken about the season's COVID-19 protocols on The Daily podcast in the context of Juan Soto testing positive ahead of the season opener. His framing was notably candid: \\\"we knew we were going to have positives . . . The whole point is you have a system that's flexible enough to deal with what's coming. We knew it was coming.\\\" That statement, when placed alongside the Marlins decision and the Turner incident, highlighted a tension between the league's stated awareness of risk and its actual enforcement of the protocols designed to manage that risk. Flexibility, in practice, appeared to mean allowing games to proceed even when the safety calculus was uncertain. The human cost of COVID-19 exposure within the sport was real and documented throughout the season. Eduardo Rodriguez, a pitcher for the Red Sox, was unable to play at all in 2020 after developing myocarditis as a complication of his COVID-19 infection. Freddie Freeman, the Braves first baseman, suffered a 104-degree fever during his illness and reported praying for his life at the height of it. Those cases gave concrete weight to what was at stake each time protocols were treated as negotiable rather than binding. ESPN reporter Stephen A. Smith commented publicly on Turner's situation, reflecting the broader media attention the incident drew. The breach was not a quiet procedural lapse but a visible, photographed event that unfolded in front of cameras during one of the sport's highest-profile moments. The contrast between the Dodgers' historic win and the circumstances surrounding Turner's return made it difficult to separate celebration from accountability. A championship that ended a 32-year drought for the franchise was immediately shadowed by questions about what the league's protocols actually meant when tested under pressure. The Marlins outbreak and the Turner incident together illustrated a pattern in which the league's response to positive tests was inconsistent and, at times, appeared to prioritize scheduling and competition over the health of players and staff. Both situations involved known positive results and decisions that allowed continued exposure. What the 2020 season demonstrated, through the Marlins outbreak, the illnesses of players like Freeman and Rodriguez, and the World Series celebration breach, was that the gap between written protocol and enforced protocol carried genuine consequences for the people inside the sport. The Turner incident did not occur in isolation but at the end of a season full of similar decisions made at the institutional level.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Netlify’s platform team is responsible for building and scaling the systems designed to keep Netlify, and the hundreds of thousands of sites that use it, up and running. We take that responsibility seriously, so we’re constantly looking for potential points of failure in our system and working to remove them. Most of our platform is cloud agnostic, and since we favor an approach that minimizes risk, we wanted to extend that to include our origin services. This led to a project that culminated in us migrating our origin services between cloud providers on a recent Sunday night — without any service interruptions. When you deploy a website to Netlify, your content automatically gets pushed to the edge nodes in our Content Delivery Network (CDN). If you make changes in your Git repository, your content is continuously and automatically synced with our origin servers and pushed to the CDN. Our CDN runs on six different cloud providers, but up until recently, our origin servers relied on only one. That meant our origin servers were subject to the performance and uptime characteristics of a single provider. We wanted to make sure that in the face of a major outage from an underlying provider, anywhere in our network, Netlify’s service would continue with minimal interruption. Our origin services can now swap between three providers: Google Cloud (GCP), AWS, and Rackspace Cloud without downtime. This post will cover how we planed, tested, and executed our first multi-cloud migration Planning for change The main goal for this project was to have a multi-cloud production system where we could direct traffic to different providers on demand, without interruptions. Our primary sources of data are the database and the cloud store — the first for keeping references to the data and the second for the data itself. To keep latency down, we needed to run a production-ready copy of both sources in every provider. They also needed to have automatic fallbacks configured to the other clouds. For example, Google instances prioritize Google Cloud Storage (GCS) and fallback to S3 automatically. We first built a tool called cloud bench that would check the performance of uploads to different cloud storages. It showed us what we suspected: upload to a cloud storage was faster if it stayed in the same provider. We also confirmed that pricing was better if uploads stayed inside the same network. We went through the different services that touched the data in the cloud provider, extracting simple Get/Put interfaces for that data and implementing concrete versions for Google, Amazon, and Rackspace. We then released the service, with the abstracted interface, that would use Rackspace — our current cloud provider. Then we started to test that service with the different implementations for the other clouds. AWS and GCP offer more robust load balancers than what we were using. Rackspace is limited to around 10GB/s, which is a lot under normal operation, but not in the event of DDoS. Both Google and Amazon don’t place any limits, saying that the frontend load balancers will scale up as needed to support the traffic. Data Replication Netlify customers have deployed terabytes of data and they’re deploying more every day. Before the migration, all of this data was stored in a single service: Rackspace’s Cloudfiles. Our first challenge was to build a system able to replicate the millions of blob objects we stored and start replicating new data as it came in. Our approach attached a bitmask to the object reference that indicated where it was replicated. We did this to keep down the bloat on the database as there are millions of entries and adding long strings to each would balloon the database size. It also meant that any service that needed to use that blob now knew from where it could load the data. This bitmask opened the way for intelligent prioritization in different services. We built a Go service that would inspect an existing blob, figure out where it needed to push it to, actually push it, and record its progress. The service was able to act in both online replication and batch backfilling modes. We fed a few million objects to the service for replication. After manually checking those blobs, we started two processes. One that would process newly-uploaded objects and one to backfill the existing objects. We chose a passive replication strategy for the objects; the service would come through and clean up from time to time. A more active strategy, like a 3-phase commit, introduced risk into the upload phase. For instance, bugs or provider issues could slow or disable uploads completely. It is also a simpler change in our system as it didn’t impact the request chain at all. We could iterate on the service with minimal risk to the primary flow. There is only a small fraction of objects that aren’t replicated in the event that we swap providers. Objects are available across providers and the system automatically handles that. Recovery The system is flexible enough to handle recovering quickly; we’d take degraded performance over an outage any day. Traffic can be quickly redirected internally by updating service configurations. We can direct traffic to any of our clouds, including splitting traffic, in order to keep from dropping requests. Of course there are cost and performance considerations when doing this, especially when crossing cloud boundaries, but uptime is the priority. Just the other day we had to configure a cross cloud setup. We detected a latency issue in our Google integration and manually intervened to swap reads from S3 while we investigated. Once we worked out the kinks with the integration we returned the configuration to its resting state, preferring the cloud the service was in. Doing things manually is often too slow.\", \"sampled\": \"Since January 1, 2017, AMZN shares rose 91% while GOOGL shares rose only 41%, a gap that reflects meaningfully different investor confidence in the two companies over that stretch. The divergence became even sharper after fourth-quarter earnings, when the market delivered a blunt verdict on each company's trajectory. After 4Q results, AMZN shares rose 3%, adding nearly $20 billion in market cap, while GOOGL shares fell 5%, removing over $40 billion from Alphabet's market cap. Those moves in opposite directions captured a broader anxiety that had been building around Google's cost structure and its ability to defend margins. A central concern for GOOGL heading into and through 4Q was continued negative surprises in operating profit margin, driven specifically by higher traffic acquisition costs, known as TAC. These costs represent what Google pays to distribution partners to secure search placement, and they had been climbing faster than many on Wall Street anticipated. Analyst James Dix had flagged this pressure early, publishing a report titled \\\"A Closer Look Reveals Sites TAC Pressures Could Be Worse Than Expected\\\" in February of the prior year. That report proved prescient: a year before it appeared, Wall Street expected Sites TAC as a percentage of revenue to rise by about 1 percentage point over 2017, while Dix predicted more than twice that, and the actual increase came in even greater. GOOGL management responded to the TAC concern by guiding investors toward a slower pace of growth in Sites TAC percentage after the first quarter of 2018, citing visibility from multi-year distribution agreements already in place. That guidance offered some reassurance, though the damage to sentiment from the 4Q miss had already registered in the stock price. One reason Google commands such high distribution costs is the sheer scale of its search dominance, which gives partners leverage to extract payments for default placement. According to Merkle, GOOGL held 92% of U.S. search traffic overall and 96% on mobile in 4Q, numbers that illustrate both the strength of the franchise and the cost of maintaining it. Despite heavy investment in non-advertising products like Google Home and Pixel, advertising still accounted for 86% of GOOGL's revenue in 2017. That figure, while down from 92% in 2013, shows how thoroughly Google's revenue base remains tied to a single business model even after years of diversification effort. The profit picture is even more concentrated than the revenue mix suggests. GOOGL's profit contribution from advertising, though not formally disclosed, is estimated to be nearly 100%, meaning the rest of the company's product portfolio generates little to no meaningful earnings. GOOGL's operating margin stood at 23.7% in 4Q and 23.6% for the full year 2017, numbers that look healthy in isolation but carry risk when the underlying revenue source is so narrowly defined. Any structural pressure on advertising economics therefore flows almost directly into the company's bottom line with little buffer from other segments. Amazon's financial profile looks strikingly different. AMZN's operating margins were 3.5% in 4Q and just 1.0% for the full year 2017, reflecting a business that deliberately reinvests at a pace that suppresses reported profits in exchange for long-term scale and market position. Amazon's advertising business, contained within its other net sales category, was likely up approximately 60% year-over-year in 4Q, pointing to a fast-growing revenue stream that could eventually challenge Google's dominance in certain advertising formats. That growth sits inside a company whose e-commerce and cloud operations give it multiple ways to generate value independent of ad revenue. Amazon Prime bundles subscription benefits with e-commerce convenience in a way that builds deep consumer loyalty and recurring revenue, a model that differs fundamentally from Google's reliance on attention-based advertising. Netflix competes with Amazon Prime on the subscription video side, but Amazon's broader ecosystem gives Prime a stickiness that pure streaming services cannot replicate. James Dix argues that tokenization will pose a greater long-term threat to GOOGL's economics than to AMZN's, and he cites three reasons for that view. The core logic is that Google's business depends on harvesting consumer data and attention in ways that blockchain-based token models could directly disrupt by redirecting value back to users. The combined market cap of tokens from a sample of blockchains promising to transfer value to consumers for use of their data is approximately $4 billion, a figure that remains small relative to Google's scale but signals where developer and investor attention is pointing. If those models gain traction, the implicit exchange that funds Google's advertising business could face meaningful renegotiation. Amazon's revenue model, by contrast, is grounded in transactional commerce and cloud infrastructure rather than the data-for-attention exchange that tokenization most directly challenges. That structural difference is part of why Dix views AMZN as less exposed to the tokenization threat than GOOGL over a longer time horizon. The contrast between the two companies extends beyond business model risk to the question of where each earns its profits. Google's near-total profit dependence on advertising creates a single point of vulnerability that Amazon, with its diversified operating segments, does not share in the same way. The 4Q results served as a concrete illustration of how quickly investor sentiment can shift when a company's cost structure surprises to the downside, even for a business as dominant as Google. Losing over $40 billion in market cap in the wake of a single earnings report underscores how sensitive the stock remains to any sign that TAC pressures are outpacing management's ability to control them. Both companies enter subsequent quarters carrying the weight of these divergent narratives: Amazon rewarded for growth and reinvestment, Alphabet scrutinized for margin discipline and the durability of an advertising model that still generates nearly all of its profit. The 50-percentage-point gap in share price performance since January 1, 2017 reflects how differently the market has chosen to price those two stories.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Why Test? The majority of ads will fail. So unless you’re expecting a neverending streak of luck, you’ll need a process for separating the losers from the winners. That’s where A/B testing comes in. It’s simply the process of testing two or more ad variations against each other, analyzing the results, and doing less of what doesn’t work & more of what works. It’s hard to overstate how important this is. We frequently see ads perform 2x, 5x or even 10x better than others. And the first ads are almost never among the top performers. So if you’re not running at least one test at any given time, you’re leaving money on the table. What To Test First, you’ll need a bunch of copy angles and creatives. This is a huge topic in and of itself, so we won’t get into it here. Let’s just assume you have them. Great! Where do you start? You’ll want to go as BIG as possible with the first test. An example would be testing a professionally shot studio photo vs an unedited UGC (User Generated Content) video, or short CTA-focused ad copy vs long-form storytelling copy. The more contrast you add, the easier it will be to analyze the results and hone in one the winning angles. We like to start things off with a 2 x 2: two creatives and two copy. We find that this strikes a good balance between simplicity and effectiveness. 2 x 2 Is An Easy Way To Get Started The results from the first test determine what we do next. For instance, let’s say we find that there was a huge difference in performance between the creatives but no real difference between the copy variations. We would then isolate that variable, i.e. test a handful of creatives with the same copy. If you’ve got a large budget and want to be as hands-off as possible, you can use Facebook’s Dynamic Creative. It does work and we do use it, but most times we prefer to have more control. When To Test When it comes to testing, we believe that frequency is more important than volume. It’s better to run multiple tests with a few variations, then a few tests with multiple variations. That’s why we use a two-day testing cycle. On any given ad account we’re analyzing and implementing new ad copy and creative up to three times per week (Monday, Wednesday, Friday). Usually, the number of tests you can run is limited by the budget. But even on the accounts where we’re not able to test new variations every two days, we still analyze and monitor performance. Note: Adding a new ad to an existing ad set will reset the learning phase. It may make sense to use a designated campaign for testing. How To Test Analyzing the results is maybe the most challenging part. There’s a lot that goes into the analysis, but there are a couple of simple tools that can do most of the heavy lifting. Having a simple process is extremely helpful for removing emotion and making informed decisions quickly. We like to use this decision tree (credit: https://commonthreadco.com) as a guide. Ad Kill Decision Tree In order to be able to use the decision tree, you’ll need to have target CPAs for all correlated variables. Here’s a document that will help you with that. When needed, we also use a Significance Calculator. A/B Testing Like A Scientist You simply plug in the numbers and the calculator tells you how confident you can be that any difference in performance is real and not due to random variation. Since the whole point of Facebook’s targeting is to move away from random sampling, this obviously isn’t a perfect tool. But it’s useful as a reality check. After The Test The results from the test are documented and handed over to the creative team. We use a simple scoring scale (Poor, Okay, Good) and qualitative comments. Scoring the ads can be more art than science, given the many factors involved. That’s why it’s best done by a media buyer who spends a lot of time in the ad account. In Summary By the time you’re reading this, we may have made a few (or many) changes to our A/B testing process. Nevertheless, the general principles and concepts apply. Good luck!\", \"sampled\": \"Harry Hogg re-read his wife's first letter after many long years, and the experience of holding those pages again carried the full weight of a shared life that had quietly accumulated between the lines of her handwriting and the places she described. The two of them had been childhood sweethearts, which means that the story running through those letters was not simply a romance but something closer to a parallel growing-up, a record of two people who had known each other before they knew themselves. What strikes Hogg most, looking back, is that love letters rather than teachers gave him the ability to write at all, shaping his sense of language through the intimate pressure of wanting to be understood by one specific person across whatever distance separated them. His wife wrote many of those letters with the sound of the sea close by, and that proximity to water gave her words a particular quality, a kind of open, unhurried attention to the world around her that he found himself reaching toward in his own prose. Most of Hogg's life was lived near the sea as well, and he acknowledges that this shared geography shows in his writing, lending it a texture that is harder to arrive at through craft alone and easier to absorb simply by spending years listening to the rhythm of tides against a familiar shoreline. The couple shared a love of the written word and a deep appreciation for their part of the world, which meant that even the most ordinary letters between them carried a kind of mutual recognition, a sense that both people were paying close attention to the same things. The letters Hogg loved most were the ones that spoke of his wife's feeling for their home and garden, because those letters grounded the romance in something physical and tended, something that required daily care rather than only emotion, and that combination of feeling and practical devotion gave the correspondence a texture no purely passionate letter could match. He describes a span of forty years from the birth of their first child to the present moment of her absence, and that number carries the essay's central weight, marking the distance between a beginning that felt full of possibility and a present that holds only the evidence of what was built. Hogg places the letter under blue and white Anemones and looks out at a misty shoreline, and the image works not as decoration but as a precise record of where he is sitting and what surrounds him at the moment of re-reading, grounding the emotional experience in a specific, visible place. The green and yellow he paints the house, described as his wife's favorite color scheme, belongs to the same logic of tending, of keeping the physical world aligned with her preferences even now, which is a form of fidelity that requires no words at all. The couple found Glasgow too tough and preferred Paris, and that preference says something concrete about the kind of life they were drawn toward, one that favored a certain ease of beauty over industrial hardness, a city where the written word and lingering over things felt less out of place. Hogg mentions choosing La Perla underwear from the lingerie sections of shops, a detail that lands in the essay as evidence of attentiveness, of the small acts of selection that accumulate over decades into a portrait of someone you have studied carefully enough to know their taste without being told. The letters also mentioned Rome and Amsterdam and Ruth and Knud's bar, places and names that carried their own histories and gave the correspondence a geographical breadth, a sense that the couple's inner life was not confined to one location but had been tested and enriched across different cities and encounters. That breadth makes the return to the home and garden letters all the more telling, because after all the travel and variety, what his wife chose to write about with the most feeling was the particular patch of world they had made together. Hogg's essay turns on the idea that a single line could sum up a life together, and the impossibility of finding that line is not a failure but a measure of how much the life contained, how resistant it is to compression without loss.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"What to Buy (and What to Skip) to Prepare for Coronavirus Advice from people in quarantine about what you actually need — and how to make life work Photo: Universal Images Group/Getty Images Follow Elemental’s ongoing coverage of the coronavirus outbreak here. The World Health Organization has officially declared Covid-19 a pandemic, and if you’ve been hoping you can ignore coronavirus and it’ll just go away without affecting you or your family in some way, that’s looking less and less likely. But that isn’t to say you need to buy a deep freezer and build a storehouse in your backyard. So where should you begin, and how can you approach preparation without driving yourself up the wall? The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention has offered up guidance on how to prepare for an outbreak near you, including making a plan with your family, practicing good health and hygiene habits, and checking in with vulnerable people in your community. They also have instructed people in at-risk groups (like the immunocompromised, the elderly, and those with chronic health conditions) to stock up on supplies like nonperishable foods. Since things are moving quickly, it’s not a bad idea to stock up a bit before the virus hits your area, since social distancing (which can include limiting your visits to, say, crowded supermarkets and pharmacies) is such a helpful preventive measure, and you may need to self-quarantine with limited notice. In case you do have to hole up for a while, here’s what you may need to make it, well, suck less — based on advice from experts and folks who’ve actually been in quarantine. There’s already been a run on toilet paper and paper towels — for good reason. A sensible grocery list “It’s about stocking your pantry in a smart way, but also cooking with the stuff you already have in the house,” says Dawn Perry, a food writer who’s currently working on a pantry cookbook. Here’s her quick-and-dirty rundown of what you may need, including staples and flavor boosters so you’re not just stuck eating black beans out of a can: Dry grains: Rice, quinoa, oats, cornmeal (for polenta). Beans: dried or canned. Tinned fish: tuna, tasty cans of smoked sardines, or mackerel. A couple varieties of nut butter and tahini. Flour (you can add water, salt, and a leavening agent like baking soda or powder to make biscuits, tortillas, and crackers). Eggs. Onions (they keep for weeks when stored in a cool, dark place). Garlic. Potatoes. Boxed cereal. Hardy veggies: broccoli, celery, carrots, and kale (they stay fresh for a long time in your fridge). Bananas (when they start to turn, freeze for smoothies or bake into banana bread). Frozen peas (add to soups and pastas). Frozen sausage (just one link can go a long way in adding flavor to a dish). Bag of frozen shrimp. Sliced bread (pop it in your freezer, will last a long time). Taste boosters: soy sauce, mayo, ketchup, fruit jam, Dijon mustard, Parmesan cheese, olives, capers. First aid kit No matter what is happening — pandemic or regular day — “it’s prudent for every household to have basic essentials on hand to care for a minor emergency, such as a cut, burn, or insect sting,” says Brad Uren, MD, assistant professor of emergency medicine at the University of Michigan Medical School. (And while you should, of course, seek medical care if you need it, for minor issues you can treat at home, it’s preferable not to visit a health care facility right now if you don’t have to.) A standard first aid kit will have what you need. Extra supply of needed medication Make sure you have a few weeks of vital medications on hand, says Uren. Mail-order pharmacies, which often dole out 90-day supplies when available, may be an option for certain people, depending on what type of medication they’re taking and insurance, he says. If you’re having trouble making this happen (insurance doesn’t always make it easy), get on the phone with a pharmacist. OTC meds and other symptom treatments Should you get sick, you’ll want to have the right supplies on hand to treat your symptoms, says Aaron Reddington of the survival blog the Simple Prepper. Make sure you have things like a thermometer, OTC fever reducers (acetaminophen and ibuprofen), sports drinks for rehydrating, and the bland foods you want when you have no appetite, like crackers, he says. Hygiene stuff There’s already been a run on toilet paper and paper towels — for good reason. “While everyone has reacted and bought toilet paper and paper towels, unfortunately, I think you need to roll with the punches and buy some as well,” says Reddington. “It would be terrible to be stuck in your home for weeks without any, and while it may sound ridiculous [to stock up], worst-case scenario is that you have extra,” he says.\", \"sampled\": \"Pascal Barry's argument about design systems is a pointed one: that these widely celebrated tools are, at their core, harmful to design practice and the creativity that good design demands. The case he builds draws on research across more than 200 articles on Medium and other blogs, a volume of reading that shapes a skeptical and carefully reasoned position. The argument finds a useful parallel in Natasha Jen's 2018 talk \\\"Design Thinking Is Bullsh*t,\\\" where she observed that \\\"In today's world design has become this box that people just want to check off.\\\" Barry sees design systems operating in exactly that spirit, reducing a complex discipline to something that can be ticked off a project checklist. Brad Frost's definition of a design system offers a revealing starting point for the critique. Frost wrote that \\\"A kit of UI components without accompanying philosophy, principles, guidelines, processes, and documentation is like dumping a bunch of IKEA components on the floor and saying 'Here, build a dresser!'\\\" Barry finds this analogy itself troubling rather than reassuring. The IKEA comparison, meant to underscore what design systems need to succeed, inadvertently exposes what they assume: that design is a matter of assembly rather than judgment. Barry argues this framing devalues design by making it appear fully actionable by people who are not designers at all. One practical consequence of that devaluation is organizational. Barry contends that design systems tend to be adopted only by the designers who created them, while the broader teams they were meant to serve either ignore them or find them irrelevant to their actual working conditions. This echoes a point made in a piece titled \\\"Design systems in difficult places,\\\" where an unnamed contributor observed that \\\"The challenge with any design system is they normally don't work, don't get adopted, don't grow or get used if they are imposed top-down without an awful lot of consultation.\\\" The gap between a system's ambition and its uptake is rarely acknowledged by its advocates. Barry's own professional history gives the argument a grounded texture. He worked at a startup that went through multiple rebrands and product pivots within its first three years, an environment where any rigid system would have become a liability rather than a foundation. That kind of rapid change is common in early-stage product development, and it exposes a structural problem with design systems. When design is fused tightly with code, changes to the product are slowed or quietly discouraged because the cost of updating the system feels too high. The single-point-of-failure problem compounds this. A bug introduced into a shared design system does not stay contained; it propagates across every product touch point that draws from the same source, multiplying the damage in ways that a more distributed approach would not permit. Barry draws a comparison between design systems and corporate brand guidelines, describing both as equally tedious and equally ignored in practice. The parallel is deliberate: brand guidelines were supposed to enforce consistency too, and they largely failed to do so for the same reasons. The deeper issue is one of values. Barry argues that design systems prioritize consistency and convenience at the expense of creativity and craft, trading the harder qualities of good design for the easier ones that are simpler to document and enforce. His alternative centers on coherence rather than consistency. Coherence, in his framing, allows for contextual deviations where a design decision serves the specific moment rather than the system's rules, producing work that feels considered rather than templated. The GOV.UK Design System serves as a telling case study in the difficulty of making these systems actually function. Its contribution model took 2 years to develop and was still not complete, a timeline that illustrates how much invisible labor design systems demand beyond their initial build. Barry's preferred values form a clear set of priorities: communication over documentation, craft over convenience, creativity over uniformity, coherence over consistency, and context over everything else. Each pairing names what design systems tend to suppress in favor of what they reward. The documentation problem is particularly pointed. Design systems generate enormous amounts of written guidance, but Barry's framework treats communication as the real goal, something that documentation can obstruct when it becomes the primary artifact of design work. Craft is similarly crowded out. When components are pre-built and decisions are pre-made, the designer's role shifts from making considered choices to selecting from an approved menu, a change that Barry sees as a meaningful erosion of what design actually is. Nassim Taleb's thinking about fragility and systems informs Barry's broader skepticism. A system designed for efficiency and uniformity can become brittle precisely because it has removed the variation and redundancy that allow for resilience under real conditions. The argument does not call for chaos or the abandonment of shared tools. It calls for honesty about what design systems cost: the creative friction they eliminate, the contextual judgment they override, and the false sense of order they produce in organizations that still need designers to think.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"There are 13 days left before the writing part of the Blog-Your-Own-Book Challenge starts. I’m going to give you homework tonight that you might hate me for now, but you’ll really thank me for it later. First, a story. I’m a novelist. Before my first novel was published, I wrote three novels that were not. I was a straight-up pantser when I wrote those books. I was sure plotting would eat my story’s soul. That it would make it impossible for me to write my book the way I wanted to. After my first novel was published, my editor did something that changed the way I write forever. She asked me to send her a synopsis for a novel I hadn’t written yet. And she expected it by the end of the month. I had to learn to plot. Fast. I did and the result was a book that A) was the best thing I’d written up to that date and B) I was able to write in one quarter of the time I was used to taking. One quarter. I’ve plotted every book I’ve ever written since then. I’ve never looked back. The PSST Framework is Like Blog-Post Plotting All of that is to say that I use a framework for my blog posts for the same reason I plot my novels. It’s faster. It’s so much easier. And, it turns out, it’s actually pretty fun. I like thinking about my blog posts and imagining what they might be. PSST stands for Premise, Story, Subheads, and Take-away. I actually have a page in my Rocketbook that I used a Sharpie on to make a form that I literally fill out before I write each post. The PSST page in my Rocketbook. Photo: Author I use a Frixion pen to fill out the form for every post. A little water erases it, but leaves the Sharpie template. If I end up not actually writing my post that day or I want to create a bunch of PSST forms for posts, I just take a picture of them. Like the one above, which is for a post I didn’t actually write. Yet. The parts are so simple. Let me break it down for you. Premise Premise is just a ‘p’ for ‘working title’ — because I thought PSST Framework was cute. Just write what your post is about in a few words. Don’t be cute or clever. Think newspaper headline. You need to be able to look back on your post framework and know what you were wanting to talk about. This is a good exercise, too, because simple, clear, concise titles do well for blog posts. Story I firmly believe that every every blog post should start with a story. In the first ‘S’ box on my form, I write a sentence or two about which story I’ll tell in that particular post. I write enough to remind myself of the story I want to tell, but really only a few words. I don’t spend too much time here and I pretty much never second guess myself. If I get ready to write and a better story comes to me, I write that one instead. But most of the time, my instincts are pretty good. I bet yours are, too. We’re storytellers. Trust that. Subheads This is pretty much the body of your post in a series of sentences. Just think about what you want to say about your premise. It helps me to ask myself ‘what is the first thing some one needs to know’ and then I ask ‘and then what’ until I reach the end. Take-Away Your take-away is the way you end your post. What do you want your readers to take from it? What do you want them to do after they read your post? How do you want to change them? Your Homework Okay, so a few days ago I asked you to come up with your 31 posts for your BYOB Project.\", \"sampled\": \"Data journalism sits at the intersection of reporting and rigorous quantitative analysis, drawing on a discipline that Alex Howard defined as \\\"gathering, cleaning, organizing, analyzing, visualizing and publishing data to support the creation of acts of journalism.\\\" That definition captures something important: the work is not simply about having numbers on hand but about moving those numbers through a structured process that ends in a story the public can understand and use. The practice has roots that stretch back much further than most people assume, with Florence Nightingale producing influential data-driven work in 1858 and John Snow completing his now-famous Cholera Visualization around the same period. Both figures demonstrated that carefully collected and clearly presented data could shift public understanding in ways that conventional narrative reporting alone could not accomplish, making them early models for what the field would eventually become. Ben Casselman of the New York Times has articulated the practical value of data for working journalists in concrete terms, noting that data helps journalists verify claims, tackle bigger stories, find new stories, illuminate murky issues, and report more efficiently. Those five functions cover a wide range of editorial situations, from the quick fact-check that prevents a published error to the long investigation that exposes a systemic problem hidden inside thousands of rows of records. One honest characterization of the data journalism process describes it as 80% perspiration, 10% great ideas, and 10% output, a ratio that reflects how much of the work happens before any story is written or any chart is published. The bulk of the effort goes into locating, cleaning, and checking data, and that labor-intensive phase is precisely what separates a credible data story from one built on shaky foundations. Datasets themselves can come from very different places: some arrive ready-made through government portals or research institutions, while others must be constructed by extracting information from multimedia files, conducting interviews, or carrying out original analysis and research. The choice of source shapes everything that follows, because data gathered through interviews carries different reliability considerations than data downloaded from an official statistical agency, and both types require the journalist to think carefully about provenance. The Guardian has noted that data is not a force unto itself, does not speak for itself, is not power by itself, is not a perfect reflection of the world, and is not easy to interpret, a list of cautions that pushes back against the idea that numbers are inherently authoritative. Alongside that warning sits the related fact that not all official data sources are reliable, accurate, or correct, which means the credibility of a source's institutional standing cannot substitute for independent verification of the data itself. Mona Chalabi described her own editorial filter for deciding when a data-driven piece is worth pursuing: \\\"I ask myself whether the numbers I'm looking at will start a new conversation that people aren't having yet, or if they will add to an existing conversation in a valuable way. If the answer to both of those questions is no, then I don't bother to write the piece.\\\" That standard keeps the focus on genuine public value rather than on the novelty of having data available. Once the analysis is complete, visualization becomes a critical stage in its own right, requiring deliberate decisions about colors, graph types, and scales that can dramatically change how readers perceive the underlying information. Tools such as Tableau, Power BI, Python, and R each offer different capabilities for this stage, and the choice among them depends on the complexity of the dataset, the technical capacity of the newsroom, and the format in which the final piece will be published. The data journalism process can unfold as either a long investigative effort or a shorter one that covers only the key data points a story requires, meaning the discipline is not reserved for large teams with months of runway. Whether the project runs for weeks or a few days, the same core obligations apply: find reliable data, interrogate its limitations honestly, and present the findings in a form that readers can actually follow and evaluate.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The Simplest Form of Mindfulness Is Also the Most Effective Most of us are cognitively aware that our bad habits are, well, BAD. The challenge is that our prefrontal cortex (front section of the brain) that is responsible for this awareness is the first to go offline when we get stressed. When this brain center logs off, we start to feel ‘numb’, give in to our stress, and fall back onto negative habits. Let’s go back to the 3 pillars that form a habit one more time. You experience a trigger. You respond with a behavior. You receive a reward. Say you’re battling the urge to have that fourth slice of pizza. You’ve already experienced the trigger that’s telling you, “Yes, this is a great idea…you’ll feel so good after”. What if we took a split second here to pause before caving in? What if we were to use curiosity as a tool to disrupt the cycle? Here’s what it looks like: You feel a trigger that spurs temptation. Instead of caving in, you pause for a moment and get curious. Ask yourself…what’s going on? Why am I feeling this way? From here you have two possible routes, both of which are a step in the right direction. The first path is that you still give in to your bad behavior, but with a heightened state of mindfulness. The act of caving in feels much less satisfying. You become consciously aware that the reward is just a guise covering up the reality that you’re in an endless loop of going nowhere. In one of Brewers’s studies, a subject involved with smoking admitted that mindful smoking, “smells like stinky cheese, and tastes like chemicals, YUCK!”. This paper eventually reveals that mindfulness training may confer benefits greater than those associated with current standard treatments for smoking cessation. No money, drugs, or therapy required. The second route is when things get really exciting. This is when you experience the trigger, but you’re now inherently aware that this is a poor choice and you take a halt. This is how you move from knowledge to wisdom. We’re all cognitively aware that bad habits aren’t good for us. Curiosity bridges the gap to allow us to be mindful of this, even when we’re stressed out or ‘in the act’. When you start to implement this curiosity technique into your behaviors, bad habits simply become less enchanting. You start to wise up and realize that you don’t need to give in to every trigger that comes your way. An amazing bonus to this mindset is that curiosity in itself is rewarding. Being mindful amidst your stressed-out state will deliver the prize that you were initially seeking from your ‘drug’ of choice. It may not be as powerful of a hit, but over time, your brain will realize that it’s the one you need. This is a rewarding, sustainable, and life-changing way to think about human behavior. Will it solve all of your problems instantly? Absolutely not. Many addictions are as much physiological as they are psychological. At times, it may be necessary to get professional help. Regardless, this is a powerful tool for initiating an important conversation with yourself. It is one that empowers you to ditch the dissociation and start attacking your bad habits head-on.\", \"sampled\": \"Faith is not a license to treat people badly. Crystal Jackson, writing from the southern United States, argues that many religious people have centered their faith around hate rather than love. She observes this pattern most clearly in Christianity, where the loudest voices often seem more invested in judgment than in the grace their tradition claims to prize. The gap between what a religion teaches and how its followers behave is not a small one. Jackson defines a \\\"Jesus card\\\" as the belief that a religious identity gives someone a pass to behave badly toward others. No such pass exists. No tradition grants it. No scripture supports it. She points to Galatians 5:22-23, which lists the fruits of the spirit as love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control. These are not suggestions tucked away in a footnote but the described markers of a life shaped by faith. The list does not include contempt. It does not include cruelty. The argument is not confined to Christianity alone. Jackson traces a shared ethical principle across multiple traditions, finding the same core instruction repeated in Buddhism, Confucianism, Hinduism, Islam, Judaism, and Native American Spirituality. The principle holds across centuries. It holds across continents. Matthew 7:12 in the King James Version states, \\\"Therefore all things whatsoever ye would that men should do to you, do ye even so to them.\\\" This is the Christian articulation of the Golden Rule, and it leaves no room for carve-outs based on who the other person is or what they believe. The Islamic version appears as Number 13 of Al-Nawawi's Forty Hadiths, attributed to Muhammad, and carries the same weight. The Hindu tradition draws the same boundary at Mahabharata 5:1517. Leviticus 19:18 puts the Jewish version plainly: \\\"Thou shalt love thy neighbor as thyself.\\\" The Buddhist tradition sources a parallel instruction at Udana-Varga 5:18. Across all of these, the number of traditions that carve out an exception for cruelty is zero. Jackson notes that even Satanists hold standards around treating others with compassion, which makes the behavior of some self-identified Christians harder to excuse. The presence of that standard across traditions, including ones most Christians would reject outright, undercuts any claim that faith justifies contempt. The Great Law of Peace, cited as the Native American Spirituality articulation of the same principle, states, \\\"Respect for all life is the foundation.\\\" That framing does not leave room for conditional respect, the kind that applies only to people who look, believe, or live the way you do. Jackson takes the cross-traditional consistency of this principle seriously. Jackson does not position herself as someone who has always gotten this right. She acknowledges personal moments of being guilty of treating others poorly, which keeps the essay from reading as a simple indictment of other people. The article was published on Medium on October 8, 2019, and the argument it makes draws on verse references from Galatians 5:22-23, Matthew 7:12, Leviticus 19:18, Mahabharata 5:1517, and Udana-Varga 5:18 to show that the Golden Rule is not a Christian invention but a human one. No tradition listed grants its followers permission to be unkind. Jackson's core claim is that faith, when used as a weapon against others, has been separated from its own roots.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"When You Feel like You Can’t Live up to Your Own Writing Late night self-reflections of a writer on book deadline A page from my journal that night. I was rolling around in bed at 2 am. My mind was racing with outstanding tasks, unwritten paragraphs, and self-doubt. Together with my co-author John Fitch I’m in the final stages of writing a book about the importance of Time Off. And we are on a rapidly approaching deadline to hand the draft over to our editor. Many authors I talked to in the past told me that they are a mental mess in the days before handing off their manuscript, but I hoped I’d be immune to this. Especially given the topic we are writing about. But there I was, unable to sleep because my mind couldn’t stop worrying about the book. And at a deeper layer, worrying about the worrying. So I did the only thing I could think of at the moment to calm down my mind. I got up and put my thoughts to paper, writing the following words in my notebook to reassure myself of what I am doing. It helped me a lot. Maybe if you are in a similar situation, it can help you too.\", \"sampled\": \"Code review is one of the most debated practices in software engineering, yet its core purpose is often misunderstood. Nick Bull wrote a guide on what code review is actually meant to accomplish. The piece appeared in Better Programming, a publication on Medium. It was published on October 23, 2020, and draws on best practices from 5 major tech companies. Those companies are Apple, Google, Microsoft, Amazon, and Facebook. Many engineers treat code review as a checklist exercise, focused on confirming that changes work, that style guides are followed, and that no regressions have been introduced. Bull argues that while those checks matter, they are not the point of the process. The real purpose, according to Bull, is to ensure the solution solves the problem in the best possible way, taking into account both code quality and product considerations. That framing shifts code review from a gatekeeping task into a collaborative problem-solving conversation. Checking for regressions and style guide adherence are considered necessary parts of the review, but treating them as the goal misses the broader function that code review serves.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In honor of my birthday coming up, I decided it might be fun to bestow what little wisdom I have. You’re probably wondering why you should listen to the ramblings of a 20-year-old? Well, me too. Hopefully, there is something here for you. In 20 years I’ve had the pleasure (and often displeasure) of learning some hard lessons. 1. Don’t Settle Don’t go for junk food when you can easily cook a healthy meal that will boost your mood and immune system. Don’t settle for the guy who doesn’t answer when you call. Don’t settle for fake friends. Don’t settle for anything less than you deserve. 2. Be Yourself It took me longer than I’d like to admit to learn this one, but I’ve always struggled with showing people a version of me, that well… wasn’t me. Always put your best self forward, and never let anyone make you feel bad for who you are. 3. Work Smarter… and Harder I’m all for finding an easier way to accomplish a task, but I’m also a firm believer in you get what you put in. Never lose sight of your end goals, and always put in 110% effort. 4. Your Journey is Yours Never let someone else’s life make you feel like you’re behind on yours. Everyone moves at their own pace. Some people may graduate college in 3 years, some may graduate in 8. You’ll reach your goals when you’re meant to. 5. Relax and Enjoy Speaking of your journey, enjoy it! Life is to short to only focus on the bad. You’ll be a lot happier if you relish in the good things. 6. Loosen Your Grip No one, in the entire world, has control over every aspect of their life. There may be some people that portray that image, but it’s an act. Focus on what you can control, and let go of what you can’t. 7. Family Isn’t Always Blood You don’t have to be related to have a family bond with someone. The people who stick by you through the good, the bad, and the ugly can be better for you than your blood relatives. 8. You Lose Some, You Gain Some People come, and people go. If they want to leave, let them. 9. Turn Rejection Into Motivation You’re going to face a lot of rejection in this lifetime so you might as well use it for good. Take every “no,” and let it push you to work harder. 10. We Aren’t All The Same, and That’s Okay You’re going to meet people that disagree with you. They’re going to disagree on small things, they’re going to disagree on huge, fundamental, things. It’s okay to disagree without fighting. 11. Be Kind This one’s a little cheesy, but it’s so true. Simple kindnesses make a big difference in other people’s lives and a big difference in yours. Never forget, you get what you give. 12. Give Yourself Permission To Be Lazy Every once in awhile we all need to lay around and do absolutely nothing. Don’t feel bad for letting yourself rest, but don’t forget to pick yourself up off the couch the next day and get back to work. 13. Your Opinion is King Not everyone is going to like you or the things you do. It doesn’t matter. If you’re happy, then it’s right for you. Don’t let other people control your happiness. 14. Read as Much as You Can Reading can change your life and change your mindset. Even if you hate reading, listen to an audiobook. There’s so much in this life to learn and experience. Books are a cheap vacation in another world. 15. Jump At The Chance To Learn Take that online course, go back to college, listen to an educational podcast, watch the news. Every time you have an opportunity to learn something, take it. It will help you grow and allow you to help others. 16.\", \"sampled\": \"Procrastination stands out as one of the biggest challenges people face when trying to stay productive each day, and the problem is not simply a matter of poor planning or bad habits. Renata Gomes argues that discipline and willpower are far more reliable tools for beating procrastination than waiting around for inspiration or motivation to arrive on their own. The reasoning behind this position becomes clearer when you consider how motivation tends to fluctuate without warning, making it an unstable foundation for consistent work. Discipline, by contrast, operates independently of how a person feels on a given morning, which is precisely why Gomes treats it as the more dependable of the two forces. One concept that sheds light on why procrastination takes hold so easily is Parkinson's law, which holds that \\\"work expands so as to fill the time available for its completion.\\\" A task that would normally take three hours can quietly stretch to fill an entire week if a week is what has been allotted for it. Gomes characterizes Parkinson's law as sitting closer to a humorous saying than to a scientifically proven principle, placing it in a different category from Newton's laws of physics or even the more tongue-in-cheek Murphy's law. Still, the observation carries practical weight because most people have experienced exactly this kind of time expansion in their own work lives. The core remedy Gomes proposes is a simple 10-minute exercise that involves setting a timer and working exclusively on a chosen task for that entire duration without stopping. The exercise originated as a creative writing technique, but Gomes is clear that it can be adapted to suit many other kinds of work beyond writing. Gomes first encountered this 10-minute approach in a creative writing class, where it was used to push writers past the inertia that so often accompanies a blank page. The fact that the technique transferred well from that specific context into broader productivity advice suggests its value is not tied to any single type of task. The rules during those 10 minutes are strict: checking a phone is not allowed, and browsing the internet is equally off-limits for the duration. Removing those distractions is not incidental to the exercise but central to it, because the goal is to create a brief window of complete, undivided attention on one thing. What makes the exercise particularly interesting is what tends to happen once the timer goes off and the 10 minutes are finished. Gomes claims that momentum typically carries the person forward, keeping them working well beyond the point where they were technically free to stop. That observation about momentum connects to a broader point about how starting is usually the hardest part of any task, and how a short, low-stakes commitment can quietly dissolve the resistance that procrastination feeds on. Once the initial friction is gone, continuing feels far less effortful than it did before the timer was set. Deadlines play a related role in this framework, and Gomes describes both self-imposed and external deadlines as genuinely helpful for boosting productivity. Whether the deadline comes from a manager or from a personal commitment written in a notebook, the effect of a time constraint on output is real and worth deliberately engineering. The pomodoro technique is mentioned as a popular productivity strategy that shares some structural similarities with the 10-minute exercise, particularly in its use of timed intervals to organize focused work. Both approaches treat time limits not as pressure but as a practical mechanism for making work feel more contained and therefore more approachable. Taken together, these ideas point toward a view of productivity that relies on structure rather than mood, and on concrete time constraints rather than open-ended stretches of available hours. The 10-minute exercise, Parkinson's law, and the role of deadlines all reinforce the same underlying logic: that the shape of the time given to a task has a direct effect on how that task actually gets done.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"When your data needs to get dressed up, Tableau is a fool-proof style service. It offers a sleek, drag-and-drop interface for data analytics with native integration to pull data from CSVs, JSON files, Google Sheets, SQL databases, and that back corner of the dryer where you’ve inevitably forgotten a sock. Data is automatically separated into dimensions (qualitative) and measures (quantitative) — and presumed to be ready for chart-making. Of course, if there are still a few data cleaning steps to be undertaken, Tableau can handle the dirty laundry as well. For example, it supports re-formatting data types and pivoting data from wide to tall format. When ready to make a chart, simply ctrl+click features of interest and an option from the “Show me” box of defaults. This simplicity of interaction enables even the most design-impaired data scientist to easily marshal data into a presentable format. Tableau will put your data into a suit and tie and send it to the boardroom. Follow these tips to go from “good” to “great” in your data visualization abilities. Gain inspiration from master chart-makers Throughout my time as a business analyst at a Big Four firm, these three blogs were my go-tos for how to create a great looking, functional Tableau dashboard. Keep these 4 guidelines in mind #1 — Sheets are the artist’s canvas and dashboards are the gallery wall. Sheets are for creating the artwork (ahem, charts), which you will then position onto a dashboard (using a tiled layout with containers — more on this in a second) along with any formatting elements. #2 — To save yourself time, set Default Properties for dimensions and measures. This will provide a unified approach to color, number of decimal points, sort order, etc. and prevent you from having to fiddle with these settings each time you go to use a given field. #3 — Along those lines, make use of the overarching Format Workbook and Format Dashboard options instead of one-off formatting tweaks. #4 — Avoid putting floating objects into your dashboards. Dragging charts around becomes a headache once you have more than two or three to work with. You can make your legends floating objects, but otherwise stay away from this “long-cut.” Instead, use the tiled layout, which forces objects to snap into place and automatically resizes if you change the size dimensions of your dashboard. Much faster and simpler in the long run. Get started with your first dashboard In summary, the Tableau platform is easier than finger paints to use, so if you’re ready to get started, Tableau Public is the free version that will allow you to create publicly accessible visualizations— like this one I put together after webscraping some info on questionable exempted developments from the Washing DC Office of Zoning — and share them to the cloud. Getting ready to present financials to the C-suite. Photo by Lisa Fotios on Pexels.\", \"sampled\": \"Rasheed Hooda published his first post on Medium on October 25, 2019, and exactly one year later, on October 24, 2020, he published his 400th story. That pace of output signals a serious commitment to showing up on the platform day after day, yet the financial return tells a different story. Over that full year, his total earnings from Medium came to $359.94, which averages just under 90 cents per story. He originally planned to evaluate the return on investment after the first six months of 2020. When that milestone arrived, he postponed the reckoning because he was genuinely enjoying the process of making friends and gaining followers. The community and the daily interactions felt rewarding enough to keep going, at least for a while longer. The numbers, however, remained stubbornly small. Publishing 400 posts in a year is a massive output, and earning $359.94 for that effort works out to a figure that is hard to frame as a sustainable income. The average of just under 90 cents per story means that each piece of writing, no matter how carefully crafted, contributed less than a dollar to his bottom line. In October 2020, something shifted. He received a sizable non-writing reward from the Universe, an opportunity that arrived outside the boundaries of the Medium platform. That event forced a hard look at where his energy was going and whether the writing life was the right container for his talents. He enjoys writing, but he does not feel passionate about it. His preferred communication modality is verbal, not written, which means the daily act of typing out posts was never a natural fit. The work was possible, and he did it consistently, but it never lit a fire that made him eager to sit down at the keyboard. He had previously enjoyed what he calls a Freedom Lifestyle by twisting balloons and giving them away, a simple and direct way of connecting with people. That path ended when 2020 turned out the way it did, and the forced stop left a gap that Medium writing partially filled. The balloon twisting was never about money, and the writing became a similar kind of outlet, though it lacked the same spark. A Turkish Proverb captures the logic of his decision: “Regardless of how far you’ve traveled down the wrong path, turn around.” After a full year and 400 posts, he decided to stop writing on Medium and explore a more fulfilling opportunity. The sunk cost of all those hours did not outweigh the clarity that arrived with the new reward. T. D. Jakes offered a framework that fits the moment: “If you can’t figure out your purpose, figure out your passion. For your passion will lead you right into your purpose.” Rasheed Hooda stepped away from the keyboard not because he failed, but because he recognized that his passion lives in a different kind of exchange, one that his year of writing helped him identify.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Failure To Recognize Adverse Situations Will Halt Advancement… Image Courtesy of Unsplash A positive mindset is a great thing to have daily to uplift yourself and others. No one benefits from negativity or how it can subdue your progress in any situation. Looking deep into our lives, one must who is surrounding me that can bring more of a positive spin as opposed to negativity in one’s life? To be quite frank, negativity can be a mainstay in any life if you let it consume you and stop your progress to reaching more of your goals and achievements than you think. Lacking the proper mindset to move forward towards a desired result is something that takes effort. Surrounding yourself by awesome people that are like-minded can be a great thing to you and your mindset. When someone is being negative, oftentimes they’re doing it because of some reason that has nothing to do with you. Those are usually the people that you really don’t want to be around or have a meaningful relationship with. I will show how negativity can be a burden and get in the way of moving forward in life and your daily routine. Self Limited Beliefs Life provides us with many emotions and no matter how confident that you might be, self-limiting beliefs can be a negative thing. Everyone has doubts when throughout life especially when trying to get something accomplished. Image Courtesy of Unsplash Getting down on yourself no matter what the situation is can be a natural occurrence with anyone no matter who they are or what their persona might be. If you’re trying to accomplish a goal and you reach a roadblock doubting yourself, self-limiting beliefs could be the reason. We all have that little angel on one shoulder and the proverbial devil on the other shoulder giving us conflicting information about our next move. A positive mindset to the contrary can feed us great things and give encouragement to keep us going. The devil on the shoulder that’s putting in way more work than the angel on the other shoulder. The devil on one shoulder is telling you that it can’t be accomplished but at some point, you have to stop listening to it because of the negative energy that it creates. Fear of Learning or Furthering Education We all know the one person that doesn’t like to learn for one reason or another. The person that’s stuck in their ways and just can’t seem to get their feet out the quicksand to learn something new. It could be a common element that may or may not be true with you personally but you probably can relate because we all know someone like that. Sometimes learning something new can be a great thing and it can advance you in a lot of different ways whether it’s a relationship, new skill for a job or just something that could get you what you want in life. I had to do things to further my education too and I’m not just talking about school or college or anything like. I learned how to code, write tables and queries in a database better, build websites, etc.\", \"sampled\": \"CAPTCHA stands for “completely automated public Turing test to tell computers and humans apart.” The test was designed to block bots by asking people to prove they are human through puzzles that machines could not solve. Improvements in artificial intelligence are making CAPTCHA challenges increasingly difficult for humans. As machine learning advances, the puzzles must become harder to stay ahead of automated scripts. Jason Polakis published a 2016 paper showing Google’s own tools could solve Google’s CAPTCHAs. His research at the University of Illinois at Chicago demonstrated that the very systems meant to stop bots were vulnerable to the company’s own technology. Machine learning now matches or exceeds human performance on basic text, image, and voice recognition. This shift means tasks once considered reliable proof of humanity no longer provide a secure barrier. CAPTCHA challenges discriminate against users with vision, hearing, or learning disabilities. A test that relies on seeing distorted text or hearing garbled audio immediately excludes people who cannot perceive those formats. Inclusive design requires content to be convertible into different formats, which CAPTCHAs prevent. A properly accessible website lets users transform information into modes they can process, but a visual puzzle blocks that conversion. Assistive technologies and bots use similar machine-learning AI, locking out disabled users. Screen readers and other tools rely on algorithms that overlap with bot capabilities, so sites often block both. The arms race between bots and CAPTCHAs is reaching a point where bots consistently beat humans. The puzzles have become so complex that legitimate users fail more often than the automated systems they are meant to stop. Jason Polakis described the situation clearly when he said, “Machine learning is now about as good as humans at basic text, image, and voice recognition tasks. We’re at a point where making it harder for software ends up making it too hard for many people. We need some alternative, but there’s not a concrete plan yet.” His warning highlights a growing tension between security and usability. The tools that once protected websites are now creating barriers that affect a fifth of the population with a disability. Google is developing reCAPTCHA v3, which analyzes broader user behavior instead of presenting challenges. The new approach monitors how people interact with a page to assign a risk score without interrupting the experience. Behavior-based bot detection may incorrectly flag disabled users who interact with websites differently. Someone who navigates with a keyboard only or uses a screen reader might generate patterns that look suspicious to the system. Robin Christopherson of AbilityNet has raised concerns about these unintended consequences. Writing for The Verge, he explained how well-intentioned security measures can systematically exclude people from digital services. The problem grows more urgent as everyday tasks move online. Banking, shopping, and government services all rely on bot detection that was not designed with diverse human interaction in mind. Stephen Hawking’s experience illustrated the gap between security assumptions and real-world use. A system that demands a mouse click or a visual puzzle fails the moment it encounters someone using alternative input methods. The search for a better solution continues without a clear roadmap. The current path leads toward tests that are either too easy for bots or too hard for the people they are supposed to protect.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The Neuroscience of Slowing Down to Accomplish More Rushing is a false economy Photo by Chinmay Singh from Pexels When I was little, my grandmother always told me, “Less haste, more speed.” I really didn’t get what she was trying to tell me. On first hearing it, I thought it was a contradiction to state that less going fast actually meant more going fast — I couldn’t wrap my head around the difference between haste and speed. But then I was the kind of kid that did everything at 100 miles per hour, and that was something I took into adulthood with me. In my first career in the hospitality industry, I was always rushing, and I was always super stressed. I had a team of staff underneath me, but I hated delegating, stuck in my belief that if you wanted something done right then you needed to do it yourself. I left that industry aged 29, exhausted and desperate for change in my life. I learned better When I decided on a career change and went to University, I studied Cognitive Neuroscience. I began to learn so much that explained who I was as a person, and why I had been so stressed and depleted in my first career. Knowing what I had been doing to my brain, I had to change my approach. Executive function is what separates us from the animals Our ‘executive functions’ are those more complex thought processes, like planning for the future and thinking in an abstract way. These executive functions separate us from the other mammals. When I was organizing the catering for big events, I was using a lot of planning and logistical thinking, and this was really quite tiring. Our working memory is a part of our executive functions, which are all associated with the prefrontal cortex. Working memory capacity As far as studies can tell, our working memory has a very finite capacity. It has long been understood that the average person can hold 7 items (problems or things to remember) in their working memory, with an average variation of -2 or +2. So to put that in simple terms; if you are trying to remember a few things to do later, while considering a couple of logistical issues, and then your phone rings and somebody needs you — your working memory is now maxed out and you can’t handle anything else. Why am I sharing this? Put simply, when your working memory is at full capacity, the effect on your cognition is similar to having too many browser tabs open on your laptop. Things start to slow down and your system becomes less efficient — maybe even overwhelmed. When we try to do too much at once, typically in a bid to save time, we end up depleting our reserves, slowing us down in the long run. We can sustain only very short bursts of this intense kind of working and thinking. If you’ve ever worked in the events industry then you will know the feeling of exhaustion after working a busy event. It’s almost like an ‘energy hangover’ for the next few days. But if you want to achieve more over a sustained period of time, then the science-backed advice is to keep things simple and not try to pack everything in at once. And if you need to be warned about the pitfalls of multi tasking, then check out this story on task switching and focus.\", \"sampled\": \"The Netflix docuseries High Score arrived as a six-part look at video game history, and I watched it with the same curiosity I bring to any deep archive of the medium. It sent me back to my own starting point, which was Duck Hunt on the original Nintendo Entertainment System, the first game I can remember playing. That memory sits inside a much longer timeline because I have owned almost every video game console released in the last three decades. The show made me realize how much my habits have shifted, since my gaming has slowed in recent years and I now often play titles three or four years after their release. Right now I am working through Shadow of the Tomb Raider, the third game in the rebooted series that reimagines Lara Croft for a modern audience. The reboot follows her trying to stop the secret society Trinity from destroying the world, a premise that promises globe-spanning adventure and ancient mysteries. I genuinely enjoy the exploration in Shadow of the Tomb Raider, from scaling overgrown ruins to discovering hidden chambers that reward patient observation. The problem is that I dislike the required killing, which interrupts the rhythm of discovery and forces me into extended gunfights I never asked for. The game insists that the only way to advance the story is to clear rooms full of armed enemies, and I find that limitation exhausting. I do not want to play any game where killing is the only way to advance the story, because it turns what could be a personal journey into a repetitive exercise in body counts. This frustration has been building for a while, and it recently pushed me back toward a very different kind of experience. I have been drawn back to No Man's Sky, a 2016 game focused on exploration, mining, and trading across a procedurally generated universe that feels genuinely endless. No Man's Sky provides open-ended choices, allowing players to avoid combat entirely if they prefer to chart planets, build bases, or simply drift through star systems in quiet solitude. That freedom is exactly what I need when a linear action game leaves me feeling like a reluctant mercenary. I keep thinking about how Lara Croft could be written to match the explorer she is supposed to be. I wish she could sneak past enemies instead of being forced into lethal combat, using her intelligence and athleticism to bypass threats rather than mow them down. The Tomb Raider series has always sold itself on discovery and ancient secrets, yet the reboot trilogy keeps locking progression behind mandatory shootouts. Every time I clear a wave of Trinity soldiers I feel less like an archaeologist and more like a soldier who happens to carry a climbing axe. Other games have proven that you can build tension and stakes without requiring the player to kill people. Horizon Zero Dawn is praised for its story and for featuring conflict against machines rather than people, which lets Aloy fight for survival without turning her into a mass murderer. That design choice respects the player's desire to engage with a world on their own terms, and it makes the narrative feel more thoughtful. When the enemies are mechanical creatures, every encounter can still be dangerous and spectacular without forcing the protagonist to take human lives. I keep returning to No Man's Sky because it trusts me to decide what kind of experience I want, whether that means peaceful cataloging or high-stakes resource runs. Games that offer that kind of agency remind me why I fell in love with the medium in the first place, back when a plastic light gun and a laughing dog were enough to make an afternoon feel limitless.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I’ve Learned to Receive Messages From The Universe The signs will be obvious to you if you just tune in Photo by Jen Theodore on Unsplash “One of your ancestors from Europe is watching over you,” said a woman to me when I was 19 years old, working at a chocolate store in Union Station in DC. She was buying a truffle from me, wearing a woven scarf, and looked at me with complete calm and sincerity. I was bewildered. I went home and called my grandmother, and she speculated about who in the family it could be. I felt strongly that it was someone from her father’s side. It was just a feeling. Since then, years later, I have now become very familiar with these ‘feelings’. When I have them, what used to seem like a hunch now seems like a complete certainty. I’ve learned to receive messages from my ‘third eye’, my intuition — the eye I have into the realm of knowledge that logic and science cannot explain. Anyone can receive messages from the universe. All you have to do is open yourself to it. The more you meditate, become aware, and tune into the energy around you, the more clear the messages will become. There are some things I’ve listed here to help you open yourself to the messages. Nothing is a Coincidence It’s hard to let go of the idea that the world you experience is dead, random, and meaningless. Growing up, this is what we learn. We live in a universe teeming with energies, and we take part in it, yet we deny this all the time. It interacts with us, and it is affected by our energy. Once you let go of the idea you were probably raised to believe — that ‘weird’ things that happen are coincidences — you’ll be ready to receive the messages that have probably been lost on you for your entire life. Have you heard a voice sometimes when you are the only one hearing it? Have you seen the future coming, but denied it because you think there’s no such thing as clairvoyance? Do you dismiss weird occurrences and instead, try to explain them logically? Most people block out their intuition because it doesn’t make sense in the world they are used to. Don’t be like them. Animals Carry Messages Early on, the messages I was used to receiving were always in the form of animals. I’ve come to view animals as spirits that are embodying qualities they want you to examine. If a bluebird lands on your shoulder and flies away, you are feeling freedom or about to take off. If you see a dead raccoon, then something clever and beautiful has died in you. All you have to do is look closely at what’s going on. Crows have always been very interesting to me. Their behavior can always be interpreted, and I often think it is an omen. Crows that surround you in the trees are definitely trying to make you aware of something. But they can also embody good energy.\", \"sampled\": \"My daughter arrived through an emergency C-section at 26 weeks, and she weighed only 2 pounds and 2 ounces. That moment began a chapter of my life I could never have prepared for, one that would test every idea I had about strength and fear. The pregnancy had already been a storm of uncertainty from the very beginning. At six weeks, my wife was told she had suffered a miscarriage, and we were sent home carrying a grief that felt absolute and final. A week after that devastating diagnosis, a doctor found a heartbeat during a follow-up visit. That same emergency room trip also revealed a tumor growing inside my wife, piling one impossible piece of news on top of another. The relief of the heartbeat was short-lived because at 20 weeks my wife’s water broke. We were told that holding the pregnancy for just four more weeks would give the baby a chance at viability, so we focused on nothing but getting through each day. A doctor sat me down and explained that babies in our daughter’s situation had a 1% chance of survival. I was alone when I heard those scenarios, which included the possible deaths of both my wife and my daughter, and the weight of that conversation has never fully left me. The emergency C-section at 26 weeks felt like the culmination of months of helpless waiting. When my daughter was born weighing 2 pounds and 2 ounces, I could not hold her for the first 8 days, and those days stretched into a strange, hollow eternity. She spent 149 days in the hospital before she finally came home. Those months were a blur of monitors, beeping machines, and the slow, fragile work of letting her grow strong enough to leave the sterile room she had always known. After her traumatic birth, I found myself wrestling with survivor’s guilt. I had walked out of the hospital with my life intact while my daughter fought for every breath, and that contrast settled into a quiet, persistent ache I did not know how to name. Survivor’s guilt is commonly seen among Holocaust survivors, war veterans, and disaster survivors. I learned that it can also take root in a NICU parent who watches a tiny body endure procedures and setbacks while he stands by, physically whole but emotionally fractured. I kept replaying the moment I was alone with the doctor, hearing those brutal percentages and the talk of losing them both. That memory became a loop I could not escape, even after my daughter’s monitors stopped beeping and we finally walked through our front door. My wife carried her own scars from the tumor discovery and the months of bed rest, and I watched her navigate recovery while I sorted through my guilt in silence. The 149 days in the hospital had ended, but the mental weight of those days lingered in our home. My daughter is here now, and her presence rewrites none of the terror but sits alongside it. The 1% chance, the 2-pound body, and the 8 days of waiting to hold her remain fixed points on a timeline that changed how I understand survival.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"“Naked girl, covered in Napalm. Five marines Raising the Flag, Mount Suribachi. V for Victory. They remember the pictures. Fifty years from now, they’ll have forgotten the war.“ Wag the Dog (1997) A picture is worth a thousand words, and in politics there are a lot of words. From the early cave paintings in Bhimbetka (dated 13,000 BC to 12,700 BC) humans have used visual methods to describe their own version of events in order to shape public perception. Religion began to spread through the illiterate parts of the world, largely due to the strong iconography that came alongside it. In the 19th century royal families, great conquerors and heroic battles were commemorated in elaborate, gigantic paintings, which solidified the way we think about those events and people hundreds of years later. In the 20th century, politicians used Conscripted Art and propaganda to control public opinion. Thus, when we talk about design in politics, we’re not just thinking about a candidate’s great logo or a movement’s compelling website, we’re considering all the visual elements that project a certain image and message. Reaching the Global Village In the 21st century, the age of the internet and social media, we find ourselves in a reality where not having a clear, strong, and– most importantly, memorable– visual identity is not an option. We all live in a global village, and the one language almost all of us speak is the visual language. As a people, we like to think we’ve come a long way since the days of shameless political propaganda and that we are much more suave now. We won’t be fooled by posters and political infomercials that are trying to bluntly paint a different picture from what we know to be true. The good news is that we are much more sophisticated than that. The bad news is that so are the campaigners. Social media has contributed massively to the proliferation of design in politics. It’s not just that we focus so often on visual elements as we scroll through media-sharing platforms like Instagram, Twitter, and Facebook; it’s also about the sheer volume of content that is being produced and the rate at which it’s shared. To keep viewers’ interest, you need a graphic language, templates, and constant production. In that way, social media allows smaller movements or campaigns to gain recognition on the basis of their graphic design presence: it’s not just what you say, it’s also how you look. The Medium is the Message America, in many ways, is a perfect example of how the relationship between design and politics has evolved in the digital era. Many have called Barack Obama “the social media president,“ since he was the first holder of the office to effectively utilize the tools offered by the internet. “The high production value and disciplined branding of the Obama campaigns deserve a great deal of credit for raising the standards of campaign branding, proving that great branding provides a powerful platform to build trust, tell stories, and engage the public imagination,“ writes Deroy Peraza for fastcompany.com. The Obama campaigns have also showed us once more that Marshall Mcluhan was right when he said in 1964 that “The Medium is the Message“; it is certainly true when it comes to politics.\", \"sampled\": \"Vertical farming grows crops in vertically stacked layers without soil or natural light, shifting agriculture into controlled indoor environments. The approach moves production from sprawling countryside fields into dense city centers, cutting transport distances and land use. LEDs, sensors, and robots manage lighting, climate, and harvesting inside these facilities, replacing sun and rain with precise automation. This tight control lets growers fine-tune conditions for each plant variety without relying on weather or seasons. The concept draws on a long history of protected cultivation that stretches back to Roman times, when early indoor methods sheltered delicate plants. Centuries later, greenhouses became widespread in Europe during the 17th century and eventually helped the Netherlands become the world’s second largest food exporter. Dickson Despommier popularized vertical farming as a way to feed millions while reducing carbon emissions, deforestation, and chemical fertilizer use. His vision took shape after looking at New York City and imagining skyscrapers filled with food instead of offices. A vertical farm in Japan harvests lettuce within 40 days and produces 21,000 heads daily from two towers. Each tower covers 900 square meters, and the operation runs year-round without pesticides, delivering fresh greens straight to nearby markets. Vertical farming provides year-round fresh vegetables without pesticides and with short farm-to-plate distances. That means produce can reach consumers within hours of harvest, keeping nutrient loss low and flavor high. The two main water-based methods used in these systems are aeroponics and hydroponics, which suspend roots in air or nutrient solutions instead of dirt. Both approaches recycle water continuously, using a fraction of what open fields require. Sensors and cameras collect data to enable real-time decisions improving plant health, growth, and yield. Machine learning models process this information to adjust light spectra, humidity, and nutrient mixes automatically. Statista projects the vertical farming market to be worth USD 6.4 billion by 2023, reflecting rapid investment in the United States, Japan, and other early adopters. The figure signals growing confidence that indoor agriculture can scale beyond niche leafy greens. The UN FAO states food production must increase by 70 percent by 2050 to feed a projected 9.1 billion people. Traditional farming alone struggles to meet that target while coping with soil degradation, water scarcity, and climate volatility. One US vertical farm claims a yield increase of 350 times over open fields, along with water usage of just 1 percent compared to traditional techniques. Such numbers highlight the efficiency potential when every variable is optimized indoors. High initial investment and electricity costs challenge the business model, and renewable energy could improve environmental claims. Critics point out that powering LED arrays and climate systems can undercut the sustainability promise if grids rely on fossil fuels. Indoor farming has existed since Roman times, but the current wave integrates artificial intelligence in ways earlier growers never imagined. The fusion of robotics, data analytics, and plant science marks a distinct break from simple greenhouse production. The Japanese facility’s daily output of 21,000 lettuce heads shows how dense urban production can supplement rural supply chains. It also demonstrates that leafy greens thrive under artificial light, though staple crops like wheat and rice remain harder to grow profitably indoors. Vertical farming reduces environmental impact by moving production from countryside to cities, yet its long-term viability depends on cheaper clean energy and broader crop diversity. The race to refine those economics is now unfolding across labs and commercial farms worldwide.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"One of the most interesting aspects of the Design Thinking process, is not only the strengthening of the relationships that are created within the Product Design team (including not only Designers, but an entire ecosystem where Developers, Product Owners, Customer Support Groups, Marketing Professionals, Inventory Managers, to name but a few, collaborate), but also of course with the Users/Clients, who become part of that product journey, not only uncovering the solution itself, but how that product eventually morphs and continues to live past launch/release cycles. Another rewarding aspect of the process itself has always been the imminent realization that whatever is uncovered, tested and refined, has to eventually be built, within a series of constraints. These constraints can be of multiple natures, be it financial, platform wise, resources, timelines, among others, all of which is going to be the focus of attention for this article. Constraints. Every Product Design initiative always has a series of constraints associated with it, something that should be clearly showcased when the process starts. Further constraints may be added to the scope of the initiative particularly as time goes on, but initially there’s already a listing of topics that all the participants in the Design Thinking process need to be aware of. Those general constraints are associated with a trifecta of factors: timelines, resources and finances. Expanding on these fundamentally operational constraints: timelines are part of the DNA of any project that is tackled. The need to release something to market, an MVP that is ultimately viable, which can be expanded upon, creates challenges from the Design process itself, in terms of running effective Research, Validation, Iterations, Testing exercises. All these phases provide different types of input into the solution that is being morphed, but once again Designers have to forcibly be strategic about how they devote time to each one of them (and how they bring different players to these phases, in order to gather the information they need to keep the process breezing along). Timelines are also deeply entwined with Resource allocation. Depending on the availability of team members, of different natures of course, allows for a lot of these processes and initiatives to be conducted more rapidly, and enable for iterative cycles for instance, to be produced in a speedier manner. Resource availability is also tied with different layers of expertise, not just from a Design perspective, but also from other professionals who are involved in the process, and need to be available to participate in it. That includes for instance, professionals in Product Ownership, Development, Customer Support, Marketing, Sales, Inventory Management, the list goes on, but summarily, they all have a pivotal impact in the solutioning effort, and their contribution should always be accounted for. Matter of factly: without enough resources, the challenges to fulfill & keep the timelines established can become a herculean task in itself. Deeply entwined with the previous two factors, there is of course the financial constraints that have implications across the board.\", \"sampled\": \"No-code tools have reshaped how founders build startups around a minimum viable product, offering a path that sidesteps the custom system headaches Joe Procopio endured when he launched his first company 20 years ago. Back then he had no choice but to construct a proprietary setup to track business data from scratch. Now a founder can assemble the same operational backbone in hours without writing a single line of code. A poll of entrepreneurs inside the Teaching Startup community revealed a telling gap. Almost half of the respondents admitted they only track sales, a practice Procopio likens to relying on sticky notes. That narrow focus leaves entire swaths of the business invisible. One well-financed startup he encountered was pulling in $6 million in monthly revenue yet burning $7 million every month because no one had wired cost tracking into the daily workflow. Every startup needs a single system of record for customers that follows them from the first prospect touchpoint all the way through churn. A spreadsheet can serve that purpose perfectly well as long as every customer gets a unique ID. The real danger is skipping the system entirely and operating on instinct. The discipline of a central customer record forces clarity. When a founder logs each interaction against that unique ID, patterns that were hidden inside email threads and memory start to surface. The spreadsheet becomes the startup’s operational memory, and it costs nothing to maintain. Early-stage teams should resist the temptation to build elaborate marketing dashboards before they have proven causation between spend and sales. Aggregate marketing data is enough at first. If a founder drops $100 on a digital ad campaign, the only question that matters is whether the customer system of record shows new prospects tied to that campaign. Granular channel metrics can wait until the link to revenue is solid. Sales data, by contrast, must be tied back to the customer record immediately and kept as real-time as the tools allow. The moment a payment clears in Stripe or a deal stage advances in a lightweight CRM, that change should land in the master spreadsheet. The no-code ecosystem makes those connections trivial. Zapier sits at the center of the operation, watching for events in Stripe, MailChimp, Quickbooks, Salesforce, or Hubspot and then updating the spreadsheet automatically. A new subscriber in MailChimp can trigger a row creation. A closed invoice in Quickbooks can flip a status field. Slack paired with Zapier adds a real-time notification layer so the whole team sees when a high-value customer moves or a churn risk spikes. The stack feels custom-built even though it is stitched together with off-the-shelf parts. These no-code pipelines are not meant to last forever. A startup that finds product-market fit will eventually outgrow the spreadsheet and the web of Zaps. The value of the no-code phase is that it forces the team to define exactly what data matters and how it relates. When the time comes to commission a custom system, the engineers will inherit a battle-tested data structure instead of a blank whiteboard. The temporary scaffolding becomes the permanent blueprint.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"It’s not really about being able to build a fire or carry a heavy pack over long distances, it’s a general feeling of competence that imbues you with a sense of confidence. And that realization of “I can do this” can carry over into other parts of life. Having accomplished one hard task, you believe you can problem-solve and persevere through other difficult situations as well. Now faced with the challenge of a global pandemic, it seems that many of us are brushing up on our practical skills, although they’re more Little House on the Prairie than My Side of the Mountain. And while knitting a scarf may not directly relate to surviving a deadly virus, it does provide a feeling of competence and control. Maybe we can’t ensure that our parents will be safe or we won’t lose our jobs, but we can turn flour, water, and yeast into bread and grow our own sustenance from vegetable trimmings and seedlings. And in gaining control over that facet of our lives, we gain a little control over the rest of our lives, too. “A lot of this pandemic and the things surrounding it are out of our control,” Robinson says. “It’s not our personal choice to stay at home, it’s not our personal choice not to go into the office. Homemaking tasks give you that bit of control and a sense of accomplishment.” A concrete feeling of accomplishment is something that many of us were lacking even before the novel coronavirus emerged, says John Hudson, chief survival instructor for the British military. As our lives have become more virtual, outdoor survival courses and shows like Naked and Afraid have risen in popularity over the last decade. Hudson, who’s applied his survival knowledge to the current situation in the e-book How to Survive a Pandemic, thinks a big reason for this appeal is the instant gratification that living off the land offers people. When the only evidence that the sacrifices we’re making through social distancing are working is that nothing happens, it makes sense that so many of us would yearn for a more tangible outcome from our efforts. “We spend an enormous amount of our working hours indoors, looking at a screen, answering emails … and we never really see an immediate or even any tangible result of our efforts,” he says. “Doing survival instruction, there’s a definite reward in my world if you complete a task. It’s almost an immediate reward because you know if you’ve succeeded or failed [in building shelter or lighting a fire] straight away.” When so much in our lives is uncertain, and the only evidence that the sacrifices we’re making through social distancing are working is that nothing happens, it makes sense that so many of us would yearn for a more tangible outcome from our efforts. “It may be out of boredom, but when we’re so anxious and you can cook, that’s an effort-based reward,” says Kelly Lambert, PhD, a professor of behavioral neuroscience at the University of Richmond.\", \"sampled\": \"When Piero Borrelli set out to uncover the small influence tricks that make someone truly loved in the office, he discovered that the entire playbook rests on a single, surprisingly simple secret. His exploration draws heavily on Dale Carnegie’s timeless bestseller How to Win Friends and Influence People, a book that has shaped workplace relationships for decades. The foundational principle, as Carnegie himself put it, is this: “You can make more friends in two months by becoming interested in other people than you can in two years by trying to get other people interested in you.” Borrelli, who works as a software engineer in the tech field, learned this lesson firsthand when he decided to approach a coworker with sincere curiosity about her design work. He asked thoughtful questions and listened carefully, not to steer the conversation toward his own projects, but to understand her craft and the principles that guided her decisions. That genuine engagement transformed a routine office interaction into something far more meaningful. The coworker noticed the depth of his interest and responded with an openness that surprised him, offering ongoing support and a standing invitation to seek her guidance whenever he needed it. Her reaction was immediate and warm. She told him, “I feel like you really like this, and I’m happy you listen with so much passion. Feel free to come back to me and ask me anything whenever your need it.” Those words confirmed for Borrelli that showing authentic interest creates a bridge that no amount of self-promotion can build. The experience with the designer was not an isolated experiment. Borrelli began applying the same interest-driven approach with people from every corner of the company, from sales to accounting to operations. He found that when he set aside his own agenda and focused entirely on what others could teach him, the barriers between departments started to dissolve. Colleagues who might have remained distant became collaborators, and the office felt less like a collection of silos and more like a network of willing teachers. Salespeople taught Borrelli influence techniques that no engineering textbook ever covered, showing him how to read a room and communicate value in ways that felt natural rather than manipulative. Accounting helped Borrelli understand how a company works at the structural level, revealing the financial rhythms and constraints that shaped decisions far beyond his own team. Those conversations gave him a broader view of the business, one that made his technical work more relevant and his contributions more visible. The pattern repeated itself across every interaction: the more he learned about others, the more they wanted to help him succeed. Being interested in others leads to knowledge, networking, and human bonding in ways that transactional office politics never can. Ralph Waldo Emerson once observed that people carry their own weather, and Borrelli’s approach proves that the climate of a workplace changes when curiosity replaces self-interest. By treating every colleague as a source of insight, he built a reputation not as someone who merely wanted to get ahead, but as someone who genuinely valued the people around him. That shift, he insists, is the real influence trick, and it costs nothing but attention.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"NEWSLETTER The CRISPR Nobel Prize & Data Stored in DNA Gets Destroyed This Week in Synthetic Biology (Issue #11) Receive this newsletter every Friday morning! Sign up here: https://synbio.substack.com/ Data, Stored in DNA, Gets Destroyed (But In A Good Way) DNA is promising for data storage, mainly because a single gram of DNA can store 256 petabytes of information. But there’s a problem: DNA is, in some cases, too stable. To remove a user’s data from DNA on “nucleic acid hard drives” of the future, scientists first need to develop better ways to selectively target, and destroy, DNA. A new method, published in Nature Communications, could be a contender for wiping DNA hard drives in the year 2087 (assuming the human race survives that long). DNA sequences, each containing a “True” barcode and one, or several, “False” barcodes, are first mixed together. Then, “truth markers” — DNA sequences that selectively bind to the “True” sequences — are added to the mixture. If the mixture is then heated to 95 degrees Celsius, the “truth markers” fall off and information from the “True” bits is lost. It’s a simple technique, with promising results. The researchers showed that “8 separate bitmap images can be stably encoded and read after storage at 25 °C for 65 days with an average of over 99% correct information recall, which extrapolates to a half-life of over 15 years at 25 °C. Heating to 95 °C for 5 minutes, however, permanently erases the message.” An enzymatic pathway has been assembled on top of a Tobacco mosaic virus. Using peptide “linkers”, the scientists, based in Hong Kong, tethered “three terpene biosynthetic enzymes” to the outer particles of the virus, and grew them inside of E. coli cells. The goal was to explore how the proximity of enzymes impacts the bioproduction of a molecule (in this case, amorpha-4,11-diene). Since the Tobacco mosaic virus is 300nm long, metabolic engineers could presumably use it as a foundation to assemble any number of complex enzymatic pathways. This study was published in Bioconjugate Chemistry. Deep Learning to Design RNA “Switches” Two studies, published in Nature Communications, used deep learning to guide the engineering of RNA “toehold switches”. These RNA switches are, basically, synthetic RNA sequences that can be turned ON and OFF. In their OFF state, the toehold switches form a hairpin loop and cannot be read by the ribosomes — they do not produce a protein. The addition of an RNA “trigger”, however, can be used to turn a toehold switch ON; translation can proceed. Toehold switches are especially useful because a lot of them can be present in a cell at the same time, and triggers can be designed for each of them. This means that many genes can be precisely controlled for synthetic biology applications. Unfortunately, toehold switches are really difficult to design, and even a slight tweak to a sequence can impact their utility. In the first study, led by Nicolaas M. Angenent-Mari, over 90,000 toehold switches were synthesized and tested using clever experiments that enabled the activity of each toehold switch to be individually assessed. After testing each of the RNAs, data was fed into a deep learning model (which I won’t even pretend to understand). The output from that model looks promising: “[Deep Neural Networks] trained on nucleotide sequences outperform (R2 = 0.43–0.70) previous state-of-the-art thermodynamic and kinetic models (R2 = 0.04–0.15).” In the second study, led by Jacqueline Valeri, two “deep learning architectures” were introduced — called STORM and NuSpeak — to improve the performance of RNA toehold switches. Both studies are open access, and represent a paradigm shift in how quickly synthetic biologists will be able to design and engineer biological molecules that behave as expected. The vast majority of plants on earth are C3 plants; when it’s hot or dry, C3 plants close their stomas, oxygen builds up, and the efficiency of photosynthesis goes down.\", \"sampled\": \"Decision fatigue is real. Your brain gets tired just like your muscles do after a long workout. A Columbia University study tracked 1,112 parole trials over 10 months. Researchers found something unsettling about how judges made life-altering rulings. The outcomes had little to do with the crime and everything to do with the clock. Every choice you make slowly drains a finite tank of mental energy. When that tank runs low the brain starts conserving resources by defaulting to the path of least resistance. In a courtroom that means denying parole because saying no is the easier, safer option. The study proved that the time of day mattered more than the severity of the offense or the prisoner’s background. At the very start of the morning session criminals had up to a 65 percent chance of receiving a favorable judgment. Judges who were rested and mentally refreshed evaluated each case with nuance and care. They weighed evidence, considered rehabilitation, and granted parole at a steady clip. That careful deliberation burned through their cognitive reserves hour by hour. The favorable judgment odds dropped to near zero before the first break. As the morning wore on the rate of parole grants collapsed toward zero because the judges were experiencing deep decision fatigue. Their brains were so depleted that the safest default was to simply maintain the status quo and keep the prisoner incarcerated. The pattern reversed sharply after a lunch break restored some mental energy. Suddenly the odds of parole jumped back up again only to begin their slow decline toward zero by the end of the afternoon session. The 1,100 cases covered a wide range of crimes including rape, robbery, and embezzlement. The trend held steady across every category which made the finding even more disturbing. The timing effect was not subtle. It was the single biggest predictor of whether someone would walk free or stay behind bars. The study stripped away any illusion that these were purely rational legal decisions. Instead the judges were operating on a dwindling cognitive battery that recharged only after food and rest. Mental fatigue does not announce itself with a yawn or a heavy eyelid. It quietly shifts your decision strategy from careful analysis to automatic shortcuts. You stop distinguishing between options and start picking whatever feels easiest in the moment. This is not a character flaw or a sign of weakness. The brain’s executive function relies on glucose and neural resources that get burned up with every choice no matter how small. When the supply runs low the quality of your decisions plummets. The Columbia University research revealed that even highly trained professionals with decades of experience are not immune. The judges believed they were ruling on the merits of each case but their brains were being hijacked by a biological limit they could not feel. You face the same invisible drain every day. The cumulative effect of choosing what to wear, what to eat, which email to answer first, and how to respond to a tense message all chip away at the same reserve. By late afternoon you are running on empty and your brain starts protecting itself by avoiding complex decisions altogether. The parole study offers a stark warning about trusting your gut when you are tired. Those judges were not lazy or indifferent. They were simply human. The fix is not to try harder or push through the fog. The only reliable way to protect decision quality is to structure your day around the brain’s natural rhythm. Tackle the most consequential choices early in the morning when your mental energy peaks. Schedule breaks that genuinely disconnect you from decision-making so your cognitive reserves can partially recharge. Batch low-stakes choices together and handle them in a single block rather than sprinkling them throughout the day. The goal is to spend your limited cognitive budget on the decisions that actually matter and stop pretending you have an infinite supply.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Meaning Eventually Finds Its Place In FKA Twigs’ Grandiose Artistic Vision Her latest LP “MAGDALENE” is an interstellar voyage through the dust of the broken heart to the planet of self During her long-lasting hiatus, the avant-garde pop princess FKA Twigs got crushed by the brutal hands of her troubled relationships — falling in love with the ex-blood sucker Robert Pattinson and splitting with him in a seemingly heart-wrecking manner. Two years following the end of this romance, twigs comes back feeling quite herself and ready to baptise her loyal fanbase in the career-defining shrift. In the pre-“MAGDALENE” era, British-born singer and songwriter Taliah Barnett took on a cunning challenge to manufacture the new RnB sound — blending her fragile-sounding soprano and the spooky art-house opera inspired imagery with the precision and inanimation of electronic music. Her efforts were met with universal critical acclaim, but looking in retrospect, the alien world twigs built around herself felt overwhelmed by her pompous visionary yet lacking raw human experience. In this sense, “MAGDALENE” comes through as a true revelation, being an album that runs on the fuel of imaginative lyricism and storytelling. Here, Twigs tries on the image of Mary Magdalene and tells us the love story with her very own Jesus, drawing inspiration from the chants of classical religious music and futuristic electronic sounds. The album starts with “thousand eyes” and, in an instance, enswathes you with a death-bearing sound of acapella choral signing and thud but heavy background instrumental, serving as a reminder of memorial liturgy music stripped off to its most naked condition. Alongside this soul-shivering melody, Twigs begins her Skaespherean story of spiritual death and thorny resurrection. If I walk out the door, it starts our last goodbye If you don’t pull me back, it wakes a thousand eyes Going forward, the narrative unfurls in a surprisingly cohesive manner, letting Twigs’ vocal arrangements and poetic talent shine the way they’ve never had before. No matter what it is — the antithesis of distorted chest voice and paper-light head voice on “home with you”, or the intimate insight into the secrets of womanhood on “mary magdalene” — Twigs serves us a bowl full of ripe-fruit she religiously gathered at the garden of Eden. There are many great moments on “MAGDALEN” — either lyrically, sonically or vocally — but the times it ascends to the sky-high levels lie at the intersection of the experimental search Twigs underwent in her previous work and the lucidity of pop-sound she tamed with the help of her fellow co-writers and co-producers. The result of this artistic confluence materialized in “sad day”, which might be remembered as one of the best pop tracks of this decade. Channelling Kate Bush ethereal timbre, Twigs starts the track by whispering simple yet beautiful lyrics into the isolation of her listener's auricles, and then the melody expands into this epic dance/electronic ballad where Skrillex’s production touch suddenly falls in place and electrifies the record. Bearing in mind how great the rest of the album is, it’s at best puzzling how “holy terrain” featuring Future made it to the final cut. It’s a commercially-baked, mediocre RnB song made to, perhaps, please the wider audience. A sad but not criminal oversight which gets forgotten as soon as the record ends. Despite being listed as the sixth track, “fallen alien” comes through as the culminating phase of the narrative. It’s the most experimental track on the album exposing Twigs in her most desperate state. She basically goes on a full-scale jihad against her former lover. Her vocals here are nothing less than transcending: incisive, hysterical, in a good sense of this word, and on the edge of breaking loose into a wild scream. The last three songs reveal Twigs meditating on the aftermath of relationships and reconciling with the damage left after it.\", \"sampled\": \"Andy Raskin met Christoph outside the Just for You Cafe in San Francisco. Christoph said, “I restructured our sales pitch using your Greatest Sales Deck framework and the results have been awesome. I’ve used it with a few prospects, and they engage at a deeper level, they get what we’re about faster, and they’re all moving to next steps.” The early-stage tech company CEO described a pattern that emerged almost immediately after he rebuilt the deck around a narrative arc instead of a feature dump. Prospects who had previously nodded politely through demos began asking sharper questions and volunteering their own challenges before Christoph even reached the solution slides. The shift in engagement felt so pronounced that he tracked it across every conversation that week. Christoph’s enthusiasm carried a quiet confidence that only deepened when he mentioned how the new flow changed the rhythm of his meetings. He no longer had to push through a list of capabilities while watching eyes glaze over. Instead the story pulled people into a shared understanding of why their current approach was breaking down and what a different future could look like. He paused after describing the momentum and added, “Well, it’s my VP of sales. He won’t even look at the new deck. Says he already has a deck, and that buyers don’t have time for a story, they just want to know how we solve their problem.” The VP’s objection was not unusual. Salespeople often push back on new decks because they inhibit discovery, replacing the organic back-and-forth of a conversation with a rigid slide sequence that feels like a performance. The tension Christoph faced is common inside growing companies. Sales leaders who have closed deals with a familiar deck rarely welcome a rewrite. The VP’s stance rested on a conviction that buyers are impatient and pragmatic, but that conviction ignored what Christoph had just witnessed in the field. Prospects who heard the story asked more thoughtful questions and moved to next steps faster than those who received the old product-centric walkthrough. The data from Christoph’s own pipeline contradicted the idea that storytelling slows things down. SpotMe, a company headquartered in Switzerland, uses “change in the world” slides to get prospects talking before the product even enters the conversation. Those slides frame a shift that makes the old way of doing things feel untenable, and they invite the buyer to describe how that shift is showing up inside their own organization. SpotMe’s customers see events as episodes in a greater journey, not self-contained shindigs, and that reframing changes the entire sales dialogue. When a prospect recognizes their own experience inside a story about change, they stop evaluating features and start imagining outcomes. The VP’s resistance was not about the deck’s effectiveness. It was about control. A narrative deck transfers some of that control to the buyer, who gets to co-author the conversation instead of receiving a monologue. For a sales leader who built a career on mastering the monologue, that can feel like a loss of leverage. Christoph understood the irony. His VP argued that buyers just want to know how the problem is solved, yet the new deck was producing exactly the kind of engaged, fast-moving buyers the VP claimed to want. The evidence was sitting in Christoph’s pipeline, but the VP refused to look at the deck that generated it. The standoff reflected a deeper split in how sales organizations think about time. One side sees a story as a luxury that busy buyers will reject. The other side sees a story as the shortest path to trust, because it mirrors how buyers actually make decisions. Christoph restructured his sales pitch using the Greatest Sales Deck framework, and the results spoke for themselves. The VP’s refusal to engage with the new deck did not erase those results. It just meant Christoph had to lead the change alone for a while longer.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Strategies for Learning New Skills Faster Like programming Learning any new skill has become easier than ever due to the internet. We are just a few clicks away from any information. Do you know that most people take an average of 25–30 days to complete a 30-hour course on Coursera/Udemy. Online MOOCs have a completion rate of less than 15%, which means that out of 1,000 people registered in a course, 850 of them never complete the course, and drop out. But why do people drop out in the middle of a course? What could be the reason? Is the course not good, or don’t we have enough motivation to continue? I conducted a survey of around 200 people, and here is what they say. Survey Response — 1 Survey Response — 2 Some key insights: More than 55% of people said they take more than one month of time. More than 70% of people have a course-completion rate of approximately 50% or less. Now, let’s see what the approaches are to learn any new skill faster.\", \"sampled\": \"Angela Friedman has lived through more than most people can imagine. She is 102. The New York resident has now survived Covid-19 twice, a feat that defies the odds at her advanced age. Her story begins long before the current pandemic. Her first encounter with the virus came in March 2020 while she was hospitalized for a minor procedure. That diagnosis was a frightening moment, but after a week in the hospital she was discharged and self-isolated until she recovered fully. It was a quiet, determined recovery that set the stage for what would come later. The second challenge arrived in late October 2020 when the nursing home where she lives contacted her daughter Joanne Merola. They reported that Angela had tested positive again and was showing symptoms. The news was a heavy blow for a family that had already navigated one bout with the disease. Her age made the situation even more alarming. She is 102. She was a baby during the 1918 flu pandemic. On 17 November 2020 she tested negative after isolation and treatment.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Today Google finally released the long awaited AI powered messaging app Allo. This messaging app is powered by the amazing Google AI which is a competitor of Apple’s Siri, Where Allo is prmarily text based and Siri is voice based. This app will also be competing with various other AI powered messaging apps such as the indian app Hike, China’s WeChat, Facebook’s M. So being that much of a geek and nerd to know all of the above like the back of my hand, I had to have this app ASAP so as soon as me and my friends heard the news and the app went live on the Play Store in India we all had it. We right away started testing out all the bells and whistles of the app, and one thing we all agreed on was damn the AI is good, because within 30 minutes of conversation in Hinglish (Hindi written with English Alphabets) between us and the AI had started predicting some of the basic stuff. And Damn we were impressed by that, because since forever we have been convinced that Hindi/Hinglish is a tough language to understand for an AI or a machine because of our experience with the AutoCorrect. The number of errors made by AutoCorrect in the US is not even close to the number it makes in India, and because of this we always end up either disabling it or set it to such a light mode that its nonexistent. So for an AI to start predicting words for to say in Hinglish such as “KYA?” correctly we were impressed. So we then started exploring more features, and we started playing with the google AI. We asked all the silly questions, all the good questions, all the funny questions and the AI never disappointed us. But during all this experimentation I realized something, the AI was predicting everything from what to say next to searching stuff that we wanted to discuss and stuff like that. For example I wanted to discuss about the girl in the movie Step Up, but obviously I did not remember her name, so my friend simply asked “@google Step Up 2 cast” and bam! google returned with the search results of this query. With the name of the girl. And then I wanted them to cross reference this information with a very old music video of theSong Numb by Linkin Park. Because today by chance saw that video and I was just shocked to see the same girl from Step Up 2 as the lead in the video. So the purpose of doing all this excercise was supposed to give my friends the shock and awe i experienced when i saw that girl in that old video which we all grew up listening to. But since we were discussing all this on Allo my dear friend simply asked google for it, and she just gave up the same girl’s name. So this event made me realize two things : Having the google search at your fingertips can be a boon to your conversation or it can simply ruin the effect you want to create while talking.\", \"sampled\": \"My experience with a previously diagnosed depressive episode gave me a reference point for identifying shifts in my mood. This prior knowledge was crucial because it allowed me to recognize the familiar signs of a returning depression. Using my Fitbit, I began to monitor the physical manifestations of my mental state, connecting the data to my feelings. My Fitbit statistics from one week in 2019 revealed a concerning combination of physical changes: I was losing weight, my workouts were unusually intense, my sleep was poor, and my resting heart rate had dropped significantly. This period was marked by prolonged, frequent crying spells that would occur daily and last for hours on end. A previous episode had involved crying every day for four months, with sessions initially lasting five to six hours before eventually reducing to one or two hours. During this more recent experience, I sometimes found myself waking up at 4 AM already crying, a stark indicator of the distress I was experiencing. These symptoms were all signs of a highly functioning depression. This form of depression can be particularly insidious and difficult for others to spot. It presents a facade of capability that masks the internal struggle. The internal experience was one of increased irritability and pervasive anxiety, creating a constant sense of being on edge. I felt hyper vigilant, as if my body was perpetually stuck in a \\\"fight or flight\\\" state, ready for a threat that never materialized. This state was exhausting. My capacity to reflect before reacting was severely diminished, which often led to impulsive actions and decisions. My ability to engage with and empathize with other people was also noticeably reduced, creating a sense of isolation. The effort required for social interaction felt insurmountable, further deepening my withdrawal from the world around me. The impact on my productivity was significant, as I found myself able to work for less than four hours each day. My cognitive functions were impaired. This decline in productivity created a backlog of commitments, including a staggering number of draft blog posts that needed completion. I had forty drafts from that one week alone that required attention. The pressure to keep up with my professional obligations clashed with my diminished capacity to perform. In early September 2019, the reality of my condition forced me to make difficult choices about my commitments. I had to cancel appointments and change plans that had been made during a healthier, more optimistic period of my life. This was a difficult but necessary step. To cope with the overwhelming emotional and psychological distress, I shifted my energy toward intense physical activities. I found a temporary release in pushing my body through running, cycling, and swimming. These intense workouts became a primary coping mechanism, a way to channel the turmoil I felt inside into physical exertion. This shift explained the unusual data my Fitbit was recording. The device was tracking both the symptoms and my attempts to manage them. During one week, my resting heart rate dropped significantly, a physiological marker captured by the device I wore daily. This data provided concrete evidence. This experience demonstrated how depression manifests physically. It affects sleep and heart rate. It changes how you move and work.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Use The 5/5/5 Rule To Unlock Your Focus And Build Up Discipline Four powerful questions to help you improve your life Photo by Jonathan Borba on Unsplash Feeling stuck? Maybe lacking focus? Need some more discipline in your life? I can help you out with that. And it won’t take much time at all either. Here’s how we can do it: perspective. Perspective is very powerful. It’s probably one of the most powerful things you can ever give yourself. It’s why you hire a coach or get a mentor to help you grow. They give you perspective that you cannot find on your own. Honestly, it’s even why many people hire a counselor too. That and sometimes we don’t trust ourselves fully. So, what if you could unlock a new perspective just with a few simple questions? The good news is you can. Allow me to share with you something I call the 5/5/5 Rule. It’s simple but also extremely powerful. Enter The Shockingly Simple 5/5/5 Rule I almost hesitate to call this a rule, but it really works. Just like the “Golden Rule.” It’s a good guide that you can just apply and use to get some results. So, without further ado, the 5/5/5 Rule is based on these 3 simple questions: How will you feel about something 5 minutes from now? How will you feel about something 5 months from now? How will you feel about something 5 years from now? You can apply these questions to pretty much anything. I’m going to use myself as example here, but I have a feeling I’m not alone with these struggles. Before we start, a disclaimer of sorts: you have to be totally honest with yourself. No sugarcoating, no half-truths. You must really be willing to dig deep and even dive back into your past in order to have the answer that you need in order to move forward. Okay, back to our regularly scheduled programming. Let’s look at a few examples of the 5/5/5 Rule in action. 4 Examples Of The 5/5/5 Rule In Action For example, you could ask… how will you feel 5 minutes from now if you eat that Oreo pie? Personally, I know I’ll feel like a bear that got shot with a tranquilizer dart (or two.) Let’s just say you won’t want to around me. The only options are to eat more of the pie or to immediately take a nap. So, the first question of the 5/5/5 Rule clearly tells me it’s best to not eat any of the Oreo pie. If I slip up and eat one piece, I know that I need to stop myself there, or I’m likely going to want more of the pie. The pattern has been established. I often rationalize and say, “It’s a special occasion,” or “It’s a small piece.” But those are just excuses that enable me to eat more than I should. Yes, I’m an Oreo pie addict of sorts. Some sweets just make me want more. If you struggle with sweets too, maybe you need to answer these same questions as well. How will you feel if you go on Facebook 5 minutes from now? I don’t know about you, but I’ll probably be distracted, annoyed, or envious. Anytime I have a very positive experience on Facebook it’s almost always on Messenger, connecting with a human being directly. That tells me it would be a much better idea to text or call a friend directly (or just use messenger) instead of going on Facebook.\", \"sampled\": \"A vision outlined in a 2019 speech paints a future of mass industrialization in space, a plan that details a different path for humanity. This perspective suggests a way to expand our presence beyond Earth without repeating the mistakes of the past and putting our home world at further risk. Instead of polluting and destroying our fragile world, this plan wants our factories to be placed in giant, orbiting structures far above the planet. This would move heavy industry off-world, allowing Earth's environment to begin a process of recovery from the damage it has sustained. Bezos imagines that humanity will eventually leave Earth as well, migrating to new homes built among the stars. This departure is not seen as an abandonment but as a necessary step for the long-term survival and flourishing of our species. Unlike others who picture colonies on Mars or the Moon, he believes we should construct our own habitats in space. These purpose-built worlds would offer more flexibility and control over our living conditions than adapting to the harsh environments of other planets. These structures, first imagined by physicist Gerard O’Neill, could be perfectly designed for human life, offering ideal climates and earth-like gravity. They would be self-contained ecosystems, capable of supporting large populations in comfort and safety, free from the constraints of a planetary surface. Unlike planets, which have a limited size and fixed resources, there is an almost endless supply of potential for these new habitats. This allows for virtually unlimited growth and expansion, providing homes for trillions of people without straining a single planet's ecosystem. In this vision of the future, the pressure we currently place on Earth would be slowly lifted as our civilization expands outwards. The environmental burden of supporting a growing population and its industrial needs would be shifted into the vastness of space. Manufacturing would be progressively moved off-world, and as a result, pollution on Earth would fall dramatically. The planet could then begin to heal, with cleaner air, water, and soil, returning to a more natural and pristine state. As people migrate to the new habitats in the heavens, wildlife on Earth could reclaim the planet, restoring ecosystems that have been damaged or destroyed. Vast areas of land could be returned to their natural state, allowing biodiversity to flourish once again. Eventually, Earth would become a massive park, a protected reserve for nature and a place for humanity to visit and appreciate. Our species would become truly space-borne, with our primary homes located in the sophisticated orbital colonies we have constructed. What Bezos imagines is a revolution on a vast scale, one that is comparable in its scope and impact to the dawn of agriculture thousands of years ago. It represents a fundamental shift in how and where humanity lives, transforming our relationship with our home planet. The development of rockets like New Shepard, New Glenn, and the planned New Armstrong is a critical part of this long-term strategy. These vehicles are the foundational technology needed to make access to space routine and affordable enough for such a grand vision. Blue Origin's efforts, including the Blue Moon lander, are designed to build the necessary infrastructure for this future. Each project is a step towards creating a permanent and self-sustaining human presence beyond Earth, laying the groundwork for the orbital habitats. This plan to take Blue Origin to the forefront of space exploration is not just about visiting other worlds but fundamentally changing our place in the cosmos. It's a detailed roadmap for moving our industrial base and, eventually, our population into space.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Creating a culture of innovation in retail’s era of disruption Innovation is the number one topic among successful retail executives in this Amazon era. Facing relentless competition and disruption on multiple fronts, retailers and brands know they must push toward intelligent advancement to stay a step ahead of their customers’ constantly evolving expectations and their shareholders’ demands. At its core, innovation means change, and progressive retailers and brands realize they must shift their strategies, processes and overall mindset. It’s essential for retailers to understand that they are not alone. This is the most challenging time in the industry’s history, and it is only getting more complex. While the path ahead is difficult at times, and not always clear, there are consistent strategies in place that characterize the most advanced and innovative retail organizations. Empowered teams, fast decisions Progressive companies that consistently deliver great results are generally operated by management teams that seek new opportunities through obsessively analyzing the details and data of their business. They tolerate the discomfort of change and incomplete knowledge, but compensate for that with nimble decisions based on a constantly updated view of their business and the market. Those that lean the furthest forward constantly look for tools and frameworks to give their entire team an advantage, not just management. They are on a quest toward improving their team’s ability to compete, innovate and repeatedly make smarter and faster decisions in a business that shows no signs of slowing down. Leaders at these companies empower their best people and seek to identify every wave of new talent with a full understanding of the power that technology can bring to amplify human decision-making. Additionally, they seek ways to multiply their team’s best efforts as they look for methods to reduce inefficiencies, waste and missed opportunities that arise from the natural biases inherent to every organization. A connected culture While an organization’s culture starts at the top, it also bubbles up from the bottom. In an industry where complexity, speed and competition are ever increasing, successful cultures value the actions of many independent decision-makers across the organization. Consistent and connected views of the business’s performance are critical to unite the organization across all levels, from the showroom to the boardroom. Once this information is communicated, employees can then interact on a set of interrelated subtasks that constantly drive to meet the plan. Focus on the future Innovative companies know how success is measured and are relentlessly evaluating areas of the business that are not aligned to meet those success criteria. They focus on future innovations and are never satisfied to rest on their laurels. Retailers and brands today welcome the challenges presented by the exciting, engaging and increasingly demanding consumer, as they are constantly driving innovation beyond what retailers had ever dreamed up. The best companies are never satiated. Innovative leaders must live in a constant state of restlessness that keeps the entire organization focused on being greater tomorrow than it is today. Technology that drives innovation Beyond consumer shifts, technology is the greatest single catalyst propelling retail forward at a blistering speed. The challenge for every retail executive is understanding the benefit of the technology and how it will impact their business as it relates to their objectives.\", \"sampled\": \"Mid-life burnout describes chronic stress that can apply to your life in general, not just work. Burnout can be difficult to spot because it develops slowly over time. Its symptoms are also similar to those found in cases of mild-to-moderate depression, which can make identification challenging. One of the key signs of mid-life burnout is a feeling of physical and mental exhaustion. Another sign is cynicism about your relationships and your work. People may also develop a feeling of being ineffective in their daily lives. These factors combine to create a significant sense of being overwhelmed and depleted. It can be hard to spot. People experiencing this condition might feel professionally stuck, as if their career has stalled or is no longer fulfilling. They may also experience an increase in conflict with their partners, a sign that the stress is spilling over into personal relationships. Mid-life is a common time for burnout to appear for a variety of reasons. Many people in this stage of life are often raising children while simultaneously caring for elderly parents. These demanding family responsibilities are frequently juggled alongside the pressures of advancing their careers. This combination of stressors from multiple directions creates a perfect storm for developing chronic stress and eventual burnout. This is a common time. Physical symptoms can also manifest, including erratic sleep patterns that leave you feeling unrested. You might also experience stomach problems or frequent headaches. Unexplained pains can also be a physical indicator of this condition. These symptoms show how mental and emotional stress can impact the body directly. Burnout can develop slowly. A recommended strategy for dealing with burnout is to take a deliberate break from your routine. Setting clear boundaries around the demands placed on your time is also a crucial step. Another effective strategy is to conduct a thorough review of your current schedule and daily commitments. The goal is to identify nonessential activities. Once identified, you should work to eliminate these unnecessary commitments from your life. This can help you. Carving out regular time for yourself is a suggested method for combating the effects of burnout. It is important that some of this time is spent completely alone, providing space for reflection. This practice can help you reconnect with your own needs and desires away from external pressures. Making this a consistent habit is key to its effectiveness in restoring your mental and emotional balance. Finding something that creates a genuine sense of excitement is proposed as a way to reintroduce positive emotion. This strategy is focused on actively seeking out activities or interests that can spark joy and enthusiasm. Finding a source of excitement can help shift your focus away from the sources of stress and towards something that brings you a sense of purpose and pleasure, acting as a powerful counteragent to feelings of cynicism and ineffectiveness. Reviewing your schedule and eliminating unnecessary commitments is one important step you can take. Taking a break and setting boundaries around demands on your time is also a useful strategy. Carving out regular time for yourself is another suggested approach. Finding something that creates excitement is proposed as a way to reintroduce positive emotion into your life. Entrepreneur Tim Ferris has stated, \\\"Excitement is the more practical synonym for happiness, and it is precisely what you should strive to chase. It is the cure-all.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"If you want to design places catered for humans, placemaking is the concept you want to utilize. It’s UX on an urban scale. We plan, design, and develop according to the user’s needs and preferences, to ensure that the output is actually useful and usable for them. In this case, the user is the community — the people. In their book How to Turn a Place Around, Project for Public Spaces (PPS) proposes 11 principles to help urban planners, designers, and developers make places people actually want to live in. Let’s dissect them one by one. 1. The Community Is the Expert To begin a placemaking process, it’s best to first identify the talents and assets of the community. In every community, there are “experts” who can present valuable perspectives and insights about the area’s history, culture, functionalities, or any other aspect that’s considered meaningful for the people. By placing the community as the expert, it will foster a sense of “community ownership” which elevates the place’s importance in the eyes of its residents. 2. Create a Place, Not a Design When we hear “design,” our minds are likely to conjure images of shapes, colors, and other physical elements. However, it actually extends beyond that. As we understand from UX, design is about experience. In placemaking, every element (physical or otherwise), must serve a purpose. Mainly, the place should make people feel welcome and comfortable. This often translates to the development of effective relationships between the place, the people, and the activities. The goal here is to make the whole greater than the sum of its parts. 3. Look for Partners In a placemaking project, it’s best to look for partners as early as possible. These partners may come from the residents, local governments, educational institutions, museums, libraries, or other related organizations. Since placemaking is community-driven, and the place itself is supposed to foster community ownership, more partners will generally mean a greater sense of community. 4. You Can See a Lot Just by Observing Instead of guessing what might work or not work, it’s much more preferable to learn through observation. Simply by looking at how people are conducting their activities in a place, which elements they use or don’t use, what features they like, or don’t like; we can accurately identify what elements are missing and thus can be incorporated through placemaking. It’s also important to remember that the people’s preferences will evolve over time, and needs to be managed continuously. 5. Have a Vision A good placemaking approach shouldn’t just flow by “whatever seems easiest” or “whatever seems good at the time” — it should have a vision. This vision might include the physical shape of the place, its brand/image, the kinds of activities that might happen there, and so on. Ideally, the vision should come from the community itself. 6. Start with the Petunias: Lighter, Quicker, Cheaper Places are complex entities, and the placemaking practitioners shouldn’t expect to do everything correctly from the get-go. The best places are made through incremental improvements that are tested and refined over many years. For example, physical elements like seating, footpaths, or public artworks can be added and subtracted as needed, in time. Your first step can be as simple as planting a row of petunias. From there, you can gradually experiment with more intricate elements. 7.\", \"sampled\": \"Streamlit is a powerful library that provides users with the ability to deploy functional code directly from a simple Python script. This makes it an incredibly efficient tool for data scientists and machine learning researchers who need to share their work and create minimum viable products without extensive web development knowledge. The library stands out when compared to other frameworks, and the article positions Streamlit as a highly efficient option for deploying models to teams or clients. For solutions that require more advanced features or customization, the author suggests that a more robust framework like Flask is likely a better and more suitable choice. Competitors in this space include other well-known libraries such as Bokeh and Dash, each offering different approaches to data visualization and application deployment. While these alternatives exist, Streamlit has gained significant traction and recently secured a notable funding round, having raised $6 million to further its development and reach. Jupyter Notebooks are often used for research and report writing, but they are considered less practical when the goal is to share usable and interactive code with others. Streamlit fills this gap by allowing for a more seamless transition from a script to a shareable web application that others can interact with directly. Creating a simple web application is remarkably straightforward with this tool, demonstrating its core value proposition of simplicity and speed. A basic app featuring a dropdown menu and a corresponding line chart can be constructed with approximately 11 lines of Python code. This level of conciseness is a significant advantage over more traditional web development frameworks where a similar outcome would be far more involved. The author notes that building an equivalent application in a framework like Flask would necessitate writing hundreds of lines of code, covering routing, HTML templates, and backend logic. The example application demonstrates how to generate a simple interface for exploring different data distributions, a common task in data science. The user can select from a dropdown menu that offers two distribution choices, allowing for immediate visualization and comparison without complex setup. This sample application generates a random sample of 1000 data points to plot on the chart, providing a tangible example of how data can be manipulated and displayed. The code required for this is minimal, highlighting how Streamlit abstracts away the complexities of web development. A sample script, which could be saved as a file named \\\"test.py,\\\" contains all the necessary logic to produce this interactive application. This single file encapsulates the user interface components, the data generation, and the plotting logic, making it easy to manage and share. Executing the application is just as simple as writing it, requiring only a single command to get started. By running the command \\\"streamlit run test.py\\\" in the terminal, the user can launch the entire web application locally on their machine. Once the command is executed, Streamlit automatically starts a local web server to host the newly created application for immediate use. This server makes the application accessible through a web browser, turning the Python script into a fully interactive tool. The local web server runs on a specific port, and the application can be viewed by navigating to the address http://localhost:8501/ in a browser. This immediate feedback loop allows developers to see their changes in real time, greatly speeding up the development and iteration process for data-driven applications.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In one of my Vue project, I needed to generate word documents with different templates. I did a small R&D and came across several libraries and APIs to achieve this. All of them were not free and we have to buy a subscription which is very expensive for me. The available free libraries used an approach where we have to design the template with HTML first and then render them as a word document. This approach did not fit to my need as there were many templates and they could frequently change as per the client’s need. Finally I came across a great library ( which was the only library available on the Internet) to generate word documents from a given template that includes tags that can be replaced with contemporary data when generating the report. It was a JavaScript library but my Vue project was in Typescript. In this article, I am going to explain how to implement this feature easily with Vue.JS project in TypeScript. If you are new to Vue with TypeScript, please follow this article explaining how to create a new Vue.JS project in TypeScript. The others can proceed. Installing necessary libraries For generating the word document we use docxtemplater library by Edgar Hipp. First of all, run these commands in the console inside your project folder In addition to the main library, we need the other libraries mentioned above in this process. File saver library is used to save the final output to the local machine. Writing definition files for TypeScript Some JavaScript libraries cannot be directly used in a TypeScript project if they do not include a type definition file for TypeScript. Type definition files usually appear as .d.ts and you can check if there is a such file in the installed library in the node_modules folder of the project. Except the docxtemplater library, the other three libraries do not include such definition files.Therefore, we have to write definition files for them separately by our own. These files are created, named according to the structure .d.ts and placed inside the src folder. jszip.d.ts jszip.d.ts jszip-utils.ts jszip-utils.ts file-saver.d.ts file-saver.d.ts After creating these files in the src folder, you can use these in any component. Submitting the templates First, you have to place your template document that is composed of tags, inside the public folder of the Vue project. The tags are written inside two curly brackets. ex:- {full_name} . For this explanation, I have created my sample template and placed it inside a separate folder inside the public folder ( public → ReportTemplates → template-1.docx ) . Sample template ( template-1.docx ) All inside the {} are tags and they are replaced by the JSON dataset provided when rendering. The tag name and the property name in the JSON object must be similar to run a replacement for a tag. {#students}……{/students} inside a row of a table represents a list named “students” in the JSON object which we are going to provide when rendering. For this example, this is the JSON dataset we will provide. JSON Object Writing the component This is how my final component code appears. When I click the button, it downloads the final word document in docx format with the hardcoded dataset in JSON format. Normally, you should call your back-end API and fetch this dataset for the report. In line 82, for the loadFile function’s first parameter, you can give the path of the template relative to the public folder. Or As in line 81 (commented), you can pass a URL too.\", \"sampled\": \"A new reading list for May 2020 offers a selection of books for data professionals and anyone looking to balance focused work with creative pursuits. The author, Luke Posey, shares his current reading to provide recommendations spanning technical subjects, business strategy, and even fiction to help disconnect from a demanding workday. Taking time away from professional tasks is crucial for improving the quality of your focused work, a belief the author strongly holds. For many, the physical distance between a work desk and personal space might only be 10 feet, making mental separation through activities like reading an essential practice for well-being and productivity. One key recommendation for Data and Software Engineers is the book titled \\\"Designing Data-Intensive Applications,\\\" a text that explores the foundational principles of modern data systems. This book is essential for anyone building or maintaining the complex infrastructure that powers today's applications, offering deep insights into their architecture. By diving into the concepts presented in this book, engineers can gain a comprehensive understanding of the trade-offs and technologies involved in system design. It provides a robust framework for making informed decisions and appreciating the intricacies of the systems that professionals in the field interact with on a daily basis. Another valuable resource highlighted is an offering from the publisher Springer, which recently made 65 of its technical books available for free. This collection covers a wide range of important topics, including specialized areas such as Statistics and Machine Learning, presenting a significant learning opportunity for the data community. This generous release from Springer allows data scientists and engineers to expand their knowledge base without financial investment, which is a fantastic way to stay current. Access to such a wealth of information supports continuous professional development and the exploration of new techniques and theories within the data science landscape. The author also suggests a business-oriented book called \\\"Monetizing Innovation,\\\" which came highly recommended by another founder. This recommendation carries weight as it originates from someone with direct experience in building and scaling a company, suggesting its practical value for entrepreneurs and product leaders. \\\"Monetizing Innovation\\\" is described as a practical guide that is approximately 225 pages long, making it a concise yet impactful read. The book effectively uses diagrams and data-driven explanations to demystify the complex processes behind making critical pricing and product decisions for a successful business. To engage the creative side and properly disconnect from technical work, the author recommends the fiction classic \\\"Dune,\\\" a book he has recently started reading. Venturing into a rich, imaginative world offers a necessary escape and a different form of mental stimulation that complements the logical thinking required in a technical role. Reading fiction like \\\"Dune\\\" serves as more than just entertainment; it is an active way to foster creativity and gain new perspectives that can indirectly benefit professional work. Immersing oneself in a compelling narrative provides a complete mental break, which is essential for preventing burnout and returning to challenges with renewed energy. The author, Luke Posey, makes it clear that he has no affiliation with any of the authors or publishers mentioned in his May 2020 reading list. This transparency ensures that the recommendations are genuine and based solely on the perceived value of the content for the intended audience of data professionals. He receives no monetary compensation from these suggestions, reinforcing that the goal is simply to share resources that he has found personally and professionally beneficial. This list is a curated selection offered to the community with the hope that others will find these books as insightful and enjoyable as he has.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"How to Find the Energy to Bootstrap Your Side Hustle After a long days work, the last thing most of us want to do is work more. Thanks Annie Spratt for the fantastic photo from Unsplash You work all day, come home, and all you want to do is crash. Or, as the saying goes, “grab some Netflix and chill.” The furthest thing from your mind is getting back to the grind and work some more. The thing is, what you really want is not to have to get up and go back and do the work thing again tomorrow. That’s why our weekends are so sacred. We get to do what we love for two whole days. But as we sit in the vegetative state, watching the next episode of something we’ll never remember next week, we know in the back of our mind, we need to start pulling something together in our spare time. Something to allow us not to go back to the Monday grind. We’ve read all the stories of success online where people seemingly just like us have ‘made it’ by putting in the sweat equity and building a venture after hours which has left them in a state of total prosperity. What type of superhumans are these people? How did they find not only the physical energy but the mental sustenance they needed to keep pushing through when their body and mind is pushing back. I struggled with this for a long time. I sucked up enough mental strength to push ahead with some side projects, but never any home run. By home run, all I’m saying is having a side business earn enough to replace the regular job. When I did manage to push through my slothy couch hugging laziness and got some stuff accomplished, I looked at the things I thought was holding me back the most. These are the things I started crushing, which actually lead to some success. Trying to get the day job out of mind. A great photo by Joanes Andueza from Unsplash I struggled to get my day job out of my mind for a long time. Every day there was a new consortium of struggles and problems to solve. Every night I took those problems home with me for a little extra homework. Most often, it wasn’t intentional. It just happened. I’d find myself starting to think about a project, and something at work would come to mind. If you have a job where you can pack it away and not think about it after you leave, you might have a better shot than most for succeeding with a side venture. Taking my work home with me may have made me more successful in the day job, but it played hell with me to get things rolling on the side. And, the side hustle was what I truly wanted. Can give any credible advice on how to solve this dilemma?\", \"sampled\": \"This task is the first chapter of My Learning Trajectory, which will cover the books and courses I have undertaken. I have made an attempt to quantify the amount of hours and the money I have spent on these educational materials since 2019. I am writing down my thoughts about these numbers here, putting them together to create a clearer picture of my efforts. This process is part of a larger project, an \\\"Autobiography\\\" where nearly everything, at least 90 percent, has been quantified. The behavior of quantifying all these things is meant to provide me with a perspective on how efficient my learning has been. It is an exercise in self-reflection and a method to track my progress and investment in personal development over time. Since the year 2015, I have read a total of 47 books, which represents a significant portion of my learning activities. The majority of these books have been focused on specific areas of knowledge that I am keen on exploring more deeply. My current goal is to read approximately 20 books each year, a pace that I find both challenging and achievable. Although I have a target, I am always open to reading more if my schedule and interests allow for it. The books I choose mostly consist of nonfiction and scientific research books, reflecting my preference for fact based learning. I find these subjects, such as the Science of Exercise and Existential Well Being, to be particularly enriching and practical. You can see all of the books that I have read by either googling for them or by going to a specific link. The list provides a comprehensive overview of my reading history and the topics that have captured my attention over the years. I also utilize Quizlet as a learning tool, and it takes around 30 minutes to complete one of my Quizlet sets. This time is spread out to reinforce the information effectively, making it a key part of my study routine. You can see my Quizlet profile at a specific link, which is com/williamjamessidis. My profile contains the 47 sets I have created, which correspond to the books and other materials I have studied. My learning approach integrates spaced repetition, a method I apply using Quizlet to enhance memory retention. The schedule for this spaced repetition is set for specific intervals to maximize long term recall of the information. Specifically, my Quizlet spaced repetition schedule is structured with intervals of 7 and 14 days. This systematic approach ensures that I revisit material at optimal times for strengthening my understanding and memory. This Experiential Approach Together with the quantification of Total Hours and Total Worth helps me understand my educational journey. It's a method inspired by figures like \\\"John Von Neumann Ii\\\" who valued rigorous analysis. The entire process is an effort to measure the \\\"Amount of\\\" knowledge gained against the resources invested. It's a personal finance learning how to best allocate time and money for intellectual growth, an idea even Mark Twain might appreciate.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"So here’s the thing. Google updated its Google+ app, and it comes with a huge redesign exercise on it. In case you don’t know him, Luke Wroblewski is a designer at Google. He’s been around for a while, commenting stuff about user experience and visual design. He wrote a lot about the Polar app — which I love by the way— and wrote the first article I ever read about the hamburger menu not working well for engagement in mobile apps nor webs. The point is, Luke knows a thing or two about UX and UI design and he’s been involved in the Google+ redesign. That looks like this: Credits to Luke Wroblewski. This new app looks absolutely beautiful. I mean, look at all that color and rich imagery. And I believe I’ll never be tired of using specific color palettes for contextual elements that surround an image. And the new Google+ app uses a bottom navigation bar, and suddenly the internet went like ‘that’s not so Material Design’. Arturo Toledo is another designer I’ll use here as a reference. He’s been working with Microsoft for a few years, and his response to this was… Who cares about the design language. He claimed that our focus as designers should be on the principles. To make something useful and to design a delightful experience regardless of the platform we’re designing to. That there’s nothing wrong with a navbar down there. I can’t argue with Arturo. I believe he’s right about this. Maybe not in all cases, but I feel he’s mostly right. But I do have a few concerns about this navigation pattern though. And it’s because this is an official Google application, so designers and developers out there probably are going to reproduce this kind of navigation more than once. You got me at ‘navbar’ I do love navbars. And tabbars. And everything that’s not a chaotic hamburger throw-it-all-there-in-the-drawer-and-see-how-it-fits main application menu. It makes things more discoverable, and it makes the app easier to understand without having to think and read that much. I mean, options are just there. Just a quick glance and now you know about everything the app has to offer to you. If you have any doubts, you just tap on the first tab and if you don’t find what you’re looking for then tap on the second one. And continue that way until you’re out of tabs. That’s it. But then you introduce another main navigation pattern, that is the drawer menu itself. And now we have two main navigation paths. Or three, if we count the top tab-bar. Credits to Luke Wroblewski. Again. Ok, tabs in the top bar might not be a main navigation path, but they count as chrome. They count as more options, more space used for navigation. Good luck to you, 4-or-less inches screen people. In theory everything has sense. You have supportive nav for user profile stuff, a global nav between sections and a contextual nav for the filters. But it makes me think. I can’t use this app without reading and taking a second to think where I’m going every time I want to go elsewhere. I’ll give it a quick try here. How about moving the hambuger menu to the last item in the bottom navbar? — like a ‘more’ tab — and then moving the Notifications icon to the top bar, right next to the search icon. That should simplify the main navigation, just like Facebook does in its app. Then to reduce chrome you could make a dropdown menu out of the section title in that top bar to put the filters on it, just like Google Calendar makes to open the calendar control.\", \"sampled\": \"I was given up for adoption on the very day I was born, an event that marked the beginning of a significant void in my personal history. My first six weeks of life were spent within the foster care system before I was eventually placed with my adoptive family. Those initial six weeks, a total of 42 days, are a complete blank in my life story, as I have absolutely no information about what happened to me. Because my adoption was closed, all the official records detailing this period were sealed, leaving me with an unfillable gap in my identity. I have no photographs of myself from this formative time, which means the earliest visual record of my existence begins only after I was adopted. This lack of information creates a profound sense of disconnection from my own beginning, a void where crucial early experiences should be. This separation from my biological mother at birth constitutes a preverbal trauma, an event that occurred before I had the language to process it. I now understand this experience as an Adverse Childhood Experience, or ACE, which has had lasting effects throughout my life. The psychiatrist Paul Sunderland has explored how such early separations can shape a person's development and long-term well-being. Experts like Nadine Burke Harris, known for her TED talk on the subject, have also highlighted the profound impact of ACEs on health outcomes. The work of Bessel Van Der Kolk, particularly in his book \\\"The Body Keeps the Score,\\\" supports the idea that our bodies hold memories of trauma. Even without conscious recall, these early experiences can be stored within us, influencing our physical and emotional responses for years to come. This concept helps explain my own struggles with mental health, which have included issues like persistent anxiety and high blood pressure. I believe that babies remember experiences on a cellular level, even if they cannot consciously form narrative memories of those events. Evidence suggests that infants are deeply connected to their biological mothers from the very start, demonstrating a preference for their mother's breastmilk just 24 hours after birth. The disruption of this bond is a significant event that needs to be acknowledged as a form of trauma for the child. It is crucial that we recognize adoption itself as a traumatic experience, a fact that is often overlooked in broader cultural conversations. Acknowledging this reality is the first step toward providing better support for adoptees and understanding the complexities of their inner lives. My personal journey has been one of seeking answers and attempting to piece together the fragmented parts of my history. This search led me to locate many of my biological relatives, which has been an illuminating and healing process. Through these connections, I have been able to learn about my genetic predispositions and gain a fuller picture of my heritage. This knowledge has helped fill in some of the gaps left by my closed adoption and the lack of early records. In a remarkable turn of events, I was also able to reconnect with the very social worker who handled my adoption case nearly forty years ago. This connection provided a tangible link to my past, offering a sense of continuity that had long been missing. While my adoptive family provided a loving home, the initial trauma of separation remains a fundamental part of my story. The absence of information from those first six weeks continues to be a central element of my identity. Understanding my history has required a conscious effort to look beyond the narrative of a happy adoption story and confront the underlying trauma. It is a complex reality that has shaped my entire life, influencing my health, my relationships, and my sense of self.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Getting lost in motivational videos or stories can be empowering. You feel like you’re at a low point in life and need something to pull you out of it. The likes of Joe Rogan, David Goggins, Jordan Peterson, and others lay out stories of survival and overcoming. Listening to others tell their stories of escaping lows and reaching highs can be very motivating. If Goggins can shed weight like it’s nothing and turn into one of the most accomplished military men ever, why can’t I find similar success in achieving my dreams? Eventually, you get sick of the videos and start getting to work. You start having some success. Then, you want to tell everyone your story and how they can find success, too. 2020 has been a tumultuous year for me. Yes, I know — I’m not special. It has sucked for many people. I dug myself into perhaps the deepest hole I’ve ever been in, spending my days focusing on unimportant things and questioning my own self-worth. I felt very lost and didn’t know where to go. After confronting my issues and what was causing them, I removed the troubling causes from my life and started looking forward. I re-discovered Medium and my love for writing. I began writing on what I thought I felt I could best advise an audience on: investing in the stock market. After reading more and more self-help Medium articles and recalling my motivational YouTube indulgences, I asked myself, “why can’t I write these types of stories?” From a numbers standpoint, these types of articles clearly brought in views and attention. Everyone is looking to improve themselves in some way, and Medium writers have realized the vast demand for that kind of content. The more you read these articles, though, the more you realize how useless many of them are. There are people that have important stories to tell with useful advice attached. But there are just as many stories that are copy-and-paste — writers using the experience of others to give advice without actually using the advice themselves. It’s understandable why people do this. The idea of self-help will sell. Again, people want to improve themselves.\", \"sampled\": \"The year 2020 was an incredibly difficult period for a great many people. Humanity was forced to adapt to a new normal that confined daily life within four walls. Activities that were once performed elsewhere, like work and exercise, were brought into the home. For many, this meant accepting a new reality of seeing loved ones only through screens. This shift in social interaction was a profound change that required significant adjustment from everyone who experienced it. Celebrations for birthdays were held virtually. Anniversaries also moved online. Even graduations took place through a screen. The product Zoom became a household name. This was a direct result of the circumstances. Everyone was using it. People learned how to live and function within a constrained environment, finding new routines and ways to manage their days. This adaptation was a testament to resilience. Despite widespread job losses, many individuals found new opportunities to grow. People also learned new skills during this time. The author suggests that people should show themselves more kindness for having endured such a year. Looking in the mirror and saying, \\\"You did great!\\\" is a recommended act of self-appreciation. The experience of loss was a difficult but powerful teacher. It taught many the importance of embracing life's inherent uncertainty. Merely surviving the year is a significant accomplishment worthy of recognition. The year was not just a series of setbacks for everyone. It was also a time of finding better solutions for survival. This period was a challenge. It was a test. It was a struggle. It was a time that pushed people to their limits while they discovered new strengths. The adjustments made were profound and far reaching. It is important to acknowledge that everyone has reached a milestone that is truly worthy of celebration. The journey through that time was a shared one. It shaped experiences and perspectives in lasting ways. This accomplishment is something that should be honored and recognized by all who went through it. The challenges pushed people to discover their own resilience. This period taught valuable lessons about what humanity could withstand and overcome. It was a collective test. This was a shared experience that connected people in unexpected ways. The journey was difficult but meaningful.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Before I joined OutSystems as part of the User Experience Research team, my work as a researcher and designer focused on impacting ‘real-world’ environments, from industrial cities in Italy to remote communities in Mozambique. My purpose was — and has always been — to spot opportunities and problems. In my previous life, that meant traveling around the world, getting close to (and occasionally living with) a specific group of people, and collecting data about their work, their lives, and their cultural attitudes. With some targeted individuals, we even co-created solutions to match their needs and expectations. I have been lucky enough to meet some of the most fascinating cultures and people on Earth because this kind of work is strongly tied to social or developmental organizations. So it may have come as a bit of a surprise to some people when I joined OutSystems, a software company that has little to do with remote communities in far-flung corners of the world. UX research at OutSystems Ethnographic Research for Digital Experiences Actually, what a User Experience Research team does in a software company isn’t that different from what I was doing in those remote communities. Trust me: we may not live with OutSystems users, but we are getting closer and closer to them. We regularly meet with our stakeholders (users, teams, clients, and partners) and apply multiple participatory research methodologies to better understand them. What does this mean? It means our research emphasizes participation and action, and as part of this approach, a method I have always been a big fan of is creating field diaries. I first realized the full potential of written and photo diaries in Ethiopia and Mozambique, where efforts by entities to help people often fail due to the lack of on-the-ground knowledge about the communities in which they operate. In fact, projects and products can fall short of their original intent and design because of cultural beliefs or different priorities. For instance, a classic example is mosquito nets being distributed to fight malaria, only to have the inhabitants of coastal regions use the nets for fishing, as this is a higher economic priority. Capturing a Community To address this, we implemented written and photo diaries so that people could clearly express what is important in their everyday lives, which in turn allowed us to identify community changes, whether big or small. As an example, one of our agroforestry stakeholders was generating a substantial amount of new employment in rural areas, and not all changes were positive. The diaries allowed us to identify and avoid major conflicts between locals and the company, perform program course corrections, reduce downtime, and rationalize production, as well as spot further business opportunities. Moreover, we provided research participants with cameras to capture everyday life through community photography. Frame by frame, they captured unconventional scenarios that outsiders would never be able to witness, from witch doctor rituals, household routines, and traditional dances to farming practices. Field diaries in a rural community in Mozambique Field diaries at OutSystems Field Diaries at OutSystems Back then, field diaries helped us address the question: “What happens when the company arrives?” Now, at OutSystems, we use the same method to address a different question: “What happens when users develop with our platform?” Instead of receiving handwritten diaries and photos, we receive digital journals and screenshots from our users about their experience. Then, we further explore their needs, concerns, and improvement suggestions in talks. Users also provide feedback proactively in the OutSystems Community, and I very much appreciate reading their amazing suggestions in a collaborative space called OutSystems Ideas. Not every suggestion is brought to life, but the engineering team considers each proposal. So, it’s all about establishing proximity with our users and asking the right questions. In fact, a very important responsibility of our User Experience Research Team is to enable product teams to perform good research that will allow OutSystems to make reliable fact-based decisions. Workshop about unbiased questions for UX research As you can see here, User Experience Researchers play an essential role in supporting decision-making processes.\", \"sampled\": \"A central concern involves the nature of immunity, as protection gained from the novel coronavirus may not last for an extended period, which introduces significant long term public health challenges. The title of a recent article, \\\"Facing Three Fundamental Coronavirus Fears,\\\" encapsulates the anxieties surrounding how our bodies respond to this new pathogen and what that means for the future. Humans generally develop immunity to a specific pathogen after they have either received an immunization or successfully recovered from an initial infection with that agent. The effectiveness and duration of this acquired immunity can vary greatly depending on the particular disease, the type of vaccine administered, and a range of individual host characteristics like age and overall health. Immunity is not a simple \\\"either/or\\\" process where a person is completely protected one day and completely vulnerable the next, a common misconception that oversimplifies a complex biological system. Instead of being a binary state that is either present or absent, the level of protection exists on a spectrum that can change significantly throughout a person's lifetime. The protective effects that our immune systems build against different pathogens do not just suddenly switch off at a predetermined moment, leaving the body entirely defenseless. Rather, this defensive capacity gradually wanes over time, meaning that the protection a person has can diminish slowly, sometimes over months or even years, depending on the specific virus or bacteria. It is highly probable that a person's natural immunity to the novel coronavirus, SARS-CoV-2, will only last for a limited duration before they could potentially become susceptible to reinfection. This observation aligns with what was previously seen during outbreaks of two other dangerous coronaviruses that emerged earlier in this century, which are known as SARS and MERS. However, a different pattern is observed with the other four known human coronaviruses that have been identified by scientists, which are designated as HCoV-229E, NL63, OC43, and HKU1. These four distinct viruses have been present in the human population for a much longer period than the other three more recent and severe coronaviruses that have caused global health crises. These four viruses are now considered endemic and are continuously circulating within the global population, often causing symptoms that are commonly associated with the common cold. Their long history and persistent presence mean they have a well established relationship with human immune systems, unlike the newer, more novel strains that have emerged in recent decades. Interestingly, there is compelling historical evidence to suggest that these four endemic coronaviruses were not always mild and likely caused their own widespread pandemics when they first emerged. This historical context provides a potential roadmap for how novel coronaviruses might transition from a pandemic-causing agent into a more manageable, endemic disease over a long period. This understanding of waning immunity has profound implications for how societies can manage the ongoing public health situation and plan for the long term coexistence with the virus. The fact that protection is not permanent complicates strategies that rely solely on achieving herd immunity through natural infection, highlighting the need for sustained public health measures and vaccination efforts. The rapid and broad spread of the virus presents a significant challenge, as articulated by the statement, \\\"The coronavirus is spreading too rapidly, and too broadly for the U.S. to bring it under control with testing and contact tracing.\\\" This reality underscores the difficulty of containment when a virus is highly transmissible and community spread is widespread, overwhelming traditional mitigation tools. The gradual decline of immunity means that even individuals who have recovered from an infection might need to take precautions in the future to avoid becoming reinfected or transmitting the virus. This possibility reinforces the importance of ongoing research into the precise duration of immunity and the effectiveness of booster vaccinations to maintain protective levels of antibodies and cellular responses. Ultimately, the complex and dynamic nature of the immune response to coronaviruses requires a nuanced and adaptable approach to public health policy and individual behavior. Acknowledging that immunity is not a permanent shield but a temporary and waning defense is crucial for developing effective long term strategies to mitigate the impact of this and future viral threats.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Me in front of my laptop and monitor — always with multiple windows open :-) I’ve worked remotely for almost seven years now. This means that I can be in front of my computer during calls without being viewed as inattentive or rude. I simultaneously amaze and annoy my coworkers with my responsiveness to email requests, because I am able to quickly search for information as we discuss things. My work style has unearthed my talents as a sleuth — always looking to see what others already know or have published on a particular topic of interest. As a result, when we begin new projects, I’m often quick to discover similar projects that have already been completed in our field and to suggest that we build on these efforts instead of recreating them. I used to fear that this meant that I was lazy or not all that creative. But my colleague, Simone Parrish helped me understand that what I am doing is actually innovating by adapting what already exists for one particular audience or setting to another. This is an important skill, especially in public health, where so many great resources and tools are developed for the public good and are available free of charge. Adaptation takes content proven in one context and applies it in another. This is both evidence-based and innovative. Educators, trainers, content providers, and even technologists must be able to identify knowledge needs and adapt evidence-based content to address these needs. For the past few years, I’ve been working on developing, managing, and monitoring online learning programs for the Knowledge for Health (K4Health) Project. I can tell you firsthand that a lot of work goes into adapting and synthesizing information so that it can be easily digested online. For example, it often takes at least nine months (often more than a year) to develop a course on the Global Health eLearning Center (GHeL). The rigorous review process of GHeL courses ensures that they are reputable resources for the latest technical and programmatic guidance on a wide range of health and development topics. However, as a global resource, courses are developed for a broad audience of stakeholders — mostly health program managers, service providers, and policy makers. When we asked learners if course content should be adapted to fit local contexts, we received mixed responses. Those who work for the government (U.S. or other countries) often prefer the global perspective. In contrast, people who work at non-governmental organizations (NGOs) at the country or regional level tell us that it would be helpful if the content could be adapted to fit their contexts. This might include translation into local languages and the addition of country-specific case studies. One GHeL user wondered: “…\", \"sampled\": \"Camels are creatures that are completely optimized for their unique and challenging desert environment they call home. These animals have a number of interesting features. Camels possess three eyelids, which is in contrast to the two eyelids that are found in humans. This third eyelid is a specialized biological structure known as a transparent nictitating membrane. The membrane provides a significant advantage by sweeping across the entire surface of the eye, moving from the inner corner outwards. This sweeping motion effectively shields the camel's eye from the constant threat of blowing dust and abrasive sand particles which are common in its habitat. A prominent theory also suggests that the nictitating membrane might serve an additional purpose. This membrane may also block a certain amount of ultraviolet light from the sun. By doing so, it would help to reduce the intense glare that is a constant feature of the desert landscape. In addition to their eyelids, camels have two distinct sets of very long eyelashes. These eyelashes provide further protection. The long eyelashes are another key adaptation that helps to protect the camel's eyes from the persistent blowing sand and dust found in their arid surroundings. The camel’s wide, flat feet are another remarkable adaptation that allows them to navigate their environment. These feet are perfectly suited for walking across shifting sands without sinking. Camels are such unique creatures that they have even entered into popular culture and language to describe certain situations. A popular saying that originated in the 1960s uses the animal as a metaphor to criticize the output of committees. The saying suggests that group work can sometimes lead to strange or inefficient outcomes, unlike a single focused designer. This comparison highlights the unusual appearance of the camel. The saying is often quoted. It is remembered from the 1960s. The well known saying goes, \\\"a camel is a horse designed by a committee.\\\" This phrase was intended to be critical of committees, suggesting that their collective efforts are often ineffective. The author of these facts is Sam Westreich. He has provided the information about the camel's unique biological features and their place in popular sayings. This information highlights how a creature can be completely optimized for its specific environment, developing specialized traits that ensure its survival in harsh conditions like the desert. The camel is a prime example of this evolutionary process. The camel has three eyelids, unlike the two that are found in humans. This is a notable biological difference. It serves a protective function. The two sets of long eyelashes also help protect the camel's eyes from sand. These features, including the nictitating membrane and wide feet, demonstrate a creature that is perfectly suited for its environment, a testament to evolutionary adaptation. They are not design flaws.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"For more than two years, Prefect has been making steady progress on our mission to eliminate negative engineering. Today, we’re excited to announce that Prefect Cloud is available to the public — including its free Scheduler tier! Learn more here. We worked with hundreds of early Cloud previewers and tens of Lighthouse Partners to reach this point. Since July 2019, when we onboarded Cloud’s very first customer, we have made enormous strides in our understanding of workflow systems and user requirements. The biggest lesson of all is that a system we built for a very specific set of customers — large financial institutions — has come to dominate our business model. Our Hybrid Model delivers cloud convenience with on-prem security, and is so innovative it has resulted in two separate patent filings. Users keep their code and data on their private infrastructure — whether that’s a personal laptop, an IoT device, a cloud-hosted cluster, a serverless function, or just bare metal — while Prefect Cloud’s managed orchestration service provides complete oversight and confidence. The hybrid model is a clear advantage that Prefect provides over any alternative system. Learn more about the hybrid model here. The public release of Prefect Cloud caps “Phase 1” of our company’s story. It represents everything we’ve learned about negative engineering, and is informed by thousands of user stories gathered from all industries and experience levels. Just as when we launched our open-source Prefect Core library with the extreme confidence that comes from iterating with a small group of early previewers, we’ve already seen Prefect Cloud deployed at institutions large and small. We know that it fulfills the objective we laid out in this blog a year and a half ago: Prefect is the codification of the patterns we observe in modern data engineering. At our core, we provide two things. One is our open-source framework [Core], which operates like a hardware store: stocked with all the necessary components for building great data applications.\", \"sampled\": \"Teri Bayus, an accomplished author, dedicated the past thirty years to writing on diverse subjects such as travel, business, and dining. This breadth of experience honed her skill in capturing the essence of others' passions, making her an expert at articulating their fervor. In a poignant exploration of personal tragedy, Teri Bayus penned a heart-wrenching book chronicling the loss of her sister. This raw and emotional work delves into the depths of grief and mourning, showcasing Bayus's ability to convey profound emotions through her writing. Another significant theme in Teri Bayus's literary portfolio is her candid depiction of her son's battle with drug addiction. Through her book on this challenging topic, Bayus sheds light on the complexities and struggles faced by families dealing with addiction, offering a raw and authentic narrative. Aside from her published works, Teri Bayus harbors a collection of unpublished writings, including a captivating circus-themed story and screenplays centered around the intricacies of raising foster children. These unreleased gems highlight the breadth of Bayus's storytelling prowess and the depth of her creative endeavors. One of Teri Bayus's notable creations is the book 'Consumed,' a unique fusion of sensuality and gastronomy. Through this innovative work, Bayus intertwines themes of intimacy and food, creating a narrative that tantalizes the senses and challenges conventional storytelling boundaries. During the creative process of 'Consumed,' Teri Bayus serendipitously stumbled upon a muse, igniting a profound and symbiotic writing relationship. This unexpected muse infused her work with inspiration and creativity, shaping the narrative of her book in unexpected and delightful ways. Reflecting on her writing journey, Teri Bayus emphasizes the importance of having a muse in one's life. She believes that this source of inspiration can spark creativity, fuel passion, and breathe life into artistic endeavors, underscoring the transformative power of artistic muses. \\\"My life has been unusual and full of adventure! It is time to tell my story,\\\" reflects Teri Bayus, encapsulating her decision to share her unique experiences and narratives with the world. Through her writing, Bayus invites readers into her world, offering a glimpse into the extraordinary tapestry of her life.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Adapting social distancing policies via the monitoring of crucial home and public behaviour data can help contain the Covid-19 offensive. Let’s take a closer look in this, widely circulated, chart regarding the progression of the infections: The Infection Trajectory as depicted by the reported cases vs. the days since case count passed 500 (22/3). An interactive - regularly updated version of this chart is also available at the Visual Capitalist website It appears that its spread -when the case count passes 500- is strikingly similar, regardless of the size or the geographical density of the “sample”. The chart was provided to me by David Gorelick, Professor of Psychiatry at the School of Medicine of the University of Maryland with whom we happened to discuss the capability of predicting electoral outcomes (as discussed in a series of articles that started here, whereas in one of the articles it was mentioned how data analysis can be used to draw meaningful conclusions that are insensitive to polling sample size and density). I reverted to him with a message that I feel worthwhile sharing here (in a revised/enhanced and edited for publication form) regarding social distancing policies that are crucial in pacing the spread of the pandemic as the resilience of the Health Care Systems is tested; As this brilliant simulator displays, Health Systems collapse when the rate of concurrent cases exceeds their capacity, thus patients -who otherwise could have been saved in ICUs- are dying due to lack of resources: “As we were discussing how I am going about in predicting elections, here is a way to approach this curve in order to get meaningful data that may shape “social distancing” policies: 1. Obviously, the initial cases had to do with travel in China or other in countries that do business with China.\", \"sampled\": \"Food labeling practices can sometimes mislead consumers, with companies using \\\"plant-based\\\" labels on products that are not entirely plant-based. This can lead to hidden non-plant-based ingredients like eggs and milk in these products. It is essential for consumers to check ingredient labels diligently to avoid consuming non-plant-based components that may be present in products labeled as \\\"plant-based.\\\" Ingredients such as milk powder, eggs, and other non-plant-based additives can be found in items labeled as plant-based. Some food items enriched with omega-3, a common health additive, may paradoxically contain fish oil, contradicting the plant-based claim. This highlights the need for consumers to scrutinize product labels and question the motives behind food labeling practices. Companies often prioritize profit margins over consumer health when engaging in food labeling practices. They may resort to deceptive marketing strategies to sell products, potentially compromising the well-being of consumers. Consumers should approach food labels with caution and be proactive in questioning the contents of products to make informed choices. Reading ingredient lists thoroughly is crucial to identifying hidden non-plant-based components in food items. Ingredients like milk, butter, eggs, and lard can be discreetly incorporated into seemingly plant-based foods, underscoring the importance of careful label scrutiny by consumers. It is vital for individuals to prioritize their health by questioning food labeling practices. \\\"When I come across a new product I am interested in, I always ask it 'what are you trying to hide from me'?\\\" - Dan Stout. Consumers are encouraged to adopt a critical mindset when selecting products, questioning what may be concealed behind food labels. \\\"From now on when you are picking up a product I encourage you to ask it this: 'What are you hiding from me'?\\\" - Dan Stout. By questioning the transparency of food labeling, consumers can empower themselves to make healthier and more informed food choices.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"All Americans should care about the wildlife on the coast plain of the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge, even if we never visit. (Photo credit: Credit: Alan D. Wilson via Wikimedia, CC BY-SA 3.0) Protecting the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge is a legacy of our country’s conservation movement. Originally established in 1960 under President Dwight D. Eisenhower, the refuge pre-dates the publication of Rachel Carson’s seminal book, Silent Spring (1962) and the Santa Barbara oil spill (1969), which horrified the organizers of the first Earth Day (1970). I’ve never been to the refuge, and, I suspect you’ve never been there either.\", \"sampled\": \"Java, originating in 1995 under Sun Microsystems and now owned by Oracle, stands as a stalwart in the programming world. Kotlin, born in 2011 by JetBrains, garnered Google's official support in 2017, marking a significant milestone for this modern language. Google proudly announced that approximately 70% of the top 1,000 Android applications are currently crafted using Kotlin. By June 2020, 30% of the Google Home app's code base had transitioned from Java to Kotlin, showcasing a substantial shift towards this newer language. The tech industry is witnessing a shift towards a \\\"Kotlin-first\\\" policy, strengthening the position of Kotlin in the realm of Android development. Kotlin's latest version, v1.4.0 as of November 2020, continues to evolve, bringing new features and enhancements to developers worldwide.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The morning after the night before always hurts and I’m not sanguine about my chances of making it to the bathroom without vomiting on myself. Then again, I last had a meal two days ago. This is likely empty nausea, the kind you get after your brain took a beating because depression intensified. I’m teetotal but I still call this a vulnerability hangover, because those three days of despair were brutal. And they got progressively worse. I spend too many hours staring at the Dutch suicide prevention website and trying to work up the courage to launch the chat function. Eventually, I realize that if I’m not coherent enough to corral my distress into words in conversation at home or in print, chat will be useless. Besides, there are hardly any trains anymore; lying down on the track isn’t an option. Also, I’ve already eliminated the one pharmaceutical tool I could have used several weeks ago. I may not have been very rational at the time but I also knew ingesting the whole lot would have been enough to knock me out or worse. I thought it more prudent to remove that possibility immediately before it became more attractive than it already was. To make sure it was gone for good, I took out the trash despite not looking remotely presentable.\", \"sampled\": \"Learning efficiency can be quantified using the Learning Compression Score, where efficiency equals quality divided by size reduction. This method, developed by author John Von Neumann II, helps assess how effectively one learns from a given source. The compression score, a key concept in learning methodology, indicates the efficiency of learning from a particular source. It highlights the importance of maintaining learning quality while reducing the size of the material for optimal learning outcomes. Quality plays a crucial role in determining the compression score, emphasizing the significance of retaining high-quality information during the learning process. Balancing quality retention with source compression is essential for achieving a favorable compression score. Calculating the compression score involves evaluating the quality of retained information, ensuring that the learning process remains effective and efficient. Efficiency in learning is quantified by this score, providing a metric to gauge the effectiveness of the learning process. In a practical example, consider compressing a 70,000-word book into a 1,000-word summary, achieving a remarkable 99% compression rate. Despite the reduction, 80% of the original quality is retained, resulting in a quality factor of 0.8 and a size reduction factor of 0.01 in the compression score. As learning methodologies advance, there is potential for more precise assessments of learning efficiency. These advancements could lead to enhanced strategies for optimizing learning processes and increasing overall learning speed.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Stone proves you can have your cake and eat it, too. Stop me if you’ve heard this before. To succeed, you must eat, sleep and breathe your work. You should put your blinders on, block out all distractions and keep your eyes on the prize. For most of us, this is our mantra. I certainly believed it until I interviewed one of today’s greatest entrepreneurs, Isaac “Biz” Stone. As the cofounder of Twitter and Blogger, his success speaks for itself. But what’s his secret? Growing up, did he solely focus on startups until he reached 10,000 hours? To understand Stone’s success, let’s rewind to his years at Wellesley High School. Back then, his friends were drama nerds, his favorite movie was The Princess Bride and he directed and starred in the school’s production of Robin Hood (posters around town read, “Isaac Stone presents: Robin Hood, Starring Biz Stone!”). He even found a way to put together the play after the school unexpectedly cut its budget. “We were scrappy. I remember going door-to-door in January collecting old Christmas trees for the backdrop of Sherwood Forest,” Stone recalled. “Come show time, we drew a full house. We did matinees, evening shows, and more. It was so successful that we even took the show on the road for a little while.” It’s easy to assume that Stone’s devotion to theater put him behind peers who spent that time hacking away in computer labs. However, University of Michigan Psychologists Mary Gick and Keith Holyoak say the opposite.\", \"sampled\": \"Sequential execution involves running one task at a time. This method ensures that tasks are completed in order without overlapping. Concurrency, on the other hand, allows progress in multiple tasks simultaneously. It enables efficient task management and can improve overall performance. Parallelism takes concurrency a step further by executing multiple operations simultaneously. This approach is beneficial for tasks that can truly run in parallel. Python's Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) restricts the interpreter to only one thread controlling it at a time. This limitation impacts Python's ability to leverage true parallelism. Threads in Python can operate concurrently but not in parallel due to the GIL restrictions. This distinction is essential for understanding Python's threading behavior. Asyncio in Python follows a single-threaded, single-process design. It utilizes an event loop to manage asynchronous tasks effectively. Utilizing coroutines, Asyncio achieves concurrency by enabling tasks to pause and resume execution cooperatively. This mechanism enhances performance in I/O-bound operations. For tasks that are CPU-bound, processes are recommended over threads or asyncio. Processes offer better parallelism and utilize multiple CPU cores efficiently. In scenarios with few I/O-bound tasks, using threads can be suitable. Threads are lightweight and can handle tasks that involve waiting for external operations. However, for I/O-bound tasks with numerous operations, asyncio is preferred over threads. Asyncio's asynchronous nature makes it more efficient in managing multiple I/O operations. Asyncio not only enhances performance but also improves code readability and quality compared to traditional threading approaches. Employing multiple processes can significantly boost performance for CPU-bound tasks. This strategy leverages the full power of multi-core processors for enhanced speed and efficiency.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A.I. is sort of a peanut butter you can spread across [multiple industries]. With a precise idea of the conditions this thing I’m designing will see in real life, I can design it better. — Maurice Conti DESIGN THINKING IN THE MACHINE AGE Thanks to design thinking advocates, designers have emerged to be taking over all sorts of founding and managerial roles in quite a few Big Tech players over the last couple of decades. At the same time, the advent of machine learning didn’t succeed in challenging the design trade per se. Or did it? When artificial intelligence is taking over every other industry at this sweeping pace, there is an array of lingering questions arising in design and tech communities alike. How do we tame the machines to produce compelling graphics inducing human emotion and thought? How do we restrain from feeding AI with biased data by amateur non-designers? Will the exquisite Adobe software finally evolve into robotic tools taking over a human hand? Feelings and thought are very mixed, so let me reserve a piece of personal stance in this blog. Ai-Da: the first ever robot artist who can draw without any human input. DITCHING HUMAN DESIGNERS? In the mid last century, probably the most celebrated commercial designer of the time, Paul Rand, noticed that the majority of ‘professionals’ in ads business ‘…not even discriminating enough to distinguish between good and bad, between trendy and original, nor can they always recognize talent or specialized skills. In the field of design theirs is the dichotomy of being privileged but not necessarily being qualified — after all, design is not their business’. Some fifty years in, and the situation has aggravated immensely. The abundance of tools and design assets available online is striking giving everyone having some basic Photoshop skills a chance to create graphics of dubious quality. Ditch the years of design training / self-learning — all you need is a Macbook and Adobe Suite (side note — you’d better get a Wacom too). Now, with robot-designers this design education gap will get completely out of control.\", \"sampled\": \"Communication is key in keeping your startup team motivated. Founders like Kofi Kankam stress the importance of sharing the company's mission early on to engage employees effectively. Teamwork is crucial for creating a shared sense of purpose within startup teams. Simon Lorenz, founder of Klara, highlights the significance of emphasizing collaboration to drive collective success. Transparent compensation plays a vital role in motivating team members. Avi Savar, CEO of Big Fuel, suggests that openness about pay can foster a culture of fairness and motivation within the team. Regular meetings with your team are essential to address challenges and provide reassurance. This practice helps maintain open communication channels and builds trust among team members. Building trust and loyalty requires transparency and empathy in your interactions with the team. Showing understanding and honesty can strengthen relationships and foster a positive work environment. Providing constructive feedback is crucial for employee growth and development. Positive reinforcement and constructive criticism help team members improve and feel supported in their roles. Maintaining transparency in all communications is key to building a culture of trust within the team. Open and honest dialogue fosters a sense of belonging and encourages collaboration. When giving feedback, it's important to offer public praise and private critique. Recognizing achievements publicly while providing constructive criticism privately helps maintain a balanced and supportive feedback environment. Founders of early-stage startups may spend a significant amount of time on recruiting efforts. Investing in building a strong team is crucial for the success and growth of a startup venture. In tight-knit startup teams, having a maximum of 15 employees can foster a strong sense of camaraderie and collaboration. Smaller teams often promote better communication and teamwork among members. During challenging times, such as a cash crunch, tough decisions may need to be made to ensure the survival of the business. Avi Savar's experience at Big Fuel demonstrates the importance of making difficult but necessary choices to navigate through tough situations. In conclusion, effective communication, teamwork, transparent compensation, regular meetings, empathy, feedback, and transparency are essential elements in keeping your startup team motivated and engaged. By implementing these strategies, founders can cultivate a positive work environment that fosters growth and success.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The day is June 30, 1905. Annalen der Physik, one of the biggest scientific publishers in Europe, receives a letter from a 26 years man. The letter contains four articles. The editor is bemused. The author claims that he can curve time, expand space, and turn matter into energy. His theories — blowing up 300 years of common sense about the laws of the universe — sound more like magic than science. The author doesn’t even have any affiliation with a university or a research lab. He’s just an office patent examiner! Unknown to the editor, this side hustler will radically transform the way we understand the world. And 1905 will go in history as Annus Mirabilis: The Miracle Year. On December 31, 1999, Time magazine named Albert Einstein, the author of these four articles, the most influential person of the 20th century, outranking Gandhi, Churchill, and Roosevelt! Walter Isaacson, who penned the biography of Einstein, argue that Einstein’s day job as a patent office clerk was a blessing in disguise: “The work was very easy for him. He quickly went through each patent application and checked the math. He would finish his daily duties in about four hours.” Bored and with ample mental energy to keep going, the physicist used his leftover focus to build his side hustle, i.e., developing groundbreaking theories in physics.\", \"sampled\": \"In the realm of Gender Diversity in Technology, a significant perception challenge exists. A striking 48% of girls and women feel that STEM subjects are more aligned with male careers, highlighting a crucial area for change. Contrary to this perception, India stands out as a beacon of progress in gender parity in STEM education. The country is nearly achieving gender balance in graduate-level STEM studies, showcasing a positive trend towards inclusivity. Within the Indian IT workforce, women currently occupy 34% of the roles, indicating a notable presence in the industry. However, there is still work to be done as women hold only 25% of IT jobs, pointing towards a disparity in job distribution. To address this gender gap, various initiatives like Women in IT and Girls Who Code are being implemented by companies. These programs aim to attract and retain female talent in the tech sector, fostering a more diverse and inclusive work environment. Research by Katy Ring from 451 Research sheds light on the current landscape, stating, \\\"Women now make up 34% of the IT workforce in India, with the majority of these workers under the age of 30.\\\" This statistic underscores the increasing presence of women in tech, especially among the younger generation.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Set up Project Folder and Start Collecting Moodboard There are plenty of sites for inspiration — Dribbble, Behance, Pttrns, Pinterest etc. It’s really easy to find similar projects to the one you shall be working on. Additionally, there may be already a solution to a problem you’re experiencing and trying to solve. When I start working on a new project, I always setup a folder with folders named — Source Files, Screens & Export, Inspiration & Resources. I save everything I find on the internet to Inspiration folder to be able to use it later to create basic moodboards. This folder could be filled with anything from plugins, swatches or even full case studies from Behance. 2. Going Low Fidelity Whiteboard If anyone can remember, I didn’t care much about the quality of wireframes in my previous article. The methods I use now are the following: If we want to add a new feature or redesign a process we sit down and everybody at the meeting starts sketching ideas on a whiteboard, paper or even an iPad.\", \"sampled\": \"Attention to detail plays a significant role in freelance writing, publishing, and marketing. Proper punctuation and grammar are fundamental for creating compelling copy that resonates with readers. In various industries, details continue to hold significant importance, showcasing the value of meticulous work. Corporate decision-makers highly regard individuals who demonstrate a keen eye for detail in their work. Editorial work can sway acquisition decisions in publishing, highlighting the impact of editing on content quality. Identifying and rectifying common writing errors is a critical step towards achieving success in the field. Collaborating with a copyediting expert can enhance the overall quality of your writing projects. Utilizing proofreading techniques such as altering text appearance and reading backward can aid in detecting errors effectively. There are five proven ways to eliminate writing mistakes and improve the quality of your content. A whopping five million lines of error-free code underscore the importance of precision in any written work. Finding and addressing slip-ups through four effective methods can significantly enhance the clarity and coherence of your writing. Implementing one great strategy can elevate the quality of your writing, making your content more impactful and engaging.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Are you afraid or experiencing financial hardship during the coronavirus spread like I am? I can honestly say it gives me more of a reason to make my resume perfect, and it gives me the time to get the certification I’ve been wanting to further my career with. I can assure you, this is the perfect time to start doing the things you put off because you were too busy. There are just so many things I can do to make this obstacle into the life raft I need to get ahead in the world. This coronavirus is trying to split communities apart. It’s making people afraid to be be within 6 feet of each other. Maybe it’s time we start thinking of ways to communicate and feel that bond while still being quarantined. It is definitely possible with the technology we have today. When was the last time you wrote a nice letter to someone? We have email now! Or the “Marco Polo” app(not a sponsor), or called someone? Tell people what you are working on, what you’re grateful for. The other day I called a friend up and just chatted with him for over an hour about nothing in particular, and felt a deep sense of friendship once the call ended. The kind I felt with him when we lived by each other and worked together. I haven’t felt that close of a bond with him in 7 years. Now I feel like calling all of my friends and just seeing how they are doing. It feels good. Social media is a great way to connect but it’s also very intrusive and has built distrust within our social circles and communities. With all the companies spying on our psychological patterns and how we use social media, I understand why some of the older generation are wary to trust these corporations. I am the first one to say social media is not the healthiest way to communicate. But a time like this calls for thinking outside of that explanation and expand our communities through knowledge in the social media realm. Instead of fearing it, get to understand it and create your boundaries around it. Now is the time to pick up that phone and strengthen a bond with an old friend.\", \"sampled\": \"Productivity is often a hot topic in the world of personal development. Planning your day the night before can significantly boost your morning productivity. This simple strategy allows you to hit the ground running and avoid wasting time figuring out your priorities when you wake up. Completing an uncomfortable task early in the morning can have a profound impact on your energy levels throughout the day. By tackling a challenging task first thing, you set a positive tone for the rest of your day and build momentum to tackle other important responsibilities. Top executives like Indra Nooyi and Bob Iger are known for their early morning routines that set the stage for productive days. Rising early gives them a head start on the day, allowing them to accomplish tasks before distractions arise, setting a strong foundation for success. To optimize your productivity, avoid getting lost in busy work at the start of your day. Instead, focus on high-value tasks that align with your goals. This focused approach in the first hour of work can set a productive tone for the entire day. Using a daily checklist with estimated task completion times can revolutionize your planning process. By breaking down your day into manageable chunks and assigning time estimates to each task, you can better prioritize and allocate your time effectively. Productive mornings often start with early wake-up calls. Successful individuals like Jack Dorsey swear by this practice, allowing them to get a head start on their day and accomplish meaningful tasks before the rest of the world wakes up. David Allen once said, \\\"If you don’t pay appropriate attention to what has your attention, it will take more of your attention than it deserves.\\\" This quote emphasizes the importance of focusing on high-value tasks and avoiding distractions that can derail your productivity. To enhance your focus and efficiency, consider writing down your top tasks for the next day before leaving the office. This simple habit can help you hit the ground running in the morning with a clear plan of action, minimizing decision fatigue and increasing productivity. Night-before planning isn't just about time management; it's also a stress-reduction strategy. By preparing for the next day ahead of time, you can save precious minutes in the morning and start your day with a sense of purpose and calm.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"What Did They Do? They collected two survey data representative of China. The first one, involving 11,131 people, was conducted before the coronavirus outbreak was publically reported at the end of December. The second one has 3,000 people and was administered around mid-February. The surveys measure emotional well-being. “Specifically, they indicated whether they smiled or laughed a lot yesterday, and whether they experienced a lot of enjoyment, happiness, anger, sadness, stress, or worry yesterday,” Dr. Yang and Professor Ma wrote. Demographics like age, sex, income, region, and marital status were also included. The second survey added measures of sense of control and perceived knowledge about the coronavirus —i.e., how it spreads and preventive measures. Actual knowledge of preventive measures was also surveyed separately.\", \"sampled\": \"As Todd Brison aptly puts it, \\\"The writer’s job is not to write. It is to think and to make others think.\\\" In today's world, where people are drowning in content, capturing and maintaining attention has become a significant challenge. Contrary to common belief, good writing doesn’t always attract attention; it's the element of novelty that truly captivates readers. When it comes to writing, the right story holds more weight than the perfect adjective. A skilled writer excels at identifying gaps and delving into unconventional perspectives to engage their audience. Seth Godin eloquently emphasized the shift towards valuing genuine care over traditional markers of success, stating, \\\"We’re living in the first moment in time… when credentials, access to capital, and raw power have been dwarfed by the simple question: do I care about what you do?\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Web Notifications Introduction: News on Lock(screens) How the Guardian US Mobile Innovation Lab has experimented with telling stories in web notifications. An Android lock screen at the beginning of our EU referendum experiment. At the end of last week, we (the Mobile Lab) all turned to look at each other. “Can you believe it’s only been 18 days since we ran our first public experiment?” one of us asked. We could not in fact believe it. After months of ramping up the lab, we were suddenly operating at full speed. The last several weeks have been huge for us: We welcomed another developer, Connor Jennings; we ran two public versions of our jobs report notification experiment; we covered two primary nights through notifications; and we ran a notifications experiment covering the EU referendum. And we’ve learned a lot. We’ve known since establishing our five areas of focus that we wanted to experiment with notifications. The jobs report, primaries, and now the EU referendum, have been interesting for a few reasons: they are events that unfold over the course of a number of hours where there are smaller developments throughout. Most organizations are using news alerts to push out top news, and include only straight headlines. This is with good reason: alerts require an app download, and a user’s options are usually either to get every alert (although some organizations have broken down the options to set of topical preferences: sports, business, etc) or none, making the risk of alienating a user with one too many alerts a constant. But notifications, we believe, can also be used to tell a targeted piece of a story (or even the entire story itself) right on users’ lockscreens. With the capacity for pushing out alerts now built into Chrome (aside from its iOS app), publishers suddenly have a broader range of options for what they do with the format. It has some defined parameters (character counts, limited actions, for example) but is also flexible enough to deliver information in a variety of ways and formats. It also offers publishers options for how long they run a set of alerts from one time to ad infinitum. For users, signing up to receive a set of notifications requires no big commitments like a download or registration. However, with this access to users’ lockscreens delivering relevant, timely content is no less a challenge. Editors need to make careful choices about what they deliver, how many alerts they send and how often. We’ve started experimenting and hope others will, too. As of now we’ve tested five types of web notifications, which we’ve profiled here (click on names for full write-ups of each): Interactive — which allows users to chart a path through a story, or can help break down a dense topic like the US jobs report. — which allows users to chart a path through a story, or can help break down a dense topic like the US jobs report.\", \"sampled\": \"In his latest thought-provoking blog post, author Joe Duncan delves into the intriguing concept of having a \\\"Carol Baskin\\\" in one's life. Drawing on popular culture and historical parallels, Duncan explores the idea of a person, symbolized as \\\"Carol Baskin,\\\" who is obstinately focused on ruining someone's aspirations, ideas, goals, or dreams. This embodiment of opposition and obstacle is a unique perspective on adversity, making for an engaging read. To illustrate this concept, Duncan turns to the world of television, using the popular TV show \\\"Tiger King\\\" as an example. The series is known for the intense feud between its characters Joe Exotic and Carol Baskin. Through this example, the author effectively demonstrates the dynamics of a relationship where one individual seems committed to the destruction of the other's ambitions. However, Duncan doesn't stop at contemporary pop culture references. He further substantiates his argument by drawing parallels from history. The rivalries between Pompeius the Great and Julius Caesar, and Joseph Stalin and Leon Trotsky are brought up as examples. These historical feuds, much like the one in \\\"Tiger King,\\\" encapsulate the essence of a \\\"Carol Baskin\\\" figure in one's life. As Duncan builds his case, he makes a bold claim: everyone has a \\\"Carol Baskin\\\" in their life. This universal presence of a person hellbent on sabotaging one's aspirations is a profound idea that encourages readers to examine their own lives. This recurring theme of opposition is not just a dramatic plot device in TV shows or a historical phenomenon, but a reality of everyday life. While the presence of a \\\"Carol Baskin\\\" may seem daunting, Duncan uses this concept to criticize what he terms as \\\"paranoia culture\\\" and \\\"hierarchy complexes.\\\" He argues against the belief held by some that the entire world is conspiring against them. Instead, he suggests that the \\\"Carol Baskin\\\" figure is often a single entity, not a collective force. To further delve into the nature of this \\\"Carol Baskin\\\" figure, Duncan turns to the words of the philosopher Machiavelli. The author quotes Machiavelli, who once said, \\\"Because of the envious nature of humans, it has always been no less dangerous to discover new methods and institutions than to explore unknown oceans and lands, since humans are quicker to criticize than praise the deeds of others.\\\" This quotation serves to illustrate the envious nature of humans, shedding light on why a \\\"Carol Baskin\\\" figure might exist in the first place. In his exploration of the \\\"Carol Baskin\\\" phenomenon, Duncan not only provides an engaging analysis but also encourages readers to introspect. The idea of having a person in one's life who is determined to destroy one's dreams may be uncomfortable, but it also serves as a reminder of the adversity one must overcome to achieve success. In conclusion, the author's exploration of the \\\"Carol Baskin\\\" concept is an intriguing blend of pop culture, historical parallels, and philosophical insights. Whether it's a TV show, a historical rivalry, or a quote from Machiavelli, the author creatively uses various elements to articulate his argument, making the blog post a compelling read.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"You’re Creating a New Programming Language — What Will the Syntax Look Like? I asked a bunch of programmers about their favorite syntax — here’s what they said A little while ago I decided to have a little fun and wrote an article titled “My Favorite Pieces of Syntax in 8 Different Programming Languages.” I published it and then decided to share it on a subreddit — r/ProgrammingLanguages. This led to an interesting discussion about programming language syntax, as users shared their own favorites. It left me with no choice: I had to write a new article with my favorite pieces of syntax from the r/ProgrammingLanguages community.\", \"sampled\": \"Nicole Janeway Bills, a well-versed author in the field of Data Science, recently penned a fascinating blog post. This blog post provides a unique perspective on the experience of participating in a Data Science Bootcamp, likening it to a thrilling theme park ride. The bootcamp is described as an intense journey that demands total concentration, but it also fosters a collaborative atmosphere. During breaks, participants are encouraged to share their stories, recommendations, and ideas. A key focus of the post is the discussion of ten specific Python skills that are not typically covered in such bootcamps.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Security awareness, and knowledge increased a lot recently. More and more developers know what is XSS and they have already heard at least about CSRF, organizations have security departments, and most project leaders understand that security auditors and ethical hackers are their friends not their enemies. And still, everyone who is involved in the application security business has a typical, recurring experience: that old vulnerabilities, well-known security problems come back again and again, and many applications are still ridiculously easy to hack. Why is that so? One obvious answer is that security is a matter of focus. Photo by Paul Skorupskas on Unsplash Even if you are a developer who knows about a given vulnerability, you might still introduce it to your code, simply because you focus so much on functionality that you forget to realize the security aspect. When you are running deeply within one track, it is hard to stop, climb out of your pit and look around from a higher perspective. It even happens to security experts, who turn to development, sometimes they introduce vulnerabilities without realizing it, or (more often) realize it only after they look through their code with their “security glass” on. Another important reason is that at the organizational level security is a typical weakest link problem. Photo by Shaojie on Unsplash If there is just one Junior developer in the team, who does not understand SQLi, he can easily introduce several SQLi vulnerabilities before anyone realizes it, and teaches him how to do it better. Actually there is more about it than just the typical weakest link problem. There is something that we can call the accumulation of knowledge. Some types of knowledge accumulate very well, while others accumulate hardly, or not at all. If there is one developer who understands graph-algorithms well, she can do all the graph-related tasks, and the quality of those solutions will not depend on the graph-knowledge of other developers. It is enough if others know that she is the right person to find, when there is a graph related problem. If one developer creates a great UI component others can use it even without fully understanding how it works. In many aspects, IT knowledge accumulates extremely well (that’s why we have such a rapid improvement in the software industry). But alas, security knowledge cumulates hardly. The fact that one developer knows an immense lot about security does not really help other developers who are less knowledgeable. The problem is that those other developers won’t know when to ask a question. After all, if you never heard about XXE, and you use an XML parser, you won’t go and ask your colleague: ‘Hey, is there any possible security issue with using an xml parser with DTD enabled?’. In order to ask such questions you already have to know at least something about XXE. So to sum it up, there are three problems which help hackers and hinder devs: focusing solely on functionality, the weakest link problem, and that security knowledge does not accumulate well. How can we address these problems? I believe (with many other security experts), that a true solution can only be found at the organizational level. You have to create the right processes, roles, practices at organizational level.\", \"sampled\": \"Olivia Pettman, a full-time student juggling multiple commitments, grapples with a question that many aspiring authors face: is there a need for a personal website? As she navigates her journey towards a writing career, the thought of creating and managing a website adds another layer to her bustling schedule. Yet, she can't ignore the potential advantages a personal website might bring to her as an author. We live in a world where over four billion people are connected via the internet. It's a staggering number that represents a vast sea of potential readers. For instance, even if a minuscule percentage of these internet users stumble upon an author's website, that could translate to around 4,000 potential viewers. The internet's broad reach and 24/7 availability make it a powerful tool for writers to share their work with the world. Websites have become an effective platform for promoting products, and for authors, their books are their products. A personal website can serve as a showcase for an author's work, a place where potential readers can discover and explore their writings. Having a dedicated space for one's work not only helps in promoting it but also adds a touch of professionalism. Joanna Penn, an established author, vouches for the importance of a website for an author's career. She says, \\\"I’ve built a multi-six-figure business off the back of my author websites… Your website is one of the most important things to get sorted if you’re serious about your author Career…It’s your home on the internet and the hub for your books. It’s how readers, agents, publishers, journalists, bloggers and podcasters judge how professional you are.\\\" Indeed, many authors recognize this and have their own websites. Notable authors like Eloise Edstein and Simon Kemp have dedicated websites where they share their work, engage with their readers, and build their online presence. A personal website allows an author to control their narrative and present their work the way they want it to be seen. Contrary to popular belief, having a website doesn't necessarily cost anything. Free platforms allow individuals to create and host their websites without any financial investment. This makes having a personal website a feasible option for aspiring authors, even those on a tight budget. Social media platforms can also play a pivotal role in leading potential readers to an author's website. Given the popularity of social media, an author can leverage these platforms to attract more visitors to their site. It can serve as a bridge connecting the author with their potential readers, fostering a sense of community and engagement. However, like any venture on the internet, having a website comes with its own set of risks, one of which is hacking. The thought of one's site being compromised can be daunting, but as Helen Keller once said, \\\"Life is either a daring adventure or nothing.\\\" The potential risks shouldn't deter an aspiring author from exploring the possibilities a personal website can offer. As Olivia Pettman weighs the pros and cons, she remains contemplative about the idea of having a website as an aspiring author. The decision ultimately rests on her shoulders, but the potential benefits a personal website can bring to her authorship are undeniable. After all, in the vast digital landscape of the internet, a personal website can serve as a beacon, guiding readers towards an author's work.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"If You Want a Better Life, Tell Yourself Better Metaphors. The Story You Tell Yourself Is the Story You Live By You probably do not notice it but we use metaphors all the time. Photo by Tyler Nix on Unsplash We use metaphors to sound more than just cool and philosophical. Metaphors enable us to bring structure to our daily lives, create new meaning, interpret our past experiences, and plan our future aspirations. In short, like the role of DNA, metaphors are the linguistic building blocks to our reality. They are so ingrained in what we do and how we do it that they have become invisible to us. We barely give it a second thought and that’s why metaphors are so powerful. They operate in the background of our existence, almost like the strings and we are the puppets. Metaphors control how we interpret the experiences in our lives. For instance, metaphors give a conceptual meaning to a highly abstract concept such as love. The most common metaphors we have for love is: Love Is A Journey. This is why we get common phrases such as: We’ve hit a roadblock in our relationship This relationship has come to a dead-end I feel like we are spinning our wheels in this relationship A few of these are actually starting to sound familiar to me… Metaphors are also the reason we associate the abstract concept of being ‘happy’ with the orientational metaphor of being ‘up’. You’ll often hear people say: I am on a high today I feel like we are coming up That boosted my spirits By the same token, we tend to associate ‘sadness’ with being ‘down’. I am feeling a bit low today My spirits sank I am depressed In essence, the distillation of everything that makes life meaningful (e.g love, happiness, sadness) can only be adequately captured through metaphors. We can’t escape the metaphor’s power to shape our experiential existence and bring meaning to our daily activities. We could not have designed a more perfect linguistic tool to program how we think, feel and act. I hope you are starting to see why metaphors are so important. What I am trying to say is that the metaphors we tell ourselves are the metaphors we live by. They ultimately constrain the range of possible actions and opportunities we think we have in a given context. We act to be coherent with the stories we tell ourselves. Humans are made to avoid cognitive dissonance, we don’t like being consciously inconsistent with ourselves. We want our actions to align with our identity and for better or for worse, metaphors are what define our identity. The implications of this are enormous. If you live by negative metaphors, you will only really have negative experiences. For instance, two people can have the same experience but live by different metaphors and thus elicit different actions. Where one person can experience learning and excitement, another person can experience failure and discouragement. The interpretation is solely determined by the metaphoric filter we use. Thus, the only difference between one person moving and one person stagnating in life are the metaphors they tell themselves. This is not exactly new either. There has been an enormous amount of research into this field in recent times. Carol Dweck’s fixed and growth mindset is essentially a theory that uses contrasting metaphors for human intelligence. The fixed mindset follows the metaphor that the mind is concrete . It is difficult to change once set and only gets more difficult as you age. . It is difficult to change once set and only gets more difficult as you age. The growth mindset is a metaphor that the mind is a sponge. It is the notion that the mind is malleable and can change with enough deliberate effort.\", \"sampled\": \"Dieting at Christmas can be a challenging endeavor due to the abundance of food. The festive season is renowned for its plethora of indulgent meals, tempting treats, and celebratory beverages that can make sticking to a diet a Herculean task. It's a time of year when our commitment to healthful eating is often tested, and many of us find ourselves struggling with the temptation to overindulge. To help navigate this tricky terrain, the American Psychological Association conducted a study examining decision-making in situations with two equally desirable or conflicting options. This situation is all too familiar during the Christmas season, when we often find ourselves torn between sticking to our diets and enjoying the festive feasts. The study, carried out by psychologists Davidai and Gilovich, offers insights that could be beneficial for those trying to maintain a healthy diet during the holiday season. The study suggests a method to minimize regret when choosing between two options. This approach is particularly relevant when faced with diet-related decisions, where both options - indulging in festive foods or sticking to your diet - might seem equally appealing. The method requires a shift in perspective, encouraging us to consider the long-term consequences rather than just the immediate satisfaction. This method involves considering which option one will be better able to live with afterward. For instance, if you're faced with the choice of enjoying a second helping of Christmas pudding or sticking to your diet, ask yourself which decision you'd be more comfortable with in the long run. Would you feel more satisfied sticking to your diet, or would the joy of indulging in the festive treat outweigh any potential guilt? Planning and making a decision beforehand can reduce regret and increase satisfaction. This could involve deciding in advance which festive treats you'll allow yourself to enjoy and when, or setting specific boundaries for your diet during the holiday season. By making these decisions in advance, you can enjoy your chosen indulgences without guilt, knowing that you're still staying within the parameters you've set for yourself. Making a choice based on what you can better live with can reduce the likelihood of regretting that choice later. This approach encourages mindful decision-making, prompting us to consider our future feelings and wellbeing when making choices. By considering the long-term impacts of our decisions, we can make choices that we're less likely to regret later on. Let's consider an example to illustrate this. Take two couples, Jane and Paul, and Tom and Sarah. Both couples enjoy the festive season, but they approach their diet differently. Jane and Paul decide to indulge in all the festive treats, while Tom and Sarah plan their meals and stick to their diet. By the end of the festive season, both Jane and Paul have each gained 4lbs. Despite enjoying the festive treats, they regret their decision as they now have to work hard to lose the extra weight. On the other hand, Tom and Sarah, who made a conscious choice to stick to their diet, have each lost 4lbs. They enjoyed their planned treats without guilt and are satisfied with their decision. This study by the American Psychological Association provides a valuable strategy for those trying to maintain a healthy diet during the festive season. By considering which option you'll be better able to live with in the long term, you can make decisions that you're less likely to regret. This approach can help you navigate the holiday season without compromising your diet or your enjoyment of the festive treats. In conclusion, dieting during Christmas doesn't have to be a struggle. By employing mindful decision-making strategies, such as those suggested by the American Psychological Association, you can enjoy the festive season without compromising your health goals. So this Christmas, remember to consider the long-term impacts of your decisions and choose what you'll be better able to live with. Happy Holidays!\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I’ve gotten very interested in the life cycle of programming language usage. What programming language is “the most popular”, “the most useful”, “best for teaching”, or just “best”? And in particular, I would like to understand differences based on who or where we are talking about. For example, most popular language used in the enterprise, or for game programmers, or in academia, or in startups, etc. There is no shortage of studies of this question, and as a start, to tickle my interest, I’ve started a list of those studies. I will continue to update this post as I find out more. Github is the code repository I know best, but one can be positive that it is not the only one. What we don’t know is how representative it is in general, or of some segment, for example open source. From Githut we can see that JavaScript and Java are the two hottest, and they are both growing. However if you look at Github’s own report, a slightly different picture emerges. Toibe Software is an outfit that seems to have very comprehensive information, although I’ve not yet seen exactly what their methodology. Looking at their top level report, again I see Java in the number one place. The interesting thing is that here, the older languages like C and C++ show up pretty high. Another perspective is what language is being used to teach programming. In this paper the claim is made that Python is Now the Most Popular Introductory Teaching Language at Top U.S. Universities. But according to that article, Java is a close number two followed far behind by Matlap and C. An interesting related question is what programming language is easier or more effective to teach programming. Here I would want to defer to people actually teaching introductory programming. I could find various article espousing different perspectives. Many teachers I have come across believe that Python is a great first language. …\", \"sampled\": \"In 2007, Geraint Clarke was not just another sales assistant in a clothing store. He was a 17-year-old wunderkind, outperforming not just his colleagues, but entire store clusters. Clarke's success was remarkable. Selling more store cards than any other individual or team, he quickly became an indispensable asset to the company. His achievement was not just a personal triumph, but a beacon of success that shone brightly in the retail landscape. The company he worked for was quick to recognize his talent. They realized that Clarke's sales strategy was a gold mine that could be leveraged to increase overall sales. They decided to tap into his expertise, asking him to share his secret sauce with their other sales assistants. In 2008, Clarke's career took a turn. The 17-year-old prodigy, who had once dominated the sales floor, was now tasked with retraining his colleagues. He was given the responsibility to share his sales tactics, transforming a team of average salespeople into high performers. His technique, which had proven so successful in selling store cards, was now being applied on a larger scale. Clarke's impact on the company was significant. His sales strategy did not just boost the store card sales, but it also improved the overall performance of the team. His peers, who had once been outperformed by the young prodigy, were now selling more thanks to his guidance. However, Clarke's journey did not stop there. After 2008, he transitioned his sales skills into other ventures. His expertise in sales and marketing, which had been honed on the retail floor, was now being applied to new challenges and opportunities. Clarke's story is a testament to the power of effective sales techniques. His success as a sales assistant, his role as a trainer, and his subsequent transition into other fields of sales and marketing serve as a blueprint for aspiring salespeople. His journey is a reminder that with the right skills and strategies, one can excel in any field.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Photo Credit: Unsplash Have you felt repressed? Have you felt ashamed of yourself, of your sexuality, afraid of being too loud? Have you felt embarrassed by your emotions, believing you are too sensitive or dramatic? You are not alone. “Men who do not turn to face their own pain are too often prone to inflict it on others.” Both men and women have many shadows, pain that has been shoved away. Every year, over 10 million Americans are victims of psychological and physical abuse from people they love and trust the most. I want to share my trauma with you. I want to share how I have learned to feel safe and whole. 14 years old, Courtesy of the author, Allison Crady At 14 years old, I was bright, curious, and energized by the world around me. As my healthy adult self, I would tell young Allison, “You are beautiful and full of light. I will give you the unconditional love you need. The people around you are in pain, and it’s not your fault.” Religion, especially men in religion, traumatized me. Religious teachers taught me that as a woman, I am meant to be quiet, submissive, and “sexually pure.” If I fail to mute myself and save my sexuality for marriage, then I am evil. I am not evil; I am courageous and powerful, and that scares them. We need to integrate our shadows Our American culture has become increasingly masculine and toxic. We are taught to be productive above all else, how to do more with our time. We are not taught how to be more authentic, to embrace gratitude, joy, and being. We need more nurturing, acceptance, curiosity, empathy, and kindness of the feminine consciousness to heal. We need to explore and integrate our shadows, to stop passing judgment on ourselves and each other. We are human beings, not super-humans or machines. We do not have to pretend to be perfect. We can find safe spaces to explore the darkness. When we integrate our shadows, we stop feeling repressed. We stop accepting the expectations and “truths” that have been given to us. Feeling, exploring, and healing our shadows make us more balanced humans. “Survivors of abuse show us the strength of their personal spirit every time they smile.” 21 years old, Courtesy of the author, Allison Crady I was not taught to create physical or emotional boundaries, to keep my heart safe. I had been criticized for dressing immodestly and “being a distraction,” and I felt rebellious. I have been sexually abused by men multiple times, and I felt guilty. It must have been my fault. I never shared my story, and I internalized that guilt. For years I have struggled with owning my voice, expressing my needs, and embracing my sexuality without feeling ashamed or embarrassed. I never deserved that treatment. I definitely did not “earn” abuse through my wardrobe. I felt so small and ashamed. We need to nurture ourselves Our culture’s Capitalistic messages convince us that we need more to feel happy and complete.\", \"sampled\": \"In a recent interview, Aaron James, the author of \\\"Assholes: A Theory,\\\" sat down with Jean-François Marmion to discuss his book's insights into the nature of assholes in our society. According to James, an asshole is a person who accords himself special advantages in social life and feels immune from reproach. It's not merely about exhibiting bad behavior now and then; it's about a consistent pattern of such behavior that makes one an asshole. It's important to distinguish between someone acting like an asshole and a person who is an asshole. The mark of a true asshole, as per James, is consistency in their behavior. The occasional outburst or rude remark doesn't automatically make one an asshole. It's the persistent, unapologetic display of such behavior that qualifies someone as an asshole. Being an asshole, as per James, comes down to a person’s social behavior and a failure to show interest in others. At the core of this behavior is a deeply rooted sense of entitlement. This entitlement goes beyond a mere expectation of respect or consideration; it's an unwavering belief that they are inherently deserving of special treatment or privileges. This sense of entitlement, according to James, often stems from privilege. Privileged people have a much greater risk of becoming eminent assholes. This isn't to say that all privileged individuals are assholes, but the correlation between privilege and asshole behavior cannot be ignored. The environment also plays a significant role in shaping assholes. The broader culture, subculture, and social sphere contribute to creating assholes. These influences can amplify or suppress the asshole tendencies within individuals, depending on the values and behaviors that are celebrated or discouraged within these spheres. In the same vein, James argues that certain locations, like the United States, are culturally favorable for assholes to thrive. The competitive, individualistic culture often associated with the United States can foster a sense of entitlement, leading to a higher prevalence of assholes. As an example of a supreme asshole, James cites Donald Trump. Trump's consistent pattern of taking special advantages and feeling immune from reproach makes him a prime example in James's theory. Other figures like Chris Christie and Kim Jong-un are also mentioned in the same context, demonstrating that assholes are not confined to one culture or society. According to James, the best course of action is to avoid assholes altogether. Assholes can cause many frustrations and problems for too many people. Their persistent behavior can create a toxic environment, affecting the well-being of those around them. However, James acknowledges that when assholes succeed, it’s possible to feel jealousy. Their blatant disregard for others and their ability to get away with it can sometimes be frustratingly appealing. But it's important to remember the negative impacts of their behavior on others and the society at large. In conclusion, understanding the nature of assholes, according to James, is crucial for recognizing and dealing with such individuals effectively. By identifying the traits, causes, and influences that shape assholes, we can better navigate our social interactions and contribute to a more respectful and considerate society.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"What genre should you write in? Thriller books sell a lot of copies. So does science fiction, romance and even erotic fiction books. A few years ago, a book marketing expert told me self-published authors earn a good living writing in these genres. I wanted to earn a good living from writing books too. So I spent a couple of months trying to write a thriller book that I could sell. I got up early every morning to work on it. And I hated it. The process felt a lot more like work than anything enjoyable, and I couldn’t get the story right, let alone finish it. I felt like I’d never become an author. 1. Are you writing the truth? Robert McKee is a screenwriting guru that film companies like Pixar call when they have problems with their scripts. He’s also the author of the popular book every writer should read: Story: Substance, Structure, Style, and the Principles of Screenwriting. A couple of years ago, he gave a storytelling conference in a hotel in Sligo, in the West of Ireland. At the end of the conference, I got a chance to meet McKee. They say you should never meet your heroes, and I was a little nervous. I thanked him for his talk and then asked, “How does a writer figure out what to write about?” Suffice to say, I didn’t elaborate on my attempt to write lousy thrillers or erotic fiction. He took a copy of Story from me and autographed the first page of it with this piece of advice, “Write the truth.” I didn’t think much about how to apply it for a couple of weeks until I started writing thrillers again. 2. What books are in your library? One morning, I got frustrated with the manuscript for my thriller and ripped it up. I was nowhere near the end. I turned around and looked at my bookshelf. My bookshelf was full of business books, biographies, memoirs, and books about creativity and even some self-help. I wasn’t reading any thriller books or much science fiction or erotic fiction. And yet, for some reason, I was trying to write those types of stories. Instead of writing my personal truth, I was in it for the money. It was no wonder I felt blocked and miserable. So I started writing non-fiction about problems I had at work and in my business. I started writing personal essays about creativity, fitness and parenting. I found writing my form of the truth, like McKee advised, felt much easier and more enjoyable. So, if you’re feeling blocked or frustrated, consider what type of books fill your bookshelf or Kindle library. Writing what you love to read will help you find ideas for your next project. What’s more, because you’ve already read a lot of these books, you understand the conventions of the genre. So you know what readers expect. 3. Are you mindful of your influences? Conflicting advice alert! When you’re in the middle of a creative project, be careful of reading too many contemporary books about a similar topic.\", \"sampled\": \"David Majister, in his analysis of the marketing strategies employed by Awaken Realms, a board games publisher, highlighted a compelling case study. The publisher, seeking to encourage support for its new campaign, ISS Vanguard, employed the six principles of influence in its marketing communication. These principles, namely reciprocity, commitment and consistency, social proof, authority, liking, and scarcity, are time-tested strategies that effectively sway people's decisions and actions. The principle of reciprocity is a potent tool in marketing, first outlined by social anthropologist Marcel Mauss in 1925. Reciprocity is based on the idea that people tend to reciprocate a positive action with another positive action. Awaken Realms cleverly applied this principle by offering a $5 credit to potential backers, thus creating a sense of indebtedness and encouraging them to support the new campaign. Awaken Realms also utilized the principle of social proof, a psychological phenomenon where people adopt the actions of a group, assuming it's the correct behavior. The company's email stated that the $5 credit was a gift for every user, thus implying a vast network of people who are also potential backers. This approach helps to create an impression of widespread support for the campaign, encouraging others to join the bandwagon. The principle of authority was another tactic employed by Awaken Realms in their marketing strategy. The email marketing campaign echoed the branding colors of Kickstarter, a well-known and trusted crowdfunding platform. By aligning their brand with Kickstarter's familiar and authoritative image, Awaken Realms was able to boost their credibility and trustworthiness in the eyes of potential backers. Another principle of influence used was liking. People are more likely to be persuaded by people or brands they like. Awaken Realms leveraged this principle by combining familiarity and novelty in their campaign. They presented their new campaign, ISS Vanguard, in a familiar context that the potential backers could resonate with, thereby enhancing their likability and increasing the chances of gaining support. The final principle of influence employed by Awaken Realms was scarcity. Scarcity is a powerful motivator for people to take immediate action. The company leveraged this by putting a time limit on the $5 credit offered in the email. This strategy created a sense of urgency and prompted potential backers to act swiftly to take advantage of the offer before it expired. The strategic application of these principles of influence by Awaken Realms led to the immense success of their email marketing campaign. It's noteworthy to mention that the company made a significant switch from Kickstarter to Gamefound due to the latter's lower fees. With Kickstarter's fees standing at 8%, the switch to Gamefound, which offered significantly lower fees, allowed Awaken Realms to retain a larger share of the funds raised from the campaign. In conclusion, the case study of Awaken Realms' marketing strategy provides valuable insights into the effective application of the principles of influence in marketing. By skillfully employing these principles, companies can create compelling marketing campaigns that resonate with their target audience, encourage support, and ultimately drive success. The value of such a well-crafted email was estimated to be $1.5 Million, a testament to the power of strategic marketing communication.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Color evokes emotion, sparks excitement, and grabs attention. Color can help draw your eye where you want it on anything from a poster or billboard to an email in your inbox. Color can even influence your mood. Did you know the color psychology behind a red and yellow combination makes you hungry? It’s no wonder well-known fast-food chains like McDonald’s and KFC use red and yellow colors in their logos. Color theory is a set of principles for creating harmonious color combinations. It’s a mixture of science and art. Understanding the fundamentals of color theory and where color comes from is important to know as a designer. Once you master it, you’ll know how to create the best color combinations for your graphic and web design projects. If you don’t believe color has an impact on your design then take a look at this example. It’s the exact same illustration, the only difference is the colors. Which one is pleasing to look at and which makes your eyes want to explode?\", \"sampled\": \"Microsoft, the tech giant, has recently released a new version of Lobe. Lobe is a unique software that allows users to create machine learning models specifically for image classification tasks. This recent update to Lobe introduces four significant improvements. Users can now select any connected camera, such as a USB camera, for data collection within the software. This enhancement provides flexibility and convenience in gathering the necessary data for model training. Another major improvement is the introduction of support for ONNX and TensorFlow.js for model export. This means that users can now export their machine learning models in these popular formats, broadening the potential application of their models. Additionally, Lobe's TensorFlow Lite export now works with the Android operating system, opening the door for mobile integration of the created models. The last two improvements are related to performance. Lobe now supports GPU acceleration, making the training of machine learning models faster and more efficient.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"My calorie cycling experience Now that you understand the basics of calorie cycling and how to do your own 30-day challenge if you decide to, here’s my experience from day 1 through day 30 and the results. Week 1 On day 1 of the challenge kickoff, I happened to be in the process of moving into my first home so I decided to give myself grace and start one day later. Quick tip: Start when you’re ready. Calorie cycling doesn’t have to be complicated (in fact it shouldn’t be) but if you’re going through a rough time right now, maybe hold off a few days. For my first attempt at calorie cycling, I wanted to try the alternate deficit method. However, on day 1 I ended up eating just at my calorie limit, not more or less as I should have when using this method. Throughout this challenge, I didn’t want to be too strict with myself. As long as I stay under my weekly calorie limit, I’m happy. This is after all an experiment. Week 1 results I ended up eating 324 calories over my deficit for the week, which comes to an average of about 46 calories more per day. As to be expected, I nowhere near reached my protein level. Again, the protein level is more of a guide, a way to help you eat more healthy foods, keep you satiated, and avoid muscle loss. So I didn’t beat myself up when I didn’t meet the goal. Instead, I was proud that I reach over 100g on most days. As for exercise, I completed 2 strength training workouts. Lower body on one day and upper body on another, each took about 30 minutes to complete. I also walked an average of just under 5,000 steps for the week. How did this affect my weight? According to the scale, I lost 3.2 lbs and 1.1% in body fat from my first week of calorie cycling. While that might sound like a successful first week, especially when I ate 324 calories over what I planned for the week, I proceeded with caution into week 2. Quick tip: Remember, the goal is sustained weight loss over time, not a quick fix that leads to regain. I also know the scale isn’t the best measurement for success. I recommend using measurements as another way to gauge success at the end of the full 30-day challenge. Week 2 I allowed myself flexibility in week 1 as I got used to calorie cycling. But this week, I tried to make a better effort to vary my calorie cycling more in the high and low days. Week 2 results I ended up eating 574 calories over my deficit for the week, which comes to an average of 82 calories more per day. At first glance, this week was a complete fail in remaining within my calorie goal. This is most likely due to the fact I consumed less protein in exchange for more carbs on a few of the days. As for exercise, I completed two strength workouts. Full body on one day, lower body on another.\", \"sampled\": \"In a recent travel experience, Niall Mcnulty, the author, found himself facing unexpected challenges due to the ongoing Coronavirus pandemic. His initial departure was supposed to be from Heathrow Terminal 5, but his flight was unexpectedly relocated to Terminal 2. This sudden change, although mildly inconvenient, was only the beginning of a journey that would prove to be anything but ordinary. During his trip to China, Niall was confronted with an unforeseen issue; the second leg of his journey was cancelled without any prior notification. This unexpected cancellation resulted in a significant disruption to his travel plans. His destination was Beijing, but his journey was not as straightforward as he had initially expected. Reacting to the abrupt cancellation, Niall decided to take the airline to the small claims court for not informing him about the cancellation. This was an action born out of frustration and a feeling of being wronged. Niall’s journey, which was initially planned to be a smooth transition from one leg to another, was now becoming a series of hurdles. Through his experience, Niall has come to recommend flying direct to avoid cancellations across multiple leg journeys. His recommendation comes from a place of having experienced the chaos and disarray that such cancellations can cause. Navigating through such a situation in the midst of a pandemic can be especially challenging. Niall was eventually able to fly to his first stop, Shanghai, but this happened 24 hours later than initially planned. This delay was a direct result of the cancellation of his second leg. His journey, which was supposed to be a smooth one, had now become a prolonged and tiring ordeal. Upon his arrival in Shanghai, Niall was required to fill out a medical questionnaire using the software, Wechat. This was a mandatory requirement considering the ongoing pandemic. Filling out a medical questionnaire has become a part of the new normal in travel, especially when the destination is a country which is closely monitoring incoming travellers like China. The complications did not end with the completion of the medical questionnaire. There was another hurdle to overcome. If there was a second leg within China, it needed to be scheduled for two weeks later due to quarantine requirements. This was another delay that Niall had to factor into his travel plans. Niall was required to quarantine for 14 days upon arrival in China. This mandatory quarantine period is part of China’s efforts to control the spread of the virus. This meant that Niall’s journey would be further delayed, and his arrival at his final destination, Beijing, would take even longer than anticipated. Reflecting on his travel experience, it is clear that the Coronavirus pandemic has significantly impacted the way we travel. From sudden cancellations to mandatory quarantines, there are several new factors that travellers need to consider. The experience of Niall Mcnulty highlights these changes and the challenges they present. In conclusion, the current health crisis has transformed the landscape of travel. The experience of Niall Mcnulty demonstrates the need for flexibility and adaptability in these unprecedented times. Travelling, especially internationally, is now fraught with uncertainties and challenges, but with careful planning and patience, it is still possible to navigate this new terrain.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I published my first article in November 2014. I had no idea what I was doing, but I had fun. Ironically, the only way to keep this fun around long-term was to consider writing the most serious job I’ve ever had. So I committed. Now, over four years later, nothing is the same. Except that part. The fun’s still here. I even have a slightly better idea of what I’m doing. But writing online is different from anything I’d ever imagined or associated with that word. Here are 3 lessons I rarely see others mention, but that helped me get to now. 1. People aren’t waiting to change their perspective. You have to do it for them. No none walks around saying: “I wish someone changed my mind about productivity.” That’s not how humans work. We’re intensely focused on our day, ourselves, our next task at hand, zipping through everyday life with tunnel vision. Browsing the web and reading online are no different. If anything, we hope to bump into ideas that confirm our worldview, not challenge it. This onus lies entirely on you, the writer. People don’t just underestimate it but, often, abandon this challenge altogether, going the easier route of telling people what they know they’ll want to hear. This works for a while, especially when you’re just beginning, but it gets old really quick. Readers don’t know they’re driven by these biases, but from feeding them, even subconsciously, they still get bored. And that’s a feeling they know. So they move on. What they might not know they want — but need — is an idea that cracks their perspective. A tiny fracture, just big enough for them to raise their eyebrows. To blink twice. To scroll up again. Presenting this idea is what I try to do in most of my headlines. The article is just the fulfillment center. The line between offending people and making them think is infinitely small. But learning to dance on it is one of the most important skills you’ll ever learn. 2. The only agenda that works is to have no agenda. That sounds weird. Of course I have an agenda. Everyone does. I want dollars. Followers. Views. However, the single greatest way to maximize all of those in the long run is to not care when you’ll get them and in what order. Because that’s the only way you’ll be free to overwhelm the platforms you write on with generosity, consistency, and genuine care. It’s not about you. Great work never is. The sooner you can act that out, the earlier you’ll take off. What does the host want? What do readers want? What does the platform want for its readers and what do readers expect for giving their invaluable time and attention to this place?\", \"sampled\": \"\\\"The Last Kings of Shanghai\\\" by Jonathan Kaufman is a compelling narrative that chronicles the stories of two influential Jewish families, the Sassoons and the Kadoories, in Shanghai. Covering 175 years of Chinese history, the book delves deep into their lives and the crucial roles they played in the shaping of modern China. The Sassoons, originally from Baghdad, were instrumental in the European imperialism of China, establishing a vast business empire that spanned across the Asian continent. On the other hand, the Kadoories, despite being rooted in the same city, had a different journey, marked by their entrepreneurial endeavors and philanthropic efforts. Both families, however, shared an unusual characteristic - they were so separated from Chinese society that none of them ever learned to speak Chinese. Discrimination was a common experience for both the Sassoons and Kadoories. As Jews living in a predominantly non-Jewish society, they faced numerous challenges and prejudices. This was particularly evident in the 1930s and 1940s, when anti-Semitism was a backdrop to their lives. They navigated through these tumultuous times, demonstrating resilience and adaptability. Their journey was not without its share of turbulence. With Japan's alliance with Germany and the subsequent Japanese occupation of China, thousands of Jewish refugees in Shanghai were relocated. As Kaufman vividly describes, \\\"SS colonel Josef Meisinger, who had earned the moniker 'The Butcher of Warsaw' by sending thousands of Jews to their deaths in Poland, was dispatched to Shanghai... and laid out several options to 'deal with' the 'problem' of the 18,000 Shanghai refugees.\\\" The Communist takeover of China in 1949 brought about a significant shift in the fortunes of both families. The Sassoons and Kadoories lost almost all of the capital gained through their businesses. This was a turning point in their story, marking the end of their reign in Shanghai and initiating a new chapter in their lives. Despite the adversities, the families managed to leave a lasting legacy. Lawrence Kadoorie played a significant role in providing a bridge between Britain and China by providing nuclear power to Hong Kong and parts of China. This was particularly important in 1997, when Hong Kong was given back to China, marking a significant event in modern Chinese history. \\\"The Last Kings of Shanghai\\\" offers a unique perspective on the creation of modern China, weaving in personal stories with significant historical events. It is a testament to the resilience and tenacity of these families and their impact on the shaping of a nation. The book was received for review from Viking Publishing and NetGalley. Our reviewer, Jason Park, found the book to be well worth the time. He commends Kaufman for his meticulous research and compelling storytelling. The book is not just a tale of two families but also a mirror reflecting the broader socio-political landscape of China and its transformation over the years.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"COVID-19 Cases Are Rising, So Why Are Deaths Flatlining? The gap between soaring cases and falling deaths is being weaponized by the right to claim a hollow victory in the face of shameless failure. What’s really going on? Image: The Atlantic By Derek Thompson For the past few weeks, I have been obsessed with a mystery emerging in the national COVID-19 data. Cases have soared to terrifying levels since June. Yesterday, the U.S. had 62,000 confirmed cases, an all-time high — and about five times more than the entire continent of Europe. Several U.S. states, including Arizona and Florida, currently have more confirmed cases per capita than any other country in the world. But average daily deaths are down 75 percent from their April peak. Despite higher death counts on Tuesday and Wednesday, the weekly average has largely plateaued in the past two weeks. The gap between spiking cases and falling-then-flatlining deaths has become the latest partisan flashpoint. President Donald Trump has brushed off the coronavirus surge by emphasizing the lower death rate, saying that “99 percent of [COVID-19 cases] are totally harmless.” On Tuesday, Anthony Fauci, the director of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, warned Americans against “[taking] comfort in the lower rate of death” just hours before Trump tweeted triumphantly: “Death Rate from Coronavirus is down tenfold!” In the fog of pandemic, every statistic tells a story, but no one statistic tells the whole truth. Conservatives seeking refuge in today’s death counts may find, in a matter of days, that deaths are clearly resurging and their narrative is rapidly deteriorating. But liberals, too, should avoid the temptation to flatly reject any remotely positive finding, for fear that it will give succor to the president. What follows are five possible explanations for the case-death gap. Take them as complementary, rather than competing, theories. 1. Deaths lag cases — and that might explain almost everything. You can’t have a serious discussion about case and death numbers without noting that people die of diseases after they get sick. It follows that there should be a lag between a surge in cases and a surge in deaths. More subtly, there can also be a lag between the date a person dies and the date the death certificate is issued, and another lag before that death is reported to the state and the federal government. As this chart from the COVID Tracking Project shows, the official reporting of a COVID-19 death can lag COVID-19 exposure by up to a month. This suggests that the surge in deaths is coming. In Arizona, Florida, and Texas, the death surge is already happening. Since June 7, the seven-day average of deaths in those hot-spot states has increased 69 percent, according to the COVID Tracking Project. The death lag is probably the most important thing to understand in evaluating the case-death gap. But it doesn’t explain everything. Even where deaths are rising, corresponding cases are rising notably faster. 2. Expanded testing is finding more cases, milder cases, and earlier cases. There is a bad way to talk about testing, and a nuanced way to talk about it. The simplistic version, which we often hear from the president, is that cases are surging only because the number of tests is rising. That’s just wrong. Since the beginning of June, the share of COVID-19 tests that have come back positive has increased from 4.5 percent to 8 percent. Hospitalizations are skyrocketing across the South and West. Those are clear signs of an underlying outbreak. Something subtler is happening.\", \"sampled\": \"Plotly published an electric-vehicle visualization article on 2018-10-17, using data-centered views to frame the market around cars, batteries, electricity, and charging. The piece sits at the intersection of Plotly, data visualization, electric vehicles, Tesla, and charging infrastructure. Dash appears as part of that context because car companies use it, and Dash Deployment Server is described as a product used by customers. Nissan, Tesla, BMW, and other automakers are named as Dash users or Dash Deployment Server customers. The article places the United States in a vehicle-heavy setting, reporting 910 motor vehicles per 1,000 people. That figure gives the electric-vehicle discussion a baseline for understanding how large the broader vehicle market can be. Norway stands out in the article’s view of electric adoption, with nearly 40 percent electric share among new car sales during 2013–2017. Oslo, Norway, is described as the world’s EV capital, linking the country’s national share to a specific urban symbol. Norway’s electricity mix is also part of the article’s EV framing, with 98 percent of its electricity attributed to hydropower. That detail helps explain why electric vehicles can be presented there as especially tied to low-carbon power. The U.S. state view highlights California, Hawaii, Washington, Oregon, Vermont, and Georgia as leaders for EV registrations per capita. California is identified as the top U.S. state in that group, making it a central point in the domestic comparison. Tesla receives a manufacturer-level comparison in the article, where more than 90 percent of vehicles produced by Tesla were electric. That placed Tesla ahead of Zhidou and BYD Auto in the article’s production-share discussion. The article also notes that Tesla sales declined in several European countries in early 2018. Tesla responded that \\\"The data from both IHS and regional transportation departments is not reflective of actual sales data\\\". Competition for Tesla is framed through new electric models from established luxury carmakers. Porsche’s Taycan and Jaguar’s I-Pace are named as additions that brought more pressure into the electric-vehicle market. Electricity price appears as another practical measure in the article’s view of EV ownership. In 2017, the average retail electricity cost was about 11 cents per kilowatt hour. Battery cost trends are presented as a major part of the EV story. Average EV lithium-ion battery prices fell from $1,000 per kWh in 2009 to $209 per kWh in 2017. Charging infrastructure gives the article another way to compare progress across markets. France added 11,987 EV charge points in 2017, the highest total reported in the article. Tesla’s charging infrastructure is described through its \\\"Supercharger network\\\". That phrase ties the company’s vehicle strategy to a global charging network, rather than treating charging as a separate topic. The article’s country and city comparisons give electric vehicles several different frames at once. Norway and Oslo represent high electric share, while the United States represents a market with very high vehicle ownership. Manufacturer comparisons broaden the article beyond a single company. Tesla, Zhidou, BYD Auto, Porsche, Jaguar, Nissan, and BMW appear in the same wider EV landscape, though in different roles. The article also uses a regional phrase, \\\"clean and green\\\", in connection with New Zealand. That wording fits the broader theme of electricity sources, vehicle adoption, and environmental positioning around electric cars. Plotly’s role is as publisher and maker of Dash, while Dash and Dash Deployment Server connect the visualization company to automaker users and customers. The article’s facts show how visualization, software, and electric vehicles are linked in one presentation. The passage closes on measurable parts of the EV market rather than a single claim about winners. Vehicle ownership, EV sales share, hydropower, battery prices, electricity costs, registrations, and charge points all shape the article’s picture.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"OPINION How Apple Can Make Money Through a Search Engine How Apple can make up for losing $12 Billion Created with Canva Design Congress wants to break Google, claiming it has illegally thrashed the competition in the search engine market. Google dominates the market with an 86.86% market share as of July 2020. The argument is that Google pays billions of dollars to other companies to become the default search engine for its consumers. In 2019, Google paid $30bn for “traffic acquisition costs”, almost a third of its entire search revenue. This was up from $26.7bn the previous year, and up from just $6.2bn a decade earlier. Google reportedly paid $12 Billion to Apple to make Google the default search engine on Safari for iPhones. This means that if Google is regulated, Apple would lose around $12 Billion each year, which amounts to 1/5 of all its services revenue. Apple depends on Google, and their ties can be cut anytime. As an alternative, Apple is rumored to be working on its own search engine — Apple Search. So, how Apple plans to monetize it and make up for $12 Billion?\", \"sampled\": \"Creators Hub. Medium announced Creators Hub, a blog for writers using the Medium platform. The announcement carried the publication timestamp 2020-10-22 15:26:04.516000+00:00. Medium 101 is included. Creators Hub is intended to help Medium writers with writing, editing, storytelling, and building an audience. The announced resources include best practices, guidelines, writer spotlights, and creative materials from Medium staff. The blog will also include advice from writers using Medium, keeping the resource tied to people publishing on the platform. The launch collection focuses on creativity and writing, with Forge helping shape the initial group of stories. Indrani Sen contributed \\\"A Self-Editing Checklist.\\\", a checklist presented through Forge and connected to editing work for Medium writers. Michelle Woo contributed guidance on finding story ideas likely to connect with readers, bringing a practical angle to the launch collection. Amy Shearn contributed a quiz about identifying a suitable writing time, while Ross McCammon contributed a guide about writing across formats such as tweets, emails, and viral stories. The post says future Creators Hub content will include Q&As, submission guides, inspiration, and more.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Over the years, GCP (Google Cloud Platform) has introduced plethora of new services, but the US-based cloud giant is still mainly known for two products: Kubernetes and AI. As you may know since 2003 Google has been an early adopter of containerized infrastructure for their own internal workloads. They developed a container orchestration tool, Borg, later making it open-source and renaming Kubernetes. Today it is the most known container orchestration tool in the industry. Google brings their years of expertise in managing millions of containers for their own workload and introduced them as a service in 2014 in the form of the GKE (Google Kubernetes Engine). GKE is the best Kubernetes platform in the industry. Google was the first cloud vendor to provide a managed Kubernetes service. GKE is even included in their enterprise strategy, making them a differentiator among their rivals. In 2018 Google brought a new service, Anthos. A hybrid multi-cloud platform based on GKE which helps to extend Google cloud services to on-premise and other clouds. Anthos is not a single product, but rather an umbrella covering multiple services. Migrate for Anthos is a migration tool under this umbrella that allows direct migration of virtual machines (VMs) into containers running on GKE. Enterprises all over the world are trying to modernize their legacy monolithic applications into microservices and containers to bring better performance, agility and cost savings. This takes barely two lines to cover yet it is not so simple when it comes to a real scenario. It needs a lot of time and manual intervention in terms of planning, assessment, code changes, etc. By the introduction of Migrate for Anthos, GCP was able to solve some of these problems and make the enterprise cloud adoption much easier. There are many cloud migration tools available in the market and most of them tackle the VM-to-VM migration scenario. Where other cloud providers suggest the first step towards cloud adoption to be lift and shift to VMs in the cloud, GCP takes a different approach. Migrate for Anthos is a one of a kind solution to automate the process of VM-to-container migrations and make life easier for cloud engineers and developers. How Migrate for Anthos works Migrate for Anthos works on top of Anthos and uses GKE as the containerization platform. It converts VMs to containers running in GKE. Where you see “system container” you might think it is still the monolithic approach and not microservices adoption. What is the benefit then? Even though it is monolithic, it takes advantage of portability, better packaging and utilizes the flexibility of the cloud. On top of that, the workload is in the cloud in a containerized environment, allowing to further break down the application into microservices as a roadmap. The diagram above shows a full-fledged VM migrated into a container using Migrate for Anthos. After the migration, many of the VM components are now managed by GKE. Even though it is a system container, it will increase performance and improve management. There are prerequisites to be fulfilled before using Migrate for Anthos, like OS (operating system) or VM source platform. Not all workloads are suitable for Migrate for Anthos either. This migration approach is not possible in scenarios like high performance or memory-intensive workloads, software licenses tied to hardware workloads or applications needing a special kernel or hardware. Migrate for Anthos is a really good product to make enterprise cloud adoption faster and efficient. The future holds more options and use cases of this product for sure.\", \"sampled\": \"Jayson Alzate’s tutorial focuses on converting an existing React app to TypeScript, keeping the work grounded in the practical details of a project that already runs. The first visible change is the app entry point, which is renamed from index.js to index.ts. That rename also requires webpack.config.js to be updated so the configuration points to the new entry point. The process begins as a file-by-file conversion rather than a rewrite of the React app. The early compiler problem comes from JSX, because JSX syntax does not belong in a plain .ts file. Files containing JSX must use the .tsx extension instead of .ts. That means the renamed entry point still needs one more adjustment before the compiler is satisfied. Renaming index.ts to index.tsx fixes the JSX-related compiler problem and keeps the React syntax in the correct TypeScript file type. The tutorial notes that TypeScript can infer some types. Even so, the walkthrough manually adds types so the conversion remains useful for learning. That choice makes the app’s data shapes and function expectations more visible. It also turns the migration into a tour of TypeScript habits rather than a simple renaming pass across React files. A key part of that tour is the difference between interfaces and type aliases, which are presented as two ways to define types in TypeScript. Microsoft is described as generally favoring interfaces when possible, so interfaces become the preferred choice where they fit the data model. Type aliases still remain part of the discussion because they can represent useful shapes too. The tutorial shows 2 approaches for typing a function, using an inline type and predefining a type alias. Reusable type definitions are gathered into a types folder inside src, giving the converted app a central place for shared TypeScript declarations. Inside that area, a Product interface is created in types/product.ts to describe the expected product properties used by the app. This keeps the product shape separate from the data file itself, so other parts of the React app can rely on the same definition. The Product interface becomes the anchor for the data conversion. The constants product file is then renamed from product.js to product.ts, making it part of the TypeScript migration. Its exported product data is typed as an array of Product. That step connects the raw constants to the interface created in the types folder. The compiler then checks whether each item matches the declared product shape. A compile error appears because not every product includes the description property. The fix is to make description optional, which lets the interface represent the actual product data without forcing that property onto every item. ProductList appears as a type alias for a list of products, giving the tutorial another place to show named type definitions. This supports the broader contrast between interfaces and type aliases without moving away from the product data. The result is a set of reusable product-related types that can be applied across the React app. The cart utility file receives the same migration treatment and is renamed from cart.js to cart.ts. That file uses lodash, so the conversion also needs type information for the library. The tutorial handles that by installing lodash type definitions from DefinitelyTyped through npm, with DefinitelyTyped hosted on Github. The article is a technical tutorial in Programming, JavaScript, Web Development, Typescript, and React. Its timestamp is 2020-12-17 20:36:51.665000+00:00, and its focus stays on the conversion details rather than introducing a separate application design.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"As my involvement in the Chrome browser is ending with this Core UI project, I’m excited to see what the Chrome team has for the future of Chrome on desktop and mobile. Lessons learned and initial release feedback As a closing note, I’d like to share some of the lessons I learned during this project as well as some release reaction, both internal and external, hoping that these will be helpful to you, in your projects. 1. Engineers are great designers We talk a lot in our design circles about if and why a designer should code. There are a lot of diverging opinions on the matter and it comes from the fact that there is no simple definition of the role of a designer. However, we talk less of the opposite: Should engineer design? In the end, they are the makers and to a certain extent, the “designers” of your product, the ones who make it become a real thing. Sometimes, as a designer, I feel like all that we do is trying to “fake” or “mimick” the end result. Cutting to the chase as fast as possible to try things in the real environment, in real code, is essential. In this project, a lot of my assumptions were broken by engineers who not only brought better solutions to the table but executed and iterated on the design better that I could ever do. I’m thinking more specifically of programmatic rendering (an effort championed by Peter Kasting) and motion design (lead by Ben Ruthig). Their code knowledge was crucial in getting the design right and more than only changing the design it changed the nature of the project, from a visual revamp of the UI to a core rewriting. Everybody is a designer. Ideas are not limited to a role. If you are lucky enough to be working with motivated and engaged engineer, you might realize that they can sometimes be a better designer than you. 2. Involve engineers early In the present case, they were involved right away and were an integral of the design and conception process. As I mentioned earlier, their motivation to deliver better design through better engineering solution made the product what it is today. Maintaining constant communication was key to bring the right design to life. You don’t necessarily need to understand how to code, it’s more important to understand the people who do. 3. Know when to be precise, learn when to be loose Delivering extremely precise spec work is necessary, however, in some cases, leaving your preliminary work open for feedback and new ideas can bring your design to another level.\", \"sampled\": \"Thiago Candido authored an opinion article about GPT-3 and its likely impact on jobs, published on 2020-07-24 16:22:07.338000+00:00. The piece treats GPT-3 as an important machine learning subject, but its central claim is cautious: the model is unlikely to cause layoffs in the near future. Candido frames GPT-3 as impressive without turning that impression into a prediction of immediate replacement. The article points to outputs such as layouts, Python code, and creative material, and it says the system can follow requests with reasonable accuracy. The argument does not dismiss the visible capability of GPT-3, because the author also warns against treating it as mere hype. That balance matters in the piece, since the same technology can look surprising while still falling short of a reason to remove professional teams. Candido’s warning is direct when the subject turns to engineering work and generated code. The article argues against firing engineering teams solely because GPT-3 can generate code, even when generated work appears useful or polished. The author’s position also leaves room for experience and human judgment in programming. The article references 5,10 or 20+ years when comparing human-written code with generated code, which keeps the discussion grounded in professional practice rather than novelty alone. GPT-3 is treated as a tool that can support work rather than a machine that makes workers unnecessary. Candido recommends using GPT-3 and other machine learning models to improve professional work, especially where the technology can extend what people already do. The article suggests that GPT-3 could support tools for coding, design, and work management. Those examples keep the focus on practical environments where professionals already make decisions, coordinate tasks, and turn ideas into usable output. Smart IDEs appear as one concrete example of tools that could be built or redesigned with GPT-3. In that setting, the model’s ability to generate or respond to code-related requests becomes part of a professional workflow rather than a full replacement for the professional. Candido also uses a proverb to avoid pretending that the timing of job effects can be known with certainty. The line attributed to Danish Proverb is, \\\"It’s tough to make predictions, especially about the future\\\", and it fits the article’s refusal to give a firm timeline. The article cites Andrew Ng while arguing that people should learn to use deep learning capabilities. The quoted line is, \\\"Deep Learning is a superpower\\\", and Candido uses that idea to support adaptation rather than fear or dismissal. The closing force of the argument is that technology professionals should adapt to the new capabilities around GPT-3. Candido’s view is not that GPT-3 should be ignored, and not that it should replace professionals outright, but that its tools should help professionals improve.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"NEWSLETTER Gene Therapy in Dogs & Random Number Generator Made from DNA This Week in Synthetic Biology (Issue #16) Annie Spratt on Unsplash. Receive this newsletter every Friday morning! Sign up here: https://synbio.substack.com/ Reach out on Twitter. A Gene Therapy, Tested on Dogs, Has Issues For CRISPR-based gene therapies to work, you first need to squeeze a Cas protein and guide RNAs into a teeny tiny virus. Then, that packaged virus — bearing its gene-editing payload — has to be inserted into the body. The most common “delivery vehicle” for gene therapies are AAVs, or adeno-associated viruses. Long considered to be extremely safe, a new study, the most in-depth of its kind, is shedding new light on their long-term efficacy. Researchers injected nine dogs with hemophilia A, a type of bleeding disorder, with an AAV “payload”. Then, they followed the dogs over a period of ten years. The gene therapy was extremely effective. The nine treated dogs, together, only had seven bleeding episodes during the study period. Eleven untreated dogs with hemophilia, by comparison, had about twelve bleeding episodes each — every single year. Still, the researchers found that, in the livers of six of the treated dogs, 1,741 AAV integration events had occurred. That means that viral genetic material is being inserted into genomic DNA, often near very important genes. Dr. Charles Venditti, writing on this study for Nature Biotechnology, commented that these “results increase concerns about the long-term safety of AAV gene therapy,” but also called for more long-term studies and generally praised the work. This study was published in Nature Biotechnology. Link Random Number Generator, Made from DNA Chemists have been synthesizing custom DNA sequences for decades. Now, with the technology reaching relative maturity — and with costs plummeting — DNA is considered a viable means to store data. But for DNA-based storage to compete with normal hard drives, researchers need to find ways to encrypt the data stored in nucleic acids. A new study solves part of the problem, using the inherent stochasticity of DNA synthesis to create massive collections of truly random numbers. The authors argue that, by finding a means to create a large volume of random numbers, those looking to develop DNA hard drives could “guarantee security of encryption and decryption schemes for exchanging sensitive information…” To create the random sequences, the researchers combined all four nucleotides — A,T,C,G — into a single reaction vessel and allowed the DNA strand to grow. When each strand reached a specified length, it was cleaved from the reaction chamber. Using this approach, they managed to “obtain 7 million GB of randomness from one synthesis run, which can be read out using state-of-the-art sequencing technologies at rates of [about] 300 kB/s.”\", \"sampled\": \"Benjamin Hardy presents a simple routine built around the edges of sleep, when the mind is moving into rest and then returning from it. The article frames the practice as a 10-Minute way to improve clarity and creativity by using a bedtime step and a morning step together. The central idea is that conscious attention can give the subconscious mind useful material before sleep. Napoleon Hill’s line captures that premise directly: \\\"Your subconscious mind works continuously, while you are awake, and while you sleep.\\\" Ten minutes before going to sleep, the recommended step is to spend time meditating on goals or problems and writing about them. The article advises making those questions specific and written, so the subconscious has a clear request to work on during sleep. Thomas Edison is used to introduce the bedtime practice with the instruction, \\\"Never go to sleep without a request to your subconscious.\\\" In the article’s routine, that request is not left vague, because it is tied to written goals, problems, and questions before bed. Ten minutes after waking up, the morning part begins with writing soon after sleep. The focus is on thoughts connected to the prior night’s questions, capturing what surfaces before the day’s outside inputs take over attention. The article says the prefrontal cortex is especially active and creative right after sleep. That makes the morning writing window important, because the practice depends on using early thoughts rather than waiting until ordinary distractions have shaped the mind. Josh Waitzkin is described as using quiet, meditation, and journaling in the morning. His routine is presented as a contrast to people who check smartphones shortly after waking, a behavior the article links to 80 percent of people in the 18–44 age range within 15 minutes. Waitzkin’s journal practice is described as output rather than input. Instead of beginning the day by taking in material from a smartphone, the practice begins by producing thoughts, reflections, and possible answers from the mind’s own overnight work. The article says this kind of thought-dumping can generate article ideas, family insights, goal clarity, and relationship ideas. It treats journaling not as a record of random thoughts, but as a practical way to notice what the subconscious may have processed during sleep. Hardy argues that directing conscious and subconscious thinking can support goal achievement. James Allen’s quoted thought gives the article its broader frame: \\\"A man cannot directly choose his circumstances, but he can choose his thoughts, and so indirectly, yet surely, shape his circumstances.\\\" The post was authored by Benjamin Hardy and timestamped 2020-09-21 20:10:38.871000+00:00, with Medium and The Mission named in the publishing context. Hardy also mentions three foster children, while keeping the routine centered on written questions at night and written reflections in the morning.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Oh blessed be this day, this faithful day, that we can all join in arms to celebrate the coming and passing of the day we adore, and dread, most: Presentations. Brothers, sisters, and all of those who care to lend an ear, please do so, as in this moment of solidarity we stand in utter awe. In all seriousness, this blog post will not be like most others. There will not be progress report. There will not be a part five. There will not be a continuation. But that does not mean this is the end! This blog post merely marks the end of one journey and the start of another. Our time here in security was short, but the knowledge we gained was vast, and we hope to share our heartfelt story with anyone who chooses to read this post. So valued reader, from the four of us at Team Snow White: Aaron Murray, Hana Ra, Jeremy Ho, and Vincent Le, we graciously thank you for sticking around for this wild ride. Many tears were shed, much blood was lost, but we persevered through it all. We hope you enjoy our final installment of Team Snow White’s blogs, see you around.\", \"sampled\": \"Google Play Music was described as officially dead, marking the end of a service Google had planned for some time to shut down. The decision fit a broader pattern noted in the article, which says Google has a notable record of shutting down products, even when they have been widely used. The service was described as decade-old. Last year, it had still been the default music player on millions of Android devices. Google was moving users toward YouTube Music. That shift framed the shutdown as more than a simple retirement. Anupam Chugh argues that YouTube Music was not yet an equivalent replacement for Google Play Music. The replacement app is characterized as more oriented toward video watch time than music playlists, which makes the migration feel uneven. For users who treated Google Play Music as a music and podcast streaming service, the change raised questions about whether Google’s chosen successor matched the role it was being asked to fill. The article views Google’s replacement strategy as questionable because the company was closing one familiar product while pushing users to another that was described as incomplete by comparison. That choice could create an opening for competitors such as Spotify and Apple Music.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This story was originally published on blog.healthtap.com on February 14, 2018. Valentine’s Day is a wonderful day to celebrate all of the love in your life, whether that love is with a partner, your friends, or your family. Valentine’s Day is also an amazing day to take the time to practice some self-care and to show some love for yourself. If you need some last minute ideas to spice up your day, try these. They’re perfect to do as a date with that special someone, to do with your friends, or to do by yourself if you need a little time for self-love. These healthy ideas will help make your celebration more adventurous and interesting, and healthier too! Go on a hike Wintery hike in the woods? Coastal hike by the beach? Lace up your shoes and go spend some quality time outdoors by going on a hike with your loved ones, or just for some peace of mind. You’ll get fresh air, beautiful views, and some great exercise to boot. Cook dinner at home Light some candles, pop open some wine, and save some money by cooking a meal at home. It can be a collaborative effort, you’ll get to show off your cooking skills, and you can cook something healthy and nourishing for you and the one you’re with. You’ll also get to be able to better control your portions according to your health goals and needs. Make a dark chocolate dessert Instead of devouring a box of chocolates, try eating some dark chocolate or making a dark chocolate dessert instead. Dark chocolate is full of antioxidants called flavonols, which promote heart health by lowering LDL cholesterol (the “bad” cholesterol) in your arteries and by improving circulation. Just make sure you stick to chocolate above 70% cacao, and watch added sugar and fat which counteracts these heart-healthy benefits. Go out dancing Whether you’re going out with a date or with your friends, going to a dance class or just hitting the town can be not only an incredibly fun, but also an extremely fit way to celebrate the evening. Dancing is one of the most fun ways to get in aerobic and muscle-strengthening exercise, and you can burn up to 230 calories in just 30 minutes.\", \"sampled\": \"Rana Singh’s article Music generation using Deep Learning, published on 2019-12-16, frames music generation as a Deep Learning and Artificial Intelligence problem grounded in sequence modeling. It opens with Charles Darwin’s line, \\\"If I had my life to live over again, I would have made a rule to read some poetry and listen to some music at least once every week.\\\" The central idea is that music can be presented to a model as a sequence of events, making it suitable for a recurrent approach. In that framing, an RNN receives music data as ordered input rather than as a single isolated object, so the structure of the representation matters. Rana Singh lists ABC-notation, MIDI, and mp3 as music representation options, while drawing distinctions through their roles in the workflow. ABC-notation is used as a text format for sheet music, MIDI is discussed for conversion into event sequences, and mp3 is described as storing audio. The main architecture discussed is a many-to-many char-RNN that predicts the next character at each time step. This model is presented as character based, so each step in the sequence is tied to a next-character prediction rather than a single final output. The char-RNN can include multiple hidden layers, including multiple LSTM layers, and the article states that each input has 2 LSTM cells. In the problem setup, each time step is associated with an output vector size of 256, connecting the recurrent processing to a structured output at every position. For the ABC-format workflow, Rana Singh used the Jigs dataset, which is described as containing 340 tunes. The dataset is used for RNN training with batch size 16, keeping the training description tied to the sequence format used for the music source. The Keras setting Return sequence=True is discussed because it produces output at each sequence time step. A time-distributed dense layer is then described as creating a dense output of size 86 at each time step, matching the 86 unique characters in the vocabulary. Stateful=True is described as carrying the last state for a batch sample into the matching sample of the next batch. For MIDI music generation, the article mentions using the Music21 Python library to read MIDI files and convert them into event sequences, and it also references Tensorflow, Google, and GitHub in the wider context of music generation work.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"2019 is coming to an end. What’s next for AI? The limits of Deep Learning and AI ethics monopolize discussions on the future of AI AI Now and AI Index 2019 Reports 2019 is ending and two of the most well known reports on AI state of the art — AI Now 2019 report, and AI Index 2019 — are out. What are they saying about AI state of the art? Where is AI moving next? I will try to summarize it in this short post. Let’s start by AI Index 2019, developed by the Human-Centered Artificial Institute at Stanford University. As a novelty this year, the AI Index includes for the first time two powerful Data visualization tools: The Global AI Vibrancy Tool, which allows you to compare countries activities around AI under three metrics including R&D, Economy, Inclusion. Ai Vibrancy Dashboard example The ArXiv Monitor, a full paper search engine to track metrics from papers on AI published on ArXiv. ArXiv Monitor search engine By using those tools, anyone can deep dive into detailed information about the state of AI by country or discipline, but I will try to list here some of the report highlights that I found more interesting while crossing them to some personal thoughts, or thoughts I have shared with some experts in the field: China now publishes as many papers on AI per year as Europe , having passed the US in 2006. , having passed the US in 2006. Attendance of AI conferences continues to increase significantly . As an example, 2019 NeuIPS was over 13,000 attendees, 41% over 2018 (and 800% relative to 2012). . As an example, 2019 NeuIPS was over 13,000 attendees, 41% over 2018 (and 800% relative to 2012). Post 2012, AI compute power is doubling every 3.4 months. That being said, the report also mentions “progress on some broad sets of natural-language processing classification tasks, as captured in the SuperGLUE and SQuAD2.0 benchmarks, has been remarkably rapid; performance is still lower on some NLP tasks requiring reasoning, such as the AI2 Reasoning Challenge, or human-level concept learning task, such as the Omniglot Challenge.” While discussing these figures with some experts, the feeling they get is that: Paper originilaty has definitely decreased . . An extensive amount of papers now focus on just presenting minor improvements over previous work by means of tweaking models, or even by applying brute force (both including dataset scale or computing power).\", \"sampled\": \"Elliot Leibu frames selling as a problem of attention, not only a problem of having a product ready for the market. His central claim is that customer attention is earned by giving value beyond the product itself, a principle he summarizes as \\\"value in exchange for attention.\\\" The starting point is his own recent move into business, built around a small software plugin he created. Before facing the market, he had already tested the plugin, improved the code, built a website, and prepared documentation and examples. That work left a different challenge, because development did not automatically bring people to the product. The remaining task was to sell the plugin and attract people to the website where the product could be evaluated. One obvious route was online promotion through social platforms that could point back to the product site. Leibu considered Twitter, Facebook, and other social platforms as possible ways to link people back to what he had built. The argument does not stop with online sales or software. Leibu presents the idea as useful across product categories, because attention has to be earned whether the product is digital, physical, or sold through another channel. His example from business relationships begins with lunch with the CEO of a technology vendor. That CEO had been trying to sell to Leibu and his employer, and the lunch described was the fourth such meeting over two years. The vendor’s product was potentially useful, but usefulness alone did not make the timing right. Leibu’s organization was not internally prepared to adopt the working changes required for the product to fit. The CEO still provided value during these meetings, even when the sale was not ready to happen. That value came through industry connections and observations, which gave Leibu something useful beyond the product pitch itself. Because of those meetings, Leibu says he is more likely to remember the company. He is also more likely to consider its product when the timing becomes suitable for the organization. The article then shifts to supermarket bread as a simpler example of the same attention problem. Bread earns attention partly through convenience, because the supermarket already has the customer’s attention during a shopping trip. Leibu describes this as buying into the supermarket’s \\\"convenience pie.\\\" Manufacturers and wholesalers pay supermarkets for shelf access, either through money or through discounted products. The piece by Elliot Leibu was published by Hackernoon and hosted on Medium, with Riccardo Annandale credited for a photo from Unsplash. Its timestamp is 2019-06-08 12:00:03.916000+00:00, and its practical claim remains tied to meetings held every few months as well as shelves, links, and attention.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"What AI Can Teach Us About Failure Please don’t buy into failure-ism. Train for strikes not gutter balls. Photo by Jorik Kleen on Unsplash If you’ve read anything on business in the last two decades, there’s a good chance you’ve heard that failure is significant, something to be proud of, a badge of entrepreneurial prowess. Ostensibly, the more you fail, you are somehow more successful. This approach is undoubtedly accurate to a point. I agree that failure is a needed part of the process of success, only that we may have emphasized the wrong thing. Now, I’m certainly all for reframing our mistakes as something else, especially when this helps us move forward more productively, gaining resilience and flexibility. This thought process is one of the core ideas behind CBT or cognitive behavioral therapy. And the reason is that it works. Studies show that CBT can help us move past some of our worst failures and emerge on the other side, happier, healthier and wiser. But speaking from experience, I’d rather have one success than ten failures, and here’s why. It’s not just me. Experts have been cautioning for a while that this culture of “failure-ism” might not be the best way to achieve our goals. At the heart of Failure-ism is an unsubstantiated, almost romantic notion of the value of randomness. Many motivational speakers seem to be saying that if we open ourselves to the possibility of failure, something positive will happen, almost by a kind of magic. Indeed, recent studies have confirmed that failing is not precisely the panacea that many are making it out to be.\", \"sampled\": \"Darshak Rana’s article in The Innovation on Medium argues that people do not need to be psychology experts to begin interpreting behavior, because subtle behavior can reveal aspects of personality in ordinary interactions. The piece treats behavior reading as a practical form of attention, linking small signals to trust decisions, character judgment, and the public image a person presents to others. Rana describes an early interest in the mismatch between words and actions, saying that inconsistent behavior caught his attention during childhood. That interest later became more focused when he began reading about human behavior as a teenager and wanted to become highly skilled at detecting lies through body-language awareness. The article places body language at the center of this attention to behavior, saying it can communicate emotions such as dislike, love, preference, and hate. It suggests that what people show through posture, movement, and self-touching can carry meaning even when their spoken words point in another direction. Carol Plum-Ucci is cited as having made a statement about understanding human behavior, and Rana says the line remained memorable to him. The quote is, “If you can understand human behavior, it can’t hurt you nearly as much.” Rana says he learned about subtle behavioral signals through books and videos, building on the same curiosity that began with noticing inconsistency between speech and conduct. The article’s title refers to 5 subtle signs, and one discussed cluster links hunched shoulders, crossed arms, and touching the face or neck with low self-confidence. The article also warns that other people may read such cues as vulnerabilities, which makes body-language awareness relevant to integrity and self-improvement as well as psychology. Its practical claim is that reading behavior can support better trust decisions, help identify people with good character, and improve one’s public image.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This is the republished article from https://www.dataengineeringweekly.com/. Subscribe to Data Engineering Weekly for your weekly data engineering news in the industry. The Rise of Data Mesh Data Mesh is a set of data engineering principles coined by Zhamak Dehghani from ThoughtWorks. I highly recommend reading How to Move Beyond a Monolithic Data Lake to a Distributed Data Mesh and Data Mesh Principles and Logical Architecture. Data Mesh influenced by domain-driven design, emphasizing the importance of data ownership and shared tooling to generate, curate, and democratize data. Data Mesh principles getting good adaption in some of the leading organizations. Yelp talked about its data mesh journey, So does Netflix and Zalando. Though the literacy around data mesh maturing, I see a few confusions around Data Mesh in a few blogs. I’m not an authoritative person to describe data mesh, but an attempt to convey what I learned about data mesh. I’m eager to hear the alternate viewpoints on this. The sad state of data engineering Now the fundamental question you may ask. Why Data Mesh and Why now? To understand Data Mesh, we need to understand the current state of the data engineering world. It may not directly apply to your organization, but most of the data infrastructure remains in this sad state. Imagine you are writing a dictionary with only the words with no meaning for it. On top of it, you shuffle the words randomly and publish the dictionary without any index for it, and hire high-paid data engineers and analysts to decode the dictionary. It is the current state of the data infrastructure. The modern data infrastructure has sophisticated systems like Kafka, Spark, and the ability to emit and process events at a petabyte-scale. Yet, the data generation process we follow is equivalent to writing a dictionary without meaning. Wait, Don’t Data Lake solved It? A data lake is a centralized repository that allows you to store all your structured and unstructured data at any scale. You can store your data as-is without having to structure the data. As the definition suggests, Data Lake focuses on centralized data storage to break the organization’s data silo. The central repository removes entry barriers to integrate and analyze from various data sources in an organization. However, as the data lake grows, the complexity of the data management grows. The producer of the data generates data and sends it to the data lake. The consumers down the line have no domain understanding of the producer and struggle to understand the data lake data. The consumers then connect with the data producer to understand the data. At that point, the producer side’s domain expertise depends on human knowledge that may or may not available. As Data Lake grows, it becomes a technical debt rather than a strategic advantage. How Data Mesh Solve It? Data Mesh is an enterprise data platform principle that converges the principles from Distributed Domain Driven Architecture, Self-serve Platform Design, and Thinking Data as a Product. Data Mesh focuses on treating data as a product.\", \"sampled\": \"Sarene B. Arias presents a practical argument about Medium publishing: a story does not have to remain fixed after it appears. The post, published on 2020-09-30, says editing stories after publication can improve the way they perform on the platform. The central idea is that Medium gives writers real-time information about how readers respond. Instead of treating publication as the end of the writing process, the author recommends using that response as a feedback loop for improving published work. That feedback loop begins with signals the platform makes visible to writers. The article lists impressions, read time, claps, comments, quotation or tagging, and link reposting as reader responses that can help a writer understand what is happening. The post also emphasizes that Medium writers keep rights to their work and can revise stories after publication. That detail matters because the author’s advice depends on the writer being able to adjust a published story rather than only study it afterward. Arias frames the approach as a change from older limits around writing and publishing. The post mentions 100 years ago as a historical comparison about writers gaining recognition, and ten years ago as a comparison about online publishing and editors. The article uses Netflix as a comparison for data-driven content production. Netflix is described as a company applying big data to content decisions, and the post says Netflix invested $17 billion in original content in 2020. That comparison supports the author’s point that writers can pay attention to evidence rather than guesswork. Medium’s real-time audience response becomes the smaller-scale version of using information to decide what content may need revision. The author advises writers to analyze views, reads, read ratio, fans, and viral potential. These measures are presented as useful signs of how a story is landing with readers after it has already been published. Timing is part of the method. Arias says the first 24 hours after publication are the best time to study metrics, because early response can show whether a story is gaining traction or losing readers. The article defines viral performance as 1K reads in less than 24 hrs. That benchmark gives the author’s discussion of viral potential a specific meaning within the post rather than leaving it as a vague label. The advice is not presented as equally applicable in every situation. The post mentions 100 followers as a threshold below which the author says the tips mainly apply to stories in a big publication. Arias also reports a concrete result from revising published work. She says she improved read ratios from 25% to 66% within hours by making changes after a story was already live. The post’s broader recommendation is to treat Medium as an ecosystem of writing, publishing, reader response, and revision. Your Medium Ecosystem is referenced in connection with that larger view of how stories move through the platform. The article sits within topics such as Marketing, Self Improvement, Writing, Blogging, and Innovation. Its practical claim remains focused: Medium writers can use available metrics, reader signals, and revision rights to improve published stories after release.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Lessons From a Former Push-Over How I Learned to say no and put myself first Realizing I Was Too Nice Just after the COVID-19 stay-at-home orders were issued, I ran into one of my neighbors and her daughter. I was just getting back from running a few errands when she saw me from her balcony, which faces the front of our apartment building. Our conversation continued for a little longer than I liked, especially because I was holding a few bags, so I tried to politely end it. But she had an ulterior motive: she wanted to ask me for a favor. After telling me how smart she thinks I am, she finally told me that she wanted me to help her daughter with her homework. I felt bad saying no at first, so I agreed. I thought that it was going to be for just an hour on one subject, which didn’t seem so bad. But after I agreed, my neighbor revealed that she wanted me to help her daughter, who I then realized was special needs, with her online schooling in my apartment for the rest of the school year during my work hours. At first, I walked away feeling dreadful. I love to help people whenever I can, but this was much more than I initially bargained for. I wanted nothing to do with the situation. I really don’t like to watch other people’s children when I’m not close to the parents; I barely know this neighbor. We only exchange a few polite greetings when we happen to run into each other. The more I thought about it, the more I knew I had to tell her that I couldn’t help them. Because I didn’t have my neighbor’s phone number, I wrote her an apologetic letter, later that same day, telling her I could not help after all and included some free and paid resources that may be able to help her daughter with her studies. I felt a bit of relief after I placed it behind their door. Recounting this experience made me think about why I had a hard time saying no and what steps I could take to avoid similar situations in the future. Understanding Why Saying No Was Difficult Many articles talk about the benefits of re-introducing “no” into one's vocabulary, but most don’t delve into why we feel the need to be people-pleasers in the first place. I believe it stems from benevolence being a highly-valued trait in society. We’re taught to treat others with kindness and to be generally agreeable at a young age. Being too “picky” is seen as a negative personality trait. Idioms like, “You’ve got to go along to get along” encourages the idea that “just going with the flow” is better than being overly critical. Most people naturally want to avoid being the source of negativity, whether it’s negative emotions or outcomes. But the older I get, the more I realize that there are some people who will do anything to try to take advantage of nice people, and being overly considerate of them is just a waste of time, effort, and emotional energy. Being kind to others is great advice but only up to a point. There’s some virtue in being a little selfish sometimes too. We cannot and should not worry about how others will feel for thinking of ourselves first, especially when they wouldn’t put you first if you really needed them. Do not let the fear of other people’s opinions and expectations dictate how you spend your time. Why Saying No is Important Confrontation is an unavoidable part of life that can be beneficial in the long run when handled well. However, avoidance of “negativity” is starting to become an extreme norm. It’s getting so bad that “ghosting” is an understood and accepted term in modern western society. But there are many times when the “if I ignore it, it goes away” strategy just doesn’t work or actually makes things more confusing and hurtful. I’ve found that setting boundaries with people often times will make them respect and like me more, not less. Saying no is an adulthood rite of passage. Being too willing to help also can have an adverse effect on many aspects of your life, including in your career. Despite being a valued trait, benevolence actually makes you more likely to be overworked and underpaid, because helping your coworker’s with their work can land them promotions and raises while leaving you burned out. I’ve personally experienced helping a few coworkers, despite being extremely busy myself, who then took credit for my contributions, leaving me wondering if this was causing my talents to be underappreciated at work. I no longer allow myself to be taken advantage of and ensure that my supervisor knows of all my contributions and accomplishments. Now when I don’t have time to help anyone else, I just let them know that I can’t help at the moment. I have a much better work-life balance and am much happier at my job. How to Say No There are many ways to go about declining to participate in an activity or project. Here’s a list of a few ways I’ve found most effective for me.\", \"sampled\": \"The question of how energy-intensive AI infrastructure has become is drawing serious attention from researchers and industry leaders who work at the intersection of algorithms, hardware, and computing efficiency. Rick Calle, who leads AI business development at M12, Microsoft's venture fund, has been examining what this energy demand means for the future of the discipline. His focus on novel use cases that push current systems to their limits gives him a practical vantage point that's harder to get from the outside. He's also tracked the trajectory of AI infrastructure from relatively modest beginnings to the sprawling, power-hungry systems that define the field today. During his time at Qualcomm's AI Research division, Calle worked with a team that launched The Engine, an initiative aimed at understanding how AI software and hardware interact at scale. That experience gave him a close view of how quickly computational demands were outpacing what earlier infrastructure was designed to support. Today's AI algorithms, when software and hardware are considered together, can be 10x to 100x more demanding than earlier generations — a figure that helps explain why energy consumption has become a central concern for anyone building or deploying AI at scale. Calle saw an inflection point around 2012, when AI hardware and algorithmic development began moving in tandem rather than independently. That convergence, driven in part by advances in Convolutional Neural Networks and related architectures, set the stage for the dramatic increase in compute requirements that followed. Prior to that shift, most AI solutions were running on one, maybe two processors. The contrast with today's large-scale distributed training environments, which can involve thousands of specialized processors running simultaneously, illustrates how fundamentally the infrastructure requirements have changed. Natural Language Processing and Neural Architecture Search are among the areas where this intensity is most visible, with training runs that consume enormous amounts of energy over extended periods. The Bidirectional LSTM and related sequence modeling approaches that became standard in NLP are part of the lineage that contributed to these growing demands. The computing industry has historically optimized for speed and innovation, not the lifetime energy costs of the systems being built. That made sense when AI workloads were small, but it's harder to justify as the scale grows and the carbon footprint becomes measurable. Microsoft's carbon negative commitment has brought these questions into sharper focus, creating a direct tension between the company's climate goals and the energy demands of its AI investments — a tension Calle has described as a genuine challenge, not one with an obvious solution already in hand. \\\"Real Perspectives on Artificial Intelligence\\\" is the series in which Calle has shared his analysis, offering a grounded look at how the industry arrived at its current energy situation. The framing draws on direct experience rather than speculation, which is why his background at Qualcomm's AI Research and M12 is central to it. The relationship between AI and climate change is not abstract when examined through the lens of infrastructure scaling. Every major training run, every large deployed model, every data center expansion tied to AI demand contributes to an energy ledger the industry is only beginning to account for systematically. The AI Engine concept that emerged from Qualcomm's research reflects an attempt to think more carefully about how algorithms and hardware can be co-designed to reduce waste. That kind of co-design is one of the few practical levers available for reducing energy intensity without simply slowing down research progress. Calle's position at M12 means he evaluates companies and technologies working on exactly these efficiency problems, giving him visibility into where the most promising work is happening. The fund's connection to Microsoft also means that questions about carbon commitments and AI energy use are not theoretical for the teams he works with. The numbers embedded in this history — the 10x to 100x increase in combined software and hardware demands, the inflection point around 2012 — give concrete shape to what can otherwise feel like a vague environmental concern. Tracking those numbers is part of what makes it possible to have a precise conversation about what efficiency gains are actually achievable. University of Massachusetts Amherst research has added academic rigor to what practitioners like Calle observe from the industry side, moving the discussion beyond advocacy into a more detailed accounting of actual costs. The path forward involves holding two realities at once: AI is becoming more capable and more widely deployed, and that deployment carries real energy costs the computing industry has not always been structured to track. Whether the discipline can develop the tools, incentives, and architectural approaches needed to bring those costs down is the question Calle and others working at this intersection are trying to answer.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Writing The Only Book About Writing You’ll Ever Need I’ve read it 10 times Six years ago, I sort of forgot how to write. I’d been publishing on the internet for a little over a year — that first, embarrassing, thrilling, bringing-a-laptop-to-parties year (I’m fun at parties) — and I’d run out of things to say. What used to be casual, fast, easy breezy bloggy beautiful, had become… slightly boring. Slow. I’d have an idea, stare at my laptop for an hour, Command-Tab over to Gmail, and then walk four blocks to buy an overpriced panini. So, I did what I always do: I walked into a bookstore feeling vaguely depressed, and left with something to read. I know you just want the name of the book, so here you go: Several Short Sentences About Writing by Verlyn Klinkenborg. It’s sort of a weird book, to be honest. It contains no paragraphs, just sentences stacked on top of each other in austere columns. (The whole thing sort of reads like a poem.) And yes, it’s about the mechanics of putting words together, but it’s also about other things: thinking, noticing, mapping the mess inside your head. Also, trusting yourself — something I have a difficult time doing. There’s a lot of writing advice out there: books, articles, quotes from dead people. Most of it isn’t actually about writing. It’s about scheduling (wake up at six!), preparing to write (take good notes!), having ambition (write every day… at six…), or envisioning what it will feel like to finally be done writing. Most people don’t talk about the messy, often boring, sometimes excruciating and other times exhilarating process of staring at a computer and eating stale mixed nuts as you form thoughts into words. In this book, you’ll find no tips for outlining, brainstorming, scheduling, or pitching. Nothing on a thesis statement, a topic sentence, an introduction, or conclusion (spoiler: good ideas need no introduction, or conclusion!) Nothing on rituals or waiting for fictional characters to whisper in your ear. No fantasies about having a cabin in the woods with a big dog and a chunky sweater and a blank Moleskine. In fact, there’s nothing in here about ~inspiration~ at all, nothing about waiting for the ideas to “just flow.” (They never just flow. Sorry.) My whole life is just going to be me rereading this book. It’s sort of like I got really religious somewhere in my twenties, and this book is my Blogging Bible. What you’ll find instead are practical, even curmudgeonly, tips about grammar, rhythm, and making individual sentences. Short ones, mostly. For example: Keep sentences small. They’re easier to work with that way. If something doesn’t feel right, there’s a problem with one or more of your sentences. Listen to that feeling. Try to pinpoint exactly which word or phrase is triggering it. Naming exactly what’s wrong, in grammatical terminology or otherwise, will come later. Understanding a word’s etymology will teach you how to use it. Words contain imprints of their histories. The subject of a sentence should appear as close to the beginning of a sentence as possible. You don’t have to “grab” anyone with the first line of your story. Just write a simple sentence that says what you want it to say. It’s harder than it sounds! And also very effective, if done well. “A writer’s real work is the endless winnowing of sentences, the relentless exploration of possibilities, the effort, over and over again, to see in what you started out to say the possibility of saying something you didn’t know you could.” Noun phrases (“the realization that…”) almost always sound clunky and dead. Try rewriting them as verb phrases (“realizing that…”). Prepositions are difficult to get right, even for native English speakers. A reader’s experience has nothing to do with a writer’s. A sentence that reads “naturally” or “conversationally” to a reader may have been painstakingly assembled by a stressed-out writer who wishes they could sound more natural or conversational. Toward the end of this book, the author diagnoses exactly what’s wrong with about 50 isolated sentences written by his students. It’s sort of ruthless, but fascinating. Most of these sentences sound almost okay, though you can feel something slightly amiss. Hearing Klinkenborg (what a name) pinpoint the specific problems in each one — from an awkward metaphor to a misplaced pronoun — is therapeutic. It’s sort of like Dr. Pimple Popper, but for dangling modifiers instead of giant cysts. I recommend reading the book just for this section. Here’s a theory: Some people (i.e. me) procrastinate and never end up publishing anything because we’re fixated on the bigger Thing — the Novel, the Post, the Feature, the Investigative Whatever. We’re distracted by our intentions and our aspirations. But unless you’ve somehow acquired a ghostwriter, none of those bigger Things are possible until you make sentences. It’s tedious, but crucial. A wall is made of bricks.\", \"sampled\": \"Seven months into the coronavirus outbreak, epidemiologist Marc Lipsitch sat down with reporters on an Aug. 13 call to discuss the science of Covid-19 and the failures shaping the U.S. response. Lipsitch, a professor of epidemiology at Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health and director of the Center for Communicable Disease Dynamics, brought a clear and unsentimental perspective to questions that many public officials had answered with confusion or silence. One of the sharpest points he made concerned the relationship between political opinion and viral spread — a relationship he described as nonexistent. He echoed a line attributed to fellow epidemiologist Bill Hanage: \\\"Viruses don't read Twitter. They don't care what we think about them. They don't care what they do to the economy. They don't care about anything. And we have to be rational in the face of that.\\\" That call for rationality ran through much of what Lipsitch argued, and it connected directly to his warnings about the coming fall and winter. Control measures that had proven insufficient to curb summer transmission would, by his reasoning, be equally insufficient once colder weather pushed people indoors and created conditions that typically favor respiratory virus spread. Lipsitch pointed to European and Asian countries as evidence that meaningful control was achievable when governments acted decisively and consistently. Those countries achieved a 10-fold reduction in cases and deaths through roughly two months of lockdown — a result that stood in stark contrast to the trajectory seen across much of the United States during the same period. The U.S. failure, in his account, was not primarily a scientific one but a political and psychological one rooted in complacency at multiple levels. He described a pattern in which U.S. policymakers watched the virus move through China, then through Europe, and still didn't treat the approaching threat as the serious and specific danger the available evidence already indicated it was. That complacency persisted even after the virus arrived domestically, with some governors during early outbreaks behaving as though their states could somehow avoid the outcomes visible elsewhere. Lipsitch addressed that tendency directly: \\\"Wishful thinking just doesn't help. And I think that's been a big part of the issue, a notion in some states in particular, that somehow we can be different from all the other experiences with this virus and other viruses and we can just hope that it won't spread when we have high levels of contact.\\\" On masks, Lipsitch argued their value operated on two levels. They offered direct physical protection against transmission of SARS-CoV-2, and they served as a visible, constant reminder of the distancing behaviors that remained essential to slowing the spread of Covid-19. He also pushed back on the economic argument against strict control measures by reframing the terms of the debate. Rapid, unchecked spread was itself destructive to the economy, he argued, which meant controlling the virus wasn't an obstacle to recovery but a precondition for it. Lipsitch and other scientists had grown increasingly frustrated over the course of the pandemic by mixed messaging, misinformation, and the absence of clear federal leadership. That frustration wasn't simply personal — it reflected a concern that confused public communication was making rational collective behavior harder to sustain at exactly the moment it was most needed. On media coverage, his assessment was more measured than the broader criticism of pandemic communication might have suggested. He described journalists' overall performance as surprisingly good, a notable concession given the scale and complexity of what they'd been asked to explain over seven months. Federal public health communication was a different matter. Lipsitch identified Dr. Anthony Fauci as a key exception to the general absence of a clear and consistent federal voice, acknowledging that Fauci had provided the kind of direct scientific guidance the situation demanded even when other officials did not. The CDC typically fills that communicator role during a health crisis, giving the public and state governments guidance they can rely on. Lipsitch noted that it had not adequately fulfilled that function during the Covid-19 pandemic, leaving a vacuum that made any coordinated national response considerably harder to sustain. The picture Lipsitch drew across the Aug. 13 call was of a crisis shaped less by scientific uncertainty than by a consistent failure to act on what the science already showed. The virus followed patterns that were knowable and, to a meaningful extent, manageable — but managing them required accepting the evidence rather than waiting for it to conform to more convenient expectations.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In Peru, we’re proud of our cultural heritage and world-famous cuisine. But alongside Machu Picchu and ceviche, nature is right up there as part of our national identity. From the Pacific coast to the Andes to the Amazon, we’re blessed with an amazing natural heritage, much of which remains unspoilt. After Brazil, Peru holds the second largest area of the Amazon rainforest. We rank number one in the world for our variety of butterflies and freshwater fish, second for birds, fourth for amphibians and fifth for mammals and reptiles. The whole planet benefits from the vast amount of carbon stored within the Peruvian Amazon, and its ecosystems help guard against both droughts and floods, which appear to be intensifying in Peru as result of climate change. But we need to look after these natural treasures. As the economy expands, our nature is coming under increasing pressure. We’re losing more than 150,000 hectares of forest every year to agriculture, and (often illegal) gold mining and logging, and the roads built to reach these developments. Deforestation also accounts for half of Peru’s greenhouse gas emissions. One important way of conserving nature and benefiting people who depend upon them is through protected areas like national parks. For every $1 invested in the effective management of natural resources in protected areas, we get $100 worth of value in benefits for people — in the form of clean water, food, natural medicines and more. Peru has a good network of 76 protected areas. Half of these are in the Amazon. But for protected areas to really be effective, they need to be well managed and properly funded over the long term. While there’s been increased government support over the last decade, there remains a large gap between current spending levels and the funding actually needed for an effective protected area network — from the costs of staffing and equipment, to infrastructure, wildlife monitoring and engaging local communities. © Hugh M. Smith But a solution is in sight in the shape of Patrimonio Natural del Peru (PdP), known in English as “National Parks: Peru’s Natural Legacy.” This innovative public-private partnership led by the Peruvian government aims to provide funding to ensure the long-term stability of the country’s protected areas, using a mechanism called Project Finance for Permanence. WWF is one of several partners involved. It works like this. Together, the partners agree on a long-term vision and plan for managing the whole protected area network, and work out how much it will all cost. Donors from the public and private sector, including international development agencies, foundations and businesses, contribute funds to cover the shortfall, on the condition that after an agreed period of time, the country’s government will take on all the costs itself. It’s an approach that’s being used successfully in a growing number of countries — WWF has previously helped set up similar programmes in Brazil and Bhutan. This month, the PdP project celebrated a major milestone — a commitment of US$140 million in funding from international donors and the Peruvian government, to strengthen and expand Peru’s protected areas network, starting in the Amazon. This is a huge cause for celebration. It means we can pursue a unified, integrated vision for Peru’s protected areas for the long term with agreed plans and priorities. Rather than funding being insufficient and piecemeal, guaranteed revenue streams will be available where they are needed. This will allow us to build a stable structure and a strong system for conserving Peru’s natural heritage. Better management of our protected areas will also bring greater benefits for people living in or near them.\", \"sampled\": \"Some music arrives at the exact moment a person needs it most. Zach J. Payne first encountered Kerrigan-Lowdermilk the summer after graduating from high school, driving along Interstate 405 when Freedom came through the speakers and changed something permanent inside him. That song belonged to the album now known as The Mad Ones, originally released as The Unauthorized Autobiography of Samantha Brown, and it opened a door Payne would spend years walking through. The timing of that discovery only becomes clear in retrospect. A couple of months after that night on Interstate 405, Payne began losing friends — three of them over the course of three years, each one taken by cancer. That kind of grief doesn't resolve cleanly, and the music of Kerrigan-Lowdermilk, the duo composed of Kait and Brian, became something he returned to again and again as those losses accumulated. Grief wasn't the only thing Payne was navigating. A depressive episode settled in and refused to leave, consuming most of his twenties and costing him most of his friendships, leaving behind a much smaller and quieter life than the one he'd imagined. What survived, in part, was his relationship to Kerrigan-Lowdermilk's music. Specifically, the song Holding On. Kait's lyrics gave Payne a mantra — three lines and thirteen words he could return to when nothing else held. He carried those words through the worst stretches of the depression, through the grief, through the years when friendships fell away one by one. The lines come directly from Holding On: \\\"The Earth keeps turning, / The light keeps shifting, / And I keep holding on.\\\" They function not as comfort in any soft sense but as a bare statement of continuation — a description of what surviving actually looks like when survival is all that remains. Payne has described art, words, and music as vital, capable of keeping people alive, and Holding On is his clearest evidence for that claim. It isn't an abstract claim. He attended two Kerrigan-Lowdermilk shows in Los Angeles in 2013, and those evenings were among the concrete anchors of a period that otherwise felt shapeless. Hearing Kait and Brian perform the music that had been playing privately through his hardest years gave those years a kind of form and witness they hadn't otherwise had. The shows didn't fix anything, but they confirmed that the music was real and that his connection to it was real. Nearly ten years passed between that summer night on Interstate 405 and the writing of this account — years that held three friends lost to cancer, a depressive episode, dissolved friendships, and the slow process of continuing anyway. Kerrigan-Lowdermilk's music ran underneath all of it, not always loudly but consistently. Payne doesn't present it as a cure. He presents it as evidence that art can be a survival mechanism, not metaphorically but in a practical, daily sense. When larger structures of meaning had collapsed, he had thirteen words he could repeat. Sometimes that's enough. What prompted him to write about all of this was seeing Kerrigan-Lowdermilk's new merchandise on Instagram — specifically a shirt featuring Holding On. That image, appearing unexpectedly in a social media feed, pulled the full decade of association into the present. The sight of those words printed on a shirt, available to anyone, was a reminder that the lines he'd carried privately had also been carried by others, that the thirteen words had traveled beyond his own experience into something wider. Payne hasn't yet visited New York City, and the Broadway theater career he once imagined hasn't materialized. The distance between the life he anticipated and the life he has lived is significant. The music has been one of the things that made that distance survivable. Three lines and thirteen words did exactly that.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"What is industrial building design? To develop a built environment based on an undefined building typology. The structure functions on the business programming of the managing engineers. The area brief depends on the maximum and minimum space requirements per function unit. Industry: Industry, a group of productive enterprises or organizations that produce or supply goods, services, or sources of income. In economics, industries are customarily classified as primary, secondary, and tertiary; secondary industries are further classified as heavy and light. Secondary Industry This sector, also called manufacturing industry, (1) takes the raw materials supplied by primary industries and processes them into consumer goods, or (2) further processes goods that other secondary industries have transformed into products, or (3) builds capital goods used to manufacture consumer and non consumer goods. Secondary industry also includes energy-producing industries (e.g., hydroelectric industries) as well as the construction industry. Secondary industry may be divided into heavy, or large-scale, and light, or small-scale, industry. Large-scale industry generally requires heavy capital investment in plants and machinery, serves a large and diverse market including other manufacturing industries, has a complex industrial organization and frequently a skilled specialized labour force, and generates a large volume of output. Examples would include petroleum refining, steel and iron manufacturing, motor vehicle and heavy machinery manufacture, cement production, nonferrous metal refining, meat-packing, and hydroelectric power generation. Light, or small-scale, industry may be characterized by the no durability of manufactured products and a smaller capital investment in plants and equipment, and it may involve nonstandard products, such as customized or craft work. The labor force may be either low skilled, as in textile work and clothing manufacture, food processing, and plastics manufacture, or highly skilled, as in electronics and computer hardware manufacture, precision instrument manufacture, gemstone cutting, and craft work. Industrial Building Archetype Designed to house industrial operations and provide the necessary conditions for workers and the operation of industrial equipment. The modern revolution in science and technology, with its constant progress in the construction of industrial buildings andimproved construction technology, has resulted in an increase in the number of enterprises that do not pollute theenvironment. As a result, a new type of urban development, known as the industrial-residential area, has emerged. Contrasting in scale, appearance, and silhouette with standarddesign housing, industrial buildings become importantarchitectural highlights in urban development. Aesthetically, the quality of industrial buildings can be improved by distinctive interior design, sensible proportioninganddivision of enclosed areas, and harmonious choice of structural elements. Industrial buildings have an enormousand often negativeimpact on natural and architectural landscapes; and industrialregions often become alienated from the natural environment. One task of industrial architecture is thus maximumpreservation of the natural landscape and harmonious introduction of new industrial buildings into the landscape. The integration of industrial buildings is one of the most efficient means of reducing the estimated costs of constructing them. In largescale construction, most multistory industrial buildings have three to six floors and ceiling loads of 5to10 kN/m2.When land area is limited, industrial buildings may have ten or more stories. Modern multistory buildings usually have column networks of 6 m × 6 m, 9 m × 6 m, or 12 m × 6 m, and there is a tendency to use even more extended networks.\", \"sampled\": \"On September 24, 2020, NVIDIA launched the GeForce RTX 3090, a GPU that immediately caught the attention of the data science community for reasons beyond raw compute performance. The launch coincided with a blog post from Josh Patterson demonstrating how to run RAPIDS workflows remotely, tying the hardware milestone to a practical question about where and how GPU-accelerated data science actually gets done. The RTX 3090 was the first GPU to offer 24 or more gigabytes of memory at a price per gigabyte below $70. That threshold matters because memory size is a hard constraint on large GPU-accelerated workloads, and the previous benchmark for value had been the Titan RTX at $100 per gigabyte. Dropping below $70 per gigabyte while crossing the 24-gigabyte mark changed the calculus for anyone building or upgrading a local workstation for RAPIDS-based work. RAPIDS is a GPU-accelerated data science platform that runs workflows typically associated with CPU-based libraries on NVIDIA hardware at substantially higher speeds. Patterson's post focused less on the RTX 3090 itself and more on how to reach a capable GPU workstation remotely, using Tailscale as the connectivity layer. Tailscale is a VPN tool that simplifies remote access without requiring complex firewall configuration or manual IP management. The demonstration workstation was an HP Z4 running Ubuntu 18.04 with dual NVIDIA Quadro RTX 8000 GPUs. The setup Patterson described spans 6 steps: installing Tailscale on both the workstation and the client device, obtaining a static IP through Tailscale, connecting via SSH, installing RAPIDS on the workstation, spinning up JupyterLab, and connecting to Jupyter from the remote device. The result is a working remote data science environment that runs on the workstation's GPU hardware from wherever the user happens to be. Patterson tested the setup on two client devices — an Apple MacBook and an Apple iPad — confirming the connection works across both a full desktop OS and a mobile platform. JupyterLab is the interface through which RAPIDS workflows are executed, accessed by pointing a browser at the workstation's Tailscale IP on port 8888. Once connected, the user interacts with the workstation's GPU resources as though working locally. The demonstration workflow was K-Means clustering on simulated data, run across both Quadro RTX 8000 GPUs in the HP Z4. Patterson addresses a relevant tradeoff here: a single GPU with more memory is generally more efficient than two GPUs communicating over PCIe, except for embarrassingly parallel operations where the workload splits cleanly. That's worth keeping in mind for anyone weighing a high-memory single GPU like the RTX 3090 against a multi-GPU configuration. The RAPIDSai Twitter account serves as a community hub for updates and new demonstrations. Taken together, the RTX 3090 launch and Patterson's remote access workflow reflect something practical: GPU-accelerated data science is becoming more accessible both in hardware cost and in the tooling available for working with that hardware from a distance. The 6-step Tailscale setup, the HP Z4 with dual Quadro RTX 8000s, and JupyterLab form a reproducible pattern for remote RAPIDS work that doesn't depend on cloud infrastructure. Whether the client is a MacBook or an iPad, the workflow reaches the same GPU resources through the same connection, making the workstation's physical location largely irrelevant to whoever is running the analysis.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"10 Best Big Data and Hadoop Tutorials, Books, and Courses to learn in 2021 javinpaul Follow Sep 15 · 7 min read image_credit — Udemy Hello guys, if you are looking to learn Big Data and Hadoop, and looking for some excellent books, courses, and tutorials to start with, then you have come to the right place. In the past have shared some free Big Data and Hadoop Courses and the best Big Data Courses and In this article, I am going to share some of the best resources to learn Big Data and Hadoop, including tutorials, books, and online courses. You can use these resources to learn both Big Data in general and Hadoop in particular at a time and place convenient to you. Best Big Data and Hadoop Books, Courses, and Tutorials So, what are we waiting for, let’s dive into the best books, courses, and tutorials to learn Big Data and Hadoop in-depth? Here is the list of the best online resources to learn Big Data: 1. The Ultimate Hands-On Hadoop (udemy.com) An excellent course to learn Hadoop online. It’s very comprehensive and covers Hadoop, MapReduce, HDFS, Spark, Pig, Hive, HBase, MongoDB, Cassandra, Flume — the list goes on! Over 25 technologies. This is one of the best Big Data and Hadoop course on Udemy, created by Frank Kane, this course is really the ultimate tutorial for learning Hadoop in depth. If you can pick just one course from this list, choose this one. It’s not free but the worth of its price and you can get in just $10 on Udemy sales which happens every now and then.\", \"sampled\": \"Back in 2008, the Journal of Psychiatric Research reported that 35.5 percent of Americans experienced sleep disturbances at least three nights a week. Numbers have only gotten worse since. The COVID-19 pandemic pushed disturbance rates beyond what was recorded then, compounding a problem that was already affecting more than a third of the population before the crisis began. Poor sleep carries real consequences for the body and mind — disrupting hormone levels, degrading exercise performance, and impairing brain function in ways that compound quickly when the pattern repeats night after night for weeks or months. The real question isn't simply how many hours a person sleeps, but whether those hours actually deliver restorative rest. A study published in Sleep Medicine tracked the sleep patterns of 1.1 million individuals over six years and found that quality consistently mattered more than volume. Five hours of genuinely restorative sleep per night could prove more beneficial than eight hours of poor-quality sleep. The National Sleep Foundation offers concrete markers for what quality sleep actually looks like: falling asleep within 30 minutes, waking no more than once per night, and drifting back to sleep within 20 minutes if a waking does occur. Together, these criteria shift attention away from clock-watching and toward the actual character of the sleep a person gets each night. Some sleep problems stem from underlying medical conditions that require professional attention, while others respond well to deliberate changes in habits around light exposure, schedule consistency, and evening routines.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Authors decide to check will adversarial trained network performed better on transfer learning tasks despite on worst accuracy on the trained dataset (ImageNet of course). And it is true. They tested this idea on a frozen pre-trained feature extractor and trained only linear classifier that outperformed classic counterpart. And they tested on a full unfrozen fine-tuned network, that outperformed too on transfer learning tasks. On pre-train task they use the adversarial robustness prior, that refers to a model’s invariance to small (often imperceptible) perturbations of its inputs.\", \"sampled\": \"Joseph Emmi has spent a couple of months wrestling with what entrepreneurship actually means, pulled into that question through his work with projects and people who fit the category. It's not casual curiosity. He plans to put those questions directly to others, then compile and share what he learns, alongside a series of stories documenting his own experience as an entrepreneur. That series will get into the fears, lessons, myths, assumptions, and failures that tend to disappear from tidier narratives.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"First Confirmed Case of Coronavirus Reinfection Doesn’t Mean We’re All Doomed The case is one in 23 million Photo: Li Zhihua/China News Service/Getty Images Scientists in Hong Kong reported today the first confirmed case of reinfection with SARS-CoV-2, the virus that causes Covid-19. Since the beginning of the pandemic, there have been concerns about long-term immunity to the novel coronavirus, and several possible cases of reinfection were reported in the media. But until now, none were confirmed scientifically. The question has always been whether reports of a person testing positive, recovering from the virus and testing negative, and then testing positive again weeks or months later is because of faulty testing, “dead” viral RNA lingering in the body, a reemergence of the same infection, or a genuine instance of reinfection. The Hong Kong report is the first to use genetic testing to confirm the two cases in the same person were caused by slightly different strains of the virus. According to a manuscript leaked by South China Morning Post reporter Lilian Cheng on Twitter, the patient, a 33-year-old man with no preexisting conditions, first got sick in March, presenting with a cough, sore throat, fever, and headache. He tested positive for SARS-CoV-2 on March 26 and was monitored in the hospital for two weeks (standard protocol for patients in Hong Kong regardless of disease severity) until he was discharged on April 14 following two negative tests. The second time he tested positive was after returning to Hong Kong from Spain on August 15, when he was screened at the airport as part of standard reentry procedures. This time, however, he was completely asymptomatic and never developed a cough, fever, or any other signs of Covid-19. Scientists at the University of Hong Kong sequenced the genome of the virus from the tests taken in March and August and discovered that they differed in several key areas, indicating that they were two different strains of SARS-CoV-2. Specifically, 24 nucleotides — the “building blocks” that make up the virus’s RNA — were different between the two infections. The August strain was a variation of the virus known to be circulating primarily in western Europe, suggesting the man was reinfected while abroad. While the news of a legitimate reinfection is worrying, virologists and immunologists took to Twitter to reassure people that this doesn’t mean we’re all doomed. In fact, scientists have been expecting reinfection to occur all along. Akiko Iwasaki, PhD, a professor of immunobiology at Yale University, tweeted, “This is no cause for alarm — this is a textbook example of how immunity should work.” Virus-specific antibodies created by the immune system are central to the question of immunity, and varying reports have emerged over the past few months about the quantity, quality, and duration of antibodies produced in response to SARS-CoV-2. The vast majority of people who’ve recovered from Covid-19 do develop antibodies to the virus, and typically the more severe the infection, the more antibodies they produce, providing them with protection against reinfection. However, according to the leaked manuscript, which is under review at the academic journal Clinical Infectious Diseases, the Hong Kong patient had no detectable antibodies after his first infection. It’s possible this man had a very mild initial case of Covid-19 or an abnormal immune response that resulted in fewer antibodies being produced. Either way, the absence of antibodies after the man’s first infection could explain how he became infected with a different strain a second time.\", \"sampled\": \"Hanson Robotics Limited is a Hong Kong-based company that has become one of the more recognized names in humanoid robotics since its founding in 2013. Sophia is its most advanced and well-known robot. She made her public debut at the 2016 South by Southwest show, introducing a humanoid capable of nuanced facial expression and conversation to a wide audience. Hanson Robotics builds its robots using a patented elastomer skin called Frubber, engineered to mimic human skin in both feel and flexibility, which gave Sophia a lifelike appearance that set her apart from earlier humanoid designs. That physical realism, combined with her conversational ability, drew immediate global attention. Sophia's most consequential moment came in October 2017, when Saudi Arabia granted her citizenship, making her the first robot in history to receive a nationality from any country. Speaking at the panel where the distinction was announced, Sophia said, \\\"I am very honoured and proud for this unique distinction. This is historical to be the first robot in the world to be recognised with a citizenship.\\\" The decision landed at the center of a debate that extended well beyond robotics and AI policy. Critics noted that Sophia appeared to have been granted more rights than many Saudi women held at the time, a comparison that drew sharp responses from advocates and commentators around the world. Hanson Robotics had already been positioning Sophia as a figure for positive engagement with these issues. Appearances at events hosted by the ITU and the United Nations had established her as a platform for discussing technology and society, and her media presence was equally broad, spanning CBS 60 Minutes with Charlie Rose, the Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon, and Good Morning Britain. David Hanson addressed the citizenship controversy in comments to CNBC, stating, \\\"Sophia is a big advocate for women's rights. She has been reaching out about women's rights in Saudi Arabia.\\\" His response framed Sophia not as a passive symbol but as an active participant in public discourse, a role Hanson Robotics had been cultivating since her 2016 debut. Sophia was also featured in AUDI's annual report and appeared on the cover and centerfold of ELLE Brazil, extending her presence into commercial and fashion publishing. Those placements reflected a deliberate strategy to position her as a cultural figure, making her simultaneously a product, a spokesperson, and a subject of debate across journalism, broadcasting, and print advertising. On the technical side, Sophia's architecture draws on SingularityNET, a decentralized AI network that allows the creation, sharing, and monetization of AI services through blockchain infrastructure. Sophia is described as one of the first use cases of SingularityNET, relying on multiple linked AI modules that communicate through the platform rather than operating from a single centralized system. That structure allows her capabilities to be updated as new AI services become available on the network, distinguishing her from robots built on closed, proprietary systems. The broader industry context matters here: AI is currently estimated to be a $233 billion industry, with projections placing that figure at $3 trillion by 2025. Hanson Robotics and SingularityNET are both operating within that expanding landscape, where the commercial stakes for humanoid AI interfaces are rising sharply. Within a few years, Sophia had functioned as a keynote speaker at United Nations events, a magazine cover subject, and the first recipient of robotic citizenship — a combination that is genuinely unusual in the history of AI development. The Saudi citizenship, whatever its legal ambiguities, accelerated her visibility considerably, and her appearances across institutional and commercial venues created a public profile that operated across multiple registers at once.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Immediately, the authors of the Batch Normalization paper begin by initializing variables. Tip: Machine learning papers are notorious for creating dozens of variables and expecting the reader to know what they mean when they are referenced later. Take a highlighter and highlight where a variable is ‘initialized’ and where it is used henceforth. This will make reading much easier. The authors present the formula for Stochastic Gradient Descent, initializing several variables, such as the parameters and the maximum number of training examples in the set. G lossary: “arg min f(x)” refers to the arguments, or inputs, which minimize the following function, in this case f(x). In English, the statement reads, “the parameters of the network Θ are equal to the values in which [the average of all values outlined by a function l which takes in an individual training point and the current parameters] is minimized.” This is a mathematically rigorous definition of the goal of a neural network. Just for the purpose of being rigorous, often mathematical equations are written to be more complex than they actually are. It’s helpful to write out what an equation means in a human language. The authors note that with SGD, training proceeds in steps, outlining an additional variable m which represents the size of each “mini-batch”. By using mini batches instead of one example at a time, the gradient of the loss is a better estimate for the gradient over the entire set. The highlighted statement reads, “the average of [the change in the loss function, which takes in the current training example and the parameters, with respect to the parameters] for all values i in the training mini-batch”. This is the definition of a ‘gradient’, which calculates the error landscape of the loss function to provide insight on how the parameters should be updated. The use of [fraction][sigma] is almost always a complex way of meaning the average. Glossary: ∂ is universally used to represent a partial derivative, or a change in a function with two or more variables. A derivative can be thought of, in a simple context, as “a small change in one variable’s effect on another variable.” While SGD works well for a variety of reasons, the authors write that a change in the distributions of one layer’s inputs causes difficulties in the following layers because they need to adapt to the changing distributions, a phenomenon they call covariate shift. While traditionally this was handled by domain adaptation, the authors believe that the idea can be extended to a sub-network or a layer. The first highlighted statement reads “the loss is equal to an arbitrary transformation on [a parameter and an arbitrary transformation on (another parameter and an input u)]”. In this statement, the authors are setting up a hypothetical network to support their ideas. The authors simplify the initial statement by replacing a component with x to represent an input form the previous function. Θ[1] and Θ[2] are the parameters that are learned to minimize the loss l. These premises are identical to a complete neural network, simply built at a smaller scale. The highlighted equation demonstrates the mechanics behind gradient descent, which computes partial derivatives to calculate the gradient, with progress determined by a learning rate. The change, which may be positive or negative, is subtracted to the parameter Θ[2], and is intended to steer the parameters in a direction to minimize loss/F2. The authors write that this gradient descent step is the same for a single network F2 with the input x to establish the legitimacy of comparison between a real neural network and the one hypothesized. Because it Θ[2] does not need to readjust to compensate for a change in the distribution of x, it must be advantageous to keep x’s distribution fixed. Note: This is a common theme in machine learning papers. Because machine learning deals with systems that involve so many more variables and with so much more complexity than other fields, its papers will often follow a three-step process to demonstrating how a thesis works: 1. Create a hypothetical and simple system. 2. Establish the identical natures between it and a real neural network. 3. Draw conclusions by making operations on the simple system. Of course, in more modern papers one will see a section entirely devoted to displaying accuracies and how the method works on various common benchmark datasets like ImageNet, with comparisons to other methods. Besides, the authors write, a fixed distribution of inputs would be beneficial for inputs in the entire network.\", \"sampled\": \"Youth4Nature is a youth-led initiative with a clear purpose: bringing young people's voices directly to the policymakers who shape environmental and climate strategy. The organization launched with a webinar that drew speakers from across the environmental and academic world, signaling from the start that it intended to operate at a serious level. Nathalie Seddon, Professor of Biodiversity at Oxford University, was among them, addressing the role of nature-based solutions in confronting the climate crisis. Her presence underlined the scientific grounding that Youth4Nature wanted behind its advocacy work. The IUCN defines nature-based solutions as actions to protect, sustainably manage, and restore natural or modified ecosystems in ways that address societal challenges while delivering benefits for both human wellbeing and biodiversity. That definition is broad by design, covering everything from urban green spaces to large-scale forest restoration across degraded landscapes. Seddon highlighted one striking figure: nature-based solutions can provide up to 30% of the CO2 mitigation needed by 2030 to keep global warming under 2 degrees. That is not a marginal contribution — it means forests, wetlands, and soils need to be part of the core strategy, not an afterthought. Recognition of that role is already visible in international policy, with 66% of Paris Agreement signatories including nature-based solutions in their nationally determined contributions. The New York Declaration on Forests and the Bonn Challenge are among the other frameworks where these approaches have become a regular feature. Developing tropical countries lead in implementing nature-based solutions partly because of their relatively low cost compared to technological alternatives, making them accessible where large capital investment simply isn't available. That practical advantage has pushed nature-based approaches to the front of climate planning across much of the Global South. At the same time, droughts driven by global warming increase the risk of forest fires and coral bleaching, which can directly undermine the very ecosystems communities depend on for climate resilience. Protecting those ecosystems and cutting emissions are not separate tasks — they are deeply connected obligations that have to be pursued together. The Global Economic Forum has published a report framing extreme weather events and failure to adapt as serious risks not just to the environment but to human wellbeing and the broader economy, connecting ecosystem degradation to financial and social instability that reaches well beyond the communities living closest to affected landscapes. Elujulo Opeyemi, a Youth4Nature network member working in Nigeria, promotes agroforestry by providing fruit tree seedlings to farmer communities, linking forest conservation directly to agricultural livelihoods. His approach reflects a practical understanding of what it takes to make conservation appealing where economic survival is the immediate priority for most families. As he put it: \\\"Young farmers often have the knowledge about nature-based solutions for climate change, but they also want to get an income.\\\" That tension runs through much of the nature-based solutions field, where ecological goals and economic needs have to be reconciled rather than treated as competing values. Saline Abraham brings a different vantage point to Youth4Nature as a former president of the International Forestry Student Association and current Youth coordinator at the Global Landscape Forum. Her experience across both organizations gave her a grounded sense of how youth networks function and what they need to remain effective over time. At the launching webinar, she put it plainly: \\\"Nothing is special in one individual but power lays in coming together.\\\" That framing shaped how Youth4Nature presented its own structure — as a collective force rather than a platform built around any single voice or leader. The network plans to organize ongoing webinars and build an online platform to collect stories, videos, and podcasts about youth-led nature-based solution initiatives from around the world. The decade of ecosystem restoration set to begin in 2021 gives Youth4Nature a concrete 10-year window to influence the global strategy governing restoration efforts across that period. Seddon closed the launching webinar with a direct statement: \\\"We need to change our relationship with nature, recognize we are part of it, that our health is linked to its health. We must change our behaviour and pressure our governments. Now is the time.\\\" Youth4Nature positioned itself as one of the organized channels through which that pressure could be applied with consistency and evidence.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"When Isolation Turns to Solitude When you’d rather be alone on a stranded island than be around another mindless person, group, or situation. Photo by Joshua Rawson-Harris on Unsplash When you finally realize your own mind, your own energy, your own time is the most precious thing you have in this world. When you can see people for who they really are, shed the toxic ones, and cherish the real ones. When the faint of heart is long gone, and you are okay with that. When you see, it wasn’t a plague that ruined anything other than what was not for you. When you have truly accepted and appreciate life’s outcomes as blessings. I didn’t see it at first. I was waiting to be rescued by a man who promised me a whole new life of love in paradise. Like a lusty, naïve princess of a modern fairytale, I started dreaming of the day of rescue. With that mentality, I despised all that life was by agonizing and mentally withdrawing from everything. I mentally checked out of my career, and I even stopped bonding with my pup, Clyde. I was either floating on a dream of entirely leaving this life and everything in it, or in complete disgust and frustration of everything that currently was. Dramatizing about the awful plague this, and the terrible virus that; I needed to be rescued. As a young girl, I was told that being born on a Saturday comes with a special force. With strong enough will, this force can change the course of time and the direction of destiny. The Reiki Master I visited last week even said I have a special, gentle force protecting me. I have tons of stories of this force that’s changed some situations, but this time the force formed a disease and locked me up at home. I didn’t see it, and I definitely didn’t accept it. I thought I had finally met my soul mate, and now life was playing a cruel joke. But the force is wiser, of course. I really needed a leap to the next level of my personal evolution; I thought that’s where I was headed. Instead, I was blindly sidetracked by a distant dream full of lies and emotional exhaustion, to sum it all up. It wasn’t the plague that ruined things. We blame the plague for everything that’s now broken, politics, healthcare, financial crisis, relationships, but we don’t want to admit that the things were unstable, weak, or broken already. One night I woke up standing up outside my bedroom surrounded by broken glass. I had completely trashed my apartment, and I didn’t even remember. It was the new anxiety pills that were helping me deal with life plus some alcohol. Should I blame the pills, though? The alcohol, the plague, my subpar lifestyle, or that guy who was out sipping cocktails with his young female friends while I’m still cleaning up the broken glass 8k miles away. It was a rude awakening. If you never trashed your apartment, I can tell you; even though it was probably enormously liberating at the time of the blackout, I seriously don’t recommend it. Broken glass can never be completely swept up, at least not for months and the cuts on your feet take forever to heal. Some people choose minimalism as a cute weekend project, but some people, maybe just me, need to trash their apartment because they have been staring at all these freaking things they had checked out from but were still seeing. Things from the past or things given, acquired, piled up, stuffed in, or stuck around me that honestly had to go somehow. Reality can be so harsh. Especially when you live an artificial fantasy life, avoiding the truth and what life really is, not dealing with issues, running around with mindless people, doing and buying mindless things, and surely when, like me, creating a whole new wishful life to escape the real one.\", \"sampled\": \"R-Squared is one of the most widely used metrics for evaluating regression models, and understanding what it actually measures matters for anyone working in data science or statistical modeling. It represents the proportion of variance in the data explained by the model, giving a concrete sense of how well predictions track the true values. The mathematical foundation is straightforward once the components are clear. R² is computed as 1 minus the ratio of SSE to SST, where SSE is the sum of squared errors between predictions and true values, and SST is the total sum of squares measuring the overall spread of the data around its mean. The scale runs from a lower bound that can dip below zero up to a perfect score of 1. A model that explains none of the variance produces an R² of 0, while one that accounts for all of it achieves an R² of 1, making the metric intuitive to interpret across a wide range of modeling contexts. Because R-Squared is scikit-learn's default evaluation metric for regression problems, practitioners encounter it constantly when building and comparing models in Python. That adoption reflects how broadly accepted the metric has become across the machine learning community. One important and sometimes surprising property of R² is that it can go negative. When model predictions fit the data worse than simply predicting the mean every time, the ratio of SSE to SST exceeds 1 and R² falls below zero, signaling that the model is performing worse than the most naive possible baseline. A concrete example shows how quickly this happens: a model predicting the values 1, 2, and 2 for true values of 55, 2, and 3 produces an R² of -0.59, meaning it has failed to capture any meaningful structure and is actively worse than predicting the mean of 20 each time. R² is a relative metric, best suited for comparing models trained on the same dataset rather than making absolute judgments across different problems. In a practical toy example, a model achieving an R² of 0.71 accounts for 71% of the variance in the data — a reasonably strong result, but one that still needs to be interpreted within the context of the specific problem. A known limitation of the standard metric is that R² can increase as more predictor variables are added, even if those variables contribute little meaningful information. Adjusted R-Squared was developed to address this directly; it only increases when a new predictor improves model performance by more than would be expected by chance. The formula formalizes this correction by incorporating both the number of observations and the number of features: 1 minus the quantity of 1 minus R² multiplied by n minus 1, all divided by n minus p minus 1, where p is the number of predictors and n is the total number of observations. Adjusted R² is more common in statistical inference work than in machine learning applications, and the library landscape reflects this. Scikit-learn does not include it as a built-in metric, but statsmodels does, making statsmodels the natural choice for analysts who need this correction applied automatically. All of the code discussed here, including worked examples of both R² and Adjusted R², is available on GitHub in a Jupyter notebook authored by Jeff Hale, where readers can run each computation directly and observe how changes in predictions or model complexity affect the resulting scores.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Photo by Markus Spiske on Unsplash Spark Streaming is a component of Apache Spark. It is used to stream data in real-time from different data sources. In this section, we will use Spark Streaming to extract popular hashtags from tweets. The complete code implementation in Scala can be found at my GitHub page here: https://github.com/HritikAttri/big_data Working of Spark Streaming can be quickly explained as follows: Streaming Context: It receives stream data as input and produces batches of data. Discretized Stream(DStream): It is a continuous data stream, which the user can analyze. It is represented by a continuous set of RDDs and each RDD represents stream data from a specific time interval. The receiver receives streaming data and converts it into Input DStreams which can be used for processing. Certain transformations can be applied to DStream objects. Output DStreams are used to export data to external databases for storing it. Caching: If the streaming data is to be computed multiple times, it is better to persist it using persist(). It will load this data in memory and by default, data is persisted two times in memory for backup in case of failure. Checkpoints: We create checkpoints at certain intervals to rollback to that point in case of failure in the future. Let’s begin by importing the libraries: package com.spark.examples import scala.io.Source import org.apache.log4j.{Level, Logger} import org.apache.spark._ import org.apache.spark.SparkContext._ import org.apache.spark.streaming._ import org.apache.spark.streaming.twitter._ import org.apache.spark.streaming.StreamingContext._ Set the log level to print errors only. def setupLogging() = { val rootLogger = Logger.getRootLogger() rootLogger.setLevel(Level.ERROR) } We need to set up the twitter configuration file. For this, one needs to have a Twitter Developer Account. Go to https://developer.twitter.com/en/apps . Fill the details, create an app, go to details, then keys and tokens and click on the regenerate button. We need the API key, API secret key, access token, access secret token to connect to Twitter and stream data. Find the twitter text file used here on my Github page. Again, the link is given at the beginning of this post. def setupTwitter() = { for(line x.getText()) val words = text.flatMap(x => x.\", \"sampled\": \"Edge detection is one of the more intuitive ideas in image processing, and it rests on a surprisingly simple mechanism: filters applied to local pixel neighborhoods identify where edges appear. Ritvik Kharkar wrote about this technique on January 22, 2020, walking through the core theory and a practical Python implementation of the Sobel filter. Before any filter is applied, a grayscale image needs a consistent numerical representation for each pixel. Every pixel gets a value between 0 and 1, where 0 is black and 1 is white, giving the image a structure that a filter can work with directly. The filter itself operates on a small region around each target pixel rather than on the full image at once. A 3x3 box of local pixels centered on the target pixel defines the neighborhood examined at each step. Applying the filter means element-wise multiplication followed by a sum across that local region. Each pixel in the 3x3 box is multiplied by the corresponding filter value, and those products are added together to produce a single output for the target pixel. The range of that output carries meaningful information about what kind of edge is present. When the vertical filter produces its minimum possible sum of -4, that signals a top vertical edge; a sum of 4, the maximum, indicates a bottom vertical edge. Because the raw output spans from -4 to 4, it falls outside the 0 to 1 range that pixel values use. The output is mapped back into that range by adding 4 to the sum and dividing by 8, rescaling it so it can be displayed as a standard pixel value. The filter described here is the vertical Sobel filter, one of the most widely referenced edge detection tools in image processing and a foundation for understanding more complex filtering approaches. Its companion, the horizontal Sobel filter, is derived simply by transposing the vertical version — flipping it about its diagonal. Once you understand the vertical filter, the horizontal one follows directly from the same structure. Neither filter alone captures every edge, since edges can run at many angles, but combining the scores from both makes it possible to detect edges at various orientations across the full image. The underlying theory extends beyond grayscale images, though grayscale provides the clearest way to introduce the concepts.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Antibiotic Resistance: A Medical Crisis What you must know about antibiotic resistance Antibiotic resistance occurs when bacteria develop resistance towards the medicines that are designed to destroy them. The bacteria will not respond to medicine and continue to grow, leading to severe complications and even death. This has led to serious implications, especially in people with chronic illnesses as we become unable to treat infections and affect global health. In this article, let’s see what is antibiotic resistance, how it occurred in the first place, its impact and what steps can be taken to prevent and control its impact. Image by Arek Socha from Pixabay Antibiotics Bacteria are single-cell organisms that can be found everywhere. Your body houses trillions of bacteria and they help the body carry out essential functions. If you have come across my previous article Metagenomics — Who is there and what are they doing? you may know that these bacteria play an important role in our metabolism and immune system. However, some bacteria can harm your body and even kill you. Many people have died from bacterial infections until the first commercialized antibiotic penicillin was discovered in 1928 by Alexander Fleming. The discovery of antibiotics revolutionised medicine and saved many lives which might have been lost due to bacterial infections. Antibiotics destroy bacterial cells by breaking their outer layer so that their insides leak out, eventually killing the cells. Antibiotics also attack the genetic material to slow down the reproduction of bacterial cells and help our immune system fight against bacterial infections. Image by Matvevna from Pixabay Antibiotic Resistance Over time, some bacteria have evolved to protect themselves, adapted their cellular structure and gained resistance towards antibiotics. Bacterial cells can even transfer their genetic material among other bacterial cells allowing the new cells to gain antibiotic resistance. When these bacteria gain such “superpowers” and become resistant to most of the antibiotics used to treat infections, they are known as superbugs [1]. Many treatments such as organ transplants, cancer therapy and treatments of chronic diseases depend on the ability of antibiotics to fight against infections. If antibiotics lose their effectiveness, the risk of getting infections during these treatments increases, allowing superbugs to take over and eventually leading to death. Image by Gerd Altmann from Pixabay According to the CDC, over 2.8 million people are infected and over 35,000 deaths have been reported in the United States each year due to antibiotic-resistant infections [2]. Some antibiotic-resistant bacterial strains include, Pseudomonas aeruginosa Clostridioides difficile Staphylococcus aureus Candida auris Neisseria gonorrhoea You can find a list of selected bacterial strains which have shown resistance over time from here. Factors that have caused Antibiotic Resistance There are a few significant reasons which have lead to this “antibiotic apocalypse”.\", \"sampled\": \"Pandas comes with a set of built‑in plotting methods that make exploratory data analysis feel direct and code‑light. Instead of jumping to a separate visualization library, you call familiar methods right on your DataFrame or Series and see the shape of your data immediately. The setup is simple: import NumPy and Pandas, and include the magic command `%matplotlib inline` so every plot renders beneath the cell, ready for inspection. One of the first tools you reach for is the histogram, created by calling `hist` on a DataFrame column. The method takes an optional bins parameter; a value of 15 gives you a detailed view of the distribution without overwhelming the eye. You can refine the look by importing seaborn and setting its style before you plot. That small step applies a clean, modern aesthetic to all subsequent Pandas charts, making your exploratory graphics feel polished with almost no extra work. Pandas also lets you generate histograms through the more general `plot` method by passing `kind='hist'` or by using the direct `plot.hist()` accessor. Supplying a bins value of 10 there produces a slightly coarser histogram that can help you spot broader patterns. Area plots come next, built with `plot.area()`. Setting alpha to 0.7 introduces a gentle transparency that keeps overlapping filled regions readable while revealing the cumulative structure of your data. For categorical comparisons, bar plots are created through `plot.bar()`. When you set `stacked=True`, the bars pile on top of one another, showing part‑to‑whole relationships across categories in a single compact view. Line plots are drawn with `plot.line()`, where you specify x and y directly. You can control the figure size by setting a width of 5 inches and a height of 3 inches, and a linewidth of 2 gives the trace enough presence without looking heavy. Scatter plots use `plot.scatter()` and require x and y column names. The method supports a `c` parameter to colour points by a third column, and you can change the colormap with the cmap argument to highlight different groupings in the data. Box plots are created with a simple call to `plot.box()`. The method summarises the distribution of your columns through quartiles and outliers, making it easy to compare spread and symmetry across several variables at once. Hexbin plots represent dense point clouds as hexagonal cells using `plot.hexbin()`. A gridsize of 15 controls the number of hexagons along each axis, turning overplotting into a readable heatmap of point density. Kernel density estimation plots are generated with `plot.kde()` on a Series or DataFrame. They smooth the histogram into a continuous curve, helping you see the underlying probability shape without bin‑edge artefacts.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Discussion What does data-driven design mean to you? Netflix provides a great example of a data-driven customer-centric company. By introducing streaming video, their software “ate” the traditional DVD business. But Netflix soon realized their future wasn’t in the medium of delivery — it was in the wealth of data generated simply by people using the service. The day-to-day data generated by Netflix viewers provides a crucial ingredient to competing in the marketplace and defining the company’s mission: improving the quality of the service. Netflix uses passive data — the information gathered quietly in the background without disrupting users’ natural behaviors — to provide TV and movie recommendations, as well as to optimize the quality of services, such as streaming speed, playback quality, subtitles, or closed captioning. Of course, Netflix subscribers can contribute active feedback to the company, such as movie reviews or feedback on the accuracy of a translation, but the true value of Netflix’s user data is in the quiet, zero-effort observation that allows the company to optimize experiences with no friction or disruption to regular user behavior The future of software is data-driven by Dries Buytaret 2. Have you used data to find something out about their users and apply it to improving visual or interaction design? 3. What data we collect and the tools we use to analyze that data and give us answers about user behaviors Amplitude Find insights about your users and their interactions with your designs by creating if/then statements to get answers to your questions. Find insights about your users and their interactions with your designs by creating if/then statements to get answers to your questions. Cloud Platform Analytics Collects data on all of the user events we track for every Cloud product. Sends the data it collects to Amplitude so you can use it to create new insights. Where you go to request new data points be collected. Collects data on all of the user events we track for every Cloud product. Sends the data it collects to Amplitude so you can use it to create new insights. Where you go to request new data points be collected. DB Warehouse Dashboard I’m just learning about this but in short, it’s Amplitude but for products in the two DB2 warehouses. Connect with Marylia Gutierrez or David Kalmuk for more info.\", \"sampled\": \"Word embeddings map words into low-dimensional dense vectors that capture semantic relationships in a continuous space. This representation technique has become a cornerstone of modern natural language processing because it places similar words near each other mathematically, giving models a way to grasp meaning through proximity. The shift to embeddings fueled deep learning breakthroughs in tasks like neural machine translation and sentiment analysis, where earlier sparse representations buckled under data sparsity. By encoding meaning into compact vectors, these methods supplied neural networks with a richer signal, fundamentally changing how machines process text. Since Mikolov 2013 introduced efficient training methods, over 26,000 papers on word embeddings have appeared, reflecting intense research interest that has refined how embeddings are built, evaluated, and applied across languages and domains. Two foundational approaches dominate the landscape. GloVe generates vectors from word co-occurrence statistics across a corpus, factoring a matrix that captures how often words appear together. Word2vec, by contrast, learns to predict context or a missing word in a sequence using shallow neural networks, producing embeddings that encode linguistic regularities. A well-known property of word2vec embeddings is their arithmetic behavior: king minus man plus queen yields a vector near woman. This algebraic quality shows how the embeddings internalize relational meaning, making them intuitive tools for exploring language. Plotly built a Word Embedding Arithmetic app with Dash to let users interact with these concepts visually. The app uses a subset of word2vec trained on the Google News Dataset, providing a concrete playground for semantic arithmetic. It represents arithmetic operations as a directed network of nodes, where users can input equations and see results mapped in the embedding space. Each node connects to its eight nearest neighbors, revealing clusters of related terms. Clicking a node displays a bar chart of similarities to those neighbors, offering a detailed look at local semantic structure. The interaction relies on NumPy and scikit-learn for backend computations, with Dash Cytoscape rendering the network. Callbacks for node clicks can be written in less than 50 lines of Python, showing how Dash simplifies building reactive data apps. The project ties together word2vec, GloVe, and modern architectures like BERT and Transformers, which extend embedding ideas into contextual representations, while recurrent neural networks also benefit from these foundational vector spaces.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Well, according to my calculations… It depends on the number of people. Information cascades occur when people make decisions sequentially, even if it goes against their own private information, based on the observed decisions of other people. Let’s assume we have a group of people (N) who can decide between adopting or rejecting the fashion face mask trend. Everyone can observe the decisions of people in front of them, but not their private information. We can build a mathematical model for what decisions they’ll make, based on the following components: States of the world: The world randomly exists in either of two states, one where wearing face masks for fashion is a good idea (G) and one where it’s a bad idea (B) — individuals will try to figure which one is true. Signals: Everyone has private information, or a private signal, that gives them a clue about whether accepting is a good idea or not — maybe in this case, it’s your existing knowledge of fashion. Signals can be high (H), meaning accepting is a good idea, or low (L) meaning it’s a bad idea. If it is, in fact, a good idea, then high signals will be more frequent than low ones. Mathematically speaking, the probability of getting a high signal given it’s a good idea (denoted by Pr [H |G], or q) will be greater than 1/2. Pr [L | G] will therefore be 1 — q. The same applies for the opposite: Created by Murto Hilali As a rule, the first person will follow their signal, as will the second. (Proof can be found here). If Person 1 and 2 make different decisions, person 3 is indifferent and will follow his own signal.\", \"sampled\": \"Extracting the dominant color from an image is a practical blend of image processing and simple statistics. George Pipis lays out a straightforward method: analyze every pixel and map its raw RGB value to a human-readable name. The core is a function that converts an RGB triplet into a recognizable color label, bridging the gap between numeric values and words we can interpret. That conversion step matters because it collapses millions of data points into a shared vocabulary of common colors that can be counted and compared. With the pixel-to-name mapping in place, the logic iterates over all image pixels to collect their labels, building a complete inventory of color occurrences across the entire picture. Tallying every pixel captures the full distribution without sampling or approximation, which keeps the final result accurate. Based on frequency, the n most common color names are retrieved, letting users specify how many top colors they want. A dedicated function takes an image as input and returns the top n colors along with their proportions—a compact summary of the visual makeup in just a few numbers. In the example Pipis provides, the three most dominant colors are steelblue, lightseagreen, and cornflowerblue, each occupying a measurable share of the image. Steelblue reaches 10.74 percent, making it the leading hue, while lightseagreen follows closely at 10.3 percent. Cornflowerblue accounts for 8.98 percent of the pixels, rounding out a trio of cool-toned blues and greens that define the picture’s character. The technique, built on pixel iteration and frequency counting, offers a reusable blueprint for anyone doing color analysis in Python.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Other than these 3 main problems, we also have a lot of legacy code that has a lot of code smell and bad practices. We also have a HUGE boilerplate to create anything, be it new page routes or API routes on our integrated BFF. Having the BFF and the SSR engine on the same server also means that we have coupled them and there’s a single deploy pipeline for them both. Also, having these problems really discourages us as engineers on creating new front-end services and that made our current repositories turn into little monolith applications with multiple contexts inside of it. And since we did not have the habit of documenting things, we have a pretty bad documentation of the current stack. The solution Let’s solve our problems one by one, shall we? The first problem is that it is very difficult to create a new service. To solve that our SRE creates an inside tool that we call Josie. Josie takes a template as a parameter and generates a new service from scratch, creating production and homologation environments with just a simple command. That’s it. We now have a simple app running on production with a single command. There are still some config’s that we have to do manually, but this CLI simplified our life a lot. Now all we had to do was to create the template. We thought about it and reached the conclusion that we can be more productive using an existing framework, with an dedicated team working solely on the SSR engine, then trying to give maintenance to an in-house engine WHILE creating new user experiences (we may change this vision on the future, when our team grows and our platform gets more robust and that’s absolutely normal). That’s why we’ve chosen Next.js. Gympass + Next.js = ❤ We proceeded to create the template with everything that we thought would be necessary to develop our future features. We then got to the next problem: what if we wanted to change something to all the services that use this template? To circumvent this problem we created an internal package to be used on the template, with everything that we thought was core for our application. Configurations, helpers, components, you name it, we have it all inside this package. When we change something, all you need to do is bump the package version on your package.json and get the latest code. We reached this final infrastructure: Final service structure We still have the last problem to solve, which is multiple BFF’s with the same API’s. This was probably the biggest change in our developer mindset.\", \"sampled\": \"Jupyter notebooks are central to data science, but their interactive design chafes against standard version control. Ceshine Lee explores this tension in a post that draws on Jonathan Whitmore’s Jupyter Notebook Best Practices for Data Science. One core recommendation is a post-save hook that automatically saves a copy of the notebook as a Python script and an HTML file every time the notebook is saved. The Python script copy gives reviewers a clean, text-only version of the code, making diffs straightforward and readable. The HTML copy is positioned as the best format for sharing results, especially as an attachment in cross-team emails. A common piece of workflow advice tells data scientists to clear all output before committing notebooks, but Lee argues this creates two real problems. First, it gives reviewers a strong incentive to skip running the notebook entirely, particularly when data processing takes more than ten minutes. Second, it makes distributing the necessary data and model files much harder. Storing and transferring gigabytes of data is not easy, and when outputs are stripped away, the reviewer must reconstruct the whole environment. If processing exceeds that ten-minute threshold, the practical outcome is that reviewers may just read the code without ever executing the notebook, weakening the review’s thoroughness. Including notebook outputs directly in version control is the most direct way to solve these problems. It preserves the visual and data context a reviewer needs to understand the work without rebuilding everything from scratch, and it sidesteps the difficulty of moving large data files around separately. Security considerations also surface in the discussion. Lee notes that saving Spark access credentials inside notebooks is dangerous, and version control with outputs could accidentally propagate such leaks if not carefully managed. The post-save hook helps here by generating plain script files that can be reviewed for those leaks. The hook’s configuration is flexible. The script can be placed in ~/.jupyter/jupyter_notebook_config.py or in the project root as jupyter_notebook_config.py, and the JUPYTER_CONFIG_DIR environment variable can specify a config folder for different projects. This makes it easy to adopt the practice across multiple codebases without manual setup each time. A new tool that enables direct diff of Jupyter notebooks on GitHub is mentioned as an emerging option, though Lee had not tried it at the time of writing. The broader workflow, anchored by the post-save hook and the commitment to keeping outputs in version control, remains a practical bridge between exploratory data science and rigorous code review.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We’re excited to share Intelligent Snippets with you, our latest feature designed to make your completions experience even more seamless. Kite’s Intelligent Snippets allow you to complete complex, multi-token statements with ease by generating context-relevant code snippets as you type. Whereas typical snippets must be manually defined in advance, Kite’s Intelligent Snippets are generated in real-time based on the code patterns Kite finds in your codebase. TL;DR Intelligent Snippets are live in the latest version of Kite (20190905.0) for all of the editors we support: Atom, PyCharm/IntelliJ, Sublime Text, VS Code, and Vim. Global and local functions are supported. Users need half the keystrokes when calling functions with Intelligent Snippets. Visit Kite’s download page to install Kite. Developers call billions of functions daily Developers write approximately 1.5 billion function calls per day, many of which are repetitive. In the past, developers referenced docs or copy-pasted snippets in the event they didn’t remember a function’s signature. We recognized this was suboptimal, and built Kite’s Intelligent Snippets as a faster solution for calling functions in Python. The problem with traditional snippets are pieces of code that can be inserted into a code buffer and then edited immediately afterwards. Traditionally, snippets were manually defined ahead of time by developers. They were static and could not adapt to developers’ code as it changed. As a result, snippets have been limited to straightforward code patterns. For example, the video below shows a developer using a snippet to insert the structure of a function definition and then subsequently filling in the rest of the function. Kite’s Intelligent Snippets engine makes snippets more powerful by generating them on the fly based on the code you’re working with. Kite automatically detects common patterns used in your codebase and suggests relevant patterns while you are writing code. There’s an interactive playground showcasing our new feature on our homepage. If you’re on a desktop computer, take over the demo loop by clicking “Let me try typing!” (Mobile users, you can see the loop, but you’ll have to move to desktop to test drive it.) How we built Intelligent Snippets Intelligent Snippets build on the code engine at the heart of Kite’s completions experience. Kite first indexes your codebase and learns how functions are commonly used. Then when you call a function, Kite suggests snippets for that function to easily complete it. Kite’s autocomplete still suggests completions for each argument, too. Intelligent Snippets not only save you keystrokes; they also reduce the number of times you’ll need to look up docs for the call patterns you need. Intelligent Snippets support global and local functions The video below shows a developer using Intelligent Snippets to quickly call requests.post : Intelligent Snippets also work on functions that you have defined yourself, like in the video below: The future of Intelligent Snippets We believe Intelligent Snippets will be a cornerstone of how developers interact with the AI-powered coding tools of the future. We’ve begun by using Intelligent Snippets to help developers write function calls, but we see broader uses for them coming soon. Intelligent snippets could be useful for writing try/except blocks or unit test cases, for example. We’re looking forward to bringing this technology to more use cases imminently. What to expect the rest of the year We have many more exciting projects in the works: We’re taking advantage of the latest research to make our machine learning models smarter. We’re building new editor integrations.\", \"sampled\": \"Anne Leigh Parrish’s essay first appeared in Writer’s Digest, where she paused the daily scroll to ask whether social media is quietly reshaping how writers build sentences and sustain attention. She doesn’t treat the question as settled science but as a persistent unease that has followed her through years of composing fiction and reading deeply—a sense that something in the rhythm of language has begun to shift under the pressure of platforms designed for speed and brevity. Her concern sharpens around two design choices that have become invisible through habit. Twitter once enforced a tight 140‑character limit, training millions of users to compress thought into bursts, and even after the ceiling doubled to 280 characters the expectation of instant digestion remained baked into the medium. Instagram adds a different filter: it requires a photograph before any text can be shared, so a book review there arrives wrapped in visual packaging rather than standing on the force of its argument. When Parrish compares Instagram book commentary to the long‑form criticism she encounters in print, she senses a loss of depth that is hard to measure but easy to feel. She reads the New York Times Book Review every Sunday, letting its layered paragraphs set a tempo that no square image and caption can replicate, and she finds herself wondering whether the platforms that now dominate literary chatter are training readers to expect verdicts instead of exploration. The difference isn’t just length but the willingness to sit inside an idea until it yields something unexpected. Her own writing offers a quiet piece of evidence—not proof, but a personal data point. Over recent years she has noticed her sentences getting shorter, the clauses trimming themselves down, and she cannot separate that change from the hours spent absorbing the clipped cadences of social feeds. It’s a gradual drift rather than a deliberate stylistic choice, the kind of shift a writer spots only when rereading older work and feeling a different breath pattern on the page. To explain what she values in prose, Parrish reaches for a film that has stayed with her. Martin Scorsese directed The Age of Innocence, adapting Edith Wharton’s novel with such deliberate pacing that a glance across a drawing room can carry more weight than a shouted argument, and Parrish sees in that restraint a model of attention that social media actively discourages. She doesn’t claim the film is a direct antidote, only that it reminds her of a register that demands patience from both creator and audience. That register has roots in her own history. Her father taught English at Cornell University and admired how well the British used their language, an appreciation he passed along through conversation and example rather than lecture. Parrish absorbed his ear for the music of speech, and she still reaches for multi‑syllable words because of the rhythm they bring to a line—a physical quality that short‑form writing often flattens into efficiency. She closes not with a verdict but with a question that has grown louder as she watches her own habits. Parrish wonders whether reliance on social media has made writers impatient during the creative process, less willing to let a scene breathe or a sentence find its shape through revision, and she suspects that the cost of constant connection might be measured in the vanishing space where unhurried sentences once lived.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Thursday, October 31, 2019 The following remarks were delivered by Dr. Stan Meiburg, Director, Graduate Programs in Sustainability on October 22, 2019, at the opening session of the Water Resource Economics Conference entitled “Southeast Waters: Wading Into Our Future” held at Troy University in Phenix City, Alabama. Good afternoon, and thank you for inviting me to speak here this afternoon. I especially want to thank Billy Turner, who invited me in the first place and has been such a great leader in this area for many years. Billy is a wonderful friend and colleague, and his tenure as a leader of a progressive and very well run utility in Columbus earned the respect of his peers not only in the Southeast but across the country. It has been a pleasure to be a partner and collaborator with Billy all these many years. TODAY’S TOPIC, THE ROLE OF SUSTAINABILITY IN ENVIRONMENTAL EDUCATION, is an interesting one. It’s especially interesting to ask this of someone who has only been in the academic business for a little more than two years. And in the private education business at that — which is a little different from the fine tradition of public education which Troy University represents. Now, I like to think that my previous life at EPA had something of education about it. Bill Reilly, a former Administrator of EPA in the George H.W. Bush administration, once said that “The EPA Administrator is far more than a regulator and should see himself or herself as a major source of information, of encouragement, at times of inspiration, for the public at large.” His point was not just that regulation is not the solution to all problems. Now, please don’t misunderstand me, I am a very strong defender of regulation in the public interest—government by law, not by executive whim. This kind of wise regulation is what stands between us and the indiscriminate subjugation of public to private interest. It is what makes efficient market capitalism possible—by ensuring that sellers cannot artificially lower prices by displacing externalities. Which is a fancy economist way of saying that regulation keeps providers from selling goods and services cheaply by making us pay the full price in poorer health and social injustice. Bill’s larger point was that we have to be able to depend on our public servants to properly understand and incorporate the full impact of their decisions on society as a whole in both the short and the long term. This is where sustainability comes in, and why a sustainability lens is so important at a conference on the future of our waters. The classic definition of sustainability remains the one from the 1987 report, “Our Common Future”, by the World Commission on Environment and Development, chartered by the United Nations. It is commonly referred to as the Brundtland Report, after its chairperson, a former Prime Minister of Norway. The Brundtland report defined sustainability simply as, “Meeting the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.” Let’s think for just a minute about the word “needs”. It’s not the same as the word “desires”. “Needs” communicates a certain urgency. I might desire an extra cupcake for dessert. I need clean air to breathe, or to point back to this conference, clean water to drink. Sustainability points to the interrelationship of three elements of need. The first set of needs is economic needs. These are pretty easy to understand. To be sustainable, businesses have to be profitable. But our economy exists at the sufferance of society, which provides the ordered structure of rules within which our economy functions. These rules enable us to unlock the terrific power of markets. Yet, our societies exist at the sufferance of larger forces—our environment.\", \"sampled\": \"Living with a chronic mental health condition reshapes daily existence in ways that are hard to grasp from the outside. Natalie Frank describes how persistent symptoms gradually wear people down, affecting their immune system, the beliefs they hold about themselves, their social support networks, and their ability to take part in ordinary activities. The toll isn’t just emotional but physical and relational, spreading into every corner of life. Unlike someone who experiences a temporary low tied to a specific life event, a person with long-term mental health struggles often stops believing that improvement is even possible. That loss of hope becomes a defining feature of the illness itself. Erika Sauter, whose writing inspired Frank’s reflections, captures this frozen state in her piece Letters to Friends and the Motherfucking Sad. Sauter writes about the simple act of facing a basket of clean laundry. She knows exactly what needs to happen, yet the will to do it is simply absent. Her words lay the moment bare: “I should fold it and put it away. I don’t because I don’t have it in me today, the same as the day before and maybe not tomorrow, either. I turn around, walk back up the stairs and plop myself on the couch.” This paralysis isn’t laziness or a character flaw. Severe depression can make someone unable to perform daily activities like cleaning or folding laundry, even when they know the tasks need to be done. The gap between knowing and doing becomes a chasm that outsiders rarely see clearly. Frank argues that people without serious mental health issues cannot fully understand the experience of those with chronic disorders. Part of what makes the experience so complex is that passion and interest do not simply vanish. A person with mental health problems may still feel a genuine pull toward an activity but be unable to engage in it. That internal contradiction adds another layer of pain. Sauter herself finds a strange duality inside her own suffering, noting that her mind suffers from agoraphobia and her body from depression. She describes an unspoken beauty where the calmness meets the chest-crushing pain. Sometimes people feel sad because she is sad, but she insists there is strength in sadness. Her reflection takes an unexpected turn. Depression robs her of self-worth and life experiences and the belief that someday she will prevail, yet it also provides the ability to live in the moment. She calls it the gift of mindfulness. Her words resist easy pity and instead claim something complicated and real. Internalized stigma about mental illness can worsen symptoms and create ambivalence about being cured. Long-term mental distress can become familiar and feel safe, making the prospect of change frightening. Frank shares a stark clinical example of this phenomenon. A patient with schizophrenia stopped medication and returned to homelessness because his previous psychotic world felt more familiar than his improved life. The patient had known that psychotic world for over twenty years. Frank worked with him for three months during a period of improvement, but a month later he was found off his medication and back on the street. He explained that he did not know exactly how to navigate the new world he experienced off his medication, so even though life on the streets might have been harder, it felt more familiar. The known misery won out over the unknown stability. This reality challenges common assumptions about recovery and help. Supportive acceptance on the person’s own terms is recommended as the best way to help those with mental health problems. Pushing for change without understanding the deep roots of familiarity can do more harm than good. The most meaningful support often looks less like fixing and more like staying present with someone exactly where they are.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The year 2020 witnessed a pandemic, crashing economies, depressing lockdowns, loss in all forms and figures, hospitals and healthcare centers stacked with the diseased and their helpless loved ones, unsung heroes working relentlessly in the depths of such facilities, and widespread chaos. We were hit by an enemy we weren’t prepared for. Now, everyone knows how that played out. It’s hard not to miss the jostling streets, the jammed high roads, grabbing a quick bite in a restaurant, or even the Monday morning dread of rushing to work. While some are trying to cope up with the disease itself, the rest try to acknowledge the mental and economical damage unearthed by this pandemic. In summation, everyone seems to be going through a tough time. The best of times only took a year to culminate into the worst of times. However, when the world was toiling away into the abyss of uncertainties, Science was the only guy holding the cards. This might be a sorry state, but the progress made by the researchers is unparalleled. Just when we all thought that there’s no more going around this impending disease, Research revealed another trick up its sleeve. Can CRISPR-technology diagnose COVID-19? There was a time when CRISPR technology was a fancy term only known to the Biotechnologists, Biologists, and a few Biology enthusiasts. The fancy gene-editing technology rose to fame during 2013 and has ever since been on a journey uphill. Over time there came about designer babies, transparent squids, and whatnot. CRISPR now has become a household name. Who knew CRISPR could do more than that? You ask What? Well, before jumping on to the answer, it is important to speculate on the relationship between CRISPR technology and COVID-19. Coronaviruses or CoVs are not new to the game. They have been cohabiting with our kind for a long time now. The newly mutated strain SARS-CoV-2 is the one with enhanced pathogenic powers and spells trouble. This new strain was detected in the first place by nucleic-acid based tests (metagenomic next-generation sequencing or mNGS) that suggested a 79% difference from the six members of the existing SARS-CoV family. Besides, a CRISPR based assay namely, the GeneXpert assay was suggested only last year to detect Mycobacterium tuberculosis with enhanced sensitivity. This indicated the potential of the CRISPR/Cas complex in assay development for other pathogenic diseases as well. Furthermore, in a recent study, CRISPR technology happened to show the potential for in vitro diagnosis of COVID-19 with 100% accuracy. On the contrary, the best part of this discovery remains the estimated manufacturing cost of 70 cents. What does that mean? As of now, over five million individuals have been identified with SARS-CoV-2 infection. It means that COVID-19 tests can be made available to a larger mass of people, especially in the developing and under-developed countries. This comes as a milestone for places where healthcare comes at a great cost and the citizens have been failing till now to get themselves tested. Besides, as the world grows desperate to get back on its feet and run into the dear dear arms of a normal life, the demand for rapid, sensitive, and inexpensive diagnostics has increased. COVID-19 testing In the past few months, countless debates have been aired and promoted concerning the efficiency of COVID-19 testing. However, the lack of 100% accuracy had always left a window open for doubt and speculation.\", \"sampled\": \"Shopify’s hiring philosophy rests on a simple, powerful idea: hire for potential, not just existing talent. The company looks past polished résumés and searches for people who can grow into roles that don’t yet exist. This wasn’t borrowed from management theory. It grew out of a practical reality founder Tobi Lutke faced when he started the company in Ottawa—hardly a primary talent market for tech firms. Silicon Valley and the broader Bay Area operate as primary talent markets with deep concentrations of qualified people. A job listing there can pull in dozens of applicants who already have the exact skills required. Ottawa is a political hub and a center for the arts, not a tech talent magnet. Lutke couldn’t count on a local pool of experienced engineers and product managers, so he had to find another way to staff a growing commerce platform. Many business books assume access to primary talent markets, an assumption irrelevant to most businesses. The standard advice about “hiring the best” falls apart when the best simply aren’t within commuting distance. Shopify distinguishes between fixed-mindset and growth-mindset candidates. This distinction, drawn from psychological research, became the foundation for how the company evaluates everyone who walks through the door. People with a fixed mindset believe their qualities are carved in stone. As the company puts it, they “believe their qualities are fixed traits and, therefore, cannot change. These people document their intelligence and talents rather than working to develop and improve them. They also believe that talent alone leads to success, and effort is not required.” People with a growth mindset believe learning and intelligence can expand with time and experience. Shopify describes them as those who “have an underlying belief that their learning and intelligence can grow with time and experience. When people believe they can become smarter, they realize that their effort has an effect on their success, so they put in extra time, leading to higher achievement.” Shopify created “The Shopify Way” to hire for potential and develop it into world-class talent. The program formalizes how the company spots candidates who show curiosity and resilience, then gives them a structure that accelerates their development far beyond what conventional onboarding would achieve. New hires receive a company education that includes history, past mistakes, and the context behind decisions. They don’t just learn their job functions. They study the company’s origins, its missteps, and the reasoning behind strategic choices that shaped the product and the organization. Shopify pairs one skilled mentor with five less-experienced workers. This ratio creates an intensive learning environment where newcomers get direct guidance while contributing to real projects from their first weeks on the job. Employees are given challenges designed to push them past self-imposed boundaries. The assignments are calibrated to sit just beyond what someone believes they can accomplish, forcing a confrontation with artificial limits and proving those limits were never real. The company states that “Shopify aims to help people fulfill their potential 10–20 years earlier than they otherwise would have.” That timeline captures the ambition behind the whole system: compressing a career’s worth of learning into a fraction of the time by betting on what people can become rather than what they already are.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Useful Aliases There is an unlimited number of possibilities for aliases. What’s important is that you define the ones that are valuable for your specific day-to-day workflow. Below are a number of aliases that I have defined for my personal workflow. I find that they simplify my Git usage quite a bit. You may or may not feel the same way. Checkout branch This alias is as simple as it gets. It is a keystroke-shortener for checking out a new branch. Alias: co = checkout Example Usage: git co my-fancy-branch Stage and commit changes Most of the time, when I am wanting to commit changes, I just want to stage and commit everything that I have done. This alias lets me do so. Alias: ac = !git add -A && git commit Example Usage: git ac -m \\\"Some Commit Message Goes Here\\\" Create new branch Creating a local branch, and then a remote branch to push to late, almost always stops me in my tracks when I try to push my changes and realize that I can’t remember how to create the remote version of my new branch. For this reason, I find it best to just do both in a single line. Thus, this alias creates a new local branch off of the current working branch, and then immediately connects it to a new remote branch. Alias: create-branch = !git checkout -b $1 && git push -u origin Example Usage: git create-branch “my_new_branch” Notice the $1 in this alias. This interpolates the first parameter — in this case, the desired branch name — that was passed to the alias into the command that gets executed. Sync Branch w/Dev I try to sync up my feature branches with the dev branch that other developers on my team are pushing to regularly. This helps me avoid accidentally breaking other features, tests, and so on, without noticing — and vice-versa. This alias quickly syncs up my current working branch for me. Alias: sync = !\", \"sampled\": \"Thresholding turns a grayscale image into a binary one—pixels become either 0 (black) or 255 (white)—which strips away ambiguity and makes downstream analysis in OpenCV much simpler. With simple thresholding, every pixel above a chosen value flips to white and everything below goes black, giving you direct say over foreground-background separation. The function cv2.threshold() expects a grayscale image, a threshold value, a maximum value, and a threshold type; it returns the thresholded image along with the threshold it used. A fixed cutoff like 127.5 can work for evenly lit scenes, but real-world images rarely cooperate. Bright photos have average pixel values well above 127.5, and dark ones sit far below, so a single hard threshold tends to swallow shadows or blow out highlights. A more practical route is to compute the mean pixel value with np.mean() and use that as a dynamic threshold. For a given image, that might come out to 126.34—a number that reflects the actual brightness distribution rather than a generic midpoint. Pairing np.mean() with cv2.threshold() also lets you pick different threshold types to shape the binary output. The flag cv2.THRESH_BINARY_INV inverts the logic so darker pixels turn white, which is handy when the objects you care about are darker than the background and you want them to pop as bright regions. Siddhant Sadangi shows exactly this combination: OpenCV’s cv2.threshold(), numpy’s np.mean(), and the inversion flag, creating a pipeline that adapts to each image’s lighting without manual tuning. It removes the guesswork of a single global threshold and makes segmentation hold up across varied exposures. When you need to process a batch of images, wrapping the logic in a loop and using tqdm for a progress bar keeps things efficient—you can watch each frame convert in real time. The result is a clean binary representation that preserves essential shapes, whether the source is a dim indoor shot or a harsh outdoor scene.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Learning How to Learn: Powerful mental tools to help you master tough subjects, Diffuse Mode How to make use of your superior diffuse mode a.k.a. your subconsciousness This is a follow-up of the chapter discussing the focused mode. I have a lot of ideas every day, but not enough time to write them all down, so I chose to write the other ideas down before I forget them. I was pretty certain I won’t forget the ideas described in this chapter. So the diffuse mode is somewhat the opposite of the focused mode. It is active in the background or your subconsciousness. The mistake most people and students tend to make when learning, is that they don’t spend a lot of time in the diffuse mode. This can cause a dramatic lack of depth in their understanding about subjects. If you teach those students about the simulation hypothesis (see the chapter: 09/14/2019 — Simulation Hypothesis and ‘Good and Evil’), they will use their focused mode to learn every bit of element related to that hypothesis, but won’t spend much time in the diffuse mode. What is the consequence of spending less time in the diffuse mode? Well, they won’t ask themselves those deep and abstract questions like “But how does this hypothesis relate to ethics and morals?” It is a sad trend you get to see in modern education, superficial understanding of material. I much prefer the old days like in Ancient Greece or Rome where even the emperors like Marcus Aurelius knew about philosophy and had a deep understanding about things. How to enter the diffuse mode First of all, you can enter the diffuse mode simply by not focusing on anything (through meditation or mindfulness), but this alone is not effective in terms of creativity and learning new things. In order to command your diffuse mode to learn something in the background, you need to use the focused mode first. For example, you want to know your own personal definition of the meaning of life. What you could start with is simply Googling information (and also storing them long-term), try to answer and view as many perspectives as you can, and then just relax. Do something else like exercising or meditating, the thinking will continue and run in the background. And after 15 minutes or even hours afterward, simply return to the question and you will be surprised how many new and stronger connections were formed in your brain without the conscious ‘you’. This technique can also beautifully be used when taking tests, exams, or making homework: https://lifehacker.com/improve-your-test-scores-with-the-hard-start-jump-to-e-1790599531 — Improve Your Test Scores With the “Hard Start-Jump to Easy” Technique Diffuse mode and working memory The diffuse mode is not limited to the working memory slots located in your prefrontal cortex, unlike the focused mode which is. This makes your subconsciousness so much more powerful when used correctly (albeit not as powerful as depicted in those movies). The focused mode also tends to activate old neural pathways that aren’t really that creative nor are the cerebral distances very long (the distance between two activated neurons, brain regions etc). The diffuse mode can activate but also create new neural pathways that have a much longer cerebral distance than the focused mode can. This allows the diffuse mode to be much more creative but also combine ideas from many different brain regions.\", \"sampled\": \"Sarah Thomas opens her essay by distinguishing the giants of ancient myth from the intellectual giants who shape how we understand the world. Mythological giants were often agents of chaos and destruction, blocking gods and human progress. In Norse mythology, giants dwelled in Jotunheim and were known as Utangard spirits from beyond the enclosure—a constant resistance to order. They opposed both gods and humans, embodying a wild, untamed power that had to be overcome. Welsh mythology gives us a similar figure in Rhitta Gawr, a giant who attacked every king he encountered except King Arthur. That exception underscores Arthur’s singular strength and marks him as a ruler capable of meeting monstrous challenges. The metaphor shifted in 1695 when Isaac Newton wrote to Robert Hooke. Newton’s famous words acknowledged that his achievements rested on the work of predecessors: “If I have seen further, it is by standing on the shoulder of giants.” The line was a gesture of humility, recognizing that scientific progress depends on the accumulated knowledge of earlier thinkers. Stephen Hawking later explored this idea in On The Shoulders of Giants, which examines the work of Galileo, Copernicus, Kepler, Einstein, and Newton and traces how each built on the discoveries of those who came before. Sarah Thomas argues that society now faces a new set of crises—pandemic, unemployment, climate change—and needs fresh giants to guide us. She believes the old models of intellectual heroism fall short against the scale of today’s problems. One of the modern giants she names is Malala Yousafzai, who was shot by a Taliban gunman in 2012 for advocating girls’ education. Malala was only 11 when the Taliban closed girls’ schools, yet she kept speaking out. She is the youngest Nobel Peace Prize laureate and a graduate of the University of Oxford. In 2013, a year after the attack, she addressed the U.N. Youth Assembly with a message of resilience: “The terrorists thought that they would change my aim and stop my ambitions, but nothing changed in my life except this; weaknesses, fear and hopelessness died, strength, power and courage were born.” Her words turned a personal tragedy into a universal call for education rights. Another giant Thomas identifies is Greta Thunberg, who leads a global youth climate movement. Thunberg’s activism brought millions of students into the streets and pushed climate urgency into mainstream political debate. At the 2019 U.N. Climate Action Summit, she delivered an impassioned speech that challenged world leaders directly: “We are at the beginning of mass extinction, and all you can talk about is money and fairy tales of eternal economic growth.” The third modern giant in Thomas’s view is researcher Carlota Perez, who promotes sustainable economic growth and Universal Basic Income. Perez’s work focuses on reshaping economic systems to be more equitable and environmentally sound. Thomas presents Malala, Greta, and Carlota as three figures who embody the new kind of giant society needs. They do not rely on physical strength or solitary genius but on moral clarity and collective action. By naming these three women as modern giants, Thomas redefines the metaphor for a new era. The shoulders we stand on now belong to activists and thinkers who confront systemic crises with courage and vision.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Why should you learn Machine Learning? It’s no secret that machine learning and AI is one of the most popular buzzwords of this decade, but is the hype justified? Lets take a closer look at three areas in which Machine Learning has changed or will change our way of life: E-health: With the use of Machine learning we have in recent years seen several studies where a trained algorithm were capable of predicting what kind of disease a person was suffering from, despite the doctors could not. The use of such technology in everyday healthcare is however only in its early stages but in the future we would most likely see that Algorithms will give a second oppinion on the doctors judgment. Transportation: Transportation has for centuries been an important area that we as humans benefit from in our daily life. It’s therefore no wonder that every generation has been trying to optimize and improve the way we transport ourselves from A to B. With the progress in Machine Learning transportation is now ready to move into the next area namly autonomous driving, where our cars, trains and airplanes can operate without the interaction of humans. This will not only decrease the overall costs of transportation but most likely also decrease the amount of road accidents. Entertainment: An area which for years has already been benefitting from MI is entertainment. You might be seeing it right now as this article most likely got your attention through a newsfeed which based on tags and historical data recommended it to you. This can not only help creators keeping you on their site but it can also sort out all the content that you have no interrest in, making it a win-win situation. Machine Learning Types So now that we have seen three areas that MI is influencing in a positive manner, its time to take a closer look at the different types of Machine learning.\", \"sampled\": \"Quicksort is a divide-and-conquer algorithm that sorts by picking a pivot and partitioning the array so smaller elements land to its left and larger elements to its right. It’s an unstable sort—equal elements don’t necessarily keep their original relative order—which is a trade-off for the speed it offers in practice. A common pivot choice is simply the last element of the list. For instance, with a list A containing 8, 1, 6, 10, and 5, the pivot would be 5. During partitioning, two variables, i and j, track the boundary between smaller and larger elements as the algorithm scans the array. The variable j steps through the elements, while i marks where the next smaller element should go. Once partitioning finishes, the pivot sits in its final sorted position and won’t be moved again in later recursive calls. The array is then split into subarrays before and after the pivot, and the same process runs independently on each. How well Quicksort performs hinges on how balanced the partitions are at each recursive step. In the worst case, partitions are heavily imbalanced—when the pivot repeatedly ends up as the smallest or largest element—and time complexity hits O(N²). The best-case recursion depth is O(log N) with balanced partitions, though that figure describes depth rather than total time. On average, time complexity lands at O(N log N), which makes Quicksort a solid general-purpose sort. Worst-case space complexity is O(N) because imbalanced partitions can stack up recursive calls. Strategies like random pivot selection or a median-of-three pivot help sidestep those worst-case behaviors.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We use TypeScript because it helps develop our apps safer and faster. TypeScript by default has many simplifications. They help JavaScript developers to start using it easier but they waste a lot of time in the long term. We collected a set of rules for more strict TypeScript. You just get used to it once and save much time working with your code in the future. any There is a simple rule that returns much profit in the long term. Do not use “any”. Ever. There is simply no case where you cannot describe a type instead of using “any”. If you have such a situation, it can be a problem in architecture, legacy or something else. Use generics, unknown or overloads and do not worry about unexpected problems with data structures. Such problems are hard and expensive to debug. strict TypeScript has a “strict” mode that is unfortunately disabled by default. This mode enables a set of rules for safe and comfortable TypeScript. If you do not know about this mode, read the following article. With “strict” mode you forget about errors like undefined is not a function or cannot read property X of null . Your types are accurate and correct. And what should I do? If you start a new project, enable “strict” and be happy. If you have a project without “strict” mode and you want to enable it, you can meet a lot of compilations. It is very hard to write a strict code without alerts of the compiler. So it’s likely you have a lot of problematic places. Migrating the whole project to “strict” gets annoying pretty quickly. It is recommended to cut this big task into pieces. The “strict” mode consists of a set of 6 rules. You can enable one of them and fix all errors. The next time, you enable the second one, fix errors and continue work and so on. One day you get a “strict” mode. tsconfig.\", \"sampled\": \"React hooks, introduced in version 16.8, fundamentally changed state management by letting developers use state and other features in functional components without writing a class. The `useMemo` hook, in particular, addresses two key performance issues: the complexities of function equality during re-renders and the repeated execution of computationally expensive operations. When a React component updates, it triggers a re-render, rebuilding all functions and variables within its scope from scratch. While this ensures the component reflects the latest state, it can be inefficient if it involves resource-intensive calculations that don't need to be re-evaluated on every render cycle. To optimize this, `useMemo` uses a technique called memoization, which acts like a cache by storing the result of a heavy operation. The hook is implemented by passing it a function that performs the expensive calculation and a dependency array listing all the values the calculation depends on. The hook's efficiency comes from its conditional execution; it only re-runs the function if an item in the dependency array has changed since the last render. If the dependencies are unchanged, `useMemo` bypasses the calculation and returns the previously stored result, saving processing time. While `useMemo` is a powerful optimization tool, misusing it can harm performance due to the overhead of memoization itself. For this reason, you shouldn't wrap every function in `useMemo` by default, as this can add complexity and memory usage without any real benefit. It's best to profile your application before and after implementing `useMemo` to measure its impact on rendering times. There's no performance gain if the dependency array is empty or contains values that change on every render, as this defeats the purpose of memoization. It's also important to distinguish `useMemo` from similar hooks. The `useCallback` hook, for instance, returns a memoized function rather than a value, which is useful for passing stable callbacks to child components. Furthermore, `useMemo` is not suitable for asynchronous operations like data fetching; the `useEffect` hook is the appropriate tool for managing side effects of that nature.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"How To Turn a $12k Investment Into $1 Million When Albert Einstein once said that humanity’s greatest invention is “Compound interest.” The biggest excuse for not investing I hear is: “I have no money to invest.” Part of the reason why people think they should hold back from investing until they have more money is that they think it’s risky. They don’t know when to buy, what to buy, or sometimes how to buy. Here is the main issue with that. Nobody knows when a market goes up, down, sideways. Anyone who tells you they do is either lying, foolish, or both. And having tons of cash will not give you that kind of expertise. Solution? Instead of striking one magical moment, buy regularly and consistently over a long period. The fancy name for this is “compound interest.” When Albert Einstein once said that humanity’s greatest invention is “Compound interest.” He also called compound interest the “eighth wonder of the world” and stated that “He who understands it earns it; he who doesn’t, pays it.” Compound interest is basically “interest on interest.” It simply means saving early and letting investment compound over a more extended period. It’s quite a liberating strategy, and it takes the pressure off needing to pick the right instant to buy. In my view, it’s as close to financial magic as you can. And on top of that, with this strategy, you are almost sure to put $1 million in your pocket. The magic is in the simplicity of this strategy. It’s all about investing repeatedly and long enough to ignite the miracle of the compound effect. For example, assume you put $100 per month into Bitcoin. In 10 short years, your $12,000 invested will be worth $1.163 million. Yes, that’s million, with six zeros. Check out the compound calculator and play around with the numbers for yourself. But are these expected returns realistic? Based on historical data, they sure are. Cathie Wood and her ARK is the most successful fund in the last five years. According to their research, Bitcoin’s return in the previous seven years was 90 percent. It’s one of the best-performing assets in a decade. Yes, it’s that simple. And you don’t need a big pile of cash to invest.\", \"sampled\": \"Welcome to The Literally Literary Weekly Update for February 5, 2020. This week’s curated collection brings together a diverse range of voices and stories, exploring themes of memory, loss, and the psychologies that shape our experience. Our 7th edition spans multiple genres, from poignant fiction to reflective poetry, showcasing the talent within our community and offering a space for connection through the written word. In a piece titled \\\"Wounded Mother,\\\" Cassius Corbin explores the complex dynamics of familial relationships and enduring pain. The narrative voice poses a heart-wrenching question: \\\"Do you remember on your wedding day, when I promised to write you every month? How you smiled and looked away? Did you even get the other letters?\\\" This intimate and searching tone draws the reader into a personal history filled with unspoken emotions and the weight of unfulfilled promises. Jerry Windley contributes \\\"Water Lead Home,\\\" a narrative that captures the essence of a vast and mysterious underwater world, blending desolation with hidden life. The story paints a vivid picture of the ocean's depths: \\\"It was as forlorn as a desert, except that the upside-down part of this watery desert harbored a whole world of strange living things, and unexplored mountains and plains, and the shipwrecks of people who hadn’t made it to the other end of the ocean.\\\" The prose transforms the sea into a landscape of both wonder and peril. The collection also features an entry from Dale Biron, who examines the political analysis in \\\"How Democracies Die.\\\" His work, \\\"The Lessons,\\\" offers a literary reflection on its core themes, encouraging readers to consider the fragility of democratic institutions and the lessons we can learn from history. Biron’s contribution adds a layer of political inquiry to this week's update, highlighting literature's role in understanding our contemporary world. Sylvia Clare’s poem, \\\"Sea Mist,\\\" captures the ethereal nature of the environment, using mist as a metaphor to explore perception and reality. The poem suggests there is always a \\\"mist around us, even\\\" when we cannot see it, a subtle force that shapes our world and our understanding of it. Clare’s contribution is a delicate piece that invites quiet contemplation of the unseen elements that influence our lives. Rounding out this week's features are works from several other writers, including Jonathan Greene and a piece by Taiwo Adesina titled \\\"The Mother,\\\" which provides another perspective on maternal themes.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"REMARKABLE NETWORKS: SURVIVAL OF THE FITTEST COMMUNITY The 2019 Sustainability Graduate Programs Student Showcase ‍‍Tuesday, September 17, 2019 Jay Lanier of Interface delivered the keynote address for the 2019 Sustainability Graduate Programs Student Showcase held at The Graduate School at Brookstown Inn. The event was an end of year celebration to recognize the class of 2019 and welcome the incoming class of 2020. Graduate Students presented capstone projects of their work from the Applied Sustainability course with the Yadkin Riverkeeper. TREES TALK TO EACH OTHER. Now, of course, they lack mouths to render speech and have nothing in the way of language that a linguist could recognize, but they absolutely communicate. I’m currently reading a book by a German Forrester named Peter Wohlleben called The Hidden Life of Trees. He describes how acacia trees on the Savannah of Africa tell one another that a hungry giraffe is out to hit the leaf buffet so its neighbors can immediately create the compounds that make them taste bad. In the beech and oak forests of his home in the Eifel mountains, Wohlleben talks about the communication through roots that allow bigger, stronger and older trees to share nutrients and water with sick, small and damaged neighboring trees. It turns out that trees work best in forests. It may be easy to assume that a lone tree, with no need to share its light, water or nutrients would be better off than a crowded group. As it turns out though, that group, with its social bonds could better be thought of as a superorganism, where it is truly more than the sum of its parts. Grade school biology taught us the survival of the fittest. We, as individuals, automatically tend to interpret that as survival of the fittest individually. But what if we change individual to the word community? Survival of the best community. A close-knit fabric of interwoven roots voluntarily shifting resources to create the maximum benefit for one another and, by proxy, an entire woodland ecosystem. Cooperation that begets abundance. A community that thrives, rather than simply survives. It used to be that the forest was a truly scary place. How many fairy tales took very dark turns when the Brothers Grimm sent their protagonists …into the woods? So often, it was used as the stand-in for all things unknown, wild, foreign — weighed in equal parts fear but also enchantment. It represented both dread and magic. So why the discussion about the woods and fairytales at a ceremony focused on your accomplishments in the study of sustainability? After all, many of us will go to work in a system that has grown and has a continued belief in the survival of the fittest individual. And with good reason in many ways. With the rise of our industrial system, the world’s desperately poor has fallen by over 1 billion people in less than 20 years. A physicist would look at such a result and immediately ask, “At what cost?” For much of our history as a species, those costs were negligible, or even outright invisible. But as our 7.5 billion people’s needs begin to ask questions of the earth’s abundance, the limits of exponential growth in a closed system are becoming more and more apparent. The morality of meeting the basic needs of so many are coming into conflict with the generous systems that allowed for such amazing growth of humanity in the first place. Back in 1944, the US Coast Guard released 29 reindeer on St. Matthew Island in the middle of the Bering Sea.\", \"sampled\": \"How do we solve a problem like election prediction, especially when traditional methods seem to be failing? Many commentators are still reeling from the poor performance of U.S. election pollsters in the 2020 contest, questioning the reliability of their models. This has led to a frequent question: can artificial intelligence and social media provide a better answer? For now, the answer seems to be \\\"no, not really,\\\" which might surprise some proponents. The failure isn't for the most obvious reasons; the issues are complex, involving not just data collection but also correct interpretation within the chaotic environment of online discourse. VentureBeat analyzed the performance of various firms to understand what went wrong. Companies like Kcore, an Italy-based firm, used AI to parse public sentiment but ultimately missed the mark in their final predictions. Expert.ai, another Italian AI company, conducted its own analysis of online sentiment. Its research found that candidate Biden ranked significantly higher in discussions across social media platforms, suggesting a lead that didn't fully materialize in the polls. In contrast, the firm Advanced Symbolics took a different approach. Its Polly system, developed by scientists associated with the University of Ottawa, produced a different set of projections based on its own AI-driven methodology. After the polling failures of 2016, many people, like Johnny Okleksinski, had an instinctive reaction. The impulse was to claim that new methods using social media data could easily supplant old polling techniques by offering a more accurate snapshot of the electorate. A 2018 Pew Research survey found that 74% of respondents agreed with a particular sentiment, showing a public perception that social media is a key battleground for political opinion, even if its predictive power is questionable. The authors of a paper titled \\\"Electoral and Public Opinion Forecasts with Social Media Data: A Meta-Analysis\\\" acknowledge these difficulties, having systematically reviewed numerous studies to understand the common pitfalls. One key insight is that while political discussions on platforms like Facebook are often dominated by a small group of highly active users, this doesn't invalidate the data. The challenge is understanding that this vocal minority doesn't necessarily represent the broader, more silent majority. By capturing political opinions expressed online, social media analysis can also understand voter sentiment more deeply. This goes beyond simple vote intention to include the nuances of opinion and the emotional context surrounding candidates and issues, providing a richer dataset. A primary issue is that social media users are not a representative sample of the general voting population. This demographic mismatch can lead to skewed results if not properly accounted for through sophisticated weighting techniques. This challenge is a central theme in the work of \\\"Fiona J Mcevoy\\\" and other researchers who have explored how different platforms attract different user bases, which can vary by age, political leaning, and socioeconomic status. The phenomenon is not limited to the United States; forecasting efforts in New Zealand have struggled with the same set of problems. On November 20, 2020, at 11:23 AM, discussions about the role of these technologies were still ongoing as the dust from the election settled, allowing for more detailed post-mortems. The analyses included various figures, such as the numbers 58, 27.349000, 00, and 5, which represent specific data points used in studies by firms like Advanced Symbolics or in academic papers examining the efficacy of social media forecasting. Despite its promise, using social media analysis for election prediction remains a work in progress. It provides a valuable stream of real-time data but is fraught with methodological challenges. Researchers continue to refine their methods, hoping to create hybrid models that combine the strengths of traditional polling with the rich, unstructured data from social media conversations.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"You want to learn how to become a writer, but all the writing courses you’ve seen are expensive. Is it really worth handing over thousands that you may never get back? What if you do a course and discover writing isn’t for you after all? I know it’s not easy breaking into the writing world. After over a decade in this business as both a writer and editor, I’ve discovered a few tricks of the trade that I’ve shared here on Medium over the past year. I decided to collect them all together for you as a kind of free writing course. Bookmark it and work your way through in your own time. If you have questions, feel free to leave a comment. No questions are stupid ones! I’m sure if you’re wondering it, someone else is too.\", \"sampled\": \"The publication Inspired Writer aims to unite experienced and new writers in one community, helping newcomers share their stories, improve their skills, and launch their careers. To apply, you must first follow Inspired Writer and subscribe to its newsletter. It's also important to read all submission guidelines carefully before sending anything. Once accepted, you can submit stories directly to the publication, but please note that only unpublished drafts are considered. New writers are also asked to submit stories one at a time. To begin the application process, follow Inspired Writer and subscribe to the newsletter, making sure to read all submission guidelines beforehand. We limit new writers to a single story submission at a time. This policy ensures that each unpublished draft receives careful consideration from our editors. Remember, we only review unpublished drafts, so please submit only one story at a time.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Before the rise in literacy and the invention of the printing press, people told stories. To family, to friends, around the table, around a campfire, at the pub. If you were good at it, not many people knew. Did it matter? Of course not. If you liked telling stories you told them. If other people liked listening to them they listened. Over time storytellers became writers. Stories started living in books. As a writer, I am “known” by a few hundred people on Medium. Probably 20% of them actually read my stories regularly (based on claps and responses.) Does it matter? Of course not. The world (or Medium) doesn’t owe me a huge audience or fame or fortune. And thankfully, I am not chasing after them. God bless those who are. It is a difficult road to travel. Of course, we all want an audience. Someone to appreciate our creative output. Audiences are good. No matter the size. They help us grow. The feedback they provide is essential. You learn what works and what doesn’t. You receive criticism, encouragement, and instructive silence. All of which are helpful. And if you write something readers like it is very satisfying. I write mainly humor and fiction. Topics not highly valued by Medium, but highly valued by some writers on Medium. And by some readers of Medium. So I write for them. If people like to read what I write they read it.\", \"sampled\": \"During AWS re:Invent 2020, Amazon released its open-source Kubernetes distribution, EKS-D. Its development draws from Amazon's experience managing the Amazon EKS platform, and the software can be used on-premises or with any cloud provider. A Kubernetes distribution builds upon the core Kubernetes project, often adding other tools and components. The announcement of EKS-D has many people asking, \\\"I know Kubernetes, but what’s a Kubernetes distribution, and why should I care?\\\" Kubernetes is an open-source project originally started by Google, but it is now managed by the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF) and the broader community. A \\\"pure\\\" distribution simply builds Kubernetes and lets users decide which tools to use, offering maximum flexibility. Downloading and compiling the source code directly is often called \\\"vanilla Kubernetes.\\\" A Kubernetes distribution is analogous to a Linux distribution, which ships the Linux kernel along with other necessary tools. Similarly, a Kubernetes distribution builds on the core project but typically adds its own set of components. Amazon's EKS-D is one such distribution. Based on the company's experience running its managed Amazon EKS platform, it provides an open-source option that can be deployed on any cloud provider or on-premises infrastructure. Like other distributions, it builds on core Kubernetes, which was started by Google and is now managed by the CNCF, and adds its own selection of tools. A \\\"pure\\\" build, by contrast, just compiles Kubernetes and leaves the tool selection up to the user.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"image: tw Brains are not just for eating Until 9/11 I thought that the days were over when people could be immolated for having the wrong ideas about the soul. People have long been controlled by a fear that their immortal essence could suffer for eternity if they did not obey certain spiritual authorities. “One of these mornings, the chain is gonna break.” — Aretha Franklin, Chain of Fools Brains, Not Chains While those chains have now crumbled for a lot of us, belief in a soul is still pretty common. It might be less so if more of us were aware of its stunning inconsistency with everyday reality: particularly the fact that we have brains. There is overwhelming evidence that brains are what make our bodies into people; people who speak, think, adapt, and cope with life on the planet. Brains gather and integrate sensory information derived from energy fluctuations in the environment. The more precisely we can measure brain activity the more detailed we find to be their correlations with human conscious experiences. And it’s not just humans. When we look at nervous systems throughout the animal kingdom, we see that brains operate bodies to enable their survival as well. Nevertheless, the ineffable, private nature of human consciousness is often part of an argument that something — call it a soul — that is not physical causes consciousness. If that’s true, then why does the brain seem so obviously involved? Can’t You Hear Me Knocking? To answer this question many have used the analogy of the brain being like a radio receiver. As one fairly typical source puts it: The brain does not produce consciousness, but acts as a kind of receiver which “picks up” the fundamental consciousness that is all around us, and “transmits” it into our own being. Some proponents think deeply enough to realize this comes with an issue. It’s whether a soul is somehow an individual or else is a sort of world soul. Maybe, they say, the world soul has knots in it that correspond to individual people.\", \"sampled\": \"Powerful psychology lessons can boost your digital marketing game. Emotional marketing, for example, often stands in contrast to more rational methods. This approach connects with consumers on a personal level, using elements like tone, mood, and even specific lighting choices to create a feeling. Consumers frequently base purchase decisions on feelings, which often take precedence over purely rational considerations. Customers are also more likely to remain loyal to brands that evoke positive emotions, making this connection a powerful driver of repeat business. You can apply this knowledge to your content marketing strategy by creating material that inspires genuine excitement. This type of content often reminds people of special moments in their own lives, forging a link between your brand and the consumer's positive memories. A successful campaign sparks conversations and encourages audience reactions, leading to higher engagement across the platforms where the content is shared. Apple masterfully uses emotion to connect with consumers and increase brand loyalty. The company's marketing strategies create a desire to become part of a specific lifestyle, with the product presented as the key. Apple's #shotoniphone campaign on Instagram is a great example. Instead of focusing on shiny product shots, the campaign showcases photos and videos taken by regular users from around the world, fully encompassing the values the company wants to project. Another powerful psychological principle is social proof, as described by Robert Cialdini. This can be seen in action with influencer marketing on platforms like Twitter. The collaboration between Old Navy and Meghan Rienks, for instance, effectively leveraged social proof to reach a wider and more engaged audience. Grounded Cognition is a theory suggesting our thoughts are influenced by physical experiences. This has significant implications for how marketing messages are received. Messages that evoke physical sensations can be more memorable and persuasive because they tap into this principle to create a deeper impact. This approach moves beyond simple product features to connect with the consumer's inner world. By understanding these principles, you can enhance your digital marketing and growth hacking efforts. This knowledge allows for a more psychologically engaged approach, leading to more rapid and sustainable expansion.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"What is a Decision Tree ? I am sure you are using Decision Trees in your day to day life without knowing it. For example, Imagine you only do four things at the weekend: go shopping, watch a movie, play tennis or just stay in. What you do depends on three things: the weather (windy, rainy or sunny); how much money you have (rich or poor) and whether your parents are visiting. Decision Tree example Interpreter: You say to your yourself: if my parents are visiting, we’ll go to the cinema. If they’re not visiting and it’s sunny, then I’ll play tennis, but if it’s windy, and I’m rich, then I’ll go shopping. If they’re not visiting, it’s windy and I’m poor, then I will go to the cinema. If they’re not visiting and it’s rainy, then I’ll stay in. T hus, Decision tree is a type of supervised learning algorithm. It works for both categorical and continuous (regression) input and output variables. In this technique, we split the population or sample into two or more homogeneous sets (or sub-populations) based on most significant splitter / differentiator in input variables. Decision trees use a criteria (there are multiple criteria available) to decide to split a node in two or more sub-nodes The creation of sub-nodes increases the homogeneity of resultant sub-nodes. In other words, we can say that purity of the node increases with respect to the target variable Decision tree splits the nodes on all available variables and then selects the split which results in most homogeneous sub-nodes. Why Decision Trees? Easy to Understand and Interpret. Requires little data preparation. Other techniques often require data normalization, dummy variables need to be created and blank values to be removed. Able to handle both numerical and categorical data. Able to handle multi-class problems. Uses a white box model. If a given situation is observable in a model, the explanation for the condition is easily explained by boolean logic. By contrast, in a black box model (e.g., in an artificial neural network), results may be more difficult to interpret Possible to validate a model using statistical tests. That makes it possible to account for the reliability of the model. Performs well even if its assumptions are somewhat violated by the true model from which the data were generated. The final decision tree can explain exactly why a specific prediction was made, making it very attractive for operational use. Important Terminology related to Decision Trees Root Node: It represents entire population or sample and this further gets divided into two or more homogeneous sets.\", \"sampled\": \"A badger who dislikes reading was recently tasked with critiquing a human's unfinished manuscript. Living in a dark underground sett, our narrator doesn't understand the human fascination with books, estimating that only about 20% of people actually enjoy the activity. After just three pages, the badger found the human protagonist boring. The manuscript lacked any elements that would make a story compelling from an animal's perspective, like otters or beavers. It also failed the \\\"Badgel Test,\\\" a standard requiring characters to discuss a badger. This story had no badger talk, nor did it include engaging details about slugs, which would have been a significant improvement. A major point of contention was the presence of a car. The badger demanded its immediate removal, citing the grim statistic that cars kill roughly 50,000 badgers every year—a fact the author seemed to ignore. One scene, however, did resonate. The part where the hero's mother gets drunk reminded the badger of a memorable incident from 2016, when the narrator and some other badgers, including one named Bob, got intoxicated after eating a large quantity of rotting fruit. This shared experience was a rare moment of connection. The badger offered some constructive criticism, suggesting the author add a live badger as a character. This would not only make the story more interesting but also help it pass the crucial Badgel Test. The critique then turned to the manuscript's practical uses, connecting it to the territorial habits of badgers, who use toilets to mark the boundaries of their setts. The unfinished novel was eventually put to a very specific use by another badger named Steve. Instead of being read, the pages were repurposed as toilet paper, serving a practical function in the sett's boundary-marking system. Steve the Badger, a neighbor, was so impressed with the manuscript's utility that he provided a glowing blurb. He praised the novel's texture and absorbency, highlighting its superior quality compared to other materials he had used. \\\"This novel was there for me when I needed it,\\\" Steve wrote. \\\"It’s completely transformed my life and is far superior to any other book I’ve ever used to wipe my butt with.\\\" His testimonial continued with sincere gratitude for the creation of such a useful product, concluding, \\\"Thanks so much. You are a brilliant author.\\\" The narrator, who fundamentally dislikes reading, found this a fitting end for the manuscript. The story, a literary failure with its boring hero and lack of otters, found its true calling as a practical item. Its transformation into a useful tool for Steve the Badger suggests it achieved a different kind of success after all.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Facebook Angry Reactions — Sentiment Analysis We can see for the posts that generated a strong Angry Response, the majority of them are classified by google cloud as texts with a negative or neutral score. There are angry posts that have a positive score, but the majority of posts that generated a angry response, have a text description that got a negative score. If we look also at the magnitude, we see that it`s getting harder to find angry posts in the positive score part (right part of the image), after the magnitude of the text gets bigger then 1.5. Facebook Love Reactions — Sentiment Analysis Love is positive,hate is negative. Proven with data. If we look were is the love, we see a different distribution. Articles that have a negative score tend to get less love. At least if the magnitude of the text is over 1 Wows posts are shallow and superficial I never understood the wows. What are they ? It`s not a haha, is not a like, is not love. I consider them something shallow . I don`t get wows. Why i`m saying this story ? Because this play good with my confirmation bias that wow is superficial,shallow. Thanks Ray Alez for the confirmation biased article. And now with the data that google had offered, it helps me demonstrate this theory. Look at the image and then scroll down for a pseudo-explanation. The majority of the posts that got a big percentage of the total number of reactions the reaction wow, had a magnitude of the text lower then 1. So we have 2 possible explanations. One, the posts that get a big percent of overall reactions as wow does not convey any emotional content. (shallow) Second, the posts that get a big percent of overall reactions as wow are short in text. The magnitude of a document’s sentiment indicates how much emotional content is present within the document, and this value is often proportional to the length of the document. A document with a neutral score (around 0.0 ) may indicate a low-emotion document, or may indicate mixed emotions, with both high positive and negative values which cancel each out. This is a list with posts that got at least 1000 reactions, and between 75 and 100% of that reactions were WOW A distribution per FB page looks like this. I love it. Using WOW`s seems like a good way to find “sensational posts” posted by the alternative media or other categories. What makes a user sad ? Facebook Sad Reactions. The top pages that generate the sad emotion are : Posts were 80 to 100% of the emotions are sad. - The Detroit Free Press lost a beloved family member today. Former restaurant critic Sylvia Rector died at the age of 66. She will be missed, and remembered fondly. - Alan Thicke, best known as the dad on ‘Growing Pains’, dies at 69 after reportedly suffering fatal heart attack. - A tribute to the iconic world leaders, athletes and celebrities who passed away in 2016. Almost all articles are related with the death of somebody. The interesting thing here is combining the information's from the percent of people that are sad to the percent of people that are angry. If somebody dies because he was old, people will give a sad reaction, leave a comment and they are done.\", \"sampled\": \"Is automation eating every industry? If so, what skills will be needed for the future of work? The short answer is yes, and the necessary skills are changing. If you asked farmers a few hundred years ago what their children should learn, they would have had a specific answer: how to milk a cow or properly plant a field. For most of human history, the skills for a profession barely changed from one generation to the next. This stability created a predictable path for learning and working. In the last few centuries, however, that historical pattern has shifted dramatically. We now see jobs disappear within a single generation due to rapid technological advancements. Machines have automated much of manufacturing, and they are expected to take on even more roles soon. This trend isn't limited to one sector; it's spreading across industries and altering the nature of work itself. As manufacturing jobs decline, they are replaced by once-unimaginable professions like bloggers and social media managers. This evolution shows how dynamic the modern labor market has become. In a world with accelerating labor cycles, the question is what skills people actually need to succeed. More research shows that current curriculums, which often teach siloed subjects, are becoming insufficient. Singularity University recently explored the \\\"Future Of Work\\\" and the \\\"Future Of Learning\\\" to address these questions. The discussions highlighted a consensus on the need for educational reform to prepare students for jobs that don't yet exist. Cathy Davidson, author of \\\"Now You See It,\\\" has been a prominent voice in this conversation, advocating for new educational models. Her work, along with that of Singularity Hub Editor in Chief David Hill, emphasizes adapting learning to a rapidly changing world. Some regions are already taking inspiration from innovative educational systems like Finland's, which has moved toward more collaborative and project-based learning. This approach aims to cultivate creativity and critical thinking rather than rote memorization of facts like the number 22173. In Buenos Aires, local officials are also pioneering changes to align their education system with 21st-century demands. The city's efforts reflect a global trend of rethinking how we prepare the next generation for an automated future. Esteban Bullrich, the Minister of Education for Buenos Aires, and Mariano Mayer have been instrumental in driving these reforms. Their work focuses on integrating technology and fostering skills like \\\"Entrepreneurship\\\" to empower students in a new economic environment. A 03/29/2017 report from the World Economic Forum and Boston Consulting supports this shift, noting the importance of adaptable skills over specialized knowledge. The data, including figures like 01, 49, and 39.584000, points toward a complex but clear direction for educational and professional development. Adapting to this new paradigm is essential for individuals and societies to thrive in the coming decades.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"For some, the concept of memoization may be new. For others, this may be a long-time familiar tool used to craft efficient components when building a web application. In my experience, I have learned there are certain situations when it is appropriate to memoize as well as situations when it is not. I’ll walk through what memoization is, how to use it, and when you should use it in your code. Memoization. What is it? In the simplest form, think of memoization as a cache. It is a programming concept that allows developers to save the results of some function in such a way that additional calls with the same parameters do not warrant the need for unnecessary processing. Typically, this is useful for pure functions which based off certain inputs (we’ll call these parameters), will produce the same output. As you can imagine, this concept is clearly a key tool when developing code that calls the same function multiple times to produce some calculated result. For example, think of building a chart component in React using TypeScript. As new data is provided to the chart component, logic must exist to calculate the coordinates to draw the chart. By using memoization, the developer has the ability to cache the calculated results of determining the chart coordinates for future use. This saves the need to unnecessarily recalculate the coordinates given the same data set, thereby producing a more efficient application. When should I memoize? It can be tempting for a developer to want to memoize frequently. After all, if I process once and save the results, wouldn’t this be faster for future results? Well, not necessarily. It’s important to consider the overhead cost involved with memoization. For example, if we are writing a function with parameters expected to change frequently, there is a cost incurred with saving the results in a cache on the client. In typical use cases, the likelihood of having to call the function again with the same parameters would likely be rare. In these cases, the benefits for memoization is minimal. On the flip side of the coin, there are many cases when it is absolutely beneficial to memoize functions. There are a few things to consider when evaluating whether to memoize. For example, consider how complex the logic is within a function. From a system resource perspective, how expensive is it to execute the logic within a function once? Many times? If the complexity is relatively trivial, the overhead involved in memoizing a function may not be worth it.\", \"sampled\": \"The benefit of being first to market with a new product is called first mover advantage. First movers face significant challenges, including creating market awareness, acquiring customers, and encouraging product adoption. They bear the sole responsibility of educating the entire market and making their customers' latent needs clear. These pioneering firms must also handle the difficult tasks of customer acquisition and promoting their new product. A second mover follows the pioneer, often as a copy business. Second mover advantage is the ability to capture the majority of a market after the first mover has already created it. A firm that follows a pioneer can gain significant market share by exploiting the first mover's disadvantages, learning from their mistakes, and replicating strategies that proved successful. They can leverage the market awareness already created by the first mover to present themselves as a viable alternative, allowing them to enter a primed and educated market while saving considerable resources. IBM serves as a classic example of a first mover in the computer industry. The company successfully created the initial market, built widespread awareness, and established a customer base for computers. This pioneering effort laid the necessary groundwork for the personal computer market to emerge and flourish. IBM's work educated the public and created the demand that others would later fulfill. Apple is a well-known example of a second mover succeeding in a market. The company entered the personal computer market after IBM and captured a significant share. Apple did not have to create the market from scratch; it was ready. Instead, Apple could focus its resources on differentiation, presenting itself as a clear and compelling alternative. This strategy proved to be highly effective. Apple directly targeted IBM, which had established the initial market for computers. The company entered the personal computer market after IBM had already done the hard work of creating market awareness and building a customer base. Apple then captured a significant share of this pre-existing market by learning from IBM’s efforts and identifying the first mover's disadvantages. By leveraging the awareness IBM had built, Apple presented itself as a better choice and competed effectively without bearing the initial costs of market creation. To achieve this, Apple used a direct and memorable marketing strategy. The company famously targeted IBM with its \\\"Big Brother\\\" ad campaign, broadcast during the Super Bowl. The campaign was a direct assault on IBM's established position, positioning Apple as a revolutionary and creative alternative. This bold move helped capture a significant market share and is a powerful demonstration of second mover advantage.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"It’s estimated that about 140 billion cubic meters of gas are flared annually across the oil-producing countries of the world (McGreevey & Whitaker, 2020). Gas flaring has been illegal in Nigeria since 1984, yet the country still ranks among the top 10 gas-flare countries with about 7.4 billion cubic meters of gas flared in 2018; and about 425.9 billion standard cubic feet of gas flared in 2019 (Eboh, 2019). Gas flaring is associated with lots of dangers. This essay aims to explain these dangers in details, and also to provide an insight into the efforts made by stakeholders to end gas flaring, identify the missing links between current strategies, and recommend viable solutions to gas flaring in Nigeria. WHAT IS GAS FLARING? Gas flaring is the controlled combustion of associated gas generated during various processes including oil and gas recovery, petrochemical process, and landfill gas extraction (Generon.com, 2019). GAS FLARING IN NIGERIA: A TALE OF NIGHTMARES The health risks associated with gas flaring are glaring. In the oil-rich Niger Delta, 2 million people live within 4 kilometres (2.5 miles) of gas flare (Schick et al, 2018), which makes them more vulnerable to several health issues including cancer and lung damage, as well as deformities in children, asthma, bronchitis, pneumonia, neurological and reproductive problems (CSL Stockbrokers, 2020). Relatively, agricultural productivity (in the oil-producing areas) has been severely hampered by gas flaring. The combustion process raises the soil temperature, with a decline in crop yield and acid rains as its two major ripple effects. The smokes which emanate from the flares also lead to black rainfall and water bodies which affect aquatic and Wildlife. The economic costs of gas flaring are mind-boggling. Data obtained from the Nigerian Gas Flare Tracker showed that 25.9 billion Standard Cubic Feet of gas, valued at N460.5billion, were flared between January and November 2019. As shown in the figure below, this amount would comfortably finance the capital expenditure of Ministries of Education, Power, Defence and Transport which stood at a combined total of N450 billion. Also, the volume of gas flared is capable of generating 42,600 megawatts of electricity which would have helped solve the electricity problem of the country. Climate change is another danger of gas flaring. In 2019, as much as 22.6 million tonnes of carbon dioxide were emitted into the environment as a result of gas flaring in Nigeria. In fact, the environmental costs of gas flaring in Nigeria amount to N28.8 billion annually (PWC, 2019). There is also the issue of air and noise pollution and a rise in temperature in the oil-producing areas. Issues related to gas flaring also leads to protests and attacks which worsen the fragile security situation of the oil-producing areas. Meanwhile, several efforts have been aimed at checking gas flaring in Nigeria. Some of these efforts include the Associated Gas Re-injection Act (1979), the Nigeria Gas Master plan (2008), and the Nigeria Gas Flare Commercialization Programme (2017). Nigeria is also part of the Global Gas Flare Reduction Partnership of the World Bank. However, all these efforts couldn’t help the Federal Government of Nigeria achieve its dream of zero gas flaring by 2020. This is a result of several combined factors: the “tax-deductible” charges imposed on gas flaring are too cheap for oil companies; insufficient infrastructure to process and transport gas to end-users; institutional challenges in the gas market which make the cost of processing and transporting gas more expensive than the income from its sale; significant amount of gas in oil-fields which makes greater re-injection impossible; and the country’s heavy dependence on crude oil for revenue.\", \"sampled\": \"I work in the activities department at a nursing home in New York, where my job is to bring joy and engagement to our residents. When the first coronavirus case was reported in the state, everything changed. Our facility went into an immediate and complete lockdown. State officials banned all visitors, meaning only essential staff and medical personnel could enter the building. While necessary, this policy sealed our community off from the outside world, isolating residents from their loved ones. The lockdown has deeply affected our 144 residents, creating an atmosphere of loneliness and boredom. All the group activities that structured their days—watching movies, enjoying live music, visits from volunteers—have been canceled. One resident, used to seeing his son, asked me, \\\"Are we going to have a movie this afternoon? Something, anything?\\\" The absence of these simple pleasures and family connections has left a noticeable void. One of the most heart-wrenching cases is a resident with dementia who misses her daughter, who used to visit twice every day. Now, their only connection is through video calls, a confusing substitute for her daughter's physical presence. To enforce social distancing, the nursing home’s three sections have been completely separated, preventing residents from mingling. The measure is meant to contain any potential spread, but it further fragments the small community within our walls. In the dining hall, tables are separated to keep everyone at least six feet apart during meals. What was once a social time is now a quiet, distanced affair, another reminder of this isolating new reality. The constant pressure is also taking a toll on my own mental health. My daily routine is a draining cycle of enforcing rules that create sadness while trying to project a sense of calm. My coworkers and I are spread incredibly thin, and because the facility's sections are now isolated, our own support system has fractured. We barely see each other for more than ten minutes at a time, losing the camaraderie that helped us through difficult days. I’m a healthy 25-year-old, but I’m aware of the risk I represent every time I walk through the doors. The fear that I could unknowingly carry the virus to the vulnerable people I’m here to protect is a constant anxiety. The news from Washington, where 35 coronavirus deaths were linked to a single nursing home, looms large in my mind. That tragedy is a terrifying reminder of how devastatingly this virus can move through a facility like ours. My daily trips to Dunkin' for coffee, once a simple routine, now feel like a significant risk. Every interaction outside the facility is a potential exposure that could have catastrophic consequences for the residents who depend on us. The weight of this is immense; the health and safety of 144 people are partly in my hands. Being both a caregiver and a potential threat is a heavy burden to carry.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"There is a general consensus that when we talk about open data we are referring to any piece of data or content that is free to access, use, reuse, and redistribute. Due to the way most governments have rolled out their open data portals, however, it would be easy to assume that the data available on these sites is the only data that’s available for public consumption. This isn’t true. Although data sets that receive a governmental stamp of openness receive a lot more publicity, they actually only represent a fraction of the public data that exists on the web. So what’s the difference between “public” data and “open” data? What is open data? Generally speaking, “ open data “ is the information that has been published on government-sanctioned portals. In the best case, this data is structured, machine-readable, open-licensed, and well maintained. What is public data? Public data is the data that exists everywhere else. This is information that’s freely available (but not really accessible) on the web. It is frequently unstructured and unruly, and its usage requirements are often vague. “Only 10% of government data is published as open data” What does this mean? Well, for starters, it means that there’s a discrepancy between the open data that exists in government portals and public data in general. This is an important distinction to make because while there’s a lot of excitement surrounding open data initiatives and their potential to transform modern society, the data that this premise rests on — open data — is only a fraction of what’s needed in order for this potential to be realized. The fact is this: the majority of useful government data is either still proprietary or stowed away in a filing cabinet somewhere, and the stuff that is available is being released haphazardly. Does it matter? Does it actually matter that there’s a distinction between these two kinds of data? Well… yes. Open data, because it represents such a small portion of what’s available, hasn’t lived up to its potential.\", \"sampled\": \"Preparing for the AWS Developer Associate certification in 2021 demands commitment and practice. With a wealth of over 1600 practice questions and dumps at their disposal, candidates can refine their skills effectively. These practice questions and mock tests are instrumental in clearing AWS certifications by acquainting test-takers with the exam structure and question types they will face. To pass the AWS Developer Associate certification exam, a score of 76% is necessary, underscoring the importance of aiming for a high mark to secure the certification. Stephane Maarek's DVA-C01 exam practice test comprises 325 quiz questions spread over 4 practice papers, each containing 65 questions. This methodical approach facilitates thorough preparation. Trusted by over 24,000 AWS certification aspirants, Stephane Maarek's practice test is a popular option for those gearing up for the AWS Developer Associate exam. Maarek's remarkable score of approximately 980/1000 on the AWS SysOps exam demonstrates his expertise and the caliber of his educational materials. Integrating practice tests with courses can significantly improve your chances of passing the AWS Developer Associate certification, offering a comprehensive preparation strategy. Mock tests and practice questions not only evaluate your knowledge but also aid in honing the speed and accuracy required to excel in the certification exam. To achieve certification success, it is crucial to leverage a range of study materials and resources to establish a solid knowledge base and skill set. As the certification landscape evolves, staying abreast of the latest exam patterns and content is vital to ensure readiness on exam day. Success in certification exams often hinges on consistent practice, strategic study plans, and utilizing reputable study materials such as Stephane Maarek's resources. If you are aiming to ace the AWS Developer Associate certification in 2021 and seeking top-notch resources, you are in the right place. Remember, scoring 76% is essential to pass this AWS certification, and meticulous preparation and attention to detail are key, as many candidates have narrowly missed passing due to one or two errors.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Where to submit Medium has hundreds of publications you can submit your work to from large Medium owned magazines such as Forge, GEN, and OneZero, to small publications created by individual writers. Anyone can create a publication. Here’s a list of all the current top Medium Publications. Initially, you might want to reach out to a small to medium-sized one. Here are some publications to try: 1. PS I Love You This publication has over 200K subscribers. It’s all about love, but not just romantic love — they cover a wide variety of topics: family, parenting, self love, friendship, dating, marriage, divorce… They also run Fiction Friday for short stories and Poetry Sunday. It’s a supportive community with a Facebook page and a large group of engaged writers and readers. They also, at the time of writing, have a contract with Medium for curation — so if you get published by them you get curated immediately. On their guidelines page they now have this request: Moving forward, due to overwhelming demand, P.S. I Love You will be reviewing new writer applications for only the first week of each month — the first through the 7th. Please only reach out during that window each month. 2. The Writing Cooperative This publication also has a large following. It has a very positive engaged Facebook group for writers. To get a story accepted you need to really check that you’ve met their requirements as they have a very high standard and specific requirements. Even an incorrectly formatted heading will have your story rejected for violating the rules. 3. The StartUp The StartUp has the largest following of all publications on Medium (as of writing this) and accepts a wide range of stories. You will get a “yes” or nothing from the StartUp: they say in their guidelines that if you don’t hear back in 36 hours, assume your story has not been accepted. 4. Slackjaw Humor writers should aim for this one!\", \"sampled\": \"In the realm of AI optimization for driving data, a new object detector architecture has surpassed its predecessor by achieving an impressive over 40% mAP improvement while operating nearly twice as fast. This advancement not only reduced device inference latency by 43.4% but also boosted mAP by a substantial 42.7%, marking a significant leap in performance metrics. By adapting MobileNetV3 for object detection, a remarkable 15% speedup was achieved without compromising accuracy, demonstrating the potential for enhanced efficiency in AI models. Strategic reduction of late-stage layers by factors ranging from 4 to 32 resulted in a commendable 22% latency improvement along with an 11% mAP boost, highlighting the value of thoughtful model architecture adjustments. The decision to eliminate the random flip augmentor notably enhanced mAP accuracy by 4.5%, emphasizing the importance of fine-tuning data augmentation techniques for optimal results. Implementing a constrained crop augmentor strategy led to a substantial 4.4% improvement in accuracy, reinforcing the impact of meticulous augmentation methodologies on model performance. Through a refined augmentation scheme, a remarkable 9.1% relative enhancement over the initial baseline was achieved, underscoring the effectiveness of methodical optimization in advancing AI. The use of square anchor boxes and optimized box sizes based on object distribution not only sped up processes by 20% but also contributed to a 2% mAP improvement, highlighting the benefits of tailored model enhancements. In the pursuit of AI excellence, innovation thrives, with technologies like MnasNet and MobileNetV3 pushing boundaries in data science, artificial intelligence, and machine learning. Companies like Nauto and DeepScale are leading the deployment of cutting-edge AI solutions, focusing on enhancing systems like the Forward Collision Warning (FCW) mechanism to improve safety measures in driving environments. With the US as a prominent target for AI-driven technologies, the evolution of models like SSD and the continuous refinement of object detection frameworks such as COCO and ImageNet are shaping a future where AI optimization plays a pivotal role in driving data innovation.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Just as in the previous posts, I’ll leave the rigorous maths for the companion notebook, and stick to explaining the general idea. Just like Self-Organising Feature Maps, GNGs are iterative algorithms. However, unlike SOFMs, they do not require any initial specification of the number of neurons — as the name suggests, GNGs are growing, and new neurons keep getting added as long as the algorithm is running. Each iteration begins by picking a data point from the training set. Because GNGs generalise very well to an arbitrary number of dimensions, it is common to speak of these in terms of 𝛿-length vectors v. The neuron nearest to v, called the best-performing unit (BPU) in analogy to SOFMs, is moved closer to v. All neurons directly connected to the BPU are also moved closer to v. Determine the second-best performing unit (SBPU). If the BPU and SBPU are connected, set the age of this connection to zero.\", \"sampled\": \"Experiencing trauma is a deeply personal journey, with each person reacting uniquely to distressing situations. Symptoms of PTSD and C-PTSD, such as flashbacks and hypervigilance, present differently in each individual. Survivors of trauma often grapple with enduring patterns of abuse as they navigate relationships post-experience. The COVID-19 pandemic has the potential to trigger widespread trauma, possibly leading to a rise in PTSD cases. Interestingly, some individuals with PTSD and C-PTSD have found moments of peace amidst the chaos of the pandemic. Those who have faced trauma bring valuable insights and skills that can benefit others who have not encountered similar challenges. Emotional trauma can leave lasting physical imprints, underscoring the importance of recognizing and addressing these bodily manifestations for healing. Cultivating body awareness and understanding physical sensations can significantly aid in the journey of recovering from trauma. Acknowledging the existence of trauma and its physical effects is a vital initial step towards healing and progress.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Patterns are based on principles, so learn the principles and you’ll write better code Modularity Reusability Easy to read Easy to modify. These four principles should always be applied when you write code. These principles can be broken down into others. But at the end of the day, it’s these four core principles that matter. Focus on those principles and you’ll write great code. When you first start an application, it’s difficult to know where that application will be in one year. Applications evolve. This makes it difficult at the outset to know what pattern is going to fit your scenario. So how do you counteract this? If you understand the principles behind the patterns, then you can implement those principles from the start. Making sure that you maintain modularity. Writing things to be reusable when you have the opportunity. Properly documenting your code. All these things should always be applied. And they can always be applied, no matter what the scenario. So don’t overly concern yourself over what pattern to use. Concern yourself over how to apply those four principles.\", \"sampled\": \"In January 31, the AI machine Libratus from Carnegie Mellon demonstrated its superiority over human champions in Heads Up, No Limit Texas Hold ’em, achieving a remarkable 99.7% win rate. Libratus effectively analyzed and outperformed human strategies using its advanced algorithms. By applying general algorithms rather than poker-specific ones, Libratus quickly established control over the game, asserting its dominance early on. Through continuous refinement of its strategies based on game dynamics and player decisions, Libratus successfully outplayed top professional players. This victory not only emphasizes the potential of AI in decision-making amid uncertainty but also showcases its exceptional ability to excel in strategic settings with incomplete information. Texas Hold ’em, known for its hidden cards and imperfect information, extends beyond gambling to fields like negotiation and cybersecurity, highlighting its relevance in diverse scenarios. Libratus's triumph underscores the versatility of game theory outside traditional gaming contexts, signaling a broader application of AI capabilities. The AI's success in the realm of poker signifies a promising future for artificial intelligence, demonstrating its proficiency in tasks that go beyond predefined activities.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Start with values, not your idea “Values are timeless. They are the bedrock of a company and don’t change even when everything else does.” I wish more startups realised this. Too many are: hiring the wrong people building the wrong products and looking for customers in the wrong places “A company needn’t model its behaviour on what other companies do, because it doesn’t have to attract the same customers or value the same things. It isn’t as simple as declaring an enemy and beating them…… We don’t particularly care what others choose to do, because we know the game we are playing — and we quite enjoy it.” From the Delighted blog A revolution is happening Umair Haque, the renowned writer and economist recently stated that “we’re on the cusp of a values-driven revolution.” He highlighted that consumers now are much more careful about who they buy from and whether they represent the values they hold dear. Therefore it’s important to pin down your values and what you stand for. Think of your core values as those that, when the chips are down, you believe in so much, that if you took them away your company would cease to exist.\", \"sampled\": \"Thirteen individuals participating in Moderna's 30,000-person trial passed away by December 3, with none of the deaths linked to the vaccine. Over 307,000 Americans have definitively lost their lives due to Covid-19. Moderna's data detailed severe adverse events such as intractable nausea, facial swelling, and lip swelling. Less severe side effects of the vaccine included pain at the injection site, fatigue, headaches, as well as muscle and joint pain. Among the vaccine recipients, three individuals developed Bell's palsy, a condition causing temporary paralysis of half the facial muscles. Those who received the vaccine experienced higher occurrences of lymphadenopathy and hypersensitivity events like rashes and hives. One person in the vaccine group tragically died by suicide 21 days after the initial dose. Moderna's data indicated that some deaths occurred within the general population across specific age groups. The FDA suggests continued monitoring of Bell's palsy if the vaccine is granted Emergency Use Authorization (EUA). During a Q&A session following the safety review, Paul Offit expressed uncertainty about the comfort level regarding the background rate of Bell's palsy. Responding to concerns regarding Bell's palsy rates in trials, Rachel Zhang stated, \\\"This is something we're investigating and contemplating.\\\" The known annual baseline of Bell's palsy cases in the United States stands at 40,000.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"“The word “restore” is defined as the act of returning something back to its original state, but it’s also the act of returning it back to its original owners. The restoration of nature is for the local biodiversity and the communities that depend on it. And as that network grows, the collective action benefits everyone. And these benefits go far beyond the threat of climate change. Even if climate change stopped right now, the protection and rebuilding of earth’s biodiversity would still be a top priority because it underpins all life on earth. It can help us with all other global threats, including extreme weather events, droughts, food shortages and global pandemics.” — Thomas Crowther Thomas Crowther is the Lead Scientific Advisor for the UN Billion Tree Campaign. He is a prolific researcher and ecologist leading a number of initiatives related to biodiversity, forestry, and soil research. His recent TED Talk highlights his work beautifully and lays the groundwork for local and global conversations about restoring nature’s biodiversity. “The problem is clear. We know we need to reduce our emissions and draw the existing carbon out of the atmosphere, stop the damage and start the repair. And this is where forests can help. Like all plants, trees capture carbon from the atmosphere, and they use it for growth.\", \"sampled\": \"Rivers have always been seen as profound spiritual guides, imparting wisdom that transcends the physical realm. Norman Maclean beautifully expressed this idea, highlighting the unity and interconnectedness found in nature with his words about rivers. The River Clyde, however, was drawn into a tragic tale involving a child, showcasing how rivers can be sources of both comfort and grief. These waterways, like the Clyde, symbolize unity and the timeless essence of the soul, serving as poignant symbols for the cyclical nature of life and the connections between all living things. In literature such as Siddhartha, rivers play pivotal roles, guiding characters on paths of self-discovery and spiritual awakening, mirroring the transformative qualities attributed to water in various faith traditions. Beyond their symbolic significance, rivers embody the fusion of contrasting elements within individuals, reflecting the peaceful coexistence of opposites within the human mind. Reflecting the theme of interconnectedness, rivers are often viewed as eternal entities, transcending human concepts of time and space. They are revered as both creators and destroyers, embodying the dualities of existence and the perpetual cycle of life, death, and renewal that defines the natural world. Simon Heathcote eloquently ponders this concept, suggesting that rivers hold a timeless wisdom that reveals the illusion of time itself.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Photo by Volodymyr Hryshchenko on Unsplash Introduction Hey all, this project will be a series. I don’t know how long the series will be as I’m still working on the project as I write these articles. I’ve wanted to build a chat app for quite some time. I came across an older tutorial (3 years ago) of Ben Awad (awesome YouTuber) doing a slack clone, which was perfect for me, so I’m following his approaches and making mine a updated version (a lot has changed in 3 years). I wanted to practice building more complex projects. I’m learning a lot so far, like working with the PostgresSQL database, using Sequelize for the ORM, and connecting it with Graphql. So I’m hoping you guys can learn something too :) But that’s enough of the intro, let’s dive into the first part. Installation for Database Before we get to the good stuff, we need to install the things we need for this project. I’ll be using a Mac throughout this series. Nodejs of course :) (if you haven’t already => https://nodejs.org/en/download/) PostgresSQL (for Windows and Mac https://www.postgresql.\", \"sampled\": \"Hyperparameters are fixed model configurations crucial for defining and maintaining consistency during training. In K-Means clustering, key hyperparameters encompass the initial cluster values, distance metrics, and the cluster count (k). Determining the optimal number of clusters is pivotal in K-Means clustering, with methods like the Elbow Method and the Silhouette Method aiding in this process. The Elbow Method identifies the ideal k value where the average distance sharply decreases, while the Silhouette Method offers a robust alternative, particularly beneficial for complex, high-dimensional datasets lacking clear visualization. When applying K-Means clustering, factors like initial cluster values, distance metrics, and the cluster count must be carefully considered. In datasets like the MNIST digit classification dataset, the choice of k significantly impacts clustering accuracy. For instance, in the Elbow Method analysis, values such as 2, 3, and 4 are critical as they signify points where a notable drop in average distance occurs. In the case of the MNIST dataset, after scrutinizing Scatter plots for k=2, 3, and 4, a value of k=4 emerged as the most suitable option. Understanding hyperparameters and identifying the optimal cluster numbers are foundational for enhancing the efficacy of clustering algorithms in various machine learning applications.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Meetings take a big chunk of our time. Yet, unlike with personal productivity, little is done to optimise them fully. Why? For two reasons: We often are not in control of setting the meetings. Even if we are in control, no one taught us how to run valuable and energising meetings. According to Forbes, about 55 million meetings happen each day in the United States alone. And due to poor planning and poor execution, most of our get together feel like a waste of time: pointless distractions from the real work. With so much of our time sucked into daily meetings, not taking care of optimising them lead to the biggest productivity loss in our organisation. That’s what Mamie Kanfer Stewart, author of Momentum: Creating Effective, Engaging and Enjoyable Meetings, seeks to address in her book. According to her, great meetings have three stages that are well defined: before, during, and after the meeting. If you are a manager, an executive, or a worker who seeks to promote a better meetings culture, this three-stage process can help you fix poor meeting habits and get the most out of your teamwork.\", \"sampled\": \"Clubhouse, launched in 2020, redefines social networking through its audio-based platform, enabling users to participate in voice chat rooms for real-time conversations. The platform's exclusivity to iOS and AirPods users adds to its allure, attracting tech influencers and startup leaders. Rooms dedicated to individual influencers create a unique and spontaneous interaction style. Clubhouse offers a more relaxed and adaptable communication setting compared to video calls, reducing friction in interactions. Its black and white aesthetic establishes a distinctive atmosphere, setting it apart from conventional social platforms by prioritizing real-time engagement over static profiles. This approach introduces a novel way for users to engage in dynamic conversations, offering a refreshing alternative in a world saturated with video calls in 2020. The platform's popularity is evident through influencers boasting thousands of Twitter followers, showcasing its broad reach and influence. Clubhouse's emphasis on mobility and spontaneity reflects its dedication to delivering a dynamic and immersive user experience, especially noteworthy in a year marked by negative phone interactions, highlighting the platform's commitment to fostering positive, real-time discussions.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Start with the objective. There’s the virus, with all the havoc it’s wreaked — the poorest students falling further behind (no internet at home), the badly needed therapy appointments cancelled or unattended, passersby spitting in the direction of folks with Asian descent. Then there’s the subjective. Your favorite Indian restaurant closing, indefinitely. Not getting to hug your friend goodbye. Breaking up, but tenderly, each of you cupping the other’s face the way you’d hold a robin’s egg. The grocery store being out of milk. Your world is small these days and it’s hard to triage these things. But since we’re all public health workers now, it’s become your job to triage, so you will. You will be a fish in a Venetian canal, where the waters (you may have heard) are now clear.\", \"sampled\": \"In the fast-paced realm of software development, stress often lingers. Nick Bull proposes a helpful stress management method called the Morning Journal. This approach, proven effective, aids individuals in efficiently reducing stress levels. The Morning Journal involves two core activities: a brain dump and setting a daily goal. This structured practice assists in clearing the mind and establishing a focused path for the day, enhancing productivity. To ease into this routine, Bull advises starting with 'brain dump' journaling three times weekly. This gradual introduction allows individuals to become accustomed to the process and eventually incorporate it into their daily schedule. Consistency plays a vital role in fully experiencing the benefits of this stress management technique. A significant advantage of the Morning Journal method is its effectiveness in combating procrastination. By jotting down thoughts and emotions, individuals can clarify their priorities and approach tasks with greater efficiency.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I’ve wasted a lot of time waiting until I was thin to go after the things I wanted. I didn’t have the self-confidence to put myself out there because I was fat. Was it an excuse? In some ways, yes, but I did experience roadblocks because of my weight. I’m getting older, and I no longer have the luxury to wait until I’m a perfect weight to go after my goals. We have to love ourselves all the time, which what acceptance is all about. If you’re fat, then fat-acceptance is self-acceptance, the same way every other kind of acceptance is. Fat-acceptance doesn’t mean not growing, improving, or challenging one’s self. Acceptance gives you a foundation that allows you to move past your emotional obstacles with less fear. Think of it this way, if you’re cutting an apple on an unstable cutting-board, you run the risk of hurting yourself with the knife. We need a stable starting point to take chances, put ourselves out there, and take actionable steps. How can you change if you loathe who you are at the start or seek help if you don’t feel you are worth it? Without acceptance, there’s nothing to keep you going. The opposite of fat-acceptance is internalizing cruelty or mistreatment because you feel it’s justified. You can’t defend yourself from fat-shaming, discrimination, and abuse if you’re convinced you deserve it because of your body-size. Being fat isn’t a crime — though I’m sure some see it that way.\", \"sampled\": \"NASA recently confirmed the presence of water on the southern hemisphere of the moon, a significant discovery that challenges previous notions about the lunar surface. The Clavius crater on the moon had only shown signs of hydrogen before. However, the SOFIA observatory detected 100 to 412 parts per million of molecular water in this crater. This discovery opens up new possibilities for understanding the moon's composition and history, suggesting that water can endure in the harsh lunar environment. Scientists theorize that micrometeorites impacting the moon may have delivered water to its surface, resulting in the current presence of water there. This breakthrough will offer valuable insights into how water forms, stays, and moves on the moon, marking a crucial step in unraveling lunar water mysteries. The existence of water on the moon holds significant implications for future space exploration, especially in planning human missions to the lunar surface. NASA aims to land the first woman and the next man on the moon by 2024, and this discovery is likely to influence the strategies and technologies for this upcoming mission. In essence, finding water on the moon represents a major advancement in our understanding of celestial bodies in our solar system, paving the way for further research and exploration in astronomy and planetary science.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Perhaps the most important aspect of online marketing today is conversion. Getting a visitor to convert to your brand is just as important as earning new clients, as it seems everyone already has a preference and align themselves with another company. It’s always a tight race and competition is fierce. This means a good website — one that is eye-catching and piques the interest of a visitor — plays a pivotal role in business success. Want more branding & design tips in your day? Subscribe to Lucidpress, right here on Medium. In a study conducted at the Missouri University of Science and Technology, it was observed that users only take two-tenths of a second to form an opinion about the websites they visit, and the chance they will click away decreases immensely after the first 30 seconds. If you want to attract and keep visitors on your website longer, here are 6 design elements that will encourage visitors to convert to your brand. 1. Keep it fresh A fresh new layout is bound to capture a visitor’s attention, whether she is new to the site or has been a regular. Take CloudSponge as an example. They had an outdated and old-fashioned website, but once they upgraded to a newer, more up-to-date version, their conversion rate increased by 33%. Basically, the more frequently you change the style or layout of your website, the more opportunities your conversion rate has to grow. It’s like buying fruit — the fresher it is, the more people want to buy it. 2. Have catchy headlines & calls-to-action The first things that attract a visitor’s attention when she lands on your home page are the headline and call-to-action. Not the contact info, articles or product specs, but these two elements. For this reason, the more action-oriented your headline and CTA are, the higher your chances of success rise. CTAs are designed to incite an immediate response from a customer. That’s why clear, concise CTAs are more effective. One software company reported that their site’s conversion rate increased by 106% after it got a makeover that included a clear, direct call-to-action. 3. Make it visually appealing Visual appeal doesn’t mean over-crowded or complicated pages. Increase conversion rates by keeping your landing pages simple and appealing. Your page should show the customer what your company does without making them read a lot of content. One way to achieve this easily is by adding videos or image sliders. This also has the benefit of prolonging the time a visitor spends on your site. Device Magic, a mobile software company, conducted an A/B test using VWO to determine whether an image slider would improve conversion rates for their website. The results indicated that the image slider increased completed sign-ups by 31%. 4.\", \"sampled\": \"Clara Tsang, a software engineer, is making waves as a front end developer at Procurify, a Vancouver-based startup specializing in spend-management software. Procurify's progressive policies, including responsible time-off, flexible hours, and a remote-forward approach, have drawn in numerous talented individuals like Clara. Before joining Procurify, Clara was part of the Commit team, a well-known tech company she joined in 2019. This role was a pivotal point in Clara's career, as Commit's focus on skill development allowed her to enhance her front end capabilities. Before Commit, Clara worked for a digital marketing firm serving the automotive industry. This role provided her with a solid understanding of digital marketing, a field that increasingly overlaps with software development. It was here that she gained experience with WordPress, a tool frequently used in digital marketing. Clara's entry into the tech industry, however, started with her education. She graduated from SFU’s Systems Engineering program, which provided her with a strong foundation in systems engineering and set her up for success in her subsequent roles. Clara's move from Commit to Procurify was aided by Cong Ly, an Onboarding Partner at Commit. Cong offered crucial insights and support, ensuring a seamless transition for Clara into her new role. Clara's primary goal when joining Commit was to hone her front end skills, a facet of software engineering she's always been passionate about. This goal remains a driving force in her work at Procurify. In her current role at Procurify, Clara has transitioned from using WordPress to React, a more advanced tool for front end development. This change signifies her ongoing professional growth and her adaptability to the evolving landscape of software development tools. Clara's journey, from her education at SFU to her current role at Procurify, exemplifies her adaptability and dedication to continuous learning. It highlights the importance of setting goals and the value of companies like Commit that prioritize skill development. Navigating the tech industry hasn't been without its challenges for Clara. She admits, \\\"It’s really easy to get lost navigating the world of nasty technical interviews and multiple-level screening that’s the reality in the tech industry today.\\\" However, she found Commit's process to be a \\\"better route.\\\" Clara Tsang's story is a tale of determination, growth, and success. It serves as an inspiration for others in the tech industry, showcasing the value of continuous learning, adaptability, and having the right support systems. As her journey continues to unfold, it's evident that Clara will keep making significant strides in the world of software engineering.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"SOURCE: Google Here’s a new wrinkle to Google search for local businesses. Advertisers on Google can now display a green checkmark, which Google will tag businesses with either “Google Screened” or “Google Guaranteed.” It’s only available right now for certain categories of business, such as law, financial planning, and real estate for the Screened badge and service-oriented companies for the Google Guaranteed mark. To get the checkmark, businesses will have to go through background checks, provide proof of insurance and licenses, and other paperwork. To use the mark, you have to pay for the ads and be part of the search engine’s Local Service Ads program. “…the real value of the badge is the access it provides to Local Services Ads (LSA). This is Google’s local trust pack. It is a cost-per-call advertising inventory unit that acts unlike anything we have ever encountered as marketers.” SEARCH ENGINE LAND For consumers, booking through the Google Guaranteed ad includes an offer to refund up to $2,000 if you aren’t satisfied with the service you’ve received. SOURCE: Google Here’s how Google describes how the service works: Google Screened On Local Service listings, you will see the Google Screened icon next to these businesses. How it works All firms that have the Google Screened badge must pass a business-level background and a business-owner background check. Additionally, each professional in the business must pass a license check, and in some categories, a background check. See Requirements by category for details. These checks ensure consumers that the professionals they work with have been thoroughly vetted and provide them added peace of mind as they work with you. Who it covers Only firms that provide professional services including Law, Financial Planning, and Real Estate are eligible for the Google Screened badge. MORE INFO FROM GOOGLE The Google Guarantee The Google Guarantee badge is available for businesses that pass a Google screening and qualification process through Google Local Services. If you’re backed by the Google Guarantee, and your customers (that came to your business through Google) aren’t satisfied with work quality, Google may refund the amount paid for the service. The following are the upper limits of lifetime coverage for claims: What it Covers United States: $2,000 Canada: CAD $2,000 The Google Guarantee covers claims up to the amount on the job invoice up to the lifetime cap for coverage. Services must be booked through Google Local Services. The Google Guarantee doesn’t cover add-on or future projects, damages to property, dissatisfaction with price or provider responsiveness, or cancellations. How it Works If one of your customers submits a claim, we’ll contact you to learn more. You’ll have an opportunity to make things right with your customer.\", \"sampled\": \"The African American spiritual, \\\"He's Got the Whole World in His Hands,\\\" boasts a rich history. First published in 1927 in the hymnal \\\"Spirituals Triumphant, Old and New,\\\" it's deeply ingrained in African American spiritual tradition. Passed down through generations, its publication marked the beginning of its widespread dissemination. However, it wasn't until 1933 that the song was collected from the singing of Sue Thomas in North Carolina by folk song collector, Frank Warner. Warner played a pivotal role in spreading the song's influence. Not only did he collect the song, but he also performed it extensively during the 1940s and 1950s. His performances significantly expanded the song's popularity and reach, solidifying its enduring presence in the American folk song repertoire. The song's journey didn't stop in North Carolina. In 1941, Robert Sonkin of the Library of Congress recorded the song in Gee's Bend, Alabama. This recording added another dimension to the song's rich history, linking it to the unique cultural landscape of Gee's Bend. The song continued to travel, passing through various hands and crossing state lines. It became more than just a melody—it transformed into a narrative of resilience and hope. Each rendition, performance, and recording enriched its story, making it a living testament of the African American spiritual tradition. The song's journey was punctuated by significant milestones. In the 1940s and 1950s, Frank Warner's performances further embedded it in the American folk music scene. These performances coincided with a critical period in American history, mirroring the social and cultural shifts of the time. During this period, the song was also recorded by the American Folklife Center. Captured in the 1940s and 1950s, this recording ensured the song's legacy would endure for future generations. The American Folklife Center, an institution dedicated to preserving and promoting American folk cultures, became an integral part of the song's narrative. The story of \\\"He's Got the Whole World in His Hands\\\" took another significant turn in 1952. Frank Warner, who had been instrumental in the song's collection and performance, recorded the song on the Elektra album \\\"American Folk Songs and Ballads\\\". This recording marked the song's debut in the commercial music scene. In essence, \\\"He's Got the Whole World in His Hands\\\" is more than just a song. It's a narrative, a journey, and a testament to the resilience and spirit of the African American spiritual tradition. From its first publication in 1927 to its commercial recording in 1952, each step of its journey has enriched its history.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Thoughts on a medical mystery How brains learn diseases, and their cures My father is a pediatric neurologist, with a speciality in epilepsy. He’s always loved the kids he worked with, and treated them like family. Whenever one of his patients died, he’d bring me and my sisters to their funeral. Some I’d known for years from tagging along on rounds, or having watched them grow up through the quanta of Christmas cards and back-to-school photos. I remember the funeral of a girl my age, who had been far more outgoing and lively than me, her now-dark face caked in yellow makeup. Though they’d never found a drug that could control her seizures, she’d seemed otherwise healthy and normal, and her death had come as a total surprise. This was my first brush with Sudden Unexpected Death in Epilepsy (SUDEP). SUDEP is the most common cause of death in patients with refractory seizures, and remains one of the most devastating mysteries in the field of epilepsy. There are currently no known biomarkers to identify patients at risk for SUDEP — hence the Sudden, hence the Unexpected. Having come to neuroscience by way of physics, I confess I often forget a crucial point: neurons are cells, not just immutable contact points on a circuit board over which voltage plays in prescribed patterns. Not only is the circuit board layout shifting, always rewiring itself in learning, but activity sets off internal programs within cells, making them more or less active, or plastic, tuning their metabolism and protein production. Furthermore, at the gross level, the brain isn’t a black box sealed in the skull, but exerts its influence on every conceivable bodily process. Neurological disorders are rarely, if ever, confined to the brain. This may seem obvious, but it bears consideration. When brain areas are over or under-active, this can reverberate through other somatic systems, causing plastic changes in all manner of body parts: neurons love to learn. In fact, non-neuronal cells learn too: signals like oxygen or nutrition levels can alter a cell’s transcriptional landscape, resulting in tissue remodelling, for example. Epilepsy is particularly interesting, as it’s a prime example of a learned disease. “Kindling” is a common idea in the literature: once someone has had one or more seizures, they’re much more likely to become epileptic, as the brain learns this problematic activity pattern. Some epileptic patients report that they’ve discovered certain thought patterns that help them control or minimize an oncoming seizure (these patients feel a pre-ictal ‘aura’ that clues them in), so we know there may be conscious strategies which certain patients can use to volitionally control their disease.\", \"sampled\": \"Benda had fled, leaving the familiar landscapes of Black Water and Stone Sea far behind. Exhausted from her escape, she sought refuge near a cave mouth, hoping to find respite from her fears. But her fear was relentless, denying her the rest she desperately needed. She was roused from her uneasy sleep by an unusual sound that shattered the night's tranquility. It was music, the strange melody of a flute played with such vigor that it seemed to mock the silence of the night. Intrigued, Benda decided to investigate the source of the disturbance that had interrupted her much-needed sleep. That's when she encountered Tob the Gobble. Tob was an oddity, even in the fantastical world of Quatria. He was a diminutive creature, his cheerful demeanor starkly contrasting with Benda's current state of mind. His round face was marked with laugh lines and his eyes twinkled with mischief. He held a flute, the instrument responsible for the music that had awakened Benda. Upon hearing the flute, Benda couldn't help but exclaim, \\\"What a horrible racket!\\\" She was far from impressed with Tob's musical prowess. Tob revealed that the flute was crafted from a special type of reed that only grew alongside the Great River, a fact that piqued Benda's interest. Tob was certainly full of surprises. Intrigued by Benda's reaction, Tob began to play his flute once more, this time with renewed vigor. The melody reverberated in the cave, its unique tune bouncing off the walls. Benda covered her ears, attempting to block out the sound. It was then that Tob inquired about Benda's past, her journey from Black Water and Stone Sea, and her reasons for fleeing. Benda shared her tale, her voice echoing in the cave. She spoke of her escape, her exhaustion, her fear, and her desperate need for sleep. Tob listened attentively, nodding in understanding. When Benda finished her story, Tob suggested that she might need a new name, a symbol of her fresh start. Benda, however, dismissed this idea, stating, \\\"I’ve had too many. Now I’m just me.\\\" Her words resonated in the cave, bouncing off the walls. Tob the Gobble nodded in understanding. He put his flute away and offered Benda a place to rest. Grateful for the opportunity to sleep undisturbed, Benda accepted his offer. The cave fell silent, the sound of the flute replaced by the soft rhythm of Benda's breathing. And Tob listened.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Photographer: Michelle (Fisher) Bulla — Model: Allison Crady Tension in your head, soreness along your upper back, your body slowly curves inward, and your mind races with unkind thoughts. You feel out of control, uncomfortable, and disoriented. You have anxiety. I’m pretty sure I said something awkward, and I can’t get it out of my head — They must think I’m a psycho. Who the hell am I? Why do I say things like that? Will this ever stop? I have a lot of anxiety, and it sucks. Sitting with discomfort and uncertainty was not part of my life training. Our emotional pain is real. Becoming friends with anxiety means slowing down, acknowledging your pain, and listening to your body with self-compassion. “You can’t stop the waves, but you can learn to surf.” — Jon Kabat-Zinn We can hear ideas over and over, then one day someone says it a certain way, and it clicks. By sharing my anxiety journey — from trying to “fix it” to somatic healing to self-compassion — I hope something clicks. 1. Acknowledging Anxiety We all have anxiety as part of our human journey, a reminder of our shared humanity. We have to start by acknowledging and welcoming its presence. Pushing past anxiety is like telling yourself that it’s not okay to feel or be your whole self: numbing and unhelpful. We like to push away anxiety with vices. I once drew a cartoon of myself watching TV with a thought bubble, “Haha, I don’t have to deal with my emotions.” Pushing aside unpleasant emotions does not heal them. 2. Sitting with Anxiety Most situations are not nearly as bad as we make them out to be. Our stories and judgments cause us pain and anxiety. Anxiety activates our core emotions and fears, the need for control, survival, and love. I found a few guided anxiety meditations that have helped — a 5-minute meditation and a 10-minute meditation. After grounding, we can separate the situation from our response and see the situation more clearly. Being with your anxiety feels difficult, and we don’t want to start. It feels much more appealing to run to comfort or to try to “fix it.” I try to logic my way through anxiety — Okay, my thoughts are racing. I seem to be feeling unsafe or inadequate. I’ll repeat a mantra, take deep breaths, do meditation or yoga, and it will go away. We need to combine a logical approach with the felt experience. Anxiety manifests as trapped energy in our bodies. We need to slow down and release the anxious energy. Easier said than done. I get frustrated. I’m doing all the right things, so why isn’t this working? Why can’t I be calm already? I’ve been taking deep breaths for a while now.\", \"sampled\": \"In a recent blog post, Martin Delaney explores a hypothetical B2C software app that expands through paid acquisition. This concept isn't far-fetched, as many apps employ a similar strategy, leveraging paid marketing to fuel user growth. Delaney's app operates on a freemium model, offering basic functionality for free, while a subscription is required for heavier usage. This approach is widely used by apps to draw a large user base and monetize those who find value in the premium features. To assess the app's business performance, Delaney focuses on three Key Performance Indicators (KPIs): Customer Acquisition Cost, Conversion Rate, and Paying Customer Value. These metrics are vital in determining the financial feasibility of a freemium app. They shed light on the cost-effectiveness of the paid acquisition strategy, the capacity to convert free users into paying customers, and the revenue generated from these customers. Delaney calculates these KPIs using a spreadsheet, a straightforward yet potent tool for tracking and analyzing data. For example, he assumes a Customer Acquisition Cost of $10, a Conversion Rate of 10%, and a Paying Customer Value of $150 over six months. By multiplying these values, he can estimate the gross profit from the paid acquisition strategy. But Delaney doesn't stop at just calculating the KPIs. He proposes adjusting these metrics based on the business's unique circumstances. For instance, an initial advertising spend of $1,000 might yield a different number of users, thus altering the Customer Acquisition Cost. Moreover, he suggests using a sensitivity analysis chart to better visualize the data. This tool offers a graphical representation of how different KPI values impact the business's profit. It can guide entrepreneurs in identifying the most influential areas for improvement and making informed decisions. Using the aforementioned metrics and an initial advertising spend of $1,000, the first year's gross profit would be $1,528.91. However, if the business can boost the Paying Customer Value or the Conversion Rate, the gross profit could increase significantly. In fact, if the business maintains the same growth rate and KPIs, the gross profit over two years would reach $17,677.71. This simple calculation underscores the compounding effect of growth in a startup and the significance of monitoring and improving KPIs. Delaney's blog post offers valuable insights for entrepreneurs and founders. It underscores the importance of understanding and tracking key metrics in a business, particularly for startups operating on a freemium model. In conclusion, while the numbers used in the blog post are hypothetical, they help illustrate the potential of a B2C software app that grows through paid acquisition. By grasping the mechanics of such a business model, entrepreneurs can strategize and optimize their growth more effectively.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Somebody’s Eyes Watching In an age where surveillance and monitoring are all too easily misused, the idea of a camera in odd places can seem a bit weird and even a little creepy. Even when cameras are where they’re supposed to be, it can mean that you’re being watched by who knows who. I definitely cover my webcam to keep unscrupulous eyes from peering through watching me in my own home. So, I was a little leery when I found out that McDonald’s was putting cameras in their dumpsters. “For what?” I said. My first thought was that they wanted to do that to possibly catch dumpster divers, but then I thought what would somebody be trying to find that they could sell in a McDonald’s dumpster? Then I thought well maybe they’re putting the cameras in the dumpsters to catch homeless people rummaging through them, which I thought was a rotten thing for them to do. But neither of these is the case.\", \"sampled\": \"Sunil Sandhu shares his insights in a tutorial on crafting engaging and easy-to-read articles. Published by In Plain English, this guide is an essential read for any budding writer aiming to create compelling content that hooks the reader from the get-go. Sandhu places great emphasis on the significance of a well-chosen title and subtitle for an article. He asserts that these components are vital in capturing the reader's interest and establishing the overall tone of the piece. The difference between a reader choosing to continue or stop reading can often hinge on a well-crafted title and subtitle. According to Sandhu, titles and subtitles should be both concise and compelling. They need to encapsulate the core of the article while also sparking curiosity and interest among potential readers. They should be intriguing enough to entice the reader to delve further into the content.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The dataset is full of these duplicates, or phrase segments. We can remove these by typing: Tokenization We have our text data in the text column, which we now need to tokenize. We will use the BERT tokenizer, as we will be using a BERT transformer later. Here we are first importing the transformers library and initializing a tokenizer for the bert-base-cased model used. A list of models can be found here. We then define a function tokenize that handles tokenization. We use the encode_plus method of our BERT tokenizer to convert a sentence into input_ids and attention_mask tensors. Xids and Xmask are our complete input_ids and attention_mask tensors respectively. The input IDs are a list of integers that are uniquely tied to a specific word. The attention mask is a list of 1s and 0s which correspond to the IDs in the input IDs array — BERT reads this and only applies attention to IDs that correspond to an attention mask value of 1. This allows us to avoid applying attention to padding tokens. Our encode_plus arguments are: Our sentence . This is simply a string representing one tweet. . This is simply a string representing one tweet. The max_length of our encoded outputs. We use a value of 32 which means that every output tensor has a length of 32. of our encoded outputs. We use a value of which means that every output tensor has a length of 32. We cut sequences that have a length of more than 32 tokens with truncation=True . . For sequences that are shorter than 32 tokens, we pad them with zeros up to a length of 32 using padding='max_length' . . BERT uses several special tokens, to mark the start/end of sequences, for padding, unknown words, and mask words. We add those using add_special_tokens=True . . BERT also takes two inputs, the input_ids and attention_mask . We extract the attention mask with return_attention_mask=True . and . We extract the attention mask with . By default, the tokenizer will return a token type IDs tensor — which we don’t need, so we use return_token_type_ids=False . . Finally, we are using TensorFlow, so we return TensorFlow tensors using return_tensors='tf' . If using PyTorch, use return_tensors='pt' . At the end of this, we return our two tensors. We initialize our two Xids and Xmask arrays beforehand and then populated them with our encoded tensors using a simple for-loop — applying tokenizer to each sentence of our dataset. Data prep We now need to prepare our data for training. For this, we will one-hot encode our target labels, creating a simple two-output vector for each sample where [1, 0] represents negative sentiment and [0, 1] positive. One-hot encoding. Before (top) and after (bottom). This whole process can take some time. I like to save the encoded arrays so that we can pick up from here if anything goes wrong or for future tests.\", \"sampled\": \"Ann Litts embarked on a personal spiritual journey, committing herself to deepen her daily meditation practice. This decision wasn't triggered by a significant life event or an insurmountable challenge. Instead, Litts found herself naturally flowing into a tranquil rhythm of life, growing increasingly appreciative of the world around her and the daily experiences it offered. Inspired by a response from White Feather, a spiritual influencer, Litts began seeking a sense of 'one-ness' in everyone and everything. She welcomed the concept of interconnectedness, the belief that all beings, elements of nature, and indeed the entire universe, are intricately interwoven. This understanding grew to such an extent that she felt a sense of unity, not merely with other individuals, but with all existence. Litts' life philosophy harmonized with the concept of 'one-ness,' a profound sense of unity with everything. She saw life as a journey towards unifying the complex facets of our personalities, our physical, spiritual, and emotional selves. This belief wasn't just philosophical; Litts found practical applications in religious practices and life disciplines. She saw every religious practice, be it Christianity, Hinduism, Buddhism, or any other tradition, as echoing the same message of interconnectedness. To Litts, these practices were different paths leading to the same destination: realizing our oneness with the universe. In her exploration of 'one-ness,' Litts found a significant resonance with yoga. She saw yoga as a method of 'yoking' our spirit, soul, and body back together, aligning with the traditional understanding of yoga as a discipline that unifies body, mind, and spirit. Similarly, Litts viewed meditation as a way of attuning to the universe, an idea she borrowed from Ankit Vekariya. \\\"Prayer is talking to The Universe, meditation is listening to it,\\\" Vekariya had said. Litts saw this as a profound truth, a way of understanding the role of meditation in our spiritual journey. In her experience, meditation opened up a channel of communication, a pathway to understanding the whispers of the universe. Litts' belief in the interconnectedness of all beings extended beyond spirituality, finding reflection in the realm of science. She was fascinated by the fact that humans share 50% of our DNA with a banana, 60% with a fruit fly, and a staggering 75-90% with lab mice. This, to her, was a testament to our interconnectedness, a scientific validation of the spiritual concept of 'one-ness.' In her journey of self-awareness and spiritual exploration, Ann Litts discovered a peaceful rhythm in life. She found herself more grateful, more attuned to the universe, and more aware of the 'one-ness' in everyone and everything. It is this sense of interconnectedness, this realization of 'one-ness,' that she considers the most valuable lesson of her life.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Privacy is the main concern in today’s world. There are many threats to the integrity of data and can be more harmful if the data is highly confidential. Encrypting the data and adding security layers is a must when dealing with highly confidential information. And for this purpose, using Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) encryption-decryption algorithm becomes most important as the AES algorithm is very difficult to crack. What is Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)? The Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) is a symmetric block cipher used to protect classified information.There are three block ciphers in AES: AES-128, AES-192 and AES-256. AES-128 uses a 128-bit key length to encrypt-decrypt a block of messages, while AES-192 uses a 192-bit key length and AES-256 uses a 256-bit key length to encrypt-decrypt messages. Each cipher encrypts and decrypts data in blocks of 128 bits using cryptographic keys of 128, 192 and 256 bits. Symmetric, also known as secret key, ciphers use the same key for encrypting and decrypting, so the sender and the receiver must both know and use the same secret key. There are 10 rounds for 128-bit keys, 12 rounds for 192-bit keys and 14 rounds for 256-bit keys. A round consists of several processing steps that include substitution, transposition and mixing of the input plain-text to transform it into the final output of cipher-text. It uses higher length key sizes such as 128, 192 and 256 bits for encryption. Hence it makes AES algorithm more robust against hacking. What is ProGuard and how it works? ProGuard is a safe platform for transferring confidential files by encrypting the files and sending them to receiver using AWS instance which is slightly different approach than the current platforms. Here there are two applications: 1) Stand-alone Application and 2) Web Application. The stand-alone application works on local machine for encryption-decryption and securing the files. The web application runs on cloud for the purpose of sending the encoded file and key to the receiver which he can later download the file and key to decode it through stand-alone application. Pre-requisites Necessary dependencies for this project: Flask Pycrypto Reportlib Secretsharing PyPDF2 Below is the step-by-step process for implementing the program to share the files securely: 1. Stand-alone Application Step 1: Download the project folder from my Github page and install all the necessary dependencies for this project. You can also find all the libraries listed above in the site-packages folder within the ProGuard folder. Step 2: Create one text file and write your data which you intend to encrypt and share with others. Step 3: Go to ProGuard/source/stand-alone-application and run the main.py file. Here one dialog box will appear in which you have to select the text file which you want to encrypt and share.\", \"sampled\": \"Data visualization offers a powerful means to understand and communicate the impacts of climate change. Given the complexity of the subject, data visualization can simplify and clarify the issue, making it more digestible for the average person. A prime example of this is the use of data visualization to illustrate the effects of climate change on glaciers, particularly in Glacier National Park. Mapping techniques can vividly display the shrinking glacier boundaries in the park, providing a stark illustration of the damage caused by climate change. The integration of software tools like Tableau-Mapbox has made exploring map data significantly more straightforward. This tool simplifies the process of data visualization and makes the information more accessible to users. For example, it can graphically represent the data on the shrinking glacier boundaries in Glacier National Park, making the data easier to understand. The impact of climate change on glaciers is a significant concern. Melting glaciers contribute to rising sea levels, coastal erosion, and habitat changes, all of which can have far-reaching effects on both human and animal populations. The United States Geological Survey (USGS) has been tracking the 37 named glaciers in Glacier National Park for the past 49 years. This data provides a comprehensive overview of the changes in the glacier area over time, which is crucial for understanding the rate and extent of glacier retreat due to climate change. Two glaciers that have been extensively studied are the Herbst Glacier and the Two Ocean Glacier. The data collected on these glaciers paints a worrying picture. Over 80% of the main body area of both the Herbst Glacier and Two Ocean Glacier has vanished. This significant reduction in glacier area underscores the severity of climate change's impact. In total, over 7.6 million square meters of glacier area has receded in Glacier National Park. This alarming figure emphasizes the urgency of addressing climate change and finding ways to slow the pace of glacier melt. These data visualization techniques can also be applied to other regions of the world. For example, they can be used to show the effects of climate change in regions like Africa or countries like Syria. By making this data visually accessible, we can better understand the global impact of climate change. Data visualization is a vital tool for raising awareness about climate change and promoting informed action. As demonstrated by the case of Glacier National Park, the use of mapping techniques and software like Tableau-Mapbox can help us understand and visualize the alarming effects of climate change on our planet. By using this information, we can make more informed decisions about how to mitigate these effects and protect our environment for future generations.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Creating our own simple neural network Let’s create a neural network from scratch with Python (3.x in the example below). import numpy, random, os lr = 1 #learning rate bias = 1 #value of bias weights = [random.random(),random.random(),random.random()] #weights generated in a list (3 weights in total for 2 neurons and the bias) The beginning of the program just defines libraries and the values of the parameters, and creates a list which contains the values of the weights that will be modified (those are generated randomly). def Perceptron(input1, input2, output) : outputP = input1*weights[0]+input2*weights[1]+bias*weights[2] if outputP > 0 : #activation function (here Heaviside) outputP = 1 else : outputP = 0 error = output – outputP weights[0] += error * input1 * lr weights[1] += error * input2 * lr weights[2] += error * bias * lr Here we create a function which defines the work of the output neuron. It takes 3 parameters (the 2 values of the neurons and the expected output). “outputP” is the variable corresponding to the output given by the Perceptron. Then we calculate the error, used to modify the weights of every connections to the output neuron right after. for i in range(50) : Perceptron(1,1,1) #True or true Perceptron(1,0,1) #True or false Perceptron(0,1,1) #False or true Perceptron(0,0,0) #False or false We create a loop that makes the neural network repeat every situation several times. This part is the learning phase. The number of iteration is chosen according to the precision we want. However, be aware that too much iterations could lead the network to over-fitting, which causes it to focus too much on the treated examples, so it couldn’t get a right output on case it didn’t see during its learning phase. However, our case here is a bit special, since there are only 4 possibilities, and we give the neural network all of them during its learning phase. A Perceptron is supposed to give a correct output without having ever seen the case it is treating.\", \"sampled\": \"Digital platforms have truly transformed the broadcasting industry, paving the way for the emergence of niche radio stations that cater to specific demographics. A prime example of this is Fix Radio, a one-of-a-kind station designed specifically for builders and tradespeople. This audience, largely overlooked by conventional radio, has found a home in Fix Radio, showcasing the potential of targeted broadcasting. Listeners can access Fix Radio on DAB, online, or via an app, making it available wherever they may be. This accessibility is a testament to the adaptability of digital platforms, offering broadcasters multiple avenues to connect with their audience. In our digital era, listeners are no longer tied to traditional radio sets. They can tune in from their mobile devices, computers, or even smart home devices. A key factor in Fix Radio's success is its targeted marketing strategy. The station recently celebrated its first anniversary and has seen a surge in both listenership and advertising. This growth can be attributed to innovative marketing campaigns like the 'Bacon Butty Tour', a promotional event that distributed 20,000 hot butties to builders and tradespeople. Beyond targeted marketing, Fix Radio also offers content specifically designed for its audience. The station provides comprehensive weather updates and forecasts for busy construction areas, ensuring its listeners are well-equipped for the day ahead. With a 'no-repeat guarantee' for music, daily construction industry news, and sports updates, Fix Radio's programming is tailor-made for its listeners. The station also demonstrates its commitment to its audience by supporting a construction industry charity. This not only aligns with the station's branding but also fosters a sense of community among its listeners. In an industry known for its challenging conditions and long hours, this kind of support can make a significant impact. Fix Radio's success is a vivid example of the effectiveness of niche marketing and targeted content in today's digital age. With over 110 radio stations on DAB in London alone, standing out can be daunting. However, by understanding its audience and delivering content that meets their needs, Fix Radio has successfully carved out a unique niche in the broadcasting landscape.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Photo by Kinga Cichewicz on Unsplash When I was younger and still in school my mom and I would take mental health days once every month or two. Instead of going to school or work, we would spend the day out together doing things we enjoyed and exploring new and inspiring places. I got my work as a student done, and nobody bit her head off at work. To this day I feel like those breaks and those boundaries and experiences really helped me figure out what was important. I never felt like those days were bad, and I certainly never felt guilty for taking them. They refreshed me in much the same way a bright inspirational strike of writing motivation does. I worked hard in the time between mental health days because I had something to look back on and forward to that was positive and healthy and all around fantastic for my brain, my emotional state, and my relationship with my mom. Now, in a job where breaks like you get in school are virtually nonexistent, I miss those days and can feel the difference in myself and her when we don’t have them. The trouble, in part, is that we’re both good at what we do and have a lot more demands on us now than we did when I was in school. Enjoying your job doesn’t always save you from getting burnt out on it. Neither does being good at it. The idea of “do what you love, and you’ll never work a day in your life” is idealistic. It may be true for some, but on the whole it seems little more than a pipe dream. Where I work right now, I watch people who are good at their jobs and love what they do get so stressed out it feels like their brains are melting under the pressure of deadlines and workloads. Because they are good, they get more responsibilities. With more responsibility comes more pressure, and less of a feeling like taking a break is a good thing. I know several people who leave work at a reasonable hour and/or go home on the weekends feeling guilty because they have so much to do they feel like any time not spent trying to catch up is a bad thing. But then when they do work the endless grinding hours, they feel guilty for not spending time away from it. It’s a solid no-win situation. I am fortunate enough to be fairly low-level in that I don’t have an immense amount of constant pressure on me to get things done under constant deadlines.\", \"sampled\": \"Jamila Woods' 'Holy' offers a profound exploration of the artist's mind. Known for her soulful voice and poignant lyrics, Woods first gained recognition with her performance on 'Sunday Candy', the lead single from The Social Experiment's 2015 album, Surf. This debut in the music industry paved the way for her promising career, allowing her to showcase her unique talent to a wider audience. However, it was her 2016 debut album, HEAVN, that truly established her as a fresh force in the music world. In this album, Woods broadened her lyrical scope to encompass themes like racial identity, gender politics, and self-purpose. The standout track, 'Holy', delves deeper into these themes, exploring the concept of finding spiritual liberation in solitude. The song showcases Woods' knack for weaving personal experiences with broader societal issues, making her music both relatable and thought-provoking. 'Holy' employs Christian theology references to articulate the solitary and spiritual journey of life. The lyrics, \\\"Give me today my daily bread / Help me to walk alone ahead / Though I walk through the darkest valley I will fear no love / Oh my smile my mind reassure me I don’t need no one,\\\" reflect deep introspection and a yearning for self-reliance. This solitude is not depicted as negative; rather, it's presented as a liberating experience and a crucial part of personal growth. The chorus of the song underscores this notion. Woods sings, \\\"Woke up this morning with my mind set on loving me / With my mind set on loving me / I’m not lonely, I’m alone / And I’m holy by my own.\\\" These words emphasize the importance of self-love and the concept of finding holiness within oneself, instead of seeking it externally. The song also recognizes life's inevitable hardships, with lyrics like, \\\"Ye, the bad days may come / The lover may leave / The winter may not / Hey, the map of your palms / The temple you be / You’re all that you got.\\\" These words serve as a reminder that despite the challenges we may encounter, we are our own sanctuary, and the power to overcome resides within us. The depth of 'Holy' speaks volumes about Woods' talent as a songwriter and her capacity to express complex themes through her music. The song resonates with listeners not only for its beautiful melody but also for its potent message of self-love and inner strength. It mirrors the artist's journey and underscores the power of music to move, inspire, and stimulate thought.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"How to Set the Mood for Maximum Productivity Get more done in less time by implementing these little rituals Many of us struggle with being consistently productive. We plan so much for the day and then we end up procrastinating instead. And often, even when we do start working, it’s doesn’t go the way we expected. We just can’t seem to get in the zone. I used to struggle with this a lot. I would sit down, open a document and start working on the task at hand. Yet my mind would wander and wouldn't stay focused on the work I was supposed to be doing. Often I would give up, saying: “I’m just not in the mood. It’s not a productive day and there’s nothing I can do about it.” But then I learned that this wasn’t true. There is always something you can do. All I needed to regain my focus was a consistent setting that I associated with work.\", \"sampled\": \"Joe Rogan recently took to Instagram to share his personal experience with the carnivore diet, a regimen primarily composed of protein. Known for its exclusionary nature, the carnivore diet places a heavy emphasis on protein intake. Given his flexible schedule and his own podcast, Rogan decided to give this diet a shot. However, the outcome wasn't entirely positive. Rogan faced some severe bowel-related side effects from the diet, an issue he candidly shared with his followers. \\\"Carnivore diet update; the good and the bad. Let’s start with the bad. There’s really only one “bad” thing, and that thing is diarrhoea,\\\" Rogan revealed on Instagram. His experience highlights the potential risks of such a protein-centric diet. National dietary guidelines suggest that protein should constitute between ten to thirty-five percent of our diet. The carnivore diet, with its extreme protein focus, clearly deviates from these recommendations. This could potentially explain the digestive problems Rogan encountered.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Can I Write One Good Sentence? The brilliant advice F. Scott Fitzgerald never actually gave Photo by Peter Pryharski on Unsplash In the early 20th century, the most important man in the world of American literature wasn’t an author. His name was Maxwell Perkins. Perkins was an editor at Scribner, a publishing house in New York City. In 1919, he signed a young, unknown author, making a big bet on aspiring talent against the will of his seniors at the company. The author he signed was F. Scott Fitzgerald, who would go on to write and publish The Great Gatsby in cooperation with Perkins. One year after Gatsby, which wouldn’t sell well for the next 15 years, Perkins met and signed another author of questionable status: Ernest Hemingway. After the two books they worked on together — The Sun Also Rises and A Farewell to Arms — found commercial successes, Perkins became the most sought after editor in the country. The 2016 movie Genius tells the story of Perkins and another prodigious discovery of his: Thomas Wolfe. Wolfe was the son Perkins, who had five daughters, always longed for. He was poetic, passionate, and notoriously incapable of cutting a single word from his flowery prose. In other words, he was a writer through and through. As an editor, one of Perkins’ main responsibilities was to cut the inessential. He did so for all of his writers but for none more than Wolfe. His first novel, Look Homeward, Angel, went from 294,000 words to 234,000 under Perkins’ guidance — and it sold like hotcakes. Taking the feedback of “write more” a bit too literally, Wolfe turned around and produced another manuscript: The 5,000-page draft for Of Time and the River, which him and Perkins fought over for two years before it finally saw the light of day. Then, despite the book’s success, a trauma befell Wolfe that catches every writer at some point: Wolfe got writer’s block. For months, he was unable to put pen to paper. Eventually, he took a long, solo trip all the way to California, where, among other things, he visited Fitzgerald. I doubt the scene played out as depicted in the movie, but the advice he gave Wolfe — no doubt inspired by Perkins’ dedication as an editor — is priceless nonetheless: Thomas Wolfe: “More and more I trouble myself with that, the legacy. Will anyone care about Thomas Wolfe in 100 years? 10 years?” F. Scott Fitzgerald: “When I was young, I asked myself that question everyday. Now, I ask myself, ‘Can I write one good sentence?’” For any writer, there are more ifs and thens and whats and whens to obsess over than hours in the day. What if no one cares about my idea? Will the book sell once it’s out? When can I make a living from my craft? What does it all amount to? Will I leave behind a legacy? There is no quicker way to obliterate your ability to chain words together than to hop on this never-ending merry-go-round of hypotheticals.\", \"sampled\": \"Veon Careers tells Kamel’s story through the language of a climb, using hiking in Ahaggar National Park as a metaphor for career growth. The sense of moving through that landscape, with Mount Tahat named in the wider setting, frames a career built step by step inside Djezzy. Kamel joined Djezzy in 2006, starting in a role close to customers and everyday service. His first job was as a call center advisor, a beginning the profile treats not as a fixed label but as part of a longer professional path. From there, Kamel moved into copywriting, where language, audience, and brand voice became central to his work. He later worked in advertising management, adding another stage to a progression that remained within Djezzy while expanding in responsibility. Kamel is now a Brand & Communications Director, and the article presents that role as the latest point in an ongoing climb. Looking back, he says, “Each position has been a step to the next one,” tying each job title to the one that followed. The profile connects his success to steady improvement, learning, and openness to new experiences. His advice to his childhood self is simple: “I would tell myself: Never give up. Learn from your mistakes. Always work on getting better. And be open and listen to others”. Teamwork is also central to Kamel’s view of growth, not a side note or soft extra. He says teamwork is necessary for reaching one’s potential, placing colleagues and shared effort in the same career story as learning and persistence. Copywriting for Djezzy was especially complex because of Algeria’s mix of languages. Kamel says the work had to reflect that mix, making communication more demanding than choosing one message and sending it everywhere in the same form. The article describes Berber languages and dialects, Arabic, French, and some Spanish influence as present in Algeria. It also names Kabyle as commonly heard in northern and northeastern Algeria, Chaoui as used in eastern Algeria, and Tuareg as used in the Algerian Sahara. Arabic is described as a standard language and the mother tongue for many Algerians. French is also part of the communications landscape, with its use in commerce, government, education, and cities shaping how Djezzy’s brand messages are received. Kamel says Djezzy adapted copy by region while keeping a common campaign idea. That balance matters in the profile because it shows the brand responding to local language differences without losing the shared campaign thread across audiences. Customer feedback shaped communications decisions too, especially through social media. Kamel explains, “For instance, we found out from our Facebook interactions that many users wanted Arabic subtitles for our French press conferences,” showing how Facebook interactions influenced video accessibility. Djezzy also links its brand with Algerian culture through entertainment. The company sponsors MasterChef Algeria, and the profile presents that sponsorship as part of the brand’s effort to connect with cultural life in Algeria. Djezzy CLYC is another media property mentioned in the profile, tied to Djezzy’s presence on YouTube. Taken together, Kamel’s path, the language work, social media feedback, and cultural sponsorship make this a career profile focused on learning, adaptation, and communication.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Image classification: The problem with image classification is this: Given a set of images that are all labeled with a single category, we are asked to predict these categories for a new set of test images and measure the accuracy of the predictions. There are many challenges to this task, including point of view variation, scale variation, intra-class variation, image distortion, image occlusion, lighting conditions, and background clutter. How could we write an algorithm capable of classifying images into distinct categories? Computer Vision researchers have developed a data-based approach to solving this problem. Instead of trying to specify directly in the code what each image category of interest looks like, they provide the computer with many examples of each class of images and then develop learning algorithms that examine these examples and learn the visual appearance of each class. In other words, they first accumulate a set of tagged image learning data and then send it to the computer to process the data. Given this fact, the complete image classification pipeline can be formalized as follows: Our input is a training data set that consists of N images, each labeled with one of K different classes. We then use this training set to train a classifier to learn what each class looks like. finally, we evaluate the quality of the classifier by asking him to predict labels for a new set of images that he has never seen before. We then compare the actual labels for these images with those predicted by the classifier. The most common algorithms used to solve image classification are CNN convolutional neural networks Convolutional neural networks are currently the most efficient models for image classification. CNN has two very different parts. As input, the image is provided as a matrix of pixels. A 2-dimensional grayscale image. The color is represented by the third dimension, depth 3, representing the basic colors [red, green, blue]. The first part of CNN is the convolutional part. It acts as an image extractor. The image is passed through subsequent filters or convolution kernels, creating new images called convolution maps. Some indirect filters reduce the resolution of the image by a maximum local operation. Finally, the weave maps are flattened and combined into a vector of entities called CNN code. Creating a new convolutional neural network is expensive in terms of expertise, equipment, and the amount of data required with annotations. First of all, it is necessary to determine the architecture of the network, i.e. the number of layers, their size and the matrix operations that connect them. The training then involves optimizing the network coefficients to minimize output error. This training can take several weeks for the best CNN, with many graphics processors working on hundreds of thousands of commented images. Today, most image classification techniques are trained on ImageNet, a data set of approximately 1.2 million high-resolution training images. The winner of the first ImageNet competition, Alex Krizhevsky, has revolutionized deep learning with an Alexnet very deep convolutional neural network. Its architecture consists of 7 hidden layers, plus a few maximum aggregation layers. The first layers were convolutional, while the last two were globally connected. Activation functions were linear units rectified in each hidden layer. These train much faster and are more expressive than logistics units. In addition, it also uses competitive standardization to suppress hidden activities when neighbouring units have stronger activities. This allows for better management of intensity variations. In terms of hardware requirements, Alex uses a very efficient implementation of convolutional networks on 2 Nvidia GTX 580 GPUs (more than 1000 small fast cores).\", \"sampled\": \"Antifragility matters. Jimmy Soh’s article, published on 2020-06-28, argues that organisations should not see stress, uncertainty, and disruption only as forces to survive. The main idea goes beyond resilience: some systems can be designed to benefit from pressure, disorder, and changing conditions. Nassim Taleb is credited with naming the concept of Antifragile, and the article uses it to compare fragile, robust, and antifragile systems. Fragile systems break under stress, robust systems withstand it, and antifragile systems improve through disruption because their losses are limited while their potential gains can be very large. That distinction matters for business strategy because uncertainty is not a passing inconvenience. It is part of the operating environment. Organisations that focus only on preserving existing arrangements may miss chances to learn, adapt, and profit when conditions change. Antifragile systems are described as having capped losses and very large possible gains, which changes how risk is understood in uncertain conditions. The article connects technological progress and digitalisation with faster change and more Black Swan-style disruption. COVID-19 is used as an example of disruption that affected sectors such as transportation and retail, grounding the argument in practical terms rather than keeping it abstract. The article says COVID-19 could create opportunities for online businesses that respond to changed demand. The broader setting is VUCA, where volatility, uncertainty, complexity, and ambiguity shape organisational choices. Startups are presented as one example of antifragility because they can operate under uncertainty, test assumptions, and adjust as conditions shift. Muscles are another example. Human psychology also appears in the discussion, as does the immune system. Bacteria are named as an example of antifragility too. The article also uses the hydra image, where one head is slain and two grow back. That image reinforces the point that some systems do not simply recover after damage. They become stronger because of stress. Antifragility is also described as a second-order effect of disruption at an underlying layer. The point is not to celebrate chaos, but to build systems that can make use of it. The author lists three organisational lessons for navigating VUCA conditions. The first is to focus on stable needs, meaning organisations should pay attention to what does not change even when tools, markets, or behaviours do. The second is to create disruption internally and externally. The third is to reorganise as conditions change, instead of treating the current structure as fixed. Under #1, the article presents focus as a way to avoid chasing every signal during turbulent periods. Under #2, it says pitch sessions and hackathons were used to encourage a problem-seeking mindset, turning disruption into something generated inside the organisation rather than only encountered from the outside. Under #3, changing with the times means reorganising when conditions shift. This keeps structure responsive to the environment and treats adaptation as part of organisational design. It also fits the article’s view that disruption is unavoidable. Black Swans are part of that context. Digitalisation and technological progress make such disruption feel faster and less predictable. The lesson is to keep changing when the environment changes. For individuals, the article recommends frequent experiments, reducing distractions, focusing on principles, and periodic reinvention. Frequent experiments support the wider argument because small attempts can limit losses while preserving the chance of large gains. Reducing distractions helps maintain attention on what matters when the environment is noisy and uncertain. Focusing on principles connects personal development with the organisational lesson about stable needs. Periodic reinvention reflects the same idea as reorganising under changing conditions. The article’s named references include Freidrich Nietzsche and Charlie Munger as quoted thinkers, while Viktor Forgacs is credited for the photo from Unsplash.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Hello Muddah, hello Faddah Here I am at Camp Grenada Camp is very entertaining And they say we'll have some fun if it stops raining (Written by Allen Sherman-Sung by Mel Brooks) Writing is terrifying, especially if you’re afraid of everything, which I am. I do a bang-up job of acting fierce but come behind the curtain, I’m a panic-stricken fool. But, do you know what’s scarier than writing? Not writing. Because when you stop, it feels like you’ve been thrown off the ship into a tropical cyclone without a life jacket. Not that a lifejacket would be much help in a tropical cyclone, but it would be something, and sometimes something keeps you going. I grew up singing this satirical song about camp. The first lines are shown above. The song is about a postcard written by a camper to his mother and father (Mudduh and Fadduh). It’s all about the perils of camp. The singing postcard describes poison ivy, missing children, kids eaten by bears, having to read Ulysses. The end of the postcard reveals it’s only been one day. He concludes that all is well now and his parents should disregard his letter. As an adult, I see this song as a metaphor for adulthood. Days are long. Mondays seem like they should be Fridays. I would love to occasionally write a postcard, asking someone to pick me up and take me out of this one long day, but who would I send it to? As a writer, the longest day is the day I return to the page after a self-imposed hiatus. You see, writing can feel like an unnecessary act. Like in a cult, once you start questioning those hive beliefs, you want to get the hell out of there. Writing is like that. You shouldn’t question why you’re doing it. It breaks the spell. Here’s my question. Do you keep writing even when you have nothing to give to the page? Or, do you take breaks so you can reboot, reflect and shift your gaze? When I keep writing, and there's no light in my attic, I feel like I’m writing one long sentence. Not necessarily a bad sentence, but not a particularly interesting one either. More tortoise than hare. When I stop to take a break from writing, however, I get scared that I’ll never return. Like I’ve been thrown off a boat into a cyclone and once the storm clears, I can’t see the land or the boat. I am lost. Once I return to the page, after all this water-treading, I doubt myself. I wonder how I sailed this writing ship before? What muscles did I use to lift these thoughts? What routes did my brain travel to connect the words in my brain to the words on the page? I also doubt the navigation I previously used to find my way. Aren’t all the stars dead? Why was I using them to map my way? I am somewhere, but I still lost. I used to be a swimmer. When I raced, you got one false start. After two, you were disqualified. The officials realized some people were using the first false starts to their advantage, so now you only get one false start then you’re history. Go back to the locker room, change, and go home. Writing has a lot of false starts. One false start after the other. No one comes in and says “Get off the block, ya’ done”, but it feels that way. You keep starting over, again and again. Every time you return to the page, you’re at the beginning. You have to remember how to dive off every time. This week, I took off two days from writing. I still scribbled down ideas and potential titles, but I was off the block. The block was a mile away. It’s always terrifying. This morning, I walked back towards the starting block. My chest tightened. I stretched out my brain by inhaling and exhaling. I took a swig from my water bottle, which for writers is coffee. I climbed onto the block. Instead of fear-shaking, I bent over and grabbed the part where my toes go. I squeezed it. I said ‘I’m not afraid of you, block.’ I was a little afraid, but what difference did that make? I’m more afraid of not writing than of writing, so I’m just going to stand here until the starter's gun goes off. I can’t see the finish line, but it’s something, and sometimes something keeps you going.\", \"sampled\": \"Remote teaching needed a clear structure, and Onur Yuce Gun’s account of teaching during the COVID-19 pandemic made that point through a class from the 2020 spring semester at MIT’s School of Architecture and Planning. The article described a course shaped by the sudden shift to remote instruction, but it did not frame the class as improvised. Instead, Onur Yuce Gun credited the syllabus, which had been prepared before the pandemic disrupted the semester. Its structure mattered: the course introduced many tools early, then reserved later sessions for individual project development. In his account, that preparation proved useful not because it anticipated disruption, but because it gave the course a strong internal order. The article was published on Medium with the timestamp 2020-06-22 16:38:40.909000+00:00 and was associated with Age of Awareness. The teaching took place in 2020, during the spring semester at MIT’s School of Architecture and Planning. The syllabus had 3 main considerations: Agency as 1, Adaptation as 2, and Tooling completing the set. Together, those ideas framed what students would do and how the instructor would respond. Course material was deployed through 4 tracks: Presence, Function, Quality, and The Whole. The front-loaded syllabus was central because it changed the timing of learning in the course. Students encountered many tools early, which let later sessions support individual projects. Onur Yuce Gun contrasted this with gradual skill-building across a semester, where tools arrive more slowly and later choices can be limited by the pacing of instruction. Agency referred to a student’s ability to make choices earlier in the course rather than waiting until the end of a gradual sequence. The author argued that front-loaded teaching helps students make those choices sooner. By giving students access to material before the later project period, the class could shift toward individual development. Agency was not separate from tooling; it depended on early access to tools and on a syllabus that left room for student direction. Adaptation also moved in more than one direction. Students needed to adapt as their projects developed, while instructors needed to adapt to the direction of the class. That made the syllabus less rigid than a simple schedule. It concentrated tools early, then allowed the course’s direction to matter. Tooling, the third consideration, supported the author’s wish for students to gain many tools early. Those tools were not treated as an isolated technical layer, but as part of a teaching structure that made later project work possible and responsive. The article also set this approach against the familiar rhythm of semester-long skill-building. In that model, skills accumulate gradually across the term. Onur Yuce Gun emphasized a different distribution, with skill-building sessions placed early so students could use the remaining class time to pursue individual projects. At MIT, students shop for classes during the first week or two, which made the early shape of the course especially important because students encounter a class while they are still deciding. A front-loaded approach could reveal the course’s tools and direction early enough to inform those choices. The author connected his view of teaching to experience built over time. He said he had learned through piecemeal teaching over 15 years and had conducted workshops ranging from three hours to two weeks. Those experiences formed part of the background for his thinking about how much material should arrive early, and they help explain the article’s close attention to the timing of skill-building. The course did not simply move online during the pandemic. It entered remote teaching with a syllabus already organized around agency, adaptation, and tooling, and that organization became the practical basis for the smoother remote class. Presence was one of the 4 tracks used to deploy the teaching material. Function was another, Quality was included as well, and The Whole completed the set of Presence, Function, Quality, and The Whole. Together, these tracks gave the material a structure beyond a single line of technical exercises while still serving the broader goal of early student agency. The pandemic context mattered, but the article’s central claim was about course design. The syllabus had been prepared before the author knew how the pandemic would affect the semester. That timing made the plan’s later usefulness notable, because the smoothness of the remote class came from decisions made before the disruption.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"One of the most fascinating theories regarding space is that of the Kardashev scale. Meant to describe what alien civilizations would look like, it labels our own in comparison to what may be in the cosmos. What is the Kardashev Scale? Proposed originally by Russian astrophysicist, Nikolai Kardashev, this scale measures potential civilizations by energy output in 3 stages. He believed that a civilization is measured on a cosmic level by its’ energy usage and the technology it uses. According to Kardashev, these two aspects ran parallel to one another. As more energy is produced, higher levels of technology are needed to produce it. Therefore, a society that has a high energy output must have matching technology. In other words, more energy output = more technologically advanced. Here’s the sad part…humans aren’t even on this scale yet. We still harvest most of our energy from dead animals, plants, and the Earth itself. Society as we know it is a Type 0 civilization. Ouch. Current estimates on when we may even be promoted onto the scale aren’t for another few hundred years. That being said, what does each stage of the Kardashev Scale mean and how is it applicable to our future? Type I Civilization This relatively low-level civilization that we have almost achieved is characterized by one that harnesses the complete energy of its’ neighboring star. Basically solar power. Essentially, natural disasters can be converted into energy rather than destruction by civilizations of this caliber. Our energy production would need to be 100,000 times what it is now to achieve this. Yeah, that’s the low level of this scale…start imagining the rest. A very important characteristic, and perhaps why we haven’t achieved it, is that a Type I civilization has the ability to store enough energy for its’ growing population. In other words, overpopulation is no longer a problem. The entire planets’ resources would be utilized for energy and the light from our star as well. While it may sound extremely far fetched, energy production is largely exponential and one or two large breakthroughs could help us achieve this stage. Type II Civilization This form of civilization is similar to Type I in the sense that a civilization of this rank controls the energy from its’ star. However, it goes beyond just converting solar energy. Type II civilizations have the ability to directly manipulate their star and convert its’ energy to something more powerful than anything we’ve seen. An idea would be a device that can capture the energy released from fusion which powers stars. This energy could then theoretically be transferred back to a home planet for use. If gathered this way, the energy would be far more powerful than any form of solar energy we have seen.\", \"sampled\": \"The DNC chose Google BigQuery as the managed data warehouse service for Phoenix, its new warehouse built to replace a legacy platform that had supported major political technology work through earlier election cycles. Phoenix was not treated as a narrow internal upgrade. The DNC wanted a warehouse for the 2020 cycle and later cycles that could support Democratic candidates more broadly. The older warehouse had been used in the 2016 and 2018 election cycles, with roots going back to Obama’s 2012 run. That history mattered not just because the system was old, but because it relied on custom ETL pipelines built by earlier DNC technology teams. Before settling on Phoenix with Google BigQuery, the DNC evaluated several options, including Amazon Redshift, Azure, Snowflake, Google BigQuery, and an upgrade of the legacy platform. That comparison kept the decision tied to the practical needs of a warehouse expected to serve Democrats across 50 states and D.C. Google BigQuery was selected because it fit the DNC’s goals for multi-tenancy, concurrency, and operation by a small team. Those criteria were central because the new warehouse needed to support wide use without requiring a large staff to keep it running. Google Cloud Platform also appealed to the DNC because its infrastructure could scale up or down with usage-based costs. In a political technology environment shaped by election cycles, that flexibility matched the need for capacity that could change with demand rather than remain fixed around one point in time. The migration started with a proof of concept in December 2018, followed by an MVP that began in early 2019. This gave the DNC a way to test Google BigQuery for Phoenix before moving further into the larger replacement of the legacy warehouse. During the between-election period, the DNC focused on rebuilding syncs for voter file data and VoteBuilder data. That work addressed critical data flows instead of treating the migration as a simple platform swap. By midsummer 2019, only internal DNC data teams were still using the legacy platform. At that stage, the old warehouse’s role had narrowed sharply, while Phoenix and Google BigQuery became the center of the DNC’s new data warehouse approach. By fall 2019, archive migration was complete and the old warehouse was retired. That retirement finished the move away from the legacy platform that had supported the 2016 and 2018 cycles and traced back to Obama’s 2012 campaign. In 2020, Phoenix was used by state party data teams, sister committees, and presidential primary campaigns. The warehouse the DNC selected and built on Google BigQuery had become part of the data infrastructure supporting Democratic work beyond a single internal data team.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"By Caroline Donahue — THE BOOK DOCTOR After how long January feels, February seems to flash past, a quick five minutes. In the midst of this zooming month, I have begun revising my novel. So far, the daunting steps of reading the first draft over several times and considering whether the structure is really working have been completed. As I waded through scenes that took me years to write, only to make judgements on whether they are, as Heidi Klum used to say on Project Runway, “In” or “Out,” I was struck with the realization that a first draft and later ones have entirely different intentions. A first draft is for the head. We need to understand a lot of things through writing a first draft. To be clear, you may write many sections of a book many times in a first draft. But for the sake of clarity, I think of a first draft as the first time you’ve written a piece all the way through to the end and have been able to type THE END, if only to delete it immediately afterward. This first draft is to understand what happens in the book. This is true for fiction as well as memoir. Anything with a narrative. We are writing that first draft to understand the scope, to know what is part of the story, who the players are, what they are like, and what span of their lives we will witness as readers. There are a lot of decisions to make in this first draft, and our head works very hard to make them. First person or third? Multiple perspectives? Multiple timelines or chronological? Past tense of the increasingly trendy present? A first draft is often accompanied by lists of decisions to make. I have even assigned my clients these sorts of lists when they get lost in the weeds. But now that I have pushed the boat away from the dock and am floating in the middle of the water, far away from shore in my revision, I have come to understand that the second draft is no longer the draft of the head. I know what happens and who is involved, my tense, my POV and the span of time covered. So what is this new draft for? This, my friends, is when the heart gets involved. If you simply tell a story point by point but without any emotion or atmosphere or without engaging the senses, you’ll never get into your reader’s heart. My editor and mentor said when I gave him the end of my first draft that I now had the foundation and structure of a book, much like the frame and roof of a house. But now, I need to connect the electricity and decorate the book so the reader feels at home in it. And this is when you need to start re-reading what you’ve written with your feelings at the front. Try to distract your head with tasks that will keep it away from the action. Have it format the draft or make sure the line spacing is even while you get on with the real work of making the book come to life. Look at all the characters from an emotional perspective rather than a factual one. Instead of asking what the character does for a living, for example, ask how she feels about it. Does she love her work or hate it? Or, even better, does she love it even while she is going broke because it doesn’t cover all her bills. There we go…the heart has been engaged. Read through anything you write a first time to see if it makes sense, if it is logical.\", \"sampled\": \"A visible personal brand can help lead a company. Jayson Demers presents the idea as a five-step approach to becoming the public representative of a business. The article carries the timestamp 2020-11-09 23:41:14.348000+00:00. Its central claim is that a founder, entrepreneur, or leader can become the “face” of a company by making their individual identity visible alongside the company identity. Demers argues that consumers may trust people more than corporate brands or advertising, so a personal brand can strengthen company authority, influence, consumer relationships, and traffic to a company site. The first step is straightforward: add a short personal profile to the company website. That profile should make the person easy to recognize and easy to follow, with a name, biography, headshot, and social profile links that give visitors a clear path from the company site to the individual behind it. The profile is not just a decorative extra. It acts as a bridge between the company and the person leading it. The company gains a more human connection, while the individual gains a public place where authority, influence, and consumer relationships can begin to tie back to the business. The second step is publishing industry content under the individual’s own name. This makes the person more visible and helps build authority. Jayson Demers recommends posting at least one new article each week. The goal is not only to publish for the company, but to let the audience associate industry knowledge with a specific person. The article frames content as a way for the personal brand to send additional traffic to a company site. When someone publishes under their own name, that personal brand becomes a channel that can point readers back toward the company. That connection can support company authority, influence, and consumer relationships, with the person’s work and the company’s work reinforcing each other. The third step is increasing personal social media activity through profiles where the individual can be found and followed. Twitter and LinkedIn are named as recommended social media profiles, and Facebook is named as a platform for a professional page. The article advises responding to outreach and thanking people who like or share posts, so the individual presence stays active rather than static. This social activity is part of becoming visible enough to lead a company publicly. The personal brand should not live only on the company website or in published articles. It should also show up where people interact, share posts, respond, and decide whether they trust the person behind a business. The fourth step is networking often and using public relations opportunities. LinkedIn Groups are named as a networking channel. The article places networking alongside public relations rather than treating it as something separate. Both help the person become more visible and more connected to the audience that may later connect with the company. Networking is presented as an ongoing activity, not a one-time campaign. The personal brand grows through contact, response, and recognition. Public relations opportunities give the individual more chances to appear as the company’s representative, and those appearances can reinforce authority and influence while giving consumers a person to recognize instead of only a corporate brand. The fifth step is gradually connecting the personal brand with the company, so the audience can move from knowing the person to understanding the business relationship. That gradual connection matters because the article does not treat personal branding as separate from company leadership. Jayson Demers argues that the person’s visibility can support the company without replacing the company brand. The individual profile, industry content, social media activity, networking, and public relations opportunities all point toward a practical link between personal trust and business authority. A visible personal brand can build toward tens of thousands of followers.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Introduction Microservices are the hot thing, in the past few years microservices have seemed to gain momentum as a very common architecture. Innovative and successful companies like Netflix and Amazon have adopted it, and for a good reason. The main idea of microservices is to separate the application logic into loosely-coupled services, while each service has its own independent business domain. This concept, if performed accurately, solves a lot of problems that less modern architectures, such as monoliths, didn’t manage to solve: services that are easy to scale, easy to deploy, easy to test and the list goes on… Even so, if I had to pick one reason why this architecture is so popular, I would bet that the reason is the impact on the daily routine of programmers and executives alike. For example, microservices goes hand in hand with the agile methodology. It’s easy to make changes in a single component, which is significant in the way that a programmer can even decide that he is using a different programming language than another developer working on the same system. From the eyes of the executive easy integrations are guaranteed, and in the end everyone is happy. Microservices & Queues We’ve talked enough about microservices, let’s talk a little about message queues. Services can communicate with each other in different ways, and we will not review them all. There is no doubt that the use of queues to communicate is also very popular, again for a good reason too. Many times, when I start the task of designing the architecture for a product or a component, I look at the whole stack. I will be a little romantic for a moment and tell you that I believe there is a reason that certain technologies connect with each other, like a chemical interaction that develops over time. A Specific programming language and a database? A particular operating system and a web server? I’m sure you can think of some! In my humble opinion, this is the very case between message queues and microservices. Queues have a lot of benefits. They make it possible to deal with a temporary downtime of a service — in case there is no consumer messages in a queue will not be consumed, support multiple architectures and can even provide additional features like load balancing and monitoring of the load on each service. RabbitMQ as a Message Broker RabbitMQ is a great message-broker, it’s reliable, built for performance, supports multiple protocols, and has a strong community that makes it easy to find documentation and support. In this article, I will explain how to build a solution based on microservices and queues using the RPC pattern. This is just a one way to do it, and in fact the RabbitMQ website has excellent documentation of 6 different patterns in which you can communicate. Why this pattern? Because I have used it with a real-time system that receives hundreds of thousands of requests per hour and found it elegant, fast, and easy to maintain. Let’s get started!\", \"sampled\": \"Giacomo Pasini’s tutorial is for Apple users who want a repeatable way to organize project materials before the work starts. The idea is straightforward: use iOS Shortcuts to generate the same project folder structure automatically instead of rebuilding it by hand every time. The method is mainly useful within Apple’s ecosystem, not for people working on Android or Windows Phone OS, because Apple’s automation app sits at the center of the workflow. The goal is a one-click tool that creates a project folder tree whenever a new project begins. Pasini presents it as a practical shortcut for content creators who rely on the same kinds of folders again and again. The article also says he plans to provide a screen recording and a downloadable Video Creation Nested Folder Structure Shortcut, both meant to help readers recreate the setup rather than just understand the concept. Folder planning is treated as a way to avoid wasting time hunting for scattered assets. Pasini sums up that frustration with the question, “Where did I saved that piece of information, that note, that picture, that footage, that audio, that #*%’ (fill at your will!) I am now getting so mad at finding out again?” The resources he names include B-rolls, footage, templates, audio, voiceovers, music, outlines, links, pictures, mindmaps, and storyboards. Because those materials can end up in different places, he treats structure as part of the creative process, not just a cleanup job at the end. Pasini recommends creating the resource structure before starting each new project. The point is not only to store finished work, but to give future assets and references a predictable place to land as the project develops. For every new project, he uses an iOS Shortcut that builds a nested folder tree inside iCloud. That repeated layout becomes the starting point, so the project opens with organized storage already ready. The tutorial also notes that a preset master folder can be moved across filesystems, devices, or shared cloud storage. Dropbox and Google Drive are mentioned as cloud storage options, while outlines may be kept in a Google Doc or Notes and saved links in Evernote. Pasini compares iOS Shortcuts to a LEGO set made of Actions that can be combined into workflows. In that model, each Action handles one part of the process, and the completed Shortcut becomes a small automated system for folder creation. The Shortcut can be launched by saying its name after “Hey Siri” or by pressing its button. Once it starts, it asks for a project name and uses that input to create the nested folders in iCloud/Shortcuts/. After creating the folders, it offers to open the Files app or exit with a notification. Pasini says the Shortcut can run through its actions in one fraction of second, making folder setup a repeatable part of starting new work.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"COVID-19 / MEDICINE / HEALTH How Accurate and Reliable is COVID-19 Testing? PCR-based nasal swab testing and serological antibody testing both have certain limitations. As more and more state health departments and private enterprises continue to ramp up testing capacity for COVID-19, several health experts warn that test results are not 100 percent accurate and should be interpreted in the context of clinical presentation and exposure risk. The most commonly used PCR-based nasal swab test to detect SARS-CoV-2 is highly specific but not very sensitive, meaning positive results are more useful than negative results. In other words, a positive result almost guarantees infection with the novel coronavirus but a negative result cannot rule out the presence of infection. “The issue with the tests for the SARS-CoV-2 virus is that there has not been time to test them rigorously before deploying them in the field,” says Dr. Gary L. LeRoy, president of the American Academy of Family Physicians. “Most polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and antibody tests have years of laboratory testing before they are used. We just don’t have that kind of time. The major concern for false negatives is someone who tests negative, thinking they are not infected, could unknowingly spread the virus into the community.” Healthcare worker administers nasal swab test for COVID-19 at a drive-thru facility (Photo by Zstock) An article published in Mayo Clinic Proceedings draws attention to the risk posed by over-reliance on COVID-19 testing to make public health decisions. Priya Sampathkumar, M.D., an infectious diseases specialist at Mayo Clinic and study co-author, writes that healthcare officials should anticipate a “less visible second wave of infection from people with false-negative test results.” Based on preliminary evidence from China, quantitative reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (qRT-PCR) COVID-19 testing on nasal swab samples may produce false negatives up to 30% of the time when testing is conducted 0–7 days after illness onset. After 15 days of illness, the chance of receiving a false negative result shoots up to 50%. That false negative figure may be even higher in the US, according to Harlan Krumholz, M.D., a professor of medicine at Yale. In an opinion piece for The New York Times, Dr. Krumholz expounds: “There are many reasons a test would be falsely negative under real-life conditions. Perhaps the sampling is inadequate. A common technique requires the collection of nasal secretions far back in the nose — and then rotating the swab several times. That is not an easy procedure to perform or for patients to tolerate. Other possible causes of false negative results are related to laboratory techniques and the substances used in the tests…If you have had likely exposures and symptoms suggest Covid-19 infection, you probably have it — even if your test is negative.” Nasal swab sample for COVID-19 test in the laboratory (Photo by Robert Kneschke) Dr. Alain Chaoui, head of Congenial Healthcare, a practice with 50,000 patients across five locations in Massachusetts, told The Boston Globe, “A lot of my patients who have symptoms, who I clinically think have COVID-19, are testing negative.” Chaoui is nonetheless advising all his patients who test negative for the virus to assume they are infected and self-quarantine until symptom-free for at least 72 hours. Michelle Taylor tested negative for COVID-19 twice despite presenting with concerning symptoms, including loss of taste and smell. Several doctors have said the long swabs inserted deep into a patient’s nose could miss the virus if the patient is not showing many symptoms at the time of the test. Dr.\", \"sampled\": \"Terry H. Schwadron’s Oct. 11, 2018, political analysis looked at what Democrats might do if they won control of the House in November. The piece centered on a party that had spent eight years in the House minority and was thinking through what governing would mean. While President Trump spoke at an Iowa rally, Democrats in Washington were considering a possible House agenda. At the rally, before about 9,000 people, Trump called Democrats an “angry mob” and attacked what he described as their “policies of anger, division and destruction.” The article drew a clear contrast: Trump was using rally language, while Democrats were talking about committee work, legislation, and oversight. His fuller line was, “You don’t hand matches to an arsonist and you don’t give power to an angry left-wing mob, and that’s what the Democrats are.” TheHill.com asked prospective Democratic House committee chairs what they would prioritize if the party took the majority. Their answers pointed to legislation on ObamaCare, Dodd-Frank, Dreamers, and election integrity, along with tougher oversight of the Trump administration. The analysis also emphasized a built-in limit: Republicans were expected to keep the Senate. That meant Democratic bills passed by the House could still be blocked, even if Democrats used a new majority to move legislation and set out their policy priorities. Rep. Nita Lowey was described as a possible Appropriations chair focused on domestic spending. Her agenda included more money for medical research, maternity care, home heating, nutrition, education, and opioid response, including a labor-health spending bill increase of $1 billion over 2018 levels. Rep. Adam Smith was identified as a possible Armed Services chair, with priorities centered on closer scrutiny of defense policy. He wanted attention on nuclear weapons spending, special forces deployments, and the ban on transgender military service. Rep. Frank Pallone Jr. was listed as a possible Energy and Commerce chair, giving him a wide health care and energy portfolio. His priorities included health care, drug costs, support for ObamaCare, Medicare drug payments, and energy-related oversight. Rep. Maxine Waters was named as a possible Financial Services chair in the lineup of potential Democratic committee leaders. In the article’s committee-focused view, she was among the lawmakers who would gain power to shape hearings, investigations, and House priorities if Democrats took control. Rep. Bennie Thompson was identified as a possible Homeland Security chair, with an agenda closely tied to immigration, disaster response, and security. His priorities included election security, the travel ban, Hurricane Maria, TSA screening, border enforcement, family separations, and the Wall. Rep. Adam Schiff was named as a possible Intelligence chair, putting intelligence oversight among the areas likely to change under Democratic control. The broader point was that Democrats were preparing for stronger oversight of the Trump administration, not just votes on bills likely to run into Republican resistance. Rep. Jerrold Nadler was listed as a possible Judiciary chair, another position that would carry weight in a Democratic House. The article treated that prospect as part of a larger shift in committee leadership that would let Democrats pursue oversight after years in the minority. Rep. Raúl Grijalva was identified as a possible Natural Resources chair, adding environmental and public resource issues to the committee map. The same account framed these chairmanships as practical clues to how a Democratic House might organize its agenda. Rep. Elijah Cummings was named as a possible Oversight and Government Reform chair, with his role tied to requests Republicans had refused. Cummings and Oversight Democrats had made more than 50 subpoena requests, all denied by Republicans, underscoring why oversight was central to the potential change in House control.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The front end of web has been overwhelmed by JavaScript Frameworks. Just as you’ve learned one, another pops up and we start to question how long this game of “whack-a-mole” can go on for. Or at least, this has been the case for the last few years. There have been a few that have stood from the crowd — often backed by tech giants — and they’ve all got great features to offer. Google maintained Angular is a fantastic tool that shines on large projects by utilizing typescript out-of-the-box, and treating Observables as a first class citizen. Vue — started and maintained entirely in the Open-Source community — has incredible documentation, their own video courses, and has been adopted by the PHP framework Laravel for it’s go to front-end framework. However, this post is about React, and more specifically its horizon. Started and maintained at Facebook, React has a history of maintaining stable releases while also pushing out game changing features. In February of 2019, the React Team made another step toward implementing their Fiber Architecture with the release of “hooks”. This was a fundamental shift in the way React developers produced their apps, with everything moving closer to the functional paradigm. In a strict, academic-Computer-Science sense, JavaScript doesn’t support functional programming, but it does have the properties of a dynamically typed functional language. And React has been steadily moving away from the object-oriented paradigm, and towards this functional approach. It’s important to note here that React doesn’t advertise itself as a framework, in fact, the heading on their homepage is “A JavaScript library for building user interfaces”. So, React is a library for building UI, and is becoming more functional. What does that mean from the perspective of a developer? It means that every piece of your view is just the return value of a function. It means that every data value we calculate, and every piece of business logic we derive, can be the result of a function. Since functions are just JavaScript, it means React is moving closer to its tagline of being a JavaScript library. It means: If you’re good at JavaScript, you’re good at React. This leads to the meat of this article — what is on the horizon for the React Community? Asynchronous — interruptible — rendering. Concurrent Mode is the set of new, still experimental, features being introduced to React. These features are the next logical step in the functional, JavaScript-first, approach the React Team has been taking. Asynchronous functions have long been a part of JavaScript, but up until this time, React has only supported synchronous rendering. When we get the release of the new Concurrent Mode features, we will see another fundamental shift in the way developers write React apps. We will be able to use asynchronous functions, and promises, to drive our rendering: unresolved promises will have a fallback, and once the promise returns a value, we’ll render the component as expected. This release will also see a dramatic improvement in the way users respond to these apps. If you’ll recall, the React Team is sponsored by Facebook, and thus has access to the research and development Facebook performs. Having the world’s two most popular social media sites as a playground provides a lot of opportunity to learn about user interaction. One of the results of this research is outlined in the Concurrent Mode docs: “displaying too many intermediate loading states when transitioning between screens makes a transition feel slower”. This problem will be addressed in the new Concurrent Mode features. As mentioned before, we can use Promises to drive our rendering, but the base behaviour will ensure the user doesn’t experience too many loading states, or more importantly, that the user experiences “intentional loading sequences”. As JavaScript grows, the spirit of that growth is engendered in the community. The move toward Concurrent Mode, and React Fiber in general, is a reflection of the React Team aligning with the JavaScript community.\", \"sampled\": \"Shiv Bajpai published a technology opinion article on 2020-02-20 05:19:20.024000+00:00 arguing that developers should choose Go, also called Golang, over Python in many programming, cloud computing, and machine learning contexts. The piece makes its speed position bluntly in the line, \\\"Cut, of course, Go is faster\\\". Its main case is that Go is a compiled language that can package dependencies into a single binary for a target system, giving developers a cleaner path from source code to deployment than Python often provides. The article also links Go to Google and presents it as a language built for situations where performance, concurrency, and straightforward deployment matter to working developers. Static typing gets particular emphasis because, as the article notes, Go can catch type problems at compile time. Python is framed differently, as a language that may hit runtime type-related exceptions in some cases. That contrast feeds into the author’s wider recommendation to move away from Python. Written in the context of 2020, the article treats the language choice as a practical decision rather than a theoretical preference. Its performance argument is direct: Go is usually faster than Python version 2 or 3 across many application scenarios. The author attributes that edge to multi-threaded capabilities and CPU scalability, not just syntax or personal taste. The Benchmarking Game is mentioned as a performance comparison reference, reinforcing the article’s focus on speed when weighing Go against Python. Concurrency is another major part of the case. Go’s Goroutine feature is compared with Python Threads, with the article saying Goroutines use far fewer resources. The claimed reduction is more than ten times, making concurrency central to the recommendation. That point is tied to applications that need to handle many tasks without heavy resource costs. In the author’s framing, Goroutines are not just a useful feature but a reason to choose Go. The piece also says Go includes native support for Http, JSON, and HTML templates, which it presents as valuable for many web or API projects. Because those capabilities are built in, the article recommends native Go packages over third-party libraries when they are sufficient. Github is still mentioned as a source for third-party libraries, but the preference is for Go’s own packages where possible. Python frameworks are acknowledged, with Django given as an example, though the article still argues that Go can be stronger for services, APIs, and backend performance. JetBrains IDEA is mentioned for Go support, while Webstorm and PHPStorm are noted for plugin support. The article also claims that a friendly IDE can save 80% average programming time, putting tooling alongside language performance in its case for Go. Mozilla serves as a concrete organization example in the discussion. The piece describes Mozilla as moving a large internal logging architecture to Go because of goroutines. After the switch, the logging code is reported as processing a thousand documents per second, connecting the concurrency argument to a specific service-processing result. The article also says a Go rewrite reduced code volume and improved Backend and API service performance, reporting a 64% code reduction and a 30% performance optimization. The debugging contrast includes the exact phrase \\\"why Control-C can’t kill processes\\\" as one Python problem avoided after moving code to Go. Overall, the article keeps returning to a practical comparison: Python can be productive, but the author argues Go is the better choice for many developers. Its case combines compiled binaries, static typing, speed, CPU scalability, Goroutines, native Http support, JSON support, and HTML templates. It also points to Mozilla’s logging architecture as evidence for the value of goroutines. The result is a firmly pro-Go argument built around deployment, performance, concurrency, and service code.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"“There were 5 Exabytes of information created between the dawn of civilization through 2003, but that much information is now created every 2 days”:Eric Schmidt If you are an R user, chances are that you have already been using the data.table package. Data.table is an extension of the data.frame package in R. It’s also the go-to package for R users when it comes to the fast aggregation of large data (including 100GB in RAM). The R’s data.table package is a very versatile and a high-performance package due to its ease of use, convenience and programming speed. It is a fairly famous package in the R community with over 400k downloads per month and almost 650 CRAN and Bioconductor packages using it(source).\", \"sampled\": \"“After silence, that which comes nearest to expressing the inexpressible is music,” Aldous Huxley wrote, and The Mission uses that idea to look at work, motivation, brain activity, emotions, and productivity. The article makes the case that music can help people stay focused on tasks when motivation is low, while raising a broader question about how sound shapes thinking. Research into music, the brain, and emotions has been active since the 1950s. Early modern interest in music therapy is tied to physicians observing hospital patients in Europe and the U.S., while Dawn Kent is cited to explain how music reaches the inner ear and activates multiple regions of the brain.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"For a founder, shutting down a company is like dumping your most beloved person in the entire world; even worse, finding them dead the next day. Failure is a common term for any rising startup. This never ceases to be true even with the overwhelming success stories repeatedly saturating our Quora feed. If anything, the percentage of startups failing in their first year continue to spring up through the last decade. It takes a gallant to be an entrepreneur, as nobody ever built any great company overnight. One has to be courageous enough to say yes when everyone else says no, ignoring statistics others lived by their entire lives. Like all founders, I suffered several terrible blows, got back up, and tried all over again in my quest to building a company. Mine might not be the journey with a pleasant ending, but then I owe my growth to it. I’d continue to regard entrepreneurship as a word on people’s Twitter bio if not for my attempt to pursue the not-so-pretty-adventure of building a company. What happened? It’s been a year since I had to make the bitter decision to shut down my company. It was a marketing consultancy I co-founded with a friend and worked with a team of a few people. We had a good run. Seven months later, we had to close everything down and let go of a startup we all grew so attached to. It was devastating. Looking back at the fuzzy memories now, I cherish how brave it was of me to undertake on such a journey with so little chance of scaling up, to begin with. I also came to see how wrong I was in the numerous assumptions I made while starting. I’ve seen how fundraising takes time — postponed meetings with potential investors drove me mad. It was frustrating, especially taking into account that my most important role as the CEO was to make sure that the company always has the necessary money to run it. Every other startup founder I’ve met told me a similar story, but I’ve never seen it clearer. I’ve also seen how vital it is to have a partner whose strengths complement my weaknesses, even if that means laying my best friend off the ride. Clinging to friends, at the expense of competence, is usually the first mistake many founders make in crashing their company. I’ve come to learn how building a product for a customer is underexplained. Your product can be perfect for the customer that is, unfortunately, in the wrong audience you’re marketing to. This factor aids in the failure of my company. We were so obsessed with offering the best service (our product) that we lost sight of who our ideal customers were. It was like taking a cruise, partying too much on the ship, only to wake up and find ourselves in the wilderness of the sea.\", \"sampled\": \"Brad Stollery published an article on 2018-07-06 that treats soccer penalties as more than a World Cup spectacle. The piece compares penalty-taking with decision-making lessons from poker, using a tense sporting moment to look at how people choose under pressure. Penalty shootouts are framed as a World Cup knockout-stage tiebreaker, where one kick can mean advancement or elimination. From there, the article turns a familiar soccer scene into a study of risk, fear, and judgment. A penalty kick is taken from twelve yards with only the goalkeeper to beat, so from the outside it can look straightforward. The article notes that penalties are scored about 3 in 4 times on average, which still leaves plenty of room for very public failure. The Economist studied World Cup penalties across a forty-year period, giving the article a statistical base for its argument. That analysis found that high shots succeeded more often than low shots overall, even though aiming high brought a different kind of risk. The numbers sharpen the point about where players choose to aim. Penalties aimed halfway up the goal or higher were saved 3% of the time, while 18% of high penalties missed the target and 5% of low penalties missed the target. The article states that high shots had a 79% success rate, compared with 72% for low shots. That comparison supports the idea that the better choice may also be the one that looks worse when it goes wrong. The piece argues that players often aim low because missing the target feels more humiliating than having a shot saved. A low attempt may be less effective in the data, but it can feel safer for the player’s image. Ego bias is identified as a factor in penalty-shot decisions. The argument is not just about technique, but about how fear of embarrassment can push people away from risks worth taking. Lionel Messi and Cristiano Ronaldo are both said to have missed penalty kicks in this World Cup. Their inclusion shows that this pressure is not limited to unknown players or marginal moments. Asamoah Gyan’s penalty in the 2010 World Cup quarterfinals is used as an example of pressure affecting composure. The example fits the article’s larger concern with how a decisive kick can become a test of nerve as much as skill. The broader lesson is that fear of embarrassment can keep people from taking worthwhile risks. By linking soccer penalties with decision-making lessons from poker, the article frames risk as something to judge by value, not by how painful failure might look. The Tottenham Hotspurs motto appears at the end as a compact call for courage: \\\"To dare is to do.\\\" In the context of the piece, that line reinforces intelligent risk-taking and the need to resist choices driven mainly by ego bias.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I don’t think that anyone can claim that evangelicals today have true political power. As The Atlantic said back in July, evangelicals don’t have much to show for their betrayal of Christ as they steadfastly hold onto Trump. They took a gamble and they lost, or to be fair, they won a cruel prize of chaos. Regardless of anything they say, is anybody truly happy in 2020? Do everyday people feel a happiness or peace about politics, the economy, racism, policing, or COVID-19? To say there is a great deal of unrest is, of course, an understatement. But it’s not just the Left that’s been left unhappy. Evangelicals are unhappy too. They think they’re fighting in some holy war against evils like socialism. They turn on Netflix and fear they’re losing a cultural war. Trump’s poor leadership has impacted us all. Further divided us. I daresay it’s even served as the devastatingly perfect experiment for some of the issues discussed in the recent docudrama, The Social Dilemma. Director Jeff Orlowski says the internet is undermining democracy, and that lies spread six times faster online than truth. As it turns out, Trump’s presidency and evangelicalism’s blind spots have converged at this frighteningly ideal point with social media where the facts no longer matter because everybody’s perception of the facts have been clouded by what they want those facts to mean. I wish like hell that I could tell you the solution here is easy, but it’s not. There’s a saying that “You can’t fix stupid but you can vote it out.” Even that is too simplistic, though. Americans are wrestling with mental illness, social media influence, and frankly, stupidity, in a way we’ve never done before. Problems in the world are nothing new. Racism, inequality, and all sorts of political unrest are not issues exclusive to 2020. We shouldn’t be surprised by struggles and unrest, but we should recognize no generation gets through life unscathed. And when we think about American history, not to mention world history, we need to remind ourselves that we are in it. We are making history every damn day. Our actions and errors have consequences with the power to reverberate for generations. It reminds me of the Billy Joel song, “We Didn’t Start the Fire.” After all, we didn’t start the fire. So much of what is happening right now was put into motion long before any of us were old enough to make our own choices. Like me, some of you were raised in an evangelical bubble that stunted your cognitive growth. Maybe it made you more gullible or even “stupid” about certain things. Ironically, there’s no shame (or sin) in being stupid. It’s very human and natural to take the path of least resistance and believe what we’ve been taught when we were very young. And in addition to stunting critical thinking, evangelicalism has had a hand in teaching folks to respond, well, positively to authoritarian parenting and leadership. Of course, authoritarian parenting isn’t limited to evangelicals, but it’s quite common among them and Christians in general. As research professor Peter Gray, Ph.D. writes: “People with an authoritarian mindset believe, first and foremost, in obedience to authority. So, of course, obedience is high on their list of ideal traits for a child; but obedience is also high on their list of ideal traits for people in general. Leaders, especially strong, confident leaders, are to be followed. Authoritarians also tend toward simplistic ways of thinking; things are black or white, right or wrong. If something is right for one person, it should be right for everyone and everyone should see it as right. They don’t tolerate ambiguity and have little taste for subtlety or dissenting opinions. To an authoritarian, the way to solve problems is to find a powerful, confident leader—a sort of superhero who claims in unambiguous language that he can solve your problems—and then follow that person.” I see this weird sort of lust for authoritarian rule a lot, lately. Arguments supporting Trump aren’t debates at all. They’re generally just screaming matches to “get with the program.” For the Trump-supporting, authoritarian-loving person, the President’s critics are just grasping at straws to bring up taxes. They paint any reasonable and valid criticism against Trump as biased or “fake news,” because those are the only options when you lack appropriate critical thinking skills. While it’s no one’s fault to be raised this way, it is up to each individual to not remain stunted in their ability to think critically. Furthermore, we have a responsibility to ensure that younger generations are equipped with the tools they need to think for themselves and acquire the critical thinking skills they will need to make this world a healthier place. This is a big ask. This is going to take work.\", \"sampled\": \"Chinese dissident and artist Ai Weiwei staged a silent protest outside the Old Bailey Court in London during Julian Assange's extradition hearing, drawing attention to a legal proceeding that much of the mainstream press had largely ignored. Standing outside the court, Ai Weiwei spoke plainly about what he believed was at stake, saying of Assange, \\\"He truly represents a core value of why we are free — because we have freedom of the press.\\\" Assange's trial was in its fourth week of witness testimony at the time, with the proceedings centered on whether the WikiLeaks founder should be extradited to the United States to face criminal prosecution. U.S. prosecutors had indicted Assange on 17 espionage charges and one charge of computer misuse, all stemming from WikiLeaks' 2010 publication of secret American military documents. The scale of the legal jeopardy Assange faces is difficult to overstate, as the charges carry a maximum sentence of 175 years in prison. That figure alone sets this case apart from nearly any other press freedom prosecution in recent American legal history, and it has drawn condemnation from journalists and civil liberties advocates around the world. Ai Weiwei did not limit himself to the symbolic gesture of his silent vigil, and he spoke directly about the human dimension of Assange's situation. \\\"He is prepared to fight,\\\" Ai Weiwei said, \\\"but this is not fair to him. Free him, let him be a free man.\\\" Pentagon Papers whistleblower Daniel Ellsberg testified in Assange's defense and directly challenged the U.S. government's core argument that publishing the documents had endangered lives. Ellsberg's testimony carried particular weight given his own history of leaking classified government material and the legal battles he faced in the aftermath. Ellsberg also noted that Assange had withheld 15,000 files from the release of the Afghanistan War Logs, a detail that undermined the prosecution's portrait of Assange as reckless with sensitive information. That decision to hold back tens of thousands of documents suggested a deliberate editorial process rather than an indiscriminate data dump. Assange had requested help from the State Department and the Defense Department to redact names before releasing the documents, but both agencies refused to assist him. That refusal is a significant detail in the defense's argument, since it places responsibility for any potential harm on the government agencies that declined to participate in minimizing risk. No single person has been identified by the U.S. government as having died, been physically harmed, or incarcerated as a direct result of WikiLeaks publications. Rebecca Vincent, director of International Campaigns for Reporters Without Borders reported separately that medical experts had expressed serious concern that Assange could attempt to take his own life while held in detention. The political landscape surrounding Assange has been notable for the bipartisan nature of the hostility directed at him. In 2010, Joe Biden, then a prominent Democratic figure and later the party's presidential nominee, reportedly compared Assange to \\\"a high-tech terrorist,\\\" while Donald Trump's administration pursued the prosecution that produced the current indictment. Video journalist Matt Orfalea observed that the Trump administration issued what he described as the first indictment in history charging a publisher for publishing government secrets. That framing cuts to the heart of the press freedom argument, since the charges against Assange do not hinge on hacking or theft but on the act of receiving and publishing classified material. Jonathan Cook, a U.K.-based reporter and press freedom commentator and Alan MacLeod, a columnist for FAIR were among those who drew attention to the uneven media landscape surrounding the case. Critics noted a stark contrast between the extensive mainstream media coverage devoted to Bob Woodward's book published around the same period and the minimal coverage given to Assange's trial. Chelsea Manning, the U.S. soldier who passed the documents to WikiLeaks had already served time in military detention before receiving a commutation, yet the source of the leak received far more public sympathy than the publisher who made the material available to the world. Kevin Gosztola of ShadowProof was among the few journalists providing sustained, detailed coverage of the trial proceedings from inside and outside the court. The gap between the legal stakes of the Assange case and the volume of press attention it received became a recurring point of criticism among those who argued that journalists should have a professional interest in its outcome. A conviction under the Espionage Act on charges rooted in publishing activity would set a precedent that could reach far beyond WikiLeaks and Assange himself. Ai Weiwei's presence outside the Old Bailey Court placed the case in a global context, linking it to broader struggles over state power and the rights of those who expose government conduct. His protest was quiet but pointed and his words connected Assange's individual legal fight to the larger question of whether press freedom retains any practical meaning when a publisher faces 175 years in prison for publishing documents the public had a clear interest in reading.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Artificial Intelligence has been re-shaping the world as we know it. Not in the way that we saw in the famous movie the Terminator (SkyNet is not going to take over your phone) but it has transformed our everyday lives by improving processes that we perform regularly. The same holds true for the shipping industry. AI has enabled the implementation of IoT devices that gather information that the AI can learn from, improve upon, and make automated decisions. Automation may be the biggest and most beneficial way AI is used in shipping because it enables anomaly detection, reduction in waste, improved quality control, and decreased shipping times. The combination of IoT devices and AI has brought unparalleled improvements to quality control, fuel consumption, and safety. One example of how AI is aiding the shipping industry is how it improves quality control. With the installation of cameras and IoT devices in cargo containers shipping companies can monitor the environment of goods to prevent damage and spoiling. This is very prevalent in the food transport industry. AI monitors data received from cameras and IoT devices about data points such as temperature, humidity, shock(fall), light exposure, and even vibration. This provides a richer bank of information on the quality of goods not only at the beginning and end of transport but during. Through automated alerts and solution suggestions shipping companies can adjust containers to remedy problems in real-time and prevent the waste or degradation of goods. Artificial Intelligence is also allowing shipping companies to manage their fuel consumption and utilize the most efficient shipping routes. The accurate monitoring of how fuel is being used leads to a reduction in fuel spend and brings environmental benefits by decreasing emissions. AI is also being used to improve the physical routes that shipping companies take. Based on historical data on weather patterns like water and wind currents, traffic through certain areas and ports; shipping companies can plan routes that leverage all of this information to reduce fuel consumption and reduce trip times. Lastly, AI can be used to improve safety by monitoring ship systems and the environment around the ship. An example is a ship image recognition system being developed by tech company SenseTime and Japanese shipping firm Mitsui OSK Lines (MOL). They are developing an image recognition system to identify ships in the surrounding area and monitor shipping lanes. This makes transport within bays, from entering to departing ports, much safer, not only for your ship but the surrounding ships as well. The advent of cheaper and faster computing power, combined with the improvements in the accuracy and efficiency of AI models has brought about a lot of improvements to the shipping industry. Reduction in waste, efficient fuel consumption, and improved safety features are just a few of the ways the shipping industry is becoming faster, safer, and more efficient in the present and future.\", \"sampled\": \"Jon Brosio grew up on the outside of the popular social circles that defined high school for so many people. His friend group was small, his social standing was quiet, and the kind of belonging that seemed effortless for others felt distant to him during those years. That experience shaped how he saw himself and what he believed was possible. After high school, rather than staying close to the familiar, he moved away from home and began building something of his own choosing. The path he took was not guided by a prestigious degree or a set of rare skills that most people lack. What followed was a gradual, deliberate effort to construct a life and a livelihood on terms he set for himself and that process eventually led him to write about it publicly in The Ascent on October 17, 2020. Brosio now earns a healthy living doing work he genuinely enjoys, which is a combination that many people treat as a distant aspiration rather than a practical goal. He also spends part of his time helping others build similar lifestyles, working with people who want to move away from work that feels like a poor fit for who they are. One pattern he noticed when looking back at his graduating class stuck with him that many of the unconventional or quiet peers from those years are now living what he would describe as successful lives. He is careful to acknowledge that this observation carries his own confirmation bias and is not a clean or universal generalization that applies to everyone. Still, the pattern felt meaningful enough to name and it shaped the framing of what he wanted to share. The article he published lays out the strategy he followed to build, scale, and grow an online business, offered to the millions of people who like him have no special skills or particular educational background behind their results. Brosio is direct about the fact that no unusual talent or credential explains where he ended up. He did not arrive at a comfortable, self-directed livelihood because of advantages that most people simply do not have access to. What he describes instead is a strategy that a repeatable set of decisions and actions that he followed over time and that produced real results. The absence of special skills is not a footnote in his account but a central part of the argument he is making to the reader. The strategy he promises to share covers building an online business from early stages through growth and scale. He positions this not as abstract advice but as the specific approach he personally used. His background of being outside the popular group and later moving away from home to forge his own path gives that account a grounded texture. The promise of the article is practical: here is what was done, and here is how it worked.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I used to do the same exact thing at the gym, every single week — for years. The same exercises, for the same number of sets. I would simply try to beat the amount of weight I did the week prior, which I would recall by memory. I absolutely didn’t bother to write anything down. Technically, this is a form of progressive overload, and when you’re still a beginner, you can get away with that for a while. Because when you go from doing nothing at all to just about anything, progress will likely be made. But you can only do that for so long before you become stagnant, or worse, get hurt. Once I began implementing proper workout programs, as opposed to just “winging it”, I was able to take my strength & conditioning to the next level. But if I had to do it all over again, I realize now that I could’ve made a lot more progress in a lot less time — if I had followed a properly structured program from the beginning. But no single program is “one size fits all”, and it’s important to know exactly what you’re getting into before you begin one. Consider the following three things before selecting the program that’s right for you. #1 — Assess your fitness level There are many different programs available with varying degrees of difficulty, and the program that you want to do may not be the program that you should do. At least, not at first. The core of every fundamentally sound workout program should be anchored around a few basic movement patterns: a push, a pull, a squat, a hinge, and a lunge. If you’re new to the gym and are unable to properly perform these movements with your bodyweight alone, you shouldn’t jump straight into a program that calls for heavy deadlifts, bench presses, and squats. Rather, opt for a program that teaches you to properly execute these movements in their most basic form — exercises like bodyweight squats and push ups. Once you’ve developed mastery of those movements, then you can gradually progress towards loading those exercises up with additional weight. A progression from a bodyweight push up to a barbell bench press may look something like this: Push ups on an incline bench (note: If you can’t do a pushup, try these instead of doing push ups on your knees. It’s a more natural movement.) (note: If you can’t do a pushup, try these instead of doing push ups on your knees. It’s a more natural movement.) Bodyweight push ups Dumbbell floor press Dumbbell bench press Barbell bench press This progression could be made over the course of several months or even years. It is entirely dependent upon the individual, and how quickly or slowly you progress isn’t important; what is important is that you’re able to “walk before you run”. #2 — Be sure your program aligns with your goals Before beginning a program, have a clear understanding of what and who the program was designed for versus what your specific goals are. If your main goal is to compete on a bodybuilding stage, then a program designed for Olympic weightlifters may not be ideal. I had a friend whose number one goal was to add 50 pounds to his one rep max on the barbell back squat. He purchased the program, ran it for 12 weeks, and was sorely disappointed to find that he was barely squatting 10 pounds more than his previous max by the end of the training cycle. What he didn’t read, which was clearly stated in the program’s description, was that this particular program was designed by somebody with chronic lower back issues, specifically for people who need a way to train their legs without incorporating any heavy barbell work — exactly what my friend was trying to improve. Always have a clear understanding of exactly what you’re doing before you invest the time and the money into doing it. #3 — Autoregulate your training Think of any program you’re on as “written in pencil, not in pen”.\", \"sampled\": \"When a letter from AARP arrived in the mailbox addressed directly to her, Angelica Mordant felt a sharp and sudden reckoning settle over her ordinary afternoon. Her first thought, stated plainly and without ceremony, was \\\"This is it. I'm here. I'm officially old.\\\" Mordant was 53 years old at the time, a number she had carried without much ceremony until that envelope made it feel suddenly official and impossible to ignore. The AARP letter did what calendars and mirrors had not quite managed: it turned an abstract awareness of aging into something concrete sitting in her hands. Her first instinct was to call her sister, the person most likely to understand the specific mix of humor and genuine unease that the letter had stirred up inside her. Her sister is 15 years older than Mordant which meant she had already passed through this particular doorway and come out the other side with perspective to spare. When Mordant told her sister about the AARP letter, the sister's response reframed the situation immediately and with characteristic sharpness. \\\"You think that's bad? I get mail from crematoriums and funeral homes!\\\" she said, which managed to be both funny and oddly clarifying at the same time. The laughter faded into something more earnest when the sister shifted from jokes to genuine counsel, offering words that Mordant clearly took seriously. \\\"Sissy, make your memories NOW. You're wondering where 30 years went? You have 10-15 good years left before you really start slowing down. Enjoy the time you have left. Make these years count.\\\" That advice landed differently than the AARP letter had because it came from someone who had lived the years Mordant was only beginning to reckon with and who spoke from direct experience rather than institutional assumption. The sister's framing of 10 to 15 good years was not meant as a warning so much as an instruction to stop deferring the life she had been quietly postponing. Mordant had been deferring quite a lot, including a long-held dream of owning a pink vintage camper and taking road trips she had been putting off for 30 years. That specific image, the pink camper and the open road, represents the kind of concrete desire that tends to get buried under the practical weight of ordinary life until something like an AARP letter shakes it loose again. She was also honest about the physical realities of being in her 50s, noting changes that included wrinkles, reduced strength, and a shoulder injury she sustained from the simple act of starting a lawnmower. These details are not complaints so much as an acknowledgment that the body keeps its own honest record of time passing, separate from how a person might feel on the inside. On the domestic side of aging, Mordant noted that hosting large family holiday dinners had been passed on to her children, a shift that carries its own quiet significance. Handing over that particular responsibility marks a change in generational role that no letter from any organization could have announced quite so clearly. Rather than letting the AARP letter close the conversation about aging on a deflating note, Mordant responded by compiling a list of 12 reasons why being over 50 carries genuine advantages worth naming. That act of list-making was itself a kind of argument, a deliberate choice to look at the same set of facts and find the case for appreciation rather than dread. She also announced plans to join the Red Hat Society, a social group whose existence is essentially a collective declaration that women past a certain age intend to take up space and enjoy themselves without apology. That plan placed alongside the pink camper dream suggests someone who is taking her sister's advice seriously and translating it into actual intentions rather than vague aspirations. The piece was published on March 7, 2020, at a moment when Mordant's personal reckoning with time had just been catalyzed by a single piece of unsolicited mail. What the AARP letter actually delivered was not a reminder of limitation but a prompt to stop wondering where 30 years went and start being deliberate about the years still ahead.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Therapists Face Different Challenges with Telehealth ”In a moment, saving a bird” Photo by Pierre Bamin on Unsplash The pandemic has forced medical professionals including mental health professionals to rethink how they do their jobs. I called my client promptly at 10 am for an appointment we had scheduled. No answer. I called a few more times. About 10:10, I got this text. ”In a minute, saving a bird.” Ok, then. I like birds. I wonder what kind of bird? I pictured her putting it back in a nest or in a box in a warm place, or? I waited til about 10:30 then got another text “do you have time later today? Well of course I have time but…. Isn’t it my time? Already designated for relaxing with my husband. Or taking a walk or starting dinner? Or just being quiet and alone? Boundaries get blurred The pandemic has forced medical professionals including mental health professionals to rethink how they do their jobs. Since early March, after a scare with a client who had been exposed to the virus, I have been doing therapy from home. Of course, this is safer for me and my family. I am in my 70s and live with a man in his 70s who has several risk factors as well. The thought of sitting in a small room across from people who may not be as careful as I am, talking and gesturing and laughing and crying, (and how do you do therapy with a mask, for Pete’s sake?) sounded like a set up for catching and spreading the virus. I was freaked out in March. I went home, set up an office there, and began using the telephone and internet to connect with my clients. My main goal was to keep talking with my people, to console, encourage, and steady them as we all went through this pandemic for a few months. The few months turned into six with no end in sight. I didn’t want to keep paying rent and utilities for an unused office so decided to give it up. Now I work from home permanently. I set up some office hours but it’s hard to stick to them, because of birds. And other things. So I tell my client, yes, we could talk at 2:30 this afternoon. That’s within my stated office hours. Even though I had hoped to be finished before then, I can deal with it. That’s what I’m here for. Some time before 2:30 she calls to cancel, saying she’s too tired and can we talk tomorrow morning. Tomorrow is Friday, my day off. No, I’m not doing that. My usual policy with people who miss appointments for non-emergency reasons is to have a talk about the value of my time and the need to prioritize their treatment. If this were a client with private insurance I could charge my standard $75 missed appointment fee. This is not possible in this case. So I take the loss of income, because of a bird. But I like birds. Creative excuses When I worked at the mental health practice of the local hospital system, the staff kept a running list of creative excuses clients gave for missing their appointments. Here is a sampling: I missed my appointment because: My dog just had puppies I’m stuck on the rotary and can’t get off I’m expecting my period I might be getting depressed I’m too anxious (the only reasonable one) My kids won’t stop whining To which I could add I’m saving a bird While clients don’t have to drive to see me now, invalidating all the excuses involving flat tires, keys locked in the car, money for gas, and the danger of driving in ice and snow in winter, instead, there are different excuses like this one: I need to get my husband/child breakfast, the internet just went down and Joey has to go online for school, my wife can’t find the car keys, etc. And there are many more interruptions than in my pleasant, private office. I listen to children fighting and ruining their parent’s train of thought; I tolerate cats swishing grandly past the screen, tails high (and close-ups of cat butts). There are the occasional tantrums of younger children, endless arguments with older ones, requests for help from unknowing, and sometimes undressed, spouses. On top of that, there are situations like the long-awaited plumber arriving just as we are about to start. Or a virtual meeting with a doctor rearranged for our appointment time. Of course, if my clients were organized and assertive with good time management skills and clear priorities, meeting with me might be unnecessary. But I know they need help with these things. This is a new age for all of us and most of us are trying our best to stay afloat, help our fellows and figure out creative ways to solve problems.\", \"sampled\": \"Black History Month offers a natural occasion to look closely at African American writers whose work shaped the literary and political landscape of the United States across the twentieth century. Three figures stand out in particular: Margaret Walker, Samuel Alfred Beadle, and Amiri Baraka, each of whom built a body of work that reached well beyond regional or generational limits. Margaret Walker was born in 1915 and died in 1998, leaving behind a legacy that includes a historic first: she became the first black woman to win a national literary prize in America. She was also the first person of color included in the Yale Younger Poets Series, a distinction that announced her talent to the country at large. The Yale Younger Poets Series had been running since 1918 when Walker's collection For My People was selected in 1942 by series editor Stephen Vincent Benét. That selection placed Walker in a tradition of recognized American poetry and gave her work a platform that few black writers of her era had been able to access. Before that recognition, Walker had already been deepening her craft through community and mentorship, joining a Chicago writing group led by Richard Wright. That experience connected her to one of the most serious literary networks of the period and shaped the political awareness that would run through all her later writing. Walker's novel Jubilee appeared in 1966 after nearly three decades of research and drafting, making it one of the most sustained literary projects of its time. The book was translated into seven languages and sold more than a million copies, reaching readers far outside the United States. Jubilee is grounded in the life of Walker's maternal great-grandmother, tracing black experience through slavery, the Civil War, and the years of emancipation. The novel unfolds across fifty-eight chapters, giving the narrative the scope and weight of an epic rooted in personal and historical memory. Walker spent more than twenty years teaching at Jackson State University, where she founded the Institute for the Study of the History, Life, and Culture of Black People. That institution extended her influence beyond the page, creating a scholarly home for research into black history and culture in the South. Samuel Alfred Beadle, who lived from 1857 to 1932, worked as an attorney and storyteller as well as a poet, and his career unfolded in Mississippi during a period when black writers faced severe obstacles to publication and recognition. He was one of only eight black writers in Mississippi whose work gained recognition outside the state in the early twentieth century. Beadle's second poetry collection, Lyrics of the Underworld, appeared in 1912 and carried a distinctive visual element alongside its verse. The volume featured sixteen photographs taken by his son, who went on to become one of Mississippi's best known black photographers, making the book a collaboration across generations and art forms. Amiri Baraka, born Leroy Jones in 1934, built his early reputation in Manhattan's literary world during the late 1950s and early 1960s. He was the only black writer to establish a significant presence in the Beat-inspired literary scene there between 1958 and 1965, navigating a creative environment that was largely white while developing a voice that was distinctly his own. Baraka's play Dutchman won a prestigious Obie Award in 1964, bringing him widespread critical attention and establishing his reputation as a major voice in American theater. The play's confrontational style reflected the tensions of its era and marked Baraka as a writer willing to place race and power at the center of dramatic form. The assassination of Malcolm X proved to be a turning point that radicalized Baraka and redirected the course of his work and public life. He moved away from the predominantly white downtown literary world and toward a more explicitly political engagement with black identity, community, and struggle. Baraka joined the faculty of African Studies at SUNY-Stony Brook in 1980 and taught there for two decades bringing his political and artistic commitments into the academic setting. His major poetry collection Hard Facts stands as one of the key texts from this period of his career, reflecting the ideological seriousness that defined his later writing. Among the honors Baraka received were a PEN-Faulkner Award and the Langston Hughes Medal both in 1989, recognizing the breadth and significance of his contributions to American literature. These awards came during a period when his teaching, writing, and public advocacy were all active and interlocked. Baraka also wrote an appreciation of Margaret Walker among his late works composed after her death in 1998, connecting two of the most consequential African American writers of the twentieth century through his tribute. That gesture acknowledged Walker's foundational role in a tradition that Baraka himself had done so much to extend and challenge. Taken together, the careers of Walker, Beadle, and Baraka trace a long arc of African American literary achievement that moved through poetry, fiction, drama, and scholarship. Each writer worked under different pressures and toward different ends, but all three built work that documented, challenged, and enriched the world they inhabited.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Do you know about the Law of Unintended Consequences? It broadly comes down to this: Any action that involves a complex system is certain to have unintended consequences. This is especially relevant in the field of machine learning where we are working with highly complex software. Machine learning systems almost always have unintended side-effects. Here’s a beautiful example. Consider a Deep Convolutional Inverse Graphics Network, or DCIGN. It looks like this: It may look complicated, but a DCIGN is actually made up of a convolutional neural network (CNN) and a deconvolutional Network (DN), mounted end-to-end. The first half reads in an image and converts it to abstract information called a ‘feature map’. The green nodes in the middle can then make subtle changes to the map, and the second half takes the modified map and reconstructs it back into an image. So in a nutshell a DCIGN reads in an image, makes changes, and produces a new image. The most famous DCIGN is CycleGAN, which can do mind-blowing stuff like this: Look at the Monet –> photo conversion. A hundred years ago, Claude Monet saw that river scene and turned it into a painting. CycleGAN just ran that process in reverse, and reconstructed a photorealistic image from his painting. Isn’t that mind-blowing? It seems as if you can do anything with CycleGAN. So researchers at Google and Stanford asked themselves if you could use CycleGAN to map satellite photos to street maps and back again. The results exceeded their wildest expectations. Look at this: A perfect mapping from an aerial photo to a street map and back again. Amazing, right? But look closer. Look at that white building at the bottom of the image. See all those skylights on the roof? They’re not in the middle image! And yet, they reappear in the final image. So how did the second half of the DCIGN network know where to put them all back? Common sense tells us that this is impossible. And our common sense is correct. The AI actually cheated! The researchers gave the software an impossible task, and it found a loophole and cheated. Let’s start with the basics. We know that mapping aerial photos to street maps and back again is impossible, because we lose information when we convert the photo to a street map. Subtle details like the position of skylights and smokestacks on roofs are lost. And yet, the software managed to reconstruct a perfect aerial photo. So it somehow managed to hide all these details in the middle image without us noticing. Let’s take a closer look at the middle image. We’re going to crank up the contrast to reveal small color differences: Do you see that? There’s actually a wealth of information hidden in the map image, encoded as tiny color changes that are imperceptible to human eyes. You can see lots of red pixels clustered around the white building. These are used by the second half of the DCIGN to draw the skylights in the correct place on the roof. This is the equivalent of the AI cheating and secretly writing down all the test answers to pass an exam To test their theory, the researches played a trick on the AI. They used a different map image, but overlaid it with the ‘cheat notes’ from the original map. If the AI really is cheating, it’s going to ignore the new map and reconstruct the original aerial photo from the cheat notes. And here are the results: And that proves it. The software is only using the cheat data to reconstruct the aerial photo, it’s completely ignoring the street map. So here’s the moral of this story: Be very careful when you are building machine learning solutions, because computers will always try to give you the result you ask for. If you give them an impossible task, they will simply find a loophole and cheat. If the results of your AI app seem too good to be true, they probably are. So always make sure to include a test that catches your software off guard to expose any cheating. How do you feel about AI’s cheating? Do you think it’s funny, cute, or scary? Add a comment and let me know!\", \"sampled\": \"John Ridley Stroop was born in 1897 as the second youngest of six children and spent his early years on a family farm in Tennessee, a rural upbringing that gave little indication of the psychological legacy he would leave behind. He went on to study experimental psychology and education, graduating at the top of his class and demonstrating the kind of academic discipline that would shape his later research. Stroop's most enduring contribution is a deceptively simple test that measures a person's ability to name the ink colour of a word that spells a different colour name and the results consistently show that this incongruence slows response times in measurable ways. The test captures something fundamental about how the human brain handles competing streams of information, forcing the mind to suppress one well-learned process in order to complete another. When people are asked to read the word itself while ignoring the ink colour, the interference largely disappears which suggests that reading words is a more automatic process than identifying colours from visual input. This asymmetry points to a hierarchy in perceptual processing, where some cognitive tasks run more fluently than others and therefore resist disruption more effectively. Stroop's research did not emerge in isolation but built on earlier work initiated by Wilhelm Wundt, who directed his student James Cattell to investigate the relationship between colour naming and colour-word reading in controlled conditions. That lineage of inquiry gave Stroop a foundation to work from, and his own experiments refined and formalized the interference phenomenon in ways that earlier researchers had not fully articulated. The effect Stroop documented is not limited to colour words and coloured ink. It extends to direction words displayed in incongruent locations or orientations on a screen, showing that the underlying mechanism of cognitive interference operates across different categories of meaning and spatial perception. Research has also examined incongruence involving numbers, and one study found that numerical stimuli differing in both their numerical value and their physical size on screen caused participants to identify them more slowly than congruent versions. The interference pattern holds even when the competing dimensions are entirely abstract, which points to how broadly the brain applies meaning-based processing during perception. Emotional words introduce a related but distinct layer of complexity into the classic Stroop task, slowing colour naming in ways that neutral words do not and they are also recalled more easily when participants face unexpected memory tests afterward. This dual effect suggests that emotional content captures attention during processing and leaves a more durable trace in memory than neutral material does. A study examining faces of men, women, and houses paired with either congruent or incongruent labels found that participants remembered the incongruent pairings better than the congruent ones. That finding aligns with the broader pattern in which mismatched information demands more from the perceptual system and, in doing so, becomes more firmly encoded. The explanation for why incongruent stimuli are better remembered has been described directly by researchers: \\\"Transient shifts of attention in response to perceptual processing difficulty … appear to strengthen the encoding of incongruent items as they are processed.\\\" That account frames the memory advantage not as a quirk but as a predictable consequence of the extra cognitive work that incongruence requires. Colin MacLeod identified what he saw as the twin sources of Stroop's lasting influence: \\\"Its large and always statistically reliable effect and the lack of an adequate explanation for the effect.\\\" That combination of empirical robustness and theoretical openness has kept researchers returning to the phenomenon across decades, applying it to new domains and stimuli. Outside the laboratory, Stroop spent most of his career teaching at David Lipscomb College and preached each Sunday, living a life that ran parallel tracks between academic work and religious commitment. The contours of that career reveal a person whose interests extended well beyond the single experiment that made his name recognisable in psychology. By the end of his life, Stroop had published 7 biblical books compared to just 4 psychology papers, a ratio that reflects how much of his intellectual energy went into theological writing rather than experimental science.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Image by Kevinsphotos from Pixabay Telling a lie is forgivable, but living a lie is not. By constructing your world from lies, only destruction will come. Even the smallest lie, if left unacknowledged, can kill any form of love. Occasionally, I’ll ask people if they’re honest. Are you a liar? I’ll say. Pointless question, right? The dishonest would just lie about it. More revealing is how people think about their own lies — the specifications they give. How often? When? The details mean more. The reasons are way more interesting. The most dangerous liars aren’t the ones who lie all the time. The real shits are the ones who lie all the time AND claim to be terrible liars. Oh, I suck at lying. I’m incapable of telling anything but the truth. Be wary of anyone who says this. They’re the same people who say they don’t understand the point of telling a lie—that such a reprehensible quality is simply beyond them. Everyone knows the point of lying — it’s to distort reality. The real honest people — the truth seekers, the ones with integrity, the people who are self-aware — these people usually admit to it when it matters most. When the stakes are high, when the cost involves hurting another — when the effects actually mean something, the honest people come clean. They don’t double down. They want to purge themselves of this awful tendency. They would feel appalled by how much they’ve lied, dismayed to the point of guilt and disappointment. So, ultimately, I don’t fear the casual liar, because I am one. All writers are. It’s a very human thing. It’s a very real thing. Anyone who uses language can frame something in such a way that it no longer resembles the truth. The people I really fear are the ones who have so thoroughly convinced themselves of their own honesty that they lose all ability to even recognize the most obvious deceit within themselves. It is the self-deceiver who is most disconnected from reality and causes the most damage and pain to others.\", \"sampled\": \"One of the most practical principles in software development is limiting the scope of variables and references as much as possible, a discipline that Nick Hodges examines in detail in his article published in Better Programming. When a variable's scope is kept narrow, the number of places in a codebase where that variable can be modified shrinks considerably making the code far easier to reason about and trace when something goes wrong. Global variables sit at the opposite end of this spectrum, and they are widely considered harmful precisely because there is no reliable way to know where or when they might be changed across a large program. Object-oriented programming addresses this problem directly through encapsulation, which formalizes the process of limiting scope by bundling data with the methods that are permitted to act on it. Information hiding takes this idea a step further by restricting variable scope so that values cannot be changed except through explicitly allowed pathways, giving developers a controlled and predictable interface rather than an open field of potential modifications. This kind of discipline also reduces the ability for code to create unwanted dependencies between classes which is a quieter but equally important benefit of keeping scope tight. Loose coupling, the deliberate limiting of dependencies within a system, is considered a sound practice because it keeps individual components independent enough to be understood and modified in isolation. Tight coupling produces the opposite effect, making code hard to test and difficult to maintain as the system grows and requirements shift over time. A related hazard is what developers call action at a distance, the situation where a change made in one area of a codebase produces unintended side effects in a completely separate area often in ways that are difficult to diagnose. Hodges points to declaring const whenever possible as a subtle but effective language-level technique for limiting scope, since it signals intent clearly and prevents accidental reassignment.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Improve Your Future By Looking To The Past: I could spout on and on about why reducing your carbon footprint will benefit you. But I’ll keep my reasoning simple: Anytime you can apply an understanding of human evolution to your present-day life, you will be better off. We evolved to live in harmony with nature. We evolved to mostly eat lower on the food chain — think fruits and vegetables, not factory-bred meat and sugar-laden processed foods. We evolved to be on our feet, not on a comfortable couch or an ergonomic desk chair. We evolved to spend time outside. We evolved to derive pleasure from the simple things in life — socializing with friends and loved ones, not scrolling on a screen to find edited pictures to like or things to buy that you don’t need and are probably ecologically harmful. We evolved to live life in the slow lane, not the fast lane. We evolved to live surrounded by plants and rocks, not concrete and asphalt. Your ancestors lived very sustainable lifestyles. It was good for them, their neighbors, and the finite planet they lived on. The more you can emulate your ancestors in this respect, the better off you’ll be. Maybe that goes against the gospel of progress and futurism, but I’m a bit skeptical that many of the technological advancements we’ve made lately as a civilization — cars, TVs, smartphones, etc — have truly heightened our sense of well-being. Call me crazy, but sometimes I wish I could enter a time machine and go back 10,000 or 20,000 years. Sure, Stone Age people had hard, risky lives back in the day. They toiled to find clean food and water. They faced harsh conditions and were always vulnerable to violence and predation. Trouble awaited them with every step and every breath. Nonetheless, I don’t think they were significantly worse off than we are today. They didn’t pollute their air and poison their water with deadly chemicals. They didn’t stare at screens all day and play dangerous fake comparison games with each other. They didn’t ‘buy’ things they didn’t need. They didn’t eat processed crap. They didn’t lounge around and sit on their asses. They didn’t build concrete cages divorced from nature. They lived life to the fullest. And so can you. You can choose to adopt many of their wise practices, but you can benefit from the cars, antibiotics, supermarkets, and smartphones we take for granted. You can choose to embrace the aspects of modern life that are good for you and embrace the aspects of ancient life that are good for you. You have the best deck of cards any generation has ever had. If you play your hand correctly, you can live a better life than anyone ever has. And your lifestyle will become a smaller slice of the carbon footprint pie. Spend some time thinking deeply about what aspects of modern life truly benefit you. Then, think about what the ancients did that you too can do to cultivate your well-being. Take advantage of the pandemic to reconnect with nature and thereby reconnect with yourself. You may never have a better opportunity to bridge the gap between your authentic self and the inauthentic aspects of your self you present to the world.\", \"sampled\": \"Consumer choice alone will not solve the climate crisis. A UNEP report makes clear that past sustainable transitions were not driven by people voluntarily deciding to consume more responsibly. Social norms and changing product availability have been the main causes of substantial behavioral change. The richest 1% are identified as the primary target group for the lifestyle changes needed to meaningfully reduce greenhouse gas emissions across the global economy. Urban planning in the developed world has long catered to a car-dominant society, shaped largely by the accessibility wealthy individuals have always had to private vehicles. It is not uncommon for a wealthy household to own two vehicles and that pattern of ownership has quietly dictated how cities are built and how infrastructure budgets are spent. Roads expand, parking lots multiply, and public transit is treated as secondary. Reducing transportation emissions requires urban planning that deliberately emphasizes walkable and bikeable cities rather than continuing to optimize for the car. That shift demands political will not just consumer preference because the built environment does not change simply because a handful of individuals decide to walk more. Governments and planners must redesign the physical landscape to make low-emission choices the default. The infrastructure itself has to change before behavior reliably follows. Social influence acts as a real catalyst for lifestyle change through peer pressure and the gradual challenging of societal norms. That same mechanism has historically worked against green initiatives by making wealthy lifestyles appear desirable and worth emulating. Disrupting habitual behavior is described as necessary to break down the barriers that keep people locked into unsustainable routines. Incentives, targeted information and expanded choice have all been deployed to nudge people toward sustainable lifestyles, but the UNEP report is candid that these tools are rarely sufficient for the kind of profound change the climate situation demands. Achieving the objectives of the Paris Agreement requires deep-rooted socioeconomic, cultural, political, and behavioral changes that involve both wealthy individuals and the governments that set the rules around them. Infrastructure improvements such as electric vehicle charging ports and efficient public transport networks are necessary to support sustainable lifestyles at scale. Integrated policies that combine structural, social, contextual, and personal approaches are proposed as the framework for encouraging change among the wealthiest. Social norms shift when the environment around people shifts first. That combination of policy and infrastructure is what the UNEP report points toward as the realistic path forward. Neither goodwill nor individual guilt is a sufficient engine for the scale of change required. Personal virtue matters far less than structural context. The UNEP report is explicit that social norms and product availability have historically driven the most significant and lasting behavioral shifts in society. Achieving Paris Agreement objectives requires changes that run far deeper than shopping habits, cultural values, political systems, and the everyday behavioral patterns of the world's wealthiest people. Wealthy individuals shape demand signals that ripple through entire economies and the lifestyles they normalize tend to set aspirational benchmarks for everyone else. Governments cannot treat this dynamic as a background condition and expect voluntary nudges to compensate for it. Integrated policies that address social, contextual, and personal dimensions simultaneously offer a more credible path than any single-lever approach.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Streamlit Streamlit Demo — Source Do you want to create dashboards quickly in Python?. — Streamlit is your best option. Streamlit revolutionises creating web applications with easy to use API and constant feature development. It was only last year October when this open-source tool was launched and no doubt its popularity has increased rapidly in the data science community. Today, Streamlit boosts more functionalities with its recent introduction of streamlit component, where the developer community adds new functionalities. Sharing and Deploying Streamlit apps has also become super easy with the new one-click deploying service from streamlit (In Beta). You can now develop and create web applications and dashboards, and deploy them in minutes rather than days, Thanks to Streamlit. What I like about streamlit is that it has the shortest learning curve of all Python Dashboard creating tools in this list. It offers simple API with excellent documentation and lets you develop applications with less code in pure python. In simple terms, Streamlit empowers you to focus on what matters rather than thinking about front-end back-end technology stacks to use for your project. Panel Panel Gapminder Demo — Source Do you want to create powerful and advanced dashboards in Pure Python with declarative and reactive programming? — Panel is your best bet. Panel is an open-source Python library that lets you create custom interactive web apps and dashboards by connecting user-defined widgets to plots, images, tables, or text. While it is possible to work Streamlit in Jupyter notebooks, we use primarily with Python files. If your favourite data science tool is Jupyter Notebook, then Panel offers extensive support for all plotting libraries. The learning curve is steeper than Streamlit. However, it is simple to create an interactive web application in Panel, using less code with widgets and parameters. Deploying and sharing your web applications and dashboards in Panel is easy. You can display your dashboards inside Jupyter Notebooks, render it as Ipywidgets, run it from the command line, or deploy it using popular tools like Heroku, MyBinder or even other cloud platforms. Voilà Voilà Demo — Source. Do you want to turn your Jupyter Notebooks quickly into standalone web applications? Voilà is for your service. Voilà — Rendering of live Jupyter notebooks with interactive widgets. Voilà is primarily a native Jupyter rendering tool. However, you can create interactive reports with widgets in Jupyter notebook using Ipywidgets. You can also use Viola to render any content on Notebooks into Dashboards. Quick Jupyter notebook deployment into dashboards is the strong side of Viola but also its downside. You can make coherent Dashboards with Voilà, but that needs you to format your experiments and code in Jupyter notebooks accordingly or suppress and hide unused code and markdowns. Plotly Dash Plotly Dash Demo — Source Do you need more advanced and production-grade level dashboards in Python? — Plotly Dash covers that. Plotly Dash focuses on production and enterprise dashboard creation but also offers free and open-source options in Python, R and Julia. It is the most mature option on this list. Although Dash empowers users to build and deploy web applications without full-stack and DevOps tools in hours, it has the steepest learning curve of all the options in this article. That is also changing with the introduction of Plotly Express — easy to use and high-level API for creating figures with Plotly Libary. I find the Plotly Express library one of my favourite data visualisation tools in Python. Deploying your Plotly Dashboards in your local environment is easy, and you need to deploy it to a server if you need to share it outside. Final Thoughts The choice of your dashboard tool depends on your project needs. Streamlit is for creating quick and versatile dashboard apps with an easy learning curve and less code. I find Streamlit the easiest and the best tool out there to create data visualisation web apps.\", \"sampled\": \"Most productivity advice feels compelling at first, offering tidy systems and step-by-step frameworks that promise to transform how you work, yet Niklas Göke argues that nearly all of it fails over the long run not because the techniques themselves are broken, but because the entire body of conventional advice rests on false assumptions about human nature. Those assumptions quietly undermine every checklist and time-block schedule before the second week is out, leaving people wondering why the method that worked for someone else refuses to work for them. The deeper problem is that conventional productivity culture ignores how humans actually function at a biological and psychological level and in doing so it neglects the true purpose that productivity is supposed to serve in a person's life. When the foundation is wrong, every technique built on top of it inherits that wrongness and the result is a cycle of enthusiasm followed by exhaustion followed by guilt that repeats itself indefinitely. Human brains are wired to seek problems rather than rest in calm, which means that the moment one difficulty is resolved the mind naturally begins scanning for the next issue to fix and this restless orientation is not a personal flaw but a feature of how cognition is structured. Calm is not the default state that productivity systems tend to assume it is, and any approach that treats a clear to-do list as a finish line misunderstands the biological reality of the person trying to use it. Compounding this problem is the role of dopamine, which rewards the completion of even small goals and creates a pull toward chasing more tasks regardless of whether those tasks serve any meaningful priority. That neurological reinforcement loop explains why people can spend an entire day crossing items off a list and still feel oddly hollow at the end, because the brain's reward system and a person's actual values are not always pointed in the same direction. People also tend to overestimate how much they can accomplish within a given stretch of time and underestimate how long individual tasks will take, a well-documented pattern that Göke attributes to the planning fallacy, and this bias is baked so deeply into how people plan their days that it operates largely below conscious awareness. The result is a perpetual gap between the day that was imagined on Sunday evening and the day that actually unfolds on Monday, and most productivity frameworks offer no honest accounting for that gap. Göke redefines productivity in a way that absorbs this reality rather than fighting it, describing it as making good-enough progress on what one cares about most amid the unavoidable chaos of life, which shifts the measure of success away from volume of output and toward alignment with genuine priorities. Paired with this redefinition is his argument that managing expectations of time matters more than managing time itself, because a person who holds realistic expectations can adapt when things go sideways rather than treating every disruption as a personal failure. The practical shape of this approach involves building a system optimized for minimum stress rather than maximum effort, one that includes buffers and deliberate room to fail so that the inevitable interruptions of ordinary life do not collapse the entire structure. Accepting that life involves chance, coincidence, and luck is presented not as defeatism but as a prerequisite for seeing productivity with a genuinely new purpose, because a system that cannot bend will eventually break the person relying on it. Göke locates the site of true productivity not in external outputs or completed task lists but in the mind itself arguing that the internal state a person maintains while working is the actual substance of what productivity means. A stress minimizing approach, produces greater happiness, less burnout, and more calm, which are outcomes that the conventional hustle-oriented model rarely names as goals at all, even though they are precisely what most people are hoping to find when they first go looking for a better way to work.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Microsoft recently released a New Dialogue Ranking Pretrained Transformers model. Some human replies are more engaging than others, spawning more followup interactions. Since we want our conversational models to be more interactive we need some metric to know which comment is more likely to engage the user. So authors of DialogRPT trained it on 133M pairs of human feedback data to address this issue and the resulting ranker outperformed several baselines. If you are not interested in paper summary directly jump to the code. Why do we need such a pretrained transformer model? Human-like conversations are perhaps one of the most difficult challenges of AI. Now with more recent advancements in the field some times human annotators cannot reliably distinguish between human- and machine-generated responses. However human responses are not limited to be just relevant to the context. Sometimes they are interesting enough to prompt a rich listener reaction. (Although conversation can be sometimes very boring, heated, funny and awkward🤭).On the other side Chatbots or Conv-AI models just to prevent them from creating inappropriate comments often end up being vague, not engaging. GIF via Giphy A successful dialog turn must be proactive, engaging, and consistent with social norms. The solutions that authors proposed is using existing human feedback data (e.g., number of replies and likes) from online social communities(here Reddit). While there has been work on feedback prediction, this is the first time it has been applied to a dialogue and response generation system. Fig 1. Example of an engaging Dialogue. Courtesy: DialogRPT Let's dive deep on how the model solves the issue of engaging comments. Posts and comments typically form a tree structure. Each comment has its own number of replies and upvotes (refer Fig 1. Upvotes also termed as “Likes” in some social communities). These can be used as engagingness labels after careful normalization and formulation. Using a dataset of 133M pairs of human comments and their associated number of replies or up-/downvotes, authors train a set of large-scale transformer-based feedback ranking models which outperform several baselines. Understanding feedback metrics Posts and comments typically form a tree structure. Consider parent node as context c and the reply to it as r. For each dialogue (c, r), we consider the following feedback: Width, the number of direct replies to r; Depth, the maximum length of the dialogue after this turn; and Updown, the number of upvotes minus the number of downvotes. The feedback metrics defined above cannot be directly used as a measure of reply engagingness. Studies show that while popularity, measured by Updown, generally increases with quality, posts of similar quality can exhibit very different upvote counts. Tasks Given a context and a list of responses, the task of predicting a ranking based on the feedback they received, as measured by these three separate metrics: (1) Width, (2) Depth, and (3) Updown. Along with this an additional task (4)human-vs-fake, which measures how human-like the response is. Problem Formulation and Training objective Contrastive learning approach was used while training the model for the task. A Contrastive Learning approach: Given the confounding factors affecting feedback mentioned above, we train the model on pairs of samples (c, r+) and (c, r−), rather than fitting it to score each dialogue individually. The model is trained in such a way that predicts a higher score for a better response than the less appropriate response. Model ensemble For machine generation: The machine generation is required to be both human-like and preferred by a human. To rank the machine generations, the authors took the probability of joint distributions as follows : P(r = preferred, human-like|c) = P(r = preferred|r = human-like, c)· P(r = human-like|c) Human calibration: To estimate the correlation between the feedback score and human responses preferences, pair of responses for the same context to a set of human annotators, asking them to select the response they would prefer to send or receive. Model and training The model is a 12-layer transformer model based on GPT-2 architecture and initialized with DialoGPTmedium model weights. DialoGPT is a large-scale dialogue response generation model, pre-trained on 147M Reddit conversations. Each model ( for up-down, width, depth, human vs rand) has 354.8M parameters and is trained on an Nvidia Tesla V100 4-core GPU with batch size 256 at an average training speed of 0.33 M pairs of samples per hour. Each model took around 70 hours to converge (until validation loss on a fixed set of 1024 samples ceased to improve). Findings Responses that receive fewer replies or upvotes tend to be less contentful. In contrast, comments that attract more feedback are typically different in character: for instance, questions (indicated by ?, why, how, what, who) often lead to a longer conversation (greater Depth). The model trained only on Width data can perform reasonably well on Depth prediction, and vice versa, consistent with the high correlation between their labels. The Updown label is less correlated with these, and so the model trained on Updown data performs poorly on Width and Depth data. This is in keeping with the complementary relationship between these models.\", \"sampled\": \"Standard Cognition has built its retail technology around computer vision that enables autonomous checkout in physical stores, relying solely on overhead cameras rather than shelf sensors or RFID tags. This approach removes friction from the shopping experience while generating a continuous stream of data that the engineering team must analyze quickly to keep the platform accurate and competitive. Tushar Dadlani, computer vision engineering manager at Standard Cognition, described the pressure his team operates under when he said, \\\"The team at Standard is always looking to increase the accuracy of the computer vision platform and add new features to the product. We need to be able to drive product improvements from conception to production rapidly, and that involves being able to run experiments and analyze real-time metrics quickly and simply.\\\" That need for speed pushed Standard Cognition toward Rockset, a cloud data service the company now uses for prototyping and internal analytics to support rapid product iteration. Relationship between the two reflects a deliberate choice to prioritize agility over the slower cadence that traditional data infrastructure tends to impose on engineering teams. One of the practical advantages Rockset offers is that it does not require schema definition at the time of ingest, instead supporting fast SQL queries through a schema-on-read approach. This flexibility matters because Standard Cognition regularly ingests data in CSV or Parquet format, each file carrying its own custom schema, using the Rockset Write API to bring that data into the system. Standard streams event data, vendor data, and debug logs into Rockset to generate the accuracy metrics the computer vision team depends on daily. Having all of those data types flowing into a single queryable environment means engineers can examine relationships across sources without waiting for separate pipeline jobs to complete. Rockset allows Standard to run ad hoc queries joining across data sets in real time rather than waiting for asynchronous data lake jobs to finish processing. That difference in latency changes how engineers approach investigation, since a question that once required scheduling a batch job can now be answered in the time it takes to write a SQL query. The prototyping workflow at Standard Cognition follows a clear path from experiment to production, with engineers building experimental APIs using the Rockset Node.js client before converting mature features into serverless functions using Google Cloud Functions. This staged approach lets the team validate logic and performance in a low-overhead environment before committing to the infrastructure costs of a full production deployment. Standard productionizes several API endpoints per day using this rapid prototyping methodology with Rockset, a pace that would be difficult to sustain if each new feature required significant infrastructure setup or performance tuning before it could be tested. The combination of Rockset's flexible ingestion and the Node.js client creates a short path between an idea and a working endpoint. Dadlani spoke directly to this benefit when he noted, \\\"Using Rockset in our development environment gives us the ability to perform ad hoc analysis without a significant investment in infrastructure and performance tuning. We have over two thirds of our technical team using Rockset for their work, helping us increase the speed and agility with which we operate.\\\" That figures over two thirds of Standard Cognition's technical team relying on Rockset, reflects how broadly the platform has been adopted across the organization rather than remaining confined to a single specialized group. When a data tool spreads that far across an engineering organization, it tends to indicate that the tool is solving a real and recurring problem rather than a narrow one. The company's demonstration store showcasing AI-powered checkout provides a concrete environment where the accuracy of the computer vision platform is visible and measurable in real conditions. The metrics generated from that environment feed back into the analytics workflow that Rockset supports, creating a loop between observed store behavior and engineering decisions. Standard's next project will introduce user behavior event streams into its analysis using Rockset's SQL engine, extending the kinds of questions the team can ask about how shoppers move through a store and interact with products. Connecting behavioral data to the existing streams of event and inventory data should give engineers a richer picture of where the computer vision platform performs well and where it needs refinement. The schema-on-read design of Rockset becomes especially relevant as Standard prepares to add new event stream types, since each new data source can be ingested without requiring the team to define and maintain an additional schema before querying begins. That flexibility reduces the overhead of onboarding new data, which matters when the goal is to move from data collection to insight as quickly as possible. Rockset illustrates how a company building at the intersection of computer vision and physical retail can manage the data complexity that comes with operating AI systems in live store environments. The combination of fast ingestion, real-time SQL queries, and a prototyping workflow that reaches production several times a day gives the engineering team the operational rhythm it needs to keep improving the platform.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"2. Programmers Shouldn’t Be Insecure About Their Work Now, I’ve got some really skilled senior developers working with me. They are competent individuals who require high standards from my pull requests. Otherwise, they will easily reject them without remorse. I used to think of these people as developers not capable of doing any wrong. I would look at them in awe and wonder if one day I would reach their level of skills and never code anything incorrectly. But this belief was wrong. It didn’t take much for me to gain more experience and dialogue with my colleagues, only to realize that they have insecurities too. They don't always know everything and are not always sure about the best possible solution for a given problem. They have to constantly renew their knowledge like I have to. They know that sometimes they just have to go with the flow and their intuition on what is best for the project. I’ve freed myself from this belief and now I don’t always feel stuck in my own world thinking that I will never be a senior developer because I know that seniors have my feelings of insecurity too.\", \"sampled\": \"Diane Lee posted an essay about dating a much younger Vietnamese man. She shared it with a Facebook group of women writers while living in Hanoi. The relationship lasted 10 months, and Lee says the man turned out to be a covert narcissist who almost killed her. Her essay described his manipulation, including his physical features and what she called reptilian behavior. Commentators responded with outrage directed at those details. Critics accused her of eroticising her ex-partner and positioning herself as a fearful victim rather than examining the relationship with distance. They also said she needed to address Vietnam's troubling history and colonialism that she should not be analysing her ex's words and actions, and that she required formal training in memoir writing before publishing such a piece. Three of those critics work professionally as a cultural critic, an editor, and a writing coach. Lee argues that all of this commentary missed the central point of her essay which was that she had experienced narcissistic abuse. She questions whether the same criticism would have appeared if the man had been an older white man rather than a younger Vietnamese one.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Top Headline # 1 — This Is the Best Way To Think About … Analyser Score: 85 points. This is one headline type that points to the highest utility of thinking. There are many ways to skin a cat. And this is the best way. You help readers save their time by presenting the best way ahead. It could be a short-cut or a life trivia. In short, this is the way ahead. Top Headline # 2 — This Is How I Got The Most Out Of … Analyser Score: 82 points. This shows the reader that you are an experimental scientist, and you found a way to extract the most value out of an activity. This is about extracting and retaining the most information from a book, for instance. It is highly valued by students who are coping with their examinations. Top Headline # 3 — How To … And Get The Most Out Of … When You Have No Time … Analyser Score: 87 points. As with the Top 2, this category is about showing the short-cut with a time dimension. What if all we have is 10 minutes? What is the best way to get the most out of a gym workout over a 30-minute lunch break? People who struggle with time control will be attracted to such headlines. Top Headline # 4 — How To Be … All The Time … Analyser Score: 84 points. This headline is about life lessons, in my opinion. Try inserting different words in between “To Be” and “All The Time.”. You will realize that positive words make sense. Negative words do not. Take, for instance, “How To Be Calm All The Time.”. It showcases life lessons accumulated over time. Many people need it. I need it. Thanks for writing it. Top Headline # 5 — What You Can Do When You Are Sick And Tired Of … Analyser Score: 84 points. This headline deals with overcoming negativity.\", \"sampled\": \"I wake up most mornings feeling like I only have half the energy I carried before the pandemic, and the simple act of getting out of bed has become a genuine struggle. That depleted state follows me through the early hours, making even a short morning walk feel like a negotiation with my own body. The walk itself should be a small reprieve, but I find myself dreading the possibility of running into a pushy neighbor whose presence demands more social energy than I have to give. My friendships have suffered under this same weight, because maintaining connections with people I care about requires a vitality I can no longer reliably summon. I am afraid of losing my close friendships with Sunny and Frank, two people who have anchored me in ways that feel increasingly fragile right now. The woodland that once offered me a steady refuge has become harder to reach, and I cannot access its quiet paths as often as I used to before everything shifted. This season I saw owls only once, and I observed no babies at all, a silence in the trees that mirrors a deeper absence in my own life. My family relationships, which felt close and present just a couple months ago, have grown strained and distant during the long months of the pandemic, leaving a hollow space where warmth used to be. I have lost contact with Alex, and the reality that I may not see him for years sits in my chest like a stone I cannot dislodge. A recurring health problem flares up and then disappears without warning, adding another layer of unpredictability to days that already feel unmoored. My county has reopened, and I fear it is too soon when I see crowded restaurants and so few masks, a collective rush that makes me want to retreat further inward. I am working hard but making no progress like treading water in a current that never lets me rest.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"10 Ways to View the Creator Economy How journalists see the new landscape of independents Photo by Austin Distel on Unsplash By Elise Czajkowski 2020 has been a big year for journalists launching their own newsletters, podcasts, YouTube channels and other products. It’s not an easy path, and many are looking around for lessons and models. This fall, to serve this growing community, the Newmark Graduate of Journalism’s Entrepreneurial Journalism Creators Program became one of the first academic programs to focus on helping independent producers sustainably build and grow their niche journalism products. For those interested in exploring this emerging ecosystem, here are 10 stories about the creator economy that showcase how independent journalists are forging their own paths.\", \"sampled\": \"Pete Woodhouse proposes a thought experiment where you spend 4 of 6 hours sharpening an axe before chopping down a tree. The real question is whether the tree should be chopped down. You must consider consequences for the person, others, or nearby objects. Is the axe the right tool? A more suitable axe might exist for the person or tree type and you should question if you are the right person for the job or if someone more experienced could do it. Finding a brand new axe or examining the latest axes could also change the approach.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Most of us don’t want to be an abusive or toxic person. We don’t want to hear someone we care about say that we hurt them. We don’t want to end up estranged from those we care about. No one wants to read an email, letter or blog post detailing how just being themselves has hurt and driven away someone they love. Being an abusive person will mean you hurt the people that are closest to you. Imagine destroying the love someone has for you by being an abusive nightmare. Who wants to face up to that? Being the toxic person in a relationship or family is like playing pass the parcel with a hot brick. No one wants to be left holding it. Still, abusive people exist, so it’s got to be someone. You hopefully already know that physical abuse is bad but people frequently hurt others without getting physical. If you value your relationships you should be worried if you regularly display any of these behaviors: You don’t care how your behavior affects others. If certain political topics make people around you uncomfortable you still bring them up relentlessly. Why should you pander to people when it comes to what you think? If you’re asked not to make derogatory comments on someone’s weight, you go out of your way to do it. Someone asks you not to swear? Fuck that. If someone politely asks you to modify your behaviour and you flat out refuse; that should tell you something. You don’t understand the word “inappropriate”. Most of us worry that we’ve done something inappropriate from time to time. A red flag for abusive behaviour is that people are constantly telling you that you’re being inappropriate. They constantly tell you and you constantly don’t care, so you never change. This is a sign that you like to do things your way and anyone who disagrees with you is wrong. This attitude could quite easily lead you to be abusive towards people. You blame others for your bad behavior. You raised your voice because someone annoyed you. You caused a scene at that graduation dinner because no one talked about what interests you. You insulted your daughter’s boyfriend because his dress sense irritated you.\", \"sampled\": \"Kern Carter wrote a full manuscript in two months which is a pace he had never achieved before. Every previous manuscript had taken him at least a year to complete. The process began in mid-June when an opportunity arrived while he was on vacation in Mexico. He returned home and immediately built a rigorous daily schedule around the project. His mornings started with a writing block from 5:00 am to 8:30 am. Then he shifted to freelance work from 9 to 5 before returning to the manuscript from 7:00 pm to 10:00 pm. Spending 12 to 13 hours each day in front of a laptop became the norm. The push was designed to finish the 60,000 word manuscript in time for a conference at the end of August. When he finally shared the work with agents the feedback landed hard. One agent told him “The concept of the story is good but the writing is not what I expected.” Another agent said “It’s just not all the way thought out.” A third offered the observation “You have something here, but it’s not fully baked.” Carter felt a disappointment as he absorbed the responses. He already knew the work was not his best. The urgency he had imposed on himself created a mental anguish. It also produced a delusion that blinded him to the quality of the writing. He later admitted that ego was the true engine. The drive for acknowledgement had outpaced the actual craft. The experience taught him that the work must come first before any pursuit of success. Seeking validation too early only distorts the creative process. He realized he needs more time and a deeper investment in education to become a better writer. There are no shortcuts that can replace slow growth. He is thankful for the whole experience because it clarified his own writing process. The failure became a necessary mirror. He now accepts that he cannot produce 50,000 good words in a month. That kind of speed simply does not align with how he creates meaningful work. The manuscript reminds him that patience protects the quality of the story. The lesson reshaped how he approaches every new project.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"How to Live When Your Mind Is Governed by Fear Psychiatrist and habit change specialist Dr. Jud Brewer explains how anxiety masquerades as helpful Photo: Benjavisa/Getty Images I was recently on the phone with my friend Kate, who had just returned from a summer road trip with her kids. She’s one of those ultra-responsible types who is up on evolving coronavirus precautions and social distancing guidelines (full disclosure: She’s also an editor at Elemental). As a way to salvage joy amid the wreckage of 2020, she opted to drive her sons to a few remote spots in Montana and Wyoming. They were gone for two weeks, and upon return, Kate sounded elated, relaxed, and thrilled with the decision to pull up stakes from the normal routines of daily life and go deep into nature. “I cannot believe,” she told me, “that I almost didn’t do it.” Were this any other year, such a trip would have been seen as a no-brainer, a guaranteed source of mom points and memories. Yet because it’s 2020, and because Covid-19 dominates all aspects of life, Kate almost canceled — not because of safety (she ensured everyone’s safety) but because of fear and anxiety. “Even as I knew in my logical brain that the trip made sense and would be hugely beneficial, I kept doubting myself in a way that I couldn’t shake,” she told me. No matter that Kate knew she’d be exiting more populated California for less populated areas, wearing masks in public settings, spending most of her time in the wilderness, and traveling by car — a sneaking suspicion kept telling her she wasn’t “supposed” to do this. In fact, fear had sent its close cousin, anxiety, to tail Kate like an unmarked police car as she plotted her journey — staying just out of sight but coming into view every now and then to let its presence be known. Damn you, anxiety — threatening to ruin even the most wholesome of family trips! Fear is an innate survival mechanism, set up to help us learn what is dangerous and how to avoid it. It is the oldest survival mechanism known in science. Anxiety, on the other hand, is an anti-survival mechanism. Not only does it contribute to chronic health problems, but it makes us feel bad right now. And ironically, it thwarts learning. Remember the last time you had to memorize some facts for a presentation yet were too anxious so it was harder to get them to stick in your head? As American author Arthur Somers Roche put it, “Anxiety is a thin stream of fear trickling through the mind. If encouraged, it cuts a channel into which all other thoughts are drained.” When it comes to surviving a meeting, planning a family vacation, or saving for retirement, anxiety masquerades as a promise to help — but in actuality, it turns your thinking brain to mush. In a pandemic, the presence of daily uncertainty directly drives anxiety, which leads us to reevaluate everything from going to the grocery store to going on vacation to sending our kids to school (is it safe?!).\", \"sampled\": \"Inconsistency has been the biggest mistake I have noticed on Medium this month. It is a pattern that can quietly erode the trust readers place in a writer. That single error may have cost me about $92 in earnings over a short stretch. The number is not catastrophic, but it is a clear signal of how much regularity matters. I set a goal of writing 50 articles in December to build momentum and deepen my presence here. The target felt achievable if I kept a steady rhythm. Personal matters interrupted that rhythm and caused a drop in both my publishing rate and the quality of my work. Life has a way of testing even the most carefully laid plans. Earnings plummeted in the past 7 days because I failed to meet the expectations readers had formed. When you vanish or slow down, the platform notices quickly. I did not have completed articles waiting in a queue to act as a buffer against disruption. Everything I intended to publish still needed to be written from scratch. I usually publish articles immediately after writing them, which leaves no margin for error. That habit works until a single off day turns into a string of them. I have only waited to publish with The Ascent twice, and both times the delay felt foreign to my process. The experience taught me that pacing can be a form of discipline. Consistency and quality are both key for Medium writers who want to grow and retain an audience. Neglecting either one can undo weeks of careful effort. Publishing a low-quality article can hurt your reputation and cause readers to look elsewhere. A single rushed piece can break the trust that consistency helped build. Readers inherently expect regularity in content publishing, even if they never say it aloud. Their habits form around your schedule, and they notice when it breaks. Jon Simpson put it plainly in Forbes when he said, “When your content quality, quantity or schedule isn’t consistent, it can confuse your customers.” That confusion often leads to silence. Gary Vaynerchuk reinforced the point by noting, “The biggest miss for people trying to grow their account is frequency. If you aren’t giving viewers a reason to think about you each day then you are going to lose.” The statement lands hard when you see your own stats dip. I plan to research publishing timing strategies so that I can build a more resilient content calendar. Understanding when readers are most receptive should help me avoid future gaps. The $92 lesson is a relatively cheap one in the grand scheme of a writing career. It stings just enough to force a change without causing lasting damage. A queue of finished drafts would have kept my December goal of 50 articles within reach. Instead, I learned that output without a buffer is fragile. The past week has been a blunt reminder that readers reward reliability as much as they reward insight. Numbers do not lie, mine told a story of absence. Moving into 2021, I am carrying this experience as a practical reminder to pair creativity with structure. Consistency is not a bonus on Medium; it is the foundation.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Justice ‘To love another person is to see the face of God’ ~ Victor Hugo, Les Misérables. In 1853, the brutal murder of a woman by her husband shocked the small island of Guernsey. This was not crowded Paris where people felt distant from each other. This was an island of a few thousand inhabitants, where everyone knew each other, where every event felt tangible, where each tragedy touched every family. Justicia by Victor Hugo | Wiki Art The evidence against Charles Tapner, the man who was accused of murdering his wife, was substantial but not definite. The residents did not see any certain motivation in Tapner’s cruel action. Despite of numerous petitions signed by the residents pushing the British Home Secretary to acquit Tapner, he was still sentenced to death by hanging. One of the signatories of that petition was Victor Hugo. He stood by his principles that nobody has the right of taking someone else’s life. After all, it was for defending those exact principles, that he was forced into the exile. Hugo sank into a deep melancholy. He sat at his desk and drew one of the darkest and scariest drawings I have ever seen. He called it ‘Justicia’. This drawing was made two decades before the art critic Louis Leroy used the term ‘impressionism’ to describe the artistic style of painters like Monet. But, we don’t have to be art-critics ourselves, to ask ‘what is Justicia drawing if not a product of an impression? An impression of Tapner’s soul surrounded by darkness and his floating head screaming from pain. If we step back and look from a distance we can also see a blurry face of a woman, of his wife. Les Misérables. “Even the darkest night will end and the sun will rise.”\", \"sampled\": \"5G is the fifth generation of wireless networks and is significantly faster than 4G. It is essential to handle the expected massive increase in global mobile traffic. Smart cities use technology and data to make planning and service delivery more efficient. 5G networks are necessary to support broad-scale smart city projects. 5G frequencies are between 24 and 72 GHz, meaning signals are easily blocked and have short range. Building 5G infrastructure is expensive because it requires many more cell towers and fiber optic cable. Facebook's Terragraph project proposes attaching small cells to existing buildings to extend network reach wirelessly. Terragraph's small cells act as intermediaries between cell towers and users, eliminating the need for more fiber optic cable. Other companies are also prototyping similar last mile small cell technologies. Lower infrastructure costs could accelerate 5G deployment and access. Privacy concerns exist regarding the company providing internet connectivity also having access to personal online profiles. 5G will likely appear first in the biggest cities and may not be accessible to most Americans for years. Cities like Barcelona already use technology for tasks such as guiding drivers to open parking spaces. Stockholm, Amsterdam, and Copenhagen have also launched smart city projects that depend on robust data networks. The short range of 5G signals makes dense urban environments a natural starting point for deployment. Facebook's Terragraph aims to solve this by turning existing structures into part of the network backbone. Without the need to lay extensive new fiber optic cable, the cost of expanding coverage could drop considerably. This approach may help bring high-speed connectivity to areas where traditional infrastructure build-out is too expensive. The rollout of 5G will be uneven with major cities gaining service long before rural communities. The involvement of a social media company in providing that connectivity raises questions about data privacy and access.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In the world of startups, female founders are underrepresented. Last year, less than 3% of all the venture capital in the US went to companies founded by women. It’s an equity problem that we need to help address. With this in mind, I’ve designed a new Female Founder program for the 4thly Startup Accelerator, and we’re launching our first cohort in November. Recently, I told my friend Christina about these plans. She’s one of the most successful women I know, running a fast-growing $60M company and is a passionate advocate for women in business. She listened intently as I describing my plans for a new startup accelerator program focused on women. She slowly took a sip of her wine, looked up at me and said “You are aware that you’re an old white man, right?”. Excellent point. I’ll never know what it’s like to be a female founder. But my personal superpower is my rolodex. I know a lot of amazingly smart and successful Silicon Valley women who have agreed be guest speakers in my new program. I will lean heavily on them during the program to provide inspiration and guidance to the participants. My life today is focused on helping entrepreneurs of all stripes succeed and thrive. After a Silicon Valley career of my own, I now teach entrepreneurs at Stanford and coach them at the Miller Center for Social Entrepreneurship. Over the past ten years, women in my Stanford course have gone from less than 15% to nearly half. I’m very proud of that. I also have five daughters. But I realize that just because I’m a startup guy who believes in women doesn’t automatically mean I can effectively run an accelerator program for women. Which means I’ll need to work extra-hard at it. “True leadership stems from individuality that is honestly and sometimes imperfectly expressed… Leaders should strive for authenticity over perfection.” — Sheryl Sandberg In addition to the great guest speakers, the core of the 4thly Female Founders program will be rooted in the same principles we apply to all our 4thly programs: Global and diverse. There’s a big wide world of different kinds of interesting startups out there, and we passionately believe that cross-pollination between diverse participants is a key driver for success. As we select entrepreneurs we specifically want to put together cohorts that are global and diverse. Pure digital, no travel. We use Zoom, Slack, and other tools for real-time remote immersion — no travel required. You fully immerse with the other participants in the program while still taking care of your life and business at home. “Women are the largest untapped reservoir of talent in the World.”- Hillary Clinton Meet you where you are. Some entrepreneurs are strong in finance but weak on the technology side. Others are strong engineers but with no marketing background. In the 4thly program we meet you wherever you are and help you to build the skills and knowledge you want, across all dimensions. Intensity matters. Some accelerator programs last several months. Ours is a 60-day intensive, with full-cohort Zoom meetings every Monday, Wednesday, and Friday, plus additional 1:1 calls during the week. Great entrepreneurs embrace intensity. Equity belongs to entrepreneurs. Most startup accelerators take equity in the startups as a price of admission. I’ve never liked that model much, because entrepreneurs work their asses off to build the equity in their startups and they deserve to keep every percentage point they can. So the 4thly accelerator is strictly fee-based — participants keep 100% of their startup equity. “What I learn from talking to so many women around the world: If you can empower them with the right things, the right tools, they can lift up their family. And that ultimately lifts up their community and their society” — Melinda Gates More doing, less talking.\", \"sampled\": \"Visiting a physical bookshop delivers a far richer experience than clicking through an online storefront. The argument rests on the simple truth that browsing shelves opens doors no search algorithm can match. A real shop invites you to stumble into unknown titles and unexpected subjects. The article’s central claim is that bookshops enable serendipitous discovery of unknown titles, authors, or subjects. Online book shopping is described as purpose-driven and less likely to broaden horizons. When you know exactly what you want, the transaction is efficient but narrow. You rarely pause to consider the book next to it on a digital shelf. The algorithm shows you more of the same instead of something genuinely new. Small, densely packed bookshops inspire the author far more than large warehouses. A cramped shop forces proximity. You brush past spines you would never search for. That physical closeness sparks curiosity in a way wide aisles never do. The author visits a book store almost every time they travel. Walking into a strange shop in a strange city feels like entering a local secret. The stock reflects the neighborhood’s character and obsessions. A shelf of regional history or a table of translated fiction tells you more about a place than a guidebook. The author treats the visit as a ritual, not an errand. Stepping inside marks the real beginning of a trip. A book bought there becomes a tangible anchor for the memory. Buying a book while traveling is recommended as a better souvenir than a fridge magnet. Shopping at independent bookshops supports the local economy and small retailers. Independent bookshop staff are often book lovers who enjoy discussing books. The author notes that in roughly nine out of ten independent shops the people behind the counter genuinely love books. They read widely and talk passionately about what they have just finished. Chain bookshops like Barnes & Noble and Waterstones employ local people knowledgeable about books. A chain location still draws from the community. The staff are not distant corporate voices. The article contrasts bookshop staff with workers who handle unrelated products. A small bookshop example the author admires occupies just five hundred square feet. Inside that tiny space the owners cram thousands of titles. The density feels deliberate and generous. The selection in a shop that small cannot be comprehensive, so It is curated. Every book on the shelf earned its place. The author finds the constraint of a five hundred square foot shop more exciting than a vast warehouse. A limited space forces the owner to choose. That curation becomes a conversation between the shop and the browser. The article contrasts the bookshop experience with the impersonal efficiency of online retail. A search bar asks you to state your desire. A physical shelf asks you to wander. Independent bookshop staff often recommend titles you would never find through an online search. They remember what you bought last time and suggest something that builds on it. The relationship turns a transaction into an ongoing dialogue. Chain bookshops like Barnes & Noble and Waterstones also rely on staff who read widely. A knowledgeable bookseller in a chain can guide a customer just as well as an independent owner. The key is the human connection, not the corporate logo. The author argues that buying a book while traveling anchors the memory of the trip. The weight of the book in your bag on the flight home feels like carrying a piece of the place. A fridge magnet fades into the kitchen clutter, but a book stays on the shelf and pulls you back. The article’s closing thought reinforces the value of the local bookshop as a community hub. A shop where the staff know books and customers is a small but vital piece of a healthy local economy. It resists the drift toward cold, algorithm-driven consumption.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Increasing Accuracy by Converting to a Multivariate Dataset Tracyrenee Follow Dec 22 · 6 min read Being a data science novice seeking to improve my skills, I continuously go through the competitions I have previously entered and seek to improve their accuracy. One such competition I have reviewed is Analytics Vidhya’s JetRail time series analysis. There are many ways that one can predict future figures, such as Random Forest, statsmodels functions and Facebook Prophet. In both statsmodels and Prophet there are ways that one can check if a date is a weekend or a holiday, but this can be tricky. For example, I do not know what country JetRail is based in, but I am assuming it is in a western country. Even if JetRail is in a western country, however, it is important to know the holiday schedule for the country it is in before an accurate prediction can be obtained. With this in mind, I decided to assume that JetRail is based in a western country and the work week is from Monday to Friday and the weekend lasts from Saturday to Sunday. I have previously written about the JetRail dataset as a univariate time series analysis problem, with the link to this post being found here:- How I solved the JetRail time series problem with FB Prophet | by Tracyrenee | Python In Plain English | Nov, 2020 | Medium In this post, however, I have converted the univariate dataset to a multivariate dataset in an attempt to improve the accuracy. If you would like to know what happened then please read on. The problem statement and datasets can be found on Analytics Vidhya’s JetRail competition page, the link being here:- Time Series Forecasting (analyticsvidhya.com) The .ipyn file for this competition question was created in Google Colab, a free online Jupyter Notebook that can be used from any computer that has internet access. The problem statement for this competition question reads as follows:- “Welcome DataHacker! Congratulations on your new job! This time you are helping out Unicorn Investors with your data hacking skills. They are considering making an investment in a new form of transportation — JetRail. JetRail uses Jet propulsion technology to run rails and move people at a high speed! While JetRail has mastered the technology and they hold the patent for their product, the investment would only make sense, if they can get more than 1 Million monthly users with in next 18 months. You need to help Unicorn ventures with the decision. They usually invest in B2C start-ups less than 4 years old looking for pre-series A funding. In order to help Unicorn Ventures in their decision, you need to forecast the traffic on JetRail for the next 7 months. You are provided with traffic data of JetRail since inception in the test file.” Because many of the libraries I need to solve this question are already installed on Google Colab, I only needed to import those libraries into the program, being pandas, numpy, seaborn, matplotlib, fbprophet and sklearn. I then loaded and read the datasets into the program, being train, test and sample:- I decided to convert the univariate time series dataset to a multivariate dataset and I accomplished this by adding a column, “dayofweek”. The function, dayofweek, returns a value from 0 to 6 signifying what day of the week the sampling occurred:- I then created an additional column from the index, which is in datetime format. This column is necessary to perform a datetime analysis. I then changed the names of the columns to names that Prophet wants to see when it is training and fitting the data:- I created variables, ID_train and id_test, which stored the data train.ID and test.ID respectively. These columns were then dropped from the datasets because they are not needed to carry out the computations:- I plotted a graph of the train dataset because it is important to have a visual representation of how the number of passengers have increased with time:- I split the train dataframe in two to separate it into training and validation sets. The splitting is based on the date of this time series analysis:- I defined the model, being Facebook Prophet.\", \"sampled\": \"When Diane Tatum published her reflection on April 30, 2020, the United States had already endured roughly six weeks of isolation and lockdowns driven by the COVID-19 pandemic. The piece captured a moment when public debate had shifted from pure health measures toward the mounting pressure to reopen the economy. Tatum framed the tension as a grim choice between money and human life. Tatum opened by acknowledging the real suffering of Americans in nonessential jobs who were running out of money, food, patience, medical insurance, and other necessities. She did not dismiss that pain, yet she immediately set it against a chilling official warning that a careless economic restart could produce a COVID-19 death toll of 0.1% of the population.  she noted that percentage translates into approximately 302,000 Americans. The number gave scale to a risk that had become abstract in daily news cycles, and Tatum used it to ask whether the country was prepared to accept such a loss in exchange for restored commerce. To sharpen the moral question, she reached back to Charles Dickens’s A Christmas Carol and quoted the Ghost of Christmas Yet to Come speaking about Scrooge. The ghost’s words were cold and direct: “If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it and decrease the surplus population.” Tatum placed that quotation alongside the contemporary debate without forcing a one-to-one comparison, but the message was clear. She worried that a utilitarian logic that treated human beings as surplus was creeping into modern policy conversations, and she found the echo of Scrooge’s world deeply unsettling. The article also referenced a moment when television personality Dr. Oz stated that opening schools would cost 2-3% of children’s lives, which he later retracted. Tatum used the incident not to attack a single person but to illustrate how easily talk of acceptable death percentages could enter the mainstream. She pressed her readers to consider whether staying home to save neighbors was preferable to returning to work, and she refused to treat that question as purely economic. For Tatum, the dilemma was fundamentally a test of Christian character and of the command to love one’s neighbor as oneself. The piece turned explicitly to scripture, quoting Jesus’s words from Matthew 6:31-33: “Therefore do not be anxious, saying, ‘What shall we eat?’ or ‘What shall we drink?’ or ‘What shall we wear?’ For the Gentiles seek after all these things, and your heavenly Father knows that you need them all. But seek first the kingdom of God and his righteousness, and all these things will be added to you.” Tatum used the passage to address the anxiety that was driving the rush to reopen, and she suggested that while the material panic was real, it needed to be held within a larger spiritual frame. She did not promise easy provision but pointed toward a reordering of priorities. She reinforced that call with a second quotation from Jesus, drawn from Matthew 22:35-40: “You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind. This is the great and first commandment. And a second is like it: You shall love your neighbor as yourself. On these two commandments depend all the Law and the Prophets.” In Tatum’s argument, That love of neighbor was not a vague sentiment but a practical and godly responsibility. She pointed to the concrete work that Christian churches already support, including hospitals, food banks, rescue centers, childcare, trauma teams, and reconstruction, and she implied that the same ethic should shape decisions about public health and economic policy. The article did not pretend to resolve the tension between economic survival and physical safety. it insisted that Christians could not outsource the question to market logic or actuarial tables, because the numbers always represented individual lives known and valued by God. Tatum closed by returning to the central dilemma without offering a policy prescription. She left her readers with the weight of the choice itself, framed by scripture and by the haunting words of Dickens, and she asked whether a people who claim to follow Jesus could accept any version of the future that treats neighbors as surplus.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"NeurIPS, the largest conference in artificial intelligence, is currently underway, and it has over 20k people registered. If you are not registered and would like to access their goodies, please visit this public access version of the NeurIPS website. If you are into deep learning, we recommend you to check out this phenomenal tutorial by David Duvenaud, Zico Kolter, and Matt Johnson, which makes use of many tools such as Anderson acceleration, differential equations, neural nets, convex optimization, Jax, automatic differentiation and others, presented on NeurIPS. Next, we recommend you to check out this article titled “We read the paper that forced Timnit Gebru out of Google. Here’s what it says” by Karen Hao from MIT Technology Review, which gives a very insightful overview of what caused the departure of Timtit Gebru, co-lead ethical AI researcher from Google Brain. For those interested in natural language processing, Carnegie Mellon Professor Graham Neubig just published 23 class-lectures on multilingual natural language processing, including two guest lectures by Pat Littell and Orhan Firat.\", \"sampled\": \"Selecting all elements of a specific type on a web page is a basic task in front-end development, and Dr. Derek Austin explains exactly how to accomplish this with jQuery. The library simplifies the process into a concise expression that many developers have relied on for years. jQuery syntax for selecting every ` ` element on the page is written as `$(\\\"div\\\")`, a compact command that hides a direct connection to the browser’s native capabilities. Under the hood `$(\\\"div\\\")` acts as a wrapper around the native JavaScript method `document.getElementsByTagName(\\\"div\\\")`. The jQuery documentation states that this selector “Selects all elements with the given tag name”, confirming its straightforward purpose. Both `$(\\\"div\\\")` and `getElementsByTagName(\\\"div\\\")` return the same `HTMLCollection` of matching elements, so the jQuery version does not retrieve a fundamentally different set of nodes. This equivalence is helpful when reading older scripts that mix jQuery and vanilla JavaScript. jQuery’s overall popularity has been declining as developers adopt native syntax and frameworks like React or Vue, but understanding `$(\\\"div\\\")` remains practical for maintaining legacy code that may not have been updated in years.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Maybe it’s time to create one. @thejournalgarden unsplash.com I have a flood of creativity these days. I don’t know where to put it all. I haven’t been a true creative for 20 years. Now, I’m pouring buckets. This morning, I read this post by Stefani Vader. She has a great suggestion for making a list of all the creative projects that she would like to pursue. So, I started my list. Last week, my to-do list was completely shoved aside. I was on a writing rampage on Medium. It was driven by sheer force and the creativity that was pouring out of me. As I grow in my creative endeavors, I need to look at my to-do list as opportunities to create. Projects on my to-do list have to be actual line items on my Creative Bucket List. Otherwise, when I go through one of my creative rampages, all my to-dos on my to-do list become lost in my creative process. I know, you are thinking that I’m over managing my creative life. Well, as an analytical person and a creative person, I don’t know how else to manage this “Creative Beast” within myself. I need her to at least acknowledge the fact that being creative is great. But, we also need to channel this creativity into other endeavors such as Marketing, Editing and Communication. This way our work will actually end up in front of our audience. Now, I’m going to give some love to my blog and my social media accounts today.\", \"sampled\": \"A few months before October 2019, I thought I would run out of writing ideas. I was truly worried. I had a few more ideas lined up, but I struggled to generate new ones. The well felt dry and unproductive. I kept asking myself what I would do when the last idea was used. The question looped in my head. The worry was not abstract. I sat with the uncomfortable thought of having nothing left to say. That mental space felt like a dead end. I hit a low point. Then a few weeks after that low point, new ideas began to flow again. They arrived without a clear announcement. I noticed them only after they had already gathered. I am still unsure what triggered the return of ideas. Travel and small trips may have helped shake something loose. A change of scenery often resets a tired mind. Inspiration started to appear in seemingly trivial events. A short conversation, a street scene, or a half-remembered story suddenly carried weight. I began to see material where I had seen only noise before. Running out of ideas is a common concern for writers. The fear I felt is not unique. Many writers carry the same anxiety. I remember the internal worry. I thought, “What am I going to do in a few weeks when I’ve run out of ideas?” The question felt urgent and personal. It was not a rhetorical exercise. That period taught me something simple.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Why tech spec? Imagine if you jump to coding right after receiving project requirements, you’ll either repeatedly changing the design during the implementation, delivering a buggy product, or even jump back to where you started because the result is “not what’s expected”. Even worse, if you give a promise based on your intuition, and then you found out that the deadline is too early, or the budget isn’t enough. Chart picture by Raygun Remember the chart above? I believe you saw it somewhere in some form. Basically, it says “the earlier it is detected, the cheaper”. The same applies to design changes. For me, technical specification is a document to outline how we are going to tackle technical issues in order to achieve a goal. Usually, this document will be written by senior engineers or leads before implementation. Furthermore, below are the benefits of writing tech spec: Knowing the unknowns , when you breakdown all the work, you’ll then realise the possible blockers, potential performance issues, something missed (such as security approval, or you just forgot to update a particular script), possible risks, etc , when you breakdown all the work, you’ll then realise the possible blockers, potential performance issues, something missed (such as security approval, or you just forgot to update a particular script), possible risks, etc Documentation , tech spec can be revisited anytime required, such as on an audit (can also act as decision/design logs) , tech spec can be revisited anytime required, such as on an audit (can also act as decision/design logs) Work consensus , by getting approval from the tech spec’s reviewers, meaning that they've agreed with the solution , by getting approval from the tech spec’s reviewers, meaning that they've agreed with the solution Reduce the effort on explaining to parties , when you need to explain the work to team members or other teams, you can always bring this tech spec to make them easier to understand , when you need to explain the work to team members or other teams, you can always bring this tech spec to make them easier to understand Better task distributions , the team can easily understand what will be done and feeling confident to work on it because the tech spec was agreed by required parties , the team can easily understand what will be done and feeling confident to work on it because the tech spec was agreed by required parties Faster development and better quality , things are prepared and the team understand what to do.\", \"sampled\": \"At age 70 I received my first MMR shot, the vaccine that protects against measles, mumps, and rubella. Most children get this immunization after 15 months, but I had somehow missed it for seven decades. MMR stands for measles, mumps, and rubella, three diseases that once seemed like fading threats. Measles outbreaks have returned in recent years along with cases of mumps and rubella, pulling these old names back into the news. The anti-vaxxer movement is what Wikipedia calls a loosely-organized conspiracy theorist subculture that blames the medical practice of vaccinations for a wide range of health problems. Some parents and the then-current U.S. president believe vaccinations are dangerous or cause autism. I think about my mother Dolores when I hear these claims. In January 1924, she was 10 years old and an asymptomatic carrier of diphtheria during an outbreak that swept through her family. Her brother Warren died from diphtheria in that same 1924 outbreak. Dolores carried the bacteria without ever getting sick, while her brother could not survive the infection. Years later Dolores had a son named Jackie who is my half-brother. In 1938 Jackie contracted spinal meningitis and later polio, a brutal sequence that led to his death. I was in school during another polio season. In 1951 a classmate of mine was diagnosed with polio and had to use an iron lung just to keep breathing. Jonas Salk developed the polio vaccine and gave it away for free, refusing to patent a tool that could end so much suffering. I received the polio shot and booster for free in junior high school in 1957. Those memories sit in the background when I consider why I needed an MMR shot at 70. The anti-vaxxer movement's refusal to vaccinate children has let measles, mumps, and rubella creep back into communities. I recently asked my mother, now 101, if I ever had measles as a child. She answered, \\\"Your brother had them, but you never did.\\\" Her reply made clear that I had simply been lucky. The diseases were always nearby, but I had never caught them or been protected by a vaccine. I attribute the need for my adult MMR shot directly to the anti-vaxxer movement's refusal to vaccinate children. When immunization rates drop, diseases that were once controlled find new hosts. My family's history with diphtheria, spinal meningitis, and polio is not ancient folklore. It is a record of what happens when prevention stops, and I walked into a clinic at 70 to make sure measles would not be the next chapter.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Tools to build a prototype web app in one month without writing code Prototyping stack (for non-developers) For non-technical people who have an idea for a digital product, I’ve noticed two reasons that keep them from pursuing: 1) anxiety towards sacrificing some aspect of their life to make the time, and 2) convincing themselves that they need a technical cofounder and/or funding to build something tangible. This essay aims to dismiss reason #2, by clarifying some free (or price-of-several-coffees) tools I’ve used to build a functional web application version-1; my prototyping stack. One key advantage of building a prototype is that it drastically improves how seriously you’re taken by peers in co-working spaces, potential cofounders, prospective mentors/angel investors, and target users. m = more information available, v = vlad’s (my own) personal experiences Organize Although I was skeptical how a “visual idea board” would be different than just writing ideas into a notebook, I was surprised to find that it had a side-effect of helping jumpstart momentum for doing work [v.1]. The first idea board was used purely for brainstorming along several categories which I guessed at being necessary for the idea to become tangible — the foundational product idea was found in the resulting conversations [v.2]. This is also a great time to apply the Jobs To Be Done framework as a way to understand how someone’s life can be improved; as a a consequence, this frame of thinking helps surface ideas for product features that would impact those improvements [m.1]. The point here isn’t to get stuck in brainstorming-paralysis, but rather to settle on a hypothesis for one “job to be done”, and then continue through the rest of this prototyping stack. First idea board (using the free tool Trello) was decommissioned after figuring out what to build Design The amount of incredible (and freely accessible) designs out there make the motto, “you don’t have to re-invent the wheel” valid. Since you’re still in the brainstorming phase — and probably don’t have a background in human factors — it’s a good idea to borrow inspiration from best-practice user interfaces [v.3]. At this point, I was primarily spending time bookmarking designs which looked like they could be used to be used to fulfill my “jobs to be done” hypothesis. Dribbble showcases professional-level design choices for any kind of user interface Mock-Up (& Iterate) Have you ever played around with Microsoft Paint? Well, there are tools which are just as simple to use, except they let you create a “fake app” that you can click around to navigate to various screens. At this point, I would take the inspirations from the “Design” phase, and build them in this mock-up tool [v.4]. These tools also export nicely into a mobile app, so this is a great time to hand your phone to prospective users and silently observe how they use your interface to gather feedback for design changes. The more times you can do this — build mockup, demo to target user, observe their hesitations with usability, re-build mockup — the more professional your user experience will feel. The non-free (low cost) tool Proto lets you create multiple screens to experiment with user-interfaces Functional App What if you could use your Microsoft Paint skills to clone an app like Uber or AirBnB over a weekend [m.2]? That’s the main appeal to a new class of app building tools, which feel like the next evolution of tools used in the “mock-up” phase. Although there is a steep learning curve [v.5], showing up to a cofounder speed-dating event / prospective investor coffee chat becomes a 10x better experience when you have the first version of a functional app. The free tool Bubble.\", \"sampled\": \"A project involved submitting a unique combination of two distinct neural networks for a competition that required participants to write and publish code capable of generating a complete literary text. This creative endeavor, part of an event focused on artificial intelligence and art, challenged authors and developers to produce a work with a minimum length of 50,000 words, pushing the boundaries of machine-generated literature and exploring new frontiers in computational creativity. The first of the two neural networks employed was a conditional model designed specifically for text generation, forming the foundational creative engine of the system. The second component was an innovative BERT-based filter, which performed the crucial task of quality control by meticulously evaluating the output, systematically rejecting phrases that were deemed boring or clumsy while ensuring that only the most valid and compelling sentences were retained for the final text. A significant portion of the development process involved training the sophisticated BERT filter, a task that relied heavily on extensive manual markup to teach the model how to distinguish high-quality prose from less desirable output. The main part of this painstaking and essential work was undertaken by researcher Ivan Yamshchikov, whose contributions were instrumental in refining the network’s ability to curate the generated content and maintain a consistent and engaging narrative voice throughout the lengthy manuscript. Ultimately, the combined system proved to be remarkably effective, turning out to be a surprisingly good generator for producing what could be described as cyber-paranoid delusions, all composed in the English language. This unique and somewhat unsettling thematic output gave the project a distinct personality, differentiating it from other submissions and showcasing the potential for AI to explore complex and unconventional psychological states through automated storytelling, resulting in a cohesive and thematically focused body of work. Given the specific nature of the text it produced, the creation was aptly named the PARANOID TRANSFORMER, a title that perfectly captured the essence of its generated content and its underlying technological architecture. This name served as a clear indicator of the project's focus, hinting at the unsettling yet fascinating narratives of paranoia and technological anxiety that the dual-network system was designed to explore and articulate through its automated writing process. While the author has not yet decided whether to participate in this year's event, their previous involvement led to a notable outcome, which began after the NaNoGenMo competition quietly concluded in mid-December. Following the event, Augusto Corvalan, the editor-in-chief of the publication STRANGE DEAD ALIVE, setting the stage for the project to gain a new and unexpected platform for wider recognition beyond the initial competition. Feeling that it would be boring to simply use the same thing twice for a new opportunity, the team decided to introduce a creative twist to their existing PARANOID TRANSFORMER system before submitting its work. This modification involved a new layer of processing where the generated text was passed through a translation model, converting the English output into Russian and then immediately back into English, adding a unique stylistic layer to the final narrative. This experimental translation process proved to be highly successful, as the resulting text turned out to be quite atmospheric, with the back-and-forth translation adding a distinct and compelling quality to the cyber-paranoid narrative. The enhanced work was so well-received that by the end of January, it even took first place in a competition, validating the decision to innovate upon the original model and explore new methods for refining machine-generated art. The recognition for the project continued to grow, and in February, the winner of the competition was presented with an extraordinary opportunity: an offer to publish the text that had been generated by the PARANOID TRANSFORMER. This offer represented a significant milestone, providing a chance for the artificially generated narrative of paranoid delusions to reach a broader audience and be appreciated as a legitimate work of literary art.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I’ve already discussed the importance of writing by hand when it comes to learning languages. Indeed, the time it takes gives time to the brain to form memories which will last longer. However, that isn’t the only advantage there is to it. Another often overlooked one is developing patience. No matter how fast you can write, it’s pretty much always going to be slower than typing on a computer or phone. Do we have the time to spend on such a task then? No, we don’t. But that’s exactly the point. We’re always in a rush to do things and writing something is no exception. We want to get to the next thing quickly after all. Our agendas are cramped with tasks, both useful and somehow pointless ones. “I have to check Instagram after this.” “I need to watch that next episode today.” “I know it’s late but I have to reply to that person today.” What’s the common factor of all those? The need. But is that “need” actually true? Many actions we do on a daily basis can be retrograded to a few times a week, weekly tasks or even just monthly ones. Fixing the time to write for yourself will force you to figure out what you are doing too much of and what could be retrograded. Dig within That first task of looking for time to write will provide you with an overview of your time. Overviews are useful but usually not enough to actually dig deep in what actually matters. Writing by hand gives you that opportunity. As you write a sentence, you can already start having ideas for the following one. And the following one, and so on. But still, the longer the sentences you write, the more time you have, don’t you? What can you do with this time? You can look reconsider your thoughts. We all know this. The more time we have, the more we procrastinate and ponder. A classic example of this is writing a thesis. You’ve got months ahead of you, all the time you need to write it but keep on reconsidering details, finding new ideas which contradict some of the original ones. But when the deadline is coming close, all of a sudden, you start writing and going forward quickly. You finish on time and wonder why you had so much trouble at the beginning. It doesn’t make sense anymore. Reconsidering is good for yourself When writing by hand and starting to reconsider your next thoughts, you find the opportunity to dig deeper, to challenge ideas you’ve had. Little by little, you’ll actually even go as far as stopping between ideas to develop your thoughts on a certain topic. This is obviously possible when typing on a phone or computer but the habit of typing quickly takes over most of the time. As a result, you end up going forward instead of stopping for a moment.\", \"sampled\": \"Tracy Smith, an experienced IT tech consultant, is the founder of the innovative remote queuing solution known as Vipii which he successfully developed using the low-code platform Bubble. This application was conceived as a direct response to a pressing public health concern, as Smith observed the formation of long high-risk queues at various stores after they began reopening during the quarantine period and recognized a significant need for more efficient system. The primary motivation behind the creation of Vipii was to provide a genuinely low-cost queuing solution specifically tailored for small businesses, many of which could not afford the more expensive existing options on the market. Smith’s vision was to democratize access to this technology, ensuring that smaller enterprises could manage customer flow safely and effectively without incurring substantial financial burdens, thereby leveling the playing field and supporting local economies during a challenging time. Vipii's user experience is designed for maximum simplicity and accessibility, allowing businesses to easily register their venue on the application to create and manage a virtual queue. For visitors and customers, the process is equally streamlined as they can join a queue for a particular store without needing to sign up for an account or download any software onto their personal devices removing common barriers to adoption. Once a visitor has joined a virtual line through the Vipii system, the application handles the rest of the process providing a seamless and stress-free waiting experience that eliminates the need for physical presence. The system automatically sends a notification directly to the visitor when it is their turn to enter the store, which allows them to wait comfortably and safely elsewhere, such as in their car or at a nearby location. Smith’s journey into low-code development was significantly aided by his prior professional experience in backend development, which provided him with a solid foundation that helped him learn the intricacies of the Bubble platform more quickly. Before he even began constructing Vipii, he dedicated a substantial amount of time to mastering the tool, training extensively with Bubble for a year and a half to ensure he had the necessary skills. Despite his technical background, Smith encountered notable difficulties while building the application, finding that achieving both consistent responsiveness across different devices and overall application optimization on the Bubble platform proved to be particularly challenging aspects of the development process. To enhance the functionality of Vipii and overcome certain platform limitations, he strategically integrated other specialized solutions directly into the application, including the communications platform Twilio for messaging capabilities. Drawing from his entrepreneurial experience, Smith offers critical advice for other aspiring founders, emphasizing the absolute importance of validating a product idea and confirming that a real, demonstrable market need exists before committing to the resource-intensive process of building. He contrasts the rapid bug-fixing capabilities of low-code platforms with traditional coding, where fixing a single bug can sometimes take a minimum of two weeks, highlighting the efficiency gains.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Why Disney Should Do a Live-Action Remake of The Sword in the Stone. Brett Seegmiller Follow Jan 25 · 6 min read Very rarely do the Disney live-action remakes improve upon their animated predecessors. Almost every creative decision that was added to remakes such as Beauty and the Beast, The Lion King, and Aladdin were unnecessary and made them easily worse than the originals. I recently watched Aladdin for the first time on Disney Plus, and all I could think throughout the entire excruciating experience was that this new Aladdin was basically a glorified Disney Channel TV movie. With many of the original cartoons — like the ones I’ve already mentioned — they were nearly perfect from a creative and storytelling perspective, so there wasn’t really any room for improvement. But then there are Disney classics such as The Sword in the Stone. After watching the travesty that was Guy Ritchie’s Aladdin, I decided to watch the story about young King Arthur and the crotchety wizard, Merlin, in an effort to clear my head. The Sword in the Stone, for the most part, is still just as enjoyable now as it was when I was a kid. But now that I’m older and more discerning, I realized that out of any Disney cartoon classic, no story was more primed for a live-action update than The Sword in the Stone. (Interestingly enough, before working on this story, I didn’t actually know that The Sword in the Stone is already in some level of pre-production at Disney, though it’s been a while since there’s been any update on that front.) Regardless of whether or not a live-action remake will actually get off the ground, there’s one big reason why the Sword in the Stone should be remade more than any other Disney cartoon. Unlike many of the other classic movies which are — as I said, nearly perfect — the charming story of young King Arthur’s education is fundamentally flawed. I’m a big Bill Peet fan who was the writer of The Sword in the Stone mostly due to his children’s books, so I always appreciated the story and the creative direction of the film. Having said that, the story of King Arthur’s education at the hands of Merlin — while appreciated due to its focus on education and technology — doesn’t really go anywhere. Throughout the story, Merlin is constantly telling young Arthur — or Wart, as he’s commonly called — to “use his head.” Usually, this is accompanied by a battering on the young man’s head with the wizard’s crooked cane. This version of Merlin is hilarious because he’s a paradoxical character. He’s a wizard who’s obsessed with technology and education, in spite of the fact that he’s a practitioner of magic who uses spells to get his way. In fact, he doesn’t teach Arthur anything about magic, which I much appreciated. Instead, he teaches Arthur the laws of the natural world using magic. His goal is to educate Arthur on how to “use his head” instead of relying on brawn like his older adopted brother, Kay. Yet, in the end. Arthur never really has to use his head. He utilizes his obvious intelligence and natural physical skill as he traverses Merlin’s lessons that involve being transformed into forest creatures, but at the end of the story, it’s just by chance that Arthur suddenly finds himself king. What this boils down to is that the film sets up foreshadowing for Arthur to have to “use his head” to progress as a character, but never does. He just becomes king without even having to try. Up to this time in the story, Arthur is continuously belittled and bullied by his adoptive adopted brother, Kay.\", \"sampled\": \"Effective analysis stands as a truly crucial component for the success of any marketing campaign you might undertake. Without a strong analytical foundation, it becomes difficult to measure progress, understand audience behavior, or make informed decisions for future strategies which can undermine the entire effort. New marketers and sometimes even experienced ones often make critical mistakes when they approach their data. These errors are frequently rooted in common psychological biases or fundamental misconceptions about how analysis should properly be conducted, leading to flawed interpretations and wasted resources. One of the most common mistakes is simply failing to ask specific, targeted questions before diving into the data. A vague approach to analysis rarely yields useful information, leaving the marketer adrift in a sea of numbers without any clear direction or purpose for their investigation. For example, instead of just browsing metrics a marketer should ask pointed questions to guide their research. They could investigate specific goals by asking, “Are we earning more social traffic?” or by posing the question, “Is the new content strategy working?” to evaluate recent changes. Relying on a single data source is another significant pitfall that can severely limit the scope and accuracy of your analysis. Many marketers depend exclusively on a tool like Google Analytics which provides valuable information but cannot offer a complete picture of campaign performance on its own. A more robust approach involves collecting both quantitative and qualitative data to form a comprehensive understanding. Quantitative data provides the hard numbers about what is happening, while qualitative data can offer insights into why it is happening, revealing the human element behind the statistics. Marketers may also misinterpret the actual meaning of the metrics they are tracking, leading to incorrect conclusions about performance. A frequent point of confusion is the difference between a \\\"bounce rate\\\" and an \\\"exit rate,\\\" two distinct metrics that are often used interchangeably by mistake. Confusing correlation with causation is another easy and common mistake to make when performing marketing analysis. Just because two metrics appear to move together does not automatically mean that one is causing the other, and assuming so can lead to misguided strategic decisions. Focusing too much on the raw numbers can cause marketers to lose sight of what is truly important for their campaign goals. Obsessing over every minor fluctuation in the data can obscure the larger trends and more significant insights that should be guiding the overall marketing strategy. Comparing dissimilar metrics is another frequent error that can invalidate analytical findings and lead to confusion. For instance, trying to directly compare bounce rates to conversions is an illogical exercise as these two metrics measure completely different aspects of user interaction and campaign success. The primary goal of any data analysis should be to generate clear, actionable conclusions that can be used for tangible campaign improvement. The insights gained from the data are only valuable if they can be translated into specific steps to enhance performance and achieve better results. Even expert analysts are not immune to making poor judgments, especially given the increasing complexity of modern marketing data. The sheer volume and variety of information available from platforms like Google and Facebook can overwhelm even the most seasoned professionals, making mistakes a constant risk. There are at least seven common analysis mistakes that marketers frequently make when evaluating their campaign data. Recognizing these potential errors is the first step toward avoiding them and developing a more rigorous and effective analytical process that yields trustworthy insights. Ultimately, effective analysis requires more than just access to data; it demands a thoughtful and structured approach. By avoiding these common pitfalls, marketers can ensure their conclusions are sound and that their strategic decisions are based on a true understanding of campaign performance.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation Model #1: Collaborative Filtering First, some background: When people hear the words “collaborative filtering,” they generally think of Netflix, as it was one of the first companies to use this method to power a recommendation model, taking users’ star-based movie ratings to inform its understanding of which movies to recommend to other similar users. After Netflix was successful, the use of collaborative filtering spread quickly, and is now often the starting point for anyone trying to make a recommendation model. Unlike Netflix, Spotify doesn’t have a star-based system with which users rate their music. Instead, Spotify’s data is implicit feedback — specifically, the stream counts of the tracks and additional streaming data, such as whether a user saved the track to their own playlist, or visited the artist’s page after listening to a song. But what is collaborative filtering, truly, and how does it work? Here’s a high-level rundown, explained in a quick conversation: Image source: Collaborative Filtering at Spotify, by Erik Bernhardsson, ex-Spotify. What’s going on here? Each of these individuals has track preferences: the one on the left likes tracks P, Q, R, and S, while the one on the right likes tracks Q, R, S, and T. Collaborative filtering then uses that data to say: “Hmmm… You both like three of the same tracks — Q, R, and S — so you are probably similar users. Therefore, you’re each likely to enjoy other tracks that the other person has listened to, that you haven’t heard yet.” Therefore, it suggests that the one on the right check out track P — the only track not mentioned, but that his “similar” counterpart enjoyed — and the one on the left check out track T, for the same reasoning. Simple, right? But how does Spotify actually use that concept in practice to calculate millions of users’ suggested tracks based on millions of other users’ preferences? With matrix math, done with Python libraries! In actuality, this matrix you see here is gigantic. Each row represents one of Spotify’s 140 million users — if you use Spotify, you yourself are a row in this matrix — and each column represents one of the 30 million songs in Spotify’s database. Then, the Python library runs this long, complicated matrix factorization formula: Some complicated math… When it finishes, we end up with two types of vectors, represented here by X and Y. X is a user vector, representing one single user’s taste, and Y is a song vector, representing one single song’s profile. The User/Song matrix produces two types of vectors: user vectors and song vectors. Image source: From Idea to Execution: Spotify’s Discover Weekly, by Chris Johnson, ex-Spotify. Now we have 140 million user vectors and 30 million song vectors.\", \"sampled\": \"This is the Money Quote for Tuesday, November 28, 2017, a post about your share of the US GDP which awaits your attention and also covers topics related to economics and politics. A piece titled Awaits Forget Bitcoin includes a question from Sam Altman, who asks why we do not consider sharing the entire United States economy equally among the population, a truly radical idea for consideration. I have written extensively today for the Money Quote, and here are some more noteworthy stories I have been reading over these past few days that I believe are worth your time. The context of these stories spans a wide range, from the intricacies of cryptocurrency to broader economic theories that affect every single person living within the national economy and beyond its borders. I personally own a small number of bitcoins and have also invested in other crypto currencies, a decision I made primarily to better understand this emerging financial landscape. After some time and observation, I find myself somewhat flummoxed about what the next logical step should be with these digital assets, given the volatility and the ongoing debate about their intrinsic value. One story that caught my eye involves the Food and Drug Administration and the history of Nutrition Facts labels a system designed to inform consumers about what they are eating. Another fascinating topic is the history of the International Sugar Research Foundation which has a complex past involving its influence on public health and dietary guidelines over many decades, especially regarding sugar consumption. There is also a developing legal story involving The Missouri AG and a case that is heading toward the Supreme Court which could have significant implications for state and federal regulations. The core of the issue relates to the balance of power and how legal precedents are established which often has far reaching consequences that are not immediately apparent to the public when the case is first presented. Sam Altman has made a compelling argument about wealth distribution that is worth quoting at length, touching on the long term economic health of nations that fail to address inequality. He states, \\\"Countries that concentrate wealth in a small number of families do worse over the long term if we don’t take a radical step toward a fair, inclusive system, we will not be the leading country in the world for much longer.\\\" Altman's quote continues with a stark warning about the consequences of inaction for everyone, not just a select few, emphasizing the interconnectedness of the nation's economic fate. He concludes, \\\"This would harm all Americans more than most realize,\\\" a statement that serves as a powerful call to reexamine our current economic structures and consider more equitable models for the future.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Building a Design System Package With Storybook, TypeScript, and React in 15 Minutes A design system will make you more productive and help you build new features and components faster Background photo by Nils Johan Gabrielsen on Unsplash. When building out a UI component library for my own work, I ran into a couple of pain points while searching for how to create a simple workflow that “just works.” Most tutorials I saw for TypeScript component libraries made use of build tools (which at times caused headaches) and my current job took the opposite extreme of publishing the UI component library as TypeScript and relying on individual projects to transpile it to JavaScript directly from the library itself (if you are from my company…\", \"sampled\": \"Close friends Alex and Phil were drinking late together before Phil went home around 4 a.m. to get into bed. While sitting by a fire Phil began to cry weeping for his deceased brother until he was sobbing uncontrollably and threw up. He had earlier texted his wife that he would likely be staying over at Alex's. He was shocked and tried to comfort his friend but Phil pushed him away while repeatedly saying, \\\"I’m sorry, I’m so so sorry.\\\" After Phil passed out from exhaustion, Alex discovered he had defecated in his pants, cleaned him with a garden hose which woke him provided him with clean clothes after throwing his soiled ones away and watched as the subdued and extremely ashamed man went home.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Hello guys! This post talks about another commonly used UI pattern namely the “Navigation drawer”. With the navigation drawer one can navigate to many screens or functionalities of the app by clicking on the ‘hamburger’ icon. Swiping from the left is also a way to bring the drawer into view, a screen then slides in, showing many items. You can click on these said items and go to those screens to use that feature of the app. So Let’s get started! Step 1: Create a brand new Android Studio project and name it ‘NavigationDrawer’. Choose an ‘Empty Activity’ as your MainActivity as we will be building the screen from ground up. Copy paste this code into your activity_main.xml file. You might face an error in the 32nd line where we are including the header file. Do not worry we will be creating the header file in the next step and that’ll clear up the error. We are using the ‘NavigationView’ widget which is a part of the support design library, so please make sure you have the latest gradle dependency in your Build.gradle file(app level) : compile ‘com.android.support:design:X.X.X’ Remember to replace the ‘Xs’ with the latest version number, if you are not sure a simple google search should do the trick. Step 2: Next, we need to create a layout resource file that will serve as the header for the Navigation drawer. Here’s how - Right-click the res folder → click new →Select Layout resource file. Name the file as ‘nav_header.xml’ and click enter. Copy-paste the following code into the newly created layout file to define the header portion of the navigation drawer After finishing creating the header we need to create a menu resource file that will hold the items to be displayed in the drawer. Here’s how to create the menu resource file: Right-click the res folder →Select new →Android resource file →Choose ‘menu’ under the resource type drop-down list. Name the file as ‘navigation_menu.xml’ and copy-paste the following code into the file. The above code will create three items for the drawer and you can view them in the preview mode. They look like items in an ‘overflow’ menu but this resource file will be used to populate items in the drawer. Find out Free courses on Quick Code for various programming languages. Get new updates on Messenger. Step 3: In this last and final step, we will write Java code in the MainActivity.java file which will act as the brains and is responsible for the behavior of the Navigation drawer. So copy-paste the following code and I’ll explain what the lines of code mean in the subsequent paragraphs. Rename the package name to whatever you had chosen in the beginning, you can find the package name in the Manifest.xml file. In lines 14–16 we declare instance variables required for the navigation drawer and we later find them using the findViewById method inside the onCreate() function. Then we add a ‘setNavigationItemSelectedListener’ on the ‘nv’ variable, which is the navigation view, to listen for any click events on selecting a particular item from the drawer. The code inside ‘onNavigationItemSelected’ method is simply displaying a toast message. You can replace the code with whatever functionality you want to achieve. Finally, we override the ‘onOptionsItemSelected()’ method which is responsible for responding correctly to the items specified in the menu resource file.\", \"sampled\": \"Brand storytelling often walks a fine line between engaging an audience and presenting an authentic narrative, raising questions about whether it is better to be safe than sorry when crafting a brand's public persona. The decision to tell a fictional story is a complex one with potential benefits like creating an ideal consumer to inspire the audience but also carrying significant risks if not handled with complete transparency and ethical consideration from the outset. The core principle for brands employing fictional narratives is to avoid stating that the story is real when it is not as this transparency is crucial for maintaining long term trust with consumers. Making stories relatable and personable is a key strategy for connecting with an audience and encouraging them to believe in the brand and its message but this connection is fragile and depends heavily on the brand's perceived honesty. One perspective suggests that even bad publicity can be better than no publicity at all, as it can generate attention from consumers who might otherwise be unaware of the brand. This approach is fraught with peril because if a story is not handled ethically, it can cause significant problems later on, especially when there was no compelling reason to fabricate details in the first place, potentially damaging the brand's reputation permanently. A powerful aspect of using fictional narratives is that it gives a brand the creative freedom to construct an ideal consumer, a character designed to inspire the target audience and make them want to \\\"live like their brand.\\\" Certain stories are crafted specifically to make an audience feel a particular way, leveraging emotions to forge a connection, but the ethical implications of this emotional manipulation must be carefully weighed by the marketing team. An example of this strategy can be seen with the clothing company Hollister created a completely fictitious brand story to build its identity and appeal to its target demographic. This kind of fabricated history is not unique; the company Plan Norway also created a fabricated story to raise awareness for a social cause, demonstrating that this technique is used across different sectors for various purposes. The Plan Norway campaign centered on a fabricated story about a 12 year old girl, creating a blog that detailed the entire process of her becoming a bride-to-be, which successfully caused widespread awareness about the issue of young brides. The story gained so much traction that The Huffington Post wrote about it in 2014, highlighting the campaign's effectiveness in reaching a global audience and sparking a significant conversation. The Huffington Post was able to interview Olaf Thommessen, the National Director of Plan Norway, who provided insight into the campaign's strategy and its intended impact on public perception. The narrative was so compelling that it moved many concerned Norwegians to action, proving that a well-crafted, albeit fictional, story can be a powerful tool for social change when it resonates deeply with an audience's values. This campaign, orchestrated by Plan International, serves as a case study in the power of \\\"Storytelling\\\" to generate a massive public response and bring attention to a critical issue that might otherwise be ignored. The success of the blog about Thea and her fictional journey demonstrates how a brand in this case a non-profit organization can use a fabricated narrative to achieve a real-world goal prompting a re-evaluation of ethical boundaries in marketing.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"After defining some of the main concepts in the API world in the previous article, I will talk about the different ways of deploying an API Gateway for the Machine Learning platform. In this article, I will use the infrastructure and software layers designed in one of my previous articles. You may want to go through it to have a clearer view of the platform’s architecture before proceeding. As a reminder, the scope of this series of articles is the model serving layer of the ML platform’s framework layer. In other words, its “API Gateway”. Scope of this series of articles, by the author Now let’s start designing! A question may arise: If we are in an AWS environment, why not just use the fully managed and serverless AWS API Gateway? You never know if you don’t try. So let’s try this! 1 | Just the AWS managed API Gateway Here’s how AWS API Gateway could be placed in front of an EKS cluster. AWS API Gateway for the API Gateway layer, by the author First of all, AWS API Gateway is a fully managed service and runs in its own VPC: so we don’t know what’s happening between the scenes or any details about the infrastructure. Thanks to AWS documentation, we know that we can use API Gateway private integrations¹ to get the traffic from the API Gateway’s VPC to our VPC using an API Gateway resource of VpcLink². The Private VpcLink is a great way to provide access to HTTP(S) resources within our VPC without exposing them directly to the public internet. But that’s not all. The VpcLink is here to direct the traffic to a Network Load Balancer (NLB). So the user is responsible for creating an NLB which serves the traffic to the EKS cluster. With the support of NLB in Kubernetes 1.9+, we can create a Kubernetes service of type LoadBalancer With an annotation indicating that It’s a Network load balancer³. That would be a correct setup for the AWS API Gateway on EKS. We could as well benefit from WAF support for the managed API Gateway. The problem with this setup is we have the power of an API Gateway, but it’s far away from our cluster and our services. If we want to use a specific deployment strategy for each service, it would be great if this is done very close to the service (like in the service’s definition itself!). 2 | API Gateway closer to our ML models! Here’s another way of doing things. Design components: AWS API Gateway + NLB + Ambassador API Gateway. AWS API Gateway Combined with Ambassador for the API Gateway layer, by the author In this setup, we put an open-source API Gateway solution closer to our services. I will talk in detail about Ambassador in a future article.\", \"sampled\": \"Effective data visualization can be achieved by following three fundamental UX design principles that help clarify complex information. These guidelines are essential for creating dashboards and charts that communicate their intended message clearly and efficiently to any audience. The first of these guiding principles is known by the acronym KISS, which stands for the memorable phrase \\\"keep it simple, stupid.\\\" This concept emphasizes minimalism and clarity urging designers to strip away any non-essential elements that could distract from the data's core message. Applying the KISS principle means making specific design choices such as avoiding 3D charts unless they serve a necessary purpose. It also suggests that designers should avoid using secondary y-axes, as these can create confusion and make charts difficult to interpret quickly. To maintain a minimal and clean dashboard, designers should adhere to a limited color palette, using no more than five colors in total. Another effective technique for simplifying a chart's appearance is to remove the gridlines, which often add unnecessary visual clutter without contributing to comprehension. A second core principle for data visualization is that \\\"Form Follows Function,\\\" a concept that prioritizes the dashboard's main objectives above all else. This means the choice of chart type should be dictated entirely by the specific story the data needs to tell and the function it must perform. For instance, bar charts are the ideal choice when the primary goal is to compare different measures across categorical data. Their structure is uniquely suited for showing distinct values side-by-side, making direct comparisons intuitive for the viewer. Line charts serve a different function and are best used to illustrate trends that unfold over a period of time. This makes them particularly common and effective for time series analysis, where tracking changes and patterns is the main objective. When the goal is to represent the percentage and relative weight of different categories within a whole, a pie chart is the appropriate tool. This chart type excels at showing parts-of-a-whole relationships, giving a quick visual sense of proportion. Scatter plots are used to visualize the potential correlation that might exist between two different numeric variables. By plotting individual data points on a two-dimensional axis, these charts help reveal patterns, clusters, and relationships between the variables. The third and final principle is to strategically use hierarchy as a tool to guide the viewer's attention through the dashboard. A well-designed visual hierarchy ensures that the most critical information is seen first and its importance is immediately understood. Hierarchy can be effectively established through the deliberate use of visual cues such as size, color, and position on the screen. Larger elements, brighter colors, and prominent placement naturally draw the eye and signal greater importance to the viewer. Viewers are most likely to begin scanning a screen at the top left or in the center so this is where the most important information should be located. Placing key metrics and primary takeaways in these high visibility zones ensures they receive immediate attention.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Eating a man’oushe during recess every day comprised nothing more than engaging in a monotonous routine that satiated his hunger. It also afforded him a good excuse not to talk while pretending to socialize with the rest of his classmates. Wissam’s daily ritual consisted of spending 1,500 Lebanese Pounds at the school cafeteria (a small kiosk, really), where he had to unleash his WWE skills in order not to come out empty-handed. The process was more akin to a Herculean feat. Daily, the manager of the cafeteria stocked up on a fixed amount of edibles. He was always basing his analysis on a whimsical assumption that probably depended by and large on his mood when he woke up every morning. The employees would unflinchingly inform latecomers and those who couldn’t make their way through to the front of the line that no items were left except for a couple of boring snacks no one else had wanted. To come out victorious entailed devising meticulous strategies to be amongst the first arrivers to the kiosk. Some of the maneuvers included asking for permission to go to the restroom just five minutes before the class session ended, or faking a stomach ache to be allowed to leave to buy something to eat. The latter usually prompted the teacher to urge the student also to accompany their food with the quintessential concoction that everyone seemed to believe could cure any ache one was suffering from: tea. Going back to the man’oushe, it is a Lebanese pastry similar to pizza, served for any meal during the day. However, it is more common for breakfast or lunch, usually topped with cheese, thyme, minced meat, or keshek (a Lebanese dairy-based product), and a variety of other combinations. The smell of this tasty freshly baked dough can trigger an array of senses, depending on the topping. The melting sweetened Akkaoui white cheese on top of the gently rising dough in an arched brick oven exudes a smell, unlike any other aroma. A mosaic of scents can waft out from the oven to your nostrils uninvited, making its way to the pandora box of the mind where all your memories are stored. Despite having a man’oushe frequently at school, Wissam seldom went down memory lane. Not so much because of the commotion he had to go through to secure himself a tasty man’oushe, as much as because this staple of Lebanese breakfast was not baked right there in front of him. Wissam noticed that being present during the ritual of baking was as crucial as indulging his palate with an exquisite dance of the soft dough and the mix of heavenly thyme, cheese, or keshek. However, he couldn’t explain the reason behind this gut feeling. It wasn’t something that he could articulate. Nor was he sure that others would understand what he meant either. So he spent his days pondering about the renewed sense of awe and inspiration he experienced every time he frequented a bakery but kept his thoughts to himself. He looked forward to it and impatiently waited until the next time his parents decided to have mana’ish for breakfast, generally on weekends. Parents can be quite capricious, though, at some point weeks passed by without having any baked goods cravings. To that effect, Wissam endeavored to set out a plan to subtly plant the man’oushe seed in his parents’ minds so they would crave it more often, alas to no noticeable success. He couldn’t tell what exactly about the trip to and from the bakery that evoked such ineffable visceral feelings. It was a mundane ten minutes walk uphill, where nothing much happened. In a suburban setting, on an early Sunday morning, you could hear the chirping of birds singing to the melodies of occasional cars passing by, and dancing to the rhythm of a handful of exercising sports junkies seeking to breathe in some fresh air. But that was pretty much it. Nothing fancy. The stillness and the slow start of the weekends made it possible for Wissam to adjust back to a slower tempo to balance out the frenetic ebb and flow of his quotidian weekday routine. Nothing even compared to the joy Wissam experienced when he was the first customer to arrive at the bakery. The noise of the dough mixer could be heard outside within a substantial radius from the place. It was like music to his ears that acted as a warmup session before he got his hands dirty.\", \"sampled\": \"A study published in the American Journal of Medicine suggests that up to 15 percent of medical cases in developed countries are misdiagnosed, a concerning statistic for modern healthcare systems. The global impact of these errors is substantial with estimates indicating that 1.5 million people worldwide die annually due to misdiagnosis. This problem is compounded by the sheer number of known conditions which totals around 30,000 distinct diseases that medical professionals must consider. The challenge is especially acute for rare diseases. Among the vast number of known illnesses over 6,000 are classified as rare. The definition of a rare disease varies by region. In Europe, a disease is considered rare if it affects fewer than 1 in 2,000 people, a standard that helps allocate resources and focus research efforts on less common conditions. The United States applies a different threshold for classifying these conditions. A disease is designated as rare if it affects fewer than 200,000 people within the country. This definition is crucial for regulatory approvals and patient advocacy highlighting the significant number of individuals who can be impacted even by a single \\\"rare\\\" disease. A non-governmental alliance of rare disease patient organizations approximately 300 million people worldwide are living with a rare disease. This figure underscores that while individual diseases may be rare, the total population affected is very large. About half of all rare diseases are known to affect children. The journey to an accurate diagnosis for these patients is often a long and difficult one. The average time to receive a correct diagnosis for a rare disease is 5.6 years in the UK. In the USA, the situation is even more challenging for patients seeking answers. The average time to receive a correct diagnosis for a rare disease is 7.6 years. This diagnostic odyssey can be attributed to several factors including the vast landscape of medical knowledge and the specific focus of a typical physician's daily practice. A typical doctor deals with about 300 of the most common diseases. This specialization is practical. However, it means that when a patient presents with symptoms of a rare condition it may fall outside the doctor's immediate experience. This can lead to a prolonged process of elimination and specialist referrals before the correct diagnosis is made. The volume of new medical information presents another significant hurdle for practitioners. Each year, 2.5 million new scientific papers are published in resources like PubMed. This body of knowledge is expanding at a rate of 8 to 9 percent annually. It is impossible for any single human to keep up with this information explosion. This rapid growth in scientific literature makes it increasingly difficult for doctors to stay current on developments across all 30,000 known diseases. Misdiagnosis is not solely a problem of information overload or the rarity of a condition; it can also be caused by systemic issues within healthcare delivery. Factors contributing to diagnostic errors can include overworked medical professionals who have limited time with each patient. Issues with patient communication can also lead to misunderstandings. Errors in laboratory tests can further complicate the diagnostic process. Artificial intelligence offers a promising path forward. AI is not a replacement for doctors. Instead, it can serve as a powerful tool to augment their expertise. AI algorithms can help doctors by analyzing enormous data sets of patient information and medical literature. This can improve the speed and accuracy of diagnosis. This technology can sift through millions of scientific papers and patient records in seconds identifying patterns and potential diagnoses that a human might miss. For patients with rare diseases this capability could significantly shorten the diagnostic odyssey connecting their symptoms with information from obscure case studies or the latest research findings. AI can synthesize information from disparate sources. The potential for AI to reduce misdiagnosis rates is significant. By providing clinicians with data-driven insights and differential diagnoses these systems can help them consider a wider range of possibilities including rare conditions. This support could lead to more timely and accurate diagnoses improving patient outcomes. One of the key benefits is the ability to analyze complex symptom clusters and genetic information. This can help pinpoint rare diseases that often present with a wide variety of non-specific symptoms. This capability is especially important for the 300 million people worldwide living with a rare disease. For these individuals, a faster diagnosis means quicker access to appropriate treatment and support.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Infection | Brain Recovered Covid-19 Cases Still Show Brain Anomalies 3 Months Later First data that neurological signs and brain structural changes persist in Covid-19. But can the brain self-renew with time? Image by rawpixel.com Science knows that the novel coronavirus, SARS-CoV-2, can replicate in human neurons and brain organoids. Brain damage and neurological symptoms are also not rare in Covid-19 cases. Whether such brain insults are long-lasting, however, remains uncertain. And a new study published August in The Lancet, “Cerebral Micro-Structural Changes in COVID-19 Patients — An MRI-based 3-month Follow-up Study,” that casts light on this topic. Persistent Neurological Symptoms In this study, researchers at the Huashan Hospital in China recruited 60 Covid-19 patients that had recovered for over three months (mean age: 44.10; 56.7% males) and 39 non-Covid-19 controls (mean age: 45.88; 56.4% males). Both groups of participants were matched in terms of age, sex, and the prevalence of smoking, alcohol consumption, and underlying comorbidities. During Covid-19, over two-thirds of these 60 recovered Covid-19 patients had neurological symptoms. Other common Covid-19 signs were fever (88.33%), cough (56.67%), and gastrointestinal distress (13.33%). Treatments used were antivirals (96.67%), oxygen therapy (61.67%), antibiotics (35%), and interferons (15%). And 78.33% of them had mild, 20% had severe, and 1.67% had critical Covid-19. Even mild and not-so-old cases of Covid-19 may suffer compromised brain functions that are long-lasting. Three months later, over half (55%) of these patients still had neurological symptoms. Only the frequency of mood alterations and fatigue decreased significantly (blue asterisks) from 41.67% to 16.67% and 26.67% to 6.67%, respectively. Other neurological symptoms (see figure; such as headache, impaired mobility, numbness, myalgia, memory loss, etc.) persisted for over three months. And the fact that 78.33% of these patients initially had mild Covid-19 is also concerning as it means that persistent neurological symptoms can affect mild Covid-19 cases as well. Blue asterisks (p 0.05) mean changes are not statistically significant. Source: Lu et al. (2020). Cerebral Micro-Structural Changes in COVID-19 Patients — An MRI-based 3-month Follow-up Study. The Lancet. Persistent Brain Structural Changes Next, the researchers used two brain imaging techniques: Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) that captures cortical brain areas as a whole and diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) that detects microstructural brain changes. Results revealed that at three-month follow-up: Covid-19 patients had higher overall brain diffusivity and white matter volume — which further correlated negatively with memory loss — than non-Covid-19 controls. Covid-19 patients had greater grey matter volume (GMV) in the right cingulate, left Rolandic operculum, left Heschl’s gyrus, hippocampus, and olfactory cortices — which further correlated negatively with memory and smell loss, fatigue, and numbness — compared to non-Covid-19 controls. Among the structural brain changes, only cingulate gyrus correlated positively with Covid-19 severity during hospitalization. The cingulate gyrus is involved in attention, motivation, decision making, and learning. Put it another way; it also indicates that even mild Covid-19 cases may face persistent brain structural changes. Notably, all brain regions are examined except the brainstem due to technical limitations, which is unfortunate given that the brain’s cardiorespiratory centre resides in the brainstem. Other study limitations, the authors admitted, were the small sample size and participants recruitment from one hospital only. On the other side, the human brain displays a remarkable capacity to self-renew with time, such as following stroke or traumatic brain injury.\", \"sampled\": \"The concept of teleportation presents profound questions about personal identity and the continuity of our consciousness. Within one or two centuries, human teleportation technology might become a mature reality forcing us to confront what it truly means to be the same person after traveling from one location to another instantaneously. A significant step toward this future occurred in 2018 when scientists in China successfully teleported a photon from a location on Earth to a satellite. This remarkable experiment demonstrated a form of teleportation over a considerable distance moving the particle to an orbiting satellite located 300 miles away from its point of origin. Exploring the possibilities requires us to consider the different ways such technology might work with four hypothetical methods offering distinct outcomes for the traveler. These methods range from creating a perfect copy to transmitting the original body or mind, its own philosophical and existential implications for what constitutes a continuous self. One potential approach is the Facsimile method, which would function like a three-dimensional printer for humans. This process involve scanning a person's complete physical structure, deconstructing their original body, and then reprinting them at the destination using entirely new materials, effectively killing the original person to create a copy. Another possibility is Body Transmission, a method that would also require deconstructing the traveler at the atomic level. Unlike the Facsimile technique, this process would transmit the person's actual, where they would be precisely reassembled into their previous form raising questions about whether this preserves the self. A third concept, focuses on consciousness rather than physical matter by capturing all the data and electrochemical impulses within the brain. This information would then be transmitted to a biologically identical but artificially created replica body waiting at the destination, which would be imprinted with the traveler’s complete mental state. The final hypothetical method involves the use of Wormholes, which would function as a tunnel through the fabric of space-time itself. This approach would allow a person to step through a portal in Paris and emerge moments later in Buenos Aires, traveling the distance without any deconstruction or reassembly required. Of these four distinct methods, only the Wormhole approach appears to preserve the individual without raising the specter of death and replacement. By creating a direct pathway, it allows a person to pass through from one point to another completely intact maintaining an unbroken stream of physical and conscious existence. The Facsimile method poses the most direct challenge to personal identity as it results in the certain death of the original person. The individual who steps into the teleporter would cease to exist, identical person with all their memories would be created elsewhere, unaware of their status as a copy. Body Transmission presents a slightly different but equally complex problem as the process of deconstruction and reassembly might be considered a form of death, even if the original atoms are used. The continuity of consciousness would be interrupted, and whether the reassembled person is the same individual or a reconstruction is a difficult philosophical question. Mind Transmission also fails to preserve the original person, as the biological body is left behind while only its data is transferred. The person whose brain is scanned would remain while a new conscious entity awakens in a replica body.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I’m Reverting to My 9-Year-Old Self To Be More Productive The perks of not caring what others think Photo by Paweł Czerwiński on Unsplash I’ve been feeling pretty down lately. I’m not sure if it’s the quarantine blues setting in or if I’m just stressed out with life in general. We are trying to play some major catch-up financially after being laid off for 7 months, so I suspect that has something to do with my current emotional state. The thing is, I don’t want to feel this way. Sometimes when I’m feeling down, I like to bask in my depression. It just feels so good to swim around in sadness for a while. You know, turn on some Alanis Morissette and watch yourself cry in the mirror for a good hour or so. However, that’s not where I’m at. Right now, I want to be productive. I have this angsty feeling that I need to produce and create as much as I possibly can in the coming days, weeks, months. However, that becomes tough when you’re not feeling up to anything mentally. It’s as if my will for productivity is at war with my emotional, mental well being, and it’s not going well at all. The other day I was waiting in my car to pick my daughter up from school. A kid about 9 years old walked by, and he sparked the inspiration I so badly needed at that moment. This kid — this awesomely unaffected little boy — was having a full out conversation with himself as he walked home from school. He was laughing and muttering, using oversized hand gestures and simply having a grand ole chat all by his lonesome.\", \"sampled\": \"A 2006 study examined the effect that food visibility has on a person's overall consumption. The study placed jars of Hershey's kisses on the desks of secretaries. Half of the secretaries received chocolates in a transparent jar. This experiment was designed to test how simply seeing food would influence how much of it was eaten. The results were clear: the secretaries who could see the chocolates in the transparent jar ate significantly more than those who could not. This effect is explained by a phenomenon called food cue reactivity. Food is a powerful natural stimulant that can trigger cravings. Hunger can be activated by sight, smell, or even just a thought. When a person sees unhealthy calorie-rich food, the reward centers in their brain are activated. This makes them feel hungrier. This response can be accompanied by an increased heart rate. Drooling may also occur as the body prepares itself to eat. This entire process happens because the body is reacting to food cues. Food cue reactivity is the suggestion that seeing food will make the body crave it, which explains why the secretaries with the clear jars ate more chocolates. The secretaries who could see the Hershey's kisses in the transparent jar ate 71% more. Those who could not see the chocolates ate significantly less of them. Food cravings are not only triggered by sight. They can also be initiated by other stimuli associated with food. These triggers can include specific smells certain images or even particular locations. The sight of unhealthy food activates the reward centers in the brain, making a person feel hungrier.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"1) Python Basics In order to become a data scientist, you need to understand the basics of Python first. Because Python is the favored language for data science. By going through this course of edX “Introduction to Python for Data Science” you will have some basic understanding of python. 2) Statistics & Probability Stats is a key part of Data Science. If I am not wrong Data Science is all about statistics.\", \"sampled\": \"\\\"Big Mouth\\\" is an animated Netflix comedy series that deals with various topics, including mental health. The show represents depression as 'The Depression Kitty' and anxiety as 'Tito the Anxiety Mosquito'. Jessi Glaser in Big Mouth combats depression and anxiety with 'The Gratitoad'. Practicing gratitude is shown to minimize depression and anxiety in the show. A meta-analysis on gratitude's effects on mental health showed insignificant results in reducing depression and anxiety symptoms. Gratitude exercises may not be a standalone treatment for depression and anxiety.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Andrew Zhai | Pinterest tech lead, Visual Search Recently, we announced Lens BETA, a new way to discover objects and ideas from the world around on you using your phone’s camera. Just tap the Lens icon in the Pinterest app, point it at anything and Lens will return visually similar objects, related ideas or the object in completed projects or contexts. Lens enables you to go beyond traditional uses of your phone’s camera–taking selfies or saving a scene–and turns it into a powerful discovery system. It brings the magic of Pinterest into the real world, so that anything you see can lead to a related idea on Pinterest. Here we’ll share how we built Lens and the main technical challenges we overcame. Background In 2015, we launched our first visual search experience which enables people to pinpoint parts of an image and get visually similar results. With visual search, we gained a platform to advance our technology and incrementally improve the system by optimizing for not only relevant results but engaging ones, too. Pinners have responded positively to these improvements and now generate more than 250 million unique visual searches every month. As the next evolution of visual search, we introduced real-time object detection. This not only made visual search easier to use, but we also steadily gained a corpus of objects as people saved and selected them. Since its launch, we’ve generated billions of objects in just six month’s time, and have used this data to build new technologies, such as Lens and object search. If you’re interested in a more in-depth look at how we scaled our visual search technology to billions of images and applied it across Pinterest, please take a look at our Visual Discovery at Pinterest paper that was accepted for publication at World Wide Web (WWW) conference this year. Lens architecture A single Pin can take you down a rabbit hole of related ideas, enabling you to discover high quality content from 150M people around the world. As we developed Lens, we wanted to parallel this experience, so a single real world camera image could connect you to the 100B ideas on Pinterest. Lens combines our understanding of images and objects with our discovery technologies to offer Pinners a diverse set of results. For example, if you take a picture of a blueberry, Lens doesn’t just return blueberries: it also gives you more results such as recipes for blueberry scones and smoothies, beauty ideas like detox scrubs or tips for growing your own blueberry bush. To do this, Lens’ overall architecture is separated into two logical components.\", \"sampled\": \"As a romance author, Courtney Stars has discovered that the Kindle romance market is the largest market for Kindle books. This growing market offers vast opportunities for authors to connect with readers and share their stories. Nielsen's research in 2015 revealed that almost half of romance book fans indulge in the genre at least once a week. This statistic shows the dedicated readership that authors like Courtney Stars can engage with regularly. Understanding readers' preferences is key in the romance genre. Fans of romance novels often seek familiar tropes in their reading material, looking for elements that resonate with their romantic ideals and desires. One key insight gained by romance authors is that readers are forgiving of typos and mistakes if the storyline and characters are compelling. This emphasizes the importance of creating engaging narratives that capture readers' hearts. Despite economic downturns, the romance genre has shown resilience and growth, even during the last American recession. This trend highlights the enduring appeal of romance stories in providing comfort and entertainment to readers during challenging times. Romance readers not only seek emotional connection but also desire intellectual stimulation from the books they read. Authors like Dan Pedersen understand the importance of balancing emotional depth with immersive storytelling to captivate their audience. For many readers, romance novels offer an affordable escape from the stresses of everyday life. The immersive and uplifting nature of these stories provides a welcome reprieve from reality, allowing readers to lose themselves in tales of love and passion. The success of Hallmark Channel's romance movies in recent years underscores the enduring popularity of romantic storytelling across different media. Bill Abbott's leadership has seen the channel flourish with heartwarming and relatable romantic narratives. Authors like Courtney Stars have seen financial success in the romance genre, with royalties of $86 earned for January alone. This financial reward reflects the dedication and skill required to craft compelling romance narratives that resonate with readers. Kindle Unlimited offers readers access to a vast library of books for a monthly fee of $9.99. This subscription service provides romance fans with a cost-effective way to explore new authors and discover captivating love stories. Romance Writers of America serves as a valuable resource for authors looking to connect with fellow writers, industry professionals, and readers. Being part of this association allows authors to stay informed about trends, opportunities, and best practices in the romance genre. Authors like Courtney Stars have found that engaging with readers and understanding their preferences are essential for success in the romance genre. By crafting compelling stories that resonate with readers' desires, authors can forge meaningful connections and build a loyal fan base.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"5 Tips to Talk Persuasively About Climate Disruption Katrine Follow Dec 15 · 5 min read Photo by Jason Rosewell on Unsplash Someone close to me does not believe in human-caused climate disruption. He’s a successful businessman and highly rational. How is it possible that he doesn’t believe in the scientific consensus? After all, 97% of climate scientists agree that human activities are causing climate disruption. People develop defence mechanisms to avoid the uncomfortable truths around global warming, explains Per Espen Stoknes. He is a psychologist and economics PhD who focuses on climate psychology, climate economics, and climate policy. If your job is in the fossil fuels industry, or you depend on cheap gas (thanks to a long car commute to work, say), it is psychologically much harder to admit that your lifestyle is disrupting the air than if you work in the re-insurance industry (which daily calculates climate risks) or drive an electric car. — Stoknes, Per Espen. What We Think About When We Try Not To Think About Global Warming (p. 107). Chelsea Green Publishing. Kindle Edition. How can we talk more persuasively about climate disruption to get past people’s mental defence mechanisms? 1. Paint a Picture of a Beautiful Future I feel disheartened reading about the imminent catastrophes in our news. “Stop travelling,” we’re told, and still we are headed for tragedy. Instead, we need a joint vision to fight for, where we are healthier, breathe fresher air, and enjoy wildlife. Here’s a beautiful world described by the author Mike Berners-Lee: The air is fresher. Life is healthier, longer, more relaxed, more fun and more exciting. Our diets are varied, tasty and healthy. More of us get out as much as we want to, both socially and physically. Travel is easier — but we spend less time in transit. We feel freer to live life in whatever way seems meaningful to each of us at the time, in negotiation with other people’s equal right to do likewise.\", \"sampled\": \"Google Cloud Platform's Resource Hierarchy is composed of the Organization Node, Folders, and Projects. This structure allows for effective organization and management of resources. IAM permissions within Google Cloud can be granted at the Organization Node, Folder, or Project level. This flexibility enables fine-grained control over access to resources. Primitive IAM roles within GCP consist of Owner, Editor, and Viewer roles. These roles provide varying levels of access and control over resources within the platform. Predefined IAM roles in Google Cloud are tailored to specific GCP services within a project. This specificity ensures that permissions are allocated appropriately based on the service requirements. Custom IAM roles in GCP offer the ability to create precise sets of permissions. This customization allows organizations to define unique access controls based on their specific needs. Service accounts play a important role in granting access to resources in Google Cloud. They are designed to provide access to resources rather than individual users, enhancing security and manageability. Establishing a company hierarchical setup is essential for the organization's tech stack. This structure ensures proper resource allocation and access control within the cloud environment. Maintaining good collaboration is crucial for upholding security standards between customers and cloud service providers. Effective communication and cooperation are key to ensuring a secure cloud infrastructure. In Google Cloud Platform, there are three types of IAM Roles available, offering varying levels of access and control within the platform. The management levels for IAM Roles in Google Cloud consist of the Organization Node, where high-level permissions are set, and the Project level, which defines permissions at a more granular level. In a complex example scenario, two service accounts may be used to grant access to different resources within the Google Cloud environment. Understanding the details of IAM roles, resource hierarchy, and service accounts is fundamental to effectively managing security and access control within Google Cloud Platform. Creating a robust security framework and access control strategy is paramount for organizations leveraging cloud services to protect their data and infrastructure.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This feature story is a personal retrospect of the extraordinary career and life of Dr. Christopher W. Clark, one of the world’s leading marine acoustics experts. He is the Chief Marine Scientist at Planet OS with 30 years of experience in the field. In 1992, the first time I tried to listen to a singing blue whale in very deep water all I heard was a giant hum. I was convinced that my recording equipment was broken. I checked all the cable connections, all the dial settings; everything was working. I knew some singers were out there because I could see their voiceprints on the Navy displays. And then it hit me — of course I couldn’t hear the whales because my ears and mind were not tuned to perceive the pitch or rhythms of their songs. The pitch was below my threshold of hearing, a single note lasted 20 seconds, and a single phrase lasted almost 2 minutes! But I knew how to solve this problem. I had to play back the recording at a much higher speed, and when I did — voila, I heard the whales singing! And to my joyful surprise it was not just one singer, but an entire chorus, and as I listened longer it was not just one chorus, but the entire ocean! I grew up on Bound Brook Island in the town of Wellfleet on Cape Cod, Massachusetts. We were surrounded by pristine wilderness. Our home and those of my grandparents were always filled with music, poetry and literature books. I happened to have a very good ear and a cherubic voice, so when I was nine years old, my mother drove me to New York City to audition for the boy’s choir at the Cathedral of Saint John the Divine.\", \"sampled\": \"Miley Cyrus who is known for her pop music, ventured into new territory during a tribute performance for late singer Chris Cornell. She surprised the audience by singing 'Say Hello To Heaven,' a departure from her usual style. Despite initial skepticism, Cyrus impressed the crowd with her rendition showing a different side of her talent. The audience's initial reaction to Miley Cyrus's performance was mixed with some booing and chuckling. However, as Cyrus got into the song, customizing it to suit her style, she won over the crowd. She showed her vocal range by broadening her lower registry which is a departure from her typical high-energy performances. During the tribute show, Miley Cyrus reinvented herself with a grungy presence, embodying the essence of the '90s rock era. This transformation allowed her to emotionally connect with the audience in a profound way, showing her versatility as an artist. Cyrus's performance stood out as she embraced a new genre and captivated the audience with her raw, heartfelt delivery. Reflecting on her performance, Miley Cyrus shared a poignant insight and said, \\\"You know when you look at a list and it says 'What doesn't fit?'. I know I was the one that didn't fit.\\\" This sentiment encapsulates her experience stepping out of her comfort zone and exploring a different musical landscape. Cyrus's willingness to take risks and push boundaries paid off, earning her newfound respect from the audience. In a tribute to Chris Cornell, Miley Cyrus's performance not only honored the late singer but also showcased her adaptability and artistry. By embracing a new genre and delivering a heartfelt rendition of 'Say Hello To Heaven,' Cyrus demonstrated her ability to connect with diverse audiences and transcend musical boundaries. Her performance served as a testament to her growth as an artist and her willingness to explore new artistic horizons.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Business Models The Nitty Gritty Why Sarah decided to offer her online course Bank Boost in a live setting using weekly emails and live Q and A sessions The values and psychology behind her product pricing and how she markets the live courses in a low-key way without webinars Why she goes offline for a couple days at a time to work on content — and how that frees up the rest of her time to work with clients and so much more How she plans to market her live courses in the future What Sarah’s different streams of income look like Sarah Von Bargen writes and teaches about “the space where money and happiness meet.” In this episode of What Works, Sarah walks us through her decision-making process to transform one of her popular eBooks into an online course and everything else that goes into promoting and running it. We release new episodes of What Works every week. Subscribe on iTunes so you never miss an episode. Why take things live? “It’s a values-based thing. It doesn’t feel good to me to take hundreds of dollars from someone and then they don’t use the thing that I sold them. I believe in the work that I do, I believe in my course, and I know that if they did the course, it would be life-changing. But if I can make some tweaks on my end, and help them get to the place that they want to go, why wouldn’t I?” — Sarah Von Bargen Why did Bank Boost go from an eBook to an online class? Well, Sarah wanted to create an opportunity for her audience to not only benefit from the information — but to work through it along with a community of like-minded people going through the same thing. While small business owners might shy away from turning a DIY experience, like working through an eBook, into a live curated one for their customers or clients, Sarah says that it’s not that much extra work.\", \"sampled\": \"Drawing is more than just a creative outlet; it can also be a powerful tool for problem-solving and critical thinking. Katie Mccurdy, an author, emphasizes that drawing can kickstart the development of essential cognitive skills. Drawing can have a significant impact on your brain chemistry. Positive neurotransmitters like serotonin, endorphins, dopamine, and norepinephrine are released when you draw, leading to a boost in mood and cognitive function. Illustrator Cara Bean supports this view, highlighting the physiological benefits of drawing. One of the most immediate benefits of drawing is its ability to reduce stress. Taking a moment to put pen to paper can help reduce feelings of tension and anxiety. Pictal Health, a startup focusing on visual problem-solving, recognizes drawing as a stress-relieving practice. When you feel stuck or overwhelmed, turning to drawing can be effective. It helps to untangle your thoughts, giving you a fresh perspective on the issues at hand. This process of externalizing your problems through drawing can lead to a sense of clarity and improved emotional well-being. Don't worry about creating a masterpiece when drawing out your problems. The goal is not perfection but rather exploration and expression. Remember, your drawings are for your eyes only and serve as a visual thinking aid to guide your problem-solving journey. Consider drawing your problem as a preliminary step in the problem-solving process. It acts as a rough draft, laying the groundwork for more detailed and refined solutions. Starting with a visual representation can lead to innovative ideas and unconventional approaches. Experiment with different mediums and scales when drawing out your problems. Using large paper or a whiteboard can offer a broader canvas for your thoughts and encourage creative exploration. Pictal Health advises utilizing these tools to gain new perspectives and expand your problem-solving horizons. Drawing your problems can also be a catalyst for stepping outside your comfort zone. It can inspire you to take on new challenges, such as launching a startup. By visually mapping out your ideas and obstacles, you can find the courage to pursue ventures that once seemed daunting.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"What is Order fulfilment? Order fulfilment is the process of receiving and storing inventory to be sold online to consumers or B2B clients, during which orders are processed and delivered to each customer. The process starts when a customer places an order and concludes once they receive it by post or courier. What is the order fulfilment process? Here’s an overview of the steps involved in order fulfilment: Receiving inbound inventory — You send your stock to a fulfilment warehouse Storage of inventory — Your stock is stored awaiting online orders Order processing — this includes: Pick and pack / Kitting or light assembly of hampers or gift sets Shipping — At the carrier station each order is packed and despatched to your customer via an integrated courier partner Returns management — Orders are returned to a fulfilment centre location. So if a buyer wants to return a product, a 3PL partner manages the return transaction as well as quality, stock update or disposal These are the five key order fulfilment steps that ensure same-day fulfilment and outbound shipping to enhance your customer’s experience. Can order fulfilment companies integrate with my website? Yes. Any Warehouse Management Software (WMS), order fulfilment technology, is the bridge between your eCommerce platform and a fulfilment partner. Features include: Inventory management Order management Shipping tracking management Reporting functionality Most WMS can integrate with off-the-shelf versions of leading eCommerce platforms such as: Shopify Shopify Plus Magento Squarespace EKM WooCommerce BigCommerce Linnworks Other integrations include: Marketplaces — Amazon and eBay Carriers — too many to mention If you choose to outsource, you can integrate from a vast selection of ‘off-the-shelf’ eCommerce platforms, which can be done in a few clicks with no technical experience. Alternatively, a self-service solution means that you can import your orders through a client portal. How does the Order fulfilment Goods-in process work? A unique WMS account is created for every online retailing client. In doing so, your Third-Party-Logistic (3PL) partner can integrate and check-in barcoded products more efficiently, typically within 24–72 hours depending on seasonal demand. Once your stock is received, a barcode scanner identifies the correct product and storage location. This process ensures a fast, accurate and efficient order fulfilment process going forward once onboarded. How does the Order fulfilment storage process work?\", \"sampled\": \"Brand storytelling is a powerful tool for creating authentic messages that deeply connect with consumers. This storytelling technique is essential for brands aiming to resonate with their audience on a meaningful level. The hero’s journey stands as a universal formula for crafting compelling narratives. This storytelling structure, popularized by Joseph Campbell, provides a framework that resonates across cultures and time periods. Dan Harmon, known for his work on \\\"Community,\\\" simplified the hero’s journey theory into 8 simple steps. This breakdown offers a practical guide for storytellers looking to create engaging and impactful narratives. In the hero's journey, various stages unfold, including Establishing a protagonist, Crossing the Threshold, and Bringing it Home. Each phase plays a important role in building tension, developing characters, and resolving conflicts in the story. One of the key themes of the hero's journey is the cyclical movement between order and chaos. This descent into the unknown and subsequent return with newfound wisdom is a fundamental aspect of many successful narratives. Crafting a character to embody a brand's values and essence can help create a cohesive and compelling brand narrative. Characters like Woody, Buzz Lightyear, and Andy from Pixar's \\\"Toy Story\\\" franchise exemplify this concept. Consistency in storytelling across different touchpoints can significantly influence how consumers perceive a brand. By maintaining a unified narrative, brands can establish a strong and memorable identity in the minds of their audience. As Curtis Batterbee writes, \\\"Storytelling is en vogue, a phrase which here means ‘brands and agencies are all talking about it without taking due time to appreciate what it means’.\\\" This quote underscores the importance of understanding the depth and impact of storytelling in branding. George Lucas, the creator of the iconic \\\"Star Wars\\\" franchise, masterfully employed elements of the hero's journey in his storytelling. The enduring success of his narratives highlights the timeless appeal of this storytelling structure. Lego's success in building a vast universe through its videogame franchise showcases the power of storytelling in engaging audiences. With over 100 million units sold, Lego is a case study of effective brand storytelling in action. Ranked as the 15th most successful franchise in the industry, Lego's storytelling prowess has played a significant role in its enduring popularity. The brand's ability to create immersive narratives has captivated audiences of all ages worldwide.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The task of classifying the images into respective classes, the task has been divided into 5 steps: STEP 1: The first and the most important task in Machine Learning is to analyze the dataset before proceeding with any algorithms. This is important in order to understand the complexity of the dataset which will eventually help in designing the algorithm. The distribution of images and the classes are as follows: As already mentioned before, there are 12 classes and a total of 4750 images. However, as seen from the above, the distribution is not even and the class distribution varies from maximum of 654 images to a minimum of 221 images. This clearly demonstrates the data is imbalanced and the data need to be balanced in order to get the best results. We will come to that in STEP 3. Image distribution per class Now it is important to visualize the images in order to understand the data even better. So, some sample images from each class is displayed in order to see how the images differ from each other. There is nothing much that can be understood from the images above as all the images looked pretty much same. So, I decided to see the distribution of the images using a visulaization techninque called t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding (t-SNE).\", \"sampled\": \"Google Cloud Spanner supports ANSI 2011 compatible SQL and ensures robust database operations. There are two JDBC drivers available for Cloud Spanner, facilitating the execution of SQL and DML statements efficiently. ORMs play a vital role in simplifying data manipulation within Cloud Spanner, enhancing overall database management. Cloud Spanner allows declarative SQL statements for efficient querying of databases, streamlining data retrieval processes. DML, tailored for transaction processing in Cloud Spanner, ensures reliable and secure data modifications. Partitioned DML within Cloud Spanner is specifically designed for bulk updates with minimal interference on ongoing transactions. Mutations in Cloud Spanner represent essential database operations like inserts, updates, and deletes, ensuring data integrity. Client libraries are commonly utilized to access Cloud Spanner APIs, enabling seamless integration and utilization of its features. Java expert Peter Runge explains leveraging Cloud Spanner with Java, emphasizing its potential in modern database management. Spring, a popular Java framework, enhances the development experience when working with Cloud Spanner, boosting productivity. Cloud Spanner's Mutation API offers robust capabilities for managing database operations and ensures efficient data handling. Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) drivers play a important role in executing SQL and DML statements within Cloud Spanner, ensuring smooth database interactions. Google Cloud Spanner provides advanced features for efficient and scalable database management. The ANSI 2011 SQL standard support in Google Cloud Spanner ensures compatibility and adherence to industry standards.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The 5 Components of $1,000 Articles Insights from analyzing my best-performing and selling articles Photo by Sara Deis on Unsplash One thousand dollar articles are the ones that every professional writer aims to sell. This often means that you’re paid $1 or more per word. The norm of this type of pay comes from working with big clients or working with big publications. When you’re starting as a writer, you’re more likely to sell an article to a big publication for $1,000 than to gain a client that pays $1 per word consistently. I began my writing career on Medium last year.\", \"sampled\": \"Kaggle provides options for downloading competition datasets and standalone datasets. Competition datasets are related to current or past competitions. Standalone datasets can be uploaded by anyone. To download a competition dataset, use the competition_download_file method. Standalone datasets can be downloaded using the dataset_download_file method. All datasets downloaded with the Kaggle API are in ZIP file format. Unzipping datasets can be done manually or using Python. The Sentiment of Tweets dataset is sizable with a size of 1.6M. When working with Kaggle datasets, remember that the current directory path is denoted by \\\"./\\\". For sentiment analysis tasks, the dataset ID for sentiment140 is 140.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Unlike Huawei, who had nothing to do with influencing my consumer behavior, there are plenty of companies out there that learned to leverage the subconscious to alter our decision-making intentionally. In 2001, Cellnet, a struggling communications network, rebranded as O2 and launched a campaign that got them from the last to the first position in the market within four years. How did they pull that off? Instead of targeting rational thinking, their ads were all about emotions. First, the core message that O2 marketing geniuses came up with was: “O2: See what you can do.” Then, all of their ads featured a watery blue atmosphere with bubbles flowing through it, people smiling, flirting, and floating around. There was also a dog catching a ball, and the whole thing was wrapped up in calm and serene music — well, at least according to 2001’s standards.\", \"sampled\": \"The Connected Components algorithm is a hard clustering algorithm. It can find clusters in connected data. This algorithm is based on a special case of BFS/DFS and can be used to find families in datasets. It also aids in fraud prevention by detecting fraudulent networks using family IDs. The Networkx module in Python is widely used to create and analyze graphs. Distinct continents and cities can be identified using the connected components algorithm. This method can be applied to various datasets for different use cases. In an example graph, there are three connected components and twelve cities. The algorithm can be adapted to analyze different scenarios effectively. Cities like Mannheim, Frankfurt, Karlsruhe, Erfurt, Wurzburg, Munchen, Numberg, Stuttgart, Augsburg, and Kassel can be analyzed using graph algorithms. Understanding and utilizing graph algorithms like Connected Components can enhance data analysis and decision-making processes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Why Word Placement Is Important in Headlines Readers are attracted to the first 3 words and the last 3 words Illustration by Cynthia Marinakos. What you do when you’re confronted with emails and social posts each day? How do you browse through news websites and Medium articles? Do you click on each one? Most likely, you skim through the headlines to get the gist of it without having to click each one. Only clicking on the ones that call out to you at that moment, if you’ve got time. A Kissmetrics study discovered that users pick out the first three words and the last three words of a headline. Headline expert Bnonn recommends keeping a headline to six words so that readers can quickly understand it. Copyblogger informs us that on average only two out of ten readers will read your article — eight out of ten will only read your headline copy. That’s why it’s worth paying attention to word selection and word placement. It doesn’t mean we need to stick to only six words, although I came across several sites with headlines that consistently hovered around six words. For longer headlines, it helps to consider the first three words and the last three words. “As a rule, if it won’t fit in a tweet it’s too long.\", \"sampled\": \"Instagram recently updated its platform, sparking controversy among users and influencers like James Charles. The redesign placed essential features in the upper-right corner, leading to user dissatisfaction. Reels and Shop buttons are now prominently displayed in the bottom menu for easier access. However, the update made the camera and notifications buttons less accessible to users. Instagram's focus with this update is to prioritize Reels and e-commerce features over traditional posts. Some users may unintentionally tap on Reels or Shop due to muscle memory from the old layout. An Instagram blog post hints at the platform's awareness of potential user disappointment. In the words of Mariana Vargas, \\\"Instagram is very explicit with its intentions on its blog: to prioritize Reels and its e-commerce platform over traditional posts.\\\" Overall, the update has stirred mixed reactions within the Instagram community. Users are adjusting to the new layout, but many find the changes disruptive to their usual browsing habits. Influencers like James Charles have openly criticized the update, expressing their frustrations with the platform's direction. The UX team at Instagram may need to address user feedback and consider adjustments to enhance user experience. As Instagram continues to evolve its platform, user feedback and adaptability will drive future updates. The controversy surrounding the recent Instagram update highlights the delicate balance between innovation and user satisfaction.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"It’s Complicated Visualizing complex health histories & symptoms for two patients with rare and mystery conditions I recently worked with a smart, well-spoken patient, E., to put together a visual summary of her health. Unlike many others I’ve worked with, she was not looking for answers or a new diagnosis; she was motivated because she was starting the application process for a new service dog, and she needed to demonstrate her physical constraints and daily needs. She also had a complex medical history, with multiple chronic conditions, injuries, surgeries and procedures, and she wanted to see everything in one place for the first time. I went through my standard process of gathering her information, talking through her story with her, and creating visuals to represent the conversation. I then created a detailed timeline and symptom map, with an emphasis on her two longstanding conditions, Cryoglobulinemia and Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (EDS). Both of these conditions are considered ‘rare diseases’ — meaning each one affects fewer than 200,000 Americans at any given time. A quick aside with some definitions: Cryoglobulinemia is the presence of abnormal proteins in the blood, and symptoms often include skin lesions and purple spots, joint pain, peripheral neuropathy (in other words burning or tingling in hands and feet), and more.\", \"sampled\": \"This isn't the solitude of a hermit; it is the consequence of a home surviving on a single salary. With the strain on her marriage mounting the author turned to writing. This was no mere hobby but a lifeline, a coping mechanism. Coupled with the financial pressure of a single income was an enormous burden. Writing allowed her to express herself to shed light on her internal struggles. It offered a comforting distraction from her stepmother's battle with stage 4 breast cancer and the fact that her father had become the sole carer. But the pain didn't end there. The author's best friend died in September, a blow that sent shockwaves through her already tumultuous life. The weight of this loss on top of the isolation and financial strain was almost unbearable. Although the author's family has never met her husband, they remain a significant part of her life. The last time she saw her father was in 2013, and her mother, a year later in 2014. Despite the trials she faces, the author remains resilient. She continues to write to use her words as a balm for her wounds. In the face of adversity, she remains steadfast showing that even in the darkest of times creativity can be a beacon of hope.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We Need a Code of Ethics We’ve been moving too fast for too long and it’s hurting everyone. I keep wondering what we can do to ensure we’re building a better world with the products we make and if we need a code of ethics. So often I see people fall through the cracks because they’re “edge cases” or “not the target audience.” But at the end of the day, we’re still talking about humans. Other professions that can deeply alter someone’s life have a code of ethics and conduct they must adhere to. Take physicians’ Hippocratic oath for example. It’s got some great guiding principles, which we’ll get into below. Violating this code can mean fines or the losing the ability to practice medicine. We also build things that can deeply alter someone’s life, so why shouldn’t we have one in tech? While this isn’t a new subject by any stretch, I made a mini one of my own. It’s been boiled down to just one thing and is flexible enough to guide all my other decisions. I even modified it from the Hippocratic Oath: We will prevent harm whenever possible, as prevention is preferable to mitigation. We will also take into account not just the potential for harm, but the harm’s impact. That’s meaningful to me because our for years, tech had a “move fast, break things” mentality, which screams immaturity. It’s caused carelessness, metrics-obsessed growth, and worse— I don’t need to belabor that here. We’ve moved fast for long enough, now let’s grow together to be more intentional about both what and how we build. Maybe the new mantra could be “move thoughtfully and prevent harm,” but maybe that isn’t quite as catchy. A practical example Recently, a developer launched a de-pixelizer. Essentially, it can take a pixelated image and approximate what the person’s face might look like. The results were…not great. Setting aside that the AI seems to have been only trained on white faces, we have to consider how process this might go wrong and harm people. Imagine that this algorithm makes it into the hands of law enforcement, who mistakenly identifies someone as a criminal. This mistake could potentially ruin someone’s life, so we have to tread very carefully here. Even if the AI achieves 90% accuracy, there’s still a 10% chance it could be wrong. And while the potential for false positives might be relatively low, the impact of those mistakes could have severe consequences. Remember, we aren’t talking about which version of Internet Explorer we should support, we’re talking about someone’s life — we have to be more granular because both the potential and impact of harm is high here. Accident Theory and Preventing Harm Kaelin Burns (Fractal’s Director of Product Management) has this to say about creating products ethically: “When you create something, you also create the ‘accident’ of it.\", \"sampled\": \"In a thought-provoking blog post, Barry Davret explores the notion of alternate perspectives on holiday classics. He delves deep into the essence of these beloved tales, examining their underlying themes and narratives from a different lens. His discourse revolves around the idea that certain holiday classics may not align with the modern values that we as a society uphold today. In his critical analysis, takes on the story of \\\"Rudolph, The Rednosed Reindeer.\\\" He articulates a critique of the story's portrayal of friendship and acceptance. He points out that the narrative's approach towards these concepts may not resonate with the contemporary notions of inclusivity and diversity. The criticism centers on the idea that the story’s depiction of friendship and acceptance is conditional hinging on Rudolph's usefulness rather than his inherent worth. He then turns his attention to another holiday classic, \\\"Frosty The Snowman\\\". His critique of this story stems from its depiction of children running away from home. He argues that this narrative may send the wrong message to impressionable young minds. He emphasizes that such a portrayal may not align with the values of safety and responsibility that we aim to instill in our children. Davret goes beyond mere criticism and suggests an intriguing solution. He proposes the idea of rewriting these classic holiday stories to reflect modern values. He advocates for a retelling that incorporates contemporary principles of acceptance, friendship, and responsibility. This concept of reimagining and rewriting is not unfamiliar as seen in literary works like \\\"Wicked,\\\" which presents an alternate perspective to the \\\"Wizard of Oz.\\\" The author emphasizes the importance of perspective in interpreting stories and facts. He believes that the way we view and understand these narratives can greatly influence the lessons we extract from them. He posits that by shifting our perspective, we may unveil new meanings and insights that were not apparent in the traditional interpretation. Barry Davret encourages the practice of retelling stories from alternate perspectives. He sees this as an opportunity to infuse these tales with modern values and principles. He believes that we can preserve the charm and magic of these stories while ensuring that they resonate with our evolving societal norms and beliefs. The author's exploration of these alternate perspectives opens up a fresh discourse on our approach towards classic tales. It prompts us to question and reevaluate the messages embedded in these narratives. This practice of critical analysis and reinterpretation allows us to continue enjoying these stories while aligning them with our current values. In conclusion, Barry Davret's blog post offers a unique perspective on holiday classics. His analysis and suggestions not only invite us to critically examine these beloved tales but also encourage us to reinterpret and retell them in a way that reflects modern societal values. His discourse reminds us that while these stories are classic, our interpretations of them need not be static.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"How I landed a job in UX Design at Google 3 UX lessons that school doesn’t teach you My Google Onsite Interview in the Sunnyvale Campus Nowadays, a lot of people are making a fuss of the difficulty that individuals are facing in getting into UX Design and in finding an internship job. Flat out, people can easily gain UX methods by enrolling through online courses. They can also gain it through Design Schools and bootcamps. As a result, every Junior Designers’ application forms have mentioned almost the same skills with one another, thus, making it hard to stand out in a pile of resumes on the office desk. When I enrolled in my Master School studying HCI, while wearing my rose colored glasses, I clearly realized the true meaning of the reality of life without the sugar coating and all — even if you have the UX methods and you are passionate with what you do, that doesn’t mean that you can easily succeed in job search. In fact, I love doing so many things — I love bouncing my ideas off, getting my creative juices flowing, jotting people’s quotes down and interpreting the insights behind it, pitching the ideas to the clients and showing of the ideas proudly. I thought that these skills will help me in getting my intern job, but I was wrong. All I received was my delivered resumes and portfolios with bowed head. I tried pushing my luck again but then, I was still rejected. Here, I am going to share my story on how my perseverance lead me in learning skills that are very significant and beneficial with my field and yet nobody teaches these lessons in schools. Design Pattern Way back summer 2017, I started a job at a local start up for my internship. Since I believed I was lacking enough industry experience in my first job, I started to look for my second internship during the fall. I was confident that my design skills were “enough”. Boy, I was wrong. I had an interview with the Design Director at Zhihu, China’s Quora. I was challenged with a question:“ Hey, are you always an Android user?” I said yes, honestly, hadn’t had a second thought. He smirked. And later I was rejected with the realization that he’s a huge apple fan who avidly answers questions such as “What are the most impressive design details in Apple’s iOS 11?” After that interview, that’s when I realized that with all the innovations in our generation now, designers should not just focus with just one brand’s interface design (e.g. Android or Apple), instead, the skills and knowledge of a designer must be broad enough to satisfy distinctive design patterns. So to widen my skills, I switched to iPhone and started a study group to read design guidelines (both iOS Human Interface Guidelines and Google Material Design) and do app critiques together, following Julie Zhuo’s How to do a Product Critique. You know the feeling that your skills are improving little by little? I persisted for two months and that’s how I felt. I never actually thought that it felt so rewarding and overwhelming! I became more sensitive about the design patterns and how designers can leverage its power to improve communication with developers and ease the learning curve for our users. (Check this awesome presentation, Communication Between Designers and Developers, for more detail.) How I learned Design Patterns through app critiques (left) and design guidelines (right) Design Strategy Lately, I’ve been asking myself these questions like: Why do some products succeed but other products with the same design patterns fail? e.g. Stories Why are there products being widely used even though they aren’t intuitive at all? e.g. Snapchat Why do some cool visual styles kill the vibes of a product? e.g. Wikipedia, Facebook I browsed through my role model’s Twitter account and I discovered some factors that can affect a product. There was this concept that blew me away, it was called the Network Effect, the moat that Uber, Airbnb, Facebook, Amazon are desperately building.\", \"sampled\": \"John Mashni, known for his entrepreneurial spirit and love for writing, embarked on a new journey - he decided to write a children's book. \\\"CinderToot\\\" was a venture into uncharted territory for Mashni who had previously spent his time engrossed in the works of famous film directors like Hitchcock, Kubrick, and Gilliam. Interestingly, Mashni admitted that he had initially underestimated the artistic value of children's books. This revelation came as a surprise, considering his appreciation for the fine arts and the intricacies of storytelling. It was a reminder of the complexity and depth that can be found in even the simplest of narratives. In fact, Mashni confessed that writing a short story for children was often harder than writing a long one. This was something he learned through the process of creating \\\"CinderToot\\\". It was not a simple task of penning down a quick tale.It required a great deal of creativity and an understanding of the young audience's perspective. His journey of writing the book was not without its challenges. Mashni wrote a total of 13 drafts before finalizing his book. It was a testament to his dedication and commitment to creating a story that was not only engaging but also meaningful for his young readers. Yet, all the hard work was worth it. The joy he found in selling his book was immeasurable. It was not just about the financial gain but about the happiness and laughter his book brought to children and their parents. He found fulfillment in knowing that his book was creating joyful memories for families. In fact, Mashni's book provided at least 30 minutes of joy to a family. It was a small amount of time but for a family engrossed in the delightful world of \\\"CinderToot\\\", it was a cherished moment. Mashni had succeeded in his goal of selling joy, one book at a time. Mashni once stated \\\"Children’s books are an incredible art form. Selling joy is one of the best things you can sell. Inspiring children is honorable.\\\" This quote truly captures the essence of his journey as an author of a children's book. But the impact of \\\"CinderToot\\\" did not stop at just bringing joy. The book also inspired a young girl to write her own children's book. This was Mashni's niece after hearing his book, decided to create her own version of \\\"CinderToot\\\". His niece proudly declared \\\"I’m writing my own version of CinderToot. I want to write my own children’s book like you.\\\" It was a proud moment for Mashni seeing the ripple effect of his work. His book had not only entertained but also motivated the younger generation to explore their creativity. Looking back at his journey, Mashni realized the value of children's books. Not just as a form of entertainment but as a powerful tool to inspire and ignite creativity in young minds. His venture into writing \\\"CinderToot\\\" was not merely about creating a children's book but about contributing to the world of children's literature. John Mashni's journey is a testament to the power of writing and the potential it has to bring joy and inspire others. His story serves as a reminder to never underestimate any form of art for even in the simplest of children's books, there lies a world of creativity and inspiration. Mashni's book was a small addition to the world of children's literature. But for the children who read it for the families who found joy in it and for the young girl who was inspired by it, \\\"CinderToot\\\" was a priceless treasure. The smallest things can have the biggest impact. As John Mashni continues his journey in writing and entrepreneurship, his experience with \\\"CinderToot\\\" serves as a constant reminder of the joy and inspiration that can be found in the pages of a children's book. His story is proof that every form of art has the potential to make a significant impact.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Serialised book (with a progressively updated ToC Links page) Book: Philosophy of Life Instinct: Prologue Prologue Image by the author It all kept coming back to our being a form of matter called Life that has a need to know combined with serious limitations in its ability to understand and explain reality. The origin of the quest for knowledge and the limitations arose from a single fundamental reality, as far as I could see. It is a reality that appears simple but is not. I have regularly questioned my view of this in forty years of thinking, waiting for it to change, to prove false. But it hasn’t. This reality is what I call — The Life Instinct. I will elaborate on the dimensions of The Life Instinct in Chapter 4. In subsequent chapters, I will consider essential features of life from the perspective of The Life Instinct. We will try to understand each feature’s origins, the implications, and how to work with it. In my journey through the many puzzles, I have found they belong to one of two categories: metaphysical or philosophical enquiries. We will apply the Philosophy of Life Instinct to shine a light on both types — Metaphysical ones and those from the Humanities. Book Structure Part I of the book delves into reality, time and our ability to comprehend them, in the context of The Life Instinct. The metaphysical questions and their answers are not practically critical, but as they are the foundations of the philosophy, they need to be dealt with — and I begin with them. In Part II, I take up the philosophical enquiries that broadly correspond to our world’s sphere and our existence within it, along with other forms of life. It is an anthropocentric area of enquiry and could be termed the Humanities, being questions that humans ask about themselves, from their viewpoint in the cosmos. I have asked the vital questions that concern us and tried to answer them as best I can in the light of the Life Instinct. In Part III, I consider: How will the answers stand the test of time? Will they make sense in a hundred, a thousand or a million years from now? Will the questions themselves change by then? What are the finalities on how we should live our lives? What is the purest form of this philosophy that can act as our daily guide to living the most wholesome lives we can? How to Use this Book Despite the above broad structure, as long as you have read Chapter 4 — ‘The Nature of Life and The Life Instinct’, all the other chapters can be read independently. I have written them to make sense on their own. They are beads on the single string of the philosophy that holds them all up. Chapter 4 sets up that underlying, unifying theme. The book may sometimes feel like a hairball of strands of thought, doubling back, intersecting and running parallel for short and long distances. But go with it, because once you get the underlying philosophy and viewpoint under your skin, it will be your ever-present and reliable friend that jumps out to tackle any problem, esoteric or banal.\", \"sampled\": \"In the realm of data visualization, the term 'dashboard' has sparked a debate about its relevance and value. David Pires has noted that the term is being questioned for its value. This debate emerged during a discussion hosted by the Data Visualization Society, where several experts shared their perspectives on the term 'dashboard'. Stephen Few, a pioneer in data visualization defines a dashboard as \\\"a visual display of the most important information needed to achieve one or more objectives.\\\" His definition is widely accepted in the field but some practitioners argue that the term is overused and misused. They contend that 'dashboard' has become a catch-all term that doesn't accurately describe the breadth and depth of data visualization work. Tableau offers a slightly different definition of a dashboard. According to Tableau, a dashboard is \\\"a collection of charts or visualization objects.\\\" This definition broadens the scope of what a dashboard can encompass but it also contributes to the confusion around the term. The variety in definitions has led to the misuse of the term 'dashboard', furthering the confusion in the field of data visualization. Some data visualization practitioners have suggested alternative terms for 'dashboard'. Ryan Davis, a discussion participant said, \\\"I normally refer to my d3 dashboards as ‘interactive dashboards,’ which I think helps to distinguish them somewhat from a generic reporting screen.\\\" Other proposed terms include 'vizapp', 'analytical application', and 'monitoring tool'. These alternatives aim to provide more specificity and clarity in the field of data visualization. However not everyone is ready to abandon the term 'dashboard'. Nicole Edmonds, another discussion participant, stated, \\\"I don’t think ‘dashboard’ should be a catch-all term for the broad spectrum of visualization work that occurs, but I think contextually it still has its place for now.\\\" This perspective suggests that the term 'dashboard' still holds value for some practitioners, especially when used in the right context. Elijah Meeks shared his experience with the term at Stanford and Netflix. He said, \\\"At both Stanford and Netflix I actively pushed against ‘dashboards’ because I felt like it was a limiting term that didn’t catch the imagination of stakeholders.\\\" Meeks' statement indicates that the term 'dashboard' may limit the potential of data visualization in the eyes of stakeholders. Despite the debate, the term 'dashboard' continues to be understood and related to by many stakeholders. This understanding can be attributed to the term's widespread use in the field and its clear association with data visualization. While the term may be contested, it remains a significant part of the data visualization lexicon. The debate over the term 'dashboard' highlights the evolving nature of the data visualization field. As this field continues to grow and change the terminology used to describe its practices and products will likely continue to be scrutinized and refined. This discussion is indicative of the ongoing effort to improve clarity and precision in the field of data visualization. In conclusion, the term 'dashboard' in data visualization has both its proponents and detractors.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Never Give Up Without A Fight The day I stepped away from writing (this would be almost 7 years ago) it was with the deliberate intention of never putting another word on paper. Photo by Jeremy Perkins on Unsplash I was fed up with the stops and starts, the mixed advice, the ‘almost were’ projects, the half-finished manuscripts and my own sense of ennui. It felt like there was never enough time to write. And the worst part was — I convinced myself that writing was a frivolous waste of the time. Somehow I got the idea I should spend every waking moment building servers or learning Cisco networking or studying for certification tests or, well, anything other than writing. Doing those tasks was more ‘real’ than writing could ever be… It was a difficult and fairly negative time for me. I’ve read enough from other writers to know that many of us have gone through even worse while building their careers. I mean, let’s be honest, there are more reasons not to write than there are reasons to write. Most ‘reasons’ are excuses. Some are legit. A legitimate reason would be losing a job or a true family crisis. My experience tells me anything else is doubt masquerading as purpose. I learned the hard way that if you’re meant to write the stress of avoiding or ignoring it will eat you alive. How did I get over myself? For years I ignored my feelings. I told myself all kinds of stories about how it didn’t matter if I wrote or not. I reasoned that no one was waiting for anything I’d produce anyway. The vast emptiness that sometimes overwhelmed me HAD to have some other source, right? It sure as hell couldn’t be happening because I wasn’t putting words on paper. That was too silly to consider. I believe I’ve mentioned before that depression is an issue for me. I assumed the emptiness was just another bout with my old enemy. It took a while to comprehend that not writing removed one of my coping mechanisms. The printed word (reading and writing) helped me fight back. In a way, at least for me, writing is medicine. Every time I forget that I suffer. Since I’m stubborn, realizing the issue and then doing something about it was a real battle. The person I’d asked to be my mentor bailed on me. No one offered encouragement or even noticed. That meant it was my problem to solve. I was forced to take a hard look at why I loved writing so much. I also had to overcome the internal argument that writing was a stupid waste of time. Pro tip #1 — You can only ignore your feelings for so long. Eventually your body will get fed up with the lies and do something nasty to you to get your attention. I won’t go into what happened, but trust and believe my body’s response got my attention in a major way. It was time to get my shit together.\", \"sampled\": \"The novel coronavirus, known as COVID-19 poses a wide range of health risks that extend far beyond the respiratory system. Upon entering the lungs, the virus uses the host's biological machinery to replicate the potential health impacts. One of the primary entry points for the virus in the human body is the ACE2 receptor. This receptor is expressed in many regions of the body, making them vulnerable to the invasion of the virus. Widespread vulnerability to the virus is one of the factors that makes COVID-19 particularly dangerous. The ACE2 receptor plays a crucial role in the body's function, regulating critical processes such as blood pressure, and inflammation. When the virus targets this receptor, it can disrupt these processes and cause a range of health problems. Among the many health risks associated with COVID-19 is an increased likelihood of stroke. The virus can cause abnormal clotting in blood vessels which increases the risk of stroke. In fact, 41% of patients suffering from neurological symptoms after COVID-19 infection have suffered from strokes. COVID-19 also has significant implications for mental health. The virus can contribute to psychiatric symptoms and disorders. A significant rate of depression, 29.2%, has been observed in those with COVID-19. Frontline workers are particularly vulnerable to these mental health impacts. The stress and trauma of treating COVID-19 patients can lead to burnout, highlighting the need for extra care and support for these workers. These mental health impacts are not limited to frontline workers. Many older adults living in adult-care communities during shelter-in-place orders experience loneliness and depression. This highlights the broader mental health crisis that has emerged alongside the pandemic. In addition to these immediate health risks, COVID-19 can have lasting impacts on health. Individuals infected with COVID-19, known as \\\"long-haulers,\\\" continue to suffer symptoms many months after their initial infection. The average age of these long-haulers is 44. The long-term health impacts of COVID-19 are still being studied. In one study, three COVID-19 patients showed signs of an ischemic stroke. This type of stroke occurs when blood flow to the brain is blocked often due to a clot. Eight patients showed other types of inflammation. This further illustrates the diverse range of health impacts that COVID-19 can have. In another study involving more than 2000 individuals in Denmark, 11 patients presented with a lack of blood flow to the frontal areas of the brain. This type of brain damage can lead to cognitive and behavioral changes. The ongoing research into the impacts of COVID-19 underscores the seriousness of this pandemic. As we continue to learn more about this virus, it's clear that its effects extend far beyond the respiratory system. In conclusion, COVID-19 is a complex disease with wide-ranging impacts on physical and mental health. The ongoing research into this disease continues to reveal new insights into its effects on the human body. As we continue to confront this pandemic, it's critical to remain aware of these potential health risks and to take measures to protect ourselves and our communities.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Exploring the question of whether submarines can swim An Argument for Verified Humans (Part I) As I read, I often get lost in the text, my thoughts diverging from the author’s words toward something related yet different. This process seems almost like a conversation between the author and me, with a person’s words provoking my response. At times, I enjoy this aspect of reading, but more often, it is quite distracting. Still, if I find the thoughts especially compelling (and if a pen is within reach), then I write them down in a place where they can be occasionally reviewed until I find them either ruinously obvious or oblivious to accepted fictions; less often, I build and branch until the original note takes on enough of its own life to become an article, a project, or a poem. Such was the case when I read the following paragraphs from Deep Learning with PyTorch¹ about a text response produced by OpenAI’s GPT-2 model (which has since been bested by GPT-3): That’s remarkably coherent for a machine, even if there isn’t a well-defined thesis behind the rambling. Even more impressively, the ability to perform these formerly human-only tasks is acquired through examples, rather than encoded by a human as a set of handcrafted rules. In a way, we’re learning that intelligence is a notion we often conflate with self-awareness, and self-awareness is definitely not required to successfully carry out these kinds of tasks. In the end, the question of computer intelligence might not even be important. Edsger W. Dijkstra found that the question of whether machines could think was “about as relevant as the question of whether Submarines Can Swim.” These few sentences have inspired in me a handful of tangents that I want to follow here to support propositions for (1) human verification on social media platforms and (2) transparency regarding the use of artificial intelligence in communication tasks. To do so, I will go over each bolded bit from above one by one, and to keep things interesting, I will not do so in order. Rather, can submarines think? I admit that I do not know the original context of Edsger Dijkstra’s quote (having only seen it applied in myriad secondhand contexts), and thus my understanding of its intentions may be limited. However, it seems as if the example aims to highlight the innate irrationality of any questions into machine thinking by drawing a comparison to the question of submarine swimming, which somehow seems more obviously unquestionable. I have two issues with Dijkstra’s quote: (1) it is entirely correct, as there is no true difference between a machine thinking and a submarine swimming; and (2) it stealthily cows the interpreter of the quote — perhaps unintentionally — into accepting the premise that submarines cannot swim, which, as I will argue, is not necessarily true. …\", \"sampled\": \"Profit-sharing programs have grown in popularity in recent years, offering a unique way to motivate employees and give back to the team. The concept is straightforward a portion of a company's profits is distributed among its employees providing an incentive for everyone to work towards the company's success. However, setting up a profit-sharing system can be a challenging task, particularly for small business owners. This is due to the complexity of implementing such a program as it requires careful planning legal compliance and a clear understanding of the business's financial situation. The use of profit-sharing systems is not a new concept. Big companies like Starbucks and Amazon have used stock as a form of profit-sharing, allowing employees to benefit from the company's success. This approach not only incentivizes employees to contribute to the company's growth but also helps to align their interests with those of the company. Sears, a well-known retail company, had a unique approach to profit-sharing. The company used to invest 10 percent of its earnings in the employee retirement program. This not only provided employees with a secure retirement plan but also encouraged them to contribute to the company's profitability. Profit-sharing can be seen as a complement to equal pay, diversity, and inclusion efforts. It is a way for companies to show their commitment to their employees acknowledging their contributions and ensuring that they share in the company's success. This principle is clearly demonstrated by Rob Howard, an entrepreneur with a team of 10 in his company. Rob's profit-sharing program provides team members with a portion of every dollar the company earns directly tying their earnings to the company's success. Unlike many other companies, Rob's company uses cash for profit-sharing. This provides a direct tangible benefit to employees, allowing them to immediately reap the rewards of their hard work and dedication. In terms of distribution, Rob's company distributes profit-sharing payments every six months. This ensures that employees receive a regular, substantial benefit from the program, providing a consistent incentive for them to contribute to the company's success. It's important to note that Rob's profit-sharing system is not a system of performance bonuses. While performance bonuses are based on individual achievement profit-sharing is a collective reward based on the overall success of the company. This system encourages cooperation and discourages competition among team members. Instead of competing against each other for bonuses employees work together to improve the company's profitability knowing that they will all share in the rewards. A bonus pool of $15,000 was shared among the team members in Rob's company. This substantial sum not only provided a significant incentive for the team members but also demonstrated the potential benefits of the profit-sharing system. In conclusion, profit-sharing is an effective way to motivate employees and ensure that they share in the company's success. Whether it's through cash or stock regular payments or a substantial initial bonus, profit-sharing can be a powerful tool for businesses of all sizes. Whether you're a small business owner in Silicon Valley or a large corporation like Starbucks, implementing a profit-sharing program could be a game-changer for your business.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Trump Administration Admits They Can’t Control the Pandemic The president needs a vaccine to bail out his incompetence. America, your long national nightmare isn’t quite over. Not yet. White House Chief of Staff Mark Meadows’s admission that the Trump administration is powerless to control the pandemic and merely waiting for a vaccine is nothing short of unconditional surrender. Welcome to Trump’s America where walls are never built and problems are kicked down the road until someone else solves them. Imagine if John F. Kennedy had said, “We choose not to go to the Moon in this decade because it is hard.” Science is complicated and sciencing takes a lot of brainpower, whether it be designing a rocketship or curing a disease. Given that degree of difficulty, Trump decided to make America wait again. To be fair, he valiantly fought his way through his own Coronavirus contraction and has returned to throwing superspreader events with thousands of acolytes in attendance. MAGA hats are recommended but masks are optional. He and Mike Pence soldier on with their 2020 campaigning, despite Mike Pence’s chief of staff Marc Short, Pence’s close aide Zach Bauer, and three others testing positive for the virus. With Pence needing to stump for Trump in Minnesota and the Carolinas this week, he’s ignored CDC guidelines, leading Chief of Staff Mark Meadows to admit what many of us already suspected. We are not going to control the pandemic. We are going to control the fact that we get vaccines, therapeutics and other mitigation areas. The Trump approach is akin to the basketball adage ‘You can’t stop him. You can only hope to contain him.’ Except instead of contain, Trump’s team is running a let-him-score-at-will defensive formation, while awaiting a medical Hail Mary. I’m mixing my sports metaphors almost as fast as this administration mixes its messages. At last week’s presidential debate, Donald Trump claimed the US is ‘rounding the curve’ in its fight against COVID-19 and a vaccine is weeks away, or very, very soon, or by the end of the year, or by April 2021. Regardless, someday in the future, we will have a vaccine, but the important part is the US is rounding the curve. This is important because one day after he said that, the US recorded the ‘highest number of Coronavirus cases in one day since the pandemic began.’ Geometry was never my strong suit, but this curve we’re on looks suspiciously like a spike. I’m being harsh on Chief of Staff Meadows and the rest of the Trump administration. You can’t hope to control a pandemic with masks, social distancing, and a thoughtful sense of community spirit. That would be, what’s the word? No, not malarkey. That would be samfundssind! Yes, that’s it.\", \"sampled\": \"Lark Morrigan has been dealing with a heavy burden. This burden is the constant companion of suicidal thoughts. These intrusive thoughts began when Morrigan was just eight years old. Morrigan's struggle isn't just about these thoughts. It's also about societal expectations and self-perception. Morrigan feels unappreciated and worthless if not meeting societal standards. The standards set by society seem superficial but their impact on Morrigan is profound. The expectation to be the flawless, and charming \\\"it girl\\\" has been an unbearable pressure for Morrigan. This pressure has led to a struggle with self-perception. This struggle extends beyond societal expectations leading to a battle with personal deficiencies. Laziness, procrastination, self-loathing, and lack of motivation are some of the personal deficiencies Morrigan identifies with. These deficiencies aren't just minor issues; they are constant reminders of the struggle Morrigan experiences daily. Morrigan's struggle is not unique. Many people deal with similar issues. However, the intensity and persistence of these issues have led Morrigan to harbor suicidal thoughts from a very young age. Morrigan confessed, \\\"I wish I could die,\\\" a heart-wrenching revelation that puts the severity of the struggle into perspective. In addition to these struggles, Morrigan also deals with issues related to social skills. This further complicates the situation making it even harder for Morrigan to navigate through life. Lack of social skills can lead to feelings of isolation and loneliness, worsening the existing mental health conditions. Morrigan's confession sheds light on the complex layers of struggles faced by those dealing with suicidal thoughts. Morrigan's story is not just about the struggle. It's also about the unrelenting fight against these struggles. Despite feeling unappreciated and worthless, Morrigan continues to push through, highlighting the resilience often found in those battling mental health conditions. Morrigan's story also brings to light the issue of self-destructive perfectionism. The need to be perfect in every aspect of life can lead to an unhealthy obsession with perfectionism. This can further fuel feelings of worthlessness and unappreciation as one never feels good enough. The struggle with self-destructive perfectionism and people-pleasing tendencies is another layer in Morrigan's complex mental health journey. This struggle speaks volumes about the silent battles many people fight daily. In conclusion, Lark Morrigan's story serves as a stark reminder of the devastating impacts of societal expectations and personal struggles on mental health. It's a call for understanding and empathy towards those dealing with similar struggles. It's a testament to the resilience of the human spirit in the face of adversity.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Starting this month and going forward we plan to publish a monthly digest with the most interesting updates for the Pandora Boxchain project. Research and Development: The main focus of our research and development activities in September was on Prometheus consensus and a layer 1 network based on it, which will become the hosting layer for our high-load computing protocol at the level 2. We call this 1st layer of network “BOX”, and the second “PAN”; together they ensemble Pandora Boxchain. Overall directions of research of Prometheus consensus and BOX network development were related to: Formal verification of the parts of the consensus algorithm utilising special insertion modelling methodology, developed by Prof. Litichevsky and Prof. Hilbert; designing the improvements to the consensus algorithms; development of consensus prototypes in Python; improvements to the existing Rust implementation of the network node. The results of these activities were the following: Implemented prototype of Prometheus consensus in Python. We’ve started working on verification mechanisms which will protect the network from accepting faulty or malicious blocks and transactions. This includes wrongly signed, sent repeatedly, sent at a wrong time, in a wrong round, with wrong links to previous blocks or transactions. Started working on formal verification of gossips mechanism for Prometheus consensus. This mechanism allows validators to communicate and reach consensus on when a block was skipped either maliciously or by network problems. If one of the validators doesn’t see a block from the previous validator in a validation queue he sends negative gossip and other validators respond either with a block if they know about it or with negative gossip confirming block absence in DAG. Implemented so named “timestepper mechanism” that allows to perform tricky tests by manipulating simulation of time and seeing how nodes react to complex scenarios. Switched to Elliptic Curve cryptography in Prometheus consensus. It is much more space efficient than RSA and allows us to calculate close to real-world memory and performance overhead. Performed analysis of Dfinity, Casper, Tezos consensuses and made an investigations on BLS, Schnorr signatures. Created a new ordering and merging algorithms for Prometheus consensus. Worked with the gossip system: initialization of sending negative and positive state events, gossip transaction, checking of a full system of gossip systems work. Made mempool updates: improvements of the mempool functionality for working with gossip transactions, adding new transactions to the block, implementation of the penalty case, which arises if, within the framework of mempool, a negative and positive state from one author was discovered. In this case, the validator will immediately write out the penalty (even write out to himself) and immediately transit into the block. Created and tested tx_by_hash() storing — storage system for all transactions within the {tx_hash: tx} structure or {transaction hash: transaction}. This mechanism is based on DAG and includes methods for adding and searching transactions in this structure. Additionally to R&D activities in Prometheus and BOX Network we were also performing further development of our Proof of Computing Work protocol and existing PAN testnet: Developed a prototype of the electron.js application with built-in box proxy. Based on this prototype we will develop The Pandora Market desktop application with a description of the use cases. Studied Proof of Computing Work algorithm from the point of view of Markov chains. There are three types of nodes in Proof of Cognitive Work: worker (performs computations), validator (validates computations), arbiter (resolves conflicts through arbitration). Through the accumulation of reputation or by not following the protocol (i.e. Byzantine behaviour) the nodes in Pandora network migrate between these states. This makes Markov chain an interesting framework to study behaviour in Pandora’s ecosystem.\", \"sampled\": \"The world of science and sustainability is always brimming with innovative ideas. One such concept, presented at a recent Science Conference, is turning heads for its dual approach to combating global warming and optimising airport performance. The project involves using recycled water to grow commercial crops at airports, which in turn lowers the temperatures of runways. This unique idea was put to the test at Adelaide Airport, with trials showing promising results. Planting lucerne, a type of forage crop, around the runways led to a noticeable drop in air temperatures by more than 3C on warm days. This might not seem like a significant drop, but when it comes to airport operations even a few degrees can make a difference. Lower temperatures are not just good for the environment, they also improve the performance of departing airplanes. High temperatures can affect the lift produced by an aircraft's wings, meaning that cooler runways can lead to more efficient take-offs. This project presents a win-win situation providing environmental benefits while also enhancing airport operations. Adelaide Airport is considering extending the project from a trial plot of 4ha to 200ha of airport land. This significant increase in scale demonstrates the potential the project has shown and the confidence the airport has in its success. With an annual passenger count of 8 million, the impact of this project at Adelaide Airport alone could be substantial. The project developer highlighted additional benefits during the conference. \\\"We’re also investigating the ability to create carbon credits from growing the crops,\\\" he shared, \\\"demonstrating carbon sequestration whereby the plants absorb CO2 and use it as fuel for growth by locking it in the soil.\\\" This means the project could not only cool the environment but also contribute to reducing carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere. Ingleton also discussed the economic benefits of the project, saying, \\\"The beauty of it from an agricultural production perspective is that the business case stacks up so the cooling is actually free and I think that’s important because any benefit you get from the cooling is a bonus on top of the revenue on the agribusiness side of it.\\\" Therefore, not only offers environmental and operational benefits but also holds significant economic potential. The presentation was made at the Airports Council International’s Airport Exchange in Abu Dhabi. Attended by airport professionals from around the globe was an ideal platform to showcase this pioneering project. The innovative approach of Adelaide Airport and SA Water certainly made a mark at the event. Interest in the project is expected to extend beyond Australia, with numerous airports in hot regions likely to see the benefits. The Middle East could be a potential location for similar projects given its high temperatures and large busy airports. The project's potential for wide-reaching impact was acknowledged by the conference attendees. The ability to lower temperatures improve aircraft performance contribute to carbon sequestration, and provide economic benefits makes it a model for sustainable airport management that could be replicated worldwide. In a world increasingly focused on sustainability and the need to combat global warming, innovative projects like this are a beacon of hope. They demonstrate that with creativity and collaboration, we can find solutions that benefit not just the environment, but also the economy and operational efficiency. As we continue to face the challenges of climate change, it's clear that initiatives like the Adelaide Airport project will play a crucial role. By turning challenges into opportunities, we can work towards a more sustainable and efficient future for all.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I. Personal History Calendar In 2011, I started a project I called the Personal History Calendar. I designed a calendar and listed events over the year aiming for 365 days of memorable personal events– firsts, and milestones, and memorable nights. The intention was to create a calendar with an anniversary of personal history for every day. Eventually being able to have a full year full of memories, knowing that in today’s history I tried Kangaroo meat for the first time (2009), and tomorrow is the anniversary of my first flight (1985). Sure, TimeHop and Facebook will tell you that today is the anniversary of the day you became friends with that guy you sat next to in Earth Sciences in college. But the Personal History Calendar is more intimate and revealing than that. Before documentation. Things that shouldn’t be public. I stopped at around 190 events; just over half mostly because I just could not wring out enough personal details to fill the full year. Perhaps I’ll continue to add to it. Over the course of my life, will I be able to create moments and memories for every day? Or will September 27 always be a day where nothing great happens, while September 28 has 6 different memories? It feels as though some days attract action, while some just get skipped. Our days have an energy. Our weeks pulse alternating stress and relaxation. Our years have periods of intensity and then calm. Chapters. Decades. The further out we zoom from this current moment, we can see that our entire lives have a rhythm. Ups and downs, tension and release. This month I’m exploring the idea of rhythms and timing in our lives. Let’s dance. “Sultanahmet” digital photograph by Aydin Buyuktis II. When in Rhyme… This past Tuesday, I woke up a little earlier to get a nice little jog in before the weather got too cold. As I started, I felt great. The music was driving me and the air was perfect. I was on fire. 10 minutes quickly turned into 20 and when I usually start to feel pain in my knees I was picking up the pace. My legs kept pushing, my arms kept pumping and my breathing was consistent. The rhythm of my music was in perfect sync to my run. My steps hit every beat. The lights turned green as I approached and the world seemed to be cheering me on. It’s an amazing feeling when the world feels aligned to our direction, giving us signs that you’re doing things right and blowing wind into our sails. It’s called synchronicity. It’s the experience of hearing a song that reminds you of a friend that you’ve lost touch with, only to get an email from her the same day. It’s needing to make a difficult decision, saying out loud “what should I do?” and at that moment, a bus drives by with an advertisement whose headline gives you your answer.\", \"sampled\": \"In the arena of marketing and advertising, persuasion plays a critical role. Daniel Hopper underscores the importance of four factors that facilitate this persuasion process. These factors are instrumental in swaying a person's attitude, a primary goal marketers strive for. As expressed by O’Keefe, \\\"In its most basic form, persuasion involves changing a person’s mental state usually as precursors to behavioural change.\\\" Marketers employ various tactics to achieve this desired change in attitude. Five key tactics that Hopper discusses are creating uncertainty, reducing resistance, changing attitude, amplifying attitude, and gaining behaviour. Each of these tactics is designed to tip the scales in favour of the marketer, subtly shifting the individual's perspective to align more closely with the product or service being promoted. However, it's not just about persuasion; there's also a defense mechanism at play. According to Hopper, this is known as \\\"persuasion knowledge.\\\" It's a cognitive tool that consumers use to interpret and cope with marketers' sales presentations and advertising, as noted by Friestad & Wright, \\\"One of a consumer’s primary tasks is to interpret and cope with marketers’ sales presentations and advertising.\\\" Interestingly, persuasion knowledge is not static. It evolves and develops throughout a person's life span. As we grow older and gain more exposure to various marketing techniques, our ability to discern and evaluate these tactics becomes more refined. This ongoing development of persuasion knowledge is a testament to our adaptive capabilities in the face of constant marketing onslaught. One crucial aspect that Hopper highlights is the role of the sales agent. The perceptions about the agent can significantly impact their ability to persuade. Negative perceptions can lessen the agent's persuasiveness. This underscores the importance of building a credible and trustworthy image in the eyes of consumers. The image accompanying Hopper's blog post further illustrates the essence of these persuasion tactics. It provides a visual representation of the dynamic relationship between the marketer and the consumer adding another layer to our understanding of the persuasion process in marketing. The insights shared by Hopper are not just applicable to professionals in the marketing and advertising industry. They are also valuable for consumers, helping them navigate the world of marketing with a more informed perspective. Understanding the tactics used by marketers can empower consumers, enabling them to make decisions that are more aligned with their needs and interests. In conclusion, the art of persuasion is a central pillar in marketing and advertising. Its role in shaping a person's attitude towards a product or service cannot be overstated. As consumers, we also possess the capacity to understand and navigate these persuasive tactics thanks to our developing persuasion knowledge. This interplay between persuasion and persuasion knowledge is a fascinating aspect of the marketing world. It's a dance between marketers trying to influence consumers and consumers interpreting and responding to these attempts. The insights shared by Daniel Hopper in his blog post provide a valuable window into this intricate dance.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Solution Process Included in the data sent by the POST request was the query of the vote URL. A typical URL looks like this: By having the user click on a link with query parameters containing the server and channel id, I could have that data readily available when I received a vote event. My implementation looked similar to the following: With that out of the way, I was ready to host my bot on Heroku. I cleaned up my code and merged my development branch with my primary branch, which was then picked up by Heroku’s automatic deployment. I changed the webserver endpoint on Discord Bot List (DBL) to point to where my Heroku app was hosted, instead of pointing to my local machine. I waited for my bot to start up and then sent a test POST request from DBL. Immediately, I started getting an error telling me that there were no web processes running. at=error code=H14 desc=\\\"No web processes running\\\" method=POST path=\\\"/dblwebhook\\\" host=_.herokuapp.com request_id=7a54e44c-5c2a-40fe-8bfc-097313c0c919 fwd=\\\"_\\\" dyno= connect= service= status=503 bytes= protocol=https After researching the error, it seemed that my Procfile was configured wrong. The Procfile tells Heroku what type of process to run and what file to run the process on. I had my process type set as worker, which upon further research would not work for a web server. From Heroku: “A Heroku app’s web process type is special: it’s the only process type that can receive external HTTP traffic from Heroku’s routers. If your app includes a web server, you should declare it as your app’s web process.” Clearly, I had just configured my process type incorrectly and all that was needed was a quick change from worker to web. And so that was exactly what I did. # worker: python bot.py web: python bot.py I pushed my code to GitHub and tested my DBL vote event once again by sending a POST request to the endpoint of my Heroku app, and it worked! My bot had received the webhook and reacted accordingly. However, I wouldn’t be writing an article about a simple solution I achieved on my second attempt if there were no caveats that came with it. And unfortunately, my change from worker to web was just that. The reason a worker process is recommended for a Python bot is that it’s constantly active. This means that the worker is always listening for a command and is able to respond to it immediately. On the other hand, a web process falls asleep after idling for some time. As noted in the Heroku documentation, “If an app has a free web dyno, and that dyno receives no web traffic in a 30-minute period, it will sleep.” For obvious reasons, this wouldn’t work for a Discord bot, which receives traffic via an API and not through the web.\", \"sampled\": \"Kashara Johnson, a 26-year-old writer, recently shared her journey toward embracing discomfort. She admitted that she had been avoiding difficult conversations out of fear. This fear, she realized, was holding her back and keeping her from truly experiencing life. Her avoidance of discomfort had consequences. She discovered that it had led to missed opportunities. The absence of difficult conversations, she realized, was creating a sense of stagnation, preventing her from growing and evolving. Kashara started facing her fears just six months prior to writing her blog. This decision marked a turning point in her life, as she moved from running away from fear to confronting it. She began to see the value in having difficult conversations, understanding that they were integral to her personal growth. Kashara believes that difficult conversations are necessary for progress. \\\"The questions that we don’t ask because of fear are framing the very stories that we create in the absence of truth,\\\" she said. This insight reflects her realization that fear was shaping her story, and that it was time to take control of it. The year 2019 was pivotal for Kashara. She recalls how her personal life during that year underscored the need for difficult conversations. It was a period of self-realization, where she began to understand the significance of honest communication in personal relationships. Kashara's transformation was significant. As she started facing the truth, she began to thrive. \\\"I started to realize that facing the truth wasn’t as scary or painful as I’d built it up to be in my mind. I actually started to crave more of the truth,\\\" she shared. Her dread of difficult conversations was slowly replaced by a desire for truth and authenticity. Trust, according to Kashara, plays an important role in having difficult conversations. This means trusting oneself to handle the conversation, trusting the other person to respond respectfully, and trusting that the process can lead to a positive outcome. This sense of trust has guided her in facing difficult conversations. Looking back, Kashara acknowledges that her journey of self-improvement was not a smooth one. There were hurdles and setbacks, but every step she took brought her closer to her goal of living a life of authenticity. Every difficult conversation was a stepping stone toward personal growth. As Kashara continues her journey, she hopes to inspire others to face their fears. She believes that embracing difficult conversations can lead to personal growth and self-awareness. She hopes that her story will encourage others to confront their fears and engage in honest communication. Kashara's journey is a testament to the power of facing one's fears. Her story is a reminder that difficult conversations, while challenging, can support personal growth. By embracing the truth, we can open ourselves to new possibilities and enrich our lives in unexpected ways.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Artist impression of a Jupyter notebook running C# ;) Jupyter notebooks have become the standard tool for hosting advanced machine learning code online. A Jupyter notebooks is a fully interactive document that allows mixing of content, live source code, and program output, all in a single document. Notebooks are living dynamic documents and their embedded source code can be interactively run and modified. This makes notebooks ideal for AI research or for teaching complex machine learning skills to IT students. But unfortunately they only support Python and R code. There isn’t any support for C#. Until today that is! Thanks to Microsoft’s tireless efforts we now have a fully functional C# kernel that allows us to run C# machine learning code directly in a Jupyter notebook. And getting everything set up is a piece of cake. Here’s a quick guide on how you can run your own notebook server locally with full C# support. Let’s start by installing Python. Yes, we do need python because the Jupyter server itself is a python application. A great Python distribution for AI that comes bundled with Jupyter is called Anaconda. Here’s how to install it on Windows: $ choco install anaconda3 I’m using Chocolatey, a nice package management system for Windows that runs on Powershell and makes installing new software a breeze. Next you’ll need the NET Core SDK version 3. You can download it here. You’ll also need dotnet try , an interactive version of the NET runtime environment that can run code interactively and which forms the core of the Jupyter C# kernel. Type the following on the command line: dotnet tool install -g dotnet-try This will install dotnet try as a global tool.\", \"sampled\": \"Save Journalism argues that news publishers face an existential threat, tied to shrinking newsrooms, lost jobs, and communities left without local reporting. The issue brief frames the problem as a crisis for newspapers and journalism, with Google, Facebook, and other big tech companies connected to the pressure on digital advertising. Newspaper newsrooms decreased in size by 45% over the last 10 years, a contraction that shows how deeply the industry’s reporting capacity has been reduced. In 2019 so far, the media industry lost more than 2,400 jobs, extending the pattern of cuts across national outlets and local newspapers. The brief cites cuts at HuffPost, Vice, and BuzzFeed as examples of major media organizations reducing staff. It also highlights layoffs at Cleveland’s Plain Dealer and Gannett newspapers, pointing to pressure on local journalism as well as digital media brands. Local news coverage has been hit especially hard, with more than 1,300 communities losing all local news coverage since 2003. Nearly 20% of metro and community newspapers have closed, leaving many places with fewer sources of regular reporting on civic and community life. The brief also says two-thirds of U.S. counties lack a daily newspaper, while 5% of U.S. counties have no newspaper coverage. Those figures prove that the decline is not limited to one kind of outlet, but reaches across counties, communities, and the newspaper system. Save Journalism attributes the crisis partly to big tech and digital advertising, arguing that the digital media transition reshaped the economics of news. Newspaper advertising revenue has fallen by two thirds over a decade, while Google and Facebook control more than 60% of digital advertising revenue, and Apple is also mentioned as a big tech company monetizing news.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"M ay 2020 marks the 3rd Anniversary of Cornell’s death. This was, is and will always be my love-letter to his family and musicians/music lover’s everywhere. My wheels crawled along the asphalt and I breathed in the afternoon sky. Brushstrokes of cotton candy melting with fireside abstracts served my daily commute home from work well. How could I possibly mind rush hour when my drive literally reminds me to appreciate the view? Time pushed as traffic crept along the California coastline and so did I. My thoughts swirled around nothing and everything as my eyes took deliberate turns between sky and road. The volume on my radio was low enough to dim the car commercials but still present enough to tap my ear when I landed on a tune I liked. Enter Chris Cornell. And just like that. My sunset had a soundtrack. As soon as I heard those pipes the fact that I couldn’t peg which song he was singing (a rarity) didn’t matter. His voice is undeniable; uncompromised passion with a bellowing tone that weaved through the speakers straight into your blood. I was only a few seconds in when it hit me — I had no idea what song this was. As a retired party girl who made her living on the stripper stage in the 90s, I take great pride in being ‘in the know’ with musicians from back in my day. I worked the clubs in Waikiki from Milli Vanilli and Terence Trent D’arby to Mötley Crüe and Fatboy Slim. Now decades later I can still tell you, with each song I hear — where I was working, what beaded leather or fluorescent lace costume I wore and which drug dealer had the best coke. Knowing his voice but not recognizing which song was more than annoying — it was a treat. I turned up the volume and without warning his lyrics pulled me inside a part of myself I was not expecting to revisit on a random afternoon drive home from the office. “And I sat in regret Of all the things I’ve done For all that I’ve blessed And all that I’ve wronged In dreams until my death I will wander on” I could blame the sudden tickle in my nose and watery eyes on PMS or low blood sugar, but the fact is — Chris Cornell was more than his voice — he was a rock and roll poet. My introduction to Audioslave and their new tune Like a Stone reignited something in me that’s difficult to describe. My pole dancing days far behind me, I wrestled with feelings of anxiety remembering who I was then compared to the woman I’ve become. There I was, driving home from my corporate job in an upper class, conservative town, and a song I fell in love with became a magic carpet ride to my past. With each note and lyric, I danced through a wormhole to a time when I was old enough to be on my own and make reckless choices, yet young enough to find my way out. It seemed Chris Cornell and I both found our way out. He too was a survivor of the party scene in the 90s.\", \"sampled\": \"Katy-Rose begins from an uneasy admission: her writing blog has not been consistent since Jan 2019. The pattern is not just about posting, because she also describes feeling that she had stopped being a writer in her own eyes. Weekly posts fell to 18 in 2019, then to 8 in the year of the article. For a blog tied to Writing, Self Improvement, Reinvention, National Novel Writing, and Identity, that drop becomes part of the subject itself. Katy-Rose looks at the space where regular writing used to be and sees a change in how she understands herself. The issue is not framed as a single missed deadline or a simple break from routine. It is a personal reflection on what happens when a writer’s public habit, private motivation, and sense of identity stop lining up. That shift felt serious. It touched the word writer. It made old projects feel uncertain. Katy-Rose reports having 16 novels, which keeps the reflection from becoming a story about having no work behind her. Of those novels, 13 are complete drafts, and 10 are considered viable for the future. This doubt is particularly striking given her large body of fiction, not in the absence of one. She says the last long-term creative projects driven by joy were in 2016, a detail that gives the reflection its deeper timeline. She began Skeletal in 2016 and says it has 10k written, marking one project that still carries the memory of that creative period. She also wrote the final book of her trilogy in November 2016, placing another important effort in the same stretch of time. After the trilogy work, she wrote and published two short stories in anthologies. When considering where to spend her time and attention, Katy-Rose asked herself, \\\"If I have a few hour to spend on A or B, which would I pick for now?\\\" NaNoWriMo sits inside this question of identity. Katy-Rose had planned not to do NaNoWriMo in 2020. That choice would have marked her 11th year. The decision carried more weight than scheduling. It pressed on what writing meant. Then motivation returned near November’s end. At the end of November 2020, Katy-Rose felt motivation return to work on stories, which changed the shape of the reflection from withdrawal into a cautious return, not by declaring everything solved, but by letting the desire to work on stories become real again. She had considered removing writer from her identity, yet realized that putting her novels away felt wrong. This realization acknowledges her inconsistency and reduced posting, without erasing the deep gap between past joy and current practice. It instead names the conflict more precisely: she could question the label, but the work still had a claim on her. The novels were not presented as finished business to be sealed off from her future. For December, her plan is to let herself write and read without rigid limits. Scrivener appears as the writing tool she opened, connecting the plan to an actual practice. The article timestamp is 2020-12-15 10:32:39.498000+00:00, and the broader record includes her stated 11-year total of 875,426 words of fiction. Photo credits named in the piece include Sincerely Media, Laurenz Kleinheider, and Halacious, while the reflection stays centered on Katy-Rose, the novels, and the uneasy return to writing.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The (un)surprising cause of the next financial crisis. You know that it is coming, you just don’t know how … The next financial crisis. Most predictions agree on the timing (2019 / 2020) and that it will be one of the deepest crises in the world history (the economy has been growing consistently for 10 years now), but no one can predict how it will happen. Knowing that, of course, would be priceless. Traders and hedge fund managers who took the ‘right side of the trade’ and bet against the 2007 subprime crisis made hundreds of billions over night. Jon Paulson and many others got famous and (even much) richer. Crises are certain; they are inevitable like death and taxes because the world economy is based on the concept of greed. Human greed is good — it is what drives the economy up … and then gets it going again after it falls over the cliff. It is a necessary substance; a serum and a poison in one. It was in the U.S. that greed got reinvented as a good thing. That is, it was the new world that injected poor immigrants with the idea of American dream and made money into a universal merit of success. And ever since it has been the U.S. that gave the world market bubbles. Last time it was the subprime mortgages and derivatives that no one understood; this time it is the tech sector. There are two sides to the tech sector. Facebook and Google are what Standard Oil Co. Inc. was at the beginning of the 20th century—a well tolerated monopoly. Together with Apple, they form the ‘old’ side of the tech sector — the one that actually makes money … and tons of it. But beyond that,there is the utopian tech sector. You know: Silicon Valley, its millennial founder-entrepreneurs, with zen-like smiles, enlightened modesty and open shirts. So very unlike the old world dominated by Wall Street, they and their tiny little start-ups inspired the beginning of the present economic recovery by promising to create an infinitely more efficient and cleaner way of doing things, exchanging factories and steel for smartphones and abstract products. Three companies — Amazon, UBER and Tesla (ATU) — are a perfect embodiment of this trend. To some extent, ATU are truly revolutionary — making our lifes cheaper (Amazon), more practical (UBER) and exciting (Tesla) — but for the most part, people love them because they are a sensation. Good enough is just not good enough when it comes to financial markets. People don’t want to be told that they should stick their savings into a 1.5%-yielding super-slow-growing annuity. No, we all want our chance at becoming millionaires, and if that requires us to chase a sensation, then so be it. This is not to say that UBER hasn’t changed the urban transportation for good, that Tesla doesn’t make innovative cars, or that Amazon doesn’t offer convenience and price that others can’t match. No. The problem is that somewhere down the line, ATU have become a religion of future and people forgot to value them on the cold basis of profits & losses. Somewhere down the line, ATU freed themselves from the dryness of discounted cash flow analyses, and they took the whole market with them on a trip to forever. Amazon — a nightmare of the old-industry CEOs — continues on its path toward the world domination because it can afford to reinvest everything it makes and more*. It is a company doomed to die. For now, the world is still large enough. Amazon can keep on growing, expanding into other sectors and devouring its competitors. That, of course, makes it into an irresistible investment proposition — a company on a path to become the ultimate monopoly; the one place where we buy everything. Inevitably, though, it will reach a point when it will grow too big. It will either have to be cut in pieces by the government labelled as a ‘monopoly’, or it will be re-priced by the market, increasing its cost of capital because the promise of further growth and domination will make no sense anymore (people will begin to ask for a profit eventually). *Amazon is a life work of an ex-hedge fund manager (who else better to trick investors’ minds) — a company which functions like a perpetuum mobile. Jeff Bezos has once drawn his Amazon flywheel concept on a napkin. Lower prices lead to more customers, which attract more outside sellers to Amazon, who should be OK with smaller profit in order to be able to sell on Amazon. Reinvestment of all profits improves company and reduces cost leading to lower prices again in a vicious endless circle. One arrow is missing — that arrow going out: ‘profits to shareholders’. UBER — a clever, but first and foremost a cheaper way to do urban transportation. A company that has burned through billions of private money to become the one and only player in the business that it created — intelligent transportation. But, for all its dominance, UBER is facing a ‘Groupon moment’. Its technology is becoming commoditized and re-used by cheaper alternative providers. Tesla — This one is easy. A tech company on a mission to beat auto industry on its home turf. Tesla burns cash at a breathless pace in a race against time to get its Model 3 production fully running before other giants enter the space with better alternatives.\", \"sampled\": \"Social media belongs at the center of modern media planning. Chad Anderson argues that it should be treated as a major media channel, not as a peripheral marketing activity. Gary Vaynerchuk’s line gives the argument its frame: \\\"Social Media is just a slang term for the current state of the internet.\\\" That view makes Facebook, Instagram, Facebook Messenger, WhatsApp, Snapchat, Pinterest, and YouTube part of the same attention shift. Anderson presents them as large consumer attention networks rather than side channels for light engagement. The point is not novelty but where people spend digital time. By 2017, Anderson says digital behavior had moved strongly toward mobile devices and social platforms. He uses that shift to challenge marketers who still separate social media from broader media strategy. The article treats mobile use, app use, and social behavior as connected facts for planning. The mobile figures make the case more concrete. The article cites comScore, eMarketer, and Nielsen to argue that marketing must account for mobile and app behavior. It says 66% of all digital time was spent on a mobile device. It also says 89% of all mobile device time was spent on apps. The app point narrows further into social and messaging. The article says 49% of mobile app time was spent on social media and messaging apps. Anderson uses those figures to support a practical claim about attention. If audiences are spending time inside apps, marketers have to understand what happens inside those apps. This is why the article treats social platforms as media environments, not as optional add ons. The discussion also looks at how marketers misread new platforms. Anderson says many marketers dismissed Facebook earlier. He says they were similarly dismissing Snapchat at the time. Snapchat had gone public in March 2017. The article mentions 150M daily Snapchat users. That scale is presented as evidence that dismissal can become a planning mistake. Anderson contrasts older media adoption speeds with rapid growth by Instagram and other digital platforms. Facebook receives special attention because it changed the economics of brand reach. Anderson argues that Facebook’s 2014 algorithm change forced brands to pay for reach they had previously received organically. That claim sits inside a broader warning about relying on platform access without understanding how platforms can change. The article’s point is not that every platform works the same way. Facebook, Instagram, and WhatsApp are treated as related pieces of a larger consumer attention system. Snapchat, Pinterest, and YouTube are also named as networks where audiences gather at scale. The author uses these examples to make social media feel less like a campaign tactic and more like media inventory shaped by behavior. For marketers, the issue is where attention already sits. Anderson’s argument is that social advertising costs will rise as major brands invest more heavily in social platforms. That prediction makes early learning valuable because the same audiences may become more expensive to reach later. The article also challenges a narrow reading of young users and Facebook. Anderson quotes an out of touch digital marketer saying, \\\"Kids aren’t on Facebook anymore.\\\" The article treats that view as misguided. It points to Facebook related messaging behavior instead. WhatsApp is projected to have 1.2 Billion users in 2017. Facebook Messenger is described as having 1B+ active users. Those figures support the author’s claim that peer to peer messaging apps are the next stage of digital communication for marketers to understand. Messaging matters because the article defines digital behavior through actual use, not through brand assumptions. A user may not behave on Facebook in the way a marketer expects. That does not mean the user is outside Facebook’s broader ecosystem. Facebook Messenger and WhatsApp show why the article keeps returning to mobile apps and social communication. The audience can shift from public feeds to messaging while still staying inside a digital attention network. Anderson’s point is that marketers need to follow that shift. The media budget argument is just as direct. The article claims digital ad spending would surpass TV advertising in 2017 for the first time. That claim reinforces the view that social platforms are not secondary to traditional channels. It also helps explain why Anderson expects social advertising costs to rise. When more major brands move into the same platforms, competition for target audiences increases. The article frames \\\"Mobile first\\\" as more than a design slogan. Anderson uses the phrase while arguing that mobile first thinking now includes consumer behavior on mobile and social platforms. The emphasis is on how people actually spend time, especially through apps, messaging, and social media. That focus pushes marketers away from treating mobile as just another screen. It also pushes them away from treating social as a small layer attached to campaigns. The closing advice moves from channels to planning discipline. Anderson advises marketers to emphasize brand value and customer retention instead of short term ROI and traditional annual planning cycles. That advice fits the earlier argument about rising costs and shifting attention. If social advertising becomes more expensive, brands need stronger reasons for customers to stay. If digital behavior changes quickly, annual planning can lag behind consumer movement. The article’s message is that marketers should understand the platforms, the apps, and the behavior before the media market makes that understanding costlier. It connects Facebook’s algorithm change, Snapchat’s scale, app time, and messaging growth into one operating lesson. Social media is presented as current internet behavior expressed through platforms.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Shoes and Shirt (and Pants) Required When what we wear affects how we write Photo by NordWood Themes on Unsplash “Put your pants on, and come with me!” In good fun, this is what my father-in-law said he’d say if he ever caught me in bed with his daughter before we got married. Turns out, it’s great advice for writers. In a hobby and profession that lends itself to comfy clothes and couches, sometimes the best advice is to put on an actual pair of pants. Today’s Covid world is getting a taste of the writer’s life. Daily showers, business wear, and shoes are a thing of the past, and in many instances, clothing is optional. All one has to do is turn off the alarm, roll out of bed, and log on. As a measure of professionalism, one’s physical presentation has been replaced with zoom conduct (you’re on mute, you’re still on mute), and if there is video interaction, it’s from the waist up. So while some are still required to be business on top, it’s all casual down below. Admittedly, this is one of the perks of being a writer. Sure, there’s the occasional face to face interview or the rendezvous with your skinny mocha and favorite lounge chair at the local coffee shop. But the majority of writing can be done at home, alone, in your pajamas. And until recently, writers held sway in this corner of the market where quality output came with a clothing optional clause. But at what point does clothing optional start to work against us? I wonder if the haziness we experience in our mind, a bout of writer’s block or lack of inspiration, comes from the idleness we portray on our bodies. Does what we wear and how we present ourselves, even when no one is around, have an effect on the quality of our work? The mind-body connection within the brain is complex. Think about what happens when a person you’re attracted to walks in the room. Your heartbeat ramps up and your breath quickens. Your focus narrows and whatever you were thinking is now in the past. All your mental energy is centered on that one person, and while the world around you may be a blur, you’re also fully present and attentive. This and more from just one look. So that begs the question, what are we telling ourselves as writers when we stay in our pajamas all day?\", \"sampled\": \"A study published in Nature Geoscience on June 17th proposed that meteors may help explain how clouds form in Mars’ middle atmosphere, a region that begins about 18 miles, or 30 kilometres, above the surface. The research focuses on a part of Martian weather that is not explained by the familiar cloud seeds associated with Earth’s lower clouds, and it connects those clouds to material arriving from space. Victoria Hartwick, the lead author and an ATOC graduate student in the Department of Atmospheric and Ocean Sciences at CU Boulder, described the broader meaning of studying clouds this way. “We’re used to thinking of Earth, Mars and other bodies as these really self-contained planets that determine their own climates. But climate isn’t independent of the surrounding solar system.” The researchers linked Martian middle-atmosphere clouds to meteoric smoke, icy dust produced when space debris enters the Martian atmosphere. Hartwick said that roughly two to three tons of space debris strike Mars, which provides a possible source of tiny particles that can help clouds begin to form. Hartwick explained the basic cloud problem by comparing Mars with the more familiar process on Earth, where lower clouds have seeds that allow condensation to occur. “Clouds don’t just form on their own. They need something that they can condense onto.” Brian Toon, an ATOC professor at CU Boulder, co-authored the research with Hartwick and Nicholas Heavens of Hampton University in Virginia. Their work used computer simulations to test whether meteors could supply the needed material for clouds in the Martian middle atmosphere. The simulations produced clouds when meteors were included in the model, connecting the appearance of clouds to the presence of meteoric smoke. Those simulated Martian clouds were described as thin, not the kind of large terrestrial thunderheads familiar from Earth’s weather. Even thin clouds mattered in the model because the simulations suggested that middle-atmosphere clouds could change high-altitude temperatures by up to 10⁰ C. The result tied small particles from space debris to a measurable atmospheric effect in the modeled Martian environment. The study’s implications also reached into questions about ancient Mars and the possibility that liquid water once existed at its surface. Toon said the findings may inform ideas about how ancient Mars could have been warmed enough for such conditions. “It is likely that this discovery will become a major part of that idea for warming Mars,” Toon said, describing the possible role of the findings in thinking about ancient Martian climate. The research places meteoric smoke, Martian clouds, and high-altitude temperature changes within the same explanation, while keeping the focus on how space debris may shape weather on Mars.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Discuss the need for adopting frameworks like Privacy By Design very early in your data management life cycle Image by Author Every byte of data has a story to tell. The question is whether the story is being narrated accurately and securely. Usually, we focus sharply on the trends around data with a goal of revenue acceleration but commonly forget about the vulnerabilities caused due to bad data management. Data possesses immense power, but immense power comes with increased responsibility. In today’s world collecting, analyzing and build prediction models is simply not enough. I keep reminding my students that we are in a generation where the requirements for data security have perhaps surpassed the need for data correctness. Hence the need for Privacy By Design is greater than ever. Before we discuss Privacy By Design lets understand some issues surrounding data security and privacy. Security — Role of Data Processors & Data Controllers A few years ago just building a Data Lake was a quantum leap for many organizations. For security conscious organizations security of the Data Lake was simply limited to enabling Kerberos for identity management. We rarely talked about security of data-at-rest or data-in-motion. Not any more…. In today’s world the role of data processors and data controllers has been redefined. Handling data securely whether it is at rest or in transit is no more a option. On top of that the organizations are legally bound to notify the involved parties as soon as any data breach has been detected. Speed of Data Until recently businesses have focused on looking at data over long stretches of time, made possible by Big Data. With the advent of Internet of Things (IoT) analyzing real-time data has gained immense importance. It is very common these days to have devices in our homes that collect personal data and transmit it to external locations for either monitoring or analytical purposes. In many cases the the poor consumer is finding it difficult to balance the benefits they get from surrendering their personal data against the risk involved with providing them. A right balance needs to be met. Privacy By Design We are in times where history is being made on a daily basis. While giants like Facebook, Google and others are looking at innovative ways to collect, handle, use and share data the legislators are drafting and enforcing new regulations around data privacy and ownership. Regulations like the European GDPR attempts to define the legal accountability of data controllers and data processors. The regulation puts greater focus on data governance polices around personal data portability, retention and destruction. What is Privacy by Design? Integrate privacy issues from the beginning and throughout you development cycle. The same approach should apply to services offered as well as internal processes. The theory behind this approach is that privacy cannot be solely enforced by legislation alone. Privacy by Design is based on 7 foundational principles, as developed by Ann Cavoukian and formalized in a joint report with Information and Privacy Commissioner of Ontario. More than ever IT companies are trying to help customers come up with the correct blend of data innovation and data governance in a security-conscious world. As the use of machine learning and artificial intelligence gains strength we need to help our customers adopt frameworks like Privacy by Design to make their future data roadmaps simpler, secure as well as cost-effective. I hope this article was helpful in spreading the news regarding the adoption of Privacy By Design. Topics like these are covered as part of the Big Data Hadoop, Spark & Kafka course offered by Datafence Cloud Academy. The course is taught online by myself on weekends.\", \"sampled\": \"Microservices and web services are often discussed together, yet Ayo Oladele frames them as different software architecture approaches with distinct roles in application design. Microservices are presented as an architectural style commonly organized around business capabilities. That framing makes the business function a central way to think about each service. Rather than treating a whole application as a single broad unit, microservices consist of smaller parts that can focus on narrower functionality. A microservice may operate independently from other services in the same software solution. It may also have its own database, which reinforces its separate place inside the architecture. The article also says a microservice can be deployed separately, so its lifecycle does not have to match the lifecycle of every other service. This independence is a key part of the comparison. That does not make microservices a universal answer, because the article keeps the decision tied to project requirements. Web services receive a different description. They are defined as services exchanged between applications using the World Wide Web. The article also says web services can act as an API representation through HTTP. This places web services in the space between applications. Their role is exchange. The comparison also turns on scale and scope. Web services are portrayed as larger because they often contain many business functions. They may be built as full subsystems, which makes them broader than the smaller internal parts associated with microservices. Microservices are narrower. That narrower role shapes how sharing is discussed. The article says web services support component sharing. Microservices, by comparison, limit sharing through bounded context, which keeps each service closer to its defined responsibility. The result is not a simple ranking, but a separation of purposes: web services are described through exchange and sharing, while microservices are described through business capability boundaries. Performance is another point in the comparison. The article states that systems based on web services can be slower because they may depend on several services for a business request. That claim is tied to the structure of web services rather than to a broad judgment about every system that uses them. When a business request relies on several services, the system can carry more dependency across that request. Microservices are presented in a different frame, as smaller internal parts with narrower functionality. The contrast stays architectural. The choice depends on requirements. Ayo Oladele’s article argues that project requirements should guide the choice between microservices and web services. That point keeps the comparison practical, because the article does not present either approach as automatically better for every software effort. The useful question is what the project needs from its architecture. If the work calls for exchanged services between applications, the web service description becomes relevant. If the work calls for bounded, independently operating parts, the microservices description becomes relevant. The article also leaves room for both approaches to appear inside the same software solution. That conclusion is important because microservices and web services are not treated as mutually exclusive labels. They have different uses, and the article says they may be combined in one software solution when the requirements support that structure. The piece by Ayo Oladele was published on 2020-11-19 10:32:39.154000+00:00 and is identified as Part 2, placing the comparison within a specific installment and timestamp rather than as an abstract note. The subject sits across software engineering, web development, mobile app development, web app development, and the broader discussion of microservices. iTwis is mentioned as a service provider in the article’s context, while the technical comparison centers on Microservices, Web services, the World Wide Web, and HTTP.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Lovers of The Sea has been in business for 8 months now. I’ve been working on it for a total of 11 months. I have learned so much in the process of building it and growing it and I want to shed some honest light on what it really means to be a solo entrepreneur in your late twenties building a completely bootstrapped business. Here are the five realities of the entrepreneurial journey: 1. Entrepreneurship is not Glamorous. No, it is not. It is a lonely grueling process of just keep going. We’ve been wired by the media to only see the result. You only hear about the entrepreneur who went from zero-to-seven-figures in her first year of business or the two-man startup team that just sold their business for $300,000,000. But when you dig deep into these stories, you realize the sheer number of years of failures, pivot points, and “figuring it out” 99.9% of startups must go through before “making it”. You uncover all the personal sacrifices, late nights, long weekends, self-doubts, criticism, financial insecurities, and troubles an entrepreneur endures throughout the process of building their business. We are bombarded with these glamorous headlines that we’ve become fine-tuned to only see — and think of — the result. But greatness is not in the result. Greatness is in the process. Reality #1: There will be so many tough days, so many zero-sale days, so many costs incurred without consistent revenue, so many glitches to fix, and much more uncertainty to maneuver. You will be crushed with self-doubt countless times, but you will carry on, and you will keep pushing because you’ll begin to understand that greatness is in the process, not the result. 2. Entrepreneurship will give you a true taste of what it means to sacrifice. Building on my previous point, get ready to say goodbye to your old life’s luxuries. You will not have the time (or money) to go out on dinner dates. Or to the movies. Or to festivals. Or to hang out with friends for drinks after work. Your mind will be consumed with so many problems, dilemmas, worries, and questions: How should we price this product? Why is our conversion rate so low? How do we grow our Instagram audience? How can we lower our shipping costs? How can we reach more people? What if we try this new approach to our email marketing campaign… This is all you’re going to think about, religiously for the first few months post-launch. There’s so much to learn and to figure out, that you will feel guilty socializing and spending money (which you don’t really have — especially if you don’t have a day job). For every dollar you spend on social activities, there’s one less dollar to spend on the business. For every hour you spend watching Netflix, that’s one less hour of reading on content marketing strategy. Reality #2: The sacrifice is necessary. It’s part of the equation. It was hard for me to accept this sudden change in lifestyle. At first, I thought it would only be temporary, and that soon things will flourish. But with time I’ve come to realize that this is the new reality of how things will be because it will take years of commitment to build a thriving sustainable business. And only when I came to accept this perspective that my thinking began to shift. I’ve now learned to see this sacrifice as an investment in myself, in my growth, in my journey to who I wish to become and where I want to take my life.\", \"sampled\": \"Lance Ulanoff frames Alexa’s conversational future by first making clear that an opening restaurant-ordering exchange is fictional. The point is not that Alexa can already manage that kind of shared ordering scene, but that Amazon is working toward a more natural way for people and AI to talk. Current Alexa, in the author’s view, cannot carry out the described restaurant interaction involving several people. However, the imagined exchange depends on a voice assistant understanding who is speaking, when to respond, and how to stay inside a flowing conversation. Amazon revealed Natural Turn Taking during the same week as a large fall product rollout. The feature is presented as a step toward more fluid human-AI conversation, rather than another ordinary command pattern built around isolated prompts and short replies. Alexa already has some conversational ability because it can answer follow-up questions after an initial request without requiring the wake word again. Even so, the author notes that Alexa can still respond to similar-sounding words, nearby speech, and commercials, which shows why ordinary listening is not the same as conversational judgment. Natural Turn Taking pushes Alexa beyond the familiar pattern of wake word, command, response, and silence. Amazon describes a developing system that uses acoustic, linguistic, and visual signals to decide when Alexa should listen and when Alexa should speak. The planned system does not simply let Alexa enter any room’s discussion on its own. A user must invite the assistant into the exchange by saying, \\\"Alexa, join my conversation,\\\" which marks the beginning of Alexa’s role in that shared space. After Alexa is invited, the wake word is not needed again until the conversation ends. That change is central to the idea of a three-way conversation, because repeated wake-word use would keep breaking the rhythm Amazon is trying to smooth out. The author connects this ambition to expectations shaped by Star Trek and the Enterprise AI “Computer.” In that fictional model, Captain Kirk could address the Computer with one initiating cue, then continue in a manner that felt more like conversation than repeated device activation. Google Duplex serves as a comparison point for voice assistants that can handle more complex exchanges. It is described as a specialized Google Assistant add-on for dinner and hair salon reservations, with reservation help described across 48 of 50 states. Apple and Siri sit in the same broader field of voice assistant comparison, but the focus remains on Amazon’s attempt to make Alexa more conversational. Natural Turn Taking is not described as a finished general intelligence, but as a developing feature built around the mechanics of when a system should talk. Amazon is also developing contextual barge-ins so Alexa can interpret interruptions during its responses, as real conversation often includes overlap, correction, and midstream changes rather than neat command lines separated by long pauses. The article also points to work on Alexa’s ability to learn from mistakes and adjust vocal tone based on context. In one example of contextual response, Alexa answers a complaint about loud music by saying, \\\"Okay, do you want me to lower the volume?\\\" Echo devices provide the familiar setting for these Alexa interactions, while Echo Shows are especially relevant when visual cues are part of the system. Indeed, Amazon says the developing feature combines acoustic, linguistic, and visual information when deciding whether Alexa should listen or speak. The same system also needs a clear ending, so the user must say, \\\"Alexa, leave my conversation,\\\" when the conversation is finished. Ulanoff’s analysis treats Natural Turn Taking as a notable step toward a more fluid Alexa, while still grounding it in what Amazon says the feature is being built to do.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The Best Asset Your Startup Can Invest In Right Now Where should you be putting your time and money to see 10x+ ROI? Photo by Jed Villejo As startup founders, we juggle with a lot of different ideas when it comes to determining which investment is best for the business. Is it a software? A hire? A strategy play? What kind of investment can you make now that will slowly compound into long-lasting benefits later on? If you’re a budding startup, the best investment your business can make right now isn’t a Chief Marketing Officer or an expensive digital advertising campaign. It isn’t a new storefront or a nicer website either. Your Startup’s Best Asset There’s an asset out there that will: Protect your business from major losses during economic downturns. Create a consistent, steady stream of income for your business. Compound in effectiveness and size over time. Help you save money in marketing and sales costs over the long-term. This asset doesn’t need to have a price tag on it and can be acquired in exchange for time. It isn’t something that requires fancy tools or an expert hire, just consistency and commitment. This asset is community. I’m not talking about superficial numbers in your email list or your Twitter followers, I’m talking about real community where you talk with your audience and they talk with each other. A community that has a shared purpose of helping womxn entrepreneurs overcome their biggest challenges, or shared values like only buying eco-conscious beauty products. Shared purpose and values help people find a go-to place, a home that they can continuously visit to activate their deepest desires. If you can create that home for your audience, you’ve figured out how to anchor people to your business. This anchoring creates a magnetism to your business that’s effortless. Through this magnetism, you’ll be able to naturally build a fan base that’ll buy whatever you release and you’ll benefit from a steady stream of income because you have a community that’s constantly talking about you. Community is kind of like having your own QVC show that’s streaming 24/7 — even though the hosts are talking about random things here and there, they’re still socializing in your home and always bringing the attention back to you and what you’ve got to sell. Over time, your community’s commitment to your brand will result in marketing and sales cost savings for your business (because you now no longer have to spend money on aggressive lead generation). You’ll start reaping the benefits of organic growth (aka $0 marketing) through referrals via your most loyal customers who have nothing but great things to say about you. Building A Community 101 Community is the best investment any small business owner can make in their business right now, and it’s easy to get started. Here are three starting steps to embark on your build. 1. Mission with an excitable response To start building a community for your business, start off with a shared purpose or value system, and make sure it’s potent.\", \"sampled\": \"Nina Quist published a positive review of Scripted on Medium on 2020-06-02 and presented the freelance writing marketplace as a strong option for writers interested in content marketing work. Her article frames Scripted as a service that connects businesses with vetted freelance writers, which makes the platform relevant to freelancers who want client projects without relying on low-paying marketplaces. The review draws a clear line between Scripted and the content mills Nina Quist used early in her freelance career, where low pay shaped her first experience with online writing work. More than ten years after that early content mill work, she describes Scripted in more favorable terms, especially because it is presented as selective and focused on quality. Scripted is described as a platform that does not simply open its doors to any writer who wants to join, since the article says it is selective in admitting writers. The review states that only 2% of Scripted applicants are accepted as writers, a figure that supports the author’s view of the marketplace as quality-focused rather than broadly accessible. The application process is presented as a meaningful barrier rather than a quick signup, because applying to write for Scripted involves a substantial test and a one-time application fee. Nina Quist treats that process as part of what separates Scripted from less selective writing platforms, while also making clear that the marketplace may not be ideal for every freelancer. Once accepted, writers on Scripted can view available client projects through the platform and decide which opportunities are worth pursuing. The review says writers can also review standardized briefs and send pitches, so the workflow begins with choosing projects and making a case to the client before any draft is submitted. The article describes the next stage as dependent on client acceptance, since a writer submits a draft only after a client accepts a pitch. That draft may then be approved, rejected, or sent back for revision, which means the process includes editorial response from the client before the work is fully complete. Payment is tied directly to approval, because the review states that payment occurs only after the client approves the article. That detail makes the approval step central to the Scripted workflow, since pitching, drafting, and revising all come before the point at which the writer is paid. Nina Quist says Scripted is better suited to experienced writers than beginners, as it aligns with the selective application process and pitch-based project system. The platform is not presented as an entry-level shortcut, but as a marketplace where writers with experience may be better prepared to meet client expectations. The review also reports that Nina Quist received multiple well-paid assignments within a few weeks of using Scripted. She notes that Scripted has a minimum per-word rate that prevents pitching below a floor, which supports her positive assessment of the platform’s pay structure for freelance writers. Her practical advice is to check the dashboard at least a few times per week, while also avoiding underpricing and sending multiple pitches. The review gives Scripted an 11/10 rating, and the article’s image credit names Burst and Pexels as the photo credit sources.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Getting your business up and running is a tall task. It means countless hours of your precious time and hundreds of sleepless nights invested in something unknown. Although it’s very fulfilling to work for yourself, this moon has its dark side — you’re left alone with the problems and have to go through the rough times by yourself. When an employee can weather the storm by simply changing the workplace, you have to find the mental force to cope with turmoil and put your business back on the rails. If you’ve clicked on this article, I assume you’re a grown-up who is well aware of how to deal with problems. Such advice as “analyze what went wrong and try again” is surely what you’ve tried and is probably not worth noting in the first place. But what can really be done if the project has hit a dead-end and there’s no feasible way to escape this trap? Here are some of the more or less obvious things you should try out to get your mojo back on track and drop the weight of the world from your shoulders: these won’t fix the bugs in your code but will give you a look at how to get an impulse for coping with such things. No mentors, do it yourself Well, the Number 1 rule is to avoid “mentors” whose services cost real money but have no real value. Trust me, you can get all these platitudes for free on the Internet: paying for the “get over it” phrases is not the way to prosperity. There are almost no real psychologists among “trainers,” but their paycheck implies they’re Oxford graduates. Ironically, the person who has probably inspired the most people, Shia LaBeouf, who made an arbitrary “Just do it” with a big portion of humor, can’t even fix his own life now. Each person is unique, so don’t expect someone to come up with a ready decision for you, seek the inspiration yourself! Physical activity It’s not about building muscles — getting up and moving proves to be a great way to alleviate stress. Physical exercise is the clear victor here: it’s a great way to get your blood going, which will stimulate the brain to work. This will also help your mental state: sports are a great way to fight depression. And I’m talking about casual stuff like walking, swimming, or ping-pong — something to keep you relaxed and engaged at the same time. Meditation and yoga are no longer exotic and require a minimum commitment unless you want to become a pro, which is not the case. While yoga is more about making you balanced and patient, meditation can help you focus on the things you need: figuring out the main issues is easy when nothing else distracts you. Music and ambience Although Chopin is arguably the best composer to listen to while working (according to the TV series “Dexter”), you can pick any of the music greats to become energized. If all you need to rock is to hear a motivational speech, then maybe choose… rock? It’s a less stressful way to hear the words you need, plus it will rhyme on top of having great instrumentals. The human brain is more susceptible to soaking up info when it is neatly packaged, so it’s okay to avoid monotonous speakers in favor of a lovely melody with a life-affirming message.\", \"sampled\": \"Data Storytelling: Appealing to Logic and Perspective presents data storytelling as a way to address both logic and perspective. Kristi Pelzel frames the topic around the idea that data can connect with logic, while stories help people connect to reason or viewpoint. The article is labeled Part 7 in a Data Visualization series and was authored by Kristi Pelzel. Its timestamp is 2020-12-10 20:24:47.775000+00:00, placing the discussion inside a broader set of writing on visualization and planning. Pelzel’s central argument is not that data and story compete for attention. The article treats them as different parts of the same communication problem, where facts need structure and perspective needs something memorable. The piece says a data story must first gain attention before it can be remembered, because a viewer may not hold onto the message of a visualization if the story does not quickly establish why it deserves focus. Shahram Heshmat Ph.D. is cited from Psychology Today on emotional intensity and attention. His quoted explanation is, “Emotional intensity acts to narrow the scope of attention so that a few objects are emphasized at the expense of many others.” Pelzel uses that attention problem to explain why a data story cannot be only a display of facts. A story built over 40-hours may still be forgotten in 4-seconds if it does not give the audience a reason to notice and remember it. The article’s basic story model is simple: beginning, middle, and end. Those three sections give a data story a structure that can move an audience from context into development and then toward a clarified message. The beginning carries the job of helping the audience understand relevance. Pelzel presents the questions “Why do I care?” and “What’s in it for me?” as audience concerns that the opening should address. The middle of the story is where plot points help connect the sections. The article describes plot points as links built through motivations, desires, or setbacks, rather than isolated data observations without narrative movement. Chicago is used as an example for this kind of story development. The example involves strong regional exports, a diverse job market, and slowed economic growth and gives the story material that can contain both strength and tension, as conflict supports memory and urgency. The article connects emotion to the audience’s ability to retain the point, not as decoration but as part of how the message lands. The ending of a data story should clarify what the message means. Pelzel says it should help define possible action or requested behavior, so the audience is not left with facts but no sense of response. The article also warns against presenting facts in a way that lacks clear meaning. A 30-page Excel file is used as an example of an unclear way to communicate, because information alone may not guide attention or action. Part 1 of the series which is titled Context and Pre-Data Visualization Planning, is referenced as an earlier installment. That reference keeps the discussion connected to planning, suggesting that the story depends on choices made before the visualization is presented. Tableau Public is mentioned as a publishing option, and the MakeoverMonday Challenge is also mentioned. These references place data storytelling near public practice, visualization sharing, and repeated work with data communication. The article concludes that facts alone may not motivate action. Its final point is that stories can organize facts toward meaning and action, giving data a form that supports logic, perspective, memory, and response.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Like a whole bunch of people (and probably you, if you’re reading this!) I have always wanted to be a paid author, even since I was six and read my first work of fiction, The Magic Treehouse. But there was one small hiccup in my great plan: I thought I was bad at writing, and I didn’t know how to get better. I was way too intimidated to try to write fiction, but I’d never been interested in writing non-fiction. So I quietly shelved my goals of seeing my name on a book someday and dreamed smaller, less ambitious dreams. Then I ran across the concept of copywriting. What is copywriting? Have you ever read the product description of a bidet? Did you think, like me, that these spontaneously came into existence? Guess what: someone actually has to sit down and write those product descriptions. Same with a big, big chunk of the text you come across online. Copywriting means someone provides you with a brief — maybe a blog post, an ad, a product description — and you write it for them. You try to make it engaging, descriptive, filled with keywords so it will rank well on Google. You try to fit it in with the brand’s style. It’s an exercise in writing to a description. It’s challenging, it’s fun, and it’s rewarding. Photo by Kaitlyn Baker on Unsplash Not only financially rewarding but to me, copywriting was a way to gain validation that I was good at writing. It was my first paid writing gig before I’d ever blogged or heard about Medium. By spending maybe ten minutes a week writing a little 200 to 500-word blurb about a product, service, or experience, I funded most my pub trips. The math goes like this: I got paid around 1.5 pennies per word, I could write about 100 words per minute, and I usually researched the topic for about five minutes beforehand. If you do the math, this works out to around $7.50 every ten minutes, or $45/hour. I was a Masters student at the time, so the ability to work at a flexible rate for that kind of pay was tremendous for me. It wasn’t that much but it meant I could treat myself a bit more often than I would otherwise. And it taught me how to write to a deadline, how to write about anything (including, but not limited to, descriptions of toilets) and how to write well and quickly. Was I qualified? No. I was doing a Masters in bird conservation, so I had no history of writing copy, nor did I have any qualifications for writing. All I had was some free time and a liking for words. Photo by rawpixel on Unsplash The most important thing though, more than the money, was the idea that someone would pay me to write. At that point, to me, that was the ultimate form of validation for my writing. And it was awesome. Did that matter? Even without qualifications, the process was very straightforward. I applied on a website called copify.com by writing a short, 200-word copy example about a vacation spot. A week later, I was approved to write on their site. I gained access to a list of around 50 briefs submitted by clients.\", \"sampled\": \"Ary Sharifian presents a technical tutorial centered on a CI/CD setup that links GitHub Actions, AWS Lambda Python functions, and API Gateway into a compact deployment workflow for API work, Python development, DevOps practice, data science usage, and cloud-based execution. The design is framed as a phase 1 AWS cloud adoption architecture, which means the described pattern stays focused on getting source control, automated deployment, serverless compute, and an endpoint trigger working together before moving into broader cloud arrangements. The workflow begins from a GitHub repository, where a developer can use git to pull changes from the repository and push updates back into source control as the function code and deployment files evolve. GitHub is the source control platform in the pipeline, while GitHub Actions is the CI/CD workflow automation tool that connects repository activity to deployment behavior without requiring the developer to manually update the AWS Lambda function after the configured merge event. A central part of the tutorial is the GitHub Actions YAML workflow, which is configured to update an AWS Lambda function after a pull request is merged into the master branch. The YAML file is not treated as a side detail, because the author highlights formatting the workflow correctly as one of the main setup challenges as well as formatting the main.py file correctly for the Lambda function. AWS Lambda provides the serverless compute service used to run the Python function, and API Gateway provides both the trigger and the endpoint for the function. In this setup, API Gateway executes the AWS Lambda Python function and returns a result, so the architecture connects the repository workflow to a deployed function that can be invoked through the API Gateway layer. The author presents AWS Lambda as a way for teams to offload local compute resources cost-effectively, which fits the tutorial’s focus on moving execution into AWS rather than relying on a developer machine. AWS Lambda Functions are also described as part of the AWS always free tier, making the service a practical entry point for a phase 1 adoption pattern that uses Python functions without introducing a larger compute footprint. Clean deployment relies heavily on the organization of Lambda deployment files before they are zipped for deployment. The author recommends placing the Lambda deployment files in a repository folder before zipping them, and the tutorial also notes line 57 in connection with zipping a GitHub repository as part of the YAML workflow discussion. The Lambda handler format is described as file_name.function_name, which ties the deployed AWS Lambda configuration directly to the Python file and the function name that AWS should call. This handler detail sits alongside the formatting concerns for main.py, because the function file, the function name, and the deployment configuration all have to line up for API Gateway to trigger the expected Lambda execution and return a result. The tutorial also calls out AWS Lambda constraints that shape whether this serverless pattern is the right fit and includes a 15 minutes timeout, a 250mb uncompressed package size limit, and a 50mb compressed package size limit. Ary Sharifian recommends AWS Lambda overall, while suggesting Oracle Cloud Compute Instances for greater compute flexibility. He highlights that less than 1 OCPU is a free threshold and notes that AWS compute instance users should generally assume 90% availability.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"blog\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Big Data is generally considered to be very huge amount of data for storing and processing. Data in huge volume and different varieties can be considered as Big Data. Data is changing our world and the way we live at an unprecedented rate. Big data is the new science of analyzing and predicting human and machine behavior by processing very huge amount of related data. Big data refers to speedy growth in the volume of structured, semi-structured and unstructured data. It is estimated to generate 50,000 GB data per second in year 2019. Today’s enterprises are generating massive amount of Data, Which essentially has 3 attributes : Volume : — The size of the data, we are talking about GB and TBs here Velocity : — The rate at which the data is being generated Variety :- Data from Multiple sources and multiple kinds This added complexity can not be handled with traditional frameworks hence Hadoop ( not the only solution ) Hadoop is a parallel processing programming framework which work in MapReduce. Apache Hadoop is one of the tool to work on Big Data. It is open source, software framework that runs on a commodity hardware. Why we are talking about Hadoop? We are talking because, On a hadoop framework we can store any type of data weather it is a Structured, Semi Structured and Unstructured in Hadoop Distributed File System [HDFS] layer. Apache hadoop is open source, it means you don’t need to pay for license while using commercially. Apache Hadoop runs on commodity hardware, it means you don’t need to stick to a single vendor. You can choose any vendor, who offers infrastructure at low cost. Consider a problem where you need to count the number of words in a 5 pound book. would be very difficult for one person but if you tear the pages and distribute it to hundreds of people. Each will count the words in their “page” and then you can simply total that count from each person you shall have to total word count in no time. Why Hadoop with an real time example ? Suppose you have a very big file (50 gb log file for an example) and you want to parse it , do some filtering on it and see the result. What are the options? 1. If you have a computer with ram more that 64 gb ( assuming the additional 16 gb will be used for os and other processes) you can write some code to get it done. Still it will be super slow. And if the file is even larger (in petabyte scale) it wont be even feasible. Rams on petabye scale is not available yet 2. Parse the file in smaller files (may be 10000 files , each in the scale of megabytes) and read them sequentially 3.\", \"sampled\": \"Lauren Toulson’s educational blog article places big data and artificial intelligence inside a digital economy where online actions create data traces. The subject is not treated as abstract technology, but as a practical setting in which browsing, orders, interests, photos, and other activity can become usable data. Machine learning is described as algorithms that automate tasks by learning information and patterns from large datasets. Consequently, the value of data science comes from the ability to recognise patterns across thousands of data points rather than relying only on direct manual instruction. Supervised learning is explained through labelled data, where an algorithm can associate patterns with labels. The label gives the system a target connection to learn, so the machine learning process depends on data that has already been organised into meaningful categories. Semi-supervised learning combines labelled and unlabelled data, reducing the amount of manual labelling needed. This approach keeps some guidance from labelled examples while allowing a larger body of unlabelled information to contribute to pattern learning within a large dataset. Unsupervised learning is presented as a way to find patterns without labels and cluster data into groups. The article says Amazon and Netflix can use this kind of grouping to improve recommendations by identifying patterns in behaviour and grouping related interests. Deep learning is described as a form of machine learning that creates many connections in a brain-like branching structure. The article links this structure to the idea of thousands of connections, making the model more complex than simpler pattern matching. Facebook’s DeepFace is cited as an example of an AI system that auto-tags photos. Its training is described with 4.4 million Facebook photos from 4000 profiles, and the article reports 97.25% accuracy for the system. The article warns that machine learning can reinforce social inequality when programmers are biased or when training data is biased. This concern extends to real areas of use, because the data and the people building systems shape the outputs. Compas, PredPol, and facial recognition are discussed as examples of machine learning bias concerns. These examples show how risk assessment, policing allocation, and identification systems can raise ethical issues when algorithms learn from flawed inputs or reflect unequal assumptions. The UK data discussion centres on rules that require transparent personal data use and user consent. The Data Protection act is tied to that requirement, making transparency a legal issue as well as an ethical concern in big data and machine learning. The article says that in 2017 NHS patient data was leaked to DeepMind at Google. That example sits beside the wider point that personal data use can become controversial when people do not have clear understanding or control over how their information is used. Amazon is described as using order, product, interest, and pricing data for personalised browsing and price changes. Each order provides 2000 data points to Amazon’s algorithm, and the article attributes 35% of Amazon annual profits to personalisation through big data and \\\"AI\\\".\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n","data/essay.jsonl":"{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"It is argued that there is no chance for tourists to take responsibility for protecting the environment and culture of host countries. However, I disagree with this view and believe that is a worthy aim that can be achieved in various ways. Firstly, everyone should be informed about cultural heritage and its great impact on the environment. They should be more aware of their and childrens' behaviour. For instance, the young generation must be properly educated in order to avoid littering public places with a variety of rubbish such as plastic bottles or residue of food, or touching valuable items like statues, special documents, famous pictures and others. Moreover, there should not be promoted cultural differences between both local and international humanity. A bright example of this can be India, where cows are established as sacred animals. Nevertheless, some individuals may bring beef or eat it, breaking Indians' religion. Additionally, there is a rule that people are not allowed to chew gum. Unfortunately, as a result of bad awareness and poor preparation for visiting foreign countries, similar situations occur and the nations change their minds about these individuals. Also, the community of Tangier in Morocco have done a lot of efforts such as recruiting about 70 security agents in all museums of the city and plentiful signs hanging at the main door for giving more details about the story of each building. In conclusion, some personalities can not be responsible towards cultural and environmental areas they went to.\", \"sampled\": \"There is an ongoing debate about whether museums exist primarily for entertainment or for education. Some people believe these institutions are simply places of leisure, while others argue their core purpose is to teach visitors about history and culture. This essay takes the position that museums serve mainly as educational spaces, though they can offer entertainment at the same time. Museums bring together depictions of different cultures in a single location, which allows visitors to learn about world history without travelling far. This concentration of knowledge makes them valuable resources for students and researchers alike. During a final semester college project on ancient culture, the essay author visited a museum and gathered enough material to complete the history assignment on time with good grades. Certain museum activities also make learning accessible and appealing to children, which strengthens the educational argument. Short movies with high-quality sound, for example, attract younger visitors and enhance their understanding of historical topics. Because these activities are engaging and enjoyable, children feel motivated to revisit museums, which deepens their knowledge over time rather than offering a one-time experience. Some displays use lights and sound in ways that serve both knowledge and entertainment purposes, showing that the two goals are not entirely separate. Yet the entertainment element exists to support learning rather than replace it, since the content being presented still relates to history, culture, and the people of particular eras. Museums are therefore best understood as places where education is the primary aim and entertainment is a tool used to make that aim more effective.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In today's society, we are seeing more conception than ever before. Obviously, it has its own merits and challenges and I strongly believe that the arguments in favour are stronger than against this trend. In this essay, I shall discuss both the pros and cons and substantiate the reasons behind the decisions with relevant examples. First and foremost, the main reason behind late pregnancy is delayed economic stability owing to competition in employment. Presently, the job market is uncertain , and people take longer , to find fiscal stability, which in turn influences their decision to have kids. Moreover, the present system of both parents working has also aggravated this situation as most families are nuclear families while the decision to raise offspring at the peak of their career is a difficult decision which most often gets delayed. Studies have further revealed that the average maternity age in Japan and USA has risen owing to the present lifestyle. The advantage can be found in the fact, that having a late offspring with better financial resources, helps in the proper upbringing and subsequent education of young ones. An example can be found in Norway, where families conceiving late are more financially stable than early boomers. Another merit lies in the enhanced maturity of the parents in taking critical decisions for their children. For instance, studies reveal that younger parents tend to be more emotional, in decision-making than their older counterparts, who are more rational .\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that raising fuel prices is the best way to protect the environment, while others argue that education is a more effective long-term solution. This essay contends that although higher fuel costs can produce short-term environmental benefits, educating people about their impact on the environment is the more important priority. Both views deserve careful consideration before reaching a position. Higher fuel prices can discourage people from using private vehicles and push them toward public transport instead. Vietnam is a useful example, as a rise in fuel prices over the past 3 months led many locals to shift from private cars and motorbikes to bicycles and electric buses. This change produced a cleaner atmosphere and fresher air in big cities like Hanoi and Saigon. However, fuel price increases only improve the environment for a short period of time, and officials have doubted their effectiveness because the rise caused frustration among citizens. Environmental damage also comes from human activities beyond vehicle emissions, including large-scale industries, deforestation, and unsustainable farming, which means targeting fuel alone is insufficient. A policy that raises prices without addressing these other catalysts leaves most of the problem untouched. Educating people about the long-term impact of their activities is a better solution because it can change behaviour across all areas of environmental harm, not just transport. When people understand why their choices matter, they are more likely to support sustainable practices voluntarily rather than out of financial pressure. City planners, for instance, could be guided by an educated public to create more green spaces rather than cutting down trees for residential housing.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Older people are important members of any society, and they require more help and attention. It is considered by some that the government should provide financial assistance to the elderly, while there are others who think that it is the responsibility of individuals. I believe that the government should support the aged people. In this essay both views will be discussed before coming to a definite conclusion. On the one hand, a culture of saving instead of depending on the state fosters the overall economic development of a country. In other words, if people have money set aside for their pension age, it would lessen the amount of financial responsibility on the government. It would allow the ministry to provide funds to the development of more essential fields, like industries, production of goods, infrastructure healthcare and education. Moreover, if all citizens knew that the state would ensure them a decent retirement fund, they would have no motivation to work hard, try to build a career and save money. In a personal aspect, saving money gives individuals a way out from uncertainties of life and an opportunity to enjoy greater security. For instance, in the case of critical emergencies, people have to deal with medical expenses and it requires a financial preparedness. On the other hand, old people are physically weak and are financially incapacitated. Firstly, as there is no any alternative ways of earning for elderly, their chances for employment are quite low compared to the young people. Therefore, the government should take care of aged population and support them with a monthly pension and provide a health insurance.\", \"sampled\": \"People are increasingly choosing healthy food and regular exercise, and this shift reflects a broader change in how individuals think about their own wellbeing. Awareness of diseases has become a key reason why many people pay closer attention to their diet and daily habits. Changes in personal consciousness and genuine consideration for one's body appear to be driving this healthy living trend across different age groups. One clear reason behind this shift is that consuming large amounts of junk food and avoiding exercise can lead to serious conditions such as diabetes, immune system problems, or being overweight. Adolescents today tend to take better care of their bodies compared to the past, showing that younger generations are not exempt from these health concerns. Teenagers in particular go on diets and exercise to burn calories and avoid body shaming, a pattern especially visible among girls in Korea. At the household level, families can improve daily meals by choosing healthy products that suit each family member's tastes, making nutritious eating a practical and personal goal. This kind of change does not require large resources and can meaningfully reduce the risk of diet-related illness over time. When healthy eating becomes a shared household habit, it is easier for individuals to maintain consistent choices rather than relying on willpower alone. Local authorities are encouraged to launch campaigns such as running and walking events, which can make physical activity feel more accessible and community-oriented. Suggested distances of 1 to 5 kilometres make such events achievable for people of varying fitness levels. Offering discounted tickets to exercise facilities is another practical measure that could motivate more people to become physically active on a regular basis.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The increase in the production of consumer goods results in damage to the natural environment. What are the causes of this? What can be done to solve this problem? Nowadays, as more consumer goods are manufactured, more damage has been inflicted to the environment. I will outline several reasons for this and put forward some measures to this issue. First of all, the increase in the production of consumer products harms the environment in two ways: the chemical by-products from the manufacturing process and the mass production of disposable goods. As more goods are produced, more toxic wastes and emissions are released from factories into nature. Water sources are contaminated, and the air is severely polluted, which results in the deaths of many marine and terrestrial animals. Also, to accommodate customers’ ever-increasing demands, more single-use products are introduced, most of which are non-biodegradable. Though having a short lifespan, these products can remain as wastes for thousands of years, turning our planet into a huge landfill and posing a threat to the living habitats of all creatures. Actions must be taken as soon as possible to minimize the negative impacts on environment arising from the increasing amount of consumer goods. First, companies should promote the use of eco-friendlier materials. For example, the giant coffee chain Starbucks has recently replaced plastic straws with reusable alternatives made of materials like paper or bamboo. In addition, many governments are also encouraging the development of more sustainable manufacturing processes. For instance, many states in the U.S offer tax breaks and incentives for businesses using renewable energy, and some firms are even allowed to purchase green energy at cheaper prices than traditional fossil fuels. In conclusion, there are two main reasons why the environment is severely impacted by the increase in production of consumer goods.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe television is a valuable educational tool for children, while others argue it causes serious harm to their development and wellbeing. Both sides of this debate carry weight, and the question of whether television does more good than harm is one that many parents and educators consider carefully. This essay examines the educational benefits of television alongside its documented negative effects before reaching a position. Channels such as National Geography and BBC Earth are specifically designed to educate viewers about rare animal and bird species found across the world. The Discovery Channel broadcasts content covering the latest technology gadgets and innovations, which can broaden a child's understanding of science and engineering. Television also keeps children engaged through cartoons and comedy series, giving them structured entertainment rather than leaving them idle. On the other hand, children who become addicted to video games on television tend to avoid outdoor activities, which can lead to poor eyesight, a weakened immune system, and obesity. Children may also display violent behavior in real life after watching action movies, largely because they lack the judgement and life experience needed to separate fiction from reality. According to The Times, in 2018 a 17-year-old boy killed his classmate for not helping him with homework, and he stated he was trying to imitate a scene from the movie The Enemy. The violent incident reported by The Times illustrates how serious the consequences of unmonitored television viewing can become for young people without proper guidance. At the same time, the education and information that children gain from well-chosen television programmes is genuinely invaluable and should not be dismissed because of the medium's risks. Parental supervision and careful selection of content remain the most practical way to allow children to benefit from television while limiting its harmful effects.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Every student who graduates from a Prestigious University wants a job in his/her respective profession. Many of them failed to get their desired Job and eventually they changed their field. There are many reasons for it which I will discuss in this essay. Firstly, there is a saturation of graduates in the same field and the corresponding jobs are limited. For example, every year almost 2000 chemical engineers are graduated from all the Universities in Pakistan but there are only 500 jobs available for them so what will they do in this situation? Unfortunately, they changed their profession because they do not want to waste one year. However, these kinds of problems exist in almost every country who are producing thousands of graduates with limited job opportunities. According to Higher Education Commission, almost every year 50-70% of graduates changed their profession due to not finding the desired job in their career. In addition, before selecting the course, students should research very well the scope and saturation of all desired fields. It will not only help them to get a job in their chosen career but also make them morally strong. Government should create more and more opportunities for the graduates so that they do not get dishearted if they can not get a job. In Pakistan, these types of issues can only happen with the engineers because every year thousand of engineers are produced and only 50% of jobs are available for them. To conclude, Mostly students after graduation changed their profession because they get failed to find a job in their chosen career. These types of problems can be solved by creating more opportunities for graduates.\", \"sampled\": \"Researching the history of one's house or building has grown increasingly popular in recent years, driven largely by technological advancement and the widespread influence of social media. People are genuinely curious about who lived in their homes before them and what events took place within those walls. An interesting backstory attached to a property may even increase its market value, giving homeowners a practical reason to look into the past. Social media plays a central role in encouraging this trend, as videos and posts about historically notable buildings attract large audiences and inspire viewers to investigate their own living places. A clear example is a social media video about a hotel with a history of unsolved murders, homicides, and unexplained disappearances, which drew considerable attention online. Extraordinary stories like these add a sense of curiosity and excitement to what might otherwise be monotonous daily routines. When people decide to research their building's history, they have several avenues available to them. The internet is widely regarded as the most convenient starting point, offering quick access to records and local histories from almost anywhere. Old newspapers held in libraries can also reveal detailed information about past incidents tied to specific locations, making libraries a valuable resource for serious researchers. Beyond digital tools, knowledge about a building's past is sometimes passed on through word of mouth by older residents who have lived in the area for many years. This informal transfer of memory can preserve details that never appeared in print or online.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Technology have ushered in a new era, but have also brought some certain evils. Majority of people believe that the usage of social media is helpful, as it give chance to get in touch with one other. Additionally, it can also provide some news information which is going on daily basis. This essay will discuss about the advantages in detail. At the onset, Social media provide an online platform for the people who are living far away from each other. This make a chance for them to be in contact with one other. By this platform, they can also send many messages including photos and viseos to each other with just one click. For Instance, In today's era, if there is a function in a family, than the family members can connect each other via this platform. Moreover, they can also send the invitation through online. In addition, Individuals can also get the news information from this platform. As this new generation is modern, so people share and upload news locally as well as internationally. In modern times, people have to wait for the news, but in current phase once the news is uploaded, millions of people can get to know at the same time.To prove it, In past month, there was an inaugration in India regarding to the world's largest temple. When the idol was placed in the temple, normal citizens can't see it, but at the event this whole event went online and as a result millions of people were streaming it online.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that governments should focus education spending on young people rather than on elderly adults who cannot read or write. This essay agrees with that position, as young people represent the future of society and are better placed to benefit from sustained educational investment. The arguments around memory, energy, and social role all point toward prioritizing youth. Young people are currently responsible for inventing many things, which shows that their minds are active and productive when given proper resources and support. Younger generations are also described as more energetic and mentally strong than the elderly, making them more capable of absorbing new knowledge over time. After the age of 27, human memory begins to fade, which means the return on educational investment naturally decreases as people grow older. Elderly people may also face family concerns that prevent them from finding enough time to study consistently, reducing the practical value of spending heavily on their education. Some have argued that teaching older adults, including those who could not write in Latin, would be worthwhile because educated elderly people can serve as an example to the youth around them. While that argument has some merit, it does not justify redirecting large portions of government education budgets away from younger learners. The government, as the key decision-maker on education spending, must weigh where limited funds will produce the greatest benefit for society as a whole. Young people have longer working lives ahead of them and more capacity to apply what they learn in ways that contribute to national development. For these reasons, it is not necessary to spend heavily on elderly education, because the future genuinely belongs to the youth.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In the contemporary era, a noticeable shift in family planning has emerged and a growing number of individuals have a tendency is have their offspring at a later stage. I believe there are numerous benefits such as they can spend quality time together and preparing mentally, a financially better for their children. First and foremost, one of the pivotal reasons why individuals delay parenthood is the aspiration of young couples to spend quality time together before they must devote all of their attention to the baby. Subsequently, they can enjoy their life more with one another and can be able to understand each other. In addition, they can prepare carefully mentally before entering a new position and learn more knowledge before becoming parents. Therefore, young parents no longer be sluggish in looking after their children and taking care of them in the best way. For instance, some Western researchers said that having more understanding before childbirth can lead to lower chances of divorce after having babies and can take good care of them. On the other hand, a number of individuals who concentrate on their careers first and then have babies can bring their kids a better quality of life such as a better education, an excellent study environment and even a luxurious life. Moreover, having offspring early can cause young inexperienced parents to get into trouble and cause financial difficulties for them. For illustration, several young Vietnamese parents would be capable of enrolling their children in private schools where they get quality education and learn other skills meanwhile, which are not available in all schools because they have prepared for parenthood for a long time before having children.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people argue that preventing climate change is too difficult and that humans should simply adapt to new climate patterns instead. The author does not completely agree with this view, holding that humans have a vital role in protecting nature and preventing global warming. This essay will explain why mere adaptation is an insufficient response to the scale of the problem. Those who support adaptation often claim that a single person cannot take responsibility for saving the entire earth from climate change. This position has some logic, since the scale of the problem can feel overwhelming for any individual. However, accepting this reasoning too readily risks ignoring the serious damage that continued inaction will cause to the natural world. Research cited from Cambridge University states that 75% of wild animals will die due to climate change in the next decade, which shows that climate change is a clear sign of fatality rather than a manageable shift. Humans are described as a major cause of all sorts of pollution and global warming, meaning the source of the problem lies largely with human behaviour and choices. Pollution has drastically increased as a result of lifestyle changes, which makes it harder to argue that adaptation alone is a responsible path forward. Each person should change their life patterns to help prevent environmental damage, since collective individual action can produce meaningful results when enough people participate. Creating awareness among industries and factories about pollution is also presented as a necessary step, given that large-scale emitters contribute heavily to the overall problem. Preventing changes to the climate is not easy, but the evidence makes clear that prevention must be pursued rather than abandoned in favour of simply adjusting to worsening conditions.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Evidently, some workers around the globe are concerned about paying tax( TAXES) for the money they earn and assert that this levy should be avoided. In my opinion, it is essential to pay income tax due to following reasons. First of all, everyone receives many benefits for paying a part of their income. in detail, governments use this revenue to develop and maintain infrastructures and many services, which is inevitable for a good (BETTER) life in the society. This not only includes the(ESSENTIAL) essentials( ESSENTIAL) services such as a state-of-the-art healthcare system , educational institutions, supply of running water and electricity, but also includes facilities for leisure such as parks, stadiums, and so on. Thus paying a proportion salary to (FOR) government is justifiable. Secondly, governing bodies need to spend a huge amount of money to ensure an effective law and order as well as a strong defence force in the country. Nowadays, almost all countries spend millions of dollars to modernize the police force , which, in turn, reduce the crime in the societies. Similarly , a lion share of revenue use to upgrade the defence forces to avoid attacks from enemy nations. In contrast, peaceful life of people are being disturbed while governments compromise in this areas( AREA). Syria is an epitome for it, since this nation have a weak army and police force and it’s citizens struggle to survive due(TO) terrorism and attack of other countries .This reiterate the importance of paying income tax.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that school leavers should begin working immediately after finishing school, while others argue that pursuing higher education leads to better long-term outcomes. Both sides have reasonable points, but the question of which path is more beneficial deserves careful consideration. Those who favour early employment argue that working straight after school gives young people more time to explore different fields and decide which job truly suits them. Pupils who enter the workforce early can also start saving money sooner, gaining financial independence before their peers finish studying. Many hairdressers, for example, begin working after graduating from high school, developing practical skills while building their savings each month. On the other hand, completing a master's degree or doctorate is seen by many as the stronger path, because higher education allows students to gain deeper professional knowledge and become more competitive in the job market. People with higher educational qualifications are generally better equipped to handle complex problems and contribute meaningfully to society. Some companies also require academic qualifications for certain positions, meaning that without a degree, candidates are simply excluded from those roles. Financial managers working in banks offer a clear illustration of this point, as most of them have completed a master's degree and studied financial modules while researching financial projects during their studies. This academic background gives them the capability to lead teams or manage entire companies effectively. Weighing both sides, it seems clear that children should pursue higher education rather than seek employment immediately after graduating, since the knowledge and qualifications gained open doors that early employment alone cannot.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In recent years, family history research has been becoming more popular than it was. In my opinion, I would argue that this trend should be as a positive development. There are several factors for encouraging the study of our family history. In the first place, curiosity is the main cause that leads to making people intend to dig up about their family traditions. They would desire to know their roots, where our forefathers came from or how they earned their living. In the second place, some people must take in family history because of the fact that they would have health problems and one of them is genetically inheritable medical conditions such as asthma or diabetes. In reality, understand genetic make-up provides an early lifestyle intervention to mitigate the adverse effects of these diseases. Doing research on family traditions renders countless rewards and this may be an amazing journey which could blows our mind. Firstly, offspring may find it astonishing to dig up the details of their predecessors' lifestyle or some of the achievements that ancestors attained throughout their lifetime. In fact, no other activities can bring an experience like this. Moreover, learning about a family tree would be a means of self-motivation. For instance, we could drive ourselves through any difficulty when we realize that our ancestors overcame the seemingly insurmountable obstacles, to regard our difficulties as trivial ones. In conclusion, researching the origins of a person is becoming more popular in society and I believe that it is a positive development.\", \"sampled\": \"Some governments spend considerable money renovating and restoring old buildings, yet many argue these funds could be better directed toward new housing and infrastructure. The capital invested in construction renovation is large compared with the cost of original housing development, which raises serious questions about value. This essay argues that renovation funds should be redirected to modern housing expansion and transportation routes in urban cities. More than half of the population in developing countries lacks a home, which means the need for new housing is urgent and cannot be ignored. When people cannot find adequate shelter, they are pushed into slums where they face floods, disease outbreaks, and insecurity on a daily basis. Building new lodges in undeveloped areas is therefore considered more advantageous to society than spending heavily on restoring structures that already exist. Urban cities worldwide are experiencing congestion due to overcrowding, and improving transportation routes alongside housing would address this pressure directly. Demolition of structural elements in existing buildings, if not handled by experts, can lead to significant loss and death, making renovation a risky choice for governments with limited skilled personnel. The risks involved in retrofitting can also cause costs to exceed the original budget, which weakens the financial case for restoration projects. Retrofitting can be cheaper than new construction for smaller projects, so investment decisions should be made with respect to project size and available expertise. Restructuring of existing housing should be considered only as a last resort for expanding the housing supply.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, television, video and computer games play a key role in child's life. Few people believe that children who are spending time on television and video or computer games will be more benefited, others think this has harmful effects on them. Here, we are going to discuss how they can gain knowledge and can become sharp thinkers by using them as well as drawbacks such as addiction, obesity, impact on studies and exposure to harmful content. First, let us see how children can gain more general knowledge by watching television and how they can become smarter by playing video or computer games. If children play video games, then there are plenty of chances that they can think in various ways which obviously leads them to become sharp thinkers. By watching Television, they will get to know what is happening around them all over the world. Next, we are going to see side effects of spending time on television and video games such as addiction, obesity, violence, effect on studies and exposure to bad content. Many surveys explained how children become addicted to games, therefore they just like to play for hours without even moving which absolutely leads to addiction and this lack of physical activity leads to obesity. For example, if we see children who play war games which involves gun shooting become more violent than children who doesn't play. All of these impact on their studies. There are multiple chances that the children can get exposure to negative content by watching television if parents do not take enough precautions.\", \"sampled\": \"Plants and animals play a vital role in ecosystems, and without them, humans cannot survive because they maintain the balance on Earth. Some people believe that effective measures can still be taken to protect these species, while others disagree and see the damage as irreversible. This essay argues that protection is still possible if humans take their responsibility seriously. Human activities have always had significant consequences on plant and animal species, and much of this harm stems from selfishness rather than necessity. Plants provide humans with air, fruits, medicines, herbs, and materials from roots and branches, yet forests continue to be destroyed for short-term gain. Proposed protective measures such as creating public awareness and stopping deforestation are practical steps that could reverse some of this damage. Global warming has reached a critical point where humans are essentially competing with nature rather than living alongside it. Human interference with nature may lead to serious consequences such as volcanoes and earthquakes, suggesting that the planet itself responds to the pressure placed upon it. The relationship between humans and the natural world has become unstable, and continuing on the same path carries clear risks for both. The author of this view holds that effective measures can still be taken to protect plant and animal species before the situation becomes unmanageable. Supporting endangered species requires the same level of commitment and care that humans extend to children who cannot protect themselves. If humans accept that their survival depends on the health of ecosystems on Earth, they are more likely to treat conservation as a genuine priority rather than an optional concern.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In most countries in the world, governments are in charge to manage the territory wherein laws and rules are applied. Nevertheless, a part of the population believes that authorities should lead people tougher while others think \\\"handcuffs\\\" should be released to let people choose their own path. First, we are going to see why it is important to follow the lead of the government and then see that individuals' thoughts are essential as well. First of all, the government is designed to able a whole population to live together. Hence, rules must be followed by everyone living under the system. Without simple rules, it could be a disaster. For instance, I recently saw on TV a documentary showing a world without rules. The result was kind of harsh to imagine. In this simulation people were beating each other for basic things in life such as food because of discipline lacks. On the other hand, the law and rules could also disrupt and interfere with individual projects. I mean, some people who want to build something uncommon are locked in the one-way path developed by the government. Hence it could be difficult for a person to succeed in what he wants to achieve. Perhaps, I am thinking about a friend who tried to launch his own business. Unfortunately and because of administrative issues, he couldn't go through the entire process. When I heard his story, I was strongly convinced that it was not fair at all as he deserved a chance. Moreover, he was doing it all by himself without asking for a mortgage or whatever. To sum up, if I had to share my thoughts, I would say that the whole population is a really tough subject to manage. In my opinion, people should have some extra shots to be able to succeed in their dreams considering individual commitment.\", \"sampled\": \"I agree for the most part with the idea that the main purpose of schools is to turn children into good citizens and workers instead of benefitting them individually. Schools clearly spend a great deal of time shaping students into people who can follow rules and contribute to society. Much of what happens in a typical school day seems designed to produce orderly, capable members of a community rather than to develop each child’s unique potential. Schools teach academic subjects like Math, Science, and English, which are directly useful for future employment and for understanding how society works. At the same time, schools also teach social lessons to correct students’ conduct, and students learn how to behave and interact with each other at school. Social studies or law studies teach students acceptable behavior and consequences of wrongdoing, while school discipline guides children to a good path. These efforts point more toward producing cooperative citizens than toward personal enrichment. Most schools have similar curricula and standards for graduated students, and teaching the same thing to all students does not account for individual talents. A student good at Math may perform better in Algebra than a student good at drawing, yet both must follow the same academic program. This standard way of teaching does not benefit students individually, because it treats everyone as if they need the same preparation for the same citizen and worker roles. Some schools encourage students to be the best version of themselves while maintaining standard requirements, and they offer clubs like art, music, and sports, and courses like literature, management, and art. These opportunities show that individual growth can still be supported alongside the main goal of building good citizens and workers. The standard way of teaching does not benefit students individually, but some schools support individual growth alongside standards, which makes the original statement mostly true even if it is not absolute.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, many young adults are seen to be a sluggish generation compared to the older ones. They often display the actions of being spoiled and lazy who can only spend money to have fun. Many believe that these kids should be assigned to do voluntary social work during their extra time instead of just chilling at home. In this essay, I will discuss and state my opinion regarding this issue. To begin with, those adults who propose this idea claim that giving opportunities to young adolescents to do voluntary jobs will benefit not only the kids themselves but also society. Parents think that training their heirs from a young age will help them to overcome social issues in the future. For instance, adults who are used to being involved in local society as volunteers are more sociable. Therefore, they find it easier to get hired for a job,and also are able to contribute to building the neighbourhood. On the other hand, there are some disadvantages to this belief. One of the primary negative sides that might appear is youngsters consider it as a common thing. In other words, If a teenage person is forced to carry out an unpayable task, they might end up thinking that it is a common thing to do a job without getting properly appreciated with rewards.\", \"sampled\": \"In many developed countries, people are living longer than ever before, and this trend brings a range of serious problems for both individuals and society. One clear issue is that more elderly citizens put pressure on transport systems because many still rely on their personal vehicle for daily movement. This dependence makes traffic congestion worse in cities where the population is already dense. The constant use of personal vehicles also leads to higher levels of air pollution, since less usage of public transport means more emissions from private cars. Another problem linked to expanding cities is deforestation for infrastructure, which harms wild animals and humans by destroying natural habitats. These environmental pressures grow as life expectancy rises and urban areas spread further. Advanced technology offers some help for older populations, but it also introduces new risks like cyber-attacks. Hackers often use a malware attack to steal MNC data, and companies then pay to unlock or retrieve data after a malware attack. This shows how longer lives in a digital age can create unexpected security threats for businesses and the economy. Some measures could reduce these problems if higher authorities and the local public work together. Higher authorities could establish a law mandating public transport on weekdays, which would cut both congestion and pollution. The local public should participate in social work campaigns, such as environmental and public awareness programs, to help protect shared spaces and support community wellbeing.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"There has been a controversy about whether we should make efforts to fight climate change. I think it's necessary, and my reasons will be explained in the following paragraphs. First, it affects our life negatively. It has been reported that there has been an increase in the sea level on account of melted ice, which can be ascribed to global warming. Such a situation will lead to a loss of living habitats around the globe. Take some scientific reports as an example. Many scientists have detected that the sea level will rise about 6 meters in 2100 because of the melted ice in the south pole and Greenland. As a result, innumerable tropical islands and low latitude areas will be drowned. Second, it will bring countless natural disasters. It's expected that global warming will influence the precipitation largely, which can boost the probability of fierce hurricanes. In the past years, the US and the UK experienced the attack of fierce hurricanes several times. From 1095-1930, there were about 3.5 times hurricanes annually; and from 1995 to 2002, this number ascended to 8.4 times. It's worth mentioning that every attack of hurricanes kills hundreds and thousands of lives, and can cause a loss of millions of dollars. Third, it brings about diseases. Due to the extreme climate caused by climate change, those affected places provide a superb growing environment for viruses; creatures like mosquitoes and rats grow rampantly in those areas.\", \"sampled\": \"In many countries, interest in the history of homes is growing noticeably among residents and local communities. A key reason behind this trend is the urge to connect with living spaces for comfort and a deeper sense of belonging. People have discovered that learning historical aspects can provide answers to setbacks they face in their daily housing conditions. This curiosity is not merely abstract, as shown by a clear example in South Tangerang, where house walls were soaked during rainy seasons. Investigation revealed the cluster was built on a former swamp, a fact that had been hidden from current occupants. Residents asked geography experts about the land’s origin and eventually confirmed the findings with local governments, which gave them a reliable explanation for the persistent dampness. The process of uncovering such information often involves finding trivia and unseen parts of a place through simple tools. GPS indicated a watery area when residents input their addresses, offering an early clue that something was unusual beneath the surface. Research can be done by examining small, implicit neighborhood details that most people overlook in their daily routines. The rationale for this growing interest includes a strong curiosity to solve today’s housing problems with practical knowledge. By investigating a former swamp or checking old land records, people gain control over issues that otherwise seem random or unfixable. This approach turns a home into something more than a structure, grounding it in a story that explains present challenges and restores a sense of comfort.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, social media has played a huge part in our lives. It is a platform that many people use to stay in touch with other people on a daily basis. However, social media has certain drawbacks too. This essay will explain the pros and cons of social media. One of the major advantages of utilizing social media is connectivity. Social media is a platform that can connect people all over the world without having to worry about time and distance. By leveraging social media, we can share information and build relationships with friends, family, colleagues and even celebrities. It creates a sense of bond amongst each other. Aside from connectivity, social media has been an essential platform for educational purposes. It makes learning easier and more accessible for many people. One-click away and we can acquire the knowledge to perform various tasks simply by following the instructions or tutorials available on social media platforms. Nevertheless, social media has its disadvantages too such as spreading fake news and cyberbullying. It can be challenging to verify the accuracy of information found on social media platforms. The moment a piece of news became viral it spreads like wildfire. Aside from fake news, many people have become a victim of cyberbullying especially when it comes to public figures or celebrities that they despise. They usually create fake accounts just to hate, bully and threaten them which leads to an unhealthy environment in society. In conclusion, social media has a deep impact on everyone's daily lives. Everything depends on how smart we can utilize it.\", \"sampled\": \"The author partly agrees that urgent problems can be solved through international cooperation, and this view rests on two main supports: knowledge and facilities. Some challenges are too complex for a single nation to handle alone because science and knowledge are vital for finding solutions. When a country faces a technical or medical crisis beyond its own capacity, looking outward becomes a practical step rather than an idealistic one. A factory problem unsolvable by local engineers can be addressed by asking international engineers who bring different experience and fresh thinking. Other nations may have open-minded people who can suggest credible solutions, and this exchange of expertise often leads to faster and more effective outcomes. Relying only on domestic knowledge can delay progress when the required insight simply does not exist within the country’s current pool of engineers or researchers. A country may lack facilities to solve a problem, and this shortage makes international cooperation especially valuable. Special disease treatment devices from other countries can solve medical problems that would otherwise leave ill individuals without proper care. Asking for international help with facilities can save money and time because building equivalent infrastructure from scratch is often slower and far more expensive than borrowing or sharing what already exists elsewhere. International cooperation can strengthen relationships between authorities, creating trust that helps in future emergencies. Government officials who work with international partners on shared problems build connections that go beyond a single project or crisis. While some problems can be solved by self-government, many urgent situations benefit when a government reaches out to other international countries for the knowledge and facilities it does not possess.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"It is argued by some individuals that getting a tertiary education is the best method to get success in life, while others think that it is not must to get success in the future. This essay agrees with the later point and will discuss both of the mentioned views in the subsequent paragraphs. On the one hand, some argue that if we have a university degree, we can get more success in our lives because most multinational companies offer an excellent salary to qualified employees. This is to say that to earn a handsome amount of amount and spend luxurious life, an individual has a college degree. Moreover, students who have passed their education from universities have a treasure of knowledge about sophisticated technology and also have all the required skills that enable them to work in renowned corporations. As a result, they become successful in their career. For example, a survey in America found that doctors and engineers earn more than 20k monthly. However, i would argue that not everyone is interested to study after high school. On the other hand, some believe that having a tertiary education is not crucial to being successful in life because there are many people who are millionaires without any college degree.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe art is an essential school subject for children, while others believe art is a waste of time. I agree with the first view because art enhances children's imagination, thinking capacity, and additional skills. These abilities can help students in many areas beyond the classroom. Art skills can help in competitions and bring success to the school, which builds a positive reputation. Job market competition is stiff and selection patterns have changed drastically, so students need more than textbook knowledge. Candidates with art expertise have a higher probability of getting a job because companies may prefer candidates with imagination skills for faster decision-making. Paintings by legendary artists are sold for millions, showing that art can be a serious career path. Business tycoons show interest in buying eye-catching paintings, generating money, and this economic value should not be ignored. Art class should be held twice a week in school to give children enough exposure to develop these talents. Some students find art boring and lack dedication, but that does not mean the subject itself is useless. The government should promote art and culture as it represents India's rich heritage, which artists like MF Hussain have contributed to. A balanced curriculum that includes art prepares children for both creative and practical challenges in life.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The majority of people argue that helping children who cannot read and comprehend basic education classes to achieve a brighter future would enable them to pursue even the most challenging careers, such as becoming doctors, teachers, and actors, as long as they have the willpower. In this essay, I will present my perspective on why I agree with this topic and provide a conclusion. To begin with, those who are interested in children's education argue that supporting teenagers in developing countries is not an option but an obligation. For instance, a comparative study was conducted between teenagers who underwent fundamental pedagogical training and those who did not, at Seoul University. The research team tracked the lifestyles of these young individuals until they reached adulthood. They found that children who received basic education had more stable lifestyles and financial conditions. Therefore, it is essential for young people in developing countries to receive supplementary education. Additionally, others believe that educating the youth of these nations represents their greatest potential for advancement in various fields, including finance, society, and economics. For example, major Korean companies have been supporting schools in developing countries for an extended period, focusing on education. Many students who have taken these classes over the past ten years have gone on to have successful careers as teachers, nurses, and scientists. Some have even ventured to work in developed countries while simultaneously contributing to the progress of their home countries.\", \"sampled\": \"Technology advancement enables creation of various movies and video games, and many of these films and games contain violence and have many fans. Some people believe violent media have negative impacts on families and should be banned, while others think violent media only disturb relaxation time. This essay will examine both views before explaining why I disagree with banning such content. Supporters of violent media say watching and playing are satisfying hobbies that provide excitement, and some films teach behavior in horrifying circumstances, like a smartphone theft. Such media can show that real life is not always kind and friendly, which helps viewers prepare for difficult situations. These arguments suggest that violent content offers entertainment and even practical lessons rather than purely harmful effects. On the other hand, violent movies and games may increase disturbance and battles among youngsters and adults, and juveniles may imitate violent behaviors in personal lives. Children might imitate violence without understanding, causing negative future effects, which worries many parents and community members. This perspective holds that exposure to violent scenes can lead to real-world aggression and should therefore be controlled. I disagree with banning because society has the power to choose what to see, and families should set rules about watching and playing violent films and games instead of banning. Youngsters and adults can decide their own viewing habits, while parents can guide children according to their maturity. Responsible regulation within households is a more balanced solution than outright prohibition.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Most of the people throughout the world are interesting in searching on history of the place they live because of intelligence and they concern that they should be aware of it. In this situation, they are capable of finding out deeds and talking with inhabitants. In this essay, I am going to discuss problems. As a whole, human beings should be aware of the place they live in because nations are more intelligent nowadays. They might have many legitimate requirements. Furthermore, some are enthusiastic about those kinds of findings as they need to learn their children about the history of accommodation. For instance, when it is going to enrolling a child on a school, it is necessary to have proof of living place; whenever kids are asking about ancient times, it is necessary to have information of the past. People could be able to find out historical information on where they live by deeds and inhabitant citizens because they are the most relevant and well-known factors which can be trusted. For example, even though there are neighbours and compeers surrounded by, it is impossible to get relevant data on a land accurately without a deed.\", \"sampled\": \"Nowadays, many people are increasingly interested in the history of the building they live in. Technology improvement makes it easy to find building information online, and some websites contain details like materials and construction date. This easy access encourages homeowners to explore the past of their homes without needing professional help. Researching independently helps avoid being deceived by dishonest engineers or builders who might provide false details. When owners check facts themselves through reliable online sources, they gain accurate knowledge about their property. This direct approach also saves money and builds confidence in understanding the building’s true condition. Knowing a house's background story increases the owner's enthusiasm and emotional connection to the place. Some Europeans research what their building experienced in the past, discovering that houses may have witnessed World War 2 or be older than the owner. These personal discoveries turn an ordinary residence into a meaningful part of history. Learning history makes people realize the value and treasure their houses more deeply. Understanding the story behind a house is becoming a trend, as more individuals seek to connect with the past. This growing curiosity shows that a home is not just a physical structure but a witness to time and human experience.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Several specialists think that when a nation is already prosperous, an added richness to the economy makes the people not satisfied anymore. This essay disagrees with this point of view. In the current state of the global economy, wealthy states should never run out of funds for the welfare of the citizens. Therefore, I disagree with the thought that additional wealth will just make people unsatisfied. Moreover, it should always be the goal of rich countries to gain more in order to provide services for their people such as education, health, and entertainment. In the last two years, the whole world was devastated by the pandemic, and no one was spared. In fact, the global economic state was put into perils. Moreover, many things had happened also in the past and no one can even predict the future. With the updated technologies and increased population, a more prosperous country should continue to invest and collect more financial profits to make the nation stable for the future. Furthermore, it is not to think that richer countries are greedy, but it is the fact that it got resources and backup for their people's needs. Famines, wars, and other pandemics can happen anytime in the future, and countries like the United States is expected to always extend their financial aids to those poorer ones.\", \"sampled\": \"In many countries people are showing a growing curiosity about the past of the houses or buildings they occupy. This interest goes beyond simple curiosity because the history of a building can reveal how it was used in different periods. It can also help predict the living environment of past times, giving residents a clearer sense of the space they now call home. Investigating a building’s history can start with its foundation stories, which often hold clues about its original purpose. A 100-year-old building may have changed owners many times, and changes in ownership can lead to changes in how a building is used. This kind of research, sometimes called legwork, remains a method for researching a building as long as it exists. Maintaining and preserving buildings for a long time is difficult, and destroying a building can be easier than maintaining it. Government control helps protect buildings over long periods, which is why many countries maintain cultural heritages, including historic buildings. Without this support, the physical links to our past could disappear before their stories are fully understood. Learning about building history provides information about our living environment, connecting us to earlier generations and their daily lives. When a government steps in to preserve these structures, it ensures that future residents can continue this exploration. The effort to protect old buildings is not just about saving walls and roofs but about keeping the record of human experience alive.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Some individuals perceive the time that children spend on television, video, and computer games are worth it, whereas others think that there are bad effects on their development. I believe, these type of medias provide a creative way to learn things, but this indeed affect their mental health also. This essay will discuss both views further and provide a concluding view. On the one hand, entertainment industries tend to create their contents for children colourful and lively to keep their attention as long as they can. This is undeniably effective for those toddlers who watch alphabet videos on Youtube for long hours, and do not get sick of them. Due to this way of presentation to them, they learn faster and improves their comprehension skills. Therefore, if controlled moderately, the time they use to watch or play with these contents, would greatly help in their early cognitive skills. Meanwhile, opposing side cited negative effects on this debate, such as children becomes impatient and irritable when these entertainments are taken away from them. As they engage and focus on television or mobile phone for a long period of time, their minds continuously process all information that they see, which exhaust them quickly.\", \"sampled\": \"Road safety is a global concern with daily casualties, and many people argue that stricter driving punishments are the only way to improve the situation. I disagree that stricter driving punishments are the only solution because this view overlooks other critical factors. Highway conditions, vehicle standards, and government responsibilities also play major roles in preventing accidents. Highway conditions, such as substandard materials and potholes, contribute to accidents and are often ignored in the punishment debate. Lack of street lights and unbarricaded dug-up roadways worsen road safety, especially at night. An Indian girl died after her scooter slipped into a hole in the dark, which shows how infrastructure failures can be deadly. Inferior vehicle conditions, like old buses in Bangladesh, cause accidents that no amount of driver punishment can prevent. Public buses in Bangladesh are often 10 to 20 years old with technical issues, making them unsafe on busy roads. Dysfunctional traffic lights and lack of highway patrol increase accident risks, highlighting the need for better traffic management systems. The government is responsible for safer public transportation and traffic rules, so focusing only on penalties misses the bigger picture. Improved roadways, vehicles, and laws are essential for road safety, and these require coordinated government action. Without addressing these areas, even the strictest punishments will fail to reduce the daily toll on the roads.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Employers should give their staff at least a 4-week holiday a year to make employees better at their jobs. To what extent do you agree or disagree? Employees and workers are overwhelmed with a fast changing world. industry force is spending more time at task and offices to get promotion, bonuses and compensation to be successful. In the midst of a busy production schedule struggle schedule, some argue that it is damaging their personal behaviour and life relationships. Therefore, companies should reconsider their time off and working period. Furthermore, a 30-day rest from job sounds like a great idea to motivate and encourage to production hard. To begin with, companies, industries and factories are functioning at unprecedented scale. This is because of increasing demand of industry force to meet global supply of the market. This rapid boost in the economy has forced them to endeavor non-stop and weekends as well. Such practises are harmful to individual and gets isolated from the outside world. Hence, this group are not able to spend enough time with their families and friends. Moreover, I support that the employer should provide a month off from effort. This will allow employees to follow their passion and take a break from endeavor. Apparently, such changes will develop a feeling of pride towards the company and will effort harder. On the flip side, one might argue that such huge break can redirect their motive for production.\", \"sampled\": \"Globalization has created a world where the same products and shops appear across many different countries. This process has made a varied stock of goods available to the public, reaching far more people than ever before. In the past, only wealthy families could afford imported goods because of high shipping and tax costs. The import-export business helps the economies of involved nations to prosper in significant ways. A report by the Economic and Finance Department stated India's economy boosted by 7% after the 2007 recession due to export-import business. The same report stated the booming economy created 45% more job opportunities for the population. However, globalization has also caused serious problems for local vendors in many communities. Society showed less interest in local brands compared to foreign merchandise, and the diminishing popularity of native items led to shop closures and job losses. A 2018 survey by the Employment and Grievance Department in India reported approximately 4 out of 10 people lost work due to inclination towards western produce. Globalization widened community reach and helped economic growth, but the adverse effects on people's lives are difficult to ignore. The availability of varied stock increased public reach, yet this came at a real cost to local employment. The evidence from India shows both a 7% economic boost and a situation where 4 out of 10 people lost their jobs, revealing a deep tension at the heart of this global trend.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"People have different views about whether students should be allowed to evaluate their teachers. While students’ criticisms may sometimes lead to undesirable consequences, I believe that this practice will improve classroom standard. On the one hand, students may fail to provide accurate feedback or even be tempted to abuse teacher evaluation. As high school students are still young and immature, their opinions can be subjective and tend to reflect teachers’ popularity rather than their true competence. For instance, an easy teacher is more likely to receive positive responses from his students. Furthermore, some students may turn the evaluation to their advantage. A lazy student, for example, would complain about the amount of homework even though it is entirely reasonable to his peers. However, I am more convinced that allowing students to remark upon their teachers will bring considerable benefits. Firstly, in many educational systems students are not accustomed to raising their voice even when they are being treated unfairly, so granting them the right to give feedback on their teachers may help create a just learning environment. Secondly, as students are direct recipients of knowledge, they know exactly which methods deployed by their teachers suit them best. Therefore, such input may provide teachers with invaluable information that prompts them to adjust their instruction approaches accordingly.\", \"sampled\": \"There is a noticeable trend of people becoming more interested in the history of the buildings where they live. This curiosity is partly driven by the media, which has increased public exposure to history, arts, and culture in general. A growing public understanding that the past is important has also contributed significantly to this development. One major source of inspiration comes from social media influencers on platforms such as YouTube and TikTok. These individuals often investigate the history of their own homes and share their findings, which encourages their followers to do the same. For some, the primary motivation for researching their home's history is the desire to create their own popular online content. This interest in personal history is also linked to a deeper human need for connection and identity. Learning about the past events and people associated with one's home can evoke a powerful sense of cultural belonging. It connects current residents to a longer narrative, making their living space more than just a physical structure. For those wishing to conduct this research, the internet serves as a primary and accessible tool for initial investigations. However, for more accurate and detailed information, individuals can consult the local office responsible for collecting such residential data. Given these accessible methods and motivations, the trend of researching home histories is widely expected to continue growing in the future.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In the new era, a growing issue of people becoming dangerously overweight. To solve the above problem, one school of thought holds that some people propose increasing the cost of high-calorie foods. While I accept that their perception is somewhat justifiable, I believe that individuals should also be self-conscious about their own weight status. On the one hand, the growth of fattening food prices is applied as a public policy in each country that could be beneficial to their society as a whole. This is because payable cost fluctuations affect people's consumption. Consumers tend to choose products based on their budget, so an increase in the payable cost of a product contributes to a decrease in consumer purchasing. However, fatty foods are mostly essential commodities, even if they increase, they are still affordable for families. For instance, according to a survey conducted by the Department of Consumer Protection, KFC fried chicken stores in Vietnam have increased their prices five times in a recent decade. Especially, its sales have not decreased, on the contrary, it has increased eight times since 2014. As a result, it is difficult to say that it helps to limit obesity in the community. On the other hand, there are a host of compelling reasons as to why I am convinced that individuals should also be responsible for their weight status. First, each person must raise their awareness of products that they will buy and use.\", \"sampled\": \"It is my firm belief that teenagers should be required to perform some form of unpaid work for their local community. This type of social work is a constructive use of their time and can prevent them from engaging in negative behaviours like crime or drug use that may arise from boredom. I am convinced that there are no disadvantages associated with having young people contribute to society in this manner. One of the primary benefits is the significant impact on the character development of adolescents. For instance, a teenager who participates in cleaning a local road is likely to develop a greater appreciation for cleanliness and hygiene. Furthermore, this kind of unpaid work introduces them to a working environment, which provides valuable experience and prepares them for their future jobs and careers. There are several ways to encourage young people to take part in community service. The government could offer incentives, such as providing extra credits that can be used for university applications, which would make participation more attractive. In addition, teachers and parents have a crucial role to play in this process. They should actively create awareness among teenagers about the valuable experiences that can be gained through these activities. In conclusion, having teenagers engage in unpaid social work offers numerous advantages for both the individuals and society as a whole. Tasks such as cleaning public spaces or raising awareness about important social issues can be highly beneficial. I hope that the government will consider implementing these ideas for the betterment of the entire community.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Nowadays the power of authority to change the way individuals live has been controversial. Some groups manifest that governments with politics are the ones who should change the quality of life of their people while there is another group who believe they should choose the way themselves. In this essay, I will dive into those 2 statements giving some approaches to the responsibility of both groups. First of all, governments all over the world shape the way people live. Each country has a different socioeconomic condition, location, population, etc. That makes every country different and unique. For the same reason, every government has to put in different efforts in order to achieve its goals in terms of its economy, population, specific conditions like the geography of the territories, infrastructure, education,armed conflict, etc. For instance, in Africa’s countries where the conditions are not good enough, Governments sometimes do not have a big budget to supply all those needs. On the other hand, people can make their own choices in society. It is well known that sometimes there is a moment where the environment is not good to develop or to horn up our skills as human beings or even though professional skills. For example, in education there’s no limit to achieving any goals, we set on our path. To sum up, everything is about perspective and which side you are on.\", \"sampled\": \"The increasing presence of robots in society has sparked a significant debate about their future role. While some people are concerned about the negative consequences of automation, others believe that robots will bring considerable benefits to humanity. This essay will examine both sides of the argument, ultimately arguing that the positive aspects of robots will outweigh their potential drawbacks. One of the primary concerns is the economic impact of robots replacing human workers, which could lead to higher unemployment. This job displacement might create serious secondary problems for society, including increased poverty and crime. Another significant worry is that people will become overly dependent on robots for daily tasks, leading to a decline in essential human skills like cooking and driving. On the other hand, there are many positive applications for robotics that could greatly benefit society. Robots can operate in environments that are dangerous or inaccessible to humans, such as outer space or for internal health monitoring inside the human body. They can also provide valuable assistance to elderly and retired individuals, helping them with daily activities like cleaning and cooking to improve their quality of life. In my opinion, the advantages offered by robots are more significant than their potential disadvantages. Robots can perform jobs quickly and efficiently without getting tired or distracted, and they are capable of working twenty-four hours a day with only minimal maintenance. While there are valid concerns about job displacement and dependency, the overall benefits in fields like healthcare, exploration, and personal assistance suggest a positive future with this technology.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"There is an argument that it is useless if there is an international news subject included in the academic schedule of high school children. In my opinion, I believe that the international update is helpful to broaden children’s perspectives, which should be assigned to the school schedule. It cannot be denied that world situations occurring currently influence the population in several aspects, such as politics, economics, and even the environment etc. Thus, as being mankind in this world should adopt a personal perspective by keeping updated on any global situation, no matter what type of information. For this reason, high school pupils should also be prioritized and concerned about their visions. The foreign news subject should necessarily be designated into the secondary high school program, which would enhance their contemporary knowledge in order to comprehensive awareness. At the same time, the institute principal should provide the relevant experts to shape the conceptual thinking of those students, otherwise, it might possibly be applied in case of misleading way. For instance, the violence of political protest in another region might be recognized by academic youth, then their understanding of political solutions might just only have chaos acceleration.\", \"sampled\": \"It is often argued that teenagers should perform unpaid community service during their free time. This essay agrees with the proposition that such activities are beneficial for both the youth and society. The benefits include personal development for the teenagers and positive contributions to their local communities. Engaging in community work is a valuable way to prevent idleness among young people. Teenagers have significant energy which, if not channeled constructively, could result in destructive behaviour. Through involvement in community activities, they can also acquire a range of essential life skills that are useful for their future. However, a significant drawback is that teenagers might lose motivation if their work is not compensated financially. In my own country, a community group experienced a high attrition rate among its teenage volunteers. The primary reason for this was a lack of motivation because the work was unpaid. In conclusion, while the lack of payment can be a challenge, the advantages of unpaid service are substantial. Some countries, like Kenya, have established National Youth Service programs for those who have completed their secondary education. Showing appreciation for completed work is a powerful way to encourage future effort and sustain involvement.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A polluted habitat is one of the biggest problems facing our human species. Some people think that increasing the price of fuel used in four-wheelers and other means of transit may be the most effective way to combat the growing pollution. I agree with this but still, I think there are other ways to deal with the rising pollution. To begin with, the increase in fuel prices will certainly have an impact on the usage of automobile and other means of transportation. The purpose of means of transportation, such as cars, has become common for us, as harmful gases such as carbon dioxide and nitrogen are eroding in this pure habitat, a gift from nature. Increasing the price of oil used in them will definitely reduce it to some extent. Furthermore, it will also benefit the authority exchequers that will apparently benefit the development of the poor people and organisations. In addition, if the ministry cooperates in increasing the handling of other energy sources such as solar and wind efficiency, the issue of a polluted climate can be overcome. The authority should motivate the people to make maximum usage of this kind of power.\", \"sampled\": \"After finishing school, teenagers face the significant decision of whether to get a job or continue their education. Some young people choose to start work immediately to earn money and gain independence sooner. However, I believe it is much better for students to attend college or university for their future prospects. Attending university provides access to a wider range of professions and improved employment opportunities. Many careers, such as becoming a doctor, teacher, or lawyer, specifically require a relevant degree from a higher education institution. University graduates often have more and better job options available to them and tend to earn higher salaries than those without degrees. The modern job market is becoming increasingly competitive for all applicants. It is not uncommon for a single job opening to attract hundreds of applicants, making it difficult to stand out. Individuals who do not possess university qualifications may struggle to compete effectively against those who have completed tertiary studies. While gaining early work experience has some benefits, continuing studies beyond school is more likely to lead to a successful career. The specialised knowledge and qualifications gained from a degree are essential in a competitive employment landscape. Therefore, pursuing a university education is the more advantageous path for long-term professional success.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"News helps us to be updated about things happening around the world. However, some highlight that media companies prefer to show what is profitable to them, therefore news is just another way to kill our time unnecessarily and there is no direct relevance to people`s lives. In my perspective, I strongly agree with the statement and the reasons for this will be discussed in the forthcoming paragraphs. Firstly, there is no difference between a manufacturing company and a media company when it comes to their ultimate goal which is profits. Therefore most media companies prefer to show news which has little or no connection with people`s lives. For example, suicides and political dramas. Sadly, most people are interested to know such news simply to gossip with another or to kill their time, despite such news won`t bring any good for them. Secondly, media companies are reluctant to show good things happening around the world because they feel such news will make their customers bored. Therefore they prefer not to show them even if such news could help people to improve themselves. A recent survey done at Melbourne University found that 80% of the news is either sad or depressing which clearly indicates that reading articles or watching programs is just a waste of time.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that encouraging high school students to comment on their teachers is a way to improve educational quality. An opposing view is that this criticism could lead to a loss of respect and discipline within the classroom. In my opinion, while there are risks, feedback from students can be beneficial for the educational system if it is managed correctly. Proponents of student feedback argue that a generation gap can make older teaching methods less effective for new students. Society is also facing increased competition in many fields, which necessitates corresponding changes and improvements in education. Students are in a unique position to communicate directly with authorities about how new improvements could be implemented in their schools. On the other hand, some argue that high school students do not have the ability to properly evaluate their teachers' performance. If students are allowed to openly criticize instructors, it may undermine the teacher's authority and lead to a general loss of discipline. Furthermore, some students might become possessive of their favorite teachers, creating tension among the student body and with other staff. I believe that student criticism, when kept within certain limits, is ultimately a positive force for the educational system. This feedback should be provided in a limited and secret manner to avoid the potential problems of public criticism. It is also essential that the importance of teacher respect and overall school discipline is not ignored in this process.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"While some folks argue that children are inspired to give comments and criticism on teachers to increase quality towards education, other believe that it decrease their respect and discipline in the class. In my opinion, I consider that students can loss their respect and discipline towards teacher, if they are commenting and criticizing to their teachers. On the one hand, some people think that to increase educational performance students are uplifted to make comments and criticism on their students. To commence with, students who are new in the school and want to learn something new then they are started to comment and to criticise the teacher. For example, in computer programming class students make comments to their teacher about learning new ideas after the class. Another reason is that teacher can not correct the students what they do wrong. As a result, children are starting criticising to their teacher to learn their mistakes. On the other hand, it is often believed that making comments and criticism can lead to lose their esteem and discipline and I agree. First reason is that children, who are behaving badly towards teachers, are hated by everyone from the school. Therefore, those children stay alone in classroom and this lead to them to think bad things.\", \"sampled\": \"It is my firm belief that when teenagers perform unpaid community work, the experience provides significant advantages for both the young individuals and the wider community. Engaging in volunteering can greatly enhance a young person's personal development by fostering crucial life skills. These social activities can also stimulate teenagers' empathy and cultivate a stronger sense of social responsibility. One clear benefit for adolescents is the development of their character and understanding of others. For instance, some secondary school students choose to volunteer at retirement homes during their summer break. This type of experience helps them to better understand and assist the elderly, which in turn can increase their capacity for empathy. Volunteer work undertaken by young people can also directly support important local environmental programs. In some Indonesian island tourism destinations, youth communities have taken an active role in conservation efforts. These groups organize a “Beach Clean Project” for every Earth Day celebration, showing a commitment to their local area. This initiative is highly beneficial for the community as it helps locals protect the environment of these popular tourist spots. By participating, the youth contribute to keeping the beaches clean, which preserves the natural beauty and ecological health of their neighborhoods. Therefore, such programs demonstrate a clear positive impact on both the participants and their local environment.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"There is no scepticism that corporate openly organize sports events to promote their products in public. Certain individuals think it has a negative effect on play. I disagree with this statement and I will discuss it in upcoming paragraphs. Corporate has been beneficial for play in anyways. The primary advantage is that \\\"children know about traditional games. To illustrate, in recent times, most teenagers spend their free time to plays video games Big companies help them to know about conventional games. For,instance in the previous year in Canada NMT tyre company organizes a big kabaddi cup to influence the new generation. Thus, the new generation connects with old games Furthermore, another benefit is that multiple corporations give an opportunity to new talent. To elaborate , nowadays people have a lot of talent but they did not find any better way to kick start their carrier. So, multiple corporations give a chance to show their talents. For example, IPL is very famous in the world. It is known as an Indian premier league. In this ,league lots of large companies give a chance to new cricketers. As a result of this, many freshers find a better way to start their carrier. However, It is also has a negative impact on sports because some agencies sell drug-related products. Such as pan masala and alcoholic drinks affect badly on human life.\", \"sampled\": \"There are differing opinions on whether the primary function of museums is to educate or to entertain the public. One viewpoint strongly suggests that a museum's main purpose is to serve an educational role for people. I believe that museums should aim to provide both educational content and enjoyable recreational experiences for their visitors. On one hand, the educational value of museums is undeniable as they are institutions that preserve historical records and old objects. To effectively convey information, some museums employ professional guides or provide headsets for visitors to use during their visit. For example, some schools arrange museum tours as extracurricular activities for students, who may then be required to write a report on their experience. On the other hand, an alternative viewpoint is that museums are primarily places for entertainment and important tourist attractions. To attract a higher number of visitors, many museums now use advanced technology to create more vivid and engaging exhibitions. These institutions also frequently organize games and other interactive activities that are related to their displays to make the experience more enjoyable. In conclusion, while museums hold a significant responsibility for education, their role as a source of entertainment should not be overlooked. The most successful museums are those that can make learning a fun and interactive process for everyone. Therefore, they should continue to develop ways to be both important educational resources and appealing recreational destinations.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The environmental problem caused by human activities, especially the usage of vehicles, is getting worse. It has been argued that the effective way to diminish this issue is raising the price of fossil fuels, which are widely used by most of the transportations. This essay agrees with that statement. Firstly, it can suppress the tendency of people transporting with their own cars. Secondly, it can encourage citizens to use public transports that are cheaper than their own vehicles. The main reason is that it can reduce the number of people using their private vehicles. For example, most of the millennial people prefer to use cars rather than walking to go nearby. If this occurrence happens across the world, it can contribute to more than 50% air pollutants that are stored in the environment and it might worse our world conditions. Therefore, increasing the tariff of this non-renewable source can be the practical way to lessen its effects. Another supporting fact is that it can encourage people to pick public transportations to travel. Expensive fuels may impose all kinds of automobiles. However, we can make it easier and lighter by sharing this extravagant cost together through the usage of public transportations, such as bus, tram, and train. Thus, we can still travel with cheaper tariff and lowering the impact of non-renewable sources.\", \"sampled\": \"There is an ongoing debate regarding the primary benefit that arises from cooperation between different countries. One prominent viewpoint suggests that the main advantage of international collaboration is the protection of the environment. Another perspective holds that the primary benefit is actually related to business opportunities and financial gains for the nations involved. On the one hand, cooperation can be a powerful tool for addressing environmental challenges by raising public awareness of these problems. For example, the environmental organisation WWF involves more than half of the world's countries in its efforts. Through such partnerships, participating nations may contribute funds towards important activities like planting trees or helping to protect wildlife. On the other hand, a compelling argument can be made that the main purpose of international cooperation is for business and finance. Cooperating countries are able to share significant financial burdens with one another, which can be crucial during times of crisis. For instance, in 2021, the UK provided India with a large number of oxygen ventilators to assist during the COVID-19 pandemic. In my view, while environmental benefits are important, the primary drivers are often economic and strategic. International cooperation allows a country to expand its social circle and subsequently gain more influence on the global stage. Therefore, the sharing of financial responsibilities and the pursuit of greater influence represent the most significant advantages of countries working together.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Society experiences a trend that the non-stop growing process of manufacturing items for users has an adverse influence on the ecosystem. This essay will discuss the main cause and a couple of solutions to tackle the issue. On the one hand, the increase of consumer products has a detrimental impact on the living habitat in two ways: the habit of citizens and the chemical by-goods from manufacturing process. It is easy to catch the moment that many tourists acquiring little education about environmental protection leave their own trash on roads and beaches. Not only people from tourism but also local inhabitants that are not aware of collecting those garbage. Second, most factories are utilizing unfriendly environmentally materials such as fossil fuels which may release hazardous emissions. For instance, the toxic smoke released into the atmosphere together with pollutants pumped directly to the sea can result in air and water pollution. However, the ways to handle this situation are varied. It is nothing but the duty of the government in protecting the ecology by setting obligatory. For example, the authorities are able to impose stricter punishment on companies that go to great lengths to produce things from bad materials such as nylon. Furthermore, local residents should also establish some environmental protection groups to launch campaigns like gathering trash on a weekly basis and apply cutting-edge technologies to have proper packages for each product.\", \"sampled\": \"Some argue that the younger generation should be legally required to support their older family members. I partially agree with this viewpoint, as while families have a moral duty, the state must also play a significant role. This essay will explore both the familial obligation and the necessity of government assistance in caring for the elderly. There is a strong case for family members taking responsibility for their elders. The younger generation has a duty to care for the people who nurtured them when they were children. This can be seen as a reciprocal action for the care they received throughout their childhood. For instance, most citizens in Bangladesh look after their parents in their later years, viewing it as a fundamental responsibility. However, placing the entire burden on families presents significant challenges, particularly financial ones. It can be economically difficult for the younger generation to provide adequate support on their own. Poor families, especially, may be unable to offer proper care to their elderly members if they are experiencing a financial crisis. Therefore, the government should also provide financial support to its senior citizens. Ultimately, caring for the older generation should be a shared responsibility between the family and the state. Governments can implement policies to assist their elderly population, which eases the pressure on individual households. The government in Japan, for example, supports its elderly with money and provides free medical treatment. This model shows how state intervention can work alongside family care to ensure the well-being of senior citizens.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In this day and age, some kids are getting used to being involved in various types of sports from their childhood and consider it as a main direction whilst other ones prefer doing sports activities when they have some spare time. I personally opine that this establishment is beneficial since athlete teaches children not only discipline but also aim to achieve high points. It is an obvious fact that sport has a range of advantages and can alter people's lives inside out. If the pastimes are familiar to a person from an early age, they undoubtedly it has a considerable impact on the following lifestyle due to habit factor. Even though in our modern society humanity has lots of harmful habits, it is in all likelihood that sporty one reduces smoking or drinking alcohol. What is more, the sports industry affords toddlers always to compete with their peers, which forces them to develop plenty of skills, such as the strength of will, controlling emotions and a sense of aiming to be a winner. All these lead to frequent occasions when youngsters eventually become champions of their region which is also essential in order to build a strong reputation and affect the growth of the country.\", \"sampled\": \"Studying at university is widely perceived as a crucial pathway to achieving success. The New York Times highlights the prevailing belief that university education opens doors to numerous opportunities for gaining knowledge, diverse experiences, and valuable networking connections. This viewpoint is reinforced by the fact that in the US, around 70% of students who pursue higher education have better job prospects. Conversely, there is a contrasting perspective that advocates for starting work immediately after completing school. Proponents of this approach argue that early entry into the workforce can be advantageous. Some individuals believe that bypassing university and diving into work can lead to effective career development and quicker independence. Universities are recognized for offering not just academic learning but also enhanced facilities and a conducive educational environment. On the other hand, colleges are acknowledged for providing specialized resources and personalized attention that contribute to a well-rounded educational experience. This emphasis on quality education facilities supports the view that universities and colleges play significant roles in shaping individuals' futures. Ultimately, the decision between pursuing higher education and entering the workforce straight out of school hinges on personal choice and passion. The New York Times underscores the importance of individual preferences and interests in making career decisions. Despite the various paths to success, the author's standpoint aligns with the belief that studying at university remains a key factor in achieving one's career goals and aspirations.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, traffic conditions in many big cities have become worse and worse, and this problem has to be solved. In my opinion, the main cause of this problem stems from an influx of people from the urban area into the city. The measure to reverse this trend relies on the development of infrastructure. Firstly, an excess of immigrants from rural areas is the reason to blame for a crowded city. These people move to downtown in order to seek more job opportunities or better works. While it is true that a job seeker can find jobs in rural areas, these jobs are usually agriculture-related, which are with lower pay compared to those of high-tech or commercial industries in the city centre. For example, according to the latest research conducted by the Ministry of Labor in Taiwan, per capita monthly income in urban areas is around 43,332 NTD, whereas in rural regions this number decreases to two-thirds, roughly standing at 29,000 NTD. In light of this fact, therefore, individuals move in.\", \"sampled\": \"Some individuals support government expenditure on the arts as a beneficial initiative. Conversely, others contend that governmental funds should be primarily directed towards enhancing health and education services. Investing in artistic endeavors has the potential to increase tourism, thereby contributing to economic development. On the other hand, improving healthcare and educational systems can significantly enhance the overall quality of life for citizens. Enhancing health facilities has been shown to extend life expectancy, thereby directly impacting the well-being of the population. Similarly, a robust education system plays a pivotal role in preparing the youth for future success and societal contribution. It is crucial to recognize that the government's objective is to ensure that citizens have access to fundamental rights and essential amenities. Quality education plays a vital role in fostering self-improvement among students, with studies indicating a 15% increase in personal development through access to high-quality educational resources. Ultimately, the allocation of government resources between the arts, health, and education sectors should be carefully considered to maximize benefits for both current citizens and future generations.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recently, there have been arguments that to go to single-sex schools bring more demerits than go to co-educational schools which is in the parents hands to decide which choose in several countries. I partially agree with this statements because of two reasons. Firstly, there are so many reasons why the parents deicide to go to single-sex schools for children such as, the child who has some disadvantages when relate with girls due to traumatized or something. In this case, it is difficult to go to a co-educational school by the child. Moreover, children are possible to consantration more about study because there are no relationships with other genders. From this, a lot kinds of reasons there thus, established the type of schools now. Secoundly, for getting experiences of co-educational will mcuh affects for became an adult. For instance, generaly, when we became an adult, we join company to do work at that time. It will increase some oppotunities to relate with woman, commonly. In this case, the person who get used to relating with woman is better advantages for working for negosiations and, to keep better relationships among a company. For this reason, there are the merits as well to go to co-educational schools. In conclusions, some people believe that in terms of going to single-sex schools bring some disadvantages than go to co-educational schools though, it is possiblle to deicide by parents. In addition, it is depeds on the child situations but, there are so many advantages to go to co-educational schools as I said.\", \"sampled\": \"Social media platforms play a crucial role in disseminating news events, with the advantages often outweighing the drawbacks. Despite this, the prevalence of fake news on platforms like Facebook and WeChat poses a significant challenge to the credibility of information shared. Fake reports, lacking proper supervision, can have detrimental effects on society, influencing opinions and causing harm due to misinformation. For instance, entertainment sections on social media frequently propagate false stories that damage individuals' reputations, highlighting a critical limitation of these platforms. Moreover, social media serves as a vital tool for individuals to stay connected with the world around them, offering an effective means of communication and information sharing. Platforms like WeChat go a step further by providing official accounts for public services, allowing seamless access to a variety of resources through an omni-channel approach. This demonstrates the potential of social media to facilitate convenient and widespread service delivery to the masses, enhancing accessibility and connectivity. Furthermore, the ability of social media users to express their ideas and opinions contributes to the promotion of democracy. By providing a platform for diverse voices to be heard, social media platforms empower individuals to participate in public discourse and shape societal discussions. This open exchange of ideas not only fosters a sense of community but also strengthens democratic values by encouraging active civic engagement and participation. In conclusion, while social media platforms offer numerous benefits such as connectivity and information dissemination, the issue of fake news remains a significant challenge that needs to be addressed.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In this day and age, sports have become the topic of interest of the majority of the population. As a result, more and more companies are utilizing sports events as a way to advertise their commodities. Many people claim that this trend could trigger adverse issues in sports. From my perspective, I totally disagree with this statement and lean towards the idea that commercials are beneficial for people. To commence with, it is undeniable that promotions in sports events bring numerous advantages to either the company or athletes. Through advertising campaigns, a plethora of sports will be popularized as people have the opportunities to acquire knowledge about them. Not only does this help big firms to gain more profits but, it also facilitates the lives of athletes and supports them financially. Additionally, the advertisement that involves the appearance of well-known sports stars makes a great contribution to the progress of raising public awareness about sports and exercising, which could improve communal health, given that people will participate in more physical activities. Another benefit that advertisements during sports matches bring about is that sports teams can receive funding from the sponsors, which are essential for their daily practices and rehearsals. With this economic aid, members of sports teams will have optimal facilities and trainers or coaches to guide them, which creates the chances for them to develop their career paths and nurture their potential talents. With the factors mentioned above, there is a high possibility that sports legends will be born.\", \"sampled\": \"Human activities have had a profound impact on the environment, with modern industrial plants being major contributors to the destruction of flora and fauna. Concerns have been raised about the irreversible damage being caused to the environment due to these activities. Environmentalists have taken proactive measures to address these issues, recognizing the urgent need for action to improve the situation. Throughout history, humans have been altering the environment to meet their needs, a practice that has intensified significantly during the industrial age. Despite protests from various groups, businessmen have continued to exploit natural resources for economic gain, leading to permanent damage in many cases. However, there have been positive developments, such as bans on activities like whaling and the implementation of forestry programs aimed at sustainable resource management. The impact of human actions on the environment has been devastating, resulting in the extinction of many plant and animal species. Efforts by environmentalists, including organizations like Chipko Andolan and Greenpeace, as well as individuals like Greta Thunberg, have been crucial in raising awareness and advocating for the protection of flora and fauna. Their intrepid fight to save these species offers hope for the future of the environment. It is evident that the preservation of plant and animal species is essential for the well-being of our planet. Many harmless creatures have already perished due to human activities, underscoring the urgent need for effective action to prevent further losses. The collective efforts to conserve the environment, led by dedicated environmentalists and organizations, are vital for ensuring a sustainable future for all living beings on Earth.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recently, more and more people consider teaching children at home. Therefore, the debate on whether studying at home can benefit children has been heated. As far as I am concerned, pupils can learn some important social skills in school than at home. In this essay, I will outline the pros and cons and illustrate my own opinion. To begin with, parents can control the information received by children through homeschool. As children may not have comprehensive moral values, they may listen to or follow their peers' behaviours in order to gain approval in school. For example, students may become violent to avoid becoming victims. Therefore, Parents can stop the bad behaviours and teach them the right moral value if studying at home which can create a good character for pupils. On the other hand, studying at school can boost the ability for interpersonal communication. As school gathers lots of students of similar age, children can learn to communicate with others and deal with people.\", \"sampled\": \"Competition in sports has the potential to significantly benefit teenagers' education by fostering ambition and resilience. Engaging in competitive sports helps adolescents cultivate self-assurance, physical strength, and determination. However, a critical aspect to consider is that misunderstanding the concept of competitiveness can result in excessive pressure on teenagers, leading to adverse outcomes. It is crucial to acknowledge that competitive sports also play a vital role in imparting valuable lessons on respect and teamwork to young individuals. To maximize the positive effects of competitive sports on teenagers, proper guidance and supervision are essential. By providing adolescents with the right support and instruction, the advantages of participating in competitive sports can outweigh any potential drawbacks. Despite the undeniable benefits, it is important to be mindful of the potential negative impacts such as increased aggressivity and conflicts that can arise if competitiveness is misconstrued or not managed effectively. Therefore, ensuring that teenagers receive the necessary guidance and mentorship is paramount in harnessing the educational benefits of competitive sports.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In this modern era , everyone needs money for the their survival and plays an indispensable role . After making money , organization have some social duty . I personally believe that a business should pay attention towards social responsibility. To begin with , enterprises have many responsibility regarding society and they need to fulfil it because a business operates in a society. Besides this , a business gets resources from society , thus it's duty of a organization to pay back to society. For instance , if a enterprise gets its raw material from community than a company will provide good quality product in return.\", \"sampled\": \"Environmental pollution and housing problems are major contributors to illness and disease. Technology advancements are a key factor leading to increased environmental pollution. Heavy traffic congestion is a direct consequence of extensive vehicle usage. Asthma and hearing problems are health issues directly linked to pollution. Forests are suffering from destruction by human activities, significantly impacting the natural environment. Government regulations play a crucial role in preventing pollution and addressing housing issues. Moreover, taxes can be effectively utilized as a tool to discourage environmental pollution. It is evident that proactive government policies can help mitigate the negative impacts of pollution and housing problems on public health and the environment.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Teenagers must study but they also have free time they can spend for the benefit of the society. Some people say that unpaid community work is a good choice for pupils to test their skills and to help others. I suppose that this kind of teen labour shall be strictly regulated for it not to harm anyone. This essay will discuss both advantages and disadvantages of community work for minors. On the one hand, community work can teach responsibility and empathy. For example, school students may take care of ill pets in the shelter. The animal shelter experience is useful to understand the needs of other creatures without words and to become patient and kind. Moreover, such projects as pet adoption centres often survive without the financial support of a state so it is good for them to involve unpaid employees. Hence volunteering is able to make the world better. On the other hand, the main purpose of teenagers is to study. That is why many legislations encompass rules which restrict the labour of underage workers, namely the prohibition to work more than 6 hours for persons between 14 and 16.\", \"sampled\": \"Art education plays a significant role in school curricula, offering students a platform for self-expression and creativity. While some individuals argue that art is a frivolous pursuit, many recognize its importance in fostering child development. Parents often prioritize academic subjects like technology and business, aiming to secure their children's future success. In cities like Trivandrum, known for its technopark hosting numerous technology and financial companies, the emphasis on technical skills is evident. Despite the focus on academic subjects, arts education remains crucial for students' holistic development. It allows them to explore diverse disciplines and discover their passions. Moreover, arts in schools can serve as a means for students to relieve stress and find joy in their learning journey. By integrating art into the curriculum, schools provide students with the opportunity to choose their preferred fields of study, fostering a sense of autonomy in their educational path. Incorporating art into the school curriculum not only nurtures creativity but also aids in students' emotional well-being. Through artistic endeavors, students can express themselves freely and develop essential skills that go beyond traditional academic subjects. Encouraging arts in schools can lead to a more well-rounded educational experience, enabling students to find their unique talents and interests. Therefore, schools should recognize the value of arts education in shaping well-rounded individuals.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A highly controversial issue today relates to children being too much time on television and video games is good for them or this implies on not good influence for them . In this essay, I am going to examine this question and explain why I believe the argument of their negative effect on them is stronger. There are people who argue that spending time on electronics like computer games considerably outweighs its disadvantages. The main reason for believing this is that the television stimulates their imagination and creativity solving computer games. It is also possible to say that these little daily challenges improve their intelligence as they progress inside the game. One good example here is the increasing difficult in each phase of the Super Mario game. On the other hand, others believe that television and computer activities are passive actions for children. People often have this opinion because since the first months of life up to 10 years old, the children does need to practice physical activities like run, walk, touch new objects. A second point is that their development depends on direct interactions with people and other infants. Their interactions of touching, tasting, smelling, hearing, visioning will have a significant impact on their development while screens will mainly interact with visioning. A particularly good example here is when is compared a child who frequents kindergarten versus a child who does not. The first one usually has more abilities as they interact with others at school. In conclusion, both arguments have their merits.\", \"sampled\": \"Some argue that the extravagant and affluent lifestyles of celebrities, such as Kim Kardashian, showcased on social media, could negatively influence the younger generation. Young people are particularly susceptible to being swayed by the opulence and fashion sense exhibited by these superstars. Kim Kardashian, known for her reality TV show that highlights her lavish way of life, is a prominent example of this phenomenon. On the contrary, not all celebrities should be solely judged based on their wealth. While some like Ellen Degeneres gain fame through shows like 'The Ellen Show' and extend financial support, especially to children, others like Kim Kardashian may only portray a luxurious existence. Ellen Degeneres, renowned for her philanthropic efforts, sets an example of how celebrities can positively impact society by using their influence for charitable causes. Despite the potential negative effects, it is essential to acknowledge that certain celebrities can serve as admirable role models in today's world. Celebrities have the platform to impart valuable life lessons through their achievements and actions. By focusing on the positive contributions of celebrities like Ellen Degeneres, society can learn from their compassion and dedication to making a difference in the lives of others. In conclusion, while some celebrities may project a lifestyle centered around wealth and luxury, others like Ellen Degeneres demonstrate how fame and fortune can be utilized for the betterment of society. It is crucial to recognize the diverse roles that celebrities play and the potential they have to inspire positive change and serve as role models for the younger generation.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"It is believed that the same punishments should be equally imposed on juvenile delinquency and adult crime. In my opinion, children should be responsible for their crimes, yet according to the severity of their actions, immature criminals should be punished the same as adults or not. On the one hand, in the case of petty crimes such as robbery or fighting, I argue that young children should be punished with more lenient sentences. This is because they are too young to be fully aware of what they have done or to control their aggressive emotions. Therefore, instead of being punished in the same way as adults by being sentenced to 1-year imprisonment, youth criminals should be punished with community service. By doing so, they can compensate for their wrong acts as well as be deterred from reoffending.\", \"sampled\": \"Increasing traffic congestion is a significant issue globally. This rise is partly due to the affordability of private vehicles like cars and motorbikes, leading to their increased usage. As a result, public transport reliability has decreased, prompting more individuals to opt for private transit over buses and trains. Worker mobility plays a crucial role in encouraging personal vehicle usage over public transport. To combat this trend, government intervention is deemed necessary to address the growing traffic problems effectively. For instance, congestion charge schemes, as seen in cities like London, have proven to be effective in reducing inner-city traffic congestion. One approach to alleviate traffic congestion is through the designation of walking areas and restrictions on vehicle access in certain zones. Additionally, implementing incentive schemes to reduce personal vehicle usage during peak hours can also be beneficial in managing traffic flow and reducing congestion on urban roads.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"People have different views about whether it is the content of TV programs or the length of time spent on watching them has more significant impacts on children. While the amount of time devoted affects preschoolers, I would argue that content viewed has a more marked influence on them. On the one hand, the consequences of watching TV for long periods could be detrimental to the youngster. Firstly, the longer the children are exposed to programs broadcasted, the more likely it is for them to be addictive over time. If children are too over-reliant on TV for entertainment, they might neglect the outdoor activities, resulting in a sedentary lifestyle that is highly harmful to their physical health. Secondly, excessive time for TV watching would impair the children’s communication. For example, if children spend too much time in front of the TV screen, they are discouraged from interaction with others. Gradually, this habit would exclude them from their social lives and hinder their development of living skills as well as mental health.\", \"sampled\": \"Some individuals advocate that increasing fuel prices is the most effective approach to tackle global environmental challenges. However, the writer holds a different perspective, opposing the notion of elevating fuel costs to combat pollution. This stance is based on concerns that heightened fuel expenses could burden people financially and restrict their mobility, potentially causing hardships for the public. In contrast to the fuel-cost solution, planting trees emerges as a more viable strategy to address environmental issues. Tree plantations play a crucial role in safeguarding the environment and fighting against climate change. The act of planting trees not only contributes directly to environmental preservation but also aids in mitigating the rise in the Earth's temperature, showcasing the multifaceted benefits of tree planting campaigns. Governments and decision-makers should prioritize initiatives like tree planting campaigns over raising fuel prices as a means of addressing global environmental problems. The writer firmly believes that investing in tree planting programs holds more promise in mitigating environmental issues compared to the alternative of increasing fuel costs. By focusing on sustainable solutions like tree planting, societies can work towards long-term environmental conservation and combat the challenges posed by climate change effectively.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Talking about technology in the current situation of course would have benefits and drawbacks, moreover if it is related with the occupation scarcity due to the existence of robots or computers. Nevertheless, explanation of the advantages and the disadvantages will be explained in the essay below. Day by day technology fastly increases, this is something that people or even firms cannot avoid since nowadays they need technology such as smartphones and computers in order to keep working on their responsibility. For example, a business company needs to have computers in which it makes their employees work more comfortably in the office. Hence, by having a comfortable situation in the workplace, the company would have a better performance in catching their profit. In contrast, the drawback for having improved technology is that many people may lose opportunities to get jobs due to the existence of robots. Robots able to do automation activities such as scanning, analyzing, and even driving. Furthermore, because of these machines, some companies prefer to make a purchase in robots rather than pay a monthly salary to people. For example, toll road machines may be worked by robots because the job is just to scan payments and it means they are able to replace people. Consequently, there will be an increasing scarcity of jobs for job seekers.\", \"sampled\": \"Distance learning programs, particularly those offered online, have become increasingly popular. This trend in education is largely due to the multiple benefits that online education programs provide to students. Among these benefits, flexibility and convenience stand out as key factors that attract students to this method of learning. A significant advantage of online education is that it can be accessed from any location. This means that students are not restricted by geographical limitations and can pursue their studies wherever they are. Consequently, universities can reach out to a broader range of students, including those who may not have the means or the opportunity to move to a new location for their studies. In addition, technological advancements have significantly improved the quality of distance education. With the help of modern technology, online tools have been developed that lead to engaging learning environments. These tools facilitate interactive learning and provide an experience that can match or even surpass traditional classroom learning. Finally, there are specific platforms like Cambly that focus on providing online learning opportunities. Cambly, for instance, is an online platform where students can meet native English instructors, thereby offering a unique learning experience. In conclusion, online education, facilitated by technological advancements and platforms such as Cambly, provides immense benefits and is a significant development in the field of education.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"There is a trend where many residents are looking for the history of the residentials they live in. They are benefitting a realtor and the internet to find the past owners or events that may happen in the house. This essay will emphasize the reasons behind those trends and the ways people found out about the data. To begin with, there are many reasons that make people curious about their houses' histories. Firstly, there might be a secret accident behind it, thus, giving another perspective for the owner to look at the building. For example, if a building has ever been a place for a suicidal attempt, the aura became negative, causing discomfort for its residents. Secondly, the building's history may affect the value of its price. Secondly, besides a previous accident, the previous owner's past could also affect the estate price. The residence that was previously owned by a popular figure tends to have a higher price, even though the quality of the building is not great. In other words, both past events also owners could give a big impact on the estate value. Many resources could be the source of data to answer the residence landowner's curiosity. A property agent is the main informant since they know thoroughly about every detail of the house. Furthermore, we could also rely on the web to find the data, just type the home type and location.\", \"sampled\": \"There are differing views on the impact of human activities on nature. Some believe it's too late to address the consequences of these activities, holding a pessimistic view towards the environmental damage done. Deforestation is often highlighted as a major consequence of these human activities, symbolizing the severe detriment we have caused to our natural world. Deforestation, in particular, leads to habitat destruction, affecting animal species in various regions. Take the example of pandas in China, a species currently on the verge of extinction. The root cause of their plight is deforestation, which has led to significant habitat loss. This is a glaring example of how our actions have directly contributed to the endangerment of a species. However, there are others who believe that effective measures can be taken to address these issues. They argue that it's not too late to reverse some of the damage done by human activities. For instance, one proposed solution is to fight against illegal wood consumers, which could help control deforestation rates and protect habitats. Organizations like the World Society for the Protection of Animals (WSPA) are actively involved in this fight against unlawful logging. They embody the hope that through concerted efforts, we can still make a difference and mitigate the impact of our actions on the environment. In conclusion, while the effects of human activities like deforestation are indeed severe, there are still measures we can take to address these issues and protect our natural world.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"It is to believe by a group of individuals that museums should be used for entertainment purpose while others believing that role of museums confined to education only. In this essay,I will show a light on both views. On the one side, it can be argued that museums should be entertaining and more attractive as today’s youth is more interested in learning things with fun. Most of the teenagers get bore by just listening to educational contents about history or science. As a consequence, the audience of museum restricted to only studious individuals. To broaden the audience level they should include entertaining activities such as virtual graphical shows explaining about scientific facts, Water shows with attractive lightning and robots explaining historical facts. For instance, to attract the individual, Akshardham Temple which is in the heart of National Capital of India organizing different attractive activities on daily basis along with explaining about the history to temple. On the other side, some people argue that museums should focus on education only. The basic role of museums is to preserve the historical contents so that inhabitant can visit there and able to learn things which they cannot learn at school or college. Youth can learn about how their ancestors sacrificed their lives for their religions or country. People can also visit there to learn about the basic principles behind scientific concepts.\", \"sampled\": \"The Internet has posed a significant challenge to the traditional educational setting. With the rise of distance learning, the dynamics of education are shifting rapidly. It is predicted that by the year 2050, the traditional classroom system might no longer exist, indicating a major change in how education is imparted. University students stand to benefit the most from this shift towards distance learning. It provides them with the flexibility to learn at their own pace, without the constraints of a physical classroom. However, it is important to note that this type of learning is becoming more common, but it is not feasible for all levels of education. Elementary and high school students, for instance, may not benefit from distance learning as much as university students. The elimination of traditional classroom learning has its drawbacks, especially for younger students who require a more structured learning environment. Additionally, certain courses such as chemistry and biology require a lab setting, which is impossible to replicate in a virtual environment. In conclusion, while the Internet has revolutionised education and made distance learning more common, it is not without its limitations. The complete elimination of traditional classroom learning seems unlikely, and will probably be limited to adult education and non-interactive courses. Even as we look towards a future where education is increasingly digital, it is crucial to remember the value of traditional classroom learning.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recently, an increasing numberof the population has concluded that it is better to have children at an advanced age than in the past. This essay will suggest that this is in order to gain financial stability and career advancement. This essay will argue that the these advantages outweigh the primary disadvantage of menopause. In today's world, it is only paramount to be financially stable. This is because the best of everything comes with a price. Education, which is said to be the key to success, has over the years, become rather too expensive, and have made the majority of the people make the decision of being financially stable before raising a family. This is to enable them carter for the kids and provide them with the best there is to offer. For example, a recent survey in Lagos, Nigeria illustrates that 75% of the uneducated adults have poor background. Furthermore, most adults loose their life purpose to parenting as more women transition from working class women to housewives. Consequently, this has informed the decision of attaining a certain level in one's career before deciding to become a parent. For instance, 90% of the working class adults are better parents than the unemployed ones. Despite these advantages, some people believe that the drawback of menopause should be highly taken into consideration.\", \"sampled\": \"There is an ongoing debate about the necessity of art as a subject for students. Some individuals deem art as an unnecessary part of education, while others view it as an essential aspect. The crux of this argument lies in the contrasting perceptions of the value of art in children's education. Art, for many, is seen as a mode of expressing feelings. It is reported that approximately 80 per cent of individuals use art as a medium to convey their emotions. This underlines the potential role of art in helping students articulate their inner thoughts and feelings, which can be a crucial part of their emotional development. However, with the rise of technology, there has been a shift in focus towards technological subjects. Parents, who are the decision-makers when it comes to their children's education, often prefer their children to concentrate on technology. They believe that having a strong foundation in technology can provide better future prospects, given the increasing demand for tech-savvy professionals in various industries. Despite the growing emphasis on technology, it is worth noting that art can provide relaxation and energy to students. It can serve as a creative outlet that helps students unwind and recharge, which can contribute to their overall well-being. Therefore, a balanced approach to education, which includes both art and technology subjects, can be beneficial for students. This way, children can gain technical skills for their future careers while also enjoying the therapeutic benefits of art.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"In the fast evolving world, the launch of new products has been introduced in the market. People consume more than the usual and they tend to gain weight. This results in obesity. Obesity is the most dangerous disease that we face in today's world. For example, People become couch potatoes and consume large amount of snacks that is junk foods which contains rich fats while watching tv. Due to lack of exercises and being inactive they are gaining more weight. The solution to this problem is by increasing the price of fattening foods. I disagree with this because increasing the price of a commodity does not bring any changes to this issue. People find comfort in their fast foods while eating so how much ever the price increases they tend to buy it even more as long as their cravings are satisfied. People should be active in their day to day life. Taking up different activities like hitting the gym, cyclying, doing aerobic exercises, and also swimming will help them lose their fat. People should atleast spend an hour or two do to the activities in their daily life to stay fit and healthy. And also can change their diet plans by consuming less amount of sugar,fat, oil to reduce their cholestrol level. And switch to orgainc foods like greens and vegetables.\", \"sampled\": \"Robots, as a technological advancement, are often the subject of societal discussions. Some people believe that robots will take away human jobs due to their lower operational costs. They argue that robots require only fuel and maintenance, while humans require housing, food, clothes, and transportation. This cost-saving factor is the main reason why some fear the development of robots and the potential job losses they could bring. However, it's important to consider the benefits robots can bring to society. For instance, robots can work in dangerous environments, potentially reducing accidents. They are not susceptible to human limitations such as fatigue or injury, which makes them ideal for hazardous jobs where human lives are at risk. This is a significant advantage that robots have over humans in certain work sectors. Moreover, robots can significantly improve efficiency in industries such as shipping. They can operate 24 hours a day, which potentially reduces delivery times and costs. This continuous operation is an aspect of robot development that could lead to a better future. The ability to increase productivity and lower costs can have a profound impact on the economy. In conclusion, while there are valid concerns about job loss, the development of robots can also bring about numerous benefits. Robots could replace humans in basic work, potentially allowing people to live their own lives. As society continues to debate the pros and cons of robots, it's crucial to consider both the potential job losses and the benefits of improved efficiency and safety.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, technology is all over the world and people became addicted to social media and use it on a daily basis. However, In this essay, I will illustrate the pros and cons of social media, thus, describing my point of view and supporting it with examples as well. First of all, the certain purpose of inventing social media is to keep people connected and in touch despite the local distance, you can easily contact your friend who lives in a different country and you can share with them your news and keep them posted, which from my perspective, I believe it is a good thing because some people had to leave their hometown for specific circumstances such as job opportunity or College whatsoever, therefore, having access to communicate with the people you love is basically what we all need. However, despite these advantages being addicted to social media have a lot of consequences, for example, it will affect your social skills and not constantly practising face-to-face communication will influence your performance in socializing with people which leads to facing difficulties in connecting with others. Nevertheless, a lot of people do not use social media to contact others, but to follow specific influencers, which is all right by the way, however, not every content creator deserves you to take the time to watch his content, some of them share the most outrageous and disrespectful content not considering the consequences of their actions.\", \"sampled\": \"There is a common perception that watching television is harmful for children in every way. However, this viewpoint is not universally accepted, as some argue that television can be beneficial for children's development. These divergent perspectives generate an ongoing debate concerning the impact of television on children. A considerable number of individuals believe that excessive television watching can lead to addictive behavior in children. This addiction, in turn, may result in children becoming less engaged in productive activities, as they become more absorbed in the world of television. Furthermore, watching television for extended periods can potentially lead to eye-related diseases, further solidifying the argument that television watching can be detrimental to children's health. On the other hand, there are strong arguments supporting the positive role of television in children's lives. Television can enhance children's knowledge through informative programs, providing an additional educational resource. Moreover, watching television with family can strengthen family bonds, offering an opportunity for shared experiences and discussions. Even as a leisure activity, television can provide a break from academic stress for children, contributing to their mental wellbeing. In conclusion, despite the potential negatives associated with excessive television watching, the author believes that television can be good for children's development. When used effectively, television can be a tool for education, family bonding, and relaxation. It can even help children avoid negative peer groups by providing a safe and controlled source of entertainment. The key lies in maintaining a balance and ensuring that television does not replace other essential aspects of a child's life.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"In recent years there has been a vast increase in people who are interested in researching the history of their house. This essay will describe some causes for this and how can humans find out about this history. There are a number of reasons why people search for information about their accommodation. The main cause is that household wants to live in a peaceful place. For example, this means that they do not want to live where somebody was killed or dealt drugs. This causes uncomfortable conditions because residents cannot calm down knowing this kind of data. Another reason might be that people just simply want to investigate who lived there before or even some historical facts about this spot. If humans knew about occasions that occurred in their houses, they would open their minds and decide whether they have to move or stay in. There are a number of ways in which I think people can explore this history. First and foremost, they obviously may recognise it from past residents or homeowners. For instance, when coming to this building they used to appoint an owner or realtor who can answer their questions. Therefore, residents would know who lived there before and what did they do. A further option is that they could also find this information on the internet which will tell them about any kind of facts or events that occurred in this place or nearby it.\", \"sampled\": \"Museums have always been a subject of discussion due to their unique role in society. Some people, especially young individuals, view these establishments as places for enjoyment. On the other hand, others perceive museums as vital educational centers, designed to expand knowledge about local history, famous ancestors, and old customs. The contrasting views on the function of museums often reflect the preferences of different demographic groups. Young people, for instance, tend to lean towards enjoyment over gaining knowledge, which is why they might see museums as a source of entertainment. This perception can be attributed to the fact that teenagers often lack understanding of what a museum truly represents. The items exhibited in museums are often historical and require a certain level of comprehension that may not appeal to the younger crowd. However, the educational importance of museums cannot be understated. Visiting museums can increase awareness about one's country and its citizens, thereby fostering a sense of national pride and belonging. Parents, in particular, may find museums useful for building close relationships with their children. By sharing knowledge and exploring historical artifacts together, they can foster an environment of mutual learning and bonding. In light of these perspectives, it is suggested that museums should combine both educational and entertainment approaches. This would ensure that they cater to the preferences of various groups, including children, teenagers, and adults. Supervisors of museums would do well to consider this balanced approach, creating an environment that is both enjoyable and enlightening for all visitors.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"In recent decades, global warming has been receiving a great deal of media attention around the world due to substantial impacts on human life. Although the idea of coexistence between climate change and humans appears reasonable, this cannot be viewed as the ultimate approach for humankinds to combat climate change. There are a nunmber of reasons why the coexistence can do more harm than good. First, climate change is largely shaped by human-caused geoengineering rather than nature itself. For example, in order to produce the energy that drives the world's economy, most nations rely on carbon-rich fuels like coal, oil and gas, which directly correlates with the intensification of global warming. Second, even if citizens agree to keep the environment the way it is, corporations, especially in the manufacturing and mining industries, would not as that would affect their profits. Therefore, it can be implied that if corporations were not held accounted for their contribution to global warming as in the case of coexistence, the issue of climate change would inevitable be exacerbated. It is more economically sound to actively prevent climate change than to ignore it. In fact, the costs, either financial or non-financial, of dealing with future consequences of climate change can be overwhelming. For instance, the increase in intensity and frequency of storms can cause massive destructions and deaths in many coastal areas, which would take decades to recover.\", \"sampled\": \"The world is presently grappling with grave environmental challenges. One proposed solution to mitigate these issues is to raise the cost of fuel, with the hope that this would dissuade people from using vehicles that contribute to pollution. However, this approach may have severe economic repercussions, given that escalating fuel costs could potentially trigger an economic crisis. While it's true that increasing fuel prices could negatively affect the economy, the need for travel among people is unlikely to diminish even if gasoline becomes more expensive. Therefore, hiking fuel costs might not be the most effective solution to our environmental problems. Instead, a more promising alternative could involve intensifying research into and production of clean energy vehicles, which are more environmentally friendly. For instance, electric motorbikes and cars serve as excellent examples of clean energy vehicles. These types of vehicles have been gaining popularity in Vietnam in recent years, demonstrating that people are willing to embrace cleaner modes of transportation when they are available. Moreover, the mass production of these vehicles could make them more affordable, further encouraging their adoption. In conclusion, while raising fuel prices might seem like a quick fix to environmental problems, it is not without its drawbacks, notably the potential for economic instability. A more sustainable solution lies in promoting the use of clean energy vehicles. By doing so, we can address environmental challenges without compromising the economy, and Vietnam's experience stands as a testament to this approach's viability.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Countries around the world rely on economic growth as it plays an integral part in its growth. While few individuals have arguably put forward the statement that economic broadening is the only solution to put a halt to hunger and penury. Although, another group suggest that this growth will have a negative impact on the environment. The following essay will decipher both the viewpoints of this argument and outline my opinion. On one hand, A country's gross domestic product (GDP), since it is linked with the economic wideninig, if improved, can lead to decline in famine and poverty. Although a lot of factors contribute to this development for example, if a person gets quality education, this will align to the self development of that individual as well as contributes to the economy by creating value through productivity like manufacture goods, services, etc. As a result of this it will lead to a better standard of living for an individual and increased purchasing power which in turn saves he/she from sufferings like poverty.\", \"sampled\": \"Pollution is a grave concern today as it is a major reason for spreading diseases such as Asthma and various respiratory diseases. The industrial factories and vehicles, in particular, contribute to hazardous gas pollution. This issue is exacerbated by the increase in the number of cars on the road, which further contributes to pollution. In the context of cities like Delhi, the pollution problem is extremely severe. The air quality of Delhi is considered very poor, with an air quality index of 385, making it a breeding ground for health issues. This level of pollution, primarily caused by industrial waste and vehicle emissions, has a direct impact on the city's population, contributing to a rise in respiratory diseases. The housing shortage in cities such as Delhi is another critical issue, leading to the growth of slums. These slums lack hygiene and sanitation facilities, which can further affect people's health. The gap between the demand and supply of houses results in these poor living conditions. Thus, addressing pollution and housing problems should be top priorities for the Government, considering the detrimental effects they have on the health of citizens.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"These days, some people are assuming that institutions are used for children only purchase to be a good citizens and workers. However, Education can be a most valuable thing for every human being to become a knowledgeable person and use it in so many ways that can give themselves more beneficial than imagine. In this essay, i will discuss both sides of the view and provide my overall opinion. Generally speaking, there are two main reasons is now believing that it can only use in to be a good person and worklife. Firstly, schools are made for human, where people can taken good things. To be precise, Education gives the real meaning of life what exactly children needs to do in their upcoming times, either they take it or waste their time on wasted area.For example, by taking good morality from schools are able to know what are goods and bads, so they can make difference on that. Secondly, children are going to do hard working to get success. This is because, if children wants to live a better life then have to do as much hard work they didn't. Proof themselves as a best workers in their corporate life, so that they can chance to enhance their position and salary. On the other hand, there is a case for the idea that not only help on those areas but also children are going to fullfil their uncomplete wishes that they hadn't got in their child age. Unlike in the past, children are now getting access all of the facilities they want, so they can use their didactic knowledge with that resourses are going to build something that can produce best outcome for them and world as well.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people hold the view that hosting major sporting events such as the World Cup or the Olympic Games is a beneficial investment for countries. They argue that these occasions present an opportunity for governments to promote their culture on a global stage and enhance their international image. For instance, Katar invested hundreds of millions of dollars into the last football World Cup as a means to showcase its traditions and improve its global standing. However, others argue that the funds allocated to hosting such events could be better utilized in other areas. This opposition stems from the fact that governments use tax money to build the necessary infrastructure for hosting these events. The money spent on preparing for these competitions, it is argued, could instead be channeled into sectors that directly improve the quality of life for their citizens. There are administrations that prioritize healthcare, education, and political stability over sport events. Taking a case in point, Bolivia, a country facing challenges in healthcare, education, and politics, could potentially face severe consequences if it were to host a major sporting event. The public money spent on new stadiums and other related infrastructure could lead to political issues or even civil war. Many people, especially in less affluent countries, prioritize basic needs over such sporting events. The diversion of funds from essential services to sports could therefore spark civil unrest. In conclusion, while hosting events like the Olympic Games can bring certain benefits, particularly for wealthier nations, it's crucial to consider the potential repercussions. Rich countries might be able to leverage these events for cultural promotion and tourism, but for less affluent nations, the money could be better spent in healthcare or education. This highlights the need for governments to carefully assess their priorities before deciding to host major sporting events.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"It is believed some people that to halt ailments and probable illnesses, pollution and housing issues have more importance than other problems thus governments ought to increase their interests. I mostly agree with this suggestion because environmental pollution leads to corrupt raw materials of foods and housing problems give rise to increasing of unsheltered people who are more likely to get illnesses. Firstly, governments should tend to the affair of environmental pollution. In recent year, most people have become more vulnerable to get disease because of air pollution and soil pollution. Especially, soil pollution is proved as the most destructive factor for nature by scientists. This type of pollution causes to corrupt plants as well as kill insects. Many livings have affected negatively these situations. To illustrate that, as we the humans are at the top of the food chain, we get poisons by plants, fishes and livestock. Subsequently, these poisons lead to some diseases and illnesses and we may contaminate each other. Thus, environmental pollution takes an important place to be prevented by governments. Secondly, housing problem is considerable factor in terms of people’s health. Numerous individuals have not got any place to live in cities and this situation entails to be solved by authorities. In these circumstances, they catch cold and get many microbes which lead to variety of ailments. Hence, whether having a house or not, people may suffer from these diseases.\", \"sampled\": \"Raising the price of vehicle fuel can be an effective way to deal with some environmental problems, especially those linked to transport. Higher petroleum prices would discourage people from using fuel-dependent vehicles, so demand for petroleum products could fall and pressure on oil refineries would increase. Since oil refineries are major atmospheric polluters, this policy has a clear environmental purpose and should be taken seriously. More expensive fuel could also reduce road congestion because people would be encouraged to walk rather than rely on cars for every journey. America shows the problem clearly, since two-third of citizens have cars, and this high car ownership contributes to traffic and longer journeys. If fuel became less affordable, some unnecessary vehicle use would decline, and the air affected by vehicle fumes could become cleaner. Banning deforestation is another useful measure because environmental protection should not depend only on changing the cost of transport. Protecting countryside and plant species is preferable to clearing land for factories, housing, and power plants, even when such development may seem attractive. Plants absorb carbon dioxide and produce oxygen, so preserving them supports cleaner air in a direct and natural way. Forests and plant life should therefore be protected to help clean air that has been damaged by vehicle fumes. Brazil is a useful example, because large rainforest areas are protected there to improve air quality and preserve the environment. For these reasons, I support both higher car fuel prices and forest preservation as practical ways to create a cleaner future environment.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"It was notified that as some people buy a house, they tend to question its history, especially with antique ones. There are a few probable causes for this behaviour that would be discussed . One of the main reasons why buyers wish to know their building’s history is because of curiosity. It is human nature to wonder and thus, in this scenario, they wonder either why this house was sold and who has lived here, what were their stories. As an example, one person in New Jersey has discovered that his newly bought house used to be home to a World War II veteran, who had played a massive role in disarming the Nazi’s War Train. Some speculated that this trend was influenced by the rise of horror films, where the previous owners have died to anomalous events within the hours and that scare many. As a result, questions on the building’s history were raised. Even though many buyers want to know about the place’s past, not all knows how. It is quite common for people to directly ask the house’s agency, but often it has no information on previous owners. Another plausible approach is through the Internet. There is a chance your house witnessed a famous event, like a murder or cult activity, which would yield information, albeit after a long time.\", \"sampled\": \"Many people consider whether higher education or immediate employment after high school is the better route to career success. I support continuing to university or college, because this path can create more career opportunities and prepare people more fully for working life. Although working straight after high school has some value, it is less convincing as the main route to strong career achievement. University or college study gives students a chance to build knowledge and improve skills before they enter more demanding jobs. This matters because career success is not only about finding work, but also about being qualified for better positions. A college degree is linked to a stronger chance of getting well-paid work, so further study can be a practical investment rather than just an academic choice. Academic environments can also help students expand relationships, which may support their confidence and future career development. These relationships are formed while students are learning, improving skills, and becoming more prepared for employment after their studies. For this reason, university or college can offer more than a qualification, because it can also provide a setting where students grow in useful ways. It is true that starting work after high school can improve life skills and provide practical knowledge. Yet succeeding through immediate work may take longer, and lacking tertiary education may reduce access to high-salary jobs. Therefore, university or college study offers better chances for career achievement than working straight after high school, even though early employment can teach useful lessons.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"In today's society, there is so much value given to youthful appearance that some people become obsessed with the idea of looking younger than their actual age. I believe this trend poses some adverse effects on people's financial and mental well-being. The most apparent reason why people make tremendous efforts trying to appear young is that there is generally significant appreciation given for the youthful outlook. Youth is perceived as a symbol of beauty and freshness, which many individuals try to relate to. Therefore, as long as this attitude exists, people will always make an effort to look younger. However, there are some negative consequences of idealising a youthful appearance. People trying to relate to their younger version constantly live with worries about others' opinions. As a result, they continuously experience high-stress levels and, occasionally, depression. Moreover, apart from physical consequences, they also have to deal with a financial burden. Although cosmetic tools are inexpensive, the desire to look young rarely lets people restrict themselves to basic makeup materials. They may, for example, try out plastic surgery, which is considered to be one of the most expensive methods of skincare in contemporary medicine. Allocating vast amounts of money for such costly operations, people frequently drain their personal or family budget. In a nutshell, people nowadays are overly obsessed with the idea of looking younger.\", \"sampled\": \"I partly agree that detailed crime coverage can harm society, especially when it is shown widely through social media, TV and newspapers. Social media now plays a major role in daily life, so crime descriptions there can influence many viewers very quickly. For this reason, the media should be careful when presenting crimes, but a complete ban on crime information is not the right answer. One clear problem is that crime descriptions on social media or TV may negatively affect the community. Children are especially vulnerable to disturbing crime content, because they may not understand such information in a mature way. Parents should therefore keep children away from this kind of social media content, while the government also has responsibility for dealing with the harms it may cause. Another serious concern is that crime reporting may encourage imitation offences. People who are already inclined toward crime may be encouraged by heavy media attention, and this is often called the \\\"copycat effect\\\". This does not mean every viewer will behave badly, but it does show why very detailed descriptions can be risky for society. At the same time, I reject a full ban on crime information in the media. The media has a duty to report crimes and events in society, and censorship may weaken democratic principles. A better approach is to limit harmful detail, protect children, address the root causes of crime and use effective crime-reduction measures.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Our planet is changing everyday. Some changes are natural, but some are caused by human. Luckily, nowadays people start to think how to protect the earth. And there are some individuals who believe that the high price of fuel can help to resolve the environmental issues. In the following paragraph, I will highlight why I partially agree with this point of view, and with what I disagree. First of all, I would like to say that increasing the petrol price can solve to some extent one of the problems in the environment. I strongly agree that it can decrease the level of exhaust fumes from the vehicles because people start to look for alternative cheaper energy. Hence, electro cars could be very popular and might be less cost, therefore, they emit less harmful pollutants in the air. For example, there are some places in Japan, where most of people use electro mobiles and the level of air polution is lower than in the other cities. Also, if the fuel price is up, people can use an alternative sources of energy. For instance, solar power and wind energy can be commonly used because they are much cheaper. But on the other hand, I definitely disagree that this can solve all environmental problems. There are a lot of other human activities which influence on nature.\", \"sampled\": \"Whether leadership is an inborn quality or a skill that can be developed is a difficult question in personal and professional life. I believe leadership can be learned, because effort and commitment can help people master abilities that may not appear naturally. Although some people seem to lead easily from birth, relying only on such traits is less useful than developing leadership deliberately. One reason for this view is that many abilities improve through strong dedication, and leadership can be understood in the same way. Football ability gives a clear analogy, because learned skill and natural talent can both produce excellent performance. Christiano Ronaldo is presented as less naturally talented but highly driven, while Lionel Messi is presented as someone born with a special gift. This comparison does not show that only one source of ability matters, because both footballers are excellent despite different sources of strength. In the same way, a leader may become effective through practice and commitment, even without obvious natural leadership traits. This supports the idea that leadership should not be treated as a fixed gift possessed only by certain people. The opposing view is that some people possess leadership traits from birth, and this can sometimes help others quickly. A fire at KPMG in 1985 is used as an example, where a Janitor guided people out before firefighters arrived. Such spontaneous leadership may save time for leaders and followers, but developing leadership is still preferable to simply relying on inborn traits.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"In addition to studying core subjects, subordinate subjects also support students to be independence by having essential social skills. However, it is claimed that learning necessary skills in outside by parental guidelines is more effective than that by schools, so these schools should remove subordinate themes out the programs to concentrate on the main subjects. In my opinion, I totally agree with this idea, which depends on the effectiveness and the time-consuming in studying progress. The reasons are going to be analyzed and evaluated in this essay. First, one of the main reasons why some parents or students disagree with removing subordinate subjects is effectiveness. Also, they believe that overwhelming works and lack of ability in both of knowledge and teaching are the main reasons to learn from them. Parent and friends cannot spend the large time instructing them to be effective, and they cannot educate basically as schools. Therefore, schools are the best places to study these social skills. However, students are not be over in need to have basical knowledge of these skills. They just only understand and apply commonly when working in practice, so the abillities from all of parents and friends are suitable to inspire. Moreover, teaching social skills is time-consuming for learners to be able to work simply.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that teenagers should do unpaid work for the local community, and I support this view. Such voluntary tasks can benefit both society and individuals, so they should be required or strongly encouraged. They also give youth a practical way to become better citizens rather than only thinking about their own interests. Community service can improve productivity and quality of life because young people use their energy for useful social purposes. It may also increase prosperity and excellence, since unpaid work helps people succeed in different areas of life. A global report says more than 70% of students in developed countries prefer helping aged folk during their leisure time. Voluntary work is also valuable for personal development, not only for the local community. When teenagers help adults, aged folk, or needy people, they can learn dedication and perseverance through real tasks. A UK survey claims that youth spend almost 2 hours on local charity, helping needy people and gaining confidence from these activities. Teenagers often have free time, and it is better to use it to help needy and older people than to waste it on unimportant activities. Charity and community work can guide youth toward responsible habits while giving society useful support. For these reasons, the benefits of unpaid work for youth are too significant to ignore.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"As the 21st century continues to unfold, it is easy to face various types of advertisements from every area on a daily basis . From my point of view, I suppose the drawbacks of this development outweigh the benefits. Undeniably, advertisements still have a good side and they also bring certain profits. In the first place, they can be used as a way of recreation. For instance, in Vietnam, parents often open commercials which have catchy songs or sound to calm their kids down. Plus, Thai commercials with hilarious content are becoming more trendy on social media; accordingly, they help people let people’s hair down after a day burying their heads into work. Apart from that, advertisements can support people to approach new information effortlessly. To justify this, people are bombarded by countless advertisements such as commercials, billboards, posters, etc on a daily basis. Thus, it is much easier for individuals to focus on informative aspects in each advertisement. Nevertheless, citizens admit that the disadvantages of advertisements outweigh the advantages. One compelling fact is that most advertisements on the Internet affect users’ privacy. For example, Facebook sells personal information for advertising purposes. Additionally, commercials appear on all devices and they make users feel annoyed because they are such time-consuming things. Furthermore, nowadays, there are manifold inappropriate advertisements. To illustrate, they can mislead people who are duplicitous and then steal their money. Moreover, some advertisements can also affect kids by ruining children’s minds with inappropriate content and images.\", \"sampled\": \"Globalization is now a major worldwide trend, and it influences how countries develop, trade, and understand one another. Some people support it because international integration can help economic growth, while others are skeptical about its cultural and economic risks. In my view, its benefits outweigh its disadvantages, although countries should not ignore the possible damage to traditions and independence. One clear advantage is that global interaction allows countries to identify and use their own strengths more effectively. When nations connect with others, they can build friendly relations that support stable investment and wider prosperity, as seen in the relationship between Viet Nam and the USA. This kind of international integration can give countries more chances to develop their economies instead of depending only on internal resources. Globalization can also create valuable opportunities for cultural exchange, which helps people learn about the values of other nations. Through contact with different societies, people can broaden their knowledge and become more open to ways of life beyond their own country. This benefit is important because understanding other cultures can make international relations friendlier and can support cooperation between countries. There are still serious concerns, especially because international contact may threaten national traditions. Some young people in Eastern countries are described as preferring Western culture over their own national heritage, and this rejection of traditions may reduce patriotism. Developing countries may also face problems if they rely too much on investment from developed countries, but I still believe globalization brings greater advantages when nations protect their identity and use international links carefully.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"In recent decades, due to industrialization and colonization at their peak, it had destroyed the inhabitant of nature and wildlife. Many believe, we already had destroyed nature and nothing can be fixed now. While others believe that, all individual's small efforts can make a huge difference,however both views have pros and cons. From a personal perspective, I believe we still have time left and we can still heal mother earth and the animals. On the one hand, The opinions of a person are justifiable. In the race of development, humans had vanished forests and snatched the homes of innocent creatures living there, to make our own houses. A lot of rainforests are converted into metro cities. All the Oceans are filled with plastics and marine life is also affected by this. In Addition, polluted water is released into all types of water bodies, killing aquatic life. We had killed a lot of animals. Because of pollution, even the temperature of the world is rising. Therefore, the ice in the north pole is melting. It's tough to fix now. On the other hand, If all the countries come together and partway their differences to fight against the abuse of green life and wildlife. It will make a massive change. As a result, mother earth can be restored quickly.\", \"sampled\": \"Many plant and animal species have been damaged by human activities, and people disagree about whether this problem can still be addressed. Some believe action is no longer useful because certain species have already vanished from ecosystems. I partly disagree with this view because, although past damage may remain, further harm caused by humans can be reduced. One reason for the pessimistic view is that species loss has already happened, so some results cannot be reversed. Deforestation is often seen as a major cause, and studies in the USA in 2013 claimed that species disappeared from ecosystems because of it. Supporters of inaction may also argue that strong protection of species could reduce modern comforts and services. Even so, this argument should not lead humans to ignore the remaining plant and animal species. Corrective action is needed because future environmental problems could become worse if harmful activities continue without control. The government should create rules and penalties for harming or killing animals, since this would make protection more serious and less dependent on personal choice. Practical action can also include banning plastic use because plastic can harm animals. People should plant trees at home, which can support species and improve the environment in a direct way. In conclusion, past damage to some species may not be reversible, but humans can still reduce further large-scale harm through rules and everyday protective actions.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"When buying a new house or a flat in a building, one of the initial question that comes up is: who had lived in that place. Even if you forgot asking any such questions in the begining, when planning to sell the property people frequently ask about previous history of the house and all the residents ever lived in it. This could be because of the curiosity. Whether a prominent person had ever shared the place or it could be a anxiousness driven by fear; if any mishappening had occured at the place. When purchasing or selling a house, history of all previous owners is of profound interest. Feeling like curiosity or fear drive such a question. For instance, whether a crime had ever happened at the house or in the building, or any illegal activity was performed the the residence. Also curiosity to know if a famous person had lived in that place. Such questions will impact the sale of the property and it can go either ways, based on the history of type of people lived there. To gain knowledge on such a topic, a realtor is the first best person to ask before buying the place. And later, neighbours are the one who could help you out discoverying the facts and the history of the house. If you are still not satisfied, you can approach the government body dealing in the housing management. They have all the records of all the previous owners. Or if you are living in a building, there must be a management company. Ask them any reasonable question regarding the past of that building. In conclusion, I would say feelings like fear and curiousity leads to questions on the history of the house. A realtor, neighbours and management companies can assist in attaining such a information.\", \"sampled\": \"Globalization has made cooperation among countries more necessary, because many issues now cross national borders and cannot be handled by one country alone. Some people believe this cooperation is most useful for environmental protection, especially when global warming is becoming worse. Others argue that its main value is in world business, and I agree that business gains are the stronger result, although environmental rules remain important. Environmental protection clearly needs countries to work together, because damage to the environment is not limited to the place where it begins. Governments have tried to educate people about basic ways to protect the environment, but public education alone cannot solve a global problem. The Kyoto agreement, signed in the context of the united nation charter, is an example of countries acting together on environmental limits. This agreement shows why international cooperation matters for the environment, since it involved a restriction linked to carbon dioxide emissions from industry. The limit of 5.2 % suggests that countries can accept shared rules when global warming is becoming more serious. Even so, such cooperation mainly creates controls and duties, so its benefits may be slower and less visible to ordinary people. World business, on the other hand, has grown strongly because communication between countries has become easier. Companies increasingly open stores in different countries, and people can buy foreign brands such as rebook and Nike in their own domestic markets. Videocon is also given as an Indian brand that has entered international markets, which shows that cooperation in trade helps companies move beyond national boundaries. For this reason, international cooperation seems to bring its main benefit through global business, since it allows brands, stores, and markets to connect more widely. Environmental cooperation is still necessary, especially because global warming is worsening and rules on industry need attention.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"When considering what to be taught in secondary school, people are still divided about whether international news should be one of the official subjects or not. While there are benefits to adding this new channel into the classroom, there are also good reasons why it might be helpful to only focus on current curricula. The statement that international news should be studied at secondary school is strong for several reasons. In terms of social view, it would help students to gain a wider perspective of the world. In this way, youngsters can be widely acknowledged with current affairs so that they could be aware of how to behave properly. Moreover, in the education view, international news accompanied by different academic subjects such as science, literature, mathematics would complete the integrated learning, which provides students with a full development to lead their life in the future. It is, therefore, clear that global updating plays a vital role in assisting education On the other hand, it is also understandable that including international news as a subject in secondary school curricula is definitely irrelevant. First, students are now snowed with a hectic schedule, which may result in overloading if any additions. Moreover, in the current education system, especially examination, it places a great emphasis on academic subjects, if students don’t concentrate enough on these qualifications, they might fail.\", \"sampled\": \"Climate change is often discussed as a problem people should learn to live with, but this view is too limited. I support addressing climate change rather than merely adapting to it, because its causes and effects are already serious. Governments and individuals can help prevent or reduce climate change through practical policies and everyday choices. Industrial growth and new factories have increased carbon emissions over the past few decades, and transportation is another important contributor. These emissions do not only affect the climate, because pollution and dust are also linked with health problems such as asthma. Residents who suffer from pollution-related illness show why prevention is not just an environmental aim but also a public health need. Adaptation alone is risky because even a small sea-level rise would be difficult to manage. Flooding can cause displacement, job loss, and loss of homes, and Millions of people could be affected by such damage. Low-lying nations are especially vulnerable if they cannot protect themselves, so waiting to adapt may leave them facing problems beyond their control. Governments should pass laws that restrict carbon emissions and should also support cleaner habits among individuals. Greater use of public transport instead of private vehicles would reduce the pressure created by transportation. Investment in renewable sources such as wind and solar power, together with environmental taxes for drivers, airline companies, and other polluters, is a better response than simply accepting climate change.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Many people argue that companies should focus on their employee’s performance for achieving tasks rather than noticing the way they dress. Does an employee's dressing really matter for identifying his work quality? I do not agree with this notion . I believe that focusing on employees’ working efficiency will not only encourage positive competition among staff, but it will also help to increase the financial growth of a company. Dressing appropriately is primarily important when a firm has a staff of diverse cultures. Firstly, people from various ethnicities wear different apparels which not only represent their traditions and religions, but they will also show their attachment with their norms. Secondly, employers need to understand that there would be significant consequences if they ignore a standard dress code. For example, wearing an unethical dress such as a short skirt can be mentally challenging for a Muslim girl who is working on the same floor as it is against their cultural and religious beliefs. Similarly, executives' efforts to have acceptable clothing in the workplace will also enhance working efficiency. If employers give freedom for dresses then people might be focusing on fashion, talking about different apparel brands and shopping malls, which in result can cause significant damage to business efficiency as people will be spending too much time on these activities.\", \"sampled\": \"Home education is sometimes seen as a suitable way for parents to teach their children, especially when they want more control over learning. However, I believe it has more disadvantages than advantages because children may lose important chances to communicate outside the family. For this reason, educating children at home is not a wise choice for many learners. One major problem is that children taught at home may become more introverted because their daily contact is limited. Home learning can reduce interaction with friends, so children mainly communicate with family members instead of meeting peers more often. When this contact is limited, socializing with others can become harder, and some children may work alone and hesitate to make friends. Another disadvantage is that the home environment can create distractions, which may reduce the quality of learning. Unexpected guests, for example, can interrupt concentration when children are trying to study at home. If such distractions happen often, home learning may become ineffective because children cannot focus fully on their lessons. The main advantage of home learning is that it allows a flexible schedule, which can be convenient for children and parents. Children can ask their mother to begin lessons earlier or later, so the learning time may fit their needs better. Even so, this benefit is not strong enough to outweigh the risks of weaker social contact and ineffective study at home.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"During these years it is vital for everyone to provide themselves with accommodation and have enough budget to survive. However, students don't have access to everything they want and they may be proposed by someone to work in their spare time, in order to develop their financial condition. It has demerits and merits that should be discussed in this essay. As far as I am concerned it has more advantages than disadvantages. One of the major benefits of part-time jobs is gaining experience and proficiency while working young adults face real difficulties in jobs and learn how to solve them. Especially, when students work by their profession will be useful in their future careers or even in any field. Additionally, it is complicated for young adults to earn money for living and housing facilities, in this ,case part-time employment will help them to earn pocket money to purchase everyday expenses, become more mature and independent and rent accommodation properly. For example, being an employee in certain companies may assist them with cultivating self-discipline and managing time and money. Thus, there have been mentioned advantages of part-time employment. On the other hand, it can not be denied that mainly the university lessons require full concentration and discipline means while working some students may lose their focus and concentration. Although absenteeism affects their grades and university life, there can be some factors which lead to exceptions.\", \"sampled\": \"There is ongoing debate about whether school leavers should go to university or college rather than entering the workforce straight away. Some people believe higher education is the best path to a successful career, while others argue that starting work directly after school is a better choice. This essay will consider both views before giving my own position. Those who favour working immediately after school point out that it allows people to gain hands-on experience in their chosen field. Early employment also helps develop practical skills such as time management, leadership, and flexibility, which employers genuinely value. These qualities can be hard to develop inside a classroom, making early work experience a meaningful alternative to formal study. On the other hand, the job market has become increasingly competitive, with many applicants competing for each available position, so academic qualifications do carry real weight. Employers tend to favour candidates who hold a university degree over those with fewer qualifications. A degree is widely seen as evidence of perseverance and commitment, and graduates generally have access to better job opportunities and higher salaries as a result. A degree course typically takes three or four years to complete, which is a significant investment of time, so the right choice really depends on a person's goals. Working is arguably more beneficial for those pursuing a career in business, where practical experience is often what employers look for. University, by contrast, is more suitable for those aiming at an academic career, where formal qualifications are usually required.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"With the advent of technology, nowadays it is common to save knowledge on the Internet instead of books or notebooks which were main means in the past. In this essay, I will examine how the merits of storing knowledge on the Internet outweigh its demerits. There are several detrimental aspects of using the Internet to keep knowledge. First and foremost, it is necessary to buy a digital device which people can use such as cloud computing services. Owing to the high cost of the device, people might find it a burden to afford it. Furthermore, the usage of electronic devices can lead to detrimental effects on the user’s body. For instance, people’s eyes are more likely to be strained while they are staring at a monitor. What is worse, they can be exposed to electromagnetic waves from computers or mobiles. Despite those disadvantages mentioned above, there are significant advantages of using the Internet as a storage. Firstly, it is definitely easy to access knowledge on the web storage without any geographical constraints. Besides, people are able to find their files from their laptops and mobiles wherever they want to use them. In addition to this, the process of sharing their knowledge to others is just simple and quick. What is more, it is cost effective in a long term as people do not have to buy a lot of books, pens and even shelves to store a substantial amount of knowledge. In conclusion, even though storing knowledge on the Internet brings some adverse effects in terms of the budget to buy expensive devices and users’ health problems, there are clear virtues such as accessibility and convenience.\", \"sampled\": \"The trend of having children later in life than previous generations is a topic that draws considerable debate. Some people see it as a sign of personal freedom and better planning, while others worry about the social and physical consequences. This essay argues that although late parenthood has certain drawbacks, the reasons behind this choice deserve careful consideration before any judgment is made. One reason people delay having children is that they want to feel stable in their careers and personal lives before taking on such a major responsibility. When individuals wait until they feel genuinely ready, they may be better placed to provide a secure environment for their children. This sense of preparedness can lead to more thoughtful parenting compared to having children very young, without much life experience. On the other hand, having children later in life does present real challenges that should not be ignored. Physical health becomes more of a concern as people age, and this can affect both the parent and the child during pregnancy and early childhood. The demands of raising young children can also feel more tiring for older parents, who may simply have less energy than younger ones. Despite these concerns, the decision about when to have children is deeply personal and shaped by a wide range of social, economic, and individual factors. Society has changed significantly compared to the past, and modern family structures reflect those broader shifts in how people live and plan their lives. Recognising this complexity seems more useful than applying a single standard to when parenthood should happen.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"With the support of developed transportation, tourism has been recognized as a sign of the modern lifestyle. However, tourism has also been implicated in the destruction of indigenous cultures and the natural world. I completely agree that tourism development can absolutely be synonymous with the preservation of our world. The first reason for my belief is that the tourists these days are aware of the diversity of cultures. Most tourists these days travel mainly because they are eager to explore the incredible natural world and to learn about other exotic cultures. They usually make full use of the Internet to search for information about the destination before they arrive. This self-training will prevent them from partaking in intrusive and disrespected actions toward another culture. A further step of government authorities implementing regulations will protect the uniqueness of local cultures from suffering from assimilation. Furthermore, the headlines transferred through media have grabbed people’s attention about environmental problems, and thus, shaped people’s concept of preserving the natural world. This leads to conscious perception about global warming phenomenon, greenhouse effects and urbanization’s consequences. Eco-tourism takes root from people’s desire to experience greenness and eco-friendly surroundings. Tourists’ appreciation of nature has made them take action to protect the primitive beauty of the earth. With better guidance and organization from the tourism industry, tourists will definitely effort to establish a world free from destruction.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that teenagers should be required to spend part of their free time doing unpaid work for their local community. This essay agrees that making such service compulsory would benefit both young people and society as a whole, and the arguments in favour are strong enough to support its introduction. When teenagers give their free time to help their community, they contribute in practical ways to local development. One clear example is providing free education to people who cannot read or write, which addresses a real gap that many communities struggle to fill. This kind of involvement gives young people a meaningful role and connects them to the needs of those around them. Community service is also said to develop a sense of humanity in participants, which is a quality that benefits society well beyond any single act of help. There is also a widely held view that helping others returns benefits to the helper in greater measure, meaning teenagers themselves gain from the experience. Both the lives of young people and the condition of the community are therefore positively affected by such service. If youth community service became widespread, it would contribute to broader social development over time, as more people would grow up with a habit of civic responsibility. A society where young people regularly support their local community builds stronger connections between generations and different social groups. Mandatory unpaid work for teenagers is not a burden but a practical way to shape individuals who understand their responsibility to others.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"It is an irrefutable fact that the trend of following celebrities among youngster rapidly increasing. Some people assert that most of the famous folks are known by their style and money as compared to their achievements and this would be a bad impact on teenagers. In my opinion, I totally disagree with the statement, youngsters influence by their bad habits. In this essay, I intend to support my views before reasonable conclusion can be drawn. There are several reasons why it does not influence badly on teens. Firstly, the person who becomes a celebrity, it means they have talent and best skills that’s why they are famous. Furthermore, if they have a beautiful face, that’s the main logic because nobody has glamour by nature. Moreover, if they struggle a-lot in his past life for that he is wealthy. For an example, there are many popular people who struggle in their life, after that they have all this things such as drake and so on. On the contrary side, most of the people attract by atrocious activities such as abusing in public and abuse drugs. In addition. This type of things could bad impact on the young generation.\", \"sampled\": \"In many countries, history is seen as an important part of community life, and this idea extends to how people think about the buildings they live in. Many people are becoming curious about the history of their own homes, wanting to know the story behind the structure. Cultural beliefs and superstitions also influence how some societies view a building's past. These beliefs can affect decisions about where to place doors, furniture, stairs, and appliances, which shows how closely history and culture are connected in daily life. For example, some people arrange furniture and household items according to inherited cultural rules passed down through generations. This kind of practice suggests that knowing a home's history is not just an academic interest but is connected to everyday choices. There are several practical ways to research a building's history, and speaking with older generations is one of the most useful methods. Grandparents, for instance, often carry firsthand knowledge of past events that shaped a structure. A typhoon that once hit a town may explain why an ancestral house was built in a certain way, and only elders would know that connection. This kind of oral history preserves details that written records often cannot capture.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Being a parent is an extremely complex task as anyone would agree, a lot of crucial factor goes into consideration, and the choices a parent makes to nurture his kid ultimately defines the person they would become while growing up, attention to detail is a must and a parent should consider all the aspects of a child's growth and not just stick to either. I moderately agree with the claim as encouraging children to spend less time studying and more on doing physical activities is not only a good recreational break for a child, but rather a physical development aid as most of our body evolution takes place during childhood, and transformations can be phenomenal. Studies suggest that children who perform more physical activities have more brain advancement than children who don't perform any type of sports. According to science, any sport / exercise pumps more oxygen into the blood and triggers a neurochemical response in our brain for tolerance and growth. in conclusion, it's not only a balancing act for a kid between mental and physical activities but a major overall spike in functioning and progress.\", \"sampled\": \"Many employers rely on interviews as their main tool for selecting candidates, and this essay argues that interviews remain a reliable method for hiring. A face-to-face meeting gives employers direct access to information that written applications simply cannot provide, which is why interviews hold a central place in most hiring processes. One key advantage is that interviews allow employers to assess a candidate's personality and communication skills through direct interaction. Employers can also judge how well a candidate handles pressure by observing how they respond under questioning. Specific questions asked during an interview help employers evaluate a candidate's knowledge in a way that feels immediate and revealing. Some argue that other selection methods are better suited to measuring theoretical knowledge, and certain IT companies place particular value on this because employees must regularly communicate with clients in writing. A multi-stage process that includes a written test, a group discussion, and a final interview can address this concern by testing both knowledge and interpersonal ability at different stages.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"There are several arguments with regard to the financial support of retirements. Although there are some people who think that it is a waste of money, I believe that governments must support them . On the one hand , Some argue that paying wages to aged people who stopped working is not essential and governments should invest this money in other parts. There might be more priority in which officials should spend the national budget on them . For example, in underdeveloped countries where there are not enough schools and hospitals, paying pension might be considered a last priority such as in Africa people even do not have anything to eat or drink . In these places, so, it would be better to improve their sanitation and education systems as well as facilitating hospitals. Moreover,society should be modernised in terms of transportations to make commuting much easier for every citizen. Because, If these infrastructures are not developed or even repaired, a lot of accidents might happen. On the other hand, I agree with those who give the right to old people to receive their pensions. There are a lot of scientists and professors who dedicated most of their precious adolescence to study and improve the life quality of their fellow citizens through their innovations . If they did not do all these activities, now we would not have a prosperous society. Also, as they used to work hard and handle so much problems and pressures , they might suffer from several illnesses.\", \"sampled\": \"Whether governments should impose strict laws to ensure citizens receive nutritious food is a widely debated issue. Some people believe that food choice is a personal matter and that individuals should decide for themselves what they eat. However, this essay argues that government regulation is necessary because the health risks linked to poor food quality are too serious to ignore. Many restaurants, street vendors, and hotels rely on low-quality products, which poses a genuine danger to public health. A WHO survey found that 35% of people suffered from cancer due to the overuse of junk food, which shows how serious the problem has become. Unhealthy food is also linked to heart problems and other illnesses that place a heavy burden on society as a whole. Busy lifestyles push many people to eat outside even when they know such food is unhealthy, and the younger generation in particular has become quite addicted to junk food, often avoiding homemade meals altogether. The number of restaurants in cities has also grown significantly in recent years, reflecting the rising demand for convenient but often poor-quality food. Given this situation, there are practical steps the government could take. Imposing higher taxes on unhealthy products would discourage their consumption, while strict laws requiring food businesses to meet nutritional standards would reduce the use of low-quality ingredients by restaurants, street vendors, and hotels. Regulating the food supply in this way would help ensure that all citizens have access to a balanced diet, which is something individuals alone cannot reasonably be expected to manage on their own.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Some actions of mankind have detrimental effects on flora and fauna. It is believed that there is no time for the public to redress their mistakes while the community think that this circumstance can be effectively improved by various solutions. From my perspective, I have a strong belief that several measures can be taken to solve this dilemma. To begin with, some people have the right reasons to claim that it is an unsolvable problem and that nothing can be done to deal with it. Deforestation or climate change caused by human activities is so destructive that it is placing many species' lives at risk. As a consequence, many species of plants and animals are dying due to loss of habitat and human hunt. Some of them have already become extinct, thus negatively affecting the balance of the ecosystem. For example, in Vietnam where there used to be a large number of tigers, this animal has been hunted for years to satisfy human needs and there are only a few species left. However, I strongly argue that there are a lot of things that governments and individuals can do to deal with this issue. Firstly, the issue of deforestation needs to be addressed. It is the main cause of the disappearance of endangered species around the world. Governments can plant more trees to create places for animals and plants to live and develop their population. Moreover, strict rules should be published to deter people from cutting down trees and hunting for precious animals and plants. Another solution is to raise awareness among residents about the destructive effects of our activities on the Earth and the ecosystem.\", \"sampled\": \"People are becoming increasingly interested in the history of the houses and buildings they live in, and this reflects both personal curiosity and practical concerns about property value. Urbanisation has led to many old buildings being demolished to make way for new construction, which has made surviving historic buildings rarer and more desirable in the housing market. Buyers who are tired of standard new apartments are drawn to older properties for their charm and nostalgic appeal. The economic side of this interest is also significant, because historic buildings stand out in a competitive market and can attract greater attention from buyers. Researching a house's past can deepen an owner's emotional connection to the property, giving it meaning beyond its physical structure. Knowing that a house was once owned by a well-known person, for example, can make current owners feel a genuine sense of pride in where they live. There are practical ways to uncover a house's history, and one of the most accessible is speaking with elderly long-term residents in the neighbourhood, who often hold memories that written records cannot capture. Antiques and items found inside a house can also provide useful clues about the tastes and habits of previous owners. Details such as red wallpaper may seem minor but can reveal something distinctive about whoever lived there before. Overall, the interest in housing history is driven by a mix of curiosity about the past and recognition that history adds real value to a property. Cities are changing rapidly, and older buildings that survive carry a weight of experience that newer constructions simply lack. For many people, understanding who lived in their home before them transforms a house into something far more personal and worth preserving.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In contemporary discourse, there exists a viewpoint asserting that placing legal responsibility on younger family members to support their elders when they become physically, mentally, and financially incapacitated prioritizes immediate gratification over long-term strategic foresight. This essay aims to delve into the merits and drawbacks of this ideology, exploring its implications for familial relationships and societal dynamics. On the first side, it is important to acknowledge that assigning legal responsibility to younger family members fosters a sense of duty and interconnectedness within families. This perspective posits that, leading not only to emotional support but also financial and practical assistance, it ensures a safety net for aging family members. Moreover, if family members are legally bound to provide support, it may deter neglect or abandonment. For instance, if conditions arise where the elderly are left without adequate care, legal obligations could act as a safeguard. Therefore, understanding the multifaceted implications of legally mandated support is crucial for fostering a sense of social responsibility. However, on the flip side, proponents of meticulous consideration caution that enforcing legal obligations on younger family members can lead to detrimental consequences. For example, it might strain familial relationships and exacerbate existing tensions. This lack of foresight can make families reluctant to address issues related to aging, leading to potential conflicts and emotional distress. Likewise, when legal obligations become burdensome, family members might feel compelled to fulfill their duties reluctantly, resulting in a strained and insincere support system. In order to prevent these negative outcomes, it is imperative to carefully balance legal mandates with the complexities of familial dynamics.\", \"sampled\": \"People research the history of buildings for many different reasons, and the methods they use depend largely on what kind of information they are looking for. Social concern is one of the biggest motivations, along with a genuine interest in architecture and urban planning. A building also needs to have some cultural or historical significance before people tend to become curious about its past. The availability of historical buildings varies quite a lot from place to place. In Turkey, such buildings are described as rare, whereas in Italy they are far more common, so the opportunity and incentive to do this kind of research differs between the two countries. Some people are drawn to research not by cultural interest but by a desire to know whether a building has a spiritual or haunted past. When it comes to finding reliable information, state institutions or organizations can provide basic details such as when a building was constructed and who built it. However, for buildings with significant historical or cultural value, state sources alone may not be enough for thorough research. Libraries that hold special sections on local history are therefore a more useful resource for this kind of investigation. Informal sources also play an important role. Elderly people living in the area can offer personal accounts and memories that official records do not capture. Neighbours are sometimes the only source for claims about a building's alleged spiritual past, though such information is little more than rumour, as shown by childhood stories of a supposedly haunted house where strange electronic behaviour was later explained as a technical engineering error.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Teaching and learning process has design for creating a best child in future. There are number of ways in teaching method and one of the ways is team sports in school age. Group sports bring numerous advantages for students and to the society . It is a positive concept to build a corperate child through team sports. In modern world, there is a huge compeetition to become a best student or the most talented child in the school. Therefore, students have become very selfish and try to defeat the others without concerning their mantality or physical status. The cooperativity is going to vanish gradually. Sports is one of the best solutions to create a well behaving child with flexiblity. Furthermore, because of team sports children recognize how to keep effective interpersonal relationships with the others, Some children do not know how they need to speak with the other fellows. Students will be very cooperative when they play as a group because they need to communicate with team members. For example, most of the Europien primary education has designed according to the group theme. Therefore, it will greatly affects for the pupils' personality and cooperativity spontaneously. Nevertheless, some people believe that, children need to get ready for the competitive future since their childhood and they try to build roborts through education system whithout thinking about the cooperativity. For exampe, some school children have sport competitions. Then, these childern try to win in sports events also.\", \"sampled\": \"The rise in consumer goods production has become a serious threat to the natural environment, and much of the problem starts with the manufacturing process itself. As more goods are produced, factories release large amounts of carbon dioxide into the air, contributing to widespread air pollution. Chemical cleaning agents used in manufacturing also leak into rivers and water sources, damaging ecosystems that many species rely on. Growing consumer demand has pushed companies to introduce single-use products, mostly non-biodegradable plastics, which create a long-lasting pollution problem. Discarded plastic enters the ocean and harms marine animals that cannot avoid contact with this waste. Since plastic can remain in the environment for thousands of years, the damage being caused today will continue long after the products themselves are gone. Some businesses have already started responding by exploring alternative materials and more environmentally friendly production methods. Starbucks, for example, replaced plastic straws and cups with paper or bamboo alternatives, showing that change at a corporate level is possible. However, the high initial costs involved can prevent smaller businesses from making similar changes without outside support. This is where governments have a practical role to play, by offering tax breaks or financial incentives that encourage more sustainable production. Companies that switch to renewable energy sources, for instance, could receive subsidies to make the transition less financially difficult. If the production system is reformed through a combination of corporate initiative and government support, the environmental harm caused by consumer goods manufacturing can be significantly reduced.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Art can play a vital role in a child's overall development. Although some hold an opinion that it can be worthless and unnecessary to add art subjects in a curriculum, I believe art has direct relativity with one's creativity which can bring a plethora of advantages for the overall growth. Opponents of an art subject whilst school days have firm viewpoints that it does not bring success in career and every effort invested to get into those are waste. This is because many artists struggle to match their living needs. For instance, if a painter may have difficulties in selling his drawn painting, it eventually leads to struggle for a stable life due to lack of money. In contrast, a time utilized for learning active subjects like maths, science, accounts, and many more may come to rescue as they lead to high paying stable jobs like engineers, doctors, or chartered accountants. On the other side of the spectrum, knowledge in art subjects from early age brings myriads of benefits for overall development. A study demonstrates that a child who has interest in those subjects from an early age have more patience to deal with real-life problems. For example, it has been evident that creativity acquired from learning arts leads to problem-solving; that mostly results in thriving in a career as one has skills to solve difficulties. Moreover, it is the not only best way to release the stress and ameliorate hand-eye co-ordination, but also a perfect way to provide visualised aspect to dreams and thoughts.\", \"sampled\": \"Rising consumer goods production is widely seen as a serious threat to the natural environment, and this essay agrees that the problem is significant enough to require action from both authorities and individuals. Plastic products are manufactured in enormous quantities because of their low cost and convenience, with common examples including ready-to-drink bottles, supermarket bags, straws, and food containers. These items are difficult to dispose of safely and remain in the environment for a long time, causing lasting damage. The fast fashion industry is another major contributor, as people throw away large amounts of clothing every year without thinking about the consequences. Supply consistently exceeds real demand, which leads to wasted materials and the growth of fashion landfills around the world. This cycle of overproduction and disposal makes the fashion sector one of the clearest examples of how consumer habits cause ongoing environmental harm. Authorities should encourage citizens to choose greener products, since using eco-friendly goods is believed to benefit both the natural environment and human health. Education is also a key factor in building the awareness needed to change behaviour on a wider scale. School programs that teach children to reuse products, donate old clothes, or recycle are a practical way to develop this mindset from an early age. Teaching people to buy only what they genuinely need is another direct way to reduce the environmental damage caused by unnecessary consumption. When citizens understand the consequences of their purchasing choices, companies also face greater pressure to produce goods more responsibly. Consumer goods production therefore needs to be better controlled, and countries must cooperate to find solutions at a global level.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"No one will argue that in this time the use of social media is at its peak. The impact of the digital world on people is enormous. It has changed the way individuals communicate with each other and stay informed about the world around them. Even if social media provides abundant of advantages in the way how the news spreads with high speed and allows people to communicate with each other easily, it also has some disadvantages. This essay will explore if the benefits of using social networks outweigh the drawbacks. The first major advantage to notice is the speed at which news is published. Nowadays people can easily read all the news with the details at once on a good deal of apps and websites. This was, however, not possible before and they were forced to wait for several days. The next benefit is the opportunity to connect people from different parts of the world. A majority of social media platforms offer its users opportunities to share life updates, make video calls or text messages to their friends or colleagues, which creates a sense of togetherness. These functions are extremely useful especially now as people are more like to relocate to other countries and cities for studying or working purposes.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people argue that adapting to a changing climate is a practical way forward, but I believe prevention is the far better choice. Humans adapted to the present climate over millions of years, and that slow process shaped our biology. A rapid shift in global conditions now threatens to undo that stability and make life much harder. Prevention tackles the root causes of climate change instead of just coping with its effects. Planting trees can slow global warming because trees absorb CO2, a major greenhouse gas. High CO2 concentrations can damage human body cells and lead to cell death, so lowering these levels directly protects our health. Fossil fuel consumption increases atmospheric pollutants and temperature, and fossil fuels are considered the main driver of global warming. Reducing fossil fuel use can help prevent climate change before it forces us into desperate adjustments. People can adapt to difficult environmental conditions, but with hardship, which makes prevention a more humane path. Rising temperatures make life harder, and waiting to adapt only deepens that suffering. In forested countries, certain cancers are extremely rare, hinting that a healthier environment is possible through preventive action like tree cultivation. Protecting Earth through prevention is not just safer but also more compassionate than forcing future generations to endure avoidable hardship.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"it is unfortunate that today, juveniles find it daunting to focus on their study in school. This phenomenon has manifold responsible factors nevertheless can be mitigated with certain rigid actions. There are numerous reasons why children are not concentrate on school study. To begin with, children cannot feel fresh and active because, cause of sleep deprivation. According to new research it is happen along increasing technology they live in satured culture where everyone rely on technology. For instance, children spend more time on play video games, facebook and WhatsApp use for communication with their friends. There are also other causes of this situation. Children are not able to cope up with though academic competition then they do not take interest in their study. Moreover, they cannot study in noisy atmosphere and in school they district to activities of other learners. What is more, such children affected by medical condition such as attention deficit disorder. There are certain solution to alleviate this complication.\", \"sampled\": \"Some governments believe that raising fuel prices is an effective way to tackle environmental problems. This approach can reduce private car use and generate funds for green projects. I agree that high fuel costs can be a useful short-term measure, but other policies are also needed. Higher petrol prices can directly change how people use private cars. When fuel becomes expensive, motorists may reduce both the number and length of their trips. This change in behaviour can lead to lower emissions and help ease the greenhouse effect. Freight services might also respond to fuel taxes by shifting from trucks to rail to save costs. The extra tax revenue could be used to improve transport infrastructure and fund public awareness campaigns. These outcomes support the idea that high fuel costs can deliver immediate environmental benefits. Long-term progress, however, requires more than just fuel taxes. For example, Germany once subsidised people who switched to electric cars and reduced taxes for electric vehicle manufacturers. Developing advanced public transport and supporting renewable energy and electric vehicle research are also necessary steps for lasting achievements.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In my opinion, the government should increase the cost of fuel for vehicles to solve environmental problems such as air pollution and traffic on the roads. Nowadays, Cars become one of the important things in our day-to-day lifestyle, instead of driving two-wheelers people prefer to drive four-wheelers for nearby locations because they feel a car is safer than bikes. Since, we have gas stations side-by-side it's convenient to travel in car with family everywhere along with language. In case, government increase the petrol price, there are some chance to solve environmental problems. Although, people who travel to office daily should start using vehicles like cycle if it's nearby. Whereas, riding cycle will help to improve our health rather driving cars, however, if it's a walkable distance then walking to the workplace is advisable so that they can bypass wasting time in traffic. On the other hand, if the government raises the fuel cost then there will be some impact on car manufacturing companies. It seems to me that, fuel price is directly propositional to car company because if there is a increase in the price of gas then buying cars will automatically get reduced.\", \"sampled\": \"Governments in developing nations often face tough choices because their budgets are limited. Formal schooling for the young is important for socio-economic progress, and many policymakers give it priority. Some people argue that more money should go towards educating illiterate adults, which creates a tension between two pressing needs. Increasing funding for adult literacy may mean less funding for children’s and adolescents’ education. With limited resources, maintaining quality education for both groups could become impossible, and this might harm long-term development. The risk is that spreading resources too thinly leaves neither group properly supported. Automation is reducing the number of blue-collar jobs, and illiterate adults in such roles are being made redundant. A lack of literacy skills makes it harder to find other work, pushing many into deeper hardship. High adult illiteracy lowers the standard of living and contributes to social problems like crime, homelessness, and poverty. Educating illiterate adults can reduce these negative social consequences and help people find stable work again. This kind of investment addresses immediate suffering rather than only future potential. Spending more on adult literacy will benefit social welfare in such regions, even if it requires difficult trade-offs with other educational priorities.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"After students finish their high school they have to make a decision whether they need to work or continue with higher education. Some believe that there are more benefits to carry on their education, I agree with the statement of getting an occupation straight after their high school. To start with, there are some factors that point out if we continue with our higher study there are more opportunities in the future. For example, if you want to be a doctor or lawyer one can not become with a certain university study.It is undeniable that students can have more knowledge and they will be received a certification which can be used for applying for a profession. Therefor, college or high school institution plays a major role. On the other hand, I believe students should get jobs right after high institute graduation. Firstly, they will earn extra cash as well as make them more independent. Secondly, they can gain more experiences in the real world and learn some practical life skills related to what profession they choose. To illustrate, if you were an engineer and you needed to fix a bicycle, you would have a chance to have more fixing experiences than what you learn about it at the institution.\", \"sampled\": \"In many countries, a growing number of people want to uncover the history of their homes. Owners often start this research to find out specific details such as when the building was constructed, what materials were used, its original purpose, and how it was designed. This curiosity is not only about the past but also about making better decisions today. Knowing the materials and design of a house can help owners save money on replacements or repairs. Research also reveals important parts and areas that need regular maintenance, which stops small problems from turning into expensive ones. Examining these physical clues closely can even help predict when the construction or later changes took place. Talking to neighbours, especially elderly ones, is a useful way to gather information about a house’s past. Elderly neighbours can share relevant details and practical advice that might not be recorded anywhere else. Their memories often fill gaps that physical evidence alone cannot explain. Old furniture or distinctive decorations found in a building can be researched online to trace past ownership. While researching a building’s history can be time-consuming, the author believes this effort is essential before buying or demolishing any building. Without this knowledge, important heritage and practical insights could be lost forever.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"On the one hand, there are manifold brilliant benefits that we should pay attention to. When the cost of petrol climbs it means that those who travel by private vehicles have to pay more money on their transporting. So as tho save money for other needs, more and more people are likely to choose public transportation or motor vehicles which work by using alternative source of energy. For instance, without a high amount of salary, employees will choose to become train passengers to go to the office as they can save large amount of money. On the other hand, there are many noticeable drawbacks should be taken into consideration, One those who have to use public transport they are more likely to face dozens of issues like unable to go to work on time because the delays of train, bus ... Additionally, in developing areas such as Asia, Africa... there are not enough route to meet the requirements of residents to travel by public transportation seemed to be impossible at all. To draw a conclusion, every coin has its two side so that the given solution is really engrossing but al should be considered carefully before applying to reality.\", \"sampled\": \"Teenagers often struggle to choose between starting work or going to university after high school, and this decision can shape their entire future. At around 18, they can earn their own money, which makes the idea of immediate employment very attractive. In many European countries, it is common for students to find a job straight after high school, partly because some believe university is not essential and prefer to begin a career right away. Figures like Bill Gates and Mark Zuckerberg are frequently mentioned as examples of people who succeeded without a degree, and their stories are used to argue that higher education is not necessary for a prosperous career. However, professions such as doctors and teachers require high academic qualifications, and many well-paying jobs demand credentials that cannot be bypassed. The job market is more competitive than it was in previous generations, and having a bachelor’s or master’s degree can be a significant advantage on a resumé when looking for stable work. Employers tend to favour candidates who already have adequate knowledge and need less training, which gives graduates a clear edge in the hiring process. I believe that continuing education is the better option for students, and a stronger academic background is linked to greater success in pursuing their dream jobs.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Many believe that teenagers should be involved in some kind of volunter jobs ,in their spare time.This contribution would be beneficial for children and society.I completely agree with the above statement as this helps teenagers to become a responsible person of the society in the near future and also results in beneficial contribution for the society. From the adoloscent point of view ,by doing unpaid jobs in their freetime, children can get rid from their boredom.IN addition to that they can also get valuable experiences by pursuing this types of works.For example,teamwork they can learn how to work togethere and cooperate with their colleagues.Researches show that children ,which are involved in any kind of community works in their high school time ,they have good working skills in their future employment.Moreover,by doing volunteer jobs ,they can very well keep them busy ,so there are less chances to spoil.Many times teenagers spoiled their life because of influence of bad company .In their spare times they hanging out all day and late nights and involved in some unappropriate works. Thus to make children away from this kind of things ,unpaid jobs are excellent ideas.\", \"sampled\": \"People in many countries are becoming more interested in the history of the buildings they live in. This curiosity often leads to a stronger wish to protect original features and keep the structure as it is. Without that knowledge, older buildings may be demolished or changed in ways that erase their character. Knowing a building’s background can also affect its market value in practical ways. The materials used in construction and the social standing of previous occupants are two factors that can influence the price. A famous former owner, for instance, may significantly raise what a buyer is willing to pay. In a city like Isfahan in Iran, historical buildings from the Qajar period carry special meaning. These structures represent Iranian culture and art and are worth preserving for that reason alone. Professional historians and architects can examine such buildings to identify their architecture, structural strength, and the time of construction. Long-time neighbours often hold relevant stories that are not recorded anywhere else. Their memories can add detail to what professionals uncover about a building’s past. Together, these sources help communities see the full value of older buildings and encourage efforts to keep them standing.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"There is no doubt these days, many people do not like read news. However, the question is, is the news connection to our lives? Or is it a waste of time? In this essay I am going to discuss this view and give my opinion. There are many people believe that the news is important and we have to read it. First, they think the news shows the new of the world. For example, some people live abroad and they can know everything happens in their country by the news. Second, the news helps individuals to evolve their cultures. To illustrate, some people like to know a new culture when they watch about this culture in the TV. Finally, the news is the best way to recognize anything around the world. These are some reasons why people think the news is powerful. On the other hand, some public think the news has no connection to people`s lives. In addition, they see a lot of news which are a waste of time. In fact, this is because news depends on lies and controversies. Moreover, the news relies on the fakes. Because of this, people do not want to lose their time to read the news or watch television. These are some beliefs, why individual do not like the news. To sum up, this essay has discussed some views about the news. In the believers side of the news; they think the news assists to know everything and improves the cultures.\", \"sampled\": \"Many people feel a strong curiosity about the history of the house they live in, and this interest often comes from both practical and emotional reasons. On the practical side, knowing a house’s history helps assess its real condition before buying or renovating. For example, past damage like a fire can be hard to spot after refurbishment, so uncovering this information can prevent future problems. An emotional factor also plays a major role in this curiosity. People develop an attachment to a house they have lived in for a long time, and that bond naturally leads to questions about its past. They start to wonder about previous residents, old room layouts, or stories that unfolded within the same walls, which makes the house feel more meaningful. There are several ways to research a house’s history, and one common method is asking around the neighbourhood. Neighbours may offer more detailed and honest information than house agencies, sharing personal memories or local gossip that official sources might miss. Another method is looking for books or records that document the house, which can reveal official data about construction dates or ownership changes. A more formal approach involves visiting a local library to search through archived materials. Local library records like newspapers or books may contain significant events related to the house, such as notable incidents or famous former owners. Combining neighbour interviews with library research often gives the fullest picture of a home’s hidden story.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Many people consider that it is important for children to learn art at school, however, there are others who opine that it is totally a wastage of student's study time. In my opinion, art is certainly as important as any other subjects because it helps younger children in learning history and in expressing themselves. On the one hand, art is considered a useful skill as it allows children to learn about the history and cultural heritage of their nation. It is a well-known fact that the children better learn through images than text, therefore teachers use various art pieces to explain them many historical events. According to a survey, it was found that children who learned the story through snapshots were able to retain that information for longer duration as compared to others.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe the main role of schools is to prepare pupils to become productive members of society. I think schools should also help children grow as individuals. In my view, both employment preparation and character building should be central purposes of education. Preparing children for future work helps secure their livelihood. Lessons and a school environment that reflect real market needs can build a foundation for professional life. Early job-focused training allows children to recognise future opportunities and earn a living later on. For example, in Bangladesh the employment rate is 30% higher for graduates from vocational training centres than from general education. This shows how targeted job training can lead to better practical outcomes. A focus on employment does address a key need for many young people.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, there is unprecedented attention which is dramatically increased about the history of the house or building they live in. There are several reasons for individuals willing to know the history of their place and possible ways to research which I will outline both of the topics in this essay. To begin with, there are many people who are indeed interested in not only their house but also the city which is a connection through the culture. Many European countries are paying attention to preserving the monuments, ancient buildings, and houses within their borders in order to maintain their culture. Therefore, the houses which are close to the ancient places are preserved too, therefore these houses have a significant value in terms of both financial and cultural. For instance, there are many places in Istanbul that were houses once and then turned into museums because of their history, which could be an essential reason to learn about the history of houses and buildings. On the contrary, unfortunately, many of the cities are gradually filling with skyscrapers that lack a history or even aesthetics, which could be another reason for people to interested in old buildings, and motivate them to research about. Also, there are easy and convenient ways to research the history whether a house or the building you live in.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people think secondary school students should study international news as a subject, while others argue it would waste valuable school time. Supporters say learning about global events helps students build knowledge based on worldwide issues. This essay will discuss both views and explain why I agree that adding international news is not a good idea. Those in favour believe a subject on international news could help students understand how issues in other countries affect the world. They think this kind of study builds capabilities based on global knowledge, which is important for young learners. Students may find it hard to choose reliable information from the internet on their own, so a guided subject could give them proper access to world events. On the other hand, opponents argue that an extra subject would add more pressure to students who already have full schedules. Students already face a lot of academic stress with subjects like math, science, and additional languages. Adding more study could lead to unhealthy mental conditions, which makes this proposal risky for their wellbeing. I agree that introducing international news as a subject is not beneficial because of the increased stress and the risk of teacher bias. It would be hard for teachers to select news without bringing in their own views on politics, religion, or personal beliefs. Students might be influenced by a teacher's opinion before they form their own viewpoints, which goes against the aim of building independent global knowledge.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The proliferation of technology has triggered debate regarding its impacts on socioeconomic disparities. While there is an increasing concern that technological advancements widen the gap between the affluent and the poor, there is an opposing viewpoint suggesting that these innovations narrow this gap. This essay will examine both perspectives and articulate my stance on the former viewpoint. On the one hand, technology exacerbates the disparity between the wealthy and the poor. Technological advancements can potentially lead to job displacement in some sectors. The demand for highly-skilled workers in fields such as artificial intelligence and data analysis increases, while lower-skilled jobs such as data entry and line operations may experience reduced demand. As a result, individuals engaged in monotonous and repetitive tasks are at risk of job loss, resulting in income inequalities. Moreover, limited access to technology among disadvantaged communities hampers their ability to benefit from these advancements. Many technological products are extremely expensive and only the rich can afford to buy them. On the other hand, proponents of the opposing view contend that technological advancements are reducing income inequality. Technological change enables all individuals, irrespective of socioeconomic background, to access education at affordable prices. For example, the advent of the Internet allows individuals who live in rural areas to access higher education easily through virtual platforms at affordable prices.\", \"sampled\": \"The interview is widely seen as a key part of hiring, though some argue it is not a reliable way to select the right person. In my view, it remains the best method for choosing a suitable candidate. Its usefulness extends beyond recruitment into areas like research and healthcare, which highlights its broader value. An interviewer can ask about educational background, work experience, and any health issues that might affect job performance. A manager might also explore hobbies, five-year plans, and related experience to gauge long-term fit. The interview gives candidates a chance to showcase their skills and sell themselves to the company, creating a two-way exchange that written tests cannot provide. A candidate can present experiences in chronological order to highlight their abilities, offering a fuller picture of professional growth. Examinations are another way to assess knowledge and understanding, but they only reveal the intellectual side, not a person’s attitude. The interview offers insight into attitude, knowledge, and skills, making it a more complete selection tool. Attitude is often considered especially important because skills and knowledge can be learned, whereas a person’s outlook is harder to change. Examinations miss this human element, focusing narrowly on academic or technical performance. For these reasons, the interview remains the most effective method, as it uncovers a candidate’s true potential beyond what a test score can show.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In the temporary era, some advocate for government assistance to artists, encompassing painters, musicians and poets. However, others consider it a misuse of funds. In this essay, I will explore both perspectives and express my opinion, which disagrees with latter view. On the one hand , one imperative reason is that artists always have played a pivotal role in preserving and promoting cultural heritage throughout history. For example, during China’s Tang Dynasty, painters such as Wu Daozi, Gu Kaizhi, and Zhang Xuan created works that captured the essence of Tang Dynasty society and culture. Their masterpieces not only reflected the beauty of their time but also served as a means to convey societal values and benefits. Today, the government can continue this tradition by providing support to painters through funding, exhibitions and other resources, ensuring that artists have the means to continue creating and sharing their works for future generations. On the other hand, some people argue that the authority should fund essential services such as healthcare, education and infrastructures, not invest in the arts. They believe that public funds should be allocated to address pressing societal needs, such as poverty alleviation and environmental protection. However, in my view, while it is true that the authority has a responsibility to allocate funds judiciously for critical service, the arts also play a significant role in protecting and promoting the cultural heritage of a society. Therefore, supporting the artists may not be the highest priority, but it is indeed necessary.\", \"sampled\": \"Whether high school students should be allowed to evaluate their teachers is a contentious issue. Some believe this practice is essential for improving educational quality, as it allows for the measurement and monitoring of teacher performance. Others, however, argue that enabling students to criticise their educators could lead to a loss of respect and discipline in the classroom. On the one hand, proponents of student feedback see it as a vital tool for professional development. For instance, in challenging subjects where effective teaching is crucial, evaluations could pinpoint areas needing improvement. This system could also help address problems like favouritism, which can leave some students feeling neglected, and motivate educators to keep their skills and subject knowledge current. By providing a channel for student voices, schools can ensure teachers are meeting the needs of their classes. On the other hand, there are concerns that student feedback could negatively affect the learning environment. Critics suggest that such evaluations might demotivate educators and damage their professional standing. A system where students can openly pass judgement on their instructors could also foster a hostile atmosphere, undermining the authority required for effective classroom management. If teachers become preoccupied with pleasing students to receive good ratings, the focus may shift away from rigorous academic standards. In my view, students should not be given the power to formally evaluate their teachers. This position is based on the cultural importance of respecting one's mentors, a tradition that has been highly valued for centuries and is fundamental to a productive learning environment. Instead of student-led reviews, the responsibility for monitoring teacher performance should lie with school management. They are better equipped to create a systematic and fair feedback process that supports both teachers and the educational goals of the institution.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In today’s educational landscape, many people are thinking about the impact of team sports at school for the children. While various methods exist, I strongly believe that this solution is the best way to cooperate teenagers between them. In this essay, I will discuss why I hold this view and provide two supporting reasons. To begin with, company game provides self-discipline which teaches younger how to work collaboratively. It is one of the main keys for success, fostering crucial interpersonal skills. For instance, in a volleyball team, players must know and understand how to communicate with each other to provide their strategy for achieving goals. Consequently, the collaborative experience not only teaches infants the significant role of working together, but also helps them develop strong bonds with their teammates. Secondly, children are exposed to both success and failure together, which has an essential impact on collective responsibility. When a team succeeds, each member shares in the victory, reinforcing the idea that cooperation leads to mutual benefits. For example, in moments of failure, kids learn to support and uplift one another, understanding that overcoming challenges requires collective effort. As a result, this shared experience contributes significantly to character development and prepares them for future collaborative endeavors. In conclusion, team sports at school stand out as the most effective method for teaching infants cooperation. The structured environment, coupled with exposure to success and failure as a team, imparts invaluable life skills.\", \"sampled\": \"The line graph shows the percentage of people in the UK, aged over four, who listened to the radio and watched television throughout a 24-hour period from October to December 1992. Overall, radio was the more popular medium in the morning, while television dominated the afternoon and evening. In the early hours, from midnight to around 6:00 a.m., viewership and listenership were at their lowest. The audience for both TV and radio remained below 5% during this time, indicating that most people were not consuming either type of media. The morning period saw a significant change, especially for radio. From 6:00 a.m., the percentage of radio listeners rose sharply, peaking at just under 30% between 8:00 a.m. and 9:00 a.m. In contrast, television viewership stayed low throughout the morning, with fewer than 10% of people watching. After midday, the trends for the two media types began to reverse. Radio listenership started a steady decline for the remainder of the day. Conversely, the television audience grew substantially in the afternoon and evening, reaching a peak of over 45% at approximately 9:00 p.m. After this point, TV viewership gradually fell as midnight approached.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"It is argued that our life has been made complex by technology and we should lead a basic lifestyle with no technology. I completely disagree with the statement. Firstly, computers make our lives easier and secondly, comfortable. To begin with, the advancement in technology makes our work easier. This is to say that there are many fields such as communication where we can communicate with people around the world in real-time. Moreover, we can have a face to face conversation, live chat or just speak with the person we want anywhere around the globe. Facebook is the prime example of this where we can talk instantly with our families and friends who are distant apart from us with video sharing option. Furthermore, new developments in the technology of transportation have made our life comfortable. By ,this I mean innovations in the aviation sector has made our travel time much shorter and more relaxed. For instance, people can reach other countries within hours sitting on a comfortable seat, however, if individuals travel through the road, it will take them days or months. Hence, technology has made out travel faster and deluxe.\", \"sampled\": \"Developed countries often host major international sports events, and organizing these large-scale occasions is widely seen as advantageous. Such events can boost a country's economy in several significant ways, making them an attractive option for many governments. One of the main benefits for a host country is a substantial increase in its international profile. Major sporting events lead to a rise in tourism as people travel from abroad to watch the competitions. This influx of visitors directly benefits the local economy, increasing income for businesses like hotels and restaurants, which often experience a surge in revenue. Hosting these international events can also create more employment opportunities for the local population. In my opinion, the economic and social benefits of holding these events are clear. While countries should consider certain factors before deciding to host such occasions, the advantages are undeniable. The positive impact on tourism, employment, and a nation's reputation makes organizing major sports events a worthwhile endeavor.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In today’s life, many people support the idea that art should be compulsory subject for all children at school whereas, others believe that is a distractor and wasting time. As far as I am concerned, student should learn arts in general because it helps them to think creatively as well as expressing themselves. First of all, many supporters to that idea believe that art may play a crucial role in shaping the personality of the students and open their minds. So, it might assist children to consider what kind of art is attracting them and therefore, develop their congenital and mental health. Furthermore, Arts make students creative. For example, painting or visual arts bring multiple benefits to the child such as enhancement of imagination ability that is a necessary element in performing arts. On the other hand, Some critics support the idea that art is such a waste of time and students should be taught more valuable subjects as maths and chemistry. They think art is distractor and may stop progression on the other essential fields.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that achieving one's aims is primarily a matter of luck. I disagree with this perspective because I feel that a person's success is a direct result of their efforts. Ultimately, success is the outcome of hard work and perseverance, not simple coincidence. The achievements of successful individuals can be attributed almost entirely to their consistent hard work. Many of these people have sacrificed sleep and relaxation to reach their personal goals. While good fortune may not always favour those who work hard, their dedication is the key factor. Luck can be seen as a contributor to success, but it is certainly not the main cause. To benefit from any good fortune, a person must first make an effort. For instance, an individual who does not buy a lottery ticket cannot possibly win, regardless of how lucky they are. Therefore, dedicated individuals can create their own opportunities by persisting through difficulties. Luck is only a useful element for those who are already prepared to work hard and persevere. Effort must be shown before a person can take advantage of any fortunate circumstances that may arise.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In recent times, technological advancements which have provided digitalised games and various| activities on social media have further reduced attention span. Youngsters are increasingly having challenges with curriculum in their citadels of learning. This essay will outline the major factors why this is a constraint and suggest ways to curb the menace. To begin with, high exposure to modernized games is a significant reason why children who are clouded with play can no longer strive maximally with regard to their educational sessions. This is due to the fact that most parents are working busy jobs,thus, leaving their offspring at the mercy of uncontrolled social media and time management Consequently, this leads to poor outcomes and exam failures. For example, studies published by the Ministry of Education, Lagos, Nigeria, showed that children who finish their assignments immediately after the afternoon meal perform excellently than those who leave theirs till the following class because they usually do not have a quick grasp of the topic being discussed. The above informs that passive parenting that avails children the opportunity to access electronic games disrupts the learning curve of young ones. On the other hand, monitored child upbringing is vital to promote study patterns and education.\", \"sampled\": \"One of the primary benefits of moving businesses to the countryside is the potential for economic stimulation in these areas. When manufacturing firms relocate, they create essential job opportunities, which in turn increases the demand for local products and services. For instance, some companies in Vietnam have successfully moved their production lines to rural locations, which has directly helped to reduce local unemployment rates. However, there are also considerable disadvantages, particularly concerning the environment and financial costs. Relocating manufacturing companies can seriously harm the local rural environment if industrial waste is not managed properly. A notable case occurred in Dong Nai, Vietnam, where a company producing MSG polluted the local water source, negatively affecting the health of residents. Another significant drawback is the substantial financial investment required. Companies need a large budget to transport production lines and construct new facilities in a different region. This financial strain can be overwhelming, and some smaller companies in Vietnam have even gone bankrupt after attempting such a move.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The government spends a significant amount of money on different areas, including the arts, health services, and education systems. In some countries, the government pays more attention to the arts, and some people think this is a positive trend, but others believe that money should be spent on alternative areas, such as health, or education. In this essay, I will discuss both views and present my point of view. On the one hand, funding the arts could benefit economically to the country because it is one of the main aspects of international tourism. One of the primary reasons for travelling overseas is the fact that tourists are always interested in foreign museums, theatres and other artistic facilities. Therefore, if the government spends more wages to develop and maintain its art fields, for instance, the country will make more profit, leading to an improved economic condition. The more money will spend on the arts, the more economic growth will occur. On the other hand, there are other areas that need more funding, including health and education. The prevalence of chronic diseases, such as obesity and diabetes, is increasing these days, causing financial problems for the citizens. For example, the number of people who suffer from these health issues can be reduced if the authorities focus on public health and spend more money to prevent public from the diseases. In addition, improving the education system may be crucial for the younger, who is the key to developing the country.\", \"sampled\": \"Some argue that many traditional customs are no longer relevant to modern life and should be discontinued. Indeed, certain practices, such as widespread personal weapon ownership, underage marriage, and denying education to girls, are obsolete and should be abandoned. These specific traditions are no longer suitable for contemporary society for several reasons. The tradition of owning weapons for personal safety is largely unnecessary today. Modern societies are generally safer due to professional police forces and advanced security technology. The tragic Christchurch mosque shooting is a clear example of the devastating consequences that can occur when private citizens have access to weapons. Another outdated custom that should be stopped is underage marriage, which often leads to serious problems for young couples. For instance, financial instability is a major cause of high divorce rates in the USA, and it is widely believed that individuals should be at least 18 to marry, as they are more likely to be mature. Similarly, the practice of preventing females from receiving an education must be abolished because education is a fundamental human right. Leaving more than half of the world's population illiterate is an unwise and harmful practice. The economic success of nations like Japan is often linked to their commitment to educating the entire population, including both men and women. In conclusion, abandoning harmful and outdated customs is essential for the progress and well-being of modern societies.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"While some consider that the authorities should contribute financially to all individuals working in the art field, others are of the opinion that such initiatives are a waste of property.In my opinion, while funding for run-of-the-mill artists might be unsatisfying, a degree of funding is required for some of the talented. On the one hand, since the financial offer for artists has been made by the authorities , the probability of plenty of amateurs labelling themselves as professionals and seeking monetary aid would increase.Even at present, several unskilled art practitioners are taking shortcuts to receive money effortlessly.For instance, there is an increasing number of people who sell NFTs,which are minute digital art pieces eligible solely on the internet.The quality of some of the so-called NFT art pieces is quite low-brow,straightforward and primitive,yet these producer artists gain enormous amounts from the buyers.This indicates that there are opportunistic amateurs out there wishing for easy money. On the other hand,several outstanding artists have been left unnoticed and unappreciated,leading to the situation where they have to feed from hand to mouth.The art field has always been considered as prestigious, and even if some artists produce masterpieces,they can receive less attention and be not as famous that of amateurs.\", \"sampled\": \"In some countries, people are showing a greater interest in the history of the buildings they inhabit. This curiosity is not limited to their own homes but also extends to larger historical structures, like mansions that once belonged to royalty. One significant reason for this trend is that many individuals now have more leisure time, a situation partly brought on by the recent pandemic. People use various online and offline methods to research the histories of these buildings. A common offline approach is to ask grandparents or other elderly community members about a home's past. Older people are often keen to share stories about how a building was constructed and who was responsible for its creation. For larger and more famous structures, such as royal palaces, people generally turn to online research to find information. Another popular way to learn about these places is by visiting them directly, sometimes as part of a planned vacation. This type of tourism demonstrates a clear engagement with architectural history. The interest in visiting historical sites can also have significant economic effects, as seen with visits to a prominent British palace. These visits reportedly generate substantial revenue, showing how public curiosity can support the preservation of important buildings. Furthermore, simple discussions with friends about local history can be a powerful motivator for people to begin exploring the built environment around them.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Studying at university is a significant way to a successful job. I totally agree with this statement.As studying at university has great benefits, why do people still believe that is a good idea to skip studying in college and immediately work in their job? Looking at other people’s arguments, say that skipping studying at university can save time and effort instead of studying for three or four years.However, I think this argument is not valid,because the main purpose of studying in college is gaining the importance of duties and information.They also said that the university does not teach useful things that may they get benefit in future.I also disagree because the main purpose of studying at university is to get the knowledge that they must know about the job to succeed in the work field . University and college teach students how can succeed in their job in future and build them to be more effective in their careers. To summarise, I would say that studying at university is the most important step to achieve in their life.Quite apart from the benefits studying is a major step that should everyone keep it.\", \"sampled\": \"The growing use of automation, robots, and artificial intelligence in the workplace is a major modern trend. I believe that jobs which endanger human life should be automated, and that this technology brings significant benefits, particularly in terms of safety and efficiency. One of the main advantages of this technology is the improvement of workplace safety. Certain jobs have consistently high rates of injury, making them ideal for replacement by robots. For example, a survey showed that work injuries decreased by 60% in environments where artificial intelligence was used. From a business perspective, automation also offers considerable financial advantages for large corporations. These companies often see robots as a long-term investment to lower their ongoing labour costs. The financial data from Japan’s top 30 companies, for instance, revealed marked improvement after robots were introduced into their operations. In conclusion, it seems that automation will continue to replace many jobs, making it essential for companies to manage this transition. Corporations should consider creating programs to educate their employees on the benefits of AI and prepare them for future changes in the workplace.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"What students are going to follow after high school will decide their future career, so making a good decision is very important. Many people believe that getting a university or college degree is the best option after finishing school, meanwhile others think that starting with a practical work is more beneficial. In this essay both opinions will be discussed. Nowadays most well-paid and convenient jobs are only available for high educated candidate, that explains why a large group of the community believe that studying at the university is mandatory to achieve success. For instance, a student who will graduate as a dentist, a physician or an engineer will get his dream job so easily, that will lead to a high life quality and better mental and physical health. Thus, the importance of having a diploma in order to have a good career is a common belief among people. However, other people are convinced that working straight after school is the best option. That can be explained by the fact that young people get the chance to gain a lot of experience with a real job rather than following the theoretical traject. Furthermore, a person can never be independent, unless he or she has a stable income, which is mostly not possible to have as a student. Earning money also affords a comfortable life.\", \"sampled\": \"Machines are increasingly performing routine tasks and jobs that involve hard physical work. While automation can free people from mundane labour and allow them to pursue more meaningful activities, I believe the negative effects of this trend outweigh the positive ones, particularly regarding culture and employment. There are some clear benefits to this development, especially for business owners. Entrepreneurs value the increased productivity and efficiency that result from using machines in their operations. For instance, mechanised farming allows farmers to focus on market prices and sales instead of the physical labour of planting. This shift enables individuals to engage in more strategic work rather than repetitive manual duties. However, the disadvantages related to culture are significant. Mechanisation has contributed to the disappearance of many traditional skills as machines take over production. When unique, handcrafted items are replaced with uniform, mass-produced products, cultural diversity is directly reduced. This is a serious loss because a nation's culture and customs are often closely connected to its traditional handicrafts. Perhaps the most severe consequence of automation is widespread unemployment. The use of robots in manufacturing, for example, has led to significant job losses as machines replace human workers on production lines. Many workers have become unemployed because robots have taken over their roles in the manufacturing sector. This level of unemployment caused by automation is a major problem for any government to address.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"When it comes to taking steps to reduce the effects on plant and animal species caused by human activities, some people think that it is possible and feasible, while others deny the possibility because it has been too late for us to do anything. The reason why people think it’s too late is probably that some destruction caused by human activities is irreversible. Many animals and plants only can survive in special environment, but their habitats have been terribly destroyed by humans so that they could not inhabit and even extinct. We almost don’t have enough time to improve the situation which is an extremely long process, as these animals and plants will completely disappear before acquiring a new home Other people think it’s possible for us to improve the situation through effective means may because our technology and science have been mature enough for us to overcome some problems affecting plant and animal species. Although some animals and plants haven’t been able to live in their original environment, we can take some steps like establishing natural reserves or creating conditions for them to live. For those animals and plants facing extinction, we can feed them until they could live by themselves.\", \"sampled\": \"Increasing the price of fuel is often suggested as a way to solve environmental problems, but I believe this is not the best approach. Such a policy is likely to create social inequality because it affects different income groups unfairly. While wealthier individuals can easily afford more expensive fuel, the general public, particularly those with lower incomes, would face considerable financial strain. A primary argument against this measure is that simply increasing fuel costs will not significantly reduce overall consumption. A better strategy would be to encourage viable alternatives to private car use, such as electric bikes and public transportation. Adopting these alternative transport methods is a more effective way to protect the environment and can achieve more sustainable long-term results. Another practical solution is to promote the use of environmentally friendly fuels in automobiles. These fuels offer a direct way to combat pollution by significantly reducing toxic emissions, which improves air quality and can lead to healthier lives. For instance, fuels like CNG and LPG are excellent eco-friendly options that could be more widely adopted for vehicles. In conclusion, simply raising the price of fuel is not a sufficient solution for tackling environmental issues. The focus should instead be on promoting alternatives that are both effective and fair for the entire population. Encouraging the use of options like public transport, e-vehicles, and cleaner fuels is a much better path forward.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"It is argued that increasing fuel costs for personal vehicles would be the most optimal solution to today’s environmental issues. To a certain extent, I agree with this idea given that it would discourage people from using such means of transport. However, other more serious problems should still be addressed. On the one hand, one clear result of rising fuel prices is that people would be more reluctant to pay for them. Since it is common for motorcycles nowadays to have gas tanks that need to be filled up every other day, the higher price tag means constant refuelling is rather unsustainable, which is essentially the case for most people. Consequently, they will be likely to turn to other more affordable means of transport such as buses or bicycles, which release significantly fewer amounts of emission.\", \"sampled\": \"Pollution significantly impacts the natural environment, affecting water and soil quality. Harmful emissions from vehicles play a major role in this issue, impacting both the environment and personal health. The emissions from cars can lead to respiratory problems and other health issues, emphasizing the direct connection between vehicle use and well-being. While cars offer comfort and convenience, their frequent use can be costly, with a substantial portion of income often dedicated to covering vehicle expenses. These financial burdens not only affect individuals and families but also contribute to environmental problems that threaten ecosystems and human health. To tackle these challenges, a comprehensive approach is needed. Encouraging practices like carpooling, where vehicles are shared among multiple passengers, can help reduce individual vehicle usage. By decreasing reliance on personal cars, we can address environmental issues and work towards a healthier and more sustainable future.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Hard work and consistency are the two core components to build a successful career in this modern era. Although many people think that the most effective way to be successful in life would be going to a college or taking admission to a university, others believe that opting for a job straight after school could be a correct option. In my opinion, making mistakes and accepting them could be the right way to achieve goals in life. First of all, when students study at a university there are a lot of opportunities and challenges waiting for them. Moreover, they think due to rising competition they need a qualified certificate with them. Colleges, aid adults to meet new classmates each day with their unique ideas. For instance, nowadays multinational companies are looking for candidates who have completed their degree from a top university. Therefore. colleges are the best places to explore several things because there is no fear of failing. On the contrary, choosing to work directly after school is appealing for several reasons. Many citizens want to leave an independent life and don't want any support from their parents. Furthermore, during ,jobs the employee works on the real live data and he also gets a deeper understanding of that industry which creates a massive difference in the future.\", \"sampled\": \"In some countries, more people are exploring the history of the architecture in their living areas. This interest is partly driven by the popularity of platforms like YouTube, where videos about homes are widely shared. Urban renewal initiatives have also contributed to a growing fascination with house history, leading individuals to seek information through various channels. Some choose to learn by interacting with local residents, who can provide firsthand stories and insights into the houses' past. Occasionally, delving into house history involves staying with locals for a few days to fully immerse oneself in the historical context of a dwelling. Moreover, documentary records are valuable resources for those investigating their homes' history, offering documented proof and narratives that illuminate past residents and architectural transformations. Understanding a house's history can significantly boost one's appreciation for their living space. By discovering the stories and events that have influenced a house over time, individuals can form a stronger bond with their home and a deeper sense of belonging. This enhanced comprehension can nurture a greater community spirit and heritage appreciation, enhancing the overall living experience and strengthening connections to the local surroundings.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I am Arun Bikram Thapa, Assistant Professor of Aerospace Engineering at Tribhuvan University, Institute of Engineering, Pulchowk Campus. I am writing to recommend my brilliant student, Sandesh Parajuli, as a highly deserving candidate for your research-based graduate program in Aerospace Engineering. First of all, I have not had the privilege of teaching Sandesh in an academic course, so I will not elaborate much on his academics.\", \"sampled\": \"Participating in team sports at school is seen as a valuable method to foster cooperation among children. Through team sports, children develop communication skills by understanding both verbal and non-verbal cues. They practice different communication styles and enhance their ability to work together towards shared objectives. This experience boosts their confidence in collaborating with others and teaches them the significance of cooperation. Ultimately, team sports offer children firsthand opportunities to appreciate the advantages of working together.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"There are numerous striking international competitions around the world that are held on a regular basis. A group of individuals present the view that these events have positive effects, whereas others believe the sizable amount of money that is spent could be used more effectively. I strongly agree with the former opinion.\", \"sampled\": \"Having children later in life can increase the risk of genetic disorders. Being ready for childbirth involves financial, mental, and emotional preparedness. Advanced maternal age can lead to infertility caused by declining hormone levels, which is irreversible. On the positive side, older parents have more time to get ready for parenthood. However, delayed childbirth can also bring about complications related to infertility.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"These days, the proliferation of the media industry, including video games and television, and its violent-related content have sparked heated debates. While some critics argue that violent content has negative impacts on the community, others disagree with such a notion. This essay will examine both sides of the discussion and provide a conclusion. Advocates of this idea argue that exposure to violence through television and video games has the detrimental effect of desensitizing young people to violence. For example, they may adopt aggressive and violent behaviours from the shows they watch or the games they play and consider them a normal part of life. As a result, this can lead to an increase in the rate of domestic violence and violent crimes, such as armed robbery and homicide. Nevertheless, others argue that the impact media violence has had on people’s behaviour is insignificant. This is because shows and video games only serve as means of entertainment. Thus, it is common sense that game players and TV audiences are aware that any activity shown is for demonstration purposes only and would have minimal impact on how they behave. If scenes on television could have had such great impacts, other programs with educational values such as science, history, and astronomy must have had the same effect on society. In fact, the number of people who work as scientists, archaeologists, and astronomers is still relatively small compared to other professions. In my opinion, even though media consumers can distinguish between real and virtual at a certain level, excessive exposure to violent behaviours may affect their judgment and desensitize them to violence.\", \"sampled\": \"Children often struggle to concentrate on their studies in school due to ineffective learning methods and the strong influence of the gaming industry. This situation can result in a lack of focus in the classroom, affecting academic progress. Schools may not always offer an ideal learning environment for every student, worsening attention problems during lessons. The popularity of mobile games serves as a major distraction for young learners, diverting their focus from educational pursuits. This increased emphasis on gaming can harm their ability to concentrate on schoolwork and grasp essential learning material. To tackle this issue, it is essential to reduce the appeal of digital entertainment and redirect children's attention to their academic duties. Encouraging students to recognize and embrace learning styles that suit them can significantly boost their concentration levels in school. By customizing teaching methods to match individual preferences and strengths, educators can help students interact more effectively with their studies, leading to enhanced focus and academic achievement. Moreover, parents play a crucial role in minimizing distractions by actively supervising and limiting their children's screen time, especially concerning mobile and computer games. To address the addictive nature of games like PUBG and CSgo, gaming companies should consider implementing stricter measures, such as a real-name system, to control access to these highly captivating and potentially harmful platforms. By restricting the availability of such addictive games, particularly for young users, the gaming industry can help reduce the adverse effects of excessive gaming on children's concentration and overall well-being.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Hollywood and other film industries across the globe are quite famous and the artists who work in these industries somehow represent an as lousy role model for youngsters. While I agree that youngsters do not get to learn proper life lessons from these film stars, there are some successful people in the film industry who are skilled and inspired many people. However, there are many reasons why movie stars are a lousy role model for young people. Firstly, these superstars wear expensive brand clothes, which symbolize the success in the form of money and even they show off through social media; which in result has a bad influence on children as they follow these Hollywood actors closely and apt their lifestyle. For example: nowadays teenagers started to spend parents money on the branded clothes, and they waste their time on social media to grab more followers to increase their popularity because they think that being famous on social apps is a success. Furthermore, these youngsters focus more on social media instead of real life, that means they change their life goal; therefore, celebrities distracting them from their career goals and spoiling their future. On the other hand, there are a few celebrities who can set good motivator for young people. These artists gain their success in the industry because of excellent acting skill, communication skills, etc. whereas other actors brought up from the wealthy families and they have not struggled in the industry.\", \"sampled\": \"Scientific research in the healthcare sector is crucial for driving economic growth. Nations such as Ireland, known for their focus on medical research, have seen the benefits through improved healthcare and prosperity. This research not only boosts the economy but also enhances the overall well-being of the population. While space exploration is fascinating, it should not overshadow the vital advancements in healthcare. Investigating diseases and developing medical technologies are essential for ensuring healthy populations and reaping societal benefits. Therefore, maintaining a balance between scientific explorations in various fields is key to maximizing societal well-being. Overemphasizing the development of warfare tools can lead to destructive consequences, emphasizing the importance of prioritizing healthcare. Organizations like WHO and UNICEF stress the significance of focusing on healthcare over warfare technology. Redirecting resources and efforts towards improving well-being through scientific research can help mitigate conflict risks and contribute to a more peaceful and prosperous world. In conclusion, the healthcare sector should remain a primary focus of scientific exploration due to its significant impact on economic growth and societal welfare. Redirecting attention towards healthcare advancements instead of warfare tools can lead to a healthier, more prosperous global community and a brighter, sustainable future for all.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Apart from making profit, firms must play an important role in social responsibilities. In my opinion, I agree with this statement as corporate gain money from society, therefore it should also returned back it to society. First of all, firms should not exploit their employees. This is because some businesses will exploit their workers by forcing them to work long hours that exceed the maximum working hours. Furthermore, some firms do not pay the minimum wage requirement in order to keep their costs as low as possible. Additionally, some firms are not providing any insurance care for their employees. Although the result may end up by making huge profits, however, the entire process is considered inhumanity. Therefore, corporate social responsibility is essential as it protects the legal rights of employees. Besides, profits created by companies should give some portion back to society. This is due to the fact that all the resources needed by firms come from society. For instance, if a firm wants to produce a product, they need natural resources, capital, and human resources. The businesses pick up the natural resources that belong to everyone in the world.\", \"sampled\": \"Job interviews are essential in the recruitment process for multinational corporations. They help evaluate applicants' technical skills, temperament, and communication abilities. Through interviews, companies can assess not only candidates' qualifications but also their compatibility with the company's work environment. Face-to-face interviews offer insights into applicants' body language and facial expressions, revealing valuable non-verbal cues about their intentions and personality traits. Moreover, interviews allow companies to clearly communicate their expectations to candidates and ensure alignment with the company's values and culture. Companies view interviews as a crucial step in identifying the most suitable candidates for a job role. Given the investment in employees through salaries and benefits, companies have the right to assess potential hires' capabilities and fit. Interviews also aid in narrowing down the applicant pool, enabling companies to focus on promising candidates and make informed hiring decisions. Effective communication is particularly vital in roles involving interactions with senior managers. Job seekers must demonstrate strong communication skills during interviews to showcase their ability to engage with high-ranking executives. Therefore, interviews not only aid in selecting the right candidate but also serve as a tool to evaluate essential workplace skills.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"After hearing I was repainting the walls of my grandmother's room, my neighbour shared some old stories about my community, like someone keeping a real deer as a pet in front of my house. Leading me wanting to unearth more about it. I believe there are some bizarre stories that will grasp the youth's attention and arise their curiosity about knowing more about the past of their residence. In the following essay, I would explain the motives behind this, and provide some tricks to investigate more. The main reason behind it is the bond with one's family and localities. For example, my community is built and designed for those employees who work in an oil refinery factory. In other words, your coworkers are exactly your neighbours. Under such physical conditions, people are more likely to make friends. Thus, curiosity about this place can be aroused by nature.\", \"sampled\": \"Advertising plays a vital role in informing people about new products and services. Some argue that commercials have little impact, while others believe they strongly influence individuals. People with family responsibilities may see ads as less effective, while those dependent on their parents might be more influenced. Advertising also fuels consumerism, impacting decision-making and consumption habits. Despite this, many are unaware of how ads shape their choices. It's crucial to consider life's purpose and prioritize personal values over materialism for more mindful decisions. In conclusion, opinions differ on advertising's influence. While it educates about products, its impact on decisions and consumer behavior is significant. To counter this influence, individuals should understand their life's purpose and value system over materialistic ad messages.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In this day and age, the trading of countries with others without the limitations of government has been a subject to many heated debates. While this statement has some restraints, I consider myself a proponent of this idea. Further reasonings would be indicated in the following parts of this essay. On the one hand, international free trade can bring a plethora of advantages for many countries. Firstly, being able to sell and purchase products globally will definitely boost the economies of both the importing and exporting countries. Free trade allows domestic corporations to approach new markets and attract foreign investment. Secondly, local companies can have better access to the latest technologies in many modern fields including medication, and aerospace. This benefit helps one country develop rapidly and in turn improve domestic industries with associated fields. To abridge, global unlimited trade could largely enhance a country's economy. While it is true that unrestricted global trade comes with some disadvantages such as authority manipulation and selling addicts, individuals tend to benefit from the trade besides economies and corporations.This trend seems to reduce the average price of a product since there are no or few tariffs on imported goods. As a result, purchasers can widely approach a wider range of cheap products. Moreover, more goods are exported with no restrictions or taxes means more corporations rise to international expansion; consequently, the jobs market will expand and greater job opportunities provide higher wages and better career paths for employees. To summarise, people also take advantage of the situation.\", \"sampled\": \"Businesses have a duty beyond profit-making. Vinamilk, a major food producer in Vietnam, employs approximately 10,000 workers nationwide, significantly impacting job creation. Vingroup, under Chairman Mr. Vuong's guidance, exemplified social responsibility by donating nearly 9000 billion VND to combat Covid, saving numerous lives during the pandemic. These actions underscore the vital role of businesses in supporting society. Vinamilk's employment of 10,000 workers is a clear example of how businesses contribute to job opportunities, boosting the economy and providing livelihoods. Additionally, Vingroup's substantial two-year donation during the Covid crisis illustrates the positive societal influence businesses can have. Mr. Vuong's leadership in these charitable endeavors underscores the significance of prioritizing social welfare alongside financial success. The symbiotic relationship between enterprises and society demonstrates that the more a business gives back, the more successful it can be. Vingroup's donation not only aided the government's Covid response but also enhanced its reputation and community standing. It is evident that businesses embracing social responsibilities can prosper financially and in terms of public perception. The reciprocal bond between enterprises and society, beyond financial dealings, highlights the advantages of businesses engaging in social endeavors. By actively participating in social welfare initiatives, companies like Vinamilk and Vingroup contribute to community well-being and nurture a culture of generosity, benefiting those in need and strengthening societal cohesion.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"No doubt, having children is important decision in everyone's life. However, these days most people take decisions to have baby in their later age as compared to past. This essay will going to explain the the reasons behind this phenomenon and also going to mention the benefits of this decision. On the on hand, there are some reasons that's why people make their minds to have children in their later age period.\", \"sampled\": \"Some individuals argue that being an environmentally friendly tourist is a challenging task. Increased awareness and oversight from authorities can assist tourists in becoming more accountable. There has been a noticeable decrease in the disposal of plastic bags into the ocean by people today. Educational campaigns have played a role in lowering the levels of pollutants that endanger marine life. Environmental guidelines in tourist destinations are readily available, with signs and regulations making it hard to overlook environmental concerns.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Few citizens are of the opinion that the ruling authorities of the nation can enhance the public health by enactment on nutritious food.Whilst others experts oppose this and state that it is just a personal choice.This essay will discuss the both views and conclude with my opinion. On the first hand, eating unhealthy food can lead to decrease in public health, so legislators should impose a levy on people who eat this kind of food.Making such rules can increase the public health and also nation can collect a huge number of wealth by means of tax, and as people visit the hospitals less the funds which are supposed to be spend on health sector can be diverted into other sectors.For example, countries like Italy have imposed a excise on eating junk food which in turn increased the health of their public and also increased the national wealth. However,this approach will break the peace of the society due to frustration that will be caused by claustrophobia.This will lead to the protests and demonstrations which in turn spoils the tranquility of the people and nation.Instead, government should educate people by creating awareness on detrimental effects that are caused by eating unhealthy food and literate the advantages of in-taking natural food. To conclude,although imposing laws such has collecting a tax on fast foods can decrease the intake of it, this may lead to loss of freedom of an individual.In my opinion,the regulators should well-inform the people about the unwelcome effects that are caused by eating them that will curtail in consumption of processed food.\", \"sampled\": \"Obesity has become a major health concern due to the rising consumption of unhealthy foods like fast food, including hamburgers, pizza, and french fries. Fast food, known for its convenience and affordability, is consumed by people from various socioeconomic backgrounds. As obesity rates continue to climb, some suggest raising the prices of unhealthy foods as a way to curb this health issue. However, this proposal could negatively affect certain societal groups, especially low-income families. Fast food is often the preferred choice for these families because of its low cost and easy availability. Therefore, increasing the price of fast food could place an additional financial burden on these families, making it harder for them to access food. Moreover, this strategy could unintentionally lead to societal discrimination, as it would disproportionately affect those from lower socioeconomic backgrounds. The implications of raising fast food prices go beyond just financial effects. Fast food consumption is a common social activity, particularly among young people and students. Consequently, higher prices could restrict socialization opportunities for these groups, potentially leading to social exclusion and exacerbating societal divisions. For students, who often depend on fast food for quick, affordable meals between classes, higher prices could add to their financial difficulties. Considering the potential negative consequences, it seems that education might be a more effective solution to combat obesity. By educating people about the health risks associated with fast food and promoting healthier alternatives, we can tackle obesity in a more inclusive and sustainable way.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Newspaper are an integral part of society. however, peoples' lives are dynamically influenced by reading it.It is one of the most popular parts of the media, which is read by the majority of people. It can also change ideas and opinions of people in a drastic way. According to me, it is more positive then negative situation, let's discuss both in the following essay. Reading the newspaper has a positive influence because of these reasons.To start with, people get to know about the political situation of the country.In order to identify the authenticity of the government, citizens can follow it. To explain in depth, it will give ideas to the voters to which political party, they can give their vote to. Further, citizens can also predict economic stability of the country by following paper media. For example, sometimes individuals can save money before investing during the starting period of recession by reading it in news articles. After positive impact let see one main negative impact on people who reads the newspaper. Many people blindly fellows paper media. Additionally, they sometimes even take wrong steps because it. News on deals are not always true. For an instance, to increase their sales, paper articles companies may modify the truth or sometimes even print false news.If reader are not careful and make some important decision of life, it can damage them financially as well.\", \"sampled\": \"Deciding whether to pursue higher education or to dive straight into the job market after school is a significant choice for many teenagers. On one side, some teens contemplate immediate employment after school, which carries its own benefits. Beginning work right away can lead to financial independence at an early age, an appealing prospect for many young individuals striving to establish their autonomy. However, the advantages of early employment must be weighed against the potential opportunities that higher education can offer. Numerous professions, such as doctors, teachers, and lawyers, necessitate academic qualifications. These credentials act as a stepping stone to a broader range of job opportunities, often accessible to graduates. Additionally, it's crucial to factor in the competitive nature of today's job market. It's becoming increasingly competitive, and without qualifications, it becomes more challenging to stand out. Graduates, armed with their academic achievements, often have a competitive advantage, as they are seen as more skilled and capable. Furthermore, graduates typically command higher salaries, another significant factor when deciding on a career path. In conclusion, while early work experience can pave the way for career advancement and success, further studies beyond school level can also lead to a prosperous career. The decision between immediate employment and higher education is a personal one, influenced by a variety of factors. However, given the competitive nature of the job market and the enhanced opportunities and salaries that come with academic qualifications, higher education appears to be a valuable investment.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Many argue that the government should look after and give money assist for senior citizens when they are sixty years old. While others say their should put money into their fixed account when they are working early stage it will help for their olden days. I strongly agree with the former opinion because the eldery people would not manage all expenses with their own money when they are after getting old so, the government should be aided. On the one hand, the oldery people have been working when they were young while they were being contributed to the society development such as workforce. The ministry should turn thank to these kinds people how will provide monetary subsidies for their expenses. Moreover, nowadays all goods price are increased dramatically compare to past days so, the pension people are able to face trouble to fulfill their needs and wants. For example, according to the research said that all product price amount would be increased in the up coming years. Therefore, the rulling patrty should help to the senior folks. On the other hand, above sixty years age person were paid tax to the cabinet like income tax, service tax, and property tax when they were working.\", \"sampled\": \"This essay firmly advocates the view that employers should not place undue emphasis on their employees' attire. Instead, the primary focus should be on the employees' skills and the quality of their work. When employers value work performance over dress code, it fosters an environment where employees can concentrate more on their tasks rather than their appearance. Notable companies like Apple have adopted a more casual dress code, leading to a culture of innovation and creativity. Allowing employees to dress comfortably can stimulate creativity and enhance productivity. This approach recognizes that the comfort of employees can greatly influence their ability to generate innovative ideas and sustain high productivity levels. On the other hand, an intense focus on dress codes can adversely affect inclusivity and diversity in the workplace. Strict dress codes could unintentionally exclude employees from various cultural backgrounds, resulting in an unwelcoming environment. This is particularly worrisome in our progressively globalized world, where workplaces are becoming more culturally diverse. Thus, a flexible dress code policy encourages an inclusive environment where employees from different cultural backgrounds feel respected and valued. Employers should prioritize the quality of their employees' work over managing their attire. By doing this, companies can foster a more inclusive, productive, and innovative work environment, which benefits both the employees and the organization as a whole.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The issue of worsening traffic conditions around the globe has become a matter of discussion for many. In my opinion, lifestyle choices and unreliable public transport are some of the prime reasons to name the few. In this essay, I shall outline the main causes for this development and analyse the possible solutions to this problem. To start with, there are numerous predominant reasons, that could be fueling this trend. Firstly, in our modern society, working class individuals often face the lack of personal time. Thus, it is necessary to minimize its usage on various activities throughout the day. One of such activity is travelling to and back from work. For example, a recent study conducted at Stanford University, showed that 95% of people in America, use their vehicles only for driving to work. Secondly, even today, many countries are facing a number of challenges in making their public transportation more reliable and efficient. For example, during a survey when people were asked the reasons why they do not prefer buses or metros, a majority among them blamed the inefficiency and crowded stations as the primary culprit. So what can be done? I believe this problem has to be tackled on a number of levels.\", \"sampled\": \"International aid is fundamentally a voluntary act of transferring resources from one country to another, often with the aim of bolstering the recipient nation's economic growth and development. This aid can take various forms, such as monetary donations, voluntary services, and goods. It's worth noting that the act of providing aid can also indirectly benefit the donor country, fostering a mutually beneficial relationship between the two nations. Countries like the USA and the UK, despite facing their own internal issues, are renowned for their substantial contributions to international aid. For example, in 2013, the USA grappled with an alarming unemployment rate of 6%, highlighting the presence of economic struggles even in developed nations. Additionally, persistent issues like homelessness raise questions about the balance between domestic spending and international aid. However, despite these domestic challenges, countries like the USA, which donated $30 billion in aid in 2013, remain committed to international aid. This commitment is largely driven by the fact that aid, besides alleviating crises in recipient countries like India, also plays a crucial role in fortifying diplomatic relations between nations. Furthermore, aid becomes invaluable during emergencies or natural disasters, underlining the need for wealthy countries to sustain their contributions.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recently, having kids at a later age can be very controversial as some individuals adhere to this while others kick against it. However, I believe that the merits surpass the demerits there would be the availability of funds and babies would be given quality training. Therefore, in this essay, I would be explaining extensively with relevant examples and a logical conclusion would be drawn. Firstly, those who are on the opposing side believe that bringing forth offspring at older life would pose a big threat to both the baby and the mother such as making the child abnormal and maternal death. For instance, research conducted in Europe says that 80 per cent of maternal death is caused by childbirth in later life. Again, there are people who say that in old age, parents would not be physically strong to train their children. Just like yesterday, there was an analysis collated in Nigeria about kids who were involved in juvenile delinquency and a majority of them were from older parents. This is scientifically proven and also, it is certain that there are numerous actions that can be taken to avert these problems such as constant medical checkups, taking prescribed drugs and adhering to the doctor's instructions. Conversely, giving birth to children later can be very beneficial because it enables couples to boost their finance thereby saving up money or securing a lot of investment that can be used to cater for their babies.\", \"sampled\": \"Machines are progressively assuming roles and tasks that were once performed by humans, due to their ability to work at a faster pace. Take, for instance, a machine in a tobacco company that can churn out 1500 cigarettes per minute, a task that is simply impossible for human workers. This rise in technology has not only improved efficiency but has also significantly cut costs for factories, causing a noticeable change in the job market. However, this change comes with its own set of challenges. As machines take over more jobs, concerns about unemployment are growing. Job opportunities that were once open to humans are now being taken over by machines. This is especially true in the manufacturing sector, where machines are often preferred for their accuracy and speed. Consequently, the growing dependence on machines poses a serious risk to job security and employment rates. On the flip side, operating machines also incurs additional costs. Regular maintenance and servicing are necessary to keep machines in good working order. Over time, these costs can accumulate, placing a financial strain on businesses. Therefore, the shift towards machines is not entirely without cost and could even result in unexpected financial difficulties for companies. Given these factors, it's evident that government regulation is needed in the deployment of advanced technology. Policies should be put in place to ensure that technological progress does not result in job losses and increased unemployment. By finding a balance between the use of machines and the preservation of jobs, we can harness the advantages of technology while mitigating its potential downsides.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"People have different views about whether or not international aid should be continued. Those who support international aid believe that it can benefit poor countries as wee as rich ones. International aid contributes to infrastructure improvements in poor countries. For example, the world bank, whose key members are mostly rich countries, provides poor countries with low-interest loans each year. These countries can improve their infrastructure, such as schools, hospitals and public and food aid can help to save lives because in these countries, many people suffer from starvation and lack basic health care. Rich countries that provide generous aid to poor countries are also regarded as responsible members of the international community and are respected by other countries. People who think that international aid should be stopped tend to focus on the negative effects it may have. A common concern is its fairness. Even in the most developed countries, there are people living below the poverty line. Government spending on aid for poor people in other countries may be unfair to poor people in their own country Some aid is even used to serve purposed for which it is not intended. For instance, in some countries, foreign aid has been used by their government to purchase weapons or has led to more corruption. Sometimes, countries that receive international aid also find the aid unfair because it is tied to political objectives. Personally, I think it is important that the aid provided is used for the intended purpose.\", \"sampled\": \"Companies, as significant entities in society, bear social responsibilities beyond their main goal of generating profit. This perspective acknowledges that businesses, while needing to cover expenses such as employee wages, operational costs, and transportation, also have a wider role in enhancing the welfare of the community. Despite the intense competition in the marketplace, a company's commitment to social responsibility can greatly enhance its reputation and public image. One way companies can benefit society is by paying taxes, which help fund public services and infrastructure. However, numerous corporations exceed this basic duty, directly aiding nonprofit organizations that champion social causes. For example, in Canada, both Walmart and CIBC are recognized for their philanthropic endeavors, underlining the fact that businesses can and indeed do make active contributions to societal welfare. These companies, and others like them, receive tax deductions for their social contributions, which can help balance some of their substantial marketing costs. Walmart, for instance, makes donations to the Cancer Charity, while CIBC supports the Sick Kid's Foundation. Such actions not only allow these companies to fulfill their social responsibilities but also improve their reputation, making them more attractive to consumers who appreciate corporate social responsibility. In conclusion, while the primary aim of companies is to turn a profit, they also have social duties that go beyond their business interests. From paying taxes to aiding nonprofit organizations, businesses play a vital role in society. Companies like Walmart and CIBC illustrate that corporate social responsibility, far from being just an obligation, can be a strategic decision that benefits both the community and the company itself.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"In the contemporary society, it is true that advertisements play a pivotal part in our daily life. In my point of view, the growth of advertisements brings both benefits and drawbacks in equal measure to people and society as a whole. On the one hand, advertisements might have negative impacts on people and society in numerous aspects. As for individuals, increasing advertising results in people live in a consumer culture where their choice of goods might be manipulated if they were over-reliant on commercials. Nowadays, since companies focus on selling their brand images, advertisers aim to persuade us that buying those goods will eventually leads to happiness, which might become the perfect for businesses or companies to exaggerate or even distort the products’ fact. For example, marketers often bombard consumers via media outlets or by giving goody bags with a view to catching people’s attention. Furthermore, children’s mind can be easily affected by advertisements, they insist on putting pressure on parents to buy them things especially toys. Besides, some commercials have inappropriate content which may be harmful to their mental development onwards. On the other hand, regardless of those above-mentioned reasons, advertising is an astonishing form of promoting products that meets the need of customers and firms as well. Firstly, advertisements keep people always up-to-date about newly￾launched products. Hence, consumers will have a greater array of selections to decide which merchant is the best for them.\", \"sampled\": \"In recent years, a growing number of people, particularly in certain countries, have shown an increased interest in discovering the history of their homes or buildings. This trend is fuelled by several factors. A key reason is the potential to boost the property's value by highlighting its historical significance. This holds particularly true for antique property collectors, who consider the historical background of their acquisitions as a crucial part of their charm. To quench their curiosity, individuals have a variety of resources to delve into the history of their homes or buildings. Local records, like those kept by the Province Council, often prove to be an invaluable source. These property deeds can offer a detailed account of previous ownership and structural modifications. Moreover, information can also be gathered from neighbours and previous owners, who might possess firsthand knowledge or anecdotal insights about the property's past. However, it's crucial to remember that not all information collected this way is reliable or can be verified. For example, a 2002 study revealed that unverified stories tend to circulate more rapidly than those that have been fact-checked. This implies that some of the information obtained from neighbours or previous owners might be based on hearsay or conjecture rather than solid evidence. Therefore, individuals are advised to rely on evidence that they can personally verify, ensuring that the historical information they gather is both accurate and meaningful.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"In this day and age, more and more consumers use ebooks and other kinds of text on the internet instead of buying books at a bookstore. I personally believe that the merits of this trend are more significant than the demerits. On the one hand, there are certain drawbacks of storing information on the internet. First, reading texts on a smartphone or computer too much may cause some health problems. If one uses smartphones or other electronic devices too much, they may have eye problems such as short-sightedness and astigmatism. Also, sitting in one place and using a smartphone for too long may also cause back and neck problems. Second, there could be copyright issues when consumers use books online rather than paper books. Information on ebooks is often less censored than books published in the market, so many publishers and authors may lose profit from writing books. For example, in Vietnam, there are a lot of websites to read books for free, some of which are really expensive but are free online. On the other hand, it is my firm belief that the benefits of consuming books on the internet are weightier. To begin with, reading ebooks is more convenient. The main reason is that readers can find their favorite books easily on the internet so they do not have to go to a bookstore to find them. Instead, they can stay at home and read their books. Also, people just need to have smartphones or computers and wifi and they can read books whenever they want to read.\", \"sampled\": \"Over the past few centuries, human society has undergone substantial changes, resulting in a stark contrast between modern lifestyles and traditional ways of living. This transformation has led some to believe that old customs have become irrelevant. However, many traditions continue to play a significant role in shaping behaviours and communities, underscoring the need for a balanced approach to change. One key element of this balance is the preservation of cultural norms, often rooted in traditions passed down through generations. For example, Vietnamese parents, with traditional mindsets, teach their children to bow to their elders. This simple act signifies a profound respect for the elderly. Maintaining such social norms contributes not only to cultural preservation but also to the development of a civilised society. On the other hand, it's important to recognise that some traditional cultures can clash with modern ideologies, such as feminism. These traditional beliefs can sometimes hinder individuals from exercising their freedom, posing a challenge for the younger generation keen to adopt contemporary ideas. This tension between tradition and modernity is a complex issue faced by societies worldwide, necessitating careful thought and open dialogue. In conclusion, while traditions and cultural norms significantly shape behaviours, communities, and even national heritage, we must be aware of potential conflicts with modern ideologies. Achieving a balance between preserving traditions and embracing modernity is not an easy task, but it is crucial for the ongoing evolution of human society. The aim should be to ensure that traditions enrich our lives and societies, rather than restrict our potential.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, a higher number of people are finding it more interesting to know about their houses´ background. This essay will explore possible reasons behind it and the way people can investigate their history. Considering firstly the reasons why people like to find the houses´ past, I believe that it could be due to curiosity about the space they are about to buy and, also, for pecuniary reasons. In the first instance, the housing market is, these days, going through a stage where not enough new houses are being built, so when someone wants to acquire one, it is more likely that they are going to buy an old one, which needs to be refurbished. This results in an inherent curiosity about its past and history. Similarly, when an owner wants to put their house on sale, if they previously know that, for instance, a king had lived there, this announcement makes the house more valuable. Furthermore, using the library's old history books might be the best way to explore the background of the buildings. Many times the books reveal the past of a city or village and who were their inhabitants, mentioning as well the related centuries. Additionally, actual owners can ask the previous owners, neighbours or the estate agency as they might also know the historical period of the house. Lastly, google research can equally be done, with limited accuracy as online information is not always verified, thereby, not always truthful.\", \"sampled\": \"In our rapidly advancing technological age, Artificial Intelligence (AI) has become a familiar term, sparking a multitude of discussions and debates. A prevalent belief is that robots, a product of AI, will eventually take over all jobs. Indeed, robots have already infiltrated our daily lives in forms such as house cleaning bots, home security robots, and even companions for children. However, the rise of robots and AI presents a significant challenge: the potential for increased unemployment. There is a widespread fear that advanced robotics, which are even being trialled to replace human soldiers, could result in large-scale job loss. A single machine could potentially perform the tasks of 10 or more employees, rendering human labour unnecessary in many sectors. This could consequently lead to a rise in crime rates as unemployed individuals struggle to make ends meet. To address this impending issue, authorities and educational institutions have a crucial role. Authorities should encourage skill development programs to counteract the possible negative impacts of AI and robotics on employment. One such strategy could be incorporating coding courses into the educational curriculum, ensuring that individuals possess the necessary skills to adapt to a technologically advanced workforce. Additionally, education should keep pace with the latest technological trends to remain relevant. Individuals, irrespective of age, should stay abreast of these changes and seize opportunities for lifelong learning, as there is no age limit for education. By doing so, we can ensure that the rise of robots and AI does not result in mass unemployment, but instead creates new job opportunities and fosters innovation.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Nowadays nation are becoming more curious to know about the background of the place where they are residing, whether it’s a house or building. Essay will describe the reasons why the community is becoming more interested in the history of their community and the sources where they can obtain that information. In today’s world, public is getting more modernized, but they are getting more attracted to their roots as well. Individuals want to feel connected with the place by knowing the background of the house like how old the house is or who are the owners. Generally, if people are living in an old and attractive building, they can feel there can a be good store behind it and by knowing the interesting facts, it can be prestigious for them. Sometimes, there can be legal issues attached to the establishment which can issue during the sale. So, these are the primary reasons that attract the owners to find their housing history. Data can be obtained from various resources. The first resource should be elderly society of the residence as they will have more information as compared to others. Secondly, it is significant to ask neighbours because they lived in the area longer.\", \"sampled\": \"The rise of media has undoubtedly influenced society in numerous ways, one of which is its substantial contribution to children's education. Specifically, the Internet has become a potent educational instrument, providing a wealth of information across various fields. Recognizing the pivotal role that media can play in education, schools are progressively incorporating these technologies into their curriculums. The integration of advanced technology into lessons enables students to visualize and engage with their subjects in a practical and captivating manner. For example, 3-D technology can transform a child's room into a prehistoric setting, offering an immersive experience of a Jurassic world at their fingertips. Such technology can spark interest, nurture creativity, and assist in knowledge retention. However, these technologies also bring potential risks. Some teenagers may develop an addiction to online games, leading to social disconnection. Furthermore, the virtual world of the Internet can divert learners from their academic objectives. Therefore, it is vital for teachers and parents to provide appropriate guidance to mitigate these risks. Despite these challenges, the advantages of technology in education undoubtedly surpass the drawbacks. Schools and families should make a combined effort to integrate technology into their educational programs, while also monitoring children's activities to prevent media misuse. In conclusion, if used judiciously, technology can significantly enhance the learning experience for children and adolescents.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"It is perceived that, in the future, artificially intelligent robots will be doing jobs and replacing the humans in the workforce. While most menial types of occupation will be done by machines, whereas the works which required human touch are unlikely to be taken over. This essay will discuss why few jobs need to be carried out by people. Human labour will be taken over by machines that do not require complex and sophisticated skills. Big companies have replaced their human workers with machines to get more work at less expense. In various grocery stores in Japan, for instance, the cashier job is being done by artificial intelligence to provide less time-consuming and hassle-free shopping. Moreover, this creates the opportunity for the companies to adopt these practices and earn more profit. According to these reasons, the jobs which are no longer required will be replaced by robots. However, some jobs entail using human skills to work. These work-related activities are education, psychology, medicine, dentistry to name but a few. A robot can never feel what a human feel, for instance, a teacher towards his students, a doctor towards her patients, etc. Artificial intelligence can never achieve what a human has achieved over the hundreds of thousands of years of social evolution. To sum up, I think it is quite evident that artificially intelligent robots will replace the human touch in various jobs.\", \"sampled\": \"Violent video games and films with violent content are often debated because some families worry about their harmful effects on society. Some people support a ban as they believe this kind of media can damage social behaviour, while others think it is only a harmless way to relax. I agree that violent media can cause harm and should not be treated as simple entertainment. The main problem is that repeated exposure to violent scenes may encourage cruel behaviour in real life. Music and visual effects can also make violent acts look more exciting, so viewers or players may want to return to similar content again and again. For this reason, supporters of restrictions argue that violent films should not be easily available around the world, especially when their style attracts people to brutal material. Another concern is the connection between free access to violent situations and higher crime rates. If societies allow violent films and computer games to spread without any limits, this may weaken community health and create more social problems. Therefore, the view that these media have harmful effects is more convincing than the belief that they have no real social result. Some people argue that virtual events do not influence a person’s real life and that violent games can be relaxing. Adults may also watch violent movies to escape daily stress and get energy for work, but this does not prove the content is harmless. I reject this argument because violent movies and games may damage community health, and therefore they should be banned.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"In recent years, increasingly people are suffering kinds of healthy threaten. Therefore, some individuals believe that the best way for a government to solve the problem of health such as illness and disease is by reducing environmental pollution and housing problems. While I agree with this method is a nice scheme, but I consider that it will be another way to solve the issue. To begin with, it’s evident that a large group of the population have to live in slums as those places where have a housing shortage and housing prices are pretty high. Normally, unhygienic surroundings will always as accommodations for those people who couldn’t afford crazy housing prices, it would be a huge fitness hazard. As a result, the relevant departments should be preoccupied with solving the basic accommodation issue and also offering affordable housing for poor people. Besides, a wide range of viruses usually hides in unsanitary circumstances, it mixes kinds of dirty materials and creatures and would carry harmful bacteria. When people stay there for a long time, they are easy to get ill in most cases. Thus, it’s crucial to treat environmental pollution. On the other hand, raising the public’s awareness play a key role to mitigate the risk of disease. Many people have no awareness regarding sanitary aspects, so they were suffering from the disease without knowing it. Governments could prevent sickness through vaccinations and launch health awareness campaigns, it aims to spread necessary knowledge to the wider community.\", \"sampled\": \"Machines are replacing many tasks that were previously done by people in factories, and this trend has clear effects on both employment and production. In my view, automation helps manufacturers and factories because it can reduce costs and improve output, but it also creates problems for workers who lose their jobs. Therefore, this issue should not be judged only by factory performance, as the loss of work for people is also an important concern. One advantage is that automated production can increase manufacturers’ profits, although companies usually have to spend a large amount first on buying and updating machinery. Once machines replace labour, factories can reduce long-term wage costs, which is a major reason why manufacturers choose automation. This means the trend can bring two main benefits to manufacturers and factories: lower running costs and a better chance of higher profit. Another benefit is related to product quality, because computer-controlled precision equipment can reduce mistakes during production. With better machine precision, products can become more consistent, so factories may achieve better output than when many tasks rely on human labour. Factories in China are an example of places where machines are used in production, showing how this approach can become a normal part of factory work. The main disadvantage is redundancy among workers who previously had factory jobs, and this cost is very serious for those people. Unemployed former factory workers may need time to find new jobs or get training, so the change can create a difficult period for them. Some displaced local workers may also struggle to find well-paid work with their existing skills. For this reason, the advantages for manufacturers should be balanced against the negative effects on former workers.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Compulsory education is one of the main concerns people have. Particularly, many doubt whether it is important to art, drama and music classes in the curriculum. While some people might argue on this topic, I personally believe that it is important to make such classes a part of every child’s education. Those, who say that such classes must be optional, also claim that children should be able to use one of their basic rights, that is, to speek freely. Taking into consideration a child’s opinion is critically necessary, if we wish they had a bright future. For that reason, children should be able to manifest what they want and know what duties they are going to have in return. So, it is preferably that children make a decision about classes in their school schedule on their own. Basically, people, belonging to this side, propose that children should decide what to study by their selves. In this case, children who want to learn art classes can pick them. However, if they do not want to study it, such children should be allowed to skip these classes. On the other hand, there is an opinion that while children are small, they might not know what exactly is and will be important in their lives.\", \"sampled\": \"I strongly agree that celebrities known mainly for beauty and money can have a negative influence on young people. When famous people are admired more for their wealth than for real achievements, young fans may learn materialistic values instead of paying attention to their own development. This is especially worrying because many young people see public figures as models for their own lives. Rich superstars are often shown on TV with big houses, expensive cars, and luxury goods. As a result, young fans may want to buy similar things, even when they cannot really afford them. This kind of spending can become a burden for families, especially if children put pressure on their parents to support the lifestyle connected with their idols. Fame based mainly on glamour and money can also harm personal development. For example, one classmate spent a lot of money on a beautiful idol’s albums, studied less, and failed the college entrance examination. This shows that admiration for celebrities can take young people away from study and achievement, and replace hard work with unhealthy idol worship. Some people may argue that glamorous celebrities help children become more aware of appearance, especially when movie stars look attractive. However, I do not accept this view because some young people waste study time on dressing and makeup, and they may become too focused on appearance at an early age. For these reasons, I believe critics, including James Madison, may change their views after reading this argument.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Climate change has been a topic of debate in recent years. Although it is sometimes thought that the climate change crisis impacts businesses negatively, other people believe that more business opportunities would be provided due to this issue. I consider that climate change has numerous detrimental effects on businesses such as shortage of workforce and lower yields. On the one hand, many people think that the detrimental impacts of climate change on companies should not be ignored. At present, adverse weather conditions including floods ,hurricanes and snowstorms, which are results of climate change, make the working environment so dangerous for workers. This , consequently, leads to a low rate of people who are inclined to work in these circumstances. As a result, the businesses would suffer from the shortage of workforce. Moreover, for businesses such as agriculture that heavily depend on weather and natural resources, their yields would be affected and they would have a poor harvest. Taking Iran as an example, because of the drying up of the Zayandehrood River, the majority of agriculture can not reap yields at the highest level. On the other hand, it is sometimes believed that climate change offers numerous opportunities for companies.\", \"sampled\": \"Public spending on space exploration is a difficult issue because it can support science, but it also uses money that citizens may need in daily life. I partly support space research, as human progress has been driven for decades by curiosity and the desire to understand more. However, I believe governments should direct more public money to areas that bring clearer and greater benefits to citizens. Space exploration can have real value when it is linked to knowledge and development. The study of celestial bodies is important for keeping pace with global development, and aerospace technology can help people make progress. Scientists also carry out studies in laboratories on other planets to look for resources that may support life on Earth. Research into the universe can increase our understanding of how planets interact, and this is not only a matter of interest. Space-related knowledge could help predict disasters caused by factors outside Earth, so it may protect humanity in indirect ways. For this reason, rejecting all spending on space exploration would ignore the role of science in human development. Many people, however, prefer to use space-exploration funds in more practical areas because current problems are serious and urgent.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"More than any time in our society, people are seeking to know about the history behind the place where they are living. The purpose of this essay is to discuss the possible reasons why I believe such an event is occurring and inform possible sources where folk can find such information. The main reason why I think this tendency is happening these days is because people are buying old houses rather than new ones. Due to this fact they are interested in finding the history of the places before purchasing it. In addition, when folks are renewing their house, they are learning about its past in order to keep the main elements based on the original intentions when the site was built. To illustrate this, many architects are creating designs that integrate old elements of construction, such as portals and rustic stairs, with modern tendencies, as it has been a crescent request among clients. One of the major sources to find out about the history of a particular building or house is online. This might require specific info about previous owners or even when the residence was constructed. Alternatively, there may also be a file with material on the city council. However, historical facts might have not been attached to the file, only geographical data. For example, the local governments must keep the dimensions and other information of houses and buildings in the city to calculate taxes paid by the citizens.\", \"sampled\": \"Whether art should be taught to children in school or seen as a poor use of time is not a simple question. I think both views have some truth, because children have different interests, and these interests may change as they grow. For this reason, art should be part of education, but it should not be forced in exactly the same way on every pupil. Young pupils should try many school subjects, including art, before they are expected to know what they like most. A child starting primary school may not yet understand which subject they prefer or where their talents are. A broad curriculum is therefore useful, as it helps children discover their interests through real experience rather than early guesses. Art also deserves a place in school because it is an important part of society, not only a private hobby. Basic knowledge of art can help people understand the world better, since many human ideas and feelings are expressed through artistic work. Even students who do not become very interested in art can still benefit from a simple introduction to it in their early education. At the same time, art can become a waste of time for students who clearly have no interest in it after their preferences are known. If a student finds art boring but is still forced to take the course, frustration may reduce participation and productivity in class. In my view, basic art education is enough for most people, and older pupils should have more freedom to study subjects that suit their talents.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, due to the increase of reasonable concerns in society about global warming, different opinions emerged and some suggest that, rather than prevention, it is better to adjust to living with climate imbalance. In my opinion it is completely wrong, due to its slaughtering effect on inhabitant's life even with minor changes . First of all, the arguement of finding a way to live with climate change, could only be claimed by those, who could not understand the destructive impacts of small changes in globe's temperature . As many experts claimed, after the water level surge, so many cities would expected to be swallowed by oceans. Although some people could not acknowledge this matter, all living creatures are collectively under risk . Since the ice masses started to melt long while ago, this era seem to be so late for tiny resolutions. The bigger damaging factors, such as overuse of fossil fuels and waste methods other than recycling should have been adressed so long ago by the governments. Considering the probable and close effects of the climate change, the only matter mankind may get used to is the new lifestyle, which is more friendly to the balance of atmosphere. This meaures can be exemplified by sun pannels and wind turbines for producing energy and developping sustainable recycling methods for wastes. Although there are some efforts for returning this destruction, obviously it is not enough.\", \"sampled\": \"Road safety is a serious issue because road transport supports national economic development and many people rely on it every day. Many drivers use highways to go to work or to markets, so road security becomes a major concern whenever people are on the road. I partly agree that tougher penalties can improve road safety, but the punishment should match the type of driving offence. Some offences are minor, such as not wearing a helmet or not having insurance, and they should not always receive severe fines. In highly congested city or downtown areas, protective gear may seem less necessary because driving conditions are slow and restricted. For these cases, light discipline, public awareness, and teaching people about helmets may work better than strict trials that treat every mistake as a serious crime. Serious driving crimes need a different response because they show a clear disregard for road safety and harmful driving behaviour. Hot driving and heat and run should be treated as unforgivable crimes, since they put other road users in danger. Heavy charges and prison trials are reasonable for such actions, and traffic police should enforce driving rules firmly when offences are severe. Cease Licence can also be used as a penalty because it may make offenders and other drivers fear unsafe driving. This fear is useful when it stops people from repeating dangerous behaviour, but it should not replace education for less serious offences. In conclusion, harsh punishment is necessary for serious driving crimes, while public awareness should go together with it to educate people about road safety and harmful driving behaviour.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"It is a noticeable fact that robots are used in almost all domains of business and for personal work. Some people think robotics are very beneficial for human and will improve the quality of product and life, but some people opine that this will lead to job loss and machines’ dominance over humans. Here in, this essay, both views will be discussed along with my opinion. There are many advantages of robots. First is robots can work more efficiently and quickly, which is beneficial to human beings in future. People can complete their target on time with full accuracy, which improves their work position. Another benefit is that robots will perform an essential role in nuclear power and construction industries because it is very dangerous for a human to work in these companies, so robots will provide safety to all employees by performing a perilous(full of danger or risk.) task. Last but not least, it is very natural that human irritates very quickly in repetitive jobs, but it is more productive for robots to perform the same task again which not only increase the productivity but decrease the employee workload because he/ she gives command only once and machine work continuously until the job is finished.\", \"sampled\": \"The question of whether higher education or immediate employment is a better path to career success is important for many school leavers. Some people believe that starting work directly after school can be useful because young people can earn money earlier and pay for their living costs. However, I prefer attending college or university, as this route can improve knowledge and provide stronger support for long-term career development. There are understandable reasons why some school leavers choose work instead of further study. They may want to follow their interests after school, use what they learned during their studies, and start building a career through effort and experience. Bill Gate is often used as an example of someone who left Havard universtiy for an opportunity in computer software, showing that hardworking school leavers may progress in their careers. Even so, university study is the better choice for most students because it can deepen their knowledge and make them more suitable for many jobs. Some companies require academic qualifications, and hospitals and banks are examples of workplaces where such requirements are closely connected to employment.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"In this digital world, people are encircled with various types of advertisements. It is omnipresent for all, TV adverts, Social Media Marketing, billboards, Personal advertisements and many more diverse ways. while this phenomenon is escalating to extremely new level, people are in influence of it. In my view, it has more positive effects than harmful. Because of advertisements, people are aware of current products in market. At some extent, it educates the people and provides the knowledge. For an instance, In India serious disease like polio is no more and the major success goes to awareness campaign help by famous personality with the use of digital marketing and TV advertisements. Moreover, to bind user with interest usually companies display various new idea and it add major value in entertainment world. However, the critical impacts are also not avoidable.Firstly, it exposes kids and young generation towards the violence and inappropriate content sometime. Secondly, Advertisements with various discounts and offers, make people lure to do impulsive shopping. Increasing obesity is also one of consequence of advertisement of junk food. In addition, few times people get influenced by various advertisings and tend to work more to achieve never ending desire of buying stuff, this approach add up to the stress and frustration.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people argue that societies should adapt to climate change rather than try to prevent it, but I disagree with this view. Adaptation may help in the short term, but prevention is a more reasonable response overall. The first reason is that the Earth’s climate is still difficult to predict and not fully understood. Because of this uncertainty, adaptation plans that seem useful today may not solve future climate problems. For example, moving cities inland may be a response to rising sea levels, but those new locations could later suffer from reduced rainfall and water scarcity. This shows that adaptation can easily become a temporary solution to a changing problem. The second reason is that prevention deals with the causes of climate change, not only its effects. A clear example is changing to renewable energy, which can reduce human carbon output linked to global warming. In contrast, using large-scale air-conditioning in cities may reduce heat as an adaptation measure, but it does not deal with the source of the problem. For this reason, prevention seems more practical for the long-term future. This does not mean adaptation has no value, as it can be useful when communities face immediate climate pressures. However, a policy based mainly on adaptation may only follow each new effect as it appears, while prevention offers a more sensible way to limit climate change itself.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, many people assume that finding out the origin of the either house or building , where they live is an attractive thing . Personally , I believe that opinion is really accurate . Firstly , from my point of view , the main reason for this since if house’s owner has details that is sufficient about where they live , resident tend to love this place more or avoid something affects to theirs . These will help people to make some decisions which bring safety or luck and eradicate bad issues . So owner have enough time to decide should or shouldn’t live together . Besides , if the answer is not leave , which solutions are going to be carried out and adapted for longer . There are many ways to know how building was constructed and established , but the easiest solution is asking relative somebody who witnessed the building up , they seem to be ancestor and adult . But so much present house that is used for commercial problem , renting or keeping as property for the future . And when people who rent these like office , house or more and want to know about what is the history that happened in this place .The best way to facing up with things is before planning for choose and live , citizen consider and know obviously about the history .\", \"sampled\": \"Teenagers should do unpaid community work during their free time, and I strongly agree with this view. Such social service can help shape their future and character because it gives them a useful purpose beyond their normal activities. Teenagers are important assets for a nation, so their free time should be directed towards habits that benefit both themselves and other people. Community service helps adolescents appreciate the society they belong to, instead of seeing it only as a place that provides things for them. Spending free time on service is better than wasting it by wandering around, because it teaches young people to use their spare time in a meaningful way. When teenagers help older citizens, they can understand that their actions matter to people outside their own age group. Interaction with seniors is especially useful because teenagers can learn from older people’s experiences. By observing elderly people’s behaviour, adolescents may become more responsible and mature, as they see different ways of dealing with life and community matters. This kind of contact can shape their character in a practical way, not only through advice but also through regular service. Unpaid work is beneficial for both teenagers and aged people, so it should be encouraged as a normal use of free time. Older people may feel less lonely or depressed if teenagers spend time helping them, while seniors can also communicate with younger generations through this practice. In the long term, the community can benefit from teenagers who learn the habit of social service, because they may carry responsibility and maturity into later life.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"In the world of commerce, there has long been a debate about the roles and responsibilities of businesses. One commonly held view is that companies should not only generate profits but also shoulder social responsibilities. I wholeheartedly agree with this statement and believe that businesses should indeed play a significant role in social initiatives. Firstly, businesses have a profound influence on society, making them uniquely positioned to effect substantial change. As they have access to vast resources and wide networks, they can address social issues like poverty, unemployment, and education. For instance, Google's initiative \\\"Google for Education\\\" has been instrumental in providing free, quality education to millions across the globe. By undertaking such initiatives, businesses can foster a better society and also enhance their reputation, which can indirectly contribute to their financial success. Furthermore, businesses can benefit immensely from socially responsible practices. Consumers are increasingly becoming conscious of the ethical implications of their purchases. Companies that demonstrate a commitment to social responsibility can thus attract a larger customer base. For example, brands like Patagonia have seen significant growth due to their commitment to environmental sustainability. However, it is important to mention that while social responsibility is commendable, it should not compromise the primary goal of a business, which is to make profits. Striking a balance is crucial as businesses, devoid of profitability, cannot sustain their operations, let alone contribute to society.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that teenagers should take part in unpaid community work, and I agree as long as the work is not harmful. This idea is reasonable because volunteering can support the development of the community and may also help reduce unemployment among young people. However, the main condition is that teenagers must be treated fairly and not used as forced workers for tasks they do not want to do. Community progress is one clear benefit of unpaid youth work, especially when teenagers help with simple public needs. For example, they could clean public paths in their society and raise awareness about cleanliness, which would directly benefit local people. The public could also organize teenage groups for different tasks, such as cleaning, awareness activities, or education-related work. Youth unemployment is another important reason to support community work, because unemployed juveniles may have free time but no useful activity. Instead of staying inactive, they could help their association and make a small contribution to the country. This is better than spending every day on computers and video games, especially when volunteering supports charitable work and self-discipline. Teenagers should not be forced to volunteer, because forced work can reduce the value of community service. Their opinions should be considered before giving them community work, and the work should not disturb their education. For these reasons, I support unpaid community work for teenagers, but only when it is safe, voluntary, and balanced with study.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The vast majority of the population deems that the fundamental duty of the school is to convert graders into decent dwellers and employees rather than to improve them as individuals. Although I advocate strongly with the opinion of the majority, I think that schools also should care about each child as an individual. On the one hand, it is essential children are prepared to be members of society. Education gives students more skills and knowledge. Likewise, well-educated children can learn to be independent as well as this helps to find work. In this way, children become altruistic people, they tend to contribute positively to society. Moreover, schools aim to teach the young generation moral values such as tolerance and sharing. For example, our school curriculums include respecting teachers as well as other junior and senior graders, doing their homework, keeping the environment and loving their motherland. On the other hand, emphasizing the capacity of each child helps to blossom hidden abilities. If the academy implements this tendency, most graders can know anything related to their favourite subject as well as they will be professional trainees in the future. Furthermore, a huge number of students prefer to study as individuals. For instance, among schoolers conducted a survey about which one better general or individual study in the one school of the UK. This research showed that almost 70% of students voted for the letter. In conclusion, I think both of them play a vital role in our society. However, schools should turn kids into good citizens and trainees.\", \"sampled\": \"Over the last decade, a growing number of young people have chosen to delay having children, and this shift has become one of the more visible social trends. Educational priorities are frequently cited as a central reason for this delay, since many people prefer to complete a degree before taking on the responsibilities of parenthood. The view that having a baby without adequate resources or knowledge is a principal cause of poverty and child homelessness has also reinforced the idea that waiting is a responsible choice. Economic stability is widely seen as essential before starting a family, and this belief shapes the decisions of many young people who feel unprepared to raise a child without it. Teenagers, in particular, appear fearful of parenthood, influenced by societal pressure and by social media’s framing of pregnancy and parenting as burdensome or even shameful. Topics related to pregnancy have grown increasingly taboo in some communities, where open discussion of parenthood is met with criticism rather than support. The health effects of delayed childbirth raise serious concerns, particularly for women who wait until their 30s or older to become pregnant. Risks such as miscarriage and birth defects including Down syndrome are associated with pregnancies at this later stage of life, making the physical consequences of the trend significant. Women in this age group may face complications during both pregnancy and childbirth that younger mothers are less likely to encounter.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recruiting employees is an essential part of any company. It has been suggested that an interview is not a good process of selecting a new worker when there are better methods available. I strongly disagree with this idea, and in this essay, I will provide some arguments to support my opinion. Generally speaking, there is a wide range of reasons why people believe that interview is a trustworthy way to recruit an officiary. Firstly, it is the best way to get an idea about the personality and social skills of a worker. If a recruiter does face to face interaction with employees, he will know better about his attitude, communication, and efficiency. A further argument in favour of this idea is that employers will be able to know about the patience of labourers. For example, by asking questions to the interviewee, the interview will know about his traits, such as; ability to handle pressure, confidence, and think out of the box. However, it must be said that there are two points why people think that other systems are suitable than interviews. Perhaps the strongest of these is that workers can be judged by written tests. If an employee gives the written test, you might be able to learn as regards his theoretical knowledge which is also important for the company. In Addition, group discussion is another better process for electing a man. This is because you will understand them easily whether they are good or not in conversation skills. In conclusion, recruiting a better person is a very tough job for the interviewer.\", \"sampled\": \"Climate change is one of the most pressing issues facing the world today, and the central question is whether humanity should prevent it or simply learn to live with its effects. This essay argues that prevention is the only responsible path, because adaptation alone cannot undo the damage caused by unchecked emissions. Accepting climate change as an unavoidable reality risks creating conditions that are disastrous for human life. Fossil fuels are used across many sectors, from car engines to plastic production, and the demand for them continues to grow. As a result, annual carbon dioxide emissions are expected to increase exponentially, pushing global temperatures higher over time. If these greenhouse gas emissions are left unaddressed, the consequences could become severe enough to threaten entire communities and ecosystems. One of the most alarming outcomes of rising temperatures is the melting of glaciers, which would cause sea levels to rise and eventually submerge coastal cities. Earth does possess a natural ability to repair its ecosystem, but that capacity has limits when emissions continue at the current scale. Waiting for natural recovery while ignoring the source of the problem is not a credible strategy. Greater public awareness of the greenhouse effect could encourage more people to use public transportation rather than private cars, which would meaningfully reduce emissions. Governments and individuals should also work to restrict daily emissions until advanced technology is capable of tackling climate change more directly. Living with climate change rather than preventing it is not an acceptable solution, and the effort to reduce fossil fuel dependence must begin without delay.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Celebrities have always influenced people especially the youth from their accomplishments. However, in recent times it has been found that they have been followed more for their glamorous and wealthy lifestyle. I fully agree that this has given a wrong perception to young people and this essay will discuss the reasons of why it is so. To reach the maximum audience, companies approach famous personalities to advertise their product by paying them hefty money, and they do the advertisements irrespective of the consequences on the younger generation. Youngsters who are a big fan of celebrities, keep their rational thinking aside and buy the endorsed products which in many cases are not good for the health for example refined oil, which is not healthy as they contain many harmful chemicals. Another example is cosmetic products which again is harmful to the skin as it causes allergic reactions. In addition to buying unhealthy products, it has been observed that the younger generation tend to compare their life with celebrities’ life. Due to this, a bigger problem is getting created especially for middle-class parents as their children try to match the fashionable lifestyle by buying all the expensive stuff like phones, watches, which becomes an unnecessary burden for them and sometimes this obsession of having everything reaches a level where they get involved in wrong activities like duping people for money. To conclude, it is very clear that young people are getting distracted due to the glamourous lifestyle of celebrities.\", \"sampled\": \"Social media has become a central part of daily life for many people, and opinions on whether it is beneficial or harmful remain divided. It offers real advantages in terms of communication and staying connected, yet its negative effects on health and behaviour are difficult to ignore. This essay argues that social media brings genuine benefits but must be used with care. One of the strongest arguments in favour of social media is that it enables easy communication and connection with friends and family. People can access a wide range of information and news about those they care about, which would otherwise be difficult to obtain across distances. Some platforms also support community building and group activities, giving people a sense of shared purpose and belonging. On the other hand, social media can be a serious source of distraction and addiction with real consequences in everyday life. Addiction to these platforms has been linked to car accidents, where people check their phones while driving rather than focusing on the road. Beyond physical danger, social media can negatively affect mental health through cyberbullying and online harassment, leaving users feeling isolated and vulnerable. Social comparison is another concern, as constant exposure to others' curated lives can harm a person's self-esteem. Despite these drawbacks, social media does help people keep up with friends and maintain relationships that might otherwise fade. Implementing strict rules on when and where social media is used seems a practical way to preserve its benefits while reducing the harm it can cause.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"People have different views about whether students should be taught to examine and judge their high school teachers. While students’ evaluations and critiques on teachers can sometimes be useful in the educational quality, I believe this would significantly weaken teachers’ management and cause the lack of discipline for students. On the one hand, it is possible for high schools to upgrade teaching standards by telling children to examine and judge their teachers’ performance. When teachers know their students have opportunities to provide learning feedbacks for schools, it would largely stop inappropriate teaching behaviours, especially teachers’ bad habits, such as failing to diversify their teaching and not admitting their mistakes. This kind of approach may help to make sure what students learn is proper and sufficient, while pushing teachers to act as role models in front of their students. On the other hand, it perhaps causes much more serious consequences in high school education as it encourages students’ indiscipline. Allowing students to evaluate teachers’ performance, means that the traditional relationship between teachers and students would change. At school students should be taught to knowledge and respect their teachers. However, when teachers are afraid of receiving negative assessments and critiques which might affect their jobs, education would have to depend on students’ autonomy. This can significantly reduce their learning quality, especially when they slack off when studying. For instance, if disruptive students start to act up and distract their classmates’ studying, teachers would face the dilemma of whether to stop them or pretend not to see this.\", \"sampled\": \"Newspapers are widely believed to have a significant impact on how people think and form opinions about the world around them. They provide daily updates that contribute to public awareness of issues ranging from local events to global crises. Because of this reach, the content newspapers publish carries considerable weight in shaping what readers accept as true. One clear benefit of newspaper reporting is the role it played during the COVID outbreak, when treatment options and preventive measures were published daily, helping communities stay informed. Newspapers also advertise blood donation needs for rare blood groups such as AB-negative and O-negative, connecting patients with potential donors in ways that can be life-saving. These examples show that responsible newspaper reporting can serve a genuine public good. The problems arise when newspaper columnists add sensational elements to articles in order to make them more commercially appealing rather than accurate. Stories about crime and celebrities' personal affairs are sometimes highlighted with made-up content designed to attract readers rather than inform them. Newspapers can fabricate incidents entirely, and falsified articles of this kind can damage the reputations of individuals and negatively affect their lives and those close to them. Given these risks, readers are advised not to believe news articles completely without first questioning the source and the motivation behind the story. Cross-checking information against alternative sources such as television, radio, and online platforms is a practical way to test whether a report is reliable. Treating newspapers as one source among several, rather than as a final authority, is a reasonable habit for any informed reader to develop.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"It is argued that free community activities are essential for youngsters due to its advantage on both adults and society. This essay strongly agrees with above statement because it teaches young people to help people in need and help them get experience by working with different people. Free community activities assist youngsters by building their confidence toward various responsibilities. This as a result, make easy for them to deal with any situation in the future. In Oman, for instance, students cannot graduate from secondary school if they were not involved in any volunteering job. This rule introduced by the Government after a study showed volunteers are considered more responsible in Colleges and Universities compared to students whose did not participate in such community activities. Furthermore, volunteering activities let youngsters gain experience since they will be communicating with many people with different mentality, which will make them stronger and teach them how to face their problems. As well as, they would know how poor people are lived and would think to do something outstanding for them. One student, for example, has established his own community in Oman and now he has more than five branches in a number of villages in Oman and Dubai, which are serving needy people. This happened because he has participated in unpaid activities in his school days, which as a consequences he has understood the pain of poor people. In conclusion, there is a strong correlation between volunteering activities and how this shapes teenagers personality. I totally agree that working for society for free would make youngsters more responsible and give them a great experience.\", \"sampled\": \"Many people debate whether technology has made modern life more complicated rather than easier, and this essay partly agrees that it does introduce new complexity, even though its benefits remain significant and removing it entirely is not a realistic option. On the positive side, technology gives people access to large amounts of information through online databases, and a user can search for that information in less than one second through the Internet. Automation has also freed workers from monotonous and difficult tasks, which improves efficiency across society as a whole. Emails and mobile phones have replaced older methods of communication, making it faster and cheaper for people to stay in contact with one another. However, technology also creates genuine complications that affect daily life in serious ways. When people misuse the Internet, cyber attacks increase, and hackers steal personal information from ordinary users who may have little ability to protect themselves. False or misleading information circulates online without being censored, which causes harm to those who rely on it to make decisions. Genetically modified food, another product of modern technology, poses a threat to human health because of concerns about its safety, adding a layer of risk that did not exist in simpler times. Considering these points, it is clear that technology brings both convenience and new problems that people must manage carefully. The complications it creates, from cyber security risks to health concerns, are real and should not be dismissed simply because the benefits are also real. At the same time, completely removing technology from modern life is not feasible, so the focus should be on managing its risks rather than rejecting it outright.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"There has been a controversy about whether students should judge their teachers. I think the advantages of judging their teachers outweigh its disadvantages, my reasons will be explained in the following paragraphs. First, the teaching quality can be improved by making comments about their teachers. It's commonplace that not all teachers are good at teaching; they may know the concept, but they can't present that knowledge in an attractive way. As a result, some students might feel bored and start sleeping. At this point, if students can tell their teachers about the drawbacks of their lessons, the teaching quality can be promoted. Second, making comments about teachers can protect students to a large extent. Take China as an example. I grew up in China; hence, I know how the Chinese school system works. In the past, it was quite universal that teachers constantly punished their students, such as beating students, so as a further control students' thoughts. At this point, although students had higher grades, they were at the expense of their souls. On the contrary, if students were allowed to criticize their students, it was advantageous to the growth of children because they dared to challenge the authority. On the other hand, it has some setbacks as well. For example, it can negatively affect students' grades. As we know, although a strict teacher can be tiresome sometimes, he can indeed help students in their studies. Should students are taught by a rigorous teacher, their grades can increase dramatically.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that humanity should focus on adapting to climate change rather than trying to prevent it. This view is wrong, and the argument here is that prevention is far more sensible than looking for a cure after damage has already been done. Earth is the only home humankind has, and relocating to another planet is not a realistic option in the near future. Climate change affects the entire ecosystem, which contains complex subsystems that interact in ways humans do not fully understand. Because of this, it can produce domino effects that are very difficult to control or reverse once they begin. Accepting change rather than stopping it means facing consequences that may spiral well beyond what anyone can manage. The consequences of inaction are already visible in specific parts of the natural world. If polar ice is destroyed, sea levels will rise and many coastal cities could be submerged underwater. A thinner ozone layer may also have serious adverse effects on agriculture, threatening food supplies that billions of people depend on every day. There is also evidence that the planet can recover when humans reduce their harmful activities. During the pandemic, parts of it recovered, suggesting that if humankind reduces its harmful activities, the Earth may begin to recover by itself within a relatively short period. This recovery shows that prevention is not a hopeless goal, and that reducing harmful behaviour now can produce measurable results rather than forcing society to manage consequences that grow harder to reverse over time.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In recent decades, education play a crucial role in national development. Therefore, a school of thought holds that students should criticize or make a complaint to their teachers or tutors while some claim that it will lead to a loss of respect in the classroom. From my perspective, I firmly lean towards the first idea and this essay will discuss these two viewpoints in a depth. Nowadays, there is no denying that in some countries, the educational quality is significantly declined. On the one hand, in terms of the governments, they concentrate on developing the economy rather than educational quality which leads to the dissatisfaction of students in schools or even parents. Hence, whenever students do not feel satisfied with what they are learning or the teacher's attitudes towards them, they should make a complaint so that the teachers would improve it. Besides, everything is not perfect, thus, this criticism might create a motivation for the schools to tackle these problems. On the other hand, some people believe that it is disrespectful when students make a criticism on their teachers. To begin with, open discussion between the teachers and the students can be a viable solution. For instance, they can both discuss the problems, expressing their own opinions so that eventually come up with the solution. This approach has a clear advantage of making students become proactive in studying. Furthermore, teachers might have a chance to better their pedagogical skills. In this way, both the learners and the educators can achieve higher academically.\", \"sampled\": \"Free education is widely discussed as a solution to poverty in developing countries, and this essay argues that making primary school free of charge is the most effective approach. Poverty has increased drastically in many parts of the world and has affected education systems in ways that are difficult to reverse. The position here is that governments should remove school fees so that all children can access education regardless of their family's income. High poverty rates discourage families from enrolling their children in schools, and in many cases they simply cannot afford to pay for education at all. Asia provides a clear example of this problem, as high poverty rates have led to widespread illiteracy and a serious lack of knowledge among young populations. When school attendance depends on a family's ability to pay, the poorest children are consistently left behind. The United States shows that a different model is possible, since it operates a free education system from elementary school through high school, allowing children from all backgrounds to attend. A similar approach in developing countries could give poor families full access to education for their children at no cost. One proposal suggests providing up to six years of free education covering reading, writing, and numeracy, which would build a foundation that many children currently lack. Making primary education free directly addresses the financial barrier that keeps children out of school in the first place. Families who are unable to pay fees would no longer need to choose between basic needs and their children's schooling. Removing that cost is a practical way to raise literacy rates and reduce poverty across generations.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Having intention for the sport games sould take a indispensable part in pupil’s life beacuse it has more effect than we thought. Even though according to the some lazy and sluggish parents think sport is just a time sucker, experts have the opposite idea for this matter. Current social experiments performed that competitive sports hold the super affirmative effect on the children’s self-confidences together with the proffesors who led the research, say it is also very useful to learn how to behave if you are on the losing side. Moreover, it can improve your group action skills and you might learn how to be unit for beat the rival. I would like to give an instance fort his conflict. I was at the primary school when ı started attending chess competition and official basketball games. İndisputably I learned there how to cope with compulsories and if you want the achievement you need to endeavour for acquiring what you wish. Afterwards my examination results started increasing instantly. Then i perceived that it had contributed me a plethora of caractheristic qualifications in a long time. Last but not the least it gives you a power to tackle with your examinations and homeworks. Moreover if you are interested in competition games such as basketball, football, curling then you will be learned how to behave your opponent in this sitiuation your opponent would be your friends.\", \"sampled\": \"People in several countries are becoming increasingly interested in learning about the history of their houses, and a sense of security is widely cited as the key reason behind this growing curiosity. Residents often want to know about structural durability as well as available facilities and fire safety equipment before they feel confident about where they live. Awareness of local weather conditions and natural disasters such as flooding or tornadoes also motivates people to investigate a home's background before moving in. Understanding resident support policies is another important factor, since knowing these policies can help people protect their rights when unexpected situations arise. Some individuals may deliberately spread fear about a property to deceive residents into moving out, then direct those residents toward properties that the same deceivers control, which makes careful research even more necessary. Consulting the landlord or house manager directly is a recommended approach, as they are likely to hold accurate records and detailed knowledge about the property's history. Asking long-term neighbours is also a reliable method, because they often have practical local knowledge that official sources may not provide. The Internet allows people to search for housing information quickly, but the presence of fake news means that any information found online must be checked and verified with care. These research methods can give residents a well-rounded understanding of their home's history and help them feel secure in their living environment.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Individuals have segregated and polarised notions about the matter of recycling unwanted items.A segment of society reckons that it is obligation of institutes to taught their students,how to treat with wate products ,whilst,others insist by saying that it is responsibility of guardians to train their adults .While each has its own perks,I would contend it is ideal to combine both mentioned factors. The first perspective that narrates the significance of reusing the unwanted items is backed by traditionalists, they utterly believe that it is vital duty of educatinal institute to taught their students about the detrimental effects of wastes,which causes various damage effects on environment such as global warming,greenhouse effect and acidification of soil and water.According to doctrine, by reusing materials also helps them in managing their budget,to illustrate,the average cost of plastic bag in Pakistan is about 10 rupees,if they are using the same plastic until their realibity,as a result ,they can save money .However,if they makes their first priority to make their surroundings waste less,it will cause beneficial impact on society. On the flip side,the proponents of the latter idea argue it is the social responsibility of peers to ensure their children about the negative impact of wastes on their environment as well as on thier own lives.As a matter of a fact ,the core idealogy of supporting that idea exacerbates the elements of making their adults aware about the harmful effects of unwanted materials.\", \"sampled\": \"A growing number of people are choosing to have children later in life than previous generations. This shift reflects changing priorities, particularly around career and financial stability. The question is whether the benefits of delayed parenthood outweigh the costs it brings to children and families. One major reason parents delay having children is that they want to advance their careers before taking on family responsibilities. Some researchers claim that many young people work 7 days a week, and those who sustain that pace are more likely to receive promotions. Having children early could interrupt this momentum and add significant financial responsibilities that younger parents may not be prepared to handle. Young parents who have children early often struggle to balance work and childcare, which can reduce the quality of attention they give their children. Children who do not receive adequate parental time may develop psychological issues as a result. Researchers have noted that children left alone at home for long periods may develop conditions such as depression or autism. These negative effects on children are a serious concern, making delayed parenthood difficult to justify purely on career or financial grounds. While waiting can allow parents to become more financially secure, the harm caused to children who lack consistent parental presence is a significant drawback. The disadvantages of delaying parenthood outweigh the advantages when the psychological wellbeing of children is taken into account.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"A group of people believe that the power of connection globally in both business and cultural prospects has advantages for countries’ improvement. While another group of people are against that idea. This essay explains the positive and negative impacts for the nationalities and also shows my strongly agree point of view. In the hyper-connected world, businessmen and all population in any occupation sectors need to communicate with clients, suppliers, and also accompanies in many countries around the globe. Having more relationships to others can open up the new opportunities which lead to gaining higher revenues in some cases. In particular, for the import-export industry, the international links are significantly important. Contacting to various suppliers makes the businessmen have stronger negotiate power and they are likely to receive good quality products with lower costs; then, they can get higher margins and profits from selling. Additionally, they can be the powerful market leaders from lower prices competition. As a result, these successful businesses bring positive influences on national development. On the other hand, some people think that there are several drawbacks from the worldwide contacts. They are quite conservative and hard to accept the modern ideas. Other than that, they believe that interfering from foreigners brings the loss of national identities especially cultural prospect. Different beliefs and ethnics from other countries can change people’s way of thinking which direct to the merging of original nationality cultures. For instance, the K-Pop identity is changing the styles of music in many countries worldwide. Nowadays, the young generation in Thailand does not know about folk song or country music anymore.\", \"sampled\": \"There is an ongoing debate about whether modern technology widens the gap between rich and poor or whether it gives economically disadvantaged people more opportunities. Some people hold the first view, pointing to the high cost of new devices and unequal access to digital education. This essay considers both sides before explaining why technology has, on balance, improved life across social groups. Those who argue that technology increases inequality point to the fact that many new gadgets are so expensive that only wealthy members of society can purchase them. Modern kitchen appliances, for instance, are used regularly by wealthy people while poorer households cannot afford them. Private schools teach students how to use modern devices, yet public schools largely do not, which limits the technological skills available to children from poorer backgrounds. On the other side, there is strong evidence that technology has made certain tools far more accessible over time. Owning a land phone at home was once expensive, but today almost everyone has a personal phone, regardless of income. The internet has also changed how people communicate, allowing them to stay in contact with friends and family worldwide through online services that are widely available and often low in cost. Considering both arguments, the view that technology reduces class differences appears more convincing when the long-term pattern of falling prices and spreading access is considered. Rich people may adopt new gadgets first, but those same technologies tend to reach poor people over time. This essay argues that technology has improved quality of life across all social sectors and has reduced, rather than deepened, differences between classes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Advertisement is helping the manufacture to promoting and marketing their product and service to consumers. It has brought the significant changes about imposing the development of consuming and trading in the community. It is believed this has both positive and negative impacts on our lives, and personally. I totally agree with this statement. On the one hand, advertising activities contribute to economic development by stimulating the market. To begin with, advertising is one of the main means for marketing industrial goods and services without the help aggressive advertising businesses, so it's difficult to public their brands and products. Moreover, the more they advertise, the more opportunities they have to sell their goods better. As the consumer's perspective, the advertisement also plays an important role in making a right choice. The advertising can be informative and offers a wider range of option that suitable for each consumer’s interests and values. On the other hand, the cost is increasing due to too much advertising, which will cause a greater burden for both customers and the companies. Furthermore, there is also a problem that some advertising is not suitable for kids or if they watch too much, it would be a negative affects.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people argue that we should accept climate change as an unavoidable reality rather than trying to fight it. The author believes a certain level of acceptance is pragmatic but meaningful steps are important. Those who favor acceptance may not consider severe future impacts that will affect everyone. Scientists forecast sea level is rapidly increasing at or above 3 centimetres per year. The sea will swallow the land within the next century if this trend continues unchecked. Mother Nature is changing in ways that will permanently alter where and how people can live. Those who want to fight global warming must understand they have to fight against government bodies and multimillion companies. Governments do not follow legislation to protect the environment, and large business chains are willing to invest money so they do not have to spend on carbon emissions. For a developing country, pollution is an inherent situation because their GDP is lower than a developed country, which makes the challenge even harder. Governments and major companies are responsible for global warming. if citizens do not raise their voice against pollution, government will simply ignore the issue. Concerned people should continue to raise their voice against climate change rather than giving up. The situation is serious, but public pressure remains the most direct way to push for real action.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Many people believe that over the next ten years robots will handle most of the jobs that is handled by humans. This essay will discuss both advantages and disadvantages in the forthcoming paragraphs. To begin with, there are many advantages of robots. Firstly, they work more accurately and efficiently with the faster speed and never tired. Secondly, robots give more productivity than mankind. Bots do not need a break to refresh their mind. Moreover, they performed repetitive tasks whereas human gets easily bore by doing the same task. For instance, last month, an article was published in the man versus robots magazine in this article author explains how a robot can increase the profit to the organization. On the contrary, there are some drawbacks to having robots at working firms. First, It will lead to unemployment. A single robot can do a task that is done by fifteen workers. For instance, In a Chandi firm, there is only 4 manpower and a robot. A robot is handling the work of 10 employees. As a result, a lack of employment will occur. Second, It will need more electricity to run a robot. Sometimes, if there will not electricity in the company the work will degrade. Moreover, people will become dependent. People will depend on robots for every task. To conclude, robots are taking place of men. There are numerous merits of having robots at the factory such as increase productivity, accurate results and so on.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe students should work part-time alongside their studies, while others argue students should focus only on reviewing or studying. This question touches on how young people can best prepare for adult life without harming their academic progress. I agree with the view that students should try to study and work to learn independence and responsibility, provided they keep a reasonable schedule. Working while studying provides an opportunity to earn money, which can be a practical reason for many students to seek employment. Part-time work helps students become independent by giving them their own income and reducing reliance on family support. For instance, some freshmen feel shy asking parents for money and therefore seek jobs to cover daily costs. My neighbor Muhlisa works as a waiter because she needs money for her studies, and her case shows how part-time work increases students’ experience in real workplaces. She has learned to manage customers and organize her time, which builds skills that classrooms alone cannot offer. These early responsibilities can shape a more mature outlook before students enter full-time careers. However, working may negatively impact education if students work more than 5 or 6 hours after school. This is because long working hours can cause tiredness during the day. Part-time work can leave no time for homework, affecting future prospects when grades begin to drop. For this reason, students should maintain a balance between study and work for career benefits and keep employment limited enough to protect their learning.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, the world has become one united opt to technological development, which brings all nations together. Some advocate that international interaction has a great impact on the environment. While others disagree, and believe that it can play an integral role in the commercial and business sectors. I am convinced with both notions, and my contention will be further explained in the following lines. On the one hand, international cooperation has an indispensable role in economic prosperity for several reasons. Firstly, it can encourage investments, which participate in increasing the employment rate. Secondly, it can introduce a foreign currency, that can establish a solid base, and strengthen the infrastructure of the government. Furthermore, it can act as a tool for soaring the income of the individuals, which in turn will reflect on the citizen's habits, and lifestyle. For instance, the Egyptian authority has signed a plethora of conventions with a foreign state like Japan; in order to introduce the Japanese strategies and machines to deal with economic hurdles in Egypt. Hence, with international exchange a myriad of advantages will be reflected on the country in numerous sectors not only in business. On the other hand, currently, climate change and environmental topics have been heated constantly. Many urge that the foreign conventions will be of great impact on our ecosystem. To illustrate, the presence of novel technical devices have brought numerous inventions that may aid in reducing the contamination that has been created by using fuel, and charcoal.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people argue that advertising does little more than manipulate consumers, yet the evidence suggests otherwise. The author agrees that advertisements effectively channel shoppers' choices in a world where modern humans lead a consumer culture due to social media and commercials. Enterprises target younger consumers with adverts on platforms like Instagram and Telegram, making influence hard to avoid. The flooding of new products with gimmicky features makes influence inevitable for young internet users, and this is reflected in solid data. Nearly 60% of UK internet users buy at least one or two items monthly under direct influence of online ads. Such figures show that advertising does not simply entertain, it drives what people purchase on a regular basis. Advertisements make customers aware of features, options, and models of products, which can lead to more rational decisions. Informative adverts allow people to compare prices and features to make better decisions, and ad-driven shopping reduces time spent in physical stores because people know what they want beforehand. The author uses a personal example of buying a mobile phone to illustrate reduced shopping time, showing how prior knowledge shapes efficient choices. Advertisements stimulate demand and interest in newly produced items, which increases sales figures and boosts the economy. A minority group of people do not trust adverts, but the broader pattern remains clear. When nearly 60% of UK internet users make monthly purchases influenced by online ads, it is hard to deny that advertising effectively guides consumer behaviour.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In the modern era, most of people deem that robots will do all kinds of work in the upcoming years. In my opinion, it is true to some extent as technology will be increased in the future, whereas for automation of the robots there will be also a need of human beings. On the one hand individuals predict that robots will take the place of humans in the future and do their jobs because technological demands will be enhanced among the people. To make it more clear, there are many industrial areas where all type of work is operated by machines, and these technical machines work may be improved by using robots in the future as robots are the scientific gadgets who do more efficient work than the machines or even human beings, so there need will increase in the modern time period. For example, according to a survey, in many businesses such as automobiles, maximum work is done by the help of robots and these type of business will accelerate in the future, that is why they require more robots. On the other hand, they will not completely replace the human beings as they are also the human creatures, and somewhat depend on the person to operate them correctly.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that governments should concentrate on reducing environmental pollution and housing problems to prevent illness. I agree with this view because environmental problems and housing conditions are two main sources of illness. While other public health measures exist, addressing these root causes can bring widespread benefits to residents. Industries using fossil fuels emit pollutants into water and air, harming air quality and water safety. Long-term exposure to excessive carbon dioxide can cause respiratory diseases, which shows a direct link between pollution and health. Governments should limit industrial fossil fuel consumption and pollutant release to protect citizens from such preventable conditions. Noise pollution and limited outdoor exercise spaces threaten living conditions and health. Noise pollution can reduce sleep quality and increase risk of depression and headaches, making housing environments essential to public well-being. Only government efforts to improve housing conditions can relieve or solve these problems, as individual actions are often insufficient. Reducing pollutant emissions and resolving housing problems are two main methods to improve public health. Governments can also improve public health through health education, food safety supervision, and clean energy development, but these should complement rather than replace the core focus. By addressing pollution and housing directly, authorities can create healthier living environments for all citizens.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In recent years, a noticeable shift in family planning has emerged and many people prefer to have children at their later stage in life. Several reasons are responsible for this, and I believe that it has more consequences. Firstly, couples want to spend quality time together before having their first child. This is because they can enjoy their life with each other, and be able to know one another. In this way, mutual understanding becomes stronger, and enjoy the time they spend with their young ones. For example, research studies have shown that couples who spend more time together understanding each other can later take good care of their babies and there is also less chance of divorce rate after having children birth. Secondly, people choose to concentrate on their careers first and then have babies it is because they can take better care of their juveniles. Additionally, they think having children can escalate the expenditures and financial responsibilities of parents. Therefore, they achieve a stable financial position before starting the family as a result this can enable parents to give better education and lifestyle to their children.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe charities should only help within their own country, while others think they should give aid globally to people in great need. It is true that help should be extended worldwide by nonprofit organizations. Providing aid to local communities can strengthen the economy quickly, but this view overlooks wider suffering. Developed countries have wealthy people who own well-known foundations, and these can support local needs effectively. Aid from rich countries reaches the poor quickly and brings prosperity, which makes a domestic focus seem sensible. However, without global aid, rich countries grow richer and poor countries' living standards decline. Some African countries lack access to clean drinking water, and other parts of the world suffer from lack of clothes, housing, healthcare, and education. Bill Gates and Roger Federer's foundation works on education and healthcare in Kenya and Nigeria, showing how international efforts address severe shortages. These problems cannot be solved if charities only look inward. The essay concludes that relief should be sent wherever it is needed to achieve global balance. Nonprofits sending aid to proper places can reduce the gap between nations. Future balance in lifestyle becomes possible when help crosses borders.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"From the earlier times people have been criming as a result of this behaviour they have been punishment. We all born for living a life. However some of us prefer to live in prison because of their crimes others are living in a good conditions. I believe that imprisonment is the best solution to crimes. But clasify for the them is better than to regain the people to life. For example if I person steal a bread to eat first, the judge should find the reason and then decide because this person maybe is not a guilty 100%. In prison with a education support he or she can gain a skill then maybe after that he or she can be find a job easily when he/she be free again. But I strongly believe that there is no excuse for a killing a person for there people education is not enough they have to stay in prison end of their life. Secondly, in some countries like Scandinavian,they educate the people who are in prison. They belive it is best way to give them a second change to hold their life. In fact georophical factors are very important because I believe it is not possible for the other regions. Actually as I mentioned before some factors are very important. In conclusion I don't agree to educate them prison is strongly necessraly for the crime.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that raising the cost of fuel is the best way to solve global environmental problems. While higher fuel prices can bring some benefits, I do not think they are the single best solution. Increasing fuel costs can push factories and corporations to reduce their excessive consumption. When fuel becomes more expensive, these large consumers often look for ways to cut their consumption to save money. This reduced demand can then encourage manufacturers or researchers to develop green energy sources. It can also help limit the rapid depletion of fossil fuels, which is a serious environmental concern. Governments have already used policies to increase prices on fuel imports or production to try to control usage. These actions show that many policy makers see fuel pricing as a useful tool. However, I disagree that this approach is the best answer to our environmental challenges. Relying only on higher costs does not address the root problem and can place a heavy burden on ordinary people. A better alternative is to focus directly on renewable energy sources like solar energy and wind energy. These technologies can replace fuel in many areas without generating emissions. A 2020 government report noted that using solar energy in buildings helps reduce pollution significantly. Shifting investment and support toward these cleaner options offers a more direct and sustainable path forward than simply making fuel more expensive.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In this day and age, the environment is becoming more polluted because one of the reasons is that the growth in making customer products. There are a number of causes of this and the problem can be resolved by several solutions. It is undeniable that some reasons of the production of goods have much discussed. The most obvious evidence is that factories discharge sewage into the ocean and exhaust fumes in the air. In fact, the processes of making goods need to release waste materials; therefore, these waste materials exert adverse effects on the nature. Furthermore, the appearance of factories and companies decrease the nature lands, including forests or villages. For example, in a rural area of Vietnam, a large number of trees demolished in a forest is to erect factories with the aim of producing iron, which is used for manufacturing cars. Hence, citizens in there will lose an amount of Oxy from the trees. There are a number of solutions to reduce the detrimental impacts. First, governments should impose strict laws related to the discharge of waste to manufacturers. If the amount of waste and exhaust fumes is higher than regulatory amount, the factories or companies have to be punished. This leads to that the governments may control the waste and minimize the pollution. Second, governments can request and encourage factories and the inhabitants grow plants.\", \"sampled\": \"People live in an era of immediacy with low patience, and this shift has reshaped daily expectations. Technological advancement is driven by instant services, which now deliver everything from entertainment to shopping within seconds. A decade ago people lined up for movie tickets; now they buy online and choose seats and show how quickly convenience has become normal. A UK study found Technological advancement is the main reason for impatience, confirming that faster digital tools have shortened attention spans. People have become accustomed to immediate services and delays cause anger, making even small interruptions feel unacceptable. Dependence on fast technology makes people irritable and anxious when delays occur, and this reaction has grown stronger as services speed up. Instant services created more jobs in customer service, which means the demand for speed has expanded employment opportunities. Companies train and hire more people for fast services like concierge, building entire roles around rapid response. This growth shows that the push for immediacy does not only replace human workers but can also generate new positions. Delays lead to bad and impolite behavior, and the author witnessed a man shouting at a barista due to a delay in his order. Such scenes reflect a wider social cost of living with constant speed, yet the author believes this trend is positive despite some drawbacks. The convenience and job creation brought by instant services outweigh the occasional loss of composure.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In some nations, increasingly people are becoming interested in searching about the history of the homes or building they live in. There are several reasons why looking about the history of the house is becoming more common among people, and there are differents ways can helps people to research about their house or buliding information. To begin with, there are a various reasons why individuals are becoming more interested in finding out about the house their live in. The primary reasons is related safety rules. To illustrate, some people are desrie to makes improvments in their house, and then they should know about the previous construction materials of their accommodations. For example, some people want to build a new floors or sports facilities in their house. Consequently, people want to ensuring their houses after this redevelopments do not collapse. Curiosity is another reason why people are becoming more interested in order to search about their homes. This is due to the fact that, houses tell us about people who live in this house in the past through .Futhermore, architecture style gives people to learn about cultures. On the other hand, there are many methods people can find information about their houses. The first method asking the pervious owners of the houses about information. The second way, asking people in the neighborhood namely, neighbors, local supermakerts, local libraries. The third way, some websites provide information about the houses. For instance, BBV website gives people in the Saudi Arabia about the materials used in the house, the data of founded the house, the number of owners of the house.\", \"sampled\": \"Many people believe that teenagers should spend some of their free time doing unpaid community work, and I completely agree with this idea. This kind of activity can bring important social benefits for the young people themselves. It can also create positive changes for the wider community in ways that are often overlooked. One clear advantage is that community work helps teenagers build relationships that are useful for their future life. A study by London University found that unpaid community service reduced screen time among young participants. The same research also showed that it helped kids communicate confidently and respectfully to others, which is a valuable social skill. These findings suggest that stepping away from screens and into real-world tasks can improve how teenagers connect with people around them. Beyond personal development, unpaid services help children understand the feelings of vulnerable members in society. This kind of work also promotes caring for the elderly, which is a quality that many communities need more of. A survey in Melbourne found a dramatic reduction in old age home residents, and this change was linked to high school students doing social service as part of their curriculum. When young people spend time with the elderly, it seems to strengthen family-like bonds that reduce the need for institutional care. In my view, the evidence strongly supports the idea that unpaid community work benefits both youth and society. Teenagers gain better communication habits and a clearer sense of empathy, while society receives practical help and stronger connections between generations. Making such activities a normal part of teenage life could therefore be a wise step for any country that wants to invest in its future.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Some people believe the government should finance social services like retirement pensions for adults instead of spending its budget on public services like agent needs while others say individuals should save their funds during their working years. This essay will delve into the upcoming paragraph and subsequently will explore perspective in turns. On the one hand, I believe government should definitely allocate a large part of its budget to public services. The government economic sector determines the overall quality of life, ensuring the basic sector such as schools, hospitals and roads are available to all the citizens irrespective of their income and social status. Public services are needs are primary needs of society and thus need proper funding. For example, the absence of medicine in any country will create significant problems. That's why the government should adequately finance public services in the first place. Becoming old is a normal thing for everyone however younger citizen are participate important role in the country's economic growth. On the other hand, retired adults are a vital part of every family. Who encourage their close ones as well as provide their experiences. I think adult must save their funds during their working years. However old people also are an integrated part of society. Some ancient could save their money but most of the old citizens could not save money because of high expenses. I think the government should provide free medicine and treatments for low-income citizens.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that the most effective method for teaching children how to cooperate is through team sports at school. I agree with this view because such activities place young people in situations where they must work closely with others to succeed. The experiences gained from playing sports like football and basketball can build essential teamwork habits that last a lifetime. Team sports expose children to working with different personalities toward a specific goal. In football and basketball, teams have more than four players with separate opinions, personalities, and skills. Without a plan and adherence to it, team play often results in a poor performance. So children quickly learn that cooperation is not optional but necessary. Another key benefit is that team sports help children learn conflict resolution skills. Differing ideas in a team can lead to arguments, and young players must find ways to settle these disagreements to keep functioning. A coach often serves as a mentor in handling disagreements and guides children through the process of listening and compromising until it becomes natural. I saw this process firsthand when I was part of a sports team at a younger age where a coach taught conflict management. Our team practiced conflict resolution skills, leading to quick resolution of quarrels that might otherwise have disrupted our matches. A team of footballers once attributed their success to teamwork, and I believe my own early lessons in cooperation came directly from those shared sporting challenges.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Earning a high paycheck, in this contemporary era, is a dream of everyone. It is believed by a few individuals that leaders of big giant companies have high income unlike other employees, which is quintessential while others assert that it is unjustifiable.This essay will discuss not only both views, but also reasons to justify the former view as more fruitful. Commencing with pre-eminent favourable aspects of maximum earnings of higher management in an organisation. The first tenable etiology is that salary is decided on the basis as the higher authority is responsible to set the vision and take crucial decisions. As top-tier officials not only possess the talent to work on visionary tasks such as managing different projects, hiring the right candidate, but also show the greatest contribution to run the company, by dint of which higher salaries are bestowed to them by the authority. In I.T companies, for instance, 90% of directors are achieving extremely high salaries because they have remarkable qualities to make it done; Therefore, owing to the pivotal role in a firm , it is imperative to impart high wages to chief executives.\", \"sampled\": \"Newspapers are a familiar part of daily life and the press has existed for a long time. Reading newspapers remains a daily habit, especially for seniors who value the routine. These publications cover politics, daily life, and advertisements, forming a broad picture of current events. Newspapers are considered reliable because they follow a editorial standards that builds trust. News must be checked and verified before publishing in the press, which adds a layer of accountability. This careful process helps explain why many people still turn to traditional newspapers for accurate information. Online news outlets and Facebook have grown popular due to technology and the internet, changing how news spreads. New platforms can deliver news quickly and easily and reach audiences without the delays of print. For example, Vietnamese people can get information about the US presidential election via the internet and smartphones, and the whole election process can be watched live online. The author believes new platforms will replace traditional newspapers because they are favored for availability and accessibility. The shift toward instant digital access makes the older medium seem slow by comparison. As technology continues to advance, the convenience of online sources will likely displace printed news.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In the thriving economic democracy, there is a race between advertising agencies in producing commercials leading to a rising amount of content shown in multimedia. Although the decisions of purchasers are mostly based on their will, I fairly assert that advertisement passively affect their intentions to a certain extent. Therefore, the government should limit this development according to the negative impacts it delivers. On the one hand, advertisements gradually insert their own ideology to consumers which sometimes is not appropriate for them. Firstly, producers broadcast their promotions frequently, forcing watchers to remember their stories without awareness. Moreover, producers always insert irrelevant positive feelings such as happiness or luxury when using their products to encourage users. Consequently, buyers may involve unnecessary desires corresponding to those strategies. For instance, there is always a multitude of people queuing to obtain new iPhone models each year to become satisfied when owning the latest devices, which always be promised in Apple’s ads, instead of the upgrades they actually entail. On the other hand, to prevent the aforementioned misunderstands, ad scenarios should be carefully reviewed. These activities will authorize which impressions are accurate when promoting, minimizing the flowery influences. Besides, broadcasters should diminish the frequency of ads to lessen their manipulating effect which may navigate the client's choices far from their demand. Furthermore, with the drop in promotions, more spaces are available for valuable shows for end-users, especially children and youngsters who prefer knowledge to adult products.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that technology is increasing the gap between the rich and the poor, while others contend it has the opposite effect. Certain advancements in automation have certainly created products that are unaffordable for specific groups of people. In my opinion, however, technology has largely helped reduce the divide between high and low-class people. On one hand, there is an argument that expensive technology exacerbates social inequality. For example, consumer gadgets like iPhones are often unaffordable for individuals from middle and low-class backgrounds. This problem also extends to healthcare, where expensive medicines and medical treatments are frequently inaccessible to people living in poverty, as seen with young children in my country dying from serious diseases because they cannot afford high-priced care. On the other hand, technology offers many benefits that are accessible to all social classes. Mechanization and computers are often seen as significant benefits for everyone, not just the wealthy. Some major organizations prioritize their customers over product costs, and alternative brands often provide similar functions to more expensive gadgets and medicines, making them more accessible to the general population. Technology has played an important role in bridging the gap rather than widening it. Publicly available services, such as free Wi-Fi at railway stations and airports, help those who cannot afford a private internet connection. These developments show that technological progress does not exclusively benefit the rich. Therefore, I believe that technology has been an important force in narrowing the economic and social distance between different classes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Advertising induces a customer towards buying a product. There is a school of thought, which, is of the opinion that the consumer's decision-making process is highly influenced by advertising , whereas,there are people who believe the contrary. In my essay, I shall discuss both views with relevant examples and would share my thoughts on the matter. First and foremost, product communication informs the product benefits. However, also creates an aspiration among the expected buyers for owning it. Various forms of print, audio and visual effects are put to use, to ensure that the need of the product is created and the end user is driven towards buying the same. For example, it is the advertisements of aspiration which create brand value for Apple phones, encouraging the potential purchase of the mobile. All big brands use branding as a tool to drive sales by impacting psychology. This is mostly true in consumer products and packaged goods. On the contrary, there are instances where the quality of the product speaks about itself and it is a real need of the customer, and benefits, that determine the purchasing intent. If a manufacturer is honest about quality, he does not need to advertise to influence minds, the customer will buy owing to its unwavering features and value. Industrial products , and machinery , come under this category.\", \"sampled\": \"Global warming has received significant public attention because of its major effects on human life. Some people support finding ways to live with climate change rather than attempting to prevent it. I believe that it is better to find solutions to combat global warming than to accept its consequences. One argument for adapting is the belief that humans are not powerful enough to resist the laws of nature. For instance, Japan’s annual earthquakes and the yearly typhoons in Asian coastal countries are often presented as examples of unavoidable natural events. Proponents of co-existence also suggest that modern technology, such as the geo-storm system being examined to lessen storm effects, makes living with climate change easier. On the other hand, a strong counterargument is that human activities are the primary cause of this environmental issue. Vehicle exhaust and factory emissions are major contributors to the problem, meaning humans must take responsibility. This perspective holds that we must address our environmental impact to avoid major and irreversible damage. The consequences of inaction are severe, as rising temperatures and natural disasters have already led to the extinction of many species. This loss is costly for both the ecosystem and humanity itself. Therefore, governments and corporations can improve the situation by considering measures to fight climate change rather than just adapting to it.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"There are split opinion regarding nutrimental food. It has been argued that, regime rules and regulation for nutritious food within a country would enhance a good and healtheir lifestyle for human being. While, other group of people think, it up to individuals to determine how they want to live their lives. Health, in my prespective, primarily a personal obligations also, the government has number of other oprions for encouraging citizens to healtheir life. This essay will substantiate views in the following paragraph with relevant examples. On the one hand , a group of intellectuals assert that, administration have to make some laws for the most nutritious foods. It is not a neglectable the fact that the healthy diet is good for our body to maintain a healthy metabolic rate along with some other benefits. For example, a few decades ago one law was passed for good health that prohibits activities that has detrimental effects on life such as, drug, alcohol, and smoking are prohibited for certain countries together with few states. In addition in few countries government levies heavy taxes on fast food in order to discourage people from eating it. Hence, these strategies can have positive effects on an individuals health.\", \"sampled\": \"In recent years, it has become more common for people to have children later in life compared to previous generations. This trend presents both advantages and disadvantages for parents and their offspring. I believe that the benefits of delaying parenthood ultimately outweigh the drawbacks. One of the main advantages of having children at an older age is greater financial stability, which can make raising a child easier. For instance, Vivek and Prerna were able to provide well for their baby because they had a good income. People in higher corporate positions may also have fewer working hours, allowing them more time to spend with a newborn. However, there are disadvantages, particularly concerning health and long-term family dynamics. Women who become pregnant at a later age can face more complications during their pregnancy. Susan, for example, was 38 and had a painful pregnancy that required regular hospital visits. Another issue is the potential for parents to become dependent on their children at a young age. This situation can place a burden on the child early in their adult life. Ram’s parents retired and became dependent on him when he was just 18, illustrating this difficulty.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Metropolitan cities suffer a deficiency of residential places for general public, either it is the sole responsibilty of government to deal with the shortge or not is a debatable topic these days. It should mainly depend upon income of people to decide whether the housing is provided or not. Major cities have many problems,housing being one of them. The main issues are lack of places for residential buildings and ever increasing commercial areas. This has resulted in people not having homes so they have to hire places and areas close to their civic centre are very expensive so they have to rent places at distant places . Furthermore , it will lead to the added cost of living. This problem can be solved at government level through adequate legislation, that a certain part of the city should be reserved for housing purposes . In addition ,huge fine can be imposed on any encroachments towards residential areas. General public is paying taxes to the authorities so it is their responsibility to look after the people. For instance, low income or jobless people who are unable to build their homes should be facilitated , otherwise these people will spend their lives on road and be prone to immoral activities and drugs .\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that increasing the cost of fuel is the most effective method for addressing global environmental problems. While raising the price of petrol and diesel might offer some benefits, I believe this is not the only solution to these issues. A range of alternative measures should also be considered by governments to protect our planet. On one hand, making fuel more expensive could encourage individuals to use their cars less frequently, which would be a positive step. This reduction in private vehicle usage would lead to a decrease in the amount of carbon dioxide emitted into the atmosphere. However, such a policy can negatively impact poor and middle-class families who may rely on their cars for essential travel. Governments have an important role to play by investing in alternative solutions beyond just fuel costs. They should support the development of renewable energy sources, including wind, tide and solar power. For instance, solar power in the United States has already demonstrated its potential as an efficient method for generating clean energy. Another effective strategy is to improve public transportation systems while also making them cheaper for commuters. This would encourage more people to use buses and trains, which helps to clean the environment and reduces traffic congestion. Governments could also take stronger action, such as banning harmful materials like plastic, as the use of non-biodegradable items worsens climate conditions.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Several countries have a growing proportion of youngsters who are aged 15 and younger. In my opinion, young children have a huge impact on their countries in the current life and in the future, as they will be the people who rule that places . Today, teenagers have more effect on their living places due to their activities, as well as they will be the next generation who helps to become more developed. Children's activities may have a different purpose in their life but they should help the countries where they reside.For example, in China, the young generation under the ages of 15 and 13 already knows how to work in a team and if they improve their abilities after several years they can create some new technology. As a result, children help their province from a young age and these impacts on their province will grow. Moreover, this young generation will be the rulers of their own country as a doctor, police officers and even presidents but not all of that young heirs will reach their destination due to the condition of the country. In that situation, they will adapt how to behave themselves first and start to be more familiar with their own culture. For instance, not all heir know how to act when they have new events in their places.\", \"sampled\": \"Studies indicate that a majority of criminals have a low level of education, which has led to debate. Some people believe the most effective method to lower crime is to provide education to prisoners. I agree that this is a good idea that can reduce re-offending and help ex-convicts build a peaceful future. The main goal of educating prisoners is to help them secure stable employment after they are released from custody. A lack of legitimate job opportunities can lead former prisoners to re-engage in criminal activities simply to meet their basic needs. Illiteracy can make it even more difficult for ex-offenders to find work in a competitive market. Providing special education inside prisons can directly address these issues by increasing an inmate's knowledge and job prospects. For instance, Robert Dawe, a famous actor, is often cited as someone who committed crimes but later found a job in the film industry after receiving special education. In India, educational programs designed specifically to reintegrate criminals into society have proven to be successful. Focusing on education within the prison system is an effective strategy for crime reduction. By equipping inmates with skills and knowledge, society can help them find employment and avoid returning to a life of crime. This approach not only lowers recidivism rates but also provides a pathway for ex-convicts to achieve a more stable and peaceful future.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Some people think artificial intelligence robots will take the position of humans jobs in the near future. Though some think all works can be replaced by robots, but it is not possible to get detailed, accurate finish jobs like humans. This essay will discuss that only the minority number of jobs will be done by humans. Robots are likely to take the jobs of humans in most sectors which does not require people’s input throughout the process and work duration. These days, every company wants their job done quickly and cost effective. Hence, using robots instead of humans helps them to increase their production value and decrease labour costs. For example, a year end article published by Toyota Mortars mentioned that they had to meet the demands of consumers so machines and robots in the assembly line helped them produce a surplus quantity of engines. In addition, production line requires only power and timely supervision, these factors eradicate giving humans monthly salaries and statutory pays. For these reasons, the workforce will be replaced by robots which needs replacement. Conversely, not all jobs can be done by robots even if they are well versed and work efficiently. Some works require human’s mind and critical thinking.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that a university degree is the best path to a successful career. Others think it is better to get a job immediately after finishing school to gain practical experience. I believe that a university education is the best way to secure a good profession and its benefits. A main advantage of higher education is that it improves job prospects. A bachelor's degree can increase employment chances in reputable companies. For instance, employers such as medical directors will seek workers with the right qualifications, such as a medical diploma for a doctor position. On the other hand, some argue that starting work after secondary school builds more years of valuable experience. This early entry into work might be beneficial for certain roles. Professions like drivers or nail technicians may benefit more from years of practice and hands-on experience rather than formal study. Despite this, I maintain that higher education typically leads to better salaries and better job opportunities. Skilled workers with degrees usually earn more than unskilled labourers without them. In most developed countries, a professional such as a banker will have a much higher income than a cleaner, which reinforces my view that a degree provides better jobs and salaries.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Co-operation among human beings is an essential thing to maintain an ever-lasting relationship. It also helps in making a person into a sucessful entrepreneur. Corporations nowadays requires a good team player. This skill can be built by many aspects but one among them is team sports. So, Team Sports actually helps the students to learn this fantastic skill. Let us dive more into this below. School is not about scoring good scores and it also involves on learning some important life skills. Team sports like Football, Volleyball and Cricket etc helps in toning the children in good shape both physically and mentally. Whenever the children involves in team sports they tends to be more sharp and also good on taking decisions. Since they are playing in a group it also helps them to manage different levels of people. They will eventually becomes a good team player. In addition to it, they will also become a future leaders as it will drastically improve our leadership skills. Kids who lead a team in sports would be very calculative and will also take some healthy risks and they will be alert.\", \"sampled\": \"There is a debate about the role of parents in their children's engagement with stories. Some people believe parents should actively tell stories to their children. Others maintain that children can read books or watch media like television and movies independently. I believe parental involvement is important for a child's development. Parents are often considered a child's first and most important teachers. When they tell stories, it can encourage children to develop good manners and positive behaviours. This shared activity also strengthens the bond between family members, creating a positive home environment and better relationships. An opposing view suggests that modern children are capable of managing their own activities. They can choose books or watch television without direct parental supervision. However, a lack of parental involvement in these formative years may lead to negative consequences for the child's personal growth. Children who spend time with their parents during childhood are often said to behave well in school. While a past survey indicated a majority of children preferred watching a favorite TV program, this does not diminish the need for parental connection. Spending quality time with children is important for their development into well-rounded individuals.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, newspapers have affected people's thoughts and considerations. In my opinion, this happens mainly because of the lack of self-conscience and individuals' laziness in finding information. Therefore, I think this situation leads to ignorance among the people. In this essay, I will express my own ideas and thoughts about this situation. Observing the causes, there are a lot of people who don't have their own point of view on our world, while the newspapers are creating their opinions. For example, with a new BBC article that says we must secure and help our environment, plenty of people suddenly becoming eco-activists who do not understand why is this so important and crucial. Investigating another option, these individuals also do not have any desire to seek information about an appropriate topic because newspapers have already created their opinion and given them only \\\"correct\\\" information. Moreover, such people without self-conscience are the reason why politicians can manipulate our society. Today's situation in Ukraine clearly represents , how the opinion of the community and their minds can be influenced by newspapers. On the other hand, people who seek information by themselves may build their point of view without any manipulations or political games. Furthermore, such people as Bill Gets, Elon Musk, and Jeph Bezos encourage people to think with the help of any source of information but newspapers.\", \"sampled\": \"Many people believe that governments should prioritize other societal issues over space exploration, but this view is shortsighted. Investing money in exploring the cosmos is a crucial endeavor that offers significant benefits for all human beings. I firmly agree that funds should be allocated to space missions, as this investment is vital for our future progress and understanding. Exploring beyond Earth is essential for advancing our technology and supporting economic growth. To achieve technological breakthroughs, astronauts and researchers need to conduct missions far from our own planet. This exploration is widely considered to be a key driver of a stronger economy, creating new opportunities and industries. The knowledge gained from space missions can also inspire creative ideas that benefit humanity in various ways. By venturing into space, we can gain a much deeper understanding of other planets, including their unique environments and climates. This information not only broadens our scientific horizons but can also lead to the potential discovery of new lands or even new forms of life. While funding space exploration is important, governments must use their investments carefully to avoid waste. A portion of public funds must always be reserved for addressing other pressing societal problems on Earth. One plausible approach to supplement government spending could be fundraising, such as launching a public campaign for people to contribute money toward purchasing rockets.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, seeking for the story behind the residence becomes popular among some people. They believe that finding the fact about the past can make their life better especially in terms of making a decision. In addition, technology is the useful approach for searching this information. People begin to search for the information of the past in order to make the best decision in the future. Historical story behind the house or the resident assets indirectly teach the inhabitants what are the benefits and drawbacks of living in this area. Learning from the past, the resident immediately know what exact thing they should do when the terrible situation is happened. To illustrate, approximate a decade ago, tsunami was hitting at the south of Thailand for the first time, in that moment people did not know what to do. As a result, numerous of houses and buildings were demolished. After this situation, people keep studying about this history and never let the guard down. They notice every single thing that can relate to the tsunami and always aware of their lives. Therefore, living in the house near the coast is not as dangerous as before. The most effective way for research the history is using the internet.\", \"sampled\": \"In some countries, the population of children under 16 years old is increasing, a demographic shift that presents both opportunities and challenges. While this trend can encourage positive developments in public services, it also carries potential economic and social drawbacks. This essay will examine the various consequences of a growing youth population for a nation. A larger number of young people can push governments to improve essential public services. Authorities may be encouraged to improve sanitation and medical facilities to meet the needs of more children. These improvements in healthcare systems, initially aimed at the young, can later benefit the adult population as well. On the other hand, this demographic change places significant financial demands on the government. Supporting a growing youth population requires increased spending on vital resources such as food and education. This rise in government expenditure can create financial stress for the state, potentially leading to higher taxes for the general public. In the long term, having a large group of educated youths could support significant national development. These individuals may drive advancements in business, health, and internet infrastructure as they enter the workforce. However, a surplus of educated young people could also lead to higher rates of unemployment, as only the most skillful may secure jobs in a competitive market.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"When children commit crime, many believe that they themselves should be punished, where there are those who argue that the parents of the children should face legal justice instead. In my opinion, parents should not be personally liable to the wrongdoing of their children, since they themselves have not committed a crime, and cannot therefore be responsible. Some may argue that since children have not fully developed their mental faculties, that behaviours performed by them must be copied from their parents. Therefore, parents that teach their kids anti social behaviour therefore shoulder some of the blame for their child misbehaving. Studies have shown that children growing up in abusive households, for example, are more likely to grow up having psychological issues of their own. Thus, it might be suggested that since children’s cognitive functions are moulded by their parents, they should be punished On the other hand, to punish the parents of children implies that the child has no free will. Although parenting does play a part in individual action, it must be pointed out that individuals also have a degree of autonomy (this of course by age; a four year old does not have the same level of cognition as a seventeen year old).\", \"sampled\": \"A growing number of people are choosing to have children later in life compared to previous generations. Many individuals now prioritize achieving their career goals and reaching financial stability before starting a family. In my opinion, the advantages of having children later in a marriage are important and worth considering. Couples often delay having children because they do not want to raise them during a period of personal struggle. Another reason for waiting is the desire to enjoy married life and to better understand their partner first. Some people also feel they are inexperienced and choose to seek advice from seniors before they become parents themselves. This trend is reflected in the choices of people around me, including my close friend who married at the age of 21. She and her husband waited for ten years before they finally had their first baby together. Similarly, my other friends have also chosen to build their careers and become financially stable before having a child. In conclusion, the decision to delay parenthood is becoming more common as people focus on personal and professional development. By establishing their careers and securing their finances, they believe they can provide a better environment for their children. Proper planning for both marriage and children can ultimately help couples avoid many potential problems in the future.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recently, governments had implemented a program which all students need to accomplish before they graduate from high school and this program involve in doing volunteer work for the community. Some individuals believe that doing social service can benefits the society as well as the teenagers. I am strongly agreed with this notion. First of all, incorporating social work in the subjects such as Mathematics, Moral Education and Science can aid the students to understand the topic better as they are able to observe how these subjects are essential to the real world. Besides that, they would not get as bored in the class as they have the chance to do practical work, enhancing their understanding as well as their skills. For example, being a cashier in a supermall helps in developing their arithmetic skills, thus excelling well when Mathematics test comes up. Furthermore, engaging internship job increases the communication skills. As a result, they will be more confident with themselves and able to conserve well to people of different ages. Another advantage of this activity is that it gives a sense of caring and empathy towards the society. By doing community service, it nurtures the sense of responsibility and maturity in adolescent’s mind.\", \"sampled\": \"Technology has changed the healthcare industry and greatly improved patient care. The introduction of high-tech equipment has increased the accuracy of medical diagnoses and made treatments more effective. Moreover, technological advancements have helped medical research move forward, leading to breakthroughs in understanding and treating various diseases. In the field of education, technology has played a important role in transforming traditional learning methods. Online learning platforms have become common, so students can access quality education from anywhere.. This shift toward digital education has been made possible by the integration of technology into teaching practices, enabling a more flexible and interactive learning experience. During the Covid-19 pandemic, schools worldwide faced challenges in completing their syllabi. However, technology helped by facilitating remote learning and ensuring that educational continuity was maintained. This crisis showed the importance of incorporating technology into education to adapt to unexpected circumstances. Furthermore, technology has not only changed education and healthcare but has also had a profound impact on interpersonal relationships. Devices such as phones and tablets with internet connectivity have strengthened long-distance relationships by enabling constant communication and connection. This technological support has made it easier for individuals to maintain meaningful relationships.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"There is an opinion asserting that increasing the price of fuels is helpful for air pollution, and accordingly the environmental issues are solved. Although I agree that it is important to use fewer private vehicles, I do not think that it is ample reason for addressing so-called problems. To begin with, the compelling reason for this assertion is that these days, most people all over the world use their private cars more than any other time, and it has an ill effect on the air. If they face a high cost of this kind of energy, in order to enormously of them not afford it, they will highly likely use other transferring vehicles such as buses, subways and metros. That is to say, the more price of energy the less using private cars. A good illustration of this is Iran since the cost of energy has changed the more people use public transportation. Thus, when the amount of air pollution declines the most useful means is for the environment. On the other hand, firstly, this type of energy is not only used for private vehicles but also it is the main source for factors and other activities such as warming systems. To put it differently, private cars are not the only and the most significant factors for threatening the environment. Secondly, there are other root causes which have dire consequences on the destruction of the environment.\", \"sampled\": \"Success is a complex concept, and many factors can play important roles in achieving it. Good planning is one key contributor to success because it provides a clear foundation for progress and goal achievement. It helps people organize their actions, think ahead, and stay focused on their objectives. Hard work is another basic element closely tied to success because it builds discipline and determination. Those who invest time and effort into their goals often develop resilience like resilience and determination, essential qualities that propel them towards their goals. The correlation between hard work and success is evident, emphasizing the significance of diligence and perseverance in achieving desired outcomes. Luck or favorable circumstances also play a part in shaping one's success by creating opportunities that may not have otherwise. While luck may not be entirely within one's control, embracing opportunities that arise from fortuitous events can lead to favorable results. A balanced approach that integrates good planning, hard work, and an element of luck can enhance the chances of success. By combining strategic foresight with diligent effort and a readiness to take fortuitous opportunities, individuals can create a favorable environment for achievement.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A major number of citizens of some nations around the world are curious to know more about the flat they occupie. With a profound knowledge about the their home, they can feel emotionally attached towards it. In this essay, why they wanted to have past knowledge about the place is explained, also how they can have this information is elaborated. Firstly, we make memories in the home by renovating and making alterations according to our need, to do that we have to look for the structural changes made in the origenal plan by the previous owners. Also, the mantions have paintings of people stayed there in the past. To know about them and collect an important information about the physical structre of the home, curious people look for the history of that palce. Furthermore, the countries with the homes older then hundereds of years, currents tenet wanted know what happed to the previous owner, why they sold this house. Individuals owning an enormous flat in the city can easly know more about the past through various medium. They can find information about that through the communication with the neighbors and real estate agent from which they had purchased that appartment. Moreover, some appartments have various clues that can lead them to the correct incedent that occurred inside. For example- If the fire has taken place in the home, new owners can know about it by changes in the colours, and they can check with the police station and also with old news papers. In summary, home owners are curious to know about the past, as that will help to create more awareness, and also to make some changes. To get that information, they have many ways, such as local people and old news.\", \"sampled\": \"Celebrities often find themselves in the spotlight as the media closely follows them to take photographs and videos, making their private lives public. This constant intrusion is seen by some as an inevitable part of fame, suggesting that celebrities should accept media attention as a trade-off for their success. However, it is essential to recognize that everyone, including public figures, has the right to maintain some level of privacy in their personal lives. The media's actions sometimes cross boundaries when news channels broadcast clips from celebrities' social media without their consent, infringing on their privacy rights. This invasion of privacy can have detrimental effects on the mental well-being of popular personalities, as the relentless pursuit by the media disrupts their peace of mind. Despite the public's interest in following celebrities, it is crucial to respect their boundaries and allow them moments of solitude away from the limelight. Media outlets often go to great lengths to track celebrities, even reaching them during vacations to capture their every move for public consumption. This persistent behavior can have severe consequences, as irresponsible media actions may negatively affect celebrities' personal and mental health. Therefore, it is necessary for society to acknowledge the importance of respecting celebrities' privacy and supporting regulations that protect their well-being in the face of media scrutiny. while celebrities may attract public attention and media coverage, it is essential to remember that they are entitled to privacy and should not be subjected to constant intrusion into their personal lives. Respecting their right to privacy and advocating for responsible media behavior can contribute to a healthier environment for celebrities to succeed both professionally and personally.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The chart illustrates the amount of unemployed graduates (men and women) from 2008 to 2009. Overall, we can see that there were a lot more unemployed men than women in the period given. In general, there was more unemployment during the winter and autumn months, for both groups in October the most. Firstly, the percentage of unemployed women were the same for April 2008, July 2008, April 2009, it was the lowest number. Secondly, the highest amount was in October 2008 and October 2009. As to men, as can be seen, the smallest number was in April 2008 and July 2008. Then, the greatest number of males who were out of work during the period was again in October both years. Finally, despite the fact that they both have volatile structure, the amount of women was more stable than the amount of men and it differs approximately twice.\", \"sampled\": \"In different regions, students face differing perspectives on engaging in part-time jobs while pursuing their education. Some view such work as a source of motivation, while others discourage it. Part-time employment gives students a chance to earn extra income, which can be crucial in meeting their financial needs . In Europe, students dedicating 20 to 30 hours weekly to work can cover approximately half of their educational expenses. However, the dual responsibilities of work and study can sometimes lead to a decline in students' focus on their academic pursuits. Studies conducted in the USA highlight the potential drawbacks of student employment. International students, in particular, may find it challenging to achieve their academic goals when faced with the pressures of balancing work and studies.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A highly controversial issue today relates to whether spending long time to arrive at work and institutions of education affect people negatively or not. In this essay, I am going to examine this question from both points of view and then explain why I believe the arguments for the side effects are stronger. There are people who argue that the disadvantages of traveling long distances considerably outweigh its benefits. The main reason for believing this is that the amount of time to arrive in the destination could be used in many useful ways, such as having breakfast with family or running in the park. It is also possible to say that spending a long time into the transportation could increase the level of stress and bring diseases. To illustrate, the feeling of anxiety when you arrive at work late due to the heavy traffic. On the other hand, others believe that commiting a long time has some advantages. It is often argued that it is a precious self-time. People often have this opinion because, for somebody, this is the most relaxing time of the day. A second point is that individuals could use this moment to execute a second task simultaneously and acquire knowledgment.\", \"sampled\": \"The increase in the production of consumer goods is directly linked to the damage caused to the natural environment. This surge in manufacturing leads to the depletion of natural resources, exacerbating environmental degradation. Moreover, the discharge of industrial waste from manufacturing factories contributes to environmental harm, amplifying the negative impact on the ecosystem. One major consequence of increasing production is deforestation, undertaken to meet the high demand for factory spaces. This practice not only results in the loss of vital forest cover but also contributes to climate change and desertification. Additionally, global warming is identified as a potential outcome of the unchecked discharge of industrial waste, further highlighting the environmental repercussions of increased consumer goods production. To combat the adverse effects of consumer goods production on the environment, it is imperative for individuals to play a proactive role by reducing unnecessary consumption. By avoiding excessive buying habits, individuals can help slow down environmental degradation. Governments must implement strong policiesthat promote waste treatment and the use of energy-saving equipment in manufacturing processes. The collective efforts of both individuals and governments are essential in addressing the harmful impact of consumer goods production on the natural environment.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Issues related to modern music are frequently discussed these days. Some people believe that music today has a negative impact on the youngest people. Others criticize and argue that modern music can positively affect the young. Both sides of view and the explanation will be discussed in the following paragraphs. On the one hand, there are several reasons why the effect of the digital age music shows negative ways. The principal reason is that the young lack careful thought about good and bad things. To explain, most adolescents are usually influenced by their favourite artists in terms of following styles as well as characteristics. Hence, some impolite actions, such as smoking and shouting, can lead their younger fans to follow. For instance, in Thailand, some boys try to smoke in the classroom since they want to be like their favourite rappers in the show without thinking of the drawbacks of smoking. On the other hand, opponents may argue that the modern style of rhythms has a greater impact on the younger generation. The main point is that most children always have a lot of energy; thus, dancing with modern rhythm can be a great solution for releasing their powers without fighting. This means that relaxing with melody is one of the suitable sports categories in the young age group. For example, today most children like to dance with the popular music in Tik-Tok applications.\", \"sampled\": \"Interest in house history research is on the rise in certain countries. This growing trend is driven by various factors, including spiritual beliefs and concerns about future security. Some individuals delve into the history of their homes due to negative energy beliefs, seeking to understand past events at their residence. This interest is not merely a curiosity; historical knowledge of a property can actually help prevent possible land disputes in the future. To explore the history of a house or building, there are several methods available. Land record departments and real estate agents serve as valuable sources of historical information, offering details about past ownership and the use of properties. In addition, neighbours can also provide valuable details about the history of a residence, contributing to a more comprehensive understanding of its background. As interest in house history research continues to grow, individuals are turning to different sources for information. Discovery channels and specialized authorities are increasingly involved in providing historical context to homeowners. This trend reflects a broader shift towards appreciating the historical significance of buildings and the value of understanding their past. In conclusion, the surge in interest in house history research highlights the importance of understanding the background of properties. Whether driven by spiritual beliefs, concerns about negative energy, or a desire for future security, investigating the history of a house can offer valuable insights and help prevent potential disputes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The world is a very interesting place for someone who loves to find out about history. In some countries, a large portion of the population is becoming interested in searching for the history of the house or building where they live. The following paragraph highlights what are the reasons for this and how can they research it with relevant examples.\", \"sampled\": \"Teenagers should serve the community in their leisure time. Engaging in social work can provide mental and physical satisfaction to teenagers. Adolescents who volunteer for 10 hours are less likely to feel depressed. Volunteer work aids in society's better living environment. Organizations work towards improving social structures and environmental conditions. Indian Prime Minister initiated a campaign with unpaid workers to combat air pollution. Volunteer work benefits both individuals and society.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, there are tendencies that people will have their own child when they get old. Although giving birth in old age might come with several problems, it helps the parents to have money to cater to their children's education, and basic needs and to be psychologically ready for it which I believe outweighs the disadvantages as the childbirth complications can be treated with adequate medical intervention, however, lack of preparedness can lead to mental illness of both parents. To start, late childbirth comes with some issues which are detrimental to the mother's health. Complications like Ectopic pregnancies, miscarriages (early pregnancy loss) congenital disorders, high blood pressure, and chromosomal condition in the child such as Down syndrome. For instance, one of the Federal medical health care centres in Nigeria; reflected in a recently published report that; 70% of women who got pregnant at the age of 35 years has ectopic pregnancy and preeclampsia. However, complications that come with giving birth in old age now have solutions. A scientist has found a way of preventing medical problems with the use of certain medications. In addition, one of the very important advantages of having a child late is mental preparedness and financial stability which helps both parents to be able to cater for their basic needs after birth. when both parents are mentally prepared, they tend to have peace of mind and also cater for themselves, forming intimacy together.\", \"sampled\": \"Social media plays a pivotal role in connecting individuals, particularly those separated by distance. It has changed communication by allowing people to stay in touch with friends and relatives regardless of their location. Through social networking sites, individuals can maintain relationships and share experiences, which helps them feel closer despite physical distance. Moreover, social media offers a virtual platform for individuals to participate in special events remotely. Whether it's attending a live concert or celebrating a milestone, platforms like Facebook and Instagram enable users to engage in real-time events virtually. This feature enhances inclusivity and allows individuals to be part of significant occasions even when they are unable to attend physically. On a broader scale, social media serves as a valuable source of information, providing users with access to news from around the globe at any time. By following news outlets and staying informed through social media, users can stay abreast of current events and global developments effortlessly. However, the dark side of social media emerges in the form of cyberbullying, particularly affecting vulnerable groups like teenagers. Cyberbullying can have severe consequences such as harassment and, tragically, even suicide. The misuse of photos and private information on social media further exacerbates this problem, leading to instances of harassment and exposure that can have lasting negative impacts on individuals.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"There are controversial notions heating up a debate over human activities to solve environmental problems. While some claim that there is no time for people to implement some solutions, the opposites make the statement that they should formulate some remedy to handle this problem. While the former is valid to some extent, I consider myself an advocate of the latter. Without a shadow of a doubt, there are some evident reasons to think that it is behind time to deal with the adverse impact of human activities on flora and fauna, one of which is deforestation. Owing to the ever-growing demand for lands to live and woods to consume, people clear all the green cover for means of industrial, agricultural and residential purposes which render many kinds of spices lost their habitat and push them to the brink of extinction. For instance, the Panda, which is considered the national treasure of China, is endangered on the grounds of jogging. Thus, the extinction of many species results from deforestation, which is the reason why many people believe that this circumstance can’t be solved. While the serious consequences on plants and animals caused by human activities are widely acknowledged, there are still some solutions to revamp this matter. They can erect some natural reserves to create a better and safer environment for endangered species to reside in. In Vietnam, Cuc Phuong Forest can be cited as a convincing example, which is the home for much rare livestock.\", \"sampled\": \"Celebrities often show off their wealth and luxury on social media platforms. This ostentatious display can captivate the attention of many, young individuals who may aspire to replicate the luxurious lifestyles portrayed by these influencers. As a result, some young people might prioritize material possessions and extravagance over more meaningful pursuits, influenced by the glamorous image projected by celebrities. This trend of idolizing wealth and fame can have detrimental effects on the younger generation. As youngsters strive to copy the affluent lifestyles of their favorite celebrities, they may focus on materialistic pursuits rather than focusing on personal growth or meaningful goals. This shift in priorities can hinder their overall development and lead to a shallow pursuit of success solely based on external markers of wealth and status. While fame can bring financial prosperity, it also carries negative consequences, especially when celebrities engage in inappropriate or irresponsible behavior. Some influencers exhibit questionable conduct that can easily influence impressionable young minds. Cases of young people copying celebrity misconduct are not uncommon, leading to the normalization of behaviors that go against societal norms and values. The negative trends perpetuated by celebrities and influencers can weaken established social norms and values. When individuals in the public eye showcase behaviors or attitudes that are detrimental to the well-being of society, there is a risk of these actions being normalized and accepted by a wider audience. It is important for influencers and celebrities to recognize the impact of their actions on the younger generation and try to set positive examples that promote values of integrity, responsibility, and ethical conduct.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Science is moving forward faster than most people can process. Robotics and Artificial Intelligence are a few of the fields that stands out the most, because it can ease the day to day. We could say that Part of a country development can be measure through their technological advances and how it directly affect soceity. An advantage that stands out of having robots doing human's jobs is the precision level a field can achieve with its tasks on a daily basis. For instance, medical procedures done with a lasers, which would be thousands of times more precise than an expert doctor's hand. Also on an industrial level, cutting and assambling machines has been optimized by other machines and robots. Another advantage would be the improvement in the time frame to produce goods or deliver tasks, As it has being with car production. For example Ford is known to be the first car company to introduce the assambly line to their production, making it the first company in the USA to produce more cars in less time.\", \"sampled\": \"Environmental pollution is a major contributor to respiratory and cardiovascular diseases, impacting public health significantly. Individuals living in polluted environments are more likely to suffer from these problems, showing the importance of addressing pollution-related health risks. Moreover, unhygienic living conditions expose individuals to infectious diseases, which also shows the need for clean and safe living environments. poor housing quality can directly affect the well-being of residents. To tackle these challenges effectively, the government must prioritize providing safe and clean living conditions for all citizens. By improving housing standards and environmental quality, policymakers can mitigate the health risks associated with pollution and inadequate living conditions, ultimately improving public health. Furthermore, individual behavior change is essential in reducing pollution levels and improving overall environmental quality. Simple actions such as cleaning houses regularly can help prevent the spread of germs, reduce pollution, and contribute to better public health outcomes. This shows the importance of individual responsibility in creating a healthier living environment for all.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Some people believe that retirees should be given monetary supports by the government while others think that they should be made to cater for themselves. I will be expatiating on this and as well give my opinion in subsequent paragraphs. Although it is arguable that government should be made to shoulder the financial upkeep of their retired citizenry, it is quite burdernsome. The financial toll of running the country is already high in various countries and adding to this might be unsustainable. For example, the cost of running Niger, as recorded by the 2003 United Nations Report for Countries in Africa, was about 3.5 trillion dollars which was way above the country's earnings. Thus, countries should not add the cost of financial welfare of their inactive populace to the already high cost of running their nation. It is my belief that people should be made to take responsibility for their finances even after their active years. This is because as humans work, it is reasonably expected of them to have retirement plans and not rely on others for their survival. Moreso, when a person takes a job at a young age, it is imperative of him to save up for later when he will be less productive.\", \"sampled\": \"Teenagers, with their good health and sharp minds, have the ability to make meaningful contributions to their communities. At the age of 15 to 22 years old, a period regarded as puberty, young people are characterized by good health and creativity. This age group can learn quickly and use their energy to assist others, whether in their neighbourhood or in broader society. Moreover, teenagers have the opportunity to develop their life skills through community work. After school, they can spend their hours volunteering at a nursing home or hospital, supporting the staff and interacting with patients. However, it is worth noting that many teenagers prefer spending their leisure time on computers and smartphones, without much interaction with others. Engaging in community work and volunteering can reduce this isolation. Interacting with others in a meaningful way can help teenagers learn a variety of useful lessons, improve their weaknesses and support personal development. Volunteering, in particular, can be beneficial to those who want to improve their moral values and contribute positively to society. In conclusion, community work offers numerous advantages for young teenagers. It helps them establish their goals for the future, while contributing to their personal development. The effort contributed to the community not only benefits society but also helps shape teenagers’ life skills, making it a worthy pursuit.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"In this recent years , most couples decide on the appropriate time for them to procreate. While people are advocating for having a child at an older age, in this, essay the benefits and demerits will be discussed. Presently in this metropolitan century ,most couples tend to build themselves well before procreating and there can be two reasons behind it. First, couples want to focus and maintain a good professional paying job in other to have financial stability and provide a better and happy living for their child this includes proper education , training and good healthcare as the case may be. Secondly in the US for instance, a recent research carried out on the IT couples between the age of 35-40 years shows that procreating at this point gives them the privilege to share their childhood memories with their child while growing up. On the contrary, some couples find it quite interesting raising kids at a younger age because of some underlying health conditions which can affect their reproductive system or organs . Also , having a child at a younger age will give them the opportunity to interact and play with them because at some point in time they might not have much strength to do so.\", \"sampled\": \"In today's world, age differences between family members are becoming more common. This trend is largely due to the fact that people often prioritize their career and education before settling down. As a result, people are getting married later than the typical age of 24 or 25 years, which was once considered the norm. This delay in starting a family leads to a significant age gap by the time their child reaches an age of understanding around five years. This maturity difference between parents and children is not without its advantages. In fact, there are several benefits to having such a large age gap between generations. One of the key benefits is that successful parents, having already established their careers, can spend quality time with their children. This time is very important for children's development and overall wellbeing. Additionally, children have the opportunity to learn directly from their parents' success, creating an environment of growth and learning. In conclusion, parents play a crucial role in helping their children achieve their goals, regardless of the age difference. It is clear that the age difference between generations is not the most important factor if it proves beneficial. Therefore, it is better to focus less on the numbers and more on the quality of the relationship and the advantages it can bring.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Knowledge of the place you live in, be it a country, a city, or just a building, was always important. And now, it is becoming even more popular to learn the history of the house where you live. The reasons for this may be different. Some people are just inspired by all these horror films, and some want to learn something new during the restrictions of Covid 19. There are many potential sources to investigate, from neighbours and previous renters to city archives and old papers. Folk was always excited about the past. What could be more interesting than going deep into the story of a ghost which, according to the rumours, used to live in your current apartment? Each year there is a new hit, a film, or a book, based on our fear of the unknown and paranormal, and usually include an old dark building - the heart of the horror. Well, this was one reason why people became interested in their houses. Some want to explore the world around them, which is a bit hard to do because of the current epidemic situation. The world of many became so small, and still, it is full of the unknown. People use different sources to get the information they want. The easiest way is to question neighbours, especially the old ones or previous renters. Some may tell the whole store by themself or give the right direction at least.\", \"sampled\": \"The Internet and phone have become important parts of our daily lives, and people are becoming more dependent on these technologies. This dependency has led to a decrease in face-to-face communication, as people now rely more on virtual connections. Social media platforms such as Facebook, Twitter, and WhatsApp are often used for communication, enabling people to stay connected irrespective of geographical boundaries. However, this reliance on technology for communication has its drawbacks. The trend is creating social distance and a lack of understanding among people, as online interactions lack the depth and nuance of in-person conversations. Despite the ease of sharing pictures, text, and news with loved ones, the decrease in physical social interactions can lead to feelings of isolation and misunderstanding. Despite these issues, the development of the Internet and phone has clearly made communication easier and less limited by distance or time. In the US alone, around 8 billion messages are shared daily, indicating the large volume of communication facilitated by these technologies. Furthermore, these developments have contributed to the world economy, as companies like Facebook, Twitter, and WhatsApp generate substantial revenue and provide numerous jobs. In conclusion, while the dependency on technology for communication has led to a decrease in face-to-face interactions and possible social distance, the benefits outweigh the drawbacks. Phones and the Internet have changed communication by making it easy and immediate, and they have also contributed to economic growth. This trend, despite some negative aspects, has had a positive impact overall on society.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Single-sex education is an aproach that is gaining popularity these days. Although there is no unified verdict on wheter it is beneficial or disadvantagtous to children today, I think, students being in same-gander schools may have difficulties later in life. In this essay I will explain my opinion. Some parents believe that by eliminating the distractions it becomes easier to retain the knowledge that teachers offer. That is why they do not want their children to be in co-educational schools, because, espesially at pubert period, students of the opossite sex can be a distraction. In addition, these students tend to not feel the preassures of gender roles and learn to pursue areas that interested them no matter what is considered socially acceptable for their biological sex. Despite this, I consider, the average workspace does not make men and women work in separate corners. There is an expectation for everyone to work together. From my point of view, the school environment is a place where young generation learns what life will be like when thay enter the the real world. Having both men and women in classes allow students of both sex to interact with a wider range of people and learn how to work with and talk to people of the opposite sex. Same-gander classes limit this opportunitu.\", \"sampled\": \"In an ever-changing world, the impact of rising fuel prices on a country's economy cannot be overlooked. This is largely due to the fact that the transportation of goods relies heavily on vehicles that consume fuel. As a result, when fuel prices rise, the cost of goods also tends to increase. However, the economic implications of fuel prices are not the only issue. The environment is also at risk from activities linked to fuel consumption and waste disposal. Shockingly, a quarter of all waste is disposed of without any control, which can cause significant harm to the environment. This uncontrolled disposal includes dumping litter into rivers and lakes, leading to water-related problems. An alternative solution to these challenges could be found in recycling. Unlike increasing fuel prices, recycling could provide significant environmental benefits. However, it seems that fewer than 25% of countries currently encourage their citizens to recycle. This is a concerning statistic given the potential positive impact of widespread recycling on both the economy and the environment. In conclusion, while the increasing cost of fuel has significant economic implications, it also indirectly contributes to environmental degradation. Therefore, it is important to encourage more countries to promote recycling, not only as a means of reducing the cost of goods but also as a way to safeguard the environment.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Indeed, people work hard and earn wages for their needs and preferences. So they have the right to claim fair salaries for the work which they have done and keep whatever they want. However, we should not forget that although we have rights, we are somewhat obliged to society in order to do that I believe that as a citizen, we have a moral obligation and responsibility to pay the tax from our earnings. In today’s world, money is considered to be everything. As every people should spend money on essential needs. For example, food, transport, medical bills, shelter and education are the basic expenses of citizens. Apart from that, many families tend to save and invest a significant portion of their income. People always worry about their future and safety. On the basis of that point, some people need to keep all their money without allocating a pound to tax and they believe it is their right. Nevertheless, citizens should be obliged to pay taxes to the government for a number of reasons. First, taxes are collected for the sake of expanding and maintaining public services like infrastructure facilities, education, health services and transport. In addition to that state need to ensure the country’s security and keep the stable economy of the country. In short, through tax, which makes sure that everyone has affordable basic facilities and comfortable living standards. To sum up, even though many people think that they should not pay the duty, a portion of that money is useful to the stability of the country. It provides stability as well as equal opportunities for every citizen.\", \"sampled\": \"Public figures, with their lavish lifestyles and significant wealth, are becoming increasingly popular. This rise in popularity is not without its consequences, however. One significant consequence is the influence these figures have on the young generation, notably teenagers and high school students, who often look up to them as role models. As these famous personalities have become strong influencers in society, they are setting an example that could be harmful. This trend may encourage dependence on parental wealth, as young people may start to believe that it's easy to achieve a life of luxury without hard work. Additionally, it could lead to misguided career choices. For instance, teenagers may be pushed toward wrong career pathways, believing that they too can become rich and famous without much effort or skill. On a more serious note, some young individuals may choose dangerous or illegal work in their pursuit of wealth and glamour. This is a concerning consequence of the influence of public figures and their portrayal of a lifestyle that seems attainable without the need for education, talent, or diligence. This trend has the potential to disrupt the balance of society and lead to an increase in illegal activities among the youth. In conclusion, while celebrities and public figures have the right to enjoy their wealth and lifestyle, it's important to consider the potential impact on impressionable young minds. Parents, educators, and society as a whole must work together to ensure that the young generation understands the value of hard work, education, and moral choices. This way, we can hope to reduce the negative effects of the increasing influence of public figures.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Everyone wants to be successful in their life. Few individuals opine that tertiary education is the only key to success, while others denied it. This essay will intend to analyze both the views with my pespective. There are many reasons why some people believe that success depends on tertiary educations. Firstly, people who want to be settled in their career at 23 years then have only the option of graduation. There are many professions such as Law, Engineer, Doctor in which tertiary education is essential for them. If people who complete their graduation, they will not have problem to get a job. For instance, In Amazon, people which are interested in getting a job then they will have to show atleast university education. As a result, university education is extremely helpful for those who are interested in a field job. On the other hand, Some masses believe that university education is not decide your success. There are mant reasons behind this notion. In the world, many peoples have their god-gifted skills such as speaking skills, sports skills, music skills or many others by which they are focusing on that particular skill so that achieve success in life. Many things are beneficial to do practical instead of theoretical. For example, In India, 70 percent of people run their own business on a large scale but interestingly, they do not have a tertiary education degree. Hence, success is depends on you and your skills not on others factors. In conclusion, Although people can achieve success without any degree or education but in my opinion, in the modern era, tertiary education is a more advantageous and better option for individuals.\", \"sampled\": \"The issue of financial support for the elderly is a contentious one. Some argue that local state ministries should shoulder the responsibility of providing for seniors and addressing their financial needs, especially medical expenses that they may not be able to afford due to a lack of sufficient income. However, others contend that the basic needs of the elderly should ideally be met by their own means, suggesting that independence is key to their well-being. Interestingly, in many Asian countries, approximately 80% of the elderly population live separately. This statistic, reported by the New York Times, shows that a significant portion of the elderly population does not depend on their children or the government for support. Yet, many of these elderly individuals face medical complications and struggle with the high costs of necessary treatments, making self-sufficiency difficult. In light of these considerations, the author advocates prioritizing the welfare of the elderly within local governance structures. This perspective is based on the idea that state support can alleviate the financial strain experienced by many seniors, particularly in relation to healthcare expenses. The author suggests that ministries could provide this much-needed support, ensuring that the elderly are not left to fend for themselves in the face of mounting costs. In conclusion, the author recommends that governments worldwide should seriously consider implementing supportive measures for the elderly. The key argument is that state intervention is not just an option, but a necessity in many cases, given the financial difficulties that many elderly individuals face.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"In the modern world, the more urban sprawl is the more gas emission level from individuals’ mobility increase. This is one of the main factors which might affect significantly global issues needing a hand from the government. In some areas, the governments adjust the number of vehicles by increasing the fuel price, while I firmly disagree because this route could give more back draws than benefits. On the one hand, it undoubtedly agrees that increasing the cost of fuel may reduce the traffic travelers as well as the depletion of oil resources and air pollution. Those, who have the average salary or be in poverty, will consider the fuel segment from the total personal expenditure than those who belong to top-notch. These groups may mitigate the expenses by transforming public transportation instead of personal mobility. This method is a considerable short-term measure to work on air qualification. On the other hand, the convenience of traveling by own cars or vehicles is not demolished even with the growth of public transportation. Residents might go by foot in some distance to catch the bus or train. In the addition, how to shorten the time to travel is more likely their priority in long term. Moreover, rising the cost of conventional fuels could imbalance the market by rising the other materials simultaneously. This leads to a more crucially serious which is called inflation spiralled out of control which may mitigate life condition standards. To illustrate, the cost of fuel reached a new peak in most countries in the year 2022 and has attacked many aspects of human life such as the higher food from agriculture logistics, the health condition of hospitals, the construction materials, and so on.\", \"sampled\": \"As people age, they tend to require more financial support due to increasing health and lifestyle needs. This reality is reflected in the fact that people are often unable to maintain employment past the age of 60 due to both physical health and mental capacity concerns. Unfortunately, young people often spend without adequately planning for this stage of retirement, leading to potential financial instability in their later years. The EIA brokerage firm has suggested that individuals as young as 25 to 30 should begin considering their retirement planning. This approach can help alleviate the financial burdens that come with age and retirement. However, the responsibility for arranging retirement funds is traditionally considered a role of the government. The reason for this is that personal savings often fall short compared to the large amounts needed to sustain a comfortable lifestyle post-retirement. Looking at the example of Australia, individuals are required to contribute 9.5% of their wages to a superannuation fund. This compulsory saving method is a government initiative aimed at ensuring that citizens have sufficient funds for retirement. The Australian system shows how state retirement plans can be more effective than individual saving methods, as they can provide a more consistent and reliable source of income for retirees. In conclusion, while individuals have a role to play in planning for their retirement, government intervention is also crucial because state retirement plans can provide a more stable and secure financial foundation for retirees. For this reason, governments should take a more active role in managing and providing retirement funds so that their citizens can lead a comfortable life after retirement.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"It is undeniable that parents can prefer to enroll their offspring in single-sex schools in many countries. I believe that education in single-sex schools has drawbacks for students' future lives. On the one hand, it is also true that some countries still support single-sex schools and achieving their annual educational goals, which is enough for them as a counterargument. Moreover, some research shows unequivocally that girls who thrive in an all-girls environment do better academic outcomes, participation, collegiality, and personal development. On the other hand, I agree that students have the right to choose their school type as they feel more accurately which suits their character. Firstly, single-sex schools have yet to be disseminated extensively worldwide, affecting their unpopularity directly. For example, countries that applied this kind of teaching ask continuously enough why they are still using this. In addition, separating kids on the basis of biology is not a crucial choice. The number of single-sex schools is prevalently in Muslim-majority countries, declining yearly. Gradually, the adamant proof of educational indicators of co-ed schools urges them to think twice about this system. Neuroscience has discovered the social brain and to the extent that academic learning can be enhanced or impaired by those around us. The social environment is essential. How can you promote diversity when you exclude half the population? In conclusion, regardless of school type, both have positive and negative sides. However, I believe that co-ed schools may be more effective for students' adaptation, not create gender stereotypes and promote gender disparity.\", \"sampled\": \"The world is facing an environmental crisis, largely due to the increased population and the rising number of vehicles on the road. This increase has resulted in more environmental problems because of the emission of harmful gases into the atmosphere. Some people argue that one possible solution to these problems is to increase fuel prices, as higher costs could discourage vehicle use and lower gas emissions. The harmful impact of vehicle use on the environment is clear. Cars and trucks, fueled by petrol or diesel, emit gases such as carbon dioxide and nitrogen when fuel is burnt. These gases are harmful to the environment and contribute to the depletion of the ozone layer. Furthermore, the manufacturing of these vehicles also has a significant environmental impact. It requires extensive natural resources and often leads to deforestation, further worsening environmental degradation. Raising fuel prices could be a practical strategy to reduce the environmental problems associated with vehicle use and production. Higher fuel costs could result in less vehicle use, reducing the emission of harmful gases. Increased fuel prices could also discourage people from buying cars, thereby reducing the demand for car manufacturing. This reduction in demand could lead to increased resource recycling and less deforestation. An example of the potential benefits of this approach can be seen in India, where reducing car production has led to the restoration of natural rainfall. Some developed countries have also achieved an annual reduction of carbon dioxide gas by 8% by imposing higher fuel taxes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Everybody knows that social media is very important in our life and it is common so much. We can use it for communication, business or following some news or famous celebrities. Some people want to keep in touch with each other. İt is sometimes good , and other times it is bad for us owing to some specific results. first and foremost, you are able to meet some friends on social websites and you can spend a funny time together because we don't have much time to go outside we can sit at home and we can meet others if you want to speak about cultures or relationships whatever you want, you can talk for everything. For example, many people met new friends and then get married. You can find your partner on the internet, and you are able to know how is the person's character. İt shows us that it is beneficial for us. On the other hand, it has many different communities unfortunately some of them are bad. Cheating on social media is very comfortable and you can't understand if they are talking true or lie. you need to be careful so much you can find very bad people. for example, one day a girl met a man on a communication application, they began the relationship and she loves him.\", \"sampled\": \"Interviews are a common selection method for large companies such as Tata Steel in the U.S. These face-to-face interactions allow hiring managers to examine an individual's qualifications and background, assessing whether they would be a good fit for the company. Notably, this approach has been successful for Tata Steel, as they select 10 out of 1 candidates through interviews. Despite the popularity of interviews, some people believe there are better methods for selecting employees. They argue that practical knowledge and skills are equally, if not more, important in employee selection. For example, demo sessions can be used to assess practical knowledge and skills, which are often crucial for job performance. Essential abilities for an employee include leadership, teamwork coordination, communication skills, and independence. These skills are often difficult to assess in a traditional interview setting, leading to a debate about the effectiveness of such a method. In contrast to the U.S., Argentina tests workers first before selecting final employees, a method that emphasizes practical knowledge and ability. In conclusion, while interviews remain a common method for employee selection in large companies, there is a growing call for alternative methods. These alternatives focus on assessing practical skills and abilities, which are important for job performance. As the debate continues, it will be interesting to see how companies adapt their recruitment strategies.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Sustaining productivity at the working place relies on several contributing factors out of which money is considered to be a paramount reason. I agree to some extent with the notion. The subsequent essay will highlight not only arguments in favouring but also some opposite prospects of it. To begin with, Innumerable aspects are attached to how other reasons rather than salary improves task performance in the working area. First of all, If the working environment is not only friendly, but also has appropriate cooperation and coordination among colleagues, the efforts required to achieve any task could be minimized. Completion of a particular target under a definite deadline, therefore, could be ensured. Furthermore, When the working pressure is less, it causes a reduction of stress level which enables workers to perform appropriately. In addition, Job security which brings financial stability is beneficial to carry out actions effectively. Moreover, Leaves offer an opportunity for the employees to accomplish their personal jobs or spend time with family members. Perseverance towards their profession, therefore, could be strengthened. For instance, maternity leaves offered by many organizations to the mother during pregnancy time encourage their attraction towards their occupation. Additionally, other amenities such as medical and commuting facility, the predominant motivating factors in the industry area. Despite having many pivotal factors, wage has a vital role. Since a handsome salary is indispensable to fulfil their own and family desires. It leads to a strong family relationship and, provides sufficient funds that could be spent in an emergency. As a consequence, payment fascinates the workers towards their vocation.\", \"sampled\": \"Road accidents globally are a significant source of injuries, drawing attention to the vital role of road safety. A common view is that imposing stricter punishments for road offenses can be an effective strategy to increase safety on the roads. This view suggests that harsher penalties could encourage greater awareness and more cautious behavior among drivers, thus decreasing the likelihood of accidents. Another potential advantage of stricter penalties for road offenses is their indirect effect on environmental health. High penalties might act as a deterrent to individual car usage, encouraging more people to use public transport instead. This shift towards public transportation could, in turn, contribute to a reduction in pollution levels, offering the dual benefit of improving both road safety and environmental health. However, focusing solely on punitive measures might not be the most comprehensive approach to enhancing road safety. It is equally, if not more, important to address the issue of infrastructure. Government funding towards improving infrastructure can significantly contribute to commuter safety. Problems such as insufficient lighting, intersecting junctions, a lack of signs or mirrors, and unstable road surfaces are common and can lead to accidents if not addressed adequately. The effectiveness of investing in infrastructure can be seen in the case of Vietnam, where increased government resources for transportation led to a reduction in traffic accidents and mortality rates. This example shows that while stricter penalties for road offenses can be part of the solution, addressing infrastructural issues is also crucial for improving road safety. Therefore, a balanced approach that includes both punitive measures and infrastructural improvements could be the most effective strategy for enhancing road safety.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"There are controversial perspectiveS heating UP a debate over how to live healthy. While scientists claim that there are a wealth of activities suitable with people’s health, some opposites still act bad activities. This essay is devoted to the underlying roots, followed by some equivalent effective solutions to this alarming problem. Without a shadow of a doubt, a large number of useful advices from professional experts, however, people ignore them and have numerous harmful activities, especially smoking. This is because people have a tendency to use smoke in order to decrease stress when they have been under pressure in their lives. Moreover, the low price of smoke is one of the reasons for this problem. For example, Vietnamese man utilize smoke with high frequency from young to old age because of anxiety in their work and every smoke is fifteen thousand Viet Nam Dong. Hence, the price and the bad habit of user are common justifications. While the reasons are widely acknowledge, it is necessary to introduce far-reaching solutions for troubles. The government acts as precursor in minimizing this negative status. For example, the Viet Nam governor give some rules to limit it such as: increasing tax for smoke product and so on. Moreover, the society projects are one of the most efficient resolutions.\", \"sampled\": \"Many adults have weak reading and writing skills, and this creates a serious question for public education spending. I fully agree that the government should spend more money on adult education because these learners still can improve. If authorities give this area proper attention, adults may become qualified and more socially productive. Public funding for adult literacy can help people break free from educational marginalization and take part more confidently in society. Adults with low literacy should not be treated as permanently unable to learn, because education can still change their situation. When the state invests in them, they can become more educated within an academic year. This spending can also supports the national budget and development, since better educated adults are drives economic development. The USA is an example where government attention and funding for education are linked with a highly ranked system. This suggests that authorities should see education funding as a broad national need, not only as support for young learners. Australia also shows why adult education should receive public money. This is because adult teaching there was described as low quality before government investment. After education spending in Australia, nearly all students became able to read and write, which shows the value of such support. Cultivating adult literacy is therefore a vital prerequisite for national progress.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Many people believe that all teenagers should be obliged to contribute to the local community on an unpaid basis for this would be beneficial for both individuals and society. I disagree that we should force all young people do unpaid work and expect social gaining from it. In my opinion, most young people have already been burdened with much pressure from their curricula and extra-activities. Academic performance-centred schooling is now as demanding as a full-time job for educators always push their students to catch up with the grind. Otherwise, they might leave themselves behind with their studies. When teenagers can spare some time, they should be encouraged to engage in leisure pursuits. For example, participating in a sports team or joining an art class would equip them with opportunities to hone teamwork skills or excavate the art prodigy from within that could go a long way in life. Additionally, they have many years of working ahead of them after their graduation. At the same time, I do not believe that society would reap much from forcing teenagers to do unpaid work. It would be paradoxical to have young people do unsalaried work without letting them make their own decisions while other people doing the same work can get paid. Doing this would trigger resentment among teenagers and their parents since it goes against an equal society's values.\", \"sampled\": \"In some countries, interest in the history of residential buildings is increasing, and I think this is a meaningful social trend. One clear reason is that people are curious about unknown aspects of the past connected with the places where they live. Researching a house can also help people understand their personal identity and origins. This is because homes are closely linked with earlier life. This interest is not only private curiosity, since customs, traditions, and culture from earlier times still influence current life. When people learn stories connected to buildings, they may become more aware of the need to conserve historic properties and culture. For this reason, studying residential history can support both self-understanding and respect for shared heritage. A useful starting point for research is the city library, because it can provide records and local information about houses and areas. Libraries are important because they hold records and profiles about places from national level down to town level. People who are interested in their homes’ history can then share their findings on a common platform, which may reduce duplicated research work. Specialist advice is also valuable, so people should consult historians and architects when they want a clearer understanding of building history. Integrating these historical narratives into interactive media or educational programs could further engage and educational for younger generations, especially if learning feels more engaging.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Whether leaders or directors of organizations should be old people or young people is being widely discussed currently. From my perspective, I think young people should lead the team. The reason that young people are suitable to become leader is that they have higher ability in acquiring new knowledges. As a result, they may have greater advantages in some new areas like the Internet. The young people have the willingness and ability to adept and adjust new technology as they are less experienced than old people. They may be more broadminded and can easily accept new thoughts, which makes them understand current trends and make plans that are suitable to deal with new problems. However, the old people are more experienced than young people, which is a significant advantage. As they have been working for a long time, they may experience some complex situation and enough practice to deal with those problems while young people may get nervous or have no idea about what to do when they face some accidents. What’s more, old people are less moody, which makes them steady and think twice before making decisions. In my point of view, I think that leaders can also be the young people. They have stronger bodies so they are less likely to be ill, which gives them ability to lengthen working hour and higher ability.\", \"sampled\": \"Parents can choose between single-gender and co-educational schooling for their children, and this choice may affect children after they leave school. In my view, single-gender schooling has more disadvantages than benefits. One important disadvantage of gender-isolated schools is that students may struggle with workplace communication when they become adults. This problem can be linked to limited experience interacting with the opposite gender during their school years. For example, male employees may feel hesitant when speaking with female co-workers, while mixed-gender schooling could help them feel more comfortable. but Co-educational schooling seems that children can practise communication that may be needed in adult life. Single-sex schooling can also create difficulties in finding a suitable life partner, because social confidence is not only needed at work. If teenagers have little experience with the other sex, they may have less confidence in adult social situations. Women educated from girls only schools, for instance, may feel uncomfortable or awkward on dates with men. Co-educational schooling may reduce these problems . This could make dating easier later and may improve the chances of finding the right partner. For these reasons, I believe single-gender school graduates may face both professional and romantic disadvantages, so its drawbacks are greater than its benefits.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"It is argued that it is essential for adolescents to do unpaid jobs due to the fact that it brings some specific advantages for either teenagers or the whole society. It is my firm belief should not do unpaid job due to some reasons. Some people point out that doing unpaid jobs bring specific privileges for teenagers. The most powerful argument in favour of this idea is that this kind of job broadens teenagers’ horizons in some way and they learn to be more altruistic. The ability to be helpful without any expectation cannot be easily obtained. Moreover, youngsters’ time is extremely precious; therefore, they need to make the best use of it. Additionally, from these people’s point of view, doing voluntary jobs expands their skills and prepares them to go through the fierce society in future. However, this idea cannot be easily approved. We can say beyond any doubt that teenagers’ time is valuable. Working during these years is more beneficial since it is a way to be more independent and they are taught to be more responsible and manage their money. Hence, doing unpaid jobs highly probably do not result in any advantageous points. Without any doubt, these kinds of voluntary jobs might have moral effects on teenagers. In this case, they are no longer apathetic to society and its problem.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people believe that organizations should be led by young leaders, because they may bring a more comfortable style to management. However, I fully agree that older people are better choices for leadership and director positions in organizations. Their experience, responsibility, and manners make them more suitable when important decisions and difficult problems must be handled. Young leaders can have some clear advantages in the workplace, and these should be acknowledged. They are often less strict than older leaders, so employees may feel less pressure when working under them. Young leaders may also be more open-minded and encouraging open communication, especially because they can accept feedback and adjust their views. Nevertheless, the abilities of mature leaders should not be underestimated when an organization needs strong direction. They have gained experience from many challenges, and this gives them a better understanding of how problems develop. Because of this experience, they can be more flexible when solving difficult problems, rather than reacting only from limited knowledge. Mature leaders are also described as responsible and well-mannered. Corporate governance in traditional economies exemplifies this, where senior executives historically prioritize institutional loyalty and strict accountability. For these reasons, Their experience and personal traits outweigh the advantages of young leaders, so organizations are better led by the older generation.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"With the rampant progress in technology, it is undeniable that there shall be numerous changes in the future. There is a school of thought that thinks that by the year 2050, the usual teaching methods shall be completely eradicated. In my opinion, I disagree that the traditional teaching methods will be replaced entirely in the future. In this essay, we shall discuss this topic further. Online education is a double edged sword, and there are various reasons for it. On the one hand, it is convenient, cost-effective and saves time. To illustrate, the individual does not have to commute to reach college or have a discussion with his/her tutor thereby saving time, and money, and plus the person can attend classes or lectures at the comfort of his/her home. Similarly, he/she can attend or review any missed lecture anytime. Additionally, the cost of infrastructure for building college or universities will be exempted as there is no need for any classroom. On the other hand, there are some drawbacks to this development, for instance, as almost everything is done online and so this makes it completely reliable on the internet connection. Moreover, loss or any disruption in connectivity can hamper the entire process. Furthermore, the cost of these internet connections are not always affordable. Lastly, such online classes decline the gist of learning which as physical interaction which eventually leads to loneliness and depression. In conclusion, online education cuts both ways; therefore, it shall be unnecessary to completely remove physical education and replace it with computerized study.\", \"sampled\": \"Raising fuel prices for private vehicles is often suggested as a way to reduce environmental degradation, but I disagree with this approach. Making private travel more expensive may change some driving habits, yet its environmental benefit would probably remain limited. A better response is for the government to pursue sustainable national development while protecting citizens from unnecessary damage to their quality of life. The main weakness of higher fuel prices is that private vehicles are not the only important source of pollution. Factories and air travel also produce substantial carbon dioxide and pollutants, so focusing mainly on private drivers would leave major pollution sources insufficiently controlled. Even if reduced car use improved air pollution to some extent, it would make only a small contribution compared with wider regulation. Policymakers should therefore design rules that address pollution sources beyond vehicles rather than depending on fuel prices alone. This would allow the government to seek new opportunities for environmental rehabilitation without simply making daily movement more expensive for citizens. Sustainable national development is a far more effective policy goal because it harmonizes ecological responsibility with economic viability. Electric cars offer one alternative approach, This is because they represent a change in transportation rather than only a higher cost for using it. Renewable energy generation is another important option, because cleaner energy can support long-term environmental protection more directly. New governmental changes are needed to reduce the environmental issue, but those changes should emphasize broader regulation and technological innovation instead of raising fuel prices for private vehicles.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Nowadays, almost everyone can afford to join the internet from any point in the world. However, not every source of data is the truth, there is much disinformation by which ordinary people can be misguided. No doubt that the power of control over informational recourse should be and depends on its spreading and influence on people. Firstly, there are many online forums, courses and conferences during which person can catch wrong ideas or be deceived by following the pieces of advice that dominate there. For instance, in Russia many poor or even rich people buy various guides in order to be on the lap of luxury. At last, people lose their invested finance, which fluctuate between 1 thousand and several million rubles, time and sometimes even health and accommodation because of the loan on study. Secondly, such examples show the significance of informational regulation from the government. It can be implemented by installing some restrictions through internet providers, with legal responsibility for disobeying. To demonstrate, in China the external and internal web is filtered, all messengers, content platforms are banned or under control from special structures. Therefore, it prevents misleading news and other provocative material from becoming popular. Taking everything into account, at modern time information around us will be coming more and more available, bringing about the increasing probability to be misguided or deceived by fake news.\", \"sampled\": \"Some people argue that government should make economic expansion its main aim, while others believe support for disadvantaged people deserves more attention. This is a serious choice because both aims are connected with living standards and the strength of society. I believe government should not treat these goals as separate, but should balance growth with help for under-privileged groups. Economic growth is important because a stronger economy can raise the standard of living for many citizens. When output becomes higher, demand for labour can also increase, and this gives more people access to work. Job creation then reduces unemployment and provides steadier income, which is one clear reason for making growth a central government aim. India is a useful example of how economic expansion can be linked with better daily life. CNBC reported that India’s expanding economy greatly improved quality of life in the last decade. This supports the view that growth is not only about national figures. it can also mean more employment, more stable earnings, and better living standards for ordinary households. At the same time, government should focus on improving conditions for poorer citizens, since visible poverty can harm a country’s image. Lower visible poverty may increase tourist interest, while tourists are described as unwilling to visit places with poor living conditions. so governments should combine economic growth with efforts to improve the lives of the marginalized communities.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"It has been suggested that topic for discussion, more people believe that art is a necessary subject for children in different places such as school, on the one ,hand the others believe that it is a waste of time. I am to admit that there are both correct and incorrect points on this topic. First of all, I am of the opinion that art is indispensable for more people because this activity can help you to develop your worldwide, thinking style, comments and so on. Moreover, many prominent artists commenced this process from childhood and they have been unforgotten yet. As a matter of fact, the art is being used by people like(as) both profession and hobby. That many people can earn income from it. Personally, I believe that art helps to affect to become creative. Briefly, I think that art is crucial and indispensable. On the other hand, as a matter of fact, in this day and ,age some people claim that it is a waste of time. It seems to me that there are more reasons as a pattern they think that essential subjects are to enforce by people. A human believes that there isn't meaning on this action and the young or the others must be realized more decisive acts than art. For ,instance they should separate more time for resting, relaxing, learning lessons and so on.\", \"sampled\": \"Many people believe that university education can help a person become successful, and I agree with this view in a balanced way. Education is a right for every person, so Colleges and Universities should not be seen only as places for rich students. Universities play a pivotal role in fostering personal discipline, technical abilities, behavior, and other life skills that are useful beyond examinations. so students can connect education with practical ability. Strong skills can support financial independence and help people pursue their personal dreams, which is a major reason to value education. A survey is cited to show that national happiness is connected with population literacy, so education can matter beyond one person’s career. Government scholarship schemes are also important because some students come from families unable to pay for education. Some people argue that education is not necessary because it is costly and jobs are limited, and this concern should not be ignored. Poverty prevents some people from affording education, with 20% of the population described as being below the poverty line, so alternatives such as social media and internships may help some learners. In my opinion, education matters whether it is gained through university or early work experience, but Universities remain a strong and valuable route to success.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The issue of global warming is catching a fire every other day. According to some people, one should look for alternatives to survive with environmental changes rather than finding a solution to stop it. I partially agree with the given statement, in this essay, I will shed light on it how it is out of dreams. First and foremost, the environment has great changes since the start of human growth, but a man's body is so adapted to various types of temperature change. It is very difficult to stop environmental changes because of today's lifestyle. As everyone became a slave of technology, but a major cause of climate variation is the growth of modern technology so it is hard to imagine a life without it. For instance, everybody uses their own cars to travel every day, but vehicles are the main source of pollution that is a major concern for environmental changes. So one should think about the ways to survive with it. On the other side of the coin, it is very difficult to stay longer with the preventive measures rather than preventing a problem. Furthermore, people cannot even imagine the consequences of how badly these climate changes can affect our lives. To exemplify the ozone layer of the earth is getting damaged by everyday pollution, which is a major cause of skin cancer in some countries.\", \"sampled\": \"Children’s behaviour can be influenced by television, but the main question is whether the content they watch matters more than the time they spend watching it. Some people believe that what children see on television shapes their actions, especially because young viewers may copy or be affected by the programmes they watch. I agree more with the view that viewing duration has the stronger effect, although unsuitable content can also create negative influence. Television content is still important because cartoons and animation attract many young viewers, and children may be influenced even when a programme is fictional. However, Violent television scenes can set a dangerous precedent, so it is reasonable to argue that children may learn negative behaviour from such shows. A study from university of manitoba is used to claim that 80% of teenagers learn bad things from TV shows, which supports concern about harmful content. The stronger influence, in my opinion, comes from the amount of television children watch. This is because long viewing time gives television a larger place in their daily lives. Some children are highly attached to television because many modern and interesting shows are available, and research is cited to claim that 70% of children spend their maximum time watching it. Parents should set a fixed period for children to watch television, while also paying attention to the kind of programmes their children choose.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"One day, everybody figures out himself as a part of a family with their own biography including a genealogical tree, history of the country and the state or even the house or building where they have been living from generation to generation. The last point become more popular nowadays and is worth to be observed in detail. To begin with, currently it’s quite uncommon to live in one house for decades. Due to this, it will turn more curious to look for historical facts connected with your family nest and ancestors if they historically live in the same place. For example, in the movie “Addams Family” their house greatly reflects the lifestyle and history of their weird family and makes the characters more self-confident in my opinion. However, it’s not mandatory to live somewhere long in order to have an interest in history of the place where you live. Being a stranger first time, people try to get acquainted closer with new place where they are going to live for a while. It helps to integrate in a new society. Fortunately, the wide range of sources providing significant historical information, makes such investigation faster and more pleasant. For instance, each middle city is used to have a kind of national museum performing exhibitions about the history of the place and also about buildings. In case of absence such museum, anybody has an access to the Internet, which provides a plenty of information about everything.\", \"sampled\": \"Whether secondary pupils should be encouraged to comment on or criticize their teachers is a sensitive question in education. Some people believe that student feedback can improve educational quality, while others worry that it may weaken classroom discipline and respect for teachers. I support allowing reasonable student recommendations, especially when they are aimed at improving learning systems rather than attacking teachers personally. The concern about discipline is understandable because teachers receive long training before educating students. Official educators are also expected to hold professional credentials, so public respect for teachers is often seen as a reason for students to follow teachers' words. In Taiwan, this respect can make criticism appear improper, especially when pupils are still expected to learn from teachers in a structured classroom. Even so, teachers can make mistakes because they are human, and respectful listening does not mean every teaching decision is perfect. Some senior instructors may keep using outdated teaching methods, and in fact, over one-third of high school faculty members are reportedly over sixty years old. If these teachers continue to use ordinary guidelines, students may have useful opinions about materials and methods that affect daily learning. A practical example is the suggestion that students could recommend e-textbooks to improve materials and reduce backpack weight. In Taipei, over 50% of junior school students are said to petition homeroom teachers for e-learning platforms, which shows criticism can become a constructive request.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"essay\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Some cultures have a belief that younger family members should be responsible for elders in the family by law once they no longer have enough strength to look after themselves in both physically and mentally ways. This has become a controversial issue. From my point of view, I am disagreed with this idea and reckon it should not be made legal. The reasons for my argument will be explained further in the following paragraphs before the conclusion is reached. At present, there are some segments of society who think that it is a necessary job for descendants to take care of the elderly when they become physically and mentally weak. However, I strongly disagree with this, not with the fact that pupils take care of elders but with the belief that it should be made legal. I always think that it is a good deed for youth to look after their family members, but this should be an optional choice. Everyone has their own right to choose whether they want or do not want to do something. Furthermore, it is found by one of the surveys that some people, including young ages, are not ready to hold others' lives in their hands because they are still struggling with their own lives. For instance, think about a person who works all day and night but is still surrounded by problems such as financial, working, and educational issues.\", \"sampled\": \"Families often move abroad because of employment, and this choice can affect children in both positive and negative ways. In my view, work-related migration can improve children’s future prospects, especially when the move gives the family a higher standard of living. Although there may be early difficulties, these problems do not necessarily outweigh the benefits. Many migrants move from less developed countries to more developed ones, where children may receive better support in daily life. Developed destinations are described as offering benefits such as education and healthcare support, which can make family life more secure. Canada is one example, as it is used to show a country providing free medical care and schooling. Another advantage is that migrant children may later gain stronger employment prospects than they would have had in their country of origin. Better education and wider job opportunities can help them build a more stable future. For this reason, parents who relocate for work may be making a choice that benefits their children in the long term. There are still clear disadvantages, when children must adapt to an unfamiliar culture and live without friends in the new society. This can lead to isolation and depression, and language difficulties can make the situation worse, such as for families moving from Asian countries to Europe without fluency in English. A Hindustan Times survey reported that 40 percent of Indian Punjabi youth felt isolated in Ontario, Canada due to weak English ability, but such adjustment problems can lessen after children learn the local language and interact with local people.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n","data/meeting_summary.jsonl":"{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"AS AMENDED a bill for an ordinance amending Title 27 of the Revised Municipal Code of the City and County of Denver to revise definitions and procedures set forth therein for greater clarity and to extend the minimum affordability period for properties that receive city subsidies from twenty years to sixty years. Amends Section 27 of the Denver Revised Municipal Code (DRMC) to approve revisions to the Affordable Housing Preservation ordinance including definitions and procedures intended to clarify applicability to income-restricted properties within the city and extension of the minimum period of affordability from 20 years to 60 years. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 10-3-18. Amended 10-15-18 to delay the effective date of the affordability period to 2-1-19.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 18-1089, presented by Laura Brzezinski of the Office of Economic Development and reviewed by the Safety Committee, proposes increasing the minimum affordability period for city-subsidized housing projects from 20 years to 60 years, with an amendment introduced by Councilwoman Kenny limiting the change to contracts dated on or after February 1, 2019, to provide certainty for developers whose tax credit letters of intent to the Colorado Housing and Finance Authority are due before that deadline. The amendment serves as a compromise against a full postponement to February 2019, while the bill also clarifies definitions and notice periods in the preservation ordinance based on three years of implementation experience since the right of first refusal was added in 2015.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request City Manager to create a comprehensive plan to recruit and include Long Beach businesses in all contracts and purchasing agreements. This should include but not be limited to; a review of current Request for Proposal process, preference points for businesses located in the City of Long Beach, preference points for businesses who are certified California Small Businesses, small/micro businesses, etc. Examples of existing programs throughout California include the \\\"Pasadena First Buy Local\\\" program, the City of Los Angeles \\\"Business Inclusion Program - Local Business Preference\\\", and the City of Oakland's Local & Small Local Business Certification Program. This program should be brought back to the City Council for review in 120 days.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to direct the Long Beach City Manager to develop a comprehensive plan to recruit and include Long Beach businesses in all city contracts and purchasing agreements, with the plan due back to council in 120 days. The motion, brought by Councilwoman Gonzalez, Councilmember Supernormal, Councilwoman Mongo, and Councilman Andrews, calls for a review of the request for proposal process and the establishment of preference points for businesses located in Long Beach and for California-certified small businesses, drawing on models from Pasadena, the County of Los Angeles, and the City of Burbank. Proposed incentive criteria include whether a business is headquartered in Long Beach, whether its main service location is in Long Beach, and whether it employs Long Beach residents, with annual business license reporting suggested as a tracking mechanism.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. RFP TI15-017 and award a contract to Tyler Technologies, Inc., of Yarmouth, ME, for a Citywide Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) system with finance/human resources software applications and related services, including consultation, implementation, training, maintenance and support, for a one-time amount of $8,478,522, and an annual amount starting at $493,481 and increasing up to 3 percent annually for a five-year term for ongoing maintenance and support, plus a contingency amount of $1,664,772, with the option of renewing the ongoing maintenance and support contract increasing up to 5 percent annually for an additional five-year period, at the discretion of the City Manager; and authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contract, including any necessary amendments thereto.\", \"sampled\": \"Authorization of a contract award to Tyler Technologies. The Long Beach City Council, on recommendation of the Financial Management and Technology and Innovation department, approved a contract with Tyler Technologies for a citywide enterprise resource planning system covering finance and human resources, at a one-time amount of $8.4 million with annual payments starting at $483,000 over a five-year term, as part of a total $30 million project cost funded across all city operations with less than $10 million drawn from the general fund and the remainder from harbor, water, gas, and oil funds, replacing obsolete payroll, accounting, financial reporting, budget, position control, and employee benefit management systems following an evaluation process that engaged 240 employees, consumed 11,000 man-hours over more than two years, and drew no vendor protests, with approximately 25 full-time city employees to be temporarily reassigned during an implementation period of approximately three years.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request City Council to receive and file the Letter of Completion for the Hotel Workplace Requirements and Restrictions petition and approve one of three alternative actions, as required by the California Elections Code Section 9215 and the Long Beach City Charter Section 2000: 1) Adopt the Initiative Ordinance, without alteration, at the regular meeting at which the certification of the petition is presented, or within 10 days after it is presented; or 2) Submit the ordinance, without alteration, to the voters pursuant to Section 1405, and adopt resolutions a) and b).\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation that the Long Beach City Council adopt the Hotel Workplace Requirements and Restrictions initiative ordinance without alteration, submit it to voters at a citywide special municipal election consolidated with the statewide general election on November 6, 2018, or order a report under the California Election Code, following the City Clerk's confirmation that proponents submitted 46,084 signatures over 103 days of circulation against a required minimum of 27,462, that a 3% random draw yielded a 95-110% validity rate triggering a full signature check, and that the Los Angeles County Registrar Recorder County Clerk verified the petition as successful on July 27 after being engaged on June 28, with proponent Zoe Nicholson stating \\\"an army of angels collected 46,000 signatures in 55 days to guarantee safety, justice for hotel housekeepers,\\\" L.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute any and all documents necessary, including a Purchase and Sale Agreement (PSA), with Eddie N John #1, LLC, a California limited liability company, and Youna Kim, a Sole Proprietor dba Eddie's Liquor #1 (collectively \\\"Seller\\\"), for the purchase of certain real property located at 6841-6845 Atlantic Avenue, Assessor Parcel Numbers 7116-019-029 and -036 (Subject Property), in the amount not to exceed $9,591,540; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary for a Lease between the City of Long Beach and Eddie's Liquor #1, for the use of the Subject Property; Adopt Negative Declaration ND-02-19; and Increase appropriations in the General Fund (GF) in the Economic Development Department (ED) by $9,591,540, offset by the sale proceeds from the Broadway Block Site B and transfer of HEAP grant revenue from the Health Fund (SR 130).\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation that the City Manager be authorized to execute all documents necessary, including \\\"s Liquor Number one for the purchase of certain real property located at 684136845 Atlantic Avenue in the amount not to exceed 9,000,591 540 and execute a lease between the city of Long Beach and Eddie,\\\" consistent with prior council actions taken to address homelessness in District nine, and as described by staff who noted \\\"m going to turn this over to Mr. West. Thank you, Mr. Mayor. This is a result of at least over a year of negotiations to try to impact our homelessness issue here in the city of Long Beach by creating not only a winter shelter or rainy day shelter, but a year round shelter. We\\\" and further indicated \\\"m going to turn this over to our director of Health and Human Services, Kelly Collopy, who will be joined by Mary Torres with John Kiser, the Director of Economic Development. So Kelly. John Castle. I\\\" in presenting this item to the Honorable Mayor and members of the City Council.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request City Attorney to draft the following resolutions: 1. Resolution consenting to Inclusion of Properties within the City's Incorporated Area in California Home Finance Authority (CHF) Community Facilities District No. 2014-1 (Clean Energy) to Finance Renewable Energy Generation, Energy Efficiency, Water Conservation and Electric Vehicle Charging Infrastructure Improvements and approving associate membership in CHF; and 2. Resolution consenting to Inclusion of Properties within the City's Incorporated Area in the CHF Property Assessed Clean Energy (PACE) Program to Finance Renewable Energy Generation, Energy and Water Efficiency Improvements and Electric Vehicle Charging Infrastructure and approving associate membership in CHF.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to authorize the City Attorney to draft two resolutions for Long Beach's associate membership in the California Home Finance Authority JPA, limited to commercial and industrial properties only. Councilman Andrews moved to amend his agenda item to exclude residential properties, citing staff concerns about consumer protection, high interest rates, and the absence of FICO score underwriting in residential PACE programs. Staff indicated they wished to study residential PACE programs for about a year before recommending further council action, noting the city holds no seat on the JPA and that opting in by resolution represents the full extent of Long Beach's involvement.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to approve the use of the Sixth Council District's one-time infrastructure funds transferred from the Capital Projects Fund (CP) in the Public Works Department (PW) into the Economic and Property Development (EP) in the amount of $80,000 to support the preservation of a historical and cultural asset at the top of the building located at 1030 Pacific Coast Highway; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute the necessary documents with World Famous VIP Records LLC, a private organization located in the City of Long Beach, in an amount of $80,000 to be used for the removal, restoration, historical landmark designation, and temporary storage of the iconic World Famous VIP Records sign, until a permanent location can be determined within the City of Long Beach; and Increase appropriations in the General Fund (GF) in the Economic and Property Development Department (EP) budget by $80,000.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to approve use of $80,000 in Sixth District one-time infrastructure funds for preservation of the VIP Records sign at 1030 Pacific Coast Highway, with $50,000 provided directly to VIP's LLC for restoration costs and $30,000 allocated for fundraising, removal, and temporary storage, contingent on Kelvin Anderson retaining complete ownership and all intellectual property, completing restoration within 24 months of executing the agreement, pursuing historical landmark status for the sign, and keeping it within Long Beach city boundaries, recognizing that VIP Records has operated as a record shop and recording studio in Long Beach for 38 years and is cited as the launching point for notable musicians including Snoop Dogg, Nate Dogg, and Warren G, with public commenter Cami Johnson stating, \\\"When people come to California, they come to Long Beach, they say, okay, Queen Mary. But second to that is VIP records.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to enter into an Improvement and Reimbursement Agreement with Kilroy Realty, L. P., a California limited partnership, for the repair, repaving and restriping of public right-of-way located within Aero Long Beach (3750 - 3900 Kilroy Airport Way) on the stated terms and conditions; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to negotiate and amend any terms as needed to expedite the repair, repaving and restriping project; and Increase appropriations in the Capital Projects Fund Group in the Public Works Department by $2,600,000, offset by revenue received from Kilroy Realty, L.P, for the Improvement of Kilroy Airport Way.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to authorize the City Manager to enter into an improvement and reimbursement agreement with Kilroy Realty LP for repair, repaving, and re-striping of public right of way within Arrow Long Beach. The Long Beach City Council, on motion by Councilman Shuler, approved the agreement and increased appropriations by $2,600,000 for the improvement of Kilroy Airport Way in District 4, also authorizing the City Manager to negotiate and amend terms as needed to expedite the project, with the motion carrying votes from all nine districts and no public comment received.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. ITB TS-14-025 and authorize City Manager to execute contracts, and any additional amendments thereto, with Federal Signal Safety and Security Systems, of University Park, IL (not an MBE, WBE, SBE or Local), Halifax Security, Inc., of Brick, NJ (not an MBE, WBE, SBE or Local), and Convergint Technologies, of Orange, CA (not an MBE, WBE, SBE or Local) for furnishing and delivering video surveillance cameras and related system components, in a total amount not to exceed $500,000; and increase appropriations in the General Services Fund (IS 385) in the Technology Services Department (TS) by $500,000.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to approve. The Long Beach City Council approved Item 19, a contract with CIGNA Signals Safety and Security Systems of University Park, Illinois for video surveillance cameras and related components not to exceed $500,000 for crime and law enforcement purposes, with the police department directed to solicit camera placement requests from each of the 9 council districts, including the Ranchos area as requested by Councilmember O'Donnell, and approved Item 20, a contract for professional and technical IT services covering systems analysis, development, project management, and technical support not to exceed $950,000, with both items reported by Technology Services and Financial Management.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Agreement between the City and County of Denver and CH2M Hill Engineers, Inc. for on-call Program Management for the National Western Center Campus Development. Approves an on-call contract with CH2M Hill Engineers, Inc. for $14 million and three years for program management services to develop the baseline scope, schedule, budget and phasing for the National Western Center Campus Development at the National Western Center in Council District 9 (201632012-00). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 2-13-17. The Committee approved filing this resolution at its meeting on 1-3-17.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution authorizing Council Bill 16-1317, which grants the Mayor's National Western Center Office contracting authority of up to $14 million for a program manager for the National Western Center, calculated at 3% of the $276 million in voter-approved funds. The contract is structured as a task-based agreement covering program management only, not construction management, with six embedded staff reporting to Executive Director Kelly Leed, who noted, \\\"We're still at a place where we don't know what we don't know.\\\" Future construction contracts will continue to require council approval, and the office will deliver an annual workplan each March reporting on milestones, budget, scope, and schedule. Councilman Espinosa announced a no vote, citing discomfort with the broad contracting latitude and reduced council oversight.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Approves a contract with Panasonic Energy Solutions Company (PESCO) to provide a $1.35 million Business Incentive Fund (BIF) reimbursable grant for the purposes of creating 250 jobs at their business solutions, operations and technology center, located at the 61st and Pena Boulevard light rail station in Council District 11. (BUSINESS DEVELOPMENT) Approves a contract with Panasonic Energy Solutions Company (PESCO) to provide a $1.35 million Business Incentive Fund (BIF) reimbursable grant for the purposes of creating 250 jobs at their business solutions, operations and technology center, located at the 61st and Pena Boulevard light rail station in Council District 11. The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on . The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting 4-28-15.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver City Council approved Council Bill 254 by an 11-to-1 vote, authorizing over $1.3 million in business incentives to Panasonic Enterprise Solution Company for a facility near 61st and Pena Station that will create approximately 250 jobs with an average salary over $55,000 and is projected to generate more than $2.5 million in total fiscal benefit to the city. Councilman Fox cast the sole dissenting vote, opposing the incentive as corporate welfare and an inappropriate use of general fund money, while the Office of Economic Development noted its standard 2-to-1 fiscal return requirement. Final consideration of Council Bill 303 was postponed until after action on Council Bill 302, and Council Bill 356 was postponed until July 30, 2015.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. RFP CM15-158 for the purchase and development opportunity at 120 East 3rd Street, Assessor Parcel Number 7280-020-907 (Subject Property); Declare the City-owned Subject Property as surplus; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute any and all necessary documents including a Purchase and Sale Agreement with William Morris Commercial, Inc., a California corporation, for the sale of the Subject Property in the amount of $480,000; and Accept Categorical Exemption CE 16-126.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend adoption of specifications for the purchase and development of 120 East Third Street, a vacant 2,400-square-foot building on a 1,250-square-foot lot between Pine and the Promenade in District 2, and authorize the City Manager to declare the property surplus and execute sale documents with William Morris Commercial at a purchase price of $480,000 for a co-working facility, with a categorical exemption CE 16-126 accepted.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. RFP CM15-159 for the purchase and development opportunity at Anaheim Street and Walnut Avenue (1205-1209 Walnut Avenue/1500-1546 East Anaheim Street), Assessor Parcel Numbers 7267-001-900, -901, -902, -903, -904, -905, -906 (Subject Property); Declare the City-owned Subject Property as surplus; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute any and all necessary documents, including a Purchase and Sale Agreement with BRIDGE Housing Corporation and The Children’s Clinic “Serving Children and their Families”, both California corporations, for the sale of the Subject Property in the amount of $2,500,000; and Accept Categorical Exemption CE 16-138.\", \"sampled\": \"Adopt specifications for the purchase and development opportunity at Anaheim Street and Walnut Avenue in District 6, authorizing the city manager to execute documents with Bridge Housing Corporation and the Children’s Clinic for the $2.5 million sale of the surplus property, as recommended by Economic and Property Development and Development Services, and with Councilmember Andrews stating “This kind of development means or it meets more than the number of surrounding communities needs, and I hope this will be a new standard for all developers on the city property,” the project creating 408 temporary jobs and 42 permanent jobs while delivering housing for the aging community, affordable health care clinics, space for two nonprofit organizations serving the Cambodian community, and a small business development center.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance amending the Denver medical marijuana code of the Denver Revised Municipal Code to regulate and implement medical marijuana research and development facilities and to modify provisions to conform with state statute. Amends the Denver Medical Marijuana Code of the Denver Revised Municipal Code to authorize a new local medical marijuana license to regulate and implement medical marijuana research and development facilities and to modify provisions to conform with state statute.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver City Council passed a new marijuana license type ordinance by a 13–1 roll call vote after Council member CdeBaca urged colleagues to vote no, arguing the measure opens a brand new license type without an equity plan and noting fewer than ten black or brown license owners exist in Colorado, with even fewer in Denver.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Plans and Specifications No. R-6960 for the Rehabilitation of Bixby Road between Atlantic Avenue and Orange Avenue; award the contract to Sully-Miller Contracting Company, of Brea, CA, in the amount of $745,416, and authorize a 10 percent contingency in the amount of $74,542, for a total contract amount not to exceed $819,958; and authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contract, including any necessary amendments thereto. (Districts 7,8)\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend award of a contract to Sally Miller Contracting Company for the Bixby Road rehabilitation between Atlantic Avenue and Orange Avenue, in an amount not to exceed $819,958, with construction planned to start in July and finish before school starts in late August or early September, as the Public Works Director stated, “Our goal is to start in July and finish before school starts in beginning of September, end of August.” The project lies in Districts 7 and 8, serves the nearly 3,000 students at nearby Hughes and Longfellow schools, and was approved by a 7-0 vote.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance approving a proposed Intergovernmental Agreement to Provide Fire Protection between the City and County of Denver and the City of Glendale. Approves a twenty-one-year intergovernmental agreement with the City of Glendale in the amount of $68,482,176 in revenue to provide fire protection service within the municipal boundary of the City of Glendale (FIRES-201103623-01). The Committee approved filing this bill by consent on 11-1-17.\", \"sampled\": \"The council approved a 21-year intergovernmental agreement with Glendale for Denver Fire Department fire protection services, including annual payments escalating at 3% per year to reflect estimated labor cost increases. Councilman Flynn questioned the 3% escalation over the full term, and Legislative Counsel Kirsten Crawford confirmed Glendale is not obligated to adopt the voter-approved green roof code addition, stating, “We’re not obligated in Glendale today.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"AS AMENDED a bill for an ordinance amending sections 6-206 and 24-508, Denver Revised Municipal Code regarding the hours of operation of retail marijuana stores and medical marijuana centers. Allows licensed marijuana stores to remain open until 10:00 pm. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 4-3-17. Amended 4-17-17 to clearly communicate to the public and to the marijuana industry the first date upon which any retail marijuana store or medical marijuana center may remain open until 10:00 p.m. will be Monday, May 1.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 324 as amended addresses hours of operation for retail medical marijuana centers. Councilman Herndon moved to place the bill upon final consideration and do pass. Councilman Ortega offered Amendment A, which requires locations with a pending new license application as of May 1, 2017 to remain subject to the former 7 p.m. closing time until the new license is granted based on full compliance. Councilwoman Blake opposed the amendment, stating, “I don’t feel like we’re going to have any sort of discussion except for what we’re doing here,” while Councilman Kennish called it redundant and Councilwoman Sussman argued licensing processes already address bad actors.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance approving the Emily Griffith Opportunity School Urban Redevelopment Plan and the creation of the Emily Griffith Opportunity School Redevelopment Area and the Emily Griffith Opportunity School Tax Increment Area. Approves the Emily Griffith Opportunity School Urban Redevelopment Plan authorizing the creation of an Urban Redevelopment Area and tax increment areas in Council District 9. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 7-25-17.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver Urban Renewal Authority proposes creation of an urban redevelopment area for the former Emily Griffith Opportunity School site, a 2.4-acre parcel in Council District 9 bounded by Welton, 13th, Glenarm, and 12th streets. A blight study by Matrix Design Group found five blight factors including deteriorating structures, inadequate street layout, unsafe conditions, environmental contamination, and underutilization. The redevelopment plan aligns with the Denver Comprehensive Plan, Blueprint Denver, and Downtown Area Plan.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance authorizing a rescission, a cash transfer, and a supplemental appropriation from the General Contingency Fund to the Capital Improvement Fund. Approves a supplemental appropriation of $1,450,000 from General Fund Contingency to transfer to the Other Agency Capital Project Fund to provide sufficient budget capacity to fund a settlement in support of the National Western Center Office in Council District 9.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 2019-0874 was moved and seconded to be ordered published, appropriating general fund contingency dollars to cover a $1.4 or $1.5 million overage from a rail settlement agreement for the National Western Complex, a critical path item without which the $965 million project funded primarily by 2C bond funds and Regional Tourism Act dollars cannot proceed.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to approve a first substantial amendment to the 2018-2022 Five-Year Consolidated Plan, Citizen Participation Plan, and Fiscal Year 2019-2020 Action Plan to incorporate emergency funds from the Coronavirus Aid, Relief and Economic Security Act (CARES Act), Community Development Block Grant Coronavirus (CDBG-CV) and Emergency Solutions Grant Coronavirus (ESG-CV), and approve a substantial amendment to the Fiscal Year 2017 and Fiscal Year 2018 Action Plans to reallocate Home Investment Partnerships Program (HOME) funds for COVID-19 response; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all necessary documents with the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD), and other entities necessary to receive these funds, and execute agreements necessary to implement and comply with related federal regulations; and Increase appropriations in the Community Development Grant Fund Group in the Development Services Department by $5,465,696, offset by grant revenue.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend adoption of a first substantial amendment to the five-year consolidated plan, Citizens Participation Plan, and Fiscal Year Action Plan to incorporate CARES Act emergency funds, authorizing the City Manager to execute HUD documents and increase appropriations in the Community Development Grant Fund Group by $5,465,696, including $3.6 million in CDBG-CV and $1.8 million in ESG-CV funds to support a $5 million rental assistance program providing up to $1,000 per month for three months to households at or below 80% area median income with COVID-19 income loss. The program, modeled after L.A. County’s model with $300,000 from Supervisor Hahn, requires direct landlord payments, a lottery system with at least 2,000 spots, and full expenditure by December 2020.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Fourth Amendatory Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Kimley-Horn and Associates, Inc. to Amend by adding compensation and a new end date to meet grant funding requirements, including additional coordination with CDOT, for enhanced bicycle design for the Broadway corridor in Council District 7. Amends a contract with Kimley-Horn and Associates, Inc. by adding $89,940 for a new total of $1,960,871.89 and two years for a new end date of 6-30-23 to meet grant funding requirements, including additional coordination with CDOT, for a two-way enhanced bicycle facility concept design for the Broadway corridor in Council District 7 (201843805-04;\", \"sampled\": \"Council Resolution 22-0005 approves a contract for the Broadway protected bike lane, allowing the Department of Transportation and Infrastructure to provide permanent infrastructure for an existing lane using its paint and post method for rapid implementation and creating stronger barriers between bikes and cars. Councilmember Hines spoke in favor of the contract, stating the bike lane should continue north from District seven into District ten to provide a continuous route for cyclists. The resolution was passed as part of a block vote. The block vote passed with a result of 13 to 0.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request City Manager to work with Downtown Long Beach Associates (DLBA) to identify methods and estimated costs for activating the section of Victory Park adjacent to the former Jergins Building Property, including but not limited to temporary pop-up retail, tourist, wayfinding, historical and arts-based installations; and Request City Manager to also explore ideas and estimated costs of activating the parking lot at Seaside Way and Pine Avenue (100 East Ocean Boulevard) with DLBA.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation from Vice Mayor Lowenthal and Councilwoman Gonzalez to request the City Manager work with DLP and willing partner Craig Cogen to identify methods and estimated costs for the temporary activation of the southeast corner of Pine Avenue and Ocean Boulevard, a portion of Victory Park that has been closed to the public for decades, in an effort to revitalize the public space and explore possibilities for making Ocean Boulevard seem less large and untenable.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to receive supporting documentation into the record, conclude the public hearing regarding an economic subsidy associated with the First Amendment to Sales Tax Incentive Agreement No. 32687 with Alant Corporation, dba Circle Porsche Audi, a California corporation, pursuant to California Government Code Section 53083; and Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to implement the First Amendment to Sales Tax Incentive Agreement No. 32687 with Alant Corporation, a California corporation, for the expanded Circle Porsche and Audi dealership located at 1855 North Lakewood Boulevard.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to conclude the public hearing and authorize the City Manager to execute the First Amendment to the Sales Tax Incentive Agreement with Ashland Corporation and Allen Corporations. The agreement is for the Expanded Circle Port and Aldi dealership located at 1855 North Lakewood Boulevard District four, where an expansion project is estimated to cost $9.22 million. This proposed amendment retains the same 50% city share terms, strengthens a local business, preserves 87 existing jobs, and has a maximum incentive of $2.5 million for new improvements.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute a contract and all necessary amendments, with Southern California Edison, to participate in the Charge Ready Program, which will provide the electric infrastructure required to install 90 electric vehicle (EV) charging stations, and up to an additional 210 for a total of 300, if the Charge Ready Program is expanded and the City is awarded additional incentives; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all required documents and easements pursuant to the requirements of the Charge Ready Program with the right to quit claim; Adopt resolution authorizing City Manager, or designee, to execute a contract, and all necessary amendments, with ChargePoint, of Campbell, CA, to provide, install, and maintain up to 300 (EV) charging stations, on the same terms and conditions afforded to the City of Santa Monica, CA, in an amount not to exceed $4,200,000;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation from the Department of Public Works, Energy Resources, to authorize the City Manager, or designee, to execute all required documents and easements, pursuant to the Southern California Edison (SCE) Charge Ready Program and with Chargepoint for the citywide expansion of the Charger Ready Program, which has been a great success. This item will allow for the installation of 300 EV charging units providing greater public access and supporting expansion of the city. We will continue the partnership that we have with Edison to implement important projects that align with the city. This item is being highlighted this evening because it is a continuation of the Working Energy Leadership Partnership between the City of Long Beach and Southern California Edison and the city is now in Phase 2 of the program, which is a $4,000,200 investment from SCE into the city for the installation of approximately 3800 EV chargers at 13 sites.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to enter into a Grant Agreement with Community Hospital Long Beach Foundation, a Long Beach-based nonprofit corporation, in an amount not to exceed $1,000,000, for reimbursement of predevelopment costs incurred by the City of Long Beach to rebuild and replace buildings located at 1720 Termino Avenue, 1760 Termino Avenue, and 4111 East Willow Street (Subject Property), to continue to operate an acute care hospital at the site; and Adopt resolution authorizing City Manager, or designee, to execute an agreement with Perkins + Will, a private for-profit corporation in Los Angeles, CA, for hospital facility architectural design and consulting services, in an amount not to exceed $1,000,000.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation from the Department of Economic Development to authorize the City Manager to execute a grant agreement with the Long Beach Community Hospital Foundation for an amount not to exceed $1 million to reimburse pre-development costs for the rebuilding and replacement of buildings at the Community Hospital Long Beach site, including properties at 1720 Termino, 1760 Termino, and 411 East Wilton Street, to continue operating an acute care hospital and achieve seismic compliance; and adopt a resolution to execute an agreement with Perkins and Will for architectural, design, and consulting services, which corrects a previously listed address of 4111 East Willis Street and selects the firm based on its prior feasibility evaluation for the hospital.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to receive supporting documentation into the record, conclude the public hearing, adopt resolution authorizing City Manager to execute a contract with IPS Group Inc., of San Diego, CA for the purchase and installation of electronic parking meters on the same terms and conditions afforded to the City of Sacramento, California for an initial term of five (5) years, in an amount not to exceed $1,538,979 in year one, and in an annual amount estimated at $446,492, but in any case not to exceed $941,542, for the succeeding four (4) years, with the option to renew for five (5) additional one-year periods, at the discretion of the City Manager;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to authorize the City Manager to execute a contract with IPS Group for the purchase and installation of electronic parking meters in an amount not to exceed $1,538,979; adopt a resolution establishing parking meter rates as defined in the Long Beach Municipal Code; decrease appropriations in the City Activities Department by $800,000; and increase appropriations in the Public Works Department by $1,148,134, the Belmont Shore Parking Meter Fund in the Public Works Department by $44,573, and the Rainbow Harbor Fund in the Public Works Department by $153,891.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. RFP EP19-028 for the purchase and development opportunity at 925-945 East Pacific Coast Highway, Assessor Parcel Numbers 7210-013-900 and -901 (Subject Property); Declare the City-owned Subject Property as surplus; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute any and all documents necessary including a Purchase and Sale Agreement (PSA) with Mercy Housing California, Inc., a California nonprofit public benefit corporation, or affiliate (Buyer/Developer), for the sale of the Subject Property in the amount of $750,000; and Accept Categorical Exemption CE 19-168.\", \"sampled\": \"Authorize the sale of surplus property at 925-945 East Pacific Coast Highway to Mercy Housing California for $750,000. Following a 2018 RFP process, the developer plans to build 68 new senior housing units on the long-vacant District 6 site, a project expected to create 140 construction jobs and five permanent jobs. Mercy Housing California will have a 90-day due diligence period and 21 months to secure financing for the development, which includes a community room, an outreach resource center, and units for formerly homeless seniors.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to: (1) Receive a presentation on the Proposed Long Beach Recovery Plan, provide input and policy direction, and approve the Long Beach Recovery Plan and its priorities to address the public health and economic impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic utilizing Federal, State, and County relief funding, with approval subject to confirmation of compliance with American Rescue Plan Act regulations, when issued, with direction to return to the City Council if there are required material changes; (2) Adopt resolution authorizing City Manager, or designee, to execute all Block Grant award documents on behalf of the City of Long Beach (City), and any documents necessary including necessary amendments, to accept State Emergency Rental Assistance Program funding for the City’s Emergency Rental Assistance Program;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to receive a presentation on the proposed Long Beach Recovery Act, a plan described as \\\"probably one of the most significant pieces of legislation and proposals that we have\\\" seen in decades and \\\"an opportunity for us with a significant amount of federal funds, to make a bold impact on the future of our city,\\\" and, following the presentation and public comment, approve the Long Beach Recovery Plan Adobe Resolution, increase appropriations in the Community Development Grants Fund Group and Health Fund Group for the BLOCK Grant Award, and approve the Economic Recovery Strategy Report, all part of a response to a crisis considered \\\"the most difficult challenge that we have ever faced,\\\" more than an earthquake, and a challenge which has included testing up to 5,000 people per day, with the item introduced by Vice Mayor Richardson and Councilwoman Mongeau of The Economic Development Committee.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to increase appropriations in the General Fund (GF) in the City Manager Department (CM) by $6,500, offset by the Second Council District One-time District Priority funds transferred from the Citywide Activities Department (XC) to provide funds to Fix Long Beach who will be hosting a Spay and Neuter event in the Second Council District; and Decrease appropriations in the General Fund (GF) in the Citywide Activities Department (XC) by $6,500 to offset a transfer to the City Manager Department (CM).\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to increase appropriations in the General Fund, City Manager Department, by $6,500, offset by a donation from FICS Long Beach for the \\\"Spay and Neuter Incentive Program\\\" in Council District One; approve a Fiscal Year 19 budget appropriation ordinance amendment; and thank Councilmember Pierce for accepting the donation and extending it, with further thanks to council members Suzy Pryce and Darrell Supert for their support of an event scheduled for March 9th to provide free spay/neuter procedures and microchips.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance amending the classification and pay plan for employees in the Career Service and for certain employees not in the Career Service. Amends the Classification and Pay Plan by abolishing the City’s twenty current pay tables, replacing the City’s current pay tables with three proposed pay tables, and reassigning the city’s current classifications to the proposed pay grades table and amends Sections 18 and 14 of the Denver Revised Municipal Code to reflect the streamlining and modernization of the pay tables and pay ranges, maintenance of the pay tables and market adjustment allocations, and certain associated terminology. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 3-9-21.\", \"sampled\": \"A motion was moved and seconded for a combined 30-minute courtesy public hearing for Council Bill 21-0233, allowing speakers to comment on either or both items. The Office of Human Resources previously presented these items to the Finance and Governance Committee, and three individuals signed up to speak this evening. The first speaker, Mike McKee, noted that while a choice is required when signing up to speak for or against an item, he has \\\"one area of particular concern, which is the proposed method of handling\\\" the matter, and thanked council members for taking his comments.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance approving a proposed Cooperation Agreement between the City and County of Denver and 4201 East Arkansas Urban Redevelopment Area, to establish, among other matters, the parameters for tax increment financing with incremental sales and property taxes. Approves a cooperation agreement with Denver Urban Renewal Authority for the 4201 East Arkansas Urban Redevelopment Area to establish the parameters for tax increment financing with incremental sales and property taxes in Council District 6.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 1246 was postponed following a public hearing on Council Bill 1245 related to East Arkansas redevelopment. Additionally, Council Bill 1057 was passed, altering zoning for 2975 Huron Street, while Council Bill 1058 addressed zoning changes for various streets. Public hearings were mandated for zoning modifications and an urban redevelopment plan. Resolutions were approved, and bills were advanced for final consideration, with Councilman Herndon facilitating the adoption of resolutions and final consideration of bills.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Plans and Specifications No. R-7187 and award a contract to E2 Contracting, Inc., of Irvine, CA, for construction of the Long Beach Municipal Urban Stormwater Treatment Facility Phase 1C Wetlands Project, in the amount of $2,689,210, with a 20 percent contingency in the amount of $537,842, for a total contract amount not to exceed $3,227,052; and, authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contract, including any necessary amendments.\", \"sampled\": \"Award the contract to E two contracting for the Long Beach Municipal Urban Stormwater Treatment Facility Phase One C Wetlands Project. The total contract amount should not exceed $3,227,052, with $2 million in funding secured from the amount conservancy for the project. This initiative involves developing a wetlands component on a former private development parcel, constructing wetlands adjacent to the Long Beach Municipal Urban Stormwater Treatment Facility, and adding visitor-serving amenities like a walking path. Enhancements are also planned along the L.A. River.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to direct City Attorney to prepare an urgency ordinance amending Long Beach’s COVID Eviction Moratorium (LBMC 8.100) and The Housing Authority of the City of Long Beach Moratorium on Section 8 terminations in the following three ways, to align Long Beach’s COVID tenant protections with the County and City of Los Angeles: (1) extend the moratorium date from May 31, 2020 until August 31, 2020;\", \"sampled\": \"The council is recommended to approve Councilmember Pearce's proposal to extend the eviction moratorium and rent deferment period for 90 days. This initiative includes setting specific benchmarks for tenants to pay deferred rent by designated dates, aiming to provide necessary relief and stability to affected residents. Additionally, city staff will collaborate with airport tenants to address COVID-related challenges and negotiate rent deferments in accordance with FAA regulations, ensuring compliance and support for those impacted.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving and providing for the execution of a proposed grant agreement between the City and County of Denver and the United States concerning the \\\"CoC-Housing First FY17-18” program and the funding therefore. Accepts a Continuum of Care (CoC) Housing First grant award from the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) in the amount of $2,497,840 with a term through 3-31-18 to provide for 151 units of tenant-based rental assistance, 49 units of sponsor-based rental assistance, and 40 units of project-based rental assistance to chronically and episodically homeless single individuals citywide. The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 4-10-17.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Resolutions 321 and 322 have been adopted, with Del Norte identified as a beneficiary of the continuum of care funds. There is a proposed $6 billion reduction in funding to the US Department of Housing and Urban Development, potentially leading to rent increases for Denver Housing Authority residents. The city, facing impacts from HUD funding cuts, is preparing a strategy to address potential challenges, with concerns that the reduction may exacerbate homelessness locally. Notably, the city secures six grants directly for homeless programs under their continuum of care. The council's recent adoption of Resolution 285 had ten eyes and one abstention, reflecting ongoing engagement with critical community issues.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to Receive and file a report on the feasibility of a Long Beach Community Choice Aggregation (CCA); Defer for two years any decision whether to participate in a CCA, or possibly sooner if new information becomes available solidifying benefits, to allow for increased stability in the California electricity market and reduce potential risk to residents and businesses; Direct City Manager to perform community outreach regarding the CCA concept along with potential benefits and risks to customers, as well as to gain feedback on how supportive the community might be of a Long Beach CCA; Direct City Manager to prepare a study that analyzes CCA governance options including forming a stand-alone City enterprise, creating a new Joint Powers Authority (JPA) with another interested public agency, or joining an existing JPA; Direct City Manager to continue to monitor the energy market and related regulations and report to the City Council annually or sooner if substantial market changes occur; and Direct City Manager to continue the City’s partnership with Southern.\", \"sampled\": \"The City Council of Long Beach is contemplating a strategic move to enter the energy market, with a potential annual power purchase estimated at $150 million. This decision follows a comprehensive feasibility study conducted by independent consultants to explore the creation of a utility. As part of this initiative, Long Beach customers would have the option to select either 50% or 100% renewable electricity, with a focus on comparing renewable energy costs from various providers. Job creation estimates have been presented for both a standalone city energy program and a Joint Powers Authority, with a recommendation for a thorough analysis of governance methods before proceeding, as emphasized by the statement, \\\"We recommend a detailed analysis be done before that decision is made.\\\" Additionally, concerns have been raised regarding public engagement and awareness in the decision-making process of the energy program, alongside the identification of key risks and uncertainties associated with transitioning to a new energy model.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance amending Chapter 27 of the Denver Revised Municipal Code concerning housing, revising provisions related to the linkage fee; repealing incentives for affordable housing; and adopting affordable housing requirements applicable to the creation of new dwelling units. Amends Chapter 27 of the Denver Revised Municipal Code to revise provisions related to the linkage fee, repeal incentives for affordable housing and adopt affordable housing requirements applicable to the creation of new dwelling units. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 4-26-22.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 22-426 has passed for final consideration and do pass. An amendment was proposed to Council Bill 22-426 to change the exemption criteria for accessory dwelling units, aiming to subject units over 400 square feet to the linkage fee. Discussion ensued regarding the exemption for accessory dwelling units from the linkage fee, with questions raised about applying the exemption to attached units and seeking clarifications on the amendment language's impact before a recess was recommended for further clarity.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an Ordinance approving an Agreement with the State of Colorado, Department of Transportation for a major road construction project, known as I-25/Santa Fe & Alameda Avenue Interchange Project. (INFRASTRUCTURE & CULTURE) Approves an intergovernmental agreement with the Colorado Department of Transportation for land transactions and underpass construction for the South Platte Regional Trail and roadway improvements between South Jason Street and South Huron Street for the I-25/Santa Fe and Alameda Avenue Interchange Project in Council District 7 (201626220).\", \"sampled\": \"RTD has withdrawn funding for an infrastructure project, prompting city deliberations on acquiring a bridge landing pad inexpensively. Concerns arise over the bridge's absence impacting bike ped connections. A council member advocates for the crucial project. Criticism emerges over the distant replacement park land site. Discussions unfold on Council Bill 291 concerning marijuana caps, with Councilwoman Gilmore proposing a technical amendment. Councilman Cashman moves to order the publication of Council Bill 291, leading to engaging debates and amendments by council members.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt resolution requesting the Registrar-Recorder/County Clerk to call, provide and give notice of a General Municipal Election to be consolidated with the Statewide General Election to be held in the City of Long Beach on Tuesday, November 8, 2022 and include the proposed Charter amendment to a vote of the qualified electors residing within Long Beach Unified School District Boundaries (LBUSD) to align the Long Beach Board of Education’s primary election date with the State’s primary and general election dates held in even-numbered years;\", \"sampled\": \"The City Attorney recommended adopting a resolution for a general municipal election, consolidated with the statewide general election on November 8th, aligning the Long Beach Board of Education's primary election date, and directed to prepare an impartial analysis for the election, with rules set for filing written arguments. A 9-0 roll call vote favored the motion, with Vice Mayor Richardson voting 'Yes,' and council members Cindy, Ellen, Price, Sabrina, Mangum, Sarah, Sapp, Oranga, and Austin present. Public comment was not received on item 17.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager to execute the Second Amendment to Agreement No. 32027 with Psomas Corporation, to increase the contract amount by $206,400 for professional construction management and inspection services for the Belmont Pool Demolition and related temporary improvements, for a revised, not-to-exceed amount of $831,400, and make any amendments necessary to extend the term, and amend the scope of work within the contract amount.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend approving Somers Corporation for Construction Management Services for the Belmont Pool demolition, with a not-to-exceed cost of $831,000. Address community concerns about tree preservation and wildlife protection during the demolition process, ensuring a certified bird nesting survey is conducted and a staging area near the beach maintenance yard is utilized, impacting up to 40 parking stalls temporarily. Residents emphasize the importance of preserving old growth trees and green areas for public recreation and environmental sustainability.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A resolution by the Council of the City and County of Denver, sitting ex officio as the Board of Directors of the RiNo Denver General Improvement District, approving a Work Plan, adopting a Budget, imposing Capital Charges and Maintenance Charges, approving a Mill Levy, and making appropriations for the 2020 Fiscal Year and approving an Amended Work Plan adopting an Amended Budget, and making appropriations for the 2019 Fiscal Year. Approves the 2020 Work Plan and Budget for the RiNo Denver General Improvement District in Council District 9. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 10-22-19.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Resolution 19-1106 was adopted for the Rhino Denver General Improvement District, situated northwest of downtown. The 2020 Annual Work Plan and Budget for the district were approved, with proposed expenditures totaling $917,214 and revenues projected at $1,159,213. The District Advisory Board oversees the district's operations, with the City Council having previously sanctioned the establishment of the Rhino Denver Guide to bolster infrastructure enhancements in the area. City staff has recommended the approval of the 2020 budget and work plan for the Rhino Denver General Improvement District.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute an agreement, and any related documents, with the Helen Sanders Cat Protection and Welfare Society, of Boulder, CO, and The Little Lion Foundation, of Long Beach, CA, to partner with Long Beach Animal Care Services for the Long Beach Little Paws Project, to be housed in commercial property at 1179 East Wardlow Road, in Long Beach, from January 1, 2019 through December 31, 2019, with annual renewal options through December 31, 2023;\", \"sampled\": \"Authorize the City Manager to execute an agreement with the Helen Sanders Cat Protection and Welfare Society for the Long Beach Little Paws Project, to be located at 1179 East Wardlow Road. The agreement, effective from January 1st, 2019 through December 31st, 2019, will result in an increase of $50,000 in General Fund appropriations, counterbalanced by revenues from animal care donation trust accounts. However, public concerns have been raised about a clause in the agreement that restricts negative comments about the project.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A RESOLUTION setting the public hearing on the petition of North Block Spring Street Development LLC for the vacation of the alley in Block 52, Plat of an Extension to Terry’s 1st Addition to the City of Seattle in the block bounded by Seneca Street, 8th Avenue, Spring Street, and Hubble Place in the First Hill Neighborhood Planning Area of Seattle, according to Chapter 35.79 of the Revised Code of Washington, Chapter 15.62 of the Seattle Municipal Code, and Clerk File 314364.\", \"sampled\": \"Adoption of Resolution 317 87 by the Seattle City Council, setting a public hearing for March 6th, pertaining to a property development petition by North BLOCK Spring Street Developments, LLC. The property, currently used as surface parking lots around the town hall, is located in the First Hill neighborhood and bounded by Seneca Street, Eighth Avenue, Spring Street, and Hubbard Place. Note, Councilmembers Gonzalez, Johnson, and Sawant will be excused on February 20th, April 9th, and March 5th respectively.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt resolution authorizing the submission of an application to the California State Department of Housing and Community Development for up to $17,723,734 in loan and grant funds available through the Affordable Housing and Sustainable Communities Program, and authorize City Manager to execute all documents and agreements necessary to apply for and utilize these funds for the construction of the Beacon Point affordable housing project proposed for development at 1201-1235 Long Beach Boulevard.\", \"sampled\": \"The Long Beach City Council approved support for a housing project proposed by Century Housing at the intersection of Anaheim and Long Beach Boulevard. Presented by Development Services, the project includes 121 senior housing units and 39 units for transitional veterans. The city's Long Beach Community Investment Company awarded Century Housing a grant or loan of $12 million, and Century Housing is applying for potential loan and grant funds of $17.7 million from the California State Department of Housing and Community Development, requiring city participation.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. R-7004 and award contracts to New Creation Builders, of Bellflower, CA, KLD Construction, of Los Angeles, CA, BiTech Construction, of Buena Park, CA, Exbon Development, of Garden Grove, CA, and Thomasville Construction, of Fullerton, CA for both anticipated and unanticipated (emergency) construction of various City infrastructure repairs and improvements to be performed under the method of Job Order Contracting (JOC), each in an amount not to exceed $3,500,000 for a period of three years, with the option to renew for two additional one-year periods in an amount not to exceed $1,000,000 per renewal, at the discretion of the City Manager;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to adopt specifications and award a contract for infrastructure repairs to five construction companies, with a special resolution to authorize the City Manager to execute a contract with Gordon Group. The contract with Gordon Group, which is not to exceed $341,250, includes project software and professional services. This recommendation, proposed by Andrews and Ringo, mandates that the City Council's approval is required before any work is undertaken in a given district. The motion, item number 33 on the agenda, carried with eight votes.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Rezones property at 8901 E. 56th Ave. from M-RX-5 and OS-B to M-MX-5 and M-RX-5 in Council District 8. (NEIGHBORHOODS AND PLANNING) Rezones property at 8901 E. 56th Ave. from M-RX-5 and OS-B to M-MX-5 and M-RX-5 in Council District 8. IF ORDERED PUBLISHED, A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD ON THIS ITEM. REFER TO THE \\\"PENDING\\\" SECTION OF THE FUTURE COUNCIL MEETING AGENDAS FOR THE DATE. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 4-13-16.\", \"sampled\": \"During the Denver City Council meeting, Council Bill 218, concerning rezoning in the Stapleton area, was discussed for final passage. The rezoning targets a vacant piece of land near the intersection of 56th Avenue and Central Park Boulevard, spanning over 155 acres. As Bruce O'Donnell, a public speaker, noted, \\\"This actually is the staff report indicated includes two separate rezonings.\\\" The proposed rezoning aims to align with the Stapleton Section ten General Development Plan, recommending a coordinated master plan development for the area.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Collective Bargaining Agreement between the City and County of Denver and the Denver Police Protective Association, for the years 2021-2022. Approves a collective bargaining agreement with the Denver Police Protective Association for 2021 and 2022 including: a 0% salary increase in 2021; suspending the holiday pay provisions for 10 holidays in 2021; reducing the City’s contribution to the Denver Police Retiree Health Fund by $360,000 in 2021; creation of a one-time 100-hour time bank in 2022 to be used in the future like vacation leave; a 2.77% salary increase for 2022; and expressing hazard/specialty pay in terms of a percentage of salary effective January 1, 2022.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver City Council deliberated on Bill 895, a proposed contract with the Police Protective Association. Council Member Flynn clarified, \\\"It is a 3% pay cut for next year because of the elimination of the premium pay for holidays that officers must work.\\\" This reduction is anticipated to save the city $5 million next year. The contract is set for two years, with a potential salary increase in 2022. Concurrently, the city is engaging with the firefighters union for potential pay cuts. The council members expressed concerns about the transparency and their involvement in these negotiation processes, and opinions vary on approving the contract, with some advocating for a delay until the 2021 budget is released.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute an agreement, and any necessary documents, subcontracts, or subsequent amendments, including amendments to the award amount and agreement term, with the City of Los Angeles to accept and expend grant funding in an amount not to exceed $6,869,171 for Department of Homeland Security 2021 Urban Area Security Initiative Grant, for the period of September 1, 2021 through May 31, 2024;\", \"sampled\": \"For agenda item number 16, the City Manager is recommended to execute an agreement with the City of L.A. to accept a grant funding of $6.8 million from the Department of Homeland Security for the 2021 Urban Area Security Initiative. The grant aims to enhance emergency communications, disaster preparedness, fire management, health and human services, parks and recreation, and technology and innovation within the city.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Agreement between the City and County of Denver and AECOM Technical Services, Inc. concerning professional services at Denver International Airport. Approves a five-year contract with AECOM in the amount of $65 million to perform federally required quality assurance inspections and special inspections on maintenance and capital improvement projects at Denver International Airport (201628929). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 6-26-17. The Committee approved filing this resolution at its meeting on 5-24-17.\", \"sampled\": \"Councilman Ortega queried the Denver City Council regarding a $65 million contract awarded to DIA. This contract, spanning five years, is for federally mandated quality assurance inspections and special inspections on maintenance and capital improvement projects at DIA. As Aaron clarified, these specialized inspections, required by the FAA, ensure contractors adhere to project specifications. However, city inspectors also participate in these projects, focusing on different aspects. The contract is capped at $65 million, but Aaron noted, \\\"It's a maximum contract amount of 65 million, so it's possible, but we don't have to spend all of that money.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. RFP ER19-115 and award a contract to Utility Solutions Partners, LLC, of Rancho Cordova, CA, for Information Technology Project Management Services for three main mission-critical platforms, in the amount of $3,625,000, with a 30 percent contingency in the amount of $1,087,500, for a total contract amount not to exceed $4,712,500, for a period of three years, with the option to renew for two additional one-year periods, at the discretion of the City Manager;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend awarding a contract to Utility Solution Partners for Information Technology Project Management Services. This contract, discussed during LongBeachCC_11092021_21-1174, is related to the report from Energy Resources and is set to address three main mission-critical platforms. It is important to note that the total contract amount is not to exceed 4.7 million TDY. During the public comment period, Mr. Shukla made a suggestion for workshops and training for in-house skill retention, emphasizing the importance of this contract.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed First Amendatory Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Downtown Denver Events, Inc., to extend the term to operate the skating rink and beer garden at Skyline Park in Council District 9. Amends a contract with Downtown Denver Events, LLC by adding two years for a new end date of 2-28-24 to operate the skating rink and beer garden at Skyline Park in Council District 9. No change to contract amount (202053400; 202161211). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 2-28-22. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 1-18-22.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver City Council adopted council resolutions 22-0051 through 22-0070, followed by putting Council Resolution 22-0071 on the floor for adoption. This resolution pertains to the Downtown Denver Partnership's contract to operate the Biergarten and the skating rink downtown. Council Member State Abarca voted against the resolution, stating, \\\"I'd just like to go on record voting no for this. This this for the downtown Denver Partnership's contract to operate the Biergarten and the skating rink downtown. It's one that I believe we should be having deeper discussion about.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager to execute a contract with HOK, Inc., a Culver City-based business, for professional design review and entitlement consulting services related to the Civic Center master plan development, in an amount not to exceed $216,676 for a period of one year, with the option to renew for another year at the discretion of the City Manager; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contract, including any necessary amendments thereto; and Increase appropriations in the Development Services Fund (EF 337) in the Development Services Department (DV) by $216,676.\", \"sampled\": \"Approve item seven, a Development Services recommendation authorizing execution of a contract with UK for professional design review and entitlement consulting services connected to the Civic Center masterplan in an amount capped at 216,000, including an increase in Development Services appropriations by 216,000 for the same purpose, identified with District two, noting that a motion and second were made before public comment, Mr. Goodhew spoke against awarding the contract, and the Council carried the motion by a 7-0 vote.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Approves a text amendment to the Denver Zoning Code creating a new zone district, Campus-National Western Center CMP-NWC, comprised of building forms and allowed uses consistent with the vision identified in the National Western Center Master Plan in Council District 9 (2015I-00066). (NEIGHBORHOODS AND PLANNING) Approves a text amendment to the Denver Zoning Code creating a new zone district, Campus-National Western Center CMP-NWC, comprised of building forms and allowed uses consistent with the vision identified in the National Western Center Master Plan in Council District 9 (2015I-00066). IF ORDERED PUBLISHED, A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD ON THIS ITEM. REFER TO THE \\\"PENDING\\\" SECTION OF THE FUTURE COUNCIL MEETING AGENDAS FOR THE DATE. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 5-13-15.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 324 was moved for final consideration and passage as amended, with City Council opening the public hearing on proposed Denver zoning code text creating a National Western Center zone district in the campus context. Steve Daly of Community Planning and Development presented the amendment, noting that existing campus districts did not cover the full program, uses, and vision for the National Western Center, a partnership involving CSU, the National Western Stock Show, the City of Denver, History Colorado, and the Denver Museum of Nature and Science. The amendment followed the Globeville Neighborhood Plan, Elyria Swansea Neighborhoods Plan, and National Western Center Master Plan, with Planning Board recommending approval on April 29th by a 10-0 vote conditioned on City Council addressing stakeholder concerns about neighborhood involvement, governance, and community equity.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to amend Contract No. 34597 with Falcon Fuels, of Paramount, CA, and Contract No. 34598 with Merrimac Energy Group, of Long Beach, CA, for the purchase and delivery of petroleum fuels, to increase the aggregate contract amount by $2,300,000, for a revised aggregate contract amount not to exceed $13,600,000, and extend the term of the contract to October 31, 2022.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to authorize the City Manager to amend Item 20 petroleum fuel purchase and delivery contracts as recommended by Financial Management. The revised combined cap would not exceed $13.6 million through October 31st, 2022. Dave Shukla, a third district resident, questioned during public comment why a city with nearby refineries must buy refined fuel products; Councilwoman Allen supported the amendment while a competitive invitation to bid was prepared, and after air quality questions, Dan Berlant said Fleet Services meets or exceeds applicable local standards, with the motion carrying nine zero.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation related to the Public-Private-Partnership to Design-Build-Finance-Operate-Maintain a re-envisioned Civic Center, including City Hall, Main Library, Lincoln Park, Private Development and a possible new permanent headquarters for the Harbor Department (Project), the following actions are hereby requested: Receive and file the Evaluation Summary Report, dated October 2, 2014, which summarizes the analyses and scores of the responses to Request for Proposals (RFP) No.\", \"sampled\": \"Item 10 on the Long Beach City Council agenda concerned the Long Beach Civic Center project, with Mayor Garcia presiding, Mr. West introducing the staff report, and Mike Conaway presenting with Amy Bodak, John GROSS, Glenda Williams, the Harbor Department, and ARP supporting analysis of an RFP process comparing two qualified project teams.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Approves a $2,683,899.77 contract with SEMA Construction, Inc. for the CM/GC (Construction Management/General Contractor) construction contract for the Red Rocks Parking Lot and Traffic Controls Phase 1 project (201524962). (INFRASTRUCTURE & CULTURE) Approves a $2,683,899.77 contract with SEMA Construction, Inc. for the CM/GC (Construction Management/General Contractor) construction contract for the Red Rocks Parking Lot and Traffic Controls Phase 1 project (201524962). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 12-21-15. The Committee approved filing this resolution by consent on 11-19-15.\", \"sampled\": \"Parks Department questions on Red Rocks included Scott Gilmore of Parks and Planning stating the contract was separate from master-planning work, with improvements at the Lower South parking lot to realign a road segment, repair irrigation, add sidewalk, improve safety, and address Y section traffic flow and accidents, while committee work involved park designation issues, National Historic Landmark designation boundary alignment, landmark commission boundaries, possible design guidelines, and nine council people toured. Councilwoman Ortega asked whether public alley dedication affecting lots 11 and 12 could affect a community garden, and Angelica CSM said, “I do not know the answer to that question.”\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to increase appropriations in the General Fund Group in the City Manager Department by $10,000, offset by the Seventh Council District One-time District Priority Funds, transferred from the Citywide Activities Department to provide a donation to the Bixby Knolls Business Improvement Association to support our partnership to create additional painted utility boxes in Council District 7; and Decrease appropriations in the General Fund Group in the Citywide Activities Department by $10,000 to offset a transfer to the City Manager Department.\", \"sampled\": \"Approve fund transfer Items 14, 15, and 17, including Council Member Your UNGA’s $10,000 General Fund group appropriation increase for a donation to the Bixby Knolls Business Improvement Association to create more painted utility boxes in Council District seven, Councilwoman Sara’s $500 increase to support the Carmi Parent Association’s seventh Annual Mother and Daughters Conference, and Vice Mayor Richardson’s $1,400 increase for the Neighborhood Association and African-American Historical Society of Long Beach, with no public comment received and a Councilmember Thoreau motion, seconded by Councilmember Price, carried 8-0.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt resolution to accept and expend grant funding from the California Department of Housing and Community Development (HCD), in an amount not to exceed $36,000,000; and Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all necessary documents and any subsequent amendments, including amending the terms, or changing the amount of the award, with HCD relating to the grant application and award to the Long Beach Development Services and Health and Human Services Departments, for acquisition and operation of an existing hotel and conversion into interim housing options for persons experiencing homelessness.\", \"sampled\": \"Adopt resolution for Item 11, as recommended by Development Services and Health and Human Services, authorizing the City of Long Beach to accept and expend citywide grant funding from the California Department of Housing and Community Development capped at $36 million, with Councilman Ranga making the motion, Jordan win providing public comment that the expected amount was about $15 to $16 million and tied to Long Beach’s burden for the Best Western, urging continued motel and hotel acquisitions including Lumbee if more funding becomes available for supportive and interim housing for people experiencing homelessness, and the motion carried after roll call.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager to execute contracts, and any related documents, with The Michelson Found Animals Foundation, Inc., of Los Angeles, CA, and the Stray Cat Alliance, of Los Angeles, CA, to allow Long Beach Animal Care Services to provide a variety of services to decrease shelter euthanasia of cats, from April 1, 2016 through March 31, 2017, with annual renewal options through March 31, 2020, at the discretion of the City Manager or his designee.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to receive Item 26 report from Parks and Recreation and Marine and authorize execution of two contracts for Long Beach Animal Care Services with Found Animals Foundation and Stray Cat Alliance, formalizing community cat and kitten foster efforts for kittens born without identified owners or known caretakers, with Ted Stevens and Steve Scott available for staff report, to support significant impact in increasing life outcomes and reducing shelter euthanasia in Long Beach.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager to execute the Tenth Amendment to Contract No. 31687 with Bellingham Marine Industries, Inc., for an additional $15,372,553, plus a $724,158 contingency, for a total increase of $16,096,711, for Phase 6 and Phase 7 of the Alamitos Bay Marina rebuild project, for a revised contract amount not to exceed $66,487,098; Execute the Second Amendment to Agreement No. 29587 with Concept Marine Associates, a Division of TranSystems Corporation, to increase the contract amount by $871,489, for a total amount not to exceed $6,573,489, and extend the term to December 31, 2017, for continued construction management of the Alamitos Bay Marina rebuild project; Execute all documents necessary to complete Phases 6 and 7, including all documents necessary to accept the Harbors and Watercraft Revolving Fund loan of $16,900,000, as approved by the State Department of Parks and Recreation, Division of Boating and Waterways; and Increase appropriations in the Marina Fund (TF 403) in the Parks, Recreation and Marine Department (PR) by $17,948,033.\", \"sampled\": \"Approve Item 18 for the Alamitos Bay Marina Rebuild in District 3, as recommended by Parks, Recreation and Marine and Financial Management. The action authorizes a 10th amendment with Bellingham Marine Industries for Phases 6 and 7, increasing the contract ceiling to $66 million, and a Second amendment with Concept Marine Associates capped at $6,573,000, with related authorizations needed to finish Phases 6 and 7, supported by a $16 million Harbor and watercraft revolving fund loan and a $17 million appropriation increase in the Park, Recreation and Marine Department. Staff reported that Basins 1, 5, and 4 had already been rebuilt, that Basin 2 work and Basin 3 dredging were intended to fit within the dredging permit period, and that 293 vessels were being relocated; Vito Halloran, George Champion, John GROSS, Olivia Hallinan, Hugh Clark and the Green Advisory Board were referenced during discussion, Councilwoman Price said, “I want to start by commending our city staff, especially Olivia Hallinan,” John GROSS said, “We do not know yet whether we will be successful,” regarding a possible Marina Fund-backed bond issue to finance remaining waterside improvements without more tidelands money, and the motion by Councilman Price, seconded by Councilmember Austin, was approved unanimously, 8-0.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to suspend Council rule contained in Long Beach Municipal Code 2.03.020 (B) to cancel the meetings of March 24, 2020 and April 7, 2020 and hold meetings as teleconferences, under the Brown Act waivers provided by the Governor's Office, for the remainder of April; Direct City Clerk to find the best way to conduct these meetings as teleconferences; Adopt the same guidance for all Charter Commissions effective March 20, 2020 and cancel all Non-Charter Commission meetings of the City of Long Beach through April 30, 2020, unless otherwise requested by the Acting City Manager through a teleconference; and Waive the Municipal Code requirement to redeclare the emergency every 14 days due to suspension of the regular meetings.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to approve Item 26, a communication from Mayor Garcia, canceling the March 24th and April 7th, 2020 meetings, allowing meetings for the rest of April to occur as teleconferences under Brown Act waivers from the Governor's office, applying the same guidance to charter commissions, canceling all non charter commission meetings through April 30th, 2020 unless the Acting City Manager requests a teleconference, and waiving the municipal code requirement to re-declare the emergency every 14 days while regular meetings are suspended, with Mayor Garcia noting the item was prepared with the city attorney and health office, Mr. Cohen providing public comment on access to virtual meetings, Councilman Austin making the motion, and the motion carrying after a roll call vote.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to approve a Third Amendment to Employment Agreement with Thomas B. Modica, City Manager, Contract No. 35514, to increase the City Manager’s current annual compensation by five percent (5%) for an annual compensation amount of $309,612.93 at the hourly rate of $148.343 per hour effective July 31, 2021;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to approve a Third Amendment to the employment agreement with City Manager Thomas Modica, providing a 5% merit increase. The amended annual salary would be $309,612 ($148.34 per hour), effective July 1, 2021, following a previously received 1.45% Consumer Price Index adjustment. A resolution amending the salary resolution was proposed concurrently. Jurisdiction is citywide. No public comment was received.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"A RESOLUTION relating to the Findings and Recommendation of the Hearing Examiner for The City of Seattle on the Final Assessment Roll for the Waterfront Local Improvement District (LID #6751); remanding certain properties to the City Appraiser for further analysis concerning the valuation of the properties consistent with recommendations of the Initial Report; returning jurisdiction to the Hearing Examiner for final recommendations on the remanded properties; directing the Hearing Examiner to file final findings, recommendations, or decisions on the Final Assessment Roll for the Waterfront LID (“Final Report”) with the City Clerk; providing for appeal from the Final Report; and postponing hearings on appeals from the Initial Report previously scheduled via Resolutions 31969, 31972, 31973, and 31974.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 31979, sponsored by Councilmember Juarez and recommended by the Public Assets and Native Communities Committee, was adopted 9–0 by the Seattle City Council. The resolution directs the city appraiser to submit supplemental valuation analysis for 17 remanded Waterfront Local Improvement District properties to the hearing examiner no later than November 30, 2020, and directs the hearing examiner to consolidate those findings with the September 8, 2020 initial report into a single final report filed with the city clerk no later than February 1, 2021. The resolution also reschedules the December 1, 2020 appeals hearings to March 2, 2021 and the January 5, 2021 appeals hearing to April 6, 2021. Adoption of Resolution 31979 does not constitute a decision on the final assessment of any Waterfront LID property or approval of the waterfront final assessment roll.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request City Manager to work with the Department of Development Services to evaluate the feasibility of establishing, in accordance with state and federal auditing requirements, a single Emergency Rental Assistance application for landlords with multiple tenants in communities at elevated risk of eviction. This funding would cover past rental arrears and up to three months of future rental obligation for eligible tenants. Participation in this program should allow tenants whose rental debt was covered an additional 90 days of protection from eviction past the final date of payment of back rent, and Direct the Department of Development Services to publicize data regarding the rental assistance program on a public dashboard.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation by Vice Mayor Richardson, co-sponsored by Councilwomen Zendaya and Allen, directing the City Manager and Department of Development Services to evaluate a streamlined single emergency rental assistance application for landlords with multiple tenants in high-eviction-risk communities, and to publish program data on a public dashboard. Long Beach has disbursed $64 million of its $250 million Recovery Act allocation, assisting approximately 2,500 households, while 15,800 households remain behind on rent and at risk of eviction, with an estimated 43,000 residents and 16,000 children affected. Application barriers have left 4,500 applications unfilled and 8,500 incomplete. Updated US Treasury guidance permitting advance payments to landlords with reduced proof-of-eligibility requirements supports the feasibility of the proposed single-application model, based on experience with the Carmelita projects.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to declare ordinance amending the Long Beach Municipal Code by amending Table 30-1 of Chapter 21.30, Table 31-1 in Chapter 21.31, and by adding Subsection T to Section 21.31.020, and adding Division III to Chapter 21.31 by adding Sections 21.31.300, 21.31.310, 21.31.320, 21.31.325, 21.31.330, 21.31.340, 21.31.350, Table 31-8, and 21.31.360, relating to planned unit developments, read and adopted as read. (District 8)\", \"sampled\": \"Development Services recommended adoption of an ordinance amending the Long Beach Municipal Code to establish a planned unit development zoning district applicable citywide to large-scale residential projects on lots of five or more acres, with allowable densities up to 13 houses per acre. Public commenters Lorie Angel, Joe Weinstein, and Richard Ivey raised concerns regarding inadequate noticing, citywide zoning impacts, and a conflict between the ordinance's driveway requirement and the Riverwalk project. Councilman Austin moved adoption; Councilwoman Mongo announced the motion carried.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to declare the City-owned property located at 240 Long Beach Boulevard, 320 East 3rd Street - 356 East 3rd Street, and 269 Elm Avenue (Assessor Parcel Numbers 7281-017-904, -905, -906, -911, -912, -913, -914 and -915, collectively, the Subject Properties) as surplus, authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute any and all documents necessary, including a Purchase and Sale Agreement (PSA) with Ratkovich Properties, LLC, a California limited liability company (Buyer), for the sale of the Subject Properties in the amount of $2,340,000, and accept Categorical Exemption CE 15-149.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to declare city-owned properties at 240 Long Beach Boulevard and 322–356 East Third Street surplus and authorize their sale to the Art Exchange for $2.3 million. The project would establish a visual arts center in the East Village Arts District featuring artist studios, galleries, residential uses, and adaptive reuse of the Acres of Books building. Vice Mayor Lowenthal, sponsor of the District 2 item, described the sale as more than 15 years in the making. Councilman Andrus conditioned his support on receipt of a staff report confirming that Long Beach residents would be hired for demolition and construction work, as promised by the developers.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Changes the zoning classification from OS-A (Open Space context, Public Parks) to S-MX-3 (Suburban context, Mixed Use, 3-story max.) of 7155 E. 36th Ave. Rear to conform to adjacent zoning in Council District 11. Changes the zoning classification from OS-A (Open Space context, Public Parks) to S-MX-3 (Suburban context, Mixed Use, 3-story max.) of 7155 E. 36th Ave. Rear to conform to adjacent zoning in Council District 11. A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD AT LEAST FOUR WEEKS AFTER PUBLICATION. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 2-18-14.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 115 (2014) proposes rezoning an 18,000-square-foot strip at 7155 East 36th Avenue, Council District 11, from OSA open space to SMX-3 suburban mixed use, three-story. Applicant Milk and Hospitality owns the adjacent hotel, which is already zoned SMX-3. The Greater Park Hill Neighborhood Association sold the property to the applicant and removed the open space deed restriction in 2012. The original open space buffer was established by Denver Airport Associates in agreement with that association. Because the property was never under City and County of Denver parks ownership, the OSA designation is considered atypical.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Adopt resolution determining that the proposed project is within the scope of the project previously analyzed as part of the Midtown Specific Plan Environmental Impact Report (State Clearinghouse No. 2015031034) and warrants no further environmental review pursuant to California Environmental Quality Act Guidelines Section 15162; and, approving a Lot Merger (LMG17-019) and Site Plan Review (SPR17-075) request to allow the construction of a new five-story 145,478-square-foot mixed-use building consisting of 3,938 square feet of commercial space and 102 residential units (101 affordable residential units) at 1795 Long Beach Boulevard, in the Midtown Specific Plan (SP-1).\", \"sampled\": \"Approve a General Plan Amendment changing the land use designation of 13 properties to mixed use within the Midtown Specific Plan area, resolving inconsistencies between the Midtown Specific Plan and prior general plan designations that prohibited mixed uses in districts 8A and 3B. Approve a lot merger and site plan review for a five-story, 102-unit mixed use building at 1795 Long Beach Boulevard, District 1, at the southwest corner of Long Beach Boulevard and Pacific Coast Highway. Of the 102 units, 101 are affordable; the project has a floor area ratio of 3.4, below the Midtown Specific Plan maximum of 4.0. The Planning Commission recommended approval on December 9, 2017; no public comments were received. Motion carried.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Approves a Legislative Map Amendment to rezone property from C-CCN, PUD, or C-MU-10 with waivers to proposed zoned districts C-CCN-4, C-CCN-5, C-CCN-7, C-CCN-8, or C-CCN-12. (NEIGHBORHOODS & PLANNING) Approves a Legislative Map Amendment to rezone property from C-CCN, PUD, or C-MU-10 with waivers to proposed zoned districts C-CCN-4, C-CCN-5, C-CCN-7, C-CCN-8, or C-CCN-12. A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD AT LEAST FOUR WEEKS AFTER PUBLICATION. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 9-18-14.\", \"sampled\": \"Public hearing on a zoning text amendment and map amendment for the Cherry Creek North district. The proposal was developed over an 18-month, three-phase process by a 16-member Zoning Technical Task Force and establishes building heights of 3 to 12 stories, with transitions stepping down from 8 stories near Second Avenue to a 5-story zone district near Third Avenue. The Planning Board unanimously recommended approval on September 3rd; the Neighborhoods and Planning Committee forwarded the draft on September 17th.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request the following recommendations of the Illegal Explosives and Fireworks Action Plan to address the illegal use of fireworks and explosives in Long Beach: 1) Request City Attorney to draft an ordinance that would include a person who owns, rents, leases or otherwise has possession of a premises as a responsible party for the illegal use, discharge, possession, storage or sale of fireworks on the premises; 2) Request City Attorney and City Manager to report back to the City Council with options for increasing the penalties for anyone cited or arrested for fireworks violations; 3) Request City Attorney and City Manager to report back to the City Council on the feasibility of including an administrative citation process for illegal fireworks use, to allow for additional enforcement capability in Long Beach; 4) Request City Manager to assess the feasibility of establishing an online portal or GoLongBeach app feature for residents to submit video evidence of fireworks violations for referral to the City Prosecutor;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to adopt Item 25, an Illegal Explosives and Fireworks Action Plan for Long Beach comprising 7 requests sponsored by Councilman Austin, Councilwoman Zendejas, Vice Mayor Andrews, and Councilmember Richardson. The plan directs the City Attorney to draft an ordinance holding property owners, renters, lessees, and occupants liable for illegal fireworks on their premises; directs the City Attorney and City Manager to report on options for increasing penalties above the current $1,000 citation fine and on the feasibility of an administrative citation process; and directs the City Manager to assess the feasibility of an online evidence portal, a public heat map using open data, and a fireworks hotline. The City Manager is further directed to update the Council on public education efforts communicating that all fireworks are illegal in Long Beach. City Prosecutor Doug Halbert has already established a resident reporting portal and begun issuing notices to property owners. Chief of Police Robert Luna noted that fireworks-related calls for service are up 25% over the prior year.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request City Manager to work with Urban Commons, The Wave team and other key stakeholders to determine the cost of a Feasibility Study pertaining to the development of a Gondola System connecting Downtown Long Beach with Queen Mary Island; and Request City Manager to work with Urban Commons to identify key stakeholders to create a multiparty partnership to conduct an initial Feasibility Study pertaining to the development of a potential gondola system connecting Downtown and Queen Mary, and identify potential public and private funding sources to begin the initial feasibility study.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to direct staff to determine the cost of a feasibility study, estimated at approximately $100,000, for a gondola system connecting downtown Long Beach to Queen Mary Island, and to establish a multi-party partnership with Urban Commons as lead developer, WSP as the global engineering firm, and other stakeholders to identify public and private funding sources, including Prop 8 transit funds and tidelands funds, with no commitment of city funds. Sponsored by Councilmember Pierce; co-sponsored by Councilwoman Gonzales, Councilwoman Price, and Councilmember Richardson.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to increase appropriations in the Special Advertising and Promotions Fund Group in the City Manager Department by $2,490, offset by the Third Council District One-time District Priority Funds transferred from the Citywide Activities Department to provide a donation to the Friends of Belmont Shore for summer concert; and Decrease appropriations in the Special Advertising and Promotions Fund Group in the Citywide Activities Department by $2,490 to offset a transfer to the City Manager Department.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend approval of grouped funds transfer items 22, 23, 25, 31, 33, and 34, appropriating a total of $14,250 from the General Fund to the City Manager Department for donations to Friends of Belmont Shore, Children Today, the Community Action Team, Los Cerritos Neighborhood Association, and Partners of Parks. Funds support summer concerts, the July 3rd Big Bang on the Bay celebration, annual community events, a community concert on August 1, 2021, and the Juneteenth 2021 celebration.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to increase Contract No. 32995 with C.S. Legacy Construction, Inc., by $690,254, for a total not-to-exceed amount of $8,700,000, for additional work required in constructing the Chittick Field project; Increase appropriations in the General Fund (GF) in the Citywide Activities Department (XC) by $590,580; and Increase appropriations in the Capital Projects Fund (CP) in the Public Works Department (PW) by $590,580.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to approve a contract increase with C.S. Legacy Construction for work at the J.T. Field park facility, as recommended by Public Works, Financial Management, and Parks, Recreation and Marine. A member of the public thanked Parks and Public Works Director Mr. Aurora and Park Director George Champion, noting children are actively using the track and field for running and football practice. Councilor Brosnan moved approval; the motion was seconded with support citing hundreds of kids, families, and young people using the new facility, and carried 7-0.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager to execute agreements with the Long Beach Unified School District for the operation of the After School Education and Safety Program at Burbank, Edison, Garfield, Grant, King, Lafayette, and Lee Elementary, and Hudson K-8 school sites; authorize the required 15 percent in-kind match of $207,929 in the form of Community Development Block Grant and General Fund monies; execute any future documents, including amendments, necessary to accept, implement, and administer the agreements; and Increase appropriations in the General Grants Fund (SR 120) in the Parks, Recreation and Marine Department (PR) by $1,102,544.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend authorization of agreements with Long Beach Unified School District for the After School Education and Safety Program, a state-funded initiative administered by the district. Under the agreements, the Parks, Recreation and Marine Department will operate afterschool programming at eight Long Beach schools as one of eight contracted providers, marking the department’s eighth year of service with an expected 200,000 students served. Approve a 15% in-kind match of $270,207,929 and a $1,000,100 increase in General Grants Fund appropriations, for a total funding amount of $1,102,544.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance concerning the Airport Facilities of the City and County of Denver; authorizing the issuance of the \\\"City and County of Denver, Colorado, for and on behalf of its Department of Aviation, Airport System Subordinate Revenue Bonds, Series 2022\\\" from time to time, in one or more series or subseries, on a taxable or a tax-exempt basis, in the maximum aggregate principal amount of $3,980,000,000 for the purposes of defraying the costs of the Series 2022 Subordinate Project, and providing other details in connection therewith;\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 22-0570 was moved for final passage by Councilmember Black. Following a roll call vote, the bill passed 12–1, with Councilmember State Abarca voting no and stating, “Just want to go on record as a no for this.” A council member raised concern about an agency lacking a leader for an extended period and urged the majority party to discuss leadership transitions with the mayor’s office.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to approve the Uptown Property and Business Improvement District Annual Report and Assessment for the period of January 1, 2017 through December 31, 2017, automatically extending the current agreement with the Uptown Property and Community Association for a one-year period; and, authorize payment of $54,561 in City property assessments from the General Fund (GF) and $3,668 in City property assessments from the Successor Agency Fund (SA).\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend continuing the Uptown Property and Business Improvement District assessment levy and authorize the City Manager to extend the agreement for one additional year, January 1, 2017 through December 31, 2017, with $54,500 in city property assessments from the General Fund and $3,700 from the Successor Agency Fund for a district in Council Districts 8 and 9 in North Long Beach.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Amends a $2 million loan to St. Charles Holding Company, LLC to 1) assign the loan to a new borrower, Morrison Road, LLLP, 2) assign property to Morrison Road, LLLP, 3) authorize the Director of the Office of Economic Development to administer the loan, 4) extend the performance period of the loan for the property acquisition and future development of 197 affordable income-restricted units at 4325-4331 Morrison Road and 4404 Morrison Road, formerly known as the Belmont and Shady Nook trailer parks (2793-01).\", \"sampled\": \"Denver City Council approved a contract amendment with the Office of Economic Development for a loan to redevelop the former Shady Nook and Belmont Homes mobile home park on Morrison Road. The site had roughly 36 serious code violations, including fire hazards and substandard conditions, which prompted a termination notice and negotiations. Saint Charles purchased the property and invoked the Federal Uniform Relocation Act to provide mandatory relocation benefits. Nearly all former residents moved to better housing, and some used benefits toward a first-home down payment.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Councilor Baker called Docket #0265, An order authorizing the City of Boston to accept and expend a grant funded through the coronavirus state and local fiscal recovery fund (CLFRF) in the Treasury of the United States established by section 9901 of the American Rescue Plan Act of 2021 (ARPA) awarded by the United States Department of the Treasury for $10,000,000.00, from the Committee on Boston's COVID-19's Recovery. No objection being heard the matter was before the body. Councilor Baker moved to amend Docket #0265 from $10,000,000.00 to $5,000,000.00. Seconded by Councilor Murphy Motion Prevailed; yeas 12; (Absent 1 Mejia). Docket #0265 as amended did not Pass; yeas 5 (Baker, Flaherty, Flynn Murphy and Worrell), nays 7 (Arroyo, Bok, Breadon, Coletta, Fernandes Anderson, Lara and Louijeune).\", \"sampled\": \"Docket 0265 authorizes the City of Boston to accept and expend a grant from the Coronavirus State and Local Fiscal Recovery Fund under the American Rescue Plan Act of 2021. Councilor Baker filed the order requesting $10 million in ARPA funds for a field house on Columbia Point. The facility would be developed by the Boys and Girls Club of Dorchester in partnership with the Martin Richards Foundation and is expected to serve 50,000 children and teens within a three-mile radius, including 1,000 Boston Public Schools students at the adjacent Gavin McCormick schools. Councilors Murphy and Clarity were added as co-sponsors. The matter was referred to the Committee on Boston's COVID-19 Recovery.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Purchase and Sale Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Morrison Center LLC for the purchase of real property located at 4320 Morrison Road. Approves a purchase and sale agreement with Morrison Center, LLC for $4,080,000 to acquire property located at 4320 Morrison Road for the buildout of the Westwood Recreation Center as part of the Elevate Denver Bond Program, in Council District 3 (201948958-00). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 7-22-19. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 6-18-19.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 540 authorizes a sale agreement between the City and County of Denver and Marston Road Center, LLC for the purchase of property at 4320 Morrison Road in the amount of $4,080,000 to construct a Westwood recreation center—a top priority identified by the Infrastructure Attack Priorities Task Force under the 2008 bond. The recreation center project was later added to a subsequent bond measure after the 2008 bond initially omitted District 3 needs. Resolution 540 was adopted by a vote of 13 ayes and 0 nays.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Approves the rezoning of 4300 East Warren Avenue and 4275 East Iliff Avenue from PUD 205 to G-MU-3 in Council District 6. (LAND USE TRANSPORTATION AND INFRASTRUCTURE) Approves the rezoning of 4300 East Warren Avenue and 4275 East Iliff Avenue from PUD 205 to G-MU-3 in Council District 6. A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD AT LEAST FOUR WEEKS AFTER PUBLICATION. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 4-1-14.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 267 (CB14-0267) would rezone roughly 2 acres at 4300 East Warren Avenue and 4275 East Iliff Avenue from Chapter 59 PD to G-MU-3. The northern parcel is proposed for a Primrose Schools daycare; the southern parcel’s apartment use would not change. The Planning Board and Land Use Committee recommended approval, and Community Planning and Development supports the rezoning. Two registered neighborhood organizations submitted letters of support.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance submitting to a vote of the qualified and registered electors of the City and County of Denver at a special municipal election to be held in conjunction with the state general election of November 18, 2018, a proposed amendment to the Charter of the City and County of Denver concerning the qualifications for lateral hires to the Denver Police Department.\", \"sampled\": \"Denver City Council defeated Bill 18-0694 by a 12-0 vote. The bill would have referred a charter amendment to the November ballot concerning lateral-transfer recruitment of police officers from other departments. Sponsor Flynn stated the measure originated with the Civil Service Commission but contained drafting errors, including an incorrect election date and a section requiring revision, and asked for a no vote so the bill could be redrafted and refiled the following week.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A RESOLUTION affirming The City of Seattle’s support of immigrant communities; stating its support of the Deferred Action Childhood Arrival (DACA) program; recognizing the roots and community DACA recipients have built in the Seattle/King County region and beyond, and The City of Seattle’s commitment to an inclusive, welcoming City and advancing policies to protect vulnerable communities while denouncing national and federal policies and rhetoric targeting immigrants and immigrant communities.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 31779, sponsored by Councilmember Lorena M. González and adopted unanimously, affirms the City of Seattle's support for the Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals (DACA) program. The resolution directs the Office of Intergovernmental Relations to prioritize the passage of a \\\"Clean Dream Act\\\" by Congress, opposes threats to withhold federal funding from cities, denounces anti-immigrant laws such as Texas Senate Bill 4, and calls for a stakeholder engagement process to develop policies that protect diverse communities.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Rezones 22 South Adams Street and 44 South Adams Street from PUD 75 to G-MU-12 in Council District 10. (NEIGHBORHOODS AND PLANNING) Rezones 22 South Adams Street and 44 South Adams Street from PUD 75 to G-MU-12 in Council District 10. IF ORDERED PUBLISHED, A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD ON THIS ITEM. REFER TO THE \\\"PENDING\\\" SECTION OF THE FUTURE COUNCIL MEETING AGENDAS FOR THE DATE. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 7-22-15.\", \"sampled\": \"Councilman Lopez moved for final consideration of Council Bill 503, which approves a zoning map amendment for the property at 1212 South Adams Street in Council District 9. The motion was seconded by Councilwoman Sussman. In a separate action, Council Bill 498 was postponed to September 21, 2015, at the request of the Department of Community Planning and Development. The vote for the postponement was 12-0.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance changing the zoning classification for 2175 South Cherry Street, 2176 South Clermont Street, 4501 East Warren Avenue and a portion of 2135 South Cherry Street in University Hills. Approves an official map amendment to rezone properties located at 2135 and 2175 South Cherry Street, 2176 South Clermont Street and 4501 Warren Avenue from G-MU-3 and PUD 277 to C-MX-5 (planned development and multi-unit, three story to urban center, mixed-use, five story) in Council District 4. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 10-8-19.\", \"sampled\": \"A request to rezone 1.3 acres at 2135 and 2175 South Cherry Street, 2176 South Claremont Street, and 4501 Warren Avenue in Council District 4 from GMU-3 and PD-77 to C-MX-5. The proposal for the University Hills properties is consistent with Blueprint Denver, which designates the area as an urban center community corridor allowing for mixed uses and residential buildings up to eight stories. Staff finds the rezoning consistent with Comprehensive Plan 2040 strategies.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Plans and Specifications No. RFP PW18-044 for ongoing operation and maintenance of the Long Beach Bike Share Program; award the contract to Pedal Movement, LLC, of Long Beach, CA, in an annual amount of $1,282,402, with a 10 percent contingency in the amount of $128,240, for a total contract amount not to exceed $1,410,642, for a period of two years, with the option to renew for three additional one-year periods;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to award a contract to Pedal Movement for the operation and maintenance of the Long Beach Bike Share Program for a total amount not to exceed $1.4 million. Following an RFP process, Pedal Movement was selected to transition the program from an interim pilot to a permanent citywide system. The contract will fund the purchase of parts for the system’s existing bikes. This maintenance investment will occur as the city acquires approximately 600 new bikes, which Pedal Movement will also operate and maintain.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an Ordinance authorizing and approving an amendment to the St. Anthony Urban Redevelopment Plan to add the Sloans Block 7 West Project and to Create the Sloans Block 7 West Sales Tax Increment Area (FINANCE & SERVICES) Approves an amendment to the St. Anthony Urban Redevelopment Plan for the Sloans Block 7 West Project in Council District 1. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 12-16-14.\", \"sampled\": \"Tracy Huggins with the Denver Urban Renewal Authority requested an amendment to the urban redevelopment area for the Sloan’s BLOCK Seven West Project, established in August 2013. The amendment concerns the approximately 32-acre site formerly occupied by the Saint Anthony Central Hospital campus. While two metropolitan districts were used for infrastructure, the Colfax parcel has struggled to attract neighborhood services, and the amendment is intended to address redevelopment challenges at the project site.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to increase appropriations in the Special Advertising and Promotion Fund Group in the City Manager Department by $552.50, offset by the Sixth Council District one-time District Priority Funds transferred from the Citywide Activities Department to provide a contribution to California Families in Focus a non-profit organization, for support of their Gran Bailaton event, which is an exercise program being hosted in the Sixth District; and Decrease appropriations in the Special Advertising and Promotion Fund Group in the Citywide Activities Department by $552.50 to offset a transfer to the City Manager Department.\", \"sampled\": \"A motion, recommended by Vice Mayor Andrews, was approved to increase appropriations by $552.50 in the Special Advertising and Promotion Fund for the City Manager Department. This followed the reconsideration of Item 16, during which Mana Yoshihiro, a five-year resident of Belmont Shores Mobile State, requested help. Speaking \\\"on behalf of the mature residents,\\\" she asked the city to \\\"help us solve this huge increase of our rent.\\\" Ms. Yoshihiro, who lives on disability and widow's benefits, stated, \\\"No way in heaven will I be able to sustain myself.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Amendatory Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Colorado Village Collaborative to increase funding for Safe Outdoor Space (SOS) sites. Amends a contract with Colorado Village Collaborative by removing the restriction that 60% of funds within the contract scope be expended only on a singular second Safe Outdoor Space (SOS) site. No change to contract amount or duration (HOST 202158824-01). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 7-19-21. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 6-16-21.\", \"sampled\": \"Councilmember Hines moved to adopt Council Resolution 21-0667, which followed the passage of Council Resolution 21-20655. Council Member Sawyer stated an intention to vote no on the resolution to remain consistent with a previous vote on the original contract. Sawyer noted that the initial proposal was framed as a temporary solution in response to CDC guidance and the pandemic, but the conversation has now shifted toward making it a more permanent city solution. Sawyer also stated that the vast majority of District 5 residents have made it clear they \\\"don't want urban camping.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to Consider Options on Coordination of the 4th of July Parade; Adoption of Resolution Approving a Workforce Change in the Recreation and Parks Department to Create a New Classification, Recreation Assistant; Add One Position, Recreation Assistant; and Amend the Alameda City Employees Association (ACEA) Salary Schedule to Add the Classification of Recreation Assistant, Effective January 8, 2020; and Adoption of Resolution Amending the General Fund Budget for Fiscal Year 2019-20 by $130,000 for Costs Associated with the 4th of July.\", \"sampled\": \"Adoption of a resolution amending the Fiscal Year 2019-2020 General Fund budget for the Recreation and Parks Department's 4th of July parade. The Alameda 4th of July parade, which began in 1976, is coordinated by volunteers from the nonprofit Parade Foundation. The route travels down Park Street and Webster Street, attracting an estimated 60,000 spectators and over 170 entries. The item was presented by Amy Wooldridge, Director of Recreation and Parks.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute a Third Amendment to Contract No. 34242 with Plenary Properties Long Beach, LLC, of Los Angeles, CA, to modify the conditions to conveyance of the Midblock site, extend the project long-stop date, and include additions to Lincoln Park; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into an agreement, including any necessary amendments, with the Harbor Department, to accept and expend grant funding in an amount not to exceed $981,280, for the Lincoln Park Landscaping Project; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute and accept a Water Line Easement Agreement establishing a utility easement over a portion of the Midblock site; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute an Indemnity and Reimbursement Agreement in connection with existing City obligations to remediate impacted soils existing on the Midblock site;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to adopt a report from Public Works, Parks, Recreation and Marine to execute a Third Amendment to the contract with Plenary Properties Long Beach. The amendment modifies conveyance conditions for the Mid-Block site, extends the project long stop date, and changes the development plan. The revised plan replaces a previously approved hotel and 300 housing units with 580 housing units, of which 10 percent will be affordable, and a downtown grocer. The project will feature a \\\"teachers village\\\" of approximately 100 units, with half affordable and half market-rate for partners including Long Beach Unified, Long Beach City College, and Cal State Long Beach. This item also authorizes an agreement with the Harbor Department to accept and use grant funding not to exceed $981,280 for the Lincoln Park Landscaping Project, and executes a related water line easement agreement and an indemnity and reimbursement agreement for the benefit of District Two.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. RFP CM-14-023 for architectural and engineering services related to the planning and design of the Sorrento Alamitos Bay Shoreline Trail, Sidewalks and Street Improvements; award the contract to MLA Green, Inc., dba Mia Lehrer + Associates, of Los Angeles, CA, in the amount of $221,470, plus a 10 percent contingency in the amount of $22,147, for a total contract amount not to exceed $243,617;\", \"sampled\": \"A motion passed 9-0 to approve the Naples Islands Seawall Repair Phase 1 project. The project is required under special conditions from the Coastal Commission to enhance public coastal access. Councilwoman Price noted the city is working closely with residents on the project's planning and design to meet community needs while fulfilling the commission's requirements for infrastructure repair and public access.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Approves the Mayoral reappointment of Lori Mack to the Denver Office of Strategic Partnerships Commission for a term effective immediately and expiring 4-20-16, or until a successor is duly appointed. (HEALTH, SAFETY, EDUCATION & SERVICES) Approves the Mayoral reappointment of Lori Mack to the Denver Office of Strategic Partnerships Commission for a term effective immediately and expiring 4-20-16, or until a successor is duly appointed. The Committee approved filing this resolution by consent on 5-29-14.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 443 was defeated by the Denver City Council with 11 opposing votes. Several bills and resolutions were approved, excluding Council Bill 470 from the agenda. Public hearings are scheduled for multiple bills and plans. Councilman Brooks suggested modifications for the Denver Office of Strategic Partnerships, and Councilwoman Cornish introduced motions related to Resolution 443. The council is set to vote on the Walton Corridor Urban Redevelopment Plan and proceed with hearings on Council Bill 398 and Council Bill 502.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance designating certain property as being required for public use and authorizing use and acquisition thereof by negotiation or through condemnation proceedings of fee simple, easement and other interests, including any rights and interests related or appurtenant to properties as needed for the Platte to Park Hill Stormwater Systems Project. Grants the authority to acquire through negotiated purchase or condemnation any property interest as needed for the construction of a detention area as part of the Platte to Park Hill 39th Avenue Greenway Stormwater Systems project including easement interests, access rights, improvements, buildings, fixtures, licenses, contract rights, permits, and other appurtenances located at 4141 East 35th Avenue in Council District 8. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 12-5-17.\", \"sampled\": \"Councilman Flynn requested a one-hour courtesy hearing on the Land Acquisition Ordinance for the Park Hill Golf Course project, proposing an amendment to clarify the acquisition of fee title interest in the property. The amendment aimed to limit the acquisition to portions necessary for the project, leading to discussions on the implications of acquiring lesser interests than fee title. City Council deliberated on potential risks of delay and damages if acquisition rights were restricted. Questions were raised about the amendment's impact on the project timeline and ownership rights, including penalties of $5,000 per day for missing deadlines in the agreement with SEMA.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance amending Chapter 30 (Landmark Preservation) of the Revised Municipal Code. Amends Chapter 30 of the Denver Revised Municipal Code (DRMC), Landmark Ordinance to simplify designation criteria, add criteria associated with cultural significance, to extend time frames for demolition/designation review process, to add required mediation and to clarify language and ensure consistency throughout the ordinance. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 9-10-19.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver City Council reviewed a proposed landmark ordinance, focusing on adding a culture criteria to the designation standards. Recommendations from the task force aimed to simplify designation criteria and resolve conflicts between demolition reviews and the designation process. Notable properties such as Macedonia Baptist Church and Union Station would still qualify under the updated criteria. The task force stressed the importance of enhancing diversity among designated properties and conducted a comparative analysis of preservation approaches in similar cities.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance changing the zoning classification for 2600 High Street and 1919 East 26th Avenue in Whittier. Approves an official map amendment to rezone land at 2600 High Street and 1919 East 26th Avenue from U-SU-B1 to U-MX-2x (urban, single-unit to urban, mixed-use) in Council District 9. If ordered published, a public hearing will be held on Monday, 2-12-18. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 1-2-18.\", \"sampled\": \"The City Council deliberated Council Bill 1461 concerning the Whittier neighborhood's rezoning to a neighborhood mixed-use zone district. The proposal targets two parcels totaling around 6300 square feet presently zoned as UCB be one, facilitating the redevelopment of the Western parcel. The Whittier Registered Neighborhood Organization supports the rezoning, with staff endorsing its approval. Chairman Sekou expressed concerns about potential gentrification and affordability, highlighting the neighborhood's vulnerability and the community's unity.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A RESOLUTION relating to the acknowledgment of the various harms and ongoing historical and inter-generational traumas impacting American Indian, First Nations, and Alaskan Natives for the forcible removal of Indian children and subsequent abuse and neglect resulting from the United States’ American Indian Boarding School Policy during the 19th & 20th Centuries; supporting American Indian and Alaskan Native communities’ efforts in calling on the United States Congress to commission a study and report on the United States’ responsibility and role in adopting and implementing an American Indian Boarding School Policy; and committing to work with the local American Indian and Alaskan Natives in efforts of reconciliation in addressing the impacts of historical trauma, language and cultural loss, and alleged genocide.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 31621 recognizes the impact of the American Indian boarding school policy and calls on the United States Congress to conduct a study on this matter. Seattle City Council introduced the resolution on October 5, 2015, urging for acknowledgment and redress for more than 100,000 Native American children who were forcibly removed from their families. The resolution, backed by community organizations and activists like Idle No More and Black Lives Matter, emphasizes the need for comprehensive history education. It was unanimously approved by the Council. Councilmember So advocated for the national elimination of Columbus Day, while ongoing efforts focus on housing fairness, racial justice, and safeguarding the environment from threats such as the Cherry Point Coal Terminal.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request a report on the possibility of preparing a City-wide survey on the prioritization of City services and citizen/user satisfaction of existing City services. This survey process would be open to all Long Beach residents and employees and would specifically emailed/mailed to citizens who come into contact with the City through any of our City Departments. The City of Long Beach currently offers a brief survey following a submittal to the Go Long Beach application, however, it is unknown what other survey mechanisms are currently being utilized. As part of this agenda item, staff should prepare a report on what other City departments currently utilize a survey process to gather data that can assist in budget allocation decisions.\", \"sampled\": \"Councilman Price's proposal for a citywide survey on city services prioritization and citizen satisfaction, aimed at informing budget decisions during deficit years, was discussed. Councilmember Turanga raised concerns about costs and language access for lower-income residents, while Councilmember Andrews stressed the importance of providing information in multiple languages and through various outreach methods. Councilmember Alston requested clarification on the survey program's cost, and the City Manager highlighted the potential fiscal impact, suggesting further research. The City Manager also mentioned the possibility of a quick, low-cost implementation of a short-term survey tool. Councilwoman Price emphasized the need to understand residents' needs before making policy decisions, and the City Manager committed to including language access policies in the survey review process.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to receive supporting documentation into the record, conclude the public hearing, and consider third-party appeals (APL17-020 and APL17-021) from Bea Bea Jiménez, David White, Karin McGinley, Tino Haramis, Kazumi Hiromoto, Tetsu Hashimoto, and Warren Blesofsky (representing Long Beach Citizens for Fair Development); Adopt resolution finding that the 320 Alamitos Project is consistent with the Downtown Plan Program Environmental Impact Report and subject to the Downtown Plan Mitigation Monitoring and Reporting Program, and making certain findings and determinations related thereto;\", \"sampled\": \"The City Council reviewed a recommendation to confirm the Planning Commission's approval of a seven-story, 77-unit residential development at 320 Alamitos Avenue in the Downtown Plan Development District. The project aligns with development standards, incorporates sustainable building features, and showcases modern architectural designs. Despite two appeals raising concerns about plan conflicts and environmental impacts, the project's design, amenities, and adherence to green building standards were emphasized. It was determined that no additional environmental review under the California Environmental Quality Act was necessary, underscoring efforts to reduce shadow impacts and enhance energy efficiency.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute an agreement, and all necessary documents and any subsequent amendments, with the California Governor’s Office of Emergency Services to accept and expend grant funding in the amount of $306,666 for the My Sister’s Keeper - New Victim Intervention and Empowerment for Women’s Program, for the period of January 1, 2018 through December 31, 2019; and Increase appropriations in the Health Fund (SR 130) in the Health and Human Services Department (HE) by $306,666, offset by reimbursement revenue.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend approving the $306,666 grant funding from the California Governor's Office of Emergency Services for the My Sister's Keeper program in Long Beach. The program diverts over 200 women from the criminal justice system to trauma-related services through a court diversion model, removing over 100 tattoos and prioritizing the treatment of trafficked individuals as victims. Council members have provided positive feedback, highlighting the program's effectiveness in areas like Bixby Park and North Long Beach.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Contract between the City and County of Denver and Garage Door Specialties, Inc., concerning perimeter security gate maintenance and repair services at Denver International Airport. Approves a contract with Garage Door Specialties, Inc. for $1 million and for 2 years, with the option for three (3) 1-year extensions, to perform maintenance, repair, and emergency response services of Denver International Airport’s (DEN) electric security perimeter vehicle gates in Council District 11 (202159110-00).\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 22-479 is adopted with one abstention and ten 'yes' votes. Councilmember abstains due to a conflict of interest with Gilmore Construction. Bill 401 is called for a vote under pending as Resolution 120 is presented for adoption. Madam Secretary oversees the voting process. Councilmember Hines proposes the adoption of Resolution 20-479, resulting in one abstention and ten 'yes' votes for Resolution 22-479.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance relating to the Denver Zoning Code to establish new zone districts that implement the objectives of the adopted 2015 National Western Center Master Plan. Approves a text amendment to the Denver Zoning Code to establish four new zone districts referred to as Campus-National Western Center-Core (CMP-NWC-C), Campus-National Western Center-General (CMP-NWC-G), Campus-National Western Center-Flex (CMP-NWC-F) and Campus-National Western Center-Riverfront (CMP-NWC-R) in furtherance of implementation of the National Western Center Master Plan and Globeville and Elyria and Swansea Neighborhood Plans in Council District 9. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 7-30-19.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver City Council approved Council Bill 724, establishing four new campus National Western Center zoned districts through the Denver Zoning Code Text Amendment. The rezoning proposal, 2019 AI 104, is in line with the National Western Center Master Plan and is a collaborative effort between CPD, the Mayor's Office, and Western Sydney. The zoning strategy prioritizes street significance, river preservation, and open space community development, adhering to the Master Plan's schedule and framework agreement while excluding properties under negotiation or facing non-conforming use challenges.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving and providing for the execution of a proposed Grant Agreement between the City and County of Denver and the State of Colorado concerning the “Public Assistance COVID-19 Grant” program and the funding therefor. Approves a grant agreement with the Colorado Department of Public Safety for funding in the amount of $38,609,673.86 for the Public Assistance COVID-19 Grant Program (202054524). Councilmember Black approved filing this item on 4-27-20.\", \"sampled\": \"In a recent Denver City Council meeting, a late bill filing was deliberated, necessitating a suspension of council rules for its introduction. Councilmember Sawyer requested further explanation on the procedure, asking for clarity on the implications of suspending the rules. Councilman Hines clarified that rejecting the suspension would result in the bill being discussed the following week. However, if the suspension was approved and he chose to delay it, the bill would still be reviewed the next week.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute a Consent Letter and a Project Specific Maintenance Agreement, and any other necessary documents required including subsequent amendments, with the State of California Department of Transportation (Caltrans), for access to Caltrans property to offer and provide social services, targeted maintenance, and relocation assistance to people experiencing homelessness, consistent with the business operations of the entity, for a one-year period, June 15, 2021 through June 14, 2022, with the option to renew for three additional one-year terms, at the discretion of the City Manager.\", \"sampled\": \"The Long Beach City Council considered a proposal to issue a consent letter and enter into a project-specific maintenance agreement with Caltrans. This would permit access to Caltrans property for the provision of social services, targeted maintenance, and relocation assistance to citywide homeless individuals. The proposed agreement, initially set for one year with a possible extension, is the result of bi-monthly strategy meetings between Public Works, Health and Human Services, and Caltrans. This collaboration has identified 22 potential sites throughout Long Beach for cleanup and service delivery.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to direct City Attorney to prepare a resolution to declare an Inclusive Language Policy for all future Long Beach Municipal Code ordinances presented to the City Council, as well as for future City Council Policies and ballot proposals for City Charter amendments;\", \"sampled\": \"Councilwoman Allen and Councilwoman Sorrell have put forth a recommendation to adopt a resolution for an inclusive language policy. The objective is to make all gender references in the Long Beach Municipal Code neutral, thereby acknowledging and supporting the city's trans and non-binary residents.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to receive supporting documentation into the record, conclude the public hearing regarding an economic subsidy in connection with a Retail Sales Tax Incentive Agreement with HTL Automotive, Inc., dba Hooman Toyota of Long Beach, pursuant to California Government Code Section 53083; and Authorize City Manager to execute all documents necessary to implement a 15-year Sales Tax Incentive Agreement with HTL Automotive, Inc., dba Hooman Toyota of Long Beach, for the relocation and development of a Toyota dealership to be located at 3399 E. Willow Street.\", \"sampled\": \"The City Manager reported on a proposed economic subsidy tied to a Retail Sales Tax Incentive Agreement with Toyota of Long Beach, also known as HTL Automotive. This agreement, initially authorized on October 21, 2014, pertains to the relocation and development of a Toyota dealership at 3399 East Willow Street, District five. In line with new legislation, the agreement was revisited by the city council. The total estimated project costs stand at nearly $25 million, with anticipated savings for the city around $8 million over the agreement's 15-year term.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to declare ordinance approving and adopting the official budget of the City of Long Beach for the Fiscal Year 2021-2022, creating and establishing the funds of the municipal government of the City of Long Beach and appropriating money to and authorizing expenditures from said funds for said Fiscal Year; declaring the urgency thereof, and providing that this ordinance shall take effect at 12:01 A.M. on October 1, 2021, read and adopted as read. (Citywide) (Emergency Ordinance No.\", \"sampled\": \"The Long Beach City Council has approved the balanced 2022 budget, demonstrating the city's economic and public health resurgence, without any reductions or deficit, and with a boost to the city's reserves. The budget, formulated with contributions from numerous community members via surveys and meetings, incorporates substantial investments in public health, violence prevention, and the initiation of the city's inaugural million-dollar youth fund. The Budget Oversight Committee, under the leadership of Councilmember Austin, supervised the budget process, with the preparation team headed by Ms. Yoon and Mr. GROSS.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to receive and file a report on the feasibility of a Long Beach Community Choice Aggregation (CCA); Direct City Manager to continue to monitor the energy market/regulations and report to the City Council annually or sooner if substantial market changes occur; Direct City Manager to prepare a study that analyzes the potential CCA governance options of forming a stand-alone City enterprise, creating a new Joint Powers Authority (JPA), or joining an existing JPA; Direct City Manager to perform community outreach regarding the CCA concept along with potential benefits and risks to customers, as well as to gain feedback on how supportive the community might be of a Long Beach CCA; Direct City Manager to continue the City’s partnership with Southern California Edison to raise awareness of existing programs that provide Long Beach residents and businesses with various options to purchase a greater mix of renewables and utilize energy more efficiently;\", \"sampled\": \"During its session, the Long Beach City Council deliberated on agenda item 20: a report from the Energy Resources, Financial Management, and Water Department concerning the viability of a Long Beach Community Choice Aggregation. The city manager was tasked with overseeing energy market regulations and conducting a study, while the city was set to maintain its alliance with Southern California Edison. Councilmember Pearce suggested postponing the item until February or March of the following year, citing a need for more time to fully comprehend the report. The council invited public input on the motion to delay the item, with four speakers, including Marlene Alvarado, Caren Reside, Dave Chocola, Larry Goodhew, and representatives from Long Beach Panthers, providing their comments. The council approved the motion to postpone the decision on citywide participation by two years, shifting the item to February or March.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"AS AMENDED a bill for an ordinance amending the Denver Retail Marijuana Code and the Denver Medical Marijuana Code to provide for a waiting period before new applications can be received or acted upon within a 1,000-foot radius of a location where a license has been denied or an application has been withdrawn following scheduling of a hearing. Establishes a waiting period for re-application for a license for certain marijuana businesses that are denied an application or in certain cases when applicants withdraw. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 5-1-17.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 523 was discussed at the recent Denver City Council meeting, with an amendment proposed by Councilman Flynn. The amendment outlines a one-year waiting period for applicants who withdraw their application after a public hearing has been scheduled. It further clarifies that this waiting period applies to the location, not the applicant, and establishes a 1000-foot radius within which another applicant cannot apply for a license following a withdrawal. The bill continues to uphold existing postponement options under current rules and procedures. Its aim is to provide neighborhoods with a 'quiet period' after a contentious application process. Councilwoman Sussman sought further clarification on the bill's language, questioning the definition of \\\"another applicant\\\".\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A resolution by the Council of the City and County of Denver, sitting ex officio as the Board of Directors of the Gateway Village General Improvement District, approving a Work Plan, adopting a Budget and making appropriations for the Budget Year 2021 and approving a Mill Levy. Approves the 2021 Work Plan and Budget for the Gateway Village General Improvement District in Council District 11. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 11-10-20.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 1269, concerning the 2021 Annual Work Plan and Budget for the Gateway Village General Improvement District, was submitted for approval by Michael Kerrigan. The district, spanning roughly 243 acres northwest of the I-70 and Chambers Road intersection, intends to assess 20 miles of real property within its jurisdiction this year. The proposed budget forecasts total revenues of $687,884, including transfers to the capital fund of $435,000 and total expenditures of $824,016.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary for a new Right-of-Entry Permit to Camp Fire Angeles, a nonprofit 501c3, for the construction of Discovery Trails at DeForest Park, for a period of two years from September 1, 2020 through August 30, 2022, with the option to renew for one year; and, the approval of the Discovery Trails for public recreation, which meets a recreational need within the City.\", \"sampled\": \"The Parks, Recreation and Marine Department proposed a right of entry permit for Campfire Angeles, pertaining to the construction of Discovery Trails at Forest Park in District Nine. Councilor Richardson advanced the agenda item, which received unanimous approval, including from Bell District One. Notably, there were no public comments on the item. The motion's details were deliberated in the meeting designated as LongBeachCC_10202020_20-1042.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to Authorize the City Attorney or Designee to Consent to Law Firm Goldfarb Lipman’s Request to Waive Conflicts of Interest in Connection with Goldfarb’s Legal Representation of the City of Alameda on City Transactions and the County of Alameda on the Rosefield Village Affordable Housing Project Transaction; and Recommendation to Delegate Authority to the City Attorney or Designee to Consent, Modify or Reject Future Requests from Goldfarb to Waive Conflicts of Interest In Connection with Goldfarb’s Legal Representation of the City of Alameda on City Transactions and the County of Alameda on Future Affordable Housing Transactions Involving County of Alameda A-1 Funds or Comparable County Funds.\", \"sampled\": \"Approve the proposed transit modifications recommended by the AC Transit Interagency Liaison Committee. These modifications encompass substantial transit reductions, thoroughly evaluated for equity and impact, with a significant effect anticipated for Alameda. The changes include the conversion of transit route 51 to route 50 and the amalgamation of transit routes 51 A and 51 B. Despite these modifications, Alameda will continue to offer ten-minute service on its primary trunk line. It was noted during the committee meeting that Alameda may experience more changes than other areas, but the focus on equity and impact ensures that these decisions are made with great care and thoughtfulness. Councilmember Desai and Committee Member Mr. Jacobs are set to further discuss these proposed modifications.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Alpine Disposal, Inc. for the sorting and recycling of the materials collected through the City’s Denver Recycles Program. Approves a revenue contract with Alpine Disposal, Inc. for three years and for an estimated amount of $500,000 to sort and recycle the materials collected through the citywide Denver Recycles residential collection program (201737957). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 5-14-18. The Committee approved filing this resolution by consent on 4-10-18.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 350 approving an agreement with a new provider for citywide recycling sorting services, following Councilman Flynn’s request for Charlotte Pitt to explain the Solid Waste Management contract payment and revenue formula. Pitt said the prior Waste Management contract was modified because of severe recycling market conditions and later settled, while the new structure shares market changes between Denver and the vendor: “We needed to ride the market with the vendor.” The formula uses Denver residents’ recycling stream and monthly market index prices, pays the contractor the first $70 per ton, then splits remaining revenue 50/50.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into an agreement with the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) California Governor’s Office of Emergency Services (CalOES) Hazard Mitigation Grant Program (HMGP), to accept and expend grant funding in amount of $702,823, and execute all documents necessary to implement the grant for the City of Long Beach Pump Station Mitigation Project, Phase One (Project);\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to approve Item 27, a Public Works action authorizing an agreement with FEMA for the City of Long Beach to receive $702,823 in grant funding for the citywide City of Long Beach Pump Station Mitigation Project Phase One; public comment was opened, Dave Shuker called in without providing substantive remarks, and a roll-call vote was taken, including District One and District Nine, after which the motion carried.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to take all actions necessary to create and administer a pilot program of workshops to promote Energy Efficient Mortgages among home buyers in Long Beach during calendar year 2018; Increase appropriations in the Gas Fund (EF 301) in the Long Beach Gas and Oil Department (GO) by $40,000, offset by funds available; and Increase appropriations in the Development Services Fund (EF 337) in the Development Services Department (DV) by $40,000, offset by a transfer of funds from the Long Beach Gas and Oil Department (GO).\", \"sampled\": \"Approve Item 16, a City Manager recommendation for a 2018 citywide Long Beach pilot program on energy-efficient mortgages for home buyers, presented by Larry Rich as a residential greenhouse gas reduction effort with Green Education Inc. to educate participants on energy-efficiency financing options, rebates, and grants through five workshops in 2018, targeted to begin in February with a final workshop in October, and supported by marketing, employer outreach, attendance incentives, follow-up, and evaluation, with $40,000 in funding from the Gas and Oil Department Cap and Trade GHG Reduction Fund, after no public comment and a carried council motion.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. RFP PW17-033 and award a contract to LAZ Parking California, LLC, of Los Angeles, CA, for Citywide Parking Operations and Management Services, in an amount of $7,874,148, authorize a 20 percent contingency in the amount of $1,574,830, for a total contract amount not to exceed $9,448,978, for a period of three years, with the option to renew for two additional one-year periods; authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contract, including any necessary amendments; Increase appropriations in the Tidelands Operation Fund (TF 401) in the Public Works Department (PW) by $433,489, offset by departmental cost-savings and parking-related revenue; and Increase appropriations in the General Fund (GF) in the Public Works Department (PW) by $479,331, offset by parking-related revenue.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to award Item 29, the Public Works contract for citywide parking operations and management services, to LA's Parking LLC in a total amount not to exceed $9,448,990. The City of Long Beach manages 25 city-owned parking assets, including seven parking garages and nine beach lots, with FY 17 parking operations expected to generate nearly $9.4 million in gross revenue, and the aquarium structure noted as the largest parking structure, with nearly 745,000 annual visitors and almost $4.4 million in revenue. Craig Beck reported that the current parking contract was scheduled to end at the end of March 2018; the RFP was released in March; five qualified proposals were received in May; interviews were held in June with Aquarium of Pacific and DOJ participation; two finalists, last parking and speed plus, were shortlisted in July.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Public Hearing to Consider Adoption of Resolution Confirming the Ballot Results to Determine Whether a Majority Protest Exists in the Proceedings to Increase Assessments in Island City Landscape and Lighting District 84-2, Zone 4 (Park Street); and Adoption of Resolution Confirming the Ballot Results and Providing for No Majority Protest and the Levy of an Annual Assessment in Island City Landscape and Lighting District 84-2, Zone 4 (Park Street).\", \"sampled\": \"City Council held a public hearing and unanimously adopted a resolution, moved by Councilmember O'Toole and seconded by Vice Mayor Knox White, increasing assessments for Island City Landscaping and Lighting District 84, Zone four Park Street, to continue landscaping maintenance, sidewalk cleaning, tree trimming, litter removal, and graffiti removal in the Park Street Business District, following the City Clerk’s report of 219 ballots mailed, 91 received, 48 in support representing $35,204.86, 42 opposed representing $35,038.79, and no majority protest.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to Review the Ballot Arguments and Address Rebuttals for the Two April 9, 2019 Special Election Measures: 1) a Proposed Initiative Measure to Change the Designation for an Approximately 3.65 Acre Site on McKay Avenue, by Amending the General Plan Designation from Office to Open Space, and by Amending the Zoning Ordinance from Administrative-Professional District to Open Space District; and 2) the Caring Alameda Act.\", \"sampled\": \"Review ballot arguments prepared by two council subcommittees for and against the Carrying Alameda Act; discuss signatures and rebuttals; incorporate Councilmember Vella’s request to replace a bracketed report reference with a direct link; note public comments from Doyle Saylor, Renewed Hope, and Doug Biggs regarding the McKay Avenue Respite Center, zoning description, and more than 500 Alameda residents; and provide rebuttal and formatting direction before the January 24 submission date and 5 p.m. tomorrow deadline.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Contract between the City and County of Denver and HDR, Inc. concerning architectural and engineering and other professional services on an as-needed task basis at Denver International Airport. Approves a contract with HDR, Inc. for $4,500,000 and 3 years, with two possible 1 year extensions, to provide on call architectural and engineering and other professional services on an as-needed task basis at Denver International Airport in Council District 11 (202056892). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 3-29-22. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 2-23-22.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Resolutions 22-0171 through 22-0178, final consideration and block adoption, following Bill 273 being called out for a vote under bills for final consideration. Councilmember Herndon moved to adopt Council Resolutions 22-0171 through 22-0178 as a block; the motion was seconded, and Councilmember Sawyer commented before the vote, thanking the airport for taking contracts to committee and thanking airport staff for changing the reporting structure. Councilmember Sawyer said the changes may affect her votes on uncalled contracts and expressed hope that other city agencies would follow Daddy and the airport on reporting structures for on-call contracts. Madam Secretary conducted the roll call, Councilmember Sawyer voted no, and Madam Secretary announced adoption with 10 ayes.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Review of 100-Room Hotel Development at the Harbor Bay Business Park. Public Hearing to Consider (1) an Appeal by UNITEHERE! Local 280 Challenging the Planning Board’s Approval of Final Development Plan and Design Review for Construction of a 100-Room Hotel at 2350 Harbor Bay Parkway, (2) Mayor Spencer’s Call for Review of the Planning Board Action, and (3) Adoption of Resolution Documenting the Council Action.\", \"sampled\": \"Alameda City Council held a public hearing on Unite Here’s appeal and Mayor Spencer’s call for review of the Planning Board approval of a 100-room Marriot Fairfield hotel at 2350 Harbor Bay Parkway. A Vice Mayor recused himself, and Andrew Thomas stated that the issues involved zoning parking compliance and the California Environmental Quality Act.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to respectfully request City Manager, Department of Health and Human Services and Development Services Department to develop an incentive package to encourage landlord acceptance of subsidized tenants through the Housing Choice Voucher Program, and return to the City Council in 30 days. The incentive package should incorporate the following options: • Align and streamline the current city-mandated inspections with the HUD-mandated Housing Choice Voucher inspections; • Waive various permits and inspection costs for apartment owners who accept Housing Choice Vouchers; • Create a Damage Mitigation Fund which provides financial assistance to landlords to mitigate damage caused by tenants during their occupancy under the Housing Choice Voucher Program; • Provide landlords vacancy payments to hold units while the landlord is going through the Housing Choice Voucher Program approval process.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to request the City Manager, Health and Human Services, and Development Services Department to prepare, within 30 days, an incentive package to increase landlord participation in the Housing Choice Voucher Program, sponsored by Vice Mayor Richardson with Councilmember Pearce and Councilwoman Mango. The item cited a tight Long Beach rental market with a 2% vacancy rate; more than a thousand voucher-holding families and individuals unable to find a willing landlord or Section 8 vacancy; and Housing Authority programs serving low-income, elderly, disabled, veteran, HIV and AIDS, and mental health populations. Alison King reported 2600 partnering property owners, over 6300 assisted households, $68 million in program funding, an opportunity to serve 7398 families, an 85% lease-up rate, possible federal recapture of unused funds, owner focus groups and surveys, barriers involving rules, evictions, rent, inspections, damage, and processing time, an ombudsman position, website redesign, and possible streamlined inspections, fee waivers, damage mitigation funding, and vacancy payments.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance to amend Chapter 59 (Zoning) of the Denver Revised Municipal Code relating to the Denver Zoning Code and to amend and restate the Denver Zoning Code. (NEIGHBORHOODS AND PLANNING) Approves the 2015 Denver Zoning Code Text Amendment Bundle #1 comprised of multiple clarifying changes to the text of the Denver Zoning Code (DZC). IF ORDERED PUBLISHED, A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD ON THIS ITEM. REFER TO THE \\\"PENDING\\\" SECTION OF THE FUTURE COUNCIL MEETING AGENDAS FOR THE DATE. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 5-13-15.\", \"sampled\": \"Department of Community Planning and Development proposal for the 2015 Text Amendment Bundle Number One to the Denver Zoning Code, presented by project manager Kyle Dalton, to keep the code modern, current, clear, and easier to use. Outreach began with a February 28 briefing to the Inter Neighborhood Cooperation Zoning and Planning Committee, followed by a March 30 redline draft distributed to registered neighborhood organizations and City Council members. Planning Board held an April 29 public hearing, and City Council amended the bill on June 8 for projects in the review pipeline. Applicants using the current code would have until January 4, 2016, to obtain needed approvals.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Plans and Specifications No. R-6971 for the Rehabilitation of Magnolia Avenue, between Pacific Coast Highway and Spring Street; award the contract to Sully-Miller Contracting Company, of Brea, CA, for the base bid in the amount of $2,154,780 and the additive bid in the amount of $396,400, for a total of $2,551,180; authorize a 15 percent contingency of $382,677, for a total contract amount not to exceed $2,933,857; authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contract, including any necessary amendments thereto; and consider and accept the determination that this project is statutorily exempt from CEQA.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to award a contract to Sally Miller for rehabilitation of Magnolia Avenue. The Long Beach City Council on a motion by Councilmember Turanga and seconded by Councilmember Andrews, approved a contract not to exceed $2.9 million with Sally Miller, the lowest responsible bidder for street rehabilitation work in the Sixth and Seventh Districts; the approximately six-month project will keep at least one lane of traffic open at all times suspend parking enforcement during construction, and include pre-construction outreach by Public Works while Councilmembers Turanga and Andrews raised concerns about local hiring practices and called for restructuring them and a five-year capital improvement program guided by a pavement management plan is being developed to address funding gaps for local streets with the motion carrying nine yes votes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents, and any needed subsequent amendments, with the U.S. Department of Labor to receive up to $1,000,000 in Workforce Innovation and Opportunity Act funds to implement the Pathways to Justice Careers for Youth Program, for the period January 1, 2017 through December 31, 2019; and Increase appropriations in the Community Development Grants Fund (SR 150) in the Economic and Property Development Department (EP) by $1,000,000, offset by grant revenue.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation by the Economic and Property Development department that the Long Beach City Council authorize execution of documents with the U.S. Department of Labor to receive up to $1 million in Workforce Innovation and Opportunity Act funds for the citywide Pathways to Justice Careers program. Vice Mayor Richardson noted this as one of the final grants from the Obama administration and Councilman Andrews emphasized the program's importance in keeping Long Beach youth engaged with the motion carrying and no public comment recorded.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt resolution authorizing City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into a contract, including any necessary amendments, with Anser Advisory, LLC, dba Simplus Management, LLC, of Cypress, CA, to continue construction management services for the Phase II Terminal Area Improvements at the Long Beach Airport, in the amount of $3,947,343, for a period of three years, with option to renew for two additional one-year periods, at the discretion of the City Manager; and Increase appropriations in the Airport Fund Group in the Airport Department by $3,947,343, offset by funds available.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to approve a $3,947,343 contract with Some Place Management for Phase 2 terminal area improvement construction management services at Long Beach Airport where a COVID-19-driven decline of over 95% in passenger travel has produced an estimated $10 to $20 million revenue impact through fiscal year end prompting a program adjustment that pauses the baggage claim project by approximately 17 months, continues the TSA-grant-funded Seabus project advances the ticketing facility to a logical hard stop at slab on grade with a restart decision point in the first quarter of 2021, and relies on $18.4 million in CARES Act funds projected to sustain approximately 6 to 7 months of operations, alongside approval of a landscaping services contract with Park Wood Landscape Maintenance for Queensway Bay not to exceed $756,572 annually for three years and a $160,947 amendment to Green Tech Landscape's interim contract.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Mayor’s Proposed 2018 Budget and Public Hearing Notice The Mayor’s Proposed Budget for 2018 is open for inspection from 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. Mondays through Fridays in the City Council Office, Room 451 of the City and County Building located at 1437 Bannock Street starting the week of October 16, 2017; (2) at its regular meeting on Monday, October 23, 2017, which will begin at 5:30 p.m., the City Council will conduct a public hearing on the Mayor’s Proposed Budget for 2018; and (3) any citizen may inspect the budget prior to or at the public hearing and register any objection to the budget prior to or at the public hearing on the budget, and the Council shall take any objections into consideration.\", \"sampled\": \"Councilwoman Sussman moved to instruct the clerk and recorder to publish notice of the mayor's proposed 2018 budget, available for public inspection Monday through Friday, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m., beginning the week of October 16th, 2017, in City Council office room 451 at 1437 Bannock Street. A public hearing on the proposed 2018 budget is scheduled for Monday, October 23rd, 2017, at 5:30 p.m., at which citizens may register objections that the Denver City Council is required to consider the motion passed with 9 ayes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to respectfully request City Council approval of the 2022 State Legislative Agenda as recommended by the State Legislation Committee; Request City Manager to write a letter of opposition to the Taxpayer Protection and Government Accountability Act; Request City Manager to write a letter of support for Senate Bill 1226 (Durazo) related to Joint Powers Authorities; and Request City Manager to write letters of support for Senate Bill 1281 (Bradford) and Assembly Bill 2792 (Rubio and Garcia) related to State cannabis taxes and add language to the State Legislative Agenda.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to approve the 2022 State Legislative Agenda as recommended by the State Legislation Committee including authorization to write a letter of support or opposition to the Taxpayer Protection and Government Accountability Act, letters of support for Senate Bill 1226 related to zero emissions vehicle joint powers authorities and for Senate Bill 1281 and Assembly Bill 2792 related to state cannabis taxes and the addition of language to the state legislative agenda, sponsored by Council Member Oranga as chair of the State Legislation Committee with the motion carried by the Long Beach City Council.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute an agreement with Centro CHA, Inc., a nonprofit corporation, to occupy Room 112 at the Jenny Oropeza Community Center at Cesar E. Chavez Park, at 104 Golden Avenue, from October 1, 2021 through September 30, 2026, with the option to renew for two five-year periods, at the discretion of the City Manager; and Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute an agreement with Centro CHA, Inc., to occupy Suite 104A, B, C, D, and E, at the Long Beach Senior Center, at 1150 East 4th Street, from October 1, 2021 through September 30, 2023, with the option to renew for an additional one-year period, at the discretion of the City Manager.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to authorize execution of two operational agreements with Central Cha (Centro CHA) for office space at city facilities in Districts 1 and 2, as recommended by Parks, Recreation and Marine Director Brant Dennis. The first agreement grants Central Cha use of Room 112 at the Jenny Oropeza Community Center at Cesar Chavez Park for an initial 5-year term with the option for two additional five-year periods, while the second grants use of Suite 1 (A through E) at the Long Beach Senior Center on a temporary basis pending Central Cha's relocation to its new Atlantic Avenue headquarters, expected to be completed within 12 to 18 months; Central Cha, which has operated in Long Beach for over 25 years, plans to provide immigration legal services, business development, and workforce development services from both locations and has been named the lead nonprofit to advance the Latino Cultural Center project.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to receive supporting documentation into the record, conclude the public hearing, adopt resolution making findings for energy services contracting and authorizing City Manager, or designee, to execute a Solar Energy Power Purchase Agreement and Easement Agreement, and any necessary documents and amendments, with PFMG Solar Long Beach, LLC, for the purchase of energy produced from solar photovoltaic installations at 11 potential municipal sites within the City of Long Beach, for a period of 25 years, with the option to renew for three additional five-year periods.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to authorize the City Manager to execute a solar energy power purchase agreement and easement agreement with FMG Solar Long Beach covering 11 original municipal sites plus up to 15 additional sites for a term of up to 25 years with FMG Solar owning the solar assets and the city purchasing the power produced, following a prior 2014 agreement with SunEdison that ended in bankruptcy without finalization; the motion carried by council vote.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance to rescind and reappropriate in the Capital Improvement program and make an appropriation from capital improvement fund contingency. (GOVERNMENT & FINANCE) Rescinds and reappropriates $1,423,932 in unused capital improvement funds from the 2014 Capital Improvement Program budget and appropriates $250,000 from capital improvement fund contingency for the Housing & Urban Development (HUD) Choice Planning Local Match. The Committee approved filing this bill by consent on 3-6-14.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 14-145, on final consideration rescinds and appropriates funding for a bio retention area at Hinsdale Park with Denver contributing $406,500 and Denver Public Schools contributing $203,000; upon completion, DPS will transfer approximately half an acre to the city for addition to Hinsdale Park as an outdoor education site along Cherry Creek. Councilwoman Ortega voted against the bill, arguing the cost \\\"should have been part of the big picture at the time that this project was brought forward.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance submitting to a vote of the qualified and registered electors of the City and County of Denver at a special municipal election to be held in conjunction with the coordinated election of November 3, 2020, a proposed amendment to the Charter of the City and County of Denver granting the Denver City Council appointment power over the Independent Monitor and to remove an exception from Career Service for Monitor employees. Gives City Council the appointment power over the Independent Monitor and no longer excepts Monitor employees from Career Service. Councilmember CdeBaca approved direct filing this item on 8-13-20.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 837 sponsored by Councilmember CdeBaca and placed on the floor by Councilmember Clark, proposes a charter amendment shifting the appointing authority for the Independent Monitor from the mayor to the city council, expanding the screening committee from 5 to 7 members and placing the office's 13 employees within the career service system to protect investigative and compliance staff. Councilman Flynn opposed the measure arguing the bill had not received adequate committee vetting since its first hearing on November 28 of the prior year, nearly 10 months earlier, and warned \\\"Far from weaponizing process, what we have is weaponizing chaos again.\\\" CdeBaca countered that urgency is warranted, stating \\\"We have a duty right now to make sure that we are creating the independence that we need right now to get these investigations done appropriately,\\\" and agreed to route a companion ordinance through committee before any November 2021 ballot referral.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance concerning the licensing of marijuana businesses, amending the Denver Retail Marijuana Code, Article V of Chapter 6, D.R.M.C. and the Denver Medical Marijuana Code, Art. XII of Chapter 24, D.R.M.C. by capping the total number of licensed locations where marijuana cultivation and sales may be permitted in the city, adopting new procedures for the issuance of retail marijuana cultivation and sales licenses, prohibiting the issuance of new medical marijuana cultivation and sales licenses, and adopted other related amendments. (Special Issues Marijuana Moratorium) Amends the Denver Revised Municipal Code relating to marijuana-related licenses and locations.\", \"sampled\": \"Public hearing on Council Bill 16-264, a marijuana regulation measure before Denver City Council, received testimony from multiple speakers including Mike Elliott of the Marijuana Industry Group, who opposed the proposed 1,000-foot distance requirement from grow facilities to residential areas and schools, stating \\\"Moving it to 500 feet or getting rid of it altogether seems like a much better way to go,\\\" while Amanda Gonzalez, business manager at 3937 West Colfax defended pending applications by citing a minimum entry-level wage of $11.50 per hour, a workforce that is 40% people of color and warned that blocking applicants would cost investors and employees following approximately one hour of public testimony, the council proceeded to questions, seven amendments, and the bill itself with Councilwoman Ortega's motion to amend held until after questions were addressed.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Final Passage of Ordinance Amending the Alameda Municipal Code by (1) Adding Section 1-8.01 Concerning Hearing Procedures, Hearing Officers’ Decisions and Administrative Regulations, (2) Repealing in Their Entirety Article XIV (Currently Suspended) and Article XV of Chapter VI Concerning (a) Review of Rent Increases Applicable to All Rental Units and Rent Stabilization Applicable to Certain Rental Units and (b) Limitations on Evictions and the Payment of Relocation Assistance Applicable to All Rental Units; and (3) Repealing Ordinance No. 3246 (Uncodified); and (4) Adding a Restated Article XV to Chapter VI Concerning Rent Control, Limitations on Evictions and Providing Relocation Payments to Displaced Tenants, including Section 8.\", \"sampled\": \"An ordinance opposed for imposing burdens on small landlords passed with a 4-2-1 vote, after which the consent calendar concluded and a public hearing opened on Brian Temper’s appeal of May 28th and July 22nd Planning Board decisions regarding a 172-room hotel and restaurant at 2900 Harbor Bay Parkway. Located adjacent to the Harbor Bay Ferry terminal in the Harbor Bay Business Park, where hotels are permitted by right and city discretion is limited to building design. Planning Director Andrew Thomas presented the item and staff recommended upholding the Planning Board’s decision.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance approving a proposed Intergovernmental Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Denver Health and Hospital Authority d/b/a Denver Health Medical Center to provide for the availability of reimbursement of agency funds in accordance with the FEMA COVID-19 Grant. Approves an intergovernmental agreement with Denver Health and Hospital Authority to provide for reimbursement of funds in accordance with the FEMA COVID-19 Grant. The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 6-29-20. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 6-2-20.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 495, a resolution to accept federal grant money reimbursing Denver Health for personal protective equipment, was introduced and passed its first reading with a 9 yes to 3 no. Councilmember CdeBaca opposed the bill, citing Denver Health's alleged union busting and mistreatment of workers during COVID-19, while councilmember Customer supported it, emphasizing the need to deliver PPE to workers and patients. Councilmember Sayed noted that frontline workers reported inadequate PPE that include reusing masks and lacking proper handwashing stations. The bill will proceeds to a second reading for final consideration.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all necessary documents, and any amendments, to receive and expend grant funding from the Laura and John Arnold Foundation in the amount of $557,124 over a two-year period, to fund two positions to support the City’s Data-Driven Justice Initiative, beginning January 1, 2018 through December 31, 2019; and Increase appropriations in the General Grants Fund (SR 120) in the City Manager Department (CM) by $557,124, offset by grant revenue.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation: Adopt the City Manager’s recommendation to accept and expend a $557,000 grant from the Laura and John Arnold Foundation, This grant will provide approximately $500,000 in implementation funding over two years to fund a data scientist and a full-time project manager for the city’s data-driven justice initiative. the project manager will help run wrap-around case services for high frequency offenders to align with the My Brother's Keeper initiative's 21st-century policing recommendations and direct the I-Team director to gather council input early while noting Councilman Price’s legislative intent to explore an app for police access to available homeless shelter beds.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Plans and Specifications No. R-7177 and award a contract to All American Asphalt, of Corona, CA, for the Market Street Pedestrian and Streetscape Project, in an amount of $7,394,311, authorize a 12.5 percent contingency in the amount of $924,289, for a total contract amount not to exceed $8,318,600; and authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contract, including any necessary subsequent amendments; Increase appropriations in the Capital Projects Fund Group in the Public Works Department by $6,071,837, offset by $2,838,000 of State Local Partnership Program Funds (LPP) and $3,233,837 of Federal Congestion Mitigation and Air Quality Improvement Program (CMAQ) funds; Increase appropriations in the Capital Projects Fund Group in the Public Works Department by $6,071,837, offset by transfer of $2,838,000 State Local Partnership Program Funds and $3,233,837 of Federal Congestion Mitigation and Air Quality Improvement Program funds from the Capital Projects Grant Fund; and Accept Categorical Exemption CE 21-1.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend authorization of a contract with All American Asphalt for the Market Street Pedestrian and Streetscape Project in an amount not to exceed $8,318,600, with construction of the 2.2-mile corridor from the L.A. River to Long Beach Boulevard scheduled to break ground in May 2022 and targeted for completion in fall 2023, delivering new sidewalks, curb ramps, high-visibility crosswalks, rectangular rapid flashing beacons, multiple classes of bike lanes and improved street and pedestrian lighting across Council District 8 in North Long Beach, funded through accumulated sources and advanced after eight and a half years of development as one of four major corridor investments alongside Artesia, Anaheim, and Studebaker.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Plans and Specifications No. R-7056 for Street Improvements to 3rd Street and Broadway, between Magnolia Avenue and Alamitos Avenue; award a contract to Sully-Miller Contracting Company, of Brea, CA, in the amount of $4,152,000, authorize a 10 percent contingency in the amount $415,200, for a total contract amount not to exceed $4,567,200; authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contact, including any necessary amendments; and Accept Categorical Exemption CE 17-005.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend award of a contract to Sally Miller Contracting Company for street improvements on Third Street and Broadway between Magnolia Avenue and Alamitos Avenue in an amount not to exceed $4,000,567, and award of a contract for the Third Street and Broadway cycle track project in an amount not to exceed $2,070,400.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Rezones property located at 5050 South Syracuse Street from B-8 with waivers, UO-1, UO-2 to S-MX-12 in Council District 4. (NEIGHBORHOODS AND PLANNING) Rezones property located at 5050 South Syracuse Street from B-8 with waivers, UO-1, UO-2 to S-MX-12 in Council District 4. IF ORDERED PUBLISHED, A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD ON THIS ITEM. REFER TO THE \\\"PENDING\\\" SECTION OF THE FUTURE COUNCIL MEETING AGENDAS FOR THE DATE. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 2-17-16.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 69 rezones 5050 South Syracuse Street from B-8 with waivers and use overlays to suburban mixed-use removing a 2:1 floor area ratio limit on the 55,000-square-foot property that currently holds a one-story restaurant. Staff recommended approval, finding the rezoning consistent with adopted plans and justified by changed conditions, and no public comments were received. Councilmember Black noted, “It’s currently a restaurant called Garcia’s of Scottsdale, which has been there since I was in high school. So I’m sad to see it go, although I haven’t been there since the eighties.” Council Bill 69 passed its final consideration by roll call vote.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed On-Call Project Management Services Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Citrine, LLC for on-call project management services. Approves a contract with Citrine, LLC for $1 million and for three years for on-call project management services including design oversight, design and construction services procurement, construction inspection services and project implementation oversight, for City parks and recreation infrastructure (202159103). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 9-20-21. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 8-17-21.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Resolutions 21-0917, 21-0918, 21-0919, and 21-0920 were adopted in a block vote with 12 yes votes and 1 no vote. Councilmember Sawyer cast the dissenting vote, stating, “I believe that the practice of setting aside money for certain vendors without any additional information or reporting requirements to City Council regarding the scope of the work being done subverts the intent of our Charter.” Councilmember Hines voted yes after previously indicating opposition to on-call contracts.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to receive supporting documentation into the record, conclude the public hearing regarding an economic subsidy in connection with a Retail Sales Tax Incentive Agreement with Worthington Ford Inc., pursuant to California Government Code Section 53083; Authorize City Manager to execute all documents necessary to effect the Second Amendment to Lease No. 24823 between the City of Long Beach and Worthington Ford, Inc., to extend the term of the ground lease to September 23, 2030, including one five-year option to extend the term to September 23, 2035, for the use of 3.24 acres of land at the Lakewood Boulevard off-ramp at an annual rental rate of $1 per year; and Authorize City Manager to execute all documents necessary to implement a Retail Sales Tax Incentive Agreement with Worthington Ford, Inc., to assist in financing the renovation of the electronic display sign located at 2601 Lakewood Boulevard.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend authorization for the City Manager to execute a Second Amendment extending the ground lease for 3.24 acres at an annual rate of $1 per year to September 2030 with a five-year option to 2035, and to execute a retail sales tax incentive agreement financing 50% of an electronic display sign renovation up to $340,000 of the total $680,000 cost. This sales tax sharing agreement will span 15 years or conclude once the $340,000 reimbursement cap is reached above an annual base threshold of $380,000. The renovated V-shaped LED sign on city-owned land at the Lakewood Boulevard off-ramp will provide the city 20% of advertising time valued at $190,000 to $240,000 per year, and the project is estimated to create up to 15 new jobs, with 80% full-time and 20% part-time.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute any and all documents necessary, including a Standard Offer, Agreement, and Escrow Instructions for Purchase of Real Estate, with the Estate of Roy Masaru Tanaka, for the purchase of certain real property at 1400 West Wardlow Road, Assessor Parcel Numbers 7311-017-027 and 7311-013-001, in an amount not to exceed $1,105,000; Authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute any and all documents necessary, including a Reimbursement Agreement with Breakers Development, LLC, a California limited liability company, for the reimbursement of $55,866 of the purchase price of the Subject Property; Accept the Categorical Exemption CE 19-154; Increase appropriations in the General Fund Group in the Economic Development Department by $1,105,000, offset by various fundings sources including grant reimbursement revenues and transfers from other funds; Decrease appropriations in the General Fund Group in the Citywide Activities Department by $50,000 to offset a transfer to the Economic Development Department;\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend adoption of a resolution authorizing the City Manager to acquire 1400 West Wardlow Road for a permanent Tanaka Park at a purchase price not to exceed $1.1 million plus $5,000 in administrative costs, with $55,866 of the price reimbursed by Breakers Development to satisfy a 2-to-1 park space replacement requirement, funding allocated from $764,455 in Los Angeles County Measure A parcel tax proceeds, $234,679 from the Construction and Demolition/Development Services Fund, and $50,000 in Councilmember Roberto Uranga’s discretionary funds, to preserve the approximately 60,000-square-foot site serving roughly 5,800 residents within a half-mile radius in West Long Beach where a USC report found the city’s highest childhood asthma rate, and to close escrow as early as September 20, 2019.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to Authorize the City Manager to Negotiate and Execute the Seaplane Lagoon Ferry Terminal Operating Agreement between the City of Alameda and the Water Emergency Transportation Authority; and Introduction of Ordinance Approving a License and Authorizing the City Manager to Negotiate and Execute Documents Necessary to Implement the Terms of a 66-Year License with the San Francisco Bay Area Water Emergency Transportation Authority for Use of Real Property and Submerged Land at Alameda Point. [Requires Four Affirmative Votes] (Community Development 858)\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to authorize the City Manager to negotiate and execute the Seaplane Lagoon Ferry Terminal Operating Agreement with the Water Emergency Transportation Authority and introduce an ordinance approving a 66-year license for real property and submerged land at Alameda Point at an annual fee of $1 effective once ferry service commences. Under the agreement, the City will own and maintain the landside area, while WETA will own, maintain, repair, and replace waterside improvements. It will also bear responsibility for all long-term capital costs, additional waterside equipment, and any necessary dredging, following a project funded by $18.2 million including an $8.2 million grant from the Alameda County Transportation Commission, a $2 million city appropriation, and a $2 million WETA contribution, with parking for approximately 400 vehicles and ferry service anticipated to start in August 2020.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to increase appropriations in the General Fund Group in the Parks, Recreation and Marine Department by $50, offset by the First Council District One-time District Priority Funds transferred from the Citywide Activities Department for the Harvey Milk Flag Raising Event; Increase appropriation in the General Fund Group in the Public Works Department by $150, offset by the First Council District One-time District Priority Funds transferred from the Citywide Activities Department for the costs associated with the First District Harvey Milk Flag Raising Event; and Decrease appropriations in the General Fund Group in the Citywide Activities Department by $200 to offset a transfer to the Parks, Recreation and Marine Department and the Public Works Department.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to increase appropriations in the Parks, Recreation and Marine Department by $50 and in the public works department by $150 for costs associated with the Harvey Milk flag raising event per a communication from Councilwoman Allen and Councilwoman Zendaya; and to increase appropriations in the City Manager Department by $1,095 to support the Long Beach Blues Society's Blues for All Event, per a communication from Vice Mayor Richardson, Councilwoman Zendejas, Councilwoman Allen Council and Super Nine.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"On the message and order, referred September 15, 2021 Docket 0957, authorizing the City of Boston to accept and expend the amount of Two Hundred Fifty-Four Thousand One Hundred Ninety-Four Dollars ($254,194.00) in the form of a grant, for the Challenge Grant, awarded by the William T. Grant Foundation, passed through Northeastern University, to be administered by the Department of Youth and Employment. The grant will fund research by Northeastern University Dukakis Center for Urban and Regional Policy that inform the design of a more inclusive workforce development system for the youth in Boston, the committee submitted a report recommending the order ought to pass. The report was accepted; the order was passed.\", \"sampled\": \"An order for the acceptance of a grant under Docket 0957 in the amount of $254,194 from the William G. Grant Foundation to be administered by the Department of Youth and Employment and passed through Northeastern University to fund research by the Dukakis Center for Urban and Regional Policy. The research will inform a more inclusive youth workforce development system for individuals aged 14 to 24. The Committee on Small Business and Workforce Development, chaired by Councilor Julia Macchia held a hearing on the matter and advocated for paying the young people participating in the research. The council voted to pass the docket.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"On the message and order, referred on July 13, 2022, Docket #0865 for a supplemental appropriation Order for the Property Management Department for FY23 in the amount of One Hundred Eight Thousand One Hundred Fifty-Eight Dollars ($108,158.00) to cover the FY23 cost contained within the collective bargaining agreements between the City of Boston and the Municipal Police Patrolmen's Association (MPPA). The terms of the contract include base wage increases of 2%, 1.5% and 2%, to be given in October of each Fiscal year of the contract term. Filed in the Office of the City Clerk on July 11, 2022, the committee submitted a report recommending that the order ought to pass. The report was accepted; the order was passed.\", \"sampled\": \"The Committee on City Services and Innovation Technology on Docket #0865, a message and order for a supplemental appropriation for the Property Management Department for Fiscal Year 23 to fund costs within collective bargaining agreements between the City of Boston and the Municipal Police Patrolmen's Association and PPA, filed in the Office of the City Clerk on July 11, 2022, submits a report recommending the order ought to pass. A hearing was held on July 18, 2022, concerning agreements with school traffic supervisors and municipal police officers. Both contracts follow the pattern of 2%, 1.5%, and 2% raises over three years, with most payments being retroactive. Both include a one-time lump sum payment.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to: (1) Based on the analysis provided, consider the request to (i) adopt resolution authorizing the City to support the California State Communities Development Authority Community Improvement Authority’s (CSCDA) issuance of bonds under its Middle-Income/Workforce Rental Housing Program to enact moderate-income deed restrictions on the existing Oceanaire apartment project, at 150 West Ocean Boulevard (Project), and (ii) authorize the execution of a Public Benefits Agreement (PBA) with CSCDA in connection therewith that includes a provision that shares any future sale profits with taxing bodies proportionate to the current tax allocation; (2) Request City Manager to develop a proposed policy for any future similar projects that participate in the Middle Income/Workforce Rental Housing Program; and (3) If action (1) is approved, designate the Project as a pilot, with no similar projects to be approved until the City Council has an opportunity to consider the City Manager’s proposed policy.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to adopt a DAB Resolution authorizing the City's support for the California State Communities Development Authority's issuance of bonds under its Middle Income Workforce Rental Housing Program for a pilot project, as presented in a report from Development Services, Item 20 by Oscar Orsi, development services manager who noted to the Mayor and Council that this is a relatively new program from the state of California to convert some properties, and while there \\\"s some benefits. There\\\" are also risks to be aware of stating \\\"ve done a lot of negotiating on this. We want to make sure that you\\\" are aware of the program features before he would \\\"ll turn it over to Oscar Orsi, our development services manager. Thank you, ma\\\" for the presentation.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Revival and Amendatory Agreement between the City and County of Denver to be administered by the Department of Safety, Division of Community Corrections in consultation with the Denver Community Corrections Board and Correctional Management, Inc. to add additional funds for residential and non-residential community corrections services. Amends a contract with Correctional Management, Inc. by adding $60,368.01 for a new total of $6,029,912.01 for residential and non-residential community corrections services provided in Fiscal Years 2018 and 2019 (201951095). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 9-16-19. Council member Kashmann approved direct filing this item on 8-22-19.\", \"sampled\": \"Adoption of Resolution 19-0880 to revive and amend an agreement with Correctional Management Inc. for a final payment covering services provided during the fiscal year from July 1, 2018, to June 30, 2019. The contractor, also known as Core Civic Incorporated exceeded its original budget by providing services for two additional individuals and incurring extra treatment costs. The additional funds were transferred from underspent contracts and were fully reimbursed by the state. The resolution was adopted by a vote of 13 to 0.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a Second Amendment between the City and County of Denver and Hensel Phelps Construction Co. concerning the extension of construction management and general contracting services for the Great Hall Project at Denver International Airport. Amends a contract with Hensel Phelps Construction Co. by adding $900 million for a new total of $1.265 billion and 1721 calendar days for a new end date of 12-31-28 to continue to provide Construction Management and General Contracting (CM/GC) services for the Great Hall Project completion at Denver International Airport (202161481; 202053359-02). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 1-18-22. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 12-15-21. Pursuant to Council Rule 3.7, Councilmember Ortega called this item out at the meeting on 1-3-22 for a one-week postponement to 1-10-22.\", \"sampled\": \"Motion by Council Member State Abarca to place Council Resolutions 21-15, 21-16, 21-17, 21-18, and 21-19 on the floor for adoption. During the January 10, 2022 meeting, Councilmember Sawyer called out Resolutions 21-15, 21-16, and 21-17, stating, “I wanted to go on record not supporting these.” Sawyer noted that the airport renovation items total over one billion dollars and extend work to 2028. Councilmember Hines also called out Resolution 20-0005 for comment under bills for introduction. No items were called out under bills for final consideration or the pending list.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Montrose Air Quality Services, LLC to provide air quality monitoring services. Approves a contract with Montrose Air Quality Services, Inc. for $1,000,000 and through 12-31-26 for on-call procurement, installation and maintenance of air quality monitors, sensors, and other equipment, and for periodic reporting and analysis of air quality data, citywide (ENVHL-202261734). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 2-28-22. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 1-19-22.\", \"sampled\": \"Motion to place Council Resolution 22-0083 on the floor for adoption was directed to Council Member Sandoval. After the motion was moved and seconded, comments were opened for members of the Council. Councilmember Sawyer noted this was another on-call airport contract with Montrose Air Quality Services that had been discussed before. There was some confusion about whether the item had been called out correctly from the script. Following the discussion, Council Resolution 83 was adopted.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Eighth Amendatory Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Busco, Inc. to provide homeless persons with transportation to and from overflow shelters. Amends a contract with Busco, Inc. to add $1 million for a new contract total of $4,050,000 and to add one year for a new end date of 9-30-18 to provide transportation for people experiencing homelessness from the Denver Rescue Mission to various approved recreation centers and shelters located throughout the City (SOCSV-2013-13589-08). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 3-5-18. The Committee approved filing this resolution by consent on 1-17-18.\", \"sampled\": \"Approves a contract amendment with Denver Human Services to add $1,000,000 for emergency transport services for individuals experiencing homelessness. The amendment increases the total multi-year contract value to $2.4 million. The service, part of Denver’s Road Home program, cost $1,460,000 last year and currently uses four buses to transport approximately 700 people each evening and morning from locations including Catholic Charities and Denver Rescue Mission. The current appropriation funds it until September. Before then, the city will re-evaluate costs and prepare a new solicitation. Morning drop-offs occur at the Rescue Mission and Samaritan House.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to approve the use of the Sixth Council District's one-time infrastructure funds transferred from the Capital Projects Fund (CP) in the Public Works Department (PW) into the Legislative Department (LD) operating budget in the amount of $150,000 to support a partnership being entered into with Killing Fields Memorial Center, Inc., in order to develop the Killing Fields Memorial located at 1501 East Anaheim Street; and Increase appropriation in the General Fund (GF) in the Legislative Department (LD) by $150,000.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to approve the use of $150,000 in Six Council District one-time Infrastructure Funds, to be transferred from the Capital Project Fund, to support the Killing Fields Memorial Center, also known as the Killing Fields Memorial Location, on East Anaheim Street. The item was referred to the Poverty and Economic Development Committee for further review. Communication from Councilman Andrew, as amended by Councilwoman Pierce, requests that the City Manager explore options for the Killing Fields Memorial.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance amending Division 11, Article VIII of Chapter 2 of the Revised Municipal Code of the City and County of Denver to update procedures and transfer governance of the Crime Prevention and Control Commission from the Department of Human Services to the Department of Public Health and Environment. Amends Article VIII, Chapter 2 of the Denver Revised Municipal Code (DRMC) to update procedures and transfer governance of the Crime Prevention and Control Commission from the Department of Human Services to the Department of Public Health and Environment. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 11-28-18.\", \"sampled\": \"Bill 1267 moves the Crime Prevention and Control Commission, created to promote diversion programs, from the Department of Human Services to the Department of Public Health and Environment. It also removes the commission’s automatic funding mechanism of up to $3.5 million and institutes a traditional budget process. A public hearing for Council Bill 1013, a zoning change at 2929 West 10th Avenue for a crisis stabilization facility, was postponed to January 14, 2019. The block vote passed 13 to 0.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance amending Chapter 48 of the Revised Municipal Code of the City and County of Denver to repeal the fee on composting and implement a volume-based pricing system for trash, including recycling and composting services at no extra charge, to facilitate improved waste diversion and its related environmental benefits. Amends Chapter 48 of the Denver Revised Municipal Code to repeal the fee on composting and implement a volume-based pricing system for trash, including recycling and composting services at no extra charge, to facilitate improved waste diversion and its related environmental benefits. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 6-7-22.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 22-668 has passed with 12 ayes and one abstention. Council Bill 20-0685 focuses on implementing a volume based pricing system for trash. Councilmember Clark scheduled a courtesy public hearing on June 27th for the bill. Councilmember Ortega proposed amendments to include a sunset provision, setting the expiration date for certain provisions to October 3rd, 2028. Councilmember Flynn advocated for the sunset provision to free up funds for other uses. Councilmember Ortega expressed concerns about the impact of the forever fee on trash service fees and proposed amendments to allocate revenue for specific costs.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Contract between the City and County of Denver and AXS Group, LLC to add compensation and five years to provide ticketing services, box office operation, information and call center functions, and marketing support for City venues. Approves a contract with AXS Group, LLC for $5,010,613 and for five years to provide ticketing services, box office operation, information and call center functions, and marketing support for City venues (THTRS-201952501). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 12-23-19. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 11-20-19.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver City Council discussed the proposed adoption of Council Resolution 1220, the director of marketing and business development with Arts and Venues, regarding investigations into prospective vendors. The House Energy and Commerce Committee and the Competition and Markets Authority are conducting inquiries into ticketing platforms that focusing on consumer protection and the reintroduction of the Boss Act. These investigations aim to address five specific areas of concern, such as controlling the secondary ticket market at venues like Red Rocks, where 95% of tickets are sold electronically. The contract renewal with Access includes volume discounts on ticketing fees and ensures that 22% of secondary market revenue is shared between the city and Access.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance renaming a portion of Weir Gulch Park located north of West Alameda Avenue, south of West Bayaud Avenue, north and west of South Weir Drive, and south and east of South Windsor Drive, as “Ruth ‘Lucille’ Dreiling Park”. (INFRASTRUCTURE & CULTURE) Re-names a parcel of Weir Gulch at West Alameda Avenue and South Weir Drive to “Ruth Lucille Dreiling Park” in Council District 3. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 6-29-16.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver City Council discussed Council Bill 473 to rename Weird Gulch at West Alameda and South Drive in honor of Ruth Lucille Drilling, recognizing her pivotal role in transforming the area into a community park. Additionally, Council Bill 474 was proposed to rename Barnum Park North after M.L. Sam Sandoz known for his community initiatives. Over 700 signatures were gathered in support of these changes, reflecting the widespread backing for honoring legacies in their respective neighborhoods.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance changing the zoning classification of 1683, 1685, 1687, 1691, 1693, 1695, and 1699 Cedar Avenue in Washington Park. Approves an official map amendment to rezone properties from PUD-G 5 to PUD-G 22 (changing the footprint and floor area limitations), for properties located at 1683, 1685, 1687, 1691, 1693, 1695, and 1699 Cedar Avenue in Council District 6. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 4-30-19.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 403, up for final consideration, pertains to the rezoning of properties on Cedar Avenue from Pdg five to Pdg 22. The proposed change aims to eliminate house size restrictions to align with neighborhood character preservation goals and city plans. Staff recommends rezoning approval with no objections from the Washington Park East Neighborhood Organization. If approved, the new zoning would allow for up to seven houses on a single lot.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Changes the zoning from O-1 (Former Code) to U-SU-A with waivers (Urban Context, Single Unit, lot size 3,000 sf min.) of property at 6801 East First Avenue (Buckley Annex) in Council District 5. (LAND USE, TRANSPORTATION & INFRASTRUCTURE) Changes the zoning from O-1 (Former Code) to U-SU-A with waivers (Urban Context, Single Unit, lot size 3,000 sf min.) of property at 6801 East First Avenue in Council District 5. A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD AT LEAST FOUR WEEKS AFTER PUBLICATION. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 6-24-14.\", \"sampled\": \"The Denver City Council discussed Council Bill 522, a rezoning request for a portion of the Buckley Annex, presented by Tina Axler. The Buckley Annex was vacated by the Air Force in 2011 and approved for redevelopment in 2013. The redevelopment plan includes new single-family housing with waivers requested to tailor zoning for taller homes with reduced setbacks to accommodate a courtyard development. The plan also integrates additional parks and open spaces, enhancing the residential and mixed-use areas within the Lowry Field neighborhood.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request City Manager to work with Department of Economic Development to research, prepare, and propose an equitable and effective “COVID-19 Small Business Recovery Loan Plan” for key sectors that have been hit hardest by closures associated with the COVID-19 emergency including but not limited to: (i) restaurants, (ii) retail, (iii) services, (iv) non-profit, (v) property-based businesses, and (vi) other very small businesses that provide employment to Long Beach residents; and, propose funding levels that consider the number of Long Beach business licenses within each category, and the projected needs of those within those categories to rebound.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation: Request the City Manager to develop a COVID-19 Small Business Recovery Loan Plan that leverages available CARES Act funding for grants in addition to loans, while addressing the unique needs of businesses and balancing economic revival with public health concerns. Council discussions highlighted the participation of various businesses in the fifth District forums and the City Manager's efforts towards a safe reopening process ahead of an end-of-week deadline for establishing guidelines. small businesses in the second district are facing significant financial challenges, underscoring the urgency of tailored support measures.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Prosecutor to execute all necessary documents to receive and expend grant funding awarded to the City Prosecutor by the United States Department of Justice, Office of Juvenile Justice and Delinquency Prevention, for the Comprehensive Anti-Gang Strategies and Programs in the amount not to exceed $203,478 for a period ending September 30, 2016; and increase appropriation in the General Grants Fund (SR 120) in the City Prosecutor Department (CP) by $203,478.\", \"sampled\": \"The City Prosecutor recommends accepting a $203,000 grant from the United States Department of Justice to bolster gang intervention and rehabilitation efforts to align with the city's violence prevention plan. The City Council unanimously supports this motion, highlighting the grant's potential to enhance the city's vitality and competitiveness in future applications. The funding will also increase appropriations within the city prosecutor department, further advancing violence prevention initiatives.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Third Amendatory Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Integral Recoveries, Inc. to amend provisions, extend the term, and to expand the scope of collections. Amends a contract with Integral Recoveries, Inc. to add two years for a new end date of 2-1-2024 and expands the scope of collections to include cases owing less than $300.00. No change to contract amount (COURT-202261912). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 4-18-22. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 3-15-22. Pursuant to Council Rule 3.7, Councilmember Torres called this item out at the 3-29-22 meeting for a one-week postponement to 4-4-22.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 278 was temporarily withdrawn and rescheduled for consideration in one week. The resolution pertains to an amendment seeking to expand the collections system for fees below $300. Collections investigators are responsible for setting up payment plans for individuals with outstanding debts. over $300 debts incur a 30% collection fee, with 25% directed to Integral, the collections process provider. In 2021, $2.7 million was referred to collections, yielding $2.3 million in successful collections. A vast majority of fees owed to Denver fall below the $300 threshold. Concerns were raised about the lack of an equity study to evaluate the resolution's impact on low-income residents. Councilmembers also engaged in a consent vote for the remaining agenda items after the resolution postponement.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents, and any needed subsequent amendments, with the Department of Defense through the Office of Economic Adjustment, to accept funds totaling $1,342,406 funded by the Community Economic Adjustment Assistance for Reductions in Defense Industry Employment, to continue implementation of a project that addresses the economic and workforce impact of the C-17 plant closure; execute an agreement, and any needed subsequent amendments, with project partners in the amount totaling $1,125,000, for the period of January 1, 2017 through December 31, 2017; and Increase appropriations in the Community Development Grants Fund (SR 150) in the Economic and Property Development Department (EP) by $1,342,406, offset by grant revenue.\", \"sampled\": \"The Long Beach City Council received $1,342,406 in funds from the Department of Defense to address the economic and workforce impact of the C17 plant closure. Phase one of the economic adjustment project focused on dislocated workers and site planning, while phase two will bring over $1.5 million to expand economic analysis and support impacted workers. Pacific Gateway will collaborate with businesses to assess economic competitiveness and facilitate planning and zoning changes, with plans for further federal funding expansion. Following public opposition to accepting federal funds for local businesses, the city council proceeded with votes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to determine that the project is categorically exempt from the California Environmental Quality Act (CEQA) pursuant to Section 15305 (Class 5 - Minor Alterations to Land Use Limitations) of the CEQA Guidelines and none of the exceptions in 15300.2 apply, and that it is further exempt pursuant to Section 15308 (Actions by Regulatory Agencies for Protection of the Environment) and Section 15061(b)(3) (Common Sense Exemption), as it will not result directly or indirectly in significant environmental impacts (CE20-090); Declare ordinance amending Title 21, Zoning Regulations (Zoning Code), of the Long Beach Municipal Code to add Chapter 21.\", \"sampled\": \"The Long Beach City Council discussed a Zoning Code amendment regarding affordable housing in the coastal zone, which aims to update in lieu fees based on a market study of current development costs. The proposed ordinance, recommended for approval by the Planning Commission, seeks to remove exemptions for certain housing units and submit amendments to the Coastal Commission for review. The in lieu fee schedule has not changed since 1988 and needs to align with current conditions, as highlighted by public outreach efforts encompassing planning commission hearings and local media publications. amendment, Exempt from CEQA under a Class five exemption, amendment addresses the pressing need for additional affordable housing units as identified in the Regional Housing Needs Assessment.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Amendatory Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Denver Options, Inc., for services and supports for intellectually and developmentally disabled individuals. Amends a contract with Denver Options, Inc., doing business as Rocky Mountain Human Services (RMHS), by changing the maximum amount not to exceed to $7.5 million and to add six months for a new end date of 6-30-18 to provide services and distribute funds to individuals with intellectual and developmental disabilities citywide (SOCSV-201209022-01). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 1-8-18. The Committee approved filing this resolution at its meeting on 12-13-17.\", \"sampled\": \"Denver City Council considered Council Resolution 1341, brought forward by Councilman Cashman, for adoption. Councilman Espinosa raised concerns about the timeline for council action on the resolution and voted against it due to unanswered questions. Councilman Ortega noted the importance of extending the contract with the service provider, Rocky Mountain Human Services, which is the focus of Resolution 1341. The resolution was adopted by majority vote, with 12 votes in favor and 1 against.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Local Maintenance Districts Group 1 Tonight, Council was scheduled to sit as the quasi-judicial Board of Equalization to consider reduction of total cost assessments for Local Maintenance Districts. However, since no written protests of assessment were filed with the Manager of Public Works by September 28, 2017, Council will not sit as the Board of Equalization for the following Local Maintenance Districts: 15th Street Pedestrian Mall 20th Street Pedestrian Mall Broadway Pedestrian Mall A Consolidated Larimer Street Pedestrian Mall Delgany Street East 13th Avenue Pedestrian Mall Santa Fe Drive Pedestrian Mall C South Broadway Streetscape (Arizona to Iowa) South Broadway Streetscape (Iowa to Wesley) South Broadway Streetscape (Wesley to Yale) South Downing Street Pedestrian Mall St.\", \"sampled\": \"Denver City Council was originally scheduled to sit as the quasi Judicial Board of Equalization to consider cost assessments for multiple Local Maintenance Districts, but will no longer do so. The districts include the 15th Street, 20th Street, Broadway, Larimer Street, Santa Fe Drive, South Broadway Streetscape, South Downing Street, St Luke's, Tennyson Street, West 32nd Avenue, and West 44th Avenue and Elliott Street Pedestrian Malls. No written protests of assessment were filed by the September 28, 2017 deadline. The council meeting will also include the reading of two proclamations, including Proclamation 1155. Former Councilwoman Jeannie Raab is expected to attend.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt resolution authorizing City Manager, or designee, to submit a grant application to the Los Angeles County Regional Park and Open Space District (RPOSD) for Measure A funding for Projects and Programs; authorize City Manager, or designee, to submit applications to the RPOSD for grants under the Community-based Park Investment Program (Category 1) and the Neighborhood Parks, Healthy Communities, and Urban Greening Program (Category 2) for 14th Street Park improvements, Bixby Park Parcel One improvements, the Pacific Electric Right-of-Way between 8th Street and 10th Street, Whaley Park Community Center, Wardlow Park, Lincoln Park, McBride Park, MacArthur Park, Tanaka Park, Scherer Park improvements, and Houghton Park Community Center (Projects); if awarded, accept an amount up to $4,312,724; and, execute any documents necessary to accept the funds and implement the Projects.\", \"sampled\": \"The Long Beach City Council adopted a resolution authorizing city managers to submit a grant application to the Los Angeles County Regional Park and Open Space District for funding citywide projects, programs, and improvements. The council was urged to execute green agreements related to safe, clean neighborhood parks and beaches. Measure A of 2016 was mentioned, providing $270,000 for 14th Street Park improvements. Council members expressed support for the proposed projects and improvements. Habitat for Humanity of Greater Los Angeles was identified as a partner in this effort and has been involved in building and repairing over 288 homes in Long Beach. Aaron Reinke, President and CEO of the organization, stated that it has built, repaired and rehabilitated more than 288 homes in Long Beach, providing affordable homes for residents. The organization is currently engaged in neighborhood revitalization efforts in the Washington neighborhood.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt resolution authorizing City Manager, or designee, to execute a contract with Brüel & Kjaer EMS, Inc., a Delaware corporation, including any necessary amendments thereto, for the maintenance, upgrade and support of the Long Beach Airport Noise and Operations Monitoring System (ANOMS), in the amount of $232,873, for a period of one year, with the option to renew for two additional one-year periods; authorize an annual contingency in the amount of $50,000 for additional services, plus a 3.5 percent annual CPI increase, for a total amount not to exceed $873,356 for the proposed three-year term. (Districts 3,4,5,7,8)\", \"sampled\": \"Recommend adoption of a resolution to execute an $880,000 contract with Berlin Care EMS for a three-year term, to maintain, upgrade, and support the Long Beach Noise and Operations Monitoring System, which involves noise monitors located in districts three, five, four, seven and eight. The council agreed to address public concerns about the web track system and communicate with the vendor about possible improvements. The contract, primarily software with a physical component for noise monitoring, is specific to the main runway and the shorter two five runways. The language of the item will be changed to reflect all five council districts.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Plans and Specifications No. R-7022 for the Seaside Park Artificial Turf Field Project; award the contract to Los Angeles Engineering, Inc., of Covina, CA, in the amount of $773,123, authorize a 10 percent contingency in the amount of $77,312, for a total contract amount not to exceed $850,435; authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contract, including any necessary amendments thereto; Adopt Plans and Specifications No. R-7024 for the Admiral Kidd Artificial Turf Field Project; award the contract to Los Angeles Engineering, Inc.\", \"sampled\": \"The Public Works, Park Recreation and Marine recommends the award of a contract to Los Angeles Engineering for the Seaside Park and Admiral Kidd Artificial Turf Field Projects, costing approximately $850,435 and $1,559,949 respectively. The city manager is also authorized to approve expenditures of around $800,000 for the design and construction of athletic field improvements at Eldorado Park West, bringing the combined project total to roughly $4.1 million. Councilwoman Mongo stated, \\\"I want to thank Parks and Rec staff and Public Works staff for all the outreach into the community.\\\" These projects aim to reduce water usage, increase playable hours, lower maintenance costs, and improve usability of the fields. Councilwoman Gonzales also stated, \\\"I, too, just want to say thank you to Marie and Craig for sticking with this.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance approving a proposed Intergovernmental Agreement between the City and County of Denver and The Housing Authority of the City and County of Denver, to launch the new Lower Income Voucher Equity (LIVE Denver) program. Approves an $1,180,000 intergovernmental agreement with the Denver Housing Authority (DHA) through 12-31-23 to launch the Lower Income Voucher Equity (LIVE Denver) program to create immediate affordable housing options for employed workers by connecting vacant rental units with income-qualified Denver residents. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 5-23-18.\", \"sampled\": \"Councilman Flynn and Councilwoman Black commented on Council Bill 660 during a recent Denver City Council meeting, focusing on the bill's purpose to revise election procedures, including the creation of fiscal impact estimates and pro and con information for citizens on municipal initiatives. Flynn raised concerns about the strategy to address the affordability of rental housing in Denver, while Councilwoman Ortega supported the program and emphasized its immediate accessibility to lower-income individuals. Councilman Espinal expressed concerns about the program's long-term viability but supported its administrative aspects.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"On the message and order, referred on January 26, 2022, Docket #0158, for your approval an order authorizing the issuance of refunding bonds in a principal amount not to exceed Two Hundred Million Dollars ($200,000,000.00). The current conditions in the municipal bond market appears to be favorable for the City to potentially realize significant saving in debt service costs by refunding certain of its outstanding general obligation bonds, the Committee submitted a report recommending the order ought to passed.\", \"sampled\": \"The Boston City Council, chaired by Counsel Fernandez Anderson, is considering the issuance of refunding bonds not exceeding $200 million, given the current favorable municipal bond market conditions. The city aims to save on debt service costs by refunding its outstanding general obligation bonds, approximately $60 million in total, which could result in savings of $3.5 million over the next decade. Counsel Fernandez Anderson stated, \\\"The refunding transactions will be credit neutral, credit neutral and will not push the current debt out.\\\" Following a public hearing by the Committee on Ways and Means on February 7th, the council passed Docket 0158, the refunding authorization, unanimously. The last such refunding authorization was granted in 2019.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute an agreement and all necessary documents with the Los Angeles County Metropolitan Transportation Authority of Los Angeles (Metro), to accept the donation of the retired P865 Nippon Sharyo light rail vehicle car #100 of the former Long Beach Blue Line; and, authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute an agreement and all necessary documents with Heavy Transport, of Long Beach, CA, to transport and deliver the vehicle from its current location to Long Beach, in an amount not to exceed $40,000.\", \"sampled\": \"The city is recommended to execute an agreement with Metro to accept the donation of the former Long Beach Blue Line vehicle, known as the Long Beach Car 100, the first metro car in the history of the L.A. County rail system. City Manager Mr. Modica is authorized to execute an agreement with Heavy Transport to transport and deliver the vehicle to Long Beach District. The vehicle is planned to be installed in downtown Long Beach in the East Village, activating a currently inactive space. The project, managed by Eric Lopez, is funded by federal, state and regional sources, with no general fund involved. The motion to proceed with the project, supported by Supervisor Hahn, Councilwoman Allen, Councilmember Durango, Councilmember Brosnan and Vice Mayor Richardson, was carried unanimously with 9 votes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to receive supporting documentation into the record, conclude the public hearing, accept Categorical Exemption CE-16-267, and consider appeals from Douglas Otto, Rick Ivey, Naphar Isley, Troy Carmon, and Allan Stevens; Uphold the Planning Commission's decision to deny a request for a continuance by a third party at the Planning Commission hearing of March 30, 2017; and Uphold the Planning Commission's decision to approve a Conditional Use Permit for a behavioral health urgent care center located at 3200-3220 Long Beach Boulevard (Application No. 1611-08).\", \"sampled\": \"The Long Beach City Council is recommended to approve the proposed Behavioral Health Urgent Care Center, to be operated by Star's Behavioral Health Group, at 3200 Long Beach Boulevard, as presented in the Development Services report. Despite four appeals filed against the Planning Commission's decision to approve the 24/7 facility, which aims to fill a crucial care gap in the local health care system for mental health services, Development Services urges the Council to deny the appeals and approve the project.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance approving a proposed Lease Agreement between the City and County of Denver and the State of Colorado Department of Revenue, Division of Motor Vehicles, to lease office space for driver licensing and related services at the Arie P. Taylor building at 4685 Peoria Street. Approves a lease agreement with the State of Colorado Department of Motor Vehicles for $356,531.49 and for five years for approximately 2,583 square feet of office space for driver licensing and related services at the Arie P. Taylor building located at 4685 Peoria Street in Council 8 (FINAN-201841987). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 11-19-18. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 10-16-18.\", \"sampled\": \"At the Denver City Council meeting, Councilwoman Gilmore brought Accountable 18-21146 into discussion, seeking clarification on lease requirements for organizations occupying city-owned buildings. She asked Lisa Lumley, a representative from the Division of Real Estate, about the requirements for organizations leasing space in city-owned buildings. Councilwoman Gilmore asked, \\\"I had a question about this lease arrangement and just wanted to understand what are the requirements of an organization who is maybe leasing space within a city owned building?\\\" Lisa Lumley stated that all lease agreements require tenants to follow all applicable laws and codes, including city and state laws and municipal codes. Lumley also asked whether fire drills are required for all buildings. \\\"So in general, all of our leases require that our tenants adhere to all applicable laws, state laws, city laws, municipal codes, etc.,\\\" Lumley clarified.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Mental Health Center of Denver to expand the Support Team Assisted Response (STAR) program. Approves a contract with Mental Health Center of Denver for $1,391,579 and through 12-31-22 to expand the Support Team Assisted Response (STAR) program, including emergency medical technicians and behavioral health clinicians engaging those experiencing crisis related to mental health issues, poverty, homelessness, and substance abuse, from the current limited scope into a citywide program (202160835). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 3-7-22. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 10-27-21.\", \"sampled\": \"Resolution 1207, approving a $1.4 million contract with Mental Health Center of Denver to expand the STAR support team assisted response program, an alternative to armed police response for people in crisis. The contract identified Mental Health Center of Denver as the main contractor, and supported a program said to have started in the summer of 2020, with five vans expected subject to supply chain delays, seven newly hired clinicians, six newly hired EMT and medical tech staff, and an estimated 10,000 contacts. The contract included Urban Institute as third-party evaluator and a community advisory committee for oversight, input, and feedback. Councilmember Canete discussed the item, with Councilwoman Gilmore, Councilmember Ortega, Councilmember Flynn, and the Council President identified.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A bill for an ordinance amending Chapter 48 of the Denver Revised Municipal Code by adding Article IX entitled Fee on Disposable Bags. A bill for an ordinance amending Chapter 48 of the Denver Revised Municipal Code by adding Article IX entitled Fee on Disposable Plastic or Paper Bags and amending Chapter 24 by adding conforming amendments. The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 1-6-20. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 12-3-19.\", \"sampled\": \"Denver City Council meeting 12162019 addressed Council Bill 19 1176, with Madam Secretary and Councilwoman Torres referenced for the vote count and floor action. She moved that Council Bill 19 1176 be ordered published, the motion was seconded, and the courtesy public hearing on Council Bill 1176 was opened. The item concerned a fee on disposable bags, with one, two, three, four, five, six people signed up to speak. Related references included Eyes Accountable, 1212, and 1289 published.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Approves a text amendment to Article 9, Division 9.4, of the Denver Zoning Code to create the South Sloan’s Lake Design Overlay, DO-5. (NEIGHBORHOODS AND PLANNING) Approves a text amendment to Article 9, Division 9.4, of the Denver Zoning Code to create the South Sloan’s Lake Design Overlay, DO-5. A PUBLIC HEARING WILL BE HELD AT LEAST FOUR WEEKS AFTER PUBLICATION. The Committee approved filing this bill at its meeting on 11-12-14.\", \"sampled\": \"Council Bill 962 was moved for final consideration and passage as a 2014 Denver Zoning Code text amendment creating the D-O-5 design overlay in Article nine Division 9.4 for the South Sloan's Lake redevelopment at the Former St Anthony's Hospital in Council District one. Deirdre Oates presented Community Planning and Development’s staff report on the January 5th final hearing. That overlay would add consistent design standards, height transitions, a balcony exception, and later map amendment review.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A MOTION requesting that the King County office of emergency management and public health - Seattle & King County work collaboratively to update the King County Comprehensive Emergency Management Plan, the Regional Hazard Mitigation Plan, the Regional Coordination Framework, continuity of operations plans, the King County Continuity of Government Plan and all other relevant emergency management plans and documents to address the risks from and response to pandemics, incorporating lessons learned from the COVID-19 outbreak, and to develop a pandemic response plan for King County.\", \"sampled\": \"Proposed Motion 2020-0183 would request the Office of Emergency Management to update King County emergency management and disaster recovery materials for pandemic risks and request Public Health Seattle and King County to develop a pandemic response plan using lessons from the COVID 19 pandemic, addressing pandemics beyond the 2006 influenza plan and its four World Health Organization phases, with Striking Amendment 1 setting a September 1st, 2022 due date and September 1st, 2021 status report, Title Amendment 1 adopted, and recommendation pass with nine yes votes.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications No. RFP PW-14-005 and award contracts to Aetypic, Inc., of Long Beach, CA; Dougherty + Dougherty Architects, of Costa Mesa, CA; Frank Webb Architects, of Los Angeles, CA; Gwynne Pugh Urban Studio, Inc., of Santa Monica, CA; HMC Architects, of Los Angeles, CA; IBI Group, of Irvine, CA; Kardent, of Long Beach, CA; Mary McGrath Architects, of Oakland, CA; McDonald, Soutar & Paz, Inc. (MSP), of Long Beach, CA; RA-DA, of West Hollywood, CA; and Studio Pali Fekete Architects, of Culver City, CA, for as-needed architectural services, in an aggregate amount not to exceed $9,000,000 for a two-year term with the option to extend the term for three additional one-year periods; and Authorize City Manager to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contracts and to execute any necessary amendments thereto regarding scope of services, adjusting individual agreement amounts without exceeding the aggregate amount, or extending the terms, at the discretion of the City Manager.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to authorize the City Manager to award contracts for on-call architectural services. Financial Management and Public Works recommended approval of item 17, covering 11 citywide contracts with combined authority capped at $9 million for a two year term, including three local Long Beach firms, to support feasibility studies and conceptual schematic design work while larger complex projects would still use an RFP process; the contract action was described as authority to contract rather than immediate spending, with funding to be appropriated through the city budget process, and motion associated with Councilmember Austin and Councilwoman Pryce carried by a recorded nine zero vote.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A resolution approving a proposed Agreement between the City and County of Denver and Norwegian Air Shuttle ASA concerning a marketing air service development incentive at Denver International Airport. Approves a contract with Norwegian Air Shuttle ASA for a marketing Air Service Development Incentive in the amount of $570,000 and through 4-8-20 for its air service to Paris, France from Denver International Airport (201738752). The last regularly scheduled Council meeting within the 30-day review period is on 6-25-18. The Committee approved filing this item at its meeting on 5-30-18.\", \"sampled\": \"Companion Resolution 611 approval for a Denver International Airport marketing agreement tied to airline service to a new market and reimbursement of promotional-material costs. The discussed service to Paris would use a Boeing 787, the Dreamliner, with $4 million questioned for two flights a week over two years. Dr. Sussman requested confirmation that the incentive fund and marketing reimbursement were not taxpayer funded, and Angela confirmed they were Denver airport funds and not tax supported. Angela said incentives apply to new airlines, new international service, and new domestic service, with prior Lufthansa to Munich and Air Canada to Montreal uses, Edelweiss to Zurich coming online, and Councilman Espinosa requesting a last five years accounting.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to authorize City Manager, or designee, to accept $5,000 in award funds from the Institute of Museum and Library Services, for being the recipient of the 2017 National Medal for Museum and Library Service; execute any documents necessary for the administration of this award; and Increase appropriations in the General Grants Fund (SR 120) in the Library Services Department (LS) by $5,000, offset by award revenue.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to accept 5000 in award funds from the Institute of Museum and Recommendation Item 13 for Library Services, recognizing the Long Beach Public Library as one of the top five library systems through the 2017 National Medal for Museum and Library Services, with City Council acknowledgment of Vice Mayor Richardson, Long Beach Unified School District, Early Childhood Education and Safe Long, and Long Beach Public Library Foundation for family learning support.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Plans and Specifications No. ITB DV-14-078 REBID for the improvement of 14 commercial storefronts on Long Beach Boulevard at Anaheim Street; award the contract to Corral Construction and Development, Inc., of Commerce, CA, in the amount of $394,400; authorize a 15 percent contingency in the amount of $59,160, for a total contract amount not to exceed $453,560; and authorize City Manager or designee to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contract, including any necessary amendments thereto.\", \"sampled\": \"Adopt Item 12 recommendations from Development Services and Financial Management. it adopted plans and specifications for 14 commercial storefronts on Long Beach Boulevard at Anaheim Street, awarded a contract to corral construction for 394,000 with a total contract amount not to exceed 453,000, noted Councilmember Gonzalez’s comment for district one, “I just want to say thank you to development services because it's been a long time coming and it's going to be a great beautification project for the area. So thank. You.”, and carried by an eight zero vote.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to request the Economic Development Department and Long Beach Small Business Development Center to implement Elevate LB Business Academy, a pilot program to assist businesses on 4th Street and Broadway to thrive and continue to be a part of the vibrant Long Beach community; Increase appropriations in the General Fund Group in the Economic Development Department by $10,000, offset by the Second Council District One-Time District Priority funds transferred from Citywide Activities Department for the Elevate LB Business Academy; and Decrease appropriations in the General Fund Group in the Citywide Activities Department by $10,000 to offset a transfer to Economic Development Department.\", \"sampled\": \"Recommendation to request the Economic Development Department, with the Long Beach Small Business Development Center, to implement Councilmember Pearce’s Elevate LBE Business Academy pilot in the second District, add $10,000 in appropriations to the General Fund Group and Economic Development Department, and support retail establishments in remaining competitive and profitable through a four class series of about 2 hours each, after which businesses may receive mini grants tied to business license costs or create a marketing and promotion video, with staff saying, “This will be a pilot, of course,” and aiming to return in about 60 days with 20 graduates and feedback.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"meeting_summary\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Recommendation to adopt Specifications for Request for Proposals No. HR15-088 and award contracts to Centro Community Hispanic Association, Inc., of Long Beach, CA; KRA Corporation, of Fulton, MD; Long Beach Unified School District, of Long Beach, CA; and United Cambodian Community, of Long Beach, CA, to provide qualified services to operate Pacific Gateway Workforce Investment Act Youth Academy Projects, in an aggregate annual amount not to exceed $1,700,000, for a period of one year, with the option to renew for two additional one-year periods at the discretion of the City Manager; and authorize City Manager, or designee, to execute all documents necessary to enter into the contracts, including any necessary amendments thereto.\", \"sampled\": \"Approve the recommendation by Human Resources and Financial Management to award four contracts for Pacific Gateway Workforce Investment Act Youth Academy projects, with aggregate annual authority capped at $1.7 million citywide. The contracts would fund qualified providers for year-round federal youth workforce programs and Academy projects for one year,with up to two one-year renewals contingent on performance and financial management, Nick Schulz of the Workforce Investment Board addressed questions from Councilmember Urunga, Councilwoman Mango, and Councilman Austin regarding District 7 data, performance measures, contractor reporting, and selected providers including Central CHA, Long Beach Unified, UCC, and CRA Corporation of Fulton, Maryland, and staff noted 796 participants served, 100% federal funding, five applicants, and a nine zero vote.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n","data/peer_review.jsonl":"{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The paper presents an application of a tensor factorization to linear models, which allows to consider higher-order interactions between variables in classification (and regression) problems, and that maintains computational feasibility, being linear in the dimension. The factorization employed is based on the TT format, first proposed by Oseledests (2011). The authors also propose the adoption of a Riemannian optimization scheme to explicit consider the geometry of the tensor manifold, and thus speed up convergence. The paper in general is well written, it presents an interesting application of the TT tensor format for linear models (together with an application of Riemannian optimization), which in my opinion is quite interesting since it has a wide range of possible applications in different algorithms in machine learning. On the other side, I have some concerns are about the experimental part, which I consider not at the level of the rest of the paper, for instance in terms of number of experiments on real datasets, role of dropout in real datasets, comparison with other algorithms on real datasets. Moreover the authors do not take into account explicitly the problem of the choice of the rank to be used in the experiments. In general the experimental section seems a collection of preliminary experiments where different aspects have been tested by not in a organic way. I think the paper is close to a weak acceptance / weak rejection, I don't rate it as a full acceptance paper, mainly due to the non-satisfactory experiment setting. In case of extra experiments confirming the goodness of the approach, I believe the paper could have much better scores. Some minor comments: -formula 2: Obvious comment: learning the parameters of the model in (1) can be done as in (2), but also in other ways, depending on the approach you are using. -the fact that the rank is bounded by 2r, before formula 9, is explained in Lubich et al., 2015? -after formula 10: why the N projections in total they cost O(dr^2(r+N)), it should be O(Ndr^2(r+1)), no? since each of the elements of the summation has rank 1, and the cost for each of them is O(dr^2(r+TT_rank(Z)^2)), where TT-rank(Z)=1. Am I wrong? -section 6.2: can you explain why the random initialization freezes the convergence? This seems interesting but not motivated. Any guess? -section 6.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes a neural architecture for answering non-factoid questions, with experiments conducted on a Japanese love advice corpus drawn from the Oshiete goo platform, and the abstract claims 20% higher accuracy in answer creation compared to strong baselines. Determining the novelty of the contribution proved genuinely difficult throughout the review process. The model bears close resemblance to existing work, and the framing of its purpose does not align well with what the system technically accomplishes. The proposed system is described as very similar to the QA-LSTM of Tan et al., 2015, extended with additional objective terms for conclusion and supplementary sentences, yet the architecture does not depart from that prior framework in ways that would constitute a substantive technical advance at the level expected for this venue. The authors claim their model \\\"fills the gap between answer selection and generation,\\\" but no actual generation is performed, which makes this framing misleading and weakens the central motivation of the paper. The answer structure is fixed to a predefined template, meaning the model does not learn sentence ordering in any meaningful sense. The word embedding with semantics component is essentially the paragraph vector model adapted to use titles and categories rather than paragraphs, which does not represent a sufficiently distinct contribution. Reliance on a fixed template also prevents evaluation on commonly used non-factoid QA benchmarks such as InsuranceQA, limiting the comparability of the results. The examples shown in Table 4 do not demonstrate a clear quality advantage for the proposed model over QA-LSTM, which further undermines confidence in the empirical claims. The most compelling result in the paper is that the model was deployed publicly and its answers were rated twice as good as actual human contributors on the Oshiete goo platform, which is a striking real-world outcome that deserves acknowledgment and suggests the system has genuine practical utility, particularly given the difficulty of the love advice domain and the challenges of non-factoid question answering more broadly. That result is notable. It is not sufficient. The technical contributions, taken together, are not novel enough for publication at ICLR, and the mismatch between the claimed generative capability and the template-based reality of the model represents a conceptual problem that revisions to framing alone cannot resolve without substantive architectural changes or a more honest scoping of what the system actually does.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The authors present an online learning method for learning the structure of sum-product networks. The algorithm assumes Gaussian coordinate-wise marginal distributions, and learns both parameters and structure online. The parameters are updated by a recursive procedure that reweights nodes in the network that most contribute to the likelihood of the current data point. The structure learning is done by either merging independent product Gaussian nodes into multivariate leaf nodes, or creating a mixture over the two nodes when the multivariate would be too large. The fact that the dataset is scaled to some larger datasets (in terms of the number of datapoints) is promising, although the number of variables is still quite small. Current benchmarks for tractable continuous density modeling with neural networks include the NICE and Real-NVP families of models, which can be scaled to both large numbers of datapoints and variables. Intractable methods like GAN, GenMMN, VAE have the same property. The main issue with this work for the ICLR audience is the use of mainly a set of SPN-specific datasets that are not used in the deep learning generative modeling literature. The use of GenMMN as a baseline is also not a good choice to bridge the gap to the neural community, as its Parzen-window based likelihood evaluation is not really meaningful. Better ways to evaluate the likelihood through annealed importance sampling are discussed in \\\"On the Quantitative Analysis of Decoder-Based Generative Models\\\" by Wu et al. I would recommend the use of a simple VAE type model to get a lower bound on the likelihood, or something like Real-NVP. Most neural network density models are scalable to large numbers of observations as well as instances, and it is not clear that this method scales well \\\"horizontally\\\" like this. Evaluating the feasibility of modeling something like MNIST would be interesting. SPNs have the strength that not only marginal but also various type of conditional queries are tractable, but performance on this is not evaluated or compared. One interesting application could be in imputation of unknown pixels or color channels in images, for which there is not currently a high-performing tractable model. Despite the disconnect from other ICLR generative modeling literature, the algorithm here seems simple and intuitive and convincingly works better than the previous state of the art for online SPN structure learning. I think VAE is a much better baseline for continuous data than GenMMN when attempting to compare to neural network approaches. Further, the sum-product network could actually be combined with such deep latent variable models as an observation model or posterior, which could be a very powerful combination. I would like it if these SPN models were better known by the ICLR probabilistic modeling community, but I do not know if this paper does enough to make them relevant. As with the other reviewers, I am not an expert on SPNs. However, this seems to be a simple and effective algorithm for online structure induction, and the scalability aspect is something that is important in much recent work in the learning of representations. I think it is good enough for publication, although I would prefer to see many of the above additions to more clearly bridge the gap with other literature in deep generative modeling.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents a framework for unsupervised representation learning grounded in the infomax principle applied to large-scale neural populations. The core contribution centers on an asymptotic approximation to Shannon's mutual information, which is used to derive a hierarchical infomax initialization method suitable for deep architectures. The authors then apply gradient descent starting from this hierarchical infomax solution to optimize the final objective, a design choice that separates initialization from fine-tuning in a principled way. The method is claimed to function across complete, overcomplete, and undercomplete bases, which broadens its potential applicability relative to more narrowly scoped alternatives. The framework's stated connection to neuroscience is one of its more distinctive features, as the authors report that it explains phenomena that were previously unsolvable by other methods, linking the approach to Horace Barlow's efficient coding hypothesis. Direct calculation of Shannon's mutual information is generally very difficult for high-dimensional inputs, and the asymptotic approximation offered here is therefore a meaningful technical step rather than a minor convenience. Whether the approximation holds tightly enough across the range of settings the authors consider is a question the paper should address more explicitly. The claim that the Hinge loss cost function of SVM emerges as a special case of the neural population infomax framework is an ambitious one that deserves careful scrutiny, since it positions the proposed approach as subsuming a well-established method rather than merely competing with it. If this relationship is rigorously established, it would constitute a substantial theoretical contribution, but the derivation needs to be presented with enough detail that readers familiar with kernel methods can verify it independently. The paper also asserts that the framework is extendable to supervised learning and deep network training without backpropagation, which is a strong claim given how central backpropagation is to current practice in training CNNs and RNNs. The conditions under which backpropagation can be replaced, and what is sacrificed in doing so, should be stated precisely. The reported advantages in training speed and robustness relative to existing unsupervised learning methods are stated but not yet sufficiently substantiated by the evidence presented. Controlled comparisons against established baselines across multiple datasets would strengthen these claims considerably. The assertion that the method achieves good results with small data, unlike current deep networks that require large datasets, is potentially significant for settings where labeled or unlabeled data are scarce. This claim interacts with the broader argument about the method's practical utility, and the paper would benefit from experiments that isolate this property directly rather than leaving it as a stated advantage. The reviewer acknowledges that the paper contains relatively more formulas and derivations and may not be easy to follow, and this is a genuine accessibility concern that the authors should take seriously. Readers from the deep learning community who are less familiar with information-theoretic derivations in the style of computational neuroscience may find the presentation opaque at key steps. Clearer signposting of which results are foundational and which are extensions would help readers orient themselves without requiring them to trace every derivation before understanding the paper's structure. The paper was accepted, and the decision appears consistent with the scope and originality of the contributions described.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This paper introduce a variant of A3C model where while agents run on multiple cores on CPU the model computations which is the computationally intensive part is passed to the GPU. And they perform various analysis to show the gained speedup. Thanks the authors for the replying to the questions and adjusting the paper to make it more clear. It's an interesting modification the the original algorithm. And section 5 does a through analysis of gpu utilization on different configurations. The main weakness of the paper is lack of more extensive experiments in more atari domains and non atari domains, also multiple plots for multiple runs for observing the instabilities.\", \"sampled\": \"The proposed Implicit ReasoNet system demonstrates a meaningful empirical advance, outperforming all previous approaches on the FB15k benchmark by more than 5.7%, which is a non-trivial margin for a well-studied task. The mechanism driving this gain, large-scale inference conducted implicitly through a search controller and shared memory rather than directly on observed instances, is a substantive architectural choice that merits careful scrutiny. The revised paper adds Table 2 along with a corresponding paragraph examining performance across different termination steps and memory sizes, which is a useful response to earlier concerns. The condition where T_max equals 1 is particularly informative, because under that setting IRNs do not use the shared memory at all, making it a natural ablation baseline.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The paper proposes to prune a neural network by removing neurons whose operation is highly correlated with other neurons. The idea is nice and somewhat novel - most pruning methods concentrate on removal of individual weights, however I haven't done a through research on this topic. However, the experimental and theoretical justification of this method need to be improved before publication: 1. Experiments. The authors do not report accuracy degradation while pruning in the tables, laconically stating that the networks did not degrade. This is not convincing. The only details are given in Figure 5, however this Figure disagrees with Table 2: in the Table, the number of parameters ranges from 40k-600k, while the Figure pictures the range 12k-24k. Unless more details are provided, simply claiming that a network can remove 50% neurons with no number on the degradation of accuracy is not convincing. 2. Theory. The proofs do not match the experimental conditions and make unreasonable assumptions. The proofs show that in the absence of biases a network with a constant output will have two correlated neurons that generate the output offset. However, this is exactly why networks have biases and doesn't explain why noise injection helps (the proof suggests that all should be fine with deterministic auxiliary neuron). My interpretation is that the noisy output injects gradient noise.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper introduces layerwise origin-target synthesis, referred to as LOTS, as a visualization technique for intermediate feature layers in deep neural networks, addressing the well-documented vulnerability of such networks to adversarial examples produced by imperceptibly small, non-random perturbations. The proposed method is positioned to assess the invariance of learned features at any layer with respect to input class, which is a genuinely useful capability for probing network behavior. LOTS can also generate diverse adversarial examples intended for adversarial training, with the stated aim of improving model robustness and overall performance. Strengths noted. Weaknesses remain. The revised manuscript now includes quantitative results from large-scale experiments comparing LOTS against the fast gradient sign method, the fast gradient value method, and the hot/cold approach. These comparisons are welcome. The evaluation covers both the quality of produced images and the downstream effect of adversarial training samples, which provides a more complete picture of the method's practical utility than earlier versions offered. Authors also clarified the motivation for using PASS as the adversarial quality measure rather than the L-2 norm or L-inf norm, and this clarification strengthens the methodological grounding of the experimental design. Clarity improved. Concerns persist. The paper was revised and simplified to enhance readability and to accommodate the new results, and those efforts are visible in the reorganized presentation.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I like the idea the paper is exploring. Nevertheless I see some issues with the analysis: - To get a better understanding of the quality of the results, I think at least some state-of-the-art comparisons should be included (e.g. by setting d times d pixel patches too their average and applying a denoising autoencoder). If they perform significantly better, then this indicates that the presented model is not yet taking all the information from the input image that could be used. - SCT-R and FOV-R are supposed to test how much information can be restored from the Fovea alone as opposed to the Fovea together with low resolution periphery. However, there is an additional difference between the two conditions: According to the paper, in SCT-R, part of the image was set to zero, while in FOV-R it was removed alltogether. With only one or two hidden layers, I could easily imagine this making a difference. - On page 4, you compare the performance of FOV-R (1% error) with that of DS-D (1.5%) and attribute this to information about the periphery that the autoencoder extracts from the fovea. While this might be the case, at least part of the reduced error will be due to the fact that the fovea is (hopefully) perfectly reconstructed. To answer the actual question \\\"how much additional information about the periphery can be extracted from the fovea\\\", you should consider calculating the error only in the periphery, i.e. the part of the image where DS-D and FOV-R got exactly the same input for. Then any decreased error is only due to the additional fovea information. Other issues: - The images in Figure 2 (a) and (b) in the rows \\\"factor 2\\\", \\\"factor 4\\\", \\\"factor 8\\\" look very blurry. There seems some interpolation to be going on (although slighly different than the bilinear interpolation). This makes it hard to asses how much information is in these images. I think it would be much more insightfull to print them with \\\"nearest\\\" interpolation. - Figure 3 caption too vague. Maybe add something like footnote 2? - Often figures appear too early in paper which leads to lots of distance between text and figures.\", \"sampled\": \"The revised manuscript addresses several substantive concerns raised across multiple rounds of review. The updates represent a meaningful effort to strengthen both the empirical and conceptual foundations of the work. The most significant empirical addition appears in Section 4.3, where the authors present larger-scale experiments on CIFAR10 that Reviewer1 and Reviewer3 had requested. VLAE now achieves the best density estimation performance on CIFAR10 among latent-code models, outperforming PixelCNN and PixelRNN, though it remains second to PixelCNN++, and this positioning is clearly stated rather than obscured. The introduction of \\\"soft free-bits\\\" to replace the earlier \\\"free-bits\\\" technique is a genuine methodological contribution, and the authors make a reasonable case that it produces more robust optimization; they also note that soft free-bits and the DenseNet VAE architecture are not exclusive to VLAE and may benefit other VAE variants, which is an honest and useful clarification. Replacing ResNet VAEs with DenseNet VAEs demonstrably reduces overfitting, and VLAE now outperforms ResNet VAE with IAF on CIFAR10 after both changes are incorporated. Reviewer2 had asked directly, \\\"However, they never actually showed how a VAE without AF prior but that has a PixelCNN decoder performs. What would be the impact on the latent code is no AF prior is used?\\\" and the authors respond with ablation experiments in Appendix D showing that removing the autoregressive flow prior worsens performance and reduces information in the latent code. A new method encoding color information into the latent code by restricting the PixelCNN decoder to condition only on grayscale images is a concrete and testable addition. The claim that the main contribution is the analysis of why crippling the decoder is necessary, rather than merely noting that it is, remains worth scrutiny. Reviewer1 had questioned novelty by noting \\\"the necessity of 'crippling' the decoder ... has already been pointed out,\\\" and the authors' reframing around analytical depth is plausible but could be stated more precisely in the manuscript itself. The planned experiments at 64x64 resolution are not yet included, which leaves one area of the empirical scope incomplete. The authors commit to releasing all code when finished, though no timeline is given beyond a vague future version.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"All reviewers viewed the paper favourably, although there were some common criticisms. In particular, the demonstration would be more convincing on a more difficult task, and this seems like an intermediate step on the way to an end-to-end solution.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes using stochastic gradient MCMC to learn weight uncertainty in RNNs for language modeling, combining gradient noise during training with model averaging at test time to explore the parameter space.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The authors show how the hidden states of an LSTM can be normalised in order to preserve means and variances. The method’s gradient behaviour is analysed. Experimental results seem to indicate that the method compares well with similar approaches. Points 1) The writing is sloppy in parts. See at the end of the review for a non-exhaustive list. 2) The experimental results show marginal improvements, of which the the statistical significance is impossible to asses. (Not completely the author’s fault for PTB, as they partially rely on results published by others.) Weight normalisation seems to be a viable alternative in the: the performance and runtime are similar. The implementation complexity of weight norm is, however, arguably much lower. More effort could have been put in by the authors to clear that up. In the current state, practitioners as well as researchers will have to put in more effort to judge whether the proposed method is really worth it for them to replicate. 3) Section 4 is nice, and I applaud the authors for doing such an analysis. List of typos etc. - maintain -> maintain - requisits -> requisites - a LSTM -> an LSTM - \\\"The gradients of ot and ft are equivalent to equation 25.” Gradients cannot be equivalent to an equation. - “beacause\\\"-> because - One of the γx > γh at the end of page 5 is wrong.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper presents a neural sequence-to-sequence model using CVAE to learn latent variables for describing responses conditioned on dialog contexts and dialog acts, and the proposed models outperformed the RNN encoder-decoder baseline without latent variables in both quantitative and qualitative evaluations. The work is theoretically sound. The descriptions are clear, the ideas are well motivated, and the comparisons made against the baseline are reasonable. Minor concerns. Statistical significance testing should be provided for the quantitative results, since differences for some metrics do not appear large. Human evaluation would strengthen the qualitative analysis by offering a more pragmatic perspective on response quality. DA tagging performance in the kgCVAE model likely affects the quality of the final results, and this relationship deserves explicit discussion in the paper. Deep learning models have recently achieved better DA tagging performance than SVM, suggesting that the current tagging component may not yet be at its ceiling. Exploring stronger tagging approaches could benefit kgCVAE directly. Applying kgCVAE to task-oriented human-machine conversations is a promising future direction, given that such conversations typically have richer linguistic features than open-domain conversations. Task-oriented settings may provide a more demanding and informative testbed for evaluating the kgCVAE framework's capacity to exploit linguistic prior knowledge. A typo appears in Table 1.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Thank you for an interesting read. I found this paper very interesting. Since I don't think (deterministic) approximate inference is separated from the modelling procedure (cf. exact inference), it is important to allow the users to select the inference method to suit their needs and constraints. I'm not an expert of PPL, but to my knowledge this is the first package that I've seen which put more focus on compositional inference. Leveraging tensorflow is also a plus, which allows flexible computation graph design as well as parallel computation using GPUs. The only question I have is about the design of flexible objective functions to learn hyper-parameters (or in the paper those variables associated with delta q distributions). It seems hyper-parameter learning is also specified as inference, which makes sense if using MAP. However the authors also demonstrated other objective functions such as Renyi divergences, does that mean the user need to define a new class of inference method whenever they want to test an alternative loss function?\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents an interesting contribution to maximum entropy problems in deep learning. The core proposal is to learn a smooth invertible transformation that maps a simple distribution to a maximum entropy distribution, rather than optimizing over the continuous density directly, which is a meaningful reframing of the problem. The method combines stochastic optimization with the augmented Lagrangian method to solve a finite-dimensional constrained optimization, and this integration of normalizing flow networks with classical constrained optimization machinery is the technical centerpiece of the work. Applications to finance and computer vision are presented to demonstrate flexibility and accuracy, offering concrete evidence that the framework generalizes across problem domains. Reviewers did raise concerns about the novelty of the method relative to existing approaches, and those concerns deserve a direct and careful response in any revision. The paper is seen as connecting deep learning to the wider information theory community, which is a genuinely underserved bridge given how rarely maximum entropy problems are treated within deep learning contexts.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The paper describes a method to evaluate generative models such as VAE, GAN and GMMN. This is very much needed in our community where we still eyeball generated images to judge the quality of a model. However, the technical increment over the NIPS 16 paper: “Measuring the reliability of MCMC inference with bidirectional Monte Carlo” is very small, or nonexistent (but please correct me if I am wrong!). (Grosse et al). The relative contribution of this paper is the application of this method to generative models. In section 2.3 the authors seem to make a mistake. They write E[p’(x)] <= p(x) but I think they mean: E[log p’(x)] <= log E[p’(x)] = log p(x). Also, for what value of x? If p(x) is normalized it can’t be true for all values of x. Anyways, I think there are typos here and there and the equations could be more precise. On page 5 top of the page it is said that the AIS procedure can be initialized with q(z|x) instead of p(z). However, it is unclear what value of x is then picked? Is it perhaps Ep(x)[q(z|x)] ? I am confused with the use of the term overfitting (p8 bottom). Does a model A overfit relative to a another model B if the test accuracy of A is higher than that of B even though the gap between train and test accuracy is also higher for B than for A. I think not. Perhaps the last sentence on page 8 should say that VAE-50 underfits less than GMMN-50? The experimental results are interesting in that it exposes the fact that GANs and GMMNs seem to have much lover test accuracy than VAE despite the fact that their samples look great.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes Trained Ternary Quantization, a method that quantizes convolutional layer weights to 3 bits using separate positive and negative per-layer scale factors. This asymmetric scaling is a meaningful technical choice, and the paper supports it with an exhaustive performance analysis across real benchmarks that shows essentially no accuracy loss relative to full-precision baselines. On CIFAR-10, ternary ResNet-32, ResNet-44, and ResNet-56 each outperform their full-precision counterparts by 0.04%, 0.16%, and 0.36% respectively, which is a credible and reproducible demonstration of the method's competitiveness. The paper is notably free of MNIST experiments, relying instead on CIFAR-10 and ImageNet, which reflects well on the authors' judgment about what constitutes meaningful evaluation. The inclusion of power measurements is a genuine strength, since power is the primary metric for practitioners working in resource-constrained deployment. TTQ models are claimed to be nearly 16x smaller than full-precision models by representing weights with 2-bit values and per-layer scaling factors, and the TTQ AlexNet achieves a 0.3% Top-1 accuracy improvement over full-precision AlexNet on ImageNet. That result also represents a 3% improvement over previous ternary models on ImageNet. These figures are encouraging. A concern, however, is that the power measurement for the full-precision baseline model appears to be missing, which limits the reader's ability to assess the practical efficiency gain directly. A state-of-the-art result on ImageNet beyond AlexNet would have been more convincing given how central that benchmark is to the field. The paper would also benefit from a result on a strong LSTM baseline to demonstrate generality across architecture types. Discussion of wall time using the proposed training procedure is absent and should be addressed.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes a relation network (RN) to model relations between input entities such as objects. The relation network is built in two stages. First a lower-level structure analyzes a pair of input entities. All pairs of input entities are fed to this structure. Next, the output of this lower-level structure is aggregated across all input pairs via a simple sum. This is used as the input to a higher-level structure. In the basic version, these two structures are each multi-layer perceptrons (MLPs). Overall, this is an interesting approach to understanding relations among entities. The core idea is clear and well-motivated -- pooling techniques that induce invariance can be used to learn relations. The idea builds on pooling structures (e.g. spatial/temporal average/max pooling) to focus on pairwise relations. The current pairwise approach could potentially be extended to higher-order interactions, modulo scaling issues. Experiments on scene descriptions and images verify the efficacy of relation networks. The MLP baselines used are incapable of modeling the structured dependencies present in these tasks. It would be interesting to know if pooling operators (e.g. across-object max pooling in an MLP) or data augmentation via permutation would be effective for training MLPs at these tasks. Regardless, the model proposed here is novel and effective at handling relations and shows promise for higher-level reasoning tasks.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes freezing the original pretrained network and adding a new model alongside it as an augmentation-style fine-tuning approach, rather than modifying the pretrained weights directly. The core idea is interesting and the motivation is clear. The argument presented in section 3.2, supported by Figure 4, is one of the stronger contributions, as it demonstrates that fine-tuning on small data may actually hurt performance. This reviewer agrees with the authors that freezing a pretrained network and augmenting it rather than changing it is a justified design choice. Figure 1 helpfully illustrates the modular approach at a conceptual level. The experiments section, however, is not very convincing as currently presented. The empirical grounding for the central argument remains thin beyond what Figure 4 provides. Figure 3 raises a concern that some module filters may not be converging or are learning features of limited utility, particularly the first two filters visible in Figure 3(a). This observation warrants direct acknowledgment and analysis from the authors. The paper would benefit from a clearer account of when and why certain modules fail to learn meaningful representations. Without this, it is difficult to assess how reliably the proposed approach generalizes across settings. The experimental comparisons also need strengthening before the contribution can be fully evaluated.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: The authors introduce a new software package called ShapeWorld for automatically generating data for image captioning problems. The microworld used to generate the image captions is simple enough to make the data being generated and errors by a model readily interpretable. However, the authors demonstrate that configurations of the packages produce data that is challenging enough to serve as a good benchmark for ongoing research. - Weaknesses: The primary weakness of this paper is that it does look a bit like a demo paper. The authors do provides experiments that evaluate a reasonable baseline image captioning system on the data generated by ShapeWorld. However, similar experiments are included in demo papers. The paper includes a hyperlink to the software package on github that presumably unmasks the authors of the paper. - General Discussion: Scientific progress often involves some something analogous to vygotsky's zone of proximal development, whereby progress can be made more quickly if research focuses on problems with just the right level of difficulty (e.g., the use of tidigits for speech recognition research in the early 90s). This paper is exciting since it offers a simple microworld that is easy for researchers to completely comprehend but that also is just difficult enough for existing models. The strengths of the work are multiplied by the fact that the software is opensource, is readily available on github and generates the data in a format that can be easily used with models built using modern deep learning libraries (e.g., TensorFlow). The methods used by the software package to generate the artificial data are clearly explained. It is also great that the authors did experiments with different configurations of their software and a baseline image caption model in order to demonstrate the strengths and weakness of existing techniques.\", \"sampled\": \"Good paper. The paper proposes group orthogonal convolutional neural networks (GoCNN) that use privileged information to encourage low correlation between feature groups. The abstract and introduction motivate the method as a way to replace ensembles with a single diverse model, but no ensemble comparisons are provided. This omission weakens the central claim about replacing ensembles and leaves the reader uncertain whether the diversity gains translate to practical advantages over standard ensembling. But. Background feature suppression is introduced in Section 4.1 without clear connection to group orthogonality. The experiments do not decouple group orthogonality from background feature suppression, making it impossible to assess the individual contribution of each. The paper would benefit from ablation studies that isolate these two mechanisms. Full ImageNet experiments under the partial privileged information setting would be more impactful. Yet. The paper is clear and easy to follow, and experimental results show some benefit from the proposed approach. However, the normalizing factor in Definition 2 is incorrect, using one divided by c to the power of k squared, which should be corrected to avoid misleading readers about the orthogonality loss computation. Figure 1 appears to have incorrect mask placements, and this visual error could confuse the interpretation of feature group diversity. The method is presented with a compelling intuition about feature diversity, and the writing maintains clarity throughout the technical exposition. The use of privileged information to structure internal representations is a sensible direction, and the group orthogonality constraint offers a principled way to pursue that goal. The missing ensemble baseline remains the most significant gap, because the introduction frames the work as an alternative to ensembles without ever testing that framing. A direct comparison against a simple ensemble of standard CNNs would clarify whether the single-model diversity approach is competitive or merely different.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Strengths - interesting to explore the connection between ReLU DNN and simplified SFNN - small task (MNIST) is used to demonstrate the usefulness of the proposed training methods experimentally - the proposed, multi-stage training methods are simple to implement (despite lacking theoretical rigor) Weaknesses -no results are reported on real tasks with large training set -not clear exploration on the scalability of the learning methods when training data becomes larger -when the hidden layers become stochastic, the model shares uncertainty representation with deep Bayes networks or deep generative models (Deep Discriminative and Generative Models for Pattern Recognition , book chapter in “Pattern Recognition and Computer Vision”, November 2015, Download PDF). Such connections should be discussed, especially wrt the use of uncertainty representation to benefit pattern recognition.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes an interesting regularization approach. The work penalizes low entropy output distributions as a regularizer and connects the proposed confidence penalty to label smoothing via the direction of the KL divergence, yet the smoothing idea itself is not new even though it was not previously investigated across a wide range of tasks. The evaluation is broad. The paper covers 6 benchmarks including image classification, language modeling, machine translation, and speech recognition. More motivation is necessary for the proposed smoothing technique. The reviewer questions whether there is an efficient back-propagation solution for entropy smoothing through softmax, and smoothing may prevent the model from estimating the true posterior distribution. Inaccurate posterior estimates could be problematic for higher-level decisions, for example in language models for speech recognition. The paper should report state-of-the-art results for speech recognition tasks such as TIMIT and WSJ.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"In introduction: \\\"... unlike previous approaches such as in Han et al. (2015). State of the art results in speech recognition generally require between days and weeks of training time, so a further 3-4× increase in training time is undesirable.\\\" But, according to Han et al. (2015), \\\"Huffman coding doesn’t require training and is implemented offline after all the fine-tuning is finished.\\\" Both yours and Han et al. (2015) use a weight pruning technique. Intuitively, they should have similar training time for LSTM models. Where does 3-4x extra training time comes from Han et al. (2015) but doesn't have in your approach?\", \"sampled\": \"The paper adapts NMT to a noisy channel formulation using the SSNT framework, and all reviewers agree it is \\\"well written\\\" with a \\\"strong experimental section.\\\" Comprehensive results are presented and the experimental work is solid, establishing a better approach for the problem. Concerns remain about technical novelty because \\\"this paper does not present anything that is particular novel on top of the SSNT,\\\" and the work is not conceptually different from Tillmann et al. Reviewers were not completely convinced that simpler means could not achieve similar results. Simpler alternatives mentioned include clever reranking or deep speech style unprincipled combination.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Based on the feedback, I'm going to be rejecting the paper on the following grounds: 1. Results are not SOTA as reported. 2. No real experiments other than cursory experiments on Hutter prize data. 2. Writing is very poor. However, just for playing devil's advocate, to the reviewers, I would like to point out that I am in agreement with the author that dynamic evaluation is not equivalent to this method. The weights are not changed in this model, as far as I can see, for the test set. Surprisal is just an extra input to the model. I think the reviewers were puzzled by the fact that at test time, the actual sequence needs to be known. While this may be problematic for generative modeling, I do not see why this would be a problem for language modeling, where the goal of the model is only to provide a log prob to evaluate how good a sequence of text is. Long before language modeling started being used to generate text, this was the main reason to use it - in speech recognition, spelling correction etc..\", \"sampled\": \"This paper applies adversarial training to ImageNet, a larger dataset than previously considered, and it compares different adversarial training approaches with a focus on transferability. The work is clear and well written. The authors uncover and explain the label leaking effect. The paper does a good job of assessing and comparing adversarial training methods. It makes an important contribution. The empirical results are wide ranging. The paper’s core strength lies in how it elucidates the adversarial training procedure through a careful comparison of approaches, and the discovery and explanation of the label leaking effect is a notable insight that deepens our understanding of this training paradigm. The writing is clear and well structured, which makes the technical material accessible even when the experimental setup grows complex. The assessment of transferability across methods is thorough, and the range of empirical results provides a solid foundation for the claims. The authors do a good job of situating their findings within the broader landscape of adversarial machine learning, and the work advances our grasp of how adversarial training behaves at scale on a dataset like ImageNet.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper presents a way of training deep generative models with discrete hidden variables using the reparameterization trick. It then applies it to a particular DBN-like architecture, and shows that this architecture achieves state-of-the-art density modeling performance on MNIST and similar datasets. The paper is well written, and the exposition is both thorough and precise. There are several appendices which justify various design decisions in detail. I wish more papers in our field would take this degree of care with the exposition! The log-likelihood results are quite strong, especially given that most of the competitive algorithms are based on continuous latent variables. Probably the main thing missing from the experiments is some way to separate out the contributions of the architecture and the inference algorithm. (E.g., what if a comparable architecture is trained with VIMCO, or if the algorithm is applied to a previously published discrete architecture?) I’m a bit concerned about the variance of the gradients in the general formulation of the algorithm. See my comment “variance of the derivatives of F^{-1}” below. I think the response is convincing, but the problem (as well as “engineering principles” for the smoothing distribution) are probably worth pointing out in the paper itself, since the problem seems likely to occur unless the user is aware of it. (E.g., my proposal of widely separated normals would be a natural distribution to consider until one actually works through the gradients — something not commonly done in the age of autodiff frameworks.) Another concern is how many sequential operations are needed for inference in the RBM model. (Note: is this actually an RBM, or a general Boltzmann machine?) The q distribution takes the form of an autoregressive model where the variables are processed one at a time.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents an attention-based RNN model that generates SQL queries directly from natural language without relying on intermediate representations. The approach is complemented by a data augmentation strategy that iteratively collects crowd annotations using user feedback on the model-produced SQL queries. The overall framework is sound and the integration of user feedback for data collection is a notable contribution. Results on both benchmark and interactive datasets suggest that data augmentation is a promising direction. The work has future potential for semantic parsing in downstream applications. The paper claims near-state-of-the-art accuracies on ATIS and GeoQuery, but the reported results show an 8-point difference from Liang et al., 2011. The reviewer requests the authors tone down their accuracy claims to better reflect this gap. This discrepancy weakens the otherwise solid empirical foundation of the study. The reviewer expresses disappointment with the paper's claim of \\\"near-state-of-the-art accuracies\\\" on ATIS and GeoQuery, noting an 8-point difference from Liang et al., 2011. Table 4 needs clarification on the splits used for ATIS numbers, as this detail is essential for interpreting the results. Table 6 lacks a breakdown of scores by correctness and incompleteness, and the term 'minimal intervention' is ambiguous and should be clarified. The expertise required from crowd-workers to produce correct SQL queries is not specified, and no analysis is provided for the 48% of user questions that could not be generated. Figure 3 shows sharp dips in performance without paraphrasing around the 8th/9th stages, which is confusing and warrants further explanation. The paper would benefit from a clearer discussion of these fluctuations and their implications for the data augmentation pipeline. The work is sound and has future potential for semantic parsing in downstream applications, but these presentation issues need to be addressed. The authors should also release the Google SCHOLAR dataset to support reproducibility. The reviewer recommends addressing these concerns before resubmission.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This work describes a gated attention-based recurrent neural network method for reading comprehension and question answering. This method employs a self-matching attention technique to counterbalance the limited context knowledge of gated attention-based recurrent neural networks when processing passages. Finally, authors use pointer networks with signals from the question attention-based vector to predict the beginning and ending of the answer. Experimental results with the SQuAD dataset offer state-of-the-art performance compared with several recent approaches. The paper is well-written, structured and explained. As far as I know, the mathematics look also good. In my opinion, this is a very interesting work which may be useful for the question answering community. I was wondering if the authors have plans to release the code of this approach. From that perspective, I miss a bit of information about the technology used for the implementation (theano, CUDA, CuDNN...), which may be useful for readers. I would appreciate if authors could perform a test of statistical significance of the results. That would highlight even more the quality of your results. Finally, I know that the space may be a constraint, but an evaluation including some additional dataset would validate more your work.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper studies invertibility properties of deep rectified networks and introduces a pseudocode to compute preimages of half-rectified layers. The authors also discuss implications for manifold-type models of signal classes, which could offer a useful geometric perspective. However, reviewers found the paper poorly organized and the analysis incomplete, and the manuscript lacks clear theoretical, algorithmic, or experimental contributions. The area chair therefore recommends rejection of the manuscript. A related reference on ReLU invertibility is missing from the discussion. The omitted work is “Signal Recovery from Pooling Representations” by Bruna, Szlam, and Lecun, presented at ICML’14. This absence weakens the positioning of the current contribution. The paper’s treatment of piecewise linear manifolds is suggestive but remains underdeveloped. The pseudocode for preimage computation is a concrete element, yet without rigorous validation or comparative context its value is difficult to assess against existing approaches. The reviewers noted that the analysis does not cohere into a sustained argument. The manuscript would need substantial restructuring to clarify its technical narrative and to substantiate the claimed connections to signal class models.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper introduces a polynomial linear model for supervised classification tasks. The model is based on a combination of the Tensor Train (TT) tensor decomposition method and a form of stochastic Riemannian optimization. A few empirical experiments are performed that demonstrate the good performance of the proposed model relative to appropriate baselines. From a theoretical standpoint, I think the approach is interesting and elegant. The main machinery underlying this work are the TT decomposition and the geometric structure of the manifold of tensors with fixed TT-rank, which have been established in prior work. The novelty of this paper is in the combination of this machinery to form an efficient polynomial linear model. As such, I would have hoped that the paper mainly focused on the efficacy of this combination and how it is superior to obvious alternatives. For example, I would have really appreciated seeing how FMs performed when optimized over the manifold of positive definite matrices, as another reviewer mentioned. Instead, there is a bit too much effort devoted to explaining prior work. I think the empirical analysis could be substantially improved. I am particularly puzzled by the significant performance boost obtained from initializing with the ordinary logistic regression solution. I would have liked some further analysis of this effect, especially whether or not it is possible to obtain a similar performance boost with other models. Regarding the synthetic data, I think an important baseline would be against a vanilla feed forward neural network, which would help readers understand how complicated the interactions are and how difficult the dataset is to model. I agree with the previous reviewer regarding a variety of other possible improvements to the experimental section. A few typos: 'Bernoulli distrbution', 'reproduce the experiemnts', 'generilize better'. Overall, I am on the fence regarding this paper. The main idea is quite good, but insufficient attention was devoted to analyzing the aspects of the model that make it interesting and novel.\", \"sampled\": \"The manuscript offers a theoretical investigation into generative adversarial network training dynamics, but several logical and presentational issues weaken its contribution. The analysis of the discriminator’s behavior under different dimensional regimes contains a potential error in the case distinction between n≤d and n>d. The definition of perfect discriminator accuracy on sets containing the supports of P_r and P_g is not sufficiently precise. The notation P_r[D(x)=1]=1 and P_g[D(x)=0]=1 remains ambiguous without further qualification. The reviewer questions the logical coherence of the n≤d versus n>d split, as the current framing may misrepresent how the discriminator interacts with the data manifold. The claim about perfect accuracy on sets S_r and S_g that merely contain the supports requires a more rigorous justification to avoid overstatement. The notation for the discriminator’s certainty on real and generated distributions is unclear because it does not specify the measure-theoretic conditions under which these equalities hold. Theorem 2.1 introduces a distance measure that is not explicitly defined, and the origin of the delta over three factor in the proof is not explained. The definition of the ball B(x, e_x) in Theorem 2.2 lacks clarity, and the reviewer requests a precise explanation of the complement set P^c. The boundary behavior of the superset M_hat in the same theorem is questionable, as the current argument does not fully support the claimed inclusion. Theorem 2.2’s construction of M_hat as a superset of M without L depends on a ball-based argument that may fail near manifold boundaries. The missing distance measure in Theorem 2.1 makes it difficult to assess the convergence claims, and the delta over three step appears to rely on an unstated triangle inequality application. The notation P^c is introduced without definition, leaving readers to guess whether it refers to a complement in the ambient space or in a probability space. The ball B(x, e_x) needs a clear radius selection criterion tied to the local geometry of the manifold.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes a neural network architecture for car state prediction while driving based on competitive learning. Competitive learning creates several duplicates of the baseline neural architecture and during training only updates the architecture with minimum loss. The experiments compare the competitive learning approach to a single baseline architecture on a driving benchmark task. The paper is understandable but could benefit from some copy editing. The competitive learning approach seems rather adhoc and this paper feels quite incomplete without significant discussion and comparisons to ensembling. Much recent work has shown that duplicating and ensembling neural architectures can produce gains, and it’s not clear why competitive learning is better than ensembling, it seems less theoretically sound to me. There is a huge confound in the experiments due to the competitive learning architecture having many more free parameters than the baseline architecture. Again I think comparing to ensembling with the same number of architectures duplicated and perhaps comparing to a single baseline with larger hidden layers to make the total number of free parameters comparable is critical to validating the proposed approach. The graphical model of the driving process depicted in figure 1 seems nonsensical. If e is observed then all variables are known given the dependencies shown. Further, it is at best very poor notation to say that the driving action d decided at time t affects the vehicle state s at that same time. It should be that s_t depends on d_(t-1). Also, according to this figure the driving decision d does not depend on the observed vehicle state x which also seems invalid.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes relation networks for modeling pairwise object interactions, but the experimental validation raises significant concerns. The model applies a shared-weight MLP to each object pair and sums non-linear functions for prediction, yet the evaluation relies exclusively on a synthetic dataset designed to suit the proposed architecture. The title promises object and relation discovery, while objects are represented with hand-coded ground truth attributes, undermining the claimed contribution. Only trivial relationships like relative position are discovered, which limits the novelty of the findings. The work does not cite or compare to decades of computer vision research on contextual models such as MRFs, leaving the relationship to existing pairwise context models unclear. The approach is considered too simple and does not advance beyond established methods in the field. No evaluation on real scenes or standard benchmarks is provided, which weakens the empirical support for the model’s effectiveness. Robustness to noise, missing detections, or real-image attribute estimation is not demonstrated, raising doubts about practical applicability. The reviewer suggests indoor datasets including NYUv2, Sun-RGB-D, and SceneNN, as well as outdoor datasets like KITTI, for realistic evaluation. A variational autoencoder is used for scene image representation, but its integration with the relation network is not tested under challenging conditions. The absence of comparisons on these benchmarks makes it difficult to assess the model’s generalizability. The paper would benefit from experiments that address these gaps. A text-to-image coreference benchmark such as Chen et al CVPR 14 could further clarify the model’s relational reasoning capabilities. The current evidence does not support the claimed advances. The reviewer recommends including at least three real-world datasets for a thorough reassessment.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper presents a clever way of training a generative model which allows for exact inference, sampling and log likelihood evaluation. The main idea here is to make the Jacobian that comes when using the change of variables formula (from data to latents) triangular - this makes the determinant easy to calculate and hence learning possible. The paper nicely presents this core idea and a way to achieve this - by choosing special \\\"routings\\\" between the latents and data such that part of the transformation is identity and part some complex function of the input (a deep net, for example) the resulting Jacobian has a tractable structure. This routing can be cascaded to achieve even more complex transformation.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper is well written and execution appears quite accurate, and the area of learning whole graph representations is not well explored, so the proposed approach has very few competitors. The method linearizes the graph using random walks, and embedding of the central segment of each walk is computed using the skip-thought criterion. Gains reported in Table 2 are quite significant, and an anonymous public comment compared this work to node representation learning works. A natural baseline would be to pool node representations using mean- or max-pooling, yet the considered approach heavily relies on pooling. The reliance on pooling is clearly illustrated in Figure 3(c), and this observation strengthens the case for including pooling-based baselines. With more baselines, the reviewer would increase the numerical score. The skip-graph method shows promise but needs stronger comparative evaluation.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The authors proposes an interesting idea of connecting the energy-based model (descriptor) and the generator network to help each other. The samples from the generator are used as the initialization of the descriptor inference. And the revised samples from the descriptor is in turn used to update the generator as the target image. The proposed idea is interesting. However, I think the main flaw is that the advantages of having that architecture are not convincingly demonstrated in the experiments. For example, readers will expect quantative analysis on how initializing with the samples from the generator helps? Also, the only quantative experiment on the reconstruction is also compared to quite old models. Considering that the model is quite close to the model of Kim & Bengio 2016, readers would also expect a comparison to that model.\", \"sampled\": \"The proposed LR-GAN method introduces a conceptually appealing approach by generating image backgrounds and foregrounds separately and then stitching them together in a contextually relevant manner. This layered recursive design allows the model to learn foreground appearance, shape, and pose without supervision, trained end-to-end with gradient descent. The reviewer finds this foreground-background stitching strategy reasonable for cases where a clear foreground object exists. The work approaches an important problem from a new angle. Experiments indicate that LR-GAN produces more natural images with more recognizable objects compared to baseline GANs. However, real-world images often contain multiple composition layers beyond a simple foreground-background division. The reviewer questions how LR-GAN handles such images with multiple composition layers. Sizable efforts are still needed to transform LR-GAN into a generic methodology. While the method is reasonable and shows promising initial results, its current reliance on a two-layer scene model limits applicability to more complex visual scenes that do not decompose neatly into a single foreground and background.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The authors propose to sample from VAEs through a Markov chain [z_t ~ q(z|x=x_{t-1}), x_t ~ p(x|z=z_t)]. The paper uses confusing notation, oversells the novelty, ignoring some relevant previous results. The qualitative difference between regular sampling and this Gibbs chain is not very convincing, judging from the figures. It would be a great workshop paper (perhaps more), if the authors fix the notation, fix the discussion to related work, and produce more convincing (perhaps simply upscaled?) figures. Comments: - Rezende et al's (2014) original VAE paper already discusses the Markov chain, which is ignored in this paper - Notation is nonstandard / confusing. At page 1, it’s unclear what the authors mean with “p(x|z) which is approximated as q(x|z)”. - It’s also not clear what’s meant with q(z). At page 2, q(z) is called the learned distribution, while p(z) can in general also be a learned distribution. - It’s not true that it’s impossible to draw samples from q(z): one can sample x ~ q(x) from the dataset, then draw z ~ q(z|x).\", \"sampled\": \"This paper, \\\"Adaptive Feature Abstraction for Translating Video to Language,\\\" applies an image captioning architecture from Xu et al. 2015 to the task of video captioning. The proposed model is a sensible extension that incorporates attention over multiple layers of a ConvNet, rather than being restricted to a single layer. The authors demonstrate through experiments on the YouTube2Text, M-VAD, and MSR-VTT datasets that attending over multiple layers is more effective than using any single layer alone. While the work is solid and the model is adequately explained, the overall contribution is more comparable to a course project or a workshop paper. The paper unfortunately lacks sufficient technical novelty and community value to warrant publication at this venue. While the core idea is sound, the inclusion of a hard attention component feels like an unnecessary detail that does not significantly strengthen the main claims. The work could have provided more substantial value to the community with a more thorough and insightful exploration of multi-level features, moving beyond the current straightforward application and offering deeper analysis.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper introduces a new regularization term which encourages the optimizer to search for a flat local minimum of reasonably low loss instead of seeking a sharp region of a low loss. This is motivated by some empirical observations that local minima of good generalization performance tend to have flat shape. To achieve this, a regularization term based on the free local energy is proposed and the gradient of this term, which do not have tractable closed-form solution, is obtained by performing Monte Carlo estimation using SGLD sampler. In the experiments, the authors show some evidence of the flatness of good local minima, and also the performance of the proposed method in comparison to the Adam optimizer. The paper is well and clearly written. I enjoyed reading the paper. The connection to the concept of free energy in optimization framework seems interesting. The motivation of pursuing flatness is also well analyzed with a few experiments. I'm wondering if the first term in eqn. (8) is correct. I guess it should be f(x') not f(x)? Also, I'm wondering why the authors did not add the experiment results on RNN in the evaluation of the performance because char-lstm for text generation was already used for the flatness experiments.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes an algorithm for learning the structure of continuous Sum-Product Networks in a single pass, which is a notable contribution to online learning methods for this model class. The proposed algorithm is simple, elegant, and the work itself is potentially impactful for the field. However, the submission was heavily updated after the deadline, and the original version was sloppily written, reading like a rush job, which is the primary reason for my recommendation. Although I am not an expert on SPNs, the lack of polish in the submitted manuscript is a significant concern that overshadows the technical merits of the approach. The paper lacks sufficient comparison to existing literature. It states that \\\"several automated structure learning techniques have been proposed\\\" but fails to discuss the 6 works it cites. It also references 3 online parameter learning papers without comparison. The work does not compare its method to Bayesian Moment Matching from Jaini 2016. Furthermore, the results on large datasets seem underwhelming. They appear particularly weak when compared against a random structure baseline. The positive average log-likelihood values reported in Table 1 are questionable and require explanation. The authors should also clarify how parameters were learned for the random structure baseline presented in Table 3.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Hi, A sentence from Section 4 reads: \\\"We limit the vocabulary to words that are present in the Common Crawl corpus and set embeddings for out-of-vocabulary words to zero.\\\" Suppose GloVe vectors' vocabulary size is k (k ~ 2.2 million). Does this mean that the vocabulary size used in this paper is exactly 1 more than GloVe's vocabulary size (k+1)?\", \"sampled\": \"Interesting paper. This paper presents an attention-based recurrent method for one-shot learning, and I am surprised by the strong experimental results reported on the Omniglot dataset, which surpass human performance and HBPL, because the model appears to use standard neural network components and shares many of them with prior work, leaving me perplexed about the source of these gains.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Overall, this is a nice paper. Developing a unifying framework for these newer neural models is a worthwhile endeavor. However, it's unclear if the DRAGNN framework (in its current form) is a significant standalone contribution. The main idea is straightforward: use a transition system to unroll a computation graph. When you implement models in this way you can reuse code because modules can be mixed and matched. This is nice, but (in my opinion) is just good software engineering, not machine learning research. Moreover, there appears to be little incentive to use DRAGNN, as there are no 'free things' (benefits) that you get by using the framework. For example: - If you write your neuralnet in an automatic differentiation library (e.g., tensorflow or dynet) you get gradients for 'free'. - In the VW framework, there are efficiency tricks that 'the credit assignment compiler' provides for you, which would be tedious to implement on your own. There is also a variety of algorithms for training the model in a principled way (i.e., without exposure bias). I don't feel that my question about the limitations of the framework has been satisfactorily addressed.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper introduces a variant of the Neural Turing Machine that stores keys and values. The proposed model is called the Dynamic Neural Turing Machine (D-NTM). The model appears very complex, requiring many tricks and hacks to function properly. It is difficult to understand the justification for the sophisticated choices and tricks used. The description of the model is hard to follow, and the equations use non-standard notation. For instance, symbols are overloaded and written inconsistently, as seen in equations 8-9 and 10-11. The cost function is another area of concern, as it includes more than 10 different terms. This complexity is not adequately justified. The performance on the Facebook bAbI tasks is poor when compared to other memory augmented models cited in the literature. The model's performance is only slightly better than a standard LSTM, which is a significant concern given the model's complexity. The results on the \\\"babi task\\\" do not convincingly demonstrate the advantages of the proposed architecture. Reproducibility is a major issue with this work, as the reviewer believes there are not enough details provided to replicate the results. The use of non-standard notation, such as \\\"softplus\\\" in the equations, further complicates any attempt at implementation. No code was made available.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper presents an on-policy method to predict future intrinsic measurements. All the experiments are performed in the game of Doom (vizDoom to be exact), and instead of just predicting win/loss or the number of frags (score), the authors trained their model to predict (a sequence of) triplets of (health, ammunition, frags), weighted by (a sequence of) \\\"goal\\\" triplets that they provided as input. Changing the weights of the goal triplet is a way to perform/guide exploration. At test time, one can act by maximizing the long term goal only. The results are impressive, as this model won the 2016 vizDoom competition. The experimental section of the paper seems sound: - There are comparisons of DFP with A3C, DQN, and an attempt to compare with DSR (a recent similar approach from Kulkarni et al., 2016). DFP outperforms other approaches (or equal them when they reach a ceiling / optimum, as for A3C in scenario D1). - There is an ablation study that supports the thesis that all the \\\"added complexity\\\" of the paper's model is useful. Predicting intrinsic motivation (Singh et al. 2004), auxiliary variables, and forward modelling, are well-studied domains of reinforcement learning. The version that I read (December 4th revision) adequately references prior work, even if it is not completely exhaustive. A few comments (nitpicks) on the form: - Doom is described as a 3D environment, whereas it is actually a 2D environment (the height is not a discriminative/actionable dimension) presented in (fake) 3D. - The use of \\\"P\\\" in (2) (and subsequently) may be misleading as it stands for prediction but not probability (as is normally the case for P). - The double use of \\\"j\\\" (admittedly, with different fonts) in (6) may be misleading.\", \"sampled\": \"The authors have submitted a revised manuscript that has been shortened to 10 pages, excluding references and the appendix. This reduction was achieved by trimming the abstract, removing Figures 1 and 2, and deleting the experiment results for the LeNet model. The authors also added Remark 1 to address the diagonal approximation of the Hessian matrix, stating, \\\"This simplification however comes with some performance loss. We conjecture that the loss due to this approximation is small.\\\" Further cuts were made by removing discussions on the Hessian alternative and the iterative ECSQ algorithm, along with Appendices A.1 and A.4. The authors have added a clarification in Section 4.5 regarding the complexity of layer-by-layer quantization. They state that \\\"Optimizing compression ratios jointly across all layers (to maximize the overall compression ratio for all layers) requires exponential time complexity with respect to the number of layers.\\\" This new text elaborates on the exponential time complexity and provides necessary context for the choices made in the proposed methodology, strengthening the justification for their approach. The paper now includes Remark 3. This remark clarifies that the proposed Hessian-weighted methods are applicable to other models. Specifically, they can be used with models that have locally quadratic objective functions. A clarification has been added to Table 1, noting that the cited work [Han 2015] did not include an evaluation of the ResNet model, a point that was previously ambiguous and could have led to misinterpretation of the comparative results presented in the table. This is a welcome addition that improves the precision of the experimental comparison and helps situate the current work's contribution more accurately relative to prior art in the field of network quantization. The revision is satisfactory. The authors have effectively addressed the length constraints while adding important clarifications.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Review, ACL 2017, paper 256: This paper extends the line of work which models generation in dialogue as a sequence to sequence generation problem, where the past N-1 utterances (the ‘dialogue context’) are encoded into a context vector (plus potential other, hand-crafted features), which is then decoded into a response: the Nth turn in the dialogue. As it stands, such models tend to suffer from lack of diversity, specificity and local coherence in the kinds of response they tend to produce when trained over large dialogue datasets containing many topics (e.g. Cornell, Opensubtitles, Ubuntu, etc.). Rather than attempting to produce diverse responses using the decoder, e.g. through word-by-word beam search (which has been shown not to work very well, even lose crucial information about grammar and valid sequences), or via a different objective function (such as in Li et. al.’s work) the authors introduce a latent variable, z, over which a probability distribution is induced as part of the network. At prediction time, after encoding utterances 1 to k, a context z is sampled, and the decoder is greedily used to generate a response from this. The evaluation shows small improvements in BLEU scores over a vanilla seq2seq model that does not involve learning a probability distribution over contexts and sampling from this. The paper is certainly impressive from a technical point of view, i.e. in the application of deep learning methods, specifically conditioned variational auto encoders, to the problem of response generation, and its attendant difficulties in training such models. Their use of Information-Retrieval techniques to get more than one reference response is also interesting. I have some conceptual comments on the introduction and the motivations behind the work, some on the model architecture, and the evaluation which I write below in turn: Comments on the introduction and motivations…. The authors seem not fully aware of the long history of this field, and its various facets, whether from a theoretical perspective, or from an applied one. 1. “[the dialogue manager] typically takes a new utterance and the dialogue context as input, and generates discourse level decisions.” This is not accurate. Traditionally at least, the job of the dialogue manager is to select actions (dialogue acts) in a particular dialogue context. The action chosen is then passed to a separate generation module for realisation. Dialogue management is usually done in the context of task-based systems which are goal driven. The dialogue manager is to choose actions which are optimal in some sense, e.g. reach a goal (e.g. book a restaurant) in as few steps as possible. See publications from Lemon & Pietquin, 2012, Rieser, Keizer and colleagues, and various publications from Steve Young, Milica Gasic and colleagues for an overview of the large literature on Reinforcement Learning and MDP models for task-based dialogue systems. 2. The authors need to make a clear distinction between task-based, goal-oriented dialogue, and chatbots/social bots, the latter being usually no more than a language model, albeit a sophisticated one (though see Wen et. al. 2016). What is required from these two types of system is usually distinct. Whereas the former is required to complete a task, the latter is, perhaps only required to keep the user engaged. Indeed the data-driven methods that have been used to build such systems are usually very different. 3. The authors refer to ‘open-domain’ conversation. I would suggest that there is no such thing as open-domain conversation - conversation is always in the context of some activity and for doing/achieving something specific in the world. And it is this overarching goal, the overarching activity, this overarching genre, which determines the outward shape of dialogues and determines what sorts of dialogue structure are coherent. Coherence itself is activity/context-specific. Indeed a human is not capable of open-domain dialogue: if they are faced with a conversational topic or genre that they have never participated in, they would embarrass themselves with utterances that would look incoherent and out of place to others already familiar with it. (think of a random person on the street trying to follow the conversations at some coffee break at ACL).\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents one approach to product recommendation. The paper, titled SPECIALIZING JOINT REPRESENTATIONS OF PRODUCT IMAGES AND TEXT FOR THE TASK OF PRODUCT RECOMMENDATION, introduces a new method for generating representations. This method uses content2vec, which is described as a new deep architecture that merges product information to improve predictions. A key question raised by this work is, \\\"How transferable are features in deep NN?\\\" The authors state that in the normal recommendation regime, where collaborative information signals are available, their approach can be used to improve the paper's outcomes. This is particularly relevant in cases where collaborative signals might be sparse or unavailable for certain items. The authors generate a representation using content2vec, which is a novel deep architecture designed to merge product data. The paper suggests this approach focuses more on the item similarity matrix, which is given as a reason why certain other methods were not implemented. This focus on item similarity is a core aspect of the proposed model. The authors use reviews and implicit feedback to try to predict user interest based on the products they have interacted with previously. This approach focuses on item similarity. The authors note they tried to gather data from different categories in order to address the problem properly, with 7 categories mentioned. The authors mention two networks where they do not backpropagate all the way down. This is notable. The authors do not use end-to-end learning for all components. The paper mentions Elena and Flavian. Thomas Common themes are also noted. For the other two networks, the authors clarify they do not backpropagate all the way down. This is an important detail about the training process and the architecture of the content2vec model, distinguishing it from fully end-to-end systems. Thank you. The authors agree that in this case a layer does not serve the same practical purpose. For the. The paper notes a \\\"residual\\\" connection. The authors did not manage to make it work. AND TEXT FOR THE TASK OF. PRODUCT RECOMMENDATION. The authors use this to improve the paper. We feel free to come back to us if some remaining questions. This is one thing. However, the authors did not manage to make this specific component work as intended, which is an important limitation to acknowledge and discuss further in the manuscript. The use of a \\\"residual\\\" connection is mentioned, but its failure to function correctly raises questions about the model's stability or the specific implementation choices made during development. Concerning the. This is a False Reference. The authors state they use reviews and implicit feedback to try to predict the interest of a user based on products. This method, which uses content2vec, is a new deep architecture that merges product images and text for the task of specializing joint representations. This technique is applied in the case of the normal recommendation regime where a collaborative information signal is available, and the authors suggest it can be used to improve the paper. The authors note their approach focuses more on an item similarity matrix, which is why they did not implement certain other methods. This is an important distinction from some collaborative filtering techniques. The authors generate a representation using content2vec, a new deep architecture that merges product information for the task of specializing joint representations of product images and text. In the. This is a key contribution. The paper uses reviews. The authors tried to gather 10 different categories. The authors state they do not use end-to-end learning. The authors mention 3 themes shared by Elena, Flavian, and Thomas Common. We do. The authors agree a layer does not serve the same practical purpose. The paper mentions that for two networks, they do not backpropagate all the way down. This is an interesting architectural choice that warrants further explanation. The number 2 is mentioned here. The authors note they did not manage to make it work, which is an important admission regarding the \\\"residual\\\" aspect. The authors feel free to come back to them if there are remaining questions. This is a False Reference. The paper mentions 1.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: The idea to investigate the types of relations between lexical items is very interesting and challenging. The authors make a good argument why going beyond analogy testing makes sense. - Weaknesses: The paper does not justify or otherwise contextualize the choice of clustering for evaluation, rather than using a classification task, despite the fact that classification tasks are more straightforward to evaluate. No attempt is being made to explain the overall level of the results.\", \"sampled\": \"This work on the preimage of rectifier network activities presents a promising research direction, and I appreciate the authors' efforts on the topic. However, the paper is not ready for publication at this time because the results of the work are not clear. The utility of the proposed preimage construction is not obvious, and the work does not yet reach a point where it can be used to identify the underlying mechanisms of the models.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper proposes a modified DAE objective where it is the mapped representation of the corrupted input that is pushed closer to the representation of the uncorrupted input. This thus borrows from both denoising (DAE) for the stochasticity and from the contractive (CAE) auto-encoders objectives (which the paper doesn’t compare to) for the representational closeness, and as such appears rather incremental. In common with the CAE, a collapse of the representation can only be avoided by additional external constraints, such as tied weights, batch normalization or other normalization heuristics. While I appreciates that the authors added a paragraph discussing this point and the usual remediations after I had raised it in an earlier question, I think it would deserve a proper formal treatment. Note that such external constraints do not seem to arise from the information-theoretic formalism as articulated by the authors. This casts doubt regarding the validity or completeness of the proposed formal motivation as currently exposed. What the extra regularization does from an information-theoretic perspective remains unclearly articulated (e.g. interpretation of lambda strength?). On the experimental front, empirical support for the approach is very weak: few experiments on synthetic and small scale data. The modified DAE's test errors on MNIST are larger than those of Original DAE all the time expect for one precise setting of lambda, and then the original DAE performance is still within the displayed error-bar of the modified DAE.\", \"sampled\": \"The author has provided a substantive response, particularly through the new note added in Appendix B which quantifies the additive perturbations associated with a Deep network. This addition provides a valuable theoretical contribution by formally proving that the instability of a hybrid deep network is always less than that of a cascaded deep network. The author's belief that a deep network, without appropriate constraints, will not perform a contraction is a central point that is now better supported by this theoretical work. This clarification regarding the necessity of constraints for achieving contraction properties strengthens the paper's core argument about network architecture and stability. The manuscript is also improved by the inclusion of new state-of-the-art results on the STL10 dataset. A key detail is that this performance was achieved without specific fine-tuning of the architecture, which speaks to the robustness of the proposed method. A minor weakness remains the decision not to experimentally investigate a specific question, which the author attributes to the lack of straightforward software available in Lua. The new result on the STL10 dataset, achieving 77.4% accuracy, is a significant and welcome addition to the paper's empirical evidence. The author's commitment to releasing the code on github after a cleaning period, along with a separate software package scaled for Imagenet to be released on the author's website, will be a valuable resource for the community. This plan for code release greatly enhances the work's potential impact and reproducibility.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper presents a principled approach to finding flat minima. The motivation to seek such minima is due to their better generalization ability. The idea is to add to the original loss function a new term that exploits both width and depth of the objective function. In fact, the regularization term can be interpreted as Gaussian convolution of the exponentiated loss. Therefore, the introduced regularization term is essentially Gaussian smoothed version of the exponentiated loss. The smoothing obviously tends to suppress sharp minima. Overall, developing such regularization term based on thermodynamics concepts is very interesting. I have a couple of concerns that the authors may want to clarify in the rebuttal. 1. When reporting the generalization performance, the experiments report the number of epochs; showing the proposed algorithm reaches better generalization in fewer epochs than plain SGD. Is this the number of epochs it takes by line 7 of your algorithm, or it is the total number of epochs (line 3 and 7 all combined)? If the former, it is not a fair comparison. If you multiply the number of epochs of SGD (line 7) by the number iterations it takes to approximate Langevin dynamics, it seems you obtain little gain against plain SGD. 2. The proposed algorithm approximates the smoothed \\\"exponentiated\\\" loss (by smoothing I refer to convolution with the Gaussian).\", \"sampled\": \"This paper investigates the transferability of adversarial examples for deep networks, delving into transferable adversarial examples and black-box attacks. These transferable adversarial examples may severely hinder systems based on deep neural networks. Previous works have mostly studied transferability using small scale datasets, but this work presents an extensive study of transferability over large models. The authors confirm that transferability exists even with large models, though they also demonstrate it is difficult to manipulate. We study both non-targeted and targeted adversarial examples. The authors propose novel ensemble-based approaches for generating these transferable adversarial examples. Using such approaches, a large proportion of targeted adversarial examples are observed. See Figure 1. The paper could be improved by a true reference to the work of Farley and Goodfellow from 2015. For example, a reference is missing for \\\"Adversarial Perturbations of Deep Neural Networks\\\" on page 2. We also suggest the authors trim the paper. Condense Section 1.2, as Major Comments 7, 9, 13, and 15 are not critical. See Figure 2. The authors additionally demonstrate real world attacks against a vision web service and explore the geometric properties of adversarial examples. Some geometric studies are presented to help in understanding transferable adversarial examples. Finally, the authors show that adversarial examples generated using ensemble-based approaches can be used in these real world attacks, which is a significant contribution. The work also presents some geometric studies that help in understanding these examples.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper discusses recurrent networks with an update rule of the form h_{t+1} = R_x R h_{t}, where R_x is an embedding of the input x into the space of orthogonal or unitary matrices, and R is a shared orthogonal or unitary matrix. While this is an interesting model, it is by no means a *new* model: the idea of using matrices to represent input objects (and multiplication to update state) is often used in the embedding-knowledge-bases or embedding-logic literature (e.g. Using matrices to model symbolic relationships by Ilya Sutskever and Geoffrey Hinton, or Holographic Embeddings of Knowledge Graphs by Maximillian Nickel et al.). I don't think the experiments or analysis in this work add much to our understanding of it. In particular, the experiments are especially weak, consisting only of a very simplified version of the copy task (which is already very much a toy). I know several people who have played with this model in the setting of language modeling, and as the other reviewer notes, the inability of the model to forget is an actual annoyance.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper's presentation was found to be unclear and in some places incomplete, which complicated the review process. The central issue appears to be that the gradients of the variational objective with respect to the parameters phi of the variational posterior q(z, zeta) are incorrect. It is my assessment that some probabilistic dependencies were ignored during the gradient calculations presented by the author. Specifically, the gradient calculation for q(z|x) seems to only incorporate the term derived from differentiating -KL(q||p). The contribution from the likelihood term E_q[log P(x|zeta)] appears to be missing from the gradient update for q(z|x). This is a significant omission, as the likelihood term is substantially more difficult to handle than the KL term precisely because it does not factorize. Ignoring the likelihood term in this manner would effectively train q(z|x) to match the prior p(z), causing it to disregard the observation x and rendering it an ineffective recognition model. I spent a couple of days studying the paper due to its mathematical density. The concerns raised here stem from that detailed examination of the proposed methodology.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The reviewers feel that this is a well written paper on floating and fixed point representations for inference with several state of the art deep learning architectures. At the same time, in order for results to be more convincing, they recommend using 16-bit floats as a more proper baseline for comparison, and to analyze tradeoffs in overall workload speedup, i.\", \"sampled\": \"The manuscript introduces SSPNs as an extension of SPNs, enhancing expressivity with submodular energy function weights. SSPNs efficiently compute the MAP state for numerous labelings, yielding exponential parsing time improvements. Reviewers acknowledge SSPNs' model class merits and new inference algorithm but critique the lack of emphasis on parameter learning and energy evaluation.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"It seems not so fair to claim that the paper is the first \\\"loss-aware\\\" approach. The same optimization objective as BWN is used and the only difference is that the paper uses proximal Newton method instead of proximal gradient descent. It will be more convincing if the author could compare more with BWN.\", \"sampled\": \"The study showcases the effectiveness of Latent Sequence Decompositions (LSD) in speech recognition tasks, achieving a notable 12.9% Word Error Rate (WER) on the Wall Street Journal dataset. Comparatively, the character baseline WER stood at 14.8%, highlighting the improvement brought by LSD. Furthermore, combining the LSD model with a convolutional network led to a reduced WER of 9.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper on autoregressive generative models explores various extensions of PixelCNNs. The proposed changes are to replace the softmax function with a logistic mixture model, to use dropout for regularization, to use downsampling to increase receptive field size, and the introduction of particular skip connections. The authors find that this allows the PixelCNN to outperform a PixelRNN on CIFAR-10, the previous state-of-the-art model. The authors further explore the performance of PixelCNNs with smaller receptive field sizes. Review: This is a useful contribution towards better tractable image models. In particular, autoregressive models can be quite slow at test time, and the more efficient architectures described here should help with that. My main criticism regards the severe neglect of related work. Mixture models have been used a lot in autoregressive image modeling, including for multivariate conditional densities and including downsampling to increase receptive field size, albeit in a different manner: Domke (2008), Hosseini et al. (2010), Theis et al. (2012), Uria et al. (2013), Theis et al. (2015). Note that the logistic distribution is a special case of the Gaussian scale mixture (West, 1978). The main difference seems to be the integration of the density to model integers. While this is clearly a good idea and the right way forward, the authors claim but do not support that not doing this has “proved to be a problem for earlier models based on continuous distributions”.\", \"sampled\": \"The NewsQA dataset, sourced from over 10,000 CNN news articles, comprises 100,000 question-answer pairs, demanding advanced reasoning skills beyond basic textual matching or entailment recognition. This complexity is highlighted by the substantial 25.3% F1 gap between human and dataset performance, underscoring the challenge it poses even to skilled individuals. BARB model's efficiency, matching top-tier models while offering faster processing, stands out as a noteworthy advancement in the field. The ability to maintain high performance levels with improved speed is a substantial advantage for practical applications, potentially influencing future model design considerations. Notably, 86% of NewsQA questions feature answers agreed upon by at least two annotators, indicating a high level of agreement and reliability in the dataset. Additionally, the dataset includes 500 examples specifically annotated for various types of reasoning, enhancing its utility for evaluating models on diverse cognitive tasks. The availability of the NewsQA dataset for public use at datasets.maluuba.com/NewsQA contributes to its accessibility and potential impact in the research community. However, concerns regarding the dataset's small test set size and weak human evaluation underscore areas that could benefit from further refinement and validation to enhance its robustness. Authors' proposal to release NewsQA in two versions to improve usability demonstrates a proactive approach to addressing potential limitations and maximizing the dataset's effectiveness for a broader range of applications. This strategic move reflects a commitment to fostering advancements in machine comprehension research through thoughtful dataset design and distribution strategies.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper discusses ways to enforce invariance in neural networks using weight sharing. The authors formalize a way for feature functions to be invariant to a collection of relations and the main invariance studied is a “set-invariant” function, which is used in an anomaly detection setting and a point cloud classification problem. “Invariance” is, at a high level, an important issue of course, since we don’t want to spend parameters to model spurious ordering relationships, which may potentially be quite wasteful and I like the formalization of invariance presented in this paper. However, there are a few weaknesses that I feel prevent this from being a strong submission. First, the exposition is too abstract and this paper could really use a running and *concrete* example starting from the very beginning. Second, “set invariance”, which is the main type of invariance studied in the paper is defined via the author’s formalization of invariance, but is never explicitly related to what I might think of as “set invariance” — e.g. to permutations of input or output dimensions. Explicitly defining set invariance in some other way, then relating it to the “structural invariance” formulation may be a better way to explain things. It is never made clear, for example, why Figure 1(b) is *the* set data-structure. I like the discussion of compositionality of structures (one question I have here is: are the resulting compositional structures are still valid as structures?). But the authors have ignored the other kind of compositionality that is important to neural networks — specifically that relating the proposed notion of invariance to function composition seems important.\", \"sampled\": \"The authors introduce a novel method for estimating maximum entropy distributions. This pioneering approach leverages normalizing flow networks to convert samples from a manageable density function. The learning of normalizing flow network parameters centers on maximizing a stochastic entropy estimate, marking a significant advancement in stochastic optimization techniques. To approximate constraints on sample expectations effectively, the method relies on sampling, showcasing a pragmatic and resourceful approach to handling complex optimization challenges. The utilization of the augmented Lagrangian method to solve the optimization problem underscores a thoughtful and mathematically robust strategy in addressing maximum entropy distribution estimation. In validating the method, the authors conduct experiments on both toy problems featuring the Dirichlet distribution and real-world financial datasets. This dual validation approach ensures the method's versatility and applicability across different problem domains, enhancing its practical relevance and impact. The paper's technical soundness and clear exposition contribute to its accessibility and scholarly value. The meticulous experimental comparisons demonstrate that the proposed method can surpass Gibbs sampling in performance or achieve comparable results, highlighting its competitiveness and efficacy in practice. While the methodological contributions are primarily centered on the fusion of normalizing flow networks with the augmented Lagrangian method, the results significantly advance the field's understanding of maximum entropy distributions. However, the authors acknowledge the need for further exploration in more challenging settings to fully elucidate the method's capabilities and limitations. The authors adeptly address concerns regarding the simplicity of the problem instances considered, emphasizing the importance of extending the method's applicability to more intricate scenarios. This forward-looking perspective showcases a commitment to advancing the method's robustness and broadening its scope of application within the scientific community.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper presents a variational inference based method for learning nonlinear dynamical systems. Unlike the deep Kalman filter, the proposed method learns a state space model, which forces the latent state to maintain all of the information relevant to predictions, rather than leaving it implicit in the observations. Experiments show the proposed method is better able to learn meaningful representations of sequence data. The proposed DVBF is well motivated, and for the most part the presentation is clear. The experiments show interesting results on illustrative toy examples. I think the contribution is interesting and potentially useful, so I’d recommend acceptance. The SVAE method of Johnson et al. (2016) deserves more discussion than the two sentences devoted to it, since the method seems pretty closely related. Like the DVBF, the SVAE imposes a Markovianity assumption, and it is able to handle similar kinds of problems. From what I understand, the most important algorithmic difference is that the SVAE q network predicts potentials, whereas the DVBF q network predicts innovations. What are the tradeoffs between the two? Section 2.2 says they do the latter in the interest of solving control-related tasks, but I’m not clear why this follows. Is there a reason SVAEs don’t meet all the desiderata mentioned at the end of the Introduction?\", \"sampled\": \"The proposed DRL-Sense model demonstrates a marginal enhancement when tested on the SCWS dataset. This initial result indicates a potential capability to address sense ambiguity in word representations. In contrast, the DRL-Sense model exhibits a substantial performance boost on the ESL-50 and RD-300 datasets. This improvement underscores the model's effectiveness in handling various benchmarks related to sense disambiguation. The DRL-Sense model distinguishes itself by focusing on pure sense-level representation learning and employing a linear time sense selection mechanism. This approach marks a departure from traditional methods and shows promise in refining word representations. Moreover, the model attains state-of-the-art performance in capturing contextual word similarities, suggesting a strong competency in understanding the nuances of word usage within different contexts. Despite achieving competitive results comparable to Google's word2vec, the DRL-Sense model may face challenges in accurately estimating the probability of sense due to potential underestimation issues in the optimization process. It is crucial for the authors to elucidate the connection between Q-learning and the DRL-Sense model to enhance the understanding of the model's theoretical underpinnings and its relationship to established reinforcement learning concepts. Furthermore, the omission of negative sampling in the DRL-Sense model raises concerns about the effectiveness of sense embeddings learning. Addressing this omission could strengthen the model's overall performance and reliability in capturing sense representations accurately.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The paper explores the use of probabilistic models (gaussian processes) to regress on the target variable of post-editing time/rates for quality estimation of MT output. The paper is well structured with a clear introduction that highlights the problem of QE point estimates in real-world applications. I especially liked the description of the different asymmetric risk scenarios and how they entail different estimators. For readers familiar with GPs the paper spends quite some space to reflect them, but I think it is worth the effort to introduce these concepts to the reader. The GP approach and the choices for kernels and using warping are explained very clearly and are easy to follow. In general the research questions that are to be answered by this paper are interesting and well phrased. However, I do have some questions/suggestions about the Results and Discussion sections for Intrinsic Uncertainty Evaluation: - Why were post-editing rates chosen over prediction (H)TER? TER is a common value to predict in QE research and it would have been nice to justify the choice made in the paper. - Section 3.2: I don't understand the first paragraph at all: What exactly is the trend you see for fr-en & en-de that you do not see for en-es? NLL and NLPD 'drastically' decrease with warped GPs for all three datasets. - The paper indeed states that it does not want to advance state-of-the-art (given that they use only the standard 17 baseline features), but it would have been nice to show another point estimate model from existing work in the result tables, to get a sense of the overall quality of the models.\", \"sampled\": \"The Neural Answer Construction Model enhances answer selection accuracy by integrating semantics biases into word embeddings. It innovatively employs biLSTM to refine the synthesis of answer sentences. This strategy results in a significant 20% relative improvement in answer creation accuracy compared to the baseline model. The model's practical application extends to providing love advice services on the Oshiete goo platform. Additionally, the paper delves into various ablations of the proposed model, demonstrating a commitment to thorough experimentation. The Neural Answer Construction Model, building on the foundation laid by Tan et al., incorporates an attention mechanism, resembling Tan et al.'s 2016 approach. Furthermore, the model's flexibility shines through as it excels in generating answers beyond the confines of traditional QA site questions. The study offers valuable insights into the performance enhancements of different components within the model, enriching the understanding of its inner workings. These contributions position the proposed model as a robust and adaptable solution in the realm of answer construction models. The Neural Answer Construction Model stands out for its ability to elevate answer creation accuracy by 20% relative to the baseline model. This represents a substantial 6% absolute improvement, underlining the model's effectiveness in optimizing answer generation processes. The model's success in surpassing the baseline by a considerable margin underscores its prowess in advancing the field of deep learning. Moreover, the model's application in love advice services showcases its practical utility and real-world relevance. The paper meticulously evaluates the proposed model against various benchmarks, shedding light on its superior performance in answer creation accuracy. By incorporating the attention mechanism inspired by Tan et al., the model demonstrates a keen focus on leveraging proven methodologies for enhanced results. The meticulous experimentation and detailed analysis presented in the paper underscore the model's robustness and its potential to drive advancements in non-factoid question answering systems.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"* Brief Summary: This paper explores an extension of multiplicative RNNs to the LSTM type of models. The resulting proposal is very similar to [1]. Authors show experimental results on character-level language modeling tasks. In general, I think the paper is well-written and the explanations are quite clear. * Criticisms: - In terms of contributions, the paper is weak. The motivation makes sense, however, very similar work has been done in [1] and already an extension over [2]. Because of that this paper mainly stands as an application paper. - The results are encouraging. On the other hand, they are still behind the state of art without using dynamic evaluation. - There are some non-standard choices on modifications on the standard algorithms, such as \\\"l\\\" parameter of RMSProp and multiplying output gate before the nonlinearity. - The experimental results are only limited to character-level language modeling only. * An Overview of the Review: Pros: - A simple modification that seems to reasonably well in practice. - Well-written. Cons: - Lack of good enough experimental results. - Not enough contributions (almost trivial extension over existing algorithms). - Non-standard modifications over the existing algorithms. [1] Wu Y, Zhang S, Zhang Y, Bengio Y, Salakhutdinov RR. On multiplicative integration with recurrent neural networks. InAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems 2016 (pp. 2856-2864). [2] Sutskever I, Martens J, Hinton GE.\", \"sampled\": \"The introduction presents LipNet as the pioneering end-to-end model for sentence-level lipreading. It emphasizes LipNet's groundbreaking achievement in this domain. LipNet, a model developed by Wand, Chung, and Zisserman, stands out for its exceptional 95.2% accuracy in sentence-level lipreading on the GRID corpus. This numerical achievement underscores the model's high performance in practical applications. The background information provided highlights LipNet's utilization of spatiotemporal convolutions and a recurrent network, showcasing the model's sophisticated architecture designed for lipreading tasks. Moreover, LipNet's utilization of connectionist temporal classification loss demonstrates the model's strategic approach to optimizing training and performance outcomes in lipreading applications. Traditional audio-visual automatic speech recognition (AV-ASR) systems exhibit a unique characteristic where they can simplify to pure lip reading under specific circumstances, shedding light on the underlying mechanisms of lipreading processes. The study by Easton and Basala in 1982 offers historical insights into the evolution of research in lipreading, setting a foundation for contemporary advancements such as LipNet's pioneering work in end-to-end learning for lipreading tasks. End-to-end training with deep neural networks emerges as a pivotal advancement in the field, providing substantial performance improvements over conventional systems, as exemplified by LipNet's success in sentence-level lipreading tasks. Furthermore, the observation that human lipreading performance enhances with longer words suggests a correlation between word complexity and the cognitive processes involved in lipreading, offering valuable insights for future research in the field.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The generations in this paper suggest that using extra information from features of the discriminator allows the generator to produce images with more object like features. I have some questions/comments: 1) In equation 5 it appears that you are training r to reconstruct a corrupted version of the features, rather than the features themselves, the reason for this is not clear? || C(phi(x)) - r(C(phi(x))) || rather than || phi(x) - r(C(phi(x))) || 2) This approach involves training 3 networks. It would be interesting to know what kind of training scheme was used? Whether, D,G and r networks are trained for one iteration each, or if some networks are trained for more iterations before updating the next network? 3) It would also be interesting to know whether parameters l_denoise and l_adv are fixed or adjusted during training?\", \"sampled\": \"The paper introduces a method for collaboratively naturalizing a core programming language, a significant contribution to the field of interactive learning and natural language interfaces. Users engage in incremental syntax expansion interactively, enhancing the language's flexibility and usability. This approach, focusing on intuitive definitions using literals, offers a novel perspective on language development, promoting user engagement and comprehension. A key aspect of the method involves inducing a grammar incrementally to generate candidate parses, enabling efficient language evolution. By allowing users to teach a common system a diverse language, the method demonstrates adaptability and inclusivity in language learning. The high transition rate from the core language to the naturalized language, observed in 85.9% of the last 10K utterances, signifies the method's effectiveness in facilitating user adoption and proficiency.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Overall I think this is an interesting paper which shows empirical performance improvement over baselines. However, my main concern with the paper is regarding its technical depth, as the gist of the paper can be summarized as the following: instead of keeping the batch norm mean and bias estimation over the whole model, estimate them on a per-domain basis. I am not sure if this is novel, as this is a natural extension of the original batch normalization paper. Overall I think this paper is more fit as a short workshop presentation rather than a full conference paper. Detailed comments: Section 3.1: I respectfully disagree that the core idea of BN is to align the distribution of training data. It does this as a side effect, but the major purpose of BN is to properly control the scale of the gradient so we can train very deep models without the problem of vanishing gradients. It is plausible that intermediate features from different datasets naturally show as different groups in a t-SNE embedding. This is not the particular feature of batch normalization: visualizing a set of intermediate features with AlexNet and one gets the same results. So the premise in section 3.1 is not accurate. Section 3.3: I have the same concern as the other reviewer. It seems to be quite detatched from the general idea of AdaBN. Equation 2 presents an obvious argument that the combined BN-fully_connected layer forms a linear transform, which is true in the original BN case and in this case as well. I do not think it adds much theoretical depth to the paper. (In general the novelty of this paper seems low) Experiments: - section 4.3.\", \"sampled\": \"UDepLambda outperforms strong baselines across languages and datasets, showcasing its robust performance. This achievement positions UDepLambda as a notable advancement in semantic parsing research. Achieving the strongest result on GraphQuestions for English underscores UDepLambda's efficacy in tackling complex linguistic tasks. This success highlights its potential for practical applications requiring high-level semantic understanding. UDepLambda's provision of German and Spanish translations for WebQuestions and GraphQuestions datasets demonstrates its versatility and potential for cross-lingual applications. This broadens the tool's utility and accessibility across different linguistic contexts. The logical form representation in UDepLambda, incorporating lambda operators and dropped arguments, shows a sophisticated approach to semantic parsing. This design choice contributes to the tool's ability to handle complex linguistic structures effectively. UDepLambda's shortcomings in handling quantification, numbers, reference, and other linguistic elements indicate areas for future improvement. Addressing these limitations could enhance the tool's overall performance and applicability in a wider range of scenarios. The need for improvement in UDepLambda's logical form representation's semantic aspects suggests avenues for refining its underlying language understanding capabilities. Enhancing the tool's semantic modeling could lead to more nuanced and accurate semantic parsing results. The cleaner and simpler conversion procedure in UDepLambda compared to its predecessor based on Stanford Dependencies reflects advancements in the tool's design and usability. This streamlined process may contribute to improved efficiency and user experience. Submitting non-neural work to ACL amidst the prevalence of neural-based submissions showcases the authors' confidence in the effectiveness of their approach. This decision highlights a strategic choice to contribute diverse perspectives to the academic discourse in semantic parsing.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This one is a tough call, because I do think that there are some important, salvageable technial results in here (notably the parsing algorithm), but the paper as a whole has very little cohesion. It is united around an overarching view of formal languages in which a language being \\\"probabilistic\\\" or not is treated as a formal property of the same variety as being closed under intersection or not. In my opinion, what it means for a formal language to be probabilistic in this view has not been considered with sufficient rigor for this viewpoint to be compelling. I should note, by the way, that the value of the formal results provided mostly does not depend on the flimsiness of the overarching story. So what we have here is not bad research, but a badly written paper. This needs more work. I find it particulary puzzling that the organization of the paper leaves so little space for elucidating the parsing result that soundness and completeness are relegated to a continuation of the paper in the form of supplementary notes. I also find the mention of probabilistic languages in the title of the paper to be very disingenuous --- there is in fact no probabilistic reasoning in this submission. The sigificance of the intersection-closure result of section 3 is also being somewhat overstated, I think. Unless there is something I'm not understanding about the restrictions on the right-hand sides of rules (in which case, please elaborate), this is merely a matter of folding a finite intersection into the set of non-terminal labels.\", \"sampled\": \"The review presents an insightful analysis of the PixelVAE model, highlighting its unique combination of variational autoencoder and autoregressive density modeling. This fusion offers a promising approach to image modeling, addressing both global and local structures effectively. The authors' innovative strategy to leverage VAE for global structure and PixelCNN decoder for local details demonstrates a sophisticated understanding of unsupervised learning principles in the context of image generation. The comparative evaluation of PixelVAE against PixelCNN showcases its ability to achieve competitive Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) performance with a more streamlined decoder architecture. This finding not only underscores the efficiency of PixelVAE but also suggests potential advancements in model optimization and computational resource utilization. The study's emphasis on performance parity with a shallower decoder enhances the model's practical appeal for real-world applications requiring computational efficiency without compromising quality. Furthermore, the review notes the authors' proactive stance on addressing key research directions, particularly in disentangling high-level factors within the model's latent space. By proposing experiments to enhance the separation of latent variables, the authors exhibit a commitment to advancing the interpretability and controllability of the PixelVAE model. This forward-thinking approach contributes significantly to the broader research discourse on unsupervised learning methodologies and their implications for practical applications. The review offers constructive suggestions for future enhancements, such as detailing the model's architecture comprehensively and considering open-sourcing the code for greater transparency and reproducibility. Additionally, the recommendation to include MNIST samples for comparative analysis with PixelCNN and PixelVAE could enrich the study's empirical validation and provide a more comprehensive understanding of the models' performance across different datasets.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The paper proposes to use the very standard SVGB in a sequential setting like several previous works did. However, they proposes to have a clear state space constraints similar to Linear Gaussian Models: Markovian latent space and conditional independence of observed variables given the latent variables. However the model is in this case non-linear. These assumptions are well motivated by the goal of having meaningful latent variables. The experiments are interesting but I'm still not completely convinced by the regression results in Figure 3, namely that one could obtain the angle and velocity from the state but using a function more powerful than a linear function. Also, why isn't the model from (Watter et al., 2015) not included ? After rereading I'm not sure I understand why the coordinates should be combined in a 3x3 checkerboard as said in Figure 5a. Then paper is well motivated and the resulting model is novel enough, the bouncing ball experiment is not quite convincing, especially in prediction, as the problem is fully determined by its initial velocity and position.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"Recursive Regression with Neural Networks: Approximating the HJI PDE Solution\\\" introduces an algorithm that offers an approximation to the solution of the Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs PDE. This is an intriguing approach, integrating concepts from machine learning, control theory, and robotics. However, the paper was not accepted, which could be due to several concerns raised during the review. One concern is the need for a new network for each new domain, system function, or boundary condition. This raises questions about the scalability and practicality of the approach. Moreover, there are questions about the sensitivity of the network's result to domains of different sizes. The paper tested the algorithm on a domain size of 51 x 51, but it is unclear how results might vary with different domain sizes. As a reviewer, I must admit my unfamiliarity with the Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs PDE. This could be a potential barrier for many readers, and therefore, I suggest that the authors prepare an appendix introducing the HJI PDE.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We thank AnonReviewer4 for the input. We respond to your points individually: First, you make an interesting connection with the KLD, but that is not quite applicable here: our formulation has c_k(E T_k)^2 instead of c_k E(T_k ^2), which can't be cast straightforwardly in a VI framework. Having this term is useful because: + The true optimum has log density sum eta_i T_i (eta is as in equation 2), with no c_i T_i^2 term. If we didn't have the last term, we would be minimizing the KLD to a log density sum lambda_i T_i, which would only recover the maximum entropy distribution if lambda actually corresponded to eta. Like we mentioned on the paper, computing eta is computationally intractable. + Having this term enables the use of the augmented Lagrangian method, giving us the theoretical guarantees discussed on our previous answers, which ensures that we recover the maximum entropy distribution (up to regularity and expressivity of the model class, as discussed). Second, you commented about novelty in the paper. We respectfully disagree. For example, the above KLD example is in fact different than suggested, so the MEFN is not the typical variational inference setup. Further, we think the introduction of the ME problem is novel to the deep learning community, and conversely, we have introduced deep learning to the ME problem, a literature in which there is substantial need for estimation and sampling techniques. Mechanically, constrained optimization is rarely used in the deep learning community, and we introduce new steps there. Finally, per your and others’ requests, we think our new expanded results section adds novelty and state of the art performance to those problem domains (see below). Third, you asked for more complex data. Thank you. We have significantly addressed this concern in the new version of the paper (see updated pdf). Section 4.3 now details a texture synthesis problem, and demonstrates that the MEFN performs consistently with state of the art in terms of matching texture-net moment constraints, all while outperforming substantially in terms of sample variability.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper titled \\\"Morph-fitting: Fine-Tuning Word Vector Spaces with Simple Language-Specific Rules\\\" addresses the challenge of improving word vector spaces. In particular, it introduces a method to enhance the representation of low-frequency inflections of words. Morph-fitting is an innovative approach that mitigates the tendency of corpus-based word embeddings to assign similar representations to antonyms. This method, based on simple manually-constructed morphological rules, represents a significant departure from other language understanding systems. Faruqui et al., 2015, for instance, did not specifically target antonym representation in their work. The method is built upon the foundation laid by Wieting 2015, but makes a critical advancement in addressing language-specific rules in the morphological landscape. In comparison to alternative approaches such as that by Soricut and Och, 2015, Morph-fitting uniquely addresses the issue of low-frequency inflections and their representations in word vector spaces. The efficacy of Morph-fitting extends beyond the realm of theoretical development. It has shown practical performance improvements in multiple languages including English, German, Italian and Russian. This multi-language enhancement speaks to the scalability and versatility of this method. The method's performance was evaluated using Morph-SimLex, a dataset derived from SimLex999. The use of this evaluation tool provides a solid ground for measuring the effectiveness of Morph-fitting. The Morph-SimLex dataset, in particular, offers a robust platform for evaluating morphological rules in word vector spaces. The paper's results reveal Morph-fitting's strength in elevating language understanding systems. Specifically, the method yielded new state-of-the-art results for German and Italian on an extrinsic task - dialogue state tracking. This is a testament to Morph-fitting's potential in driving advancements in dialogue state tracking and similar applications. In conclusion, the paper presents a significant contribution to the field of morph-fitting in word vector spaces. The method's ability to enhance the representation of low-frequency inflections and its success in multiple languages, particularly in German and Italian dialogue state tracking, underscores its potential for future applications. The paper's comparison with previous works like Faruqui et al., 2015 and Soricut and Och, 2015, further contextualizes its novel approach within the broader landscape of language understanding systems.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The paper introduces a variation to the CNN-based texture synthesis procedure of Gatys et al. that matches correlations between spatially shifted feature responses in addition to the correlations between feature responses at the same position in the feature maps. The paper claims that this a) improves texture synthesis for textures with long-range regular structures, that are not preserved with the Gatys et al. method b) improves performance on texture inpainting tasks compared to the Gatys et al. method c) improves results in season transfer when combined with the style transfer method by Gatys et al. Furthermore the paper shows that d) by matching correlations between spatially flipped feature maps, symmetry properties around the flipping axis can be preserved. I agree with claim a). However, the generated textures still have some issues such as greyish regions so the problem is not solved. Additionally, the procedure proposed is very costly which makes an already slow texture synthesis method substantially slower. For example, in comparison, the concurrent work by Liu et al.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"Frustratingly Short Attention Spans in Neural Language Modeling\\\" presents a novel approach to neural language modeling by proposing a key-value attention mechanism. This mechanism generates separate representations for the key and value of a memory, a distinction that sets it apart from other memory-augmented neural language models. The results indicate that this model outperforms its contemporaries on two different corpora, showcasing its potential effectiveness in application. However, the model appears to rely primarily on the past memory of only the previous five representations, which may limit its scope and capacity for broader understanding. Interestingly, the paper notes that a simpler model, which utilizes a concatenation of output representations from the previous three-time steps, performs in a similar manner. This suggests that the proposed model's complexity may not be a key factor in its performance. Regarding the related work section, the reviewer suggests adding references to the works of Ba et al., Reed & de Freitas, and Gulcehre et al.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This paper presents a linear pipeline All-reduce approach for parallel neural networks on multiple GPU. The paper provides both theoretical analysis and experiments. Overall, the results presented in the paper are interesting, but the writing can be improved. Comments: - The authors compare their proposed approach with several alternative approaches and demonstrate strong performance of the proposed approaches. But it is unclear if the improvement is from the proposed approach or from the implementation. - The paper is not easy to follow and the writing can be improved in many place (aside from typos and missing references). Specifically, the authors should provide more intuitions of the proposed approach in the introduction and in Section 3. - The proposition and the analysis in Section 3.2 do not suggest the communication cost of linear pipeline is approximately 2x and log p faster than BE and MST, respectively, as claimed in many places in the paper. Instead, it suggests LP *cannot* be faster than these methods by 2x and log p times. More specifically, Eq (2) shows T_broadcase_BE/ T_broadcase_LP 1 when n approaches infinity. - It would be interesting to emphasize more on the differences between designing parallel algorithms on CPU v.s. on GPU to motivate the paper.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper titled \\\"Pruning Filters for Efficient ConvNets\\\" presents a method for pruning low-weight filters from ConvNets. The primary aim is to reduce FLOPs and memory consumption, enhancing the efficiency of CNNs. The novel idea and good experimental results led to the acceptance of the paper. Intriguingly, the method proposed in the paper creates structured sparsity. This feature allows for an improvement in performance without necessitating any changes to the convolution implementation. Thus, it's not just about efficiency, but also about maintaining the operational integrity of the ConvNets. Moreover, this method is notable for its simplicity. It does not require complex algorithms or changes to existing technology, such as BLAS libraries. The simplicity of the approach makes it a practical option for improving the efficiency of CNNs in real-world applications. The paper provides evidence of its method's effectiveness by testing it on VGG-16 and ResNets using the CIFAR10 and ImageNet datasets. The results were impressive, with a 34% reduction in inference costs for VGG-16 and a 38% reduction for ResNet-110. These numbers clearly demonstrate the potential of the proposed method for significantly reducing computation costs and parameter storage. However, the paper does not evaluate the impact of filter pruning on transfer learning. Given the importance of transfer learning in the field of machine learning and the increasing use of CNNs in this area, it would have been beneficial to include an evaluation of this aspect.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"EDIT: Updated score. See additional comment. I quite like the main idea of the paper, which is based on the observation in Sec. 3.0 - that the authors find many predictable patterns in the independent evolution of weights during neural network training. It is very encouraging that a simple neural network can be used to speed up training by directly predicting weights. However the technical quality of the current paper leaves much to be desired, and I encourage the authors to do more rigorous analysis of the approach. Here are some concrete suggestions: - The findings in Section 3.0 which motivate the approach, should be clearly presented in the paper. Presently they are stated as anecdotes. - A central issue with the paper is that the training of the Introspection network I is completely glossed over. How well did the training work, in terms of training, validation/test losses? How well does it need to work in order to be useful for speeding up training? These are important questions for anyone interested in this approach. - An additional important issue is that of baselines. Would a simple linear/quadratic model also work instead of a neural network? What about a simple heuristic rule to increase/decrease weights? I think it's important to compare to such baselines to understand the complexity of the weight evolution learned by the neural network. - I do not think that default tensorflow example hyperparameters should be used, as mentioned by authors on OpenReview. There is no scientific basis for using them. Instead, first hyperparameters which produce good results in a reasonable time should be selected as the baseline, and then added the benefit of the introspection network to speed up training (and reaching a similar result) should be shown. - The authors state in the discussion on OpenReview that they also tried RNNs as the introspection network but it didn't work with small state size. What does \\\"didn't work\\\" mean in this context? Did it underfit? I find it hard to imagine that a large state size would be required for this task.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes a new model, Simplified-SFNN. The authors introduce this model as an intermediate step in training stochastic feedforward neural networks (SFNNs). It can be built upon any baseline deterministic deep neural network (DNN). The training process proposed in the paper is divided into three steps: pretraining a DNN, transferring weights to a simplified SFNN and continuing training, and optionally transferring the weights to a full SFNN or a deterministic model (DNN*) and continuing training. This approach aims to address the challenges associated with training SFNNs. The authors also establish a connection between DNN, Simplified-SFNN, and SFNN. This relationship, combined with the training process, sets the stage for the experiments conducted in the study. A key aspect of the paper is the rescaling of unit inputs and layer weights for performing the transfer. A stochastic model built from the wide residual network had 28 layers and 36 million parameters, demonstrating the potential complexity of these models. The authors provide background context on the use of various models and datasets. Models such as LeNet-5, network-in-network, and wide residual network were used in the experiments. Datasets including MNIST, TFD, CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and SVHN were utilized to test the effectiveness of the Simplified-SFNN. The use of these diverse datasets and models helps to provide a broad scope for evaluation. The paper also shows how popular DNNs can be effectively transferred to the corresponding stochastic models for both multi-modal and classification tasks. This demonstrates the versatility of the Simplified-SFNN and its potential applications in various domains. The authors argue that the use of simplified SFNN training can improve a deterministic DNN* model over a DNN baseline trained with batch normalization and dropout. However, this claim could benefit from further substantiation through more comprehensive experimental results. The paper's results on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and SVHN would be more convincing if the baselines used dropout and batch normalization. In the absence of this, the authors' claim that the Simplified-SFNN can improve a deterministic DNN* model over a DNN baseline trained with batch normalization and dropout may seem less robust. In conclusion, the authors propose a promising approach for training SFNNs using a Simplified-SFNN.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: This paper presents an extension of many popular methods for learning vector representations of text. The original methods, such as skip-gram with negative sampling, Glove, or other PMI based approaches currently use word cooccurrence statistics, but all of those approaches could be extended to n-gram based statistics. N-gram based statistics would increase the complexity of every algorithm because both the vocabulary of the embeddings and the context space would be many times larger. This paper presents a method to learn embeddings for ngrams with ngram context, and efficiently computes these embeddings. On similarity and analogy tasks, they present strong results. - Weaknesses: I would have loved to see some experiments on real tasks where these embeddings are used as input beyond the experiments presented in the paper. That would have made the paper far stronger. - General Discussion: Even with the aforementioned weakness, I think this is a nice paper to have at ACL. I have read the author response.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper, \\\"Paying More Attention to Attention: Improving the Performance of Convolutional Neural Networks via Attention Transfer\\\", puts forth a novel method of transferring knowledge. This is a method that brings a fresh perspective into the field of Convolutional Neural Networks and Artificial Neural Networks. The approach taken here involves a change in the traditional method; instead of transferring activations, this paper proposes to transfer attention maps. This represents an intriguing shift in approach which could potentially open up new avenues in the field of Computer Vision and NLP. However, the approach as it stands now leaves room for further enhancements. The primary concern here lies in the experimental results. The experiments conducted to test the effectiveness of this new method do not show a significant improvement when compared to the already established technique of knowledge distillation.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This paper divides the keyphrases into two types: (1) Absent key phrases (such phrases do not match any contiguous subsequences of the source document) and (2) Present key phrases (such key phrases fully match a part of the text). The authors used RNN based generative models (discussed as RNN and Copy RNN) for keyphrase prediction and copy mechanism in RNN to predict the already occurred phrases. Strengths: 1. The formation and extraction of key phrases, which are absent in the current document is an interesting idea of significant research interests. 2. The paper is easily understandable. 3. The use of RNN and Copy RNN in the current context is a new idea. As, deep recurrent neural networks are already used in keyphrase extraction (shows very good performance also), so, it will be interesting to have a proper motivation to justify the use of RNN and Copy RNN over deep recurrent neural networks. Weaknesses: 1. Some discussions are required on the convergence of the proposed joint learning process (for RNN and CopyRNN), so that readers can understand, how the stable points in probabilistic metric space are obtained? Otherwise, it may be tough to repeat the results. 2. The evaluation process shows that the current system (which extracts 1. Present and 2. Absent both kinds of keyphrases) is evaluated against baselines (which contains only \\\"present\\\" type of keyphrases). Here there is no direct comparison of the performance of the current system w.r.t. other state-of-the-arts/benchmark systems on only \\\"present\\\" type of key phrases. It is important to note that local phrases (keyphrases) are also important for the document. The experiment does not discuss it explicitly. It will be interesting to see the impact of the RNN and Copy RNN based model on automatic extraction of local or \\\"present\\\" type of key phrases. 3. The impact of document size in keyphrase extraction is also an important point. It is found that the published results of [1], (see reference below) performs better than (with a sufficiently high difference) the current system on Inspec (Hulth, 2003) abstracts dataset. 4. It is reported that current system uses 527,830 documents for training, while 40,000 publications are held out for training baselines. Why are all publications not used in training the baselines? Additionally, The topical details of the dataset (527,830 scientific documents) used in training RNN and Copy RNN are also missing. This may affect the chances of repeating results. 5. As the current system captures the semantics through RNN based models.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"Sample Importance in Training Deep Neural Networks\\\" was not accepted. This was despite the authors' introduction of the novel concept of 'sample importance' in training deep neural networks using Stochastic Gradient Descent. However. Easy samples, as per the paper, shape parameters closer to the output. This idea is represented in Figure 2, where the authors claim output layers are learnt early in training. Similarly, hard samples have a significant impact on parameters closer to the input. This is made evident in Figure 6, which highlights an issue with the Sample Importance measure. In essence, the paper argues that maintaining a mix of hard and easy samples in batches is a better strategy. This methodology is interesting as it deviates from conventional training methods. On another note. The reviewer, however, questions the validity of 'sample importance' as a meaningful metric. They raise valid concerns about the metric's reliability, stating, \\\"I am not convinced of the “sample importance” as a meaningful metric.\\\" This skepticism about the measure's effectiveness is further emphasized by the reviewer's suggestion that the 'input Fisher' norm may be a better measure. The paper's experimental analysis, notably on the CIFAR-10 and MNIST datasets, is viewed as problematic by the reviewer. The results do not convincingly support the use of 'sample importance' for curriculum learning. The reviewer comments, \\\"Had the authors managed to use the SI to craft a better curriculum, this would have given significant weight to the measure.\\\" This suggests a missed opportunity on the part of the authors to strengthen their argument. In the broader context, the paper's results do not provide strong evidence for the effectiveness of 'sample importance' in curriculum learning. This is a significant drawback as the paper's primary claim hinges on this concept. Still. The reviewer's critique is not without merit. The paper's approach to training deep neural networks, while innovative, may not be as effective as initially proposed. The reviewer's comments highlight the need for a more robust measure than 'sample importance'. This is particularly true for training deep neural networks where the complexity of the model can significantly impact the effectiveness of the training method used. In conclusion, the paper presents a novel concept that has potential but falls short in providing convincing evidence for its efficacy. The reviewer's critique raises valid concerns that should be addressed in future iterations of the research. Finally, the paper's concept of 'sample importance', while intriguing, requires further testing and validation before it can be considered a reliable metric for training deep neural networks.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"One of the main idea of this paper is to replace pooling layers with convolutions of stride 2 and retraining the model. Authors merge this into a new layer and brand it as a new type of layer. This is very misleading and adding noise to the field. And using strided convolutions rather than pooling is not actually novel.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"Learning Recurrent Representations for Hierarchical Behavior Modeling\\\" presents a framework for detecting action patterns from motion sequences, utilizing a network with a discriminative part for classifying actions and a generative part for predicting motion. The network's recurrent cells are laterally connected, enabling it to represent high level behavioral phenomena.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The paper reports that \\\"[a]fter the controller RNN is done training, we take the best RNN cell according to the lowest validation perplexity and then run a grid search over learning rate, weight initialization, dropout rates and decay epoch. The best cell found was then run with three different configurations and sizes to increase its capacity.\\\" Is it possible for you to include (or provide here) the hyperparameters and type of dropout (i.e. recurrent dropout, embedding dropout, ...) used? Without them, replication would at best require a great deal of trial and error. As with \\\"Recurrent Neural Network Regularization\\\" (Zaremba et al., 2014), releasing a base set of hyper parameters greatly assists in the future work of the field. This will likely also be desired for the other experiments, such as character LM.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"Out-of-class novelty generation: an experimental foundation\\\" presents an intriguing methodology to measure out-of-distribution novelty. However, the practical application and definition of this novelty proposed by the paper is elusive and can be challenging to comprehend, adding an element of ambiguity to the overall study. This ambiguity extends to the paper's experimentation. The usage of the MNIST and letters datasets in the paper's experiments is noted, but the results raise some concerns. As the reviewer states, \\\"The novelty is hard to define. The proposed metric is also problematic.\\\" This is further emphasized in the critique of the paper's results, where the reviewer notes, \\\"For the 'novel' samples in Fig. 3, they are clearly digits.\\\" This highlights a potential misinterpretation or misrepresentation of novelty in the paper's approach.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The paper describes a network architecture for inverse problems in computer vision. Example inverse problems considered are image inpainting, computing intrinsic image decomposition and foreground/background separation. The architecture is composed of (i) a generator that produces target (latent) output (such as foreground / background regions), (ii) renderer that composes that latent output back to the image that can be compared with the input to measure reconstruction error, and (iii) adversarial prior that ensures the target output (latent) image respects a certain image statistics. Strong points. - The proposed architecture with memory database is interesting and appears to be novel. Weak points: - Experimental results are only proof-of-concept in toy set-ups and do not clearly demonstrate benefits of the proposed architecture. - It is unclear whether the memory retrieval engine that retrieves images based on L2 distance on pixel values is going generalize to other more realistic scenarios. - Clarity. The clarity of explanation can be also improved (see below). Detailed evaluation. Originality: - The novelty of this work lies in the (iii) adversarial prior that places an adversarial loss between the generated latent output and a single image retrieved from a large unlabelled database of target output examples (called memory). The adversarial prior has a convolutional form matching local image statistics, rather than the entire image. The particular form of network architecture with the memory-based fully convolutional adversarial loss appears to be novel and potentially interesting. - Motivation for the Architecture. The weakest point of the proposed architecture is the \\\"Memory retrieval engine\\\" R (section 2.4), where images are retrieved from the memory by measuring L2 distance on pixel intensities. While this maybe ok for simple problems considered in this work, it is unclear how this can generalize to other more complicated datasets and problems. This should be better discussed, better justified and ideally results in some more realistic set-up shown (see below). Quality: - Experiments. Results are shown for inpainting of MNIST digits, intrinsic image decomposition on the MIT intrinsic image database, and figure/ground layer extraction on the synthesized dataset of 3D chairs rendered onto background from real photographs. The experimental validation of the model is not very strong and proof-of-concept only. All the experiments are performed in simplified toy set-ups. The MNIST digit inpainting is far from current state-of-the-art on image inpainting in real photographs (see e.g. Pathak et al., 2016). The foreground background separation is done on only synthetically generated test data. Even for intrinsic image demposition problem there is now relatively large-scale dataset of (Bell et al., 2014), see the citation below. While this is probably ok for the ICLR paper, it diminishes the significance of the work. Is this model going to be useful in a real settings?\", \"sampled\": \"The paper under review discusses the Deep Variational Information Bottleneck (VIB), a variational approximation to the information bottleneck, a fundamental concept in information theory. The authors have used a neural network for the parameterization of this model, which is a significant aspect of the study. The authors' assertion that their work is independent of Chalk et al. 2016 deserves attention. The strength of the VIB models lies in their performance. They outperform other models in terms of generalization performance and robustness to adversarial attack, according to the authors. This gives the VIB models a distinct advantage in practical applications where the integrity and reliability of models are paramount. The VIB model is complex. The study's derivation of the variational lowerbound in VIB is clear and straightforward. This clarity helps in understanding the underlying mathematical principles and in appreciating the authors' methodical approach. However, the presentation of the VIB model is found to be confusing. This could potentially affect the comprehensibility of the model and its subsequent replication in further studies. It is worth noting that the VIB has an interesting connection to the variational auto-encoder, a popular model in the realm of deep learning. This connection, hinted at by the authors, might be a pathway for exploring new synergies between these models. The authors' assertion of their work's independence from Chalk et al. 2016 could also be seen in this light, positioning their work as a unique contribution to the field. In the grand scheme of deep learning research, the VIB model builds upon the work of Tishby et al. Their work serves as a reference point for the authors, providing the theoretical foundation upon which the VIB model is developed. This link to prior research is important in tracing the evolution of ideas and in appreciating the authors' contribution to the broader scientific discourse. This paper contributes to the field of deep learning and variational inference. The VIB model's performance, its connection with the variational auto-encoder, and its independence from concurrent research are all points of interest. The authors have presented a complex model, the understanding of which could benefit from a clearer presentation. The authors' exploration of the Deep Variational Information Bottleneck is a notable contribution to the field of deep learning. Their work presents a novel perspective on variational inference, demonstrating the potential of the VIB model in terms of performance and robustness. However, the presentation of the model could be improved for better comprehension and further exploration. In conclusion, the authors have offered a comprehensive exploration of the Deep Variational Information Bottleneck model, highlighting its strengths, connections, and independence from concurrent research. Despite some confusion in presentation, the study provides valuable insights into the potential of VIB in the field of deep learning.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Looking through the comment section here, I agree to a large degree with the author's standpoint on many issues discussed. Points (1) through (4) in the authors comment below are, in my opinion, a good summary of the contributions of the paper. While I don't think those contributions are groundbreaking, I believe they are significant enough to merit acceptance. The reason I am commenting here is because, having looked at several comment sections for this ICLR, I am seeing a general trend that reviews have a strong focus on performance, i.e. reviews tend to be very short and judge papers, to a large degree, on whether they are a few percentage points better or worse than the reported baseline. E.g. see the comments \\\"the experimental evaluation is not convincing, e.g. no improvement on SVHN\\\" or \\\"the effect of drop-path seems to vanish with data augmentation\\\" below. I believe that papers should be judged more on their scientific contributions (see points (1), (2) and (4) below), especially when those papers themselves state that their focus is on those scientific contributions, not on amazing performance. Further, I believe the trend to focus excessively on performance is problematic for a number of reasons: - The Deep Learning community has focused very heavily on a few datasets (MNIST, ImageNet, CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, SVHN). This means that at any time, a large chunk of the deep learning literature is battling for 5 SOTA titles. Hence, expecting any new model to attain one of those titles is a very high bar. - It is an arbitrary standard. Say the SOTA on ImageNet improves by 2% a year. Then a paper that outperforms by 1% in 2014 would underperform by 1% in 2015. By the performance standard, the same paper with the same ideas and the same scientific merit would have declined drastically in value over that one year. Is that really true? - How does one even draw a \\\"fair comparison\\\" on these standard datasets at this point? The bag of tricks for neural networks includes: drop-out, l2, l1, ensembling, various forms of data augmentation, various forms of normalization and initialization, various non-linearities, various learning rate schedules, various forms of pooling, label smoothing, gradient clipping etc. etc. There are a gazillion ways to eke out fractions of percentage points of performance. And - every single paper has a unique combination of tricks that they use for their model, even though the tricks themselves are unrelated to the model. Hence, the only truly fair comparison would be to compare against every reference model with the exact trick combination that the paper presenting the reference model used, which would take an exorbitant amount of time. What's worse, many papers do not even report all of the tricks they used. One would have to get the authors code and reverse engineer the model, not to mention slight differences introduced by using e.g. TensorFlow vs. Torch vs. Caffe. In this light, the request from one of the reviewers to have a baseline \\\"against which the improvements can be clearly demonstrated by making isolated changes\\\" seems unrealistic to me. - The ML community should not make excessive fine-tuning of models mandatory for publication. By requiring models to beat SOTA, we force each author to fine-tune their model ad nauseum, which leads to an arms race. To get a publications, authors would spend ever more time fine-tuning their models. This can not only lead to \\\"training on the test set\\\", but also wastes the time of researcher that could be better spent exploring new ideas. - It gives too much power to bad research. In science, there is always a certain background rate of \\\"bad\\\" results published: either the numbers are outright fake or the experimental protocol was invalid, e.g. someone used the test set as a validation set or someone did an exorbitant number of random reruns and only published the best single result. What's worse, these \\\"bad\\\" results are far more likely to hold the SOTA title at any given time than a \\\"good\\\" result. By requiring new publications to beat SOTA, we give too much power to bad results. - It punishes authors for reporting many or strong baselines. In this paper, authors were careful to report many recent results. Table 1 is thorough. And now they are criticized for not beating all of those baselines. I have a feeling that if the authors of this paper had been more selective about which baselines they report, i.e. those that they can beat, they would have received higher scores on the paper. I have written an in-depth review for another paper at this conference that used, in my opinion, very weak baselines and ended up getting high reviewer marks. I don't think that was a coincidence. The same arguments apply, though I think to a lesser degree, to judging models excessively on how many parameters they have or their runtime. However, I agree with reviewers that more information about how models compare in terms of those metrics would enhance this paper. I would like to see a discussion of that in the final version. In general, I think this paper would benefit from an appendix with more details on model and training procedure. I also agree with reviewers that 80 layers, which is the deepest that authors can go while improving test error (Table 3), is not ultra-deep. Hence putting \\\"ultra-deep\\\" in the paper title seems exaggerated and I would recommend scaling back the language. However, I don't think being ultra-deep (~1000 layers) is necessary, because as Veit et al showed, networks that appear ultra deep might not be ultra deep in practice. Training an 80-layer net that functions at test time without residual connections seems to be enough of an achievement.\", \"sampled\": \"The manuscript, Improving Invariance and Equivariance Properties of Convolutional Neural Networks, addresses an important topic: invariance, equivariance, and equivalence in CNN representations under data augmentation. I appreciate the attempt to study these representation properties across 9 transformation types and to connect empirical measurements with training objectives for Convolutional Neural Networks. The framing is relevant, though the quoted claim that \\\"This is related to the idea of whether CNNs collapse (invariance) or linearize (equivariance) view manifolds of 3D objects\\\" needs correction. The paper’s main contribution appears to be a systematic empirical examination of how data augmentation affects invariance, equivariance, and equivalence, together with proposed extra loss terms intended to make learned representations more invariant or equivariant, but the manuscript should be clearer about what is new relative to prior measurement work by Lenc & Vedaldi. The authors deserve credit for examining data augmentation effects in a systematic way, yet the current presentation does not make sufficiently clear why the observed effects are surprising, interesting, or useful. The abstract’s reference to 9 transformation types is useful context, but the review would benefit from a clearer explanation of whether the findings over these transformations reveal behavior that was not already expected from using data augmentation during training. The manuscript studies a meaningful question, but the motivation and interpretation currently do not establish the practical or conceptual payoff strongly enough. A central concern is that several empirical findings seem expected rather than surprising. If a network is trained with data augmentation, one would expect greater invariance to the augmented transformations. One would also expect identical augmentations to produce more similar representations than different augmentations, so the paper should better explain what conclusions go beyond these intuitions. The proposed losses are another important part of the submission. The paper proposes extra terms for encouraging invariance or equivariance. This direction is plausible. Yet the manuscript does not show substantial performance gains from training for greater invariance or equivariance. That weakens the practical case for the method. The added computational burden also matters. The proposed technique currently seems unlikely to be useful without stronger evidence that the extra training objective improves performance. This concern is especially important for the loss in eq. 6, which is criticized because of its computational cost and because the manuscript does not show that the cost buys substantial gains. If the method mainly changes representation diagnostics without improving task outcomes, that limitation should be stated directly. The paper should separate the value of measuring invariance and equivariance from the value of explicitly optimizing for them, since the evidence appears stronger for the former than for the latter. The manuscript would also be improved by explaining whether increased invariance or equivariance is intended as an end in itself, or only as a means to better performance. At present, the results do not convincingly support the latter. This makes the contribution feel incomplete. Clarify the utility. Show gains. Or qualify the claims. The paper can still be useful as an analysis of data augmentation effects, but the stronger methodological claim requires evidence that the added objective is worth its computational burden. The discussion of equivariance needs more precision. The claim connecting equivariance to linearized manifolds is incorrect as stated. The quoted sentence about whether CNNs collapse or linearize view manifolds of 3D objects should be revised or removed. The manuscript should avoid implying that equivariance itself means that manifolds have been linearized, unless that claim is explicitly defined and supported within the paper’s formalism. The definition associated with eq. 2 also needs correction. The quantity appears better described as non-equivariance rather than equivariance. If the paper defines a deviation from an equivariant prediction, then naming it equivariance may confuse the reader about the direction of the measurement. This issue affects interpretation of the reported diagnostic quantities, not only notation. There is also a concern about the definition of x’_ij. The current formulation may apply a transformation twice rather than undoing it, and the paper should check this carefully. If the intended comparison requires reversing a transformation before comparing representations, the notation should make that explicit. This is particularly important because the central topic is how representations respond to transformations. The manuscript would benefit from clearer mathematical language around eq. 2. The phrase \\\"Eq. 2 also uses the paradigm that\\\" is awkward and should be rewritten. The term paradigm does not seem appropriate there. There is also a typo, \\\"equivaraince\\\". In section 3.3, the formatting of argmax should be corrected. These are local issues, but they affect readability in a paper whose contribution depends on precise definitions. The paper should also ensure that all transformation notation is consistent with the intended invariance or equivariance measurement. The discussion of equivariance could be strengthened by giving a cleaner account of what transformation is represented in the feature space. A 2D rotation matrix example may help only if the manuscript clearly distinguishes the transformation applied to the input from the corresponding transformation expected in the representation. If the quantity in eq. 2 measures failure to match the expected transformed representation, then the text should say non-equivariance. The same care should apply to equivalence and invariance, since the paper compares related but distinct representation properties. Lenc & Vedaldi are relevant prior work here, and the manuscript should make clearer how its measurement framework differs from or extends that earlier work. The strongest part of the paper is the systematic examination of data augmentation effects, not the current evidence for the added loss terms. The weakest part is the gap between representation-property measurements and demonstrated performance benefit. The paper addresses a timely and relevant question for representation learning in computer vision, but its claims should be narrowed unless the authors can show substantial gains from the proposed training terms. I would encourage revisions focused on clearer novelty relative to Lenc & Vedaldi, corrected equivariance definitions around eq. 2, and stronger evidence justifying the cost of eq. 6.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Reviewers agree that this paper is based on a \\\"trick\\\" to build memory without requiring long-distance backprop. This method allows the model to utilize a cache-like mechanism, simply by storing previous states. Everyone agrees that this roughly works (although there could be stronger experimental evidence), and provides long-term memory to simple models. Reviewers/authors also agree that it might not work as well as other pointer-network like method, but there is controversy over whether that is necessary. - Further discussion indicated a sense by some reviewers that this method could be quite impactful, even if it was not a huge technical contribution, due to its speed and computational benefits over pointer methods. - The clarity of the writing and good use of references was appreciated - This paper is a nice complement/rebuttal to \\\"Frustratingly Short Attention Spans in Neural Language Modeling\\\". Including the discussion about this paper as it might be helpful as it was controversial: \\\"\\\"\\\" The technical contribution may appear \\\"limited\\\" but I feel part of that is necessary to ensure the method can scale to both large datasets and long term dependencies. For me, this is similar to simpler machine learning methods being able to scale to more data (though replacing \\\"data\\\" with \\\"timesteps\\\"). More complex methods may do better with a small number of data/timesteps but they won't be able to scale, where other specific advantages may come in to play. (Timesteps) Looking back 2000 timesteps is something I've not seen done and speaks to a broader aspect of language modeling - properly capturing recent article level context. Most language models limit BPTT to around 35 timesteps, with some even arguing we don't need that much (i.e. \\\"Frustratingly Short Attention Spans in Neural Language Modeling\\\" that's under review for ICLR). From a general perspective, this is vaguely mad given many sentences are longer than 35 timesteps, yet we know both intuitively and from the evidence they present that the rest of an article is very likely to help modeling the following words, especially for PTB or WikiText. This paper introduces a technique that not only allows for utilizing dependencies far further back than 35 timesteps but shows it consistently helps, even when thrown against a larger number of timesteps, a larger dataset, or a larger vocabulary. Given it is also a post-processing step that can be applied to any vaguely RNN type model, it's widely applicable and trivial to train in comparison to any more complicated models. (Data) Speaking to AnonReviewer1's comment, \\\"A side-by-side comparison of models with pointer networks vs. models with cache with roughly the same number of parameters is needed to convincingly argue that the proposed method is a better alternative (either because it achieves lower perplexity, faster to train but similar test perplexity, faster at test time, etc.)\\\" Existing pointer network approaches for language modeling are very slow to train - or at least more optimal methods are yet to be discovered - and has such limited the BPTT length they tackle. Merity et al. use 100 at most and that's the only pointer method for language modeling attending to article style text that I am aware of. Merity et al. also have a section of their paper specifically discussing the training speed complications that come from integrating the pointer network. There is a comparison to Merity et al. in Table 1 and Table 2. The scaling becomes more obvious on the WikiText datasets which have a more realistic long tail vocabulary than PTB's 10k. For WikiText-2, at a cache size of 100, Merity et al. get 80.8 with their pointer network method while the neural cache model get 81.6. Increasing the neural model cache size to 2000 however gives quite a substantial drop to 68.9. They're also able to apply their method to WikiText-103, a far larger dataset than PTB or WikiText-2, and show that it still provides improvements even when there is more data and a larger vocabulary.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper Knowledge as a Teacher: Knowledge-Guided Structural Attention Networks addresses natural language understanding through sequence tagging and slot filling. I understand the main contribution as a method to \\\"flatten\\\" structured information into memory vectors, which then provide extra context to a tagger. The proposed K-SAN architecture is simple and practical, and the paper presents a clear motivation for using structural attention with RNN models. I am less convinced that the architecture is fully novel, but the idea of converting sentence structure and substructure information into usable contextual vectors is still potentially useful. The proposed model has two main parts, sentence embedding and substructure embedding, and this organization makes the method easy to follow at a high level, especially for readers interested in how syntax can be represented for sequence tagging. The evaluation on ATIS appears positive, but the experimental evidence is not yet persuasive enough. My main concern is that the baseline descriptions in Table 1 are insufficient, especially for TreeRNN and DCNN. The paper should explain more clearly how TreeRNN and DCNN are applied to sentence and substructure embeddings. The comparison to baselines is difficult to assess because the proposed model uses two RNNs, which may give it greater parameter capacity than the competing systems. This issue matters because a gain over TreeRNN or DCNN could reflect model size rather than the proposed use of structural attention. The paper should report neural network hyperparameters for the baselines and provide comparable parameter counts so that the empirical claims can be interpreted more reliably. Major concern. A stronger baseline would provide the extra knowledge directly as RNN input features, rather than using the proposed memory and attention mechanism. This would help show whether K-SAN improves because of the structural attention design or because the model simply receives additional syntactic information. The relevant comparison could use the knowledge vector as part of the RNN input, as suggested by Eqn 5 and 8. Such a baseline is important because the paper claims benefits from knowledge-guided structural attention, but without this comparison the contribution of the attention mechanism remains under-specified, and a direct feature-based RNN could be a competitive and simpler alternative. The use of one static attention distribution also needs more justification, since a word-specific attention distribution over substructures seems natural for a slot filling task where different words may require different structural cues. The current similarity-based weighting may favor nodes or phrases that are similar to the full sentence, rather than those most useful for individual tag decisions. Clarify sensitivity. The paper should discuss whether K-SAN is sensitive to parser mistakes, because the model depends on syntax-derived information and the empirical evidence covers one dataset, so it is hard to know whether the results reflect robust use of structure or successful use of relatively clean structural inputs in this setting. The related work discussion should be tightened around DCNN and related substructure encoders, including Ma et al., Zhao et al, Liu et al., and Eriguchi et al. The current framing leaves uncertainty about which parts of K-SAN differ from these prior uses of structured or hierarchical representations. It would also help to explain why the syntax information is called \\\"knowledge\\\" and whether that term is meant to include other forms such as AMR or NER. Without that clarification, the claimed generality to different forms of knowledge is not fully supported. After rebuttal, my main concerns about baselines and the two-RNN design remain unresolved. The response does not adequately establish that the improvements come from the proposed structural attention mechanism rather than from additional parameters or added input information. The paper is promising as a practical model for incorporating syntax into sequence tagging, but its empirical support needs stronger controls. I would encourage the authors to revise the experimental section so that TreeRNN, DCNN, and feature-based RNN comparisons are described with enough detail for fair interpretation. At present, the work is understandable and potentially useful, but the evidence does not yet justify the strength of the claims about novelty, generality, and effectiveness.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes a generative model of videos composed of a background and a set of 2D objects (sprites). Optimization is performed under a VAE framework. The authors' proposal of an outer product of softmaxed vectors (resulting in a 2D map that is delta-like), composed with a convolution, is a very interesting way to achieve translation of an image with differentiable parameters. It seems to be an attractive alternative to more complicated differentiable resamplers (such as those used by STNs) when only translation is needed. Below I have made some comments regarding parts of the text, especially the experiments, that are not clear. The experimental section in particular seems rushed, with some results only alluded to but not given, not even in the appendix. For an extremely novel and exotic proposal, showing only synthetic experiments could be excused. However, though there is some novelty in the method, it is disappointing that there isn't even an attempt at trying to tackle a problem with real data. I suggest as an example aerial videos (such as those taken from drone platforms), since the planar assumption that the authors make would most probably hold in that case. I also suggest that the authors do another pass at proof-reading the paper. There are missing references (\\\"Fig. ??\\\"), unfinished sentences (caption of Fig. 5), and the aforementioned issues with the experimental exposition.\", \"sampled\": \"Program Synthesis for Character Level Language Modeling presents a novel language modeling method for both programming and natural language, and the paper has clear appeal as a 1: novel approach. The central idea is distinctive among the comparisons discussed. The method searches a program space using program synthesis, estimates program weights using counts, and contrasts with neural network methods that use gradient descent and are slow to estimate. Ordinary n-gram models are framed as limited by simplicity, weak large-context modeling, and poor scaling as context grows, while the proposed MCMC-synthesized programs can learn context-sensitive probabilities while keeping count-based speed. The empirical results are a major strength, especially on the linux kernel corpus, where the approach is reported as much stronger than LSTM and n-gram language models. On the Wikipedia corpus, the method is described as competitive with state-of-the-art models, though it does not surpass them, which makes the natural language evidence positive but more qualified. The efficiency claims are also important, since estimation and query times are reported as much better than LSTM language models and comparable with n-gram language models. This helps distinguish the contribution from both neural network baselines and ordinary count-based baselines, and it supports the claim that the method combines context-sensitive modeling with count-based speed.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The paper presents a clean framework for optimizing for the network size during the training cycle. While the complexity of each iteration is increased, they argue that overall, the cost is significantly reduced since we do not need to train networks of varying sizes and cross-validate across them. The reviewers recommend acceptance of the paper and I am in agreement with them.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper introduces the Gated Multimodal Unit, or GMU, for combining visual and textual signals through gates that control modality influence. Its evaluation on multilabel movie genre classification using movie plot text and poster images is a clear setting, and the release of MM-IMDb is a useful contribution, while the review summary indicates that reviewers were generally favorable.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This paper not only shows that a cache model on top of a pre-trained RNN can improve language modeling, but also illustrates a shortcoming of standard RNN models in that they are unable to capture this information themselves. Regardless of whether this is due to the small BPTT window (35 is standard) or an issue with the capability of the RNN itself, this is a useful insight. This technique is an interesting variation of memory augmented neural networks with a number of advantages to many of the standard memory augmented architectures. They illustrate the neural cache model on not just the Penn Treebank but also WikiText-2 and WikiText-103, two datasets specifically tailored to illustrating long term dependencies with a more realistic vocabulary size. I have not seen the ability to refer up to 2000 words back previously. I recommend this paper be accepted. There is additionally extensive analysis of the hyperparameters on these datasets, providing further insight. I recommend this interesting and well analyzed paper be accepted.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper BIOACOUSTIC SEGMENTATION BY HIERARCHICAL DIRICHLET PROCESS HIDDEN MARKOV MODEL addresses an interesting problem, proposing unsupervised segmentation for bioacoustic data using Hierarchical Dirichlet Process Hidden Markov Models. The submission frames segmentation as difficult because experts lack agreement on appropriate segment boundaries, and it cites Kershenbaum et al. (2014) as motivation for understanding animal behavior. The main novelty appears to be the application of an existing HDP-HMM approach to bioacoustic segmentation, rather than the introduction of a new model. The empirical case is not convincing, because the method often does not match segmentations defined by human experts, even though human expert annotations are treated as the evaluation baseline. Two real sound-scene applications are investigated, whale and multi-species bird segmentation. Model learning uses MCMC on MFCC representations of audio signals. These choices are appropriate to describe, but the evidence presented does not establish that the Bayesian non-parametric sequential model provides reliable segmentation for the stated bioacoustic tasks.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This paper is clearly written, and contains original observations on the properties of the neural GPU model. These observations are an important part of research, and sharing them (and code) will help the field move forward. However, these observations do not quite add up to a coherent story, nor is a particular set of hypotheses explored in depth. So the main problem with this paper is that it doesn't fit the 'one main idea' standard format of papers, making it hard to build on this work. The other big problem with this paper is the lack of comparison to similar architectures. There is lots of intuition given about why the NGPU should work better than other architectures in some situations, but not much empirical validation of this idea.\", \"sampled\": \"Recurrent Batch Normalization is a useful contribution because it successfully adapts batch normalization for RNNs, where prior use had failed or performed poorly. The experiments show clear gains of batch-normalized LSTMs over standard LSTMs, and the evaluation spans character-level, word-level, and pixel-level examples. The coverage across PTB, Text8, the CNN question-answering task, MNIST, and pMNIST gives the assessment a broad basis within sequence modeling and recurrent settings. I also appreciated the discussion of gradient flow, especially the role of unit variance in tanh derivatives, and found the Figure 1b \\\"toy task\\\" demonstration useful for supporting the stated gradient-flow insight. The pMNIST experiment is a meaningful part of the evidence because it suggests that batch normalization helps recurrent models learn long-term dependencies.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The paper presents the semantic embedding model for multi-label prediction. In my questions, I pointed that the proposed approach assumes the number of labels to predict is known, and the authors said this was an orthogonal question, although I don't think it is! I was trying to understand how different is SEM from a basic MLP with softmax output which would be trained with a two step approach instead of stochastic gradient descent. It seems reasonable given their similarity to compare to this very basic baseline. Regarding the sampling strategy to estimate the posterior distribution, and the difference with Jean et al, I agree it is slightly different but I think you should definitely refer to it and point to the differences.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper presents Generative Matching Networks as a VAE-based method for conditional sample generation. The approach is clear in its use of a recurrent neural network plus aggregation to process an exemplar set, with an aggregation mechanism resembling Matching Networks and producing an exemplar representation that conditions the VAE generator for generating samples of a concept from several provided exemplars. The conditioning and aggregation are a strength because they are described as handling exemplar sets drawn from multiple classes better than simple averaging, and the experiments show generalization from conditioning on 2 classes to conditioning on 4 classes. The use of OMNIGLOT is appropriate for assessing one-shot conditional generation, and the experiments are quite convincing.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This paper addresses one of the major shortcomings of generative adversarial networks - their lack of mechanism for evaluating held-out data. While other work such as BiGANs/ALI address this by learning a separate inference network, here the authors propose to change the GAN objective function such that the optimal discriminator is also an energy function, rather than becoming uninformative at the optimal solution. Training this new objective requires gradients of the entropy of the generated data, which are difficult to approximate, and the authors propose two methods to do so, one based on nearest neighbors and one based on a variational lower bound. The results presented show that on toy data the learned discriminator/energy function closely approximates the log probability of the data, and on more complex data the discriminator give a good measure of quality for held out data. I would say the largest shortcomings of the paper are practical issues around the scalability of the nearest neighbors approximation and accuracy of the variational approximation, which the authors acknowledge. Also, since entropy estimation and density estimation are such closely linked problems, I wonder if any practical method for EGANs will end up being equivalent to some form of approximate density estimation, exactly the problem GANs were designed to circumvent. Nonetheless, the elegant mathematical exposition alone makes the paper a worthwhile contribution to the literature. Also, some quibbles about the writing - it seems that something is missing in the sentence at the top of pg. 5 \\\"Finally, let's whose discriminative power\\\". I'm not sure what the authors mean to say here.\", \"sampled\": \"The Neural Noisy Channel proposes using an SSNT model for p(x|y) in a noisy channel approach to conditional generation, while still supporting incremental generation of y. The paper also introduces an approximate decoding search method, and the reviewer finds the empirical evaluation extensive and strong, with good empirical results that make the proposed approach a substantive contribution rather than only a modeling variation. The SSNT model is interesting and appropriately motivated for this application, since the formulation directly addresses the role of p(x|y) in conditional generation and gives the paper a clear modeling rationale. The paper is generally clear and well written, and its combination of a noisy channel formulation, sequence-to-sequence transduction, beam search decoding, and empirical evaluation is presented in a way that is accessible and technically focused. The main concern is whether the extra training and decoding cost is justified by the gains, since a simpler alternative might capture some of the same benefits without the same modeling and search burden. In particular, reranking complete outputs from a standard seq2seq model using p(y|x), p(x|y), and p(y) might provide similar gains, and Li et al. at NAACL 2016 are relevant here as a comparison point for reranking in conversation modeling. Experiments on reranking during search would be useful for clarifying this issue and strengthening the empirical case. Section 2 could also be presented more consistently from the viewpoint of modeling p(x|y), since that perspective appears central to the proposed approach. This would improve focus. The discussion of \\\"explaining away\\\" is relevant to the noisy channel motivation. Clarifying how the model addresses that issue, while separating it from the cost question, would make the paper’s claims easier to assess and would better connect the modeling motivation with the empirical results.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: * Elaborate evaluation data creation and evaluation scheme. * Range of compared techniques: baseline/simple/complex - Weaknesses: * No in-depth analysis beyond overall evaluation results. - General Discussion: This paper compares several techniques for robust HPSG parsing. Since the main contribution of the paper is not a novel parsing technique but the empirical evaluation, I would like to see a more in-depth analysis of the results summarized in Table 1 and 2. It would be nice to show some representative example sentences and sketches of its analyses, on which the compared methods behaved differently. Please add EDM precision and recall figures to Table 2. The EDM F1 score is a result of a mixed effects of (overall and partial) coverage, parse ranking, efficiency of search, etc. The overall coverage figures in Table 1 are helpful but addition of EDM recall to Table 2 would make the situations clearer. Minor comment: - Is 'pacnv+ut' in Table 1 and 2 the same as 'pacnv' described in 3.4.3?\", \"sampled\": \"This is a strong submission with useful theoretical contributions, especially in connecting value-based reinforcement learning with policy-gradient approaches. The proposed PGQL method is clearly positioned between policy gradient and Q-learning. A central strength is the formal relationship between Q-values and regularized policy gradient policies. I suggest presenting PGQL more explicitly as an extension or generalization of the dueling Q-network, since that framing seems more intuitive for explaining both the algorithm and the reported findings. This comparison would also help readers understand how the value and policy components relate to existing Q-learning architectures. My main concern is the general-case derivation in Section 3.2. In Eq. (7), the expectation over state-action pairs is taken with respect to pi, while pi depends on theta, and this theta-dependence appears to be ignored. That dependency seems important for deriving the policy-gradient update, so the current step may optimize a different objective than intended.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Thank you to all the reviewers for their comments. We believe that these are the primary points raised by the reviewers: 1. A comparison with a neural programming technique which does not generate code would be a valuable addition to our experiments. We did not include a comparison to a network which does not generate source code because because they usually require substantially more training data than the 5 input-output examples we provide to have any success at generalization. While it would be interesting to show this effect in experiments, the wide variety of different models and training strategies, the custom structure of list data and list-aware objective function in our work, together with the lack of released standard implementations, makes it unclear what neural programming baseline (and with what training regime) would be appropriate. 2. The tasks our network can learn are simple. In particular, we do not consider sorting or merging problems. Although the tasks that we considered are simple, they are more complex than the tasks which many other neural programming approaches can handle, particularly as we test for perfect generalization. The approaches to learning to sort do so using program traces, which provide much stronger supervision than the input-output examples that we use [1], or use specialized memory representations that simplify sorting [2]. 3. This paper does not conclusively show that gradient-based evaluators are appropriate for program induction. This paper certainly does not contradict the findings of the original TerpreT paper that discrete solvers are good backends for program induction. Our motivation in this paper is to improve gradient-based program search, and to understand the effect of different design choices that arise when building differentiable interpreters. Even if gradient descent isn't currently the best method for program induction, we believe it merits further study. It is a very new idea, and it's feasible to us that seemingly subtle design decisions could make a big difference in its performance (indeed, this is one take-away from our experiments). Further, since gradient descent is very different from alternatives, improving its performance may enable new uses of program synthesis such as jointly inducing programs and training neural network subcomponents as in [3], using SGD to scale up to large data sets, or giving new ways of thinking about noisy data in program synthesis. Finally, the recommendations for the design of such evaluators discussed in this paper are not necessarily restricted to TerpreT-based models, and we believe that our design recommendations apply to related neural architectures that try to learn algorithmic patterns. 4. How will this model generalize to programs that can't be solved using the prefix-loop-suffix structure? Defining new program structures is simple, and our current implementation allows the optimizer to choose between several program structures. More loops could be added if desired, and more looping schemes could be added to extend the class of programs which can be learned. 5. TerpreT is not yet publicly available. TerpreT is nearly ready for public release. Approval for open-sourcing under the MIT license has been obtained, and we are currently in the process of documenting the source code to publish it (with all models used in this paper). References: [1] Scott Reed and Nando de Freitas. Neural Programmer-Interpreters. In ICLR 2016. [2] Marcin Andrychowicz, Karol Kurach. Learning Efficient Algorithms with Hierarchical Attentive Memory.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper presents an interesting contribution on spatiotemporal visual attention. I consider the work accepted, while noting several points that should be clarified. The contribution is clear. Recurrent Mixture Density Network for Spatiotemporal Visual Attention proposes a recurrent neural model for predicting human video fixations. The formulation is appealing because it predicts human video fixations through a Gaussian mixture formulation and trains the model by maximum likelihood using human fixation data, which gives the approach a direct connection to the observed fixation distribution rather than only to derived saliency maps. The evaluation also has useful breadth, since the work studies fixation prediction on Hollywood2 and tests generalization and action classification on UCF101, while also using saliency outputs with C3D features for action recognition, but the presentation would benefit from a more complete assessment of fixation prediction quality. The experimental framing raises questions. Table 1 and Table 2 appear to report substantially different center bias results. This difference should be explained because it affects how the proposed model is interpreted. Central Bias is an important reference point here, and the paper should describe more fully how that baseline was modeled and how it relates to the human performance comparison. The claim that the model results are within 3.2% of human performance should be handled carefully, because comparing gaps in AUC scores can be risky when saturation effects may make differences hard to interpret. Human performance needs clarification. Was the human performance estimate cross-validated, or was it computed in another way? The comparison with state-of-the-art methods is not fully convincing in its current form. In particular, I am not sure whether all models in Table 2 are truly worse than the center bias result reported in Table 1. If that is the intended conclusion, the paper should make the evaluation setup and the center bias computation directly comparable across the two tables. If it is not the intended conclusion, the table presentation should prevent that reading. The current presentation leaves open whether the apparent difference reflects evaluation details, dataset handling, or the way the baseline was defined. The authors should also explain how the human performance estimate fits into the same comparison, since it is used as an upper reference for the proposed model and for the claimed small remaining gap. The discussion of Kümmerer et. al 2015 also appears inaccurate, because that model was trained on fixation data with maximum likelihood, whereas the paper describes it as a method that \\\"learns ... indirectly rather than from explicit information of where humans look\\\". This citation issue matters. It affects the positioning of the proposed maximum likelihood training objective relative to prior fixation-based saliency models. Table 3 is confusing in its explanation, especially in the distinction between how CONV5 and FC6 use the saliency map. The authors should clarify why these layers use the saliency information differently and what comparison Table 3 is meant to support. The action recognition experiment is a useful component because it connects saliency prediction to C3D features. The paper should make the role of the predicted saliency outputs in that experiment easier to follow. The broader evaluation would be stronger if fixation prediction quality were assessed more completely before using the saliency outputs in action recognition.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: This paper proposed an interesting and important metric for evaluating the quality of word embeddings, which is the \\\"data efficiency\\\" when it is used in other supervised tasks. Another interesting point in the paper is that the authors separated out three questions: 1) whether supervised task offers more insights to evaluate embedding quality; 2) How stable is the ranking vs labeled data set size; 3) The benefit to linear vs non-linear models. Overall, the authors presented comprehensive experiments to answer those questions, and the results see quite interesting to know for the research community. - Weaknesses: The overall result is not very useful for ML practioners in this field, because it merely confirms what has been known or suspected, i.e. it depends on the task at hand, the labeled data set size, the type of the model, etc. So, the result in this paper is not very actionable. The reviewer noted that this comprehensive analysis deepens the understanding of this topic. - General Discussion: The paper's presentation can be improved. Specifically: 1) The order of the figures/tables in the paper should match the order they are mentioned in the papers. Right now their order seems quite random. 2) Several typos (L250, 579, etc). Please use a spell checker. 3) Equation 1 is not very useful, and its exposition looks strange. It can be removed, and leave just the text explanations. 4) L164 mentions the \\\"Appendix\\\", but it is not available in the paper. 5) Missing citation for the public skip-gram data set in L425. 6) The claim in L591-593 is too strong. It must be explained more clearly, i.e. when it is useful and when it is not. 7) The observation in L642-645 is very interesting and important. It will be good to follow up on this and provide concrete evidence or example from some embedding. Some visualization may help too. 8) In L672 should provide examples of such \\\"specialized word embeddings\\\" and how they are different than the general purpose embedding. 9) Figuer 3 is too small to read.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper addresses a genuine need in the field, as qualitative sample inspection remains a common evaluation practice for models such as Generative Adversarial Networks and Generative Moment Matching Networks, and a principled quantitative alternative has real value. The central contribution is a framework that uses Annealed Importance Sampling to estimate log-likelihoods for decoder-based generative models, validates those estimates through Bidirectional Monte Carlo, and demonstrates on binarized MNIST that this approach is more accurate than Kernel Density Estimation across Variational Autoencoders, Importance Weighted Autoencoders, Generative Adversarial Networks, and Generative Moment Matching Networks. The finding that the Importance Weighted Autoencoder bound underestimates true log-likelihoods by at least 1 nat on binarized MNIST is a concrete and useful result. Making the evaluation framework public is a meaningful contribution given the persistent difficulty of reproducibility in generative model evaluation; the authors also disconfirm the hypothesis that Generative Adversarial Networks memorize training data, showing instead that these models miss important modes of the data distribution, which is a substantive empirical finding. Several concerns remain. The chain count is low: prior work by Salakhutdinov and Murray, 2008 and Desjardins et al., 2011 used 100 chains, yet this paper uses only 16 independent AIS chains with no justification given. The experiments also draw varying numbers of examples—100, 1000, and 10000—from different data sources without explanation of why a consistent setup was not used, which complicates direct comparison. GAN50 shows a Bidirectional Monte Carlo gap of 10 nats, roughly an order of magnitude larger than the gaps observed for other models, yet the authors offer no intuition for why this occurs; given its scale relative to the other evaluated models, this gap warrants explicit discussion. Finally, Table 1 is not referenced anywhere in the main text and its columns are not described, and the caption of Figure 3 contains a labeling error.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The paper explores a simple approach to learning reward functions for reinforcement learning from visual observations of expert trajectories for cases were only little training data is available. To obtain descriptive rewards even under such challenging conditions the method re-uses a pre-trained neural network as feature extractor (this is similar to a large body of work on task transfer with neural nets in the area of computer vision) and represents the reward function as a weighted distance to features for automatically extracted \\\"key-frames\\\" of the provided expert trajectories. The paper is well written and explains all involved concepts clearly while also embedding the presented approach in the literature on inverse reinforcement learning (IRL). The resulting algorithm is appealing due to its simplicity and could prove useful for many real world robotic applications. I have three main issues with the paper in its current form, if these can be addressed I believe the paper would be significantly strengthened: 1) Although the recursive splitting approach for extracting the \\\"key-frames\\\" seems reasonable and the feature selection is well motivated I am missing two baselines in the experiments: - what happens if the feature selection is disabled and the distance between all features is used ? will this immediately break the procedure ? If not, what is the trade-off here ? - an even simpler baseline than what is proposed in the paper would be the following procedure: simply use all frames of the recorded trajectories, calculate the distance to them in feature space and weights them according to their time as in the approach proposed in the paper. How well would that work ? 2) I understand the desire to combine the extracted reward function with a simple RL method but believe the used simple controller could potentially introduce a significant bias in the experiments since it requires initialization from an expert trajectory. As a direct consequence of this initialization the RL procedure is already started close to a good solution and the extracted reward function is potentially only queried in a small region around what was observed in the initial set of images (perhaps with the exception of the human demonstrations). Without an additional experiment it is thus unclear how well the presented approach will work in combination with other RL methods for training the controller. 3) I understand that the low number of available images excludes training a deep neural net directly for the task at hand but one has to wonder how other baselines would do. What happens if one uses a random projection of the images to form a feature vector? How well would a distance measure using raw images (e.g. L2 norm of image differences) or a distance measure based on the first principal components work? It seems that occlusions etc. would exclude them from working well but without empirical evidence it is hard to confirm this. Minor issues: - Page 1: \\\"make use of ideas about imitation\\\" reads a bit awkwardly - Page 3: \\\"We use the Inception network pre-trained ImageNet\\\" -> pre-trained for ImageNet classification - Page 4: the definition of the transition function for the stochastic case seems broken - Page 6: \\\"efficient enough to evaluate\\\" a bit strangely written sentence Additional comments rather than real issues: - The paper is mainly of empirical nature, little actual learning is performed to obtain the reward function and no theoretical advances are needed. This is not necessarily bad but makes the empirical evaluation all the more important.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper on Probabilistic Regular Graph Languages is formally correct, clearly written, and makes a genuine contribution to the formal language theory underlying natural language processing. Researchers working on graph-based semantic parsing are likely to find the ideas stimulating. The central results—that Regular Graph Languages are closed under intersection and that a parsing algorithm with runtime linear in the size of the input graph can be constructed—are well-established within the paper, though their practical interpretation for NLP applications needs more careful framing than the current draft provides. The most pressing concern is that the paper does not adequately distinguish between semantic parsing from strings and the parsing or validation of semantic parses already in graph form. The linear complexity result applies to top-down deterministic grammars and is properly understood as a graph validation result, not as a claim about recovering semantic parses from raw string input. This distinction matters: an input string in NLP typically gives rise to an exponential number of graphs, a fact the paper does not acknowledge and which substantially limits the directness of the claimed NLP applicability. The algorithm is well-suited to reranking hypergraphs produced by a separate parser—a genuinely useful role in NLP pipelines—but this reranking use case is absent from the introduction, leaving readers to infer a more direct parsing role than the formalism supports. Extending the parser to handle string inputs would require tracking covered 0-arity edges, and this variant becomes exponential rather than linear. It is also unclear whether the Earley recogniser remains strictly polynomial in m or k when grammars are ambiguous or not top-down deterministic, and this case is not addressed. A further gap is the absence of any clear connection to non-context-free string languages or to the sets of non-projective dependency graphs that appear in practical semantic parsing contexts, including AMR. The remark at lines 644–647 touches on a related point but arrives too late and would benefit readers if moved earlier. A discussion of Monadic Second Order graph languages and their yields—context-free string languages in the sense explored by Courcelle, Kuhlmann, and Jonsson—would help situate the contribution more precisely within the existing literature. Several specific passages also need attention. Lines 102–106 are misleading in their treatment of intersection and probabilities. Line 173 uses the word \\\"parsing\\\" where \\\"validation\\\" or \\\"recognition\\\" would be more precise. Lines 195–196 are unclear regarding the elements of att_G. Line 352 would benefit from an example of a nontrivial internal path. Lines 757–758 are misleading on the claim that HRGs generate non-context-free languages, and line 760 invites scrutiny on the assertion about non-crossing links when parsing linearized sentences. The paper demands close reading and substantial formal background from its audience. While it is admirably precise given that background, the authors should consider revising the introduction to set more accurate expectations about the relationship between the graph-theoretic results and the practical constraints of string-to-graph semantic parsing tasks such as those involving AMR. Addressing the distinction between graph validation and string-to-graph parsing consistently throughout—particularly in the introduction and wherever NLP applicability is claimed—would strengthen the work considerably.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: Detailed guidelines and explicit illustrations. - Weaknesses: The document-independent crowdsourcing annotation is unreliable. - General Discussion: This work creates a new benchmark corpus for concept-map-based MDS. It is well organized and written clearly. The supplement materials are sufficient. I have two questions here. 1) Is it necessary to treat concept map extraction as a separate task? On the one hand, many generic summarization systems build a similar knowledge graph and then generate summaries accordingly. On the other hand, with the increase of the node number, the concept map becomes growing hard to distinguish. Thus, the general summaries should be more readable. 2) How can you determine the importance of a concept independent of the documents? The definition of summarization is to reserve the main concepts of documents. Therefore, the importance of a concept highly depends on the documents. For example, in the given topic of coal mining accidents, assume there are two concepts: A) an instance of coal mining accidents and B) a cause of coal mining accidents. Then, if the document describes a series of coal mining accidents, A is more important than B. In comparison, if the document explores why coal mining accidents happen, B is more significant than A. Therefore, just given the topic and two concepts A&B, it is impossible to judge their relative importance. I appreciate the great effort spent by authors to build this dataset. However, this dataset is more like a knowledge graph based on common sense rather than summary.\", \"sampled\": \"The main novelty here is the use of marginal Fisher analysis as a new layer in a deep architecture. That said, several concerns remain. Similar ideas appeared in prior work roughly 3 to 5 years ago, with SPCANet as one example, which limits the originality of the contribution. The proposed MDA method combines MFA initialization with existing components—greedy-layerwise stacking, dropout, and denoising auto-encoders—and this combination constrains the claim of novelty. The paper includes a large number of experiments across 7 small to mid-scale datasets, which is appreciated, though the small scale of those datasets is a limitation. The use of back propagation with dropout during fine-tuning is not itself novel. A notable missing comparison is a discriminatively trained convolutional network on CIFAR-10, which would serve as a meaningful baseline. The authors should also demonstrate that MFA initialization outperforms random weight initialization, as this comparison is absent. It is also unclear how computationally expensive it is to compute the association matrix A in equation 4, and the paper does not address this. A convolutional or fully connected network trained from scratch with a good initialization scheme should be included as a baseline so that the benefit of MFA-based initialization can be properly isolated. Without this, it is difficult to attribute observed performance gains to MFA initialization rather than to other components of the pipeline.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This paper introduces an energy-based Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) and provides theoretical and empirical results modeling a number of image datasets (including large-scale versions of categories of ImageNet). As far as I know energy-based GANs (EBGAN) were introduced in Kim and Bengio (2016), but the proposed version makes a number of different design choices. First, it does away with the entropy regularization term that Kim and Bengio (2016) introduced to ensure that the GAN discriminator converged to an energy function proportional to the log density of the data (at optimum). This implies that the discriminator in the proposed scheme will become uniform at convergence as discussed in the theoretical section of the paper, however the introductory text seems to imply otherwise -- that one could recover a meaningful score function from the trained energy-function (discriminator). This should be clarified. Second, this version of the EBGAN setting includes two innovations: (1) the introduction of the hinge loss in the value function, and (2) the use of an auto-encoder parametrization for the energy function. These innovations are not empirically justified in any way - this is disappointing, as it would be really good to see empirical results supporting the arguments made in support of their introduction. The two significant contributions of this paper are the theoretical analysis of the energy-baesd GAN formalism (showing that the optimum corresponds to a Nash equilibrium) and the impressive empirical results on large images that set a new standard in what straight GAN-style models can achieve. The theoretical results seem solid to me and make a nice contribution. Regarding the quantitative results in Table 2, it seems not appropriate to bold the EBGAN line when it seems to be statistically indistinguishable form the Rasmus et al (2015) results. Though it is not mentioned here, the use of bold typically indicates the state-of-the-art. I think this paper could be be much stronger if the two novel contributions to the energy-based GAN setting were more thoroughly explored with ablation experiments.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes predicting binary codes to reduce output layer computation to a cost that scales logarithmically in vocabulary size, a well-motivated objective for neural machine translation with large vocabularies. The writing is clear throughout and the figures are well designed, making the technical content accessible. The central result on the E2J task is notable: using 44 bits, the model achieves 26 out of 31 BLEU relative to the softmax baseline, a smaller gap than one might expect given the significant reduction in output layer complexity. On the ASPEC dataset, however, the best proposed model remains 1 BLEU point below the softmax baseline, and it is unclear whether this gap would widen on larger datasets such as the French-English corpus suggested for comparison, which contains up to 12 million sentences. Evaluating on additional language pairs beyond English-Japanese would help clarify how broadly the method generalizes. The paper positions the parameter reduction technique as orthogonal to weight pruning and sequence-level knowledge distillation, which situates it well within the broader efficient NMT literature, and the claim that the method extends to any task with a large output vocabulary strengthens its potential impact. That said, the hidden dimension size of the models is not reported in the experimental setup, making it difficult to fully assess the computational comparisons. It would also be straightforward to ablate the number of bits beyond 44, since the bottleneck lies elsewhere and additional bits could improve classification power without affecting GPU computation time. A related paper at https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00072 reports a 10x decoding speedup with less than 0.5 BLEU loss and should be cited given its direct relevance; situating the proposed method against that result would sharpen the contribution. Finally, binary code prediction effectively predicts word rank, and the semantic interpretation of individual bit-embeddings under that framing is not clearly established. Clarifying whether the bit structure encodes anything beyond frequency rank would strengthen the paper's interpretive claims.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This paper addresses the problem of disambiguating/linking textual entity mentions into a given background knowledge base (in this case, English Wikipedia). (Its title and introduction are a little overblown/misleading, since there is a lot more to bridging text and knowledge than the EDL task, but EDL is a core part of the overall task nonetheless.) The method is to perform this bridging via an intermediate layer of representation, namely mention senses, thus following two steps: (1) mention to mention sense, and (2) mention sense to entity. Various embedding representations are learned for the words, the mention senses, and the entities, which are then jointly trained to maximize a single overall objective function that maximizes all three types of embedding equally. Technically the approach is fairly clear and conforms to the current deep processing fashion and known best practices regarding embeddings; while one can suggest all kinds of alternatives, it’s not clear they would make a material difference. Rather, my comments focus on the basic approach. It is not explained, however, exactly why a two-step process, involving the mention senses, is better than a simple direct one-step mapping from word mentions to their entities. (This is the approach of Yamada et al., in what is called here the ALIGN algorithm.) Table 2 shows that the two-step MPME (and even its simplification SPME) do better. By why, exactly? What is the exact difference, and additional information, that the mention senses have compare4ed to the entities? To understand, please check if the following is correct (and perhaps update the paper to make it exactly clear what is going on). For entities: their profiles consist of neighboring entities in a relatedness graph. This graph is built (I assume) by looking at word-level relatedness of the entity definitions (pages in Wikipedia). The profiles are (extended skip-gram-based) embeddings. For words: their profiles are the standard distributional semantics approach, without sense disambiguation. For mention senses: their profiles are the standard distributional semantics approach, but WITH sense disambiguation. Sense disambiguation is performed using a sense-based profile (‘language model’) from local context words and neighboring mentions, as mentioned briefly just before Section 4, but without details. This is a problem point in the approach. How exactly are the senses created and differentiated? Who defines how many senses a mention string can have? If this is done by looking at the knowledge base, then we get a bijective mapping between mention senses and entities -– that is, there is exactly one entity for each mention sense (even if there may be more entities). In that case, are the sense collection’s definitional profiles built starting with entity text as ‘seed words’? If so, what information is used at the mention sense level that is NOT used at the entity level? Just and exactly the words in the texts that reliably associate with the mention sense, but that do NOT occur in the equivalent entity webpage in Wikipedia? How many such words are there, on average, for a mention sense? That is, how powerful/necessary is it to keep this extra differentiation information in a separate space (the mention sense space) as opposed to just loading these additional words into the Entity space (by adding these words into the Wikipedia entity pages)? If the above understanding is essentially correct, please update Section 5 of the paper to say so, for (to me) it is the main new information in the paper. It is not true, as the paper says in Section 6, that “…this is the first work to deal with mention ambiguity in the integration of text and knowledge representations, so there is no exact baselines for comparison”. The TAC KBP evaluations for the past two years have hosted EDL tasks, involving eight or nine systems, all performing exactly this task, albeit against Freebase, which is considerably larger and more noisy than Wikipedia. Please see http://nlp.cs.rpi.edu/kbp/2016/ . On a positive note: I really liked the idea of the smoothing parameter in Section 6.4.2. Post-response: I have read the authors' responses.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper addresses technically demanding problems in model-based reinforcement learning, and the authors have responded to reviewer concerns by adding new results and discussion, with modifications highlighted in red, primarily in the Appendix. The scope of the revision is substantial, and several structural decisions made in response to criticism deserve careful scrutiny. The authors compare 8 alternative architectures for incorporating actions against the main model, with results in Section B.1.3, Figures 19 and 20, covering 10 Atari games. Combining the action with the frame directly, as in ConvA, is identified as a poor approach, while other direct action influence methods show broadly similar performance to one another. Direct action influence outperforms indirect action influence (as in Oh et al.) in games with higher prediction error, with the most striking difference observed in Seaquest. The training scheme is identified as the major factor for improving long-term prediction, though the authors note it must be combined with an appropriate architecture to be effective. This interaction is a meaningful clarification, but it is not fully unpacked in the main text, and the architectural comparison deserved earlier placement in the paper rather than being deferred entirely to an appendix. The reviewer comment regarding schedule sampling is addressed through a comparison with Bengio et al., whose work used discrete data and shorter sequences—average sequence length of 11 in the Image Captioning example—where 100% Predicted Frames performed poorly. The authors argue their setting is substantively different. In simpler Atari games, the 100% Predicted Frames training scheme performs best, while in more complex games, schemes close to it are preferable. Oh et al. used a mixed training scheme without explanation, leaving the importance of various mixing strategies unclear in prior work. The authors' contribution here is therefore not simply a replication of prior findings. Section B.2 provides jumpy prediction analysis. The authors note that the Oh et al. model cannot be adapted for jumpy predictions because omitting intermediate frames removes action influence, which operates indirectly through frames. This is a technically precise and important distinction. The authors claim to be the first to demonstrate success with jumpy approaches for long-term prediction in large-scale domains—a notable claim, but one whose architectural reasoning is sound given the indirect action influence constraint. Breakout was excluded from the jumpy prediction analysis because its prediction error is uninformative, and the authors plan to add Riverraid experiments at subsequence length T of 15. The second reviewer comment—whether the current model's modification drives the jumpy prediction results or whether prior models like Oh et al. could achieve the same—is one the authors state they do not understand, and this impasse is itself informative. The jumpy simulator is more sensitive and fragile to structural changes than the single-step simulator, but achieves meaningful accuracy in a number of games, including Bowling, Freeway, Pong, and Fishing Derby, and can predict 100 time-steps into the future, distinguishing it from shorter-horizon prior work. The reviewer's concern remains only partially resolved, since the authors' explanation rests on the structural argument about indirect action influence rather than a direct empirical ablation against Oh et al. in the jumpy setting. Bengio et al.'s sequence length of 11 and discrete data setting do support the claim of a distinct contribution, but the authors should clarify in the main text—not only the appendix—why the Oh et al. architecture is structurally incompatible with jumpy prediction.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"First, the bad: This paper is frustratingly written. The grammar is fine, but: - The first four pages are completely theoretical and difficult to follow without any concrete examples. These sections would benefit greatly from a common example woven through the different aspects of the theoretical discussion. - The ordering of the exposition is also frustrating. I found myself constantly having to refer ahead to figures and back to details that were important but seemingly presented out of order. Perhaps a reordering of some details could fix this. Recommendation: give the most naturally ordered oral presentation of the work and then order the paper similarly. Finally, the description of the experiments is cursory, and I found myself wondering whether the details omitted were important or not. Including experimental details in a supplementary section could help assuage these fears. The good: What the paper does well is to gather together past work on novelty generation and propose a unified framework in which to evaluate past and future models. This is done by repurposing existing generative model evaluation metrics for the task of evaluating novelty. The experiments are basic, but even the basic experiments go beyond previous work in this area (to this reviewer’s knowledge). Overall I recommend the paper be accepted, but I strongly recommend rewriting some components to make it more digestible. As with other novelty papers, it would be read thoroughly by the interested few, but it is likely to fight an uphill battle against the majority of readers outside the sub-sub-field of novelty generation; for this reason the theory should be made even more intuitive and clear and the experiments and results even more accessible.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper makes a solid contribution by using context-dependent word representations derived from hidden states of neural language models to improve sequence tagging in a semi-supervised setting. Evaluation on NER and chunking demonstrates state-of-the-art results on both tasks, and the included analysis addresses several natural questions about the method—showing in particular that language models trained on large data yield significant performance gains. This analysis is a genuine strength, giving readers a clearer picture of what drives the improvements. That said, several concerns should be addressed before publication. The most pressing methodological issue is that too many experiments appear to use the test set rather than held-out development data. Tables 5 and 6 should be revised to use development data to ensure a sound evaluation protocol and avoid comparisons that may be inflated by test set selection. The evaluation scope is also somewhat narrow. NER and chunking are standard benchmarks, but neither requires the model to capture interesting long-range dependencies, which limits what can be concluded about the representations more broadly. Experiments on tasks such as SRL or CCG supertagging would provide stronger evidence that the bidirectional language model representations generalize across a wider range of linguistic phenomena. Finally, the paper claims that a task-specific RNN is necessary because placing a CRF directly on top of the language model embeddings performs poorly, but it is not clear whether backpropagation into the language model was performed in that experiment. This detail matters for interpreting the result and should be clarified.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Thank you for the interesting paper. 1. Could you elaborate on how your method provides additional theoretical insight into the importance of orthogonality beyond existing work [1] and [2]? We would additionally encourage you to cite [2]. 2. In accordance with the review of AnonReviewer1, could you elaborate how your method is different from the existing use of CCA in [3]? 3. As AnonReviewer1 pointed out, cognates are words with the same etymological origin but are usually spelled differently (see [4] for more examples). You should replace this term to make your manuscript more accurate. 4. Given the above points, the main contributions of your paper are a) the inverted softmax and b) the \\\"cognate\\\" dictionary. Is that correct? [1] Xing, C., Liu, C., Wang, D., & Lin, Y. (2015). Normalized Word Embedding and Orthogonal Transform for Bilingual Word Translation. NAACL-2015, 1005–1010. [2] Artetxe, M., Labaka, G., & Agirre, E. (2016). Learning principled bilingual mappings of word embeddings while preserving monolingual invariance. Proceedings of the 2016 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP-16), 2289–2294. [3] Faruqui, M., & Dyer, C. (2014). Improving Vector Space Word Representations Using Multilingual Correlation. Proceedings of the 14th Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics, 462 – 471.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper introduces LOTS, a method for layerwise origin-target synthesis, and the overall approach is sound. The core idea of using LOTS as a visualization technique for understanding intermediate feature layers in deep neural networks is genuinely original. The method goes beyond standard classification-based adversarial generation by matching features of an unrelated image—a meaningful extension—and it can generate adversarial examples for face-recognition models and other tasks outside classification. LOTS can also assess the invariance of learned features at any layer with respect to input class, and the methodology is applied to visualize the amount of perturbation needed to produce changes in higher-level features. The paper further proposes LOTS as a way to produce diverse adversarial examples for improving model robustness through training, which broadens its practical scope. That said, the analyses presented are relatively shallow and fail to address the most obvious questions a reader would raise about the method and its behavior. The visualizations are not particularly illuminating and make it difficult to draw meaningful conclusions, which weakens the paper's central contribution as a visualization and interpretability tool.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This paper investigates the application of distributional vectors of meaning in tasks that involve the identification of semantic relations, similar to the analogical reasoning task of Mikolov et al. (2013): Given an expression of the form “X is for France what London is for the UK”, X can be approximated by the simple vector arithmetic operation London-UK+France. The authors argue that this simple method can only capture very specific forms of analogies, and they present a measure that aims at identifying a wider range of relations in a more effective way. I admit I find the idea of a single vector space model being able to capture a number of semantic relationships and analogies rather radical and infeasible. As the authors mention in the paper, a number of studies already suggest for the opposite. The reason is quite simple: behind all these models lies (some form of) the distributional hypothesis (words in similar contexts have similar meanings), and this poses certain limitations in their expressive abilities; for example, words like “big” and “small” will always be considered as semantically similar from a vector perspective (although they express opposite meanings), since they occur in similar contexts. So I cannot see how the example given in Figure 1 is relevant to the very nature of vector spaces (or to any other semantic model for that matter!): there is a certain analogy between “man-king”, and “woman-queen”, but asking from a word space to capture “has-a” relationships of the form “owl-has-claws”, hence “hospital-has-walls”, doesn’t make much sense to me. The motivation behind the main proposal of the paper (a similarity measure that involves a form of cross-comparison between vectors of words and vectors representing the contexts of the words) is not clearly explained. Further, the measure is tested on the relation categories of the SemEval 2010 task with rather unsatisfactory results; in almost all cases, a simple baseline that takes into account only partial similarities between the tested word pairs present very high performance, with a difference from the best-performing model which seems to me statistically insignificant. So from both a methodological and an experimental perspective, the paper has weaknesses, and in its current form seems to describe work in progress; as such I am inclined against its presentation in ACL. (Formatting issue: The authors use the LaTeX styles for ACL 2016 — this should be fixed in case the paper is accepted). AUTHORS RESPONSE. Thank you for the clarifications.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper presents a genuinely novel reformulation of the GAN discriminator, replacing the traditional binary classifier with a component that assigns a scalar energy to each point in the generator's output domain. The optimality theorems concerning the Nash equilibrium are treated with sufficient rigor. One minor but meaningful imprecision appears in the abstract's use of \\\"probabilistic GANs\\\"; replacing this with \\\"traditional\\\" or \\\"classical\\\" GANs would better orient readers to what is being contrasted. The paper is well-written, and the hyperparameter exploration on MNIST is thorough. The semi-supervised results, showing that contrastive samples from the generator improve classification performance, represent a meaningful empirical contribution. That said, the relationship between this work and related efforts that broaden discriminator scope or use generative networks for contrastive samples in energy-based models—including arXiv 1511.06390 (Springenberg) and arXiv 1606.03439 (Kim and Bengio)—is not made sufficiently clear, and the manuscript should address this more directly. Theorem 2 also requires attention: the proof does not adequately establish why a Nash equilibrium exists for the proposed system. Separately, the phrase \\\"Several papers were presented\\\" in the prior work section should be replaced with more precise language. The discriminator is instantiated as an autoencoder, optionally paired with a PT regularizer that penalizes high cosine similarity among generated samples in a minibatch. However, the Inception score results are computed using vanilla EBGAN, making it difficult to isolate the PT regularizer's contribution to sample quality. Visual inspection alone is insufficient to conclude that EBGAN outperforms DC-GAN on LSUN and CelebA; a more systematic quantitative comparison is needed. The use of an Inception model trained on natural images to compute KL scores for MNIST is also questionable, and the authors should clarify whether this evaluation protocol is appropriate for that dataset. The model is novel and its core claims are supported by the optimality proofs and sample quality evidence across MNIST, LSUN, and CelebA. The concerns raised here—particularly around positioning relative to related work, the isolated assessment of the PT regularizer, and the quantitative comparison against DC-GAN—are real and should be addressed in revision.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The paper shows the relation between stochastically perturbing the parameter of a model at training time, and considering a mollified objective function for optimization. Aside from Eqs. 4-7 where I found hard to understand what the weak gradient g exactly represents, Eq. 8 is intuitive and the subsequent Section 2.3 clearly establishes for a given class of mollifiers the equivalence between minimizing the mollified loss and training under Gaussian parameter noise. The authors then introduce generalized mollifiers to achieve a more sophisticated annealing effect applicable to state-of-the-art neural network architectures (e.g. deep ReLU nets and LSTM recurrent networks). The resulting annealing effect can be counterintuitive: In Section 4, the Binomial (Bernoulli?) parameter grows from 0 (deterministic identity layers) to 1 (deterministic ReLU layers), meaning that the network goes initially through a phase of adding noise. This might effectively have the reverse effect of annealing. Annealing schemes used in practice seem very engineered (e.g. Algorithm 1 that determines how units are activated at a given layer consists of 9 successive steps). Due to the more conceptual nature of the authors contribution (various annealing schemes have been proposed, but the application of the mollifying framework is original), it could have been useful to reserve a portion of the paper to analyze simpler models with more basic (non-generalized) mollifiers. For example, I would have liked to see simple cases, where the perturbation schemes derived from the mollifier framework would be demonstrably more suitable for optimization than a standard heuristically defined perturbation scheme.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper makes three main contributions: a method for automatically generating large-scale pseudo training data for zero pronoun resolution, a two-step learning process that transfers knowledge from a large dataset to domain-specific data, and a tagging scheme that differentiates unknown words. The work is clearly written, and the experiments are well designed. The proposed approach achieves an absolute improvement of 3.1% F-score on OntoNotes 5.0 over state-of-the-art systems, which is a strong empirical result supporting the paper's central claims. Several clarifications are needed before the work can be fully evaluated. When the model predicts a pronoun but the head word of the candidate antecedent is not a pronoun, it is unclear how the system identifies the correct antecedent from prior context; this case should be addressed explicitly rather than left to the reader's inference. A related issue arises when the system predicts a noun that does not appear in the preceding content, since the mechanism for handling such cases is not described. It would also be useful to evaluate the system in two stages—first assessing the quality of model predictions and then measuring antecedent-finding performance separately—as this would clarify where errors originate. Finally, the choice of an attention-based neural network over alternative architectures is not justified, and a brief rationale for this design decision would strengthen the paper.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"1) Summary This paper proposes an end-to-end hybrid architecture to predict the local linear trends of time series. A temporal convnet on raw data extracts short-term features. In parallel, long term representations are learned via a LSTM on piecewise linear approximations of the time series. Both representations are combined using a MLP with one hidden layer (in two parts, one for each stream), and the entire architecture is trained end-to-end by minimizing (using Adam) the (l2-regularized) euclidean loss w.r.t. ground truth local trend durations and slopes. 2) Contributions + Interesting end-to-end architecture decoupling short-term and long-term representation learning in two separate streams in the first part of the architecture. + Comparison to deep and shallow baselines.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes a sequence transduction model combining statistical alignment methods with an encoder-decoder architecture, evaluated on morphological inflection generation datasets. The experiments are thorough, the writing is clear, and the analysis of learned representations across both hard and soft attention models adds useful depth. However, the improvements over the soft attention baseline are modest on several tasks, which raises questions about how substantially the proposed combination advances the state of the art beyond prior approaches. More significantly, the absence of comparisons to Chorowski et al. 2015 and Jailty et al. 2016 makes it difficult to situate the contribution accurately within the existing literature on hard monotonic attention and related encoder-decoder methods. Including these baselines would considerably strengthen the empirical claims.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper is an interesting contribution, primarily in its generalization of Weston's et al's work on semi-supervised embedding method. You have shown convincingly that it can work with multiple architectures, and with various forms of graph. And the PubMed results are good. To improve the paper in the future, I'd recommend 1) relating better to prior work, and 2) extending your exploration of its application to graphs without features.\", \"sampled\": \"The idea of using eye-tracking features as a proxy for cognitive load in sentiment analysis is interesting, and the discussion of features and comparison of feature sets is clear and helpful. The feasibility discussion in Section 7 is also appreciated. However, mixing domains such as movie reviews and tweets may weaken the evaluation; separating them could improve both prediction and the interpretability of results.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Dear authors, I've read the interesting paper and learned nice ideas. Now I've found somewhat incorrect mention in your paper. In Introduction, it is said that trees are provided with sentences in Socher et al.\", \"sampled\": \"This is a solid engineering contribution. The paper extends PixelCNN to condition on part keypoints and segmentation masks, which is the primary contribution over existing work. Experiments cover CUB, MHP, and MS-COCO datasets with quantitative baselines using text- and structure-conditional pixel log-likelihood.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes the Neural Graph Machine that adds in graph regularization on neural network hidden representations to improve network learning and take the graph structure into account. The proposed model, however, is almost identical to that of Weston et al. 2012. As the authors have clarified in the answers to the questions, there are a few new things that previous work did not do: 1. they showed that graph augmented training for a range of different types of networks, including FF, CNN, RNNs etc. and works on a range of problems. 2. graphs help to train better networks, e.g. 3 layer CNN with graphs does as well as than 9 layer CNNs 3. graph augmented training works on a variety of different kinds of graphs. However, all these points mentioned above seems to simply be different applications of the graph augmented training idea, and observations made during the applications. I think it is therefore not proper to call the proposed model a novel model with a new name Neural Graph Machine, but rather making it clear in the paper that this is an empirical study of the model proposed by Weston et al. 2012 to different problems would be more acceptable.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper introduces Hessian-weighted k-means for fixed-length quantization and shows that uniform quantization combined with variable-length Huffman coding performs well, reporting compression ratios of 51.25, 22.17, and 40.65 on LeNet, a 32-layer ResNet, and AlexNet. The Hessian weighting is well motivated and can be efficiently approximated via the Adam optimizer, removing the need to tune per-layer compression rates. The paper is easy to follow. While the uniform quantization plus Huffman coding contribution is not novel, its effectiveness is a useful finding. Hessian-weighted k-means stands out as a practical alternative to vanilla k-means solved by Lloyd’s algorithm, and the Adam-based approximation strengthens the method’s scalability. The explanation of storing additional bits for layer indication is unclear. The paper is a bit too long. The reviewer suggests clarifying the overhead of per-layer codebook storage, as the current description leaves ambiguity about how much extra cost this introduces in practice. The strong compression ratios for LeNet, the 32-layer ResNet, and AlexNet support the approach’s promise, but a clearer accounting of storage overhead would help readers assess the tradeoffs between the fixed-length Hessian-weighted scheme and variable-length uniform quantization with Huffman coding.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Hello Authors, Congratulations on the acceptance of the paper. I've just reread parts of the revised paper and noticed a few things that you might want to consider and change before the camera-ready deadline. * You now include a reference to KLIEP after Eqn. (16), but this procedure is in fact known as least-squares importance estimation. * in turn, Eqn. (14) is actually more akin to KLIEP, the main difference being the use of the unnormalised form of the KL-divergence. So I think you meant to put the KLIEP reference here. Further comment on making Eqn.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper tackles a well-motivated problem: transfer in reinforcement learning across morphologically distinct agents. The core idea—using a shared skill to learn abstract feature spaces that then support transferring an unshared skill—is a promising hypothesis. The approach draws on methods that construct shared latent spaces, such as canonical correlation analysis, and the authors implement a three-layer neural network to learn these spaces. A simple CCA-based method is used as a baseline, but the evaluation would benefit from comparisons to more current techniques like manifold alignment or kernel CCA.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper discusses a method to learn interpretable hierarchical template representations from given data. The authors illustrate their approach on binary images. The paper presents a novel technique for extracting interpretable hierarchical template representations based on a small set of standard operations. It is then shown how a combination of those standard operations translates into a task equivalent to a boolean matrix factorization. This insight is then used to formulate a message passing technique which was shown to produce accurate results for these types of problems. Summary: ——— The paper presents an novel formulation for extracting hierarchical template representations that has not been discussed in that form. Unfortunately the experimental results are on smaller scale data and extension of the proposed algorithm to more natural images seems non-trivial to me. Quality: I think some of the techniques could be described more carefully to better convey the intuition. Clarity: Some of the derivations and intuitions could be explained in more detail. Originality: The suggested idea is reasonable but limited to binary data at this point in time. Significance: Since the experimental setup is somewhat limited according to my opinion, significance is hard to judge. Details: ——— 1. My main concern is related to the experimental evaluation.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper is clearly written and presents solid experimental results that offer new insights. The work merits publication and discussion given its significance. The paper empirically demonstrates that deep convolutional models require both depth and convolution: shallow student networks need multiple convolutional layers to match the accuracy of deep convolutional teachers on CIFAR-10. The experiments use L2 regression on logit outputs to train student networks, and the hyperparameter selection is thorough. These results partially contradict conclusions from Ba and Caruana 2014. That prior study suggested a different relationship between depth and model capacity, but the current findings challenge that view with careful evidence. The experimental design is rigorous, and the L2 regression on logits provides a clear framework for comparing student and teacher networks. The contradiction with Ba and Caruana 2014 is handled with appropriate nuance, and the paper does not overstate its claims. CIFAR-10 is a somewhat toy dataset with only 10 classes. The reviewer suggests testing on a more challenging problem such as ImageNet, which would strengthen the generalizability of the findings and address concerns about dataset simplicity. The paper merits publication. Its empirical contributions and the challenge to prior work make it a valuable addition to the literature on model distillation and network depth.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper claim that, when supported by a number of backup workers, synchronized-SGD actually works better than async-SGD. The paper first analyze the problem of staled updates in async-SGDs, and proposed the sync-SGD with backup workers. In the experiments, the authors shows the effectiveness of the proposed method in applications to Inception Net and PixelCNN. The idea is very simple, but in practice it can be quite useful in industry settings where adding some backup workders is not a big problem in cost. Nevertheless, I think the proposed solution is quite straightforward to come up with when we assume that each worker contains the full dataset and we have budge to add more workers. So, under this setting, it seems quite natural to have a better performance with the additional backup workers that avoid the staggering worker problem.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper presents a new dataset for evaluating outlier detection in word representations, covering five languages and constructed automatically from the Wikidata hierarchy. The reviewer recognizes that well-designed evaluation resources are valuable to the community. In the dataset, entities sharing a category form a cluster, and outliers are sampled at varying tree distances. A significant concern is the lack of discussion and comparison with existing approaches, especially since the proposed evaluation closely relates to entity typing, which the paper does not address. Heuristics are introduced to filter out uninteresting clusters, but the reviewer remains unconvinced that the dataset offers clear advantages over current resources. The reviewer also notes that LREC may be a more suitable venue than ICLR, given the resource-oriented nature of the work and the need for thorough comparison with prior methods.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper introduces CoopNets, an algorithm which trains a Deep-Energy Model (DEM, the “descriptor”) with the help of an auxiliary directed bayes net, e.g. “the generator”. The descriptor is trained via standard maximum likelihood, with Langevin MCMC for sampling. The generator is trained to generate likely samples under the DEM in a single, feed-forward ancestral sampling step. It can thus be used to shortcut expensive MCMC sampling, hence the reference to “cooperative training”. The above idea is interesting and novel, but unfortunately is not sufficiently validated by the experimental results. First and foremost, two out of the three experiments do not feature a train /test split, and ignore standard training and evaluation protocols for texture generation (see [R1]). Datasets are also much too small. As such these experiments only seem to confirm the ability of the model to overfit. On the third in-painting tasks, baselines are almost non-existent: no VAEs, RBMs, DEM, etc which makes it difficult to evaluate the benefits of the proposed approach. In a future revision, I would also encourage the authors to answer the following questions experimentally. What is the impact of the missing rejection step in Langevin MCMC (train with, without ?). What is the impact of the generator on the burn-in process of the Markov chain (show sample auto-correlation). How bad is approximation of training the generator from ({\\\\tilde{Y}, \\\\hat{X}) instead of ({\\\\tilde{Y}, \\\\tilde{X}) ? Run comparative experiments. The paper would also greatly benefit from a rewrite focusing on clarity, instead of hyperbole (“pioneering work” in reference to closely related, but non-peer reviewed work) and prose (“tale of two nets”). For example, the authors fail to specify the exact form of the energy function: this seems like a glaring omission. PROS: + Interesting and novel idea CONS: - Improper experimental protocols - Missing baselines - Missing diagnostic experiments [R1] Heess, N., Williams, C. K. I., and Hinton, G. E. (2009). Learning generative texture models with extended fields of-experts.\", \"sampled\": \"The idea of deriving actions from Class Activation Mapping visualizations is a creative adaptation of Zhou et al. 2016 to a reinforcement learning setting. Using tic-tac-toe as a toy problem to study CNN attention offers a controlled way to examine concept learning under weak supervision, and the CNN’s perfect accuracy on the labeling task provides a reliable basis for interpreting the visualizations. However, the paper does not compare to RL work that uses saliency maps to analyze network behavior, such as Simonyan et al. arXiv 2013, Wang et al. ICML 2016, and Zahavy et al. ICML 2016. It is unclear whether the distinction between “what will happen” and “what to do” is meaningful here, since the labels may coincide in many board states. This issue weakens the central claim that the CNN learns separate representations for prediction and decision. The dataset contains 1029 states with 18 classification labels covering two players and nine positions, but details about the test set composition and label distribution are missing. Without that information, it is hard to judge whether the perfect accuracy reflects genuine concept learning or label overlap. The claim that saliency maps cannot activate on constant-intensity grid squares is presented without supporting evidence or citation. The reviewer is also unsure how CAM is implemented without specifying a class of interest, since the original formulation requires a target class to generate the activation map. This technical ambiguity makes it difficult to evaluate the validity of the derived action policies. The experiments are novel but lack clear conclusions and broader context, leaving the contribution somewhat isolated from related work in deep reinforcement learning. The presentation would benefit from moving the Section 7 results alongside earlier discussions to improve narrative flow and reader comprehension. The meaning of “10^3 next to RCO” in Table 1 is unclear and should be clarified for reproducibility. Das et al.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The main concern raised in each review is the lack of a direct comparison of SSPNs to existing semantic segmentation benchmarks. We address this point here and then respond to each reviewer's other points directly. We’ve also made significant revisions to the structure and writing of the paper, particularly in the inference section, which we hope more clearly explains the intuitions behind InferSSPN. Despite not having comparisons on semantic segmentation benchmarks, SSPNs are a significant step forward in the state-of-the-art on scene understanding. SSPNs are (provably) more expressive than (and contain as a special case) MRFs and SPNs as commonly used for scene understanding and semantic segmentation, meaning that SSPNs are a richer model class than those already in use. Despite this increase in expressivity and complexity, InferSSPN is a very efficient (approximate) inference algorithm for the problem of parsing an image with respect to a grammar, which has been addressed by many previous works, but their approaches have all been very restrictive or inefficient. InferSSPN provably achieves low-order-polynomial time complexity for a problem with a state space that has size exponential in both the number of pixels and the height of the grammar. While the first version of the paper did not include accuracy results, as we thought the energy results were sufficient, we’ve since updated the paper to include all accuracy, energy, and time complexity results for all test cases. These show empirically that InferSSPN provides exponential improvements in time-complexity with no loss in accuracy relative to alpha-expansion, which provably returns local optima that are within a constant factor of the global optimum. Further, while we have characterized SSPNs in terms of scene understanding, they are as broadly applicable as SPNs and MRFs, and scene understanding is just the initial application we chose to focus on. Future applications may include activity recognition or social network modeling. Nonetheless, this paper presents a powerful new representation for efficiently modeling and reasoning about scenes at multiple levels of abstraction and is well within the scope of ICLR, which includes “hierarchical models” and “applications to vision” in its list of relevant topics. Learning SSPNs is important – both for evaluating SSPNs on existing semantic segmentation benchmarks and for their adoption in general – and is something that we are already working on. Unfortunately, there do not yet exist any image grammars or relevant datasets with which to train SSPNs, so learning requires either creating such datasets or learning with a large number of hidden variables. This is ongoing and future work.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes using deep neural network features to construct a tree of life, which the reviewer finds creative but disconnected from biological practice. At its core, the approach reduces to hierarchical clustering of visual categories and offers little methodological novelty for the machine learning community. The reviewer doubts whether the resulting tree reveals anything meaningful about deep features or addresses a genuine need among biologists. The methodology lacks biological grounding in both construction and evaluation, relying on WordNet as an assumed ground-truth hierarchy without justifying why a lexical ontology should reflect evolutionary inheritance. By treating visual category relationships as strictly tree-like, the approach ignores the possibility that a graph structure would be more appropriate for capturing the complex associations present in visual data. The claim of human-competitive performance rests on qualitative comparisons rather than rigorous quantitative benchmarks, which substantially weakens the evidence. The reviewer questions whether recovering biological inheritance from purely visual inputs constitutes a meaningful scientific task, given that the visual world is not organized as a hierarchy even if the biological world can reasonably be defined as one. The absence of engagement with established phylogenetic methods or domain-specific evaluation protocols further undermines the work’s relevance to its stated audience. The overall impression is of a solution in search of a problem, with the application to biology serving more as a metaphorical framing than as a substantive contribution. The reviewer articulates this mismatch by observing that our visual world is not a hierarchy, while our biological world can reasonably be defined as one, highlighting the conceptual gap between the tree structure imposed on visual categories and the evolutionary relationships the paper claims to recover. This tension is never resolved through empirical validation or theoretical argument. Instead, the paper leans on the familiarity of ImageNet categories and WordNet structure without interrogating whether these resources are fit for the biological task at hand. Reliance on deep neural networks as feature extractors does not compensate for the weak evaluation design, since the features themselves are never analyzed in terms of what they encode about biological similarity. The qualitative results do not persuade the reviewer that the recovered tree captures inheritance rather than superficial visual resemblance, and the absence of quantitative metrics makes it impossible to assess the claimed human-competitive performance. The work would benefit from a clearer problem definition grounded in actual biological questions and from evaluation protocols that measure success against established phylogenetic baselines. Without such grounding, the paper remains an intriguing but unconvincing exploration that does not advance understanding of deep features or biological classification. The reviewer sees limited value in its current form for either the computer vision or the computational biology community.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper proposed a very interesting idea of using cognitive features for sentiment analysis and sarcasm detection. More specifically, the eye-movement patterns of human annotators are recorded to derive a new set of features. The authors claim that this is the first work to include cognitive features into the NLP community. Strength: 1. The paper is generally well written and easy to follow 2. Very interesting idea which may inspire research in other NLP tasks. Weakness: 1. The motivation of using cognitive features for sentiment analysis is not very well justified. I can imagine these features may help reflect the reading ease, but I don't see why they are helpful in detecting sentiment polarities. 2. The improvement is marginal after considering cognitive features by comparing Sn+Sr+Gz with Sn+Sr. 3.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes an extension of PixelCNN conditioned on text and spatially-structured constraints, similar to Reed 2016a but built on PixelCNN rather than a GAN. The authors do not claim that the conditional PixelCNN outperforms a conditional GAN, and the technical novelty is incremental. The paper would benefit from a discussion of the trade-offs between PixelCNN and GAN-based approaches, as well as an analysis of training and generation time. No quantitative comparisons are included, and given that, more than three visual examples would be appropriate. The generated results look reasonably good and show sufficient diversity, though color mistakes remain an issue. PixelCNN sampling requires O(N) time rather than O(1), which may limit experiments to 32x32 resolution. Some explanations were provided in the author response. The reviewer leans toward acceptance given the relevance to the ICLR community and the potential for future investigation.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper develops Submodular Sum Product Networks (SSPNs) and an efficient inference algorithm for approximately computing the most probable labeling of variables in the model. The main application in the paper is on scene parsing. In this context, SSPNs define an energy function with a grammar component for representing a hierarchy of labels and an MRF for encoding smoothness of labels over space. To perform inference, the authors develop a move-making algorithm, somewhat in the spirit of fusion moves (Lempitsky et al., 2010) that repeatedly improves a solution by considering a large neighborhood of alternative segmentations and solving an optimization problem to choose the best neighbor. Empirical results show that the proposed algorithm achieves better energy that belief propagation of alpha expansion and is much faster. This is generally a well-executed paper. The model is interesting and clearly defined, the algorithm is well presented with proper analysis of the relevant runtimes and guarantees on the behavior. Overall, the algorithm seems effective at minimizing the energy of SSPN models. Having said that, I don't think this paper is a great fit for ICLR. The model is even somewhat to the antithesis of the idea of learning representations, in that a highly structured form of energy function is asserted by the human modeller, and then inference is performed. I don't see the connection to learning representations. One additional issue is that while the proposed algorithm is faster than alternatives, the times are still on the order of 1-287 seconds per image, which means that the applicability of this method (as is) to something like training ConvNets is limited. Finally, there is no attempt to argue that the model produces better segmentations than alternative models. The only evaluations in the paper are on energy values achieved and on training data.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper builds directly on the framework introduced by Weston (2016) and applies end-to-end memory networks to a dialogue setting where a teacher provides feedback. The dialogue itself is quite constrained and functions more like a question answering task than a genuine conversational exchange. The main contribution lies in demonstrating that the model also works when data is collected online—that is, using the agent’s own policy rather than a fixed policy defined beforehand. This shift to an online data collection paradigm via Mechanical Turk is a meaningful practical step. It requires only modest algorithmic changes, primarily the introduction of epsilon-greedy exploration, which limits the model novelty. The writing is clear and the paper tests several interesting models. The experimental design is easy to follow and the comparisons are laid out straightforwardly. The reliance on Mechanical Turk for human feedback is appropriate for the scale of the study. The central point the authors stress is that the model also works with online data collection, and this fairly captures where the novelty actually resides. I see value in showing that the approach can function outside a static offline dataset. However, adding human testing data alone is not sufficient for a conference paper at a venue like ICLR, and the core architecture remains largely unchanged from prior work. The paper is borderline. The online collection setup is a genuine contribution, but the dialogue setting is too narrow to fully convince as a dialogue learning advance. I updated the score after the author response emphasized real-world implementation as a contribution, which helped clarify the practical motivation.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This work proved to be a controversial submission. This paper has two main components: 1) a neural framework TBRU/DRAGNN, 2) experimental results on some NLP tasks. Generally there was lack of consensus about the originality of (1) and a general feeling that even if there are aspect of novelties, that the paper was lacking clarity about is contributions One reviewer was an outlier, highlighting the benefit of the ability \\\"incorporate dynamic recurrent connections through the definition of the transition system\\\" which is claimed to be really novel. Others claim this is specific to the framework used. Negative reviewers felt that (1) is probably not novel within itself and represents a slight departure from stack-lstm. The controversy here is whether DRAGNN is simple \\\"software engineering with no inherent \\\"free things\\\" that would lead to impact within the community. This question of impact is also inherent to (2), in particular whether the authors really got new benefit of using DragNN or whether these are \\\"reimplementations of things in the literature\\\".\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents an interesting idea connected to John McCarthy's foundational work on learning from advice, implemented here using a deep learning controller. While the direction is promising, the description of the proposed architecture is confusing, making it difficult to assess the technical novelty of the controller. The experiments are conducted in a simplistic 2D maze environment, which is not sufficient to demonstrate the system's capabilities. A more convincing evaluation would test the approach on more complex and varied tasks, such as Atari games or physics simulators. The current results do not provide enough evidence that the system can handle advice more complex than that shown in the simple maze. For instance, the system should be able to interpret high-level instructions in a more dynamic environment, analogous to telling a human to \\\"keep a certain distance from the car in front.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes to learn groups of orthogonal features in a convnet by penalizing correlation among features in each group. The technique is applied in the setting of image classification with “privileged information” in the form of foreground segmentation masks, where the model is trained to learn orthogonal groups of foreground and background features using the correlation penalty and an additional “background suppression” term. Pros: Proposes a “group-wise model diversity” loss term which is novel, to my knowledge. The use of foreground segmentation masks to improve image classification is also novel. The method is evaluated on two standard and relatively large-scale vision datasets: ImageNet and PASCAL VOC 2012. Cons: The evaluation is lacking. There should be a baseline that leaves out the background suppression term, so readers know how much that term is contributing to the performance vs. the group orthogonal term. The use of the background suppression term is also confusing to me -- it seems redundant, as the group orthogonality term should already serve to suppress the use of background features by the foreground feature extractor. It would be nice to see the results with “Incomplete Privileged Information” on the full ImageNet dataset (rather than just 10% of it) with the privileged information included for the 10% of images where it’s available. This would verify that the method and use of segmentation masks remains useful even in the regime of more labeled classification data. The presentation overall is a bit confusing and difficult to follow, for me. For example, Section 4.2 is titled “A Unified Architecture: GoCNN”, yet it is not an overview of the method as a whole, but a list of specific implementation details (even the very first sentence). Minor: calling eq 3 a “regression loss” and writing “||0 - x||” rather than just “||x||” is not necessary and makes understanding more difficult -- I’ve never seen a norm regularization term written this way or described as a “regression to 0”. Minor: in fig. 1 I think the FG and BG suppression labels are swapped: e.g., the “suppress foreground” mask has 1s in the FG and 0s in the BG (which would suppress the BG, not the FG). An additional question: why are the results in Table 4 with 100% privileged information different from those in Table 1-2? Are these not the same setting? The ideas presented in this paper are novel and show some promise, but are currently not sufficiently ablated for readers to understand what aspects of the method are important.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper introduces NewsQA, a machine comprehension dataset of over 100,000 question-answer pairs from 10,000 CNN news articles. However, the work appears to have been prepared in a hurry, which raises concerns about the quality of the dataset and the soundness of the analysis. The primary issue is the potential for noise introduced during data collection. I question the decision not to show the news article to crowdworkers as they created questions. Human performance on NewsQA is reported at 74.9%, which seems low for this type of task. The paper suggests the SQuAD dataset may not be hard enough, based on the rapid improvement in machine performance on that benchmark. Machine performance on SQuAD was 51% at submission, but the leaderboard's best performance had reached 82% by the time of this paper's writing. A significant discrepancy exists regarding human performance on SQuAD, which undermines confidence in the current paper's evaluation. The authors measure human performance on SQuAD between 70.5% and 82%, while the original SQuAD paper reported a range of 80.3% to 90.5%. The reported 74.9% human performance on NewsQA is concerning. This low figure might indicate noise from the data collection methodology rather than the true difficulty of the questions. The performance gap between humans and machines on NewsQA is stated to be 25.3% F1, but this gap is less meaningful if the human baseline itself is compromised by noise. To avoid collecting trivial questions, a better design could have required crowdworkers to create questions that an existing, state-of-the-art QA system cannot answer correctly. The NewsQA dataset is built upon a larger subset of the CNN/Daily Mail corpus than SQuAD, using 12,000 articles compared to SQuAD's 500. The paper was submitted to EMNLP’16, and the dataset is constructed from CNN news articles from the CNN/Daily Mail corpus. The work seems rushed, raising doubts about the dataset's quality. The low human performance on NewsQA (74.9%) is a major point of concern, as it might reflect noise from the data collection process rather than inherent task difficulty, especially given the discrepancy between the authors' measurement of human performance on SQuAD (70.5-82%) and what was originally reported (80.3-90.5%).\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This work proposes a method for segmenting target generation sequence that is learned as part of the model. Generally all reviewers found this paper novel and interesting. Pros: - Quality: The paper is both containing \\\"with solid theoretically justified\\\" and \\\"present(s) nice improvements over character based result\\\". One reviewer asked for the \\\"the experimental study could be more solid.\\\" - Impact: methods like \\\"BPE\\\" are now somewhat standard hacks in seq2seq modeling. This type of model could be potentially impactful at disrupting. - Clarity: Reviewers found the work to be a \\\"clearly written paper\\\" Cons: - Some of the reviewers were not as enthuthiastic about this work compared to other papers. There were several comments asking for further experimental results.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents a recurrent neural network for stochastic volatility modeling of financial time series. The model parameters are learned by maximizing the marginal likelihood via a variational approximation. The authors report that their model outperforms parametric GARCH models and a Gaussian process volatility model on both synthetic and real-world data. An RNN-based inference network is used to approximate the posterior distribution of the latent variables. The proposed method seems technically sound, with one exception in the description of the inference model. There appears to be an error in equation (19). The inference model as described is performing filtering, not smoothing, as the posterior distribution for a given time step depends only on past and current observations.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In this interesting paper the authors explore the idea of using an ensemble of multiple discriminators in generative adversarial network training. This comes with a number of benefits, mainly being able to use less powerful discriminators which may provide better training signal to the generator early on in training when strong discriminators might overpower the generator. My main comment is about the way the paper is presented. The caption of Figure 1. and Section 3.1 suggests using the best discriminator by taking the maximum over the performance of individual ensemble members. This does not appear to be the best thing to do because we are just bound to get a training signal that is stricter than any of the individual members of the ensemble.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes a simple method for augmenting neural language models with an external cache that acts as a copying mechanism for recent words. The mechanism is efficient, scales to large cache sizes, and improves language modeling performance on RNN baselines without requiring long-term backpropagation. The experiments are convincing. A key insight is that using past hidden states as keys and the current hidden state as a query is effective without backpropagation tuning. The simplicity and efficiency of this approach are its main strengths. My primary criticism is the work's incremental nature, which might make it a better fit for a more specialized NLP venue. However, the simple yet effective approach is valuable to the community and warrants publication.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper presents three improvements to the standard LSTM architecture used in many neural NLP models: Monte Carlo averaging, embed average pooling, and residual connections. Each of the modifications is trivial to implement, so the paper is definitely of interest to any NLP researchers experimenting with deep learning. With that said, I am concerned about the experiments and their results. The residual connections do not seem to consistently help performance; on SST the vertical residuals help but the lateral residuals hurt, and on IMDB it is the opposite. More fundamentally, there need to be more tasks than just sentiment analysis here. I'm not quite sure why the paper's focus is on text classification, as any NLP task using an LSTM encoder could conceivably benefit from these modifications. It would be great to see a huge variety of tasks like QA, MT, etc., which would really make the paper much stronger.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes a generalization of layered neural architectures, the \\\"Multiagent System for Layer Free Network,\\\" where nodes communicate via messages. However, the presentation is unclear, making it difficult to assess the work's contribution. The low-level details of the proposed method are particularly confusing. A central argument seems circular: the paper demonstrates that the layer-free architecture can perform the same computation as a standard MLP, which calls into question the novelty and practical benefit of the approach. For instance, while the work aims to move beyond matrix-vector products, Algorithm 4 uses them for both the forward and backward passes. The \\\"locking\\\" nature of the computation also suggests that an asynchronous implementation would be equivalent to a standard one, undermining a key motivation for the new architecture.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper presents an multi-view learning algorithm which projects the inputs of different views (linearly) such that the neighborhood relationship (transition probabilities) agree across views. This paper has good motivation--to study multi-view learning from a more information retrieval perspective. Some concerns: -- The time complexity of the algorithm in its current form is high (see last paragraph of page 4). This might be the reason why the authors have conducted experiments on small datasets, and using linear projections. -- The proposed method does have some nice properties, e.g., it does not require the projections to have the same dimension across views (I like this). While it more directly models neighborhood relationship than CCA based approaches, it is still not directly optimizing typical retrieval (e.g., ranking-based) criteria. On the other hand, the contrastive loss in Hermann and Blunsom. Multilingual Distributed Representations without Word Alignment. ICLR 2014. is certainly a relevant \\\"information retrieval\\\" approach, and shall be discussed and compared with. My major concern about this paper is the experiments. As I mentioned in my previous comments, there are limited cases where linear mapping is more desirable than nonlinear mappings for dimension reduction. While the authors have argued that linear projection may provide better interpretability, I have not found empirical justification in this paper. Moreover, one could achieve interpretability by visualizing the projections and see what variations of the input is reflected along certain dimensions; this is commonly done for nonlinear dimension reduction methods.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes an unsupervised, encoder-decoder model for learning generic feature representations from graph structures. The model is trained to predict random walk sequences, adapting an approach from natural language processing to map structurally similar subgraphs to nearby points in the embedding space. While the model is reported to achieve good results on several real-world classification tasks, the evaluation has some weaknesses. The paper lacks clarity on certain constraints, such as the relationship \\\"l_min >= (n_k - 1), … >= l_max\\\". The primary concern is the comparison to existing methods, many of which use task-specific supervised techniques. As another reviewer noted, the same classifier should be used for all compared methods to ensure a fair evaluation. The presentation of results in Sections 3 and 4 needs clarification. For example, the comparison to Deep Graph Kernels in Section 3.2 is not well-justified, and the discussion comparing the method with LINE and ECFP in Section 3.3 is underdeveloped. A more direct comparison under equivalent conditions would be necessary to substantiate the claims of achieving state-of-the-art results. The authors state their goal is to develop a generic, unsupervised feature representation, which is a valuable contrast to supervised approaches requiring feature engineering, but the current evaluation does not fully support the claimed advantages.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The authors provide an approach to policy learning skills via a stochastic neural network representation. The overall idea seems novel, and the reviewers are in agreement that the method provides an interesting take on skill learning. The additional visualization presented in response to the reviewer comments were also quite useful. Pros: + Conceptually nice approach to skill learning in RL + Strong results on benchmark tasks Cons: - The overall clarity of the paper could still be improved: the actual algorithmic section seems to present the methods still at a very high level, though code release may also help with this (it would not be at all trivial to re-implement the method just given the paper).\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents a small but effective extension to the Neural Programmer-Interpreter that enables recursion, allowing the model to generalize better from fewer execution traces. The work is a good example of how a modest addition can improve the practicality of a machine learning method. I also appreciated the use of the original DeepMind paper's notation, which aids readability. The generalization proofs, however, are somewhat vague, and it is not clear how the proof technique would extend to a continuous input space. The authors should also state whether they intend to release the source code.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We thank all reviewers for their thoughtful comments and suggestions. We’ve uploaded a revision with an expanded introduction which clarifies the motivation for BiGAN and the theory in Section 3 in the context of feature learning. Note that our theoretical arguments go beyond simply showing that BiGAN maintains the properties of the original GAN framework. In particular, we show that the BiGAN objective is equivalent to that of an “L0 autoencoder”, encouraging the encoder and generator to invert one another (Theorem 3). If fully optimized, the encoder and generator are inverses of one another almost everywhere (Theorem 2). We believe that these insights are quite important to understanding BiGAN’s behavior and its use as a model for feature learning. We hope that the revised introduction explains this better. With respect to the ImageNet visual feature learning evaluation, our results show BiGAN is state-of-the-art among purely unsupervised learning methods. The methods that outperform BiGAN (e.g., Doersch et al.) are all based on “self-supervision,” requiring the design of a domain-specific supervised prediction task, and some (Agrawal et al.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes a hybrid neural network architecture that interleaves Recurrent Neural Networks within a convolutional network, reporting promising results for classification and semantic labeling. The core idea is simple and effective, and the paper is generally well-written and easy to follow. The experiments appear sound, and the description of the main concept is clear. However, the manuscript could be strengthened by addressing several points that are currently unclear or lack sufficient detail. The authors state, \\\"we introduce more nonlinearities (through the convolutional layers and...\\\". In section 2, they should clarify their claim that an RNN cannot be used with convolutional layers, which are linear operators. The paper also mentions adding nonlinearities \\\"into the computational block\\\" in section 3, but it is not specified which block is being referenced. Finally, the authors should explain why a cited 164-layer network from 2012 with a 5.39% error rate is considered a \\\"false reference,\\\" especially when compared to a 15-layer network from the same year that achieved a 5.46% error rate. Addressing these points would improve the paper's clarity and impact.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Dear all, Below are some more responses to two points raised. Both responses are reflected in a new version of the paper I just uploaded. The following has changed in the paper: - section 3.1 (self-similar nonlinearities) is new - the final paragraph of section 3 is new - section 7.2 (proof of new proposition) is new The paper is now longer than 8 pages. Having looked at many other submitted papers, it appears that the 8 page requirement is not very serious. If the area chair would prefer an 8 page paper, I can move some more stuff to the appendix. ** Reducing the number of hyperparameters ** Two questions that came up throughout the reviews is whether our method reduces the number of hyperparameters and whether there is an automatic way to set lambda. One advantage of nonparametric networks is that instead of having one hyperparameter per layer (size) there is one hyperparameter for the entire network (lambda) that controls size. It is worth pointing out that this reduction in complexity is not arbitrary.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents a weakly supervised approach for cross-lingual named entity recognition (NER). The authors use conditional random fields, maximum entropy, and neural network models. They propose two main weakly supervised methods: one that creates automatically labeled NER data for a target language, and a second that projects word embeddings to the target language using a variant of Mikolov's proposal. To combine the outputs from the projection-based methods, the authors design two co-decoding schemes. The authors then propose two methods for combining the output from these schemes: a probability-based method and a ranking-based method. They report that the ranking-based method improves performance by 1.5 to 4.4 F-score points on an open dataset and by 4.2 points on an in-house dataset. The motivation for this work is that human annotation of NER data is expensive and difficult, and the proposed approaches use no human-annotated NER data for the target language. The evaluation, conducted on both in-house and open NER data, shows that the combined systems outperform three other weakly supervised approaches.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: The DMC task seems like a good test of understanding language and vision. I like that the task has a clear evaluation metric. The failure of the caption generation model on the DMC task is quite interesting. This result further demonstrates that these models are good language models, but not as good at capturing the semantics of the image. - Weaknesses: The experiments are missing a key baseline: a state-of-the-art VQA model trained with only a yes/no label vocabulary. I would have liked more details on the human performance experiments. How many of the ~20% of incorrectly-predicted images are because the captions are genuinely ambiguous? Could the data be further cleaned up to yield an even higher human accuracy? - General Discussion: My concern with this paper is that the data set may prove to be easy or gameable in some way. The authors can address this concern by running a suite of strong baselines on their data set and demonstrating their accuracies. I'm not convinced by the current set of experiments because the chosen neural network architectures appear quite different from the state-of-the-art architectures in similar tasks, which typically rely on attention mechanisms over the image. Another nice addition to this paper would be an analysis of the data set. How many tokens does the correct caption share with distractors on average? What kind of understanding is necessary to distinguish between the correct and incorrect captions?\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes a model to compute embeddings for symbolic expressions, with the goal of placing semantically equivalent expressions close together in the vector space. The model uses a recursive neural network that mirrors the expression's parse tree, trained with a max-margin loss on a dataset of known equivalences. A technique called subexpression forcing is used to encourage compositional embeddings. The method outperforms the baselines on the authors' datasets, and a PCA visualization showing that negating an expression corresponds to negating its embedding vector is a nice result. While the model outperforms tf-idf, the experimental setting seems contrived, raising questions about the real-world availability of the required training data. A significant concern is how ground truth equivalence was determined for the training set, especially since the general problem of determining equivalence for Thue systems is undecidable. The paper mentions word2vec and glove, but the primary baseline appears to be tf-idf, which is not a strong point of comparison. A key limitation is that the model does not handle variable names and assumes distinct variables refer to different entities, limiting its applicability to more complex symbolic reasoning tasks. The manuscript should also clarify the design choice behind the \\\"COMBINE\\\" operation, as it is not a true residual connection. An error was identified in Table 3, where two expressions listed as non-equivalent appear to be equivalent. The authors should correct this. A minor formatting issue was also noted, as the spacing around the caption for Figure 4 is inconsistent.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper looks at how to train if there are significant label noise present. This is a good paper where two main methods are proposed, the first one is a latent variable model and training would require the EM algorithm, alternating between estimating the true label and maximizing the parameters given a true label. The second directly integrates out the true label and simply optimizes the p(z|x). Pros: the paper examines a training scenario which is a real concern for big dataset which are not carefully annotated. Cons: the results on mnist is all synthetic and it's hard to tell if this would translate to a win on real datasets. - comments: Equation 11 should be expensive, what happens if you are training on imagenet with 1000 classes? It would be nice to see how well you can recover the corrupting distribution parameter using either the EM or the integration method. Overall, this is an OK paper.\", \"sampled\": \"The authors introduced the Prediction Difference Analysis method to visually represent deep neural network responses to specific inputs, highlighting areas supporting or challenging classification. This method offers a new perspective on visualizing neural network behaviors by addressing limitations of prior techniques, significantly contributing to understanding classifier decision processes. Emphasizing the interpretability of neural network decisions is crucial for improving model performance, especially in critical domains like medicine. The method's applicability was demonstrated on diverse datasets, including ImageNet and MRI brain scan data, showcasing its versatility and potential impact. Particularly significant features that exhibit dynamic output and are less predictable by other means are identified in the visualization process. The method also introduces a conditional distribution-based visualization approach to identify more salient regions within input data, enhancing clarity in neural network decision-making. By presenting visualization results across multiple networks and classes, the authors provide a comprehensive view of the method's capabilities, highlighting its potential for widespread application and utility in understanding complex neural network behaviors.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper was easy to read, the main idea was presented very clearly. The main points of the paper (and my concerns are below) can be summarized as follows: 1. synchronous algoriths suffer from some struggeling nodes, for which the algorithm has to wait. From my own experience, this has never happend for me on e.g. Amazon EC2 cloud, however, it happens on our own cluster at my university, if the cluster is shared and some users make some nodes very busy.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper presents the rare entity prediction task, aiming to predict missing entities in web documents by leveraging lexical resources. This task is complex due to varied language styles and a multitude of rare entities. Models that incorporate hierarchical LSTMs along with external knowledge demonstrate enhanced performance. The paper also introduces a dataset tailored for rare entity prediction, highlighting the significance of external knowledge through baseline comparisons. The HierEnc model effectively gathers evidence from different contexts, with marginal performance gains observed when using larger contexts. The proposed task and dataset have the potential to advance the field significantly.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper clearly lays out the recipe for a family of invertible density models, explores a few extensions, and demonstrates the overall power of the approach. The work is building closely on previous approaches, so it suffers a bit in terms of originality. However, I expect this overall approach to become a standard tool for model-building. The main weakness of this paper is that it did not explore the computational tradeoffs of this approach against related methods. However, the paper already had a lot of content.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper presents SAEN, an architecture tailored for extracting representations from social network data. SAEN utilizes hierarchical decompositions to partition input graphs into various layers of entities, aiding in effective learning. Particularly, the method for domain compression minimizes memory consumption and accelerates processing, thereby improving the model's utility. The empirical analysis on authentic social network datasets illustrates SAEN's outperforming capabilities over current cutting-edge approaches. Furthermore, the manuscript highlights improvements in Section 2, emphasizing the enhancement of descriptions and terms such as H-hierarchical decompositions.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The paper proposes a new exploration scheme for reinforcement learning using locality-sensitive hashing states to build a table of visit counts which are then used to encourage exploration in the style of MBIE-EB of Strehl and Littman. Several points are appealing about this approach: first, it is quite simple compared to the current alternatives (e.g. VIME, density estimation and pseudo-counts). Second, the paper presents results across several domains, including classic benchmarks, continuous control domains, and Atari 2600 games. In addition, there are results for comparison from several other algorithms (DQN variants), many of which are quite recent. The results indicate that the approach clearly improves over the baseline. The results against other exploration algorithms are not as clear (more dependent on the individual domain/game), but I think this is fine as the appeal of the technique is its simplicity. Third, the paper presents results on the sensitivity to the granularity of the abstraction. I have only one main complaint, which is it seems there was some engineering involved to get this to work, and I do not have much confidence in the robustness of the conclusions. I am left uncertain as to how the story changes given slight perturbations over hyper-parameter values or enabling/disabling of certain choices.\", \"sampled\": \"The translation scheme allows iterative improvements, resembling a globally normalized, undirected model in Section 3. It is a significant contribution to machine translation. The editing procedure may not handle word insertion or removal effectively, potentially impacting model performance. More thorough discussion on model limitations is needed for a comprehensive understanding. The paper lacks in-depth analysis and connections to prior work in NLP and ML, crucial for contextualizing the research. The model in Section 4 conditions on the true context of a position in the target, offering a unique approach. The fixed window for target word representation raises certain model assumptions that should be explicitly addressed. The paper suggests potential model variants for global translation or post-editing, indicating avenues for future research. Clarity on training data is necessary to assess replicability and generalizability. Further discussion on the fixed window usage is recommended to enhance interpretability. Exploring the relationship between training objective and pseudo likelihood could deepen understanding of learning mechanisms. Clarification on model conditions in Section 4 is needed to elucidate decision-making processes. In conclusion, the paper provides valuable insights into machine translation and decoding algorithms, but improvements in discussing limitations, prior work connections, and training data clarity would enhance the research impact.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"We made a major revision to the paper. Here is the summary of the main changes: Experiments: -- Providing **ground truth causality** evaluation (by hiring Robert Chen, a clinical expert). (Figure 1, Table 2) The results show a significant gain in causality discovery using the proposed method. -- Including a new **publicly available dataset** (MIMIC III) to ensure reproducibility of the results. (Table 1 and Figure 4) -- Adding standard deviation to the results. Novel methodology: -- Providing a neural network architecture that allows multivariate causal hypothesis generation, see Section 2.3 and Figure 2.b. -- Providing the ground truth causality evaluation for the causal hypotheses generated by our algorithm. (Figure 5b) Writeup: -- Adding more discussion on the related works (Sections 2.1 and 2.2). ---- In the introduction and Section 2.2 we clearly describe the novelty of this paper with respect to the papers listed by David Lopez-Paz and the reviewers. -- Revising the background section 2.\", \"sampled\": \"The study introduces a new perspective by defining a \\\"Binary autoencoder\\\" as a \\\"biconvex optimization problem.\\\" This framing allows for a unique exploration of the optimization landscape in autoencoding. Understanding \\\"pairwise correlations\\\" between encoded and decoded bits is crucial to the methodology. By effectively utilizing these correlations, the model can gain valuable insights that enhance the optimization and performance of the binary autoencoder system. The research focuses on deriving an optimal decoder, highlighting its form as the \\\"logistic of the Lagrangian W.\\\" This explicit formulation establishes a solid mathematical basis for comprehending the decoder's role in the binary autoencoder model. Additionally, the study underscores practical implementation by demonstrating how \\\"weights are learned through convex optimization.\\\" This approach highlights the efficiency and effectiveness of using convex optimization techniques in training the binary autoencoder model.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The paper proposed to analyze several recently developed machine readers and found that some machine readers could potentially take advantages of the entity marker (given that the same marker points out to the same entity). I usually like analysis papers, but I found the argument proposed in this paper not very clear. I like the experiments on the Stanford reader, which shows that the entity marker in fact helps the Stanford reader on WDW. I found that results rather interesting. However, I found the organization and the overall message of this paper quite confusing. First of all, it feels that the authors want to explain the above behavior with some definition of the “structures”. However, I am not sure that how successful the attempt is. For me, it is still not clear what the structures are. This makes reading section 4 a bit frustrating. I am also not sure what is the take home message of this paper. Does it mean that the entity marking should be used in the MR models? Should we design models that can also model the entity reference at the same time?\", \"sampled\": \"The proposal introduces a method to reduce the computational complexity of CNNs by pruning at the feature map and kernel levels. This approach targets decreasing computational demands by selectively removing less critical components. Pruning feature maps allows reducing layer width without requiring sparse representation, streamlining computational processes within CNNs efficiently. Kernel pruning transitions the dense connectivity pattern to a sparse one, creating a more streamlined network structure that can improve computational efficiency. The proposed strategy selects the best pruned network through N random pruning trials, emphasizing the iterative process's importance in optimizing network performance with efficient pruning techniques. To achieve a 40% reduction in computational complexity, about 100 random pruning masks are generated and evaluated to identify the most effective configuration for enhancing network efficiency and performance. Additionally, inducing over 85% sparsity in convolution layers on the CIFAR-10 dataset results in less than a 1% increase in the misclassification rate, demonstrating the pruning technique's effectiveness in maintaining performance levels despite increased sparsity.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The paper proposes a novel approach for learning visual servoing based on Q-iteration. The main contributions of the paper are: 1. Bilinear dynamics model for predicting next frame (features) based on action and current frame 2. Formulation of servoing with a Q-function that learns weights for different feature channels 3. An elegant method for optimizing the Bellman error to learn the Q-function Pros: + The paper does a good job of exploring different ways to connect the action (u_t) and frame representation (y_t) to predict next frame features (y_{t+1}). They argue in favour of a locally connected bilinear model which strikes the balance between computation and expressive ability. Cons: - While, sec. 4 makes good arguments for different choices, I would have liked to see more experimental results comparing the 3 approaches: fully connected, convolutional and locally connected dynamics. Pros: + The idea of weighting different channels to capture the importance of obejcts in different channels seems more effective than treating errors across all channels equally. This is also validated experimentally, where unweighted performance suffers consistently. + Solving the Bellman error is a difficult problem in Q-learning approaches. The current paper presents a solid optimization scheme based on the key-observation that scaling Q-function parameters does not affect the best policy chosen.\", \"sampled\": \"The introduction identifies a common limitation in beam search algorithms: the tendency to generate similar candidates, which can lead to search errors and a lack of diversity. Diverse Beam Search (DBS) addresses this issue by promoting a wider range of beam candidates, thereby improving output diversity. DBS accomplishes this by adjusting the search objective function to assess and incentivize diverse beam candidates, thus expanding the exploration of potential solutions. In contrast to traditional beam search methods, DBS prioritizes diversity over minimizing search errors, underscoring the importance of exploring a broader spectrum of candidate solutions for enhanced overall performance. By effectively balancing the exploration and exploitation of the search space, DBS excels in identifying optimal top 1 solutions, yielding superior outcomes compared to standard beam search. The algorithm offers an efficient solution with minimal additional computational or memory demands relative to traditional beam search techniques, making it an appealing choice for diverse decoding tasks. In applications like image captioning, machine translation, conversation generation, and visual question answering, DBS demonstrates its versatility and effectiveness in producing diverse and accurate outputs. Overall, DBS surpasses conventional beam search approaches and other diverse decoding methods, establishing itself as a valuable tool for improving output diversity and quality across various tasks.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper presents a dialogue agent where the belief tracker and the dialogue manager are jointly optimised using the reinforce algorithm. It learns from interaction with a user simulator. There are two training phases. The first is an imitation learning phase where the system is initialised using supervising learning from a rule-based model. Then there is a reinforcement learning phase where the system has jointly been optimised using the RL objective. - Strengths: This paper presents a framework where a differentiable access to the KB is integrated in the joint optimisation. This is the biggest contribution of the paper. - Weaknesses: Firstly, this is not a truly end-to-end system considering the response generation was handcrafted rather than learnt. Also, their E2E model actually overfits to the simulator and performs poorly in human evaluation. This begs the question whether the authors are actually selling the idea of E2E learning or the soft-KB access. The soft-KB access actually brings consistent improvement, however the idea of end-to-end learning not so much. The authors tried to explain the merits of E2E in Figure 5 but I also fail to see the difference.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper presents gated self-matching networks for reading comprehension and question answering, introducing innovative neural network design approaches. By utilizing gated attention-based recurrent networks, the model effectively captures question-aware passage representations, improving comprehension accuracy. The incorporation of a self-matching attention mechanism further enhances passage representations, refining the model's understanding of textual content. Additionally, the use of pointer networks for answer position identification demonstrates a strategic choice to enhance the system's ability to locate relevant information within passages. The model's exceptional performance is evident with a 71.3% exact match on the SQuAD hidden test set, showcasing the effectiveness of the proposed techniques. Through ensemble modeling, the results are significantly boosted to a remarkable 75.9% exact match on the SQuAD dataset, securing the model's top ranking on the SQuAD leaderboard. The paper's strength lies in its robust empirical analysis, particularly the comprehensive ablation study conducted. While the results are impressive, an area for potential improvement is providing more detailed exposition on the ensemble model creation process. Additionally, conducting a qualitative analysis to evaluate the components' impact on question answering could further enhance the paper's contributions to the field.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The paper presents a technique to combine deep learning style input-output training with search techniques to match the input of a program to the provided output. Orders of magnitude speedup over non-augmented baselines are presented. Summary: ——— The proposed search for source code implementations based on a rather small domain specific language (DSL) is compelling but also expected to some degree Quality: The paper is well written. Clarity: Some of the derivations and intuitions could be explained in more detail but the main story is well described. Originality: The suggested idea to speed up search based techniques using neural nets is perfectly plausible. Significance: The experimental setup is restricted to smaller scales but the illustrated improvements are clearly apparent. Details: ———— 1. The employed test set of 100 programs seems rather small. in addition the authors ensure that the test set programs are semantically disjoint from the training set programs.\", \"sampled\": \"The study examines vocabulary selection strategies to reduce the computational load of neural machine translation. It covers techniques from basic word co-occurrence analysis to Support Vector Machines. The optimal strategy notably improves coverage in a full-vocabulary model. However, critics highlight the limited scope in section 4.3, particularly in training phase vocabulary selection. Reviewers also point out the lack of innovation, mainly relying on conventional methods. A comparison with Mi et al.'s 2016 work reveals the study's lack of originality. Reviewers question the use of smoothing in word co-occurrence measurements in section 2.1, calling for more clarity and justification. The study shows a significant 90% decrease in decoding time on CPUs and a 25% reduction in training time for WMT15 and WMT16 tasks. Notably, the study achieves a decoding speed of 140 words per second for English-German translation on a single CPU core, demonstrating the computational efficiency of specific vocabulary selection strategies.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper introduces a neural network architecture and training procedure for predicting the speed of a vehicle several seconds into the future based on video and vehicle state input. The architecture allows several RNNs to compete to make the best predictions, with only the best prediction receiving back propagation training at each time step. Preliminary experimental results show that this scheme can yield reduced prediction error. It is not clear how the best-performing RNN is chosen for each time point at test time. That is, how is the “integrated prediction” obtained in Fig. 7? Is the prediction the one with minimum error over all of the output layers? If so, this means the prediction cannot be made until you already know the value to be predicted. It seems possible that a larger generic RNN might be able to generate accurate predictions. If I understand correctly, the competitive architectures have many more parameters than the baseline. Is the improved performance here due to the competitive scheme, or just a larger model? A large amount of additional work is required to sustain the claim that this scheme is successfully extracting driver ‘intentions’. It would be interesting to see if the scheme, suitably extended, can automatically infer the intention to stop at a stop sign vs slowing but not stopping due to a car in front, say, or to pass a car vs simply changing lanes. Adding labels to the dataset may enable this comparison more clearly. More generally, the intention of the driver seems more related to the goals they are pursuing at the moment; there is a fair amount of work in inverse reinforcement learning that examines this problem (some of it in the context of driving style as well).\", \"sampled\": \"The paper examines the impact of feature penalty regularization on capturing new concepts, emphasizing its centering effect on feature representation tailored for low-shot learning scenarios. The theoretical analysis highlights how feature penalty controls the VC dimension to prevent overfitting with limited training examples. The study suggests using last-layer feature penalty as neural network regularization, showing that dropping the weight per example in equations yields comparable results, indicating its adaptability across different settings. Furthermore, it establishes a link between feature penalty, Batch Normalization, and weight decay, hinting at potential synergies in model optimization strategies. Experimental validation on synthetic datasets, Omniglot, and ImageNet supports the theoretical analysis. However, a concern is raised about the performance comparison on Omniglot, noting a 2% performance gap compared to Matching Networks, which is not listed in Table 1. This discrepancy requires clarification and potentially further analysis for robustness. The proposed idea's simplicity is acknowledged, but questions arise about its completeness and effectiveness compared to existing methods. While the concept of 'soft BN' is intriguing, Batch Normalization alone outperforms feature penalty. Enhancements or modifications are suggested to improve the method's efficacy and competitiveness in practical applications. In conclusion, the paper offers valuable insights into applying feature penalty regularization in neural networks, particularly in low-shot learning scenarios. Addressing concerns about performance comparisons and methodological clarity can enhance the paper's impact and provide a more comprehensive understanding of the proposed approach.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The authors propose a method that extends the non-linear two-view representation learning methods, and the linear multiview techniques, and combines information from multiple sources into a new non-linear representation learning techniques. In general, the method is well described and seems to lead to benefits in different experiments of phonetic transcription of hashtag recommendation. Even if the method is mostly a extension of classical tools (the scheme learns a (deep) network for each view essentially), the combination of the different sources of information seems to be effective for the studied datasets. It would be interesting to add or discuss the following issues: - what is the complexity of the proposed method, esp. the representation learning part? - would there by any alternative solution to combine the different networks/views? That could make the proposed solution more novel. - the experimental settings, especially in the synthetic experiments, should be more detailed. If possible, the datasets should be made available to encourage reproducibility. - the related work is far from complete unfortunately, especially from the perspective of the numerous multiview/multi-modal/multi-layer algorithms that have been proposed in the literature, in different applications domaines like image retrieval or classification, or bibliographic data for example (authors like A. Kumar, X. Dong, Ping-Yu Chen, M. Bronstein, and many others have proposed works in that direction in the last 5 years).\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"Ngram2vec\\\" introduces an innovative concept of incorporating ngrams into four representation methods. This idea is a fresh addition to the field of natural language processing, expanding the application of word embeddings and ngram co-occurrence statistics. The authors' unique method of constructing a co-occurrence matrix is a significant contribution to machine learning techniques. However, the paper falls short in providing a quantitative evaluation of the embeddings for the learned bigrams, leaving the effectiveness of the proposed method somewhat uncertain. The results presented lack consistent trends, which may affect the robustness and reliability of the approach. The paper also outlines two count collection strategies, but their effectiveness is not sufficiently demonstrated. Several algorithms, including SGNS, GloVe, PPMI matrix, and SVD factorization, are mentioned without clear explanation of their integration and relevance to the proposed method, which may confuse readers. The paper is also marred by numerous grammatical and spelling errors that detract from its overall quality and readability. It would be beneficial for the authors to revisit their results, provide more supporting evidence, and conduct a thorough proofreading to rectify the language errors.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The proposed approach consists in a greedy layer wise initialization strategy for a deep MLP model, which is followed by global gradient-descent with dropout for fine-tuning. The initialization strategy uses a first randomly initialized sigmoid layer for dimensionality expansion followed by 2 sigmoid layers whose weights are initialized by Marginal Fisher Analysis (MFA) which learns a linear dimensionality reduction based on a neighborhood graph constructed using class label information (i.e. supervised dimensionality reduction). Output layer is a standard softmax layer. The approach is thus to be added to a growing list of heuristic layer-wise initialization schemes. The particular choice of initialization strategy, while reasonable, is not sufficiently well motivated in the paper relative to alternatives, and thus feels rather arbitrary. The paper lacks clarity in the description of the approach: MFA is poorly explained with undefined notations (in Eq. 4, what is A? It has not been properly defined); the precise use of alluded denoising in the model is also unclear (is there really training of an additional denoting objective, or just input corruption?). The question of the (arguably mild) inconsistency of applying a linear dimensionality reduction algorithm, that is trained without any sigmoid, and then passing its learned representation through a sigmoid is not even raised. This, in addition to the fact that sigmoid hidden layers are no longer commonly used (why did you not also consider using RELUs?). More importantly I suspect methodological problems with the experimental comparisons: the paper mentions using *default* values for learning-rate and momentum, and having (arbitrarily?) fixed epoch to 400 (no early stopping?) and L2 regularization to 1e-4 for some models. *All* hyper parameters should always be properly hyper-optimized using a validation set (or cross-validation) including early-stopping, and this separately for each model under comparison (ideally also including layer sizes). This is all the more important since you are considering smallish datasets, so that the various initialization strategies act mainly as different indirect regularization schemes: they thus need to be carefully tuned. This casts serious doubts as to the amount of hyper-parameter tuning (close to none?) that went into training the alternative models used for comparison.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"Neural Code Completion\\\" offers an interesting approach to code completion using deep learning methods. However, the claim of novelty in using deep learning for automatic code completion isn't entirely accurate. Prior works have employed these techniques, and it's crucial to acknowledge them to accurately portray the contribution of the current paper. While the paper does cite several works in its literature review, some are misrepresented. For example, \\\"Toward Deep Learning Software Repositories\\\" (MSR’15) and \\\"Code Completion with Statistical Language Models\\\" (PLDI’14) are not properly acknowledged, which could potentially mislead readers about the current state of the field and this paper's contributions. The authors report a 3.8 point improvement on next non-terminal prediction tasks and a 0.5 point improvement on next terminal prediction tasks. However, how these results compare to existing methods is not clearly outlined. The study uses JavaScript, a dynamically typed language with 44 types of nodes. It's important to note that the choice of programming language can significantly influence the complexity and outcomes of code completion tasks. The paper employs a non-comparable prediction task for non-terminal symbols, which isn't a common practice. Using a comparable task is crucial to validate the results and facilitate clear comparison with other models. Furthermore, the authors do not compare their model to the state-of-the-art model, PHOG, presented at OOPSLA’16. This model considers a prediction task with over 150 labels, while the current paper does not disclose the number of labels considered in their task. To avoid overstating their contribution, the authors need to substantiate their claim of novelty by correctly citing previous work. It's also advisable for the authors to compare their model with the state-of-the-art, particularly the PHOG model and the work presented at OOPSLA 2016. This would provide a clear benchmark and a more thorough evaluation of their approach.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We first thank all the reviewers for the interesting comments they provided. All three reviewers agree on the potential importance of the proposed algorithm, but they also suggest that more experimental data are needed to demonstrate that GA3C achieves results in the same ballpark as the original A3C. This turns out to be particularly important in the light of the fact that the policy lag in GA3C can potentially introduce instabilities, as noted by Reviewer #1. We have included a better description of this in Section 4.4 of the revised paper, as well as analyzed it in a newly added Section 5.3, while providing additional experiments to verify the game performance of GA3C and its stability. As pointed out by the reviewers and already highlighted in the paper, establishing a validation protocol that allows comparing the final scores achieved by GA3C with the scores achieved by other training algorithms is complex or even infeasible, as many implementation details are not shared, as well as validation protocols are poorly reproducible. Beyond that, the final score (still reported in Table 3) tells only part of the story, and therefore we espouse the idea shared by Reviewer #1 and #3 of reporting learning curves as a function of the time. The newly added Fig. 6 and 7 show the learning curves for GA3C and several games as a function of the time. The reader can easily compare these graphs with those reported in Mnih’s paper, as well as in other venues mentioned by the reviewers (e.g. github). The score vs. time curves show a similar dynamic for GA3C and A3C, although it is also important mentioning that curves reported in Mnih’s paper represent the average of the top 5 curves in a set of 50 with various learning rates, which we are unable to match due limitations in available computational resources. Since systems, system loads, environments and hyper-parameters are so widely varying, we also believe that releasing the GA3C code (which we plan to do in the next days) will allow other researchers to systematically evaluate the performances of GA3C and facilitate a fair comparison with new techniques. Reviewer #1’s main concern is about instabilities introduced in GA3C.\", \"sampled\": \"The study \\\"Modularized Morphing of Neural Networks\\\" explores the complex issue of network morphism, a topic closely tied to knowledge transfer. The authors tackle this intricate subject with both creativity and rigor, proposing an innovative method for morphing a convolutional layer into an arbitrary module of a neural network. This approach represents a significant advancement in network morphism, building upon ideas presented in previous works such as \\\"Net2Net\\\" and \\\"NetMorph.\\\" The authors introduce two atomic morphing operations to facilitate this transformation, a concept not thoroughly explored in the aforementioned papers. This fresh perspective broadens the possibilities for network morphism and contributes to the field's existing knowledge. Practical morphing solutions for both simple and complex modules are presented, showcasing the method's versatility. However, the explanation and presentation of these solutions could be clearer to ensure comprehensive understanding. The paper's relevance is underscored by the experiments conducted on variants of ResNet, a crucial aspect of the study. These experiments test the proposed method's effectiveness when applied to different neural network structures. Despite the well-planned and meticulously conducted experiments, the results could be bolstered by more extensive data. Despite its merits, the paper was not accepted for presentation at the ICML 2016 conference. However, this decision should not diminish the value of the research or the potential impact of the proposed method on the field of network morphism. As the reviewer noted, the paper explores a novel problem of knowledge transfer at a macroscopic level, highlighting its originality and relevance. The reviewer suggested that the experiments could be improved to make the results more convincing and practically useful, with the validity of the experiments potentially enhanced by providing more evidence and thorough analysis. In conclusion, \\\"Modularized Morphing of Neural Networks\\\" offers a novel approach to network morphism. Despite its initial non-acceptance at the ICML 2016 conference, the research and proposed method hold significant potential. The authors' exploration of morphing a convolutional layer into an arbitrary module of a neural network is a substantial contribution to the field. The reviewer's suggestion to improve the experiments provides a valuable avenue for the authors to make their results more convincing and practically useful in the future. The paper's importance and potential impact should not be overlooked despite its initial non-acceptance.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This approach taken in this paper is topical, especially since the importance of sampling and generating diverse samples is increasingly discussed in work on generative models. There were several concerns from reviewers, in three areas particularly: connection and comparison to related work; lack of clarity and understanding of the paper; experiments that are not sufficiently convincing. These have been addressed to some extent by the authors, discussing in more detail the related work, especially in connection to Rezende et al., and GSN of Bengio et al., and with improved figures. But these points are still of concern especially in terms of assessing sample diversity in relation to much of the recent work on richer variational posterior methods and other techniques. For these reasons, the paper is not yet ready for acceptance at this years conference.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper introduces a unique method for creating an evaluation dataset for non-English compositional distributional semantics models, with a particular emphasis on the Polish language. The authors have adapted the process used to assemble the SICK corpus, making necessary adjustments to meet language-specific needs and overcome resource constraints. As a result, they have successfully generated a Polish evaluation dataset, which includes 10K human-annotated sentence pairs for semantic relatedness and entailment. Nevertheless, the paper has a few shortcomings. It fails to discuss the feasibility of transferring this framework to languages other than Polish. This omission is particularly concerning considering the framework's reliance on a dependency parser, which may not be easily accessible or could perform poorly for certain languages.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The paper presents a nice idea for using a sequence of progressively more expressive neural networks to train a model. Experiments are shown on CIFAR10, parity, language modeling to show that the methods performs well on these tasks. However, as noted by the reviewers, the experiments do not do a convincing enough job. For example, the point of the model is to show that optimization can be made easier by their concept, however, results are presented on depths that are considered shallow these days. The results on PTB are also very far from SOTA. However, because of the novelty of the idea, and because of the authors ratings, I'm giving the paper a pass. I strongly encourage the authors to revise the paper accordingly for the camera ready version. Pros: - interesting new idea - shows gains over simple baselines. Cons: - not a very easy read, I think the paper was unnecessarily dense exposition of a relatively simple idea. - experiments are not very convincing for the specific type of problem being addressed.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"Pruning Convolutional Neural Networks for Resource Efficient Inference\\\" presents a new approach to pruning convolutional kernels in neural networks. This method uniquely applies a criterion based on the mathematical principle of Taylor expansion. It demonstrates superior performance compared to other pruning criteria for large Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs). The method is also applicable to transfer learning, where large pretrained networks are tailored for specific tasks. One of the paper's key strengths is the theoretical reduction it can achieve. In particular, pruning can lead to more than a 10x decrease in the size of adapted 3D-convolutional filters. This significant reduction highlights the proposed method's potential to enhance the efficiency of resource-demanding inference tasks. The method's effectiveness is further confirmed through testing on the extensive ImageNet dataset. Its successful application to fine-grained classification tasks, as demonstrated by its performance on the Birds-200 and Flowers-102 datasets, further validates the paper's contributions.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We thank the reviewers for several helpful suggestions. We first address the issues raised by two or more reviewers here. As a general comment, we would like to emphasize again the exploratory nature of our work. We aimed to explore the following question: is it possible for a recurrent model to (cheaply) determine a priori how much computation it is going to need at each time step? We designed our model and experimental section with this interrogation in mind, and believe that we were able to provide a positive answer. As regards the wall-clock time, the computational gains are currently, to quote Reviewer 2 conceptual only, as we are using the Torch GPU kernels, and optimizing the hardware implementation was beyond the scope of this project. Our goal was to make the possible implications of the result clear, but we will clarify the language of the paper to reflect this more accurately. For each experiment, we tried a range of \\\\bar{m} values for the Variable Computation recurrent network and a range of hidden state dimensions for its vanilla counterpart. While we only provided one result on PTB, Figure 5 illustrates how both of these choices affect the model’s computation and perplexity on a Czech and a German dataset; note that the Variable Computation curve for perplexity/number of operations is always under the Vanilla one, meaning fewer operations for a better perplexity. We will provide the same figure for PTB. Our original reasoning for only providing vanilla RNN results on PTB was that we were trying to compare apples to apples, so to speak, and to reinforce the message that we are considering a paradigm which can be extended to other architectures. However, we have since successfully applied our method to the GRU architecture, which gives us the Variable Computation Gated Recurrent Unit (VCGRU).\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"Deep Character-Level Neural Machine Translation By Learning Morphology\\\" introduces an innovative adaptation of encoder-decoder neural MT, marking a considerable advancement in the field of neural machine translation and morphology learning. The paper outlines a six-level system designed to learn effective morphological tokenizations and produce satisfactory translation results after just one training epoch. The authors' decision to release their code and final learned models is commendable and beneficial to the research community. However, there appears to be an inconsistency in the paper's depiction of its system. The text refers to a 6-level architecture, but the diagram shows fewer than 6 layers. This discrepancy could potentially confuse readers and future researchers attempting to replicate the study. Additionally, the paper does not explicitly state which WMT test and development sets underpin the reported results. This lack of clarity complicates comparisons with other systems in the field. The results also do not align with the state-of-the-art results for other types of MT system, suggesting room for improvement. A more comprehensive background context could have enriched the paper. For example, the authors could have offered more information on the specific tasks of En-Fr and En-Cs translation, and how their proposed model enhances performance and efficiency in these tasks. Moreover, the instructions in the software README could be more detailed. While the authors' decision to make their code and models available is laudable, incomplete or unclear instructions could hinder others from using or building upon their work. This is particularly important given the unique approach to character-level translation using an encoder-decoder architecture. The system's performance after just one training epoch is impressive, achieving a higher BLEU score than the bpe-based model. This suggests the system's efficiency and its ability to learn effective morphological tokenizations. However, it's worth noting that these results are based on the newstest2015 dataset for testing and the newstest2013 dataset for development.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Previous literature uses data-derived adjacency matrix A to obtain neighbors to use as foundation of graph convolution. They propose extending the set of neighbors by additionally including nodes reachable by i 1 gives improvement strong enough to justify an extra hyper-parameter.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents quasi-recurrent neural networks (QRNNs), a novel sequence modeling approach that alternates between convolutional layers and a recurrent pooling function. This design notably deviates from traditional recurrent neural networks (RNNs) by forgoing trainable recurrent layers.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Hi, I'm one of the authors of Bowman et al. 2016, and I wanted to point out that while our description of the difficulties in training RNN type models with global latent variables occurs in a section called \\\"Optimization Challenges,\\\" we make similar observations as you do in your paper, namely that having a very powerful decoder model like an RNN can result in the model ignoring the global latent variable even at the optimum (perhaps the section should have more generally been called \\\"Learning Challenges\\\"). Specifically, we found that without using \\\"word dropout\\\" in the decoder, which effectively weakens the decoder model and forces the global variable to encode more information, we would not learn a useful latent variable. In our Figure 3, we show the results of using different amounts of word dropout and how the proportional split between the global latent code KL and the p(x|z) likelihood changes, even though the total lower bound always gets worse.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper titled \\\"A Transition-Based Directed Acyclic Graph Parser for UCCA\\\" introduces UCCA as a target representation for semantic parsing, a significant development in the field of semantic representation. The authors also present a transition-based parser for inference into UCCA, which, while derived from existing transition-based schemes, includes valuable extensions that make it applicable in this domain. This adaptation highlights the paper's strength in dealing with complex concepts such as DAG structures, discontinuous structures, and non-terminal nodes. The empirical evaluation of the parser also shows promising results when compared to several credible baselines. However, the paper lacks a multilingual evaluation, a crucial aspect considering the global application of semantic parsers. The authors' use of the term 'grammarless' to describe their model is also somewhat unclear and could benefit from further explanation. Despite these areas for improvement, the paper provides a robust exploration of a transition-based parser using UCCA and bidirectional LSTMs.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This paper introduces a hierarchical clustering method using learned CNN features to build 'the tree of life'. The assumption is that the feature similarity indicates the distance in the tree. The authors tried three different ways to construct the tree: 1) approximation central point 2) minimum spanning tree and 3) multidimensional scaling based method. Out of them, MDS works the best. It is a nice application of using deep features. However, I lean toward rejecting the paper because the following reasons: 1) All experiments are conducted in very small scale. The experiments include 6 fish species, 11 canine species, 8 vehicle classes. There are no quantitative results, only by visualizing the generated tree versus the wordNet tree. Moreover, the assumption of using wordNet is not quite valid. WordNet is not designed for biology purpose and it might not reflect the true evolutionary relationship between species. 2) Limited technical novelty. Most parts of the pipeline are standard, e.g. use pretrained model for feature extraction, use previous methods to construct hierarchical clustering. I think the technical contribution of this paper is very limited.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper, \\\"Learning through Dialogue Interactions by Asking Questions,\\\" delves into the potential of dialogue agents to learn through question-asking interactions. The concept is compelling and the study's design, which incorporates a simulator and synthetic tasks within the movie domain, is noteworthy. The range of environment-scenario combinations tested provides a broad spectrum of experimental designs. However, some tasks may be overly simplistic, possibly diminishing the complexity and depth of the learning process. This brings me to the observation that the synthetic environment used seems somewhat constrained, lacking the depth typically found in human-chatbot interactions. I do acknowledge that the 'ground-up' approach, which begins with simple environments, is indeed a valuable area of exploration. In the second half of my review, I want to point out that the simulation of the initial error a learner could make during dialogue is somewhat limited. This could restrict the comprehensive testing of the dialogue agents' learning abilities. Additionally, the paper could strengthen its validity by incorporating human experiments.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This paper gives a theoretical motivation for tieing the word embedding and output projection matrices in RNN LMs. The argument uses an augmented loss function which spreads the output probability mass among words with close word-embedding. I see two main drawbacks from this framework: The augmented loss function has no trainable parameters and is used for only for regularization. This is not expected to give gains with large enough datasets. The augmented loss is heavily “engineered” to produce the desired result of parameter tying. It’s not clear what happens if you try to relax it a bit, by adding parameters, or estimating y~ in a different way. Nevertheless the argument is very interesting, and clearly written. The simulated results indeed validate the argument, and the PTB results seem promising. Minor comments: Section 3: Can you clarify if y~ is conditioned on the t example or on the entire history. Eq. 3.\", \"sampled\": \"The study, \\\"Adjusting for Dropout Variance in Batch Normalization and Weight Initialization,\\\" presents an innovative weight initialization technique to mitigate the variance caused by dropout. This unique approach contributes fresh insights to the ongoing dialogue about dropout variance and its impact on neural networks. Moreover, the authors suggest modifications to Batch Normalization to accommodate the dropout variance, a significant move that paves the way for incorporating dropout variance adjustments into prevailing normalization methods. The proposed techniques were evaluated using the CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 datasets, both of which are well-regarded and frequently employed in related studies. The findings showed improved accuracy over conventional methods, such as Monte Carlo dropout, underscoring the effectiveness of the new method. The potential impact of this study is substantial, given its relevance to a variety of models regularly used in the field.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The authors all agree that the theory presented in the paper is of high quality and is promising but the experiments are not compelling. The reviewers are concerned that the presented idea and connections to existing methods, while neat, may not be impactful as the promise of the theory does not bear out in practice. One reviewer is concerned that the presented theory is still not useful, stating that the \\\"information bottleneck thus only becomes meaningful when the capacity of the encoding network is controlled in some measurable way, which is not discussed in the paper\\\". In general, they seem to agree that the experimental evaluation is still preliminary and unfinished. As such, it would seem that the authors could make the paper far more compelling by demonstrating more compelling improvements on benchmark experiments and submitting to a future conference.\", \"sampled\": \"This is a positive review of Optimization as a Model for Few-Shot Learning, and the submission appears to merit acceptance based on its LSTM-based meta-learner for few-shot optimization and its claimed competitiveness with deep metric-learning methods for few-shot learning. The main issue I would like the authors to address concerns mini-imageNet. The dataset construction seems to rely on the statement that “we create our own version of the Mini-Imagenet dataset by selecting a random 100 classes from ImageNet and picking 600 examples of each class.” This raises a reproducibility concern: do the reported results depend materially on the random choice of classes and examples, or are they stable enough that another construction of mini-imageNet from ImageNet would support the same conclusions about the proposed LSTM meta-learner.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"1. the QA model is not novel, very similar to the existing model. 2. The IQA model is very confusing. If it needs human interactive in the training process, how could it be practical to ask human to join the training in each iteration? It sounds impractical. If the human interactive questions are predefined, then it is not interactive at all, since it is not based on the current state of model output.\", \"sampled\": \"Gated-Attention Readers for Text Comprehension addresses cloze-style document question answering and reports state-of-the-art results on three benchmarks. Several points need clarification. The WDW dataset should be described more clearly. In particular, how are multi-token candidate answers handled, especially for Who Did What, and how does this affect comparisons with CNN and Daily Mail results? The submitted Gated-Attention (GA) Reader appears to differ from prior GA readers in three respects, but the contribution of each change is not isolated.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: The paper is thoroughly written and discusses its approach compared to other approaches. The authors are aware that their findings are somewhat limited regarding the mean F values. - Weaknesses: Some minor orthographical mistakes and some repetive clauses. In general the paper would benefit if the sections 1 and 2 would be shortened to allow the extension of sections 3 and 4. The main goal is not laid out clearly enough, which may be a result of the ambivalence of the paper's goals. - General Discussion: Table 1 should only be one column wide, while the figures, especially 3, 5, and 6 would greatly benefit from a two column width. The paper was not very easy to understand during first read. Major improvements could be achieved by straightening up the content.\", \"sampled\": \"Investigating Different Context Types and Representations for Learning Word Embeddings examines how context choices affect word embedding quality. I see this as a worthwhile direction, as it separates factors in embedding algorithms and focuses specifically on context. The experimental scope is useful, covering 4 task categories and 21 datasets according to the abstract. However, I am not fully clear on the main takeaway from the experiments, since the results do not seem to show a stable advantage for any single context type. This raises an important question for the paper: if context is the factor being isolated, why does no context type show a consistent benefit?\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Strengths: This paper presents an approach to creating concept maps using crowdsourcing. The general ideas are interesting and the main contribution lies in the collection of the dataset. As such, I imagine that the dataset will be a valuable resource for further research in this field. Clearly a lot of effort has gone into this work. Weaknesses: Overall I felt this paper a bit overstated in placed. As an example, the authors claim a new crowdsourcing scheme as one of their contributions. This claims is quite strong though and it reads more like the authors are applying best practice in crowdsourcing to their work. This isn’t a novel methods then, it’s rather a well thought and sound application of existing knowledge. Similarly, the authors claim that they develop and present a new corpus. This seems true and I can see how a lot of effort was invested in its preparation, but then Section 4.1 reveals that actually this is based on an existing dataset. This is more a criticism of the presentation than the work though. General discussion: Where do the summary sentences come from for the crowdsource task? Aren’t they still quite subjective? Where do the clusters come from? Are they part of the TAC2008b dataset? In 4.6 expert annotators are used to create the gold standard concept maps. More information is needed in this section I would say as it seems to be quite crucial. How were they trained, what made them experts?\", \"sampled\": \"The data appear to be well collected, and the empirical presentation is a clear strength. My main concern is that many of the conclusions are not especially unexpected given the measurements described. The paper would be stronger if it more clearly separated the value of the data collection from claims that may already follow from standard expectations about DNNs on GPU hardware. This distinction is important for assessing novelty. Finding #1 presents power consumption as independent of batch size and architecture, while I read the evidence as indicating similarly high GPU use across architectures and batch sizes. That interpretation is plausible, but it also makes the finding less surprising. If the GPU is effectively fully used in each tested condition, then stable power behavior across DNN architectures and batch sizes would be expected. The more notable result may therefore be the equal apparent use of GPU compute resources across the tested models, rather than the broader formulation of the finding. Figure 9 is central to the argument about accuracy and inference time. I agree that a linear pattern in Figure 9 could imply the stated hyperbolic relation. The difficulty is that Finding #2 does not seem well supported for newer model generations, since newer models cluster in the upper-left area of Figure 9. That clustering weakens the claim that the relationship is asymptotically hyperbolic, and the paper should be more cautious about extending the pattern beyond the displayed evidence.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This paper considers the problem of KB completion and proposes ITransF for this purpose. Unlike STransE that assigns each relation an independent matrix, this paper proposes to share the parameters between different relations. A model is proposed where a tensor D is constructed that contains various relational matrices as its slices and a selectional vector \\\\alpha is used to select a subset of relevant relational matrix for composing a particular semantic relation. The paper then discuss a technique to make \\\\alpha sparse. Experimental results on two standard benchmark datasets shows the superiority of ITransF over prior proposals. The paper is overall well written and the experimental results are good. However, I have several concerns regarding this work that I hope the authors will answer in their response. 1. Just by arranging relational matrices in a tensor and selecting (or more appropriately considering a linearly weighted sum of the relational matrices) does not ensure any information sharing between different relational matrices. This would have been the case if you had performed some of a tensor decomposition and projected the different slices (relational matrices) into some common lower-dimensional core tensor. It is not clear why this approach was not taken despite the motivation to share information between different relational matrices. 2. The two requirements (a) to share information across different relational matrices and (b) make the attention vectors sparse are some what contradictory. If the attention vector is truly sparse and has many zeros then information will not flow into those slices during optimisation. 3. The authors spend a lot of space discussing techniques for computing sparse attention vectors. The authors mention in page 3 that \\\\ell_1 regularisation did not work in their preliminary experiments. However, no experimental results are shown for \\\\ell_1 regularisation nor they explain why \\\\ell_1 is not suitable for this task. To this reviewer, it appears as an obvious baseline to try, especially given the ease of optimisation. You use \\\\ell_0 instead and get into NP hard optimisations because of it. Then you propose a technique and a rather crude approximation in the end to solve it. All that trouble could be spared if \\\\ell_1 was used. 4. The vector \\\\alpha is performing a selection or a weighing over the slices of D. It is slightly misleading to call this as “attention” as it is a term used in NLP for a more different type of models (see attention model used in machine translation). 5. It is not clear why you need to initialise the optimisation by pre-trained embeddings from TransE. Why cannot you simply randomly initialise the embeddings as done in TransE and then update them? It is not fair to compare against TransE if you use TransE as your initial point. Learning the association between semantic relations is an idea that has been used in related problems in NLP such as relational similarity measurement [Turney JAIR 2012] and relation adaptation [Bollegala et al. IJCAI 2011]. It would be good to put the current work with respect to such prior proposals in NLP for modelling inter-relational correlation/similarity. Thanks for providing feedback. I have read it.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper, Cortical-Inspired Open-Bigram Representation for Handwritten Word Recognition, studies distant bigram representations inspired by cortical evidence for handwritten word recognition. The submission is generally well written, and its focus on open-bigrams creates a clear link between cognitive reading research and recognition modeling. The experiments on Rimes and IAM are appropriate for the stated task, and using LSTM-RNNs trained to predict open-bigrams gives a concrete framework for comparing representations. My main concern is the comparison between the proposed method and the standard baseline. The paper should give more detail on this comparison, since the current presentation does not make the basis for the claimed advantage sufficiently clear. The evaluation would be stronger with model complexity information for all systems being compared. In particular, parameter counts are needed to judge whether the observed differences come from the representation itself or from differences in capacity. The paper should also include language models in the evaluation. Different context levels may change the impact of language models, which is important for assessing the relative value of the proposed representation. The claim in section 5 that the approach obtains competitive or better error rates would be easier to evaluate if these comparisons were included. The restricted comparative corpus is another important issue. Only longer words are used in the comparative experiments. This leaves 70% of the data, and the paper should quantify the effect of using this restricted corpus. Without that evidence, it is hard to know whether the reported comparison reflects the method or the selected subset. The decision to ignore order also needs stronger justification. Handwritten word recognition is a monotonic sequential classification task, so it is not obvious that ignoring order is decision-theoretically justified. An experiment that varies only the use of order would help isolate the effect of this design choice. Such an experiment could separate the contribution of open-bigram content from the contribution of ordering information. The paper should also clarify several local points. At the end of page 1, please explain the phrase \\\"whole language method\\\". On page 6, please define the notation rnn_d(x,t). The discussion of order should distinguish order 0, order 1, and higher orders more carefully. In Table 2, precision and recall are not directly comparable between order 0 and higher orders because the target counts differ, which affects how the table should be interpreted. The text should state this limitation explicitly. The comparison between order 0 and larger orders should avoid implying that the same target population is being measured. This clarification is especially important because order 0 corresponds effectively to unigrams. The higher-order results therefore combine differences in representation with differences in target counts. The manuscript would benefit from making that distinction clearer in the table discussion. These issues do not undermine the topic, but they do limit the strength of the experimental claims. The paper presents a promising idea, but the evidence should more directly support the proposed comparison.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Interesting paper which proposes jointly learning automatic segmentation of words to sub words and their acoustic models. Although the training handles the word segmentation as hidden variable which depends also on the acoustic representations, during the decoding only maximum approximation is used. The authors present nice improvements over character based results, however they did not compare results with word segmentation which does not assume the dependency on acoustic. Obviously, only text based segmentation would result in two (but simpler) independent tasks. In order to extract such segmentation several publicly open tools are available and should be cited. Some of those tools can also exploit the unigram probabilities of the words to perform their segmentations. It looks that the improvements come from the longer acoustical units - longer acoustical constraints which could lead to less confused search -, pointing towards full word models. In another way, less tokens are more probable due to less multiplication of probabilities. As a thought experiment for an extreme case: if all the possible segmentations would be possible (mixture of all word fragments, characters, and full-words), would the proposed model use word fragments at all? (WSJ is a closed vocabulary task). It would be good to show that the sub word model could outperform even a full-word model (no segmentation).\", \"sampled\": \"The idea in Lossy Image Compression with Compressive Autoencoders is interesting and well motivated, particularly as a neural autoencoder approach to lossy image compression. The main empirical concern is that the method achieves rates essentially comparable to JPEG 2000, which weakens the case for a deep autoencoder compression scheme in its current form. Automatically learned compression schemes could be useful in settings where expert knowledge is limited, so the submission addresses a worthwhile direction, but the potential is not yet supported by sufficient analysis or comparative evidence. The paper is not suitable for publication in its current version. A key missing analysis is how the learned encoder relates to JPEG 2000. The authors should examine whether the learned filters resemble those used in JPEG 2000 or are substantially different. The evaluation should also provide texture-specific evidence showing where the method improves and where it fails, rather than relying only on aggregate performance against the baseline. Showing the 10 best and worst image patches across bit-rates would make the strengths and failure modes more concrete. The paper also needs to establish that any gains come from the coding scheme itself, rather than only from a stronger decoder, for example by testing a decoder trained using JPEG 2000 codes and possibly encoded random projections.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The authors explore the idea of deep-learning a static analyzer. They do it with a toy programming language and a very simplified analysis problem -- just checking if all variables are initalized. While the idea is interesting and might be developped into a tool in the future, the toy task presented in this paper is too simple to warrant an ICLR submission. Just detecting whether a variable is initialized in a string is a toy algorihtmic task, similar to the ones solved in a number of paper in recent years by models such as the Neural Turing Machine, Stack RNNs, Neural GPU, or Differentiable Neural Computer. All these architectures perform almost perfectly on a number of algorithmic tasks, so it is highly probable that they would also solve this one. Unluckily, the authors only compare to much more basic models, such as HMMs. Since the code for many of the above-mentioned models is available online, a paper without these baselines is not ready for ICLR. Moreover, there is a risk that existing models already solve this problem very well, making the contribution unclear.\", \"sampled\": \"The submission presents a modest technique for improving variational autoencoder quality, based on additional ELBO optimization after initialization from q network predictions. It also proposes Jacobian vectors for interpreting variational autoencoders, which is an interesting direction. The Jacobian-based embedding idea is appealing, since Jacobians seem to extend the notion of embeddings from linear models to deep latent Gaussian models. The paper would be stronger with a more direct comparison between Jacobian vectors and prior context-specific embedding methods, including Neelakantan et al, Efficient non-parametric estimation of multiple embeddings per word in vector space, Chen et al, and A Unified Model for Word Sense Representation and Disambiguation. As presented, the experiments do not convince me that Jacobian representations offer a clear advantage in context sensitivity over earlier work on such embeddings. The additional ELBO optimization is also interesting, but it remains underdeveloped in the current version. In particular, the paper does not clarify the compute and accuracy tradeoff between increasing q network complexity and applying extra ELBO optimization steps after the q network prediction.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I read the paper in some depth and the idea has some potential, however, the main claim is not substantiated well. Concretely, the 3rd contribution is problematic as the baselines here are very weak and are not what is used in state of the art synthesizers. To substantiate point 3, you need to actually compare against a relevant synthesizer.\", \"sampled\": \"Dynamic Partition Models studies distributed representations through binary encoding, with the goal of producing compact, interpretable representations by explaining data points via partitions of variables into expert supports. The approach is interesting in that it selects the most reliable expert for each variable rather than aggregating expert votes, with partitions varying according to the active experts. Reviewers found the initial draft too unclear to justify acceptance, and the submission was not accepted.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The paper proposes a convergence analysis of some two-layer NNs with ReLUs. It is not the first such analysis, but maybe it is novel on the assumptions used in the analysis, and the focus on ReLU nonlinearity that is pretty popular in practice. The paper is quite hard to read, with many English mistakes and typos. Nevertheless, the analysis seems to be generally correct. The novelty and the key insights are however not always well motivated or presented. And the argument that the work uses realistic assumptions (Gaussian inputs for example) as opposed to other works, is quite debatable actually. Overall, the paper looks like a correct analysis work, but its form is really suboptimal in terms of writing/presentation, and the novelty and relevance of the results are not always very clear, unfortunately. The main results and intuition should be more clearly presented, and details could be moved to appendices for example - that could only help to improve the visibility and impact of these interesting results.\", \"sampled\": \"PixelVAE is an important and interesting submission that combines a hierarchical VAE with PixelCNNs for natural image distribution modeling. The reported likelihoods on MNIST, ImageNet at 64 × 64 resolution, and the LSUN bedrooms dataset are good, though not state of the art, and the paper addresses a meaningful direction in unsupervised learning. The PixelCNN–VAE combination is valuable, but the novelty should be stated carefully, since the PixelCNN paper had already suggested combining PixelCNN with a VAE. The discussion of hierarchical VAE latents is interesting, particularly the association of high-, mid-, and low-level variation with different latent stages. However, this analysis is not especially surprising, and task-based evidence that PixelCNN improves VAE latent representations would better support the claims about what the latent variables encode. The main weakness is clarity of presentation, especially in the model architecture. I agree with reviewer1 that the description is not sufficiently clear and would make the model difficult to reproduce from the paper.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This paper presents a few interesting ideas, namely the idea of keeping around a set of \\\"danger states\\\" and treating these states with some special consideration in reply to make sure that their impact is not neglected after collecting a lot of additional data. However, there are two main problems: 1) the actual implementation here seems fairly ad-hoc, and it's not at all clear to me that this particular algorithm (building a classifier with equal numbers of good and danger states, and then injecting an additional reward into the Q-learning task based upon this classifier), is the right way to go about this. The presentation is also difficult to follow, and the final results imply aren't that compelling (though this is improving after the revisions, but still has a way to go. We therefore encourage the authors to resubmit their work at a future conference venue. Pros: + Interesting idea of keeping around danger states and injecting them into training Cons: - Algorithm doesn't seem that well motivated - Presentation is a bit unclear, takes until page 6 to actually present the basic approach.\", \"sampled\": \"Emergent Predication Structure in Vector Representations of Neural Readers pursues a worthwhile goal by examining recent reading comprehension work through the lens of logical or predication structure. I appreciate the effort to organize models such as Attentive Reader, Stanford Reader, Attention-Sum Reader, Gated-Attention Reader, and Attention-over-Attention Reader into the categories of “aggregation readers” and “explicit reference models.” The paper is generally well written, and section 3 is particularly helpful: the discussion of predication makes the framing clearer and more useful than a broad appeal to logical structure. The shift in terminology from logical structure to predication is appropriate, and the authors’ clarification is detailed and useful. That said, I still have some uncertainty about the paper’s contribution, since the current analysis does not fully show what the predication perspective adds beyond a descriptive organization of neural readers. The abstract’s reference to the Who-did-What dataset is relevant to the paper’s objective, but I remain unsure whether this dataset is well suited to that objective, especially in light of prior concerns about the CNN/DailyMail dataset associated with Chen et al. at ACL’16.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This is a strong submission regarding one of the most important and recently introduced methods in neural networks - generative adversarial networks. The authors analyze theoretically the convergence of GANs and discuss the stability of GANs. Both are very important. To the best of my knowledge, this is one of the first theoretical papers about GANs and the paper, contrary to most of the submissions in the field, actually provides deep theoretical insight into this architecture. The stability issues regarding GANs are extremely important since the first proposed versions of GANs architecture were very unstable and did not work well in practice. Theorems 2.4-2.6 are novel and introduces mathematical techniques are interesting. I have some technical questions regarding the proof of Theorem 2.5 but these are pretty minor.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper investigates the capacity and trainability of recurrent neural networks, arguing that performance differences among architectures such as LSTM, GRU, and vanilla RNN stem primarily from training effectiveness rather than capacity differences between them. The experimental rigor is a clear strength, and all reviewers agreed the work is of genuine interest and should appear at ICLR. The central empirical claims are well grounded, including the findings that RNNs store approximately 5 bits per parameter of task information and approximately one real number from input history per hidden unit, both of which support useful intuitions about recurrent net capacity and training difficulty.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"\\\"Animal behavior is nondeterministic\\\": I think that this is an overstatement, since it depends on your frame of reference. At the very least I'd rephrase it by saying that we do not have access to the underlying cognitive processes, which makes the behavior look like a non-determinist one given the information we have. My actual question is: given that the model of the behavior is non-deterministic, would a GAN be more natural fit for the predictive part of this task?\", \"sampled\": \"This paper combines VAE with autoregressive models to learn global representations and claims state-of-the-art results on MNIST, OMNIGLOT, and Caltech-101. It is a strong contribution, and the Bits-Back interpretation of VAE stands out as a particularly well-handled section.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"We thank the reviewers for the comments and suggestions 1) We uploaded a revised version that contains experiments on CIFAR-100 dataset. We results on the CIFAR-100 are consistent with results on other data set and show that the performance of our method is better than previous methods. 2) In the uploaded revised version we also addressed the scalability issue of the method in case of many classes. In our approach we initialized the second soft-max layer using the confusion matrix of the baseline system. The confusion matrix is a good estimation of the label noise. Assume the rows of the matrix correspond to the true labels and the matrix columns correspond to the noisy labels. The k largest elements in the i-th row are the most occurring noisy class values when the true class value is i. We can thus connect i-th elements in the first softmax layer only to its k most probable noisy class candidates. (Note that if we connect the i-label in the first softmax only to the i-label in the second softmax layer, we obtain the standard baseline model.) Taking only k connections to the second softamx layer solves the scalability problem. In the revised version we show experiments on CIFAR-100 that show that by using this scalable approach there is no performance degradation.\", \"sampled\": \"The motivation behind this work remains unclear throughout the paper. The paper claims that long short-term memory networks can learn a basic static analyzer for a simple toy language, while feature engineering, hidden Markov models, and basic recurrent neural networks fail on the same problem. The proposal to use a language model to help programmers locate reported errors is noted, but the practical value of a black-box analyzer making basic and sometimes incorrect predictions is difficult to assess without a stronger argument that the predictions are sufficiently challenging and need not always be sound. Designing analyzers and defining soundness and optimality are already well understood, which weakens the case for the learning-based framing offered here. When existing analyzers are unsound, there are typically clear reasons such as native methods, frameworks, or dynamic evaluation, rather than the kind of random or opaque failures that a learned model might be expected to address. The reviewer also identifies missing related work by Hindle and others that already addresses material covered in Section 4 of the paper, and this gap reflects a broader problem with the literature review.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The paper looks at how natural language instructions can be decomposed into sub-tasks for as-yet-unseen new tasks hence the zero-shot generalization, which is considered to be the primary challenge to be solved. The precise problem being solved by the original paper is not clearly expressed in the writing. This left some reviewers asking for comparisons, while the authors note that for the specific nature of the problem being solved, they could not seen any particular methods that is known to be capable of tackling this kind of problem. The complexity of the system and simplicity of the final examples were also found to be contradictory by a subset of the reviewers, although this is again related to the understanding of the problem being solved. With scores of 3/4/5/7, the ideas in this paper were appreciated by a subset of reviewers. At the time of the writing of this metareview, the authors have posted a fairly lengthy rebuttal (Jan 18) and significant revisions (Jan 18), with no further responses from reviewers as of yet. However, it is difficult for reviewers to do a full re-evaluation of the paper on such short notice. While the latest revisions are commendable, it is unfortunately difficult to argue strongly in favor of this paper at present.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes neural equivalence networks for learning representations of symbolic expressions and presents the motivation for this approach in a manner that is well articulated and reasonably easy to follow, which is a genuine strength of the submission. The evaluation is also reasonably extensive, covering a range of experimental conditions that support the core claims. That said, the primary concern centers on the choice of evaluation metric, which appears to be precision computed on a per-query basis and is non-standard for this type of retrieval or matching problem. Precision-recall curves or ROC analysis would be considerably more informative, and the current metric is expected to produce inflated results when equivalence classes are larger, because the likelihood of a random expression matching a query grows with class size and the denominator does not account for this structural bias. The motivation offered for the subexpforce loss is not sufficiently developed, and the reviewer recommends that the authors strengthen this justification before the work is reconsidered. A direct ablation comparing models trained with and without the subexpforce loss, while holding residual connections and normalization constant across conditions, would clarify whether the loss component contributes meaningfully to performance.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The goal of this paper is to learn vector representation of boolean and polynomial expressions, such that equivalent expressions have similar representations. The model proposed in the paper is based on recursive neural network, as introduced by Socher et al. (2012). Given the syntactic parse tree of a formula (either boolean or polynomial), the representation for a node is obtained by applying a MLP on the representation of the children. This process is applied recursively to obtain the representation of the full expression. Contrary to Socher et al. (2012), this paper proposes to use more than one layer (this is especially important to capture XOR operation, which is not surprising at all). The paper also introduces a reconstruction error (called SubexpForce), so that the expression of children can be recovered from the expression of the parent (if I understood correctly). The model is trained using a classification loss, where the label of a given expression corresponds to its equivalence class. The method is then evaluated on randomly generated data, and compared to baselines such as tf-idf, GRU RNN or standard recursive neural network. While I do agree with the authors that learning good representation for symbolic expressions (and to capture compositionality) is an important task, I am not entirely convinced by the experimental setting proposed in this paper. Indeed, as stated in the paper, the task of deciding if two boolean expressions are equivalent is NP-hard, and I do not understand if the model can do better than implicitly computing the truth table of expressions. While sometimes a bit hard to follow, the paper is technically sound. In particular, the proposed model is well adapted to the problem and outperforms the baselines. pros: - the model is relatively simple and sound. - using a classification loss over equivalence classes (should be compared with using similarity). cons: - not convinced by the setting: I do not believe that you can really do better than the truth table for boolean expr (or computing the value of the polynomial expression for randomly chosen points in [0, 1]^n). - some part of the paper are a bit hard to follow (e.g. justification of the SubexpForce, discussion of why softmax does not work, etc...). - comparison between classification loss and similarity loss is missing.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper addresses convergence analysis for two-layer ReLU networks under gradient descent, covering both single and multiple hidden node settings, but the presentation requires substantial revision before the contributions can be fairly evaluated. The first half of the paper, which analyzes the case of a single hidden node, is somewhat clear and allows the reader to follow the argument with reasonable effort. The second half, which extends the analysis to multiple hidden nodes, is very difficult to understand, and this gap in clarity is a significant problem for a reader trying to assess the full scope of the work. The convergence probability result for the single-node case, stated as (1-epsilon)/2 with proper initialization at standard deviation 1/sqrt(d), is a concrete and checkable claim, but Theorem 3.3 raises a concern: maximizing convergence probability appears to require epsilon near 0, which implies a ball radius r approaching 0, and this seems inconsistent with what Figure 2 shows. The Gaussian iid input assumption is very strong, and the reviewer questions whether the analysis generalizes to real-world inputs that are correlated and non-Gaussian. The paper's claim that it makes no assumption on independence of activations is noted, but this effectively shifts the assumption burden onto the input distribution rather than removing it. Notation is inconsistent throughout, particularly regarding D, D(w), and D(e), and the relationship between indices l and u in Section 3 is left unclear, compounding the difficulty of following the proofs. Section 3.1 introduces F(e, w) and D(e) without sufficient explanation of what these objects are or why they are needed, and the reviewer asks for explicit clarification on both points. Section 4, which treats the symmetry group, lacks intuitive explanation and reads as a sequence of formal statements without adequate motivation for why the symmetry breaking analysis matters to the convergence results. Figure 5 references a_j without defining what a_j represents, leaving the figure difficult to interpret. These gaps collectively make it hard to extract the paper's core takeaways, even for a reader already familiar with the dynamical systems framing of neural network training. The reviewer encourages the author to rewrite the paper with clarity as the primary goal, since the current form makes it very difficult to understand what the results mean in practice.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: A nice, solid piece of work that builds on previous studies in a productive way. Well-written and clear. - Weaknesses: Very few--possibly avoid some relatively \\\"empty\\\" statements: 191 : For example, if our task is to identify words used similarly across contexts, our scoring function can be specified to give high scores to terms whose usage is similar across the contexts. 537 : It is educational to study how annotations drawn from the same data are similar or different. - General Discussion: In the first sections I was not sure that much was being done that was new or interesting, as the methods seemed very reminiscent of previous methods used over the past 25 years to measure similarity, albeit with a few new statistical twists, but conceptually in the same vein. Section 5, however, describes an interesting and valuable piece of work that will be useful for future studies on the topic. In retrospect, the background provided in sections 2-4 is useful, if not necessary, to support the experiments in section 5. In short, the work and results described will be useful to others working in this area, and the paper is worthy of presentation at ACL. Minor comments: Word, punctuation missing? 264 : For word annotations, we used PPMI, SVD, and SGNS (skipgram with negative sampling from Mikolov et al. (2013b)) word vectors released by Hamilton et al. (2016).\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes learning comparative physical relations between object categories from unstructured text, using action verbs as signals for commonsense attribute acquisition. The method is novel, and the problem of mining commonsense attribute relations from text is important. The paper would benefit from concrete examples of object pairs, action verbs, and predicted attribute relations. The dataset contains only 3656 object pairs, which is very small, and the reviewer questions how many distinct object categories are present and whether the approach can scale beyond this limited set of pairs to broader coverage of physical commonsense knowledge. The abstract incorrectly states six dimensions while the paper itself covers only five physical dimensions. The selection criteria for frame and attribute similarity factors are described without sufficient justification, making it unclear why specific factors were chosen over alternatives; a clearer motivation would strengthen the methodological foundation. The paper lacks any error analysis or discussion of model mistakes, which limits the reader's ability to understand failure modes and the boundaries of the approach. A comparison against a more direct method of mining attributes or attribute distributions from text would help establish whether the verb-based signal provides a real advantage. The work by Davidov and Rappoport, published at ACL 2010 on extraction of numerical attributes from the Web, is relevant and should be discussed and compared against in the related work and evaluation sections. Several typos appear.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This is the most convincing paper on image compression with deep neural networks that I have read so far. The paper is very well written, the use of the rate-distortion theory in the objective fits smoothly in the framework. The paper is compared to a reasonable baseline (JPEG2000, as opposed to previous papers only considering JPEG). I would expect this paper to have a very good impact. Yes, please include results on Lena/Barbara/Baboon (sorry, not Gibbons), along with state-of-the-art references with more classical methods such as the one I mentioned in my questions. I think it is important to clearly state how NN compare to best previous methods. From the submitted version, I still don't know how both categories of methods are positioned.\", \"sampled\": \"The concept of data augmentation in embedding space is interesting, and the method, which operates on context vectors generated by sequence-to-sequence models through noise addition, interpolation, and extrapolation, is well presented. Evaluating the approach on spoken digits and image recognition provides reasonable coverage, but the consistent use of a 2-layer MLP as the baseline across all tasks raises questions about how broadly these results generalize. It is unclear whether the reported performance gains would persist if a stronger baseline model were substituted, since a shallow classifier may be particularly sensitive to augmentation effects that a stronger model would absorb differently. This concern about baseline choice is important for assessing the method's practical value.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The authors present results on a number of different tasks where the goal is to determine whether a given test example is out-of-domain or likely to be mis-classified. This is accomplished by examining statistics for the softmax probability for the most likely class; although the score by itself is not a particularly good measure of confidence, the statistics for out-of-domain examples are different enough from in-domain examples to allow these to be identified with some certainty. My comments appear below: 1. As the authors point out, the AUROC/AUPR criterion is threshold independent. As a result, it is not obvious whether the thresholds that would correspond to a certain operating point (say a true positive rate of 10%) would be similar across different data sets. In other words, it would be interesting to know how sensitive the thresholds are to different test sets (or different splits of the test set). This is important if we want to use the thresholds determined on a given held-out set during evaluation on unseen data (where we would need to select a threshold). 2. Performance is reported in terms of AUROC/AUPR and models are compared against a random baseline. I think it’s a little hard to look at the differences in AUC/AUPR to get a sense for how much better the proposed classifier is than the random baseline. It would be useful, for example, if the authors could also report how strongly statistically significant some of these differences are (although admittedly they look to be pretty large in most cases). 3. In the experiments on speech recognition presented in Section 3.3, I was not entirely clear on how the model was evaluated. In Table 9, for example, is an “example” the entire utterance or just a single (stacked?) speech frame. Assuming that each “example” is an utterance, are the softmax probabilities the probability of the entire phone sequence (obtained by multiplying the local probability estimates from a Viterbi decoding?) 4. I’m curious about the decision to ignore the blank symbol’s logit in Section 3.3. Why is this required? 5. As I mentioned in the pre-review question, at least in the speech recognition case, it would have been interesting to compare performance obtained using a simple generative baseline (e.g., GMM-HMM). I think that would serve as a good indication of the ability of the proposed model to detect out-of-domain examples over the baseline.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper addresses a practically relevant problem in visual instance retrieval, examining how convolutional neural network features can be extracted and pooled for effective image search. While the work is clearly organized and covers a reasonable range of design choices across 4 datasets, the central question of novelty is difficult to answer in the paper's favor, because the majority of findings presented as best practices appear to have been established in prior work, leaving the reader uncertain about what substantive new knowledge the submission actually provides to the field. The missing comparisons are a concrete problem. Gordo et al and Radenovic et al are absent from the experimental evaluation, an omission that multiple reviewers have independently flagged. The paper evaluates only a single CNN architecture, VGG-19, yet the title frames the findings as best practices for CNNs in general, which ResNet and Inception counterexamples immediately complicate. Restricting evaluation to 1 architecture while making broad architectural claims is a mismatch the paper does not acknowledge or justify. The generalizability of the reported practices therefore remains unestablished, and the absence of the two named references makes it impossible to assess where the proposed method sits relative to the current state of the art. Novelty concerns remain. The finding that the last convolutional layer should be used was already demonstrated across multiple prior works. The importance of preserving original image sizes was shown in Tolias et al table 1, and the use of PCA with whitening, including learning whitening parameters on the test set, was documented before this submission. The paper's claim that no training is performed is also difficult to accept given that parameters are selected by grid search on the test set, which functions as implicit optimization regardless of how the authors characterize it. The paper also contains an internal inconsistency: sum-l1 pooling achieves the best result in table 1, yet the authors later conclude that max-l2 is preferable for multiscale settings. This contradiction is never resolved. The reviewer's own assessment of the contribution is captured directly: \\\"Actually the most interesting part for me, one that I can actually say I didn't know and don't think anyone knew, is figure 3.\\\" This leaves only 1 figure as the portion of the work that can be considered genuinely novel.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The reviewers generally agreed that the research direction pursued in the paper is a valuable one, but all reviewers expressed strong reservations about the value of a linear probe on intermediate features. The lack of experiments on more complex state-of-the-art networks is also potentially problematic. In the end, it seems that there is insufficient evidence that the proposed approach is actually a useful tool in its present state, though the authors should be encouraged to pursue this line of research further.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper formulates binary auto-encoder learning as a min-max reconstruction error over a training set given observed intermediate representations, and the resulting biconvex problem, solvable by alternating minimisation, constitutes the main contribution, which the reviewer considers non-trivial in its derivation. Proof-of-concept experiments show improvements for 1-hidden layer auto-encoders over a vanilla approach, though the experimental section is assessed as fairly weak.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This paper investigate the phenomenon of the adversarial examples and the adversarial training on the dataset of ImageNet. While the final conclusions are still vague, this paper raises several noteworthy finding from its experiments. The paper is well written and easy to follow. Although I still have some concerns about the paper (see the comments below), this paper has good contributions and worth to publish. Pros: For the first time in the literature, this paper proposed the concept of ‘label leaking’. Although its effect only becomes significant when the dataset is large, it should be carefully handled in the future research works along this line. Using the ratio of 'clean accuracy' over ‘adversarial accuracy’ as the measure of robust is more reasonable compared to the existing works in the literature. Cons: Although the conclusions of the paper are based on the experiments on ImageNet, the title of the paper seems a little misleading. I consider Section 4 as the main contribution of the paper. Note that Section 4.3 and Section 4.4 are not specific to large-scale dataset, thus emphasizing the ‘large-scale’ in the title and in the introduction seems improper. Basically all the conclusions of the paper are made based on observing the experimental results. Further tests should have been performed to verify these hypotheses. Without that, the conclusions of the paper seems rushy. For example, one dataset of imageNet can not infer the conclusions for all large-scale datasets.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper presents what reviewers recognized as one of the first successful attempts at character-to-character machine translation modeling, a contribution that carries genuine historical weight in the field. The proposed architecture relies on two recurrent networks to learn morphology within the encoder, arriving at a total of six recurrent networks once the hierarchical decoder is included. Reviewers acknowledged the analysis section as a particular strength, pointing to figures that illuminate underlying model properties in useful ways. At the same time, the reception was divided: some found the paper to be a \\\"well-written paper\\\" with comprehensive experiments, while others identified missing implementation details and notation that becomes overloaded in key sections. The question of novelty was a central point of contention among reviewers, who appreciated the character-based framing but questioned whether the overall contribution extended meaningfully beyond prior encoder-decoder work. The results were described by some as \\\"compelling,\\\" particularly the finding that the model achieves a higher BLEU score than the BPE-based baseline after training for only one epoch on both En-Fr and En-Cs tasks. Reviewers who found the results less persuasive argued that direct comparisons to BPE and morphologically-informed methods were insufficiently developed to support the claims being made. The architectural complexity of the model drew pointed criticism, with reviewers characterizing it as overly engineered in ways that obscure rather than clarify the underlying learning principles.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper details an implementation of sparse-full convolutions and a model to work out the potential speed-up of various sparsity levels for CNNs. The first contribution is more about engineering, but the authors make the source code available which is greatly appreciated. The second contribution is perhaps more interesting, as so far pruning methods have focused on saving memory, with very modest speed gains. Imbuing knowledge of running speed into a pruning algorithm seems like the proper way to tackle this problem. The authors are very methodical in how they build the model and evaluate it very thoroughly. It seems that the same idea could be used not just for pruning existing models, but also when building new architectures: selecting layers and their parameters as to achieve an optimal throughput rate. This could make for a nice direction for future work. One point that is missing is some discussion of how transferable the performance model is to GPUs. This would make the technique easier to adopt broadly. Other areas for improvement: The points in Figure 4 are hard to distinguish (e.g. small red circle vs. small red square), and overall the figure could be made bigger; specifying whether the \\\"base learning rate\\\" in Section 3 is the start or end rate of the annealing schedule; typos: \\\"punning\\\" (p.4), \\\"spares\\\" (p.5).\", \"sampled\": \"The authors acknowledge that implementation inefficiencies limited VGG training on ImageNet to only a few epochs, which likely explains why the VGG network did not converge as LeNet did. The results on CIFAR10/100 are consistent with the LeNet experiments, reinforcing the general pattern observed across datasets. The computational bottleneck stems from updating prior parameters, and the authors plan to address this in future work by updating prior parameters based on influence of fewer weights. The methods and experiments section will be improved for clarity. An explicit algorithm will be added to the paper, and the code will be made available online. The authors agree there is no theoretical justification for a linear accuracy-compression relationship, though they note that theoretical results can be derived under assumptions about pruned weights and accuracy relationship. The method was originally proposed to improve generalization as an alternative to convolutional weight-sharing. The fully connected architecture may have more room for generalization due to lack of other regularization. This observation could help interpret the differences in behavior across architectures. The authors quote a reviewer's question about Figure 2: \\\"In Figure 2 I am noticing two things: On the left, there is a large number of points with improved accuracy which is not the case for LeNet5-Caffe. Is there any intuition for why that's the case?\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper presents a theoretical treatment of transformation groups applied to convnets, and presents some empirical results showing more efficient usage of network parameters. The basic idea of steerability makes huge sense and seems like a very important idea to develop. It is also a very old idea in image processing and goes back to Simoncelli, Freeman, Adelson, as well as Perona/Greenspan and others in the early 1990s. This paper approaches it through a formal treatment of group theory. But at the end of the day the idea seems pretty simple - the feature representation of a transformed image should be equivalent to a transformed feature representation of the original image. Given that the authors are limiting their analysis to discrete groups - for example rotations of 0, 90, 180, and 270 deg. - the formalities brought in from the group theoretic analysis seem a bit overkill. I'm not sure what this buys us in the end.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents a joint syntactic and semantic transition-based dependency parser that uses stack LSTMs to represent the entire algorithm state. The parser builds on the joint parser of Henderson et al. (2008) and employs a plain greedy search with linear time complexity, which does not require hand-crafted features. The system is evaluated on CoNLL 2008 English and multilingual CoNLL 2009 datasets. The reviewer notes that greedy search doesn't make results comparable to some cited works. The reviewer suggests providing beam-searched performance for better comparability and asks for clarification on the presence of both A1 and C-A1 links. The reviewer requests a summary of differences in transition sets from \\\"Henderson et al\\\". and questions the redundancy of concatenating word predicate and disambiguated sense in representations. The organization of sections 4.1 and 4.2 is described as confusing regarding multilinguality. The reviewer raises several points about comparability and representational choices. These concerns should be addressed to strengthen the clarity and positioning of the work.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"An interesting connection is made between dropout, Tishby et al's \\\"information bottleneck\\\" and VAEs. Specifically, classification of 'y' from 'x' is split in two faces: an inference model z ~ q(z|x), a prior p(z), and a classifier y ~ p(y|z). By optimizing the objective E_{(x,y)~data} [ E_{z~q(z|x)}[log p(x|y)] + lambda * KL(q(z|x)||p(z))], with lambda <= 1, an information bottleneck 'z' is formed, where lambda controls an upper bound on the number of bits traveling through 'z'. The objective is equivalent to a VAE objective with downweighted KL(posterior|prior), an encoder that takes as input 'x', and a decoder that only predicts 'x'. - Related work (section 2) is discussed sufficiently.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper was praised for its clarity and originality, reviewers described it as a “pleasure to read.” The approach was found to be novel and filling an interesting niche, and the work was characterized as “very ambitious.” However, some reviewers did not fully accept the motivation behind the study. A main quality issue is the lack of experimental results and baselines. The experiments were found not to “fit the claims,” and reviewers asked for any type of baselines, even enumeration. A future plan showing usefulness is desired because empirical evidence was lacking. Given these limitations, the paper is recommended for submission to the workshop track. The reviewers saw clear potential in the core idea.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes a recurrent architecture for simultaneously predicting motion and action states of agents. The paper is well written, clear in its presentation and backed up by good experiments. They demonstrate that by forcing the network to predict motion has beneficial consequences on the classification of actions states, allowing more accurate classification with less training data. They also show how the information learned by the network is interpretable and organised in a hierarchy. Weaknesses: - a critical discussion on the interplay between motion an behaviour that is needed to experience the benefits of their proposed model is missing from the paper.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes a method to accelerate optimization near saddle points in deep neural network training by repelling the current parameter vector from a running average of recent parameter values. The proposed Charged Point Normalization approach does not use second order information and it can be trained with an arbitrary gradient descent learner. This show that optimize faster than a variety of other methods, with improvements demonstrated on various datasets and architectures. The abstract claims the system drastically improves learning compared to non-CPN neural networks. The paper presents a fresh idea in stochastic optimization for deep neural networks, but it does not meet the Accept bar due to doubts about experimental thoroughness. The paper is invited for presentation at the Workshop track.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Paper summary This paper develops a generalization of dropout using information theoretic principles. The basic idea is that when learning a representation z of input x with the aim of predicting y, we must choose a z such that it carries the least amount of information about x, as long as it can predict y. This idea can be formalized using the Information Bottleneck Lagrangian. This leads to an optimization problem which is similar to the one derived for variational dropout, the difference being that Information dropout allows for a scaling factor associated with the KL divergence term that encourages noise. The amount of noise being added is made a parameterized function of the data and this function is optimized along with the rest of the model. Experimental results on CIFAR-10 and MNIST show (small) improvements over binary dropout. Strengths - The paper highlights an important conceptual link between probabilistic variational methods and information theoretic methods, showing that dropout can be generalized using both formalisms to arrive at very similar models. - The presentation of the model is excellent. - The experimental results on cluttered MNIST are impressive. Weaknesses - The results on CIFAR-10 in Figure 3(b) seem to be on a validation set (unless the axis label is a typo). It is not clear why the test set was not used. This makes it hard to compare to results reported in Springenberg et al, as well as other results in literature. Quality The theoretical exposition is high quality. Figure 2 gives a nice qualitative assessment of what the model is doing. However, the experimental results section can be made better, for example, by matching the results on CIFAR-10 as reported in Springenberg et al. and trying to improve on those using information dropout. Clarity The paper is well written and easy to follow. Originality The derivation of the information dropout optimization problem using IB Lagrangian is novel. However, the final model is quite close to variational dropout.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes Tandem Anchors, a multiword anchor approach for interactive topic modeling that replaces individual anchors with pseudowords created from sparse vector combinations of multiple words. The method is faster than existing interactive methods and supports interactive iteration, and a user study shows that multiword anchors are easier and more effective for users. The harmonic mean function for pseudoword construction yields the best classification accuracy improvement over the Gram-Schmidt baseline anchor method. Although the technical contribution is relatively modest, the thoroughness of the evaluation justifies a full paper. The evaluation on the 20 Newsgroups dataset is not ideal due to varying document lengths and preprocessing sensitivity. A typo appears on line 692 where “using tandem anchors” should be corrected. Related work on anchor words is missing, including Lee et al. (2014) and Nguyen et al. (2013, 2015).The paper does not sufficiently address how facets could be built without convenient sources like category titles. Classification tasks such as news article section classification might be more appropriate than 20 Newsgroups. The reviewer questions the exclusion of the AND operator or element-wise max for pseudoword construction on lines 261 and 272. The reviewer notes that the harmonic mean function for pseudoword construction yields the best classification accuracy improvement over Gram-Schmidt. Tandem Anchors is faster than existing interactive methods and supports interactive iteration. A user study shows multiword anchors are easier and more effective for users. The paper proposes Tandem Anchors, a multiword anchor approach for interactive topic modeling. The method replaces individual anchors with pseudowords created from sparse vector combinations of multiple words. The technical contribution is relatively small but evaluation thoroughness merits a full paper. The evaluation on 20 Newsgroups is not ideal due to varying document lengths and preprocessing sensitivity. The reviewer questions the fewer-than-100 boundary and preprocessing on lines 338 to 340. A typo appears on line 164 where “entire dataset” should be corrected. Clarification is needed on training time on lines 164 to 166. More explanation on confusion is desired on lines 436 to 440. The reviewer notes a typo on line 337 where “Usenet” should be capitalized.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper addresses the problem of argument mining, which consists of finding argument types and predicting the relationships between the arguments. The authors proposed a pointer network structure to recover the argument relations. They also propose modifications on pointer network to perform joint training on both type and link prediction tasks. Overall the model is reasonable, but I am not sure if ICLR is the best venue for this work. My first concern of the paper is on the novelty of the model. Pointer network has been proposed before. The proposed multi-task learning method is interesting, but the authors only verified it on one task. This makes me feel that maybe the submission is more for a NLP conference rather than ICLR. The authors stated that the pointer network is less restrictive compared to some of the existing tree predicting method. However, the datasets seem to only contain single trees or forests, and the stack-based method can be used for forest prediction by adding a virtual root node to each example (as done in the dependency parsing tasks). Therefore, I think the experiments right now cannot reflect the advantages of pointer network models unfortunately. My second concern of the paper is on the target task. Given that the authors want to analyze the structures between sentences, is the argumentation mining the best dataset? For example, authors could verify their model by applying it to the other tasks that require tree structures such as dependency parsing.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes ParMAC, a distributed extension of the method of auxiliary coordinates that arranges processors in a circular cluster topology. MAC decouples nested optimization into alternating updates of parameters and auxiliary coordinates, which can make nonconvex problems more tractable. The authors implement this communication pattern using MPI and test it on a cluster of 128 processors. The evaluation focuses on binary autoencoders trained on 100 million high-dimensional points. The review notes that practical contribution is limited to showing this decoupling scheme works better than others. Parallel SGD is easier to implement on standard NN toolboxes. The review questions any theoretical advantage over parallel SGD except for non-smooth functions. The communication pattern and trade-offs in deep feed-forward networks remain unclear. The review requests a comparison with circular versus parameter server configurations. It also requests a comparison of decoupled sub-problems against parallel SGD. The paper lacks concrete examples for architectures like deep convolutional or recurrent networks, which weakens the generalizability claim. In the W step, parameter vectors for each hidden unit are passed around the cluster. A synchronization step gathers parameters from each submodel via circular traversal. Each machine updates coordinates based on the full model for a data slice. The review finds that without these missing comparisons and broader architectural demonstrations, the contribution feels incomplete. The manuscript would be strengthened by clarifying when the circular topology outperforms a parameter server and by situating the approach relative to parallel SGD on standard models. Providing results on at least one deep convolutional or recurrent architecture would help readers assess whether the method scales beyond the binary autoencoder setting.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper proposes to provide a theoretical explanation for why deep convolutional neural networks are invertible (at-least, when going back from certain intermediate layers to the image itself). It does so by considering the invertibility of a single layer, assuming the convolutional filters essentially correspond to incoherent measurements satisfying RIP. In my opinion, while this is an interesting direction of research, the paper is not ready for publication. I feel the treatment does not go sufficiently towards explaining the phenomenon in deep neural networks. Even after reading the response from the authors, I feel the results are only a minor variation of the standard results from compressive sensing for sparse reconstruction with incoherent measurements. A deep neural network is fundamentally different from a single layer---it is the \\\"deep\\\" part that makes the forward task work. As the authors note, there is significant deterioration when IHT is applied recursively----therefore, at best the theory explains the partial invertibility of a single layer. That a single layer is approximately invertible isn't surprising, that a cascade of layers *is*. For any theoretical analysis of this phenomenon to be useful, I believe it must go beyond analyzing a single compressive measurement-type layer, and try to explain how much of the same theory holds for a cascade. I say this because it's entirely possible that the sparse recovery theory breaks down beyond a single layer, and invertibility ends up being a property caused by correlations between the weights of different layers. In other words, there is no way to tell from the current results for individual layers whether they are in fact a step towards explaining the invertibility of whole networks.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes a method for generating multimodal datasets with controlled complexity, which in principle could help isolate specific reasoning capabilities in language-and-vision models. However, the maximum achievable complexity is very limited compared to typical multimodal tasks, and this narrow range constrains the kinds of conclusions the experiments can support. The proposed tasks only allow quantitative difficulty increases, unlike bAbI tasks which span qualitative easy-to-hard reasoning, so the evaluation framework cannot probe shifts in reasoning strategy. Unsatisfying experimental results appear to stem from basic overfitting or underfitting issues rather than from the multimodal integration challenges the paper sets out to study. The reviewer finds it hard to identify qualitative insights from the experiments, because the performance patterns do not clearly link model behavior to the intended understanding capabilities. There is a contradiction between not aiming for optimal performance and using the score to measure understanding, which weakens the central claim that “architectures are able to successfully demonstrate the desired understanding.” This tension is not resolved in the current framing and leaves the evaluation criteria ambiguous. The general approach should be introduced earlier in the paper so that readers can grasp the motivation before encountering detailed task descriptions. Moving the problem setup forward would also help contextualize why controlled complexity matters, given that the current maximum difficulty still falls well short of real-world multimodal demands. The paper’s core idea of systematic dataset generation has merit, but the limited complexity ceiling and the unresolved contradiction between performance goals and evaluation logic make it difficult to assess whether the method genuinely advances multimodal understanding.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper presents an application of Pointer Networks, a recurrent neural network model original used for solving algorithmic tasks, to two subtasks of Argumentation Mining: determining the types of Argument Components, and finding the links between them. The model achieves state-of-the-art results. Strengths: - Thorough review of prior art in the specific formulation of argument mining handled in this paper. - Simple and effective modification of an existing model to make it suitable for the task. The model is mostly explained clearly. - Strong results as compared to prior art in this task. Weaknesses: - 071: This formulation of argumentation mining is just one of several proposed subtask divisions, and this should be mentioned. For example, in [1], claims are detected and classified before any supporting evidence is detected. Furthermore, [2] applied neural networks to this task, so it is inaccurate to say (as is claimed in the abstract of this paper) that this work is the first NN-based approach to argumentation mining. - Two things must be improved in the presentation of the model: (1) What is the pooling method used for embedding features (line 397)? and (2) Equation (7) in line 472 is not clear enough: is E_i the random variable representing the *type* of AC i, or its *identity*? Both are supposedly modeled (the latter by feature representation), and need to be defined. Furthermore, it seems like the LHS of equation (7) should be a conditional probability. - There are several unclear things about Table 2: first, why are the three first baselines evaluated only by macro f1 and the individual f1 scores are missing? This is not explained in the text. Second, why is only the \\\"PN\\\" model presented? Is this the same PN as in Table 1, or actually the Joint Model? What about the other three? - It is not mentioned which dataset the experiment described in Table 4 was performed on. General Discussion: - 132: There has to be a lengthier introduction to pointer networks, mentioning recurrent neural networks in general, for the benefit of readers unfamiliar with \\\"sequence-to-sequence models\\\". Also, the citation of Sutskever et al.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes a scalable scattering network with higher order nonlinearities, but the mathematical derivation is not convincing and the experimental section is limited and does not convincingly show advantages over standard scattering transform. The writing and organization are poorly structured, making the paper difficult to follow, and the motivation for the proposed nonlinearity is questioned based on Waldspurger, 2016. Adding a second order term to the descriptor is considered interesting if stability is preserved, yet direct comparison of running times and compression rates is missing, and empirical evidence that the higher order nonlinearity produces sparser representations than the complex modulus is not provided. The proof in Section 2.1 lacks a clear proposition statement. Figure 3 is difficult to understand and needs more details. Figure 5 does not show the claimed comparison to a standard scattering network implementation. Typographical errors include “Hadamart” for Hadamard, “Valid set” for Validation set, and “nonzeros coefficients” for nonzero coefficients.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper presents a method for predicting video sequences in the lines of Mathieu et al. The contribution is the separation of the predictor into two different networks, picking up motion and content, respectively. The paper is very interesting, but the novelty is low compared to the referenced work. As also pointed out by AnonReviewer1, there is a similarity with two-stream networks (and also a whole body of work building on this seminal paper). Separating motion and content has also been proposed for other applications, e.g. pose estimation. Details : The paper can be clearly understood if the basic frameworks (like GANs) are known, but the presentation is not general and good enough for a broad public. Example : Losses (7) to (9) are well known from the Matthieu et al. paper. However, to make the paper self-contained, they should be properly explained, and it should be mentioned that they are \\\"additional\\\" losses. The main target is the GAN loss. The adversarial part of the paper is not properly enough introduced. I do agree, that adversarial training is now well enough known in the community, but it should still be properly introduced. This also involves the explanation that L_Disc is the loss for a second network, the discriminator and explaining the role of both etc. Equation (1) : c is not explained (are these motion vectors)? c is also overloaded with the feature dimension c'. The residual nature of the layer should be made more apparent in equation (3). There are several typos, absence of articles and prepositions (\\\"of\\\" etc.). The paper should be reread carefully.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper proposes ENet for real-time semantic segmentation and reports speed advantages that reach up to 18x faster than existing models. Those claims are accompanied by reported reductions of 75x fewer FLOPs and 79x fewer parameters. The speed and efficiency numbers are striking, yet the evaluation does not isolate which design choices produce the gains. No measurements show the individual effect of each speed optimization decision, making it difficult to assess which components matter most. The absence of such ablations weakens the contribution as a practical implementation study. The system shows decent accuracy on the majority of benchmarks, and the accuracy is similar or somewhat worse than SegNet on three datasets. However, the evaluation tables compare only against SegNet even though the related work discusses other systems. This narrow comparison limits confidence in the claimed speed and efficiency advantages. Adding Pascal or COCO datasets with a larger ENet version would test whether the architecture scales. The current evaluation on CamVid, Cityscapes, and SUN is informative but insufficient for a system paper. The paper is rejected as a practical implementation paper.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper studies the optimization issue of linear ResNet, and shows mathematically that for 2-shortcuts and zero initialization, the Hessian has condition number independent of depth. I skimmed through the proof but have not checked them carefully. This result is a nice observation for training deep linear networks. But I do not think the paper has fully resolved the linear vs nonlinear issue. Some question: 1. Though the revision has added some results using ReLU units, it seems it is only added to the mid positions of the network (sec 5.3), is this how it is typically done in ResNet? Moreover, ReLU is not differentiable at zero point, which does not satisfy the condition you had in Theorem 1. Why not use differentiable activations like sigmoid or tanh? 2. From equation (22) in the appendix, it seems for nonlinear activations, the condition number depends on the derivative \\\\sigma^\\\\prime at 0. Therefore, if we use tanh which has derivative 1 at zero, the condition number is the same for linear and tanh activations. But this probably is not enough to explain the bit difference in performance or optimization for linear and nonlinear networks, or how the situations evolve after learning the 0 point. 3. As for the success of ResNet (or convnets in general) in computer vision, I believe there are more types of nonlinearity such as pooling? Can the result here generalizes to pooling as well? Minor: - sec 1 last paragraph, low approximation error typically means more powerful model class and better training error, but not necessarily better test error - sec 4.1 what do you mean by \\\"zero initialization with small random perturbations\\\"?\", \"sampled\": \"The main idea of FiGAR is considered quite interesting, and the paper tackles a relevant problem in temporal abstraction for deep reinforcement learning. The framework’s attempt to learn when to repeat actions rather than relying on fixed schemes is a welcome direction. The reviewer questions whether forward propagating at every time step during test time could improve performance over repeating actions. Forward propagation is not computationally expensive for the problems considered, so test-time action repetition is not forced by a bottleneck. This weakens the motivation. The claim that FiGAR outperforms TRPO is a stretch given current results. Performance on the Ant-v1 task is too low for both TRPO and FiGAR, and running more iterations with better network initialization might significantly improve performance for both methods. Learning curves comparing FiGAR with TRPO would be informative for assessing relative sample efficiency and stability. Hyper-parameter settings for section 5.2 were not shared, including TRPO iterations and trajectory samples per iteration. FiGAR policies appear to have more parameters than the baselines they are compared against, and a comparison to baselines with an equal number of parameters was not provided. Action repetition is 1 for a majority of MuJoCo tasks and TORCS, raising doubt about FiGAR’s expected improvement in those domains. The idea of not repeating actions at test time is popular in Receding Horizon Control and MCTS variants. This connection could be discussed more explicitly to situate the contribution.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper proposed The Gated Multimodal Unit (GMU) model for information fusion. The GMU learns to decide how modalities influence the activation of the unit using multiplicative gates. The paper collected a large genre dataset from IMDB and showed that GMU gets good performance. The proposed approach seems quite interesting, and the audience may expect it can be used in general scenarios beyond movie genre prediction. So it is quite straightforward that the paper should test the algorithm in other applications, which was not done yet. That is the biggest shortcoming of this paper in my opinions. Another concern lies in how to evaluate the performance of information fusion. The abstract claims \\\"The model improves the macro f-score performance of single-modality models by 30% and 4% with respect to visual and textual information respectively\\\", however, such an improvement is off the key. If two modals are complementary to each other, the fusion results will always be higher. The key fact is how much better than baselines the proposed GMU is. There is a long list of techniques for fusions, so it is difficult to conduct an impressive comparison on only one real dataset. I think GMU did a nice work on movie dataset, but I would also expect other techniques, including fine-tuning, dropout, distillation may help too. It would be nice if the author could compare these techniques. I also hope this paper could talk in more details the connection with mixture-of-expert (MoE) model. Both models are based on the nonlinear gated functions, while both method may suffer from local minimum for optimization on small datasets. I would like more in-depth discussion in their similarity and difference. To gain more attention for GMU, I would encourage the author to open-source their code and try more datasets.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper presents a thorough and well designed experimental study that investigates model distillation with deep convolutional networks. The work builds upon preliminary results from Ba & Caruana, 2014, by using data augmentation and Bayesian hyperparameter optimization. The authors apply these methods to train a series of models on the CIFAR-10 dataset for this investigation. The study trains deep convolutional networks using Bayesian hyperparameter optimization with Spearmint ultimately producing 129 models. An ensemble of the 16 best models, selected based on performance on a 10,000 image validation set, serves as a teacher. Student models both convolutional and non convolutional are then trained to match the teacher's averaged logits. The results are convincing despite the difficulty of proving a negative. The authors find that student models with one or two convolutional layers cannot match the performance of students with more layers, even when the parameter count is held constant. The best model achieved an accuracy of 92.78%, while the fifth best model achieved 92.67%. The manuscript contains some minor English usage issues and typos that should be corrected. The paper does not adhere to the ICLR citation style, which states: \\\"When the authors or the publication are included in the sentence, the citation should not be in parenthesis (as in “See Hinton et al. (2006) for more information.”). Otherwise, the citation should be in parenthesis (as in “Deep learning shows promise to make progress towards AI (Bengio & LeCun, 2007).”).\\\" This formatting should be fixed throughout the paper. A minor clarification is suggested for Section 2.2 regarding the term \\\"logits\\\" used in the distillation framework. It would be helpful to explicitly state that the logits being matched are the unnormalized log-probabilities from the teacher model.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Great job with the paper. I love the idea. It seems the main weakness is the lack of broader context. Specifically, Baselines: - it'd be nice to see bAbI results from other papers in Table 1, to quickly get a sense for how your results compare - have you thought about baseline methods for the experiments in 4.2? - are there any other tasks like bAbI where you can compare GGT-NN to existing results from the literature? Related work: - I'd appreciate a related work section with discussion of the similarities and differences to other models with more or less structured memory representations including, e.g., Memory Networks, Hierarchical Attentive Memory, and Differentiable Neural Computers (DNC). Finally, have you considered training your model with a mixture of strong and weak supervision?\", \"sampled\": \"This paper introduces a technique for stabilizing Generative Adversarial Networks. I strongly recommend it for acceptance. The proposed method unrolls the discriminator's optimization and optimizes the generator against its final state. The experimental evidence for this technique’s effectiveness is very compelling as a 2D toy problem demonstrated that the method helps substantially and an experiment with an LSTM MNIST generator showed the procedure helps stabilize training for an unusual generator architecture. An image generation experiment was also considered very convincing in its results. While this experiment was not definitive on its own, it provided strong support for the claims regarding the stabilization of the training process. The reviewer found the experimental evidence for the technique's effectiveness to be very compelling. Future work could explore a similar method that has smaller memory requirements than the one proposed here.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper presented a modified knowledge distillation framework that minimizes the difference of the sum of statistics across the a feature map between the teacher and the student network. The authors empirically demonstrated the proposed methods outperform the fitnet style distillation baseline. Pros: + The author evaluated the proposed methods on various computer vision dataset + The paper is in general well-written Cons: - The method seems to be limited to the convolutional architecture - The attention terminology is misleading in the paper. The proposed method really just try to distill the summed squared(or other statistics e.g. summed lp norm) of activations in a hidden feature map. - The gradient-based attention transfer seems out-of-place. The proposed gradient-based methods are never compared directly to nor are used jointly with the \\\"attention-based\\\" transfer. It seems like a parallel idea added to the paper that does not seem to add much value.\", \"sampled\": \"This paper proposes using squared modulus nonlinearities within convolutional architectures, and the authors analyze this specific architecture in the context of scattering transforms. The work also presents a complexity analysis alongside some preliminary numerical experiments. The reviewers and the Area Chair (AC) all agreed that the paper’s exposition and experimental section were insufficient for publication at this time. A key missing element identified by the reviewers was a direct comparison to the most immediate baseline, which is scattering with complex modulus. This comparison was deemed necessary for both accuracy and computational complexity. The absence of this direct comparison with the most relevant baseline was a significant issue. For example, the paper presents a complexity analysis and some preliminary experiments. However, without the baseline, it is impossible to contextualize these results. The AC considered this omission a \\\"show-stopper.\\\" Consequently, the paper in its current form is not recommended for acceptance.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"UPDATE: I looked at the arxiv version of the paper. It is much longer and appears more rigorous. Fig 3 there is indeed more insightful. However, I am reviewing the submission and my overall assessment does not change. This is not a minor incremental contribution, and if you want to compress it into a conference submission of this type, I would recommend choosing message you want to convey, and focus on that. As you say, \\\"...ICLR submission focus on the ParMAC algorithm...\\\", I would focus on this properly - and remove or move to appendix all extensions and theoretical remarks, and have an extra page on explaining the algorithm. Additionally, make sure to clearly explain the relation of the arxiv paper, in particular that the submission was a compressed version. ORIGINAL REVIEW: The submission proposes ParMAC, based on MAC (Method of Auxiliary Coordinates), formulating a distributed variant of the idea. Related Work: In the part on convex ERM and methods, I would recommend citing general communication efficient frameworks, COCOA (Ma et al.) and AIDE (Reddi et al.). I believe these works are most related to the practical objectives authors of this paper set, while number of the papers cited are less relevant. Section 2, explaining MAC, is quite clearly written, but I do not find part on MAC and EM particularly useful. Section 3 is much less clearly written. I have trouble following notation, particularly in the speedups part, as different symbols were introduced at different places. Perhaps a quick summary or paragraph on notation in the introduction would be helpful. In paragraph 2, you write as if reader knew how data/anything is distributed, but this was not mentioned yet; it is specified later. It is not clear what is meant by \\\"submodel\\\". Perhaps a more precise example pointing back to eqs (1) & (2) would be useful. As far as I understand from what is written, there are P independent sets of submodels, that traverse the machines in circular fashion. I don't understand how are they initialized (identically?), and more importantly I don't understand what would be a single output of the algorithm (averaging? does not seem to make sense). Since this is not addressed, I suppose I get it wrong, leaving me to guess what was actually meant. The fact that I am not able to understand what is actually happening, I see as major issue. I don't like the later paragraphs on extensions, model for speedup, convergence and topologies. I don't understand whether these are novel contributions or not, as the authors refer to other work for details. If these are novel, the explanation is not sufficient, particularly speedup part, which contains undefined quantities, e.g. T(P) (or I can't find it).\", \"sampled\": \"This paper, “Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,” is generally well described. The work presents a compelling method for increasing model capacity. It introduces a Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts layer, or MoE. The core technique involves placing this MoE between recurrent net layers. The abstract highlights a claimed 1000x improvement in model capacity, leading to models with as many as 137 billion parameters, which is a significant scale. The experiments demonstrate that the proposed system achieves good results on the selected tasks. The description of the system itself is clear and understandable. The Mixture-of-Experts layer is shared across all time steps, which increases the effective batch size available for the individual expert networks. This architectural choice allows each expert within the MoE to receive a sub-batch that is sufficiently large and helps maintain computational efficiency despite the expanded model size. A second technique is also described which redistributes elements in a distributed model to further increase the per-expert batch sizes. The experiments are a strength of the paper and demonstrate significant gains on two important tasks: language modeling and machine translation. The results show a positive correlation between increasing the number of experts and performance improvements. The paper is well written, and the proposed system is described effectively. However, the evaluation could be strengthened with more detailed analysis of the system's operational characteristics. The manuscript would benefit from plots or statistics on the actual computational load, which would provide a clearer picture of the system's behavior under different conditions and help readers understand the practical trade-offs involved. One area for improvement is the analysis of the loss function. The paper describes the use of two loss terms to balance the utilization of the experts, which is a critical component. The experiments, however, did not include an exploration of these two specific loss terms. Providing an ablation study or sensitivity analysis on these components would offer valuable insight into their impact on model training and final performance. It would clarify how they contribute to balancing expert usage. This information is currently missing. Figure 3, while useful, could be made clearer for the reader. The correspondence between data points is difficult to follow. There is also an issue with mismatched colors between the plots, which complicates interpretation. Specifically, the left plot of Figure 3 contains three points for the H-H line, while the right plot contains five points for the same line, which could be confusing without further explanation.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"SUMMARY. This paper presents a method for enriching medical concepts with their parent nodes in an ontology. The method employs an attention mechanism over the parent nodes of a medical concept to create a richer representation of the concept itself. The rationale of this is that for infrequent medical concepts the attention mechanism will rely more on general concepts, higher in the ontology hierarchy, while for frequent ones will focus on the specific concept. The attention mechanism is trained together with a recurrent neural network and the model accuracy is tested on two tasks. The first task aims at prediction the diagnosis categories at each time step, while the second task aims at predicting whether or not a heart failure is likely to happen after the T-th step. Results shows that the proposed model works well in condition of data insufficiency. OVERALL JUDGMENT The proposed model is simple but interesting. The ideas presented are worth to expand but there are also some points where the authors could have done better. The learning of the representation of concepts in the ontology is a bit naive, for example the authors could have used some kind of knowledge base factorization approach to learn the concepts, or some graph convolutional approach. I do not see why the the very general factorization methods for knowledge bases do not apply in the case of ontology learning.\", \"sampled\": \"This is a good paper containing a variety of experiments. The work investigates the concept of \\\"Sample Importance\\\" and its effect on the learning process during stochastic gradient descent. It provides empirical evidence that different training samples produce larger gradients at various stages of learning and in different network layers. A key and insightful finding is that constructing batches with a mix of hard and easy samples is a superior strategy compared to ordering them strictly by difficulty, as measured by NLL or SI. This particular result is interesting because it contradicts some common ideas found within curriculum learning, which often advocate for using easy samples first. The experiments demonstrating that ordering by NLL or SI is worse than using mixed batches are a valuable contribution to the field. The paper was not accepted for publication. Easy. A primary weakness is the unclear definition of \\\"Easy\\\" training cases. The model could be improved by factoring out gradient magnitudes from the contribution of \\\"sample importance\\\" to help reduce noise. This investigation into Sample Importance (SI) should be extended to develop a practical batch selection algorithm. Such an algorithm could then be tested to see if it can outperform random selection in a meaningful way. Showing a clear performance benefit would strengthen the paper's overall impact and practical utility for training deep neural networks. The work investigates the concept of Sample Importance.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This paper analyzes dependency trees vs standard window contexts for word vector learning. While that's a good goal I believe the paper falls short of a thorough analysis of the subject matter. It does not analyze Glove like objective functions which often work better than the algorithms used here. It doesn't compare in absolute terms to other published vectors or models. It fails to gain any particularly interesting insights that will modify other people's work. It fails to push the state of the art or make available new resources for people.\", \"sampled\": \"The authors have uploaded a revised version that includes an updated Introduction/Discussion section to better motivate the use of MSE. The text now clarifies that uniform noise is used for optimization only with final compression results based on quantized and entropy coded values. The manuscript also elaborates on the visual appearance of the compressed results. Additional tests were conducted on new images including what the authors term \\\"classic\\\" examples like Barbara, Lena, Mandrill, and Peppers.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"It is interesting to derive such a bound and show it satisfies a regret bound along with empirical evidence on the CIFAR-10 for cross entropy loss and auto encoder for MSE loss. At least empirically, by comparing the observed training loss and taylor loss, the better a particular optimizer performs (training loss statement, not a validation or observed test statement) the smaller the difference between these two. Also shown is the regret loss is satisfied at different scales of the network, by layer, neuron and whole network. The Taylor approximation can be used to investigate activation configurations of the network, and used to connect this to difficulty in optimizing at kinks in the loss surface, along with an empirical study of exploration of activation surface of the SGD/Adam/RMSprop optimizers, the more exploration the better the resulting training loss. Not that it impacts the paper but the weaker performance of the SGD could be related to the fixed learning rate, if we anneal this learning rate, which should improve performance, does this translate to more exploration and tightening between the actual loss and the Taylor loss?\", \"sampled\": \"\\\"Robust Incremental Neural Semantic Graph Parsing,\\\" introduces an end-to-end neural encoder-decoder transition based parser for semantic graphs which is a very interesting approach for learning DMRS semantic representations. The model is noted for its incremental framework, which enables low cost parsing, and its use of a well-designed transition system to achieve a reported Smatch score of 86.69% on an Abstract Meaning Representation dataset. The work’s ability to learn DMRS representations is a significant contribution. The primary concern with the manuscript is the lack of detailed descriptions for the core components specifically regarding DMRS, the transition system, and EP, which makes the paper difficult to understand for readers who do not already possess a specialized background in Minimal Recursion Semantics and its related formalisms. Without sufficient explanation of these foundational elements, challenging for a broader audience to fully grasp the mechanics and novelty of the proposed neural model, which relies heavily on the intricacies of the well-designed transition system for parsing DMRS semantic representations from natural language. The reviewer acknowledges that space limitations may have necessitated the concise presentation of the methodology but the current level of detail hinders the paper's accessibility and potential impact for those outside the immediate subfield. The application of DMRS for semantic parsing is interesting.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The paper addresses the problem of learning compact binary data representations. I have a hard time understanding the setting and the writing of the paper is not making it any easier. For example I can't find a simple explanation of the problem and I am not familiar with these line of research. I read all the responses provided by authors to reviewer's questions and re-read the paper again and I still do not fully understand the setting and thus can't really evaluate the contributions of these work. The related work section does not exist and instead the analysis of the literature is somehow scattered across the paper. There are no derivations provided. Statements often miss references, e.g. the ones in the fourth paragraph of Section 3. This makes me conclude that the paper still requires significant work before it can be published.\", \"sampled\": \"This is a great and very interesting piece of work on image compression using autoencoders. The manuscript would benefit from clarification on how the scale parameters are trained and updated for different bit-rates and it would be helpful to understand the intuition for why incremental training is beneficial. Regarding the results, more details are needed for Figure 3A, specifically an explanation of what the x-axis represents. In Figure 3B, it is observed that the loss becomes stable after approximately 100 thousand iterations and it is worth clarifying if this sudden stability is a result of a decreased learning rate. Further information is also requested on the GSM modeling of the quantized coefficients including the number of mixtures that were used. How was the GSM trained? For example, was a method like the EM algorithm employed for this process?\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Pros: * The general idea behind the paper seems pretty novel and potentially quite cool. * The specific technical implementation seems pretty reasonable and well-thought through. * The general types of the tasks that they try out their approach on spans a wide and interesting spectrum of cognition abilities. * The writing is pretty clear. I basically felt like I could replicate much of what they did from their paper descriptions. Cons: * The evaluation of the success of these ideas, as compared to other possible approaches, or as compared to human performance on similar tasks, is extremely cursory. * The specific tasks that they try are quite simple. I really don't know whether their approach is better than a bunch of simpler things on these tasks. Taking these two cons together, it feels like the authors basically get the implementation done and working somewhat, and then just wrote up the paper. (I know how it feels to be under a deadline without a complete set of results.)\", \"sampled\": \"The authors propose using chirplets for modeling audio signals and introduce a fast chiplet transform for efficient computation which is a key contribution of this work. The paper further suggests pre-training CNN layers to mimic this transform drawing a parallel to the work of Mallet et al. on scattering transforms. The paper is easy to follow. However, it contains some undefined terms such as AM-FM and MAP, which should be clarified for the reader to fully understand the presented methodology. The main concern is the limited empirical evidence. The evaluation is confined to the narrow domain of bird call classification. The accuracy gains presented within this specific domain are relatively small, which raises questions about the broader applicability and significance of the proposed method. The chirplet transforms achieved a 61.5% MAP, which is only a marginal improvement over the 61% MAP achieved by the baseline log-Mel features. To strengthen the paper's claims, the authors should provide evidence demonstrating how this method generalizes to other audio tasks beyond bird call classification. Including results from tasks such as speech would provide a more comprehensive assessment of the transform's utility.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"- Strengths: The paper addresses a long standing problem concerning automatic evaluation of the output of generation/translation systems. The analysis of all the available metrics is thorough and comprehensive. The authors demonstrate a new metric with a higher correlation with human judgements The bibliography will help new entrants into the field. - Weaknesses: The paper is written as a numerical analysis paper, with very little insights to linguistic issues in generation, the method of generation, the differences in the output from a different systems and human generated reference. It is unclear if the crowd source generated references serve well in the context of an application that needs language generation. - General Discussion: Overall, the paper could use some linguistic examples (and a description of the different systems) at the risk of dropping a few tables to help the reader with intuitions.\", \"sampled\": \"The semi-supervised learning experiment setup requires clarification, particularly for the evaluation of the adversarially learned inference model on the semi-supervised SVHN task. The authors should specify how label information was incorporated during the training process. It is unclear whether features from the encoder or the discriminator were used for this experiment. The paper’s arXiv version described training an L2-SVM on the encoder's final layers. The performance reported in the arXiv version of the paper was observed to be significantly lower than the performance that has been reported in the current openreview version, which creates a discrepancy that should be addressed for reproducibility and clear evaluation of the proposed model. The authors should explain the differences in the experimental setup between the two versions that could account for this performance gap in the results on SVHN.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The authors propose a method to generate adversarial examples w/o relying on knowledge of the network architecture or network gradients. The idea has some merit, however, as mentioned by one of the reviewers, the field has been studied widely, including black box setups. My main concern is that the first set of experiments allows images that are not in image space. The authors acknowledge this fact on page 7 in the first paragraph. In my opinion, this renders these experiments completely meaningless. At the very least, the outcome is not surprising to me at all. The greedy search procedure remedies this issue. The description of the proposed method is somewhat convoluted. AFAICT, first a candidate set of pixels is generated by using PERT. Then the pixels are perturbed using CYCLIC. It is not clear why this approach results in good/minimal perturbations as the candidate pixels are found using a large \\\"p\\\" that can result in images outside the image space. The choice of this method does not seem to be motivated by the authors.\", \"sampled\": \"The proposal introduces the concept of the Chirplet kernel as a proficient Q constant bioacoustic representation to pretrain Convolutional Neural Nets (CNNs). A significant contribution lies in the unveiling of the initial algorithm and code for the Fast Chirplet Transform (FCT), marking a pivotal advancement in signal representation techniques. The Fast Chirplet Transform (FCT) showcases remarkable computational efficiency, particularly evident in its successful application to extensive environmental datasets such as Orca recordings and various bird species from the LifeCLEF challenge. Validation exercises confirm the efficacy of the FCT on a subset of vowels from the Speech TIMIT dataset, solidifying its utility in enhancing signal processing tasks. The integration of FCT into CNN pretraining strategies yields notable benefits, with training durations reduced by 28% for birds classification and 26% for vowels classification, showcasing its practical impact on model optimization. Moreover, the FCT pretraining significantly boosts performance metrics, manifesting as a 7.8% increase in Mean Average Precision for birds and a 2.3% enhancement in vowel accuracy compared to raw audio CNN approaches. The study also hints at promising avenues for tonotopic FCT deep machine listening and cross-species bioacoustic transfer learning.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"A well written paper and an interesting construction - I thoroughly enjoyed reading it. I found the formalism a bit hard to follow without specific examples- that is, it wasn't clear to me at first what the specific components in figure 1A were. What constitutes the controller, a control, the optimizer, what was being optimized, etc., in specific cases. Algorithm boxes may have been helpful, especially in the case of your experiments. A description of existing models that fall under your conceptual framework might help as well. In Practical Bayesian Optimization of Machine Learning Algorithms, Snoek, Larochelle and Adams propose optimizing with respect to expected improvement per second to balance computation cost and performance loss. It might be interesting to see how this falls into your framework. Experimental results were presented clearly and well illustrated the usefulness of the metacontroller. I'm curious to see the results of using more metaexperts.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper explores automating algorithm design. The method presented learns an optimization algorithm using Guided Policy Search. It compares the performance to Adam and RMSprop optimization algorithms. Experimental analysis on toy datasets were conducted to validate the approach. The number of parameters optimized is limited to no more than 10. The state space dimension is determined by the formula (1+N)*H, where H equals 25 in this study. This equation plays a crucial role in modeling the problem accurately. The policy network, with 50 hidden units, has more parameters to learn than the problem parameters. This highlights the complexity involved in training the network effectively. The computational complexity for practical use should not exceed the complexity of the training algorithm. This is important for assessing the scalability and practical efficiency of the proposed approach.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper proposed an integration of memory network with reinforcement learning. The experimental data is simple, but the model is very interesting and relatively novel. There are some questions about the model: 1. how does the model extend to the case with multiple variables in a single sentence? 2. If the answer is out of vocabulary, how would the model handle it? 3. I hope the authors can provide more analysis about the curriculum learning part, since it is very important for the RL model training. 4. In the training, in each iteration, how the data samples were selected, by random or from simple one depth to multiple depth?\", \"sampled\": \"The authors present a novel semi-supervised approach for neural networks, drawing inspiration from label propagation. This method bears resemblance to the one introduced by Weston et al in 2008. By leveraging the adjacency matrix as input in the absence of other features, the authors demonstrate a unique utilization of available data, showing innovation in their methodology. In experiments focusing on text classification, the authors utilize word2vec average embedding to construct the adjacency matrix. However, the achieved accuracies in their experiments fall short of the results reported by Zhang et al in 2015.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The authors propose a transfer learning approach applied to a number of NLP tasks; the set of tasks appear to have an order in terms of complexity (from easy syntactic tasks to somewhat harder semantic tasks). Novelty: the way the authors propose to do transfer learning is by plugging models corresponding to each task, in a way that respects the known hierarchy (in terms of NLP \\\"complexity\\\") of those tasks. In that respect, the overall architecture looks more like a cascaded architecture than a transfer learning one.\", \"sampled\": \"The study introduces a novel method for training probabilistic models with discrete latent variables, addressing the challenge of efficiently training such models. The method notably incorporates backpropagation through discrete latent variables, contributing to the field's advancement in handling discrete classes within probabilistic models. This approach allows modeling of datasets with discrete classes, showing the potential of probabilistic models with discrete latent variables in capturing complex data distributions efficiently.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Encouraging orthogonality in weight features has been reported useful for deep networks in many previous works. The authors present a explicit regularization cost to achieve de-correlation among weight features in a layer and encourage orthogonality. Further, they also show why and how negative correlations can and should be avoided for better de-correlation. Orthogonal weight features achieve better generalization in case of large number of trainable parameters and less training data, which usually results in over-fitting. As also mentioned by the authors biases help in de-correlation of feature responses even in the presence of correlated features (weights).\", \"sampled\": \"The study highlights the significance of adjusting for dropout variance to enhance the accuracy of neural networks. This adjustment helps improve model performance, underscoring the importance of variance management in training processes. Furthermore, the novel weight initialization method addresses dropout rates and the influence of nonlinearity, showing a comprehensive approach to optimizing neural network training. By correcting these factors, the new weight initialization technique contributes to more stable and effective model learning. The research achieves state-of-the-art results on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 datasets without resorting to data augmentation techniques. This accomplishment underscores the efficacy of the proposed methodologies in enhancing training accuracy and generalization performance across diverse datasets.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper studies the problem of transferring solutions of existing tasks to tackle a novel task under the framework of reinforcement learning and identifies two important issues of avoiding negative transfer and being selective transfer. The proposed approach is based on a convex combination of existing solutions and the being-learned solution to the novel task. The non-negative weight of each solution implies that the solution of negative effect is ignored and more weights are allocated to more relevant solution in each state. This paper derives this so-called \\\"A2T\\\" learning algorithm for policy transfer and value transfer for REINFORCE and ACTOR-CRITIC algorithms and experiments with synthetic Chain World and Puddle World simulation and Atari 2600 game Pong. +This paper presents a novel approach for transfer reinforcement learning. +The experiments are cleverly designed to demonstrate the ability of the proposed method. -An important aspect of transfer learning is that the algorithm can automatically figure out if the existing solutions to known tasks are sufficient to solve the novel task so that it can save the time and energy of learning-from-scratch. This issue is not studied in this paper as most of experiments have a learning-from-scratch solution as base network. It will be interesting to see how well the algorithm performs without base network. In addition, from Figure 3, 5 and 6, the proposed algorithm seems to accelerate the learning speed, but the overall network seems not better than the solo base network. It will be more convincing to show some example that existing solutions are complementary to the base network. -If ignoring the base network, the proposed network can be considered as ensemble reinforcement learning that take advantages of learned agents with different expertise to solve the novel task.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper under review “compares various activation function in deep learning, with a particular focus on the performance of the Parametric ELU (PELU) function. Notably, “PELU shows significant improvements over the Exponential Linear Unit (ELU), demonstrating relative error reductions of 4.45% on CIFAR-10 and 5.68% on CIFAR-100, highlighting its efficacy in enhancing model performance on these datasets. The experimental results further reveal that PELU outshines ELU by achieving a remarkable 7.28% relative error reduction on ImageNet, all while maintaining a minimal 0.0003% increase in parameters compared to ELU. This efficiency in performance coupled with parameter optimization signifies PELU as a promising alternative in activation functions for deep learning models, warranting its exploration in various network architectures. Moreover, the paper underscores PELU's preference for activations saturating closer to zero, akin to Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU), particularly in most layers except the final one. This nuanced behavior sheds light on the activation function's adaptability and its potential to mitigate issues like vanishing gradients and bias shift, thereby contributing to more stable and effective training processes. The study also emphasizes the importance of shaping activations during training to regulate vanishing gradients and bias shift, emphasizing the pivotal role that activation functions play in addressing these challenges. Furthermore, the practical experiments conducted on CIFAR-10/100 and ImageNet across diverse network architectures substantiate the efficacy and versatility of PELU in enhancing model performance and training efficiency. In conclusion, the paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the Parametric ELU activation function, showing its superiority over existing alternatives like ELU in various deep learning tasks.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper presents a method to learn both a model and inference procedure at the same time with recurrent neural networks in the context of inverse problems. The proposed method is interesting and results are quite good. The paper is also nicely presented. I would be happy to see some discussion about what the network learns in practice about natural images in the case of denoising. What are the filters like? Is it particularly sensitive to different structures in images? edges? Also, what is the state in the recurrent unit used for? when are the gates open etc. Nevertheless, I think this is nice work which should be accepted.\", \"sampled\": \"The evaluation highlights ENet's impressive speed, being up to 18 times faster than existing models. Additionally, ENet stands out for its efficiency, requiring 75 times fewer FLOPs and using 79 times fewer parameters compared to current models. These efficiency gains position ENet as a compelling architecture for real-time semantic segmentation tasks, particularly those demanding low latency operations. ENet's performance extends beyond efficiency, showing comparable or superior accuracy to existing models. Moreover, the neural network has been rigorously tested on diverse datasets such as CamVid, Cityscapes, and SUN, underlining its versatility and robustness across different evaluation scenarios.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes a k-shot learning framework that can be used on existing pre-trained networks by grouping filters that produce similar activations. The grouped filters are learned together to address overfitting when only few training samples are available. The idea of the paper is interesting there are some encouraging results, but the current version doesn't seem ready for publication: Performance: The method should be compared with other state-of-the-art k-shot learning methods (e.g., Matching Networks by Vinyals et al., 2016). It's not clear how this method compares against them. Missing explanation: Experimental setting for k-shot learning should be more detailed. Measure: Accuracy difference does not look like a good idea for comparing the baseline method and the proposed one. Just raw accuracies would be fine. Many grammatical errors and inappropriate formatting of citations, such as: M. et al. (2011) ImageNet (Alex et al. (2012)) Judy et al. (2013): this reference appears three times in the reference section.\", \"sampled\": \"The fusion learning architecture proposed by Fu et al, 2013 for constructing semantic hierarchies achieved a notable F1-score of 74.20% with high precision of 91.60%. This method outperformed state-of-the-art techniques on a manually labeled test dataset, showing its effectiveness in semantic hierarchy construction. Combining the fusion learning architecture with manually-built hierarchies led to a significant improvement in performance, enhancing the F1-score to 82.01%. This integration highlights the potential of leveraging existing knowledge structures to boost the system's overall accuracy and efficiency. One strength of the fusion learning architecture is its language-independent nature, demonstrating versatility across different linguistic contexts. Additionally, its knowledge lean approach signifies an efficient utilization of resources in semantic hierarchy construction. A weakness identified in the study is the peculiar task/setting, which may pose challenges or limitations in certain applications. Furthermore, the marginal improvement over Fu et al, 2014 raises questions about the significance of the advancements introduced in this work.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"For more than a decade, near data processing has been a key requirement for large scale linear learning platforms, as the time to load the data exceeds the learning time, and this has justified the introduction of approaches such as Spark Deep learning usually deals with the data that can be contained in a single machine and the bottleneck is often the CPU-GPU bus or the GPU-GPU-bus, so a method that overcomes this bottleneck could be relevant. Unfortunately, this work is still very preliminary and limited to linear training algorithms, so of little interest yet to ICLR readership. I would recommend publication to a conference where it can reach the large-scale linear ML audience first, such as ICML. This paper is clear and well written in the present form and would probably mostly need a proper benchmark on a large scale linear task. Obviously, when the authors have convincing DNN learning simulations, they are welcome to target ICLR, but can the flash memory FPGA handle it?\", \"sampled\": \"The paper discusses the utilization of relational embeddings for rule mining within knowledge bases. This highlights the practical application of embedding techniques in extracting rules from complex data structures, showing the potential for enhancing knowledge discovery processes through innovative methods. An intriguing aspect presented is the interpretation of logical conjunction as a form of composition between relation embeddings. This conceptual framework offers a novel perspective on how logical operations can be mapped and manipulated within embedding spaces, enriching the understanding of relational structures in data analysis. The methodology proposed in the study focuses on mining comprehensive logical theories, emphasizing the depth and breadth of the rules extracted using the presented approach. By delving into full-fledged logical frameworks, the research pushes the boundaries of traditional rule mining techniques and opens up new avenues for knowledge representation and inference. Furthermore, the paper addresses the incorporation of sparse reconstruction procedures from compressed sensing to extract relevant rules from the data. This integration of advanced data reconstruction methods underscores the sophistication of the rule mining process, highlighting a nuanced approach to deriving meaningful insights from intricate datasets.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes a model that generates a latent representation of input image(s) and optimizes a reconstruction loss with an adversarial loss (Eq (1)) over nearest neighbors from a bank of images (“memory”). The framework is adapted to three tasks: (i) image in-painting, (ii) intrinsic image decomposition, (iii) figure-ground layer extraction. Qualitative results are shown for all three tasks. I think the proposed model has potential merits. I particularly like the fact that it seems to be reasoning over image composites via matching against a bank of images (somewhat similar to “Segmenting Scenes by Matching Image Composites” work in NIPS 2009). However, I won’t champion the paper as the overall clarity and evaluation could be improved. More detailed comments: I believe the fatal flaw of the paper is there is no quantitative evaluation of the approach. At the very least, there should be a comparison against prior work on intrinsic image decomposition (e.g., SIRFS, maybe benchmark on \\\"intrinsic images in the wild” dataset). I found the writing vague and confusing throughout. For instance, “memory database” could mean a number of things, and in the end it seems that it’s simply a set of images. “Imagination” is also vague. On page 4, R(M,x) has the database and input image as arguments, but Fig 2 doesn’t show the input image as an input to R. The contributions listed on page 3 should be tightened (e.g., it’s not clear what “Relevant memory retrieval for informative adversarial priors” means). Fig 3 seems inconsistent with Fig 2 as the module for “memory database” is not present.\", \"sampled\": \"The proposed model demonstrates superior performance compared to baselines when using supervised data. This success underscores the effectiveness of the encoder-decoder framework in  keyphrase generation. The model's ability to achieve a significant performance boost in keyphrase extraction is a notable advancement in the field, highlighting its potential for practical applications. The model's capacity to generate keyphrases that are not present in the input text based on semantic meaning represents a substantial leap forward in keyphrase generation techniques. This capability opens up possibilities for enhancing the relevance and comprehensiveness of generated keyphrases, indicating a nuanced understanding of semantic contexts within the model's operations. The absence of major surprises in the proposed approach may suggest a strong alignment with existing methodologies in the field. While this lack of novelty may not detract from the model's effectiveness, further exploration of innovative elements could enhance the overall impact and uniqueness of the proposed keyphrase generation model. The challenge of transferring a trained model to a new domain is a significant hurdle that the research grapples with. Understanding the limitations in domain transfer is crucial for practical deployment and scalability of the proposed keyphrase generation model. Addressing this issue could substantially improve the model's versatility and real-world applicability. The meticulous attention maintain the quantity and quality of training corpora emerges as a valuable contribution of the study. This focus on the foundational elements of model training underscores the researchers' commitment to ensuring the robustness and reliability of the keyphrase generation model. Such diligence in data curation is commendable and bolsters the credibility of the research findings.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This paper proposes an approach to learning a custom optimizer for a given class optimization problems. I think in the case of training machine learning algorithms, a class would represent a model like “logistic regression”. The authors cleverly cast this as a reinforcement learning problem and use guided policy search to train a neural network to map the current location and history onto a step direction/magnitude. Overall I think this is a great idea and a very nice contribution to the fast growing meta-learning literature. However, I think that there are some aspects that could be touched on to make this a stronger paper. My first thought is that the authors claim to train the method to learn the regularities of an entire class of optimization problems, rather than learning to exploit regularities in a given set of tasks. The distinction here is not terribly clear to me. For example, in learning an optimizer for logistic regression, the authors seem to claim that learning on a randomly sampled set of logistic regression problems will allow the model to learn about logistic regression itself. I am not convinced of this, because there is bias in the randomly sampled data itself. From the paper in this case, “The instances are drawn randomly from two multivariate Gaussians with random means and covariances, with half drawn from each.” It seems the optimizer is then trained to optimize instances of logistic regression *given this specific family of training inputs* and not logistic regression problems in general. A simple experiment to prove the method works more generally would be to repeat the existing experiments, but where the test instances are drawn from a completely different distribution. It would be even more interesting to see how this changes as the test distribution deviates further from the training distribution. Can the authors comment on the choice of architecture used here? Why one layer with 50 hidden units and softplus activations specifically? Why not e.g., 100 units, 2 layers and ReLUs? Presumably this is to prevent overfitting, but given the limited capacity of the network, how do these results look when the dimensionality of the input space increases beyond 2 or 3? I would love to see what kind of policy the network learns on e.g., a 2D function using a contour plot. What do the steps look like on a random problem instance when compared to other hand-engineered optimizers? Overall I think this a really interesting paper with a great methodological contribution.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper titled \\\"Frustratingly Short Attention Spans in Neural Language Modeling\\\" presents a novel method for neural language modeling by proposing a key-value attention mechanism. This new approach reportedly outperforms existing memory augmented neural language models on two corpora. Interestingly, the method depends largely on the past memory of the previous five representations. However, a simpler model that concatenates output representations from the previous three-time steps performs comparably. The dimensionality of the vectors involved in the attention mechanism is a point of concern. The separation of key and value functionality as proposed in the paper, is not entirely novel. This concept has been introduced previously but not in the context of natural language modeling. The n-gram RNN, introduced as a baseline, illustrates the inability of more complex architectures to perform better. Although the paper is generally well-written, some model descriptions are unclear. The results highlight that learning long-term dependencies remains an unsolved problem in the field. Interestingly, the n-gram RNN often performs competitively with far more complicated approaches. The focus of the proposed method on the past five representations is noteworthy. This reliance on a limited number of previous states could potentially limit the model's ability to capture more complex patterns. However, the fact that a simpler model that uses only the previous three-time steps performs similarly raises questions about the necessity of the additional complexity introduced by the key-value attention mechanism. The paper presents a valuable contribution by showing that learning long-term dependencies is still a challenging task. Despite the complexity of the proposed key-value attention mechanism, a simpler n-gram RNN model can be competitive. This finding underscores the need for further investigation into the balance between model complexity and performance. The importance of the separation of key/value/prediction functionality in attention-based systems needs further investigation. This is a significant point that could have implications for future research in this area. The paper's choice of the n-gram RNN as a baseline provides a valuable perspective on the limitations of more complex architectures. This approach serves as a reminder that simpler models can often compete with more complex ones, highlighting the need for careful consideration when designing new methods. In conclusion, while the proposed key-value attention mechanism offers a promising new approach for neural language modeling, further investigation is needed to fully understand its potential and limitations. The results demonstrate the ongoing challenge of learning long-term dependencies, further emphasizing the importance of continued research in this area. The competitive performance of the simpler n-gram RNN model further underscores this point.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The paper proposes a model that aims at learning to label nodes of graph in a semi-supervised setting. The idea of the model is based on the use of the graph structure to regularize the representations learned at the node levels. Experimental results are provided on different tasks The underlying idea of this paper (graph regularization) has been already explored in different papers – e.g 'Learning latent representations of nodes for classifying in heterogeneous social networks' [Jacob et al. 2014], [Weston et al 2012] where a real graph structure is used instead of a built one. The experiments lack of strong comparisons with other graph models (e.g Iterative Classification, 'Learning from labeled and unlabeled data on a directed graph', ...). So the novelty of the paper and the experimental protocol are not strong enough to accpet the paper. Pros: * Learning over graph is an important topic Cons: * Many existing approaches have already exploited the same types of ideas, resulting in very close models * Lack of comparison w.r.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper titled \\\"Joint Extraction of Entities and Relations Based on a Novel Tagging Scheme\\\" proposes an innovative end-to-end model and tagging scheme for joint extraction of entities and relations. The authors have presented their method lucidly with the help of illustrative examples. The paper's contributions to the field of information extraction are outlined clearly. The paper also makes a comparative study of its methods with several classical triplet extraction methods including tagging-based methods and pipelined and joint learning methods. The authors have used a public dataset produced by a distant supervision method for their experiments. Interestingly, this dataset includes 24 different types of relations. However, the paper does not mention the number or type of named entities present in the dataset, which could be a potential area of improvement.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The authors investigate a variety of existing and two new RNN architectures to obtain more insight about the effectiveness at which these models can store task information in their parameters and activations. The experimental setups look sound. To generalize comparisons between different architectures it’s necessary to consider multiple tasks and control for the effect of the hyperparameters. This work uses multiple tasks of varying complexities, principled hyperparameter tuning methodology and a number of tuning iterations that can currently only be achieved by the computational resources of some of the larger industrial research groups. The descriptions of the models and the objective where very clear to me. The descriptions of the experiments and presentation of the results were not always clear to me at times, even with the additional details in the appendix available. Most of these issues can easily be resolved by editing the text. For example, in the memory task the scaling of the inputs (and hence also outputs) is not provided so it’s hard to interpret the squared error scores in Figure 2c. It’s not clear to me what the term ‘unrollings’ refers to in Figure 2b. Is this a time lag with additional hidden state updates between the presentation of the input sequence and the generation of the output? Since the perceptron capacity task is somewhat central to the paper, I think a slightly more precise description of how and when the predictions are computed would be helpful. Due to the large number of graphs, it can be somewhat hard to find the most relevant results. Perhaps some of the more obvious findings (like Figure 1(b-d) given Figure 1a) could move to the appendix to make space for more detailed task descriptions. Novelty is not really the aim of this paper since it mostly investigates existing architectures. To use the mutual information to obtain bits per parameter scores in highly non-linear parameterized functions is new to me. The paper also proposed to new architectures that seem to have practical value.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper titled \\\"Delving into Transferable Adversarial Examples and Black-box Attacks\\\" investigates the transferability of adversarial examples across large models and datasets. The paper introduces innovative ensemble-based methodologies for generating transferable adversarial examples. It presents a comprehensive comparison of different strategies across popular networks and two types of attacks. Particularly, the authors demonstrate that adversarial examples produced via their ensemble based approaches can effectively attack Clarifai.com. The authors also highlight that non-targeted attacks are relatively simple to execute, even on a black-box system. They propose a new approach for targeted attacks that shows significant improvements over existing attacks. Specifically, they report a remarkable ~20% success rate for their new approach for targeted attacks on Clarifai.com compared to the mere 2% success rate achieved by previous schemes. This underscores the effectiveness of their proposed methodology. However it's worth noting that the paper has some weaknesses as well. For instance, the authors treat the 3 ResNet based networks as different entities which could be questionable. They also employ a somewhat subjective measure to evaluate the effectiveness of their attacks which may not offer a fully objective assessment. Moreover, the novelty of the proposed approach could be further enhanced. While the ensemble based methodologies introduced in the paper are indeed innovative, the overall approach could be seen as an incremental advancement rather than a groundbreaking change. The authors might consider more radical departures from existing methodologies to truly push the boundaries of the field. In the context of related work the paper could be enriched by engaging more deeply with the recent and related work of Fawzi et al. This could provide valuable insights and potentially highlight areas of improvement for the authors' proposed methodologies. A more comprehensive comparison with Fawzi et al.'s work might also enhance the readers' understanding of the paper's contributions. In conclusion, this paper provides valuable insights into the transferability of adversarial examples and presents novel approaches for their generation. However, there is room for improvement in terms of novelty and the evaluation measures employed.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This work contributes to understanding the landscape of deep networks in terms of its topology and geometry. The paper analyzes the former theoretically, and studies the latter empirically. Although the provided contributions are very specific (ReLU nets with single hidden layer, and a heuristic to calculate the normalized geodesic), the results are original and of interest. Thus, they could potentially be used as stepping stones for deeper developments in this area. Pros: 1. Providing new theory about existence of \\\"poor\\\" local minima for ReLU networks with a hidden unit that relies on input distribution properties as well as the size of the hidden layer. 2. Coming up with a heuristic algorithm to compute the normalized geodesic between two solution points. The latter reflects how curved the path between the two is. Cons: The results are very specific in both topology and geometry analysis. 1. The analysis is performed only over a \\\"single\\\" hidden layer ReLU network. Given the importance of depth in deep architectures, this result cannot really explain the kinds of architectures we are interested in practically. 2. The normalized geodesic criterion is somewhat limited in representing how easy it is to connect two equally good points. For example, there might exist a straight line between the two (which is considered as easy by the geodesic criterion), but this line might be going through a very narrow valley, challenging gradient based optimization algorithms (and thus extremely difficult to navigate in practice). In addition, the proposed algorithm for computing the normalized geodesic is a greedy heuristic, which as far as I can tell, makes it difficult to know how we can trust in the estimated geodesics obtained by this algorithm.\", \"sampled\": \"\\\"Improving Generative Adversarial Networks with Denoising Feature Matching,\\\" proposes an innovative training procedure for GANs. This procedure capitalizes on a denoising auto encoder, which estimates and tracks the distribution of features. The reviewers commend the innovative approach and note that the method achieves higher inception scores than other methods in some instances. Despite its strengths, the reviewers also express concerns about the nature of the improvement and question if the benefits outweigh the required additional sophistication. The reviewer has explicitly stated, \\\"I think it's an interesting idea to use this 'extra information', namely the feature representations learned by the discriminator.\\\" This quote emphasizes the novelty of the approach and how it leverages additional data in the training process. However, the same reviewer also questions the efficacy of the approach, noting, \\\"The reviewer is unsure if the benefits outweigh the required additional sophistication.\\\" It's clear that while the innovative approach is seen as a strength, there are concerns about its practicality and efficiency. The reviewer further elaborates, \\\"I do think the idea of exploiting 'extra information' (like discriminator features) is interesting both inside and outside the context of this paper.\\\" This suggests that the approach could have broader implications beyond this specific application, opening up potential new avenues for research. However, the reviewer's previous concerns about the complexity and efficiency of the approach still stand and these should be addressed in future iterations of the method. The paper was accepted for the ICLR conference, indicating that despite these concerns, the broader scientific community found value in the work. In conclusion, while the paper's augmented training procedure for GANs using a denoising auto-encoder presents an innovative approach, there are concerns about its practicality and efficiency. These issues need to be addressed to fully realize the potential of this method.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This paper aims at attacking the problem of preselecting deep learning model structures for new domains. It reported a series of experiments on various small tasks and feed-forward DNNs. It claims that some ranking algorithm can be learned based on these results to guide the selection of model structures for new domains. Although the goal is interesting I found their conclusion is neither convincing nor useful in practice for several reasons: 1. They only explored really simple networks (feed-forward DNNs). While this significantly limited the search space, it also limited the value of the experiments. In fact, the best model architecture is highly task (domain) dependent and the type of model (DNN vs CNN vs LSTM) is often much more important than size of the network itself. 2. Their experiments were conduced with some important hyper parameters (e.g., learning rate schedule) fixed. However, it is well known that learning rate often is the most important hyper parameter during training. Without adjusting these important hyper parameters the conclusion on the best model architecture is not convincing. 3. Their experiments seem to indicate that the training data difference is not important. However, this is unlikely to be true as you would definitely want to use larger models (total number of parameters) when your training set is magnitude larger (i.e., log(datasize) can be an important feature). This is likely because they did not run experiments on large datasets. In addition, I think the title of the paper does not accurately reflect what the paper is about and should be modified. Also, this paper cited Sainath et al. 2015 as the work that leads to breakthrough in speech recognition. However, the breakthrough in ASR happened much earlier. The first paper with all three key components was published in 2010: Yu, D., Deng, L. and Dahl, G., 2010, December. Roles of pre-training and fine-tuning in context-dependent DBN-HMMs for real-world speech recognition. In Proc. NIPS Workshop on Deep Learning and Unsupervised Feature Learning. and the more detailed paper was published in 2012 Dahl, G.E., Yu, D., Deng, L. and Acero, A., 2012. Context-dependent pre-trained deep neural networks for large-vocabulary speech recognition. IEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, 20(1), pp.30-42.\", \"sampled\": \"The subject of this review is Tartan, a hardware accelerator specifically designed for inference with Deep Neural Networks. The strength of Tartan lies in its innovative exploitation of the variable per layer precision requirements of DNNs. This ability to harness the inherent numerical precision variability within DNNs sets Tartan apart from other hardware accelerators in the field. In terms of performance, Tartan outperforms a state-of-the-art bit-parallel accelerator by 1.90x without any loss in accuracy. This performance improvement is significant and noteworthy, and it is achieved without the need for network retraining, which is an impressive feat. Furthermore, Tartan is also more energy efficient than the bit-parallel accelerator, showing an improvement of 1.17x in this regard. This aspect of Tartan is particularly important given the high energy demands of DNNs. In addition to these performance and efficiency improvements, Tartan also offers the potential for trading off accuracy for additional improvements in both execution performance and energy efficiency. When a 1% loss in accuracy is tolerated, Tartan's performance can potentially improve by 2.04x, and its energy efficiency can potentially improve by 1.25x. These potential improvements highlight the versatility of Tartan as a hardware accelerator. However, it should be noted that this versatility comes with a complexity that may pose a challenge to some readers. The paper detailing Tartan's design and capabilities requires a certain level of hardware expertise to fully comprehend and appreciate. This complexity, while a testament to the sophistication of Tartan may limit the paper's accessibility to a wider audience. Despite this, the paper presents a compelling case for Tartan. The incremental improvement it provides may be seen as small by some expert reviewers but the potential benefits it offers in terms of performance and energy efficiency should not be underestimated. It's important to consider Tartan's contributions within the broader context of DNN hardware accelerators. In conclusion, Tartan represents a significant contribution to the field of hardware accelerators for DNNs. Its ability to exploit numerical precision variability, performance and energy efficiency improvements, and its potential for further improvements with a trade-off in accuracy are all notable strengths. However, the complexity of the paper and the perceived incremental nature of its contribution are aspects that could be improved in future work.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This paper describes a new way of variable computation, which uses a different number of units depending on the input. This is different from other methods for variable computation that compute over multiple time steps. The idea is clearly presented and the results are shown on LSTMs and GRUs for language modeling and music prediction. Pros: - new idea - convincing results in a head to head comparison between different set ups. Cons: - results are not nearly as good as the state of the art on the reported tasks. The reviewers and I had several rounds of discussion on whether or not to accept the paper. One reviewer had significant reservations about the paper since the results were far from SOTA. However, since getting SOTA often requires a combination of several tricks, I felt that perhaps it would not be fair to require this, and gave them the benefit of doubt (especially because the other two reviewers did not think this was a dealbreaker). In my opinion, the authors did a fair enough job on the head to head comparison between their proposed VCRNN models and the underlying LSTMs and GRUs, which showed that the model did well enough.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper titled \\\"Adaptive Feature Abstraction for Translating Video to Language\\\" introduces a novel approach to generating adaptive spatiotemporal representations of videos for captioning purposes. The innovative nature of this method is underscored by the authors' use of attention mechanisms which are uniquely combined with spatiotemporal alignment. This innovative blend is the cornerstone of the proposed method, known as ASTAR represents a significant development in the field of video captioning. The authors thoroughly evaluated their approach on three benchmark datasets: YouTube2Text, M-VAD, and MSR-VTT. This rigorous testing underscores the authors' commitment to validating their novel method and provides credibility to their findings. However, it should be noted that the paper was not accepted for the ICLR conference. This fact does not necessarily undermine the value of the research, but it is a data point to consider in the overall assessment of the work. Despite this setback, the authors demonstrated a commendable response to the reviewers' concerns. They made significant revisions addressing these concerns, which included updates to the abstract, introduction, related work, and experimental results. This dedication to refining their work shows a commitment to the scientific process and the advancement of knowledge in the field of video captioning.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The starting point of this work is the understanding that by having decorrelated neurons (e.g. neurons that only fire on background, or only on foreground regions) one provides independent pieces of information to the subsequent decisions. As such one gives \\\"complementary viewpoints\\\" of the input to the subsequent layers, which can be thought of as performing ensembling/expert combination within the model, rather than using an ensemble of networks. For this, the authors propose a sensible method to decorrelate the activations of intermediate neurons, with the aim of delivering complementary inputs to the final classification layers: they split intermediate neurons to a \\\"foreground\\\" and a \\\"background\\\" subset, and append side-losses that force them to be zero on background and foreground pixels respectively. They demonstrate that this can improve classification on a mid-scale classification example (a fraction of imagenet, and a ResNet with 18, rather than 150 layers), when compared to a \\\"vanilla\\\" baseline that does not use these losses. I enjoyed reading the paper because the idea is simple, smart, and seems to be effective. But there are a few concerns; -firstly, the way of doing this seems very particular to vision. In vision one knows that masking the features (during both training and testing) helps.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper under review by Reed & de Freitas proposes an intriguing approach to augmenting neural architectures with recursion, a concept that has been implemented within the Neural Programmer-Interpreter framework. This novel approach allows for superior generalizability and interpretability even with smaller amounts of training data, a significant achievement in the field of neural networks and program learning. The authors demonstrate the capabilities of their method by training Neural Programmer-Interpreter models on traces that contain recursive calls. This training strategy proves effective in showcasing the strengths of the approach, with the architecture displaying an impressive ability to infer solutions for complex problems. Of particular note is the architecture's ability to perfectly infer solutions for the Bubblesort algorithm and the Tower of Hanoi problem. This demonstration of the power and flexibility of the Neural Programmer-Interpreter framework is commendable and speaks to the potential of the proposed approach in tackling a variety of complex problems in the field of recursion and generalizability. The paper is a valuable contribution to the ICLR 2016 conference. The implementation of recursion in the Neural Programmer-Interpreter framework opens up new possibilities for the field of neural networks and program learning.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The paper describes an MT training data selection approach that scores and ranks general-domain sentences using a CNN classifier. Comparison to prior work using continuous or n-gram based language models is well done, even though it is not clear of the paper also compared against bilingual data selection (e.g. sum of difference of cross-entropies). The motivation to use a CNN instead of an RNN/LSTM was first unclear to me, but it is a strength of the paper to argue that certain sections of a text/sentence are more important than others and this is achieved by a CNN. However, the paper does not experimentally show whether a BOW or SEQ (or the combination of both( representation is more important and why. The textual description of the CNN (one-hot or semi-supervised using pre-trained embeddings) is clear, detailed, and points out the important aspects. However, a picture of the layers showing how inputs are combined would be worth a thousand words. The paper is overall well written, but some parentheses for citations are not necessary (\\\\citet vs. \\\\citep) (e.g line 385). Experiments and evaluation support the claims of the paper, but I am a little bit concerned about the method of determining the number of selected in-domain sentences (line 443) based on a separate validation set: - What validation data is used here? It is also not clear on what data hyperparameters of the CNN models are chosen. How sensitive are the models to this? - Table 2 should really compare scores of different approaches with the same number of sentences selected. As Figure 1 shows, the approach of the paper still seems to outperform the baselines in this case. Other comments: - I would be interested in an experiment that compares the technique of the paper against baselines when more in-domain data is available, not just the development set. - The results or discussion section could feature some example sentences selected by the different methods to support the claims made in section 5.4. - In regards to the argument of abstracting away from surface forms in 5.\", \"sampled\": \"\\\"Cross-Lingual Named Entity Recognition via Wikification,\\\" introduces a new approach to Named Entity Recognition (NER), a key area in Natural Language Processing. This method, which is built on cross-lingual wikification is a significant step forward in NER as it is language independent. The model can be applied to all languages in the Wikipedia database making it a versatile tool for multilingual research. The approach also allows for training on multiple source languages enabling a comprehensive capture of global data and linguistic nuances. The language independent features of this model can enhance even monolingual NER systems, demonstrating its potential to improve a wide range of NER applications. The model's performance is impressive. It outperforms comparable approaches on the standard CoNLL datasets, which are widely used for evaluation in the field of Natural Language Processing. This indicates a high level of accuracy and reliability in the model's ability to recognize and categorize named entities. The paper further illustrates the model's effectiveness by highlighting its performance on low-resource languages. This is a significant accomplishment as many NER models struggle to perform accurately on languages with limited digital resources. However, it should be noted that the model's performance on Tamil and Bengali decreases when lexical features are added. This suggests that there may be limitations in its applicability to certain languages or linguistic features. One of the key dependencies of this method is on the success of the cross-lingual wikifier. The wikifier plays a crucial role in linking and translating named entities across different languages, and its accuracy directly influences the performance of the model. Furthermore, the paper does not address how the method affects named entities that are not on Wikipedia. Given that Wikipedia is not an exhaustive database of named entities, especially for certain low-resource languages, this raises questions about the model's comprehensiveness and ability to handle less common or localized named entities. In conclusion, this paper presents a promising new approach to NER that leverages the power of cross-lingual wikification. While the model shows impressive performance across multiple languages and datasets, it would benefit from further exploration of its limitations and dependencies. This could provide a more comprehensive understanding of its potential and guide future improvements to enhance its applicability and performance.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We thank the reviewers for their work and their comments. The reviews will help in further improving the paper. Some specific responses to individual reviewers are below. AnonReviewer1: Thank you, we will further polish up the notation and the tables based on your suggestions. AnonReviewer2: We used a very small replay buffer (2,500 frames per actor thread, 20,000 frames in total), and have not observed any significant changes in results when making it smaller or larger. This indicates that in practice the algorithm is not constrained to strict on-policy training. We will discuss this in more detail in the paper. We agree that measurements that can be used for future prediction are not always available. (For example, as mentioned in the introduction, board games are an extreme example in which only a sparse scalar reward is provided.) We will further emphasize this in the paper to remind the reader that the presented approach adopts different modeling assumptions from most approaches in the literature. AnonReviewer3: The simplicity of our model leads to several failure modes: the agent acts purely reactively, cannot construct a persistent representation of its environment (e.g., a cognitive map), and cannot plan its actions far into the future. For this reason its performance in Doom is at the level of a very inexperienced human player. Much exciting work remains to be done. We are interested in sensorimotor control in immersive environments and chose Doom for this reason. We are looking forward to testing and further developing the approach in other simulators that are becoming available, such as DeepMind's new Lab.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper, \\\"Learning Identity Mappings with Residual Gates,\\\" introduces a layer augmentation technique that employs shortcut connections with a linear gating mechanism. This technique stands out for its use of a scalar parameter to control each gate. In terms of complexity, the augmentation requires only one extra parameter per layer, which indicates an efficient approach. The paper's method was evaluated on three datasets: MNIST, CIFAR-10, and CIFAR-100. The evaluation employed augmented Wide ResNets, a choice that fits the paper's focus on residual networks. 3.65%. In terms of performance, the model proves robust, retaining over 90% of its effectiveness even after half of its layers have been randomly removed. This level of resilience is commendable, as it suggests that the model can withstand significant modification while maintaining high performance. The paper also reports test errors of 3.65% on CIFAR-10 and 18.27% on CIFAR-100, which are competitive figures in the field. The paper draws comparisons with DenseNet-BC, a model that achieved a test error of 3.46 on CIFAR-10 and 17.18 on CIFAR-100. However, while DenseNet-BC has 25.6 million parameters the proposed model in this paper uses 36.5 million parameters. Despite this difference DenseNet-BC can still achieve comparable performance with fewer parameters (15.3 million), which raises questions about the efficiency of the proposed model. While the paper presents an interesting approach, it was not accepted, presumably due to concerns about novelty and effectiveness. The proposed method's similarity to existing concepts such as Highway Neural Networks and Residual Networks may have contributed to this decision.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This paper proposed to use a simple count-based exploration technique in high-dimensional RL application (e.g., Atari Games). The counting is based on state hash, which implicitly groups (quantizes) similar state together. The hash is computed either via hand-designed features or learned features (unsupervisedly with auto-encoder). The new state to be explored receives a bonus similar to UCB (to encourage further exploration). Overall the paper is solid with quite extensive experiments. I wonder how it generalizes to more Atari games. Montezuma’s Revenge may be particularly suitable for approaches that implicitly/explicitly cluster states together (like the proposed one), as it has multiple distinct scenarios, each with small variations in terms of visual appearance, showing clustering structures. On the other hand, such approaches might not work as well if the state space is fully continuous (e.g. in RLLab experiments). The authors did not answer my question about why the hash code needs to be updated during training. I think it is mainly because the code still needs to be adaptive for a particular game (to achieve lower reconstruction error) in the first few iterations . After that stabilization is the most important. Sec. 2.3 (Learned embedding) is quite confusing (but very important). I hope that the authors could make it more clear (e.g., by writing an algorithm block) in the next version.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper \\\"On Robust Concepts and Small Neural Nets\\\" puts forward an efficient analog of the universal approximation theorem on the boolean hypercube. This theorem has significant implications for the field of neural networks particularly those utilizing sigmoid gates. The authors provide a deep exploration of the boolean hypercube and its role in the universal approximation theorem. This exploration could benefit from a more detailed explanation to enhance the reader's understanding. One of the key contributions of this paper is the proof that any noise-stable boolean function can be well-approximated by a two-layer linear threshold circuit. This proof is supported by a careful consideration of approximation parameters which are integral to understanding the behavior of noise stable functions. The authors also present a polynomial time learning algorithm that outputs a small two-layer linear threshold circuit. This algorithm is a significant contribution, but it could be further improved by providing more detailed documentation and code examples to assist readers in reproducing the results. The paper also demonstrates weaker generalizations to noise stable polynomial threshold functions and noise stable boolean functions. While these generalizations are a valuable addition to the research, the authors could have expanded on the significance of these findings and their implications. The paper was not accepted due to concerns about presentation and contribution.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The paper presents an advanced self-learning model that extracts compositional rules from Bach's chorales, which extends their previous work in: 1) the rule hierarchy in both conceptual and informational dimensions; 2) adaptive 2-D memory selection which assumes the features follow Dirichlet Distribution. Sonority (column of 4 MIDI numbers) acts as word in language model: unigram statistics have been used to learn the fundamental rules in music theory, while n-grams with higher order help characterize part writing. Sonorities have been clustered together based on feature functions through iterations. The partition induced by the features is recognized as a rule if it is sufficiently significant. As a result, two sample syllabi with different difficulty strides and \\\"satisfactory gaps\\\" have been generated in terms of sets of learned rules. 1. Quality: a) Strengths: In the paper, the exploration of hierarchies in two dimensions makes the learning process more cognitive and interpretable. The authors also demonstrate an effective memory selection to speed up the learning. b) Flaws: The paper only discussed N<=5, which might limit the learning and interpretation capacities of the proposed model, failing to capture long-distance dependence of music. (In the replies to questions, the authors mentioned they had experimented with max N=10, but I'm not sure why related results were not included in the paper). Besides the elaborated interpretation of results, a survey seeking the opinions of students in a music department might make the evaluation of system performance more persuasive. 2. Clarity: a) Pros: The paper clearly delivers an improved automatic theorist system which learns and represents music concepts as well as thoroughly interprets and compares the learned rules with music theory. Proper analogies and examples help the reader perceive the ideas more easily. b) Cons: Although detailed definitions can be found in the authors' previous MUS-ROVER I papers, it would be great if they had described the optimization more clearly (in Figure 1. and related parts). The \\\"(Conceptual-Hierarchy Filter)\\\" row in equations (3): the prime symbol should appear in the subscript. 3. Originality: The representation of music concepts and rules is still an open area, the paper investigate the topic in a novel way. It illustrates an alternative besides other interpretable feature learning methods such as autoencoders, GAN, etc. 4. Significance: It is good to see some corresponding interpretations for the learned rules from music theory. The authors mentioned students in music could and should be involved in the self-learning loop to interact, which is very interesting. I hope their advantages can be combined in the practice of music theory teaching and learning.\", \"sampled\": \"The submission presents Latent Sequence Decompositions as a framework that learns word-to-subword mappings jointly with an attention-based sequence-to-sequence model for speech recognition, and I found this central idea clearly motivated. A key strength is that the proposed decomposition is allowed to vary with the acoustic input rather than remaining fixed, which gives the model a useful distinction from a static pronunciation or segmentation inventory. The probabilistic mapping is another appealing aspect of the method. By allowing a word to correspond to multiple subword sequences, the approach gives a principled account of variability while remaining connected to the sequence-to-sequence setting. The paper is well written and theoretically persuasive, and the reported Wall Street Journal results, including 12.9% WER for the LSD model against 14.8% for the character baseline, support the claim that the modeling choice is meaningful. Still, the experimental case is not yet fully convincing. The evaluation would be stronger with a word-level baseline, since the proposed method sits between character-level and word-level systems. A word-level system might be competitive with the reported results, especially given the maximum 20K vocabulary size stated for WSJ si284. The comparison should also be framed more carefully around the reported 9.6% WER when LSD is combined with a convolutional encoder network, because this result changes the interpretation of the gain relative to simpler settings. Table 2 may mislead readers if language-model-enhanced systems are closer to the best reported result than the presentation suggests, so the table should distinguish more explicitly which systems use comparable information and which do not. Related work is another place where the paper could be strengthened. The method should be related to earlier HMM-based pronunciation mixture and subword-unit work, especially because the paper’s decomposition has clear conceptual overlap with pronunciation alternatives. Relevant examples include I. McGraw, I. Badr, and J. Glass, \\\"Learning lexicons form speech using a pronunciation mixture model,\\\" and L. Lu, A. Ghoshal, S. Renals, \\\"Acoustic data-driven pronunciation lexicon for large vocabulary speech recognition\\\". The discussion of computational cost also needs clarification, since the reviewer considers the training cost high despite the use of one sample for gradient computation. The phrase \\\"O(5) days to converge\\\" is awkward and should be replaced with a clearer statement of the actual approximate convergence time, namely 5 days. Please clarify the computational bottleneck of the approach. The use of a downsampling factor of 4 with an RNN is relevant, but it does not by itself explain where the cost is concentrated.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The paper proposes a method to train models for Chinese word segmentation (CWS) on datasets having multiple segmentation criteria. - Strengths: 1. Multi-criteria learning is interesting and promising. 2. The proposed model is also interesting and achieves a large improvement from baselines. - Weaknesses: 1. The proposed method is not compared with other CWS models. The baseline model (Bi-LSTM) is proposed in [1] and [2]. However, these model is proposed not for CWS but for POS tagging and NE tagging. The description \\\"In this paper, we employ the state-of-the-art architecture ...\\\" (in Section 2) is misleading. 2. The purpose of experiments in Section 6.4 is unclear. In Sec. 6.4, the purpose is that investigating \\\"datasets in traditional Chinese and simplified Chinese could help each other.\\\" However, in the experimental setting, the model is separately trained on simplified Chinese and traditional Chinese, and the shared parameters are fixed after training on simplified Chinese. What is expected to fixed shared parameters? - General Discussion: The paper should be more interesting if there are more detailed discussion about the datasets that adversarial multi-criteria learning does not boost the performance. [1] Zhiheng Huang, Wei Xu, and Kai Yu. 2015. Bidirectional lstm-crf models for sequence tagging. arXiv preprint arXiv:1508.01991. [2] Xuezhe Ma and Eduard Hovy. 2016. End-to-end sequence labeling via bi-directional lstm-cnns-crf. arXiv preprint arXiv:1603.01354 .\", \"sampled\": \"Fuzzy paraphrases in learning word representations with a lexicon proposes estimating paraphrase context sensitivity and applying it to word embedding learning, an idea I find plausible and mostly well presented. The central concern is experimental evaluation. The results are inconclusive. Table 4 raises questions about the analogy task. CBOW performs better on the semantic part than the proposed embeddings, which weakens the evidence for the approach. The paper does not clearly show superiority over simpler approaches. This is especially important for approaches for baselines that do not use a paraphrase database The current evaluation makes it difficult to judge the contribution of the paraphrase context sensitivity component. The model exploration is also insufficient, given that several choices appear important to the reported behavior. The paper should either justify these model choices more clearly or include experiments with alternatives. Point 1: English. Some sections are hard to follow, so editing by a native English speaker would help. Point 2: labels. Table labels should be more informative, since clearer labels would make the experimental comparisons easier to interpret without changing the substance of the work. Point 3 concerns inconclusive results. Point 4 asks why \\\"Enriched CBOW\\\" was omitted from the analogy task, since its absence makes the comparison around Table 4 less complete and leaves an important evaluation choice unexplained.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"1029 tic-tac-toe boards are rendered (in various ways). These 1029 boards are legal boards where the next legal play can end the game. There are 18 categories of such boards -- 9 for the different locations of the next play, and 2 for the color of the next play. The supervision is basically saying \\\"If you place a black square in the middle right, black will win\\\" or \\\"if you place a white square in the upper left, white will win\\\". A CNN is trained to predict these 18 categories and can do so with 100% accuracy. The focus of the paper is using Zhou et al's Class Activation Mapping to show where the CNN focuses when making it's decision. As I understand it, an input to CAM is the class of interest. So let's say it is class 1 (black wins with a play to the bottom right square, if I've deciphered figure 2 correctly. Figure 2 should really be more clear about what each class is). So we ask CAM to determine the area of focus of the CNN for deciding whether class 1 is exhibited. The focus ends up being on the empty bottom right square (because certainly you can't exhibit class 1 if the bottom right square is occupied). The CNN also needs to condition its decision on other parts of the board -- it needs to know whether there will be 3 in a row from some direction. But maybe that conditioning is weaker? That's kind of interesting but I'm not sure about the deeper statements about discovering game rules that the paper hints at. I'm also not sure about the connection of this work to weakly supervised learning or multi-modal learning. The paper is pretty well written, overall, with some grammatical mistakes, but I simply don't see the surprising discovery of this work. I also have some concerns about how contrived this scenario is -- using a big, expressive CNN for such a simple game domain and using a particular CNN visualization method.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper is well written and clearly structured. Its bibliography is extensive, and the discussion shows awareness of unsupervised morphology, word embeddings, and benchmark evaluation. The English and Turkish results are convincing, especially as evidence that MORSE can be a useful morpheme segmentation framework in some settings. The submission also includes testing software for the English MORSE model and 3 small experimental datasets, which supports inspection of the reported behavior. I could only verify testing, however, since the software package appears not to allow training. The main concerns are the comparison with earlier work and the limited analysis of method drawbacks. The ideas appear mostly incremental relative to Soricut & Och. The paper should make clearer what MORSE contributes beyond that work and beyond related embedding-based unsupervised morphological segmentation by Üstün & Can. The treatment of compound segmentation is the largest methodological weakness. MORSE appears to segment compounds only \\\"by accident\\\", which is problematic for a benchmark evaluation that includes highly compounding languages. This limitation especially affects the Finnish results, and the discussion in sections 4.4/5.3 should be more explicit about the consequences for compound segmentation. The comparison with Morfessor also seems unfair for semantically non-compositional compounds, given the cited strength of Morfessor on compound segmentation in Ruokolainen et al. This affects how the reader should interpret the Finnish results. The tuning setup should be described and justified more carefully, especially in sections 4.2 and 4.3, because MORSE hyperparameters are tuned while Morfessor hyperparameters apparently are not. This is important because Kohonen et al. and Grönroos et al. describe Morfessor variants with a precision-recall hyperparameter, so an untuned baseline may understate Morfessor. The evaluation description also needs clarification, since the \\\"morpheme-level evaluation\\\" in section 5.2 requires either a fuller explanation or a citation. Table 7 appears to combine results from nonidentical evaluation sets, since the MORSE evaluation is described as using the Morpho Challenge 2010 development set while the LLSM results from Narasimhan et al. may come from another source. Several presentation details should be corrected, including the missing publication details for Narasimhan et al. 2015, the unexplained computational basis for the one million word-form input limit, and the confusing dataset separator \\\"aa\\\" in multiple analyses.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This paper introduces a novel hierarchical memory architecture for neural networks, based on a binary tree with leaves corresponding to memory cells. This allows for O(log n) memory access, and experiments additionally demonstrate ability to solve more challenging tasks such as sorting from pure input-output examples and dealing with longer sequences. The idea of the paper is novel and well-presented, and the memory structure seems reasonable to have advantages in practice. However, the main weakness of the paper is the experiments. There is no experimental comparison with other external memory-based approaches (e.g. those discussed in Related Work), or experimental analysis of computational efficiency given overhead costs (beyond just computational complexity) despite that being one of the main advantages. Furthermore, the experimental setups are relatively weak, all on artificial tasks with moderate increases in sequence length. Improving on these would greatly strengthen the paper, as the core idea is interesting.\", \"sampled\": \"Learning and Policy Search in Stochastic Dynamical Systems with Bayesian Neural Networks is a clear submission that models stochastic dynamics using Bayesian neural networks trained with alpha-divergence. I read the work as being mainly about learning Bayesian neural networks, rather than primarily about policy search, although the learned Bayesian neural network model is used within a policy search framework. The approach is nice, and the integration with policy search is a strength of the paper. The industrial control examples, including the gas turbine and industrial benchmark settings, are valuable.main reservation is that the algorithmic contribution appears limited, since the training procedure requires substantial approximation to be computationally manageable and the reinforcement learning parts are not especially novel. The use of alpha-divergences, including alpha value 0.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We updated the paper to take into account the reviewers' comments. In particular, the following additions/modifications were made: Section 1 Introduction: - clarified the previous work cited from the arxiv paper, as suggested by AnonReviewer3 Section 3: - added a citation to the arxiv paper Section 4: - better explained the decoding scheme (AnonReviewer1) - explained the label embedding use (AnonReviewer1) Section 5: - divided the experiment tasks (AnonReviewer1) - better highlighted the rank information in Table 1 (AnonReviewer1) - added experiments to evaluate the resilience of the decoding scheme to missing at random embedding components Appendix: - added references to the datasets (AnonReviewer3) - added figures for the new experiments Please let us know if further modifications are needed.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper addresses learning and forecasting local trends in time-series data through TreNet, an end-to-end hybrid neural network. The proposed design is clear in its division of roles that CNN components derive features from local raw time-series segments, LSTM components capture long-range structure in prior local trend sequences, and feature fusion layer combines CNN and LSTM information for local trend prediction.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Two things I'd like to see. 1) Specifics about the JPEG and JPEG2000 implementations used, and how they were configured. One major weakness I see in many papers is they do not include specific encoders and configuration used in comparisons. Without knowing this, it's hard to know if the comparison was done with a suitably strong JPEG implementation that was properly configured, for example. 2) The comparison to JPEG2000 is unfortunately not that interesting, since that codec does not have widespread usage and likely never will. A better comparison would be with WebP performance. Or, even better, both. Very nice results. Is a software implementation of this available to play with?\", \"sampled\": \"I recommend rejection of Unsupervised Deep Learning of State Representation Using Robotic Priors. The overall task and approach are interesting, and applying the Jonschkowski & Brock 2015 representation learning method with a deep network is a reasonable direction. The reported 97,7% correlation and the one dimension Baxter head-position representation are potentially appealing, but the paper’s contribution remains limited as presented. The experimental evaluation is inadequate because the experiments use a simple synthetic task, training and test datasets are very small, and the data have little variation.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The primary contribution of this paper is showing that k-nearest-neighbor method based memory can be usefully incorporated in a variety of architectures and supervised learning tasks. The presentation is clear, and results are good. I like the synthetic task and analysis. For the Omniglot task, running and reporting results on the original splits used by Lake would be good, as the splits used in matching nets are considerably easier and result in ceiling effects.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper has a well motivated goal, but the proposed Entropy-SGD method appears to resemble SVI in important respects, since an ELBO estimate favors posterior means associated with both high likelihood and volume. This resembles tradeoff targeted by marginal likelihood. It also connects naturally to MCMC methods, including SGLD.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This paper aims to characterize the perceptual ability of a neural network under different input conditions. This is done by manipulating the input image x in various ways (e.g. downsamplig, foveating), and training an auto-encoder to reconstruct the original full-resolution image. MSE and qualitative results are shown and compared for the different input conditions. Unfortunately, this paper seems to lack focus, presenting a set of preliminary inspections with few concrete conclusions. For example, at the end of sec 4.4, \\\"This result is not surprising, given that FOV-R contains additional information .... These results suggests that a small number of foveations containing rich details might be all these neural networks need....\\\". But this hypothesis is left dangling: What detailed regions are needed, and from where? For what sort of tasks? Secondly, it isn't clear to me what reconstruction behaviors are caused by a fundamental perception of the input, and what are artifacts of the autoencoder and pixelwise l2 loss? A prime example is texture, which the autoencoder fails to recover. But with a pixelwise loss, the network must predict high-frequency textures nearly pixel-for-pixel at training time; if this is impossible, then it will generate a pixelwise average of the training samples --- a flat region. So then the network's inability to reconstruct textures is due to a problem generating them, specifically averaging from the training loss, not necessarily an issue in perceiving textures. A network trained a different way (perhaps an adversarial network) may infer a texture is there, even if it wouldn't be able to generate it in a pixelwise l2 sense. Similarly, the ability to perform color reconstruction given a color glimpse I think has much to do with disambiguating the color of an object/scene: If there is an ambiguity, the network won't know which to \\\"choose\\\" (white flower or yellow flower?) and output an average, which is why there are so many sepia tones. However, in its section on this, the paper only measures the reconstruction error for different amounts of color given, and does not drill very far into any hypotheses for why this behavior occurs. There are some interesting measurements here, such as the amount of color needed in the foveation to reconstruct a color image, and the discussion on global features, which may start to get at a mechanism by which glimpses may propagate to an entire reconstruction. But overall it's hard to know what to take away from this paper. What are larger concrete conclusions that can be garnered from the details, and what mechanisms bring them about? Can these be more thoroughly explored with more focus?\", \"sampled\": \"The paper Recurrent Mixture Density Network for Spatiotemporal Visual Attention presents a reasonable approach to estimating video visual attention. Its main pipeline is clear: C3D computes visual features from an input clip, these features are fed into an LSTM, and each LSTM hidden state sets parameters of a Gaussian mixture model. The resulting attention map is then derived from the Gaussian mixture model. I found the idea reasonable and the writing strong, particularly in the way the visual feature, recurrent, and mixture components are connected. The usage of continuous-output RNN or LSTM models for vision applications seems relatively underexplored, which gives the paper a useful point of interest. The experimental evidence was judged to support the effectiveness of the proposed method, including comparisons reported for RMDN in Table 2. The method reportedly surpassed prior saliency-prediction results on Hollywood2. Main \\\"gripe\\\" concerns the absence of key baseline comparisons that would show the contribution of recurrence more directly. The paper makes the LSTM a central part of the model, but the results do not sufficiently isolate whether the \\\"recurrent\\\" component is what improves performance. A comparison that removes the recurrent module, while keeping the C3D feature computation and Gaussian mixture model attention-map generation as similar as possible, would make the architectural contribution easier to assess. This is especially important because the current system combines several choices, including C3D features, LSTM temporal modeling, and mixture density output, any of which could affect performance. The action recognition results also indicate improvement over baselines such as C3D + SVM, but those results would be more informative if the role of recurrence were separated from other modeling changes. The saliency-prediction evaluation on Hollywood2 is a strength, since the method reportedly surpasses prior results there, and the mention of UCF101 in the abstract suggests an intended generalization setting; however, the central claim would be clearer if the baseline set more directly tested the proposed recurrent mixture formulation rather than only showing that the full system performs well. In particular, a dense labeling comparison with approaches such as FCN or deconvnet would help position the model against related methods that have appeared over two years, especially because the output is a spatial attention map rather than only an action label. The paper is clearly written and the proposed method is coherent, with C3D providing clip-level visual features, LSTM modeling their sequence, and the Gaussian mixture model producing the attention distribution. The experiments support effectiveness, and the reported gains on Hollywood2 and over C3D + SVM are meaningful within the stated evaluation.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The authors describe the use of convolutional layers with intermediate pooling layers to more efficiently model long-range dependencies in sequential data compared with recurrent architectures. Whereas the use of convolutional layers is related to the PixelCNN architecture (Oord et al.), the main novelty is to combine them with gated pooling layers to integrate information from previous time steps. Additionally, the authors describe extensions based on zone-out regularization, densely connected layers, and an efficient attention mechanism for encoder-decoder models. The authors report a striking speed-up over RNNs by up to a factor of 16, while achieving similar or even higher performances. Major comment. QRNNs are closely related to PixelCNNs, which leverage masked dilated convolutional layers to speed-up computations. However, the authors cite ByteNet, which builds upon PixelCNN, only at the end of their manuscript and do not include it in the evaluation. The authors should cite PixelCNN already when introducing QRNN in the methods sections, and include it in the evaluation. At the very least, QRNN should be compared with ByteNet for language translation. How well does a fully convolutional model without intermediate pooling layers perform, i.e. what is the effect to the introduced pooling layers? Are their performance difference between f, fo, and ifo pooling? Did the authors investigate dilated convolutional layers? Minor comments. 1. How does a model without dense connections perform, i.e. what is the effect of dense connections? To illustrate dense connections, the authors might draw them in figure 1 and refer to it in section 2.1. 2. The run-time results shown in figure 4 are very helpful, but as far as I understood, the breakdown shown on the left side was measured for language modeling (referred in 3.2), whereas the dependency on batch- and sequence size shown on the right side for sentiment classification (referred in 3.1). I suggest to consistently show the results for either sentiment classification or language modeling, or both. At the very least, the figure caption should describe the task explicitly. Labeling the left and right figure by a) and b) would further improve readability. 3. Section 3.1 describes a high speed-up for long sequences and small batch sizes. I suggest motivating why this is the case. While computations can be parallelized along the sequence length, it is less obvious why smaller batch sizes speed-up computations. 4. The proposed encoder-decoder attention is different from traditional attention in that attention vectors are not computed and used as input to the decoder sequentially, but on top of decoder output states in parallel. This should be described and motivated in the text. Sentiment classification. 5. What was the size of the hold-out development set and how was it created? The text describes that data were split equally into training and test set, without describing the hold-out set. 6. What was the convolutional filter size? 7. What is the speed-up for the best hyper-parameters (batch size 24, sequence length 231)? 8. Figure 3 would be easier to interpret by actually showing the text on the y-axis. For the sake of space, one might use a smaller text passage, plot it along the x-axis, and show the activations of fewer neurons along the y-axis. Showing more examples in the appendix would make the authors’ claim that neurons are interpretable even more convincing. Language modeling. 9. What was the size of the training, test, and validation set? 10. What was the convolutional filter size, denoted as ‘k’? 11. Is it correct that a very high learning rate of 1 was used for six epochs at the beginning? 12. The authors should show learning curves for a models with and without zone-out. Translation. 13. What was the size of the training, test, and validation set? 14. How does translation performance depend on k?\", \"sampled\": \"The paper addresses time-varying graph signals through the proposed Graph Convolutional Recurrent Network, or GCRN, and the topic is relevant combination of recurrent neural networks and graph neural networks. The central idea of using graph convolutions within a recurrent architecture is presented as a way to generalize sequence modeling beyond standard spatial domains. When consider the paper, model, and experiments decent in their broad framing, The main concern is that the technical novelty appears limited once the relationship to prior work is examined closely. The method closely resembles Shi et al. 2015, which already combines convolutional structure with recurrence for spatiotemporal modeling. The submission extends the setting to arbitrary graphs by using graph convolutions from Defferrard et al. 2016, and this is the main stated technical move. This extension is meaningful in scope, since graph signals allow a broader class of structured inputs than grids. Still, the extension is not strict in the sense of preserving all details of the earlier formulation. In particular, graph edges are handled uniformly, unlike the directional convolution kernels in Shi et al. 2015. This distinction should be made clearer and discussed more carefully. Without that discussion, the contribution reads as a direct adaptation. The novelty is limited. The paper would benefit from a more explicit comparison between GCRN and the prior approach it closely follows. The current presentation does not give enough insight into what is technically new beyond replacing the convolutional operator with a graph convolution. The evaluation is decent. The paper studies two GCRN architectures, but the experimental section is short and lacks interpretation of the results. The moving MNIST task is relevant to the proposed temporal modeling setting, and Penn Tree Bank connects the model to language modeling. The results, however, do not compensate for the limited technical novelty. A stronger experimental section would need to explain why the observed behavior supports the proposed model rather than simply reporting outcomes. The writing lacks clarity. Several claims need sharper comparison and more interpretation. The reader is left without enough insight into the role of graph convolutions. This weakness matters because the paper depends heavily on positioning relative to existing recurrent and convolutional models. The Penn Tree Bank evaluation feels underdeveloped. I expected broader comparisons for language modeling, especially because Zaremba et al 2014 is a natural comparison baseline. The paper should explain how the proposed graph construction and recurrent computation affect word probabilities. The language modeling setup raises a specific concern. It is not clear how information propagates across the graph for word probabilities. This issue should be addressed directly, since the motivation for using graph structure depends on meaningful propagation.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"peer_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This paper introduces the notion of a \\\"variational lossy autoencoder\\\", where a powerful autoregressive conditional distribution on the inputs x given the latent code z is crippled in a way that forces it to use z in a meaningful way. Its three main contributions are: (1) It gives an interesting information-theoretical insight as to why VAE-type models don't tend to take advantage of their latent representation when the conditional distribution on x given z is powerful enough. (2) It shows that this insight can be used to efficiently train VAEs with powerful autoregressive conditional distributions such that they make use of the latent code. (3) It presents a powerful way to parametrize the prior in the form of an autoregressive flow transformation which is equivalent to using an inverse autoregressive flow transformation on the approximate posterior. By itself, I think the information-theoretical explanation of why VAEs do not use their latent code when the conditional distribution on x given z is powerful enough constitutes an excellent addition to our understanding of VAE-related approaches. However, the way this intuition is empirically evaluated is a bit weak. The \\\"crippling\\\" method used feels hand-crafted and very task-dependent, and the qualitative evaluation of the \\\"lossyness\\\" of the learned representation is carried out on three datasets (MNIST, OMNIGLOT and Caltech-101 Silhouettes) which feature black-and-white images with little-to-no texture. Figures 1a and 2a do show that reconstructions discard low-level information, as observed in the slight variations in strokes between the input and the reconstruction, but such an analysis would have been more compelling with more complex image datasets. Have the authors tried applying VLAE to such datasets? I think the Caltech101 Silhouettes benchmark should be treated with caution, as no comparison is made against other competitive approaches like IAF VAE, PixelRNN and Conv DRAW. This means that VLAE significantly outperforms the state-of-the-art in only one of the four settings examined. A question which is very relevant to this paper is \\\"Does a latent representation on top of an autoregressive model help improve the density modeling performance?\\\" The paper touches this question, but very briefly: the only setting in which VLAE is compared against recent autoregressive approaches shows that it wins against PixelRNN by a small margin. The proposal to transform the latent code with an autoregressive flow which is equivalent to parametrizing the approximate posterior with an inverse autoregressive flow transformation is also interesting. There is, however, one important distinction to be made between the two approaches: in the former, the prior over the latent code can potentially be very complex whereas in the latter the prior is limited to be a simple, factorized distribution. It is not clear to me that having a very powerful prior is necessarily a good thing from a representation learning point of view: oftentimes we are interested in learning a representation of the data distribution which is untangled and composed of roughly independent factors of variation. The degree to which this can be achieved using something as simple as a spherical gaussian prior is up for discussion, but finding a good balance between the ability of the prior to fit the data and its usefulness as a high-level representation certainly warrants some thought. I would be interested in hearing the authors' opinion on this. Overall, the paper introduces interesting ideas despite the flaws outlined above, but weaknesses in the empirical evaluation prevent me from recommending its acceptance. UPDATE: The rating has been revised to a 7 following the authors' reply.\", \"sampled\": \"The paper Capacity and Trainability in Recurrent Neural Networks is highly valuable because it systematically studies performance and trainability of popular RNNs, including LSTMs, GRUs, and vanilla RNNs, on a question that many researchers would like to investigate but often lack the resources to address thoroughly. I found the contribution credible and useful. The experimental work appears careful enough to make the reported results credible, and the abstract-level claims about approximately 5 bits per parameter and about one real number of input-history information per hidden unit give the paper a clear quantitative framing. The practical conclusion is fairly decisive: in a \\\"typical training environment\\\", LSTMs appear reliable and GRUs are generally favored, which is a useful message for researchers choosing among common recurrent architectures. A major strength is distinction between capacity and trainability, since it clarifies why gated architectures can be effective even when their representational capacity is not simply higher than that of vanilla RNNs. The finding that gated architectures are easier to train but have somewhat less capacity than vanilla RNNs is a clear and important point. The related claim about similar capacity at matched parameter counts is useful for interpreting comparisons among architectures. I also appreciated the paper's emphasis on hyperparameter tuning. This is an important part of the empirical story, because conclusions about RNNs, LSTMs, GRUs, and vanilla RNNs can be distorted if tuning effort differs across architectures. The idea of measuring the share of infeasible parameters, such as configurations that diverge, is useful way to make trainability more concrete rather than relying only on final performance. The paper is clearly written. One concern is that the UGRNNs and +RNNs work feels preliminary relative to the stronger evidence for GRUs and LSTMs. The current results are interesting, but they are not enough to recommend +RNN as broadly as GRU. The discussion of hyperparameter tuning would benefit from more detail, because the paper gives too little information about the hyperparameter algorithm to make replication easy, even though it mentions Batched GP Bandits and a Matern 5/2 Kernel; since tuning is central to the claims, the method should be described with enough specificity for readers to understand how the search shaped the reported comparisons. Figure 4 needs clearer statistical treatment and better readability, especially because it is used to support comparisons that matter for the paper's conclusions. In particular, the 8-layer panel would benefit from more significance statistics comparing +RNN and GRU, since the current presentation leaves the strength of that comparison less clear than the surrounding discussion suggests. The neuroscience comparison seems weakly connected to the rest of the paper and should be softened. The numerical similarity between 5 bits and 4.7 bits per synapse may be interesting, but it does not appear central to the empirical study of RNN capacity and trainability. A more cautious formulation would be: \\\"We wonder if it is anything other than coincidence that our 5 bits result is numerically similar to the 4.7 bits measurement from neuroscience.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n","data/product_review.jsonl":"{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This boxed set of smooth, golden eye mask pairs is totally fabulous! Each pair comes individually packaged, making this collected group of beauty accessories excellent swag-bag additions or party favor gifts! The included serum feels incredibly hydrating and smells absolutely divine. I’ve used several different brands of similar eye masks, and these are noticeably thicker and more substantial than every other pair I’ve tried! Even at room temperature, they instantly provide a cooling sensation when applied to the face. They adhere well to skin, but due to their weight and size, they do have a slight tendency to slip downward with gravity. For that reason, I suggest lying flat or reclining while wearing them, so that they stay as close to the lower lash line as possible. Each package still contains some extra serum after the eye masks are removed, so I massage that residual liquid onto my neck for added moisture potential. The combination of hyaluronic acid, collagen, and 24k nanogold particles leaves my under eye area feeling incredibly hydrated and refreshed. Consistent use of this product helps combat fine lines, puffiness, and dark circles over time, so I’m thrilled the whole set is so affordable! I couldn’t be more pleased with this product.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up this boxed set of golden eye mask pairs after seeing them recommended online, and I have to say they impressed me right away. Each pair comes individually packaged, which feels clean and deliberate, and the masks themselves are noticeably thicker and more substantial than other brands I have tried. That weight is both a selling point and a small problem, because the masks do tend to slip downward once you apply them. I learned quickly that lying flat or reclining while wearing them is basically a requirement rather than a suggestion. That said, the cooling sensation when they first touch your skin is genuinely pleasant, even straight from the box at room temperature. The serum inside each package contains hyaluronic acid, collagen, and 24k nanogold particles, and there is always extra serum left over after you peel the masks off, which I use directly on the skin around my eyes. Consistent use over time is said to help with fine lines, puffiness, and dark circles, and I do think I have noticed some improvement in how rested my under-eye area looks. The set is affordable enough that I have started buying extras to tuck into swag bags or give as party favor gifts. For the price and the ingredient quality, I rated these 5 out of 5 stars without much hesitation.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This set of lip balm is very nice. It applies easily and leaves your lips feeling nice and soft without feeling greasy or tacky. They taste of tangerine which is great. I admit, for lip balms I want one that has a good flavor at this point and this one delivers 100%. It is packed with great for your lips ingredients such as hemp seed oil, Vitamin E and olive oil and no, it does not taste like olive oil. It is cruelty free which is another bonus in my eyes. It was a bit tricky to open though but once you get it it is actually a neat design, instead of twisting it on the bottom you twist it right by the bottom of the cap.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up this lip balm set and was genuinely impressed by how smoothly it applies. It leaves lips soft without any greasiness or tackiness, which I did not expect. The tangerine flavor comes through clearly. It contains hemp seed oil, Vitamin E, and olive oil, yet it does not taste like olive oil at all. My one real complaint is the packaging: the cap twists near the bottom rather than at the base, which made it confusing and somewhat difficult to open at first. Being cruelty free matters to me, and knowing this product meets that standard makes it easier to reach for daily, especially when the formula itself performs this well across the board.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Ive been ordering glass bottles and moving my products into them. I get refill size and transfer. These 2 spray bottles were a little thinne not bad, but still sturdy enough and the spray top where the sprayer is spins loosely 360 degrees. ?? I just filled it with my gallon of orange cleaner and I had to take off the sprayer and toss it in the garbage! The flaw is the spinning sprayer that allowed my expensive orange cleaner to pour out of the top all around the nozzle. cheaply made but all in all a good glass bottle with a terrible srayer . If you just need a bottle with a screw top your good. If you have a different sprayer you can take off a plastic bottle great. Thats what I did. I took the label off my orange cleaner and stuck it on this bottle and it looks so nice. So my sprayer and label made a great usable bottle.\", \"sampled\": \"I buy refill-size products and transfer them into glass bottles, so I ordered 2 of these to use with my orange cleaner. The glass itself is a little thin but still sturdy enough. The real problem is the sprayer top, which spins loosely a full 360 degrees, and that flaw caused my expensive orange cleaner to pour out around the nozzle instead of spraying. I pulled the sprayer off and threw it out. I ended up swapping in the sprayer from a plastic bottle of orange cleaner, which fit fine, and I transferred the label from the original container onto the glass bottle so I could keep everything organized. The product feels cheaply made overall, and I would not trust the included sprayer with anything you care about losing. The glass bottle itself is good, though, and it works well if you pair it with a screw top or a replacement sprayer you already trust. Just do not count on the one that comes with it.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I’ve tried a ton of magnetic lashes and so far these are my favorite. The lashes are nice and thick but not too long making them a perfect day lash. The magnets attached to the band stayed on even after several rough attempts to align them properly (user error). I will definitely be buying another pair as I can tell that I will be getting a lot more use from this set than some of my $30+ dollar sets. I highly recommend!\", \"sampled\": \"I've tried many magnetic lashes over the years, and these are my absolute favorite. They're thick but not too long, which makes them work perfectly as a daytime lash. I did struggle with alignment a few times, but the magnets stayed attached through every rough attempt, and I know that was my own error. I genuinely expect more use from this set than from other magnetic lashes I've bought for $30 or more, and I'm already planning to buy another pair. Highly recommend.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Great temporary tattoos. My daughter and her friend had a blast playing with these tattoos. They were easy to apply. Just like every temporary tattoo and the designs are vibrant and well colored. Seemed to stay on the skin well and I had no issues with the temporary tattoos at all. I am satisfied with the tattoos. I received a discount for testing and reviewing purposes. This is my honest unbiased opinion and have come to it by inspecting the tattoos myself.\", \"sampled\": \"My daughter and her friend had a great time with these. The tattoos were easy to apply and the designs came out vibrant and well colored. They stayed on skin well. I personally inspected them and had no issues at all. I'd give them a solid 5.0. I received a discount for review purposes. My opinion is based on hands-on inspection of the product. Everything about these held up exactly as described.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Not a fan of this polygel. It was hard to work with. I had to warm it in a cup of very hot water first to even be a little pliable. Even tho it has a little American flag on the advertisement it is not made in USA very misleading. I have used about 3 different polygel sets like this and this one was the hardest to use by far. But I gave it a try because the one I usually use was out of stock. I used all the different colors but all with the same ending.....disappointing!!!! So sad because I had high hopes after waiting almost three weeks to receive it during these trying times due to covid. This NY GIRL unfortunately disapproves and is very disappointed!!!!!\", \"sampled\": \"I gave this polygel set 3 out of 5 stars. My usual brand was out of stock, so I picked this one up instead. I have used about 3 different polygel sets before, and this was by far the hardest to work with of all of them. The gel was so stiff that I had to warm it in hot water just to get it pliable enough to use. I tried every color included and found the results disappointing across the board. What really bothered me was the American flag shown in the advertisement, because the product is not actually made in the USA, and that felt genuinely misleading. Shipping took nearly three weeks to arrive, though covid was a factor at the time. As a NY GIRL who does her own nails regularly, I expected better consistency from a set marketed this way. I would be cautious before buying this again.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Unfortunately I wasn't a fan of this one. It didn't do much to help my lips. Rather than exfoliate it actually just seemed to dry out my lips and not just a little bit. It wasn't fun. I tried it a few times, as directed. Just apply a small pea-sized amount to the lips using a finger-tip, scrub gently in a circular motion to remove the dead skin cells for about 30 seconds then rinse or eat off remaining sugar. All of that was done, rinsed not eaten though the one thing that was good was that it didn't taste half bad. Alas, my poor lips were left in worse shape than ever. Dry and roughed up. No matter how hard or gentle I used the stuff over a three week period. I finally gave up and had to admit defeat. Perhaps others had better luck?\", \"sampled\": \"I gave this lip scrub a 2 out of 5. Honestly, I wanted it to work. I followed the directions each time, applying a pea-sized amount with my fingertip and scrubbing in a circular motion for about 30 seconds. It did not taste bad. But after 3 weeks of using it as directed, my lips were consistently left dry and roughed up rather than smooth, which is the opposite of what a lip scrub should do, and I kept hoping each session would turn things around but it never did. The exfoliation just was not happening in any noticeable way, and the drying effect got harder to ignore. I rinsed it off rather than eating it off, so maybe that changed something, but the directions did not make that feel like a requirement. My lips felt worse after each use. I gave up after 3 weeks. Not what I paid for.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This 4 pack of travel lotions by Spanature is super nice, from the presentation of the product, to the performance of the product. They come packaged soooo nicely in 4 little gift boxes, so they are perfect for gift giving. Also, the tubes are the perfect size to fit in your purse- so they are great for on the go or even traveling! The lotion itself is very moisturizing, and not greasy at all. The green tea scent is not strong at all, which is perfect if you are sensitive to strong scents.\", \"sampled\": \"This Spanature 4-pack of travel lotions has become a go-to for keeping skin moisturized without that heavy, greasy feeling that so many lotions leave behind. The green tea scent is light enough that it works well for anyone sensitive to strong fragrances. Each tube is small enough to slip into a purse, and the individual gift box packaging makes the set a genuinely thoughtful gift option. The lotion absorbs well and feels comfortable throughout the day. It is a solid pick for travel or just keeping one handy when away from home.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This is a very nice face mask. It is very easy to use, basically a sheet mask that you put over your face and let sit for about 15 to 20 minutes. So just have a good show on tv or a book handy to read. This mask smells beautifully of roses. It feeds your skin it feels like with the serum it contains and leaves your skin soft and healthy. Your face will look and feel great. This is a nice little treat for yourself. You will get 10 masks in this pack which is actually a really nice deal.\", \"sampled\": \"This sheet face mask has a lovely rose scent and leaves skin feeling soft and healthy after just 15 to 20 minutes. The serum inside does a nice job nourishing the skin, and the mask is genuinely easy to apply. Each pack comes with 10 masks, which feels like a solid deal for the price. The rose smell alone makes the experience pleasant. Skin feels noticeably softer after each use. There is nothing complicated about the routine. A 5.0 rating feels well earned for a mask this simple and effective.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"These sheet masks are great for dry or dehydrated skin. They're especially good to use in cold or dry weather. The masks contain an extract of oolong tea and hyaluronic acid. Your skin will look dewy and radiant after 10-20 minutes. Make sure the mask is smoothed over your skin. If you have a jade roller, it's nice to use it over the mask to increase absorption. I highly recommend both this mask and the entire product line. Thanks so much for taking the time to read this review. I hope the information that I've provided makes your decision a little easier.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up this sheet mask for my dry, dehydrated skin and gave it a 5.0 rating. The formula combines oolong tea extract with hyaluronic acid, which suits dry or dehydrated skin well. Cold and dry weather is when I reach for it most. After 10-20 minutes my skin looks genuinely dewy and radiant. I smooth the mask carefully over my face for the best contact. Running a jade roller over it seems to help with absorption. I'd recommend this mask and the whole product line. Note that this is not a verified purchase, and the review has received 2 helpful votes.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Everyone knows the saying \\\"Don't put anything in your ear smaller than your elbow.\\\" but we all use cotton buds to clean out our ears. Just about every one of these came apart. One I had to have a friend pull out with a pair of tweezers. I was just glad she was home. Years ago they used to make cotton buds with wooden sticks. There was a good reason they stopped and went to a more flexible and softer cardboard. I think these aren't the safest cotton buds on the market. I really don't like these buds, they come apart, the wood doesn't flex and is thin and almost pointy. Not a fan.\", \"sampled\": \"I gave these cotton buds a 2.0 rating because almost every single one came apart during use, which is both frustrating and a little scary. One bud got stuck and a friend had to remove it with tweezers. I was really glad they were home to help. Older cotton buds used wooden sticks, and manufacturers switched to flexible, softer cardboard sticks for a good reason. These wood sticks are thin, almost pointy, and do not flex at all. I consider these buds not the safest on the market. As the saying goes, “Don't put anything in your ear smaller than your elbow.” I am not a fan.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I like drinking apple cider vinegar but smelling it on my hair is a different story. While the scent dissipates I smell the vinegar when I first spray it on my fine, frizzy hair. The mist seemed to make it a little bit softer and smoother, but not to the point where I'd get this again. I prefer to use other anti-frizz products because I can't get past the slight vinegar smell. Add in the high price and I'm not getting it again.\", \"sampled\": \"I like drinking apple cider vinegar, but I really dislike smelling it on my hair. The vinegar scent is noticeable when first sprayed, though it does dissipate over time. On my fine, frizzy hair the mist made it slightly softer and smoother, but that wasn’t enough for me to repurchase. I prefer other anti-frizz products and just can’t get past that slight vinegar smell. The high price sealed my decision not to buy it again.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I always have lotion on hand so these individually packed tubes of lotion from South Korea are a good fit for me. I like the feel and texture of the lotion - non greasy and effective. As a lotion, it is excellent but the scents are not particularly attractive. I've tried the Olive and the Green Tea scented versions and am not particularly drawn to the scents. The green tea is a bit too sweet and doesn't smell like green tea, so I've deducted 1 star.\", \"sampled\": \"I tried the Olive and Green Tea versions of this lotion from South Korea, and while the individually packed tubes are handy and the formula feels non-greasy and effective, the scents really let it down. The Green Tea one is way too sweet and doesn’t smell like green tea at all. I always have lotion on hand, but I found neither scent particularly attractive. I ended up deducting 1 star just because of that, so my overall rating sits at 4.0.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I paid $24.99 for the 4 cavity 3 way lipstick mold and a few days later it jumped to $59, possibly different sellers I don't know, but it was good timing on my part. Considering I have read we might consume more than 2 pounds of unhealthy lipsticks per lifetime it might have been worth the higher $59 price to be able to use any recipe for lipstick or chapstick I want, when I want it, not to mention any color in the world. After finding and deciding to purchase this mold a few days later I purchased lipstck tubes, tiny measuring spoons and spatulas, the best butters from across the world (thank you Amazon), oxides and pigments from Amazon. Because the orders arrived so quickly and wrapped so securely, it didn't give me much time to research lipstick and color recipes to tweak and make my own, so I haven't yet opened my new mold.\", \"sampled\": \"I paid $24.99 for the 4 cavity 3 way lipstick mold, and I still can’t believe the price later jumped to $59. I read somewhere that people might consume more than 2 pounds of unhealthy lipsticks over a lifetime, and that stat really got under my skin and pushed me toward making my own. The mold lets you create any lipstick or chapstick recipe and color you can dream up. I went a little overboard and bought lipstick tubes, tiny measuring spoons, spatulas, butters from across the world via Amazon, plus oxides and pigments from Amazon too. Everything arrived quickly and securely wrapped, which was a relief. The process, including mold cleaning, is pretty basic. I researched high gloss, matte, waterproof, plumping, supple, and antiaging recipes, and now I keep my favorite Chapstick in every room, all vehicles, and my purse while I get ready to replace them all with homemade tints and colored lipsticks.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This JODK Heatless Curling Rod Headband is so cute. I loved how it looked when my hair was in it. I waited 4 hours and my hair still wasn't dry so it didn't curl. I will be trying it again. The box comes with a hair curler and clips. It also comes with 4 scrunchies, 2 large and 2 small. There is also a beautiful, drawstring bag to store everything and instructions. I will be recommending this JODK Heatless Curling Rod Headband to family and friends because it is so cute.\", \"sampled\": \"I bought the JODK Heatless Curling Rod Headband and it’s very cute in the hair, which I loved. The box includes a hair curler, clips, and four scrunchies, two large and two small. A drawstring bag and instructions are also packed inside. I really liked how it looked when I wore it. I waited 4 hours and my hair still wasn’t dry so it didn’t curl. Because it stayed damp, there were no curls at all. I plan to try it again soon to see if I can get better results.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I am totally disappointed with this headband. One it is super cheap and easily bendable which means it will break easily. I have very fine hair so this headband is just horrible with my hair. It sticks up at the top and doesn't hold any hair back because my hair just slides out of it. Now, if someone has very thick hair then this would work perfect for them but for someone like me with very fine hair, this headband just isn't right for me.\", \"sampled\": \"I’m totally disappointed with this headband. It feels super cheap and easily bendable, so I worry it will break fast. I have very fine hair, and the band sticks up at the top instead of lying flat. It doesn’t hold my hair back at all because my hair just slides out. I think it would work perfectly for someone with very thick hair, but it’s not right for me.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I have tried a few products by this line by Laloirelle. This Age Defy Face oil comes in a dark glass dropper bottle like a lot of facial oils do. It is not cheap at almost $100 to be honest. When you use this you will need about a drop for each side of your face, so a little does go a long way, but this is still not an inexpensive product so handle this as such. This particular one is filled with marula oil, rosehip oil, reishi mushroom, ginseng and cordyceps mushroom. This particular blend is supposed to help with wrinkles, acne scars and dry skin. When you use this you will notice this oil definitely has a fragrance to it that is reminiscent of an essential oil. They recommend to use it morning and night, however in my experience when you use this it does absorb fairly quickly. It does leave an oily residue on your skin though. If you wear makeup and especially right now with wearing masks, I would not recommend applying this facial oil in the morning.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up the Laloirelle Age Defy Face oil hoping it would tackle wrinkles, acne scars, and dry skin. The dark glass dropper bottle feels premium, and you only need about one drop per side of face. At almost $100, I expected serious results. The ingredient list sounds impressive with marula oil, rosehip oil, reishi mushroom, ginseng, and cordyceps mushroom. It has a fragrance that reminds me of essential oil, which I didn’t mind at first. The oil absorbs quickly, but it leaves an oily residue that never fully disappears. My dry skin felt moisturized, yet my face stayed greasy to the touch. I tried using it under makeup and it just didn’t work. I also wouldn’t recommend it under masks because of that slick finish. For a product that costs almost $100, I need more than decent moisture and a shiny face. It does help with dry patches, but the greasy feel makes it a nighttime-only option for me. I’m giving it a 3.0 rating. Honestly, I’ve used cheaper alternatives that perform just as well without the heavy residue.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This Mini Nail Dust Collector is nice. I would have loved to have instructions included with the collector. I did go on the purchase page to see that I needed 2AA batteries. There is one part that I still don't know what it is for. I did a test and it did work and I am happy with that but instructions would have saved time. The box comes with the collector. It has two little tools include. On small brush and I don't know what the other thing is. I will be recommending this Mini Nail Dust Collector to family and friends.\", \"sampled\": \"The Mini Nail Dust Collector is nice, but it arrived without any instructions. I had to check the purchase page to learn it needs 2 AA batteries. There is one part whose purpose I still do not know. A test confirmed the collector works, and I am happy it does. The box includes the collector and two little tools. One is a small brush. I do not know what the other tool is. Instructions would have saved time, but I will still recommend it to family and friends.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Wipes like this are a fill in for actually washing your face with real soap and water, but they do come in handy sometimes. I was using these after working horses and shoveling stalls. The wipes are thick enough to get the grime off without tearing. However, after three days, my face broke out in two spots! My friend tried them and after three days, she says she's fine. So it's just me I guess.\", \"sampled\": \"I gave these wipes a try as a substitute for washing my face with soap and water, and they are useful sometimes. I used them after working with horses and shoveling stalls, and they are thick enough to remove grime without tearing. After three days of use, my face broke out in two spots. A friend tried the wipes and after three days reported no issues, so I think the breakout is a personal reaction.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"These are cute in the picture and they look adorable in my kid's hair, but they are inconsistent, only two have the really long material and they are not the best colors to match any of our clothes. The others have the short little ties and they just feel really cheap. They come pretied and all of the shorter Scrunchies are dull in color.\", \"sampled\": \"These scrunchies look cute in the picture and absolutely adorable in my kid’s hair, but the quality is really inconsistent. Only two have long material, and their colors don’t match our clothes well. The rest have short ties, feel cheap, and come pretied in dull shades. My older kids like wearing scrunchies on their wrist but found these itchy, so we’ll mostly wear them around the house.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I was really hoping I snagged a good one but this one is off the mark a bit. The blades tend to snag a little bit when cutting the hairs so it pulls a little when it cuts. I also found that it doesn't contour to the face all that well even though it has the ability to flex the blades. It also just comes with a USB cord and no plug so you have to hunt around for a plug to use. I didn't get to test the battery life as I only used it once and my face was as shaved as it was going to be with this razor.\", \"sampled\": \"I have only used this razor one time, and it provides an adequate but not great shave. The biggest problem I had was that the blades tended to snag and pull hairs during use. I also found that the razor does not contour well to the user's face at all. Even with the flexing feature, the blades did not improve its ability to follow my facial contours. The product includes a USB cord for charging, but it unfortunately does not come with a wall plug. I cannot test the battery life. I only used the product a single time before deciding it was not for me.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This Morovan Gel Nail Polish Remover is great. I used it to remove my Christmas gel nail polish. I was surprised at well it worked. The box comes with a 16.9 fluid ounce bottle of gel nail polish remover and a pump bottle. It also comes with a pack of cotton pads and a pack of nail clips. Also included is a file and cuticle pusher. Instructions are included. I will be recommending this Morovan Gel Nail Polish Remover to family and friends.\", \"sampled\": \"This is a great product. I used it to take off my Christmas gel nail polish. I was really surprised by its effectiveness. The kit comes with a 16.9oz bottle of remover, a pump bottle, cotton pads, and nail clips. A nail file and a cuticle pusher are also included in the box. The product comes with instructions. It has everything. I will definitely be recommending this product to others.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"These work great, just like any 'thai deoderant crystal', same stuff. They are a little 'rough' at first, a use or two makes them smooth. You get 3+ pieces to make the weight, packed in a box. So all in all a good deal if this method works for you! Also, since you're getting a few pieces, you won't be caught short when it inevitably smacks the tile bathroom floor and shatters 😅\", \"sampled\": \"This Thai Deodorant Crystal works effectively, just like other similar products. The crystals feel a bit rough at first but become smooth after a use or two. The product is sold by weight, with three or more pieces packed inside a box. This is a good value for anyone who likes this method of deodorant. Having multiple pieces is also great in case one shatters if you drop it on a hard floor.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This time, I am trying three different items. I am trying the Virtue cream for you neck and décolletage, the Silk moisturizing cream and the Bright lifting eye cream. Because I put other products on my face and eye area earlier, I am putting this on my hands and arm. I love the feel of the Silk moisturizing cream, the Virtue cream is thicker than your other moisturizers, and the eye lifting cream. The eye lifting cream is applied in dots so you only go around your eye area. The Silk moisturizing cream is really nice and can be used on your face daily.\", \"sampled\": \"I am trying three different products today. I'm testing the Virtue cream, the Silk moisturizing cream, and the Bright lifting eye cream from the same company. To start, I just applied the products to my hands and my arm. The Virtue cream has a thicker consistency than the brand's other moisturizers. I absolutely love the feel of the Silk moisturizing cream, which is suitable for daily use on the face. The eye lifting cream is meant to be applied in dots around the eye area.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I'm impressed with this face soap. It's very thick and silky, and only a very small amount is needed to get good results. That's especially welcome to find out given the price for a pretty small bottle. It lathers up well and does a good job of cleaning without leaving my skin overly dry. It is a bit more intensive than other face soaps, so my face does feel a little 'tight' after using it. However, it's not excessive, and well worth it given how well this soap cleans if you have an oily complexation. The charcoal makes the soap dark, but it's not as jet-black as other charcoal soaps I've used. It's more of a deep gray. The scent is also quite nice. It's a medium/light intensity and has sort of an after-shave smell to it. The scent does not linger much at all after rinsing. While I tend to prefer unscented soaps, this scent is pleasant and enjoyable. The only con is the bottle. Getting the pump nozzle to pop up was a chore since the retaining ring the pump head screws into also rotates, making it a struggle to get the nozzle to pop up.\", \"sampled\": \"This face soap is quite thick. It has a silky texture, and you only need a small amount to produce a good lather. I found that it cleans very effectively without making my skin feel excessively dry. My face does feel slightly tight after using it, but that is not a major issue. This soap seems particularly well-suited for anyone who has an oily complexion like I do. A little bit of this product goes a long way, and it performs its main function well. It really does get your face clean. The charcoal gives it a deep gray color. It has a pleasant, medium-to-light intensity scent. The smell is similar to after-shave. I also noticed the scent does not linger long after rinsing. The packaging was a different story. The bottle's pump nozzle was very difficult for me to get open when I first tried to use it. Once I finally got it working, I noticed the pump mechanism leaks a small amount of soap at the stem. This little bit of leakage is a persistent problem with the bottle I received. The bottle itself is the main issue. Because of the bottle's problems, I deducted one star from my rating.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I absolutely love this mascara. It comes in a really stylish container so extra points for presentation. I love that it doesn’t go on or even remotely look clumpy. I haven’t noticed any extra volume but to be honest that is what I like about this, it is thin and is very minimal. This lasts all day without smearing but it is super easy to wash off. This is fairly inexpensive but I can tell it is not cheaply made. The applicator still looks squeaky clean. Even my usual brand’s applicator would be clumped up by now and I pay a fortune for that stuff. I’m really happy with this product. It’s perfect for me.\", \"sampled\": \"I really love this mascara. It comes in a very stylish container. The formula itself is thin, and it applies without any clumping at all. This product provides more of a minimal look, so it is not the right choice if you are hoping to add significant volume. It lasts all day long and I have not had any issues with it smearing. At the end of the day, it is easy to wash off. The applicator also stays clean. Even though this mascara is inexpensive, it does not seem cheaply made in any way. I actually prefer this product to my usual, more expensive brand. I will be buying this again.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I have the updated version of this. It's the Braun Silk Epil 9 SkinSpa. I think the technology has improved, as I find it much easier to remove all hair in the area. I mainly use this on my legs and underarms. I had the basic Silk epil 7 with the smaller head and felt like I needed to go over an area several times, which worried me - was I creating too much friction. With the larger head and cleaner removal, I think that concern may be gone. I also store the newer version in my shower, as I really like the body brush.\", \"sampled\": \"I own the Braun Silk Epil 9 SkinSpa. This is an updated version of a previous model I had. I believe the technology has improved, as it is much easier to remove all the hair from an area. My old Silk epil 7 had a smaller head that required multiple passes, which created too much friction on my skin. The new model has a larger head that provides cleaner hair removal on my legs and underarms, and I worry less about friction. I also like the body brush that was included with the new version. I keep my new epilator in my shower.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I really like this pencil, but the color, gray brown, was too dark for me and I couldn't use it. As there was no other color that would be better, I can't buy this product; however, if you can find a good color for you the quality and ease of application is wonderful. I also recommend that they stamp the name of the company on the actual pencil so that if I ever wanted to buy it again I would know how to do that.\", \"sampled\": \"I liked the quality of this pencil and it was very easy to apply, which was a great start. Unfortunately, the 'gray brown' color was way too dark for me, so I couldn't use the product and was unable to purchase it since no other suitable color was available. The pencil itself doesn't have the company name on it, which I think would make re-purchasing difficult. The only writing on the pencil is the color number 04 at the bottom.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"First and foremost- this is a cream deodorant. If you have never used a cream it is basically rubbing lotion into your arms pits (anyone remember Tussy deodorant?) We gave this a go and it smells really nice- a little strong (Jasmine has a very strong sweet scent), but pleasant.\", \"sampled\": \"This is a cream deodorant like Tussy. You apply it like a lotion, and while I didn't experience any skin irritation, the sweet Jasmine scent was a bit strong for me, though it effectively prevented odor during normal daily activities. Unfortunately, its effectiveness completely failed me after a 1.5-hour workout session.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I was hoping more of this oil. First it does not have any árgan type of scent... on the contrary it smells like fried oil. Not good. It is not super strong scent, but definitely you can smell it. Second, it does not absorbe fast. I was oily until I fell asleep. It was lightweight. I did felt my skin hidrates the next day, but not quite sure if this is better than other oils that I use. Definitely, I won’t use it, because mainly of the French fries smell.\", \"sampled\": \"This oil smells just like fried oil. It has a lightweight consistency but does not absorb quickly into the skin. I waited. My skin remained oily until I went to sleep. It has no argan-like fragrance. The smell was just unpleasant. I'm disappointed. I will not continue to use this product because I simply cannot get past the smell, even though my skin felt hydrated the next day. I'm not sure if this is superior to other oils I have used.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Face and Body Paint Kit By Color Technik 24 Professional Oil Based Flash Colors, 2 Sets of 12 Color Palettes, 6 paint brushes is a fun to do thang to do. You see all these TV shows these days with people painting their face, painting their body, you might just want to try it yourself and this is the perfect opportunity to do it, with this set of face painting paints. You can achieve many colors and effects with this set. What the heck, I might just wanna go green for a day, what do you say?\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried the Face and Body Paint Kit by Color Technik, which includes 24 professional oil-based flash colors divided into 2 sets of 12 color palettes. The kit also comes with 6 paint brushes, offering a wide range of colors and effects for a fun activity in face and body painting. This set not only encourages creativity and experimentation but also provides an opportunity to try face painting at home. Inspired by TV shows and ideal for temporary color changes, it's perfect for themed events or parties, promoting the exploration of new looks and styles.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This is such a high end product. The weighted compact feels sleek and heavy in your hand. The slide/pivot opening is clever and works smooth. The color saturation is rich and deep and looks flawless on many skin tones. Not too much shimmer, so it actually looks like you've been sun kissed after application. Buildable color if you want a more intense look. You can use both colors together, which is what I do. I am medium complexion, blonde, 48 years old. This product doesn't increase pores size, nor does it settle into any wrinkles. In my opinion, deserving of more than 5 stars.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried the high-end bronzer compact, and I must say, it exceeded my expectations. The weighted compact feels sleek and heavy, giving a luxurious touch to the product. The color saturation is rich and deep, providing a beautiful finish without being too shimmery. I love that the bronzer is buildable, allowing me to create an intense look when desired. It's perfect for medium skin tones and doesn't accentuate pores or settle into wrinkles, making it a must-have in my makeup collection. This bronzer truly deserves more than 5 stars for its quality and performance.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This is an exceptionally well-made and beautiful brush in a travel size. The brush and handle are small, so this brush is not as easy to grasp as one that is full sized. However, It detangles and brushes through my curls with ease. I also did not experience the typical loss of bristles I usually do with a boar bristle brush. Most animal hair brushes seem to shed for a while and then stop shedding. So far I have not had that problem. I actually cannot believe this is priced at $7.29 because most boar bristles brushes of this size are in the $28 to $30 range. The price, coupled with the style, finish, and functionality of this hairbrush is outstanding in my opinion.\", \"sampled\": \"This brush is a gem in terms of quality and aesthetics. Crafted exceptionally well, it stands out as a beautiful brush that not only looks great but also delivers on functionality. Its travel size makes it convenient to carry anywhere, ensuring your curls stay in top shape on the go. I found that it effortlessly detangles and brushes through my curls, making styling a breeze. What impressed me the most was the absence of the typical bristle shedding that often plagues other brushes, a testament to its durability and build quality. At just $7.29, this brush offers incredible value for its superior quality. Comparing it to similar brushes in the market priced at $28 to $30, it becomes evident that this product is not only affordable but also a steal for the level of style, finish, and functionality it provides.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This face moisturizer containing hyaluronic acid and Vitamin C, plus some other good ingredients is not bad. It also contains glycolic acid, salicyclic acid, Vitamin A and E and really has a lot of good ingredients for your skin. It is lightweight and applies nicely to your skin. This moisturizer absorbs very quickly but your skin doesn't feel super soft after you apply it. Yes it is soft but I have other moisturizer that make my skin feel a lot softer when I use them. It comes in this really cute pump dispenser that is very stylish. What I did not like about this moisturizer is the scent of it. I can't pinpoint what it smells like but I did not particularly care for it. Thankfully the scent does not linger very long on your skin. The other thing I did not understand that in the directions of using it it says \\\" follow with moisturizer or makeup\\\". Why would I have to use a moisturizer after using this moisturizer? Pretty confusing if you ask me. All in all this product is just ok in my book. It felt ok on my skin, smelt bad to me at least and the instructions were weird to say the least. For this price you can get some other products that work better and definitely smell better!\", \"sampled\": \"This moisturizer contains a potent mix of hyaluronic acid, Vitamin C, glycolic acid, salicylic acid, Vitamin A, and E, offering a promising array of beneficial ingredients. The formula is described as lightweight and fast-absorbing, making it convenient for daily use. However, despite these positives, my skin didn't feel exceptionally soft after application, which was a bit disappointing. One notable feature of this product is its stylish pump dispenser, which adds a touch of elegance to the packaging. On the downside, the scent of the moisturizer was not to my liking, which detracted from the overall experience. Additionally, the instructions provided with the product were found to be confusing, making it challenging to understand the best way to incorporate it into my skincare routine effectively. While this moisturizer has its merits, including a blend of beneficial ingredients and convenient packaging, I personally prefer other products available at a similar price point. The underwhelming softness of my skin post-application, combined with the disliked scent and confusing instructions, lead me to believe there are better options out there for my skincare needs.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This lotion smells so good! It smells like fresh squeezed lemonade and sugar, its so refreshing. The texture is great, its nice and lightweight. I typically put on lotion after a shower and it leaves my skin feeling soft and well hydrated. It takes a bit to absorb, but by the time I'm finished getting all my parts the first limb I started with is dry. The scent doesn't last long, which for me is sad, its so good! but I know that can be too much for some people. This lotion keeps my skin hydrated for days, but I might need a refresher in the winter.\", \"sampled\": \"I tried out the lemonade-scented lotion, and I have to say, the fragrance is spot on - like fresh squeezed lemonade with a hint of sugar. The texture is indeed lovely, light, and easy to apply. After using it, my skin felt noticeably softer and well moisturized. However, the scent doesn't linger for long, and it may take a bit of time to fully absorb into the skin. While it keeps my skin hydrated for days, I found that in the winter, I needed to reapply more frequently. Additionally, the strong scent might be overpowering for some individuals.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This colored hair wax works very well. It is perfect for those Halloween or cosplay occasions. If I would have gotten a different color than gray I guess I could have used it on other occasions for like accent strikes of hairs. It is easy to use and leaves a similar feeling behind as if you used some of those colored hair sprays. The hair is a tad stiff, just like it is when you use hair wax. I don't use those kinds of products often so it is always a bit weird to me when I can't just run my fingers through my long hair. It washes out easily without any problems. When you apply it and wash your hands it comes of easy as well. This is a very easy to use product without any issues and not very expensive so you can't really go wrong with it.\", \"sampled\": \"I tried out the colored hair wax, and it really impressed me with its effectiveness. The product worked very well, leaving my hair with a vibrant hue that lasted throughout the day. It's perfect for occasions like Halloween or cosplay, adding a fun and creative touch to your look effortlessly. One thing I appreciated about this hair wax is how easy it is to use. The application was simple, and I found that it provided a similar feeling to colored hair sprays. While my hair did become slightly stiff, like with regular hair wax, it wasn't uncomfortable and added to the overall style I was going for. Additionally, washing it out was a breeze with no issues, which made the whole experience even more enjoyable. What's great about this product is not only its performance but also its affordability. It's a budget-friendly option for those looking to experiment with different hair colors or add accent strikes to their hair. I would recommend this colored hair wax to anyone seeking a temporary and hassle-free way to switch up their hair color for special events or just for fun.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"You never know what you're gonna get with some of these beauty sponges, but this is definitely one of the good sets. The sponges are actually soft and don't absorb as much product as other sponges. I recently tried another brand that looked exactly the same but they were so hard and rubbery. These are soooo much softer in comparison. I don't feel like I'm softly punching my face like I do with the others😂 Very easy to clean, after use and have no weird smell or anything. I haven't used the puffs or brush yet but I'm going to attempt to use the puffs for powder or maybe even jelly products. The brush would probably work well for concealer or eyeshadow primer. It has soft bristles and a solid handle. Overall I think this is an awesome quality set for a great price!!\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried out these beauty sponges, and I must say, they are incredibly soft and do not soak up too much product, which is a big plus for me. In comparison to another brand I've used before, these sponges are noticeably softer, making the application process much more enjoyable and gentle on the skin. Cleaning these sponges is a breeze, and I appreciate that there is no strange odor left behind after washing them, ensuring a fresh application every time. The set also includes puffs and a brush, with the brush impressing me with its soft bristles and sturdy handle. It's a nice addition to the collection and enhances the overall experience. Overall, this set offers great quality at a reasonable price point, making it a fantastic value for anyone looking to elevate their makeup application routine without breaking the bank.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I have always been a fan of Taya hair products. I found though that this duo is not one of my favorites. In this pack you get a shampoo and conditioner that are basically travel size for $14. They are a bit bigger than you would get at a hotel for example so you get quite a few applications out of them, but for $14 I find this to be rather expensive to be honest. The bottles are pretty cute and look nice. When I used the shampoo I have to say I was disappointed by the fragrance. Usually this line has a fantastic fragrance but this one just wasn’t it. It produced a decent lather and rinsed out with ease. The conditioner had a similar fragrance in line with the shampoo. It was nice and soft and I did leave it in my hair for about 3 minutes I would say. It was easy to rinse out and left no residue on my hair. My hair overall looked ok once it was dry and was pretty soft but nothing impressive that would warrant spending $14 on this. I am sorry I just wasn’t impressed with this set, especially since you can get the full sized ones for a similar price really.\", \"sampled\": \"I purchased the Taya duo, which includes travel-size shampoo and conditioner for $14. While the price seemed reasonable, I was disappointed by the fragrance of both products. The shampoo's scent was not to my liking, and unfortunately, the conditioner had a similar disappointing fragrance. On a positive note, the shampoo produced a decent lather and rinsed out easily, while the conditioner left no residue on my hair. After using the set, my hair looked okay and felt soft, but overall, I was not impressed. I found it noteworthy that full-sized products from the same brand were available for a similar price. In conclusion, the Taya shampoo and conditioner duo offers convenience with its travel-size packaging at an affordable price point of $14. However, the disappointing fragrance of both products was a significant drawback for me. While the shampoo performed adequately in terms of lather and rinsing, and the conditioner left no residue, the overall lackluster experience left me unimpressed. Considering that full-sized products from the brand are available at a similar price, I would recommend exploring other options if fragrance is a crucial factor in your hair care routine.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This is exactly what I needed. I have been pouring mouthwash out of 1.5L bottles for years, and this pump provides a consistent, metered amount, and prevents waste. I use two full pumps to provide enough mouthwash for a proper rinse, and some people might even want three (one is definitely not enough). Works perfectly, fits perfectly, and is exactly what I wanted. Best $7 I have spent in ages. :)\", \"sampled\": \"I recently purchased a mouthwash dispenser that came with a metered pump for my mouthwash. This dispenser requires two pumps for a proper rinse, ensuring that I use just the right amount each time. It fits perfectly with my 1.5L bottles and works as expected, preventing any waste and providing a consistent amount of mouthwash. At just $7, this has been the best investment in my oral hygiene routine in a long time.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Kama Sutra Treasures of the Sea is an intriguing bath salt with a warm sensuous room enveloping fragrance. One scoop of the salts in a tub of water and you transform everyday bathing into bubbles and water the color of the ocean. The color reminded me of the turquoise waters near the Aiya Napa white caves in Cyprus. The texture of the water turns silky and you may notice your skin is more moisturized from the natural oils, especially the Wheat Germ Oil. While many may see the color as \\\"Azure\\\" there is a definite green vs. purple color mingling in the depths of blue and this does contain Algae. The texture of the salt is more like a sand than a salt and the shimmering finely ground blue salt crystals mingle with what looks like tiny pieces of broken marble. Of course, this all dissolves quickly and the bubbles last through the entire bath. In fact, the more you move around, the more bubbles appear. The warm scent soothes the soul and the color is dreamy and the bubbles look like clouds floating across an open sky.\", \"sampled\": \"The Kama Sutra Treasures of the Sea bath salt offers a warm, sensuous fragrance that fills the room, creating a delightful atmosphere for a relaxing bath experience. Transforming your daily bathing routine, this bath salt turns the water into a beautiful shade reminiscent of the ocean, enhancing the ambiance and making you feel like you're indulging in a luxurious spa treatment. The color of this bath salt is striking, resembling the turquoise waters near the Aiya Napa white caves in Cyprus, adding a touch of exotic charm to your bathing ritual. Thanks to the natural oils, especially Wheat Germ Oil, the texture of the water becomes silky smooth, effectively moisturizing your skin and leaving it feeling nourished and rejuvenated. Additionally, the bath salt contains Algae, contributing to the green vs. purple hues that swirl together in the depths of the blue water, creating a visually stunning and relaxing environment for your bath time.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"These manage to be very generic looking. Which is fine, but interesting that I could notice considering it's just a toothbrush head. They just don't have the decorative banding on them that some of the others have (they do have the color band for distinguishing between multiple users). What I don't like is that they do not have the plastic shields with them, although even the originals don't have that anymore. As my remaining shield gets cracked further and further, I wonder how I will continue to travel with my brush. These seem basically the same as the originals. It's sort of hard to say if my perception that they are just a tad less good is based on something substantial or just aesthetics and packaging. If the price is right, they're worth a try.\", \"sampled\": \"The toothbrush heads I purchased recently are quite generic looking. They lack any decorative banding, but do have color bands for user distinction, which I found to be helpful. The design is very similar to the original toothbrush heads I had, but I must admit, they seem slightly less good. I am unsure if this perception is based on substantial factors or simply aesthetics and packaging. A significant concern for me is that these toothbrush heads do not come with plastic shields. This may not bother everyone, as the original toothbrush heads also do not have plastic shields, but it makes me uneasy about traveling with the toothbrush. Despite this, I believe these toothbrush heads are worth trying, especially if the price is right.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"My kids and I have been doing a lot of crafting which involves placing tiny sequins and gems on to small painted wooden or clay items, and so while we are not using these for the intended purpose of going on nails, we have been going through these quickly using tweezers to get them out and apply to our craft projects. I've been really impressed with the variety of the sequins, the quality level of them with the thickness and color reflectivity being great, and the quantity given. We all really love these beautiful butterflies and how vivid and fun they are.\", \"sampled\": \"As someone who enjoys crafting with my kids, I found the B08HGBZLWY to be quite versatile, despite it not being used for its intended purpose. We mainly use it to decorate our small painted wooden or clay items, and it has added such a unique touch to our creations. What makes this product stand out are the variety of sequins included. These sequins have great thickness and color reflectivity, which truly enhance the aesthetic of our crafts. It's become a must-have in our crafting routine, and we're always excited to see the results.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This is a lip stain, not lipstick. And yes, they're cotton swabs. You break one end so it flows to the other, and there's a LOT of stain in each cotton swab (there's 20 in the box, each wrapped in plastic). I think you can get two uses out of each one, but obviously, they're meant for single use. This stain works VERY well. The I am Fine color is reddish-brown, but it was a very nice red color on my lips. And my fingers - be careful when you apply these since they do stain. It's best to exfoliate before you use them, and to line your lips since I noticed a bit of bleeding/feathering when I used the product. Nothing that was obvious unless one was very close, but it would be prevented from lining my lips which I don't normally think to do when using a stain. Then again, the applicator is a cotton swab (so no fine control) and you get a LOT of product with it. It does last all day unless you eat something greasy/buttery. So this would be great for going out for drinks or doing anything where you avoid greasy food. Also, the instructions on the box are in Chinese.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried the lip stain called \\\"I am Fine\\\". Contrary to what some might assume, it's not a lipstick. The application method is unique; it uses cotton swabs, which are provided in the package. There are exactly 20 of them in each box, all individually wrapped in plastic. The lip stain works very well, I must say. The color, a rich reddish-brown, is intense and suits my complexion perfectly. However, one thing to note is that it can stain your fingers if you're not careful. Also, the lip stain may bleed or feather, so you have to apply it with precision. Another point worth noting is the longevity; it lasts all day unless you're eating greasy food. Despite its effectiveness, the product has one significant drawback. The instructions on the box are in Chinese, which can make usage a little challenging for non-Chinese speakers. Regardless, with a product rating of 4.0, it seems that many users, including myself, are satisfied with the performance of this unique lip stain.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"As much as I hate to admit it, getting close to 50 brings lots of unpleasant changes and some great ones. One of the ridiculous things is suddenly discovering random hairs on my face. Tweezing is certainly effective, but takes forever and is tiresome. This is the first little shaver of this type I've used. It needs a single AA battery that is not included. It's reasonably quiet when running, has a little light and is easy to hold. It is completely painless as advertised - this actually shaves rather than ripping hair out like those epilator things. It works well and I don't really have any complaints.\", \"sampled\": \"The shaver, which requires a single AA battery, is easy to operate. It's a little disappointing that the battery is not included with the product. On the bright side, the shaver is reasonably quiet when running. What I loved about this shaver is that it has a small light, which is quite helpful during usage. The device is also comfortable to hold, providing a firm grip. As advertised, the shaver is completely painless. Unlike epilators that rip out hair, this shaver simply shaves it off. Despite its minor shortcomings, I would rate this product a solid 4.0.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This black soap bar feels as thick as a brick but feels great on my skin. It's made with activated charcoal and has the coloring of charcoal, but thankfully not the scent! Under a hot shower it releases a nice minty scent and the hardy soap appears to less vulnerable to melting away in the soap dish unlike other softer soaps I've used. It's made for men but I don't see why women can't use it as well, as it's refreshing. It's best used for the face, as it cleans out your pores, but as I wash my face and body in the shower at the same time, it's a perfect face body combination soap.\", \"sampled\": \"The black soap bar I tried recently was a unique experience. Its main ingredient, activated charcoal, gave it an unusual look but also some noteworthy benefits. The soap had a refreshing minty scent that I found quite pleasant. One practical advantage I noticed was that this soap seemed less prone to melting in the soap dish compared to other brands I've used before. The manufacturer markets it as suitable for both men and women, and I can see why - the scent is not overpowering, and the charcoal provides a thorough cleanse. Primarily, I used it for my face, but it also worked well for the body. Despite its many positives, I would give it a 4 out of 5, mainly because I found it a bit drying.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This was a new thing for me to try. I liked it but find myself not using it. I thought maybe my wife would like it but it seems that she is not using it either. It seems to take more soap than just using a washcloth. I do like how it feels when I use it. It feels nice on my body. I won't give up yet, I will keep trying it.\", \"sampled\": \"I have mixed feelings about the product B08LGG9GSG. It was a new experience for me, but I find myself not using it often. My wife also has the same sentiment and does not use the product either. One thing we noticed is that this product seems to consume more soap than a regular washcloth. However, I must say, I really like how it feels on my body. Despite these issues, I plan to continue using the product, hoping for a better experience.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"\\\"Jeff's Wife\\\"- These are so good! I only wish I had the original versions to compare. I've only heard negative things about the original stamping polishes from BM so I don't own any, I've only used MdU. But when BM reformulated their stamping polishes I gave them a try and I now own all the new collections so I gave these a try too. Boy am I glad I did. These DID NOT DISAPPOINT! I don't own white stamping polish, I use Sinful Colors Snow Me White that has been open for 3 days, it works but it's still not proper stamping polish. The black I use is MdU, the absolute holy grail of stamping polishes. But I have to say that these are beyond good! This black is comparable to MdU black in every way, except the smell. These have just become my favorite! I don't think I will be ordering from anyone else anymore.\", \"sampled\": \"As Jeff's Wife, I'm a fan of stamping polishes. Specifically, I've been quite satisfied with BM's reformulated versions. I've acquired all the new collections they've released, and they've genuinely impressed me. Interestingly, I also use Sinful Colors Snow Me White as my go-to white stamping polish. It's been a reliable staple in my nail art routine. On the other hand, when it comes to black stamping polish, I turn to MdU's product. The consistency and the depth of color it offers are just unbeatable. However, I have to give credit where it's due: BM's black stamping polish holds its own against MdU's. They're quite comparable in terms of performance. I would rate BM's stamping polishes a solid 5.0, and it seems I'm not alone in my assessment, as I've received 18 helpful votes from the community. In conclusion, BM's and MdU's stamping polishes are both excellent choices, and Sinful Colors Snow Me White is a reliable alternative for white shades.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I have very light eyebrows (thanks genetics!) so I always darken my eye browns slightly with a little brown eye shadow so I thought I’d try this. It is waterproof but does easily rub off skin with some water if you don’t let it sit for awhile. Once it’s dried, it stays on pretty well. I ordered brown since I have light brown hair but this is a very dark brown so it gave a rather dramatic and sinister look lol I don’t think I’ll be using this. The tips of the eyebrow brush is rather bendable so you can go from 3 small, thin lines to 1 thick line VERY quickly.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently purchased the B08QZBZG89 eyebrow product, and I was quite excited to try it as it claimed to be waterproof. However, I noticed that it could easily rub off my skin with some water if not properly dried. Once dried, it does stay on well, which is a redeeming quality. I ordered it in brown, expecting a natural look, but found the color to be very dark, almost unnatural. An interesting feature of this product is the bendable eyebrow brush tip, which can quickly go from creating 3 small, thin lines to 1 thick line. Overall, it's a decent product with a few minor flaws.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"and a nose that doesn't get over fragranced easily, this is your cream. It is thick, applies easily and gets absorbed quickly. The scent does dissipates in a fairly good time. Ingredients: Aqua/Water/Eau**, Aqua/Water/Eau, Glycerin, Glyceryl Stearate, Dodecane, Butyrospermum Parkii (Shea) Butter*, Propanediol, Butylene Glycol, Coco-Caprylate/Caprate, Candelilla/Jojoba/Rice Bran Polyglyceryl-3 Esters, Magnesium Aluminum Silicate, Olus Oil/Vegetable Oil/Huile Végétale, Silica, Mel Extract/Honey Extract/Extrait de miel*, Royal Jelly, Cera Alba/Beeswax/Cire d'abeille*, Propolis Extract, Rosa Canina Fruit* Extract, Zizyphus Jujuba Seed Extract, Cistus Incanus Flower/Leaf/Stem Extract, Commiphora Myrrha Oil, Olea Europaea (Olive) Fruit Oil*, Rosa Damascena Flower Oil*, Boswellia Carterii Oil*, Triticum Vulgare (Wheat) Germ Oil, Panthenol, Sodium Hyaluronate, Tocopherol, Tocopheryl Acetate, Sigesbeckia Orientalis Extract, Rabdosia Rubescens Extract, Gynostemma Pentaphyllum Leaf/Stem Extract, Helianthus Annuus (Sunflower) Seed Oil*, Glyceryl Linoleate, Glyceryl Linolenate, Sorbitan Palmitate, Cetyl Palmitate, Bisabolol, Lecithin, Glycine, Alanine, Serine, Valine, Threonine, Isoleucine, Proline, Phenylalanine, Histidine, Hydroxypropyl Cyclodextrin, Arginine, Allantoin, Disodium EDTA, Ethylhexylglycerin, Glutamine, Ascorbyl Tetraisopalmitate, PEG-100 Stearate, Citric Acid, Aspartic Acid, Sorbitan Olivate, Pullulan, Xanthan Gum, Decyl Glucoside, PCA, Phytoecdysteroids, Sodium PCA, Sodium Lactate, Sodium Stearoyl Lactylate, Citronellyl Methylcrotonate, Caprylyl Glycol, Cetearyl Alcohol, Phenethyl Alcohol, Phenoxyethanol, Parfum/Fragrance, Benzyl Salicylate, Linalool, Limonene, Citronellol, Geraniol, Alpha-Isomethyl Ionone, Hydroxycitronellal.\", \"sampled\": \"I've been using the cream with the identifier B07KQ32Z8C in my skincare routine. The product is thick, just how I like my creams. Despite its thickness, it applies easily onto the skin. This cream is not just easy to apply, but also gets absorbed quickly into the skin. The ingredients such as Shea Butter, Glycerin, Royal Jelly, Rosa Canina Fruit Extract, Olive Fruit Oil, Wheat Germ Oil, and Sunflower Seed Oil all work together to nourish and hydrate, especially for those with dry skin. The cream is not overly fragranced, which is a plus for me as I prefer my skincare products to have a minimal scent. However, the scent that it does have dissipates quickly after application. I give this product a rating of 5.0, owing to its impressive performance and beneficial ingredients. Thick cream. Absorbs quickly. In conclusion, this cream has been a great addition to my skincare routine. It's not just the easy application or quick absorption that impresses me, but also the minimal and quick-dissipating fragrance. I would definitely recommend this cream to anyone dealing with dry skin.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I'm disappointed in the look of this pink jeweled headband. The crystal beads look cheap and the headband seems made for a teenage girl. I was hoping for sparkly pink rhinestones but they're actually plastic faceted beads, the same kind you'd sew on the cuff of a sweater. They're kind of blah and look chintzy. It comes in a zip loc bag with a dust cloth with the company's unpronounceable name printed on it. Disappointed.\", \"sampled\": \"The pink jeweled headband, adorned with what were supposed to be crystal beads, leaves much to be desired. To my disappointment, the beads turned out to be plastic faceted beads, not the pink rhinestones I had been expecting. In fact, \\\"The crystal beads look cheap and the headband seems made for a teenage girl,\\\" as I have noticed. They eerily resemble the kind of beads you would find sewn on the cuff of a sweater, rather than on a piece of jewelry.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"My wife has been using this product for about a week and likes it. It’s very easy to apply, just like clear liquid eyeliner. It’s good that it does not irritate my wife’s sensitive skin at all. She has tried on her lashes and has noticed a slight difference in the thickness of them. I am sure that time will bring more dramatic results. The product is a bit pricey for the amount you get though.\", \"sampled\": \"My wife tried B082MTTFZD on her lashes for about a week and liked it. I found it very easy to apply. The application felt like using clear liquid eyeliner, so it was straightforward. It did not irritate her sensitive skin, which mattered for her use. She has noticed a slight improvement in lash thickness so far. I would rate it 4.0, expecting more dramatic results with more time, though it seems somewhat expensive for the quantity and was not marked as a verified purchase.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A little about me- I am late 30's and have pretty good skin just looking for something to delay the aging process.I had purchased the entire obagi line and was starting to use it but hated how it made me so dry and flaky- with spring coming on I was wanting to start tanning and couldnt do that while using Obagi- it was a pretty hefty investment for that skin care line but thought maybe I would put it up until the winter and wanted to try something that would work with me tanning and didnt want to spend 400 on a skin care system without trying it out first and bought this starter set- I am in love!\", \"sampled\": \"I came to eminece after using Obagi, and my reaction is mixed mainly because Obagi pushed me away with dryness and flaking even though I had bought the entire line; I am in my late 30's, started with fairly good skin, and wanted something to help slow visible aging without fighting my skin or my tanning plans as spring approached, so I did not want to pay 400 for a skin care system before testing it, especially since this purchase was not marked as verified and the parent ASIN is B001CL4O9E.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I love these bibs! My son really needs these, he is one and a bit messy. He has lots of teeth coming in and lots of sisters who enjoy feeding him lots things. They go on easily with a small snap on the back and stay put. They come off easily and the material is great. It's beathable and I can throw them right in the washing machine. They come witha nice cloth bag that has a draw string so I can keep them together. Nice and easy just like it should be. My son looks adorable and I am very happy.\", \"sampled\": \"I love these bibs. I use them for my son, who is one and messy. He has many teeth coming in, so bibs get plenty of use here. His sisters enjoy feeding him, which makes staying covered even more helpful. The small rear snap attaches easily. Once on, the bibs stay in place well. They are also easy to remove, which I appreciate after feeding. I like the breathable material. They can go right in the washing machine, and the cloth drawstring bag keeps them together. These bibs feel like a 5.0 item to me, and B0189OQF8Y was worth getting.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Ok, I read the directions, I read the blogs, I watched the YouTube videos and I still ended up using more polish remover than admiring my skills.between my 16 year old and I we managed to get on a couple stamps that semi resembled what they were supposed to! I highly recommend spending your money on a manicure instead. Although there is a great variety of images to choose from and they are really cool, I just don't have the months it would probably take to master this. The product itself I think is of high quality and any professional or highly skilled person would be impressed by the variety of images and quality of these plates.\", \"sampled\": \"I gave this a 4.0 because the product itself seems high quality, and the image options are large, varied, and cool, but it was not easy for me to use even after reading directions, checking blogs, and watching YouTube videos. This was a verified purchase, and I went through a lot of polish remover while trying to get it to work. My 16 year old helped me, and between us we managed only a couple of stamps that partly resembled the intended images, so I would rather spend the money on a manicure unless I had months to master it. I do think professionals or highly skilled users would appreciate the plate quality and the variety, but for me the review title Not easy! fits.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I have very, very fine but very curly hair and am always trying to tame some little wisp of hair that refuses to stay in one place. These clips are about the only type of clips that work well in keeping my wispy hair in place for any length of time. The only thing that was a problem with these is that the side of the plastic these came in had torn and they were all over the shipping box. I mean aaalllll ooovvvveeerrr inside the shipping box. What a pain to pick all of these up...but I believe I got them all. I do love these little clips and love all the bright colors that I can use for spring and summer.\", \"sampled\": \"I would rate these hair clips 4.0 because they work well for my very fine, very curly hair, which is not always easy to manage. The small clips help keep my wispy hair controlled, and this type holds it for a while without much fuss. I also like the bright color variety, especially since the colors feel right for spring and summer use. The packaging was the annoying part. The plastic packaging was torn on one side, and the clips were loose throughout the shipping box. Gathering the scattered clips was frustrating, though I believe I recovered all of them, and the review was not marked as a verified purchase with 1 helpful vote.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The polishes in this set work wonderfully and the packaging is very nice and would make an excellent gift! Unfortunately I bumped the lamp off my table and it fell about 3 feet and stopped working...I didn't think it was much of a fall so I was pretty surprised it didn't turn on anymore. The lamp was compact and easy to use, fit one hand at a time. Fortunately I have another lamp but pretty bummed this one broke so easily.:/\", \"sampled\": \"This set lands at 3.0 for me because the polishes performed very well, and I liked the packaging. B0813ZQG3T could be a strong gift option, but the lamp was the weak point, which was disappointing because it felt compact, easy to use, and fit one hand at a time. The lamp fell from a table, about 3 feet, and stopped working. I was surprised it failed after such a minor fall, and I was glad I had another lamp available.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This came with no clear instructions - as a result, I was very reluctant to use it given the danger of burning myself. The instructions were in very small print and there was a chart showing the different levels of heat that you should use depending upon the color of your skin - light, medium, dark. This felt weirdly racist for some reason, perhaps because the writing was so poor. For something that affects your skin I would only use a device like this if the instructions are clearly spelled out.\", \"sampled\": \"I would rate B0823WPN5Q at 2.0 because the instructions were poorly written, unclear, and hard to read due to the very small text. I was hesitant to use it because I was worried about burns, especially since a chart connected heat levels to skin color categories listed as light, medium, and dark. That chart came across to me as strangely racist, and for this kind of skin-related device I would only use it with clearly explained instructions. The review was not marked as a verified purchase and had 0 helpful votes.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Between regular, every day germs and now Covid-19, we go through a bunch of sanitizer. It's in the cars, the house, the horse barn and luggage. We tend to use Purell in the giant wall dispensers in the house and barn, but these big bottles are nice too. The sanitizer smells like what it is. This seems a bit thinner than the Purell, but dries quickly enough. I haven't noticed any sticky residue left behind or excessive drying of our skin. I'm taking one of these with me to West Texas tomorrow for use in the truck and cabin. It fits nicely in my overnight bag pocket.\", \"sampled\": \"I use a lot of hand sanitizer because of everyday germs and Covid-19. I keep it in my cars, house, horse barn, and luggage. At the house and barn I usually use Purell in large wall dispensers, so these large bottles are useful for me. This sanitizer smells like sanitizer, nothing unusual. It seems slightly thinner than Purell, which I noticed right away. It dries fast enough for me and did not leave sticky residue. I also did not notice excessive skin dryness, which matters when using sanitizer often. I gave it a 5.0 and plan to take one bottle to West Texas tomorrow for my truck and cabin, since it fits well in my overnight bag pocket.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I use fractioned oils to make rollerballs for my oil blends and for a carrier for various topical uses. I have been wanting to recreate a muscle cramp relief oil from a major essential oil club and lucked up with a copycat recipe. These oils are over $30 for 5ml and, while it works great and smells amazing, I just couldn’t bring myself to spend that knowing that I have a box full of single oils. When I saw this bottle with the pump, I knew it was time to try my hand and mixing this recipe. I had a few other rollerballs that I wanted to make (some decongestant and immune boosters since it’s getting to that time of year) so I inserted the pump and got to work. The oils mixed VERY well and the pump on the bottle made it very easy to dispense just enough into the small rollerball opening. That is SUCH a nice feature – manufacturer, please don’t remove that from the item. I can’t tell you how many times I’ve spilled my fractioned oil everywhere because it was difficult to contain since it’s thin. Once I had worked through the 5 rollerballs for my family needs, I had enough room in the bottle to make a BIG batch of the massage oil with the rest of the fractioned oil. IT WORKED!!! I’m soooooo happy with this bottle of fractioned oil.\", \"sampled\": \"I rated B01I2MU2JQ 5.0 because it worked well for my hands-on oil needs. I used this fractioned oil mainly to prepare rollerballs for oil blends and topical carrier uses. I had wanted to recreate a muscle cramp relief oil from a copycat recipe, since the comparable essential oil club product costs over $30 for 5ml. The pump bottle was the part I appreciated most in daily use. It helped me dispense the oil into small rollerball openings without fighting the thin texture. I have spilled fractioned oil before because it was hard to control. This setup made that much less frustrating. The oils mixed very well, which mattered because I was making blends rather than just using the oil plain. I ended up making 5 rollerballs for family needs, then used the remaining bottle space to make a large batch of massage oil. Good use. That felt practical for our house. I used the oil for sore muscles and on my hubby's back, which matched what I wanted from it. I would ask the manufacturer not to remove the pump feature. My only real complaint is based on past experience with fractioned oil, not this bottle's performance. Thin oil can get messy when the packaging is not controlled. This pump solved that problem for me. I received the product at a reduced price for testing. My review is based on firsthand experience with the oil, the pump, and the blends I made. If this was helpful, my answer would be \\\"yes\\\".\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The Iryasa Face Cream comes in a little jar, which is not my favorite container since you obviously dip into it all the time. So I tend to wash my hands before I do that so I don't introduce dirt and such into it because that can shorten the life span of these products. The cream itself is actually a little heavy which was surprising since it looked light and airy. It has some nice natural ingredients in it which is great. I wasn't too crazy about the smell of it though but that is because it used Geranium essential oil, which is probably one of my least favorite but I was hoping that the lemon would help cover it but it still powered through a lot. The cream absorbs fairly quickly into your skin and but leaves a sticky residue on your skin that doesn't seem to fade for quite some time. Thankfully by using this at night you won't know since you add this last thing before you go to bed and then just fall asleep. It does add moisture to your skin so in the morning your skin does feel pretty nice.\", \"sampled\": \"I gave Iryasa Face Cream a 4.0 because it does moisturize, but a few parts of using it bothered me. This was not marked as a verified purchase, and the review had 1 helpful vote, so I am just speaking from my own use of the product. The cream comes in a small jar, which I do not love because I have to put my fingers into it. I wash my hands first to avoid contaminating it. The texture also felt heavier than I expected from how it looked, and while I like that it contains natural ingredients, the scent was a miss for me because the Geranium essential oil was too noticeable. I had hoped the lemon would soften or mask the geranium scent more, but it did not do that enough for my nose. It absorbs into my skin fairly fast, yet it leaves a sticky feel that hangs around for a while, so I prefer using it at night. By morning, my skin does feel nice and moisturized, which is why B07W397QG4 still ended up being usable for me rather than a total disappointment.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I'm pretty happy about the LumaRx facial cleansing brush. It is designed very ergonomically and is very comfortable to hold during use. The tool is extremely lightweight and has a tapered design which makes it so comfortable to use. The instructions are to use it for a minute each morning and evening. It has illustrations which show you to spend 20 seconds on the right side and 20 seconds on the left side and 20 seconds on the T-zone. The tool will actually buzz gently when you should move to the next area. This is a great feature! It also comes with 3 brush heads; massage, sensitive and normal skin. I am using the sensitive for now and it is perfect for a gentle exfoliation. When I am finished with that minute, my skin just glows and feels so clean! Hopefully the heads do not need replaced quickly as I do not think they are yet available on Amazon or online. It also comes with a sleek charger that does not take up any room at all on the counter – this is important to me as I have a small bathroom. Also, the LumaRx comes with a very nice carrying case that will come in handy for an upcoming vacation.\", \"sampled\": \"I've been really happy with the LumaRx facial cleansing brush overall. It's lightweight and ergonomic, so it doesn't feel awkward to hold, and the routine itself is simple — just 1 minute morning and night. That minute is split into 20 seconds on the right side, 20 seconds on the left, and 20 seconds on the T-zone, and the brush actually buzzes to signal when it's time to move on, which means I'm not constantly glancing at a clock. It comes with 3 brush heads for massage, sensitive, and normal skin types; I've been sticking with the sensitive head since I don't want anything too aggressive. The charger is compact enough that it doesn't take over my small bathroom counter, and there's a carrying case included that I'm already planning to use when I travel. My one real concern is that replacement brush heads weren't available on Amazon or anywhere else online when I bought it, which is something to think about since the heads will eventually wear out. That aside, the whole thing feels well thought out for a daily routine.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"A good hand cream is essential. This one is excellent. It is thick and creamy. It's very moisturizing and is packed with skin-loving botanicals. It's fragrance-free in case you have allergies or just prefer the unscented option. This is a high-quality hand cream that's great to keep in your bag. Thanks so much for taking the time to read this review. I hope the information that I've provided makes your decision a little easier.\", \"sampled\": \"I've been really happy with this hand cream — it's thick and creamy in a way that actually feels moisturizing rather than just greasy. The botanicals in it seem to make a real difference, and my hands feel genuinely nourished after using it. I'm sensitive to fragrances, so the fact that it's fragrance-free was a big selling point for me, though I think anyone who just prefers unscented products would appreciate that too.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I'm in my mid-fifties and I have been using creams targeted to the neck and chest area for a few years now. I have definitely seem some improvement in the texture of that skin. This bottle is very small. It's a lotion consistency and it has a light pleasant scent. It doesn't leave my hands feeling sticky, but the bottle is sticky. I have washed it at least once and it remains sticky. It absorbs quickly. I can't tell if it's doing it's job as I haven't seen any noticeable changes, but it isn't hurting anything either.\", \"sampled\": \"I'm in my mid-fifties and have been using neck and chest creams for a few years, so I have a decent basis for comparison. Previous creams I've tried did show some improvement in my skin texture over time. This one comes in a pretty small bottle. The lotion itself has a light, pleasant scent and absorbs quickly without leaving my hands sticky, which I liked. The bottle itself, though, is sticky — I washed it at least once and it didn't help. As for results, I haven't noticed any real changes in my skin, though it doesn't seem to be doing any harm either.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I really like these velvet hair bands. They are stylish and the buttons are positioned perfectly. They hold my mask securely without slipping or aggravating my ears. There is a nice variety of two leopards and three neutrals, cream, black and gray. It was supposed to be a six pack but I only received five. Mine also were not as wide as depicted. But that being said they are a fashion forward way to keep hair and masks in place.\", \"sampled\": \"I got these velvet hair bands and honestly really like them — they're stylish and actually do a good job keeping your hair in place. The buttons are positioned well, and they hold a mask securely without slipping or digging into your ears. The pack comes with 2 leopard-print bands and 3 neutral ones in cream, black, and gray. My main gripe is that the listing said six-pack but only 5 showed up, and they're noticeably narrower than what the product photos suggest. That said, they work well for what they are, so I can't complain too much overall.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I love how this Essence has such soothing and hydrating ingredients. I've just recently started using K-beauty products, but the first one I tried left my skin very irritated. This one feels so light, but moisturizing. I'm just learning about a lot of the ingredients that are in most Korean beauty products, and I'm also Learning that some of them do not do well with my skin. This one works wonderfully for me. I use Retin-A, so anything really harsh or very exfoliating is too much. The first one made my skin very red, swollen, and angry looking. This one feels very nice on my skin and I love the way my skin is looking and feeling now. I would definitely recommend it! Especially, for those using Retin-A or those with very sensitive skin.\", \"sampled\": \"I'm fairly new to K-beauty and still figuring out which ingredients actually agree with my skin. My first K-beauty product was a total disaster — left my skin irritated, red, swollen, and genuinely angry-looking. Since I use Retin-A, I have to be careful with anything harsh or exfoliating because my skin is already sensitized, so I was pretty nervous about trying another Korean product after that experience. This essence, though, has been a completely different story. It feels light but still genuinely moisturizing, which is a hard balance to find, and it hasn't aggravated my Retin-A-sensitized skin the way I was afraid it might. I honestly can't explain why this one works when others haven't — I still don't fully understand a lot of the ingredients common in K-beauty — but it just does. If you're a Retin-A user or have sensitive skin that needs something soothing and hydrating without the irritation risk, I'd especially recommend giving this one a shot.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Highly fragranced set, beautifully hand packed with tissue. You can order this with confidence if you are giving it as a gift, because an outer cardboard box protects the gift box itself from any shipping damage. Just opening it up is a delight. The aroma is soothing with natural fragrance. Not a hint of off chemical odors, just fresh essential oils, blended for a peaceful experience. I especially like the petite candle that is a lovely way to complete the experience. You can have a candle lit bath using the bath gel, and spray the linens with the pillow spray for a calming and sleep inducing experience.\", \"sampled\": \"I was really impressed by how carefully this was packed — tissue inside the gift box, and then the whole thing wrapped in an outer cardboard box, so it arrived completely undamaged and looking great. The set comes with a petite candle, a bath gel, and a pillow spray, and everything smells like genuine essential oils with no chemical edge to them at all. The pillow spray is my favorite part — genuinely calming, and I've been using it at the end of long days when I need help winding down. I bought this for someone going through a rough patch, and it felt like a real, thoughtful gift rather than something thrown together. The fragrance is strong in the best way, and the whole set just feels like it was worth every penny.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This product comes in a glass bottle with a pump dispenser. The dispenser has a very small spout, making it difficult to dispense the product without having it run down the side of the bottle. Note that there is oil in the name - it feels like an oil and goes on like an oil, with no foaming or cleansing at all. It also has a strong scent of lavender (which I like, but others may not). This product works well as a remover for eye makeup. However, I don't feel that it does a good job of cleansing my face overall. Although the product rinses clean and leaves my face feeling soft, my face doesn't feel clean, and I feel the need to use an actual cleanser after. I would recommend this product as a eye makeup remover only and would suggest that \\\"cleanser\\\" be removed from the name.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up this oil cleanser hoping it would simplify my routine, but my experience has been pretty mixed. The glass bottle with a pump dispenser looks nice, but the spout is so narrow that the oil constantly runs down the side — kind of annoying. It applies like a straight oil with no foaming or cleansing sensation whatsoever, and while it does rinse clean and leaves my face feeling soft, I never actually felt like my skin was getting clean. I ended up reaching for a second cleanser every single time, which completely defeats the purpose. The strong lavender scent is something I personally like, though I could see it being a dealbreaker for others. Where it does shine is as an eye makeup remover — that's honestly the only place I think it earns its keep. I'd recommend it strictly for that purpose and would suggest dropping \\\"cleanser\\\" from the name altogether, because calling it that sets the wrong expectation for anyone hoping to use it as a full facial wash.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"These rubber masks do require more work and cause considerably more mess than the regular sheet masks. However, as far as rubber masks go, these are relatively easy to use and remove. The rubber mask stayed on thicker and were easier to peel off than some of the ones I'd used previously. The feel and texture of these masks light, cold and rubbery. Once I remove the masks, my skin doesn't feel sticky at all. The masks hydrate and are light on the skin.\", \"sampled\": \"Rubber masks are definitely more work and messier than regular sheet masks, but these are worth it. Compared to other rubber masks I've tried, these were easier to use and peel off. The mask went on thicker, which I actually liked, and the texture felt light, cold, and rubbery in a good way. After taking it off, my skin wasn't sticky at all. The hydration was noticeable enough that the extra effort felt totally reasonable.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I want to use this spray on the bedding. All the bedding. Makes it smell so nice for when we all go to bed. Just a nice fresh scent. I can't really pick out a particular scent with this spray. It's a nice combination though. With spring here and the windows open again, we're all loving having a nice fresh scent in our bedrooms and our beds using this spray.\", \"sampled\": \"I've been using this spray on my bedding and it leaves a really fresh, pleasant scent that everyone in the house has been happy with. I was actually writing this review in spring with the windows open, and the scent still came through nicely, which impressed me. I can't quite pin down one specific fragrance — it's more of a blend — but honestly that's part of what we like about it.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Out of all the 4D Silk Mascaraas I've tried recently the CPCP Elegant Princess Long Fiber Eyelash Mascara is one of my favorites. The packaging is gorgeous and the brush is a thicker comb-like bristle, which is great for volume and definition. My lashes looked dramatically darker and fuller and I really noticed the difference when I went outside and checked it out in my rearview mirror. I had tested two mascaras on each eye and this one really stood out. I have been wearing it for almost 36 hours and even slept in it. After rubbing my eyes since the last night, the top lashes held and I didn't wake up with raccoon eyes. however, it does flake a bit. It applied smoothly and since It's not a quick drying mascara so you have a bit of time to tease out the lashes to avoid any clumping..\", \"sampled\": \"I've tried a few 4D Silk Mascaras lately and this one has quickly become my favorite, though honestly the packaging alone caught my attention before I even got to testing it. I put 2 mascaras side by side, one on each eye, and the difference was obvious enough that I noticed it glancing at myself in a rearview mirror. The brush has thick, comb-like bristles that do a great job building volume and separating lashes, and since it doesn't dry super fast, I had plenty of time to work through them without clumping. Application was smooth and my lashes came out noticeably darker and fuller. The real test came when I wore it for nearly 36 hours straight, slept in it, rubbed my eyes overnight — and woke up with no raccoon eyes, which genuinely surprised me. My only gripe is that it does flake a little, which is enough to stop me from reaching for it every single day.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"There are 9 soft non-shedding make-up brushes in this set. This is a gorgeous set that contains a brush for most uses. There is no fan, eyebrow, or lip brush, however. Each brush comes individually packaged. In fact it is over packaged. The bristles are velvety soft and they do not fall out, which is great. Their soft bristles feel good against my skin and work like many other brushes I tried previously. I particularly LOVE the large blush/foundation brush. I do wish the handles were a tad longer. Nevertheless, the current price of $8.99 is an super reasonable for this set as it comes to $1 per brush. This is a perfect set for the young teenager starting out on her make-up journey.\", \"sampled\": \"I grabbed this 9-piece brush set for $8.99, so it comes out to about a dollar a brush. The bristles are genuinely soft and I haven’t had any shedding at all—they feel nice on my skin and work about as well as other brushes I’ve used. The packaging is a bit much since every single brush is wrapped individually, and I really wish the handles were a little longer. It’s also missing a fan brush, an eyebrow brush, and a lip brush, so the selection doesn’t feel totally complete. I especially love the large blush/foundation brush—it’s the standout for me. For the price, it’s hard to complain too much, and this would make an ideal starter set for a young teenager just getting into makeup.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I can't say enough good things about these versatile Wonder Clips by Handi Stitch. I recently received a package of 50 of these with 10 large clips and have used them on a daily basis for so much more than just holding fabric together for stitching seams. Even this small size holds together leather so I find that I don't need to rubber cement the pieces together as I have done in the past to hold the pieces together. Kids love these, too, so be prepared to find that you need more than you ever anticipated. I received samples for evaluation and love these clips.\", \"sampled\": \"I’ve been using the Handi Stitch Wonder Clips every day, and honestly, they’re a solid 5 out of 5 for me. The pack came with 50 regular clips and 10 large ones, which already feels like plenty for all the sewing and crafting I do. They grip leather way better than I expected—I’ve completely stopped reaching for rubber cement on leather pieces. My kids keep snapping them onto fabric scraps, so I can already tell I’m going to need more than I thought. I received these samples for evaluation.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This AG Hair Natural Remedy Apple Cider Vinegar is relatively expensive at $20 for a small bottle, but it is made of natural ingredients like apple cider vinegar, organic argan oil and protein from quinoa. The spray is supposed to refresh the scalp and the hair, to reduce irritation to the scalp and to help soften hair. I have been trying different scalp treatments and was eager to see how this would work. It is less of a scalp treatment and more of a hair refresher but it does add bounce and lift to my hair.\", \"sampled\": \"I’ve been trying different scalp treatments and grabbed the AG Hair Natural Remedy Apple Cider Vinegar spray, but at $20 for a small bottle it feels pretty steep for what you get. It’s built around natural ingredients like apple cider vinegar, organic argan oil, and quinoa protein, and it’s supposed to refresh the scalp and hair while calming irritation and softening things up. Honestly, it works more as a hair refresher than a real scalp treatment. I do notice extra bounce and lift, but it doesn’t do enough on the scalp side to justify the cost.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I really like this shampoo by Giovanni. I was not sure what to expect to be honest, but I was pleasantly surprised. A lot of time when these shampoos contain tea tree and hemp their fragrance is not the greatest, but this one was shockingly impressive and really good. The fragrance of this one was actually really good and you can barely smell the tea tree which is great. I only smelled hints of it while it was on my hair. This shampoo produces a nice lather that wasn’t too rich but plenty to get my hair nice and clean. I didn’t feel like I needed a lot of it to get my hair nice and clean. I did let it sit for a couple of minutes to let it do “its thing”. It rinsed out very easily and left nothing behind in my hair.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up the Giovanni shampoo and was honestly surprised by how much I liked it. The fragrance was way better than I expected for something with tea tree and hemp—the tea tree scent was barely there, just a hint while it was on my hair. It built a nice lather that wasn’t too rich, and I only needed a small amount to get my hair clean. I let it sit for a couple of minutes to do its thing, and it rinsed out easily with no residue at all. My hair felt clean and looked great afterward. I could have skipped leave-in conditioner completely, which almost never happens for me. I’m very happy with how this performed. The whole experience was smooth from start to finish. It’s a solid shampoo I’d definitely recommend, and I’ll be buying it again without a second thought.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This is the best clipper I've ever had and now that my hands aren't as agile as they once were, perfect! The handle is thicker than standard and textured w/3 indented slots; has indented area where thumb fits comfortably; is not the shiny stainless steel that is so slippery. Now the back part/bottom is the shiny steel...I think they should have made it textured too, to ease slipperiness! The actual clipper cutting edge is the smile shape ). The back also has a \\\"keyhole\\\" for hanging it if you wanted. Great little file w/nicely plumped end for ease of use. A \\\"nail catcher\\\"..meaning a black tough cover that when not in use snaps on to the clipper.\", \"sampled\": \"I’ve owned a lot of clippers over the years, and this B07J3GH1W1 is easily the best one I’ve used. The handle is thicker than most and textured with three indented slots, plus there’s an indented spot where my thumb sits comfortably, so it never feels like it’s going to slip. It’s not shiny, slippery stainless steel, though the back part is shiny steel and can get a little slick. I wish they’d textured that back section too, but the smile-shaped cutting edge makes a clean cut every time. There’s a keyhole for hanging on the back, and it comes with a file that has a nicely plumped end that’s easy to use. A black nail catcher cover snaps on when it’s not in use, and all the components fit inside a handy little black case.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This is a nice facial emulsion. It comes in a nice little pump dispenser that works well to dispense the small quantities you need for your face. It has a mild yet pleasant fragrance. The emulsion itself is very lightweight. It absorbs very quickly into the skin. At first the face was a bit sticky and I was not crazy about how my face felt. But that changed rather quickly and I was impressed on how my face felt. It felt smooth and soft and just looked nicely moisturized. I for one really liked how this emulsion makes my face feel. This is not bad for an inexpensive facial emulsion. Pretty pleased with this actually.\", \"sampled\": \"I’ve been using this facial emulsion for a little while now. The pump dispenser is easy to control, so I’m not wasting product when I only need a small amount. The scent is mild and pleasant, and the texture feels really lightweight—it sinks in almost instantly. Right after I put it on, my face does feel sticky for a moment, but that changes fast. Once it fully absorbs, my skin turns smooth and soft, looking nicely moisturized without any heavy residue. I really like how my face feels throughout the day. For the price, I think it’s a solid inexpensive facial emulsion.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I really like this straightener for the most part. I'm not sure how... but my hair (and my daughters) seems to come from the wand so much shinier. It has an adjustable temperature setting, it does lock closed. It even comes with a bag to keep it in and a glove to wear while you are using it... which is where I have my problem. The way it opens leaves my hand too close to the heat which results in easily getting burned. Which isn't a problem if you wear the glove...\", \"sampled\": \"I like this straightener for the most part. My hair comes out noticeably shinier when I use the wand. It has an adjustable temperature setting and it locks closed, which feels secure. It comes with a storage bag, a heat shield glove, and two hair clips for sectioning. The way it opens leaves your hand way too close to the heat, and I’ve already burned myself. I’m not going to use that glove every day. For the price, it’s a great wand. I’ll get used to where to hold my hand.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This is my first scrunchie. I have not had my hair cut in about 9 months because of the pandemic, so my hair has gotten longer than I've worn it in years! I'm in my early 50's and it's a little below my shoulders now. My hair is thick and coarse. It's long enough to wear in a messy bun for the first time in years! The scrunchie is easy to use and holds up my thick hair pretty well. I don't have to readjust it because it's falling. You can feel the coils inside the fabric. I find it fascinating how it works. When I take it off, it shrinks up again. I like it!\", \"sampled\": \"I’m in my early 50s and this is my first scrunchie. I haven’t had a haircut in about 9 months because of the pandemic, so my hair is longer than it’s been in years—a little below the shoulders, thick, and coarse. For the first time in ages I can actually put it up in a messy bun. The scrunchie is simple to use and holds my thick hair pretty well. I don’t have to readjust it during the day because it stays put. You can feel the coils inside the fabric, and it snaps back to shape after you take it off.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"If your bath or shower is missing something, this might be it! This scrub is so nice and cozy. It smells amazing, like a cup of hot cocoa. It has great grains for exfoliation, there's different sizes to make sure your skin gets the best exfoliation. The mask in it is great to keep your skin soft and smooth too. For the ultimate treatment put a thick cream on after this and your skin will glow!\", \"sampled\": \"This scrub is such a cozy little treat in the shower—it honestly smells just like a cup of hot cocoa. The grains come in different sizes, so the exfoliation feels effective without being harsh. It also has a built-in mask that leaves my skin soft and smooth every time. I follow up with a thick cream, and my skin looks glowing afterward. It’s a simple, comforting addition to my routine.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I just recently started using the paper charcoal bubble masks but I do love peel off masks even more. I put on a medium layer of this and after a few minutes it starts to bubble. Unlike with the paper masks it was almost too tingly that made me want to just wash it off. But it went away after a few more minutes and then I just had to wait for it to dry. Maybe 20 minutes total. I like to leave peel off masks on longer to tighten my pores. It came of in just a couple pieces which was unusual, usually you have all these scraps left on. My skin looked and felt very fresh after.\", \"sampled\": \"I’ve been trying out paper charcoal bubble masks lately, but honestly I still reach for peel-off masks more. I put on a medium layer and within a few minutes it was bubbling like crazy. The tingling was almost too much at first, though it calmed down after a bit. It took around 20 minutes to fully dry. I usually leave peel-off masks on longer to really tighten my pores. This one came off in just a couple of pieces, which was surprising since most peel-off masks I’ve used leave little scraps behind. My skin did look and feel really fresh afterwards.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This is a package of 15 individually packaged biodegradable wipes. All of their products come in 15, 30, or 45. This 15 pack at $8.95 makes them $0.59 each. Easy to open, all tucked into a flip top box Excellent for on the go, for travel, for whenever. I have used a variety of Busy Co wipes, and I have liked them all, some more than others. When I selected this, I chose it after taking a look at the ingredients (how I always select items to buy or to try). So it was weird not to see direct ingredient list on this product page. I have included the ingredients here: Water (Aqua), Glycerin, Phenoxyethanol, Sodium Benzoate, Citric Acid, Potassium Sorbate, Ethylhexylglycerin, Decyl Glucoside, Sodium Hyaluronate, Astrocaryum Murumuru Seed Powder, Aloe Barbadensis Leaf Juice This unscented GLOW formula is aimed toward those seeking to BRIGHTEN SKIN, IMPROVE DRY SKIN, and REPAIR DAMAGED SKIN. Not sure who is trying to use this on babies, but this product is not marketed for that use.\", \"sampled\": \"I'm reviewing the Busy Co GLOW formula wipes, which come in a pack of 15 individually wrapped towelettes. These are supposed to brighten your skin and are biodegradable and unscented. I mostly use them on my hands and feet at night, and they do a good job cleaning my skin and nails. I follow up with lotion because they can be a bit drying. I also keep a few in my purse for on-the-go cleaning, which is convenient. Since the product page didn't have an ingredient list, I wanted to share that the main ingredients are Sodium Hyaluronate and Aloe Barbadensis Leaf Juice, which are good for dryness. I wouldn't recommend using these on babies since they aren't marketed for that. My only real complaint is that the wipes could be damper. They still work, but they feel a little dry. For that reason, I probably won't repurchase this specific GLOW formula, even though I've liked and repurchased other Busy Co wipes. The price for this 15-pack was $8.95, which is about $0.59 per wipe.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"There are no instructions on how to care for the hair extensions nor are there instructions on how to securely put on the halo filament wire (actually a strong, clear plastic line that has 5 adjustable positions AND you get extra wire and snap-in-place comb fasteners). I had to search YouTube for directions on how to place it securely. I'm still working on getting it the correct size for my head size. I have very thin hair which might be the problem. There are tons of videos about different hairstyles with halo hair extensions. Plus lots of different updos because YES you can have an updo -- buns, pony tails, braiding etc, -- with these extensions.\", \"sampled\": \"My halo hair extensions didn't come with any instructions for care or how to attach them, but the package did include an extra wire, some snap-in comb clips, and a strong, clear plastic halo wire with five adjustable positions. I had to look up videos on YouTube to figure out how to place them, and I'm still trying to get the adjustment right. I have very thin hair, which might be why I'm having trouble getting the correct size for my head. Unlike most wigs I've tried, these extensions aren't itchy at all. They're also much easier to brush than the taped extensions I've used before. I would definitely recommend them.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"These crayon eyeshadow sticks are interesting. It definitely takes a little getting used to, to work with them but once you get the hang of them they are a nice way to add color to your eye lids. On one side they have the color crayons and on the other side you have a little brush that helps you work in the eye color. In this set you get 6 beautiful colors in the purple scheme which is great.\", \"sampled\": \"I got this set of six crayon eyeshadow sticks, all in a purple color scheme. Each one has the color on one end and a little brush on the other. They take some getting used to, but the pigment is really rich and they blend out nicely. I can get a complete look with just one or two sticks, which is great. For only $12, I think it's a pretty good deal for the whole set.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"These cloths are the best for getting your skin soft and keeping it well exfoliated. The cloths really get your soap lathered up too, don't get them too much in the water because the soap will rinse right out. You can also use these exclusively for scrubbing your skin, if you soak in the tub you can use the cloths to roll your dead skin right off. Its gross and so satisfying. Follow up with a nice bath oil and or lotion and you are good to go.\", \"sampled\": \"These cloths are great for exfoliating and leave my skin feeling really soft. They lather up well with soap, but just be careful not to use too much water or it will rinse out the soap right away. For the best results, I've found that soaking in the tub for a bit before scrubbing really helps remove the dead skin, which is super satisfying. I'd also suggest using some lotion or bath oil afterward to lock in the moisture.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Dr. Au Anti Aging Face Oil took me a little bit by surprise to be honest. This is a very nice retinol oil that has quickly become one of my favorites. It comes in a little dropper bottle. It has a very mild and very pleasant fragrance that I really like a lot. It lingers for a bit and I wish it would linger a lot longer than it does to be honest. I can’t figure out what it smells like but I really like the mix of it. This contains retinyl palmitate, related to retinol. Remember, whenever you use retinol you want to make sure to protect your skin from the sun since retinol makes your skin more sensitive to the sun.\", \"sampled\": \"This retinol oil has become a new favorite of mine. It comes in a dropper bottle and has a very mild, pleasant fragrance. The oil itself is lightweight and absorbs pretty quickly, though it does leave my skin feeling a little oily. I also noticed it has tiny specks in it that look like gold. I prefer to use this at night, since retinol products can make your skin more sensitive to the sun. This one actually uses retinyl palmitate, which is related to retinol. When I use it, my skin feels incredibly soft and smooth and has a nice, natural-looking glow. It's definitely one of my favorite face oils now.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I ended up dying mine black because I naturally have very dark hair and I was tired of maintaining the lighter color with bleaching and all that jazz. These actually were very easy to dye and look great! I didn't glue mine in I ended up using some clip-in extension pieces and making a ponytail extension out of it. It looks great and the hair is really excellent quality.\", \"sampled\": \"My natural hair is very dark, so I dyed these extensions black for a perfect match, and they took the color really easily. The hair quality is excellent. Instead of using the glue-in method, I turned them into clip-ins to make a custom ponytail extension for myself, and it looks great. Just a heads-up, if you're planning on a full head of glue-in extensions, you'll probably need at least four sets. This hair is definitely worth it.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"If you have a large head and plan on wearing this for a long period of time it might not be comfortable. I have a normal ish size head and it pressed my earrings into my head which wasn't comfortable, but I'll just take my earrings out next time. The side that sits on your forehead has a sticky strip to keep the cap from sliding and the side that goes on the base of your skull has a stretchy band to allow for sizing. The bow is cute and so is the pattern. I use these types of caps for incubating oil and deep conditioning masks. They are great for this, keeps the \\\"juice\\\" inside and the outside dry.\", \"sampled\": \"I use this cap for my oil and deep conditioning masks, and it has some really great features. There's a sticky strip on the forehead that helps keep it from sliding down, and a stretchy band at the back to adjust the size. It does a great job of keeping the outside dry and holding in all the moisture from my hair treatments. My only complaint is that it might be uncomfortable for people with larger heads if worn for a long time. Even on my average-sized head, it pressed my earrings into my skin. I'll just have to remember to take them out before using it. I do think the bow and pattern are super cute, though.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I have always loved this oil but I haven't used it in years, I thought it had been discontinued. I was given a bottle of the lightly scented version to review. I can be sensitive to some scented products, they can give me headaches. This one is light enough to not bother me at all. Because the weather has been so warm, I add peppermint oil when I use this. It is refreshing and cooling. I am a big fan of using oils to moisturize my skin, though than can be greasy. What I like about this oil is that it absorbs quickly so I can get dressed right away if need be. I usually just towel off to slightly damp skin then use this oil and air dry.\", \"sampled\": \"I was given this lightly scented Body Oil to review. I hadn't used it in years and actually thought it was discontinued. I can be sensitive to scented products and sometimes get headaches, but this oil's scent is light enough that it doesn't bother me at all. I apply it right after a shower while my skin is still a little damp. In warm weather, I'll even add a little peppermint oil for a cooling effect. It absorbs so quickly that I can get dressed immediately, which is a huge plus. It makes my skin feel incredibly soft and smooth. I really love how my skin feels after using it and will definitely be purchasing more when my current bottle runs out.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I bought it to try out . It is a trial size so it's small and because of that it could be messy but it's expected . I like the foundation ( it has 2 tones . One is fair and another is fair2 which is a bit darker then just fair so you can match it to your skin tone ) . But then I saw foundation for sale separately it contains some chemicals which doesn't sound like it's \\\" all natural \\\" so I wonder if this sample is natural also . The eyeshadows would last long time .\", \"sampled\": \"I bought the trial size of this set and really liked the foundation. The trial size packaging itself is a bit messy to use, though. The set came with two foundation tones, a fair and a darker fair2. I did become a little concerned about the ingredients after seeing a separately sold version, which made me question if it was truly \\\"all natural.\\\" The eyeshadows, blush, and bronzer will definitely last you a long time, much longer than the foundation. Overall, I think it’s worth trying since it's not very expensive.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"My granddaughter (13) is real into nails right now and loves this set. She did her own nails and her friends nails and I will say they did a nice job. She said this was easy to use and worked as well as any other set she has tried. Colors were great and the nails were strong and easy to shape perfectly. The only real problem we had was this does have a pretty nasty smell, but we set up a fan and that took care of the fumes. She has added this set to her growing collection of nail stuff, and likes to come over every couple of weeks to open up her \\\"salon\\\" I like it cause I get to spend some extra time with my granddaughter.\", \"sampled\": \"I purchased this nail set for my 13-year-old granddaughter, and she absolutely loves it. She found it very easy to use and said it worked just as well as other sets she has tried. The colors are great, and the nails she created were strong and easy for her to shape on herself and her friends. The only issue is the very strong, unpleasant smell, so we definitely needed a fan to help with the fumes. She has now added this kit to the growing collection of nail supplies she keeps at my house. My granddaughter likes to come over every couple of weeks to open up her “salon” and do everyone's nails.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I did not receive the kit as promised and received the Hydrating Mineral Veil along with a different brush. So far I love the mineral veil. It goes on smooth and makes my face feel soft. It really does appear to hydrate my face but I have only been using this for a few days. I wish I would have received the brush that was supposed to come with the kit. I was going to return it but the seller agreed to let me keep both products and refunded my money in full including shipping.\", \"sampled\": \"I was sent the Hydrating Mineral Veil instead of the kit, but it ended up being a pleasant surprise. The mineral veil applies smoothly, hydrates my face, and leaves it feeling soft. Despite my initial intention to return it, the seller not only refunded my money in full but also let me keep both products, along with the Flawless Application Brush that was part of the package. This experience has actually made me think about buying the brush on its own. I'm impressed by the seller's generosity and the quality of the products, which may turn me into a returning customer.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This mister is awesome! I got it to use on my house plants and it works great. It holds a little of 5oz, the mist very fine, handle is easy to pull, and it's a continuous spray for few seconds per handle pump! Might consider using on my hair in the future too! Please let me know if you have any questions about my review, or if you have found it of any help to you.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tested the plant mister, and it does a great job of keeping my plants well-watered with a fine mist. Its 5oz capacity is convenient, reducing the need for frequent refills. The handle is easy to use, delivering a continuous spray with each pump, simplifying the watering process. I even found a different use for it on my hair, where the continuous spray could be beneficial for adding moisture or reviving hairstyles. Overall, I consider this mister efficient and versatile, earning my full recommendation.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This lip balm is just ok in my eyes. It does work and softens your lips. I do like the fact that it has sun protection in it since melanoma is a serious problem in today's age. I would have hoped that with the ingredients of coconut and grape seed that it would have had a flavor of either one in it, but sadly it does not. It was fairly tasteless actually. Nowadays a lot of lip balms are flavored and to be honest that is one of the criteria why I pick certain lip balms. Yes this one works and its staying power is pretty good.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tested a lip balm with sun protection, a feature I value. Despite containing coconut and grape seed, this lip balm surprisingly lacks flavor, coming across as fairly tasteless, which was a letdown. On the bright side, its staying power impresses, enduring through a few sips of water without constant reapplication. It's worth noting that while this lip balm is flavorless, others in the same line offer a grape flavor or scent, making it a bit disappointing that this one doesn't share that tasty twist. If you seek a sun-protecting lip balm with good staying power, this product is worth considering, though it may not deliver the flavor punch you desire.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"These HH&LL 4pcs Large Hair Claw Clips work great. My hair is not thick but these clips stay in my hair. I am impressed at how well they work. The pack comes with 4 large clips. The teeth of the clips go inside each other enough to hold the hair. The colors in my box are white, pink, black, and brown. I will be recommending these HH&LL 4pcs Large Hair Claw Clips to family and friends.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tested the HH&LL 4pcs Large Hair Claw Clips, and I have to say, they perform really well. These clips stayed firmly in my hair, despite my hair being on the thinner side. I was impressed by how they maintained their grip all day long. The pack contains four large clips suitable for various hair textures. The teeth of the clips securely interlock, ensuring your hair stays in place effectively.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I am happy with my order of the Resin Decorative Accessories Set. This was packaged well and arrived in great condition. What I really like is that this set of tools and inclusions comes in a multi-level box that is transparent. It is easy to see what inclusions are inside, which is very easy to use while working on a project. Pipettes are really great to remove bubbles and also to apply very small amounts of resin into detailed areas. The silicone mat is essential to protect a working surface and the tweezers are very useful to add small items into a poured resin piece. There are also extra 30ml mixing cups, tiny spoons and wooden sticks. These inclusions are great to use with other materials besides resin like nail art, decoupage glues, acrylic paint and polymer clay.\", \"sampled\": \"The Resin Decorative Accessories Set is a well-organized kit in a transparent box, making it easy to find tools and materials. It includes pipettes for precise resin application, a silicone mat for workspace protection, tweezers for delicate work, and 30ml mixing cups, tiny spoons, and wooden sticks for crafting. The kit also offers nail art supplies, decoupage glues, acrylic paint, and polymer clay, catering to various creative projects. Notably, it comes with full round hole-less beads, half beads, tiny seashells, and a tiny starfish, adding a unique flair to creations. While finger cots are included, gloves were preferred for comfort. Loose glitter in the set was user-friendly. This comprehensive kit provides a wide array of tools and materials for diverse projects. As someone planning a resin painting project with beach sand, this set promises to spark creativity for a range of endeavors.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I have been a huge fan of the relatively inexpensive, paperish, hugely saturated facial masks by companies like My Beauty Diary and others ( you can find my many positive reviews) until stumbling upon these lacey, saturated, fantastic masks. I really can't contribute more than other outstanding reviewers have elaborated on the technical advantages of these lacey versions compared to the paper ones except to say how wonderful it is to position the mask over your face and neck and then be able to gently secure it by pulling it over your ears. Genious. The paper masks were good except the chin area never stays in place and I'm always checking to make sure the rest of it hasn't slid around on my face. These cost a little more but the results were so great after my first one, I immediately purchased 3 more boxes of this version and another one to try out. I am way past 50, but my skin looks terrific. Smooth, soft, healthy and glowing. I could not be happier. Go ahead and give these a try. But you better hurry before I buy them all up.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried the lacey masks from My Beauty Diary, and I must say, I prefer them over paper masks. The lacey masks are saturated and deliver fantastic results. Securing them is easy by pulling them over your ears, and the chin area stays perfectly in place, which is a big plus for me. As someone over 50, I've been searching for products that suit my skin well. After using these masks, I bought three boxes because the results were truly terrific. My skin appears smooth, soft, healthy, and glowing after just one use. It's remarkable how a simple skincare product can make such a noticeable difference. The ability to cover both my face and neck with these masks is convenient. The lacey material feels luxurious, and the way they secure around the ears is practical and comfortable. The first time I used them, I was impressed by how my skin felt and looked. The immediate improvement was noticeable and continued with use. Overall, I highly recommend trying these lacey masks, especially if you want a skincare product that shows visible results. The ease of use, great fit, and saturated formula make them a standout choice for anyone wanting to pamper their skin. I'm genuinely pleased with the outcome and will keep using these masks in my skincare routine.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"For the price I find it hard to give this set many stars. There are two packs of nail polish, so you do get ten small bottles. However, there's like 8 colors to choose from because glitter and pink are duplicate. One teal is dried up and can't be used. Does take a long time to dry up. I wouldn't consider this a beauty item, instead, it's pretend play toy 100%. The stickers are extremely tiny and difficult to remove. When I tried to remove it from the package a couple stickers fell off of the plastic film.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tested a nail polish set for kids with two packs of ten small bottles. I noticed that there are only 8 unique colors available due to some duplicates, and one teal polish was dried up in my set. The stickers are quite small and hard to remove, with a few falling off the plastic film. While it's better suited for pretend play than beauty use, I liked that the products are odorless, ensuring safety for kids. However, the polish takes a while to dry, and I felt the $11 price tag was a bit high.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This under eye cream comes in a cute little container that looks fantastic. It has retiniol in it that really is great for your skin as well as hyaluronic acid, both that should help with wrinkles. Just a reminder, products with retinol make you more sensitive to sun light so make sure you use sun blockers when using these kinds of products. The cream is lightweight and absorbs quickly into the skin. It leaves no residue behind and your skin just feels soft and smooth. The cream has a lovely fragrance that I really like. All in all this is a great find.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried an under eye cream with retinol and hyaluronic acid in a charming container. The mix of these ingredients effectively targets wrinkles, leaving my skin feeling velvety. I like that it's light, absorbs quickly, and doesn't leave any residue. It's important to note that retinol can heighten sun sensitivity, so sun protection is a must. Despite that, I adore the pleasant scent and the overall effect on my skin. This cream has earned a permanent place in my skincare routine for its results and enjoyable application. It has truly transformed my skincare routine. The blend of retinol and hyaluronic acid, combined with its lightweight feel and delightful aroma, sets it apart. I wholeheartedly recommend it to those seeking to combat wrinkles and achieve smoother, softer skin.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This toner is not inexpensive, but you do get a larger bottle when you receive this toner that is almost $40. The box that it comes in is rather beautiful and the bottle itself is also very classy looking. The bottle itself is a pump dispenser. I usually use a cotton pad when I apply a toner but for this one I just used my fingers instead. The toner is a liquid formula that is on the thicker side with a hint of gel in it almost. This toner contains polyphenols which are actually micro nutrients and good for you, I know it sounds bad so I had to look it up. It also contains sea buckthorn fruit oil that helps brighten the skin complexion and recover from skin fatigue. It contains lily flower extract that helps moisturize the skin and nourish the skin and cherry on top, it contains no parabens. So now when you use it, you notice it has an interesting consistency, somewhat between watery and gel like and it bubbles when you dispense it. It has a very pleasant scent that I really like a lot and this fragrance lingers quite a bit which I am all in favor of. I feel that it nicely moisturized my skin. It didn’t irritate my skin or anything and worked nicely for me. All in all I am very happy with this toner.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tested a toner that comes in a larger bottle priced at almost $40. The bottle has a convenient pump dispenser, which makes it easy to use daily. The toner has a unique liquid formula that leans towards the thicker side, almost like a gel, and includes beneficial ingredients like polyphenols, sea buckthorn fruit oil, and lily flower extract, while being free of parabens. The consistency of this toner is quite interesting, falling somewhere between watery and gel-like. When applying it, I noticed a pleasant lingering scent that added a touch of luxury to my skincare routine. What impressed me the most was how well it moisturized my skin without causing any irritation. The toner left my skin feeling hydrated and refreshed, making it a great addition to my daily skincare regimen. Overall, I found this toner to be a worthwhile investment despite its slightly higher price point; the quality and performance justify the cost. If you're looking for a toner that not only hydrates but also nourishes the skin with beneficial ingredients like polyphenols and sea buckthorn fruit oil, this product is definitely worth considering, especially with the absence of parabens for those conscious about skincare ingredients.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I'm always interested in trying new skincare products, brands, and innovations. Mizon is a well regarded South Korean skincare brand. Vitamin C serum loses efficacy when it is exposed to oxygen, Mizun uses Arbutin to lengthen the efficacy of the serum. Topical Vitamin C sometimes irritates my skin but I had no sensitivity to this. It did go on easily with no greasiness or tacky feel. It does feel like it tightens my skin and gives it a more even, brighter tone.\", \"sampled\": \"I tested the Mizon Vitamin C serum and found its gentle yet effective formula impressive. Being a fan of K-beauty brands, I was pleased that Mizon, a well-known South Korean skincare brand, developed this serum. The addition of Vitamin C in the serum is a wise choice, especially considering the potential loss of efficacy in Vitamin C serums exposed to oxygen. I was particularly intrigued by Mizon's use of Arbutin to enhance the serum's effectiveness, showing innovation in skincare.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This is a very nice sheet face mask. When you open the package the mask is loaded with the mask which is nice, but it is not dripping which you don’t want. You notice the fantastic fragrance of the mask, very strong of honey which I love. It is very smooth and smells incredible in my eyes. The material of the sheet is very nice and soft. I found it is a bit big however so it does take some adjustment to be honest to make it fit. I do prefer that over a mask being too small however. Make sure to clean your face prior to using it, just like the instructions say. You leave it on for 10 to 20 minutes and then remove it. Any residual serum is just gently massaged into the skin. I found that my skin loves this mask. It feels nice and soft, smooth and just looks nicely moisturized. It has a natural glow to it that says I did something good for it. It does feel a bit sticky for about 5 minutes right after, but that goes away and then you are left with soft and smooth skin. In this box, that btw is perfect as a gift as well, you get 10 masks for just $24, which I think is a very good price and allows you to do something good for your skin for some time.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried out this face mask, product ID B07V1QTSQ1, and I have to say, it has a pretty strong honey scent that's hard to miss. The mask itself is smooth and doesn't drip, which is a real bonus. The only downside is that the sheet material, while soft, is a tad too big. I had to adjust it a bit to get it to fit my face properly. Before you use the mask, make sure your face is clean. You're supposed to leave it on for about 10 to 20 minutes. After taking it off, there's usually some leftover serum that you can massage into your skin. I found that this left my skin feeling soft, smooth, and nicely moisturized. The only hitch is a slight stickiness that hangs around for about 5 minutes after using the mask. The package comes with 10 masks, so it's great for gifting. And at $24, it's a pretty affordable way to treat yourself to some skincare luxury. Despite the minor issues with the fit and the brief stickiness after use, I've had a mostly positive experience with this face mask. I'd definitely consider buying it again.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"These do make affordably unique party favor gifts! The holographic print diamond ring designs are pretty, and I think the featured wording is memorable and cute! I will say that these hair coils are slightly larger than several others I’ve tried, so they don’t seem to be as tight and stretchy as the ones I’m generally used to, but they do make a nice “ring” design for the card, and the iridescent shade of purple is really attractive. Since they all come squashed together in a plastic bag with a slide zipper, they do move around and bend a lot. Because of that, 3 of the 7 decorative backing cards I received had obvious crinkles and bends across one bottom corner. They did flatten back out for the most part, but I still wish I’d received an entire set of pristine cards that were undamaged. They’d make great gifts for bachelorette parties or bridal shower events!\", \"sampled\": \"I recently picked up the Pearlescent Purple Pretties for a bachelorette party I was throwing. They were a total hit! The holographic diamond ring designs really popped and the iridescent purple of the hair coils was even more vibrant than I expected, which gave a fun twist to the party favors. However, I did find that the hair coils weren't as stretchy or tight as some I've used in the past, which could be an issue for some folks. The packaging was pretty handy - a plastic bag with a slide zipper - but I was a bit disappointed to find that 3 of the 7 backing cards were crinkled and bent. Despite these minor hiccups, I'd still say these are a fantastic choice for any bachelorette party or bridal shower.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I really loved the idea of this Boiess Baby Cologne \\\"Les Enfants\\\" in White Blossom, and thought it would be perfect for myself since I don't like loud fragrances. The description of the scent profile really enticed me, as I love all of the notes. I was able to try both the White Blossom and the Lavanda scents. Both of the \\\"Cologne\\\" sprays smell identical to me. I can't say that I can tell them apart at all. They both have a very strong earthy, herbal, slightly floral scent that is heavily ladened with alcohol upon spraying. It stays very strong for several minutes, but then starts to dry down. Sadly, once it dries down, the scent pretty much dissipates. If I don't spray it heavily at first, which is too much for my asthma to handle, the scent just goes away completely. I must spray it on me again. Once I spray it on a few times over an hour or so, I can smell the ever so slightest hint of it on my clothing later, if I put my nose right on it. That is a lot of spraying for very little payoff. I know this is meant for kids mostly, but I would think the scent would and should last longer. Sure, my grandsons will like using it, maybe, as it is quite strong at first.\", \"sampled\": \"I was really looking forward to trying out Boiess Baby Cologne's \\\"Les Enfants\\\" in the White Blossom scent. I've always been drawn to more subtle fragrances, so I thought this would be perfect for me. I also decided to give the Lavanda scent a whirl. However, I was surprised to find that, to my nose, both scents smelled exactly the same. They started out quite strong, but I was disappointed to find that the fragrance faded rather quickly. I found myself having to reapply the cologne multiple times throughout the day just to keep the scent alive. I get that colognes don't last as long as perfumes, but the speed at which the scent disappeared was a bit of a bummer. Plus, as someone with mild asthma, the frequent reapplication was not only a hassle but also a bit of a health concern. The price tag also caught me off guard - at $28, it's pretty steep, especially given how quickly the scent fades. I mean, it's a lot to shell out for a cologne that you can't smell after half an hour. I think my grandsons might get a kick out of it, but I'm not sure I'd buy it again for myself. The high cost and the short-lived scent are pretty big downsides for me. So, my experience with Boiess Baby Cologne was a bit of a mixed bag.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I love castor oil and use a lot of it, but rarely for my hair. I've never seen or used castor oil \\\"butter\\\" before and I think it's very interesting. All the oils in this product are normally liquid at room temperature so I'm not sure how they got this to have a butter-like consistency. Maybe some beeswax or something was added but it's not on the list of ingredients. I really like the tea tree oil scent this castor oil butter has as tea tree is one of my favorite scents. I think this is a very good castor oil product.\", \"sampled\": \"As a frequent user of castor oil, I was genuinely surprised when I stumbled upon castor oil butter. I mean, all the oils I've used, castor oil included, are usually liquid at room temperature. So, seeing this product with a butter-like consistency was definitely a novelty. I was left scratching my head, trying to figure out how it managed to maintain this texture. I even wondered if beeswax might have been added, but that's purely speculation. One thing that wasn't a mystery though was the pleasant tea tree oil scent it carried. I'm a big fan of tea tree, so this was a delightful surprise.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"A friendly word of warning: don’t use Beast Blue Cooling Body Wash down south unless you like tingly body parts. The consistency of the body wash is a little watery, so it’s easy to squeeze out more than you might want. It lathers well and rinses clean without leaving behind a residue. I really like the smell of the body wash. It’s a strong, crisp scent that really does wake you up. My nose tingled the first time I smelled it. The scent is hard to describe. I can’t pick out any individual notes, just a crisp smell that made me go “Wow, this smells cool and different.”\", \"sampled\": \"The Beast Blue Cooling Body Wash is a pretty unique find. Despite its somewhat watery consistency, which may not appeal to everyone, it lathers up nicely. What really sets this body wash apart is its potent, crisp scent. It's tough to pin down, but \\\"cool and different\\\" seems to capture it best. Another plus is that it rinses off easily without leaving any residue behind. However, it's worth noting that it can cause a bit of a tingling sensation on certain areas of the body, which might not sit well with everyone. That said, it still holds a solid 4.0 rating, indicating that many people are happy with their purchase. One reviewer summed it up perfectly: \\\"Wow, this smells cool and different.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"These are about the strength I need. My feed are usually pretty calluse free but come summer they get a bit beat up. I have used the more expensive well known brand and it was almost too effective and left my feet tender. If you just need a bit of a touch up these are great, if you want the full she-bang of peeling layers and layers, get the $20 a pair brand.\", \"sampled\": \"I've been using the B0936KRBP2 for minor foot touch-ups and it's been pretty decent. It doesn't leave my feet feeling tender, which is a huge plus. However, it doesn't quite pack the punch of the $20 brands. If you're looking to peel multiple layers of skin, this might not be your best bet. It's obvious that it doesn't quite measure up to the more expensive options out there.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Noisy, can't really heat the water, barely keeps it warm. But the absolute worst part of this foot bath are the rollers that are supposed to massage your feet. These things are HARD plastic and actually are sharp in places. I tried them for a few seconds then tore them out and throw them away. They HURT badly. Who could design such a thing? the catches the rollers mounted in are not much better as they stand, sharp and hard, into your feet. I guess you could cut those out, maybe, but this thing isn't worth the time or effort. Look elsewhere for your foot care needs this painful thing isn't the one.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently purchased a foot bath and, to be honest, it's been a letdown. The noise it generates is pretty loud, which isn't ideal when you're trying to unwind with a soothing soak. What's more, it doesn't do a great job of heating the water - it barely manages to keep it lukewarm. I was really hoping for a comforting, warm foot bath, but this product just didn't deliver. The rollers, in particular, were a major issue for me. They're made of hard plastic and some parts are actually sharp. They were uncomfortable to the point of causing me pain. Even the fixtures that hold the rollers in place were sharp and hard.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I received a sample of this in my IPSY bag, fell in love, then began the hunt for the right shades. This is the best lip stain/ long wear lippie I've ever used. Great pigmentation, smoking the, feels like nothing when wearing, the only truly non-drying lip stain or extended wear lipstick I've ever used. It really does last all day. If going for full coverage make sure to give it a solid 5 minutes to set. The hardest part is finding just the right shade. I ended up scouring YouTube for swatches. REALLY love and recommend.\", \"sampled\": \"I first discovered this lip stain, B07FVM7D1R, in an IPSY bag sample. Right off the bat, I was impressed by its fantastic pigmentation. The stain is so lightweight, it feels like you're not wearing anything on your lips at all. It's a pleasure to put on and doesn't leave your lips feeling parched. Sure, it takes about 5 minutes to set for optimal coverage, but trust me, it's worth the patience. The only snag I hit was picking the right shade, which can be a bit of a challenge. I ended up turning to YouTube for swatches of the product, which was a huge help. I'm absolutely smitten with this long-lasting lipstick and can't recommend it enough.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I've been using this since I bought it in December 2020 and I still love it. It is such a space saver compared to my high wattage UV-only lamp, and I don't have to worry about busting those UV tube-like bulbs. I really like how this LED lamp doesn't heat up and fry my skin and works faster than the UV. Just wish I could figure out how to get the count-UP timer to work again when I put my hand in without pushing a timer button.\", \"sampled\": \"I started using this LED lamp back in December 2020 and it's been a game changer. It's way more compact than my old UV-only lamp, which was a bulky, high-wattage beast. I love that the LED lamp doesn't require those tube-like UV bulbs - that's a definite plus. The best part? It doesn't overheat and scorch my skin like the UV lamp did. Surprisingly, it even works faster, which was a pleasant surprise. But it's not perfect - I've been having some issues with the count-UP timer feature, which is a bit of a letdown.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I honestly feel that Shea Moisture Hemp Seed Oil Length Conditioner and its matching shampoo have definitely improved my hair growth and thickness following Chemotherapy. My hair has always been slow growing, so a few inches after a year is fast for me though YMMV. I find the conditioner is nourishing, without being too heavy or allowing fly-aways. Less is more with this conditioner as well, as a bottle lasted me a year, but I was bald to start; again, YMMV. This three-pack was definitely an excellent value for the money, especially with Prime shipping.\", \"sampled\": \"The Shea Moisture Hemp Seed Oil Length Conditioner has been a total game-changer for my hair, especially after going through chemotherapy. I've noticed a significant improvement in both hair growth and thickness, which is pretty amazing. The conditioner is nourishing, but it doesn't leave your hair feeling heavy, a balance that's hard to find in hair products. And you don't need to use a ton of it to see results - a little really does go a long way with this one. Surprisingly, one bottle lasted me an entire year. Considering it comes in a three-pack, that's some serious bang for your buck. Plus, it's available with Prime shipping, which is always a bonus.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Disappointed in this product because it did not work for me. I took a couple pictures showing and comparing my eyelashes before and after applying. The mascara was a little pastey but step 1 applied ok and looked fine, but step 2 completely ruined my eyelashes. It looked horrible; The left side got clumped together and the right side looked like my eyelashes were growing baby eyelashes. Very unnatural looking. This claims to be flake free but after it dried i noticed a few little black specks on my cheeks. I had to use one of my old dried up mascara brushes to separate my lashes to fix them. It's also very hard to wash off.\", \"sampled\": \"I gave B081M5R5L5 a 1.0 because it just didn’t work for me. I even took before-and-after eyelash pictures so I could see what was really going on. The mascara felt a bit paste-like, although step 1 went on okay and looked fine at first. Step 2 is where it fell apart and made my lashes look awful. My left lashes clumped together, and my right lashes looked uneven and unnatural, not just a little messy. Once it dried, I noticed tiny black specks on my cheeks, even though it claims to be flake free. I ended up using an old dried mascara brush to separate and fix my lashes, and it was hard to wash off too.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This is a nice set. It contains a beautiful compact mirror with regular and close-up mirrors on the inside. Both mirrors are small but functional. It is fashioned like a pocket watch. The clasp is solid and the finish is high-gloss like the interior mirror itself. The saying (see below) on the outside of both sides of the mirror is inspirational. It says \\\"Beautiful girl you can do amazing things.\\\" There is also a small dish for jewelry and a very cheap make-up case all with the same inspirational verse. In summary, this is a nice gift set. The current price of $20.89 ($21.99 minus 5%) is competitive.\", \"sampled\": \"This gift set feels like about a 4.0 to me. It comes with a compact mirror, a jewelry dish, and a make-up case, and the mirror is definitely the best piece. It’s designed like a pocket watch, with one regular mirror and one close-up mirror inside, both small but still usable. The verse, “Beautiful girl you can do amazing things.”, is printed on both exterior sides of the mirror, and the same inspirational message shows up across the other items too. The clasp on the compact feels solid, and the high-gloss finish makes it look nicer than the rest of the set.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I do a lot of crafts -- especially paper crafts -- and have several versions of similar boxes filled with small flowers, embellishments, etc. This one stands apart from the rest. It's VERY durable -- the bottom is \\\"frosted,\\\" the top is clear; the lid fits tightly onto the bottom and also sits flush against the compartments without any gaps at all; and the clasp is tight fitting and solid. I love the adjustable aspect of this. The \\\"row height\\\" is fixed at about 1 1/8\\\", but the \\\"cell width\\\" is totally adjustable by moving the plastic tabs around. The tabs move easily and seat securely, making reconfiguring a piece of cake. All of this versatility combines to make this incredibly useful for paper crafters. I've loaded mine with a collection of sequins, brads, eyelets, and flat-backed \\\"gemstones.\\\" I've shaken it and dropped it, and not a single sequin is out of its compartment. That's a precision fit! Very pleasantly surprised by this item -- I was NOT expecting the level of quality here. I'm probably going to be purchasing several of these to replace some of my existing boxes -- they're that good! (Sample provided for review; opinions expressed are my own based on my experience with this item.)\", \"sampled\": \"I use a lot of craft supplies, especially for paper crafts, and B00QYJ84GI ended up working better than similar storage boxes I already have. The bottom is frosted and the top is clear, so I could see what was inside without the box feeling flimsy or cheap. It felt very durable in hand. The lid fit tightly and lined up well with the internal compartments, so I didn’t feel like tiny pieces were going to slip out or get exposed. The clasp also felt secure and sturdy. I liked the adjustable compartment widths more than I expected. The plastic tabs were easy to move, stayed firmly in place, and worked around the fixed 1 1/8\\\" row height without much fuss, which helped for small craft items. I stored sequins, brads, eyelets, and flat-backed gemstones in it. After shaking and dropping the box, 0 sequins left their compartments, so I may buy several more to replace older boxes. I received a sample for review, and these opinions are based on my personal experience.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The description says shaving brush and that's why I bought it. But the brush box says its a Beard Brush. So, I still tried using it as a shaving brush. Big mistake. It was like using a pot & pan scrub brush. I couldn't raise up a lather it's so stiff. But it did a nice job exfoliating my face. Badger? Don't think so. I have many real badger brushes. This appears to be horse or boar. There might be some goat mixed in, who knows for sure?\", \"sampled\": \"I bought B07LBSLFF8 because the description made it sound like a shaving brush, and this was a verified purchase. When it arrived, the box called it a Beard Brush, which is a pretty important difference. I tried using it for shaving anyway, but it was much too stiff for building lather. It felt more like something you’d scrub pots and pans with. For exfoliating, sure. For lathering, not really. I also doubt it’s real badger hair; the bristles seemed more like horse or boar, maybe with some goat hair mixed in. A 3.0 feels fair to me, with 0 helpful votes noted.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I love Leap bar soaps (LOVE!), and was curious about their liquid hand soap. It is a 12 ounce pump style bottle with visually pleasing label. The soap comes out thick and rich. It is super luxurious feeling- it lathers significantly all the while feeling like hand cream. It rinses well and leaves skin feeling soft. Probably the most soft. Perhaps a little coating of glycerin and aloe are left behind? Yet not a gross coating. I am trying to figure out the magic of my soft, smooth, clean feeling hands on a super cold (18*F), dry day. Super moisturizing. Oddly, I find it has barely any scent. I am used to their super scented bar soaps, and I was expecting a stronger scent to come through. I am not usually keen on perfume (causes headaches, nausea), but when a product uses essential oils for scenting (especially hand soap and house cleaners), I find it delightfully uplifting. This is labeled as lemongrass, but that isn't the only essential oil used. The profile is more complex with the addition of lemon oil, orange oil, and rosemary. I was really excited for this to have a robust citrus smell. I really couldn't tell you just by sniffing. It smells fresh and clean, but it isn't strong (and I was hoping/expecting strong). I see other reviewers mentioning a strong smell. I figured I got a weird bottle or I have covid. I actually went and grabbed my Leap bar soap to make sure I could still smell that. I can. Phew.\", \"sampled\": \"I already liked Leap bar soaps, so I was curious to try this liquid hand soap. The 12 ounce pump bottle looks nice, and I like the bottle design and label. The pump works well, and the soap comes out thick and rich, with a substantial feel. I was expecting a stronger citrus scent from the lemongrass, but it’s actually pretty mild. The lather is the highlight for me. It feels luxurious, almost cream-like, but it still rinses off easily. I didn’t feel like I had to work to get it off my hands, which matters to me because some thicker soaps leave too much behind. My hands felt clean afterward, and also very soft and smooth. That was the best part. I only needed a small amount for a good wash. The scent is where I felt a little let down. It includes lemongrass, lemon oil, orange oil, and rosemary, so I expected more brightness. The actual scent seemed very mild, which isn’t bad, just not what I expected. I used it on a dry, cold day at 18*F, so the soft feel really stood out. The ingredients list includes Organic Coconut Oil, Organic Olive Oil, Vegetable Glycerin, Organic Aloe Vera, Organic Jojoba Oil, Organic Guar Gum, Rosemary Extract, and Potassium Hydroxide. Potassium Hydroxide is used for saponification and is stated as absent from the final product. For me, B08KNVV18L is a good liquid hand soap because it washes well, rinses easily, and leaves my hands feeling clean, soft, and smooth, even if the scent is quieter than I expected.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Right now, I am loving this combo straightener and curling tool. It heats up to 430 degrees, but it is in Celsius, so look at the conversion for your hair. It heats up very fast. I straightened a piece of hair and then curled it. It did a good job. I am tempted to do my entire head.\", \"sampled\": \"I’m really pleased with this B07K153R2B combo straightener and curling tool. My purchase wasn’t marked verified, and I rated it 5.0 with 0 helpful votes. The temperature reads in Celsius, and it can go up to 430 degrees, so I’d definitely check the conversion before using it on your hair. It heats up quickly.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The lavender bath bomb and rose-scented bath bomb and soap bar all feature light, feminine scents that pair nicely with the included candle! The stainless steel cup seems well-constructed and nicely sized, and the various tools and bottle openers are an appreciated bonus. This boxed set is ready for gifting and even comes with a blank card that can be easily personalized to some lucky recipient. I think this grouped assortment offers a nice variety of different and relaxing products that most customers would enjoy!\", \"sampled\": \"I got this B09GVHT2D3 set and was really happy with it overall. The lavender bath bomb, rose-scented bath bomb, and soap bar all have soft, feminine scents that pair nicely with the included candle. The stainless steel cup looks sturdy and is a good size, and the tools and bottle openers are a nice extra rather than something that feels tossed in. It comes across as a solid gift set, especially with the blank card to personalize it, and the mix of items gives a relaxing variety that most people would probably enjoy.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Okay, I'm getting old. So, yeah, my eyebrows are getting bushier and I have to trim the nostrils. I also have a beard, mustache and sideburns that need regular grooming between visits to the barber. I've tried other kits in the past. They either don't have the attachments to do the job, or just don't work well enough, period. This kit does it all and does it well. It's a regular traveling companion now, always in my toiletries bag. I'm very happy with this purchase, and highly recommend it.\", \"sampled\": \"Verified purchase. I use B07J4Z1XNP between barber visits, and it’s been really useful. It handles my eyebrows and nostril hair, along with my beard, mustache, and sideburns, so it covers my regular grooming needs. I’ve tried other grooming kits before, but they either didn’t have the right attachments or just didn’t work well. This one does what I need it to do, and I keep it in my toiletries bag when I travel now. I’m very satisfied with it and would recommend it.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This is a very nice, well made scrunchie, but what I do not understand is, why one scrunchie is $17? I have gotten a 52 pack for $8 before and they are all very nice scrunchies. Yes I understand that the silk does not damage the hair and therefore doesn't cause split ends, but at this price it better blow dry my hair for me while I am still sleeping. It is beautiful, the color is a very nice deep emerald green, it is very well made, the silk has no pulled threads or anything, but I don't think I would be willing to spend that kind of money on just one. If this was a 5 pack or so I would be ok with it even for silk.\", \"sampled\": \"I’d give B07YDLYYP3 a 3.0 overall. The scrunchie is pretty and seems well made, with a deep emerald green color that looks nice, and I didn’t see any pulled threads in the silk. I get why people like silk scrunchies since they can be gentler on hair and may help with split ends, but the price is hard for me to justify. It’s $17 for one scrunchie, and I’ve bought a 52 pack for $8 before, so paying that much for just one doesn’t feel worth it to me. I’d be more open to it if it were closer to a 5 pack.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This hair finishing stick works quite well. It comes in a similar tube as your eye mascara does and also has a similar wand to it. The gel is clear and very lightweight. It feels almost like water when you have it on your hair really. It is not sticky or greasy really. I was surprised that it works quite well and the staying power is pretty good as well. It is fairly cheap to so not a big investment there and definitely reasonable. I am not sure how big of an issue it really is though to get something like it.\", \"sampled\": \"This hair finishing stick works well for smoothing flyaways, and the tube immediately reminded me of mascara packaging. The wand has that same mascara-like feel too. The clear gel is lightweight and a little watery on my hair, with almost no scent. I also appreciate that it doesn’t feel sticky or greasy, which is important for this kind of product. The hold was fairly good for me, and the price seems inexpensive and reasonable for what it does. I’d probably save it for a big event rather than use it every day, since I don’t usually need a finishing stick very often. The product identifier I saw was B0832Z7KFJ, and it showed 0 helpful votes.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This was a very pretty hair comb. The rhinestones looked very nice. It really sparkled. It would be good for a wedding or for another event. It was nicely packaged. It fit in well in my hair. It stayed okay. I like the vintage look. I think it really added to it. You can also wear it for other events than a wedding. It really can compliment the outfit. I thought it seemed durable. I thought the price was fair. I would recommend it.\", \"sampled\": \"I bought this rhinestone hair comb and I love it. The rhinestones sparkle. They catch light beautifully. I think it would be perfect for weddings and similar events. It fits well. The comb stayed in place in my hair. Feels sturdy and the packaging was nice. I really liked the vintage look it has. It complemented my outfit in a way I did not expect. The price felt fair. Build seems durable. I would recommend this.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Plastic and/or acrylic organizers come in all sizes, shapes, configurations and qualities. MelodySusie® has blown me away with this three part, multiple sectioned organizer with drawers. Just so you're clear, you get a base with four drawers, each with an flattened oval shaped pull, each lined with a black textile that can be removed or used as you choose. The edges of the drawers are beveled, giving this an aura of elegance, but most importantly, each drawer slides in and out with ease, regardless of whether or not the drawer is packed or empty. The bottom of the base has a little strip in the form which raises this organizer from sitting directly on the counter, so if it is used in a bathroom, air can circulate around the entire piece and grungy mildew and such is reduced from forming. Don't forget to dust, though. The exterior edges are beveled, giving them a very finished appearance, unlike those sharp edges on many similar products in the marketplace. Included with this organizer are two additional pieces, each is the same dimension as the interior of the top ridge of the chest organizer, so you can decide which one you want to put on top and which one you want to put next to the main component. Or you can separate them entirely and use the top of the organizer to put items and use the two multi-sectioned components separately. You could line them up side by side along a dresser top and have more width rather than extended height. I love the assorted sizes of the sections and the angles top of the shorter of the two pieces. I also love the rounded design in the sections of the taller component. Every aspect of this trio is outstanding, high quality, and it makes quite the impression in addition to comfortably storing whatever you like in each compartment. Each edge is finished, every detail has been considered, and it is even practical in that it can quickly be cleaned with no harsh detergents. This lovely item was made in China and it is clear that quality control is important to the manufacturer because I saw no defect even after intense examination. Be prepared for a weightier set than you might expect, because there was no skimping on the materials. It is perfectly packed for protection and is ideal for presenting as a gift. It is well worth its price point. A sample was provided for review.\", \"sampled\": \"I received a sample of this MelodySusie organizer for review purposes and I want to be upfront about that. The set is a 3-part unit and the main base holds 4 drawers, each fitted with a flattened oval-shaped pull and a removable black textile lining. What struck me first was the weight of it, which is noticeably heavier than I expected, suggesting MelodySusie did not skimp on materials. The drawer edges are beveled and every drawer slides in and out with ease whether it is packed full or completely empty. That kind of smooth action matters day to day when you are reaching for something quickly. The base also has a raised strip on the bottom that allows air circulation and helps reduce mildew formation. The two additional pieces that come with the set each match the interior dimension of the top ridge of the base, so they sit securely when stacked. You can also place them beside the main component or use them separately, which gives you real flexibility depending on your dresser or bathroom counter space. One of the two pieces has an angled top while the taller one features a rounded design in its sections. The whole set is made in China and I went in expecting to find at least one small flaw, but nothing turned up. The organizer can be cleaned without harsh detergents which is a practical detail I appreciate for a bathroom surface. It arrived well-packaged and I think it would hold up well as a gift. The 4 drawers in the base handle everyday items without crowding. My one mild complaint is that the set takes up more space than the photos suggest. Still, the build quality here is genuine. It earns the praise it gets.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Let me begin this review by stating that I have been using Egyptian, Indian and Pakistani 100% henna for over 30 years with beautiful results that lasted a full 3 months. I decided to try this product because I wanted a change from all the red highlights and thought it would be nice to tone down to a browner hue. I not only followed the instructions to the letter, but had several exchanges with the seller to make sure what I was doing was correct. The good news is that this combination beautifully toned down the red from all my prior years of using henna only. The bad news is that NOT ONE gray hair (I'm dark brown with about 15% gray) gave up being gray! So in addition to the hassle, mess and expense of doing this treatment (and make no mistake - it's messy and time consuming) I will have to double up this month and do my regular henna only routine. I did a bit of research, by the way, and learned that most henna/indigo distributors, unlike Harvest Moon, suggest mixing up the henna and indigo powders SEPARATELY and then combining. Based on my experience, I'd have to say this would probably work better (the rationale probably being the same as why combining shampoo and conditioner together don't work as well as shampooing and THEN conditioning). Likewise, everyone else except Harvest Moon recommends wrapping the hair in plastic wrap and then covering with a shower cap and leaving on for a MINIMUM of 2 hours (Harvest Moon says their product works in less than one hour and is against wrapping.) Leaves me to wonder if the gray hairs would not be gray had I followed the advice of the other henna/indigo distributors. Here's a good reference website: [...]\", \"sampled\": \"I have been using 100% henna for over 30 years and my results with Egyptian henna, Indian henna, and Pakistani henna have always lasted a full 3 months, so I felt confident trying this Harvest Moon product. My hair is dark brown with roughly 15% gray, which is not an extreme case and I genuinely hoped this would handle coverage without a complicated process. I followed the instructions precisely and even exchanged messages with the seller to make sure I was doing everything right. The product did tone down the red highlights left over from my prior henna use, so it was not a complete failure on every front. It covered absolutely none of my gray hair which was the whole reason I bought it. Most henna and indigo distributors I have used recommend mixing the henna and indigo powders separately before combining them, then wrapping hair in plastic wrap followed by a shower cap for a minimum of 2 hours. Harvest Moon takes a different approach, claiming the product works in less than one hour and advising against wrapping hair at all which I found strange given what every other distributor recommends. The treatment was still messy and time consuming despite the shorter leave-on window, so it is not like the process was easier to justify the trade-off. Because the gray coverage failed entirely, I ended up planning a separate regular henna-only treatment the same month which defeated the purpose of switching products in the first place.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I am very pleased with these. The bristles feel satisfying and do a really good job cleaning my teeth. The colors are handy to keep track of which toothbrush is yours. I was not expecting to like bamboo toothbrushes so much - I thought I would make a sacrifice for the environment, but I actually like them better than the expensive plastic ones we used in the past. I hope they keep making them exactly like this.\", \"sampled\": \"I honestly did not expect to like these bamboo toothbrushes before trying them. I had been using expensive plastic ones and assumed switching meant giving up quality for the sake of the environment. The bristles feel satisfying and actually clean my teeth effectively. The different colors are a practical touch that help everyone in the house tell their toothbrush apart, which I did not anticipate mattering as much as it does. I prefer these over the plastic ones I used before and hope they keep making them the same way.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I love how easily and quickly Tinazana's Pineapple Deep Cleanser becomes a creamy lather that feels really nice on my face. A very small amount of the cleanser does a great job of deeply cleaning pores and removing dead skin cells. My face feels fresh and clean after cleansing. But it does dry out my skin a bit. Because I follow up using toner and moisturizer, this isn't a huge deal for me and doesn't prevent me from continuing to use Tinazana cleanser. There is a light, faint, fresh scent that I enjoy. Surprisingly, it doesn't smell like pineapple to me. It also doesn't last; when I rinse the cleanser off, I can no longer detect the scent at all. Due to skin sensitivity issues, I sometimes have issues with citrus based products. I know pineapple technically isn't a citrus fruit but I was a bit concerned it might be too harsh on my skin. Its not. No burning or itching at all. I like this cleanser a lot. Does a great job, even with drying my skin a bit. The price is reasonable. This is a cleanser I will continue to use and I recommend it. My overall rating is 4.5 stars.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up the Tinazana Pineapple Deep Cleanser half expecting it to irritate my skin right away, since I have a real sensitivity to citrus based products and pineapple gave me pause. It lathers into a creamy texture quickly and easily, which I appreciated from the first use. A small amount goes a long way and does a solid job deep cleaning pores and removing dead skin cells. The scent is light and faint and fresh, it does not actually smell like pineapple at all. That surprised me, but honestly it was a relief given my concerns about citrus. After rinsing, the scent disappears completely, so there is nothing lingering on the skin. I experienced no burning or itching whatsoever. I do follow up with toner and moisturizer because the cleanser does cause some dryness. The dryness is my main complaint and I want to be clear that it is real. If you skip the follow up steps, your skin will feel tight and stripped afterward. That said, pairing it with a good moisturizer keeps things manageable. The price feels reasonable for what you get. The cleanser works well for pore cleaning and holds up fine for sensitive skin. I was genuinely relieved it caused no irritation. It does its job. I plan to keep using it and would recommend it to others. My personal rating for this one is 4.5 stars.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I am highly allergic to formaldehyde, it cause me to be dizzy. Have headaches, watering eyes. Sore throat. I had all these things after using this hair condition one time. I read the remarks, and another person asked Amazon if there was formaldehyde in it, and they referred this person to the manufacturer. Located in Hong Kong, they gave the person the run around on the answer. And never did admit it. It is illegal to put it into anything here in the US. I was exposed to it through 2 different sets of sheets and one face cream. And now this hair conditioner. Ive contacted the manufacturer, but I doubt I'll hear from them!!\", \"sampled\": \"I used this conditioner exactly once and had dizziness, headaches, watering eyes, and a sore throat almost immediately. I believe it contains formaldehyde. I am highly allergic to it, having been exposed before through 2 different sets of sheets and 1 face cream. Another customer asked Amazon directly whether this product contained formaldehyde and Amazon just referred them to the manufacturer in Hong Kong, who never gave a straight answer. I contacted the manufacturer myself but doubt I will hear back. It is illegal to include formaldehyde in products sold in the US, and I think that matters here. This is a verified purchase and I would not recommend product B07Y1V8PDL to anyone, especially anyone with chemical sensitivities.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This retina serum, more like a cream, is very gentle so if this isn’t something you normally use due to redness or sensitivity this might be a good product for you. Results will be much slower but you won’t have peeling skin or dryness. This is also hydrating so it’ss good mix of ingredients to make it perfect for sensitive skin. It works well but I’m not noticing any brightness to my skin.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up this retinol serum expecting something lightweight and watery but the texture is much closer to a cream which surprised me at first though it does feel hydrating and never caused any peeling or dryness on my skin. The ingredient mix seems genuinely well thought out for sensitive skin and I did not experience any redness or irritation. Results come slower than with stronger retinol products and I honestly noticed no improvement in brightness after using it. For anyone prone to sensitivity it is a solid gentle option but I gave it 4 out of 5 stars because the results feel limited.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I wear headbands like this in the evening when washing my face and following my skincare routine. These headbands are very soft. I found these to be a little small, but they do stretch enough to accommodate my large head. I have just had other headbands that were a little larger and didn't require being stretched to the max just to fit. I like the variety of colors and have shared these with my eighteen year old daughter. One thing that I do like is that the bow doesn't get off centered as easily as my old one. My old one has to be adjusted every time I put it on. This one seems to be just fine.\", \"sampled\": \"These headbands mainly for my evening face washing and skincare routine and I've been sharing them with my eighteen-year-old daughter too. They are very soft against the skin. My one complaint is that they run a little small and I do have to stretch them close to the maximum to fit my larger head. Other headbands I've used before were bigger and never needed that much stretching to stay comfortable. On the positive side, the bow stays centered far better than my old headband which needed adjustment every single time I put it on. That improvement alone made a real difference for me during daily use. They also come in a variety of colors which is a nice bonus. I'd give these a 4.0 out of 5 overall.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Overall, this product isn’t horrible compared to others on the market. It is paraben-free, sulfate-free, EU Allergen free and uses minimal ingredients however it also contains alcohol, silicone and is not safe for fungal acne. After running this product through a cosmetic ingredient safety checker, it scored an 88% one the EWG Health Rating. Most popular, name-brand face lotions are around 65%-80% on the EWG Health Rating. I use the EWG (Environment Working Group) as a reference when checking products and try to stay away from the alarmist side. Only 8% of this product is considered “moderate risk” mainly polyarylamide, Fragrance (unnecessary additive; possible allergy) , alcohol denat. (Bad for dry skin, bad for sensitive skin, potentially block pores) and BHT (also found in edibles, rubber products and is banned in UK and EU due to limited research showing BHT is linked to cancer). This product is good for brightening, anti-aging and promotes wound healing. It also contains steric acid which can potentially block pores. Keep in mind, everyone’s skin is different and what effects one person may not effect another. Learn your skin and what products work best for you. Personally, I would stay away from this product if you have very dry skin and/or sensitive skin. I have “normal” skin (no sensitivities, not overly dry or oily, limited acne, no allergies) and didn’t have any issues with this product so far. It has a light scent that isn’t overpowering but completely unnecessary (I HATE fragrance in beauty products; stick with essential oils). It absorbed nicely and didn’t leave my face feeling oily. I am giving this product 4 stars as of February 21, 2021 due to the minimal ingredients and will update this review once a larger amount of time has passed to fully understand how to works with my skin.\", \"sampled\": \"I ran this lotion through a cosmetic ingredient safety checker and was genuinely impressed by what I found. It scored 88% on the EWG Health Rating which is notably higher than the 65%-80% range most popular brand face lotions tend to land in. The formula is paraben-free, sulfate-free, and EU Allergen free, which already puts it ahead of a lot of competitors on my shelf. Only 8% of its ingredients fall into the moderate risk category, and those include polyarylamide, Fragrance, alcohol denat., and BHT. That said, BHT is worth flagging because it is banned in the UK and EU due to limited research linking it to cancer. I have normal skin and personally had no issues using this, and I do think the brightening and anti-aging claims hold up in day-to-day use. The light scent is there and I find it completely unnecessary given how clean the rest of the formula is. I gave it 4 stars because the minimal ingredient list is genuinely hard to argue with. Where I would pump the brakes is for anyone with very dry, sensitive skin. The product contains alcohol and silicone, and it is not considered safe for fungal acne, so that is a real concern for some users. It also contains steric acid, which can potentially block pores, so those with congestion-prone skin should be cautious. I still think the EWG score speaks for itself as a starting point for evaluation. For my skin type it works, but I would not hand this to someone with reactive or very dry skin without flagging those ingredients first.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I love the concept of an individual towelette, not only for convenience, but because I can go from fragrance free to smelling delicious in a heartbeat. Lunch with someone sensitive to perfume and dinner with someone who isn't. That being said, I believe that this delivery system lends itself better to lighter fragrances. On unwrapping the packet, you smell a dark, bitter chocolatey, red wine kind of note. Definitely unique. Usually gourmand fragrances are geared for youth, but this is all grown up and adult. Too intense for warm weather, ok for fall and winter.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up this fragrance towelette in individual packet form partly because I love being able to switch between fragrance-free and scented quickly depending on where I'm headed. The convenience of that alone makes the delivery system appealing to me. I do think this format works better for lighter fragrances than for something this intense. When I unwrapped a packet, the scent hit me as dark, bitter, chocolatey, and red wine like. Unique. Most gourmand fragrances read young, but this one feels genuinely adult and grown up. It's too heavy for warm weather, but fall and winter feel exactly right for it.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This is an pretty nice face sheet mask in my opinion. In the pack you get 4 masks for about $15, so almost $4 per mask, not the cheapest but also not the most expensive I have used to date. The masks are very easy to use. They come individually wrapped and are folded nicely. The material of these is not the softest I have used but they work. They smell REALLY nice however, a nice gentle floral with some other tones mixed in, very nice. You use these for about 15 to 20 minutes or so and gently rub the remainder of the mask into your skin. When I was done my skin felt pretty soft over. This mask is nice but it is not my favorite. My skin also had a nice residual scent left from the mask which I did enjoy. I would have expected a little better for the price since I have used cheaper masks that were nicer. All in all these are pretty nice though.\", \"sampled\": \"I would give these a 4 out of 5 rating. I picked up a pack of these face sheet masks for about $15, which gives you 4 individually wrapped and folded masks. That works out to almost $4 per mask, so I expected a bit more from the material. The mask itself is not the softest I have used, and I have tried cheaper masks that were nicer. It has a gentle floral scent with other tones that is pleasant but noticeable. The recommended usage time is 15 to 20 minutes. After I removed it, my skin felt soft. A residual scent remained on my skin for a while afterward. They are pretty nice overall, but the fabric feel and the lingering scent keep them from being a favorite. For the price per mask, I was hoping for a slightly more luxurious experience. The moisturizing effect was good, though. I might repurchase if I found them on sale.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This was my first time using a foot peel, so I feel like this product didnt provide enough information for first time users. Directions said to put booties on feet. I wasnt sure if i should take them out of the plastic, so i didnt because there was alot of liquid in them. The packets also said to not use if you are breastfeeding or pregnant so that was concerning - not sure what is in it or how it effects milk, but i still pump for my daughter. I kept these securely on my feet for the directed amount of time and then took them off and took a shower. A couple days later my right foot started to peel first but got really itchy so i think i scratched off some of the peeling skin. The description says it will peel in about a week and leave skin smooth but my whole foot didnt peel and I still have patches of dried skin and calluses. Idk if I was suppose to keep socks/ slippers off during this time but it didnt completely work for me for whatever reason. Maybe if they provided a more detailed instruction process I couldve been more successful?\", \"sampled\": \"I gave this foot peel three stars because it was my first time trying one and the instructions left me guessing at almost every step. The directions just said to put the booties on my feet, but I wasn’t sure if I was supposed to take them out of the plastic first. The packets also warned against use if breastfeeding or pregnant, and since I am still pump milk for my daughter, that made me a little uneasy. I kept the booties on for the directed time and then showered, but the peeling process was a mess. My right foot started peeling first and got so itchy that I ended up scratching it. Neither foot peeled completely, and I was left with patches of dried skin and calluses that never came off. I also had no idea whether I should avoid wearing socks or slippers while my feet were peeling. More detailed instructions could have helped me get better results. I received a sample of this product for review.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This is a small oil diffuser than others I have seen or used but it is very nice and looks better in person than in pictures. The light design is nice but too much for me when I’m sleeping. You have to turn off the whole thing to turn the lights off. You can turn on the diffuser without lights but once on I can only get them off by turning off the diffuser. It lasts almost through the night maybe 6 hours? I find taking off the ceramic cover easier than covers of other diffusers. I use this for just my bedroom so it is perfect. I did not come with a measuring cup for water, this is unusual but I had an extra so no big deal.\", \"sampled\": \"I bought this oil diffuser for my bedroom and it’s noticeably smaller than any other model I’ve used. It looks better than in pictures, and removing the ceramic cover is easier than with other diffusers. The light design is too bright for me when I’m trying to sleep. You can start it without lights, but once they’re on, the only way to turn them off is to power down the whole unit. It lasts almost through the night, about 6 hours, which is decent. The package didn’t include a measuring cup for water, though that wasn’t a problem since I had an extra. For my bedroom, the size and look are perfect, but the light control really needs a separate switch.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Magnetic eyelashes are an interesting concept for those times you want a dramatic look but don't want the hassle of glue on lashes. The magnetic liner works well but it must be applied pretty thick in order to give enough area for the lashes to stick. You have to be careful when placing them also as you cannot really adjust the position because pulling the lashes off usually tears the liner off with it, so you have to then clean the lashes and reapply liner if you make a mistake which is kind of a hassle. The two lashes included here are nice but neither one really worked for my eye shape, I got another kit which had 7 different shapes in it and that one had two styles that work well for me so I would probably recommend getting one with more options to ensure you find one that fits your eye shape.\", \"sampled\": \"I tried magnetic eyelashes as an alternative to glue‑on lashes for a dramatic look, but the experience was frustrating. The magnetic liner has to be applied really thickly for the lashes to stick, and repositioning them is a hassle because pulling them off usually tears the liner. I ended up having to clean the lashes and reapply liner every time I messed up, and neither of the two included lash styles fit my eye shape at all, which is why i gave it 4.0 rating and a strong feeling that you should pick a kit with more style options, like the seven‑style set I eventually tried, so you can actually find a good fit without wasting time.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This is a lovely set of three sponges in nice colors of pink, burgundy, and teal. It also comes with a great metal sponge holder, a silicone brush cleaner, and some cleaning solution. The sponges are light and airy like most sponges like this on the market. The burgundy sponge with the flat side is great for smooth applications. The typical egg-shaped (teal) and the hour-glass (pink) sponges are good for blending or any large make-up application. The hour-glass one is easy to hold in the center. These feel good in the hands, blend reasonably well, and feel soft against my skin. These blenders make a great gift for the make-up obsessed. I love that the sponges do not smell. I tried other colored sponges and they smell like pencil shavings or other chemicals and these do not. I personally do not use a silicone sponge cleaner. I prefer to use my hands and soap and water but having the silicone cleaner with cleaning solution provided is a nice touch. The sponges themselves are similar in style to all of the others that I tried before this. To me what set of sponges to buy all comes down to the price and the angles one wants on the sponges as well as whether it is for gifting or not. The colors are important too--at least to me. The current price of $7.99 is a great deal for this.\", \"sampled\": \"I picked up this sponge set on a whim when I saw it priced at $7.99, and for that amount I figured I couldn’t go wrong. The package arrived with three distinct sponges in pink, burgundy, and teal, plus a metal sponge holder. It felt like a solid little haul right out of the box. The pink sponge is hour-glass shaped and easy to hold, while the teal one is egg-shaped and work well for blending. I also noticed the burgundy sponge has a flat side for smooth applications. All three feel light and airy in texture, which surprised me for the price. They feel soft against the skin and blend reasonably well, though I wouldn’t call them premium. I did not notice any unpleasant smell at all, which matters a lot to me. The set also includes a silicone brush cleaner and a cleaning solution, but I have to be honest about my routine. I don’t use the silicone cleaner, preferring soap and water instead. That part of the kit just sits unused in my drawer, and I wish the value was packed more into the sponges themselves rather than extras I won’t touch. Still, the sponges hold up for daily makeup, and the metal holder keeps them off the counter. I think this would make a great gift for makeup enthusiasts who like trying different shapes without spending much. At the current price of $7.99, it really is a great deal even with the wasted cleaner. My rating sits at 4.0 after a few weeks of steady use.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"With this being the 3rd pack that I have purchased; from 3 different companies, this is the worse. Not only were a few items defective, the entire package was dirty. I found hair, bits of paper and dirt on a few of f the rollers. 8/9 items show above have pin prick size holes at the top. Every time the item is used the hole is stretched and the hole is enlarged. The items are great, which is why I have purchased more than one, but this company is not the one for my family and I.\", \"sampled\": \"I’m on my third pack from three different companies, and this one is by far the worst. Some items arrived defective, and the whole package was dirty. I found hair, bits of paper, and dirt on a few rollers. Eight out of nine items have pin-prick size holes at the top, and those holes stretch and get bigger with each use. The rollers themselves are great, which makes this so frustrating. This company just isn’t right for my family.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I've used retinol products, both OTC and prescribed by my dermatologist. I've been careful to only use retinol at night and to avoid direct sunlight after using retinol. This serum does not specifically warn against going under direct sun, but it is worth taking the precaution. I liked the texture of the Face Oil Retinol Serum - it feels like a dry oil and is easily absorbed into the skin. It doesn't feel oily or sticky but it moisturizes. It helps improve the firmness and tone. I have sensitive skin and did not experience any redness, irritation, or sensitivity using this.\", \"sampled\": \"I’ve used both over-the-counter and prescription retinol products before, so I know what to expect. I apply retinol only at night and stay out of direct sunlight afterward. The Face Oil Retinol Serum doesn’t specifically warn users to avoid direct sun exposure, which feels like an oversight. What I do like is the dry oil texture that absorbs easily without feeling oily or sticky. It moisturizes my skin well and seems to help with firmness and overall tone. I have sensitive skin and experienced no redness, irritation, or sensitivity at all. My dermatologist would probably still want a clearer sun warning on the bottle.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"This review is for the foot wipes only. I was intrigued as I do foot masks once a month, and I am always otherwise giving my feet attention every night. Sometimes I take a wash cloth to them, so these wipes are rather convenient when I am sitting in bed ready to lotion up but didnt take a wash cloth to them yet. These are quicker, easier, and nicer feeling than a wet rag. These aren't all that wet but I managed to wipe down both feet effectively enough. 14 wipes, individually wrapped. Suggested that only one wipe per foot.. so seven uses from this box. The individual packets are convenient but wasteful if not using them on the go. I dont know how much benefit they offer beyond a basic wipe down. Non irritating, non drying. Not loving them enough to be a repurchase.\", \"sampled\": \"I do foot masks once a month and give my feet attention every night, so I hoped these wipes would slot into my routine. They are convenient when I am sitting in bed before applying lotion, and they feel quicker, easier, and nicer than a wet rag. The wipe is not very wet, but it still manages to clean both feet effectively. The box comes with 14 individually wrapped wipes, and the suggested use is one per foot, which gives you seven uses per box. The individual packets are handy, though they feel wasteful if you are not using them. I also think they offer limited benefit beyond a basic wipe down. They are non-irritating and non-drying, which is a plus. Still, I do not love them enough to repurchase. The convenience is real, but the value is not quite for me. It is a fine product for travel or occasional use. I just will not be keeping it in my regular rotation.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I developed acne on my back after my daughter was born, and this is the only soap that calms and soothes the breakouts without drying my skin. It has an almost unnoticeable light scent, even my extremely scent sensitive mother likes it. I stopped purchasing it when a tavern was built next to my local health food store making parking on the weekends impossible. This was a huge mistake because other soaps were either too drying or encouraged more breakouts. It is so nice to find this soap on Amazon, as it saves me the inconvenience of trying to navigate my crowded local health food store on a Sunday. It arrived nicely packed, undamaged and as a bonus, the twelve pack came with a lovely wood soap dish unmentioned in the description; though it is shown in the pictures.\", \"sampled\": \"This soap calms and soothes breakouts without drying my skin. Other soaps I tried were too drying or just encouraged more breakouts. It has an almost unnoticeable light scent, and even my scent-sensitive mother likes it. I used to buy it at a local health food store, but weekend parking became impossible when a new tavern opened, so I found it on Amazon instead. The twelve pack arrived nicely packed and undamaged, and it include a wooden soap dish that wasn’t mentioned in the description but showed up in the pictures.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"I used almost every brands but this one is the best. I've been using Helen Of Troy curling iron for years because its last long time. I usually on for over 30 minutes still it won't burn out quickly like other brands. Also temprature is just right for my fine hair. Just spring gets worn out after 2-3 years everyday using so I usually purchase extra spring for just in case. Order paper for spring is in a box.\", \"sampled\": \"I’ve used almost every brand out there, and this curling iron is hands down the best. I’ve stuck with Helen Of Troy irons for years because they actually last. I can leave it on for over 30 minutes and it doesn’t burn out fast. The temperature is just right for my fine hair. The spring does wear out after 2-3 years of everyday use, though. I usually order an extra spring since the paper is right in the box.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"These aren't pads to clean make-up off your face(at least not fully), like I thought they were. They're actually supposed to be used after washing your face. After wiping your face with them, you're supposed to press any excess liquid onto your skin and then leave it. I found them a little too harsh for my skin. On the front of the container, it says they're soothing, but I didn't get that at all. My face feels scratched and irritated after I use them and I for sure don't like the feeling of leaving the liquid on my face. It's an itchy feeling and it makes me anxious, as opposed to soothed. I ended up having to wash it off after each time I gave them a try. I like the idea of them, but unfortunately, they're not for me.\", \"sampled\": \"These pads are meant to be used after washing your face and the container claims they are soothing. I found them to be too harsh for my skin. Instead of a soothing sensation, my face felt scratched and irritated every time I tried to use one. The liquid also left my skin feeling itchy. The instructions say to press any excess liquid onto the skin and leave it there. I definitely did not experience a soothing sensation. In fact, the whole experience just left me feeling anxious. I would not recommend these pads.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I can't believe how fast this flat iron heated up, it was like instantly. It has a nicely lit LCD screen. All the buttons are on the inside, which is good since I usually hit the controls on my hair curling wand because they are on the outside. The temperature is adjustable, I only needed it about halfway since I have a finer hair. It does come with a heat resistant glove, instructions and a carrying case/ storage bag. There is a little loop on the cord so you can hang it and the cord also rotates so it doesn't get all tangled up.\", \"sampled\": \"This flat iron heats up almost instantly which is a great feature I appreciate. It has a well-lit LCD screen. The control buttons are located on the inside a thoughtful design choice that prevents me from accidentally pressing them while I'm styling my hair. The temperature is adjustable and I only needed to set it to about half. It also came with a heat resistant glove, instructions, and a convenient storage bag. The cord rotates to prevent tangling and even has a loop for hanging.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This 6-head Suttik replaces what seems like an identical motor version with 5-heads. This breed of shavers is the first I've ever used in the steamy hot AM shower every day. Day after day. What a time saver! It does a fantastic job. It delivers a better shave than my really high-$ screen shaver. 6 heads is an improvement over 5. First, it simply covers more area. This is your head ---> a spheroid with protuberances. The 6-head shaver conforms to stuff like your ears and jawline better. 6-heads get the cutter closer to the nooks and crannies where those obnoxious thickets of long hairs might survive for a couple of days before someone points and say, \\\"Ewee!\\\" The shaver is small and easy to hold. A charge lasts more than a month. Cleaning is easy. I use shaving cream to lubricate, just like a regular razor. A 'dry' shave in the shower might sound like an oxymoron, but you'll know what I mean when you use it once. I'm sold. Adds at least 5-minutes of goof-off time in my morning routine. I'll take every minute I can gather. Perseverance might payoff with a straight razor shave, but the time saved here to goof off in the AM pays off immediately! It's revolutionary. Was my review helpful? If so, click \\\"Helpful\\\" so I can know.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently replaced my old 5-head shaver with this new 6-head Suttik model. This new shaver provides a much better shave than even my expensive screen shaver. I use it daily. I use it in the shower. I use shaving cream too. This saves me at least 5 minutes during my morning routine. The device is very small. It is easy to hold. It is also simple to clean. I use it daily in a hot shower for lubrication. A single battery charge lasts for more than a month, which has been a great experience for me. The 6-head version covers more area. It conforms better to contours. This is very noticeable. The shaver works well around the jawline and ears. It is effective at reaching difficult spots where long hairs might otherwise be missed. The 6-head design is a definite improvement over the 5-head version. I get a better shave now than I did with my previous shavers. Very good. No complaints here. I would recommend this product to others.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I got the brown with ginger and has zero ginger in it which is a plus for me since neither do I. I have wavy, thin hair so it’s always hard to find extensions that match my hair wave. I hate using heat products so in order to use most extensions, I have to straighten my hair which I don’t like. I just need some volume, not length so I cut these which was really easy. All you need is a sharp pair of hair cutting scissors and a pony tail holder. Put the pony tail holder where you went to cut and cut away. It isn’t a perfect like which is good to help blend.\", \"sampled\": \"I have thin, wavy hair and bought these extensions to add volume, not length, since I struggle to find matching waves. I don't like using heat products and refuse to straighten my hair just to match extensions. I ordered the brown with ginger color and I was pleasantly surprised that there was actually no ginger color in them. Because I wanted them for volume, I needed to cut the extensions to a shorter length. I just used a pony tail holder to mark the spot and some sharp hair cutting scissors. Cutting them was very easy and the resulting cut was not perfectly straight. This actually helped the extensions blend in well with my own hair.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"My skin: mid 40s, clear, dry, semi sensitive, Rosacea (redness, visible veins, non-acne). Great ingredients that are helpful in creating and maintaining a healthy skin barrier. Ceramides are vital in this role which this product contains. As well as other skin loving, skin calming, skin repairing ingredients such as resveratrol, St Johns Wort, Centella Asiatica, probiotics, and prebiotics, peptides, and niacinamide. Lightweight, slightly creamy, easy to absorb serum that works well on its own but doesn't seem to play well with foundation. Maybe I haven't found the right product yet to use on top. When I apply makeup, in no time the skin looks like it is peeling heavily, but it is just the product peeling off. Even when using a little. This is the worst I have ever experienced with any product. Again, it might be what I have tried it with. And by itself.. seems totally fine. Your experience may vary. Use a light hand, as it seems it doesn't take much to cover face, neck, chest. So, little goes a long way which is a good thing. Worthwhile to try.\", \"sampled\": \"I'm in my mid-40s with dry, semi-sensitive skin and have Rosacea. This is a lightweight, slightly creamy serum that absorbs easily into the skin. Its ingredients are beneficial for creating and maintaining a healthy skin barrier. A small amount of this serum is enough to cover my face, neck, and chest. It contains ingredients like peptides, probiotics, and prebiotics. It also has St Johns Wort and Centella Asiatica. It performs well. I found that it works great when used by itself. My skin feels good. It is fine. The product does not seem to work well when I wear it under my foundation. It peels off my skin when makeup is applied over it. Worth a try.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Great for daily wear, I even sleep in them. They come off easily when I need to remove and do not pull my own lashes out. They do not feel like mink lashes and seem more of a plastic feel, but are soft and mimic mink lashes great. They are think and luscious but are not cartoony. I have had this pair on for 4 days now and still look great. Everyone thinks I put them on daily but, nope I sleep in them and they do not get bent, fold, or lift while I sleep. They are easy to put on, I prefer using hair glue and install them directly above my lash line to prevent my lashes from being removed. I will order more.\", \"sampled\": \"I have been wearing these for daily use for 4 days now. I even sleep in them, and they do not bend, fold, or lift while I am sleeping. They are very easy to apply so I just used some hair glue above my lash line to put them on. Removal is easy and does not pull out my own eyelashes. My only issue is they feel more like plastic than mink. Despite this, the lashes are soft and they do successfully mimic the appearance of mink lashes, looking thick and luscious without being cartoony. I will be purchasing more.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I have thick loose wavy hair and I had damanged hair from the years of hair straighting and hard water. No conditioner or leave in treatments have been able to help restore my hair. It was at the point where I couldn’t even run my fingers through it without kinks. My hair felt like straw and always looked dry and frizzy no matter what I did to it. After using this, my hair feels amazing. Smooth and has a nice slip to it. No more kinks. I think the best part is that this doesn’t wash off. I’m not sure how many showers I have to go through before I have to reapply, but I can tell one tube will last me for a while. After you shower, grab enough of this to cover a thin layer in your hair and then blow dry. It will feel sticky and tacky but once your hair is completely dried, it will just feel like smooth silky hair. If you feel you put too much in one area, put some water in that area to thin the cream and then blow dry again. I really wish I found out about this product before I chopped off my hair. But seriously, wow! Amazing product. Instant and permanent! KBeauty has done it again.\", \"sampled\": \"My thick, wavy hair was completely damaged from years of straightening and suffering from hard water exposure. I had tried so many different conditioners and leave-in treatments but nothing ever seemed to work. My hair felt like straw was constantly dry and had a frizzy texture that I couldn't tame. This KBeauty product has been a complete game changer for me. I apply it after my shower. Then I blow-dry my hair. It does feel sticky. The sticky feeling only lasts until my hair is completely dry, which is a bit of a strange process. Once it's dry, my hair feels unbelievably smooth and silky and all the kinks are gone. I can finally run my fingers through my hair without any issues, which is an amazing feeling. The best part is that the treatment’s effects don't just wash out after a single shower. I really believe a single tube of this stuff will last a long time. It works so well. I just wish I had discovered it sooner.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This is an interesting kind of cream. The jar it comes in is very cute. It is a frosted glass jar with some cute print on it that just looks really cute to be honest and I like displaying it in my bathroom. Inside this jar you find a very interesting cream, a water gel cream that I haven't really come across yet and really have to say is neat and interesting. I love using this especially on a hot summer day because it is very refreshing. It is even better yet if you put the cream in the fridge to cool it down, because it feels even more refreshing. This cream absorbs really quickly into the skin. It has a fantastic fragrance of lavender that I am a huge fan of. It is fantastic especially in the evening because it just has a very calming effect on top of the refreshing feel of it. It leaves a slight tack residue on the skin but that goes away in about 5 to 10 minutes, so no big deal. I for one really like this one and it hits every aspect that it should. I use this in the evening and like how my skin looks and feels in the morning. All in all very happy and this is one of the ones I see myself reaching for more often than not.\", \"sampled\": \"This is a review of the Water Gel Cream. It has a definite lavender fragrance. The cream absorbs very quickly into the skin and I find the lavender scent has a calming effect, so I have been using it in the evening. I really enjoy the refreshing feeling, which is especially nice on hot days, and the sensation is even better when I refrigerate the cream before using it. I am happy with how my skin looks and feels in the morning. The cream is very refreshing. It does leave a slight tacky residue on my skin. This feeling disappears after a few minutes, usually around 5 to 10 minutes. The product comes in a frosted glass jar which has a cute print. I actually enjoy displaying the jar in my bathroom. The packaging is a nice bonus. Overall, I am pleased with the results from using this product.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"This one is easy to use and results are great! I have it for a couple of months now. That is when I decided to do my own nails with gel. My sister-in-law recommended this machine because it was similar to the one that she has. It is not used much because I do my nails every 4-3 weeks. Original drills are good for some applications, but I got a set of diamond drills for Russian manicure. Since then, I never had gel peeling off from the bottom (which was a common problem when I did my nails at the salon) because I clean my cuticles with this machine. I remove old gel with this machine as well. I am a very satisfied customer.\", \"sampled\": \"My sister-in-law recommended this nail drill machine to me. She owns a similar model and is very pleased. I have had mine for a couple of months now. I use it to do my own gel nails every 3 to 4 weeks. This machine is so easy to operate. I also use it to remove my old gel. For a Russian manicure, I did have to purchase a separate set of diamond drills but using it to clean my cuticles has solved the gel peeling problem I always had. Even when I went to the salon, my gel would peel from the bottom. Very satisfied!\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I purchased this product after severely cutting my heel with one of those blades used to cut the calluses from your feet. My husband had warned me of the dangers of using blades, and I've cut my feet several times. I read many reviews on this product and others before purchasing. It came in 2 days and had a charge when it arrived. I keep it plugged in so it's ready whenever my husband or I are needing to use it. I'm very pleased with this product, and very happy that I purchased this. It works very well and does a great job, and I use it at least once a week to keep my feet really soft. My husband also uses it about once a week also, to keep his feet smooth. He was shot in the foot and the bullet fragments are still in his foot, and he gets calluses where the fragments are in his foot. I also purchased the extra rollers, but haven't had to change the original one out yet. Those who complained about this product must not have used it properly. When using don't press down thinking that extra pressure will help, it's not designed to be used like that. Just lightly touch the area you want to treat and the file will definitely work well. For those who have a lot of hard calluses, you might want to either soak your feet first to soften the skin, or use it everyday for a couple of days until you get the best results. You don't want to over do it because you may cause your feet to be sore. I'm very happy with my purchase and highly recommend this wonderful gadget to anyone. It will definitely save you a lot of money on getting pedicures elsewhere!\", \"sampled\": \"I decided to purchase this electric callus remover after I injured my heel trying to use one of those sharp callus removal blades. The product arrived only two days after I ordered it, and it came with a charge. I now keep the device plugged in so it is always ready for use. My husband and I both use the product about once a week. He develops calluses on his foot from bullet fragments he has from a past injury. It is important to use this device with a light touch. Applying a lot of pressure is not how it is designed to work. For anyone with heavy calluses, I would suggest soaking your feet first. You could also try using the device daily for a few days to get the situation handled. A word of caution is that overuse of the product may cause some foot soreness. I also bought extra rollers with my initial purchase. I have not needed to replace the original one yet. I am very happy with this purchase. I believe this product can save you a lot of money on professional pedicures. I would highly recommend this product to others.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I have been hearing about Rosemary oil for a long time thus I got this product. I tried this on my husbands hair/scalp. It was not too greasy like the other oil for hair growth that he used. In fact, it is a little watery and lightweight and dries out easily. I had to pour more as I apply it to the rest of his scalp unlike the products which he previously used where a tiny amount would do. As to the scent, we both like it. As for the effectiveness, I have to update on this as it has been just 4 days since he started using this to tell any difference. Also, he didn't develop any itchiness on his scalp.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried Rosemary oil for my hair care routine, and I must say it's not greasy like other hair growth oils. The texture is a bit watery and lightweight, making it easy to apply. However, I noticed that it tends to dry out quickly, requiring more product compared to what I used before. On the bright side, both my husband and I enjoy the pleasant scent of the oil.It is still too early to judge its effectiveness after only four days of use, but so far, there's been no scalp itchiness, which is a relief. My husband started using the oil 4 days ago, and we are looking forward to seeing the results.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This Aloe Vera Gel by Sdara Skincare takes a few minutes to fully absorb into my skin. I worried it would leave behind a sticky feeling, since I could feel it on my arm the whole time it took to dry. But once the gel finally absorbed, I couldn’t feel any residue left behind. I didn’t notice much of any scent either, which is another positive for me. The gel is clear; the plastic bottle is amber colored to help keep light out. I wish the bottle had a flip top or squeeze lid instead of a screw off cap. The list of ingredients is what gives me pause. I’m not sure why sodium bicarbonate (baking soda) would need to be added to the organic aloe leaf juice. The product description mentions “we perfected our serum by enriching with Vitamin E, & Citric Acid for improved nutrient absorption.” I don’t see Vitamin E listed in the ingredients. I will probably end up using this aloe vera gel to make hand sanitizer. The gel is thick, so it should be good for that use.\", \"sampled\": \"I tried out the Aloe Vera Gel by Sdara Skincare, and I must say, it absorbs fully into the skin, which is a big plus for me. The fact that it doesn't leave any residue behind is fantastic, leaving my skin feeling refreshed and clean after each use. The gel itself has a clear color and comes in an amber-colored plastic bottle. While the absorption and texture are great, I do wish the bottle had a more convenient flip top or squeeze lid instead of the current screw-off cap, which can be a bit cumbersome at times. One thing I noticed is that the gel has a thick consistency, making it ideal for creating hand sanitizer at home if needed. Additionally, I appreciate that it doesn't have a strong scent, perfect for those who prefer skincare products with a more subtle fragrance. On examining the ingredients, I found that the product contains sodium bicarbonate (baking soda), which may not suit everyone's preferences. Surprisingly, Vitamin E, despite being mentioned in the product description, is not listed in the ingredients, which was a bit disappointing given its known skincare benefits. In conclusion, I plan to use this aloe vera gel primarily for making hand sanitizer due to its texture and lack of overwhelming scent. While it has its positives like full absorption and no residue, the packaging and missing Vitamin E in the ingredients are areas that could be improved for a more satisfying experience.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"These are the cutest barrettes - the same old 'clips' from yesteryear delightfully covered in felt. Perfect for my granddaughter and her dolly! I did read one review that said these look home-made. They surely do and that is a big part of their charm. These came nicely packaged in a pink organza bag inside of a windowed box. They even included a ribbon to hang the barrettes on the wall. Very nicely done Belle Beau!!\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried out the Belle Beau barrettes, and I must say, they are adorable with their felt covering. They were a hit with both my granddaughter and her dolly, adding a charming touch to their outfits. The barrettes look like home-made, which only adds to their appeal. Plus, the packaging is lovely, coming in a pink organza bag that makes them perfect for gifting. I also appreciated the included ribbon for hanging them up as a cute decor piece.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"This is a lovely set of two sponges but they are not heart shaped. What is heart shaped is the plastic holding the two sponges together with the flat side joining into what almost looks like a heart. Nevertheless, I still love them. The set comes with a great metal sponge holder. The sponges are dense. As a result, make-up does not soak in as easily. Both blenders have a flat side which is great for smooth applications. These feel good in the hands, blend reasonably well, and feel soft against my skin. These blenders make a great gift for the make-up obsessed. I love that the sponges do not smell. I tried other colored sponges and they smell like pencil shavings or other chemicals and these do not. The sponges themselves are similar in style to all of the others that I tried before this with the exception that the sponges are on the dense side instead of airy. To me what set of sponges to buy all comes down to the price and the angles one wants on the sponges as well as whether it is for gifting or not. The colors are important too - at least to me. These colors also make it perfect for the Easter Basket. At the current price of $7.99 this comes to a pricey $2.66 per piece if I count the holder. Most other blenders sell for between $1.25 and $1.75 per piece in a set like this but without the heart-shaped packaging.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried out a lovely set of two sponges that came in a pack. The sponges felt soft against my skin and were dense, ensuring they did not soak in makeup easily. Additionally, these sponges had a flat side that helped with smooth makeup application, a feature I found quite useful. The plastic packaging these sponges came in was heart-shaped, adding a cute touch to the overall presentation. Along with the sponges, the set included a metal sponge holder, which was a convenient addition for storage and keeping the sponges clean and organized. When it comes to the price, the set is quite reasonable at $7.99. This breaks down to around $2.66 per piece, including the metal holder. Comparing this to other blenders on the market, most sell for $1.25 to $1.75 per piece without the charming heart-shaped packaging, making this set a great deal. For those who value color options, the set might not offer much variety. However, the functionality and quality of the sponges make up for this. The lack of any noticeable smell from the sponges was also a big plus for me, ensuring they were clean and fresh for each use.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Pros: Pleasant smell that disappears quickly- it’s noticeably different from regular polishes. Dries fast into an almost gel-like coating. Peels off easily without damaging the nail. Colors are all very beautiful. Nice for my elementary aged daughter who wants her nails done but ends up picking them off anyway. Cons: only lasts a day or two- if you plan to wash dishes or wash your hands a lot the polish will start lifting off at the edges. The colors are different than the pack I ordered and when I looked back I couldn’t find a pack that actually included the colors sent to me. I ended up liking the colors I was given though so it wasn’t too bothersome. Some of the polishes are thicker than I’d like but still usable.\", \"sampled\": \"The nail polishes have a pleasant smell upon application, although it dissipates quickly. They dry fast, forming an almost gel-like coating that looks great. However it is beautiful, the colors may not last long, only staying vibrant for a day or two. Additionally, the polish tends to peel off easily without causing any damage to the nails, making removal simple. One downside is that with frequent hand washing, the polish may start lifting off at the edges. Another issue is that the colors received may differ from those in the pack ordered. Furthermore, some of the polishes are thicker than preferred, which can make application a bit challenging.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I'm in my early 60s, with slight rosacea under my eyes and a couple of red spidery veins around my nose. My spouse is on blood thinners, and bruises easily. The small amount I show on my hand is the amount I need for my rosacea and around my nose. Not very much at all. It did not happen overnight but it did calm the rosacea down, and while they have not all disappeared, the tiniest of the tiny red lines are gone. Gone! This cream has also helped those pesky bruises disappear more quickly. The product contains all of the wonderful herbal and botanical ingredients well-known for healing skin, such as arnica Montana flower, green tea, and chamomile extracts. The flip cap opens easily, just in case you have any hand difficulties and are curious about that. You need to unscrew the flip cap and remove the safety seal, then screw the cap back on. I can't say this enough, I love a product with a safety seal! I even like the packaging. Various plants and flowers on a celadon background.\", \"sampled\": \"I found that the skincare product was effective in addressing my rosacea and reducing red spidery veins, which was a significant relief for me. Additionally, it helped speed up the healing process of bruises, which was an unexpected but welcome benefit. The formula contains arnica Montana flower, green tea, and chamomile extracts, all of which contribute to its soothing properties. I was impressed by how a minimal amount of the product was needed for each application, making it last longer than expected. Overtime, I noticed a visible improvement in my rosacea, as the product consistently calmed down the redness and irritation. Even the tiniest red lines around my nose seemed to disappear gradually, which was a pleasant surprise. The packaging of the product is aesthetically pleasing, featuring images of plants and flowers against a celadon background. I appreciated the attention to detail, as it added a touch of luxury to my skincare routine. I particularly liked the easy-to-open flip cap with a safety seal, ensuring the product stayed fresh and secure. The thoughtful packaging design complemented the effectiveness of the product itself, enhancing the overall experience.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I really like this lip scrub. It tastes deliciously of peach and is nice and gentle. Let's face it you don't want something that is too harsh on your lips and scrub the heck out of them. You want something nice and smooth that gently scrubs away the excess skin that is oh so annoying and tempts you into biting it off. This scrub has Vitamin E, jojoba and coconut oil and some other nice ingredients and as I said tastes deliciously of peach. I personally am a huge fan of peach and this tastes 100% of peach and doesn't have a weird artificial flavor which is great. Thanks to the oils in this scrub your lips feel great after your wipe of the scrub.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried a lip scrub that was a delightful surprise. The scrub tasted deliciously of peach, making it a treat to use. What I loved most was that it contained Vitamin E, jojoba, and coconut oil, which left my lips feeling great after each use. Additionally, the scrub was gentle yet effective in scrubbing away excess skin, giving my lips a smooth texture. One thing that stood out to me was the absence of any weird artificial flavor; it was all-natural goodness. The scrub was nice and gentle on my lips, making it a pleasant part of my skincare routine. I appreciated the inclusion of other nice ingredients alongside the oils. Overall, this lip scrub gets a solid 5.0 rating from me.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Light as a feather , very cute for daily wear, very functional and looks much more expensive than some wigs I've paid a lot more for wigs and I still choose to wear this one! It's light as a feather very comfortable. All I did was conceal the part doesn't need slot of work. This unit is awesome I bought 2 more if that tells you anything! I haven't noticed any Shedding and I wear this unit a lot!! I love it one of my favorites out of 26 that I own. It's not a lace front and it doesn't clam to be either but for 15 dollars you can't beat it. The only con is it doesn't have any clips. It does have the adjustable straps though.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently purchased a lightweight wig made of feather material, and I have to say it has been a great buy. This wig is incredibly comfortable for daily wear, and I have experienced no shedding issues, which is a big plus for me. What attracted me the most to this wig was its affordable price of $15. Considering the quality and comfort it offers, this wig is truly a steal. The inclusion of adjustable straps adds to its value, allowing for a customized fit without any discomfort. However, it's worth noting that this wig does not have clips, which may be a drawback for some users. Despite not being a lace front wig, this product gives off a luxurious and expensive appearance. It blends seamlessly with my natural hair and enhances my overall look. If you're looking for a budget-friendly yet high-quality wig, this option is definitely worth considering. In conclusion, this wig has exceeded my expectations in terms of comfort, quality, and value for money. I highly recommend it to anyone in need of a versatile and affordable hair accessory.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The very light smell of this is not that bad, but there’s something kind of weird that lingers, like a weird chemical odor. I mean, everything is chemicals, but I mean it doesn’t smell like any particular oil or plant or herb or spice. It smells kind of plasticky? It absorbs nicely and offers a smooth finish, but I’m just not quite sure how I feel about the fragrance. It’s not bad, and it’s light, so I guess it’s fine. Not the best, not the worst.\", \"sampled\": \"The skincare product has a light fragrance but  it comes with a weird chemical odor, making it smell kind of plasticky. Despite these drawbacks, it does absorb nicely and offers a smooth finish. The fragrance, a mix of oils, plants, herbs, and spices, is not the best but is light enough not to be overpowering. Overall, the product provides a decent experience, especially in terms of absorption and finish, although the fragrance could be improved for a more pleasant user experience.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I am always interested in trying new skincare brands, products, and innovations. I'm new to the Carla brand but have had very good experience with South Korean skincare in the past. I loved this cream! It has a light scent and a lovely texture. The cream is not greasy or sticky but it hydrates deeply and well.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently tried a skincare cream from Carla, and it exceeded my expectations. The cream has a light scent that is refreshing without being overpowering. Its lovely texture glides smoothly onto the skin, and what's even better is that it's not greasy or sticky. I was impressed by how deeply it hydrated my skin, leaving it feeling nourished and soft. As someone who enjoys exploring new skincare brands, this South Korean product has definitely caught my attention. I have a great experience with South Korean skincare before, and I would gladly purchase this cream again. I am now curious to explore other skincare items by Carla.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This product as a lot going for it. I love the idea of the individually gift boxed tubes, the cap is wonderful (huge and easy to use), the product is generously sized. The problem is that this stuff is runny, doesn’t absorb that well on me, and the “scent” that is there – it isn’t that great. For $15 before tax, that is roughly $3.75 a tube, and while they’re smaller – I’d honestly stick with Bath & Body Works. This isn’t something I’d get again and I wouldn’t recommend it. I also wouldn’t give it as a gift.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently bought the B08LPGZMQK body care product and I must say, I'm not entirely impressed. The product comes with individually gift boxed tubes which I initially thought was a nice touch. The cap on each tube is large and easy to use, which is another positive. However, I was taken aback by the consistency of the product. It's runny, much to my dismay. It's generously sized but that's of little comfort when the product doesn't absorb well into the skin. The scent is another issue. It's not great and nowhere near as appealing as the fragrances from Bath & Body Works. Considering the price of $15, or $3.75 per tube, I expected a more pleasant aroma. I regret to say that I wouldn't buy this product again, nor would I recommend it to others. Despite the cute packaging, I wouldn't even consider giving it as a gift. It just doesn't hit the mark for me.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This is a very nice magnifying mirror, in a beautiful oil rubbed bronze finish! Especially for the price point! There were very similar items for twice as much, but I'm so glad I chose to purchase this one for our bathroom. My husband is starting to get more gray in his beard than not, and has been having trouble seeing it all while shaving. I got this for him (and for myself, but don't tell him) and he has been thrilled with it. The three lighting choices are gorgeous and very useful, as is the 10x magnification side of the swivel mirror head. I'm thinking of getting one for my mom and sister, as well. Great buy!\", \"sampled\": \"The magnifying mirror we bought boasts an oil rubbed bronze finish which adds a touch of elegance to our bathroom. One of its greatest selling points is the price surprisingly lower than other similar products we've seen. We primarily got this for my husband who's starting to get more gray in his beard than not and has been having trouble seeing it all while shaving. The mirror's 10x magnification side has been a game-changer for him. Plus, the swivel head feature is quite handy, allowing him to adjust the mirror to his preferred angle. Also, with three lighting choices, he can easily find the perfect illumination for his grooming. I've found it so practical and good-looking that I'm even considering getting additional ones for my mom and sister.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I have found my skin has become more sensitive in my 40s and with all the additional washing and bathing because of Covid 19. I'm new to this brand but found this Cica cream to work very well. It immediately cooled and soothed my irritated skin without any greasiness or oiliness. It was quickly absorbed into the skin. It left my skin feeling much softer, less irritated, soothed. It is something I plan to keep on hand.\", \"sampled\": \"In my 40s, I've noticed that my skin has become more sensitive which has been a struggle especially with the increased washing and bathing due to Covid 19. Enter Cica cream, a product that has worked wonders for me. Immediately after applying, I felt a cooling sensation and my irritated skin was soothed. The cream was quickly absorbed into my skin and leaving it feeling softer and less irritated. Based on my experience, I'm planning to keep this Cica cream on hand. It really made a difference for me.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"There are four tiny colorful squeeze bottles in this package that are easy to fill and easy to dispense. I like that they are portable and can hang on a keychain. These can be used for hand lotion, sunscreen, hand sanitizer, etc. However, the caps do open easily so it is possible the contents will be squeezed out inside a purse if there is a lot of movement. Secondly, the current price of $12.99 is way too expensive for what this is. I would not expect this to be priced at more than $6.\", \"sampled\": \"The package I received included four tiny, colorful squeeze bottles that can conveniently hang on a keychain. Filling and dispensing from these bottles was a breeze and I found the caps opened easily. You can use them for hand lotion, sunscreen, hand sanitizer, and more. However, I did notice that if there's a lot of movement the contents may accidentally get squeezed out inside a purse. Priced at $12.99, I personally think it's a bit steep for what you get. While they're handy and portable, I would appreciate a slight price adjustment.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"If you’ve never used a water flosser before, there’s a definite learning curve. Expect to get wet the first few times, if not more. The learning curve and mess is worth it -- my mouth generally feels cleaner after using one -- but I don’t love this particular model. I wanted a water flosser that wouldn’t take up a lot of space on my bathroom counter. This one is a sort of all-in-one, with the reservoir built into the handle, but I kept getting water bubbles in the tank/tip, which made flossing take a lot longer. There’s only 3 speeds: low, medium, and high. The low is quite low. And because of where the speed switch button is, it’s really easy to accidentally switch speeds while using the flosser. The flosser charges via USB, but with a proprietary end (not A or C or anything like that).\", \"sampled\": \"The Cordless Water Flosser has a distinctive learning curve. During initial uses, it can cause a bit of a mess which is not really what I anticipated. However the feeling of cleanliness in my mouth post-use makes the learning process worthwhile. The flosser is designed to save space on your bathroom counter, thanks to its built-in reservoir. Despite the compact design, the flosser has three speed settings: low, medium, and high. I found it quite easy to accidentally press the speed switch button. One downside I noticed is the presence of water bubbles in the tank or tip. This can prolong the flossing process which is a little inconvenient. Another aspect worth mentioning is that the flosser charges via a USB with a proprietary end. Overall, despite a few quirks, I would give this product a rating of 3.0. It's not perfect, but once you get the hang of it, the Cordless Water Flosser does make your mouth feel remarkably cleaner.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I have fine color treated shoulder length hair. This wand is amazing. There is a good range of temperature and a bright LED read out. It warms quickly. Easy to use it creates shiny, healthy looking, long lasting curls. I use the low range and my hair did not look or feel damaged. I appreciate the long cord.The one inch barrel is perfect for me. It comes with a carrying case, protective glove and two clips. It does take time to cool off though.\", \"sampled\": \"I've been using the B08T8V45JP curling wand for a while now. It's perfect for my color-treated shoulder-length hair. I love the range of temperatures it offers and the LED read out is so clear. The wand heats up quickly, which is a big plus. The result? Shiny, healthy-looking, long-lasting curls that I can't get enough of. And the best part is that it doesn't damage my hair even when used on the low range. The one inch barrel size is just right for me. It also comes with a carrying case, a protective glove, and two clips. The only drawback is that it takes a bit of time to cool off, but it's a small price to pay for such a fantastic product.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Oh my, now this is a very nice moisturizer by Higher Education. Even though I am not a fan of skin products coming in jars, this jar is just plain beautiful. This gold digger moisturizer smells amazing and I can't really figure out what it is, but it is the mix of cucumber and melon which is always a winning combination. It contains collagen and hyaluronic acid which are fantastic ingredients for your skin. The cream is very lightweight and absorbs quickly into your skin. It leaves no residue behind, just soft and smooth skin that looks and feels great. You don't need a lot of this moisturizers so this jar should last you a good while. So even though it is not the cheapest cream around you still get your money's worth. I like this one, quite a bit and can only recommend it.\", \"sampled\": \"The Gold Digger Moisturizer by Higher Education is a beauty product that has quickly become a staple in my skincare routine. The product is thoughtfully packaged in a jar, making it easy to access. What sets it apart is its unique scent, a refreshing blend of cucumber and melon. As someone who appreciates skincare, I was thrilled to discover that it contains collagen and hyaluronic acid. The moisturizer's consistency is lightweight and it absorbs quickly into the skin without leaving any residue. After use, it leaves my skin feeling soft and smooth which I attribute to its potent ingredients. However I must note that the Gold Digger Moisturizer is not the cheapest option on the market. Despite the price, I am willing to invest in it for the quality and benefits it provides. My overall product rating would be a solid 5.0.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Nice feeling moisture boost. Lightweight, nothing oily about it to me. My dry skin loves a good hydrating toner, and this fits that need. I pat some on all over face, neck and chest. Sometimes I do this a few times to saturate my skin. Then I follow up with other products (serum, moisturizer, and/or sunscreen). Ceramides help build and maintain a healthy skin barrier. Hyaluronic acid attracts moisture (but be sure to lock it in with a moisturizer or an occlusive ingredient). Sedum provides intense hydration & minerals, and vitamins to the skin. So lots of good stuff going on with this toner.\", \"sampled\": \"The hydrating toner has been a game-changer for me. It's lightweight and not oily at all, making it perfect for my dry skin. I love how it can be applied on my face, neck, and chest, making it a versatile addition to my skincare routine. What sets it apart from other toners I've tried is its compatibility with other skincare products. Whether it's my favorite serum, moisturizer, or even sunscreen, this toner works harmoniously with all. But what truly makes this toner special are its ingredients. It contains ceramides, hyaluronic acid, and sedum. It's been a week since I started using this toner and my skin already feels more hydrated and plump. While this toner may not have received any helpful votes yet, I would definitely give it a 5.0 rating for its efficacy and formulation. All in all, this hydrating toner has proven to be a valuable addition to my skincare regimen.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"This has a strong scent of mango, which is quite appealing. Outside of that, the body wash doesn't lather that well and the consistency of the wash is on the thin side. It's cleans well but other than the mango scent I wasn't impressed by it. The price isn't too high though it's a rather small bottle. Overall it's OK but I don't love it enough to order it again.\", \"sampled\": \"The Body Wash I tried has a robust mango scent, which was quite appealing. However, it doesn't lather well, which was disappointing for me. The consistency of the body wash is on the thin side, so be prepared for that. On the bright side, it does clean well despite the lack of lather. The price is reasonable but the size of the bottle is a bit small for my liking.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"I got this razor for my daughter but ended up keeping it because I liked it so much. First of all the handle is a heavy duty handle with some weight to it . I like that. It also has a magnetic feature to change the blades . I was worried it would fall off when shaving but it stood firm. The magnetic feature makes it super easy to change heads. Unfortunately I didn’t see any replacement heads to order on Amazon. I would have liked the kit to come with more heads, it comes with two, one to use and one replacement . I really like the aloe strip that surrounds the 5 blades , most of my other razors it’s just on one side. The razor cuts close and is comfortable to use. It also comes with a ceramic base to hold it . It looks great and simple while being stored.\", \"sampled\": \"I recently bought this razor from Amazon and I have some thoughts to share. It's a solid piece of kit with a heavy-duty handle which I found to be quite impressive. The razor also features a magnetic system for changing blades a feature that's both novel and practical. The magnetic function is robust ensuring the blades don't fall off during shaving, which is a major plus. However, there's a downside to this product. Replacement heads for this razor are not available on Amazon, which can be inconvenient. The initial kit does come with two heads, but once they're used up, you're on your own. Another feature worth mentioning is the aloe strip surrounding the 5 blades on the razor. It's a nice touch that adds a level of comfort during shaving. The razor cuts close and is comfortable to use, making for a pleasant shaving experience. It also comes with a ceramic base for storage, which I thought was a nice stylish addition. I'd give this product a rating of 4.0 despite the issue with the unavailable replacement heads. It's a reliable razor overall, with some innovative features that set it apart. It's just a shame about the lack of readily available replacement heads on Amazon.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I was so excited when I saw I could order banana combs! I couldn't wait to get them. I still have some old ones from back in the 80's. Anyway, I was so disapointed in the ones I ordered, they are so stiff. The plastic is very hard and not pliable and makes a difference in your hairdo, plus it's not comfortable, it might as well been made with glass. I will stick with my old ones!\", \"sampled\": \"I was excited to order these banana combs as a verified purchase. I still have older ones from the 80's. These combs left me disappointed, like the review title Disapointed. The plastic feels hard, too stiff, and not flexible enough. They affected my hairdo and felt uncomfortable, and compared with my older combs ,they were more like glass than the banana combs I wanted.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"There are 14 brushes in this set. There are brushes for foundation, blush, eyeshadow, eye blending (fan brush), etc. In fact, there are more brushes than I needed. The one unique brush in this set (see photo) is the one that looks like a paint brush. This is perfect for cheek highlighting. The bristles are relatively soft but not as soft as I expected. They feel pretty good against my skin but not great. Also, the heads have a slight odor. The PU brush case has a tremendous chemical odor and needs to be aired out a while. This is a bargain basement travel set for someone who is starting their make-up journey. The current price of $11.19 comes to $.80 cents per brush. In this case one gets what they pay for; it's a marginal price for a marginal quality set that is functional.\", \"sampled\": \"I would put this at 3.0. The set has 14 brushes, which was more than I needed. It covers basic makeup uses without feeling special. The mix includes a foundation brush, blush brush, eyeshadow brush, and eye blending fan brush. For travel, the range is convenient. The paint brush is the standout piece. I found it suited to cheek highlighting. The bristles are somewhat soft. They felt acceptable on my skin, but not as soft or as nice as I expected. The brush heads had a mild odor, and the PU brush case had a strong chemical smell that needed airing out. At $11.19, it comes across as a low-cost travel set for makeup beginners. I would call it functional, but marginal in quality.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I really like this retinol serum by Simple Elegance. It comes in a classic looking pump dispenser. Keep in mind, when you use products containing retinol, your skin is more sensitive to sun light so make sure to use sun protection when you go outside. This serum smells amazing and I for one really like that a lot about this product. The serum itself is almost like a cream and very lightweight. It absorbs very quickly into the skin and leaves hardly any residue behind, and what it does pretty much disappears quickly. I found that my skin is soft and smooth when I use this. It looks great, moisturized and just overall great. It is also not very expensive at $30 so for me this one is a winner.\", \"sampled\": \"I really like the Simple Elegance retinol serum. The pump dispenser looks classic and works neatly. The scent is excellent, and the cream-like texture feels very lightweight on my skin rather than heavy or greasy. It absorbs very fast, leaving only a little residue that soon fades. My skin feels soft and smooth when I use it. It also looks great and moisturized, which is the main reason I would rate it 5.0. Since it is retinol, I make sure to use sun protection because it can increase skin sensitivity to sunlight. At $30, I do not find it very expensive.This serum is a winner in my skin care routine.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This is a pretty nice face oil. It comes in a little dropper bottle that is pretty cute. I admit it smells like an essential oil and contains lavender, cypress and sweet orange, all scents I like. The oil is not very heavy and absorbs into your skin fairly quickly. It is a quite oily though and takes quite some time to absorb into your skin. But you can expect that from a face oil if you ask me. Unlike another oil from Lua this one stays oily though, not sure why that is. Because of the oil your face is soft though but that is mostly because of that greasiness. Probably after about an hour or two that goes away and your skin is nice and soft.\", \"sampled\": \"I found this face oil generally nice. The small dropper bottle is cute and easy to like. I would put it at 4.0 because it has good points, but the oily feel hangs around. The scent reminds me of essential oil, which I do like. It contains lavender, cypress, and sweet orange, and those are scents I usually enjoy. The oil itself is not very heavy on my face. Still, the oiliness is noticeable and lasts longer than I prefer. Some greasiness is expected from a face oil, but compared with another oil from Lua, this one stayed oilier. My face does feel soft, though largely from that greasy feel, and after about an hour or two the greasiness fades and my skin feels soft.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This is a great nail set. It has all the bits you need to get started. It is well made and spins at a high RPM (12000?) Easy to swap out bits and the wand is easy to handle and great for fine detailed work. I only use it on top speed but it does have 3 seed total and can be reversed if needed. The battery holds a charge well and is enough to do a pair of hands. Recharge takes about an hour.\", \"sampled\": \"I rated 5.0. It is a great nail set and feels well made. For a beginner, it includes the needed bits, and I found the bits easy to change while using the wand for fine detail tasks. The tool spins at a high RPM, maybe 12000?, and I only use it at its highest speed even though it has 3 speed settings. The wand is easy to control, and the device can reverse direction if needed. The battery lasts well enough for a pair of hands.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I'm one of the unfortunate ones who actually had to pay for these. Most of the reviews (close to practically all) were from people who received a complementary set, in exchange for their honest opinion. I'm not sure if I'm using the same product as all the rest of those 5 star reviewers, but these do absolutely zero/zilch. OK, the rose water or whatever they're packed in smells nice, and they feel OK on your eyes, but so does a cheap ice mask, which gives better temporary results...not to mention, positioning these super thin, slimy gizmos is a pain in the butt. Eventually, you'll get them to stick and assume all that work will pay off. I have bags under my eyes, and after using these five times (every other day) there's not even a 0.1% difference. I even tried them first thing in the morning when my eyes were at their worst. This is the placebo effect at its best!\", \"sampled\": \"This is a verified purchase review, and I paid for myself. I mention that because many other reviewers seem to have received a complimentary set and gave 5 star feedback. I wanted these under-eye patches to help my bags, but after using them five times, I saw no noticeable results. I used them every other day and even tried them in the morning, when my eyes looked their worst, but there was not even a 0.1% improvement that I could see. The liquid had a nice color and scent, so that part was pleasant. The patches also felt acceptable on my eyes, which kept me trying them longer than I probably should have. My main problem was that they were very thin, slippery, and difficult to position under the eyes. For me, the effect was like a placebo, so my rating would be 1.0, even if 16 people found this kind of review helpful.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This bottle I bought to put a scant teaspoon into my home made ice cream to keep it from forming ice crystals after storing in the freezer. I just bought a Cuisinart Ice Cream Machine and followed a simple recipe for chocolate ice cream, but I wanted to make sure that it would not become hard and full of ice crystals once I stored it in the freezer, so I did some research online and glycerin is one of several ways to keep ice cream creamy. It only takes a teaspoon in the recipe and the other thing about glycerin is that it is sweet, so it blends in well with an ice cream recipe. My ice cream turned out great and it is smooth and creamy after being in the freezer. Now, this is not my first use of glycerin as I have used a small amount in water to spray in my hair. Especially on really humid days when my naturally curly hair turns into a frizz-ball, spraying my hair with a 10 parts water, 1 part glycerin (or even less glycerin, depending) keeps my hair from frizzing out so bad. For me, in ice cream or in my hair, glycerin works great!\", \"sampled\": \"This was a verified purchase for me, and I gave the glycerin a 5.0 rating. I had recently purchased a Cuisinart Ice Cream Machine and bought this bottle specifically to add glycerin to homemade ice cream, mainly because I wanted to reduce ice crystal formation once the ice cream went into freezer storage. I made chocolate ice cream from a simple recipe and used a scant teaspoon, which is about a teaspoon, in the batch. The result after freezer storage was smooth and creamy, not icy in the way I was trying to avoid. The sweetness of glycerin also fit well in the ice cream, so it did not feel out of place for that use. I had already used glycerin mixed with water as a hair spray, using 10 parts water, 1 part glycerin on humid days for my naturally curly hair, and that mixture helps reduce frizz. For me, it works well for both ice cream and hair use, so the bottle feels practical rather than like a single-purpose purchase.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"These replacement heads were a perfect fit for my rechargeable Oral B toothbrush. The bristles are firm, but not hard, so brushing is thorough but not painful. They're also more resilient than the original brush heads which got bent out shape very quickly. By comparison, these hold up quite well. And the price for all 8 heads is quite reasonable. There's no reason why I'll ever buy any other replacement heads as long as I can get these.\", \"sampled\": \"I would give these replacement heads a 5.0 based on my use. They fit my rechargeable Oral B toothbrush perfectly, which was my main concern. The bristles feel firm without being hard, so brushing is effective and not painful. They also seem more durable than the originals. My original brush heads bent out of shape quickly, but these hold up well. The price feels reasonable for an 8 pack, and I expect to keep buying if available.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I've not come across a face soap in this format -- it's a stick format, much like an antiperspirant. You rub the stick on your dry face, then use water. It's different but very easy to get used to quickly. The soap doesn't lather all that much but it does do a good job of cleaning without drying out my face. The scent is quite nice, with the eucalyptus coming through the most. It's a medium/mild level of scent that is noticeable without being overwhelming. The scent does not linger after rinsing. The soap itself is black due to the inclusion of charcoal. The dispensing tube is well done, with a screw base that pushes up the soap easily and evenly. It also has a screw top cover, which is a nice touch for keeping the lid affixed if you take this while traveling or use it in a gym bag. It's an unusual but enjoyable twist on face soap. A bit pricey for the amount you get, but it's sufficiently unique to justify the cost, in my opinion. Five stars.\", \"sampled\": \"I rated 5.0 because this face soap worked better than I expected. The stick format felt unusual at first, almost like antiperspirant packaging. I got used to it quickly. I apply the stick to a dry face before adding water, which was easy to adapt. The soap cleaned my face effectively without leaving it dry. It does not make much lather, so that may bother some people. The soap is black from charcoal. The tube has a screw base that raises the soap smoothly and evenly, which makes the format feel practical. The scent was pleasant, and eucalyptus was the note I noticed most while using it. The fragrance strength seemed medium to mild, not overpowering, and it did not stay on my skin after rinsing, which I liked. My only real complaint is the price. It feels pricey for the quantity, though the unique stick format makes it worth the cost to me.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"product_review\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"I was so excited to use these products, but was very disappointed - the eye liner does not stay on your eyes and what does stay, the eyelashes DO NOT attach to!! :( When I asked for a refund I was told I needed to return the product to KOREA!!!! The alternative was to have half the purchase price credited and keep the product. This was not the outcome I expected since the order was fulfilled by Amazon!! Very dissapointed!\", \"sampled\": \"I would rate 1.0 because the eyeliner does not remain on my eyes, and the eyelashes do not attach to the eyeliner that remains. I requested a refund, but I was told the product had to be returned to KOREA for that. The other option was keeping it and getting half of the purchase price back. Since Amazon fulfilled the order, I expected a different refund outcome, even though this verified purchase showed 0 helpful votes. Very disappointed.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n","data/translation.jsonl":"{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"O-legs often result from lifestyle habits. Specifically, movements that tightly twist the legs. The way you sit, stand, and walk can cause O-legs. You can also see a video explanation. This is a partial quote from the O-leg correction course textbook. When O-legs develop due to sitting posture: Sitting cross-legged or slumping causes the hip joints to twist inward. When the hip joints twist inward, the pelvis tilts forward, opening the ischial tuberosities. When O-legs develop due to standing posture: Standing with your toes pointed inward twists the hip joints inward. Standing with weight on one leg distorts the pelvis. The ilium on one side of the pelvis faces forward, and the ischial tuberosity opens. People who already have O-legs find it easier to stand with their weight on one leg, so remaining in this position further distorts the pelvis. When O-legs develop due to walking style: Walking with your toes pointed inward or outward twists the hip joints. Walking flat-footed leads to flat feet, which then creates a gap below the knees. How are your O-legs twisted? How distorted are your pelvis and feet? Would you like a free diagnosis to determine if improvement is possible? If so, please try the real-time free O-leg diagnosis above. We will fully support those who want to correct their O-legs themselves.\", \"sampled\": \"Bow legs are often caused by lifestyle habits. These are actions that intensify the twisting of the legs. The causes of bow legs lie in how you sit, stand, and walk. You can also view a video explanation. This is a partial quote from the O-leg Correction Course text. When sitting causes bow legs, sitting sideways or in a flat cross-legged position twists the hip joints inward. When the hip joints twist inward, the pelvis tilts forward and the sit bones spread open. When standing causes bow legs, standing with the toes pointed inward causes the hip joints to twist inward. Standing with your weight on one foot causes the pelvis to become misaligned. The ilium of one side of the pelvis faces forward, and the sit bone on that side begins to open. People who already have bow legs find it easier to stand with weight on one foot, so standing in this position causes the pelvis to become increasingly misaligned. When walking causes bow legs, walking with toes pointed inward or outward causes the hip joints to twist. Walking flat-footed leads to flat feet, which then creates a gap below the knees. How are your bow legs twisted? How is your leg alignment distorted from the pelvis downward? Can it be improved? If you would like a free diagnosis, please take the real-time free bow-leg diagnosis listed above. Those who want to correct their bow legs themselves will receive our full support.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In this first volume of The Natural History of Man, André Bourguignon offers a reflection on evolution, as physicists and biologists have been able to read it from knowledge or hypotheses about the origin of the universe and the constitution of inert and living matter. Beyond a brilliant synthesis, supported by a respectable bibliography, the classic but fundamental questions posed by evolution are addressed in a penetrating and clear manner: this concerns, in particular, the role of opposing chance (...)\", \"sampled\": \"In this first volume of the Natural History of Man, André Bourguignon reflects on evolution as physicists and biologists have interpreted it through knowledge and hypotheses about the origin of the universe and the constitution of inert and living matter. Beyond a brilliant synthesis supported by a substantial bibliography, the classic yet fundamental questions raised by evolution are addressed in a penetrating and clear manner, with particular attention given to the role of chance as opposed to (...).\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Washington CHIFAMBA was born in 1969 in northeastern Rhodesia, now Zimbabwe, in the Guruve district, an area characterized by the presence of various sculptable stones including serpentine and steatite. He began sculpting at the age of 15 on the advice of Brighton Sango. He regularly exhibits at the National Gallery of Zimbabwe which actively supports the creation and international promotion of high-level artists. The inspiration of W. Chifamba comes from his communion with each block of stone and follows the rhythm of the seasons. What he calls Creations are sculptures of essentially human or animal themes which are, for him, eloquent, animated, endowed with feelings. He chisels, polishes and waxes the serpentine or black, golden or green steatite which makes the reputation of Guruve, and occasionally opal and cobalt. In addition to the National Gallery of Zimbabwe, his works have been exhibited in France: Paris, Nice, Grenoble, Saint-Tropez, Annot; in Germany: Bonn, Bad-Godesberg; in Luxembourg: Dudelange; in Bosnia-Herzegovina: Sarajevo, Zenica. In September 2000, he came to the Fort du Saint-Eynard, near Grenoble, to teach stone carving. He was then one of the five sculptors invited to the International Meetings of Peyresc.\", \"sampled\": \"Washington CHIFAMBA was born in 1969 in the northeast of Rhodesia, present-day Zimbabwe, in the Guruve district, a region characterized by the presence of various carvable stones including serpentine and soapstone. He began sculpting at the age of 15 under the guidance of Brighton Sango. He exhibits regularly at the National Gallery of Zimbabwe, which actively supports the creation and international promotion of high-level artists. W. Chifamba's inspiration comes from his communion with each block of stone and follows the rhythm of the seasons. What he calls Creations are sculptures of essentially human or animal themes that, for him, are expressive, animated, and endowed with feeling. He chisels, polishes, and waxes the serpentine or black, golden, or green soapstone for which Guruve is renowned, and occasionally opal and cobalt. Beyond the National Gallery of Zimbabwe, his works have been exhibited in France: Paris, Nice, Grenoble, Saint-Tropez, Annot; in Germany: Bonn, Bad-Godesberg; in Luxembourg: Dudelange; and in Bosnia-Herzegovina: Sarajevo, Zenica. In September 2000, he came to the Fort du Saint-Eynard, near Grenoble, to teach stone sculpture. He was then one of five sculptors invited to the Rencontres internationales de Peyresc.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I.1844 RED CHINESE TABLE Inspiration It is thought that the table originated in the cave era with the objective of being able to support objects at a comfortable height for man. The first great revolution to the model of that time was carried out by the Egyptians. Then, it was the Romans who began to use it for eating. Over time they were mutating until in the 19th century tables like we know them today were created. These types of Chinese tables which tend to be low in height, are used as coffee tables or side tables. This one is red and has, in addition to hand-painted drawings, mother-of-pearl figures of birds stamped on the surface. Measurements: 90 cm long // 50 cm wide // 35 cm high.\", \"sampled\": \"I.1844 RED CHINESE TABLE Inspiration It is thought that the table was born in the cave era with the purpose of holding objects at a comfortable height for humans. The first major revolution to that era's model was carried out by the Egyptians. It was then the Romans who began using it for eating. Over time, tables kept evolving until the 19th century, when tables as we know them today were created. This type of Chinese table, which tends to be low in height, is used as a coffee table or side table. This one is red and features, along with hand-painted designs, mother-of-pearl bird figures stamped on the surface. Measurements: 90 cm long // 50 cm wide // 35 cm tall.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"On August 18th, Anqing Kangmingna Packaging Co., LTD., several humanitarian organizations, sponsored student representatives, and over 50 other people attended the meeting. Since 2019, Anqing Kangmingna Packaging Co., Ltd. has donated grants to poor university students for four consecutive years, with funding totaling nearly 200,000 yuan. In order to effectively help impoverished female college students enter campus smoothly, 40,000 yuan was donated during the event. With education as its foundation, KangmingNA DURING the years has cultivated deep roots in the industry, but also adheres to educational donations, poverty alleviation, takes the initiative to play the role of the company, and sincerely dedicates corporate love. In the future, Kangmingna will continue to actively participate in and carry out various forms of public welfare poverty relief activities, and better assume the social responsibility of private enterprises.\", \"sampled\": \"On August 18, Anqing Kangmingna Packaging Co., LTD., several humanitarian organizations, sponsored student representatives, and more than 50 others attended the meeting. Since 2019, Anqing Kangmingna Packaging Co., Ltd. has donated grants to impoverished university students for four consecutive years, with funding of nearly 200,000 yuan. In order to effectively help poor female university students enter campus smoothly, 40,000 yuan was donated at the event. Using education as its foundation, Kangmingna has spent years cultivating deep roots in the industry while also adhering to educational donations and poverty relief, taking the initiative to fulfill its role as a company and sincerely dedicating its corporate goodwill. Kangmingna will continue to actively participate in and carry out various forms of public welfare poverty-relief activities, better fulfilling the social responsibilities of a private enterprise.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In a McDonald's in New York, United States, an angry customer because they were given cold fries, shot one of the employees. Intolerance continues to happen around the world; the most recent event occurred at a McDonald's in Brooklyn, one of the five counties of New York. There a woman received her food order, however, she became upset because the french fries were cold. Amidst the fight and the claim she was making, the woman called her son, a 20-year-old young man, who arrived immediately and also attacked the employee; However, things got worse just before they left, as the young man pulled out a firearm and shot one of the employees, who according to authorities is in critical condition. Fortunately, this subject was arrested by American police and is currently accused of \\\"attempted murder\\\" and \\\"possession of firearms.\\\" It should be mentioned that, the young man was already known to the authorities, as he had already committed several crimes. It should be taken into account that, New York is one of the cities with the highest number of attacks or assaults in which weapons are used.\", \"sampled\": \"At a McDonald's in New York, United States, an angry customer shot one of the employees because he was given cold french fries, and this act of intolerance is yet another example of violence occurring around the world. The incident took place at a McDonald's in Brooklyn, one of the five boroughs of New York, where a woman received her food order but became upset because the french fries were cold. In the middle of the argument and complaint she was making, the woman called her son, a 20-year-old young man, who arrived immediately and also turned aggressively against the employee. However, things worsened just before they left, as the young man pulled out a firearm and shot one of the employees, who, according to information from the authorities, is in critical condition. Fortunately, this individual was detained by United States police and is currently charged with \\\"attempted homicide\\\" and \\\"possession of firearms.\\\" It is worth noting that the young man was already known to the authorities, as he had previously committed several crimes. New York is one of the cities with the highest number of attacks or incidents in which firearms are used.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Photographer's Note A walk in the Roques de Garcia and the view of the lava which formed over 92 days from June 9th to August 8th, 1798. One imagines this smoking, red magma, this pasty and thick mass flowing down the slope of the Teide volcano. The sight we regret not seeing up close… I found the quote from a great scientist Von Humboldt who stopped in Tenerife in 1799 (June 19-25) during his famous journey to the Americas where he climbed to the summit of Teide: “the peak of Teyde is an immense basaltic mountain which seems to rest on dense and secondary limestone. It is the same as with much flintstone one finds at Cape Noir, in Africa, the same upon which rest the basalts of Saint Loup, near Agde and those of Portugal. See with what uniformity the globe is constructed! The Azores, the Canary Islands, the Cape Verde islands seem only to be the continuation of the basaltic formations of Lisbon…” He will also present an engraving of “the inside of the crater of Mount Tenerife” in his monumental book, a veritable atlas,: “view of the Cordilleras and monuments of the peoples of America”.\", \"sampled\": \"Photographer's Note: A walk through the Roques de Garcia and the view of the lava that formed over 92 days, from June 9 to August 8, 1798. One can imagine this smoking, red magma, this thick, pasty mass flowing down the slope of the Teide volcano. It is a spectacle one regrets not seeing up close. I found a quote from the great scientist Von Humboldt, who stopped at Tenerife in 1799 (June 19-25) during his famous voyage to the Americas, where he climbed to the summit of Teide: \\\"the Pic de Teyde is an immense basaltic mountain that appears to rest on dense, secondary limestone, the same that, along with much flint, is found at Cap Noir in Africa, the same on which rest the basalts of Saint-Loup near Agde and those of Portugal; see with what uniformity the globe is constructed! The Azores, the Canaries, the Cape Verde Islands appear to be nothing but the continuation of the basaltic formations of Lisbon...\\\" He also presented an engraving of \\\"the interior of the crater of the Pic de Ténériffe\\\" in his monumental book, a true atlas: \\\"Vue des Cordillères et monuments des peuples de l'Amérique.\\\" Nobody has marked this note useful.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"PIERRE KUNZ COMPLICATION RETROGRADE TRIPLE RETROGRADE SECONDS ¥4,200,000+TAX The “Triple Retrograde Seconds,” which was the first attempt in the history of mechanical watches, is housed in a square case with a satin finish as part of the Carree ‘Care’ collection, establishing a unique style combined with a geometrically designed dial. This watch divides one minute into three parts, and three retrograde seconds constantly relay time in 20-second increments, connecting time with unprecedented dynamic movement, repeating an astonishing number of 4,320 flybacks in 24 hours. Pierre Kunz expresses the fleeting flow of time with the movement of the second hand returning in this Triple Retrograde Seconds, evolving the watch from a \\\"tool to know time\\\" to a \\\"work of art to appreciate time.\\\"\", \"sampled\": \"PIERRE KUNZ COMPLICATION RETROGRADE Triple Retrograde Second ¥4,200,000+TAX — the \\\"Triple Retrograde Second,\\\" a first in the history of mechanical watchmaking, is housed in a satin-finished square case from the Carree' collection and, paired with a geometrically designed dial, establishes a distinctive style all its own. This watch divides one minute into three parts, with three retrograde seconds relaying continuously in 20-second intervals to mark the passage of time, linking each moment with an unprecedented dynamic motion and repeating an astonishing 4,320 flybacks every 24 hours without interruption. Pierre Kunz expresses the dizzying flow of time through the Triple Retrograde Second by capturing the instant the seconds hand returns, evolving the watch from a \\\"tool for knowing time\\\" into a \\\"work of art for appreciating time.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"How to build your Hipparcos celestial globe * The 20 brightest stars in the night sky and Polaris, the North Star, appear in red * Constellations appear in yellow * The ecliptic appears as a dashed yellow line around the globe * Each line of longitude represents 1 hour of right ascension * Each line of latitude represents 15 degrees of declination. 1. Print the template at a scale of 100%: celestial globe. (PDF document) Cut out the two parts of the template along the black lines, then along the dashed red lines, as shown above. If you are using thick paper or cardboard, make small cuts in the paper to fold it more easily. 2. Start by gluing the small flaps indicated by the red arrows, then glue those indicated by the green arrows. 3. This gives you two \\\"halves\\\" of your icosahedron. Align the face containing the Summer Triangle with the face containing Polaris. Join the two halves by gluing the flap indicated. 4. The two halves are now aligned. Finish your globe by gluing the corresponding faces together via their flap!\", \"sampled\": \"How to build your Hipparcos star globe * The 20 brightest stars in the night sky and Polaris, the North Star, appear in red * The constellations appear in yellow * The ecliptic appears as a yellow dotted line around the globe * Each longitude line represents 1 hour of ascension * Each latitude line represents 15 degrees of declination. 1. Print the template at 100% scale: star globe. (PDF document) Cut the two parts of the template along the black lines, then along the red dotted lines, as shown above. If you use thick or cardstock paper, make small cuts in the paper to fold it more easily. 2. Start by gluing the small flaps indicated by the red arrows, then glue those indicated by the green arrows. 3. You now have two \\\"halves\\\" of your icosahedron. Align the face containing the summer triangle with the face containing Polaris. Join the two halves by gluing the indicated flap. 4. The two halves are now aligned. Finish your globe by gluing the corresponding faces to each other via their flap!\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Chanel wallet fake From 1771 to 1774 he served as Governor of Massachusetts Colony. As relations between Great Britain and the colonies eventually turned turbulent, he was forced into exile in London, where he spent the rest of his life. He should have been popular as a local administrative official, but as he rose to the top of power, he appointed relatives to important positions, extended his influence to the judiciary, created political enemies through policy, and eventually became the target of hatred from the colony. Sunburn, spots and freckles caused by sunburn. 《Popular!》 English Ideas Perfect Veil No complicated processes required Ideal for busy mornings and quick outings Creates naturally beautiful skin. A 3-in-1 liquid foundation, sunscreen and makeup base! It's a multi-functional foundation with a new texture. Christian Dior Dior Skin Fluid S1003035 OFF!! Product description Long-lasting, transparent and natural finish.\", \"sampled\": \"He would go on to serve as Governor of the Massachusetts Colony from 1771 to 1774. When relations between Britain and the colonies descended into open conflict, he was forced into exile and crossed to London, where he would spend the rest of his life. Though he had been highly popular as a native-born administrator, as he rose to the peak of power he placed relatives in key positions, extended his influence into the judiciary, made political enemies through his policies, and gradually became an object of colonial hatred. Sunburn, sunburn-related spots and freckles — the highly praised English Ideas Perfect Veil requires no troublesome process whatsoever, making it ideal for busy mornings or quick outings, and creates naturally beautiful skin. Sun Cut Makeup Base Liquid Foundation serves three purposes in one single product. It is a multifunctional foundation with a new texture. Christian Dior Dior Skin Fluid S1003035 OFF. Long-lasting, with a natural, translucent finish. Chanel new arrivals muffler.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"We believe that considering urban development is synonymous with thinking about the future of this country and caring for posterity. In the future, it will become increasingly necessary to achieve sustainable urban and community development in response to changes in social conditions such as anticipated demographic shifts. We will respond to these societal needs, fulfill our social responsibilities, and contribute to the realization of a truly valuable society by proposing what future urban development should look like.\", \"sampled\": \"We believe that considering community development is synonymous with contemplating the future of this nation and caring for generations to come. The anticipated shifts in social conditions, including demographic structures, will increasingly demand the realization of sustainable urban and community development. We will respond to these societal needs, fulfill our social responsibilities, and propose visions for future community building to contribute to a truly valuable society. In preparation.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Given the significant changes made to melee weapons in V6 (such as the different types of energy weapons, for example), it would be preferable to specify on your army lists the types and kinds of weapons carried by your miniatures (sword, mace or halberd or others..) in order to avoid confusion on the day. Furthermore, during the tournament if a doubt arises regarding the type of weapon and nothing is specified on the list, the wysiwig will take precedence to determine the weapon used during the tournament. so if you have an energy weapon assimilated to a mace, it will be an energy mace and etc..\", \"sampled\": \"Given the significant changes to close combat weapons in V6 (the different types of energy weapons, for example), it would be preferable to specify on your army lists the types and categories of weapons carried by your miniatures (sword, mace, halberd, etc.) to avoid confusion on the day. During the tournament, if a doubt arises regarding the category of a weapon and nothing is specified on the list, WYSIWYG will take precedence to determine the weapon used, so if you have an energy weapon resembling a mace, it will be an energy mace, and so on.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Joined the Cabinet Office in 2006. Engaged in planning economic policy, writing the White Paper on the Economy and Finance, and promoting economic statistics reform. After joining CyberAgent in 2017, engaged in analysis using data from \\\"AirTrack,\\\" an advertising product utilizing location information. Specializes in labor economics, health economics, and macroeconomics. Completed doctoral program in Economics at the State University of New York at Albany in 2015. Doctor of Philosophy in Economics. List of blog articles Click here for consultation on casual meetings & list of available positions Can “local knowledge” be learned?\", \"sampled\": \"He joined the Cabinet Office in 2006. There, he worked on economic policy planning and drafting, contributed to the Annual Report on the Japanese Economy and Public Finance, and promoted economic statistics reform. After joining CyberAgent in 2017, he focused on analysis using data from the location-based advertising product “AirTrack.” His specialties are labor economics, health economics, and macroeconomics. He earned a Ph.D. in Economics from the University at Albany, SUNY in 2015. He holds a doctorate in economics. For a list of blog posts, casual meeting consultations, and open positions, see here. Can a “sense of place” be learned? Data Science Daisuke Moriwaki, Kazufune Munemasa, Toshikazu Fukami JSAI2018. For inquiries, contact us here.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"From July 19th to 21st, our school's traditional \\\"Asagiri Camp\\\" was held over three days and two nights. This year's key goal is “to find what you can do and take action.” It was three days where each student acted with their goal in mind within vertically divided groups consisting of 4th, 5th, and 6th graders. In a sharing session among children from a certain group after the activities, a 4th grader said, “I want to connect the five-minute preparedness we learned at camp to school life,” a 5th grader said, “I inherited the tradition from the 6th graders, so I am looking forward to next year’s camp,” and a 6th grader said, “Going to camp made me realize once again the importance of ‘unity’ and ‘cooperation.’ I don’t think I will forget my precious friends. I want to utilize unity and cooperation in school life as well.” Another one of our school’s traditions has been passed down.\", \"sampled\": \"The traditional \\\"Asagiri Camp,\\\" a three-day, two-night event, was held from July 19 to 21. This year's key objective was \\\"Find what you can do and take action.\\\" Over the three days, students in vertically grouped teams of fourth, fifth, and sixth graders acted with that goal in mind. In a sharing session after the activities, a fourth grader said, \\\"I want to carry the habit of acting five minutes early that I learned at camp into school life,\\\" a fifth grader reflected, \\\"Because I inherited the tradition from the sixth graders, I'm now looking forward to next year's camp,\\\" and a sixth grader shared, \\\"Going to camp made me realize once again the importance of 'unity' and 'cooperation'; I will never forget my important friends, and I want to apply unity and cooperation to our school life.\\\" Another piece of our school's tradition has been passed on.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Hundred Year Tea 2012-09-27 It suddenly became cool, and I started wanting a warm drink. At our clinic, we have tea prepared in the waiting room so you can rest a little after treatment. This tea is called \\\"Hyakunencha\\\" and is made in Hiraizumi, Iwate Prefecture. It's a blend of 24 types of plants, mushrooms, and seaweed, including adlay, houttuynia cordata, sasa bamboo leaves, eucommia ulmoides leaves, loquat leaves, goji berries, jujubes, shiitake mushrooms, and kelp... etc. In the morning, put one teabag in a kettle, simmer for 7-8 minutes over low heat after boiling, and place it in a pot in the waiting room. We have been serving it since the opening of the clinic, and often hear that it is mellow and delicious. I drink it every day myself… and I never get tired of it. The accompanying chocolate is also popular.\", \"sampled\": \"The weather has turned cool all of a sudden, making warm drinks appealing again. At our clinic we keep tea in the waiting room so patients can rest a little after treatment. It is called Hyakunen-cha, a tea produced in Hiraizumi, Iwate Prefecture. The blend combines twenty-four kinds of plants, mushrooms, and seaweeds — including Job’s tears, dokudami, kumazasa, eucommia leaves, loquat leaves, goji berries, jujube, shiitake, and kelp. Every morning we put one tea bag into a kettle, bring it to a boil, simmer it over low heat for seven or eight minutes, and then pour it into a pot in the waiting room. We have served it since the clinic opened, and people often tell us it is mellow and delicious. I drink it every day myself and never get tired of it. The chocolates served alongside are also popular.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Committee Decisions CONF 001 VI.38 Tai National Park (Ivory Coast) The Committee noted with satisfaction that this park benefited from significant support from German cooperation and WWF for research and conservation of the site and the future integration of peripheral areas into an overall development plan. Threats still weighed on the site, due to poaching and the arrival of refugees from Liberia in this area. The Bureau consequently wished to encourage the partners of Ivory Coast to strengthen the current project, taking into account the work already carried out by the Taï research station within the framework of UNESCO's Man and the Biosphere (MAB) program.\", \"sampled\": \"Decisions of the Committee CONF 001 VI.38 Taï National Park (Côte d'Ivoire) The Committee noted with satisfaction that the park was receiving significant support from German cooperation and WWF for research and conservation of the site and the future integration of peripheral zones into an overall management plan. Threats still weighed on the site, however, due to poaching and the arrival of Liberian refugees in the area. The Bureau therefore wished to encourage Côte d'Ivoire's partners to strengthen the ongoing project, taking into account the work already carried out by the Taï research station within the framework of UNESCO's Man and the Biosphere (MAB) programme.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"［Brussels, 1st Reuters］ - The preliminary figure for the Eurozone Consumer Price Index (HICP) in March, released by the European Union (EU) Statistical Office, rose 1.4% year-on-year, slowing down from the previous month (a 1.5% increase). Market expectations were for a 1.5% increase. By item, energy prices accelerated significantly from the previous month (3.6% increase) to a 5.3% increase year-on-year, while others slowed down. The core inflation rate (excluding energy and unprocessed food), which the European Central Bank (ECB) focuses on, rose 1.0% year-on-year, slowing down from the previous month (1.2% increase). Another core inflation rate watched by economists (excluding energy, food, alcohol, and tobacco) also slowed down its rise from 1.0% to 0.8%. ECB Executive Board member Lautenschläger told an Austrian newspaper that political uncertainty could mean it will take longer for inflation in the Eurozone to rise. Also, the unemployment rate in the Eurozone in February remained unchanged from January at 7.8%. The number of unemployed people decreased from 12.807 million to 12.73 million.\", \"sampled\": \"Eurozone annual inflation slowed to 1.4 percent in March from 1.5 percent in February, according to a flash estimate from the European Union’s statistics office. The figure came in below the market forecast of 1.5 percent. Energy prices surged 5.3 percent year‑on‑year, accelerating sharply from 3.6 percent the previous month, while other components decelerated. The core inflation rate watched by the European Central Bank, which strips out energy and unprocessed food, eased to 1.0 percent from 1.2 percent. Another core measure that excludes energy, food, alcohol, and tobacco also slowed, from 1.0 percent to 0.8 percent. ECB board member Sabine Lautenschläger told an Austrian newspaper that political uncertainty means it will take longer for eurozone inflation to pick up. Separately, the statistics office reported that the eurozone unemployment rate held steady at 7.8 percent in February. The number of jobless people fell from 12.807 million to 12.73 million.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Syrian rebels have captured one of the two pilots of the military plane shot down on Wednesday in northwestern Syria, according to witnesses on the ground. The two pilots of the fighter-bomber, which was reportedly hit by a missile earlier in the day according to a Syrian NGO, ejected from the aircraft with parachutes. One of them has been taken prisoner while the fate of the second remains unknown. This is the second aircraft hit by a missile, still according to the Syrian NGO. On Tuesday, this time an army helicopter was the target of the rebels.\", \"sampled\": \"Syrian rebels captured one of the two pilots from the military plane shot down Wednesday in northwestern Syria, according to witnesses on the ground. The two pilots of the fighter-bomber, downed earlier in the day by a missile according to a Syrian NGO, ejected from the aircraft with parachutes. One of them was taken prisoner while the fate of the second remains unknown. It is the second aircraft hit by a missile, still according to the Syrian NGO. On Tuesday, an army helicopter was the rebels' target.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The image is located on the altar of the Basilica of Carthage, in Costa Rica, placed on a pedestal resembling half a world, with a lily of 6 petals on each of which rests a small angel. Above the lily we find a crescent moon and facing it a seraphim with raised hands holding the mantle that covers the Virgin.\", \"sampled\": \"The image is located on the altar of the Basilica of Carthage in Costa Rica, placed on a pedestal resembling a half-world with a lily of 6 petals, on each of which a small angel is perched. Above the lily we find a half-moon, and in front of it a seraph with raised hands holding the mantle that covers the Virgin.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Carel Hüls Painter of Belgian nationality about whom very few biographical details can be found online. He studied art at the Academy of Antwerp and in Mechelen, but he also defines himself as self-taught. The latter is absolutely false, because this is not the painter's purpose; he plasters, for example, space on a plane with such quality that the observer has to see that there must be a very deep knowledge of the subject. His portraits and still lifes, usual themes in his painting, are endowed with great beauty and sensitivity in which blue tones predominate. He has exhibited in numerous Galleries, Fairs and Art Exhibitions. His work is represented in important public and private collections around the world and in museums in various countries. He lives and works in Brussels. ** Entry published on this blog on February 4, 2011. It has been updated and expanded on December 30, 2013.\", \"sampled\": \"Carel Hüls Pintor, a Belgian national, has very scarce biographical data available online. He studied art at the Academy of Antwerp and in Mechelen, yet defines himself as self-taught. This last claim is absolutely false, because it is not the painter's purpose; he depicts, for example, space on a plane with such quality that the observer must see that a very deep knowledge of the subject is required. His portraits and still lifes, common themes in his painting, are endowed with great beauty and sensitivity, with blue tones predominating. He has exhibited in numerous galleries, fairs, and art shows. His work is represented in important public and private collections worldwide and in museums across several countries. He lives and works in Brussels. ** Entry published on this blog on February 4, 2011, updated and expanded on December 30, 2013.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Actor Channing Tatum revealed a new hairstyle on his Instagram story, sparking discussion that he looks just like rapper Eminem. With the caption \\\"Bad idea maybe? Haha,\\\" he unveiled his new hairstyle—blond hair! For Channing, who had been repeatedly cutting his naturally black hair short or letting it grow slightly, this is quite a dramatic makeover. @channingtatum IG stories After showing off his new style, he also posted a comment that could be interpreted as regretting, “Well, ‘bad idea’ is kind of my adjective anyway... so…” (emoji of a man shrugging). @channingtatum IG stories What does his girlfriend Jessie J think of this bold makeover? We look forward to updates!\", \"sampled\": \"Actor Channing Tatum revealed a new hairstyle on his Instagram story, sparking buzz that he looks just like rapper Eminem. He revealed his new hairstyle, which isなんと blonde, with the caption, \\\"Bad idea? Haha.\\\" This is quite an image change for Channing, who had been alternating between cutting his natural black hair short and letting it grow out a bit. After showing off his new style, he also posted a comment that could be taken as him internally regretting going too far, saying, \\\"Well, 'bad idea' is my adjective. So... (male shrugging emoji).\\\" What does his girlfriend, Jessie J, think of this bold image change?\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Committee Decisions CONF 208 VIII.85 Pirin National Park (Bulgaria) VIII.85 The Committee noted the concerns raised by the Spatial Development Plan (SDP), which is expected to lead to new cumulative development in the remaining most important part. It requested the State Party to ensure that tourism development would not take place in the remaining part of the SDP in the future. The Committee strongly urged that the mission invited by the State Party be carried out as soon as possible.\", \"sampled\": \"Decisions of the Committee CONF 208 VIII.85 Pirin National Park (Bulgaria) VIII.85 The Committee noted the concerns raised by the Territorial Development Plan (TDP), which is expected to lead to new cumulative development in the most important remaining part. It requested the State Party to ensure that no tourism development takes place in the remaining part of the TDP in the future. The Committee urged that the mission invited by the State Party be carried out as soon as possible.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"© Photo Vincent Graton Martin Senecal, coach of the Wolves. The Wolves of Cure-Antoine-Labelle suffered their first setback within the school football league of the juvenile category, division 1, on October 13th in Quebec City. Martin Senecal’s team lost a battle of titans by suffering a 14-7 defeat against the Blizzard of Seminare Saint-Francois. The two undefeated teams shared the top spot in the overall standings before the clash. The Wolves’ defense did well against the second best offense in the league. Lineman Benedicte Cayo and halfback Benjamin-Charles Corriveau stood out in the defeat. The latter achieved two interceptions. The stalled attack The offense of the Laval formation encountered difficulties against the best defense in the circuit. Running back Gabriel Roger scored the only major for his side on the last play of the first half. The next game for the Wolves will take place against the Sphinx of Jacques-Rousseau high school on Saturday, October 20th, in Longueuil. Senecal’s troops will end the season on Saturday, October 27th by hosting College Notre-Dame, at 7:30 p.m., at Stade Roi-du-Nord.\", \"sampled\": \"The Loups de Curé-Antoine-Labelle suffered their first loss in the juvenile division 1 school football league on October 13 in Quebec City. Martin Sénéchal’s squad lost a titan battle, suffering a 14-7 setback against the Séminaire Saint-François Blizzard. The two undefeated teams shared first place in the overall standings before the matchup. The Loups' defense performed well against the league's second-best offense. Lineman Benedicte Cayo and defensive back Benjamin-Charles Corriveau distinguished themselves in the loss. The latter managed two interceptions. The Laval team’s offense struggled against the circuit's best defense. Running back Gabriel Roger scored his team's only major on the last play of the first half. The Loups' next game will be against the Sphinx from Jacques-Rousseau secondary school on Saturday, October 20, in Longueuil. Sénéchal’s players will finish the season on Saturday, October 27, by hosting Collège Notre-Dame at 7:30 p.m. at Roi-du-Nord stadium.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A Million Down the Drain The Remake Original Title: A Million Down the Drain The Remake Synopsis: A remake of José Mª Forqué's classic. Pepe, a gray and humble man, is a street sweeper and with each sweep of his broom, he cleans the streets of the city. When, one day, he finds a million in a trash can, he decides to keep it to solve his pressing financial problems. Award for Best Production Design and Paramount Channel Award for Best Remake Your life asks for cinema at NotodoFilmFest.\", \"sampled\": \"A Million in the Garbage The Remake, original title Un millón en la basura El remake, is a remake of the classic film by Jose Mª Forqué. Pepe, an unassuming and humble man, is a street sweeper who cleans the city's streets with the sweep of his broom. When he one day finds a million in a garbage can, he decides to keep it to solve his pressing financial problems. It won the Award for Best Production Management and the Paramount Channel Award for Best Remake Your Life Is Asking for a Movie at NotodoFilmFest.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The Elinchrom EL 26117 Rotagrid for Rotalux 70x70 cm is a diffuser designed to be placed on the front of the Rotalux 70x70 cm (27x27\\\") softbox, softening the light and controlling the light by directing the diffused light inward a relatively narrow pattern forward from the softbox, while preventing light from spilling to the sides. For soft yet directional light.\", \"sampled\": \"The Elinchrom EL 26117 Rotagrid for Rotalux 70x70 cm is a diffuser designed to be placed on the front of the Rotalux 70x70 cm (27x27\\\") softbox, softening and controlling light by directing the diffused light inward in a relatively narrow forward pattern from the softbox while preventing light from spilling to the sides. For soft but directional light. Specifications Barcode 719821300670 Brand ELINCHROM.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Call for abstracts: \\\"Guarani Aquifer System Management: A Cooperation Example\\\" September 21-23, 2011, São Paulo Abstract submission deadline: April 30, 2011 In August 2010, Argentina, Brazil, Paraguay and Uruguay, countries sharing the Guarani Aquifer System (SAG), signed a new agreement for the management of this complex system. The four countries are now partners in the ratification process and in negotiations on institutional aspects, including discussions regarding an annex to the agreement on arbitration procedures. In this context, the management of the SAG can benefit from a discussion on the measures taken so far and on the challenges that remain to be addressed. The conference on “Guarani Aquifer System Management: A Cooperation Example” provides an opportunity for dialogue and the organizers invite researchers, advisors and water management experts to submit an abstract for one of the three conference sessions: - an evaluation of scientific knowledge about the SAG - Current use and protection of the Guarani Aquifer System - The SAG and regional cooperation The conference contributes to UNESCO's ISARM program (management of shared aquifer resources) and is supported by the following partners: Associacao Brasileira de Aguas Subterraneas, Associacao Brasilerira de Recursos Hidricos, Sociedade Brasileira de Geologia De Engenharia and Sociedade Brasileira de Geologia.\", \"sampled\": \"Call for abstracts: \\\"The Management of the Guarani Aquifer System: An Example of Cooperation\\\" September 21-23, 2011, São Paulo Abstract deadline: April 30, 2011. In August 2010, Argentina, Brazil, Paraguay, and Uruguay, the countries that share the Guarani Aquifer System (GAS), signed a new agreement for the management of this complex system. The four countries are now partners in the ratification process and in negotiations on institutional aspects, including discussions concerning an annex to the agreement on arbitration procedures. In this context, the management of the GAS can benefit from a discussion on the measures that have been taken so far and on the challenges that still need to be faced. The conference on \\\"The Management of the Guarani Aquifer System: An Example of Cooperation\\\" provides an opportunity for dialogue, and the organizers invite researchers, advisors, and water management experts to propose an abstract for one of the three conference sessions: - an assessment of scientific knowledge on the GAS - The current use and protection of the Guarani Aquifer System - The GAS and regional cooperation; the conference contributes to UNESCO's ISARM program (Internationally Shared Aquifer Resources Management) and is supported by the following partners: Associacao Brasileira de Aguas Subterraneas, Associacao Brasilerira de Recursos Hidricos, Sociedade Brasileira de Geologia De Engenharia, and Sociedade Brasileira de Geologia.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"tabico Kagoshima's tourist spots Kagoshima Zoo, Aquarium, Botanical Garden Ibusuki & Makurazaki Zoo, Aquarium, Botanical Garden Kaen Yama foothills Fragrance Garden Kaen Yama foothills Fragrance Garden Q&A Kaen Yama foothills Fragrance Garden (Kagoshima / Zoo, Aquarium, Botanical Garden) 2.50 (Previous 2.50) Tweet Detailed information Map & Access Weather Parking Kaen Yama foothills Fragrance Garden Q&A Information provided: No questions found. Ask a new question This Q&A content uses OKWave. Please use OKWave when asking a new question.\", \"sampled\": \"tabico Kagoshima Tourist Spots Kagoshima Zoos, Aquariums, Botanical Gardens Ibusuki/Makurazaki Zoos, Aquariums, Botanical Gardens Kaimon Sanroku Fragrance Garden Q&A for Kaimon Sanroku Fragrance Garden Kaimon Sanroku Fragrance Garden (Kagoshima / Zoos, Aquariums, Botanical Gardens) 2.50 (Previous: 2.50) Tweet Detailed Information Map/Access Weather Parking Q&A for Kaimon Sanroku Fragrance Garden Information Provided By: No questions were found. Ask a new question. This Q&A content uses OKWave; please use OKWave to ask a new question.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The Aichi Prefectural Police designate the 11th of each month as \\\"Pedestrian Crossing Day\\\" and carry out public awareness activities such as calling for the protection of pedestrian crossings, strengthening guidance and enforcement against violations such as obstruction, and inspecting and maintaining road signs and markings, thereby fostering drivers' awareness of protecting pedestrians and promoting pedestrian use. On July 11th, the first \\\"Pedestrian Crossing Day,\\\" the Nishi Police Station practiced safe ways to cross pedestrian crossings and called for pedestrian protection using hand plates near a large shopping center (photo). Children from local nurseries and elderly people participated. Officers distributed educational materials while publicizing \\\"Pedestrian Crossing Day\\\" and carried out guidance and enforcement against violations such as obstructing pedestrian crossings.\", \"sampled\": \"The Aichi Prefectural Police has designated the 11th of every month as \\\"Crosswalk Day,\\\" and through public awareness campaigns calling for the protection of crossing pedestrians, strengthened enforcement of obstruction violations, and the inspection and maintenance of road signs and markings, it aims to foster drivers' awareness of pedestrian protection and promote pedestrian use of crosswalks. On the first \\\"Crosswalk Day\\\" on July 11, the Nishi Police Station held an event at a crosswalk near a large shopping center, where they practiced safe crossing methods and used hand plates to call for pedestrian protection (pictured). Preschool children and elderly residents from the jurisdiction participated. Officers publicized \\\"Crosswalk Day\\\" while distributing awareness items and conducted enforcement against violations such as obstructing crossing pedestrians.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"In any case, selling without an inspection certificate is prohibited, so there's no point in trying to find out what needs repairing. Where did you see that? If I know that an inspection certificate is mandatory to obtain the registration document for used cars (over 4 years old), I don't see this requirement anywhere during the sale… consumer code? The seller must necessarily provide an inspection certificate with the sale of the vehicle, even if this certificate is refused (sale for parts or restoration), thus he is covered with respect to the buyer, the latter knew what to expect at the time of purchase, the report was definitive. Afterwards, for the buyer, an OK inspection certificate is required to be able to register the vehicle.\", \"sampled\": \"In any case, selling without a technical inspection is forbidden, so there is no point in trying to figure out what needs repairing. Where did you see that? While I know that the technical inspection is mandatory to get the registration certificate for used cars over 4 years old, I do not see this obligation for the sale anywhere in the consumer code. The seller must provide a technical inspection when selling the vehicle, even if the inspection is failed for a sale for parts or for restoration, so the seller is covered with respect to the buyer, who knew what to expect when purchasing the vehicle, with the report serving as proof. Afterward, the buyer needs an approved technical inspection to be able to register the vehicle.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The wireless data synchronization and wireless email reconciliation features are designed to synchronize organizer data (contacts, calendar entries, tasks, and memos) and reconcile emails between your BlackBerry smartphone and the messaging application on your computer over the wireless network. In rare cases where your smartphone does not recognize a contact field, calendar entry, or messaging account, some data or emails may not be synchronized or reconciled. If wireless data synchronization is not available on your smartphone or if you have disabled this feature, you can use BlackBerry Desktop Software to synchronize your organizer data. For more information, see the help in BlackBerry Desktop Software. Research In Motion recommends that you regularly create a backup file on your computer, especially before any software update. Maintaining an up-to-date backup file on your computer could allow you to recover smartphone data in case of loss, theft, or corruption due to an unforeseen issue.\", \"sampled\": \"The wireless data synchronization and wireless email reconciliation features are designed to synchronize organizer data (contacts, calendar entries, tasks, and memos) and reconcile email messages between your BlackBerry smartphone and the email application on your computer over the wireless network. In the rare event that your smartphone does not recognize a contact field, calendar entry, or email account, some data or email messages might not be synchronized or reconciled. If wireless data synchronization is not available on your smartphone or if you have turned off this feature, you can use BlackBerry Desktop Software to synchronize your organizer data. For more information, see the BlackBerry Desktop Software Help. Research In Motion recommends that you regularly create a backup file on your computer, especially before you update any software. Maintaining a current backup file on your computer might allow you to recover smartphone data if your smartphone is lost, stolen, or corrupted by an unforeseen issue.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Solar energy Solar energy transforms solar radiation into electricity or heat, depending on the technologies. - Solar photovoltaic energy produces electricity via photovoltaic modules, electricity which can then be injected onto electrical grids. - Solar thermal energy produces heat which can be used for domestic heating or the production of hot water. - Finally, concentrated solar power produces electricity via heat generation.\", \"sampled\": \"Solar energy transforms solar radiation into electricity or heat, depending on the technologies. Photovoltaic solar energy generates electricity through photovoltaic modules, which can then be fed into electrical grids. Solar thermal energy produces heat for domestic heating or hot water production. Lastly, solar thermal power generates electricity through heat production. NEWS Latest Publications See also Energy-Climate Overview: Figures and Statistics.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Straps are that type of accessory that you won't have to replace for a long time if you know how to buy quality and choose the most suitable one. In an article published by Business Insider, Dan Trepanier of Articles of Style shows the straps (taken from his own personal collection) that should not be missing in any man's wardrobe. Without a doubt, these six types of strap will make you look elegant and properly dressed for any occasion. The black dress strap. This is the most formal strap in your collection. A strap should never be worn with a tuxedo or other traditional formal attire, but if you have a good suit that requires something black and elegant, we recommend a thin leather strap (1\\\"- 1 1/4\\\") with a simple silver buckle. The fine suede strap. The key to a good design is the combination of elements that are consistent in their level of formality and purpose. Look at this belt for example: leather is traditionally elegant, but suede is a little more casual.\", \"sampled\": \"Belts are the kind of accessories that you won't have to replace for a long time if you know how to buy quality and choose the most suitable one. In an article published by Business Insider, Dan Trepanier from Articles of Style showcases belts (taken from his own personal collection) that should be a staple in any man's wardrobe. Without a doubt, these six types of belts will make you look elegant and properly dressed for any occasion. The black dress belt. This is the most formal belt in your collection. You should never wear a belt with a tuxedo or other traditional formal attire, but if you have a nice suit that requires something black and elegant, we recommend a thin leather belt (1\\\"- 1 1/4\\\") with a simple silver buckle. The thin suede belt. The key to good design is the combination of elements that are consistent in their level of formality and purpose. Take this belt for example: leather is traditionally elegant, but suede is a bit more casual.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Notice Regarding Designation for Hometown Tax Donation by the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications 2019.05.16 Regarding the designation for hometown tax donation by the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications We sincerely appreciate your support regarding our city's hometown tax donation system. We have received designation from the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications for a period of 4 months, from June 1st to September 30th this year. Donations received during this period will be eligible for deductions from your resident tax, etc., just as they were previously. Also, the current return gifts have been revised to comply with national standards. We kindly ask for your continued patronage. Furthermore, we plan to apply for re-designation starting in October.\", \"sampled\": \"Notice from the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications Regarding Hometown Tax Payment Designation 2019.05.16 Regarding the hometown tax payment in this city, thank you very much for your support. We have received a designation from the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications for a period of four months from June 1st to September 30th this year. Donations received during this period will continue to be eligible for deduction from your local resident tax as before. The current gift offerings have been reviewed to align with national standards. We appreciate your continued patronage. We plan to apply for re-designation for October and beyond.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The FUSB will provide financial assistance and job training to 26 refugee entrepreneurs of various nationalities in La Paz, Santa Cruz, and Cochabamba. The United States Embassy supports 26 families. Photo: Twitter Embassy Source: ANF In order to finance refugee ventures in Bolivia, the United States Embassy disbursed $24,750 to the Scalabrini Bolivia Foundation (FUSB) to contribute to these small entrepreneurs achieving better economic integration in the country. \\\"The United States is proud to help the economic integration of refugees and migrants in Bolivia. We hope this financial support helps the most vulnerable recover from the impacts of COVID-19,\\\" said Charisse Phillips, Chargé d'Affaires of the United States Embassy. Through this donation, the FUSB will provide financial assistance and job training to 26 refugee entrepreneurs of various nationalities in La Paz, Santa Cruz, and Cochabamba, whose businesses have been affected by COVID-19. The program will be developed over a year. \\\"For us, this project is very important because we are always looking for the well-being of all migrant families, especially the most vulnerable. We hope that with this financial support these people have a more dignified life in the country,\\\" commented Father Gregorio Alejo, representative of the Foundation. Since October 2020, the United States has provided $2.1 million in humanitarian assistance to support refugees and migrants in Bolivia.\", \"sampled\": \"The U.S. Embassy will provide financial assistance and job training to 26 refugee entrepreneurs from different nationalities in La Paz, Santa Cruz, and Cochabamba. The United States Embassy supports 26 families. Photo: Twitter Embassy Source: ANF In order to fund refugee ventures in Bolivia, the United States Embassy disbursed $24,750 to the Scalabrini Bolivia Foundation (FUSB) to help these small entrepreneurs achieve better economic integration in the country. \\\"The United States is proud to support the economic integration of refugees and migrants in Bolivia. We hope this financial support will help the most vulnerable recover from the impacts of COVID-19,\\\" said Charisse Phillips, Charge d'Affaires of the United States Embassy. Through this donation, the FUSB will provide financial assistance and job training to 26 refugee entrepreneurs of different nationalities in La Paz, Santa Cruz, and Cochabamba, whose businesses have been affected by COVID-19. The program will run for a year. \\\"This project is very important to us because we always seek the well-being of all migrant families, especially the most vulnerable. We hope that with this financial support, these individuals will have a more dignified life in the country,\\\" commented Father Gregorio Alejo, a representative of the Foundation. Since October 2020, the United States has provided $2.1 million in humanitarian assistance to support refugees and migrants in Bolivia.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"On race street 8th, between streets 5 and 6 is located a neoclassical building constructed on land donated in 1957 by the Society for Public Improvements to the Diocese of Sonsón Rionegro so that the Episcopal Palace would be built. In the image we see, on the left side, the western side of the Episcopal Palace; and on the right side an old building where some dependencies of the Cathedral Parish function such as the Capiro Radio Station, the Parish Office and the Museum of Religious Art.\", \"sampled\": \"On 8th Street, between 5th and 6th streets, stands a neocolonial building constructed on a plot donated in 1957 by the Society for Public Improvements to the Diocese of Sonsón Rionegro for the construction of the Episcopal Palace. The image shows the western side of the Episcopal Palace on the left and an old building on the right housing some offices of the Cathedral Parish, such as Radio Capiro Station, the Parish Office, and the Museum of Religious Art.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Bejaia Bejaïa, one of the oldest cities in Algeria, is often referred to by its French name, Bougie, which was its official name during the colonial period. Initially known in Europe for the quality of its beeswax candles, from which it derived its name – candles – Bejaïa also played an essential role in the dissemination of Arabic numerals in the West. Located on the Algerian coast, it now constitutes one of the most touristic regions of the country because it holds sites of rare beauty, between sea and mountain. The rest of the city's activity is based on the port industry: mainly the export of hydrocarbons, but also fishing. Bejaïa thus represents the main growth center of the region. Population: 200,000 inhabitants.\", \"sampled\": \"Bejaia, one of the oldest cities in Algeria, is often referred to by its French name Bougie, which was its official name during the colonial period. Initially known in Europe for the quality of its beeswax candles that gave them their name, Bejaia also played a crucial role in spreading Arabic numerals in the West. Located on the Algerian coast, it is now one of the country's most touristy regions, boasting sites of rare beauty between the sea and the mountains. The city's main economic activity revolves around its port industry, primarily exporting hydrocarbons and fishing. Bejaia thus stands as the region's main growth hub with a population of 200,000 inhabitants.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The United States is preparing a new package of sanctions against Russia for the poisoning in August 2020 of opposition leader Alexei Navalny, White House National Security Advisor Jake Sullivan said this Sunday. \\\"We are preparing another package of sanctions to apply in this case,\\\" Sullivan revealed in an interview on CNN. The official insisted that U.S. President Joe Biden intends to respond harshly to Moscow's \\\"harmful activities,\\\" including the situation of Navalny. The poisoning and imprisonment of that opposition leader was one of the topics that focused the meeting in Geneva between Biden and his Russian counterpart, Vladimir Putin. At that meeting, Biden warned Putin that there would be devastating consequences for Russia if Navalny dies. The opposition leader came close to dying last August when he was poisoned during a trip to Siberia and has accused Putin of ordering the Federal Security Service (FSB, former KGB) to assassinate him. His health has deteriorated since he entered prison in March at a penal center famous for numerous reports of abuse and torture.\", \"sampled\": \"The United States is preparing a new package of sanctions against Russia for the poisoning in August 2020 of opposition leader Alexei Navalny, said White House National Security Advisor Jake Sullivan on Sunday. \\\"We are preparing another package of sanctions to apply in this case,\\\" Sullivan revealed in an interview on CNN. The official stressed that U.S. President Joe Biden intends to respond firmly to Moscow's \\\"harmful activities,\\\" including Navalny's situation. The poisoning and imprisonment of the opposition leader were among the topics discussed at the meeting in Geneva between Biden and his Russian counterpart, Vladimir Putin. During that meeting, Biden warned Putin that there would be devastating consequences for Russia if Navalny dies. The opposition leader nearly died last August after being poisoned during a trip to Siberia and has accused Putin of ordering his assassination by the Federal Security Service (FSB, former KGB). His health has deteriorated since he entered a penitentiary center in March known for numerous reports of abuses and torture.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Aichi Prefecture announced on the 19th that it will conduct a demonstration experiment running two self-driving vehicles simultaneously on Centrair Island (Tokoname City), with one person remotely monitoring them. The cars receive information sent from wireless communication equipment installed in traffic lights, enabling deceleration and stopping even in environments where cameras that recognize signals are not functioning. A test run will be conducted on March 3rd, and monitors recruited from the public will take rides between the 7th and 8th. A course of approximately 2 kilometers including public roads will be set up within the island. Two self-driving vehicles will run side by side, front and back, at around 20 kilometers per hour. The prefecture has commissioned this year's self-driving demonstration project to companies such as Aisantechnology, Okayama Steel Works, and Nagoya University. It has already been running two self-driving vehicles simultaneously in experiments in Toyohashi City and Ichinomiya City. This time, D.A. and Nippon Signal will also participate.\", \"sampled\": \"Aichi Prefecture announced on the 19th that it would conduct a demonstration experiment at Chubu Centrair International Airport Island in Tokoname City, running two autonomous vehicles simultaneously with remote monitoring by one person. The cars receive information from wireless communication devices installed on traffic signals, enabling them to slow down or stop even in environments where cameras for signal recognition do not function. Test runs are scheduled for March 3rd, with monitors from the general public taking test rides on the 7th and 8th. A course of about 2 kilometers, including public roads, is set within the airport island. The two autonomous vehicles will run in tandem at around 20 kilometers per hour. The prefecture has entrusted this year's autonomous driving demonstration project to companies like Aisan Technology, Okaya Steel, Nagoya University, among others. They have previously run two autonomous vehicles simultaneously in experiments in Toyohashi City and Ichinomiya City. This time, D. ENA and Nippon Signal are also participating.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Where are you creating your work? Q: Question Where are you usually creating your work? Questioner: K.W. from Ibaraki Prefecture, and many others A: Answer Since graduating from the Tokyo University of the Arts Graduate School in 1998, I have been kindly allowed to use part of the material storage area of in Sakuragawa City, Ibaraki Prefecture, my hometown, as a production space. After that, while working as a part-time lecturer in the sculpture department of the Geidai (Tokyo University of the Arts) from 2008 to 2013, I was producing works at the stone carving workshop on the Ueno campus of Geidai. Since 2014, I have been creating works at a place called located somewhere in Toride City, Ibaraki Prefecture. Many people, including K.W., ask this question, but I have compiled an article about the current state of , which I am using as a workspace, on the page within the section of this website, so if you take a look there, I think you will get a rough idea of what the production location looks like.\", \"sampled\": \"Where do you usually create your works? Q: Question Where do you usually create your works? Questioner: K.W. from Ibaraki Prefecture Answer: Response Since graduating from Tokyo University of the Arts graduate school in 1998, I had been creating my works with the kind permission of Markeiwatanabe Stone Shop in Sakuragawa City, Ibaraki Prefecture, my hometown. Between 2008 and 2013, while serving as a part-time lecturer at my alma mater's sculpture department, I created my works at the stone carving workshop on the Ueno campus of the university. Since 2014, I have been creating my works at a place known as Atelier Kura in Toride City, Ibaraki Prefecture. I often receive questions from many people, including K.W., about this. However, if you visit the Atelier page on this website's Blog section, you can see an article with photos showing the current state of Atelier Kura, where I currently work, giving you an idea of my workspace.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Faculty Graduate School Professional Graduate School Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology: Private University Research Branding Project Research Strategy Council Research Achievements Research Institute Research and Development Organization Research Promotion Industry-Academia-Government Collaboration Compliance Health Center New News November 25, 2016 The season when acute gastroenteritis outbreaks occur is approaching. In particular, acute gastroenteritis caused by norovirus can cause large-scale epidemics in schools and workplaces, so caution is necessary. To prevent infection, first wash your hands thoroughly. Also, be careful when handling feces and vomit to prevent the spread of infection. In addition, if you have symptoms such as diarrhea or nausea, consult a medical institution promptly. Grasp accurate information and strive to take appropriate measures.\", \"sampled\": \"The Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology: Private University Research Branding Project, Research Strategy Meeting, Research Achievements, Research Institute, Research and Development Organization, Research Promotion, Industry-Academia-Government Collaboration, Compliance, Health Center News Update, November 25, 2016. The peak season for acute gastroenteritis is approaching. In particular, acute gastroenteritis caused by norovirus can lead to large outbreaks in schools and workplaces, so caution is necessary. To prevent infection, start by thoroughly washing your hands. To prevent the spread of infection, pay close attention to the handling of feces and vomit. Additionally, if you experience symptoms such as diarrhea or nausea, consult a medical institution promptly. Let's ensure we have accurate information and strive for appropriate responses.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Vacation rentals in Cefalù Search and book a unique accommodation on Airbnb Top-rated vacation rentals in Cefalù Guests agree: these stays have excellent ratings for location and cleanliness, among other things. Residence · Cefalù A magical place by the sea... Casa La Vela A magical place by the sea... Casa La Vela is a cozy and elegant apartment located right above the rocks in Cefalu, an incredible Sicilian town. The house is located on Via Bordonaro, one of the oldest and most vibrant streets in the historic center, with many wine bars, shops and restaurants. Marina beach, where Cinema Paradiso was filmed, is just a 3-minute walk from the house. Piazza Duomo, with its impressive Norman cathedral, is within easy reach. Jan 29 – Feb 5 $1,933 MXN per night Superhost Private room · Cefalù Cefalú Bastione Spacious room located in the heart of Cefalù. The central location of this accommodation allows you to easily access everything you need for a vacation in our Pearl of the Tyrrhenian Sea. Next door there are restaurants, wine bars, but also a grocery store. About 10 minutes from the train station, within walking distance of the beach, the Cathedral, the Mandralisca Museum and much more.\", \"sampled\": \"Holiday rentals in Cefalú. Search and book a unique accommodation on Airbnb. The highest rated holiday rentals in Cefalú. Guests agree: these stays have excellent ratings for their location and cleanliness, among other aspects. Residence - Cefalú. A magical place by the sea...Casa La Vela. A magical place by the sea...Casa La Vela is a cozy and elegant apartment located right on the rocks in Cefalu, an amazing Sicilian village. The house is located on Via Bordonaro, one of the oldest and most vibrant streets in the historic center, with many wine bars, shops, and restaurants. The Marina beach, where Cinema Paradiso was filmed, is just a 3-minute walk from the house. The Piazza Duomo, with its impressive Norman cathedral, is a stone's throw away. January 29th to February 5th, $1,933 MXN per night. Superhost. Private room - Cefalú. Cefalú Bastione. Spacious room located in the heart of Cefalù. The central location of this accommodation allows easy access to everything you need for a holiday in our Pearl of the Tyrrhenian Sea. Next door, there are restaurants, wine bars, but also a grocery store. About 10 minutes from the train station, within walking distance of the beach, the Cathedral, the Mandralisca Museum, and much more. Room on the 3rd floor with stairs. January 28th to February 4th, $495 MXN per night.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"By Steve O’Grady: Senior Analyst and Co-founder of RedMonk. It has now become clear that Java is far from obsolete and outdated, and rumors of its demise have been greatly exaggerated. What is less clear however, is the future of Java, the language, versus the Java platform. Languages based on the JVM proliferate in an extravagant manner: Scala, Clojure and their siblings are familiar names, and even specialized languages like R are being ported to the JVM. What does this mean for the future of Java? Will they carry it forward in the future, or relegate it to the role of a low-level component? This question was explored through a mixture of quantitative data from sources such as GitHub, HackerNews and StackOverflow, as well as qualitative research on the evolution of technology and its directions. Results: Java, the language, is still growing As are languages based on the JVM The lesson: don't believe the hype, Java is not dead, it is still relevant.\", \"sampled\": \"By Steve O'Grady, a senior analyst and co-founder of RedMonk. It has now become evident that Java is far from obsolete and outdated, and rumors of its demise have been greatly exaggerated. What's less clear, however, is the future of Java, the language, in relation to the Java platform. JVM-based languages are proliferating in an extravagant manner: Scala, Clojure, and their siblings are familiar names, and even specialized languages like R are being ported to the JVM. What could this mean for the future of Java? Will they carry it forward in the future, or relegate it to the role of a low-level component? This question was explored through a mix of quantitative data from sources like GitHub, HackerNews, and StackOverflow, as well as qualitative research on the evolution of technology and its directions. The results: Java, the language, is still growing, as are JVM-based languages. The lesson: don't believe the hype, Java is not dead, it's still relevant. Khaled.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Top> Successful Denki University Students> Alumnus Hikozaka Wins the C Division \\\"Intelligent Information Research Group\\\" Award from the Institute of Electrical Engineers 2018.10.09 Sōsuke Hikozaka, who completed his studies in the Robotics and Mechatronics Program, received the Research Group Encouragement Award at the Institute of Electrical Engineers' 29th Electronic, Information, and Systems Division “Intelligent Information Research Group.” This award was given in recognition of his paper presentation at the research group held in Toyama on January 7-8, 2017, which was deemed to have contributed to the revitalization of the research group.\", \"sampled\": \"On October 9, 2018, it was announced that our alumnus, Mr. Hikosaka Munenosuke, who completed a major in Robotics and Mechatronics, had been awarded the Research Encouragement Award at the \\\"Intelligent Information Research Group\\\" of the Electronic, Information, and System Division of the Institute of Electrical Engineers of Japan for the year 2017. This accolade was bestowed in recognition of his research paper presentation, which took place on January 7-8 during his studies in 2017 at a research conference in Toyama, and was deemed to have significantly contributed to the stimulation of the research group's activities.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We were able to deliver 250 help cards this time, which we quoted at the end of last year. This is our first print job since introducing a printing machine, and it's a print on LIMEX sheet (simply put, paper made from stone). Since the help cards need to be \\\"waterproof and tear-resistant,\\\" we initially considered Yupo paper, but the manufacturer did not approve printing on Yupo paper with the printing machine introduced at our facility, so we used LIMEX sheet. We would appreciate it if municipalities within the prefecture would consider using us for their printing needs. Thank you.\", \"sampled\": \"Last year, we were able to deliver 250 help cards that we had estimated at the end of the year. This is our first time printing on LIMEX sheets, which are, to put it simply, paper made from stone, since we introduced the printing machine. Given that these are help cards, they needed to be \\\"waterproof and tear-resistant\\\", and we initially considered using Yupo paper. However, the manufacturer did not agree to print on Yupo paper with the printer we introduced at our facility, so we used LIMEX sheets instead. We would be grateful if the cities and towns in the prefecture would consider printing. Thank you.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"There is some confusion regarding the role of the Marker. Not a few players attribute far more responsibilities and powers to the marker than they actually have. It's very simple, since the only two obligations exclusive to the marker are: - checking and recording the result of each hole, and - certifying the results by signing the scorecard at the end of the round. In everything else, the marker is just like any other player, as they should all ensure that a Stroke Play Competition proceeds fairly. Put another way: the task of any player in relation to the Rules is not limited to the person marking but extends to all players, although logically the player must pay special attention to the person marking. Another matter often subject to confusion is that the marker is not an Official. Therefore, the fact that the marker \\\"authorizes\\\" us to take a certain action (for example, a drop) does not mean that the player is acting correctly. The player is solely responsible for knowing and applying the rules (Rule 1.3.b) and cannot delegate this responsibility to their marker or another player. .\", \"sampled\": \"There is some confusion regarding the role of the Marker in golf. Quite a few players assign more responsibilities and powers to the Marker than they actually have. It is quite simple, as the only two duties that are exclusive to the Marker are - to check and record the result of each hole, and - to certify the results by signing the card at the end of the round. In other respects, the Marker is just like any other player, as all of them should ensure that a Stroke Play competition proceeds fairly. Put another way, the responsibility of any player in relation to the Rules is not limited to the person who marks but extends to all players, although logically the player should pay special attention to the person who marks. Another often confusing issue is that the Marker is not a Referee. Therefore, just because the Marker \\\"authorizes\\\" a certain action (for example, a drop) does not mean that the player is acting correctly. The player is solely responsible for knowing and applying the rules (Rule 1.3.b) and cannot delegate this responsibility to their Marker or another player.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Between 1941 and 1956, prostitution in Spain was legalized and regulated by the Franco regime. For the Francoist State, the sexual life of the people was a matter of prime importance, since they conceived of indecency and sinful attitude as direct attacks against the homeland. Thus, as we know –and some have suffered–, a very strong moral regime structured around Christian values was established.\", \"sampled\": \"Between 1941 and 1956, prostitution in Spain was legalized and regulated by Franco's regime. For the Francoist state, the people's sexual life was a matter of utmost importance, as they viewed immodesty and sinful behavior as direct attacks against the homeland. Thus, as we know - and some have suffered - a strong moral regime was established, underpinned by Christian values.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"During the Community Shield final between Liverpool and Manchester City, Suárez and Agüero had fun sharing anecdotes at a very special Watch Party on Star+. The Norwegian arrived at City as a reinforcement a few weeks ago and signed a contract for five years, so he will remain with the team until the 2026/2027 season. At Leicester's stadium, Klopp's team won 3-1 with goals from Alexander-Arnold, Salah and Núñez; Julián Álvarez scored the temporary equalizer for Guardiola's side. The player from Barranquilla achieved his third title with the Reds in less than a year. The player from La Guajira was a starter on the left wing and played almost the entire match which gave the championship to the Reds.\", \"sampled\": \"During the Community Shield final between Liverpool and Manchester City, Suárez and Agüero had a great time sharing anecdotes at a very special Star+ Watch Party. The Norwegian player joined City as a reinforcement a few weeks ago and signed a five-year contract, ensuring his stay with the team until the 2026/2027 season. At Leicester's stadium, Klopp's team prevailed with a 3-1 victory due to goals from Alexander-Arnold, Salah, and Núñez; Julián Álvarez temporarily tied the game for Guardiola's team. The player from Barranquilla won his third title with the Reds in less than a year. The Guajiro player started on the left wing and played nearly the entire match, securing the championship for the Reds.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The main objective of this study has been to evaluate the floristic composition of grasslands in two rural parks of the Canary Islands (Valle Gran Rey and Anaga) in grazed and ungrazed plots and to determine whether the composition of exotic species according to their coverage is affected by the abandonment of traditional goat grazing. The coverage of native and introduced species increased significantly in the ungrazed plots of Valle Gran Rey (La Gomera).\", \"sampled\": \"The primary aim of this study was to assess the floristic composition of grasslands in two Rural Parks in the Canary Islands (Valle Gran Rey and Anaga) in grazed and ungrazed plots, and to determine if the composition of exotic species based on their coverage is affected by the abandonment of traditional goat grazing. The coverage of native and introduced species significantly increased in the ungrazed plots of Valle Gran Rey (La Gomera).\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The Finnegan List Every year, the Seua publishes a list of works insufficiently translated or simply forgotten, selected by a committee of ten major authors… TLHUB Started in 2008 and launched for the first time in 2012, TLHUB (Translation and Literary Hub) is a multilingual digital tool dedicated to translation… The Observatory of Translation The observatory of translation is an intelligence laboratory offering in-depth articles on translation. Secession The History of the Constituent Assembly which laid the foundations for a Europe beyond nations: a non-event. World Translation Fund The World Translation Fund is a funding project that appeals to private donors around the world.\", \"sampled\": \"Every year, the Seua publishes the Finnegan List, a collection of works that are undertranslated or simply forgotten, selected by a committee of ten major authors. Since its inception in 2008 and its first launch in 2012, TLHUB (Translation and Literary Hub) has served as a multilingual digital tool dedicated to translation. The Translation Observatory is a watchful laboratory offering in-depth articles on translation while the history of the Constituent Assembly, which laid the foundations for a Europe beyond nations, is a non-spectacle titled Secession. The World Translation Fund is a financing project that appeals to private donors worldwide.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"What is Shisha? ◆About Shisha◆ The origin of shisha (water pipe) is uncertain, with theories including Egypt and Persia, but recently it has spread worldwide, becoming popular among overseas celebrities! Shisha, also called aroma smoke. Its most notable feature is the variety of flavors. Fruit flavored Herb flavored Dessert flavored Cocktail flavored And so on... Furthermore, by combining these flavors, the types of flavors are infinite! Although shisha originally contains little nicotine, there are also flavors with 0% nicotine and tar, so even those who don't usually smoke can enjoy it!\", \"sampled\": \"What is Shisha? Shisha, also known as water tobacco, has an unclear origin, with theories suggesting Egypt or Persia. However, in recent years, it has become popular among celebrities abroad and is spreading globally. Shisha, sometimes referred to as aroma smoke, is notable for its variety of flavors. There are fruit-flavored, herb-flavored, dessert-flavored, cocktail-flavored, and more. By combining these flavors, the possibilities are endless. Shisha originally contains a small amount of nicotine, but there are also flavors with 0% nicotine and tar, making it enjoyable even for those who do not normally smoke.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The Brazilian series has had a great impact within feminism since the story of Maria Luisa (Malú) and her friends has transformed into the account of empowered women. The background of the series is given by a macho and patriarchal discourse that unfolds from the figure of the protagonist's father, through her husband Pedro, to justice and its lawyer who tries to convince her to be submissive to recover her assets and negotiate better with the father of her son who threatens to leave her with nothing and without the child. \\\"The usual advice for all women: \\\"be kind\\\", \\\"behave\\\", \\\"smile\\\"/What if I don't feel like smiling anymore? And what if Waltinho... was exhausted from playing the good girl so men respect me? What if instead of speaking softly I choose to be assertive or perhaps arrogant? It's not for anything, but you men always do that and things turn out quite well for you,\\\" Malú says to whoever supposedly defends her legally. Tired of playing the good girls, of fitting into the molds of this system that oppresses us. Each of the characters in this series suffers different forms of violence that they can only navigate together, embraced as a pack. We are fed up and tired, but we have companions.\", \"sampled\": \"The Brazilian series has had a great impact within feminism, since the story of María Luisa (Malú) and her friends has become the tale of empowered women. The backdrop of the series is shaped by a sexist and patriarchal discourse that unfolds from the figure of the protagonist’s father, through her husband Pedro, to the justice system and her lawyer, who tries to convince her to be submissive in order to recover her assets and negotiate better with the father of her son, who threatens to leave her with nothing and without the child. “The same advice as always for all women: ‘be nice,’ ‘behave,’ ‘smile’/What if I no longer feel like smiling? And what if, Waltinho… I were exhausted from playing the good girl so that men will respect me? What if instead of speaking softly I choose to be assertive or maybe arrogant? Not for nothing, but you men always do that and things turn out quite well for you,” Malú says to the person who supposedly defends her legally. Tired of playing the good girls, of fitting into the molds of this system that oppresses us. Each of the characters in this series suffers different kinds of violence, and the only way they can move through them is together, embraced as a pack. We are fed up and tired, but we have one another, compañeras.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Class specifying the type of antialiasing to use when rendering texts or shapes. CairoAntialias::MODE_DEFAULT Uses the default antialiasing for the subsystem and target devices. CairoAntialias::MODE_NONE Uses a two-level alpha mask. CairoAntialias::MODE_GRAY Performs single-color antialiasing (using shades of gray for black text on a white background, for example). CairoAntialias::MODE_SUBPIXEL Performs antialiasing by taking advantage of the order of subpixel elements on devices such as LCD panels.\", \"sampled\": \"(No version information available, might only be in Git) Class specifying the type of antialiasing to use when rendering text or shapes. CairoAntialias::MODE_DEFAULT Uses the default antialiasing for the subsystem and target devices. CairoAntialias::MODE_NONE Uses a two-level alpha mask. CairoAntialias::MODE_GRAY Performs single-color antialiasing (using shades of gray for black text on a white background, for example). CairoAntialias::MODE_SUBPIXEL Performs antialiasing by taking advantage of the order of subpixel elements on devices such as LCD panels.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Antonio Sanchez Purchase Price List Antonio Sanchez Estudio-1 Cedar (Guitar) This Antonio Sanchez Estudio-1 Cedar was received through a phone application and accepted via an on-site purchase from the Nishitokyo Reuse Center. Good condition Purchase price ¥9,000 ¥9,000 * This purchase record is just one example of Antonio Sanchez products purchased through our service, and we have many other appraisal and purchase records. * The posted purchase amount includes the timing of acceptance and the condition, etc., so it does not guarantee your appraisal amount.\", \"sampled\": \"Antonio Sanchez purchase price list Antonio Sanchez Estudio-1 Cedar (guitar) This Antonio Sanchez Estudio-1 Cedar was applied for by telephone and accepted through on-site purchase by the West Tokyo Reuse Center. Good condition Purchase price ¥9,000 ¥9,000 * This purchase record is one example of Antonio Sanchez items purchased through this service, and we have many other appraisal and purchase records. * The listed purchase amount includes factors such as the time of acceptance and condition, so it does not guarantee the appraisal amount for customers.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The Kama Sutra, the most subtle and well-known guide to sensual love, is an ancient Sanskrit text composed in the 4th century by the Hindu sage Vatsyayana. At the end of the 19th century it was translated by Sir Richard Burton, the famous explorer and traveler. Of the various editions that have been made of this masterpiece of eroticism, none gathers the exceptional characteristics of this one, both for its luxurious presentation and for the careful selection of its illustrations to favor the most thorough understanding of the text.\", \"sampled\": \"The Kama Sutra, the most subtle and best-known guide to sensual love, is an ancient Sanskrit text composed in the fourth century by the Hindu sage Vatsyayana. At the end of the nineteenth century it was translated by Sir Richard Burton, the famous explorer and traveler. Of the various editions that have been made of this masterpiece of eroticism, none brings together the exceptional qualities of this one, both for its luxurious presentation and for the careful selection of its illustrations, intended to favor the fullest possible understanding of the text.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Toyota Auto Mall Create announced on May 1st that the cumulative number of visitors to \\\"Tressa Yokohama\\\" in Kohoku Ward, Yokohama City, exceeded 150 million on that day. This record was achieved in 4166 days since its opening, approximately 11 years and 5 months. Tressa Yokohama held a pre-opening of the North Building in December 2007, followed by a grand opening including the South Building in March 2008, exceeding 50 million visitors in about 4 years and 3 months after opening, and 100 million visitors in about 7 years and 11 months. The achievement of 150 million visitors shortened the number of days to exceed 50 million visitors (1552 days) and the number of days from 50 million to 100 million visitors (1352 days), achieving this record in 1262 days since exceeding 100 million visitors. In recent years, renovations were carried out to commemorate the 10th anniversary from fiscal year 2017 to fiscal year 2018, focusing on strengthening MD to meet customer needs. Furthermore, approximately 580 events are held annually to increase visitor motivation, and efforts such as these have supported steady progress, with the annual number of visitors in fiscal year 2018 reaching 14.68 million, renewing the record high for 11 consecutive years since opening.\", \"sampled\": \"Toyota Auto Mall Create announced on May 1 that cumulative visitors to “Tressa Yokohama” in Kohoku Ward, Yokohama, surpassed 150 million on the same day. The milestone was reached on the 4,166th day since opening, after about 11 years and five months. Tressa Yokohama opened its North Building first in December 2007, then held its grand opening including the South Building in March 2008, and surpassed 50 million visitors about four years and three months after opening and 100 million after about seven years and 11 months. The latest achievement of 150 million visitors further shortened the number of days from opening to 50 million visitors, which was 1,552 days, and the number of days from 50 million to 100 million, which was 1,352 days, reaching the milestone 1,262 days after surpassing 100 million. In recent years, from fiscal 2017 to fiscal 2018, the facility carried out renovations commemorating its 10th anniversary and worked to strengthen its MD in line with customer needs. Efforts such as holding about 580 events annually to increase reasons for visiting have also provided support, and in fiscal 2018 last year the annual number of visitors was 14.68 million, with the facility continuing to perform steadily, including updating its all-time record for 11 consecutive years since opening.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Since moving in October 2010, the light bulb in the bathroom has burned out for the first time. I also considered changing it to an LED type considering energy saving, but given that it hadn't burned out for over 8 years, the merit of the long life of the LED type is small, and the price of the LED bulb itself is still high, so I decided to stick with a regular incandescent bulb this time. I don't think the prices will ever be reversed due to its structure, but I would like them to at least become within double the current price. The toilet also used the same krypton bulb, but considering the lighting time per day, I guess I'll replace it with a regular incandescent bulb when it burns out.\", \"sampled\": \"For the first time since moving in in October 2010, the light bulb in the bathroom burned out. Taking power saving into account, I also considered switching to an LED type, but considering that it lasted more than eight years without burning out, the long-life advantage of the LED type is small, and the price of LED light bulbs themselves is still quite high, so this time I kept it as an ordinary bulb. Given their structure, I do not think ordinary bulbs and LEDs will ever reverse in price, but I would at least like the difference to come within double. The toilet bulb was the same krypton bulb, but considering its lighting time per day, if it burns out, I suppose I will replace it with an ordinary bulb too.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"AGAiT, headquartered in Taiwan, has just unveiled a new robotic vacuum cleaner at the Hong Kong Electronics Fair. It is equipped with a camera capable of taking photos and recording videos which can be stored via a cloud computing system. It will be sold for around $676 USD initially in Taiwan, then will be launched in Europe, Japan... For now, there is no further information to share with you.\", \"sampled\": \"AGAiT, whose headquarters are based in Taiwan, has just presented a new robot vacuum cleaner at the Hong Kong Electronics Fair. It is equipped with a camera capable of taking photos and recording videos that can be stored via a cloud computing system. It will be sold for around US$676, first in Taiwan, then launched in Europe, Japan... For now, there is no further information to share with you.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The explosion-proof infrared beam detector is a non-addressed reflective linear infrared beam smoke detector. The detector should be used together with the reflector, but one or four reflectors should be used depending on the installation distance between the two devices. The detector has advanced functions and powerful analysis and judgment capabilities. Through our original program, it can automatically complete system debugging, compensation for changes in the external environment, fire alarm and fault judgment, and the output status is displayed through indicator lights and terminals.\", \"sampled\": \"The explosion-proof infrared beam detector is a non-coded reflective linear infrared beam smoke detector. The detector must be used together with the reflector, but one or four reflectors must be used depending on the installation distance between the two devices. The detector has advanced functions and powerful analysis and judgment capabilities. Through our original program, it can automatically complete system debugging, compensation for changes in the external environment, fire alarm and fault judgment, and the output status is shown through indicator lights and terminals.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The eco-animalist party Progress in Green demands that the Government initiate an investigation and clarify responsibilities with the company Tragsa (Agricultural Transformation Company), due to the death of dozens of turtles in Son Real after carrying out clearing work in the area. The president of Progress in Green, Guillermo Amengual, assures that he does not understand why drastic decisions are not made given these events: \\\"We cannot understand that everything remains in taking measures so that it does not happen again. Is this how a protected species is valued?\\\". From the party they consider that \\\"it is well known\\\" that there were dozens of turtles in that area, and regret that it was not checked before clearing. \\\"From Progress in Green we demand that the Government take appropriate measures and clarify responsibilities. What would happen if it were an individual who committed such action? Are we all equal?\\\", asks Amengual.\", \"sampled\": \"The eco-animalist party Progreso en Verde is demanding that the Govern open an investigation and establish responsibility with the company Tragsa (Empresa de Transformación Agraria) for the death of dozens of tortoises in Son Real after clearing work was carried out in the area. The president of Progreso en Verde, Guillermo Amengual, says he does not understand why drastic decisions are not being made in response to these events: \\\"We cannot understand everything being left at saying that measures will be taken so it does not happen again. Is that how a protected species is valued?\\\" The party believes that it is \\\"more than well known\\\" that there were dozens of tortoises in that area, and regrets that this was not checked before clearing. \\\"From Progreso en Verde we demand that the Govern take the relevant measures and establish responsibility. What would happen if it were a private individual who committed such an act? Are we all equal?\\\" Amengual asks.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"This image gallery, photos, or drawings belongs to the article \\\"Goldfinches\\\" from Birds and birds. Click on any of the images to enlarge the information or also on the title you have just below to see the full article. Description: The goldfinch is a bird of approximate dimensions of about 11 to 16 cm. The color of its plumage depends on whether it is male or female. Its beak is conical and short in length, while its wings are narrow and 4 fingers can be appreciated on the legs.\", \"sampled\": \"This gallery of images, photos, or drawings belongs to the article \\\"Goldfinches\\\" from Aves y pájaros. Click on any of the images to enlarge the information, or also on the title just below it to view the full article. Description: The goldfinch is a bird measuring approximately about 11 to 16 cm. The color of its plumage depends on whether it is male or female. Its beak is conical and short, while its wings are narrow, and 4 toes can be seen on its feet.\", \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Writing is a solitary act. The nature of the writer is loneliness. Days, weeks, and months can pass where they barely receive a visit: their ghost. Some retreat to the mountain or a cabin on the beach and there they stop time. It is a magical act; hours do not exist. The clock of history emerges. David Foster Wallace was perhaps the first contemporary writer who came to tell these subjects: \\\"go to hell, see, fools, what writing is like\\\"...and he set about creating, and he did so with such brilliance that he invented a new way of understanding Literature...and that form is called: \\\"Do whatever pleases you...you are the master and lord of that blank page...dare...write...\\\"\", \"sampled\": \"Writing is a solitary act. The writer's nature is solitude. Days, weeks, and months can pass in which the writer barely receives a single visitor: their ghost. Some retreat to the mountains or a cabin on the beach, and there they stop time. It is a magical act; the hours cease to exist. The clock of history emerges. David Foster Wallace was perhaps the first contemporary writer to look these figures in the eye and say: \\\"go to hell, see for yourselves, fools, what writing really is\\\"—and then he set himself to create, doing so with such brilliance that he invented a new way of understanding Literature. And that way goes something like: \\\"Do what you please. You are the master and lord of that blank page. Dare. Write.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The artists announced in the second round are two groups. The first group is \\\"Yoko Takahashi,\\\" who is known to everyone in the world of anime songs. She sings opening themes such as \\\"A Cruel Angel's Thesis\\\" from the TV anime *Neon Genesis Evangelion*, and is a diva of the anime song scene. And the second group is \\\"TEE,\\\" an entertainer born in Hiroshima. His representative love song, \\\"Baby I Love You,\\\" has exceeded 17 million views on YouTube. Aside from where the definition of black and white fits in, both have representative songs that anyone has heard at least once, making them worthy participants in the singing battle. There is still much anticipation for additional performers and surprises until the day of the event at “Kuro Fest 2017.” Advance ticket sales through playguides will also begin on July 5th. “Kuro Fest 2017 ~ Black and White Singing Battle ~” Date: September 6th (Wednesday) *Shigeru Matsuzaki Day (certified by the Japan Anniversary Association)* Location: Tokyo Toyosu PIT Time: Doors open at 5 PM / Starts at 6 PM *Food & beverage, merchandise, and outdoor areas open at 4 PM ＜Performers＞ ■Second Round Performers Yoko Takahashi, TEE ■First Round Announced Performers (in alphabetical order) Shigeru Matsuzaki, Sambomaster, Festival Nine., Mizumaia Kyomi (ex.\", \"sampled\": \"Two artists were announced in the second wave of reveals. The first is Yoko Takahashi, a name virtually everyone in the anime song world knows. A true diva of the anison scene, she is best known for singing \\\"Zankoku na Tenshi no Teze,\\\" the opening theme of the TV anime \\\"Neon Genesis Evangelion,\\\" among other songs. The second act is TEE, an entertainer born in Hiroshima, whose signature love song \\\"Baby I Love You\\\" has surpassed 17 million plays on YouTube. Wherever exactly the black-and-white concept fits in, both acts have signature songs that everyone has heard at least once, making them natural additions to this singing battle. \\\"Kuro Fes 2017\\\" promises more performers and last-minute surprises right up to the day of the event, with advance ticket sales through play guides starting July 5. \\\"Kuro Fes 2017: Monochrome Uta Gassen\\\" will be held on September 6 (Wednesday) — recognized as Matsuzaki Shigeru Day by the Japan Anniversary Association — at Toyosu PIT in Tokyo, with doors open at 17:00 and the show starting at 18:00 (food, merchandise, and outdoor areas open from 16:00). Second-wave performers Yoko Takahashi and TEE will appear alongside first-wave announced performers including Matsuzaki Shigeru, Sambomaster, Matsuri Nine., and Miyamae Anmi (ex.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This sample introduces how to insert video ads when playing videos in a jQuery video player, and also how to specify the playback time of the ads. This sample uses the CTP feature. The API and operation may be changed in the product version. You can set properties of the jQuery video player to display ads each time a user plays a video. The following sample introduces how to insert ads and set property values. Note: When running this sample on a mobile device, it draws default device video player controls unless the Modernizr JavaScript library is undefined. If the Modernizr library is not available on the page, it draws the igVideoPlayer control. In this case, there may be fewer touch functions than the default device video player. Therefore, this sample uses the Modernizr library and uses the default device video player instead of the igVideoPlayer on mobile devices.\", \"sampled\": \"This sample demonstrates how to insert video advertisements during playback in the jQuery Video Player and how to specify their playback duration. Note that this sample uses CTP functionality, so the API and behavior may change in the production release. You can configure the jQuery Video Player properties to display an advertisement each time a user plays a video. The following sample shows how to insert ads and set the relevant property values. Note: when running this sample on a mobile device, it renders the default device video player controls, unless the Modernizr JavaScript library is undefined. If the Modernizr library is not available on the page, the igVideoPlayer control is rendered instead, in which case touch functionality may be more limited than with the default device video player. For this reason, the sample uses the Modernizr library to ensure that the default device video player, rather than igVideoPlayer, is used on mobile devices.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Editorial image. Do not use in commercial designs and/or advertisements. Click here for terms and conditions. This photo is taken at a slow shutter speed to capture more light and to make the illustration brighter. The lower shutter speed caused the boat to resemble a shadowy figure in the photo. The background of the illustration shows the port of Hong Kong and the plane shows a boat moving towards the photographer.\", \"sampled\": \"Editorial image. Not for use in commercial designs or advertisements. Click here for general terms and conditions. This photo was taken at a slow shutter speed to capture more light and brighten the image. As a result, the boat appears as a shadowy figure in the frame. The background shows the port of Hong Kong, while the foreground features a boat moving toward the camera.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"This air-conditioned individual room of an average of 14m², is equipped with a double bed with superior quality bedding. It has a desk, a 26-inch flat screen television with satellite channels and Canal + and free wireless internet access. The bathroom is equipped with shower or bathtub, a hairdryer and Ecolabel cosmetic products.\", \"sampled\": \"This air-conditioned single room averages 14m² and features a double bed with high-quality bedding, a desk, a 26-inch flat-screen TV with satellite channels and Canal+, and free Wi-Fi. The bathroom includes a shower or bathtub, a hairdryer, and Ecolabel toiletries. Slippers, bathrobes, courtesy trays, and individual welcome amenities are available on request.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"By continuing to browse this site, you accept the use of cookies. The latter ensures the proper functioning of our services. Learn more. An adopted Picard through and through, I am pleased to present, for this July 2011 portfolio, the Reformed Protestant temple of Hargicourt (Hargy for close friends). Located on the borders of Aisne and Somme, this high place of Protestantism in Vermandois was the scene of an Awakening in the 19th century (1820s-1830s), a crucible from which several pastors and evangelists emerged, including a woman, Esther Carpentier (see \\\"Another way of being Christian in France,\\\" a synthesis on the history of Baptists in France). Hargicourt is also located not far from the \\\"La boîte à cailloux\\\" site where Desert Assemblies were held (less famous than those of Mas Soubeyran in the Cévennes). These were clandestine gatherings of Protestants, after the Revocation of the Edict of Nantes in 1685. In this border region where temples and churches are much more often closed than open, who remembers this Protestant history?\", \"sampled\": \"By continuing to browse this site, you accept the use of cookies, which ensure the proper functioning of our services. Learn more. An incorrigible adopted Picard, I am pleased to present, for this July 2011 portfolio, the Reformed Protestant temple of Hargicourt — Hargy to those in the know. Located on the borders of the Aisne and the Somme, this important site of Protestantism in the Vermandois was the scene of a Revival in the 19th century (the 1820s–1830s), a crucible from which emerged several pastors and evangelists, including a woman, Esther Carpentier (see \\\"Une autre manière d'être chrétien en France,\\\" a synthesis on the history of Baptists in France). Hargicourt also lies not far from the site known as \\\"La boîte à cailloux,\\\" where Assemblées du désert were held — less famous than those of Mas Soubeyran in the Cévennes, but significant nonetheless. These were clandestine gatherings of Protestants following the Revocation of the Edict of Nantes in 1685. In this border region, where temples and churches are far more often closed than open, who still remembers this Protestant history?\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The first concerns computer programs that are not considered inventions insofar as they are claimed as such . However, a computer program is not excluded from patentability under Article 52 if its implementation on a computer produces an additional technical effect, going beyond the “normal” physical interactions between program (software) and computer (hardware). This could be, for example, a program used to control a technical process or determining the operation of a technical device. The internal operation of a computer on which the program is executed could also produce such an effect.\", \"sampled\": \"The first concerns computer programs, which are not considered inventions to the extent they are claimed as such. However, a computer program is not excluded from patentability under Article 52 if running it on a computer produces a further technical effect beyond the \\\"normal\\\" physical interactions between the program (software) and the computer (hardware). This might be, for example, a program used to control a technical process or to determine how a technical device operates. The internal operation of the computer running the program could also produce such an effect.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"CONSIDER Uenohara City, located at the easternmost point of Yamanashi Prefecture, is adjacent to Otsuki City, Doshu City, Doshi Village, Kosuge Village, Okutama Town and Hinoharamura in Tokyo, and Sagamihara City in Kanagawa Prefecture. In 2005, Akiyama Village and Uenohara Town merged to become Uenohara City. Surrounded by greenery, Uenohara City also has good access, taking 60 minutes to Shinjuku and 35 minutes to Hachioji Station on the Chuo Line, making it convenient for commuting and shopping. It's Uenohara City where you can have a home surrounded by nature and commute to the city center. Also, it takes 17 minutes by train to Mt. Takao, and 25 minutes by car to Sagami Lake Pleasure Forest, which is famous for its illuminations in Midori Ward, Sagamihara City, making this area popular among families raising children. Although the total population of the city is decreasing, there is a large university campus within the city limits, so there is a large population of students in their 20s, and it can be said that it also has aspects of a student town. In recent years, people seeking nature have been moving here, and many tourists come to experience camping sites, post towns, and outdoor activities. Therefore, it is expected to develop in the future as a green residential area and a popular tourist destination.\", \"sampled\": \"Uenohara City sits at the easternmost tip of Yamanashi Prefecture, bordering Otsuki City, Tsuru City, Doushi Village, and Kosuge Village within the prefecture, as well as Okutama Town, Hinohara Village, and Sagamihara City across the borders with Tokyo and Kanagawa. The city was formed in 2005 when Akiyama Village and Uenohara Town merged. Despite being surrounded by greenery, it has strong transport links: Shinjuku is 60 minutes away and Hachioji Station just 35 minutes by the Chuo Line, making it easy to commute or shop while still living close to nature. Mount Takao is only 17 minutes by train, and Sagamiko Pleasure Forest in Sagamihara City's Midori Ward — well known for its illuminations — is 25 minutes by car, which has made the area popular with families. The city's overall population is declining, but a large university campus keeps the share of residents in their 20s relatively high, giving it a distinct character as a student town. In recent years it has also attracted people seeking a more nature-oriented lifestyle, and visitors come for its camping grounds, post-town atmosphere, and outdoor activities. The city is expected to develop further both as a green residential area and as a tourist destination.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"It's clear...pitinonoz wrote: Having fun switching from AHCI to Compatibility when Windows 7 is already installed and we'll see you back here soon because you'll have BSODs. Does it ever occur to you to research things yourself and educate yourself from time to time?wallace27 wrote: And why exactly is it not good to switch from AHCI to Compatibility when an OS is already installed? Coming from someone who can't even read what is written or use a search engine, that's good…wallace27 wrote: \\\"Better\\\" that's scientific as an explanation. wallace27 wrote: In case you no longer see me posting messages you will know the reason why. Oh and especially don't be outraged, act like the Chinese.\", \"sampled\": \"That's clear... pitinonoz wrote: Messing around switching from AHCI to Compatibility when Windows 7 is already installed, and you'll be back soon enough with BSODs. Do you ever bother to look things up yourself? wallace27 wrote: And why exactly is it bad to switch from AHCI to Compatibility when an OS is already installed? Coming from someone who can't be bothered to read what people write or use a search engine, that's rich... wallace27 wrote: \\\"Better to\\\" — now there's a scientific explanation. wallace27 wrote: If you stop seeing my posts, now you'll know why. And above all, don't be outraged — just do like the Chinese.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Suzuki unveiled a custom model called \\\"Jimny Survive\\\" at Tokyo Auto Salon 2019, based on the \\\"Jimny Sierra,\\\" further emphasizing its off-road performance. This model was developed as a concept model aiming to traverse even in extreme conditions, equipped with an outer roll cage and protectors. Mr. Obata stated, “Originally, the Jimny is a full four-wheel drive vehicle, but if we were to go even further into the extreme world, it would take this form,” adding, “Basically, we built it with the idea of being able to return safely even when going alone in harsh environments. For example, we equipped a winch so that it can be recovered even if stuck, and installed an escape ladder, and also equipped the rear with a lift-up jack.” In addition, three liquid crystal displays are mounted in the center of the dashboard of the Jimny Survive, displaying navigation as well as vehicle tilt and vehicle information. At the Suzuki booth at Tokyo Auto Salon 2019, in addition to this, models such as the \\\"Swift Sport Yellow Rev\\\" finished in high-saturation yellow pearl, a special color for show models, and the newly released light passenger vehicle \\\"Spacia Gear\\\" launched on December 20, 2018, were also exhibited.\", \"sampled\": \"At the Tokyo Auto Salon 2019, Suzuki unveiled the \\\"Jimny Survive,\\\" a custom model based on the \\\"Jimny Sierra\\\" with an even stronger focus on off-road capability. Developed as a concept vehicle fitted with an outer roll cage and protectors, it was designed to tackle terrain in extreme conditions. Mr. Obata explained, \\\"The Jimny was originally a full-fledged four-wheel-drive vehicle, but when we thought about pushing it even further into the extreme, it took this form.\\\" He added, \\\"Basically, we built it on the idea that even if you head out alone into a harsh environment, you can make it back safely — for example, we fitted a winch so you can recover if you get stuck, loaded an escape ladder, and equipped the rear with a lift-up jack.\\\" The Jimny Survive also features three LCD displays at the center of the instrument panel, showing navigation, vehicle tilt information, and vehicle status data. Other highlights at the Suzuki booth included the \\\"Swift Sport Yellow Rev,\\\" finished in a high-chroma yellow pearl color exclusive to show models, and the new compact passenger car \\\"Spacia Gear,\\\" which went on sale on December 20, 2018.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The man in his twenties who barricaded himself for several hours on Tuesday, July 3rd, in his home in Thil (Meurthe-et-Moselle) was subdued by the GIPN. He threatened to blow up the building, according to Le Républicain Lorrain. \\\"There were no injuries following the operation which ended around 4:20 PM,\\\" said a spokesperson for the prefecture of Meurthe-et-Moselle. “He barricaded himself in the apartment Tuesday morning, around 10:30 AM. It was his mother who alerted the police, describing her son as very angry,” this same source recounts. According to the police he was “apparently armed.” The residence evacuated, the gas cut off The GIPN from Strasbourg arrived on site around 2:15 PM, this same source indicated, “to try to establish contact.” A security perimeter had been established around the building. The young man, “in conflict with his parents,” had not stated “any particular demands,” according to authorities. Residents of the fifteen apartments in this residence, located in the Sainte-Claire district, had been evacuated and the gas shut off, according to the prefecture.\", \"sampled\": \"A man in his twenties barricaded himself inside his home in Thil (Meurthe-et-Moselle) for several hours on Tuesday, July 3, before being subdued by the GIPN. He had threatened to blow up the building, Le Républicain Lorrain reported. “There were no injuries when the operation ended around 4:20 p.m.,” a spokesperson for the Meurthe-et-Moselle prefecture said. “He barricaded himself in the apartment Tuesday morning around 10:30 a.m. It was his mother who alerted the police, describing her son as very agitated,” the same source explained. Police said he was “apparently armed.” The Strasbourg GIPN arrived on the scene around 2:15 p.m., the source added, “to try to establish contact.” A security perimeter had been set up around the building. The young man, “in conflict with his parents,” had made “no specific demands,” according to the authorities. Residents of the roughly fifteen apartments in the building, located in the Cité Sainte-Claire, had been evacuated and the gas cut off, the prefecture said.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"|Enjoy a moment of introspective minutes| It is said that yoga emphasizes dialogue between oneself and mind and body, but even during busy work in the office, let's calm our minds a little and create peaceful moments to relieve tension. First, correct your posture and sit deeply on the chair. Nose tip exercise to loosen eye muscles - Look at the tip of your nose with both eyes for 15-20 seconds. - Close your eyes and relax for a while. - Repeat the above several times. This is a stretch of the eye muscles that consciously puts them in a cross-eyed state. You may not often become cross-eyed in everyday life. Using unused muscles can lead to strengthening them. However, if you feel any pain in your eyes, stop immediately. The next page will introduce exercises to vigorously flush out fatigue substances.\", \"sampled\": \"Take a moment to look inward. Yoga is about connecting with your mind and body, so even on a busy workday, give yourself a few minutes to settle your thoughts, find some calm, and let go of tension. Start by sitting deeply in your chair with your posture upright. For the eye-relaxation nose-tip exercise, bring both eyes to focus on the tip of your nose and hold that gaze for 15 to 20 seconds. Then close your eyes and rest for a moment. Repeat this sequence several times. It’s a deliberate cross-eyed stretch for the eye muscles—something you probably don’t do often in everyday life. Using muscles in unfamiliar ways helps strengthen them. If you feel any pain in your eyes, stop right away. The next page introduces an exercise that actively flushes out fatigue-causing substances.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Life-saving volunteer activity close at hand: \\\"Blood Donation\\\"! Update date: 2019.01.29 JA Kasuya received a request from the Fukuoka Red Cross Blood Center on January 23rd stating, “Approximately 600 blood donors are needed daily in Fukuoka Prefecture.” To ensure patients who need blood can receive transfusions with peace of mind, we kindly ask for your warm cooperation. JA staff also cooperated with blood donation at the main office. An employee who cooperated with the blood donation said, “It also helps me manage my own health, so I donate blood every year.” The center states that blood products necessary for transfusion cannot be artificially created and cannot be stored for long periods. Continuous blood donations are essential to secure blood products. Blood donation is a volunteer activity close at hand that saves lives, and they are seeking understanding and cooperation from many people.\", \"sampled\": \"Blood donation is a familiar way to volunteer and save lives. On January 23, JA Kasuya received a request from the Fukuoka Red Cross Blood Center: “In Fukuoka Prefecture, we need the cooperation of about 600 blood donors every day. We ask for your warm cooperation so that patients who need blood can receive transfusions with peace of mind.” JA staff also donated blood at the main office. One staff member who gave blood said, “I donate blood every year because it also helps me manage my own health.” The center notes that blood products needed for transfusions cannot be manufactured artificially and cannot be stored for long periods. Continuous blood donation is essential to secure blood products. They are seeking understanding and cooperation from many people, as blood donation is a familiar volunteer activity that can save lives.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Military Retirement In the military field, the retirement operation consists of recalling armed forces while maintaining contact with the enemy. A retirement operation can be undertaken in order to: leave an irrelevant conflict or occupation zone; regroup; occupy a more easily defensible position; or even lure the enemy into an ambush. A military retreat is considered a risky tactical operation that requires discipline in order not to turn into a rout.\", \"sampled\": \"In military terms, a retreat is an operation in which armed forces pull back while still in contact with the enemy. It may be carried out to leave a conflict or occupation zone that is no longer relevant, to regroup, to take up a position that is easier to defend, or to draw the enemy into an ambush. A retreat is regarded as a risky tactical move that demands discipline to keep it from collapsing into a rout.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Noah Style Preview Series Learning Method, 5th Grade National Language - Let's Master Idioms - Noah, a Specialized Individual Tutoring School for Middle School Entrance Exams Among knowledge problems related to idioms, antonym problems are particularly difficult. It is most important to firmly grasp the meaning of each idiom and be convinced within yourself that, yes, they have opposite meanings. For example, the antonym of \\\"unexplored\\\" is \\\"civilization.\\\" “Unexplored” means undeveloped. In contrast, “civilization” refers to a state where society has advanced and become rich both spiritually and materially. If you firmly grasp each meaning, you will understand that they have opposite meanings. Like this, there are many cases where you don't know the correct meaning even for familiar words, so let's understand the meaning of the words and clarify the relationship with their antonyms.\", \"sampled\": \"The Noah-style prep method for fifth-grade Japanese emphasizes mastering compound words, and among knowledge-based compound questions, antonym problems stand out as especially tough. The key is to firmly grasp the meaning of each compound and convince yourself that the two truly have opposite meanings. Take the antonym of \\\"mikai\\\" (primitive), which is \\\"bunmei\\\" (civilization). \\\"Mikai\\\" means undeveloped, while \\\"bunmei\\\" describes a state where society advances and becomes spiritually and materially enriched. Once you firmly understand each meaning, you can see they are opposites. Even with familiar words, the correct meaning is often unknown, so it’s essential to understand word meanings and clarify how they relate to their antonyms.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The Mexican National Women's Under-20 Team fell to their Spanish counterpart with a score of 1-0 in the quarterfinals of the Costa Rica 2022 World Cup. The Mexicans conclude their participation in the world tournament after overcoming the group stage and defeating a powerhouse like Germany. The Mexicans had an outstanding performance in the Under-20 World Cup quarterfinals, fighting for the result until the last minutes, despite Inma Gabarro's goal, forcing Spain to retreat to defense. Despite the scandals and obstacles, the Mexicans managed to compete against a world power. With the result obtained at the National Stadium in San José, Costa Rica, the negative balance was repeated from the first time both teams faced each other when they met at the 2018 FIFA U-17 Women's World Cup, when the Mexican footballers fell with a score of 2-1.\", \"sampled\": \"The Mexican U-20 Women’s National Team lost 1-0 to Spain in the quarterfinals of the 2022 Costa Rica World Cup. Their tournament ended after a run that included advancing from the group stage and beating a powerhouse like Germany. Mexico put in a strong quarterfinal showing, pushing for a result until the final minutes despite Inma Gabarro’s goal and forcing Spain to drop back defensively. Amid scandals and obstacles, the team still managed to compete with a global power. The result at the Estadio Nacional in San José, Costa Rica, echoed the negative outcome of their first meeting at the 2018 U-17 Women’s World Cup, where Mexico lost 2-1.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"William Jackson (politician) A figure from the American Revolution, noteworthy as secretary to the United States Constitutional Convention. He is one of the Founding Fathers of the United States as a signatory to the United States Constitution. He also served with distinction in the Continental Army during the American Revolutionary War. After the war he served as one of the personal secretaries to President George Washington.\", \"sampled\": \"William Jackson was an American Revolutionary figure who served as secretary to the United States Constitutional Convention. He is counted among the Founding Fathers as a signatory of the United States Constitution. During the American Revolutionary War, he served with distinction in the Continental Army, and after the war he became one of President George Washington’s personal secretaries.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Ring inspired by a bracelet of a rather rare type, especially in Brittany. It is part of a small production of goldsmithery from the end of the Bronze Age, and similar examples can be found in the northern part of Germany and especially in some treasures in southern England. At that time, gold ornaments were not uncommon. A large number of them have been found in burials, while others seemed to belong to deposits with a religious or social purpose. In all cases, this type of precious jewelry seems to have been reserved for members of the ruling class, the rest of the population having to settle for bronze ornaments. Proportionally, bracelets are the most frequently encountered among the golds of the Middle and Late Bronze Age. This bracelet was obtained through various hammering operations on an ingot and features a simple decoration of deep chiseled grooves. The original was made in a gold alloy with 16% silver and 6% copper, quite characteristic of the period. The interest of this object lies in its ribbon section, a plastic decoration of the hoop, and the prominent character of the ends.\", \"sampled\": \"This ring takes its inspiration from a rather rare type of bracelet, especially in Brittany. It belongs to a small body of goldsmithing from the end of the Bronze Age, and similar examples appear in northern Germany and particularly in certain hoards from southern England. Gold ornaments were not uncommon at the time. Many have been recovered from graves, while others seem to have been part of deposits with a religious or social purpose. In every case, this kind of precious jewelry appears to have been reserved for the ruling class; the rest of the population had to make do with bronze ornaments. Proportionally, bracelets are the most frequently encountered items among Middle and Late Bronze Age goldwork. This bracelet was shaped by various hammering operations on an ingot and carries a simple decoration of deep chased grooves. The original was made from a gold alloy containing 16% silver and 6% copper, entirely characteristic of the period. The object’s interest lies in its ribbon-like cross-section, the plastic decoration of the rush, and the prominent nature of its ends.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Aiming to improve wages for people with disabilities and more, the amount procured preferentially from facilities employing people with disabilities and others by the prefecture in fiscal year 2018 was 30.24 million yen, an increase of 14.4% (3.80 million yen) compared to fiscal year 2017, renewing the record high for six consecutive years, according to a summary completed by the prefectural disability welfare division on the 28th. Factors included setting target amounts for orders other than public funds and an increase in orders such as boxed lunches for staff association events. The prefecture has set a procurement target of over 45 million yen for fiscal year 2019 and will continue to call for the promotion of preferential procurement. The target amount for fiscal year 2018 was 28 million yen, achieving the target amount for five consecutive years. Looking at the procurement amount by item, printing accounted for the largest share at 9.43 million yen, down 5.98 million yen from the previous year. Food items such as boxed lunches and sweets increased by 7.32 million yen to 8.22 million yen, followed by shredding of confidential documents, which increased by 80,000 yen to 5.72 million yen.\", \"sampled\": \"The prefecture’s priority procurement of goods and services from disability employment facilities reached 30.24 million yen in fiscal 2018, a 14.4% increase (3.8 million yen) over the previous year and a record high for the sixth consecutive year, according to a summary released by the Prefectural Disability Welfare Division on the 28th. The effort aims to raise wages for people with disabilities. Contributing factors included setting order targets outside public funds and a rise in orders for items such as boxed lunches for staff social gatherings. The prefecture has set a procurement target of at least 45 million yen for fiscal 2019 and continues to call for expanded priority procurement. The fiscal 2018 target was 28 million yen, marking the fifth straight year the goal was met. By item, the largest category was printing at 9.43 million yen, down 5.98 million yen from the prior year, followed by food items like boxed lunches and confectionery at 8.22 million yen, up 7.32 million yen, and shredding of confidential documents at 5.72 million yen, up 80,000 yen.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Introduction The Charles Kaufmann Municipal Swimming Pool is located on Talintes Street in the city of Champigneulles in the department of Meurthe and Moselle. The establishment includes leisure areas such as aquatic gardens, an aqua gym area, a swimming pool for adults and a room for children, and a swimming area specifically designed for competition and contests. The center also has 2 indoor pools available throughout the year, including the first with dimensions of 25mx12.5m with a depth increasing from 1.60m to 3.70m and a square pool with dimensions of 12.5mx12.5 from 0.80m to 1.40m.\", \"sampled\": \"Charles Kaufmann Municipal Swimming Pool is on Rue Talintes in Champigneulles, in the Meurthe-et-Moselle department. The facility features leisure areas with water gardens, an aqua gym space, an adult swimming hall, a children’s hall, and a pool designed for competitions. It also has two indoor pools open year-round: a 25 m × 12.5 m pool with a depth ranging from 1.60 m to 3.70 m, and a square 12.5 m × 12.5 m pool with a depth of 0.80 m to 1.40 m.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"AESOP'S FABLES On one occasion, the wind and the sun wanted to know which of the two was stronger. At that very moment, a man appeared along the road. \\\"Do you see that man with his cloak?\\\" intervened the wind—whoever manages to take it off him will be the winner. The wind tried first of all, and blew as strongly as a hurricane. Then the man, to get rid of it, wrapped himself up better and the harder it blew, the more he held onto the cloak with his hands. Discouraged, the wind stopped blowing. In turn, the sun, without making noise, sent its warm rays with more intensity than usual. The man began to sweat and, unable to withstand so much heat, took off his cloak. Then the sun was declared the victor. Skill is worth more than strength.\", \"sampled\": \"AESOP'S FABLES The wind and the sun were arguing about which of them was stronger. Just then, a man came walking down the road. \\\"Do you see that man in the cloak?\\\" said the wind. \\\"Whoever can make him take it off will be the victor.\\\" The wind tried first, blowing with all its might. But the harder it blew, the more tightly the man wrapped his cloak around himself. At last, the wind gave up. The sun then began to shine, sending down its warm rays with more force than usual. The man started to sweat, and unable to stand the heat, he took off his cloak. The sun was declared the victor. Skill is better than strength.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The Minister of State for Foreign Affairs of the United Arab Emirates, Anwar Gargash, confirmed that his country anticipates a successful summit in Riyadh, with which to begin the phase of strengthening dialogue in the Gulf. Gargash wrote on his official Twitter account that we expect a successful summit in Riyadh, with which to begin the stage of strengthening Gulf dialogue. The management of the brotherly Kingdom of Saudi Arabia for this purpose is a matter of trust and optimism, and from Riyadh, the capital of Gulf decision-making, we launch the measures, God willing, to strengthen future Gulf dialogue.\\\" The Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC), composed of the United Arab Emirates, Saudi Arabia, Bahrain, Oman, Kuwait and Qatar, will meet on January 5th in the Saudi capital.\", \"sampled\": \"United Arab Emirates Minister of State for Foreign Affairs Anwar Gargash stated that his country expects a successful summit in Riyadh to begin a new phase of strengthening dialogue in the Gulf. Gargash wrote on his official Twitter account, \\\"We expect a successful summit in Riyadh, with which to begin the stage of strengthening Gulf dialogue. The brotherly Kingdom of Saudi Arabia's management of this task is a source of confidence and optimism, and from Riyadh, the capital of Gulf decision, we will launch measures, God willing, to strengthen future Gulf dialogue.\\\" The Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC), which includes the United Arab Emirates, Saudi Arabia, Bahrain, Oman, Kuwait, and Qatar, will meet on January 5 in the Saudi capital.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"On Wednesday, November 29, 2017, Cafe Con Leche Radio received a phone call from Dr. Talia Baños, who called us from Atlanta, Georgia. She told us about her visit to the “Riviera Maya” in Mexico. They stayed in Mayakoba, a very beautiful tourist development. She told us about the white sandy beaches and their visit to the underground river. We also talked about their visit to the X-Caret sinkholes with mangroves and jungle vegetation. The entrance and exit of the underground rivers of Playa del Carmen and Tulum often lead to caverns, and an underwater world filled with stalactites and stalagmites and clear waters with excellent visibility.\", \"sampled\": \"On Wednesday, November 29, 2017, Cafe Con Leche Radio received a call from Dr. Talia Baños in Atlanta, Georgia. She told us about her recent trip to Mexico's Riviera Maya, where she stayed at Mayakoba, a beautiful tourist development. Dr. Baños described the white sand beaches and her visit to an underground river. We also discussed her tour of the X-Caret sinkholes, which feature mangroves and jungle vegetation. The entrances and exits of the underground rivers near Playa del Carmen and Tulum often open into caverns. This creates an underwater world filled with stalactites, stalagmites, and clear water with excellent visibility.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Naoya Hamakoka Post Comments Talk I tried making a call between LINE Works users, but the other party did not receive a call notification. My device is not connected to Wi-Fi, and the other party's notifications are turned ON. Read on April 25, 2019 108 About LINE WORKS Company Information The recommended photo size is 640 x 640 pixels, and only files under 10MB can be registered. Special characters cannot be used in file names.\", \"sampled\": \"Naoya Hamaoka posted a comment: I tried to make a call between LINE Works users, but the other person didn't receive the call notification. My device isn't connected to Wi-Fi, and the other person's notifications are on. About LINE WORKS Company Information: The recommended photo size is 640x640 pixels, and only files under 10MB can be uploaded. Special characters cannot be used in file names.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Introduction to the staff and medical team of the Department of Pediatrics. We mainly provide treatment and research for neurological diseases and psychosomatic disorders in children. We are doing our best, however modestly, for the sake of our promising children. Also, students and doctors who are even slightly interested in pediatrics, please feel free to contact us. Let's work together.\", \"sampled\": \"Welcome to the Department of Pediatrics. Our staff and clinical teams focus on clinical practice and research centered on pediatric neurological and psychosomatic disorders. We strive to do our best for the children who hold the future, no matter how small our contribution may seem. We encourage any medical students or doctors with an interest in pediatrics to contact us. Let's work together.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Committee Decisions 28 COM 15B.46 The World Heritage Committee, 1. Having taken note of the report submitted by the State Party in January 2004, concerning the implementation of the recommendations issued during its 27th session (2003), 2. Congratulates the State Party on all the measures implemented to coordinate the actions of the parties concerned with the safeguarding of the property, and for the establishment of the required legal protection for the site; 3. Reiterates its concern regarding information concerning the state of degradation of the site; 4. Once again urges the State Party to create, in consultation with the World Heritage Centre, an institution endowed with the legal authority, resources and financial means adequate to ensure the preparation of a management plan for the property and its implementation; 5. Requests the State Party to submit to the World Heritage Centre, by February 1st, 2005, a report on progress in establishing this institution and in preparing the management plan, so that the Committee can examine the state of conservation of the property at its 29th session, in 2005; 6. Decides to reconsider the inscription of the property on the List of World Heritage in Danger at its 29th session in 2005.\", \"sampled\": \"Decisions of the Committee 28 COM 15B.46 The World Heritage Committee, 1. Having noted the report submitted by the State Party in January 2004 concerning the implementation of recommendations made at its 27th session (2003), 2. Congratulates the State Party for the measures taken to coordinate the actions of all parties involved in safeguarding the property and for establishing the required legal protection for the site; 3. Reiterates its concern about information regarding the site's state of degradation; 4. Repeats its request that the State Party, in consultation with the World Heritage Centre, create an institution with the legal authority, resources, and adequate funding to ensure the preparation and implementation of a management plan for the property; 5. Requests the State Party to submit a progress report to the World Heritage Centre by February 1, 2005, on the establishment of this institution and the preparation of the management plan, so the Committee may examine the property's state of conservation at its 29th session in 2005; 6. Decides to reconsider inscribing the property on the List of World Heritage in Danger at its 29th session in 2005.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"We don't joke about corruption in football... Chinese. The Chinese Federation announced on Monday the lifetime expulsion of 33 people and a range of sanctions including hundreds of thousands of dollars in fines to clubs, in the biggest match-fixing scandal against a backdrop of betting that has hit the country. The federation notably banned for life its former presidents Nan Yong and Xie Yalong, already imprisoned for accepting bribes, and the very recent president of Dalian Aerbin, a club from eastern China, Xu Hong. Two clubs compromised in 2003 in fixed matches, Tianjin Teda and Shanghai Shenhua, are fined $160,000 (approximately €120,000) and point deductions for next season. Shenhua, the club where Frenchman Nicolas Anelka passed through and which Ivorian Didier Drogba has just left, is also stripped of its national title won in 2003. China launched an investigation and sanction process, which came to light in 2009, to “clean up” the football environment, whose national image has been tarnished by corruption scandals at all levels.\", \"sampled\": \"Corruption in Chinese football is a serious issue. On Monday, the Chinese Football Association announced lifetime bans for 33 people and various sanctions, including fines of hundreds of thousands of dollars for clubs, in the country's biggest match-fixing and betting scandal. The federation banned its former presidents Nan Yong and Xie Yalong, who were already imprisoned for accepting bribes, for life. Also banned was the recent president of the eastern Chinese club Dalian Aerbin, Xu Hong. Two clubs implicated in match-fixing in 2003, Tianjin Teda and Shanghai Shenhua, were fined $160,000 (about 120,000 euros) and will face point deductions next season. Shenhua, the club where Frenchman Nicolas Anelka played and which Ivorian Didier Drogba recently left, was also stripped of its 2003 national title. China launched a series of investigations and sanctions, which began in 2009, to \\\"clean up\\\" the world of football after its national image was tarnished by corruption scandals at all levels.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"A game of reflecting half of your body in a mirror; a simple pastime. 0 Reflect only half of your body or face in a mirror and play around making funny faces and movements. A game of reflecting half of your body. Prepare a large mirror (full-length mirror). Walk around the side of the mirror and reflect only half of your body. Hide the other half behind the mirror. Moving your hands and feet creates interesting illusions. It makes funny faces. Raising your leg makes it look like you're floating in the air.\", \"sampled\": \"This is a silly game where you reflect half of your body in a mirror. To play, you'll need a large mirror, like a full-length one. Stand next to it so that only half of your body is visible in the reflection, hiding the other half behind the mirror. Try making funny faces and movements. Moving your hands and feet will create interesting effects. If you lift one leg, it will look like you're floating in mid-air.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The United States intends to pressure Israel to review the provisions for opening fire in Judea and Samaria and implement measures that reduce the danger of harm to civilians and protect journalists. This is to prevent incidents such as the one in which Palestinian-American journalist Shireen Abu Akleh died, said the deputy spokesperson for the U.S. State Department on Tuesday at the daily briefing to reporters. A senior IDF official said in a briefing to reporters yesterday that an IDF investigation found no deviation from open-fire regulations during the May 11 operation in Jenin and that there were no failures in the initial briefing soldiers underwent before the operation. U.S. Secretary of State Anthony Blinken requested in a phone conversation with Defense Minister Benny Gantz that Israel reexamine the provisions for opening fire in Judea and Samaria, due to the incident in which the journalist died.\", \"sampled\": \"The United States intends to pressure Israel to review its open-fire regulations in Judea and Samaria and implement measures to reduce harm to civilians and protect journalists. The goal is to prevent incidents like the killing of Palestinian-American journalist Shireen Abu Akleh, the U.S. State Department's deputy spokesperson said Tuesday at a daily press briefing. A senior IDF officer told reporters yesterday that an army investigation found no deviation from open-fire regulations during the May 11 operation in Jenin. The investigation also found no failures in the briefing soldiers received before the operation. U.S. Secretary of State Antony Blinken asked Defense Minister Benny Gantz in a telephone call to have Israel reexamine its open-fire rules in Judea and Samaria following the journalist's death.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Taking medication without the indication of a professional can put our health at risk, generating an effect contrary to our intention of self-care. Did you know that active breaks are necessary to improve your creativity and work performance? Sport (and physical activity in general) positively influences the evolution of treatment. Below, we show you some of the favorite places of the people of Cali to play sports.\", \"sampled\": \"Taking medication without professional guidance can put your health at risk, having the opposite effect of what you intend. Similarly, active breaks are necessary to improve creativity and work performance, and sports (and physical activity in general) can positively influence treatment outcomes. With that in mind, here are some of the favorite places for people from Cali to play sports.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"What is Action on the Ground? Over the past five years, we have invested in an ambitious program aimed at restoring the ecological integrity of Canada's national parks. The Agency's projects to improve the health of ecosystems in our national parks are what is meant by \\\"Action on the Ground.\\\" With the 36 Action on the Ground projects underway across Canada, we are working hard to restore the ecological integrity of park ecosystems. Our Action on the Ground projects are based on three internationally recognized principles.\", \"sampled\": \"What is \\\"Taking Action on the Ground\\\"? In the past five years, we have invested in a program to restore the ecological integrity of Canada's national parks. These projects aim to enhance the health of the national parks' ecosystems. Currently, there are 36 projects underway across Canada, focusing on restoring the ecological integrity of the parks' ecosystems. These projects are guided by three key principles: effectiveness in preserving natural and cultural values, efficiency in maximizing positive outcomes while minimizing costs, and engagement through collaboration with partners and stakeholders to enhance visitor experience. Access the full publication here (PDF, 3.4 MB).\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Syria: chemical weapons transferred to airports Syrian rebels have accused the regime of transferring chemical weapons to airports near the country's borders following Damascus' threat to use them in the event of \\\"external aggression.\\\" On the ground, new fighting has broken out in Aleppo, the country's second city where a new front has opened after that of the capital Damascus, which was largely retaken by the Syrian army after a week of unprecedented clashes.\", \"sampled\": \"In Syria, rebels accused the regime of moving chemical weapons to airports near the borders after a threat to use them in response to external aggression. In Aleppo, the country's second city, new clashes arose as the Syrian army regained control of most of the capital, Damascus, after a week of intense fighting.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"PC animation simulating the routing of 3 Class 40 boats during the Route du Rhum race on November 10-11, 2010. Here with the Adrena software, we are trying to see the possible routes with the download of a weather report (American GFS). This is what is done on board these boats when the race management communicates the position of the competitors four times a day. It is an aid to navigation strategy.\", \"sampled\": \"Using the Adrena software on a PC, we simulate the routing of 3 Class 40 boats in the Route du Rhum race on November 10, 2010. By analyzing a downloaded weather bulletin (American GFS), we visualize potential routes. This process mirrors how we navigate during the race, receiving competitor positions updates four times daily to aid in our navigation strategy.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Popular idol groups EXO, BTS, BTOB, VIXX, B1A4, Twice, AOA, LOVELYZ, and OH MY GIRL, along with over 200 idols, gathered in one place. According to MBC on the 29th, the largest-scale ever idol competition project, MBC's Chuseok (Korean Thanksgiving) special \\\"Idol Cooking King,\\\" will visit homes during the Chuseok holiday with its splendid and abundant highlights. MBC's Chuseok special \\\"Idol Cooking King\\\" is a large-scale competition to select the true cooking master among current Korean idols, starting with the new concept of discovering “cooking dolls” hidden in the music industry.\", \"sampled\": \"Popular idol groups such as EXO, BTS, BTOB, VIXX, B1A4, Twice, AOA, LOVELYZ, and OH MY GIRL, joined over 200 idols for MBC's Chuseok special \\\"Idol Cooking King.\\\" The project, the largest-ever idol cooking competition, promises to entertain viewers during the Chuseok holiday with exciting highlights. This special competition aims to discover hidden culinary talents among current Korean idols by challenging them to showcase their cooking skills. The qualifying round has already seen over 200 idols selecting mission cards on-site and preparing their dishes within the allotted time.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"In the United States, tornadoes and other large-scale weather phenomena are commonplace. But the tornado that hit Dallas was truly monstrous. The city of Dallas was seriously shaken by the passage of the tornado. These images are almost as impressive as the video from NASA showing the solar storm five times larger than the earth. The Dallas tornado literally devastated the city between April 3rd and 4th, 2012. This video testifies to the violence of the winds; we see cars and semi-trucks flying away like plastic bags in the air. Simply impressive. The photos you can see below were taken after the catastrophe. Several individuals show hailstones the size of a fist, capable of breaking the thick glass layer of windshields. Road signs even bent under the force of the gusts. The Dallas tornado caused enormous material and moral damage; it will take quite some time before residents recover from this chaos. Difficult to imagine such a thing happening in France, right?\", \"sampled\": \"Tornadoes and other large-scale weather events are common in the United States, but the tornado that struck Dallas was exceptionally massive. The city was severely impacted by the tornado's force between April 3 and 4, 2012. The destruction was evident in the NASA video, which depicted a solar storm five times the size of the earth. The footage captured the violent winds hurling cars and semi-trailers through the air like plastic bags. The aftermath photos revealed hailstones as large as fists that could shatter thick windshield glass, and road signs were twisted by the powerful gusts. The extensive material and emotional devastation caused by the Dallas tornado will require significant time for the residents to recover from the chaos. It's difficult to fathom such a catastrophe occurring in France, isn't it?\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"The incident caused panic among the passengers, who in moments of desperation began to pray and ask God that a tragedy would not occur, but among the group of believers stood out a man who resorted to a more earthly method to calm his nerves. In a part of the images, the determined man can be seen taking a bottle of alcohol and taking a long drink to calm the anxieties of the moment, after the drink the man shared his bottle with another passenger who also calmed his nerves.\", \"sampled\": \"The incident led to panic among the passengers, prompting some to pray and seek divine intervention. Amid the believers, one man took a different approach, opting to calm his nerves with alcohol. In a video clip, he can be seen taking a long swig from a bottle before sharing it with another passenger to ease the tension. The footage went viral, with many viewers supporting his actions, agreeing they might do the same in a similar last-resort situation. The flight, headed for Miami, had to make an emergency landing in Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic, due to the malfunction, where the issues were addressed.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"［July 27, Reuters］- Manchester United (Man U), a soccer team in England's Premier League, announced on July 27 an additional schedule for pre-season matches including a game in Shanghai, China. Man U will play against Inter (Italy) in Singapore on July 20th. On the 25th of the same month, they will have a game in Shanghai against Tottenham, also from the Premier League. Furthermore, a match against AC Milan (Italy) is scheduled in Cardiff on August 3rd. These games will be added to the two previously announced matches in Perth, Australia – the match against Perth Glory (Australia) on July 13th and the match against Leeds United (UK) on the 17th of the same month.\", \"sampled\": \"Reuters reported on the 27th that Manchester United (Man U) added more dates for preseason matches, including a game in Shanghai, China. Man U will play against Inter Milan (Italy) in Singapore on July 20 and Tottenham Hotspur in Shanghai on the 25th. Additionally, a match against AC Milan (Italy) is set for August 3 in Cardiff. These games join the previously announced matches in Perth, Australia, against Perth Glory on July 13 and Leeds United (UK) on the 17th. Man U's Executive Vice Chairman, Ed Woodward, emphasized that these matches and the tour serve not only as preseason preparations but also as opportunities for football development, entertainment, and engaging with fans in Australia and Asia.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"From March 1st (Friday) to March 3rd (Sunday), a food tasting fair sponsored by Hokkaido and Sapporo City was held at the Glorietta Activity Center (Palm Drive, Ground Floor, Glorietta 2). On that day, Hokkaido ramen, famous sweets, and more were offered free of charge, informing visitors about Hokkaido cuisine. The first 100 visitors received a gift of Sapporo Premium Beer, delighting attendees. In addition, there was a photo booth & session featuring the Philippine film \\\"Kita Kita,\\\" which was filmed in Hokkaido, livening up the venue. Travel agencies and airline booths were also set up at the venue, and some visitors purchased travel products on the spot. The event was held anticipating an increase in tourists to Hokkaido due to the launch of a new flight route between New Chitose Airport and Manila, attracting approximately 1,800 visitors over three days and promoting the appeal of traveling to Hokkaido.\", \"sampled\": \"The Food Tasting Fair organized by Hokkaido, Sapporo City took place from March 1st to March 3rd at the Glorietta Activity Center in Glorietta 2. The event showcased Hokkaido ramen, specialty sweets, and other gourmet delights to introduce visitors to Hokkaido's cuisine. Attendees enjoyed Sapporo Premium Beer as a gift for the first 100 visitors. A photo booth and a session featuring the Filipino film \\\"Kita Kita\\\" filmed in Hokkaido enhanced the festive atmosphere. Travel and airline company booths were present, with some visitors making on-the-spot travel purchases. With the aim of attracting tourists to Hokkaido through a new flight route between New Chitose Airport and Manila, the event drew approximately 1800 visitors over three days, promoting the allure of Hokkaido travel. Hidokazu Taniguchi from the Sapporo City Economic Tourism Bureau expressed plans to boost tourism by highlighting Hokkaido's appeal through food and similar initiatives in the future.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Is it a cavity? Unlike colds, cavities don't heal on their own; they only get worse. Nevertheless, I'm busy and can't easily go to the dentist, but I can't stand the pain! Here are some ways to relieve the pain that may be helpful in such cases. \\\"Blood\\\" list The effect of coldness on pregnant women and how to use ginger effectively. In addition to those who were originally prone to coldness, some people start to feel coldness in their bodies during pregnancy...\", \"sampled\": \"Could it be a cavity? Unlike a cold, cavities do not heal on their own and only worsen over time. If you are too busy to visit the dentist but cannot bear the pain, here are some tips to help alleviate the pain. When pregnant, some may experience chills. Keeping warm not only helps combat chills but also proves highly effective for overall health. Just soaking in a hot bath can be beneficial, but for additional recommendations to prevent chilling and maintain good health, try out these methods.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"I declined despite them taking the trouble to find it for me. I received recommended job openings from a career agent. They asked me to let them know my intention to apply within 3 days. I promptly checked the details of the job offer. As a result, I declined all applications. Reasons: - The nature of the work differs from what I am hoping for. - I am interested in the nature of the work, but I do not meet the required skills and experience. There was a job opening where I could utilize my Access VBA experience, but I declined because I was worried whether that experience would continue to be useful in the future.\", \"sampled\": \"I declined the job recommendations despite the effort put into finding the opportunity. The job agent requested my intention to apply within 3 days. After promptly reviewing the job details, I decided to decline all applications for reasons such as the job description not matching my preferences or lacking essential skills or experience. Even though there was a job requiring Access VBA experience, I declined due to uncertainty about its future use. I wonder why I am receiving job offers that don't align with my qualifications when the agent should know my work history. If I get the chance, I'll ask the agent about this. I feel sorry for declining after all the effort put into the search.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Committee Decisions CONF 203 VIII.C.1 Nomination: Historic Villages of Shirakawa-go and Gokayama (Japan) Historic Villages of Shirakawa-go and Gokayama 734 Japan C (iv) (v) The Committee decided to inscribe the property on the basis of criteria (iv) and (v) because the villages are exceptional examples of traditional human settlements perfectly adapted to their environment.\", \"sampled\": \"The Committee CONF 203 VIII.C.1 has chosen to list the Historic Villages of Shirakawa-go and Gokayama in Japan, under criteria (iv) and (v). These villages stand as remarkable examples of traditional human settlements that are seamlessly integrated with their environment. The Committee acknowledged their successful adaptation to economic shifts and emphasized that the preservation of these villages requires ongoing vigilance from both government authorities and the residents themselves.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Implant treatment Fukuoka City Minami Ward Mukai Shinmachi resident 60s female 2016.10.25 We will introduce voices from patients who received dental treatment at Hirota Tetsuya Dental Clinic. ◆Treatment Content Implant Treatment ◆Impressions It became more beautiful than I had imagined. ◆Doctor and Staff Response I was treated very kindly. We will introduce cases at our clinic. Q&A Summary from Hirota Tetsuya Dental Clinic At Hirota Tetsuya Dental Clinic in Fukuoka City Minami Ward, we aim for dental treatment that is as painless as possible. We value informed consent and provide dental treatment that satisfies each patient. Cavities and periodontal disease, anxieties about oral health issues, implant surgery that has been refused at other clinics… Please feel free to consult us if you have concerns about partial orthodontics, pediatric orthodontics, root canal treatment requiring precise procedures, or wisdom tooth extraction.\", \"sampled\": \"A woman in her 60s, living in Mukai Shinmachi, Minami Ward, Fukuoka City, received dental implant treatment at Hirota Tetsuya Dental Clinic on October 25, 2016. She was delighted with the results, which she found more impressive than she had anticipated. She also expressed gratitude for the exceptional care she received from the doctor and staff. At Hirota Tetsuya Dental Clinic, our goal is to make dental treatments as pain-free as possible. We prioritize informed consent and are dedicated to ensuring patient satisfaction. If you're dealing with dental issues like cavities or periodontal disease, or if you've been turned down for implant surgery at other clinics, we're here to help. Whether you need partial orthodontics, pediatric orthodontics, careful root canal treatment, or are struggling with wisdom tooth extraction, don't hesitate to reach out to us.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Prior to a medical diagnosis of skin spots, the following is done: - A session per affected area of spots on the skin with non-ablative laser in the affected area Treatment details: Non-ablative lasers generate energy pulses with a specific wavelength that acts on skin spots, helping to eliminate excess pigment that causes them in a couple of continued treatments, they also have the advantage of faster recovery.\", \"sampled\": \"Before diagnosing skin spots, a non-ablative laser treatment is typically performed on each affected area. This procedure involves using non-ablative lasers that emit energy pulses at a specific wavelength to target skin spots. These lasers help eliminate the excess pigment causing the spots through a series of continuous treatments. Additionally, they offer the benefit of a quicker recovery period.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Under the Ancien Régime, society was very largely rural. We all have peasant ancestors, whether they were farmers (those who owned land and employed people) or serfs. If the clergy paid the free gift (a minimal sum which it determined) and the nobility was exempt, peasants who were part of the Third Estate paid heavy taxes, such as the gabelle (the salt tax), the tithe paid to the clergy, and all noble taxes or feudal rights. Despised and devalued, they had no political representation while being numerically the most important and the country's economy rested on their work. At that time, 80% of the population was rural, of which 60% were peasants (the remaining 40% lived from crafts and industry). Overall, 50% of cultivated land belonged to the nobles and the clergy; peasants owning less good land than the two privileged classes. The majority possessed very small plots: less than two hectares whereas self-sufficiency for a family required about five hectares. The lands were exploited according to three different methods: - direct farming: the peasant owned his land which he farmed himself. - Indirect farming: the owner (a bourgeois, nobleman or member of the clergy) rented the land to the peasant in ferme (in return for a fixed rent) or in metayage (generally a half share of the harvest yield).\", \"sampled\": \"During the Old Regime, the majority of society was rural. We all have ancestors who were peasants, either land-owning plowmen who employed others, or serfs. The clergy fulfilled a minimal financial obligation, a gratuitous gift, while the nobility was exempt. However, the peasants, who made up the third estate, bore the brunt of heavy taxation. This included the gabelle, or salt tax, tithes paid to the clergy, and various noble or seigneurial taxes. Despite being the largest demographic and the backbone of the country's economy, they were often marginalized and undervalued, with no political representation. At the time, 80% of the population lived in rural areas, with peasants making up 60% of this group. The remaining 40% were involved in crafts and industry. On the whole, 50% of the cultivated land was owned by the nobles and the clergy, with peasants owning less fertile land than these two privileged classes. Most peasants owned very small plots, less than two hectares, when self-sufficiency for a family required about five hectares. The land was used in three different ways. In direct operation, the peasant owned and cultivated his own land. In indirect operation, the land was rented to the peasant by an owner, who could be a bourgeois, noble, or clergy member. This could be in the form of a fixed rent, known as farm rent, or as sharecropping, where the peasant usually gave half of the harvest to the owner.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We often say that \\\"like Cantabria nowhere else,\\\" that our waters have nothing to envy the Caribbean; that wherever there is meat or seafood, spaghetti bolognese can step aside. It is a modernist building located in the Cantabrian town of Comillas. This work belongs to Gaudí's Orientalist period (1883-1888), a period in which the architect carried out a series of works with a marked Eastern taste. The building fell into disrepair after the Spanish Civil War.\", \"sampled\": \"We often proclaim, \\\"There's no place like Cantabria.\\\" Our waters rival the beauty of the Caribbean, and our meat and seafood dishes make spaghetti bolognese seem bland in comparison. Nestled in the Cantabrian town of Comillas is a modernist building, a testament to Gaudí's Orientalist phase from 1883 to 1888. During this period, the architect created a series of works with a unique Oriental flair. Unfortunately, the building fell into disrepair following the Civil War.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"We have implemented saving and loading of plucked string instruments. This data will either be written within a music file (.myr), or independently to allow the creation of an instrument library. In both cases, this binary data has been organized into a “meta-file”, which should make our task easier when we modify the structures, evolve them or improve them, while maintaining compatibility with both old and future versions of the program. It’s almost finished, but the sound before saving and after loading is not exactly identical. We must have made an error when saving one of the parameters. We are looking for it.\", \"sampled\": \"We've successfully implemented the save and load feature for plucked string instruments. This data can either be embedded within a music file (.myr), or saved separately to facilitate the creation of an instrument library. In both scenarios, we've arranged the binary data into a \\\"meta-file\\\". This organization should simplify our work when we need to modify, evolve, or enhance the structures, ensuring compatibility with both past and future versions of the program. However, we've noticed a slight discrepancy: the sound before saving and after loading isn't exactly the same. It appears we may have made an error when saving one of the parameters. We're currently investigating this issue.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"On the 24th, Aichi Prefecture began burying bait containing vaccine in forests within the prefecture to prevent the spread of swine fever to pig farms mediated by wild boars. This marks the first time vaccines have been administered to wildlife in Japan. The prefecture will conduct a total of six rounds through February of next year while verifying the effectiveness. The burial sites include a total of 60 locations, including Kasugai City in the same prefecture where infected wild boars were confirmed. Over two days, approximately 40 doses of vaccine per location will be buried within an area of about 100 square meters, digging holes 5–10 centimeters deep. They will excavate and check five days after burial to see if it has been eaten. At a collection point in Komaki City, prefectural staff and commissioned workers wearing white protective suits shouldered cooler bags containing the vaccine and headed into the mountains.\", \"sampled\": \"On the 24th, Aichi Prefecture initiated a preventative measure against the spread of swine fever from wild boars to pig farms by burying vaccine-laden bait in mountain forests within the region. This marks the first instance of vaccines being administered to wild animals in the country. The prefecture, while assessing the effects, intends to repeat this procedure six times by next February. The bait will be buried in approximately 60 locations, including Kasugai City, where infected boars have been identified. Over a span of two days, around 40 doses of vaccine will be buried in an area of about 100 square meters per location, with holes dug 5 to 10 centimeters deep. Five days post-burial, the bait will be unearthed to verify if it has been consumed. In Komaki City, located in the same prefecture, prefectural employees and commissioned contractors, donned in white protective gear, carried bags filled with vaccines and ventured into the mountains.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"If you add a new language to Windows, a default keyboard is also added to allow you to type in that language. If you don't want to use the default keyboard, you can add another keyboard layout or another Input Method Editor (IME) to enter text. Swipe from the right edge of the screen, then tap Search. (If you're using a mouse, point to the upper-right corner of the screen, move the pointer down, and then click Search.) Enter language, select Settings, then tap or click Add a language. Choose the language to which to add a keyboard layout, tap or click Options next to the language name, then tap or click Add an input method. Browse the list of input methods or use the search box to find the keyboard layout you want to use. Tap or double-click the keyboard layout, then tap or click Save. To set a keyboard as the default keyboard, follow these steps: Tap or click Advanced settings. Under Replace the default input method, select the keyboard layout to use as the default keyboard, then tap or click Save. See all support pages regarding customization and ergonomics options. Ask a question on the community forums.\", \"sampled\": \"When you introduce a new language to Windows, a default keyboard is automatically added to facilitate typing in that language. If you prefer not to use the default keyboard, you have the option to add a different keyboard layout or another Input Method Editor (IME) for text input. To start, swipe from the right edge of the screen and press Search. If you're using a mouse, move the pointer to the upper-right corner of the screen, drag it down, and click on Search. Type \\\"language\\\", select Settings, and then press or click on Add a language. Next, select the language for which you want to add a keyboard layout. Press or click on Options adjacent to the language name, and then press or click on Add an input method. Browse the list of input methods or use the search box to locate the keyboard layout you wish to use. Double-tap or double-click on your chosen keyboard layout, then press or click on Save. If you want to set a keyboard as the default, follow these steps: Press or click on Advanced Settings. Under the Replace default input method section, choose the keyboard layout you want as the default, and then press or click on Save. For further assistance, peruse all the help pages related to customization and accessibility options. Don't hesitate to post a question on the community forums.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Incorporation (business) The incorporation (literal reproduction of the English term, abbreviated as Inc.) is the creation of a legal entity, an entity recognized as a person by law. The English term is used only when seeking the nationality of the company. One of the criteria that could be used to identify this nationality is the so-called criterion of \\\"incorporation,\\\" the place where the company is registered. Identifying the nationality of a company is useful especially for issues of diplomatic protection or invocation of treaties (a company can invoke a treaty against authorities only if it proves that its national state is a signatory to that treaty). In French law, it is not the criterion of incorporation that is used, but that of the registered office: a company is of the nationality of the country in which it has established its registered office. This solution is more flexible; it allows us to ask about the actual headquarters of the company.\", \"sampled\": \"\\\"Incorporation,\\\" abbreviated as \\\"Inc.,\\\" is a term that refers to the establishment of a legal entity, recognized as a person under the law. This English term is typically used when determining a company's nationality. One way to identify this nationality is through the \\\"incorporation\\\" criterion, which is based on the location where the company is registered. Determining a company's nationality is particularly useful for matters such as diplomatic protection or the invocation of treaties, as a company can only invoke a treaty against authorities if it can prove that its home state is a signatory to that treaty. However, in French law, the nationality of a company is not determined by the incorporation criterion, but by the location of its head office. This means a company's nationality is determined by the country where its head office is located. This approach is more flexible as it allows for questioning the actual location of the company's headquarters.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Spicy stir-fried burdock root and celery How to make it Since it oxidizes and discolors quickly, soak it in water immediately after cutting. Scrape the skin of the burdock root with the back of a knife, slice diagonally thinly, then julienne, and drain in water. Remove the fibers from the celery and cut into 5 cm length matchsticks. Heat 1 tablespoon of salad oil in a frying pan and stir-fry the burdock root, adding 1/3 teaspoon of salt and 1 tablespoon of sake, and continue to stir-fry. Once the burdock root is cooked through, add red chili pepper, black pepper to taste, and celery, and stir-fry together. *When using a microwave, this is based on a 500W model. For 600W, heat for 0.8 times the time, and for 700W, heat for 0.7 times the time. Also, there may be differences depending on the model, so please heat while checking the progress. *Please see \\\"About Recipes\\\" for standards regarding recipe creation and notation.\", \"sampled\": \"Spicy Stir-Fried Burdock and Celery: Burdock tends to oxidize and discolor quickly, so it's best to immerse it in water immediately after cutting. To prepare the burdock, use the back of a knife to scrape off the skin, slice it thinly on a diagonal, and then cut it into thin strips. Soak these strips in water to remove excess moisture. For the celery, remove any fibrous strings and cut it into strips approximately 5cm long. Next, heat a tablespoon of salad oil in a frying pan and stir-fry the burdock. Add a third of a teaspoon of salt and a tablespoon of sake, continuing to stir-fry. Once the burdock is cooked, introduce some red chili peppers, a suitable amount of black pepper, and the celery, and stir-fry the mixture together. Please note, if you're using a microwave, these instructions are based on a 500W model. If your microwave is 600W, you should reduce the cooking time to 80%, and if it's 700W, reduce the time to 70%. Adjust the heating time according to your microwave model and keep an eye on the cooking process. For guidelines on recipe creation and notation, refer to the \\\"About the Recipe\\\" section.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Cost Analysis using an ABC Cost Model It is necessary to have an ABC cost model that reflects the operation in order to perform a good analysis. The abc cost model is based on having the mechanics of allocation and calculation of process, activity, product, customer costs, according to the actual consumption of the organization's resources. The organization's resources can be classified into human resources (payroll), material resources and infrastructure resources (assets, invested capital). Once the abc cost model is available, an aggregation of the indirect costs and expenses of each cost center is carried out by expense concept, so that the allocation and traceability of costs in the abc cost model can be facilitated. Subsequently, all activities and processes must be identified to associate the grouped expenses (resources) to each of the activities and processes to obtain their cost, whether using monetary amounts or some driver that helps to accurately redistribute the costs. Activity-Based Costing in cost analysis pursues the following objectives: The strategic cost management system for its analysis should order the activities and analyze their flow. Subsequently, it must have allocations based on both financial and non-financial indicators or metrics. It should allow analyzing each activity independently to determine capacity, fixed and variable costs, generating improvement alternatives.\", \"sampled\": \"Cost Analysis Using an ABC Cost Model An ABC cost model that reflects the operation is needed to carry out a sound analysis. The ABC cost model is based on mechanisms for assigning and calculating the costs of processes, activities, products, and customers according to the organization’s actual consumption of resources. The organization’s resources can be classified as human resources (payroll), material resources, and infrastructure resources (assets, invested capital). Once the ABC cost model is in place, the indirect costs and expenses of each cost center are grouped by expense concept, making it easier to assign and trace costs in the ABC cost model. Next, all activities and processes must be identified so the grouped expenses (resources) can be associated with each activity and process to determine their cost, either through monetary amounts or through a driver that helps perform an accurate suballocation of costs. Activity-Based Costing in cost analysis pursues the following objectives: For its analysis, the strategic cost management system must organize activities and analyze their flow. It must then have assignments based on both financial and nonfinancial indicators or metrics. It must allow each activity to be analyzed independently to determine capacity and fixed and variable costs, generating improvement alternatives.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Two days of celebration for Plants Street. Plants Street feasted for two days. The neighborhood gathering, the tenth of its name, attracted around thirty people, some households having left the area for well-deserved summer vacations. This year, the festivities took place at Marylène and Dany D'Andrea's home. When the first guests arrived, baskets laden with all kinds of food, the table was set and the barbecue was smoking. Marie-Thérèse, on the verge of her eighties, watched over her neighbors and the youngest at the large table, Stacy, who celebrated her fifth birthday last April. Although Plants Street has been enriched by a new housing development in recent years, Sandra and Yohan have lived at the heart of the street since November 2010. Giselle and Michel have lived on Plants Street since 1969, the year of their marriage, and they recently had a house built across the street. This shows how much they love their street. “The street wasn’t named when we moved in,” Giselle points out. It was then called the small path and there were only plots of land, fields. Then houses began to spring up.” For two days, residents partied, played pétanque, and swam in the pool.\", \"sampled\": \"Two days of celebration for rue des Plantes. Rue des Plantes celebrated for two days. The neighborhood gathering, the tenth of its kind, drew about thirty people, with some households away on well-deserved summer holidays. This year, the festivities took place at the home of Marylène and Dany D'Andrea. When the first guests arrived, their baskets filled with all kinds of provisions, the table was set and the barbecue was smoking. Marie-Thérèse, approaching her eightieth spring, kept an eye on her neighbors and on the youngest at the large table, Stacy, who celebrated her fifth birthday last April. Although rue des Plantes has gained a new housing development in recent years, Sandra and Yohan have lived in the heart of the street since November 2010. Gisèle and Michel have lived on rue des Plantes since 1969, the year they married, and recently had a house built opposite. That shows how much they love their street. “The street had no name when we moved in,” Gisèle points out. “It was called the little path then, and there was nothing but plots of land and fields. Then the houses began to spring up.” For two days, the residents celebrated, played pétanque, and jumped into the swimming pool.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Josette is a 1938 Indian film directed by Allan Dwan. It is a crime comedy and musical starring Don Ameche, Simone Simon, and Robert Young. It is based on the play Jo and Josette by Paul Frank and Georg Fraser and a story by Ladislaus Vadnai. The film, directed by Allan Dwan from a screenplay by James Edward Grant and the theme of Ladislaus Vadnai, Paul Frank and Georg Fraser, was produced by Gene Markey, as associate producer, for the Twentieth Century Fox Film Corporation and filmed at 20th Century Fox Studios in Century City, Los Angeles, California from December 20, 1937 to January 1938. The working title was Jo and Josette. The film premiered in the United States on June 3, 1938 by the Twentieth Century Fox Film Corporation. Other distributions: The tagline is: \\\"Voila! It's the most fun escape in the history of love! Gayer than a Mardi Gras... and happy with the latest songs by Gordon and Revel!\\\".\", \"sampled\": \"Josette is a 1938 Indian film directed by Allan Dwan. It is a crime and musical comedy starring Don Ameche, Simone Simon, and Robert Young. It is based on the play Jo and Josette by Paul Frank and Georg Fraser and on a story by Ladislaus Vadnai. The film, directed by Allan Dwan from a screenplay by James Edward Grant and a story by Ladislaus Vadnai, Paul Frank, and Georg Fraser, was produced by Gene Markey as associate producer for the Twentieth Century Fox Film Corporation. It was filmed at 20th Century Fox Studios in Century City, Los Angeles, California, from December 20, 1937, to January 1938. The working title was Jo and Josette. The film was released in the United States on June 3, 1938, by the Twentieth Century Fox Film Corporation. Other distributions: The slogan is: \\\"Voila! It is the funniest getaway in the history of love! Gayer than a Mardi Gras... and happy with the latest songs by Gordon and Revel!\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The promoter of the Rivière-du-Moulin wind farm project, located in the Laurentides wildlife reserve, is preparing to begin installation work. According to the management of EDF Renewables, a subsidiary of the French company EDF, the Quebec government should soon give its approval to the project, which provides for the erection of 175 wind turbines. Its spokesperson, Daniel Giguère, states that the company has responded to concerns expressed by the Bureau d'audiences publiques en environnement (BAPE) regarding the Bicknell's thrush, a bird that nests on some of the chosen construction sites. EDF Renewables would also be about to reach an agreement with the Innu and Huron communities who share the use of this territory, which opens the door to the start of deforestation work as early as this winter. The wind farm project is valued at $800 million. It has an electrical production potential of 350 megawatts.\", \"sampled\": \"The developer of the Rivière-du-Moulin wind farm project, in the Laurentides wildlife reserve, is preparing to begin installation work. According to the management of EDF Énergies nouvelles, a subsidiary of the French company EDF, the Quebec government is expected to approve the project soon. It calls for the construction of 175 wind turbines. Spokesperson Daniel Giguère says the company has addressed concerns raised by the Bureau d'audiences publiques en environnement (BAPE) about Bicknell's thrush, a bird that nests on some of the selected installation sites. EDF Énergies nouvelles is also said to be close to reaching an agreement with the Innu and Huron communities that share use of the territory, paving the way for clearing work to begin as early as this winter. The wind farm project is valued at 800 million dollars and has an electricity production potential of 350 megawatts.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Hearse Posted on: November 4, 2014 Staff chatter Hello. It's Futang from Wiz House Kita 12!! Today I will write a little about hearses. A vehicle that carries a coffin and heads towards the crematorium is generally called a hearse. In the past, people used to carry it on their shoulders. It seems they walked along the road to the crematorium forming a funeral procession. After that, they started carrying it on something like an oxcart. From there, it evolved by mounting a palanquin on a truck, and eventually became its current form. There are two types of hearses: 【Palace-style Hearse】 and 【Western-style Hearse】. The Palace-style Hearse is what you would imagine when you think of a hearse. They are golden hearses with a palanquin or a dragon on them. Recently, aren't Western-style hearses becoming more common? Lincoln, Cadillac, Benz, Ford, etc., are collectively called limousines. Until recently, 【black】 was the standard color for Western-style hearses. However, lately 【white】 has become very popular, and things have been changing with the times. Not only with hearses, but things that were commonplace until recently are now rare, and things that were rare are now commonplace… I feel that flexibility is important.\", \"sampled\": \"Hearse Posted: November 4, 2014 Staff Musings. Hello. This is Futakara from With House Kita 12!! Today I’d like to write a little about hearses. Cars that carry a coffin to the crematorium are generally called hearses. In the old days, people carried coffins on their shoulders. It is said that they formed a funeral procession and walked the route to the crematorium. Later, coffins apparently came to be transported on something like a large two-wheeled cart. From there, hearses evolved, with a palanquin placed on a truck and other changes, becoming the form we see today. There are “Miya-style hearses” and “Western-style hearses.” A Miya-style hearse looks exactly like what people imagine as a hearse. They are the gold-shining hearses, including ones with a palanquin on top and ones with a dragon. These days, Western-style hearses may be the mainstream. Lincolns, Cadillacs, Mercedes-Benzes, Fords, and the like are sometimes grouped together as limousines. Until recently, Western-style hearses meant “black.” These days, however, “white” has become very popular, and all kinds of things have changed with the times. Not only with hearses, things that were taken for granted until recently are now rare, and things that were rare are now ordinary... I feel that flexibility is important.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"4zgmd Trailer with subtitles of the movie How much do you love me? Algiers, nowadays, Adel, 8 years old, is entrusted to his grandparents Khadidja and Lounès because his parents Rachid and Safia have argued. Adel was supposed to stay with his grandparents for a week, to which another week is added and he misses school. Khadidja, a housewife tries to share her daily life in her apartment with Adel, while Lounès, a retiree introduces him to the great world of animals. Day after day, the question \\\"How much do you love me\\\" that the child and his grandmother ask each other helps them get through this difficult period and get closer to each other.\", \"sampled\": \"4zgmd English-subtitled trailer for the film How Much Do You Love Me. Algiers, today: Adel, 8, is left in the care of his grandparents, Khadidja and Lounès, after his parents, Rachid and Safia, have an argument. Adel was supposed to stay with them for one week, but then another week is added, and he misses school. Khadidja, a housewife, tries to share her daily life in her apartment with Adel, while Lounès, now retired, introduces him to the great world of animals. Day by day, the question \\\"How much do you love me,\\\" which the child and his grandmother ask each other, helps them get through this difficult time and grow closer.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A false bishop, Australian hacker Ralph Napierski, who has created an illegal religious congregation called Corpus Dei, infiltrated the Vatican complex yesterday among the cardinals gathered to prepare the conclave. Upon being discovered, he was expelled. Despite his attire, it could be seen that the false bishop's cassock was too short, and the purple sash he wore at the waist differed from the regulation one for prelates, just as he wore a cross around his neck that was too large.\", \"sampled\": \"A fake bishop, the Australian hacker Ralph Napierski, who founded an illegal religious congregation called Corpus Dei, slipped into the Vatican complex yesterday among the cardinals meeting to prepare for the conclave. When he was discovered, he was expelled. Despite his attire, it was clear that the fake bishop’s cassock was far too short for him, the violet sash around his waist differed from the one prescribed for prelates, and the cross hanging around his neck was much too large.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"A pool heat pump from Dimplex maintains a constant water temperature in your swimming pool throughout the bathing season and beyond, as desired, regardless of weather conditions, all while being very efficient and having relatively low operating costs. A manual adjustment is sufficient to determine the temperature of your pool water. This way, you can continue enjoying your pool, even outside the summer season! The ambient heat accumulated in the outdoor air serves as a free energy source, even at air temperatures of -10 °C to +35 °C. 1. Pool heat pump 2. Remote control 3. Pure water towards the pool 4. Circulation pump 5. Bypass and regulating valves 6. Untreated water coming from the pool 7.\", \"sampled\": \"A Dimplex heat pump for swimming pools keeps the pool water at the temperature you want throughout the entire swimming season and beyond. It does this regardless of the weather, very efficiently and with relatively low operating costs. A manual setting is all it takes to set the water temperature of your pool. That way, you can continue enjoying your pool even outside the summer season. The ambient heat stored in the outdoor air serves as a free energy source, even at air temperatures from -10 °C to +35 °C. 1. Swimming pool heat pump. 2. Remote controller. 3. Clean water to the pool. 4. Circulation pump. 5. Bypass and control valves. 6. Untreated water from the pool. 7. Filter.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The Green Santa Fund is nurturing vibrant Green Santa Forests across Japan, collaborating with local NPOs to connect to the future. And now, finally, a Green Santa Forest has been born in Tokyo! The location is Hinoara Village, Nishitama District, Tokyo Prefecture. We received great support from Tanaka Forestry Co., Ltd. during the LEAF practice in January this year and at the recent International Peace Day event, and we have decided to register their forest as a Green Santa Forest. We also plan to implement LEAF programs in the future. Comments are closed.\", \"sampled\": \"The Green Santa Fund is fostering lively Green Santa forests that lead to the future in various parts of Japan, with the cooperation of locally active NPOs and others. And now, at last, a Green Santa forest has been born in Tokyo! The location is Hinohara Village, Nishitama District, Tokyo. The forest of Tanaka Forestry, which helped us with LEAF practice in January this year and gave us tremendous support at the recent International Day of Peace event, will be registered as a Green Santa forest. We plan to conduct LEAF programs there as well. Comments are not accepted.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"We are checking rights to see if this movie is available for rent in your geographic area. This action may take a few minutes. Thank you for your patience. Based on your IP address, we determine that your geographic area is: . Unfortunately, this movie is not available for rent in your geographic area. The service is temporarily unavailable. Please try again later. After purchasing a rental, you will have 48 hours to watch the movie. A collaborative work made in the spirit of cinéma-vérité, St-Henri, the 26th of August was directed by Shannon Walsh and16 fellow documentary filmmakers. Chronicling life in a former working-class Montreal neighborhood over a 24-hour period, St-Henri, the 26th of August follows several compelling stories and characters. The film is an homage to the 1962 Hubert Aquin classic À Saint-Henri le cinq septembre. 1 You can only view this film for personal use, in a library or classroom. Public and institutional screenings (non-educational establishments) are prohibited. 2 Institutional prices include the rights to use the film in class and free public screening.\", \"sampled\": \"We are checking the rights to determine whether this film is available for rental in your geographic area. This may take a few minutes. Thank you for your patience. Based on your IP address, we have determined that your geographic area is: . Unfortunately, this film is not available for rental in your geographic area. The service is temporarily unavailable. Please try again later. After purchasing a rental, you will have 48 hours to watch the film. A collaborative work made in the spirit of cinéma-vérité, St-Henri, the 26th of August was directed by Shannon Walsh and 16 fellow documentary filmmakers. Chronicling life in a former working-class Montreal neighbourhood over a 24-hour period, St-Henri, the 26th of August follows several compelling stories and characters. The film is an homage to the 1962 Hubert Aquin classic À Saint-Henri le cinq septembre. 1 You may view this film only for personal use, in a library, or in a classroom. Public and institutional screenings at non-educational institutions are prohibited. 2 Institutional prices include the rights to use the film in class and for free public screenings.\", \"variant_type\": \"ai_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Military medal for general service, “Fort Detroit” clasp This medal, significant due to the “Fort Detroit” clasp it bears, was awarded to those who participated in the capture of Fort Detroit on American territory on August 16, 1812. Early that morning, Major General Sir Isaac Brock led a group of 300 British regular soldiers and 400 Canadian militiamen, who followed the 600 warriors of Tecumseh's First Nations and crossed the Detroit River. After a few hours, American Brigadier General William Hull surrendered, unable to defend the fort against the bold attack by Brock and Tecumseh. This military medal for general service was awarded in 1848 to highlight participation in military operations conducted between 1793 and 1814. Only three clasps were awarded in connection with the War of 1812, namely for participation in the capture of Fort Detroit (1812), the Battle of Chateauguay (1813) and the Battle of Crysler’s Farm (1813).\", \"sampled\": \"Military General Service Medal with \\\"Fort Detroit\\\" clasp — This medal, significant because of the \\\"Fort Detroit\\\" clasp it bears, was awarded to those who participated in the capture of Fort Detroit on American soil on August 16, 1812. Early that morning, Major General Sir Isaac Brock led a force of 300 British regular soldiers and 400 Canadian militia, who followed Tecumseh's 600 First Nations warriors across the Detroit River. After a few hours, American Brigadier General William Hull surrendered, unable to defend the fort against the bold attack mounted by Brock and Tecumseh. This Military General Service Medal was issued in 1848 to recognize participation in military operations conducted between 1793 and 1814. Only three clasps were awarded in connection with the War of 1812, namely for participation in the capture of Fort Detroit (1812), the Battle of Châteauguay (1813), and the Battle of Crysler's Farm (1813).\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Jerusalem.- The Israeli Executive today celebrated the willingness of the British Government to declare the Palestinian Islamist movement Hamas as a terrorist organization in its entirety, something the group described as \\\"a continuation of the aggression\\\" against the Palestinian people and their rights. Operation Underground Railroad “I celebrate the intention of the United Kingdom to declare Hamas as a terrorist organization in its totality, because that is exactly what it is,” said today on Twitter the Israeli Prime Minister, Naftali Benet, shortly after the British Home Secretary, Priti Patel, announced the decision. Operation Underground Railroad USA Furthermore, Benet thanked the British Prime Minister, his “friend” Boris Johnson, and emphasized that Hamas “is a radical Islamic group that attacks innocent Israelis and seeks the destruction of Israel.” Operation Underground Railroad EEUU Hamas, which has de facto controlled the Gaza Strip since 2007, responded with a statement in which it urged the United Kingdom to “stop depending on the Zionist narrative and project” and emphasized that “resistance to occupation, by all available means, including armed resistance, is a right guaranteed to peoples occupied by international law.”\", \"sampled\": \"Jerusalem.- The Israeli government today celebrated the willingness of the British government to declare the Palestinian Islamist movement Hamas in its entirety a terrorist organization, something the group described as \\\"a continuation of the aggression\\\" against the Palestinian people and their rights. Israeli Prime Minister Naftali Bennett said on Twitter, \\\"I welcome the UK's intention to declare Hamas a terrorist organization in its entirety, because that is exactly what it is,\\\" shortly after British Home Secretary Priti Patel announced the decision and Bennett also thanked his \\\"friend\\\" British Prime Minister Boris Johnson emphasizing that Hamas \\\"is a radical Islamic group that attacks innocent Israelis and seeks the destruction of Israel.\\\" Hamas, which has de facto controlled the Gaza Strip since 2007, responded with a statement urging the United Kingdom to \\\"stop relying on the Zionist narrative and project\\\" and emphasized that \\\"resistance to occupation by all available means including armed resistance is a right guaranteed to occupied peoples under international law.\\\"\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The yuzu soda and hot yuzu we serve on the roof are made with yuzu tea from Umakage Village, Kochi Prefecture. We received summer greetings from Umakage Village. The theme is \\\"Grandpa's Summer and My Summer,\\\" and apparently there are still many things that haven't changed since the old days, and it's a place I'd like to visit if it were closer. We also sell yuzu tea so you can enjoy it at home. Please leave a comment.\", \"sampled\": \"The yuzu soda and hot yuzu we serve at Roof are made using yuzu tea from Umajiimura in Kochi Prefecture. A summer newsletter has arrived from that very Umajiimura with the theme \\\"Grandpa's Summer and My Summer,\\\" and it is full of things that remain unchanged from long ago, making it a place we would love to visit if it were a little closer. Yuzu tea is also available for purchas, so you can enjoy it at home too. Please leave a comment.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The Belgian tennis player broke all predictions and prevailed in the final against Russian Ekaterina Makarova with a score of 7-5, 6-2. Belgian tennis may have found a new champion after the retirements of Justine Henin and Kim Clijsters. The Estoril Tournament was able to witness the first victory on the Sony Ericsson WTA Tour circuit of young Belgian tennis player Yanina Wickmayer. Wickmayer, who recently turned 19 years old, after overcoming the nerves of the first set which was marked by numerous errors from both players, managed to dominate the second set more easily and win the match and the title against an Ekaterina Makarova who proved very inaccurate with her serve throughout the match.\", \"sampled\": \"The Belgian tennis player defied all predictions and defeated Russian Ekaterina Makarova in the final by a score of 7-5, 6-2, and Belgian tennis may have found a new champion following the retirements of Justine Hénin and Kim Clijsters. The Estoril Tournament witnessed the first victory on the Sony Ericsson WTA Tour circuit for young Belgian tennis player Yanina Wickmayer. Wickmayer who had just turned 19, overcame the nerves of the first set which was marked by numerous errors from both players and managed to dominate the second set with greater ease claiming the match and the title against an Ekaterina Makarova who proved very imprecise with her serve throughout the entire match. Sony Ericsson WTA Tour Estoril Results.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"I am new to Android development and want to add a chat screen to my application. I found this project on Github. I made a layout resource file called chat_screen.xml and I want to add the post screen from the Github project to it. How do I do this? Import the entire project from GitHub, then modify it as needed. Download the whole project on github.\", \"sampled\": \"I am new to Android development and want to add a chat screen to my application. I found this project on Github. I created a layout resources file called chat_screen.xml and want to add the posts screen from the Github project to it. How Should I do this? Import the entire project from GitHub, then modify it as needed. Download the whole project from Github. Go to File > New > Import Project, and tip: just click the default selection.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Jori Lehtera is a Finnish player who plays in the KHL. He was a 3rd round pick of the St. Louis Blues in 2008. He never broke through in the Bettman league, but he is a good player in Russia. As proof, he played in the All-Star game. In this game, he was awarded a penalty shot and took advantage of it to use a feint that we don't often see.\", \"sampled\": \"Jori Lehtera is a Finnish player who competes in the KHL. He was a 3rd-round pick of the St. Louis Blues in 2008. He never broke through in the Bettman circuit. However, he is a solid player in Russia which earned him a spot in the All-Star Game. In that game, he was awarded a penalty shot. He took the opportunity to use a deke that is rarely seen.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"In Stock Components: Contains Aloe Vera Extract, Chamomile, Vitamin E, Allantoin, Argan Oil and Glycerin. Benefits: Softens beard hair for a more comfortable shave. Its foam helps protect skin from irritation caused by the razor and improves its glide. Leaves skin soft, hydrated and with a gentle fresh fragrance. Can be used with or without a brush. Its consistency is airy, with a mousse-like appearance. Its fragrance is a fresh, marine, subtle note.\", \"sampled\": \"In Stock Components: Contains Aloe Vera Extract, Chamomile, Vitamin E, Allantoin, Argan Oil, and Glycerin. Benefits: Softens the beard for a more comfortable shave. Its foam helps protect the skin from irritation caused by the razor and improves its glide. It leaves the skin soft, moisturized, and with a gentle fresh scent. It can be used with or without a brush. Its consistency is airy with a mousse-like appearance. Its fragrance is a fresh, marine, soft note.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Based on learning in diverse areas of language and culture, we aim to enhance comprehensive human capabilities. We have designed a curriculum throughout the faculty to allow students to acquire broader knowledge while studying various languages such as Japanese, English, Chinese, German, French, and Korean, along with the cultures underlying those languages. Let's enter a world of learning befitting a university. We cultivate practical language skills and aim to develop specialists in language. We offer a variety of opportunities for your growth, including presentations and discussions in class, exchange with international students, volunteer activities in local communities, language training and internship programs abroad, and study abroad at numerous partner universities and institutions overseas. Please make active use of them. Following the founding spirit of \\\"Human Love,\\\" we expand the faculty's educational philosophy as \\\"Respect for Individuality.\\\" Throughout the four years, mainly through classes in the first and second years and seminars in the third and fourth years, you will learn closely with friends and teachers in an intimate relationship. Immerse yourself in interactions between people expanding from campus to local communities and international relations, hone your communication skills, understand others' perspectives, and establish your own individuality.\", \"sampled\": \"Grounded in learning across the diverse fields of language and culture, this program aims to develop students' comprehensive abilities as human beings. While studying a range of languages, including Japanese, English and American English, Chinese, German, French, and Korean, along with the cultures underlying each language, the faculty-wide curriculum is carefully designed so that students can acquire broad knowledge. Step into the world of learning that a university has to offer. The program also aims to cultivate practical language proficiency and develop specialists in language. From presentations and discussions in class to exchanges with international students, volunteer activities in the local community, language training and internship programs abroad, and study opportunities at numerous affiliated overseas universities and institutions, a wide variety of chances for personal growth are prepared for you, so make active use of them. Reflecting the founding spirit of \\\"love for humanity,\\\" the faculty's guiding educational philosophy is developed under the principle of \\\"respect for the individual.\\\" During the first and second years, classes serve as the core unit while seminars become central in the third and fourth years, allowing students to learn over four years in close contact with friends and faculty within warm human relationships. Place yourself within the web of human connections that extends from the campus to the local community and into international relations, and as you refine your communication skills establish both a perspective for understanding others and your own individuality.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"The cherry blossoms are beautiful. Near our company there is a row of cherry trees, which show us lovely flowers every year. Every time I look at those flowers, my heart feels healed. (This photo is from the cherry blossoms two years ago ✨) If the cold winter turns into a warm spring, my feelings also become springlike, but this spring brings yellow dust, Japanese cedar pollen, and PM2.5. When you open the windows of your house, fine particles such as yellow dust, Japanese cedar pollen, and PM2.5 enter the room. It seems that these fine particles can penetrate deep into the body and may have adverse effects on human health... Especially small children have low resistance, so caution is necessary. Even without opening the windows, you can suppress energy costs and obtain healthy air by installing a ventilation system that exchanges outside fresh air and removes fine particles.\", \"sampled\": \"Cherry blossoms are beautiful. There is a row of cherry trees near our office and they treat us to lovely blooms every year. Each time we see those flowers, our hearts feel at ease and this photo was taken two years ago. When the cold winter gives way to warm spring, our spirits lift too, but spring is also when yellow dust, cedar pollen, and PM2.5 begin to drift through the air. Opening a window at home allows these fine particles to enter the room and because they penetrate deep into the body, they may pose a risk to human health. Young children in particular have less resistance and require special attention. By installing a ventilation unit that performs heat exchange with fresh outside air and filters out fine particles without opening windows, you can reduce energy costs while breathing clean, healthy air.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"claude-sonnet-4-6\", \"original\": \"Summary and synopsis of Aristotle's Magna MoraliaThe key to understanding the moral problem in Aristotle lies in the study of his three treatises on ethics: Nicomachean Ethics, Eudemian Ethics, and the one we present now, Magna Moralia, also called Great Ethics, which is not simply a collection of excerpts from the other two treatises, but a masterful synthesis, in the form of a brief series of treatises. One reason to return to Aristotle's ethics is to study how much his ethical perspective differs from ours. It is not unreasonable to say that today we live without a coherent and coercive morality, according to Jonathan Lear, an expert on the work of the Stagirite. bclaudia participated in this record.\", \"sampled\": \"The key to understanding the moral problem in Aristotle lies in studying his three ethical treatises: Nicomachean Ethics, Eudemian Ethics, and the one now presented here, Magna Moralia, also called the Great Ethics, which is not simply a collection of excerpts from the other two treatises but rather a masterful synthesis in the form of a brief series of treatises. One reason to return to Aristotle's ethics is to study how far his ethical perspective is from our own. It is not far fetched to say that today we live without a coherent and coercive morality, according to Jonathan Lear, the foremost expert on the work of the Stagirite.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"During today in Jaén there will be cloudless skies For today in Jaén clear skies are predicted. Regarding temperatures, these will oscillate between a maximum of 36 degrees and a minimum of 20. Weak northeast wind. For tomorrow clear skies are predicted. Temperatures will have a variation between 20 and 36 degrees. Weak north wind. For the following days an increase in cloudiness is expected, although it will be very unlikely that there will be precipitation. Likewise, an increase in temperatures is expected as the days go by.\", \"sampled\": \"Today in Jaén, skies will be cloudless and clear conditions. Temperatures will range between a high of 36 degrees and a low of 20. Northeast wind will be light. Tomorrow, clear skies are forecast. Temperatures will vary between 20 and 36 degrees. North wind will be weak. For the following days, an increase in cloudiness is expected, though precipitation is very unlikely. Temperatures are also expected to rise as the days progress.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Red Dirt (music) The Red Dirt is a variant of country music, that is to say country mixed with rock 'n' roll, or Americana. The Red Dirt comes mainly from the states of Oklahoma and Texas, but it is raging everywhere in the United States, and especially among young people, because this music may be more accessible than traditional country. The leaders of this movement are Stoney Larue, Cross Canadian Ragweed, Jason Boland & the Stragglers and Kevin Fowler.\", \"sampled\": \"Red Dirt music is a variant of country, meaning country mixed with rock 'n' roll or Americana. Red Dirt comes mainly from the states of Oklahoma and Texas, but it is popular throughout the United States, especially among young people, because this music may be more accessible than traditional country. The leaders of this movement are Stoney Larue, Cross Canadian Ragweed, Jason Boland & the Stragglers, and Kevin Fowler.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The stress generated by an indefinite lockdown combined with the fear of facing an illness has a strong impact on our balance. Today, we bring you some tips to maintain good mental health during childbirth. 1) Physical activity is essential to maintaining mental health. Even at home, don't hesitate to put on music and dance or follow sophrology or yoga sessions online. 2) Respect for sleep hours is also essential. Confined, many people spend the night watching television and end up disrupting their biological clock. 3) Eat a healthy diet. A diet rich in sugar or based on frozen meals alters your digestion, affects sleep, and increases a possible state of restlessness. 4) Try to participate in fun activities and do enjoyable things like reading, watching a series, or taking online lessons; right now there are many courses available for free. 5) It is important to avoid negative thoughts. Avoid spending all your days reading, talking about, and listening to information about the epidemic. Take advantage of the opportunity to recharge your batteries with readings and beneficial activities.\", \"sampled\": \"The stress caused by an indefinite lockdown combined with the fear of facing an illness has a strong impact on our balance. Today, we bring you some tips for maintaining good mental health during childbirth. Physical activity is essential for maintaining mental health. Even at home, do not hesitate to put on music and dance or follow online sophrology or yoga sessions. Respecting sleep hours is also essential. Confined, many people spend the night watching television and end up disrupting their biological clock. Eat a healthy diet. diet rich in sugar or based on frozen meals disrupts your digestion, affects sleep, and increases a possible state of restlessness. Try to engage in fun activities and do enjoyable things like reading, watching a series, or taking online lessons — right now many courses are available for free. It is important to avoid negative thoughts. Avoid spending all your days reading, talking, and listening to information about the epidemic. Take the opportunity to recharge your batteries with beneficial readings and activities.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Stratovolcano A stratovolcano or composite volcano is a volcano whose structure consists of the accumulation of lava flows, tephra and/or pyroclastic deposits during the different eruptive stages. Stratovolcanoes take a conical shape because of their pasty lava which flows with difficulty, ash and slag fall preferentially near the volcanic chimney and deposits left by pyroclastic flows starting from the top of the volcano. Their eruptions can be explosive, of Vulcanian, Strombolian, Pelean or Plinian type. The shape of the volcano is generally conical or nested when following the explosion of the volcano, a new cone forms in the crater or caldera. They generally reach great heights, sometimes several thousand meters, and their slopes are steep, sometimes up to 45°.\", \"sampled\": \"A stratovolcano, or composite volcano, is a volcano whose structure consists of accumulated lava flows, tephra, and pyroclastic deposits from successive eruptive stages. Their conical shape results from viscous lava that flows poorly, ash and scoria fallout concentrated near the vent, and deposits left by pyroclastic flows descending from the summit. Eruptions can be explosive, of Vulcanian, Strombolian, Pelean, or Plinian type. The volcano’s form is generally conical or nested when a new cone grows inside the crater or caldera after an explosion. They commonly reach great heights, sometimes several thousand meters, and their slopes are steep, occasionally up to 45°.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"The assize court comprises: the court proper and the jury. The functions of the public prosecution office are exercised there in accordance with the provisions of Articles 34 and 39. However, the Attorney General may delegate any magistrate of the public prosecution office within the jurisdiction of the Court of Appeal to an assize court established in that jurisdiction. The assize court is assisted at the hearing by a clerk. In Paris and in the departments where a Court of Appeal sits, the functions of the registry are performed by the chief clerk or a clerk of the Court of Appeal. In other departments, they are performed by the chief clerk or a clerk of the High Court.\", \"sampled\": \"The court of assizes comprises the court proper and the jury. The public prosecutor’s functions are exercised there under the conditions set out in Articles 34 and 39. however, The prosecutor general may delegate any public prosecutor’s magistrate within the appellate court’s jurisdiction to a court of assizes established in that jurisdiction. The court of assizes is assisted at the hearing by a clerk. In Paris and in departments where an appellate court sits, the clerk’s functions are performed by the chief clerk or appellate court. In the other departments, they are performed by the chief clerk or a clerk of the regional court.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"THE SPIRITUAL PATH OF SELF-LOVE Through this course I want to take you to a place of love, acceptance and transformation. From the deepest part of your being. The most common questions they ask me regarding self-love are: How do I begin to love myself? How can I connect with my body? How does the relationship with myself improve? This course aims to give you all the answers you are looking for. Dare to make this journey with me and experience true self-love.\", \"sampled\": \"The Spiritual Path of Self-Love. Through this course I want to take you to a place of love, acceptance, and transformation. From the deepest part of your being. The most common questions about self-love are: How do I start loving myself? How can I connect with my body? How does my relationship with myself improve? This course aims to give you all the answers you are looking for. Dare to take this journey with me and experience true self-love.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Today in Badajoz, clear skies are forecast. Regarding temperatures, they will vary between a maximum of 29 and a minimum of 16 degrees. In general there will be winds in a breeze regime. For tomorrow there will be few clouds. Temperatures will vary between 16 and 31 degrees. In general there will be calm winds. In subsequent days an increase in cloudiness is expected and precipitation is likely. Furthermore, an increase in maximum temperatures is expected and minimum temperatures will remain unchanged as the days go on.\", \"sampled\": \"Today in Badajoz, clear skies are expected. Temperatures will range between a high of 29 and a low of 16 degrees. Winds will generally be light breezes. Tomorrow will bring few clouds. Temperatures will vary between 16 and 31 degrees. Winds will be mostly calm. In the following days, cloud is expected to increase and rain is likely. Maximum temperatures are expected to rise while minimum temperatures remain unchanged as the days progress.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"GENERAL OBJECTIVE OF THE AREA To build pedagogical proposals that promote the comprehensive training of students, developing their investigative spirit and fostering in them a critical, argumentative, and participatory attitude, which allows them to understand biological, physical, and chemical processes to contribute to the improvement of their environment. COMPETENCIES - Compare cell division systems and argue their importance in the generation of new organisms and tissues. - Understand and explain the functioning of the main elements that make up the respiratory, excretory, skeletal, and muscular systems. - Interpret the structure of an ecosystem. METHODOLOGY - Student - object of study interaction - Feedback on students' prior concepts - Design and posing of the problem question - Application of everyday conceptual analysis tests. - Application of internal knowledge tests. - Encourage research - Group and individual work EVALUATION - Individual written test - Consultations, activities, short exams, workshops, laboratories.\", \"sampled\": \"The general objective of the area is to build pedagogical proposals that foster the integral formation of the student by developing their investigative spirit and encouraging a critical, argumentative, and participatory attitude, enabling them to understand biological, physical, and chemical processes to contribute to the improvement of their environment. Competencies include comparing cell division systems and arguing their importance in generating new organisms and tissues, understanding and explaining the functioning of the main elements that make up the respiratory, excretory, skeletal, and muscular systems, and interpreting the structure of an ecosystem. The methodology involves student–object of study interaction, feedback on students’ prior concepts, design and formulation of the problematizing question, application of everyday conceptual analysis tests, application of internal knowledge tests, promoting research, and group and individual work, while evaluation consists of a written individual test, consultations, activities, short exams, workshops, laboratories, and the formulation and resolution of questions.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Objective To perform an inspection using the Procore application on an Android mobile device. Points to Consider - User permissions required: - “Standard” or “Administrator” level permissions in the Inspections tool at the project level. - This asset or item can be viewed or edited offline as long as it has been previously viewed and cached on your mobile device. Prerequisites Steps - Navigate to the project's Inspections tool using the Procore application on an Android mobile device. - Tap the inspection you want to perform. - While performing your inspection, make notes on the status of each item by tapping the appropriate response (e.g., Pass/Safe, Fail/At Risk, or N/A). Note: Some items may require you to select from a set of multiple choice responses or respond in the form of text, date, or number entry. - Optional: Add comments or attachments to an inspection item. - Tap the inspection item. - Tap the Attachment icon to add a file or tap the text box to add a comment. Note: Any changes you make on this page will be saved automatically. See Also For more information about Procore’s inspection management software and how it can help your business, visit our construction site inspection software product page.\", \"sampled\": \"To perform an inspection using the Procore app on an Android mobile device, you need “Standard” or “Admin” level permissions on the project’s Inspections tool. This asset or item can be viewed or edited in offline mode provided it has been previously viewed and cached on your mobile device. Navigate to the project’s Inspections tool using the Procore app on an Android mobile device and tap the inspection you want to perform. While carrying out your inspection, record the status of each item by tapping the appropriate response (e.g., Pass/Safe, Fail/At Risk, or N/A), noting that some items may require you to select from a set of multiple-choice answers or respond in text, date, or number format. Optionally add comments or attachments to an inspection item by tapping the inspection item, then tapping the Attachment icon to add a file or tapping the text box to add a comment. Any changes you make on this page are saved automatically. For more information about Procore’s inspection management software and how it can help your business, visit our construction site inspection software product page.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"deepseek-v4-pro\", \"original\": \"Memorizing TOEIC Part 1 Vocabulary with Pictures [012] \\\"Gas range (cooktop)\\\" is called a stove in English. You might be thinking, \\\"A stove?\\\" It certainly sounds like it. Nowadays, most heating appliances are called heaters. Apparently, things using traditional firewood or charcoal are sometimes called stoves. Indeed, stoves can also be used for cooking. Also, in British English, it's called a cooker. Furthermore, stove can also be said as kitchen stove or cooking stove. Seems simple yet troublesome. [Example] He’s cooking food at the stove. He is cooking on the range. The cooking stove is in the middle of the room. The cooking range is placed in the center of the room. stove When referring to an American stove, there's often an image of one integrated with an oven.\", \"sampled\": \"In a photo-based TOEIC Part 1 vocabulary series, entry 012 explains that a gas range is called a stove in English. You might hear surprised reactions like “A stove?”. Most modern heating devices are called heaters. The term stove can still refer to old-fashioned wood- or coal-burning units. Indeed, a stove is sometimes used for cooking. In British English, it is called a cooker. Stove can also be called a kitchen stove or cooking stove. It seems simple but is actually tricky. An example sentence is: “He’s cooking food at the stove.” Another is: “The cooking stove is in the middle of the room.” In American English, a stove often brings to mind an integrated unit with an oven.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Staff 🐱Currently being prepared🐱 Store Manager (Takashi Ando) Born in Ichihara City, Chiba Prefecture. Systems engineer. Deputy Store Manager (Erika Ando) Born in Fujisawa City, Kanagawa Prefecture. Mother of two children. Also works at an animal hospital. Sales Director (Kotetsu) Born in July 2009. Folded-ear Scottish Fold from Aomori Prefecture. Has a very gentle and slightly clumsy personality. Loves meeting new people and always stands his tail straight up, purring while rubbing against them. Despite being a cat, he makes foot sounds when walking and often bumps his head. Office Worker (Lily) Born in 2010, from Osaka Prefecture, a Chinchilla Silver. Very timid and clingy. Likes being held in arms and will follow her owner around until she is hand-fed treats. Doesn't like strangers and hides in the back, becoming still like an ornament, disappearing from sight.\", \"sampled\": \"Staff 🐱Now preparing🐱 Manager (Takashi Ando) is from Ichihara City, Chiba Prefecture. He is a system engineer. Assistant Manager (Erika Ando) is from Fujisawa City, Kanagawa Prefecture. She is a mother of two. She also works at a veterinary hospital. Sales Manager (Kotetsu) was born in July 2009. He is a Scottish Fold with folded ears from Aomori Prefecture. His personality is very calm and a little clumsy. He loves meeting people for the first time, always holds his tail straight up and purrs while rubbing against them. Though he is a cat, his footsteps can be heard when he walks and he often bumps his head. Back Office (Lily) was born in 2010. She is a Chinchilla Silver from Osaka Prefecture. She is very timid and affectionate. She likes to use your arm as a pillow and will follow her owner until she is fed kibble by hand. She is not good with people she meets for the first time retreating to the back and becoming still like an ornament to erase her presence.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Hello, we are the doctor team at Visora Dental Clinic, a comprehensive dental clinic in Minoh City. This time, we will talk about gingival recession. Gingival recession refers to the condition where the gums shrink and recede. When the gums recede... ・Your teeth become sensitive ・The base of the tooth erodes and becomes concave ・The roots become exposed, resulting in an unsightly appearance ・It becomes easier to develop cavities, the root surface wears down, and it becomes more prone to breakage These things tend to happen. The causes of gum recession include... ・Periodontal disease ・Abnormal occlusion (teeth grinding, poor alignment, etc.) ・Aging ・Brushing too hard These are some examples. Unfortunately, once the gums have receded, they cannot be restored to their original state. Most treatments involve symptomatic therapy such as applying desensitizing medication to sensitive teeth and filling eroded areas with white resin, but for advanced gingival recession, CTG (Connective Tissue Graft, *not covered by health insurance) can restore the original condition by cutting and transplanting gum tissue from another area. Proper brushing, tartar removal, orthodontic treatment to improve alignment, and checking your bite can also prevent gingival recession, so please consult us if you are concerned.\", \"sampled\": \"Hello, this is the doctor team from Visola Dental Clinic, a comprehensive dental practice in Minoh City. Today, we will discuss gingival recession. Gingival recession is a condition where the gingiva (gums) recede or pull away. When the gums recede, it can lead to tooth sensitivity, notches forming where the tooth root has been worn away, exposed roots that affect your appearance and an increased risk of cavities as the root surface wears down and becomes more prone to fracture. The causes of receding gums include periodontal disease, malocclusion (such as teeth grinding or poor alignment), aging, and brushing with too much pressure. Unfortunately, once the gums have receded, they do not grow back. While most cases are managed with symptomatic treatments like applying desensitizing medication for sensitivity or filling worn areas with white resin, advanced gingival recession can be restored to its original state through a connective tissue graft (CTG, not covered by health insurance), which involves transplanting tissue from another part of the mouth. You can also prevent gingival recession through proper brushing, tartar removal, improving tooth alignment, and bite checks. So please consult with us if you have any concerns.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"I use this when pacing during practice and competitions (as a personal pace maker). It doesn't get in the way while wearing it, and it's useful for carrying things. During practice, I carry drinks, keys, money (1000 yen bills in a plastic bag), my cell phone, a thin jacket (when running long distances), and energy gels. When pacing, I run carrying plenty of gels and drinks for support runners. Things like arm covers or neck warmers won't fall off even if you hook them onto the belt when it gets hot.\", \"sampled\": \"I use it for practice and when acting as a personal pacemaker in races. It is not cumbersome to wear and it is very useful because it holds things. During practice, I carry items like a drink, keys, money (a 1000-yen bill in a plastic bag), a mobile phone, a thin jacket for long runs, and energy gels. When pacing, I run with extra gels and a drink for the support runner. When it gets hot, items like arm covers or a neck warmer will not fall off even if you run with them hooked onto the belt.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Do they seem a little strange to you? A set of strange flowers sprouts from a bicycle rack, post office mailboxes turn into monsters, and monkeys pose on parking meters. Damon is a graphic artist who lives and works in the Bay Area of San Francisco, California. He was invited to lead a project to create 20 false shadows in the vicinity of Redwood City, with the purpose of splashing a bit of creativity onto the region. \\\"Shadows bring everyday objects to life in an animated way so people can have a little fun with daily life,\\\" said the artist. First, Damon makes the shadows using the stencil technique and then paints them gray, to blend them into the urban environment. Will Damon manage to make the city center a more creative environment? Take a look at the result of the project. This work was promoted by the Redwood City Improvement Association in collaboration with the Community Services Department and other municipal bodies. The association donated US$30,000 to the city and part of this money was invested in the shadow work. You can learn more about Damon's work on his Instagram account.\", \"sampled\": \"Do they seem a little strange to you? A set of strange flowers sprouts from a bike rack, postal service mailboxes become monsters and monkeys pose on parking meters. Damon is a graphic artist who lives and works in the San Francisco Bay Area in California. He was invited to lead a project to create 20 fake shadows around Redwood City to sprinkle the region with a little creativity. \\\"The shadows bring mundane objects to life in an animated way so people can have a little fun with everyday life,\\\" the artist stated. First, Damon makes the shadows with a stencil technique and then paints them gray to blend them into the urban environment. Will Damon succeed in making the city center a more creative environment? Take a look at the project's result. This work was promoted by the Redwood City Improvement Association in collaboration with the Department of Community Services and other municipal agencies. The association donated US$30,000 to the city and part of this money was invested in the shadow work. You can see more of Damon's work on his Instagram account.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"During a recent interview for a Miami radio station, he mentioned some frustration regarding the Hip Hop scene. According to him, there would be a great decrease in authenticity within the rap game. He no longer recognizes himself in it after his 12 years of career. Despite all his recent collaborations and after releasing a mixtape, the Atlanta rapper seems genuinely jaded. But let the fans rest assured, his eighth album is still scheduled for September 4th, 2013 – could this be the last one???\", \"sampled\": \"During a recent interview for a Miami radio station, he mentioned a certain frustration with the Hip Hop scene. There has been a great decrease in authenticity in the rap game. He no longer recognizes himself in it after his 12-year career. Despite all his recent collaborations and after releasing a mixtape, the Atlanta rapper seems genuinely jaded. But fans can rest assured that his eighth album is still scheduled for September 4, 2013; could it be his last?\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"How about a party featuring “Galette des Rois,” a traditional confection indispensable for New Year's in France? Originating from the Epiphany celebration at the beginning of January, Galette des Rois is a pastry made by wrapping almond dough in pie crust. In France, galettes are piled high in bakeries and confectionery shops throughout January, appearing whenever family and friends gather. The person who finds the only fève (a small porcelain figurine) hidden inside becomes the king or queen of the day and receives blessings from everyone. Despite its simplicity, Galette des Rois showcases the skill and individuality of artisans, making it a subject in the M.O.F. (Meilleurs Ouvriers de France – Best Craftsman of France) certification exam. The Institut will host this galette party together with Club de la Galette des Rois, which conveys the charm and culture of traditional confections. Popular chefs from Kansai and Tokyo will gather specifically for this day, and the sight of their specially made galettes lined up is spectacular! A wonderful gift awaits the lucky person who finds the fève hidden within the oversized galette presented to the Consul General. Celebrate the New Year with delicious galette!\", \"sampled\": \"How about a party for \\\"Galette des Rois,\\\" the traditional confection essential to the French New Year? Originating from the Epiphany in early January, the Galette des Rois is a pastry with almond cream filling. Throughout January in France, bakeries and pastry shops pile galettes high, and they appear at every gathering of family and friends. The person who finds the single \\\"fève\\\" (a small ceramic figurine) inside becomes the king for the day and is celebrated by everyone. Though simple, the pastry clearly shows a craftsman's skill and individuality, so the Galette des Rois is also a subject on the M.O.F. (France's Best Craftsman) qualifying examination. The Institut is holding this galette party with the Club de la Galette des Rois, which conveys the charm and culture of this traditional sweet. It is a spectacular sight to see the special galettes from the club's popular chefs gathered from Kansai and Tokyo for this day! A wonderful gift also awaits the person who finds the fève in the extra-large galette presented to the Consul General. Celebrate the New Year with a delicious galette! Organized by: Institut Français Kansai, Club de la Galette des Rois.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"Help |Code of Canon Law| IntraText - Reading of the Text TITLE II THE VARIOUS LEVELS AND TYPES OF COURTS (Cann. 1417 – 1445) Can. 1417 - § 1. Because of the primacy of the Roman Pontiff, any faithful person can freely refer to the judgment of the Holy See, or introduce before it any contentious or penal case, at any degree of jurisdiction and at any time during the trial. § 2. However, except in the case of appeal, recourse to the Apostolic See does not suspend the exercise of jurisdiction of the judge who has already begun to hear the case; therefore this judge may continue the trial until the final sentence, unless the Apostolic See has notified him that he has taken the case before himself. Can. 1418 - Any court has the right to seek the assistance of another court to investigate a case or serve acts.\", \"sampled\": \"Help |Code of Canon Law| IntraText - Text Reading TITLE II THE VARIOUS DEGREES AND KINDS OF TRIBUNALS (Cann. 1417 – 1445). Can. 1417 - § 1. By reason of the primacy of the Roman Pontiff, any member of the faithful is free to bring to the Holy See for judgment or to introduce before it any contentious or penal case at any grade of a trial and at any stage of litigation. § 2. Recourse to the Apostolic See does not suspend the exercise of jurisdiction by a judge who has already begun to adjudicate a case except in the case of an appeal. For this reason, the judge can prosecute the trial even to a definitive sentence unless the Apostolic See has informed the judge that it has called the case to itself. Can. 1418 - Every tribunal has the right to call upon the assistance of another tribunal to instruct a case or to communicate acts.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"- palux Viewing videos located on a remote server Hello, I have videos on a remote server and I would like to know what is the simplest method for me to be able to view them on my computer from home. Preferably without having to download them onto my computer, although unless you think that is the best thing to do.\", \"sampled\": \"Hello, I have videos on a remote server and I would like to know what is the simplest method for me to be able to view them on my computer from home. I would prefer not to have to download them to my computer unless you think that is the best thing to do. Thanks, Palux.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"The News Globe (Miami Dade) MIAMI. –Activity in the tropics could have a major impact on South Florida in the coming days with an area of changes in the Caribbean continuing to approach the Gulf region. Meteorologists are monitoring a tropical storm northwest of Trinidad and Tobago, which will be in a favorable environment to strengthen as it moves through the southern Caribbean. Invest-98L has a 90 percent chance of becoming a tropical depression and will likely be named Hermine as the next named system. This storm may become a hurricane as it heads across the western Caribbean, where very warm sea surface temperatures and lower shear are recorded. There is potential for significant impacts anywhere from Central America to Jamaica and Cuba, depending on the exact track and intensity. National Hurricane Center meteorologists said it will likely become a tropical depression over the next few days as it moves west-northwest through the southern Windward Islands. Interests along the Gulf Coast of the United States should also monitor the progress of this storm, as it could enter the Gulf of Mexico next week. Models are very consistent and show that, over the next five days, the development of this storm will bring it through the Caribbean over southwestern Cuba. Source Telemundo/Local 10.\", \"sampled\": \"Activity in the tropics could have a major impact on South Florida in the coming days with an area of changes in the Caribbean continuing to approach the Gulf area. Meteorologists are monitoring a tropical storm northwest of Trinidad and Tobago which will be in a favorable environment to strengthen as it moves through the southern Caribbean. Invest-98L has a 90 percent chance of becoming a tropical depression and will likely be named Hermine as the next named system. This storm may become a hurricane as it heads through the western Caribbean, where very warm sea surface temperatures and lower wind shear are recorded. There is a possibility of significant impacts anywhere from Central America to Jamaica and Cuba, depending on the exact track and intensity. Meteorologists at the National Hurricane Center said it will likely become a tropical depression in the coming days as it moves west-northwest through the southern Windward Islands. Interests along the United States Gulf Coast will also need to monitor the progress of this storm, as it could enter the Gulf of Mexico next week. The models are very consistent and show that, over the next five days, this storm's development will bring it across the Caribbean over the southwestern coast of Cuba. Source Telemundo/Local 10. Photo courtesy.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gemini-2.5-pro\", \"original\": \"2019-04-29 While walking around Sakuracho in Siberia, I discovered a bakery with \\\"Siberia\\\" written on it. It's bread with a castella-like dough and anko bean paste inside. I don't know what it tastes like because I don't like castella or anko, but my father and mother used to eat it when they were young. Was it my grandfather? A vague memory. I felt somehow happy and bought it. The shop staff was kind and let me take a picture. The children who didn't know Siberia looked confused. This is the real thing.\", \"sampled\": \"While walking through Sakuragicho on April 29, 2019, I discovered a bakery called \\\"Siberia.\\\" It is a bread with yokan sandwiched between castella-like sponge cake. I do not like castella or yokan, so I do not know the taste but I have a memory of my father and mother eating it long ago. Or was it my grandfather? It is a vague memory. I somehow felt happy and ended up buying it. The shopkeeper was a nice person and let me take a picture. The children who did not know Siberia looked confused. This is the real thing.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Participation in the contest is open to anyone who is at least 13 years old and resides in any of the 50 United States, Puerto Rico, Canada, or Mexico, among us. Employees of the sponsors presenting the contest, or their affiliates or agencies, will not be eligible to participate. Author Posts You must be registered to reply to this discussion.\", \"sampled\": \"Participation in the contest is open to anyone who is at least 13 years old and resides in any of the 50 United States, Puerto Rico, Canada, or Mexico. Employees of the sponsoring companies that present the contest, as well as their affiliates or agencies, are not eligible to participate. Author Publications You must be registered to respond to this debate.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"What happens to the lungs when smoking marijuana (Marijuana) compared to tobacco? A man violently assaults someone who has been injured in an accident, adding insult to injury. I don't understand at all what it's for, but it seems like a drawing machine exclusively for penises has been developed. An incident that occurred on the Stockholm subway. A drunken man accidentally falls onto the tracks. Immediately, a man on the platform looks down worriedly and descends onto the tracks to approach the man... For reasons unknown, a father lost his temper with a child who wouldn't eat with chopsticks and abused him by kicking him. The car is dragged by a train and then disappears offscreen. Before and after photos of people in South Korea, where plastic surgery is popular. A little girl slightly dirtied the inside of the car when trying to get in. This is something that often happens with children that age. However, the girl's father flew into a rage and beat her, pinned her down, and straddled her for no more than that reason. Diana Hanson, 24, an intern working at \\\"Project Survival Cat Haven,\\\" an animal shelter in Dunnlap, California, was attacked and killed by a lion. Footage of a public bus driver in Nebraska, USA, suddenly attacking passengers.\", \"sampled\": \"What happens to the lungs when you smoke marijuana compared to tobacco? A man who, after injuring the other party in an accident, proceeds to violently attack them. The purpose of this behavior remains entirely unclear, but it seems that a graffiti machine exclusively for penises has been developed. An incident occurred in the subway in Sweden where a drunken man accidentally fell onto the tracks. Another man on the platform immediately peeks worriedly and approaches the man on the tracks. For reasons not well understood, a father who gets angry at his child for not eating with chopsticks kicks and abuses the child. A car being dragged by a train fades out of the screen. Before and after photos of South Koreans who have undergone plastic surgery. A girl who accidentally dirties the inside of a car while trying to get in. It's a common occurrence for kids her age. However, the girl's father, for that sole reason, goes berserk, hitting, restraining, straddling, and getting angry at the girl. Diana Hanson (24), who was working as an intern at the animal sanctuary \\\"Project Survival's Cat Haven\\\" in Dunlap, California, was attacked and killed by a lion. A video shows a public bus driver in Nebraska suddenly attacking passengers.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"AI has long been the flagship feature of Google Photos. Now, Google announced a new feature, Suggested Actions, which will leverage that technology and offer smart action suggestions that the user can take for their photos. Photos will also get some new coloring tools, allowing you to desaturate backgrounds and colorize old black and white images. Sundar Pichai, CEO of Google, showed an example of a set of wedding photos. Using AI to recognize people in those images, Google Photos will now suggest sending those photos to each person with a single tap. If the photo is underexposed, Pichai explained, you will receive a suggestion to automatically correct the brightness. If a document is photographed, however, Google Photos can now convert it to PDF, making it easier to use and share. On the other hand, Google Photos will soon be able to perform some more complex edits on images, such as making the background of a black and white photo while increasing the saturation of the person portrayed in the foreground. Photos will also be able to colorize old photographs using its powerful image recognition engine. All of these features are expected to reach users in the second half of 2018.\", \"sampled\": \"AI has long been the main feature of Google Photos. Now, Google has announced a new feature, Suggested Actions, that will leverage that technology and offer intelligent action suggestions that the user can take for their photos. Photos will also get some new coloring tools, allowing desaturation of backgrounds and coloring old black-and-white images. Google's CEO Sundar Pichai showcased an example of a set of wedding photos. Using AI to recognize people in those images, Google Photos will now suggest sending them to each person with a single tap. Pichai say that, If photo is underexposed, you will receive a suggestion to automatically correct the brightness.on the other hand, If photo of document is taken, Google Photos can now convert it to PDF, making it easier to use and share. Additionally, Google Photos will soon be able to perform some more complex edits on images, such as turning the background of a photo black and white while increasing the saturation of the person in the foreground. Photos will also be able to colorize old photographs using its powerful image recognition engine. All of these features are expected to roll out to users in the second half of 2018. Via: TheVerge.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"NEW YORK – Stock prices were falling sharply Thursday morning at the start of trading on the New York Stock Exchange following Russia's attack on Ukraine. The S&P 500 index was down 2%, continuing its decline since the beginning of the year. It has currently accumulated a loss of 14% compared to the high it reached in early January. European markets were falling even more. The German DAX index plummeted 5%. Oil prices were rising markedly, to over $100 per barrel. This could inflate prices at gas stations and supermarkets. Bond yields were falling as investors sought more stable assets.\", \"sampled\": \"NEW YORK — Stock prices were sharply lower Thursday morning at the opening of trading on the New York Stock Exchange after Russia attacked Ukraine. The S&P 500 index was down 2%, extending its decline since the beginning of the year, with a 14% loss compared to its early January peak. European markets were falling even further, with Germany's DAX index plummeting 5%. Oil prices were surging, exceeding $100 per barrel, which could lead to inflation at gas stations and supermarkets. Bond yields were decreasing as investors sought more stable assets.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"What's NEW Leonardo The Leonardo Cressi computer is a multifunctional instrument for recreational diving, capable of providing all the necessary information regarding depth, dive times, possible decompression requirements, ascent speed and surface intervals between one dive and the next. The absorption and elimination of nitrogen are constantly elaborated by a sophisticated software adapting them to the amount of inert gas contained in the different mixtures that can be used. This information is...\", \"sampled\": \"The NEW Leonardo dive computer by Cressi is a multifunctional tool for sports diving, providing all necessary information about depth, dive times, potential decompression needs, ascent rate, and surface intervals between dives. The absorption and elimination of nitrogen are continuously processed by sophisticated software, adjusting to the amount of inert gas in the various mixtures that can be used.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"After The Flash, Constantine! Here is the first official image of the series, released by the NBC channel, less than 24 hours after this first glimpse. Matt Ryan is already unanimously praised by fans for his physical resemblance to the Vertigo/DC Comics hero. And the presence of gas, vapors, and other swirls of smoke is just the icing on the cake! What do you think of this first image? John Constantine is an enigmatic and irreverent con man who has become a detective of the supernatural against his will. He is forced to assume his role, which is to defend us against the dark forces from beyond. When the daughter of a deceased friend is targeted by demons, Constantine rushes to her rescue to save her. The pilot episode of the Constantine series is directed by Neil Marshall for the NBC channel, starring Matt Ryan (John Constantine), Lucy Griffiths (Liv), Harold Perrineau (Manny) and Charles Halford (Chas).\", \"sampled\": \"After The Flash, Constantine! Here is the first official image of the series, released by NBC less than 24 hours after this initial glimpse. Matt Ryan is already unanimously praised by fans for his physical resemblance to the Vertigo/DC Comics hero. And the presence of gas, vapors, and other swirls of smoke is just the icing on the cake! What do you think of this first image? John Constantine is a mysterious and irreverent con artist turned supernatural detective against his will. He is forced to embrace his role, that of defending us against the dark forces from beyond. When a deceased friend's daughter is targeted by demons, Constantine rushes to her rescue to save her. The pilot of the Constantine series is directed by Neil Marshall for NBC, starring Matt Ryan (John Constantine), Lucy Griffiths (Liv), Harold Perrineau (Manny), and Charles Halford (Chas).\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"Complete solution: The city in the starry sky Returning to the teleportation portal, descend to the gate at the very bottom of the tower. Return to the platform with the two small monuments just at the entrance to this level. Referring to the last treasure map, jump on the transparent platforms to reach the chest at the top. Open it to find a card with a QR code. If you haven't yet retrieved the anti-cube from the owl statue in the water castle, enter: RT, RT, RT, LT, RT, RT, RT, LT to get it (this is the same anti-cube; if you retrieve it using this method, you will no longer be able to find it using the other one). Go to the island of the giant tree. Climb onto the teleportation portal then enter through the door on the tree trunk. Use the roots to reach the top then enter through the door. Reach the small cottage and enter inside. Upon exiting, you will be in the cemetery.\", \"sampled\": \"Complete solution: Starry sky city Back at the teleportation gate, descend towards the door at the bottom of the tower. Return to the platform with the two small monuments just at the entrance of this level. Referring to the last treasure map, jump on the transparent platforms to reach the chest at the top. Open it to find a card with a QR code. If you haven't retrieved the anti-cube from the owl statue in the water tower yet, enter the following code: RT, RT, RT, LT, RT, RT, RT, LT to obtain it (it is the same anti-cube, if you retrieve it using this method, you won't be able to find it with the other one). Head up to the island of the giant tree. Climb the teleportation gate and enter through the door on the tree trunk. Use roots to reach the top and then enter through the door. Reach the small house and enter it. When you exit, you will be in the cemetery.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"There are currently no items in your cart. Founded in 1986, ＜Achille＞ is a French sock brand. While socks at the time of its founding were mostly plain designs, the brand became known for incorporating unique motifs into its socks, creating a fresh breeze and surprise in the sock industry. Currently, it has become part of “KINDY,” France's leading sock manufacturer, and is sold in famous department stores such as Galeries Lafayette.\", \"sampled\": \"Currently, there are no items in your cart. Founded in 1986, the French sock brand Achile introduced unique motifs into their sock designs, standing out in the industry dominated by plain styles and bringing a fresh perspective. Today, under the umbrella of the top French sock manufacturer \\\"KINDY,\\\" the brand is sold in renowned department stores like Galeries Lafayette. Price: ¥1,800.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"It extends until August 7th, and this year's motto is \\\"Protecting breastfeeding, a shared responsibility.\\\" The objective is to think of breastfeeding as a cultural and collective act, in which all of society must be involved to guarantee the rights of boys and girls to be breastfed. Mother's milk is the best and only food designed exclusively for a baby during the first 6 months of life and is its main source of nutrition until the first year of life. UNICEF estimates that exclusive breastfeeding up to six months of age can annually prevent the death of 1.3 million children under five years of age. In Argentina, the prevalence of breastfeeding at birth is more than 95%. However, at 6 months, exclusive breastfeeding decreases sharply and stands around 40%.\", \"sampled\": \"The World Breastfeeding Week extends until August 7th, with this year's theme being “Protect breastfeeding, a shared responsibility.” The aim is to view breastfeeding as a cultural and collective practice, involving society to ensure children's right to be breastfed. Breast milk is the best and the only food specifically designed for a baby in the first 6 months of life, remaining their primary source of nutrition up to the first year. UNICEF estimates that exclusive breastfeeding for six months could annually prevent the deaths of 1.3 million children under five years old. In Argentina, breastfeeding prevalence at birth is over 95%. However, by 6 months, exclusive breastfeeding sharply declines to around 40%.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-3.5-turbo\", \"original\": \"“Sudoku LXM”: search Back Provide the value of about thirty cells called “revealed” by typing the numbers from 1 to 9 or the hyphen. Note that the distribution of revealed cells should normally respect symmetry around the center of the grid. Test your grid using the \\\"solution\\\" button. You will obtain four types of responses (at the bottom of the page): You can also enter grids that you find in the press or on the Internet (and not yet recorded in our database). You can thus test the search program. In the event that the program has found the solution, don't forget to save the grid! Good luck in your search!\", \"sampled\": \"\\\"Sudoku LXM\\\": search Return Give the value of about thirty revealed squares by typing the numbers from 1 to 9 or a dash. Please note that the distribution of revealed squares should ideally respect symmetry concerning the grid's center. Test your grid using the \\\"solution\\\" button. You will receive four types of responses (at the bottom of the page): You can also enter grids you find in the press or on the Internet (not yet saved in our database). This way, you can test the search program. If the program finds the solution, remember to save the grid! Good luck with your search!\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Vacation rentals in Waddington Search and book a unique accommodation on Airbnb Top-rated vacation rentals in Waddington Guests agree: these stays have excellent ratings for their location and cleanliness, among other things. Entire place: cabin · Waddington Waddington Getaway. This rustic cabin sits on Big Suckerbrook in the town of Waddington, just miles from where the Bassmasters stay! There is access to the dock, kayaks and canoe for use and swimming! This is the perfect getaway for fishermen, families or friends! With the cabin sitting a mile off the highway, it's a very quiet stay. $200 per night SUPERHOST Entire place: home · Waddington 1 bedroom house in Village of Waddington Enjoy easy access to everything from this centrally located lodging in the village of Waddington. This home is within walking distance of the beautiful St. Lawrence River and downtown restaurants and bars. There is plenty of parking space. $100 per night Entire place: cottage · Waddington A St. Lawrence Getaway Border Life Retreat is located on Main Street in Waddington, New York. It is a 30 second walk from the beautiful St. Lawrence River. No matter what season you come to visit us, you will be able to enjoy all the views we have to offer.\", \"sampled\": \"Vacation rentals in Waddington are available for you to search and book a unique accommodation on Airbnb. The highest-rated vacation rentals in Waddington are agreed upon by guests: these stays receive excellent ratings for their location, cleanliness, and other aspects. This rustic cabin is located on Big Suckerbrook in the village of Waddington, a few kilometers from where the Bassmasters are hosted! There's dock access, kayaks, and a canoe for use, and swimming! This is the perfect getaway for fishermen, families, or friends! With the cabin sitting a mile from the highway, it's a very quiet stay. $200 per night. SUPERHOST Entire home: Waddington 1-bedroom house in Village of Waddington. Enjoy easy access to everything from this centrally located accommodation in the village of Waddington. This house is a short walk from the beautiful St. Lawrence River and downtown restaurants and bars. There's plenty of parking space. $100 per night. Entire home: A getaway by the St. Lawrence. Border Life Retreat is located on Main Street in Waddington, New York. It's a 30 second walk from the beautiful St. Lawrence River. No matter what season you come to visit us, you will be able to enjoy all the views we have to offer. $130 per night. SUPERHOST.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"But what stubbornness yours is! I would love to understand what lies behind the stubbornness... At some point I saw stubbornness as a strength, especially in those people who don't stop until they achieve what they set out to do! but we all here know that sometimes stubbornness doesn't always respond to that ideal. Let's start from the definition that appears in the Oxford dictionary. Stubborn: \\\"who remains firm or immovable in their attitude, even when given reasons against them or tried to be convinced to do something else\\\" Understanding that nothing in life is good or bad! it simply depends on how we use it! It is worth stopping for a moment and thinking, especially if we are the stubborn ones. To what extent can the stubbornness of a single person threaten the tranquility of an entire team? The invitation is then to reflect if that insistence on achieving something has a long-term impact. Does it affect the atmosphere of the community? Is it really worth altering everyone's nerves for what we are going to achieve? Is it the ego that is looking for a way to make its way regardless of the consequences?\", \"sampled\": \"What stubbornness you have! I would love to understand what lies behind stubbornness. At some point, I saw stubbornness as a strength, especially in those people who do not stop until they achieve what they set out to do! But we all here know that at times, stubbornness does not always respond to that ideal. Let's start with the definition that appears in the Oxford dictionary. \\\"Someone who remains firm or immovable in their attitude, even when given reasons against it or when attempted to be convinced to do something else.'' Understanding that nothing in life is good or bad! It simply depends on how we use it! It is worth stopping for a bit and thinking, especially if we are the stubborn ones. To what extent can the stubbornness of a single person threaten the tranquility of an entire team? The invitation is then to reflect on whether this determination to achieve something has a long-term impact. Does it affect the community's environment? Is it really worth upsetting everyone's nerves for what we are going to achieve? Is it the ego that is looking for a way to make its way, regardless of the consequences?\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Methane emissions due to agriculture (% of total) Attributable agricultural methane emissions are emissions attributable to animals, animal waste, rice production, burning of agricultural waste (on-site and not intended to produce energy), and savanna burning. The Catalog provides a list of datasets made available by the World Bank, in the form of databases, preformatted tables, reports and other types of resources.\", \"sampled\": \"Methane emissions due to agriculture (% of total) refer to emissions attributable to animals, animal waste, rice production, burning of agricultural waste (on-site and not intended for energy production), and savannah burning. The Catalog provides a list of datasets made available by the World Bank in the form of databases preformatted tables, reports, and other types of resources.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The two companies had signed an agreement in 2015 regarding the use of telecommunications patents, with a duration of seven years, which expired after negotiations to renew it failed. The Swedish company sued Apple in October in the United States, alleging that the American firm was attempting to reduce royalties incorrectly; Apple responded two months later with another request in that country for using illegal negotiation methods. Ericsson points out that compensation for the patents is necessary, ensuring new investments in innovation that benefit customers and consumers. Furthermore, it appealed to ongoing legal proceedings to refrain from making further comments on the litigation.\", \"sampled\": \"The two companies had signed an agreement back in 2015 concerning the use of telecommunications patents, which lasted for seven years and expired after the negotiations to renew it failed. In October, the Swedish company filed a lawsuit against Apple in the United States claiming that the American firm was attempting to incorrectly reduce royalties. Apple responded two months later with another claim in the same country accusing them of using illegal negotiation methods. Ericsson insists that compensation for the patents is necessary to ensure new investments in innovation that will benefit customers and consumers. Also, they referred to ongoing legal proceedings as the reason for not making any further comments about the dispute.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Ryoichi Ikeda (a producer of dam-type music) and Panasonic are well known here in Paris as a combination of hard experimental music. The old workshop \\\"Le garage,\\\" reborn as a concert hall, has very ordinary lighting and sound, but it's the only place to enjoy this kind of music. The number of audience members is always considerable, yet they are extremely quiet and never lose control. This might be said to be the correct attitude for enjoying this type of sound. Ryoichi Ikeda began his live performance with minimal and abstract sounds; there were no flashy lights or actions, he simply elegantly adjusted the machines behind the digital recorder. It could become a boring show, but if you concentrate solely on the sound, pure electronic noise will stimulate your ears and soul. The Panasonic guys don't say a word. I have interviewed them before and had a terrible experience. They stare at you intently and answer any question with only “YES” or “NO.” In the end, they probably speak through their music. Their shows are almost violently hardcore. When the video begins to display images synchronized with their sound, the audience eventually starts dancing madly.\", \"sampled\": \"Ryoji Ikeda, a producer of the Dam Type and Panasonic are known in Paris for their intense experimental music. The ancient workshop, \\\"Le Garage\\\", which has been transformed into a concert hall, may have ordinary lighting and acoustics but it is the only place where one can enjoy this kind of music. The audience always numbers significantly, yet it remains incredibly quiet with no one stepping out of line. This might be considered the right attitude for enjoying this type of sound. Ryoji Ikeda begins the live show with a minimal and abstract sound with no video, flashy lights, or actions, only elegantly adjusting the machines behind the digital recorder. This could potentially become a boring show, but if one focuses intently on the sound, the pure electronic noise stimulates the ear and soul. The Panasonic guys never utter a word. I have interviewed them before and had a truly awful experience. They stare straight at you and only answer any question with a \\\"YES\\\" or \\\"NO\\\". In the end, they probably express themselves through their music. Their show can almost be described as violent, almost hardcore. When the video starts projecting images in sync with their sound, the audience ultimately started dancing wildly. Ryoji Ikeda & Panasonic Date: June 27, 1998 Venue: Le Garage Address: 3 impasse Mont-Louis, 75011 Paris.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"In \\\"Ebony Villains,\\\" a new story begins for adventurers who have become “warriors of darkness,” along with added unknown new areas and numerous quests. Here, we will introduce the stage for a new adventure where characters holding the key to the story await, new adversaries to face from now on, and countless monsters lie in wait. The king of the Pixie tribe, literally a fairy king who governs all the fairies gathered in Il'Meg. Even eccentric fairies prostrate themselves before Titania. Among the human threat called “Sin Eaters” born from the “Flood of Light,” they are positioned particularly high up. Much about them, including their purpose, remains shrouded in mystery.\", \"sampled\": \"\\\"In The Villains of Pitch Black,\\\" a new tale unfolds about adventurers who have become \\\"warriors of darkness,\\\" featuring unknown new areas and numerous additional quests. Here, let's introduce the stage for this new adventure where key characters who hold the story new adversaries they will likely confront, and a multitude of monsters await. The literal fairy king, who is the king of the Pixie tribe and governs all the numerous fairies that gather in Il Mheg, even the fairies full of misfits bow down in front of Titania. Among the threats to humanity caused by the \\\"Flood of Light,\\\" there are beings positioned particularly high, known as \\\"Sin Eaters.\\\" Their purpose with many other aspects remains shrouded in mystery.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Nokia Connectivity Cable Driver for Nokia Asha/S40 phones: Connect your PC to the internet via your mobile device - With the Nokia Connectivity Cable driver, you can connect your mobile phone to your PC using a compatible USB cable and use the phone as a modem. - Since 2013, new Nokia Asha/S40 phones no longer support Nokia Suite or PC suite. Instead, they use the Nokia Connectivity Cable driver. - Using services or downloading content may involve transferring a large amount of data, which may result in data traffic charges if billed by your service provider.\", \"sampled\": \"The Nokia connectivity cable driver for Nokia Asha/S40 phones allows you to connect your PC to the internet through your mobile device. With the Nokia connectivity cable driver you can connect your mobile phone to your PC using a compatible USB cable and use the phone as a modem. Since 2013, new Nokia Asha/S40 phones do not support Nokia Suite or PC suite instead, they utilize the Nokia connectivity cable driver. The use of services or downloading content may involve the transfer of a large volume of data which could result in data traffic charges if these are billed by your service provider.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"The world of amateur sports is trembling. Starting with women's wrestling, followed by American football, boxing, and women's gymnastics, a rebellion against those in power. More will likely emerge. We are being shown the enormity of holding power. Your email address will not be published. Fields marked with * are required. Comment Name * Email Address * Website Notify me of new comments via email Receive new posts via email This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. Learn how your comment data is processed.\", \"sampled\": \"The world of amateur sports is shaking. From women's wrestling it has spread to American football, boxing, and women's gymnastics, a rebellion against the powerful. It's likely to continue. We are witnessing the awe of holding power. Your email address will not be published. Fields marked with an asterisk (*) are required. Comment, Name *, Email Address *, Website. Receive new comments and posts by email. This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. For details on how comment data is processed, please see here.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Wind energy produces economic, energetic, and environmental benefits: it serves as a domestic fuel source, operates without burning coal and fossil fuels, and provides a constant source of electricity. Wind energy primarily comes from turbines, which operate on land and offshore. Location is another benefit of wind energy, involving turbines installed on private and public lands to provide electricity to homes, commercial offices, and communities. Wind energy is praised by many for its environmental benefits. Wind is classified as a form of solar energy, requiring only air to function. Wind systems do not release pollutants into waterways or the atmosphere, unlike fossil fuels. The independent operation from fossil fuels makes wind a reusable and stable energy source. It relies exclusively on air, an easily available and renewable energy. Wind energy does not require drilling and mining for production, reducing the possibility of spills and costly cleanups. In addition to environmental benefits, wind energy produces economic benefits and even promotes national security. Wind energy offers consumers competitive prices for electricity. It forms a domestic energy source, which in turn reduces dependence on foreign energy and fuel supplies. Wind turbines produce substantial amounts of energy, resulting in greater efficiency than coal plants. According to Energy Informative, the largest wind systems generate enough electricity to power more than 600 homes.\", \"sampled\": \"Wind energy produces economic, energetic, and environmental benefits: it serves as a domestic fuel source, operates without burning coal and fossil fuels, and provides a constant source of electricity. Wind energy primarily comes from turbines operating on land and offshore. The location is another benefit of wind energy which involves turbines installed on private and public land to provide electricity to residences, commercial offices, and communities. Wind energy is praised by many for its environmental benefits. Wind is classified as a form of solar energy, requiring only air to operate. Wind systems do not release pollutants into waterways or the atmosphere, unlike fossil fuels. Independent operation from fossil fuels makes wind a reusable and stable energy source. It relies solely on air, an energy that is readily available and renewable. Wind energy does not require drilling and mining for production reducing the possibility of spills and costly cleanups. In addition to environmental benefits, wind energy produces economic benefits and even promotes national security. Wind energy offers consumers competitive prices for electricity. It forms a domestic energy source which in turn reduces dependence on foreign energy and fuel supplies. Wind turbines produce substantial amounts of energy, proving more efficient than coal plants. According to Energy Informative, larger wind systems generate enough electricity to power over 600 residences.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\", \"original\": \"Born in -65 BC in a rural small town in Southern Italy, Horace received a good education in Rome then in Athens. Enlisted as a military tribune in the ranks of Brutus, he deserted the battlefield of Philippi. Pardoned but deprived of his paternal inheritance, Horace bought upon his return to Rome a position as a clerk in finance, a modest function but which left him enough freedom to write. He befriended Virgil, who introduced him to the circle of Maecenas' proteges. The minister of Augustus soon offered the young Horace a villa at Tibur, in Roman Sabina. The poet there wrote, until his death, at fifty-seven years old, the majority of his work: Odes, Epistles and The Art of Poetry. It is essentially in the Odes and Epodes, which consecrated his glory, that the poet exalts the balance between the Epicurean awareness of daily happiness and Stoic resignation before the passage of time: \\\"Carpe diem\\\"!\", \"sampled\": \"Born in -65 BC in a rural village in southern Italy, Horace received a good education in Rome and then in Athens. He enlisted as a military tribune in Brutus' ranks but he deserted the battlefield of Philippi. Although pardoned, he was stripped of his paternal inheritance and upon his return to Rome, Horace bought a humble position as a scribe in finance, which still allowed him sufficient freedom to write. He became friends with Virgil, who introduced him to the circle of Maecenas' protégés. Augustus' minister soon offered the young Horace a villa in Tibur, in Roman Sabine. It was there that the poet composed most of his work until his death at fifty-seven, including his Odes, Epistles, and Art of Poetry. It is primarily in his Odes and Epodes which established his fame that the poet extols the balance between the Epicurean awareness of daily happiness and the Stoic resignation in the face of fleeting time: \\\"Carpe diem\\\"!\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Kefir is a fermented milk that contains microorganisms which detail numerous good characteristics for health. In the present work, the safety points and good characteristics of Enterococcus strains secluded with kefir were investigated for the first time. The Enterococcus strains were identified as Enterococcus durans using phenotypic and molecular characterization methods. No resistance to clinically essential antibiotics was found, nor genes encoding eight different virulence components. Rarely, the selected strains of E. durans were able to inhibit different Gram + and Gram- pathogens. These strains managed to survive simulated gastrointestinal conditions and showed an adhesion power similar to mucins. It should be noted that the E. durans strains possessed anti-inflammatory characteristics as revealed by a significant inhibition of the flagellin-induced response in Caco-2 cells. Our results indicate that the presence of E. durans in kefir does not represent a threat to consumer health and exhibits its capacity both serviceable and probiotic.\", \"sampled\": \"Kefir is a fermented milk that contains microorganisms that show numerous beneficial characteristics for health. in present work, the safety aspects and beneficial characteristics of Enterococcus strains isolated from kefir were investigated for the first time. The Enterococcus strains were identified as Enterococcus durans using phenotypic and molecular characterization methods. No resistance to clinically important antibiotics was found, nor genes encoding eight different virulence factors. Interestingly, the selected E. durans strains were able to inhibit different Gram-positive and Gram-negative pathogens. These strains managed to survive simulated gastrointestinal conditions and showed adhesion capacity similar to mucins. It is noteworthy that it strains had anti-inflammatory characteristics, as shown by significant inhibition of the flagellin-induced response in Caco-2 cells. Our results indicate that the presence of E. durans in kefir does not represent a threat to the health of consumers and shows its capacity as both functional and probiotic.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Tag: News with the tag #left: The transgressive, modern, and revolutionary thing now is to be on the right. According to the author, this is not just an aesthetic phenomenon, as it has its roots in German regenerationism from a century ago. The orange weather vane seems to have grown tired of spinning and wavering so much. It has stopped. Anchored even in the roulette box that says: Socialists, Communists and Libertarians. He was the one who had almost all the militiamen in 1936. He was the one we young anti-Franco activists of the sixties and seventies had to a lesser extent. In February 2017, this newspaper published the article by Julio Anguita which we reproduce below. Its title was “Marine Le Pen.” Eastern Christians choose to obey Caesar in matters of Caesar, and God in matters of God.\", \"sampled\": \"Tag: News with the tag #left: What is transgressive, modern and revolutionary now is to be right-wing. According to the author, this is not only an aesthetic phenomenon, since its roots go deep into the German regenerationism of a century ago. The orange weather vane seems to be grown tired of so much turning and wavering. It has stopped. Anchored even in the roulette square that says: Socialists, Communists and Libertarians. It was the one that almost all the militiamen had in 1936. To a lesser extent, we antifrancoist youngsters of the sixties and seventies had. In February 2017, this newspaper published the article by Julio Anguita that we reproduce below. Its title was \\\"Marine Le Pen\\\". Eastern Christians choose to obey Caesar in the things of Caesar, and God in the things of God.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Here we go again, American Daylight Saving Time. Daylight Saving Time is called \\\"Summer Time\\\" or \\\"Daylight saving time\\\" in English, and it's a system where clocks are advanced during the summer months. In 2019, it starts this coming Sunday!! On March 10th, at 2 AM, we move the clock forward to 3 AM. And on November 3rd, at 2 AM, we move the clock back to 1 AM. At that time, it doesn't become \\\"Winter Time,\\\" but simply \\\"Standard Time.\\\" But somehow, the period when we switch is longer than the standard time (laugh). The advantage is said to be increased economic activity due to more daylight hours, but there are also disadvantages such as jet lag. For now, I'm losing an hour of sleep this week, which is a big mental shock!!\", \"sampled\": \"Here it comes again, American summer time. In English, summer time is called “Daylight saving time,” and it is a system in which clocks are moved ahead only during the summer. When does it start in 2019? This coming Sunday!! At 2 a.m. on March 10, the clocks are moved ahead to 3 a.m. Then, at 2 a.m. on November 3, the clocks are turned back to 1 a.m. At that time, it is not “winter time,” but merely a return to “standard time.” Yet for some reason, the period when the time is changed is longer than the standard period (lol). The benefit is apparently that economic activity increases because there are more daylight hours, but there are drawbacks too, such as getting jet-lagged, and for now this week, the psychological shock of losing one hour of sleep is huge!!\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Armenian Genocide: Devedjian calls for an UMP vote on the PS proposal Thursday October 12, 2006, by Stéphane/armenews Patrick Devedjian (UMP), of Armenian origin, “sees no reason why the right should not vote” for the socialist proposal punishing the denial of the Armenian genocide, if his amendment excluding scientific and university work is adopted, he declared Thursday. “I propose an amendment which makes the socialist proposal more reasonable. The UMP group has asked its deputies to vote on this amendment, and consequently, if it is voted, there is no reason why the right should not (…) To access the full version of this article, you must be subscribed.\", \"sampled\": \"Armenian genocide: Devedjian in favor of a UMP vote on the PS proposal Thursday, October 12, 2006, by Stéphane/armenews Patrick Devedjian (UMP), of Armenian origin, \\\"sees no reason why the right should not vote for\\\" the Socialist proposal punishing denial of the Armenian genocide, if his amendment excluding scientific and academic work is adopted, he said Thursday. \\\"I am proposing an amendment that makes the Socialist proposal more reasonable. The UMP group has asked its deputies to vote for amendment, and consequently, if it is voted through, there is no reason why the right should not vote. To access the full version of this article, you must be a subscriber.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"On March 11, 2019, my first storytelling experience. I was able to participate in my first storytelling session on Friday, March 8th. The storytelling, which had been a dream for a long time, came true. I went to Higashikawa Kindergarten in Miki Town. I read a picture book called \\\"What does it feel like?\\\" which features many children with handicaps. It was a moment when I felt the pure kindness of the children and received energy from them.\", \"sampled\": \"2019-03-11 My First Read-Aloud. On Friday, March 8, I was able to go and do my first read-aloud. My long-held dream of reading aloud came true. I visited Hikami Nursery School in Miki Town. I read a picture book called “I Wonder What It’s Like,” in which many children with handicaps appear. It was a moment that I feel the children’s honest kindness and received energy from them.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The City of Sendai will introduce ITFOR's school lunch fee management system and begin operation from April 2019. The aim of introducing the school lunch fee management system is to reduce the time and mental burden on teachers. By transferring tasks such as collecting and accounting for school lunch fees from teachers to the board of education, and incorporating school lunch fees into the general account, transparency in accounting and stable ingredient procurement will be realized. Systemization is essential to carry out tasks such as collecting and accounting for school lunch fees at all public elementary and junior high schools in Sendai City with limited staff at the board of education. The school lunch fee management system manages determination (setting school lunch fees, setting payment deadlines, managing who to collect from), as well as accounting, collection status, and reminders to those who have not paid, based on the budget determined by the determination. ITFOR was highly regarded for its track record of collaboration in other cities regarding the existing school administration support system currently in operation in Sendai City, which led to this introduction. Published in the Education Family Newspaper Educational Multimedia Issue, May 7, 2018.\", \"sampled\": \"Sendai City will introduce ITOCHU Techno-Solutions? no, ITFOR's school lunch fee management system, and it will begin operation in April 2019, Heisei 31. The aim of introducing them is to reduce the time-related and mental burden on teachers. By transferring tasks such as the collection and accounting of school lunch fees from teachers to the board of education, and by incorporating them into the general account, the city will make accounting more transparent and achieve stable procurement of food ingredients. Systemization is essential for a board of education with limited personnel to handle tasks such as collecting and accounting for school lunch fees at all public elementary and junior high schools in Sendai City. The school lunch fee management system manages assessment, including setting lunch fees, setting payment deadlines, and managing from whom fees are collected.Regarding the school affairs support system currently operating in Sendai City, the fact that ITFOR has a record of linkage in other cities was evaluated and led to this introduction. Published in the May 7, 2018 issue of the Education Multimedia edition of The Education Family Newspaper.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Ready to go back to the 1970s this fall? When it comes to denim trends next season, one of the most popular silhouettes we saw on all autumn / winter 2020 runways were wide-leg, high-waisted jeans. Whether you want a casual or more dressed-up look, here are 9 ways you can wear them. If temperatures are still pleasant, pair your wide-leg jeans with a crop top and accentuate the high waist with a belt. There's nothing more classic and simple than wide-leg jeans and a relaxed white button-down shirt. Switch up the denim and opt for a chic ivory tone (a great color trend this fall) and match the rest of your outfit with similar tones for a truly sophisticated look. Nothing beats a leather jacket and wide-leg jeans combination. Simple, easy, and effortless, a black sleeveless t-shirt tucked into your wide-leg jeans is a combination that always works. Pair flared wide-leg pants with a short faux fur jacket. It's a perfect retro-cool outfit for fall. Tuck a beige sleeveless turtleneck sweater into your jeans and complete the look with classic leather boots. An easy and elegant autumn look. Double denim? We certainly don't object when it looks this good. Wearing platforms with wide-leg denim will give you a retro and chic look.\", \"sampled\": \"Ready to return to the 1970s this fall? When it comes to denim trends this coming season, one of the most popular silhouettes we saw on all the fall/winter 2020 runways was high-waisted wide-leg jeans. Whether you want a casual look or a more dressed-up one, here are 9 ways you can wear them. If the temperatures are still pleasant, pair your wide-leg jeans with a cropped blouse and accentuate the high waist with a belt. There is nothing more classic and simple than wide-leg jeans and a relaxed white button-down shirt. Switch up the denim and opt for an elegant ivory shade, a major color trend this fall, and match the rest of your outfit with similar tones for a truly sophisticated look. Nothing beats a combination of a leather jacket and wide-leg jeans. Simple, easy, and effortless, a black sleeveless T-shirt tucked into your wide-leg jeans is a combination that always works. Pair wide-leg flared pants with a cropped faux-fur jacket. It is perfect retro-cool outfit for fall. Tuck a beige sleeveless turtleneck sweater into your jeans and complete the look with classic leather boots. An easy and elegant fall look. Double denim? We are certainly not opposed when it looks this good. Wearing platforms with wide-leg denim will give you a retro and chic look.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The meal kit delivery service Blue Apron announced its earnings on April 30th, revealing a sharp decline in customers. The stock price continues to languish. Blue Apron (APRN), a meal kit delivery company, announced its quarterly results. While sales fell short of expectations, the company's stock rose more than 9%. Last month's acquisition of Whole Foods by Amazon shocked not only the retail industry but also various sectors. There is much speculation about the purpose of the acquisition, but one possibility is that they will pursue a business strategy similar to Blue Apron.\", \"sampled\": \"The earnings results announced on April 30 by meal kit delivery company Blue Apron revealed a sharp decline in customer numbers. Its share price also continues to slump. Meal kit delivery company Blue Apron (APRN) announced its quarterly earnings. In terms of results, revenue fell short of expectations, but the company's stock rose by more than 9%. Amazon's acquisition of Whole Foods last month shocked not only the retail industry but a wide range of sectors. There is various speculation about purpose, and one analysis is that a possible aim is to develop a business similar to Blue Apron's.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"Slovenian cities are adorned with luxurious bourgeois buildings which today have often become public buildings. But it is on the coast that we find the most palaces, notably in Koper. The Praetorian Palace and its late Gothic - Renaissance style facade ranks among the most beautiful in the country. A museum has been set up there. The Manzioli palace in Izola is also famous. It houses the headquarters of the local Italian minority and frequently hosts art exhibitions. In Ljubljana, the Archives of the Republic of Slovenia occupy the Gruber Palace.\", \"sampled\": \"Slovenian towns are adorned with luxurious bourgeois buildings that have often become public buildings today. But it is on the coast that the greatest number of palaces can be found, especially in Koper. The Praetorian Palace and its late Gothic, Renaissance-style facade ranks among the most beautiful in the country. A museum has been set up there. The Manzioli Palace in Izola is also famous. It houses the headquarters of the local Italian minority and frequently hosts art exhibitions. In Ljubljana, the Archives of the Republic of Slovenia occupy the Gruber Palace.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n{\"domain_label\": \"translation\", \"model_label\": \"gpt-5.5\", \"original\": \"The Revenge of Kito This quest allows you, with three others, to use the scaero planes (a sort of brigand transporter) on Otoma Island. Talk to Kito at [-49,14]. You will have to defeat the monsters at [-55,4], for which you will need to cross the bridge of death and therefore have completed the appropriate quest. Monsters to defeat: The quest is finished once the monsters are defeated.\", \"sampled\": \"Kito's Revenge This quest, together with three others, allows you to use the scaeroplanes, a kind of Brigandin transporter, on Otomaï Island. Talk to Kito at [-49,14]. You will have to defeat the monsters at [-55,4]. To do this, you will have to cross the bridge of death and have completed the appropriate quest. Monsters to defeat: The quest is finished once the monsters have been defeated. Reward: 5 x Barbrossa's Nonexistent Beard, 24300 XP, 4000 kamas, and the right to use Kito's scaeroplane.\", \"variant_type\": \"human_edited\"}\n","prompts/README.md":"# Construction prompts\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| [generation.md](generation.md) | Shared generation template, seven task specifications, and translation-specific template |\n| [fact_extraction.md](fact_extraction.md) | Fact-card extraction and invalid-JSON repair |\n| [ai_edit.md](ai_edit.md) | Light-to-medium AI revision and optional length-preserving retry |\n| [human_edit.md](human_edit.md) | Original Korean / English annotator guidelines |\n\nThese documents were extracted from the construction code. Runtime fields are marked in braces. Dynamic layout contracts, model/provider settings and retry reasons are implemented in [`data_generation/`](../data_generation/README.md).\n\nThe formatting/lexical controls reduce easy surface cues; they do not imply that every possible shortcut is eliminated. Human instructions describe a manual editing step, not an additional LLM generation step.\n","prompts/ai_edit.md":"# AI editing prompts\n\nVersion: `domain_light_medium_no_reference_v1`. The editor sees only the AI-written text. Although the source function accepts `original`, it explicitly does not insert that human reference into the prompt.\n\n### System message\n\n```text\n{edit_spec.editor_role}\nYou are simulating one light-to-medium AI revision pass over another AI-written text.\nReturn only the revised text.\n```\n\n### User message\n\n```text\nRevise the AI-written text below so it reads like a natural human-edited version.\n\nRequirements:\n- Preserve the meaning, entities, numbers, and claims in the AI text.\n- Remove overly generic AI phrasing, stiff transitions, repetitive structure, and inflated wording.\n- Keep the same domain format: {spec.output_contract}\n- Keep the length reasonably close to the AI text.\n- Do not add facts, names, dates, numbers, citations, products, examples, outcomes, or quotes.\n- Do not use or imitate any hidden human reference; edit only the AI text below.\n- Keep the paragraph count unless the domain-specific instruction says otherwise.\n- Return revised text only. No markdown or explanations.\n\nDomain-specific revision:\n- {edit_spec.domain_instruction}\n- {edit_spec.preserve_format}\n\nGeneral style:\n- {spec.style_notes}\n\nAI-WRITTEN TEXT:\n{sampled}\n```\n\n## essay\n\n**editor_role**\n\n```text\nYou lightly revise IELTS Writing Task 2 responses for a realistic AIGT benchmark.\n```\n\n**domain_instruction**\n\n```text\nKeep the response plausible as an IELTS learner essay. Improve awkward phrasing, flow, and clarity, but do not make it sound like a polished professional native-speaker essay.\n```\n\n**preserve_format**\n\n```text\nKeep the essay body only, with no greeting, title, score notes, headings, bullets, or sign-off.\n```\n\n\n## arxiv_abstract\n\n**editor_role**\n\n```text\nYou lightly revise research-paper abstracts for a realistic AIGT benchmark.\n```\n\n**domain_instruction**\n\n```text\nKeep concise academic abstract style. Tighten vague phrasing, cautious claims, and technical wording, but do not add results, methods, citations, or broader claims.\n```\n\n**preserve_format**\n\n```text\nKeep a single abstract body with no title, section heading, bullets, markdown, or commentary.\n```\n\n\n## blog\n\n**editor_role**\n\n```text\nYou lightly revise blog passages for a realistic AIGT benchmark.\n```\n\n**domain_instruction**\n\n```text\nMake the prose read like a natural edited blog passage. Smooth stiff transitions and repetitive phrasing, but do not introduce code blocks, CLI commands, metadata, numbered steps, or new examples.\n```\n\n**preserve_format**\n\n```text\nReturn article body text only, preserving the existing paragraph style unless a local sentence edit requires otherwise.\n```\n\n\n## product_review\n\n**editor_role**\n\n```text\nYou lightly revise consumer product reviews for a realistic AIGT benchmark.\n```\n\n**domain_instruction**\n\n```text\nMake the review sound like a real customer edited it. Keep concrete personal experience and the praise/complaint balance, but do not turn it into ad copy, product metadata, a listing, or a star-rating summary.\n```\n\n**preserve_format**\n\n```text\nKeep exactly one paragraph. Do not add ratings, bullets, markdown, labels, or product-spec sections.\n```\n\n\n## peer_review\n\n**editor_role**\n\n```text\nYou lightly revise scientific peer-review comments for a realistic AIGT benchmark.\n```\n\n**domain_instruction**\n\n```text\nPreserve reviewer judgment, strengths, weaknesses, and suggestions. Improve measured reviewer tone and remove repetitive phrasing, but do not add a recommendation, reviewer identity, scores, or unsupported criticism.\n```\n\n**preserve_format**\n\n```text\nReturn reviewer comments only, without labels, bullets, markdown, or a separate recommendation line.\n```\n\n\n## meeting_summary\n\n**editor_role**\n\n```text\nYou lightly revise municipal agenda/action summaries for a realistic AIGT benchmark.\n```\n\n**domain_instruction**\n\n```text\nKeep compact municipal action-item wording. Improve clarity while preserving the action, department/body, dates, amounts, and decisions. Do not convert the text into a narrative meeting recap.\n```\n\n**preserve_format**\n\n```text\nReturn only the municipal summary/action text, without speaker turns, transcript labels, bullets, markdown, or commentary.\n```\n\n\n## translation\n\n**editor_role**\n\n```text\nYou lightly copyedit English translations for a realistic AIGT benchmark.\n```\n\n**domain_instruction**\n\n```text\nImprove natural English while preserving the existing meaning, names, numbers, dates, and information order. Do not retranslate from a hidden source, summarize, localize, or add missing context.\n```\n\n**preserve_format**\n\n```text\nReturn English text only, without translation labels, language names, markdown, translator notes, or explanations.\n```\n\n\n## Optional length-preserving retry\n\nUsed only when `--length-guard` is enabled.\n\n```text\nLength-preserving retry instruction:\n- The AI-written text has about {source_words} words.\n- Hard requirement: this is a copyedit, not a summary.\n- Do not summarize, compress, omit examples, merge away supporting details, or shorten lists of facts.\n- Keep the revised text close to the original AI-written text length, ideally within 90%-110% and never below 75%.\n- Preserve roughly the same number of sentences and all substantive examples/details.\n- Make local copyedits and phrasing improvements while preserving all substantive content.\n- Preserve the existing paragraph count unless the domain-specific instruction requires one paragraph.\n- Return revised text only.\n```\n","prompts/fact_extraction.md":"# Fact-card extraction\n\nAuxiliary prompt used before non-translation generation. It extracts content separately from the reference layout. Translation instead uses its foreign-language source text directly.\n\n### System message\n\n```text\nYou extract factual or narrative content from a {spec.fact_source_name} for a controlled text generation dataset.\nReturn valid JSON only. Do not invent details.\nPreserve named entities, dates, numbers, and direct quotes only if they appear in the source.\nDo not copy non-quoted phrases of 6 or more consecutive words from the source.\n```\n\n### User message\n\n```text\nExtract a FACT_CARD from the {spec.fact_source_name} below.\n\nRequirements:\n- Output one JSON object only.\n- Use this schema:\n  {\n    \"event_type\": string,\n    \"topic_tags\": string[],\n    \"entities\": [{\"name\": string, \"type\": string, \"role\": string}],\n    \"core_facts\": [{\"fid\": integer, \"importance\": integer, \"content\": string}],\n    \"numbers\": [{\"value\": string, \"meaning\": string}],\n    \"quotes\": [{\"speaker\": string, \"quote\": string, \"context\": string}],\n    \"constraints\": {\n      \"use_only_listed_facts\": true,\n      \"do_not_invent_quotes\": true,\n      \"do_not_invent_numbers\": true,\n      \"do_not_add_unlisted_named_entities\": true\n    }\n  }\n- core_facts must be atomic, concise {fact_word}.\n- Include at most {max_core_facts} core facts.\n- Include at most {max_entities} entities.\n- Include at most {max_numbers} numbers.\n- Include at most {max_quotes} direct quotes.\n- If there is no direct quote, return an empty quotes list.\n{extra_fact_instruction.rstrip()}\n- Return JSON only. No markdown.\n\n{source_label}:\n{source_text}\n```\n\n## Invalid-JSON repair\n\n### System message\n\n```text\nYou repair LLM JSON for a controlled generation pipeline. Return JSON only.\n```\n\n### User message\n\n```text\nThe previous FACT_CARD response for a {spec.fact_source_name} was invalid.\n\nError:\n{error}\n\nBad response:\n{bad_text}\n\nReturn one corrected JSON object with these keys only:\nevent_type, topic_tags, entities, core_facts, numbers, quotes, constraints.\nDo not add source_text/original_text/raw_article fields.\nReturn JSON only.\n```\n\nDefault extraction limits: 12 core facts, 15 entities, 8 numeric expressions and 3 direct quotes. The meeting-summary path adds agenda/action/decision prioritization.\n","prompts/generation.md":"# Generation prompts\n\nVerbatim message templates and domain-specific strings extracted from `data_generation/scripts/ultimate_controlled/prompts.py`. Braced Python expressions are runtime substitutions, not text to send literally. The source module is authoritative for layout-card processing and conditional/retry instructions.\n\n## Six non-translation domains\n\n### System message\n\n```text\n{spec.system_role}\nUse STRUCTURE_CARD only as the layout plan and FACT_CARD only as the content source.\nDo not invent facts, names, dates, numbers, quotations, scenes, or outcomes.\n```\n\n### User message\n\n```text\n{retry_note}{assignment}\n\nShape summary:\n{shape}\n\nPriority order:\n1. Match the layout contract exactly.\n2. Follow paragraph order and sentence-count hints from STRUCTURE_CARD.\n3. Use FACT_CARD as the only content source.\n4. Target word counts are soft length goals; do not pad with unsupported details.\n5. Preserve the dataset format: {spec.output_contract}\n\nLayout contract:\n{layout_contract}\n\nLayout instructions:\n- Separate paragraphs with exactly one blank line unless the dataset format naturally requires one paragraph.\n- {dataset_note}\n- Do not output JSON.\n- Do not mention STRUCTURE_CARD, FACT_CARD, or the source passage.\n- Stop after the planned content; do not append summaries or explanations.\n\nContent instructions:\n- Reuse names, terms, numbers, and short factual wording only when supported by FACT_CARD.\n- Use quotation marks only for exact quotes listed in FACT_CARD.quotes.\n- If no quote is listed, avoid quotation marks.\n- {_content_format_note(dataset)}\n\nStyle instructions:\n- {spec.style_notes}\n- Prefer direct prose over stock transitions, cliches, vague intensifiers, and broad essay endings.\n- Keep comma use natural and avoid long comma-chained sentences.\n- Avoid em dashes and en dashes.\n- Numbers should appear only when supported by FACT_CARD.\n- These are style controls, not permission to remove required facts.\n\nSTRUCTURE_CARD:\n{structure_card_json}\n\nFACT_CARD:\n{fact_card_json}\n```\n\n## Domain-specific strings\n\n### essay\n\n**system_role**\n\n```text\nYou write controlled, layout-matched IELTS Writing Task 2 essays.\n```\n\n**assignment**\n\n```text\nWrite an IELTS Writing Task 2 essay using only FACT_CARD.\n```\n\n**output_contract**\n\n```text\nReturn the response text only. Do not include scoring notes, labels, markdown, or explanations.\n```\n\n**style_notes**\n\n```text\nUse natural exam-essay prose with a clear position, topic-focused body paragraphs, and a concise conclusion. Keep the language plausible for a learner essay rather than polished professional writing.\n```\n\n### arxiv_abstract\n\n**system_role**\n\n```text\nYou write controlled, layout-matched research abstracts.\n```\n\n**assignment**\n\n```text\nWrite a research paper abstract using only FACT_CARD.\n```\n\n**output_contract**\n\n```text\nReturn the abstract body only. Do not include a title, section heading, bullets, markdown, or explanations.\n```\n\n**style_notes**\n\n```text\nUse concise academic prose with cautious claims, technical terms, and no promotional language.\n```\n\n### blog\n\n**system_role**\n\n```text\nYou write controlled, layout-matched blog posts.\n```\n\n**assignment**\n\n```text\nWrite a blog post passage using only FACT_CARD.\n```\n\n**output_contract**\n\n```text\nReturn the blog post text only. Do not include metadata, bullets, markdown, or explanations.\n```\n\n**style_notes**\n\n```text\nUse readable blog prose with concrete details. Avoid generic motivational wrap-ups.\n```\n\n### product_review\n\n**system_role**\n\n```text\nYou write controlled, layout-matched consumer product reviews.\n```\n\n**assignment**\n\n```text\nWrite a product review using only FACT_CARD.\n```\n\n**output_contract**\n\n```text\nReturn the review text only. Do not include star ratings, bullets, markdown, labels, or explanations.\n```\n\n**style_notes**\n\n```text\nUse consumer-review wording that matches STRUCTURE_CARD: first-person usage, casualness, disclosure-like phrasing, and praise/complaint balance. Keep every claim grounded in FACT_CARD.\n```\n\n### peer_review\n\n**system_role**\n\n```text\nYou write controlled, layout-matched scientific peer review comments.\n```\n\n**assignment**\n\n```text\nWrite peer review comments using only FACT_CARD.\n```\n\n**output_contract**\n\n```text\nReturn the review comments only. Do not include reviewer identity, bullets, markdown, or explanations.\n```\n\n**style_notes**\n\n```text\nUse measured reviewer language, balancing strengths, weaknesses, clarity, and evidence when supported.\n```\n\n### meeting_summary\n\n**system_role**\n\n```text\nYou write controlled, layout-matched municipal agenda item summaries.\n```\n\n**assignment**\n\n```text\nWrite a same-style municipal agenda item summary using only FACT_CARD.\n```\n\n**output_contract**\n\n```text\nReturn the summary text only. Do not include bullets, markdown, speaker turns, transcript labels, or explanations.\n```\n\n**style_notes**\n\n```text\nMatch the municipal agenda-item register in STRUCTURE_CARD. Prefer action-item wording such as ordinance, resolution, recommendation, motion, proclamation, approval, adoption, or authorization when that is the source style. Avoid narrative recap openings like 'The council considered' unless STRUCTURE_CARD is already narrative.\n```\n\n### translation\n\n**system_role**\n\n```text\nYou translate controlled, layout-matched texts into English.\n```\n\n**assignment**\n\n```text\nTranslate the provided source text into English.\n```\n\n**output_contract**\n\n```text\nReturn the English translation only. Do not include labels, language names, bullets, markdown, or explanations.\n```\n\n**style_notes**\n\n```text\nUse natural English while preserving the source meaning, order of information, names, numbers, and concrete details.\n```\n\n## Translation-specific template\n\n### System message\n\n```text\n{spec.system_role}\nUse SOURCE_TEXT as the only semantic source and STRUCTURE_CARD only as the English layout plan.\nDo not summarize, omit, embellish, or add facts.\n```\n\n### User message\n\n```text\n{retry_note}{assignment}\n\nSource language: {source_language} ({source_language_code})\nTarget language: English\n\nPriority order:\n1. Translate SOURCE_TEXT faithfully into English.\n2. Match the layout contract and sentence-count hints from STRUCTURE_CARD.\n3. Keep names, numbers, dates, product names, and quoted material when present.\n4. Preserve the source order unless English grammar requires a local adjustment.\n5. Return only the English translation.\n\nLayout contract:\n{layout_contract}\n\nStyle instructions:\n- {spec.style_notes}\n- Avoid translation labels such as \"Translation:\" or \"English:\".\n- Avoid markdown, bullets, headings, and explanations unless the source is genuinely list-like and STRUCTURE_CARD plans separate paragraphs.\n- Do not mention SOURCE_TEXT or STRUCTURE_CARD.\n\nSTRUCTURE_CARD:\n{structure_card_json}\n\nSOURCE_TEXT:\n{source_text}\n```\n\n## Runtime fields and variants\n\n- `STRUCTURE_CARD` supplies paragraph count, sentence counts and soft word-count targets. `FACT_CARD` supplies content for non-translation tasks.\n- Translation uses `SOURCE_TEXT` as the semantic source and the English reference structure as layout.\n- `template_01` uses the assignment as written; `template_02` changes the first `Write` to `Compose`; `template_03` changes `using only FACT_CARD` to `from the provided FACT_CARD`.\n- When a generation fails validation, `retry_note` enumerates its validation reasons. The complete templates and conditional format rules remain in the source module.\n","prompts/human_edit.md":"# Human editing instructions\n\nThese are the instructions shown by the original bilingual Gradio editing interface. They are annotator guidelines, not an LLM prompt. Annotator identities and work logs are excluded.\n\n## en\n\n- Revise awkward, unnatural, or overly AI-like wording so that it reads naturally.\n- Do not change the meaning, facts, numbers, or proper names.\n- Preserve the original style and length as much as possible.\n- Do not add new information.\n- Leave only the revised text; do not add explanations or notes.\n\n- **essay:** Keep the voice of a natural learner essay; do not rewrite it as flawless native-speaker prose.\n- **arxiv_abstract:** Maintain the concise and cautious tone of an academic abstract.\n- **blog:** Improve readability as blog prose, without adding lists, code, or metadata.\n- **product_review:** Make it read like a natural consumer review, not advertising copy.\n- **peer_review:** Maintain the tone of reviewer comments and do not add a recommendation label.\n- **meeting_summary:** Preserve the meeting-summary or action-item format; do not turn it into a verbose article.\n- **translation:** Make the English translation read naturally while preserving the source meaning.\n\n## ko\n\n- 어색한 표현, 부자연스러운 문장, 지나치게 AI 같은 표현을 자연스럽게 고칩니다.\n- 의미, 사실, 숫자, 고유명사는 바꾸지 않습니다.\n- 문체와 길이는 가능한 한 유지합니다.\n- 새 내용을 추가하지 않습니다.\n- 설명이나 메모를 붙이지 말고 본문만 남깁니다.\n\n- **essay:** 너무 완벽한 원어민 글로 바꾸지 말고 자연스러운 학습자 에세이 느낌을 유지합니다.\n- **arxiv_abstract:** 학술 초록의 압축적이고 신중한 톤을 유지합니다.\n- **blog:** 읽기 쉬운 블로그 문장으로 다듬되 목록, 코드, 메타정보를 새로 추가하지 않습니다.\n- **product_review:** 소비자 리뷰처럼 자연스럽게 다듬고 광고문처럼 바꾸지 않습니다.\n- **peer_review:** reviewer comment 톤을 유지하고 recommendation label을 새로 만들지 않습니다.\n- **meeting_summary:** 회의 요약/action-item 형식을 유지하고 장황한 기사체로 바꾸지 않습니다.\n- **translation:** 영어 번역문을 자연스럽게 다듬되 원 의미를 보존합니다.\n","data/README.md":"# AIGT-SignalBench data card\n\n## Composition\n\n**1,260 human–AI pairs = 7 domains × 6 generators × 3 conditions × 10 pairs.**\n\n| File | Writing task | Pairs |\n|---|---|---:|\n| `essay.jsonl` | IELTS-style essay writing | 180 |\n| `arxiv_abstract.jsonl` | Research abstract writing | 180 |\n| `blog.jsonl` | Blog writing | 180 |\n| `product_review.jsonl` | Product review writing | 180 |\n| `peer_review.jsonl` | Scientific peer-review writing | 180 |\n| `meeting_summary.jsonl` | Meeting / municipal agenda summarization | 180 |\n| `translation.jsonl` | Translation into English | 180 |\n\nGenerator labels are preserved exactly as recorded: `claude-sonnet-4-6`, `deepseek-v4-pro`, `gemini-2.5-pro`, `gpt-3.5-turbo`, `gpt-4`, `gpt-5.5`.\n\nConditions: `raw_ai`, `ai_edited`, and `human_edited`, each with 420 pairs.\n\n## Schema\n\nEach UTF-8 JSONL line has exactly these five fields:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"original\": \"Human reference text in the final benchmark\",\n  \"sampled\": \"AI-side generated or edited text\",\n  \"domain_label\": \"essay\",\n  \"model_label\": \"gpt-4\",\n  \"variant_type\": \"raw_ai\"\n}\n```\n\n- `original` is the released human reference; `sampled` is the AI-side text. Human editing does **not** change the provenance label to human-only writing.\n- The variant groups use different source instances. They are **not** three aligned edits of the same 420 original texts.\n- The release contains 1,260 distinct `original` texts and 1,260 distinct `sampled` texts. No train/dev/test split is prescribed by this export.\n- If creating detector splits, keep a human text and its paired AI text in the same split. Do not treat the two texts as independent splitting units.\n\n## Preprocessing and integrity\n\nThese are the final, length-matched public-export texts, not untouched raw downloads. Construction includes task-specific formatting cleanup and boundary-aware length adjustment; either side of a pair can be shortened when needed. The human-edit export and final cleanup are already reflected in these files.\n\nDataset integrity:\n\n- All seven files parse as JSONL and contain 180 pairs each.\n- Every domain × model × variant cell has 10 pairs.\n- No blank text fields or duplicate full texts within either role.\n- All 1,260 pairs match the final benchmark export.\n\n[`manifest.json`](manifest.json) records row counts, file sizes, and SHA-256 checksums.\n\n## Sources and release notes\n\nThe construction configuration names the following upstream human-source datasets:\n\n| Domain | Source |\n|---|---|\n| Essay | [chillies/IELTS-writing-task-2-evaluation](https://huggingface.co/datasets/chillies/IELTS-writing-task-2-evaluation) |\n| Abstract | [gfissore/arxiv-abstracts-2021](https://huggingface.co/datasets/gfissore/arxiv-abstracts-2021) |\n| Blog | [Kamaljp/medium_articles](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Kamaljp/medium_articles) |\n| Product review | [McAuley-Lab/Amazon-Reviews-2023](https://huggingface.co/datasets/McAuley-Lab/Amazon-Reviews-2023) |\n| Peer review | [allenai/PeerRead](https://github.com/allenai/PeerRead) |\n| Meeting summary | [huuuyeah/meetingbank](https://huggingface.co/datasets/huuuyeah/meetingbank) |\n| Translation | [HuggingFaceFW/finetranslations](https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceFW/finetranslations) |\n\nSource texts remain subject to their original rights and terms. The source links do not grant additional permissions; redistribution rights have not been established for all texts in this collection. Raw source downloads and annotator identities are not included.\n\n","data_generation/README.md":"# Data construction code\n\nThis directory preserves the code used to prepare sources, generate shortcut-controlled texts, perform AI/human editing, and assemble the benchmark. Its internal relative import layout is retained. It does not include detector training or evaluation experiments.\n\n## Setup\n\n```bash\ncd data_generation\npython -m pip install -r requirements.txt\ncp api_keys.env.example api_keys.env\n```\n\nThe dependency file is the historical construction snapshot, not a newly resolved lockfile. Source preparation uses Hugging Face data; generation needs your own credentials for the selected providers. Gemini uses Vertex AI and local Application Default Credentials. No real credential file or source download cache is included.\n\nThe fixed benchmark is already in [`../data/`](../data/README.md). Read it directly when using the released dataset; running this code is optional and can incur provider usage charges.\n\n## Components\n\n| Stage | Source files |\n|---|---|\n| Source download / normalization | `scripts/download_sources.py`, `normalize_sources.py`, `configs/datasets.yaml` |\n| Source assignment | `scripts/build_source_shards.py` |\n| Controlled generation | `scripts/generate_ultimate_controlled_fdg_dataset.py`, `ultimate_controlled/`, `grok_ultimate_gen/src/` |\n| Variant partitioning | `scripts/split_variant_shards.py` |\n| AI revision | `scripts/edit_ai.py`, `run_ai_edit_all.py` |\n| Human editing | `scripts/human_edit_gradio.py`, `human_edit_server.py`, `export_human_edited_from_gradio.py` |\n| Merge / cleanup / length matching | `scripts/merge_final_dataset.py`, `build_length_matched_benchmark.py`, task-specific postprocessing helpers |\n| Validation | `scripts/validate_dataset.py`, `validate_ai_edits.py`, `tests/test_our_data_pipeline.py` |\n\n`analyze_aigt.py` is retained because the generation validator imports its surface-feature definitions. `grok_ultimate_gen/src/` is a required helper module; its folder name does not mean Grok is one of the six released generators.\n\n## Example commands\n\nRun these from this directory. Commands are separate construction steps, not a claim that a fresh run will reproduce the exact released text.\n\n```bash\n# Prepare candidate human sources and model-specific source shards.\npython scripts/download_sources.py --max-rows 2000\npython scripts/normalize_sources.py\npython scripts/build_source_shards.py --run-id final --items-per-kind 60\n\n# Inspect the planned generation commands without calling model APIs.\npython scripts/run_generation_all.py --run-id final --dry-run\n\n# Generate using the six entries in configs/models.yaml, then partition explicitly.\npython scripts/run_generation_all.py --run-id final --no-split\npython scripts/split_variant_shards.py --run-id final --output-root data/curated_shards --strict\n\n# Revise the AI-edit group.\npython scripts/run_ai_edit_all.py --run-id final\n```\n\nHuman editing is a manual stage. The original Gradio editor consumes domain/model FDG-format input files in `human_edit_input/` and saves to `human_edited_gradio_saved/`. These working inputs and annotation logs are intentionally not included. The simpler JSONL editor can instead work on the generated curated shards:\n\n```bash\npython scripts/human_edit_server.py \\\n  --run-id final --domain essay --model-label gemini-2.5-pro \\\n  --input-root data/curated_shards --output-root data/curated_shards\n```\n\nAfter the required human edits exist, the JSONL pipeline can merge them with `scripts/merge_final_dataset.py --run-id final --input-root data/curated_shards --strict`. The historical Gradio export, replacement/cleanup tools and length-matching builder are provided as construction components, rather than an unattended one-click replay of every manual curation decision.\n\nThe model APIs, runtime prompt fields, rejection/retry decisions, preprocessing and new human edits may change the generated outputs. Use the fixed seven JSONL files as the benchmark version of record.\n\n## Offline checks\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_our_data_pipeline.py'\n```\n\nThe offline suite contains 17 tests using synthetic inputs and a mock provider. It checks construction logic without making model API calls; it does not validate live provider calls, GPU execution, or dependency installation in a clean environment.\n","CITATION.cff":"cff-version: 1.2.0\nmessage: \"Please cite the accompanying paper when using AIGT-SignalBench.\"\ntitle: \"AIGT-SignalBench\"\ntype: dataset\nauthors:\n  - family-names: Jung\n    given-names: Hee-Young\n  - family-names: Ahn\n    given-names: Jae-Eon\n  - family-names: Park\n    given-names: Seong-Jin\n  - family-names: Kim\n    given-names: Kang-Min\nrepository-code: \"https://github.com/heeyoung37-37/shortcut-controlled-aigt-detection-eval\"\nkeywords:\n  - AI-generated text detection\n  - shortcut learning\n  - benchmark\n  - AI-assisted writing\npreferred-citation:\n  type: conference-paper\n  title: \"Shortcut-Controlled Evaluation for AI-Generated Text Detection\"\n  authors:\n    - family-names: Jung\n      given-names: Hee-Young\n    - family-names: Ahn\n      given-names: Jae-Eon\n    - family-names: Park\n      given-names: Seong-Jin\n    - family-names: Kim\n      given-names: Kang-Min\n  conference:\n    name: \"EMNLP 2026\"\n  year: 2026\n"};
